B.aNb=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 68-year-old right-handed female with a history of hypertension, type 2 diabetes, and atrial fibrillation (on warfarin) is transferred to the acute inpatient rehabilitation unit on post-stroke day 3. She was found by her daughter with slurred speech and inability to move her right arm. CT angiography confirmed a left middle cerebral artery occlusion. She received IV alteplase within 3.5 hours of symptom onset. MRI shows a large left MCA territory infarct involving the frontal and parietal lobes. She has right hemiparesis (arm significantly worse than leg) and expressive aphasia with relatively preserved comprehension.")
B.aN1=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","Key areas to assess on admission to acute rehab:\n- **Stroke etiology**: Cardioembolic (atrial fibrillation) vs. large vessel atherosclerosis. Was TEE or bubble study performed?\n- **Anticoagulation status**: Warfarin was held for tPA; when to restart? INR trend?\n- **Swallowing**: Has a formal swallow evaluation been completed? Any signs of aspiration (coughing with liquids, wet vocal quality)?\n- **Bladder function**: Foley catheter present? Urinary retention vs. incontinence?\n- **Mood/Cognition**: Screen for post-stroke depression (PHQ-9). Assess attention, neglect, and executive function.\n- **Skin integrity**: Braden scale score? Any pressure injury risk areas?\n- **DVT risk**: Immobility duration, prophylaxis status (pharmacologic vs. mechanical).\n- **Prior functional level**: Independent in ADLs? Community ambulator? Driving?")
B.aSN=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 148/88 mmHg, HR 78 bpm (irregularly irregular), RR 16, SpO2 96% on room air, Temp 37.1C.\n\n**Neurologic Exam**:\n- **Mental Status**: Alert, oriented to person and place. Follows 1-step commands reliably. Non-fluent speech with effortful, telegraphic output (Broca's aphasia). Naming impaired (2/5 objects). Repetition impaired. Comprehension relatively intact for yes/no questions.\n- **Cranial Nerves**: Right lower facial droop (UMN pattern). Tongue deviates to the right. Gag reflex intact bilaterally. Visual fields full to confrontation.\n- **Motor**: Right upper extremity 1/5 throughout (flaccid). Right lower extremity 3/5 hip flexion, 3/5 knee extension, 2/5 ankle dorsiflexion. Left side 5/5 throughout.\n- **Tone**: Flaccid in right upper extremity. Mildly increased tone in right lower extremity (MAS 1).\n- **Sensation**: Diminished light touch and proprioception in the right hand. Intact in right lower extremity.\n- **Coordination**: Unable to test on right. Intact finger-to-nose on left.\n- **Functional**: Requires maximal assist for bed mobility and transfers. Unable to stand independently.")
B.aRz=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The attending physician asks you to discuss the diagnostic workup and severity assessment for this patient.")
B.aPA=new A.c("+ What is this patient's estimated NIH Stroke Scale score, and what additional diagnostic studies would you order on admission to rehab?","**Estimated NIHSS Score: approximately 12-14** (moderate stroke)\n- 1a. Level of consciousness: 0 (alert)\n- 1b. LOC questions: 1 (answers one correctly due to aphasia)\n- 1c. LOC commands: 0 (follows commands)\n- 2. Best gaze: 0 (no gaze palsy)\n- 3. Visual fields: 0 (full)\n- 4. Facial palsy: 1 (minor \u2014 UMN-pattern lower face weakness)\n- 5a. Left arm motor: 0\n- 5b. Right arm motor: 3 (some effort against gravity but cannot sustain)\n- 6a. Left leg motor: 0\n- 6b. Right leg motor: 2 (some effort against gravity)\n- 7. Limb ataxia: 0 (attributed to weakness, not ataxia)\n- 8. Sensory: 1 (partial loss)\n- 9. Best language: 2 (severe aphasia \u2014 non-fluent, limited output)\n- 10. Dysarthria: 1 (mild slurring)\n- 11. Extinction/inattention: 0\n\n**Additional studies to order**:\n1. **Echocardiogram (TTE or TEE)** \u2014 evaluate for intracardiac thrombus given atrial fibrillation\n2. **INR and CBC** \u2014 monitor anticoagulation status and for heparin-induced thrombocytopenia if on prophylactic heparin\n3. **Lipid panel and HbA1c** \u2014 optimize secondary prevention\n4. **Swallowing evaluation** \u2014 speech-language pathology bedside assessment, with VFSS if concern for silent aspiration\n5. **Baseline FIM scoring** \u2014 document admission functional status for rehab justification\n6. **ECG / telemetry** \u2014 monitor rhythm given known atrial fibrillation\n7. **PT/OT/SLP evaluations** \u2014 establish therapy goals and treatment frequency")
B.aW1=new A.c("+ The patient's INR returns at 1.3. Her cardiologist recommends switching to a DOAC. How do you manage anticoagulation in the acute rehab setting?","**Anticoagulation management considerations**:\n\n1. **Timing of restart**: Current guidelines suggest restarting anticoagulation **4-14 days** after an ischemic stroke, depending on infarct size. For a large MCA territory infarct, it is prudent to wait closer to **14 days** due to increased hemorrhagic transformation risk. A repeat CT head at day 7-10 can guide this decision.\n\n2. **DOAC selection**: For non-valvular atrial fibrillation, **apixaban 5 mg BID** or **rivaroxaban 20 mg daily** are appropriate. Apixaban has favorable data for lower bleeding risk. Confirm CrCl > 25 mL/min and check for drug interactions.\n\n3. **Bridge therapy**: While awaiting DOAC initiation, use **subcutaneous heparin (5000 units BID or TID)** for DVT prophylaxis only \u2014 this is NOT therapeutic anticoagulation. Alternatively, enoxaparin 40 mg SQ daily.\n\n4. **Fall risk consideration**: This patient is at high fall risk. While DOACs carry lower intracranial hemorrhage risk than warfarin, fall precautions must be implemented (bed alarm, hip protectors, supervised mobility).\n\n5. **Repeat imaging**: Obtain a non-contrast CT head before initiating full therapeutic anticoagulation to rule out hemorrhagic transformation of the infarct.")
B.aRL=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","The speech-language pathologist reports that the patient coughed on thin liquids during the bedside swallow evaluation and recommends a videofluoroscopic swallow study (VFSS). Results show mild penetration with thin liquids but no frank aspiration. The SLP recommends nectar-thick liquids and a soft mechanical diet.")
B.aWt=new A.c("+ Outline your comprehensive rehabilitation and medical management plan for this patient.","**Rehabilitation Program**:\n1. **Therapy intensity**: Minimum 3 hours/day of multidisciplinary therapy (PT, OT, SLP) per CMS acute rehab criteria. Goal is 15 hours/week across disciplines.\n2. **Physical Therapy**: Bed mobility training, transfer training, pre-gait activities, progressive weight-bearing on the right lower extremity. Consider body-weight-supported treadmill training when appropriate. Ankle-foot orthosis (AFO) evaluation for foot drop.\n3. **Occupational Therapy**: ADL training with adaptive equipment (rocker knife, built-up handles). One-handed techniques for dressing. Right upper extremity PROM to prevent contracture. Constraint-induced movement therapy is NOT appropriate at this stage given 1/5 strength (requires at least 10-20 degrees of active wrist extension).\n4. **Speech-Language Pathology**: Intensive aphasia therapy (melodic intonation therapy, semantic feature analysis). Augmentative and alternative communication (AAC) device assessment. Continue dysphagia management.\n\n**Medical Management**:\n5. **DVT prophylaxis**: Enoxaparin 40 mg SQ daily (until therapeutic anticoagulation is initiated). Sequential compression devices (SCDs) on bilateral lower extremities.\n6. **Dysphagia**: Nectar-thick liquids and soft mechanical diet per SLP recommendations. Upright positioning during and 30 minutes after meals. Oral care protocol to reduce aspiration pneumonia risk.\n7. **Blood pressure**: Target <140/90 per AHA secondary prevention guidelines. Continue home antihypertensives; avoid aggressive lowering in first 2 weeks.\n8. **Depression screening**: Initiate PHQ-9. If positive, consider SSRI (sertraline or citalopram \u2014 both safe post-stroke and may have motor recovery benefit).\n9. **Shoulder protection**: Arm trough on wheelchair, sling only for transfers if subluxation develops. Educate all staff on proper handling of the hemiplegic upper extremity.")
B.aR5=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The utilization review nurse questions whether this patient meets criteria for acute inpatient rehabilitation versus a skilled nursing facility (SNF). The insurance company is requesting justification.")
B.aXG=new A.c("+ Justify this patient's admission to acute inpatient rehabilitation using CMS criteria. How would you document FIM scores to support medical necessity?","**CMS Acute Inpatient Rehabilitation Criteria** (all must be met):\n1. **Medical necessity for physician supervision**: Active cardiac monitoring (atrial fibrillation with anticoagulation management), dysphagia requiring modified diet, fall risk management \u2014 requires daily physiatrist oversight that SNF cannot provide.\n2. **Ability to tolerate intensive therapy**: Patient must participate in a minimum of **3 hours per day, 5 days per week** of multidisciplinary therapy (the '3-hour rule'). Despite her deficits, she is alert, follows commands, and is motivated \u2014 she can tolerate this intensity.\n3. **Need for multidisciplinary coordinated team**: Requires simultaneous PT, OT, and SLP. Stroke is one of the **CMS-13 qualifying diagnoses** for inpatient rehabilitation.\n4. **Expectation of significant functional improvement**: Realistic goals include improving from maximal to moderate assist in transfers and ambulation within the expected length of stay (14-21 days for stroke).\n\n**FIM Score Documentation**:\n- **Admission FIM scores** (estimated): Eating 4 (min assist \u2014 modified diet), Grooming 2 (max assist), Bathing 1 (total assist), Dressing upper 1, Dressing lower 1, Toileting 2, Bladder 3 (mod assist \u2014 timed voiding), Bowel 5, Bed/chair transfer 2, Toilet transfer 2, Tub/shower 1, Walk/wheelchair 2, Stairs 1, Comprehension 5, Expression 2, Social interaction 4, Problem solving 4, Memory 5.\n- **Total admission FIM**: approximately 47/126.\n- **Projected discharge FIM**: approximately 75-85/126 (representing meaningful functional gains).\n- Document **FIM efficiency** (points gained per day) \u2014 insurance expects gains of at least 1-2 FIM points per day to justify continued stay.")
B.b_h=new A.c(u.j,"The patient's adult daughter arrives and is visibly anxious. She asks: 'Is my mother ever going to talk normally again? Will she be able to walk? I'm scared she'll never be the same.'")
B.b30=new A.c("+ Demonstrate how you would counsel this family member about prognosis and expectations.","I would use a structured, empathetic approach:\n\n**1. Acknowledge emotions first**:\n'I can see how worried you are, and that is completely understandable. Having a loved one go through a stroke is frightening, and I want to make sure you have the information you need.'\n\n**2. Provide honest, hopeful prognostic information**:\n'Your mother has had a significant stroke, and I want to be straightforward with you. The good news is that she is medically stable and she understands what we say to her \u2014 her comprehension is a real strength. Her speech difficulty is called Broca's aphasia, and with intensive speech therapy, many patients make meaningful improvement over the first 3-6 months and can continue to improve up to a year or longer. She may always have some word-finding difficulty, but most patients regain functional communication.'\n\n**3. Set realistic expectations about motor recovery**:\n'For walking, her leg has more strength than her arm, which is actually a favorable sign. Many stroke patients with this pattern do regain the ability to walk, often with an assistive device like a brace or cane. Her arm recovery is harder to predict \u2014 the arm was more severely affected, and we will work intensively with therapy to maximize what she can recover.'\n\n**4. Emphasize the role of the team and family**:\n'We have a full team dedicated to her recovery \u2014 physical therapists, occupational therapists, and speech therapists will work with her every day. Your involvement is incredibly important too. I would encourage you to attend therapy sessions when possible so you can learn how to support her at home.'\n\n**5. Invite questions and establish ongoing communication**:\n'I want you to know that I am available to answer your questions throughout her stay. We will have a family conference within the first week to set goals together. Recovery is a marathon, not a sprint, and we will be here every step of the way.'\n\n**Key communication principles demonstrated**: Empathy, honesty without false reassurance, shared decision-making, prognostic framing that acknowledges uncertainty while maintaining hope.")
B.aDN=s([B.aNb,B.aN1,B.aSN,B.aRz,B.aPA,B.aW1,B.aRL,B.aWt,B.aR5,B.aXG,B.b_h,B.b30],t.E)
B.PH=new A.a5("stroke-mca-right-hemiplegia","Acute Left MCA Ischemic Stroke \u2014 Right Hemiplegia",B.aDN,!1)
B.aSm=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 55-year-old right-handed male with a history of poorly controlled hypertension (non-adherent with medications) presents to the outpatient PM&R clinic 6 weeks after a left basal ganglia intracerebral hemorrhage (ICH). He was initially managed in the neuroscience ICU with an external ventricular drain for mild hydrocephalus, which was removed after 5 days. He completed 2 weeks of acute inpatient rehabilitation and was discharged home with his wife. He now reports increasing stiffness in his right arm and leg that is interfering with dressing, transfers, and sleep. His wife notes that his right hand is clenched in a fist and his arm pulls tightly against his chest.")
B.b_0=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","Key areas to assess in post-stroke spasticity:\n- **Onset and progression**: When did stiffness begin? Gradual vs. sudden worsening? Sudden worsening may indicate a noxious stimulus (UTI, ingrown toenail, DVT, heterotopic ossification).\n- **Functional impact**: Which specific tasks are limited? Dressing, hygiene (palm care), transfers, ambulation, sleep positioning?\n- **Pain**: Is the spasticity painful? Nocturnal spasms? Spasticity-related shoulder pain?\n- **Current medications**: Any anti-spasticity agents already trialed? Baclofen, tizanidine, dantrolene?\n- **Bowel/Bladder**: Constipation and urinary retention are common noxious triggers that worsen spasticity.\n- **Seizure history**: Post-ICH seizures occur in 10-15% of patients; anticonvulsants may have secondary anti-spasticity effects.\n- **Blood pressure management**: Is hypertension now controlled to reduce recurrent ICH risk?\n- **Mood**: Post-stroke depression and anxiety can amplify the perception of spasticity and pain.")
B.aSV=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 136/82 mmHg, HR 72 bpm, BMI 29.\n\n**Neurologic Exam**:\n- **Mental Status**: Alert, oriented x3. Speech fluent without aphasia. Cognition grossly intact (MoCA 24/30 \u2014 mild executive dysfunction).\n- **Tone (Modified Ashworth Scale)**:\n  - Right elbow flexors (biceps/brachialis): **MAS 3** \u2014 considerable increase in tone, passive movement difficult\n  - Right wrist flexors: **MAS 3** \u2014 wrist held in 40 degrees of flexion\n  - Right finger flexors: **MAS 2** \u2014 fingers clenched, can be passively extended with effort\n  - Right ankle plantarflexors: **MAS 2** \u2014 catch and resistance through range\n  - Right knee extensors: **MAS 1+** \u2014 catch followed by minimal resistance\n- **Motor (Medical Research Council scale)**: Right shoulder abduction 3/5, elbow flexion 4-/5 (limited by spasticity), wrist extension 2/5, finger extension 1/5. Right hip flexion 4/5, knee extension 4/5, ankle dorsiflexion 3/5.\n- **Passive ROM**: Right elbow extension limited to -30 degrees (unable to fully extend). Right ankle dorsiflexion limited to -10 degrees (equinovarus posturing).\n- **Clonus**: 5 beats sustained at right ankle.\n- **Gait**: Circumducts right lower extremity. Uses a single-point cane. Vaults on left side due to equinovarus on right. No AFO currently.\n- **Skin**: Maceration and early fungal infection noted in right palm due to clenched fist.")
B.aRk=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The examiner asks you to interpret the physical examination and determine the clinical significance of the findings.")
B.aT_=new A.c("+ How do you differentiate spasticity from contracture in this patient, and what is the clinical significance?","**Differentiating spasticity from contracture**:\n\n1. **Velocity-dependent testing**: Move the joint slowly, then quickly.\n   - **Spasticity**: Resistance increases with velocity. A 'catch' is felt at a threshold angle (the Tardieu angle R1). At slow speed, more ROM is achievable (R2).\n   - **Contracture**: Fixed resistance at the same angle regardless of speed. R1 = R2 (no difference between fast and slow stretch).\n\n2. **Applying the Tardieu Scale to this patient**:\n   - Right elbow: At slow speed (R2), the elbow extends to -10 degrees. At fast speed (R1), the catch occurs at -45 degrees. **R2 minus R1 = 35 degrees** \u2014 this large difference confirms a significant **dynamic (spastic) component** with relatively preserved tissue length.\n   - Right ankle: At slow speed (R2), dorsiflexion reaches -5 degrees. At fast speed (R1), the catch is at -15 degrees. **R2 minus R1 = 10 degrees** \u2014 the small difference and the fact that even R2 does not reach neutral suggests a **mixed picture with developing soft tissue contracture**.\n\n3. **Clinical significance**:\n   - The **elbow** has predominantly dynamic spasticity \u2014 highly amenable to **chemodenervation** (botulinum toxin) because the underlying tissue length is preserved.\n   - The **ankle** has a contracture component forming \u2014 intervention is more urgent to prevent fixed deformity. Aggressive stretching, serial casting, and chemodenervation should be combined.\n   - The **clenched fist with palmar maceration** is a hygiene emergency \u2014 fungal infection can worsen if the hand cannot be opened for cleaning.\n\n4. **Imaging if needed**: If contracture is suspected at the ankle, a lateral weight-bearing radiograph can rule out bony block. Ultrasound can assess muscle/tendon shortening and guide injection.")
B.aXB=new A.c("+ What is the Modified Ashworth Scale and what are its limitations?","**Modified Ashworth Scale (MAS)**:\n- **0**: No increase in tone\n- **1**: Slight increase, catch and release OR minimal resistance at end range\n- **1+**: Slight increase, catch followed by minimal resistance through less than half the ROM\n- **2**: More marked increase through most of the ROM, limb easily moved\n- **3**: Considerable increase in tone, passive movement difficult\n- **4**: Rigid in flexion or extension\n\n**Limitations**:\n1. **Ordinal scale, not interval** \u2014 the difference between MAS 1 and 2 is not equivalent to the difference between 3 and 4. Cannot perform parametric statistics.\n2. **Poor inter-rater reliability** \u2014 especially at the 1 and 1+ levels. Different examiners may score differently based on speed and force applied.\n3. **Does not distinguish spasticity from other causes of hypertonia** \u2014 cannot differentiate spastic (UMN, velocity-dependent) from rigid (extrapyramidal, non-velocity-dependent) from dystonic tone.\n4. **Does not capture functional impact** \u2014 a patient with MAS 3 may be more functional than one with MAS 2 if the spasticity is functionally useful (e.g., extensor tone assisting with standing).\n5. **The Tardieu Scale** is considered more physiologically accurate because it separately measures the dynamic (spastic) and static (contracture) components by comparing fast and slow stretch angles.")
B.aQb=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You determine that the patient has functionally limiting spasticity with a predominantly dynamic component in the upper extremity and a mixed pattern in the lower extremity. You discuss treatment options.")
B.aS3=new A.c("+ Describe your tiered approach to spasticity management for this patient.","**Tier 1 \u2014 Reversible Aggravators (address immediately)**:\n- Treat the palmar fungal infection (topical antifungal, hand hygiene program with abductor splint to open the palm)\n- Screen for and treat noxious stimuli: UTI, constipation, ingrown toenails, poorly fitting shoes\n- Optimize positioning: resting hand splint in functional position, ankle positioning boot at night\n\n**Tier 2 \u2014 Physical Modalities and Therapy**:\n- **Sustained stretching program**: 30 minutes daily of prolonged low-load stretch to elbow flexors, wrist/finger flexors, and ankle plantarflexors\n- **Serial casting**: Consider for the right ankle to progressively gain dorsiflexion range over 2-3 weeks (change cast every 5-7 days)\n- **Splinting**: Custom-fabricated resting hand splint for night use, articulated AFO for gait\n\n**Tier 3 \u2014 Oral Medications**:\n- **Baclofen 5 mg TID**, titrate to 20 mg TID as tolerated. Side effects: sedation, cognitive dulling, lowered seizure threshold.\n- **Tizanidine 2 mg at bedtime** (useful for nocturnal spasms). Monitor LFTs. Side effects: sedation, dry mouth, hypotension.\n- *Note*: Oral medications treat global spasticity but have systemic side effects. In focal/regional spasticity like this case, chemodenervation is preferred.\n\n**Tier 4 \u2014 Chemodenervation (recommended primary intervention for this patient)**:\n- **OnabotulinumtoxinA** injection to targeted muscles:\n  - Right biceps/brachialis: 100 units (elbow flexion spasticity)\n  - Right flexor carpi radialis/ulnaris: 50 units each (wrist flexion)\n  - Right flexor digitorum superficialis/profundus: 50 units each (finger clenching)\n  - Right medial gastrocnemius/soleus: 75-100 units (equinovarus)\n  - Right tibialis posterior: 50 units (foot inversion component)\n  - **Total dose**: approximately 375-400 units of onabotulinumtoxinA (FDA max 400 units per session). If higher doses are needed, consider splitting into two sessions 2 weeks apart, or using incobotulinumtoxinA (Xeomin) which has a higher approved ceiling of 400 units per treatment area\n- Use **ultrasound or EMG guidance** for accurate needle placement\n- Onset: 3-7 days. Peak effect: 2-4 weeks. Duration: 3-4 months. Schedule follow-up injection at 12 weeks.\n\n**Tier 5 \u2014 Intrathecal Baclofen (ITB) Pump**:\n- Consider if botulinum toxin fails or if spasticity becomes diffuse/bilateral\n- Requires a **screening trial**: 50-100 mcg intrathecal bolus via lumbar puncture. Positive response = reduction of at least 2 points on Ashworth within 4-6 hours.\n- Surgical implantation of programmable pump in the abdomen\n- Advantages: continuous delivery, minimal cognitive side effects, adjustable dosing\n- Risks: catheter malfunction, infection, withdrawal syndrome (potentially fatal \u2014 seizures, rhabdomyolysis, hyperthermia)")
B.aMB=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","You submit a prior authorization request for onabotulinumtoxinA injections. The insurance company denies the request, stating that the patient must first fail two oral anti-spasticity medications for at least 30 days each before chemodenervation can be approved.")
B.b1c=new A.c("+ How do you appeal this denial? What evidence and documentation would you provide?","**Peer-to-peer appeal strategy**:\n\n1. **Clinical rationale for urgency**: Document that the patient has a palmar hygiene emergency (fungal infection from clenched fist) that constitutes a medical complication requiring timely focal intervention. Delaying treatment 60+ days for oral medication trials risks permanent contracture formation and worsening skin breakdown.\n\n2. **Evidence-based argument**: Cite the **AAN Practice Guideline (2016)** which recommends botulinum toxin as a **Level A (established efficacy)** treatment for upper and lower extremity post-stroke spasticity. Oral agents have only Level C evidence and are associated with significant systemic side effects (sedation, cognitive impairment) that may impair rehabilitation participation.\n\n3. **Functional documentation**: Provide objective data \u2014 Modified Ashworth Scale scores, Tardieu angles, functional limitations (cannot dress, cannot perform hygiene, gait impairment requiring assistive device). Include photographs of the palmar maceration if possible.\n\n4. **Step-therapy exception criteria**: Many insurance formularies allow exceptions when:\n   - The required first-line agents are **medically contraindicated** (baclofen lowers seizure threshold \u2014 relevant in post-ICH patient with seizure risk)\n   - There is risk of **irreversible harm** from delay (contracture formation, skin infection)\n   - The condition is **focal, not generalized** (oral agents treat global tone; the Guidelines support focal chemodenervation for focal/regional spasticity)\n\n5. **Letter of medical necessity**: Include diagnosis codes (G81.11 \u2014 spastic hemiplegia right dominant side; G24.02 \u2014 drug-induced acute dystonia if applicable), treatment codes (CPT 64642-64645 for chemodenervation), and a clear statement that the requested treatment is not cosmetic but is medically necessary to prevent complications and restore function.\n\n6. **Escalation**: If peer-to-peer fails, file a formal appeal with supporting literature. If external review is needed, request an independent medical reviewer with PM&R or neurology board certification.")
B.aTy=new A.c(u.j,"During the informed consent discussion for botulinum toxin injections, the patient becomes visibly anxious and states: 'I don't want any needles. I heard botulinum toxin is a poison \u2014 it could paralyze me or kill me. I'd rather just live with the stiffness.'")
B.aXF=new A.c("+ How would you address this patient's fears and engage in shared decision-making?","I would use the **Ask-Tell-Ask** framework with motivational interviewing principles:\n\n**Step 1 \u2014 Ask (Explore the concern)**:\n'I appreciate you being honest with me about your concerns. Can you tell me more about what you've heard about botulinum toxin? I want to understand exactly what worries you so I can give you accurate information.'\n\n**Step 2 \u2014 Tell (Educate with empathy)**:\n'You're right that botulinum toxin comes from a bacterial protein, but the doses we use in medicine are extremely small and are injected directly into specific muscles \u2014 not into the bloodstream. It has been used safely for over 30 years in millions of patients. The medication temporarily relaxes the overactive muscles for about 3 months, then gradually wears off. It does not spread throughout the body or cause generalized paralysis.'\n\n**Address the needle fear specifically**:\n'I also hear that you are uncomfortable with needles, and that is very common. We use very thin needles \u2014 similar to acupuncture needles \u2014 and I can apply a numbing cream beforehand. The procedure takes about 15 minutes. Many patients tell me it was much less uncomfortable than they expected.'\n\n**Step 3 \u2014 Ask (Check understanding and elicit values)**:\n'Now that you have more information, I want to understand what matters most to you. You mentioned you would rather live with the stiffness \u2014 but I noticed the skin infection in your palm from not being able to open your hand. Is being able to care for yourself independently something that's important to you?'\n\n**Connect treatment to patient goals**:\n'If opening your hand to wash it, being able to dress yourself, and walking without tripping are things you want to work toward, this treatment can help us get there. But this is your decision. We can also start with stretching and an oral medication, and if those aren't enough, revisit injections later. There is no wrong answer \u2014 I just want you to have all the facts.'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Autonomy, informed consent, health literacy (avoiding medical jargon), motivational interviewing, non-coercive shared decision-making.")
B.aD0=s([B.aSm,B.b_0,B.aSV,B.aRk,B.aT_,B.aXB,B.aQb,B.aS3,B.aMB,B.b1c,B.aTy,B.aXF],t.E)
B.QS=new A.a5("stroke-ich-spasticity","Hemorrhagic Stroke \u2014 Post-ICH Spasticity Management",B.aD0,!1)
B.b_Y=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 72-year-old male with a history of hypertension, hyperlipidemia, and coronary artery disease is admitted to the acute inpatient rehabilitation unit 5 days after a right middle cerebral artery ischemic stroke. Nursing reports that he has been coughing during meals, particularly with thin liquids. He has refused several meals stating the food 'goes down the wrong pipe.' His wife reports he has lost approximately 8 pounds over the past week. He has a left hemiparesis (arm 3/5, leg 4/5) and left-sided neglect. An initial bedside swallow screen by nursing was flagged as 'fail,' and a formal speech-language pathology evaluation has been requested.")
B.b1B=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","Key areas to assess for post-stroke dysphagia:\n- **Pre-stroke swallowing history**: Any prior dysphagia, GERD, esophageal stricture, or head/neck cancer? History of recurrent pneumonias?\n- **Current oral intake**: What textures and consistencies provoke symptoms? Thin liquids vs. solids? Is the patient avoiding certain foods?\n- **Aspiration signs**: Coughing or throat clearing during/after meals, wet or gurgly vocal quality after swallowing, recurrent fevers, unexplained desaturation.\n- **Nutritional status**: Pre-admission weight, current weight, serum albumin and prealbumin levels. Calorie count ordered?\n- **Hydration**: Urine output, BUN/Cr ratio, signs of clinical dehydration.\n- **Oral health**: Dentition status, oral hygiene, presence of oral thrush (poor oral hygiene increases aspiration pneumonia risk significantly even without frank aspiration).\n- **Cognition and neglect**: Left neglect may cause the patient to miss food on the left side of the tray, pocket food in the left buccal space, and have impaired awareness of swallowing difficulty.\n- **Medications**: Any sedating medications that could impair swallow coordination? Anticholinergics causing dry mouth?")
B.aRC=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 142/78 mmHg, HR 80 bpm (regular), RR 18, SpO2 94% on room air (baseline 96%), Temp 37.4C.\n\n**General**: Thin-appearing male, appears mildly dehydrated. Mucous membranes dry.\n\n**Cranial Nerve Exam (focused on swallowing)**:\n- **CN V (Trigeminal)**: Jaw strength intact. Sensation intact bilaterally.\n- **CN VII (Facial)**: Left lower facial droop. Reduced lip seal on the left \u2014 drooling noted from left commissure. Cannot puff cheeks fully (air escapes left side).\n- **CN IX (Glossopharyngeal)**: Gag reflex present but diminished on the left. Palatal sensation reduced on left.\n- **CN X (Vagus)**: Soft palate elevates asymmetrically (left side droops). Voice has a wet, gurgly quality at baseline. Voluntary cough is weak.\n- **CN XII (Hypoglossal)**: Tongue protrusion midline. Tongue strength mildly reduced on lateral push to the left. Tongue range of motion adequate.\n\n**Bedside Swallow Evaluation (by SLP)**:\n- Ice chip trial: Delayed swallow initiation (>3 seconds). Cough after swallow.\n- 5 mL thin liquid via spoon: Cough during swallow with wet vocal quality afterward. Required 2 clearing swallows.\n- 5 mL nectar-thick liquid: Mild delay in initiation, no cough, voice clear after swallow.\n- Puree bolus (applesauce): Swallow triggered appropriately, mild oral residue in left buccal sulcus (neglect), no cough.\n- Cervical auscultation: Wet breath sounds after thin liquid trials.\n\n**Nutritional labs**: Albumin 3.0 g/dL (low), Prealbumin 12 mg/dL (low \u2014 indicates acute malnutrition), BUN/Cr ratio 28 (suggestive of dehydration).")
B.b34=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The speech-language pathologist recommends an instrumental swallow evaluation. The examiner asks you to discuss the diagnostic options.")
B.aQg=new A.c("+ Compare and contrast VFSS (videofluoroscopic swallow study) and FEES (fiberoptic endoscopic evaluation of swallowing). Which would you recommend for this patient and why?","**Videofluoroscopic Swallow Study (VFSS / Modified Barium Swallow)**:\n- **Procedure**: Patient swallows barium-coated food/liquid of various consistencies under fluoroscopy. Real-time lateral and AP views recorded.\n- **Strengths**: Visualizes all phases of swallowing (oral preparatory, oral transit, pharyngeal, upper esophageal). Can assess oral bolus control, hyoid/laryngeal excursion, UES opening, and esophageal clearance. Best for evaluating the **timing and coordination** of the entire swallow.\n- **Limitations**: Radiation exposure, requires transport to radiology, brief study (may miss intermittent aspiration), barium is not a normal food texture, cannot assess secretion management.\n- **Scales used**: **Penetration-Aspiration Scale (Rosenbek)** \u2014 1 (no entry into airway) to 8 (silent aspiration, material enters airway and no effort to eject).\n\n**Fiberoptic Endoscopic Evaluation of Swallowing (FEES)**:\n- **Procedure**: Flexible laryngoscope passed transnasally to visualize the hypopharynx and larynx. Patient swallows food dyed with green food coloring.\n- **Strengths**: Portable (can be done at bedside \u2014 ideal for patients who cannot be transported). No radiation. Can assess **secretion management**, vocal fold function, and sensation (FEESST variant with air pulse). Can use real food. Can be repeated frequently to monitor progress.\n- **Limitations**: 'White-out' phase during the pharyngeal swallow (cannot see the actual moment of swallow due to pharyngeal contraction). Cannot visualize the oral or esophageal phases. Cannot directly assess aspiration during the swallow \u2014 infers it from residue pre- and post-swallow.\n\n**Key distinction \u2014 Aspiration vs. Penetration**:\n- **Penetration**: Material enters the laryngeal vestibule but remains ABOVE the true vocal folds (PAS 2-5).\n- **Aspiration**: Material passes BELOW the true vocal folds into the trachea (PAS 6-8).\n- **Silent aspiration**: Aspiration without a cough reflex (PAS 8) \u2014 occurs in up to **40-70% of aspirating stroke patients** and is the most dangerous because it goes undetected clinically.\n\n**Recommendation for this patient**: I would order a **VFSS** as the initial study because:\n1. He needs a comprehensive assessment of all swallow phases \u2014 his bedside eval suggests both oral phase dysfunction (left-sided oral residue from neglect) and pharyngeal phase dysfunction (delayed trigger, penetration with thin liquids)\n2. We need to determine if compensatory strategies are effective (chin tuck, head turn to weaker side)\n3. He is stable enough for transport to radiology\n4. The results will guide specific diet texture recommendations and therapy targets")
B.aRm=new A.c("+ The VFSS results show penetration with thin liquids (PAS 3), aspiration with large-volume thin liquids (PAS 6 \u2014 cough response present), and mild vallecular residue with solids. Chin tuck posture eliminates penetration with thin liquids. How do you interpret these results?","**Interpretation**:\n\n1. **Primary deficit**: Pharyngeal phase dysphagia with **delayed pharyngeal swallow initiation** and **reduced laryngeal elevation/closure**, leading to penetration and aspiration with thin liquids. The cough response with aspiration (PAS 6 rather than 8) is a positive sign \u2014 he is not a silent aspirator.\n\n2. **Oral phase findings**: Vallecular residue with solids suggests **reduced tongue base retraction** \u2014 the bolus is not being propelled posteriorly with sufficient force. Left buccal pocketing from neglect contributes to oral residue.\n\n3. **Compensatory strategy effectiveness**: The **chin tuck eliminated penetration** with thin liquids \u2014 this is a favorable finding. The chin-tuck posture narrows the airway entrance and widens the vallecular space, providing additional airway protection during the swallow.\n\n4. **Aspiration risk stratification**: This patient is at **moderate aspiration risk**. He aspirates with large volumes of thin liquids but has a protective cough reflex and responds to postural compensation. He is NOT a candidate for NPO status or a feeding tube at this time.\n\n5. **Diet recommendation based on VFSS**: **IDDSI Level 4 (pureed to soft) solids** with **IDDSI Level 2 (mildly thick / nectar-thick) liquids**. Can trial thin liquids with strict chin-tuck posture under SLP supervision and advance as tolerated.")
B.aMA=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Based on the VFSS results, you develop a comprehensive dysphagia management plan. The patient is frustrated with the modified diet and is losing motivation.")
B.aT2=new A.c("+ Outline your treatment plan including diet modifications, compensatory strategies, and rehabilitative swallowing therapy.","**A. Diet Modifications**:\n1. **Liquids**: IDDSI Level 2 (mildly thick) for all unmonitored intake. May trial thin liquids (IDDSI Level 0) with chin-tuck posture during supervised meals only.\n2. **Solids**: IDDSI Level 5 (minced and moist) to Level 6 (soft and bite-sized). Avoid mixed consistencies (e.g., cereal in milk, soup with chunks) as these are the highest-risk textures.\n3. **Calorie supplementation**: Add protein shakes (thickened to nectar consistency), calorie-dense foods (avocado, nut butters mixed into purees). Target >1500 kcal/day. Daily calorie counts for 1 week.\n4. **Hydration**: Track intake/output. Target 1500 mL/day. If unable to meet oral hydration needs, consider IV fluid supplementation.\n\n**B. Compensatory Strategies**:\n1. **Chin tuck**: During all liquid intake. Instruct patient to 'tuck your chin to your chest before and during the swallow.'\n2. **Effortful swallow**: Bear down during the swallow to increase tongue base retraction and improve vallecular clearance.\n3. **Double swallow technique**: Swallow twice per bolus to clear pharyngeal residue.\n4. **Left cheek sweep**: After each bite, use the tongue or finger to sweep the left buccal sulcus to clear pocketed food (addresses neglect-related residue).\n5. **Upright positioning**: Sit at 90 degrees during meals and remain upright for **30 minutes** after eating.\n6. **Small bolus sizes**: Use a teaspoon (not tablespoon) for liquids. Take single sips rather than continuous drinking.\n7. **Reduce distractions**: Turn off TV, minimize conversation during meals to improve swallow attention.\n\n**C. Rehabilitative Swallowing Therapy (SLP-directed)**:\n1. **Lingual strengthening exercises**: Tongue press against palate, lateral tongue resistance with tongue depressor. Goal: improve tongue base retraction to reduce vallecular residue.\n2. **Mendelsohn maneuver**: Voluntarily hold the larynx in the elevated position during the swallow to prolong UES opening. Practice 10 repetitions, 3 times daily.\n3. **Shaker exercise (head lift)**: Supine head raises (sustained and repetitive) to strengthen suprahyoid muscles that open the UES.\n4. **Expiratory muscle strength training (EMST)**: Resistive expiratory device to improve cough strength and subglottic pressure.\n5. **Neuromuscular electrical stimulation (NMES)**: VitalStim or similar device to the submental/laryngeal muscles as adjunct during therapy. Evidence is mixed but may augment conventional therapy.\n\n**D. Oral Hygiene Protocol**:\n- Chlorhexidine oral rinse (0.12%) twice daily \u2014 **reduces aspiration pneumonia risk** even in patients who aspirate, by reducing oral bacterial load.\n- Supervised tooth brushing after every meal.\n- This is one of the most impactful and underutilized interventions for aspiration pneumonia prevention.\n\n**E. Monitoring and Reassessment**:\n- Weekly weight checks\n- Prealbumin levels every 2 weeks to track nutritional recovery\n- Repeat instrumental swallow study (FEES at bedside) in 2-3 weeks to assess for improvement and diet advancement\n- If no progress after 4 weeks, discuss nasogastric tube or PEG placement with patient and family")
B.aRq=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The dietary services department reports difficulty consistently preparing the correct thickened liquids and modified textures for this patient. Nursing reports that night shift staff are unsure which diet the patient is on. The patient was found drinking unthickened water from a cup left at his bedside by a well-meaning visitor.")
B.b_F=new A.c("+ How do you address these systems-level failures to ensure safe dysphagia management?","**Interdisciplinary coordination strategy**:\n\n1. **Standardized communication**:\n   - Ensure the **IDDSI framework** is used consistently across all disciplines (SLP, dietary, nursing, physicians). IDDSI provides standardized terminology and testing methods (e.g., fork drip test, spoon tilt test) to verify food and liquid consistency.\n   - Place a **bedside diet sign** with clear, large font: 'IDDSI Level 2 Mildly Thick Liquids / IDDSI Level 5 Minced & Moist Solids / CHIN TUCK WITH ALL LIQUIDS / NO THIN LIQUIDS AT BEDSIDE.'\n   - Update the diet order in the EMR with specific instructions and ensure dietary receives automated notifications for any changes.\n\n2. **Nursing education and protocols**:\n   - Brief the nursing staff (all shifts) during huddle: explain what dysphagia precautions mean, the risk of aspiration pneumonia, and the rationale for the modified diet.\n   - Implement a **meal tray verification** protocol: nursing checks each tray against the bedside diet sign before serving.\n   - Assign **supervised meal assistance** \u2014 this patient needs monitoring during meals due to neglect (food pocketing) and required compensatory strategies.\n\n3. **Dietary services quality control**:\n   - Schedule a meeting with the dietary manager to review proper IDDSI preparation standards. Provide the IDDSI framework testing tools.\n   - Request a dedicated 'dysphagia diet preparation station' or designated staff member trained in thickened liquid preparation.\n   - Implement pre-thickened commercial products (e.g., pre-thickened juice, gel water cups) when available to reduce preparation variability.\n\n4. **Visitor/family education**:\n   - Conduct a formal family meeting with the patient's wife to explain aspiration risk. Teach her which foods/liquids are safe and which are prohibited.\n   - Remove all thin liquids from the patient's room. Provide thickened water and approved beverages in clearly labeled containers.\n   - Post a 'NOTHING BY MOUTH UNLESS APPROVED BY NURSING' sign near the patient's bed.\n\n5. **Incident reporting and follow-up**:\n   - File an incident report for the thin water event (near-miss for aspiration).\n   - Use the event as a **learning opportunity** at the next interdisciplinary team meeting \u2014 not to assign blame, but to identify system gaps.\n   - Implement a weekly dysphagia safety audit for the first month.")
B.aOq=new A.c(u.j,"The patient's wife arrives with a container of homemade soup and says: 'The hospital food is terrible and he's starving. I made his favorite chicken noodle soup \u2014 he's been eating it for 50 years without any problems. I'm going to feed it to him now. You doctors are overreacting about this swallowing thing.'")
B.aWj=new A.c("+ How do you respond to this family member who wants to provide food that is not consistent with the prescribed diet?","I would use a calm, respectful, and educational approach while maintaining patient safety:\n\n**1. Validate the concern and intent**:\n'Mrs. [Name], I can tell how much you care about your husband, and I completely understand your frustration. You're right that the modified diet food doesn't taste as good as your home cooking, and I wish we could give him whatever he wanted. The fact that you made his favorite soup shows how dedicated you are to his comfort.'\n\n**2. Explain the danger in understandable terms**:\n'The reason we are being so careful is that the stroke has affected the muscles and nerves that control swallowing. Even though he ate soup perfectly fine before, his swallow works differently now. We did a special X-ray test and saw that thin liquids \u2014 like the broth in soup \u2014 actually go toward his lungs instead of his stomach. The noodles and chunks of chicken mixed with broth are what we call a mixed consistency, which is the most difficult texture to swallow safely. If food enters his lungs, it can cause a serious pneumonia that could put him back in the ICU.'\n\n**3. Offer a constructive alternative**:\n'Here is what I would suggest: I will work with our dietary team to see if we can puree your chicken soup to a safe consistency \u2014 that way he gets the flavor he loves in a form that is safe for him to swallow. You can also bring in other foods from home as long as we check the texture first. I want him to enjoy eating again \u2014 we just need to do it safely.'\n\n**4. Enlist her as a partner, not an adversary**:\n'You are going to be a critical part of his recovery, especially when he goes home. Would you be willing to meet with our speech therapist this week? She can show you exactly which foods are safe, how to thicken liquids properly, and what to watch for when he's eating. Many families find that training very reassuring.'\n\n**5. Address the specific safety issue firmly but kindly**:\n'For today, I have to ask that we not give him the soup in its current form. I know that is disappointing, but my job is to keep him safe while he recovers. Let me get the dietary team to work on a version he can have, and we will bring it to him as soon as it is ready.'\n\n**Key principles**: Empathy and validation first, health literacy (no jargon), concrete explanation of risk, offering alternatives rather than just saying no, empowering the family as partners in care, firm but respectful boundary-setting.")
B.aCK=s([B.b_Y,B.b1B,B.aRC,B.b34,B.aQg,B.aRm,B.aMA,B.aT2,B.aRq,B.b_F,B.aOq,B.aWj],t.E)
B.Rd=new A.a5("stroke-dysphagia","Post-Stroke Dysphagia & Aspiration Risk",B.aCK,!1)
B.aTB=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 60-year-old right-handed female retired schoolteacher is seen in the outpatient PM&R clinic 3 weeks after a right middle cerebral artery ischemic stroke. She completed a brief acute inpatient rehabilitation stay and was discharged home with her husband. She has a residual left hemiparesis (arm 4/5, leg 4+/5) and is ambulatory with a single-point cane. Her husband reports that she bumps into doorframes on the left side, leaves food on the left half of her plate, and does not respond when he speaks from her left side. He is also concerned because she insists on driving to the store, stating 'I feel perfectly fine.' She did not acknowledge any deficits during her rehab stay and her therapists documented 'limited awareness of left-sided deficits.'")
B.b0N=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","Key areas to assess for hemispatial neglect:\n- **Awareness of deficits (anosognosia)**: Does the patient acknowledge any left-sided weakness or visual difficulty? Anosognosia is common with right hemisphere strokes and is a major barrier to rehabilitation.\n- **Safety incidents**: Falls, bumping into objects, burns on the left hand from not recognizing hot surfaces, leaving stove burners on.\n- **ADL function**: Does she eat from only the right side of the plate? Can she dress the left side of her body? Does she groom only the right side of her face?\n- **Reading and writing**: Does she miss the left side of text (reading starts mid-line)? This is called 'neglect dyslexia.'\n- **Pre-stroke function**: Was she independent in all IADLs? Driving history? Employment status?\n- **Mood**: Right hemisphere strokes are associated with emotional dysregulation, flat affect, and aprosodia (inability to convey/interpret emotional tone).\n- **Visual symptoms**: Does she report any vision loss? Patients with pure neglect often deny any visual difficulty, whereas patients with hemianopia may report 'not seeing well on one side.'\n- **Medications**: Review for any sedating agents that could worsen cognitive function.")
B.aVG=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 132/76 mmHg, HR 74 bpm (regular), RR 14, SpO2 98% on room air.\n\n**Neurologic Exam**:\n- **Mental Status**: Alert but demonstrates flat affect. Oriented to person, place, and time. Speech fluent but with reduced prosody (monotone). When asked 'How are you doing since the stroke?' she responds 'Fine, I'm ready to get back to normal.'\n- **Visual Fields (Confrontation testing)**: Reduced detection of finger wiggle in the left visual field bilaterally. When tested with simultaneous bilateral stimulation (double simultaneous stimulation/extinction testing), she consistently extinguishes the left-sided stimulus \u2014 reports only the right.\n- **Neglect-specific testing**:\n  - **Line bisection test**: Bisects horizontal lines significantly to the RIGHT of true center (average deviation 3.2 cm to the right across 5 lines).\n  - **Star cancellation test**: Crosses out 27/27 stars on the right half of the page but only 8/27 on the left half. Entire left upper quadrant missed.\n  - **Clock drawing**: Numbers 1-8 are crammed into the right half of the clock face. Left half is empty.\n  - **Scene copying**: When asked to copy a simple house drawing, she omits the left wall, left window, and left side of the roof.\n- **Motor**: Left deltoid 4/5, biceps 4/5, wrist extensors 4/5, grip 4/5, hip flexors 4+/5, knee extensors 4+/5, ankle dorsiflexors 4/5. Right side 5/5 throughout.\n- **Sensation**: Reports intact sensation bilaterally when tested individually. However, with double simultaneous stimulation, consistently extinguishes left-sided touch.\n- **Gait**: Ambulates with single-point cane in right hand. Tends to veer leftward (bumps into left wall). Does not scan to the left when turning corners. Near-fall in clinic when she did not see a chair on her left side.")
B.aU7=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The examiner asks you to synthesize the examination findings and explain the neuroanatomic basis of this patient's deficits.")
B.aW0=new A.c("+ Distinguish hemispatial neglect from homonymous hemianopia in this patient. Does she have one or both, and what is the neuroanatomic explanation?","**This patient has BOTH left hemispatial neglect AND a probable left homonymous hemianopia \u2014 but the neglect is the dominant and more functionally limiting deficit.**\n\n**Distinguishing the two conditions**:\n\n1. **Homonymous hemianopia (HH)**:\n   - A visual field cut affecting the same side in both eyes (left temporal field of right eye + left nasal field of left eye) due to damage to the right optic radiation or visual cortex.\n   - Patients with pure HH are typically **aware** of the deficit and **compensate** by turning their head/eyes to the affected side.\n   - Confrontation testing shows consistent loss of the left visual field.\n   - Can be confirmed with **formal perimetry** (Humphrey or Goldmann visual field testing).\n\n2. **Hemispatial neglect**:\n   - A failure of **attention** to the contralesional side of space, NOT a primary sensory deficit.\n   - Caused by damage to the **right posterior parietal cortex (inferior parietal lobule)**, **right temporoparietal junction**, or **right inferior frontal gyrus** \u2014 areas that form the ventral attention network.\n   - Key distinguishing features in this patient:\n     a. **Extinction on double simultaneous stimulation** \u2014 she detects left-sided stimuli when presented alone but misses them when competing right-sided stimuli are present. This is pathognomonic for neglect, not hemianopia.\n     b. **Line bisection deviation to the right** \u2014 the patient's internal representation of space is shifted rightward. A patient with pure HH would bisect near center or slightly to the left (toward the blind field).\n     c. **Asymmetric cancellation performance** \u2014 missing left-sided targets in a visual search task reflects attentional neglect.\n     d. **Clock drawing with right-side crowding** \u2014 this is a representational (internal spatial map) deficit, not a visual field cut.\n     e. **Anosognosia** \u2014 denial of deficits is characteristic of right hemisphere neglect, not hemianopia.\n\n3. **Neuroimaging correlation**:\n   - A right MCA stroke typically damages the right parietal and temporal cortex. The right **inferior parietal lobule and superior temporal gyrus** are critical for spatial attention. Damage here produces the most severe and persistent neglect.\n   - If the optic radiations (running through the temporal and parietal white matter) are also involved, a left homonymous hemianopia co-occurs.\n   - Order **formal visual field testing (Humphrey perimetry)** to confirm and quantify the hemianopia separately from the neglect.\n\n4. **Why this distinction matters clinically**:\n   - Hemianopia can be partially compensated with consistent head turning \u2014 patients with awareness will adapt.\n   - Neglect is much harder to remediate because the patient is **unaware** of what they are missing. They do not spontaneously compensate.\n   - Neglect is the **strongest predictor of poor functional outcome** after stroke, more so than motor weakness.\n   - Driving safety is profoundly impacted by neglect \u2014 even if visual fields recover, persistent neglect is an absolute contraindication to driving.")
B.b0a=new A.c("+ What additional standardized assessments would you order to comprehensively evaluate this patient's neglect?","**Standardized neglect assessments**:\n\n1. **Behavioral Inattention Test (BIT)** \u2014 the gold standard comprehensive neglect battery:\n   - **Conventional subtests**: Line crossing, letter cancellation, star cancellation, figure copying, line bisection, representational drawing.\n   - **Behavioral subtests**: Menu reading, article reading, map navigation, address copying, telephone dialing, coin sorting, card sorting.\n   - Provides a composite score with clear cutoffs for clinically significant neglect.\n\n2. **Catherine Bergego Scale (CBS)** \u2014 assesses the **functional impact** of neglect in daily activities:\n   - 10 items scored 0-3: grooming (left side of face), dressing (left sleeve/shoe), eating (food on left side of plate), mouth cleaning, gaze orientation, knowledge of left limb, auditory attention to the left, collisions with objects on left, navigation/finding way.\n   - Can be scored by therapist observation (objective) or patient self-report. The **discrepancy** between the two scores quantifies anosognosia.\n\n3. **Formal visual field testing (Humphrey automated perimetry)** \u2014 to isolate and quantify the hemianopia separately from attentional neglect.\n\n4. **Functional Independence Measure (FIM)** \u2014 to document the impact of neglect on overall functional status and track improvement.\n\n5. **Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA)** \u2014 to screen for concurrent executive dysfunction, which commonly co-occurs with neglect after right hemisphere stroke.\n\n6. **Driving-specific assessments** (when appropriate):\n   - **Useful Field of View (UFOV)** test \u2014 measures divided attention and processing speed under visual field constraints.\n   - Formal on-road driving evaluation by a certified driver rehabilitation specialist (CDRS) \u2014 this is the definitive assessment but should NOT be ordered until neglect has been maximally treated.")
B.aUf=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You confirm that the patient has significant left hemispatial neglect with probable left homonymous hemianopia. Her BIT score falls well below the cutoff, and the Catherine Bergego Scale confirms moderate neglect with poor self-awareness. You develop a cognitive rehabilitation plan.")
B.aXC=new A.c("+ Describe your comprehensive treatment approach for this patient's hemispatial neglect.","**A. Visual Scanning Training (Top-Down Approach)**:\n- Systematic training to actively scan leftward during functional tasks.\n- Begin with structured tabletop exercises: reading tasks with a red anchor line on the left margin ('find the red line before you start reading each line'), cancellation tasks with progressive difficulty.\n- Progress to functional scanning: teach the patient to scan left before navigating doorways, crossing streets, reaching for items.\n- Use a **lighthouse strategy**: imagine a lighthouse beam sweeping from far left to right before initiating any task.\n- Frequency: daily, 30-45 minute sessions with OT.\n\n**B. Prism Adaptation Therapy (Bottom-Up Approach)**:\n- Patient wears prism glasses that shift the visual field approximately **10 degrees to the right**.\n- During therapy, she performs repeated pointing tasks (reaching for targets) while wearing the prisms. After removal, the **sensorimotor aftereffect** shifts her spatial attention leftward.\n- **Evidence**: Multiple RCTs demonstrate that even brief sessions (20 minutes) of prism adaptation improve neglect symptoms, and effects can persist for hours to weeks.\n- Protocol: 10-20 minutes of prism adaptation daily for 2 weeks, followed by reassessment.\n- Can be combined with scanning training for synergistic effect.\n\n**C. Limb Activation / Spatiomotor Cueing**:\n- Have the patient actively use the **left hand** in left hemispace before initiating tasks. Active movement of the contralesional limb in contralesional space activates the damaged right hemisphere attention networks.\n- Example: Before eating, reach with the left hand to touch the left edge of the tray.\n\n**D. Environmental Modifications**:\n- Position important items (call bell, phone, water) on the LEFT side \u2014 this forces the patient to attend leftward. This is counterintuitive but supported by evidence.\n- Place a bright-colored tape strip on the left side of doorframes, wheelchair armrests, and walkways as visual anchors.\n- At meals, turn the plate 180 degrees halfway through the meal, or use a plate with a high-contrast left-side marker.\n\n**E. Technology-Assisted Rehabilitation**:\n- **Virtual reality (VR) neglect training**: Immersive environments that require leftward exploration. Emerging evidence supports improved spatial awareness.\n- **Eye-tracking feedback**: Real-time feedback on gaze patterns can increase awareness of scanning asymmetry.\n\n**F. Addressing Anosognosia**:\n- This is the most critical barrier to rehabilitation. If she does not believe she has a deficit, she will not engage in compensatory strategies.\n- **Video feedback**: Record her performing tasks (eating, walking) and show her the footage. Seeing herself leave food on the left or bump into objects on video can be a powerful tool for building awareness.\n- **Structured confrontation**: During therapy, the therapist guides her to discover the deficit herself (e.g., 'Can you count all the stars on the page? Are you sure you got them all? Let me help you check the left side.').\n- Avoid argumentative approaches \u2014 anosognosia is a neurologic deficit, not a psychological denial. Patience and repeated gentle confrontation over time.\n\n**G. Reassessment timeline**:\n- Repeat BIT and Catherine Bergego Scale at 4 weeks and 3 months.\n- Most neglect recovery occurs in the first 3 months but can continue up to 12 months.\n- Approximately 40% of patients have persistent neglect at 1 year \u2014 these patients require long-term compensatory strategies and environmental modifications.")
B.aVJ=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","At the 3-month follow-up visit, the patient's neglect has improved on formal testing (BIT score improved but still below normal). Her motor deficits have largely resolved. She continues to insist that she is ready to drive and asks you to clear her for driving. She has also asked about returning to work as a substitute teacher.")
B.aZG=new A.c("+ How do you approach the driving evaluation referral, and what are the medicolegal considerations? How do you address her request to return to work?","**Driving evaluation and clearance**:\n\n1. **Current driving status**: The patient should NOT be driving at this time. Residual hemispatial neglect, even if improved, poses an unacceptable safety risk for herself and others. I would clearly document in the chart that I have advised her not to drive.\n\n2. **Referral to a Certified Driver Rehabilitation Specialist (CDRS)**:\n   - A CDRS conducts a comprehensive evaluation including: clinical visual/cognitive assessment, behind-the-wheel road test in a dual-controlled vehicle, assessment of reaction time, divided attention, and scanning behavior in real traffic conditions.\n   - The CDRS can also recommend adaptive equipment or vehicle modifications if appropriate.\n   - Locate a CDRS through the Association for Driver Rehabilitation Specialists (ADED) directory.\n\n3. **Pre-driving assessments to complete first**:\n   - **Formal visual field testing** (Humphrey perimetry) \u2014 many states require a minimum binocular visual field (typically 110-140 degrees horizontal) for unrestricted licensure.\n   - **Useful Field of View (UFOV)** test \u2014 evaluates divided attention and processing speed. An abnormal UFOV is the strongest predictor of on-road driving failure after stroke.\n   - Updated **BIT/Catherine Bergego Scale** \u2014 must demonstrate near-normal or normal scores before on-road testing is justified.\n\n4. **Medicolegal considerations**:\n   - **Physician reporting obligations vary by state**. Some states (e.g., California, Pennsylvania) require mandatory physician reporting of conditions that impair driving. Other states have voluntary reporting. Know your state's law.\n   - Document the conversation in detail: that you advised against driving, the medical rationale, and that the patient was informed of the risks.\n   - If she drives despite medical advice and causes an accident, the physician may have liability if the counseling was not documented or the appropriate referrals were not made.\n   - Consider involving the family (husband) as an ally in enforcing the driving restriction.\n\n5. **Return to work assessment**:\n   - Teaching requires sustained attention, multitasking, spatial awareness (monitoring an entire classroom), reading/writing, and safety supervision of children. Residual neglect would impair all of these.\n   - Refer for a **Functional Capacity Evaluation (FCE)** tailored to cognitive demands of the job.\n   - Contact her employer's HR department (with her consent) to explore **reasonable accommodations** under the ADA \u2014 e.g., a classroom aide, reduced class size, preferential seating arrangements.\n   - If she cannot meet the essential functions of the job even with accommodations, discuss **Social Security Disability Insurance (SSDI)** application. She may qualify based on residual cognitive deficits from stroke.\n   - Document work capacity limitations clearly for any disability applications.")
B.aWD=new A.c(u.j,"Despite your counseling, the patient becomes upset and states: 'I don't understand what the problem is. I can see fine. I walk fine. My arm works. There is nothing wrong with me. I drove for 40 years without a single accident. You are taking away my independence and it is not fair. I'm going to drive whether you like it or not.'")
B.b1I=new A.c("+ How do you manage this conversation with a patient who has anosognosia and is refusing to accept driving restrictions?","This is one of the most challenging conversations in rehabilitation medicine because **anosognosia is a neurologic deficit, not stubbornness or denial**. The patient literally cannot perceive her own impairment. I would approach this with patience, empathy, and firm safety boundaries.\n\n**1. Acknowledge her feelings and validate her frustration**:\n'I hear you, and I understand how frustrating this must be. Driving means independence, and I can see that being told you cannot drive feels like we are taking something important away from you. You have every right to be upset, and I respect that.'\n\n**2. Use concrete demonstration rather than argument**:\n'I want to show you something. When I tested your ability to find targets on a page, you found all of them on the right side but missed many on the left side \u2014 and you were certain you had found them all. This is what your brain is doing when you drive: there could be a pedestrian, a cyclist, or a car approaching from your left, and your brain would not alert you to it. You would feel completely confident, but the danger would be invisible to you.'\n\n**3. Involve the family as allies (with the patient present)**:\n'Mr. [husband's name], can you share what you have observed at home?' (The husband can describe her bumping into things on the left, missing food on the plate \u2014 concrete examples from her daily life that she has not been able to recognize on her own.)\n\n**4. Frame this as temporary and offer a path forward**:\n'I am not saying you will never drive again. What I am saying is that right now, it is not safe \u2014 and I would not be doing my job if I let you do something that could hurt you or someone else. Here is my plan: we continue intensive therapy targeting this specific problem. In 4-6 weeks, I will retest you. If your scores improve to the normal range, I will refer you to a driving specialist who will take you out on the road in a car with dual controls and evaluate you in real traffic. If you pass that test, you can drive. I want to help you get there.'\n\n**5. Address the safety boundary directly and compassionately**:\n'I need to be honest with you about something important. If you choose to drive against medical advice and something happens, there could be serious legal consequences for you \u2014 including loss of your license, liability for any injuries, and potential criminal charges. More importantly, if you hurt someone or yourself, that is something we could have prevented. I care about your safety, and I care about the people on the road.'\n\n**6. Document and follow through**:\n- Document the conversation in detail in the medical record.\n- Provide written instructions stating she should not drive.\n- If your state has mandatory reporting, follow the appropriate reporting process.\n- Schedule close follow-up (2-4 weeks) to reassess and maintain the therapeutic relationship.\n- Consider a referral to neuropsychology for formal cognitive assessment and additional anosognosia intervention.\n\n**Key principles**: Respect for autonomy balanced with duty to protect (beneficence and non-maleficence), recognition that anosognosia is neurologic not volitional, concrete demonstration over abstract argument, offering a pathway to the desired goal, clear documentation, and knowledge of state-specific reporting obligations.")
B.aCX=s([B.aTB,B.b0N,B.aVG,B.aU7,B.aW0,B.b0a,B.aUf,B.aXC,B.aVJ,B.aZG,B.aWD,B.b1I],t.E)
B.PL=new A.a5("stroke-neglect","Hemispatial Neglect & Visual Field Cut",B.aCX,!1)
B.aYB=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 58-year-old right-handed male is seen on the acute inpatient rehabilitation unit 8 weeks after a left frontal ischemic stroke resulting in mild right hemiparesis and non-fluent aphasia. His wife reports that he has become increasingly withdrawn from therapy sessions over the past 2 weeks, spending most of the day in bed. She describes frequent, sudden crying episodes that seem disproportionate to the situation, stating: "He starts sobbing when I walk in the room, but then stops just as fast." His FIM scores have plateaued despite adequate motor recovery potential.')
B.aNG=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","In assessing mood and behavioral changes after stroke, your evaluation should focus on:\n- **Pre-Stroke Psychiatric History**: Any prior depression, anxiety, or psychiatric medication use.\n- **Temporal Onset**: Acute vs. gradual change in mood; relationship to stroke onset and lesion location (left frontal lesions carry highest risk for PSD).\n- **Characterization of Crying Episodes**: Involuntary vs. mood-congruent; duration; triggers; associated subjective sadness.\n- **Functional Impact**: Decline in therapy participation, ADL engagement, appetite, and sleep patterns.\n- **Medication Review**: Beta-blockers, benzodiazepines, or other medications that may exacerbate depressive symptoms.\n- **Substance History**: Pre-stroke alcohol use and current withdrawal risk.\n- **Social Supports**: Caregiver burden, family dynamics, and discharge environment.\n- **Screening Tools**: PHQ-9 (adapted for aphasia if needed), Stroke Aphasic Depression Questionnaire (SADQ) for patients with significant language deficits.")
B.aSp=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Vitals**: BP 138/82, HR 68, RR 14, Temp 98.4F, SpO2 97% on RA.\n- **General**: Appears fatigued, poor eye contact, psychomotor retardation noted. Flat affect at baseline but demonstrates two spontaneous crying episodes during the exam lasting 15-20 seconds each, self-resolving.\n- **Neurological**: Alert, oriented x3. Non-fluent aphasia with 3-4 word phrases, comprehension intact for 2-step commands. Right upper extremity 4-/5 proximally, 3+/5 distally. Right lower extremity 4/5 throughout. Sensation intact. No neglect.\n- **Mental Status**: PHQ-9 score of 16 (moderately severe depression). Patient endorses anhedonia, poor sleep, fatigue, and feelings of worthlessness. Denies suicidal ideation.\n- **Functional Status**: Mod-A for transfers (previously Min-A), requiring increased cueing for participation in OT/PT sessions.")
B.b_P=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The co-occurrence of depressive symptoms (withdrawal, anhedonia, PHQ-9 = 16) and involuntary, incongruent crying episodes suggests **both Post-Stroke Depression (PSD) and Pseudobulbar Affect (PBA)** may be present simultaneously.")
B.aSL=new A.c("+ Distinguish Post-Stroke Depression (PSD) from Pseudobulbar Affect (PBA) and outline the diagnostic approach.","**Post-Stroke Depression (PSD)**:\n1. Sustained low mood, anhedonia, sleep/appetite disturbance, psychomotor changes.\n2. Emotional expression is **congruent** with internal mood state.\n3. Prevalence: ~30-35% of stroke survivors.\n4. Risk factors: Left frontal lesion, prior depression, social isolation, functional disability.\n5. Diagnosis: PHQ-9 >= 10; DSM-5 criteria for Major Depressive Disorder due to another medical condition.\n\n**Pseudobulbar Affect (PBA)**:\n1. Involuntary, sudden episodes of crying or laughing that are **disproportionate or incongruent** to mood.\n2. Episodes are stereotyped, brief (seconds to minutes), and self-limited.\n3. Caused by disruption of cortico-pontine-cerebellar pathways that modulate emotional expression.\n4. Diagnosis: **Center for Neurologic Study-Lability Scale (CNS-LS)** score >= 13.\n5. Key distinguishing feature: Patient often reports they do NOT feel sad during crying episodes.\n\n**Critical Teaching Point**: PSD and PBA can **coexist** in the same patient. The PHQ-9 captures the sustained mood disorder, while the CNS-LS captures the involuntary affective dysregulation. Both should be assessed independently.")
B.aS0=new A.c("+ What additional workup should be considered to rule out other etiologies of behavioral change?","1. **Laboratory Studies**: TSH (hypothyroidism), B12/folate, CBC (anemia), CMP (metabolic derangement), urinalysis (occult UTI causing delirium).\n2. **Imaging**: Review existing brain MRI to confirm left frontal infarct location and rule out new or expanding lesion, hydrocephalus, or hemorrhagic conversion.\n3. **Medication Reconciliation**: Evaluate for drug-induced depression (beta-blockers, corticosteroids, levetiracetam if on seizure prophylaxis).\n4. **Sleep Study Consideration**: Post-stroke sleep-disordered breathing (central or obstructive apnea) can present with fatigue, poor participation, and mood disturbance.\n5. **Neuropsychological Screening**: Brief cognitive assessment (MoCA) to evaluate for executive dysfunction or apathy syndrome, which can mimic depression but requires different management.")
B.aRO=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","After confirming the dual diagnosis of PSD (PHQ-9 = 16) and PBA (CNS-LS = 18), you develop a comprehensive treatment plan.")
B.b0D=new A.c("+ Outline the pharmacological and non-pharmacological management for co-occurring PSD and PBA.","**Pharmacological Management**:\n1. **For PSD**: Initiate **Sertraline 25 mg daily**, titrate to 50-100 mg over 2-4 weeks. SSRIs are first-line for PSD with strong evidence (FLAME trial supported fluoxetine; sertraline has favorable side-effect profile). Avoid TCAs due to anticholinergic burden and cardiac risk.\n2. **For PBA**: If PBA symptoms persist despite SSRI therapy (SSRIs can partially treat PBA), consider adding **Dextromethorphan 20 mg/Quinidine 10 mg (Nuedexta)** \u2014 the only FDA-approved treatment for PBA. Counsel that quinidine is a CYP2D6 inhibitor; check for drug interactions.\n3. **Avoid**: Benzodiazepines (impair neuroplasticity and recovery), typical antipsychotics (risk of post-stroke apathy).\n\n**Non-Pharmacological Management**:\n1. **Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT)**: Adapted for aphasia; use visual aids, simplified language, and caregiver involvement.\n2. **Behavioral Activation**: Structure daily schedule with meaningful activities to combat withdrawal.\n3. **Therapy Modifications**: Schedule high-demand therapies during peak alertness; use motivational interviewing techniques.\n4. **Family Education**: Teach distinction between PSD and PBA so caregivers do not misinterpret involuntary crying as worsening depression.\n5. **Reassessment**: Repeat PHQ-9 and CNS-LS at 4-week intervals to track response.")
B.aO1=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","You recognize that mood disorders are frequently underdiagnosed in your rehabilitation unit, and many patients are discharged without adequate psychiatric follow-up.")
B.aP5=new A.c("+ Describe a systems-level approach to integrating mental health screening and treatment into the inpatient rehabilitation setting.","1. **Standardized Screening Protocol**: Implement universal PHQ-9 screening for all stroke patients at admission, weekly during stay, and at discharge. Add CNS-LS for patients with reported emotional lability.\n2. **Embedded Psychology/Psychiatry**: Advocate for a dedicated rehabilitation psychologist as part of the interdisciplinary team who attends weekly team conferences and provides bedside interventions.\n3. **Tiered Consultation Model**: Mild symptoms (PHQ-9: 5-9) managed by rehab physician with therapy referral; moderate-severe (PHQ-9 >= 10) triggers automatic psychiatry or psychology consult.\n4. **Discharge Transition**: Establish warm handoffs to outpatient mental health providers; include mood medication reconciliation in the discharge summary; schedule follow-up within 2 weeks of discharge.\n5. **Staff Training**: Provide nursing and therapy staff with education on recognizing PSD vs. PBA vs. grief reaction vs. apathy to improve early identification.\n6. **Quality Metric**: Track depression screening completion rates and time-to-treatment as a rehabilitation unit quality indicator.")
B.aPP=new A.c(u.j,"Role-Play: During your evaluation, the patient becomes tearful for 10 seconds, then abruptly stops. He looks at you and says: \"I'm not depressed, doc. I don't know why I keep crying. It just happens. My wife thinks I'm losing my mind.\"")
B.b3I=new A.c("+ Provide a model response that validates the patient's experience, explains PBA in accessible terms, and addresses the co-existing depression.","I would respond: \"Mr. [Patient], I want you to know that what you are experiencing is a **real, recognized medical condition** \u2014 you are not losing your mind, and your wife does not need to worry about that. After a stroke, the brain's wiring that controls emotional expression can be disrupted. This means your brain may trigger crying even when you don't feel sad inside. We call this **Pseudobulbar Affect**, and there is a specific medication that can help control it.\n\nNow, I also want to be honest with you about something else. While the sudden crying may not match your mood, I have noticed some other changes \u2014 like pulling back from therapy, difficulty sleeping, and not enjoying things the way you used to. These are signs that the stroke may also be affecting your mood in a deeper way, which is incredibly common and affects about one in three stroke survivors. This is called **Post-Stroke Depression**, and it is not a sign of weakness \u2014 it is a biological consequence of the brain injury.\n\nThe good news is that we can treat both of these conditions effectively. I would like to start a medication that can help lift your mood and energy so you can get the most out of your rehabilitation. I also want to involve our psychologist to give you additional support. Would you be open to trying this approach?\"")
B.aEg=s([B.aYB,B.aNG,B.aSp,B.b_P,B.aSL,B.aS0,B.aRO,B.b0D,B.aO1,B.aP5,B.aPP,B.b3I],t.E)
B.QI=new A.a5("stroke-depression-pba","Post-Stroke Depression & Pseudobulbar Affect",B.aEg,!1)
B.b1E=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 52-year-old right-handed male presents to the emergency department with acute onset of severe vertigo, intractable nausea, difficulty swallowing, and hoarseness that began 6 hours ago while turning his head during a golf swing. He reports a "burning" sensation on the left side of his face and right arm/leg. He has a history of mild hypertension and a recent chiropractic neck manipulation 3 days prior. CT head is negative for hemorrhage, and he is transferred to the stroke service. You are consulted for rehabilitation assessment on hospital day 3.')
B.aWR=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","In assessing a patient with suspected lateral medullary infarction, your evaluation should focus on:\n- **Onset Mechanism**: Head/neck trauma or manipulation raising suspicion for **vertebral artery dissection** as the underlying etiology.\n- **Dysphagia Severity**: Aspiration risk, current diet level, need for alternative nutrition.\n- **Vestibular Symptoms**: Severity of vertigo, nystagmus pattern, fall risk, and functional mobility limitations.\n- **Pain Characterization**: Onset, location, and quality of facial/body pain; screen for **central post-stroke pain (CPSP)** which occurs in up to 25% of Wallenberg syndrome.\n- **Autonomic Function**: Assess for orthostatic hypotension, Horner syndrome, and bowel/bladder changes.\n- **Respiratory Status**: Vocal cord paresis can compromise airway protection.\n- **Vascular Risk Factors**: Hypertension, smoking, hyperlipidemia, connective tissue disorders (fibromuscular dysplasia, Ehlers-Danlos).")
B.b_Z=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Vitals**: BP 152/88, HR 72, RR 16, Temp 98.6F, SpO2 96% on RA.\n- **General**: Alert, nauseated, prefers to lie still with eyes closed.\n- **Cranial Nerves**: Left-sided ptosis and miosis (**Horner syndrome**). Left facial hypalgesia (V1-V3 distribution). Absent left gag reflex. Left vocal cord paresis with hoarse, breathy voice. Left palatal droop with uvula deviation to the right.\n- **Sensory**: **Crossed pattern** \u2014 decreased pain/temperature on the LEFT face (ipsilateral, descending trigeminal tract) and RIGHT body (contralateral, lateral spinothalamic tract). Proprioception and vibration intact bilaterally.\n- **Cerebellar**: Left-sided dysmetria on finger-to-nose and heel-to-shin testing. Wide-based gait with lateral pulsion to the LEFT (ipsilateral).\n- **Motor**: 5/5 strength in all extremities (no hemiparesis \u2014 corticospinal tract is spared).\n- **Swallow**: Bedside water swallow test shows immediate coughing and wet vocal quality. NPO status maintained.")
B.aWG=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The combination of ipsilateral Horner syndrome, ipsilateral facial sensory loss, contralateral body sensory loss, ipsilateral cerebellar ataxia, and ipsilateral pharyngeal/laryngeal dysfunction localizes to the **lateral medulla**, consistent with **Wallenberg Syndrome**.")
B.aN3=new A.c("+ Localize the lesion anatomically and identify the vascular territory. What specific workup should be ordered given the clinical history?","**Anatomical Localization \u2014 Lateral Medulla**:\n1. **Descending Sympathetic Tract** \u2192 Ipsilateral Horner syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis).\n2. **Spinal Trigeminal Nucleus/Tract** \u2192 Ipsilateral facial pain/temperature loss.\n3. **Lateral Spinothalamic Tract** \u2192 Contralateral body pain/temperature loss.\n4. **Inferior Cerebellar Peduncle** \u2192 Ipsilateral cerebellar ataxia and lateropulsion.\n5. **Nucleus Ambiguus (CN IX/X)** \u2192 Ipsilateral palatal weakness, vocal cord paresis, dysphagia.\n6. **Vestibular Nuclei** \u2192 Vertigo, nystagmus, nausea.\n\n**Vascular Territory**: Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery (PICA) or vertebral artery.\n\n**Workup for Vertebral Artery Dissection**:\n1. **MRI Brain with DWI**: Confirm acute lateral medullary infarct.\n2. **MRA or CTA of the Neck**: Evaluate for vertebral artery dissection (intimal flap, string sign, or pseudoaneurysm). MRI with fat-saturated T1 sequences can show intramural hematoma.\n3. **Conventional Angiography**: Gold standard if non-invasive imaging is equivocal.\n4. **Hypercoagulable Panel**: Consider if dissection confirmed, especially in younger patients.\n5. **Echocardiography**: Rule out cardioembolic source if dissection is not identified.")
B.aOM=new A.c("+ Describe the expected findings on a Modified Barium Swallow Study (MBSS) in Wallenberg syndrome and the rehabilitation implications.","**Expected MBSS Findings**:\n1. **Unilateral pharyngeal weakness**: Reduced pharyngeal contraction on the ipsilateral (left) side with pharyngeal residue in the left piriform sinus.\n2. **Impaired laryngeal elevation and closure**: Due to nucleus ambiguus involvement, resulting in reduced epiglottic inversion.\n3. **Aspiration**: Typically occurs during or after the swallow (not before), due to impaired airway protection and pharyngeal stasis.\n4. **Reduced UES opening**: Cricopharyngeal dysfunction is common in lateral medullary strokes.\n\n**Rehabilitation Implications**:\n1. **Compensatory Strategies**: Head turn to the LEFT (affected side) to close off the weak pharyngeal wall and redirect bolus through the stronger side.\n2. **Supraglottic Swallow Technique**: Voluntary breath-hold before and during swallow to protect the airway.\n3. **Diet Modification**: Begin with IDDSI Level 4 (pureed) foods and Level 1-2 (slightly/mildly thick) liquids based on MBSS results.\n4. **Neuromuscular Electrical Stimulation (NMES)**: May augment laryngeal elevation during swallowing therapy.\n5. **Prognosis**: Dysphagia in Wallenberg syndrome has a relatively favorable recovery trajectory; most patients resume oral diet within 2-3 months.")
B.b1a=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT",'At week 3, the patient\'s dysphagia has improved to a modified diet, but he now reports severe, constant burning pain in his right arm and leg rated 8/10, worsened by light touch. He describes it as "like my skin is on fire." This is consistent with **Central Post-Stroke Pain (CPSP)**.')
B.b_r=new A.c("+ Outline a comprehensive management plan addressing dysphagia rehabilitation, vestibular therapy, and central post-stroke pain.","**Central Post-Stroke Pain (CPSP) Management**:\n1. **First-Line**: **Amitriptyline 10-25 mg nightly** (strongest evidence for CPSP) or **Pregabalin 75 mg BID**, titrate to effect. Avoid opioids (ineffective for central neuropathic pain).\n2. **Second-Line**: **Duloxetine 30-60 mg daily** (SNRI with neuropathic pain benefit) or **Lamotrigine 25 mg daily**, titrate slowly (evidence for thalamic and brainstem CPSP).\n3. **Adjuncts**: Topical lidocaine 5% patches for allodynic areas; TENS for desensitization.\n4. **Refractory Cases**: Referral for repetitive transcranial magnetic stimulation (rTMS) targeting motor cortex.\n\n**Vestibular Rehabilitation**:\n1. **Gaze Stabilization Exercises**: VOR x1 and x2 protocols to improve visual stability during head movement.\n2. **Habituation Exercises**: Graded exposure to provocative movements to reduce motion sensitivity.\n3. **Balance Training**: Progressive static-to-dynamic balance activities; address lateropulsion with weight-shifting exercises toward the affected side.\n4. **Fall Prevention**: Assistive device prescription (rolling walker initially, progress to single-point cane).\n\n**Ongoing Dysphagia Management**:\n1. Continue SLP-directed swallowing exercises (effortful swallow, Mendelsohn maneuver).\n2. Serial MBSS or FEES at 4-6 week intervals to guide diet advancement.\n3. Target full oral diet by 8-12 weeks post-onset.")
B.aZd=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The neurology team recommends starting warfarin for the confirmed vertebral artery dissection, while the primary care physician prefers antiplatelet therapy with aspirin alone. You are asked to weigh in on the secondary prevention strategy.")
B.b3u=new A.c("+ Discuss the anticoagulation vs. antiplatelet debate in cervical artery dissection and the physiatrist's role in this decision.","**Current Evidence**:\n1. The **CADISS trial** (2015) \u2014 the largest RCT comparing antiplatelet vs. anticoagulation in cervical artery dissection \u2014 found **no significant difference** in recurrent stroke rates at 3 months (antiplatelet 2% vs. anticoagulation 1%, p = NS).\n2. Both strategies are considered acceptable per AHA/ASA guidelines (Class IIa, Level B evidence).\n3. **Anticoagulation (Heparin bridge to Warfarin, INR 2-3)**: Historically favored due to theoretical reduction of thrombus propagation at the dissection site. Duration typically 3-6 months with repeat imaging.\n4. **Antiplatelet (Aspirin 325 mg or Dual Antiplatelet)**: Preferred when hemorrhagic risk is higher (large infarct, hemorrhagic conversion, planned procedures) or when compliance with INR monitoring is a concern.\n5. **DOACs**: Increasingly used off-label (TREAT-CAD trial suggested non-inferiority of aspirin vs. DOACs), but not yet standard of care.\n\n**Physiatrist's Role**:\n1. Advocate for the strategy that optimizes **safe rehabilitation participation** \u2014 anticoagulation may limit certain interventional procedures (injections, aggressive ROM) and increases fall-related bleeding risk.\n2. Ensure fall prevention protocols are in place regardless of strategy chosen.\n3. Coordinate with neurology for repeat vascular imaging at 3-6 months to assess for vessel recanalization, which guides duration of therapy.\n4. Facilitate the interdisciplinary discussion and document the shared decision in the rehab plan of care.")
B.aPO=new A.c(u.j,'Role-Play: The patient is visibly frustrated and confused. He says: "I thought strokes cause paralysis. My arms and legs work fine \u2014 so how can this be a stroke? Why can\'t I swallow? Why is my face drooping? None of this makes sense to me."')
B.b3d=new A.c("+ Provide a model response that explains Wallenberg syndrome in patient-friendly language and addresses his concerns.","I would respond: \"I completely understand your frustration, and your question is a really good one. Most people think of strokes as causing weakness on one side of the body, and that is true for the most common type. But your stroke happened in a very specific area in the back of the brain called the **brainstem** \u2014 think of it as the 'control center' that connects your brain to your body.\n\nThis particular area does not control arm or leg strength, which is why yours are working well. Instead, it controls things like **swallowing, balance, sensation, and voice**. That is why you are having trouble with those specific functions. The burning pain you feel is because the stroke damaged the area that processes temperature and pain signals, so your brain is now misinterpreting normal touch as painful.\n\nThe good news is that this type of stroke tends to have a **better recovery** than the kind that causes paralysis. Your swallowing is already improving, and we expect it to continue getting better with therapy. We have effective treatments for the burning pain as well.\n\nAs for how this happened \u2014 the blood vessel that feeds this area of your brain was injured, likely related to the neck manipulation you had. We are treating that to prevent any future events. You are in the right place, and we have a clear plan to get you back to the golf course.\"")
B.aGa=s([B.b1E,B.aWR,B.b_Z,B.aWG,B.aN3,B.aOM,B.b1a,B.b_r,B.aZd,B.b3u,B.aPO,B.b3d],t.E)
B.Qz=new A.a5("stroke-wallenberg","Wallenberg Syndrome (Lateral Medullary Stroke)",B.aGa,!1)
B.b28=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 65-year-old right-handed female is evaluated on the acute inpatient rehabilitation unit 3 weeks after a left middle cerebral artery (MCA) ischemic stroke resulting in right hemiplegia and global aphasia. Nursing staff report that she grimaces and withdraws during right upper extremity dressing and transfers. Her daughter notes a visible "dropping" of the right shoulder compared to the left. The patient is unable to verbalize her pain due to aphasia, but the nursing pain assessment using behavioral observation tools suggests moderate-to-severe discomfort. Her therapy participation has declined significantly over the past 5 days.')
B.aZ6=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","In assessing hemiplegic shoulder pain with subluxation, your evaluation should include:\n- **Pain Assessment in Aphasia**: Use validated behavioral pain scales (e.g., ABBEY Pain Scale, FLACC adapted for adults) since the patient cannot self-report. Observe for guarding, facial grimacing, and withdrawal during passive ROM.\n- **Subluxation History**: Timing of onset, relationship to flaccidity phase, handling techniques used by staff and family.\n- **Pre-Stroke Shoulder Pathology**: Prior rotator cuff disease, osteoarthritis, adhesive capsulitis, or shoulder surgery.\n- **Motor Recovery Stage**: Assess Brunnstrom stage \u2014 subluxation is most common during the **flaccid stage** (Stages 1-2) before tone returns.\n- **Positioning and Handling Practices**: Evaluate whether staff are using proper transfer techniques (never pulling the hemiplegic arm), lap tray use, and arm positioning in bed/wheelchair.\n- **CRPS Screening**: Assess for disproportionate edema, color/temperature changes, and allodynia in the hand that would suggest evolving Complex Regional Pain Syndrome.")
B.aSf=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Vitals**: BP 142/78, HR 74, RR 14, Temp 98.2F, SpO2 98% on RA.\n- **Inspection**: Visible **inferior subluxation** of the right glenohumeral joint. The acromion appears prominent with a palpable sulcus between the acromion and humeral head.\n- **Palpation**: **2-fingerbreadth sulcus sign** at the right glenohumeral joint. Tenderness over the supraspinatus insertion and bicipital groove.\n- **Tone**: Right upper extremity is **flaccid** (MAS 0/4). Right lower extremity MAS 1+/4 (beginning spasticity).\n- **Motor**: Right upper extremity 1/5 proximally (trace deltoid, supraspinatus), 0/5 distally. Right lower extremity 2+/5 hip flexors, 3-/5 quadriceps. Brunnstrom Stage 2 (UE), Stage 3 (LE).\n- **ROM**: Passive right shoulder flexion to 110 degrees (painful beyond 90 degrees). External rotation to 20 degrees with guarding. Left shoulder full ROM.\n- **Hand Examination**: Mild dorsal hand edema on the right. No erythema, no temperature asymmetry, no allodynia. Fingers are flaccid without intrinsic-minus posturing.\n- **Provocative Tests**: Neer impingement sign positive on right. Empty can test not reliably assessed due to weakness.")
B.aTQ=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The 2-fingerbreadth subluxation with flaccid hemiplegia, impingement signs, and bicipital groove tenderness suggests **glenohumeral subluxation with secondary impingement** as the primary pain generator. The mild dorsal hand edema requires monitoring for early CRPS.")
B.b_f=new A.c("+ Provide a structured differential diagnosis for hemiplegic shoulder pain and describe how to differentiate subluxation-related pain from CRPS, rotator cuff injury, and adhesive capsulitis.","**Differential Diagnosis of Hemiplegic Shoulder Pain**:\n\n1. **Glenohumeral Subluxation**: Caused by loss of supraspinatus and deltoid tone (the primary dynamic stabilizers of the GH joint). The humeral head migrates inferiorly under gravity. Pain arises from capsular stretch and traction on the suprascapular nerve.\n   - *Key Finding*: Palpable sulcus sign, worse in upright position, improves when arm is supported.\n\n2. **Rotator Cuff Injury (Impingement/Tear)**: The flaccid shoulder is vulnerable to traction injuries during improper transfers or overhead pulley use.\n   - *Key Finding*: Positive Neer/Hawkins signs, pain with passive flexion >90 degrees, tenderness at supraspinatus insertion. **Ultrasound** is the preferred initial imaging modality to assess cuff integrity at bedside.\n\n3. **Adhesive Capsulitis (Frozen Shoulder)**: Results from prolonged immobilization of the hemiplegic arm.\n   - *Key Finding*: Global loss of passive ROM (especially external rotation), hard capsular end-feel, typically develops weeks-to-months after stroke onset.\n\n4. **Complex Regional Pain Syndrome (CRPS Type I)**: Also called Shoulder-Hand Syndrome in the post-stroke population.\n   - *Key Finding*: Disproportionate burning pain, vasomotor changes (color/temperature asymmetry), sudomotor changes (edema, sweating), and trophic changes. Budapest Criteria require findings in >=3 of 4 categories. **This patient has mild edema but lacks the other criteria \u2014 warrants close monitoring.**\n\n5. **Brachial Plexus Traction Injury**: From improper handling of the hemiplegic arm during transfers.\n   - *Key Finding*: Weakness pattern inconsistent with UMN lesion, sensory loss in peripheral nerve distribution.")
B.aPd=new A.c("+ What imaging would you order and what specific findings would you expect?","1. **Shoulder Ultrasound (Preferred First-Line)**:\n   - Assess rotator cuff integrity (partial vs. full-thickness tear of supraspinatus).\n   - Measure the acromiohumeral distance to quantify subluxation (normal >10 mm; subluxation <7 mm).\n   - Evaluate for biceps tendinopathy or effusion.\n   - Advantage: Bedside, dynamic assessment, no radiation, cost-effective.\n\n2. **Plain Radiographs (AP with arm at side)**:\n   - Quantify subluxation by measuring the gap between the inferior acromion and superior humeral head.\n   - Rule out fracture (especially if trauma history) or heterotopic ossification.\n\n3. **MRI Shoulder**: Reserved for cases where ultrasound is equivocal or surgical intervention is being considered. May show rotator cuff pathology, labral tears, or capsular thickening.\n\n4. **Electrodiagnostic Studies (EMG/NCS)**: Consider if brachial plexus injury or suprascapular neuropathy is suspected \u2014 may show axillary or suprascapular nerve injury from chronic traction.")
B.b0U=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Ultrasound confirms a partial-thickness supraspinatus tear and 2 cm inferior subluxation without full-thickness cuff disruption. You develop a multimodal treatment plan.")
B.aNO=new A.c("+ Outline the comprehensive management plan including orthotic support, NMES, positioning, injection therapy, and therapy modifications.","**1. Shoulder Support/Orthotic Selection**:\n- **GivMohr sling or Bobath-style shoulder support**: Preferred over traditional arm slings because they provide glenohumeral reduction without promoting flexion synergy or elbow contracture.\n- Use during upright activities (sitting, standing, ambulation) to reduce inferior subluxation force.\n- **Remove during supine positioning and therapy** to allow ROM exercises.\n- **Lap tray or arm trough** on wheelchair at all times when sling is not in use.\n\n**2. Neuromuscular Electrical Stimulation (NMES)**:\n- Apply surface NMES to the **posterior deltoid and supraspinatus** muscles.\n- Protocol: 30-minute sessions, 2-3 times daily, with parameters of 30-35 Hz frequency, 200-300 microsecond pulse width, on/off cycle of 10:12 seconds.\n- Evidence supports NMES for **reducing subluxation** and may reduce pain (Cochrane review). Most effective when initiated early in the flaccid phase.\n- Progress to functional electrical stimulation (FES) integrated with task-specific training as motor recovery emerges.\n\n**3. Positioning Protocol**:\n- **Supine**: Pillow under the right arm to maintain slight abduction and neutral rotation. Scapula protracted.\n- **Side-lying (affected side)**: Shoulder protracted, elbow extended, supported on pillow.\n- **Side-lying (unaffected side)**: Right arm supported on pillow at shoulder height.\n- **Seated**: Arm supported on lap tray or armrest at all times. Never allow arm to dangle.\n\n**4. Injection Therapy**:\n- **Subacromial corticosteroid injection** (ultrasound-guided): 40 mg triamcinolone + 3 mL lidocaine to address subacromial bursitis and partial cuff tear inflammation.\n- Repeat no more than 3 times per year. Reassess in 2-4 weeks.\n\n**5. Therapy Modifications**:\n- Pain-free passive ROM only; avoid aggressive overhead stretching.\n- Scapular mobilization and protraction exercises.\n- Begin active-assisted ROM as motor recovery progresses (Brunnstrom Stage 3+).\n- **Absolute contraindication: Overhead pulleys** \u2014 high risk of impingement in the subluxed, hemiplegic shoulder.")
B.b2f=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient's daughter asks about obtaining a shoulder support device for use after discharge. She is concerned about insurance coverage and whether the device will be available in time.")
B.b3N=new A.c("+ Discuss the DME prescription process, insurance justification, and documentation requirements for shoulder support devices in hemiplegic patients.","**DME Prescription and Justification**:\n1. **Prescription Requirements**: A detailed DME prescription must include the specific device (e.g., GivMohr sling, HCPCS code L3660 or L3670), diagnosis codes (ICD-10: I63.512 \u2014 left MCA infarct; M75.111 \u2014 right rotator cuff tear; G81.91 \u2014 right hemiplegia), medical necessity statement, and expected duration of use.\n\n2. **Letter of Medical Necessity (LMN)**:\n   - Document the 2-fingerbreadth subluxation with objective measurement.\n   - State that the device is required to prevent further subluxation-related injury, reduce pain, and enable participation in rehabilitation.\n   - Note that the patient cannot independently reposition the arm due to flaccid hemiplegia and aphasia.\n   - Include trial of the device during inpatient stay demonstrating functional benefit.\n\n3. **Insurance Considerations**:\n   - Medicare Part B covers medically necessary DME with proper documentation.\n   - Prior authorization may be required for certain shoulder orthoses.\n   - Ensure the DME supplier is Medicare-enrolled and provides in-home fitting/adjustment.\n   - Appeal process: If denied, submit peer-to-peer review with supporting literature on subluxation prevention.\n\n4. **Discharge Planning**:\n   - Order DME at least **5-7 business days before anticipated discharge** to ensure delivery.\n   - Train caregivers on proper application, wearing schedule, and skin checks under the device.\n   - Schedule outpatient follow-up within 2 weeks to reassess fit and subluxation status.\n   - Provide written home exercise program with illustrated positioning guidelines adapted for the aphasic patient (use pictures and diagrams).")
B.aQi=new A.c(u.j,"Role-Play: The patient's daughter is emotionally distressed during your family conference. She says through tears: \"It's been 3 weeks and her arm just hangs there. She can't even tell me if she's in pain. When is her arm going to work again? Will she ever use it? I need you to be honest with me.\"")
B.b37=new A.c("+ Provide a compassionate, honest response that addresses prognostic uncertainty, validates the family's distress, and outlines a realistic plan.",'I would respond: "I can see how difficult this is for you, and I want you to know that your concern for your mother shows incredible love and dedication. You deserve an honest answer, and I will give you the best information I have.\n\nRight now, your mother\'s arm is in what we call the **flaccid stage** of recovery \u2014 the muscles are not yet receiving strong enough signals from the brain to move. This is expected at 3 weeks after a stroke of this severity. The fact that her leg is already starting to show some movement is actually an encouraging sign that her brain is beginning to rewire.\n\nI want to be straightforward with you: **full recovery of arm function after a severe stroke is uncommon**, especially when there is very little movement at this stage. However, we do see meaningful improvements over the next 3 to 6 months, and some patients continue to gain function beyond that. What I can promise you is that we will use every tool available \u2014 electrical stimulation, specialized therapy, proper positioning, and pain management \u2014 to give her arm the best possible chance.\n\nEven if full recovery does not occur, we will focus on **reducing her pain, preventing complications**, and teaching her how to accomplish daily tasks with adaptive techniques. Regarding her pain \u2014 we are using behavioral observation tools since she cannot tell us directly, and we are treating her pain aggressively.\n\nI know uncertainty is one of the hardest things to sit with. I will continue to give you honest updates as we see how she responds to treatment over the coming weeks. You are an important part of her recovery team, and I want you to feel comfortable asking me questions at any time."')
B.aEy=s([B.b28,B.aZ6,B.aSf,B.aTQ,B.b_f,B.aPd,B.b0U,B.aNO,B.b2f,B.b3N,B.aQi,B.b37],t.E)
B.QH=new A.a5("stroke-shoulder-subluxation","Post-Stroke Shoulder Subluxation & Hemiplegic Shoulder Pain",B.aEy,!1)
B.aSl=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 34-year-old left-handed female, an avid marathon runner with no significant past medical history, is admitted to the inpatient rehabilitation unit 10 days after waking from sleep with left-sided weakness, left facial droop, and slurred speech. MRI confirmed an acute right MCA territory infarct involving the frontal and parietal cortex. CT angiography of the head and neck showed no large vessel stenosis or dissection. Standard stroke workup including lipid panel, HbA1c, telemetry, and carotid duplex were unremarkable. A transthoracic echocardiogram with agitated saline (bubble study) demonstrated a **right-to-left shunt consistent with a patent foramen ovale (PFO)** with significant shunt volume (>20 microbubbles in the left atrium within 3 cardiac cycles).")
B.aQ9=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","In assessing a young adult with cryptogenic stroke, your evaluation should include:\n- **Risk Factor Assessment**: Absence of traditional vascular risk factors (hypertension, diabetes, hyperlipidemia, smoking, atrial fibrillation) raises suspicion for alternative etiologies.\n- **Migraine History**: Migraine with aura is associated with PFO and may be an independent stroke risk factor.\n- **Hypercoagulable Risk Factors**: Oral contraceptive use, recent prolonged travel/immobilization, personal or family history of venous thromboembolism (DVT/PE), pregnancy losses.\n- **Recreational Drug Use**: Cocaine, amphetamines, or other vasoconstrictive substances.\n- **Family History**: Early stroke (<55 years), thrombophilia, connective tissue disorders.\n- **Functional Baseline**: Pre-stroke activity level (marathon training volume), occupation, and cognitive demands to guide rehabilitation goals.\n- **Psychological Impact**: Assess for acute stress reaction, anxiety about recurrence, and impact on identity (athlete, career, family planning).")
B.aOy=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Vitals**: BP 118/72, HR 56 (athlete's bradycardia), RR 14, Temp 98.4F, SpO2 99% on RA.\n- **General**: Well-appearing, athletic build, visibly anxious. No stigmata of connective tissue disease (no joint hypermobility, normal skin elasticity, normal habitus).\n- **Neurological**: Alert, oriented x4. Mild left facial droop (lower motor neuron pattern resolved, now subtle UMN pattern). Speech fluent without dysarthria. Left upper extremity 4/5 proximally, 3+/5 grip and intrinsic hand muscles. Left lower extremity 4+/5 hip flexors, 4/5 quadriceps, 4-/5 ankle dorsiflexion. Left-sided hypoesthesia to light touch (arm > leg). Mild left hemispatial inattention on line bisection testing.\n- **Cerebellar**: No dysmetria or ataxia.\n- **Cardiac**: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmur auscultated, no peripheral edema.\n- **Functional Status**: Amb with CGA and single-point cane x 150 feet. Min-A for lower body dressing. Independent with grooming and feeding. FIM motor score 72.")
B.aTh=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The combination of a young patient without traditional vascular risk factors, a confirmed right MCA territory infarct, and a positive bubble study with significant right-to-left shunt establishes the diagnosis of **cryptogenic stroke with a patent foramen ovale (PFO)** as the probable mechanism via paradoxical embolism.")
B.b1s=new A.c("+ Outline the complete workup for cryptogenic stroke in a young adult, including the evaluation of PFO significance and hypercoagulable screening.",'**PFO-Specific Evaluation**:\n1. **Transesophageal Echocardiography (TEE)**: Gold standard for PFO characterization \u2014 assess size, presence of atrial septal aneurysm (ASA), and shunt severity (spontaneous vs. provoked). ASA + large PFO confers highest recurrence risk.\n2. **Transcranial Doppler (TCD) with Bubble Study**: Functional assessment of right-to-left shunt; can quantify shunt grade (Grade III = >25 microbubbles = high-risk shunt).\n3. **RoPE Score Calculation**: Risk of Paradoxical Embolism score (0-10) estimates the probability that the PFO is stroke-related. Higher scores (>=7) favor the PFO as causal. Factors: age <30 (+5 points max), absence of HTN/DM/prior stroke/smoking, cortical infarct on imaging.\n\n**Hypercoagulable Workup**:\n1. **Thrombophilia Panel**: Factor V Leiden mutation, Prothrombin G20210A mutation, Protein C and S activity, Antithrombin III level.\n2. **Antiphospholipid Syndrome Screen**: Lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin antibodies (IgG/IgM), beta-2 glycoprotein I antibodies. Requires repeat confirmation at 12 weeks.\n3. **Homocysteine Level**: Elevated homocysteine is an independent stroke risk factor in young adults.\n4. **CBC with Peripheral Smear**: Screen for polycythemia vera, thrombocythemia.\n\n**Additional Workup**:\n1. **Extended Cardiac Monitoring**: 30-day event monitor or implantable loop recorder to exclude paroxysmal atrial fibrillation (found in up to 10-15% of "cryptogenic" strokes with prolonged monitoring).\n2. **Vessel Wall MRI**: If vasculitis or intracranial dissection is suspected.\n3. **Lower Extremity Duplex Ultrasound**: Evaluate for DVT as the source of paradoxical embolism.\n4. **Toxicology Screen**: Cocaine, amphetamines if clinical suspicion warrants.')
B.aMI=new A.c("+ Discuss the evidence for PFO closure versus medical management for secondary stroke prevention.","**Landmark PFO Closure Trials**:\n1. **RESPECT (2017 Extended Follow-Up)**: PFO closure with Amplatzer device vs. medical therapy. Closure reduced recurrent ischemic stroke (HR 0.55, p = 0.046). Benefit most pronounced in patients with ASA or large shunt.\n2. **CLOSE (2017)**: PFO closure vs. antiplatelet vs. anticoagulation. Closure resulted in 0% recurrent stroke vs. 6.0% with antiplatelet (HR 0.03, p <0.001). Strongest trial result.\n3. **REDUCE (2017)**: PFO closure vs. antiplatelet therapy. Closure reduced clinical stroke (1.4% vs. 5.4%, HR 0.23, p = 0.002).\n4. **DEFENSE-PFO (2018)**: Closure superior to medical therapy in high-risk PFO subgroup.\n\n**Guideline Recommendations**:\n- AHA/ASA (2020): PFO closure is reasonable for patients aged **18-60** with cryptogenic stroke and high-risk PFO features (ASA, large shunt, RoPE >=7). **Class IIa, Level B-R**.\n- Antiplatelet therapy alone is recommended if PFO closure is not pursued.\n- Anticoagulation may be considered if concurrent DVT or thrombophilia is identified.\n\n**For This Patient**: With a high RoPE score (young, no vascular risk factors, cortical infarct) and a large shunt (>20 microbubbles), she is an **ideal candidate for PFO closure** followed by dual antiplatelet therapy for 3-6 months, then aspirin monotherapy long-term.\n\n**Post-Closure Considerations**: Atrial fibrillation can occur transiently after device placement (5-7%); requires monitoring. Endocarditis prophylaxis for 6 months until device endothelialization.")
B.aNo=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient is a software engineer who works from home. She asks about returning to running and when she can go back to work. She also inquires about short-term disability options.")
B.b3B=new A.c("+ Discuss return-to-sport clearance after stroke, work accommodations, and disability resources for a young adult stroke survivor.","**Return to Sport \u2014 Marathon Running**:\n1. **Cardiac Clearance**: Must complete PFO closure and post-procedure echocardiography confirming successful closure before returning to vigorous exercise. Typically 4-6 weeks post-device placement.\n2. **Neurological Clearance**: Ensure no residual deficits that compromise safety during running (balance, foot drop, spatial awareness). Current left ankle dorsiflexion weakness (4-/5) and hemispatial inattention are barriers that must resolve.\n3. **Graded Return Protocol**: Walking program \u2192 jogging (4-6 weeks post-closure) \u2192 progressive distance running. Monitor for exertional symptoms (headache, neurological symptoms). Avoid Valsalva-heavy exercise initially (increased right-to-left shunt risk pre-closure).\n4. **Hydration and Temperature Management**: Post-stroke patients may have impaired thermoregulation; counsel on fluid intake, avoiding extreme heat, and running with a partner.\n5. **Realistic Timeline**: Return to competitive marathon running may take 6-12 months depending on motor recovery and cardiopulmonary reconditioning.\n\n**Work Accommodations and Disability**:\n1. **Short-Term Disability**: Most employer-sponsored STD plans cover 60-70% of salary for 3-6 months. Requires physician documentation of functional limitations.\n2. **FMLA**: Eligible for up to 12 weeks of job-protected unpaid leave (employer must have >=50 employees).\n3. **Phased Return to Work**: Start with reduced hours (4-6 hours/day), gradual increase over 4-6 weeks. As a remote software engineer, cognitive fatigue from screen time and left hand fine motor limitations (keyboard/mouse use) are the primary barriers.\n4. **Workplace Accommodations (ADA)**: Ergonomic keyboard/mouse, voice recognition software (Dragon), scheduled rest breaks, flexible scheduling. Request through employer's HR department with physician letter.\n5. **Cognitive Rehabilitation**: If hemispatial inattention persists, address with targeted OT to improve dual-screen computer use and code review accuracy.")
B.aPG=new A.c(u.j,"Role-Play: The patient is sitting upright in bed, visibly anxious. She says: \"I'm 34 \u2014 I didn't think strokes happened to people my age. I was training for my fourth marathon. My doctor says I have a hole in my heart. Am I going to have another stroke? Can I ever run again? I'm scared to even move.\"")
B.aPh=new A.c("+ Provide a model response that addresses her fear of recurrence, explains the PFO in accessible terms, and supports her identity as an athlete.",'I would respond: "I hear you, and I want to start by saying that everything you are feeling right now \u2014 the fear, the frustration, the uncertainty \u2014 is completely normal. Having a stroke at 34 is unexpected and frightening, and it is okay to feel shaken by this.\n\nLet me explain what we have found and why the outlook is actually encouraging. You were born with a small opening between the two upper chambers of your heart called a **PFO \u2014 a patent foramen ovale**. Everyone has this opening before birth, and in most people it closes on its own. In about 25% of adults, it stays open and usually causes no problems at all. But in rare cases, a small blood clot from the veins can slip through that opening and travel to the brain, which is what we believe happened to you.\n\nHere is the most important thing I want you to hear: **this is a fixable problem**. There is a minimally invasive procedure where a cardiologist places a small closure device through a catheter \u2014 no open-heart surgery \u2014 to seal that opening permanently. Multiple large clinical studies have shown this reduces the risk of another stroke to **near zero** for patients like you.\n\nNow, about running. I know your identity as a marathon runner is a huge part of who you are, and I do not want this stroke to take that from you. Once the PFO is closed and you have recovered \u2014 and your recovery is already going well \u2014 there is **no medical reason you cannot return to running**, including marathons. We will build a gradual return-to-running plan together with your cardiologist.\n\nYour fitness level before the stroke is actually one of your biggest advantages right now. Athletes tend to recover faster because their cardiovascular and musculoskeletal systems are strong. Your left side is already showing good improvement, and I expect that to continue.\n\nWe are going to take this one step at a time \u2014 literally. Right now, the goal is rehabilitation. Then PFO closure. Then we get you running again. I will be with you through each of those steps."')
B.aDb=s([B.aSl,B.aQ9,B.aOy,B.aTh,B.b1s,B.aMI,B.aNo,B.b3B,B.aPG,B.aPh],t.E)
B.QL=new A.a5("stroke-young-adult-pfo","Cryptogenic Stroke in a Young Adult with Patent Foramen Ovale (PFO)",B.aDb,!1)
B.b2N=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 70-year-old retired electrician is admitted to the acute rehabilitation unit 3 weeks after sustaining sequential bilateral middle cerebral artery (MCA) territory infarctions. The first stroke involved the left MCA territory 5 weeks ago, causing right hemiparesis and global aphasia. Two weeks later, while still in the acute hospital, he suffered a second stroke in the right MCA territory, resulting in left hemiplegia and left neglect. He is now quadriparetic with severe cognitive impairment, minimal verbal output, and requires total assistance for all activities. His wife and three adult children are at the bedside and are in significant distress about his prognosis.")
B.aVj=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **First Stroke (5 weeks ago)**: Left MCA territory infarct; presented with acute right hemiplegia and global aphasia; CT angiography showed left MCA M1 occlusion; received IV tPA but no mechanical thrombectomy due to late presentation\n- **Second Stroke (3 weeks ago)**: Right MCA territory infarct during acute hospitalization; developed sudden left-sided weakness and leftward gaze deviation; CT showed new right MCA territory infarction; mechanical thrombectomy performed but with limited recanalization (mTICI 2a)\n- **Cardiac Workup**: Echocardiogram revealed dilated left atrium, moderate mitral regurgitation, and a 1.2 cm left atrial appendage thrombus; new-onset atrial fibrillation detected on telemetry between the two strokes; INR was subtherapeutic (1.3) at time of second stroke on heparin bridge\n- **PMH**: Hypertension (poorly controlled), type 2 diabetes mellitus (A1c 9.2%), hyperlipidemia, obesity (BMI 34), 40-pack-year smoking history (quit 5 years ago), moderate alcohol use\n- **Current Functional Status**: Requires total assist for bed mobility, transfers, and all ADLs; PEG tube placed for nutrition due to severe dysphagia (failed bedside and VFSS with aspiration on all consistencies); Foley catheter in place; bowel incontinent\n- **Cognition**: MMSE attempted -- scored 4/30; unable to follow 2-step commands; no purposeful verbal output beyond occasional moaning; intermittent eye opening; does not track consistently; occasional withdrawal to painful stimuli bilaterally\n- **Medications**: Apixaban 5 mg BID, metoprolol succinate 50 mg daily, atorvastatin 80 mg daily, insulin glargine 20 units QHS, amlodipine 10 mg daily, famotidine 20 mg BID (all via PEG tube)")
B.aYj=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 148/86, HR 76 (irregular), Temp 37.1C, RR 16, SpO2 96% on RA\n\n**General**: Obese male in bed, eyes intermittently open, not tracking examiner. PEG tube in place, site clean. Foley catheter draining clear yellow urine.\n\n**Neurological**:\n- **Mental Status**: No purposeful verbal output; occasional moaning to stimulation; does not follow commands; no reliable yes/no communication; intermittent eye opening (appears more reflexive than purposeful); GCS 8 (E2 V2 M4)\n- **Cranial Nerves**: Bilateral gaze preference (variable -- sometimes leftward, sometimes midline); pupils equal and reactive; corneal reflexes present bilaterally; absent gag reflex on right, weak on left; facial asymmetry bilateral (left worse than right)\n- **Motor**:\n  - Right upper extremity: 1/5 shoulder, 1/5 elbow, 0/5 wrist/hand (spastic flexion posturing)\n  - Right lower extremity: 2/5 hip flexion, 2/5 knee extension, 1/5 ankle\n  - Left upper extremity: 0/5 throughout (flaccid)\n  - Left lower extremity: 1/5 hip flexion, 1/5 knee extension, 0/5 ankle\n- **Tone**: Right side -- spastic (MAS 2 in elbow flexors, 2 in wrist flexors); Left side -- flaccid\n- **Sensory**: Inconsistent withdrawal to pain bilaterally; unable to assess light touch or proprioception reliably\n- **Reflexes**: Right side 3+ with bilateral Babinski signs; left side 2+ (may increase as flaccidity resolves)\n\n**Skin**: Stage 2 pressure injury on sacrum (3 cm x 2 cm, shallow, pink wound bed); early redness over bilateral heels\n\n**Cardiovascular**: Irregularly irregular rhythm; no murmur appreciated\n\n**Pulmonary**: Coarse breath sounds bilaterally; occasional productive cough; managing oral secretions poorly")
B.aWq=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","This patient has suffered devastating bilateral MCA territory strokes resulting in quadriparesis and severe cognitive impairment. Understanding the etiology and preventing further events is critical, even when the prognosis is poor.")
B.aTw=new A.c("+ Discuss the workup for recurrent stroke in this patient, including cardiac source evaluation and secondary prevention strategy.","**Etiology of Sequential Bilateral Strokes**:\n\nThis patient's bilateral MCA strokes are most likely **cardioembolic** in origin based on:\n1. **Left atrial appendage thrombus** identified on echocardiography\n2. **New-onset atrial fibrillation** detected between the two events\n3. **Subtherapeutic anticoagulation** (INR 1.3 on heparin bridge) at the time of the second stroke\n4. **MCA territory distribution** -- large vessel occlusions typical of cardiac emboli\n\n**Comprehensive Stroke Workup -- Confirming and Expanding**:\n\n**Cardiac Evaluation**:\n- **Transesophageal echocardiogram (TEE)**: Superior to TTE for visualizing left atrial appendage thrombus, atrial septal abnormalities, and aortic arch atheroma; confirm thrombus resolution on anticoagulation\n- **Extended cardiac monitoring**: If not already on continuous telemetry, ensure at least 30-day cardiac monitoring to quantify atrial fibrillation burden and assess rate control\n- **CHA2DS2-VASc Score**: Age 70 (1), hypertension (1), diabetes (1), vascular disease (1), prior stroke/TIA (2) = **6** -- very high risk; lifetime anticoagulation mandatory\n- **HAS-BLED Score**: Assess bleeding risk to guide anticoagulation choice\n\n**Vascular Imaging**:\n- CT or MR angiography of head and neck (if not already completed): Assess for concurrent large vessel atherosclerosis, carotid stenosis, or intracranial stenosis as contributing factors\n- Assess bilateral MCA patency status post-intervention\n\n**Laboratory**:\n- Hypercoagulability panel: Consider antiphospholipid antibody syndrome (lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin, beta-2 glycoprotein) -- particularly in recurrent stroke\n- A1c optimization (currently 9.2% -- significant contributor to vascular risk)\n- Lipid panel (ensure LDL < 70 mg/dL on high-intensity statin)\n\n**Secondary Prevention Strategy**:\n1. **Anticoagulation**: Apixaban 5 mg BID (already initiated) -- preferred DOAC for stroke prevention in atrial fibrillation; superior to warfarin for this indication based on ARISTOTLE trial data; no need for concurrent antiplatelet therapy (increases bleeding without benefit)\n2. **Rate/rhythm control**: Metoprolol for rate control; cardiology consultation for rhythm control strategy (cardioversion vs. ablation if patient recovers sufficiently)\n3. **Left atrial appendage closure**: Consider Watchman device if anticoagulation becomes contraindicated (e.g., falls, hemorrhagic conversion)\n4. **Aggressive risk factor management**: Blood pressure goal <130/80; A1c goal <7%; high-intensity statin; smoking cessation maintained; weight management\n5. **Repeat imaging**: MRI brain at 3-6 months to assess for hemorrhagic transformation on anticoagulation")
B.aWT=new A.c("+ What is the prognosis for meaningful neurological recovery in bilateral MCA strokes with this level of cognitive impairment?","**Prognosis in Bilateral MCA Strokes**:\n\n**Cognitive Recovery**:\n- Bilateral MCA territory infarctions carry a **very poor prognosis** for meaningful cognitive recovery\n- The combination of left hemisphere damage (language networks) and right hemisphere damage (attention/visuospatial networks) results in profound cognitive impairment that typically does not recover to a functional level\n- The left MCA stroke caused global aphasia; the right MCA stroke added left neglect and further attentional deficits -- together these eliminate most avenues for cognitive rehabilitation\n- MMSE of 4/30 at 3-5 weeks post-stroke predicts very limited recovery; most studies show patients with initial MMSE <10 after bilateral strokes rarely improve to functional independence\n\n**Motor Recovery**:\n- Bilateral hemiparesis (quadriparesis) eliminates the typical compensation strategy of using the unaffected side\n- The right side (from the earlier left MCA stroke) has had 5 weeks and shows only trace to poor voluntary movement with developing spasticity\n- The left side (from the more recent right MCA stroke) is flaccid and shows minimal voluntary movement\n- Functional ambulation is extremely unlikely; ability to participate in transfers is uncertain\n\n**Dysphagia**:\n- Bilateral hemispheric strokes cause severe oropharyngeal dysphagia\n- Failed VFSS with aspiration on all consistencies -- PEG tube dependence is likely permanent\n- May trial repeat swallow evaluation at 3-6 months, but bilateral cortical swallowing center damage has poor recovery trajectory\n\n**Overall Functional Prognosis**:\n- Most likely outcome: long-term total dependence for all ADLs and mobility\n- High risk for medical complications: aspiration pneumonia, DVT/PE, pressure injuries, UTIs, contractures\n- Mortality rate for bilateral large MCA strokes is high -- approximately 40-60% at 1 year in published series\n- Those who survive typically require 24-hour skilled nursing care\n\n**Prognostic Indicators to Follow**:\n- Serial cognitive assessments every 2-4 weeks to detect any trajectory of improvement\n- Emergence of consistent command-following or purposeful communication would significantly change prognosis\n- Return of motor function, particularly in the right upper extremity (longer time since injury)\n- Development of reliable yes/no communication (even with eye blinks) would be a positive indicator")
B.aWz=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Despite the poor prognosis, the patient is admitted to acute rehabilitation. You must design a program appropriate for his functional level while addressing goals of care.")
B.b02=new A.c("+ How do you approach cognitive rehabilitation and functional goal-setting in a patient with severe bilateral hemispheric damage?","**Rehabilitation Approach for Severe Bilateral Stroke**:\n\n**Justification for Acute Rehabilitation Admission**:\n- Patient must demonstrate ability to tolerate 3 hours of therapy per day (can be divided among disciplines)\n- Even in severe impairment, therapy can focus on: positioning, family training, preventing complications, and assessing recovery trajectory\n- If patient cannot tolerate or participate, consider subacute rehabilitation (SNF) with plan to reassess\n\n**Cognitive Stimulation Program**:\n- **Sensory stimulation protocol**: Structured multisensory stimulation (visual, auditory, tactile, olfactory) to promote arousal and awareness\n- **Environmental enrichment**: Familiar voices (family recordings), music therapy, photographs, favorite TV/radio programs\n- **Coma recovery scale assessment**: Use the JFK Coma Recovery Scale-Revised (CRS-R) serially to track emergence from minimally conscious state vs. vegetative state\n- **Monitor for improvement**: Any consistent command-following, visual tracking, or purposeful vocalization would indicate meaningful cognitive recovery potential\n\n**Functional Goals (Realistic for Current Level)**:\n1. **Positioning program**: Turning schedule Q2H for pressure injury prevention and healing; proper bed positioning to prevent contractures; upright positioning in tilt-in-space wheelchair for 2-4 hours daily to improve arousal and respiratory function\n2. **ROM and contracture prevention**: Daily passive ROM to all extremities; resting hand splints bilaterally; ankle positioning devices to prevent equinus\n3. **Pulmonary hygiene**: Aggressive suctioning protocol; chest physiotherapy; upright positioning to reduce aspiration risk from oral secretions\n4. **Family training**: Educate family on positioning, skin checks, ROM exercises, signs of complications (DVT, UTI, AD equivalent, aspiration pneumonia)\n5. **Skin management**: Offload sacral pressure injury; heel suspension boots; nutritional optimization for wound healing (adequate protein via tube feeds)\n6. **PEG tube management**: Ensure adequate nutrition (25-30 kcal/kg, 1.2-1.5 g protein/kg); head of bed elevated 30-45 degrees during and after feeds\n\n**Goals of Care Discussion**:\n- Initiate a family meeting within the first week of rehabilitation admission\n- Present realistic prognosis with compassion but honesty\n- Discuss what constitutes meaningful recovery vs. survival with severe disability\n- Explore the patient's previously expressed wishes (advance directives, conversations with family)\n- Address code status, feeding tube continuation, and what level of medical intervention the family desires\n- Involve palliative care consultation to help facilitate these discussions\n- Set a time-limited trial: reassess at 2-4 weeks; if no measurable improvement on CRS-R or functional measures, revisit goals and consider transition to comfort-focused care or long-term care facility")
B.aRJ=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient has no advance directive. His wife reports he never discussed his wishes regarding life-sustaining treatment. The family is divided about the plan of care, and the patient cannot participate in decision-making.")
B.aUO=new A.c("+ Describe the process for determining decision-making capacity, surrogate decision-making hierarchy, and guardianship/conservatorship when family members disagree.","**Decision-Making Capacity Assessment**:\n\n**Capacity vs. Competency**:\n- **Capacity** is a clinical determination made by physicians\n- **Competency** is a legal determination made by courts\n- This patient clearly lacks decision-making capacity: he cannot understand information, appreciate his situation, reason about options, or communicate a choice (all four Appelbaum criteria are absent)\n\n**Surrogate Decision-Making**:\n\n**Hierarchy (varies by state but generally)**:\n1. Health care proxy/durable power of attorney for health care (if designated) -- this patient has none\n2. Legal guardian (if appointed by court)\n3. Spouse\n4. Adult children\n5. Parents\n6. Siblings\n7. Other close relatives or friends\n\n**In this case**: The wife is the default surrogate decision-maker as the spouse, in most state hierarchies. However, the adult children are challenging her decisions.\n\n**Standards for Surrogate Decision-Making**:\n1. **Substituted judgment standard** (preferred): What would the patient have wanted? Based on prior conversations, values, religious beliefs, lifestyle choices. The surrogate should attempt to make the decision the patient would have made.\n2. **Best interest standard** (fallback): When the patient's wishes are truly unknown, the surrogate should choose what a reasonable person would consider to be in the patient's best interest, weighing benefits and burdens.\n\n**When Family Disagrees -- Conflict Resolution**:\n\n1. **Family meeting with interdisciplinary team**: Include attending physician, social worker, chaplain, nursing, and all involved family members. Explore the root of disagreement -- often it is grief, guilt, misunderstanding of prognosis, or differing values.\n2. **Ethics committee consultation**: If the family meeting does not resolve the conflict, involve the hospital ethics committee for a formal consultation. The ethics committee can mediate, clarify ethical principles, and provide recommendations.\n3. **Palliative care consultation**: Palliative care specialists are trained in facilitating goals-of-care conversations and can help bridge differences.\n4. **Time-limited trial**: Propose a defined period (e.g., 4 weeks) of continued treatment with specific milestones. If milestones are not met, reconvene to reassess. This gives all family members time to process and observe the trajectory.\n5. **Guardianship/Conservatorship (last resort)**:\n   - If the family cannot reach consensus and the conflict is irreconcilable, any party can petition the court for guardianship\n   - The court appoints a guardian with legal authority to make health care decisions\n   - Process involves: petition filing, medical evaluation, court hearing, guardian appointment\n   - Can take weeks to months; emergency guardianship available for urgent decisions\n   - Court may appoint a family member or an independent guardian (attorney or professional guardian)\n\n**Long-Term Care Placement**:\n- If patient remains severely impaired after rehabilitation trial, discharge options include:\n  - Skilled nursing facility with long-term care\n  - Veterans' home (if applicable)\n  - Home with 24-hour caregiver support (extremely costly; typically not sustainable)\n- Medicaid spend-down may be necessary for long-term nursing facility coverage\n- Social work should begin discharge planning early given the likely prolonged need for placement")
B.aTq=new A.c(u.j,"You convene a family meeting to discuss goals of care. The patient's wife (age 68) states firmly: \"I want everything done. He's a fighter. I want full code, I want the feeding tube, I want aggressive rehab. God will heal him.\" The patient's eldest daughter responds angrily: \"Mom, look at him! He wouldn't want to live like this. He told me years ago that if he ever couldn't take care of himself, he'd rather die. You're being selfish keeping him alive for yourself.\" The wife begins crying. The other two adult children are silent and visibly uncomfortable.")
B.b1D=new A.c("+ Demonstrate how you would facilitate this family meeting, address the conflict between the wife and daughter, and guide the family toward a unified decision.",'**Model Response -- Facilitating a High-Conflict Family Meeting**:\n\n*[First, allow a moment of silence. Acknowledge the pain in the room.]*\n\n"I can see how much all of you love [patient\'s name], and I can see how painful this is for every one of you. Before we go any further, I want you to know that there are no wrong feelings here. You are all coming from a place of love, even if you see the path forward differently.\n\nMrs. [name], I hear you. Your hope and your faith are powerful, and I respect them deeply. And [daughter\'s name], I hear you too. It sounds like your father shared something very personal with you about his wishes, and you are trying to honor that. Both of those things matter.\n\nBefore we discuss what to do, I want to make sure everyone has the same understanding of where [patient\'s name] is medically. May I share what we know?"\n\n*[Present the medical facts with compassion but clarity]*\n\n"Your husband and father has had two very large strokes affecting both sides of his brain. Right now, he is not able to speak, follow commands, eat, or move his arms or legs in a meaningful way. His brain injury is severe.\n\nI want to be honest with you because I believe you deserve honesty: in my medical experience, and based on the research we have, the chances of him recovering to a point where he could communicate, recognize you, or participate in his own care are very small. I cannot say it is impossible -- and I would never take away your hope -- but I want you to have realistic expectations so you can make decisions that truly reflect what he would want.\n\nNow, [daughter\'s name], you mentioned that your father told you he would not want to live if he could not take care of himself. Can you tell us more about that conversation? When was it, and what specifically did he say?"\n\n*[Allow the daughter to share; then turn to the wife]*\n\n"Mrs. [name], did your husband ever talk to you about what he would want in a situation like this? Even indirectly -- maybe about a friend or family member who was very ill?"\n\n*[Listen to the wife\'s response; validate both perspectives]*\n\n"Here is what I want to suggest. I do not think we need to make every decision today. What I would propose is this: we continue rehabilitation for the next four weeks. During that time, we will do everything we can to give him the best chance of showing us improvement. We will measure his progress using specific tools.\n\nAt the end of four weeks, we will meet again. If he has shown meaningful improvement -- if he is starting to follow commands, communicate, or participate in his care -- we will continue the current path. If he has not shown improvement, I would ask that we revisit this conversation and consider whether continuing aggressive treatment is truly what he would want, or whether we should focus on keeping him comfortable and dignified.\n\nIn the meantime, I do not think we need to change the code status today. But I would like to involve our palliative care team -- and I want to be clear, palliative care is not the same as giving up. Their job is to help manage his comfort and to help your family navigate these decisions together.\n\nI also want to acknowledge something directly: this disagreement between family members is incredibly common and does not mean anyone is wrong or selfish. You are each processing grief differently, and you are each trying to honor a man you love. Our social worker and chaplain are available to meet with any or all of you individually if that would help.\n\nFor you two --" *[addressing the silent adult children]* "-- I want to make sure your voices are heard too. Do you have any thoughts or questions you would like to share?"\n\n**Key Communication Principles Demonstrated**:\n1. **Validated both sides** without taking sides or undermining either position\n2. **Presented medical reality** with compassion but did not sugarcoat prognosis\n3. **Explored the patient\'s values** by asking both the wife and daughter for specific recollections\n4. **Proposed a time-limited trial** as a bridge -- allows the wife to maintain hope while setting objective criteria for reassessment\n5. **Included the silent family members** -- they may hold crucial information or become future decision-makers\n6. **Named the conflict** directly without judgment ("this disagreement is incredibly common")\n7. **Offered resources** (palliative care, social work, chaplaincy) without forcing them\n8. **Did not demand an immediate code status decision** -- allowed time and trust to build\n9. **Separated palliative care from "giving up"** -- a common misconception that must be addressed\n10. **Maintained the physician role as guide and mediator** rather than unilateral decision-maker')
B.aED=s([B.b2N,B.aVj,B.aYj,B.aWq,B.aTw,B.aWT,B.aWz,B.b02,B.aRJ,B.aUO,B.aTq,B.b1D],t.E)
B.Qq=new A.a5("stroke-bilateral-mca","Bilateral MCA Strokes with Severe Cognitive Impairment",B.aED,!1)
B.b3q=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 63-year-old right-handed female is referred to outpatient PM&R 4 months after a left thalamic hemorrhagic stroke. She presents with severe, unrelenting burning pain affecting her entire right side \u2014 face, arm, trunk, and leg \u2014 that began approximately 6 weeks after the stroke. She rates the pain 9/10 and states it has not responded to multiple medication trials. She is tearful throughout the interview and tells you, "I cannot live like this anymore. The pain never stops. I have thought about ending it." Her husband confirms she has become increasingly withdrawn, has stopped eating regularly, and has been giving away personal belongings.')
B.aPo=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **Stroke Details**: Left posterolateral thalamic hemorrhagic stroke 4 months ago. Presented with sudden right-sided weakness, sensory loss, and headache. CT head showed 1.5 cm left thalamic hemorrhage. Managed conservatively in neurology ICU.\n- **Pain Onset**: Initially had right-sided numbness post-stroke. At approximately 6 weeks post-stroke, developed spontaneous burning, searing pain in right face, arm, trunk, and leg. Pain is constant with severe exacerbations triggered by light touch, temperature changes, and emotional stress.\n- **Pain Character**: Burning, searing, 'like acid under the skin.' Superimposed lancinating/shooting pain. Allodynia \u2014 unable to tolerate clothing on right arm, bedsheets on right leg. Hyperalgesia \u2014 light touch feels like burning.\n- **Failed Treatments**: Gabapentin titrated to 1200 mg TID (minimal relief, sedation), pregabalin 300 mg BID (no improvement), amitriptyline 75 mg QHS (orthostatic hypotension, discontinued), duloxetine 60 mg daily (nausea, stopped after 2 weeks), oxycodone 10 mg Q6H (no meaningful pain relief, constipation).\n- **Functional Status**: Prior to pain onset, had recovered to modified independence with a quad cane. Right hemiparesis mild (4/5 throughout). Since pain worsened, she has become homebound. Cannot tolerate wearing clothes on right arm \u2014 wears loose draping. Cannot sleep more than 2-3 hours. Has stopped physical therapy because touch from the therapist is unbearable.\n- **Psychiatric**: PHQ-9 score 26 (severe depression). Reports suicidal ideation \u2014 has thought about taking all her medications at once. Has been giving away jewelry and photo albums to her daughters. Husband found her writing letters to family members last week.\n- **PMH**: Hypertension (poorly controlled \u2014 the cause of thalamic hemorrhage), type 2 diabetes, hyperlipidemia, obesity.\n- **Medications**: Gabapentin 1200 mg TID, oxycodone 10 mg Q6H, lisinopril 20 mg daily, metformin 1000 mg BID, atorvastatin 40 mg daily, aspirin 81 mg daily.\n- **Social**: Retired schoolteacher. Married 38 years. Two adult daughters. Husband is primary caregiver. Previously very active in church and book club \u2014 has not attended either in 3 months.")
B.b1X=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 158/92 mmHg, HR 88 bpm, RR 16, SpO2 98% on RA, Temp 36.8C, BMI 31.\n\n**General**: Tearful female in loose-fitting clothing. Right sleeve draped loosely \u2014 grimaces and withdraws when sleeve brushes her arm. Appears fatigued and distressed.\n\n**Neurological**:\n- **Mental Status**: Alert, oriented x3. Flat affect with intermittent crying. Speech fluent without aphasia.\n- **Cranial Nerves**: Right lower facial droop (mild, residual). Right-sided facial allodynia \u2014 withdraws from light touch to right cheek.\n- **Motor**: Right upper extremity 4-/5 throughout. Right lower extremity 4/5 throughout. Left side 5/5. Mildly increased right-sided tone (Modified Ashworth 1).\n- **Sensory**: **Right side** \u2014 Profound allodynia (cotton wisp produces 8/10 burning pain). Hyperalgesia (pinprick produces 10/10 pain with radiation). Decreased proprioception at right toes and fingers. Temperature dysesthesia \u2014 both warm and cold stimuli produce burning pain. **Left side** \u2014 intact to all modalities.\n- **Coordination**: Right-sided dysmetria on finger-to-nose (mild \u2014 related to sensory impairment more than cerebellar dysfunction).\n\n**Musculoskeletal**: Right shoulder mildly subluxed (1 finger-breadth). Limited right shoulder ROM \u2014 90 degrees flexion, 80 degrees abduction (pain-limited, not structural).\n\n**Gait**: Ambulates with quad cane and contact guard assist. Right foot circumduction pattern. Slow, guarded gait \u2014 winces with each right foot strike.\n\n**Psychological Observation**: Makes poor eye contact. States twice during exam that she 'cannot go on like this.' Husband appears exhausted and tearful.")
B.aRe=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","You are evaluating this patient's central post-stroke pain (CPSP) syndrome. Describe the pathophysiology, diagnostic criteria, and how to differentiate thalamic pain from other post-stroke pain etiologies.")
B.aZq=new A.c("+ Explain the pathophysiology of central post-stroke pain (Dejerine-Roussy syndrome), its diagnostic criteria, and differential diagnosis of post-stroke pain.","**Pathophysiology of Central Post-Stroke Pain (CPSP)**:\n- CPSP results from damage to the spinothalamic tract or its thalamic relay nuclei (ventral posterolateral and ventral posteromedial nuclei), disrupting normal pain processing.\n- **Central sensitization theory**: Loss of thalamic inhibitory circuits leads to spontaneous firing of deafferented pain pathways. The damaged thalamus generates aberrant pain signals without peripheral nociceptive input.\n- **Disinhibition theory**: Damage to the lateral thalamic pain system (discriminative) removes inhibition from the medial thalamic pain system (affective), leading to unopposed affective pain perception.\n- Onset is typically **delayed** \u2014 1 to 6 months post-stroke (occasionally up to 2 years). This latency corresponds to maladaptive neuroplastic reorganization.\n- Affects up to **8-10%** of stroke survivors; more common with thalamic and brainstem strokes.\n\n**Diagnostic Criteria for CPSP (Klit et al. / IASP)**:\n1. Pain develops after a stroke (within a timeframe consistent with lesion development)\n2. Pain is in a body area corresponding to the CNS lesion\n3. Sensory signs (positive or negative) are confirmed in the painful territory by clinical examination\n4. Other causes of pain are excluded (nociceptive, peripheral neuropathic)\n\n**Clinical Features**:\n- **Constant burning, aching, or freezing** baseline pain\n- **Superimposed paroxysmal** lancinating or shooting pain\n- **Allodynia** (pain from normally non-painful stimuli) in >50% of cases\n- **Hyperalgesia** (exaggerated response to painful stimuli)\n- **Dysesthesia** \u2014 temperature stimuli produce abnormal painful sensations\n- Pain distribution corresponds to the vascular territory of the stroke\n- Pain is often **disproportionate** to the degree of motor impairment\n\n**Differential Diagnosis of Post-Stroke Pain**:\n1. **Central post-stroke pain** (this patient): Neuropathic, delayed onset, corresponds to lesion territory, allodynia/hyperalgesia present\n2. **Hemiplegic shoulder pain**: Mechanical \u2014 subluxation, rotator cuff impingement, adhesive capsulitis, spasticity. Localized to shoulder. No allodynia in distant territories.\n3. **Spasticity-related pain**: Muscle cramping and stiffness. Worse with movement. Responds to antispasticity measures.\n4. **Complex regional pain syndrome (CRPS)**: Post-stroke CRPS (shoulder-hand syndrome). Swelling, color changes, temperature asymmetry in affected hand. Distinct from CPSP.\n5. **Musculoskeletal pain**: From abnormal gait mechanics, deconditioning, falls. Nociceptive quality, localized, no allodynia.\n6. **Headache**: Post-hemorrhagic headache. Different character \u2014 dull, pressure-like, not neuropathic.\n7. **Pre-existing pain conditions**: Diabetic neuropathy (this patient has diabetes \u2014 must distinguish peripheral neuropathic from central neuropathic pain). Diabetic neuropathy is typically distal, symmetric, stocking-glove distribution vs. CPSP which follows the vascular territory.")
B.aTL=new A.c("+ What advanced pharmacologic and interventional treatment options exist for refractory central post-stroke pain?","**Pharmacologic Management (Evidence-Based Hierarchy)**:\n\n**First-line agents**:\n- **Amitriptyline 25-75 mg QHS**: Best-studied medication for CPSP. NNT ~4. This patient had orthostatic hypotension \u2014 consider nortriptyline (less anticholinergic/hypotensive) at 25 mg QHS, titrate to 75-100 mg.\n- **Lamotrigine 200-400 mg daily**: Strongest evidence among anticonvulsants for CPSP specifically (NNT ~4 in landmark trial by Vestergaard et al.). Must titrate slowly (25 mg/day for 2 weeks, then 50 mg/day increase every 2 weeks) to avoid Stevens-Johnson syndrome. **This is the next best option for this patient.**\n- **Duloxetine 60-120 mg**: Patient stopped after 2 weeks due to nausea \u2014 consider re-trial with slower titration (start 30 mg for 1-2 weeks, take with food). Dual mechanism (serotonin/norepinephrine) is beneficial for central pain.\n\n**Second-line agents**:\n- **Carbamazepine/oxcarbazepine**: May help lancinating component. Monitor sodium levels.\n- **Mexiletine**: Oral sodium channel blocker \u2014 used in refractory CPSP. Requires cardiac monitoring (ECG before and during treatment).\n- **Low-dose naltrexone (1.5-4.5 mg)**: Emerging evidence for central pain via microglial modulation.\n\n**Combination therapy**: Tricyclic antidepressant + lamotrigine. Nortriptyline addresses sleep and depression; lamotrigine addresses central pain specifically.\n\n**Interventional Options**:\n\n1. **Repetitive transcranial magnetic stimulation (rTMS)**:\n- High-frequency rTMS to the **primary motor cortex (M1)** contralateral to pain (left M1 in this patient)\n- Evidence: 40-50% of CPSP patients report significant pain reduction\n- Non-invasive, repeatable. Initial series of 5-10 daily sessions, then maintenance\n- Level B recommendation (probably effective) per AAN guidelines\n\n2. **Motor cortex stimulation (MCS) \u2014 surgical**:\n- Epidural electrode placed over M1. Considered for refractory cases failing all pharmacologic and non-invasive options.\n- 50-60% response rate in carefully selected patients. Trial stimulation before permanent implant.\n\n3. **Deep brain stimulation (DBS)**:\n- Targets: Periventricular/periaqueductal gray (PAG/PVG) or ventral posterolateral thalamus\n- Reserved for most refractory cases. Variable outcomes (30-50% long-term success).\n\n4. **Intrathecal drug delivery**:\n- Intrathecal baclofen has been trialed for CPSP with some benefit in select patients\n- Intrathecal ziconotide (N-type calcium channel blocker) may be considered\n\n5. **Transcranial direct current stimulation (tDCS)**:\n- Anodal tDCS over M1. Less robust evidence than rTMS but more accessible, portable, and lower cost.\n- Can be used as adjunct to pharmacotherapy.\n\n**Recommended Strategy for This Patient**:\n1. Initiate lamotrigine with slow titration\n2. Start nortriptyline 25 mg QHS (better tolerated than amitriptyline)\n3. Begin taper of gabapentin and opioids (neither effective)\n4. Refer for rTMS trial\n5. Address psychiatric emergency (suicidality) simultaneously")
B.aXI=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Develop a comprehensive management plan for this patient's refractory central post-stroke pain, including immediate safety planning for suicidal ideation.")
B.b_j=new A.c("+ Outline a management plan integrating pain treatment, psychiatric crisis intervention, and functional rehabilitation.",'**Immediate Psychiatric Safety Assessment and Plan**:\n\n**Risk stratification \u2014 this patient is HIGH RISK**:\n- Active suicidal ideation with a plan (overdose on medications)\n- Preparatory behaviors (giving away belongings, writing letters)\n- Severe depression (PHQ-9 = 26)\n- Chronic uncontrolled pain as driving factor\n- Access to lethal means (stockpile of medications including opioids)\n\n**Immediate actions**:\n1. **Do not leave the patient alone**. Keep her in the exam room with a staff member.\n2. **Directly ask**: "You mentioned ending it. Have you thought about how you would do it? Do you have a plan? Do you have access to medications or other means?" (Asking about suicide does NOT increase risk \u2014 it opens the door to intervention.)\n3. **Lethal means restriction**: Ask husband to secure ALL medications in a locked container and dispense them dose by dose. Remove opioids from home if possible. Prescribe only small quantities.\n4. **Psychiatric consultation**: Same-day or next-day urgent psychiatric evaluation. Consider whether inpatient psychiatric hospitalization is warranted given preparatory behaviors. If she expresses imminent intent, initiate involuntary hold.\n5. **Safety plan** (Stanley-Brown model): (a) Warning signs she recognizes; (b) Internal coping strategies; (c) People she can contact for distraction; (d) Family/friends who can help; (e) Professionals to contact \u2014 therapist, crisis line (988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline); (f) Making the environment safe.\n6. **Document** the risk assessment and safety plan in the medical record.\n\n**Pain Management Revision**:\n1. **Stop ineffective medications**: Taper gabapentin over 2-3 weeks (not effective, causing sedation). Begin opioid taper \u2014 oxycodone is not effective for CPSP and adds overdose risk.\n2. **Start lamotrigine**: 25 mg daily x 2 weeks, then 50 mg daily x 2 weeks, then 100 mg BID. Target 200-400 mg/day.\n3. **Start nortriptyline**: 25 mg QHS, titrate by 25 mg weekly to 75-100 mg. Monitor orthostatic BP. ECG at baseline.\n4. **Topical therapies for allodynia**: Lidocaine 5% patches to right arm and leg (where allodynia is worst). Capsaicin 8% patch (applied in clinic \u2014 single application can provide 3 months relief).\n5. **rTMS referral**: Schedule left M1 high-frequency rTMS series.\n6. **TENS unit**: May provide modest relief. Low-frequency TENS over painful areas.\n\n**Functional Rehabilitation**:\n1. **Desensitization program**: Graded tactile exposure to the allodynic areas. Start with self-applied stimuli (the patient controls the touch). Progress from light fabrics to firmer pressure over weeks.\n2. **Aquatic therapy**: Warm water (92-94F) provides gentle, diffuse sensory input that may modulate pain perception. Buoyancy reduces weight-bearing pain. Start 2x/week.\n3. **Mirror therapy**: Place unaffected left hand in mirror to create visual illusion of pain-free right hand movement. Evidence for central pain modulation.\n4. **Graded motor imagery**: Left-right discrimination, imagined movements, then mirror therapy \u2014 staged approach to cortical reorganization.\n5. **Sleep hygiene**: Pain disrupts sleep, poor sleep worsens pain. Nortriptyline will help. Add sleep hygiene counseling. Consider melatonin 3-5 mg.\n\n**Caregiver Support**:\n- Husband appears exhausted. Assess caregiver burden (Zarit Burden Interview). Refer to caregiver support group. Provide respite care options.\n- Include husband in safety planning \u2014 he is the primary observer of warning signs.')
B.b2s=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","This patient's refractory pain condition requires coordination across multiple specialties and careful medication management with a suicidal patient. How do you organize this care?")
B.aTe=new A.c("+ Describe the multidisciplinary team coordination and systems-level considerations for managing refractory CPSP with comorbid suicidality.","**Multidisciplinary Pain-Psychiatry Team**:\n\n1. **Physiatrist (PM&R)**: Quarterback of the rehabilitation plan. Coordinates pain management, functional rehabilitation, and specialist referrals. Manages medication changes.\n2. **Psychiatrist**: Urgent evaluation for suicidality. Medication management for depression/PTSD. Decision regarding inpatient vs. outpatient psychiatric care. Ongoing risk monitoring.\n3. **Psychologist**: Cognitive behavioral therapy for chronic pain (CBT-CP). Acceptance and commitment therapy (ACT). Safety planning collaboration.\n4. **Neurologist**: Co-management of central pain pharmacology. rTMS or neuromodulation referral. Monitoring for seizure risk with lamotrigine.\n5. **Pain medicine specialist**: Interventional options if pharmacologic management fails. Intrathecal pump evaluation if warranted.\n6. **Physical therapist**: Desensitization, aquatic therapy, gait training. Must be trained in allodynia management \u2014 standard hands-on PT will worsen symptoms.\n7. **Occupational therapist**: Adaptive strategies for dressing and ADLs given clothing intolerance. Desensitization of hand.\n8. **Social worker**: Caregiver support resources, community mental health connections, insurance navigation for rTMS coverage.\n9. **Pharmacist**: Medication reconciliation critical \u2014 multiple CNS-active drugs, drug interactions, overdose risk assessment.\n\n**Systems-Level Safety Protocols**:\n- **Prescription Drug Monitoring Program (PDMP)**: Check before prescribing. Ensure no other providers are prescribing controlled substances.\n- **Small-quantity prescriptions**: Given suicidality, prescribe only 1-2 week supplies of all medications. No 90-day refills.\n- **Pharmacy lockbox program**: Some pharmacies offer medication lock boxes and daily dispensing for at-risk patients.\n- **Electronic health record flags**: Place a behavioral health alert for suicidal ideation so any provider seeing this patient is aware.\n- **Care coordination meetings**: Monthly multidisciplinary meeting (or more frequently initially) to align pain and psychiatric treatment.\n\n**Insurance and Access**:\n- rTMS may require prior authorization. Appeal if denied \u2014 document failed medication trials.\n- Lamotrigine and nortriptyline are inexpensive generics \u2014 access should not be a barrier.\n- Aquatic therapy may need specific prescription language to be covered.\n- If patient is Medicare-eligible (age 63), most services are covered. Supplement gaps with social work assistance.")
B.b_v=new A.c(u.j,'During your examination, the patient says clearly: "I have thought about taking all my pills at once. I wrote goodbye letters to my daughters. I just want the pain to stop." Her husband begins sobbing. You must conduct an immediate suicide risk assessment and safety intervention.')
B.aNq=new A.c("+ Demonstrate how you would respond to this patient's active suicidal ideation with a plan, conduct a safety assessment, and communicate with both the patient and her distressed husband.",'**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Stop everything. Give this your full attention.**\n[Put down your pen/tablet. Turn to face the patient fully. Speak calmly and directly.]\n"Thank you for telling me that. I know that was not easy to say, and I am glad you said it. What you just told me is the most important thing we are going to talk about today, and I need to make sure I understand exactly what you are feeling."\n\n**2. Directly assess the suicidal ideation \u2014 do not dance around it.**\n"You said you have thought about taking all your pills. I need to ask you some direct questions, and I want you to answer honestly. There is no wrong answer.\n- Have you decided when you would do this?\n- Have you gathered the pills together?\n- Is there anything right now that is stopping you from doing it?\n- Have you thought about other ways of ending your life besides the pills?"\n\n**3. Acknowledge her husband\'s distress without losing focus on the patient.**\n[Turn briefly to husband] "Mr. [Name], I can see how painful this is for you. Your wife just did something incredibly brave by being honest with us. I need to focus on her safety right now, and then I am going to include you in the plan. Can you stay with us?"\n[Return focus to patient]\n\n**4. Validate the pain without validating the solution.**\n"I hear you that the pain is unbearable. Four months of burning pain that nothing has touched \u2014 I believe you. That is not something anyone should have to endure, and I understand why you feel desperate for it to stop. But I want to be direct with you: I do not believe we have exhausted your treatment options. We have not tried the medications that have the best evidence for your specific type of pain. We have not tried brain stimulation. I am not willing to accept that this is as good as it gets for you, and I am asking you not to accept that either."\n\n**5. Contract for safety and take concrete action.**\n"Here is what I need from you right now. I need you to agree to stay safe today while we put a plan in place. In return, I am going to do three things before you leave this office: First, I am going to start you on a medication that specifically targets the type of pain you have \u2014 something we have not tried yet. Second, I am going to arrange for you to see a psychiatrist this week, not because you are crazy, but because your pain has pushed you to a crisis point and you deserve specialized support. Third, I need your husband to take all of your medications home and lock them up. You will get your doses one at a time from him. Can you agree to that?"\n\n**6. Include the husband in the safety plan.**\n"Mr. [Name], I need your help. Tonight, when you get home, I need you to gather every medication in the house \u2014 prescription, over-the-counter, everything \u2014 and put them in a place she cannot access without you. A locked box or a locked cabinet. You will give her each dose at the scheduled time. I also need you to watch for warning signs: if she becomes very calm suddenly after being distressed, if she talks about being a burden, if she tries to be alone for extended periods, or if she gives away more belongings \u2014 call 988 or go to the emergency room immediately."\n\n**7. Provide crisis resources.**\n"I am going to give you both a card with the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline number. You can call or text 988 any time, day or night. I am also going to put my direct clinic number on here. If you are in crisis before your psychiatry appointment, I want you to call us."\n\n**8. Close with hope grounded in honesty.**\n"I am not going to promise you the pain will go away completely. But I have treated patients with this exact condition who went from where you are now to a life they wanted to live. The path is not quick, and it is not easy, but it exists. I am asking you to give me the chance to walk it with you. Will you come back next week?"\n\n**Key Principles**: Direct inquiry about suicide plan and means. Lethal means restriction (the single most effective suicide prevention intervention). Validation of suffering without endorsing death as solution. Concrete, immediate safety actions. Including the caregiver as a safety resource. Providing hope grounded in realistic treatment options. Follow-up appointment as a reason to stay alive.')
B.aG0=s([B.b3q,B.aPo,B.b1X,B.aRe,B.aZq,B.aTL,B.aXI,B.b_j,B.b2s,B.aTe,B.b_v,B.aNq],t.E)
B.QQ=new A.a5("stroke-thalamic-pain","Thalamic Pain Syndrome (Dejerine-Roussy)",B.aG0,!1)
B.b2Z=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 48-year-old male is admitted to the inpatient rehabilitation unit 6 weeks after a basilar artery occlusion resulting in a ventral pontine infarction. He is fully conscious and cognitively intact but has complete quadriplegia and anarthria. His only voluntary movements are vertical eye movements and blinking. He was initially misdiagnosed as being in a vegetative state in the ICU for 9 days before a neurologist identified preserved consciousness through eye blink responses. He communicates through a partner-assisted eye-blink code (one blink = yes, two blinks = no). His wife and 14-year-old daughter are at bedside. He has a tracheostomy and is ventilator-dependent. He has a PEG tube for nutrition.")
B.aRV=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **Event**: Sudden onset vertigo, dysarthria, and progressive quadriparesis over 6 hours. Presented to ED where CT angiography revealed basilar artery occlusion. Attempted endovascular thrombectomy was unsuccessful. MRI confirmed extensive ventral pontine infarction bilaterally with sparing of the tegmentum (preserving consciousness and vertical eye movements).\n- **ICU Course**: Intubated for airway protection. Initially assessed as 'unresponsive' \u2014 documented as 'comatose' for 9 days. Wife repeatedly told staff he was 'in there' because she noticed he would blink when she spoke. Neurology consultation on day 9 confirmed locked-in syndrome via structured eye-blink assessment.\n- **Tracheostomy**: Performed day 12. Currently ventilator-dependent (AC mode, FiO2 30%, PEEP 5, RR 14). Tolerates tracheostomy collar trials for 30-60 minutes.\n- **Nutrition**: PEG tube placed day 14. Tube feeds at goal rate. NPO \u2014 video fluoroscopic swallow study showed absent pharyngeal swallow initiation.\n- **Neurological Status**: Cognition fully intact \u2014 scored 28/30 on a modified MMSE administered via eye-blink responses (errors were due to communication lag, not cognitive deficit). Vertical eye movements and eyelid opening/closing are the only voluntary motor functions. No horizontal eye movements. No facial movement. No limb movement. No head or trunk control.\n- **Medical Complications**: Two UTIs (indwelling catheter). One episode of atelectasis requiring bronchoscopy. DVT prophylaxis with enoxaparin.\n- **Pre-morbid**: Software engineer. Married 18 years. Daughter is an honor student. Avid cyclist. No prior medical history except hypertension diagnosed 2 years ago \u2014 had stopped taking lisinopril 6 months prior to stroke.\n- **Medications**: Enoxaparin 40 mg SQ daily, pantoprazole 40 mg via PEG, acetaminophen 1000 mg via PEG Q8H, docusate 100 mg via PEG BID, tube feeds (Osmolite 1.2 at 75 mL/hr).")
B.b0T=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 128/78 mmHg, HR 72 bpm, RR 14 (ventilator), SpO2 98% on 30% FiO2, Temp 37.1C.\n\n**General**: Alert male in hospital bed with tracheostomy connected to ventilator. Eyes open and tracking the examiner. Blinks purposefully in response to questions.\n\n**Neurological**:\n- **Consciousness**: Fully alert. Follows complex commands via eye blinks. Demonstrates humor \u2014 blinks 'no' emphatically when asked if the hospital food is good (he cannot eat).\n- **Cranial Nerves**: Vertical eye movements intact \u2014 full upgaze and downgaze. Horizontal eye movements absent bilaterally. Pupils equal, reactive. No facial movement (CN VII bilateral paralysis). No jaw movement. No tongue movement. Corneal reflexes absent bilaterally.\n- **Motor**: 0/5 in all four extremities. No voluntary movement in any muscle group below the eyes. Diffuse flaccidity. No head control. No trunk control.\n- **Sensory**: Intact \u2014 blinks to indicate he feels light touch and pinprick in all extremities (confirmed by systematic testing with yes/no blink responses). Reports pain in bilateral shoulders via eye-blink pain assessment.\n- **Reflexes**: Bilateral Babinski signs. Hyperreflexia in all extremities (3+). No clonus.\n\n**Respiratory**: Ventilator-dependent. Breath sounds clear bilaterally. Tracheostomy site clean. Tolerates 30-60 minute collar trials.\n\n**Skin**: Stage 1 pressure injury over sacrum (non-blanchable erythema). Heels intact with offloading boots in place.\n\n**Musculoskeletal**: Bilateral shoulder pain with passive ROM (blinks rapidly indicating pain at 80 degrees flexion bilaterally). Early contracture formation \u2014 bilateral ankle plantar flexion contractures (dorsiflexion limited to -10 degrees). Bilateral elbow flexion contractures (extension limited to -15 degrees).")
B.b2h=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","You must establish an effective communication system for this patient and plan for long-term augmentative and alternative communication (AAC). Discuss the diagnostic evaluation and communication technology options.")
B.aQ7=new A.c("+ Describe the AAC assessment and technology options for a patient with locked-in syndrome whose only voluntary movements are vertical eye movements and blinking.","**AAC Assessment for Locked-In Syndrome**:\n\n**Step 1: Characterize Residual Motor Function**\n- This patient has: vertical eye movements (upgaze, downgaze), eyelid opening/closing (blink)\n- He does NOT have: horizontal eye movements, facial movement, limb movement, phonation, head movement\n- Sensation is intact \u2014 proprioceptive and tactile feedback channels are available\n- Cognition is intact \u2014 he can learn complex systems\n\n**Step 2: Low-Technology AAC (Immediate)**:\n- **Partner-assisted eye-blink code**: Already in use. Yes (1 blink) / No (2 blinks). Functional for basic needs but extremely slow for complex communication.\n- **Eye-gaze letter board**: Communication partner holds a transparent letter board (E-TRAN board) between themselves and the patient. Letters are arranged in groups at the corners and center. Patient looks at the group containing the desired letter, then at the specific letter. Partner reads the selection. Can achieve 2-4 words per minute with practice.\n- **Vertical scanning alphabet board**: Letters presented sequentially on a vertical display. Patient blinks at the desired letter. More independent than E-TRAN but slow.\n\n**Step 3: High-Technology AAC (Goal)**:\n\n1. **Eye-tracking computer systems**: \n- **Tobii Dynavox** (e.g., Tobii I-Series or TD Pilot): Eye-tracking device mounted to a computer screen. Infrared cameras track the patient's gaze position. He looks at letters, words, or icons on screen to compose messages. Text-to-speech output.\n- Requires calibration for vertical gaze (most systems optimize for both horizontal and vertical \u2014 may need customization since he only has vertical).\n- Communication rates: 8-15 words per minute with practice.\n- Can also control computer, access internet, email, social media \u2014 restoring cognitive participation.\n\n2. **Eye-blink switches**:\n- If eye-tracking calibration fails due to absent horizontal gaze, an infrared eye-blink sensor can serve as a binary switch.\n- Combined with scanning software (letters highlighted sequentially, patient blinks to select), this provides independent communication.\n- Slower than full eye-tracking (3-6 words per minute) but reliable.\n\n3. **Brain-computer interface (BCI)** \u2014 emerging technology:\n- EEG-based systems detect cortical signals associated with intended movements or attention.\n- P300 speller: Letters flash on screen, patient attends to desired letter, EEG detects the P300 evoked potential.\n- Investigational but increasingly available in specialized centers.\n\n**Step 4: Assessment by SLP and Assistive Technology Specialist**:\n- Formal AAC evaluation by SLP with AT expertise\n- Trial of multiple devices to determine optimal system\n- Training patient AND family/caregivers in the selected system\n- Funding: AAC devices are covered by Medicare as durable medical equipment (DME) with proper documentation of medical necessity. Typical cost $8,000-$15,000 for eye-tracking system.")
B.b29=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Develop a comprehensive inpatient rehabilitation plan for this patient with locked-in syndrome, including ventilator weaning, complication prevention, and long-term planning.")
B.b2C=new A.c("+ Outline the rehabilitation management priorities for locked-in syndrome, including ventilator management, contracture prevention, and discharge planning.","**Inpatient Rehabilitation Priorities**:\n\n**1. Communication (Highest Priority)**:\n- Establish reliable AAC system as detailed in Domain B\n- Every clinical decision must include the patient's input via his communication system\n- Document his communication method in the medical record so all staff can interact with him\n- Train all nursing staff, therapists, and family in the eye-blink code\n\n**2. Ventilator Weaning**:\n- Current: tolerating 30-60 minute tracheostomy collar trials\n- **Progressive ventilator free breathing (PVFB)**: Increase collar trial duration by 15-30 minutes per session, 2-3 times daily, monitoring SpO2, HR, respiratory distress\n- Goal: Extend to daytime ventilator independence, then nighttime\n- Complete ventilator liberation is achieved in **some** locked-in patients (approximately 50-60%) as brainstem respiratory centers in the tegmentum may be spared\n- Respiratory therapy: Chest physiotherapy, assisted cough techniques (mechanical insufflation-exsufflation), suctioning protocol\n- Monitor for sleep-disordered breathing \u2014 central apnea is common after pontine strokes\n\n**3. Contracture Prevention**:\n- **ROM program**: Passive ROM to all joints 2x daily by PT/OT. Focus on ankles (already -10 degrees dorsiflexion), elbows (already -15 degrees extension), shoulders, and hips.\n- **Positioning**: Scheduled position changes every 2 hours. Ankle dorsiflexion splints worn at night. Resting hand splints in functional position.\n- **Standing program**: Tilt table standing beginning at 30 degrees, progressing to 70-80 degrees. Benefits: contracture prevention, bone density, cardiovascular conditioning, psychological well-being.\n- **Serial casting**: If ankle contractures progress despite splinting, serial casting to gradually restore neutral dorsiflexion.\n\n**4. Skin Protection**:\n- Stage 1 sacral pressure injury \u2014 requires aggressive prevention of progression\n- Pressure-relieving mattress (alternating pressure or low-air-loss)\n- Turning schedule every 2 hours documented and enforced\n- Nutrition optimization \u2014 ensure adequate protein (1.2-1.5 g/kg/day) and calories\n- Daily skin inspection with documentation\n\n**5. Medical Management**:\n- **DVT prophylaxis**: Continue enoxaparin. Bilateral SCDs when in bed.\n- **Bladder**: Transition from indwelling catheter to intermittent catheterization to reduce UTI risk. Schedule every 4-6 hours.\n- **Bowel**: Scheduled bowel program (docusate + senna + glycerin suppository every other day). Prevent impaction.\n- **Pain**: Bilateral shoulder pain \u2014 likely from capsular stretch in flaccid shoulders. Shoulder supports (lap tray when seated). Gentle ROM within pain-free range.\n- **Depression screening**: PHQ-2 via eye-blink responses weekly. Extremely high risk for depression (estimates 60-70% in locked-in patients).\n\n**6. Discharge Planning**:\n- **Timeline**: Prolonged inpatient rehabilitation \u2014 likely 2-4 months minimum\n- **Home modifications**: Hospital bed, Hoyer lift, ventilator setup, suction equipment, AAC device mounting\n- **Caregiver training**: Wife and potentially hired caregivers trained in tracheostomy care, ventilator management, tube feeding, transfers, skin care, ROM exercises, communication system\n- **Home nursing**: Will require 24-hour care \u2014 minimum 16 hours/day skilled nursing plus family\n- **Follow-up**: PM&R, pulmonology, neurology, SLP, ophthalmology (corneal protection given absent corneal reflexes)")
B.b2z=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","This patient was misdiagnosed as vegetative for 9 days. Discuss the systems failures that led to this diagnostic error and how to prevent it.")
B.aUc=new A.c("+ What systems-level failures contribute to misdiagnosis of locked-in syndrome as vegetative state, and how can institutional protocols prevent this?","**The Diagnostic Error**:\n- Locked-in syndrome is **misdiagnosed as vegetative state or coma** in up to **40-50%** of cases initially (Laureys et al., 2005). Average time to correct diagnosis is **2.5 months** in historical series.\n- This patient was misdiagnosed for 9 days \u2014 the wife's observations were initially dismissed by staff.\n\n**Systems Failures That Contribute to Misdiagnosis**:\n\n1. **Failure to assess consciousness in patients who cannot move**:\n- Standard neurological assessment relies on motor responses (GCS motor score). A patient with no motor output is scored as GCS 3-4 even if fully conscious.\n- Locked-in patients cannot demonstrate awareness through movement \u2014 only through vertical eye movements and blinks, which may be missed if not specifically sought.\n\n2. **Failure to listen to family observations**:\n- This patient's wife reported he was responsive. Family observations of consciousness are correct in the majority of cases and should trigger formal assessment.\n- Cultural bias: Families are sometimes dismissed as 'in denial' rather than taken seriously as diagnosticians.\n\n3. **Lack of standardized consciousness assessment protocols in the ICU**:\n- The GCS was not designed to differentiate locked-in syndrome from vegetative state.\n- The **Coma Recovery Scale-Revised (CRS-R)** is the gold-standard behavioral assessment for disorders of consciousness and includes specific items that can detect locked-in syndrome.\n\n**Institutional Prevention Protocols**:\n\n1. **Mandatory CRS-R assessment**: All patients with suspected disorder of consciousness should receive serial CRS-R assessments by trained clinicians. This scale specifically evaluates for eye-tracking, command-following via eye movements, and communication through eye blinks.\n\n2. **\"Assume consciousness\" protocol**: Until proven otherwise, treat all patients with acute brainstem lesions as potentially conscious. Speak to the patient, explain procedures, provide orientation information.\n\n3. **Family input documentation**: Create a standardized form for family observations of possible consciousness. Mandatory review by the attending physician.\n\n4. **Neurology consultation trigger**: Automatic neurology consultation for any patient with brainstem stroke who appears unresponsive, specifically to evaluate for locked-in syndrome.\n\n5. **Staff education**: Annual training on differentiating vegetative state, minimally conscious state, and locked-in syndrome. Include nursing staff \u2014 they spend the most time at bedside and are most likely to notice subtle signs.\n\n6. **EEG in early evaluation**: Locked-in patients have a **normal or near-normal EEG** (reactive posterior dominant rhythm), unlike vegetative state patients. Early EEG can provide immediate evidence of cortical consciousness.\n\n7. **Incident reporting**: This misdiagnosis should be reported through the hospital's patient safety reporting system as a diagnostic error. Root cause analysis should be performed.")
B.aUv=new A.c(u.j,'Using his eye-blink communication system, the patient painstakingly spells out over 20 minutes: "I-W-A-N-T-T-O-D-I-E. N-O-M-O-R-E-V-E-N-T. L-E-T-M-E-G-O." His wife is in the room. She gasps and begins crying. His 14-year-old daughter is in the hallway. You must respond to this request.')
B.aUU=new A.c("+ How do you respond to a cognitively intact locked-in patient who communicates a desire to die by withdrawal of life-sustaining treatment, with his distraught family present?",'**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Honor the communication. Do not dismiss or redirect.**\n[Maintain eye contact with the patient. He spent 20 minutes composing this message \u2014 it is deliberate and important.]\n"I hear you. You spent a long time telling me this, and I respect that you want me to understand how you feel. I am going to take this seriously, and I am not going to dismiss what you said."\n\n**2. Attend to the wife\'s acute distress without abandoning the patient.**\n[Turn briefly to wife] "Mrs. [Name], I know this is devastating to hear. I want you to know that your husband expressing this does not mean a decision has been made. It means he is suffering and he trusts us enough to be honest about it. I need to talk with him about this, and I would like you to stay if you can, but if you need a moment, our social worker is right outside."\n[If daughter approaches] "I think it might be best if [daughter] stays in the family lounge for now. Can we have someone sit with her?"\n\n**3. Assess the nature of the request \u2014 is this depression, suffering, or an autonomous, enduring wish?**\n"[Patient name], I want to understand what you are feeling. I am going to ask you some yes/no questions.\n- Are you in physical pain right now? [Blink response]\n- Are you feeling hopeless about the future? [Blink response]\n- Do you feel like a burden to your family? [Blink response]\n- Have you felt this way since you woke up in the ICU, or is this a recent feeling? [Complex \u2014 may need reformulation]\n- If we could find a way to communicate more easily, connect you to the world, and manage your discomfort, would that change how you feel? [Blink response]\n- Have you thought about this request for a long time, or did something happen today that made it worse? [Reformulate as needed]"\n\n**4. Acknowledge the legitimacy of his suffering AND his autonomy.**\n"You are a 48-year-old man who cycled 50 miles for fun, who wrote software, who held his daughter. And now you cannot move, you cannot speak, and you are breathing through a machine. I cannot imagine what that is like, and I am not going to pretend I can. Your feelings make sense. You have every right to feel this way."\n\n**5. Provide honest context about the trajectory \u2014 locked-in syndrome has a unique prognosis.**\n"I also want to tell you something that I think matters. You are 6 weeks out from your stroke. Most people with your condition at 6 weeks feel exactly the way you do. But research on locked-in syndrome survivors \u2014 people who have lived with this for years \u2014 shows something surprising. The majority report meaningful quality of life. Many report life satisfaction similar to healthy individuals. This is not intuitive, and I am not saying it to dismiss your suffering. I am saying that how you feel right now, at 6 weeks, may not be how you feel at 6 months or 6 years. And the technology we are going to give you \u2014 the ability to communicate freely, to use a computer, to write to your daughter, to connect with the world \u2014 that changes things."\n\n**6. Clarify the ethical and legal framework.**\n"As your physician, I want you to know your rights. You are a competent adult. You have the legal right to refuse any medical treatment, including the ventilator. That is not in question. But a decision like this should not be made during a crisis, and you are in a crisis right now \u2014 you are 6 weeks into the most devastating experience of your life, you do not yet have the communication tools you need, and you have not had a chance to see what your life can look like with proper support. I am asking you \u2014 not telling you \u2014 to give us time. Let us get you the eye-tracking computer. Let us get you talking to a psychologist who specializes in this. Let us get you home. And then, if you still feel this way, we will have that conversation again, and I will respect your decision."\n\n**7. Involve ethics and psychiatry \u2014 not as gatekeepers but as support.**\n"I am going to do two things. First, I am going to ask our psychiatrist to meet with you \u2014 not to override you, but to make sure depression is not driving this decision. Depression is treatable, even in your situation. Second, I am going to request an ethics consultation, which is standard whenever a patient considers withdrawal of life-sustaining treatment. This is to protect YOUR interests and make sure your voice is heard clearly."\n\n**8. Return to the wife.**\n"Mrs. [Name], your husband is suffering, and he is being honest about it. That honesty is a sign of trust in you and in us. Right now, no decisions are being made. What we are going to do is get him better tools to communicate, address his depression, and give him time to experience life outside this hospital. He deserves to make this decision from the best possible position, not the worst."\n\n**Key Ethical Principles**: Respect for autonomy (he is competent and has the right to refuse treatment). Beneficence (treating depression and providing communication tools may alleviate the suffering driving the request). Non-maleficence (acting on a request driven by treatable depression would cause harm). Temporality (decisions about life-sustaining treatment should not be made in the acute phase when the patient has not yet experienced their fullest potential recovery and adaptation). The goal is not to override his autonomy but to ensure the decision, if ultimately made, is informed, stable, and not driven by a treatable condition.')
B.aFj=s([B.b2Z,B.aRV,B.b0T,B.b2h,B.aQ7,B.b29,B.b2C,B.b2z,B.aUc,B.aUv,B.aUU],t.E)
B.Q8=new A.a5("stroke-locked-in-syndrome","Locked-In Syndrome",B.aFj,!1)
B.aVY=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 6-year-old girl with sickle cell disease (HbSS) is admitted to the pediatric rehabilitation unit 10 days after presenting with acute left hemiparesis and dysarthria. MRI/MRA confirmed a large right middle cerebral artery territory infarction secondary to moyamoya-pattern vasculopathy. She underwent emergent exchange transfusion and was stabilized in the PICU. She has a history of two prior hospitalizations for vaso-occlusive crises but no prior strokes. Her transcranial Doppler (TCD) velocities had been elevated (210 cm/s in the right MCA) on screening 3 months ago, and she was scheduled to start chronic transfusion therapy, but the appointment was missed. Her parents speak limited English (primary language Spanish) and are visibly anxious. The father quietly asks the nurse if the hospital will ask about their immigration status.")
B.aWp=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **Sickle Cell History**: Diagnosed at birth via newborn screening. Genotype HbSS. Two prior vaso-occlusive crises (ages 4 and 5), both managed with IV fluids and analgesics. No prior acute chest syndrome, splenic sequestration, or stroke. Baseline hemoglobin 7.5-8.0 g/dL.\n- **Stroke Prevention Screening**: TCD screening performed 3 months ago showed right MCA velocity 210 cm/s (abnormal >200 cm/s \u2014 indication for chronic transfusion therapy per STOP trial). Hematology scheduled chronic transfusion initiation, but the family missed the appointment.\n- **Current Stroke**: Acute onset left-sided weakness and slurred speech while at school. Teacher called 911. CT head negative for hemorrhage. MRI: acute right MCA territory infarction involving frontal, parietal, and temporal lobes. MRA: moyamoya-pattern collaterals with severe right ICA stenosis.\n- **Hospital Course**: Exchange transfusion in PICU (target HbS <30%). Started on chronic simple transfusion protocol. Neurosurgery consulted for possible future revascularization (encephaloduroarteriosynangiosis \u2014 EDAS).\n- **Current Functional Status**: Left hemiparesis \u2014 arm 2/5, leg 3/5. Left hemispatial neglect present. Dysarthria \u2014 intelligible to familiar listeners only. Requires max assist for transfers and ADLs. Continent but needs help toileting. Emotional \u2014 cries frequently, asks when she can go home.\n- **Developmental History**: Previously meeting all milestones. Attended first grade. Right-hand dominant. Enjoyed dance class. Social and outgoing personality.\n- **Family**: Parents are from Guatemala. Father works in construction (day labor). Mother is primary caregiver. Two younger siblings (ages 3 and 1). Family lives in a one-bedroom apartment. No health insurance \u2014 care funded through emergency Medicaid. Parents are undocumented immigrants.\n- **Medications**: Hydroxyurea 500 mg daily (resumed), folic acid 1 mg daily, chronic transfusion protocol (target HbS <30%, Hgb 9-10 g/dL).")
B.aRb=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 98/62 mmHg, HR 100 bpm, RR 20, SpO2 99% on RA, Temp 36.9C, Weight 20 kg (25th percentile), Height 115 cm (50th percentile).\n\n**General**: Small girl in hospital bed, awake, crying intermittently. Mother at bedside holding her right hand. Father standing near the door, appearing anxious.\n\n**Neurological**:\n- **Mental Status**: Alert but intermittently inattentive. Follows commands in Spanish (primary language). Answers simple questions. Oriented to name and place. Neglects left-sided stimuli on double simultaneous stimulation.\n- **Cranial Nerves**: Left lower facial droop. Dysarthria \u2014 slurred speech, intelligible 70% to familiar listeners, 40% to unfamiliar. Tongue deviates left. Visual fields \u2014 left homonymous hemianopia (confirmed with confrontation in a playful manner using toys).\n- **Motor**: Right upper and lower extremity 5/5. **Left upper extremity**: shoulder 2/5, elbow 2/5, wrist 1/5, grip 1/5. **Left lower extremity**: hip 3/5, knee 3/5, ankle 2/5. Left-sided hypotonia.\n- **Sensory**: Diminished light touch and proprioception on left side. Neglects left side during play.\n- **Reflexes**: Left-sided hyperreflexia (3+). Left Babinski positive.\n\n**Musculoskeletal**: No contractures yet. Full passive ROM. Left shoulder subluxation (1 finger-breadth).\n\n**Functional**: Cannot sit unsupported for more than 10 seconds (leans left and cannot correct). Requires max assist for bed mobility. Cannot stand independently. Right hand functional for feeding with setup.\n\n**Behavioral**: Cooperative with familiar staff. Cries when mother leaves the room. Engages when offered coloring activities with right hand. Tries to use left hand and becomes frustrated when it does not respond.")
B.aPq=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","Discuss the unique pathophysiology of stroke in sickle cell disease, the role of TCD screening, and the approach to secondary stroke prevention in this population.")
B.aPc=new A.c("+ Explain the pathophysiology of stroke in sickle cell disease, the significance of the missed TCD screening follow-up, and the secondary prevention strategy.","**Stroke Pathophysiology in Sickle Cell Disease**:\n- Children with HbSS have an **11% risk of overt stroke** by age 20 (highest risk ages 2-9).\n- **Large-vessel vasculopathy**: Repeated sickling causes endothelial damage, intimal hyperplasia, and progressive stenosis of the distal ICA and proximal MCA/ACA. This creates a moyamoya-pattern collateral network.\n- **Mechanisms**: (1) Arterial stenosis leading to hypoperfusion and watershed infarction; (2) Thrombosis at stenotic segments; (3) Impaired vasodilatory reserve due to chronic anemia and endothelial dysfunction.\n- This patient's moyamoya-pattern vasculopathy with right ICA stenosis is classic.\n\n**Significance of Elevated TCD Velocities**:\n- The **STOP trial** (1998) established that TCD screening identifies children at high stroke risk.\n- **Normal**: <170 cm/s. **Conditional**: 170-199 cm/s. **Abnormal**: >=200 cm/s.\n- Children with abnormal TCD (>=200 cm/s) have a **40% stroke risk over 3 years** without intervention.\n- **Chronic transfusion therapy** reduces stroke risk by **>90%** (from 10%/year to <1%/year) \u2014 the STOP trial was halted early due to dramatic benefit.\n- This patient had a TCD of **210 cm/s** \u2014 meeting criteria for chronic transfusion. The missed appointment was a critical preventive care failure. **However**, blame must be contextualized: the family faces language barriers, lacks insurance, has transportation challenges, and lives in fear of immigration enforcement.\n\n**Secondary Prevention Strategy**:\n1. **Chronic transfusion therapy**: Simple or exchange transfusions every 3-4 weeks. Target: HbS <30% of total hemoglobin, maintain Hgb 9-10 g/dL. This is **lifelong** \u2014 the STOP II trial showed that discontinuing transfusions leads to reversion of TCD velocities and recurrent stroke risk.\n2. **Iron chelation**: Chronic transfusions cause iron overload. Begin chelation (deferasirox) when ferritin >1000 or after approximately 1 year of transfusions. Monitor ferritin and liver iron concentration.\n3. **Hydroxyurea**: Continue as adjunct. The TWiTCH trial showed hydroxyurea can substitute for transfusions in primary prevention if TCD normalizes \u2014 but this patient has had a stroke, so transfusions remain the standard of care for secondary prevention.\n4. **Revascularization surgery**: Neurosurgery consultation for EDAS (encephaloduroarteriosynangiosis) \u2014 indirect revascularization procedure that promotes collateral growth. Indicated for progressive moyamoya vasculopathy with recurrent ischemia despite transfusion.\n5. **Screening**: Repeat MRI/MRA in 3-6 months. TCD on contralateral (left) side. Screen for silent cerebral infarcts (present in 30% of SCD children, associated with cognitive decline).\n6. **Cognitive screening**: Neuropsychological testing when stabilized \u2014 SCD children have high rates of executive dysfunction, processing speed deficits, and academic difficulties even without overt stroke.")
B.aWP=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Develop an age-appropriate inpatient rehabilitation plan for this 6-year-old with left hemiparesis, neglect, and dysarthria in the context of sickle cell disease.")
B.aPy=new A.c("+ Outline a pediatric rehabilitation plan that accounts for her developmental stage, sickle cell-specific needs, and family circumstances.","**Pediatric Stroke Rehabilitation Plan**:\n\n**1. Motor Recovery (PT/OT)**:\n- **Constraint-induced movement therapy (CIMT) \u2014 modified pediatric protocol**: Restraint of right (unaffected) hand with a mitt for structured play sessions (2-3 hours/day) to force use of left arm. Shaping tasks: reaching for toys, stacking blocks, grasping crayons. Evidence supports CIMT in pediatric stroke with significant UE improvement.\n- **Task-oriented training**: Functional activities meaningful to a 6-year-old \u2014 dressing a doll (bilateral hand use), coloring/drawing, feeding herself, putting on shoes.\n- **Left leg strengthening**: Progressive weight-bearing activities \u2014 standing at a play table, stepping over obstacles, kicking a ball. Ankle-foot orthosis (AFO) for left foot drop (ankle 2/5).\n- **Balance training**: Seated balance on therapy ball, progressing to supported standing, then dynamic standing activities.\n- **Subluxation management**: Arm trough when in wheelchair. Shoulder support during upright activities. No overhead pulleys or aggressive PROM.\n\n**2. Neglect Remediation**:\n- **Visual scanning training**: Games that require looking left \u2014 finding hidden objects on the left side of a picture, turning pages of a book (items placed on left).\n- **Prism adaptation therapy**: Emerging evidence in children \u2014 brief prism goggle sessions followed by pointing tasks to recalibrate spatial attention.\n- **Environmental cueing**: Place preferred toys, food, and visitors on the left side. Red tape line on left edge of tray as visual anchor.\n- **Left-sided sensory stimulation**: Vibration, textured toys, and tactile games targeting left arm and leg.\n\n**3. Speech-Language (SLP)**:\n- **Dysarthria treatment**: Oral motor exercises (lip/tongue strengthening). Rate control strategies. Over-articulation practice with age-appropriate games (animal sounds, singing).\n- **Language assessment**: Assess in Spanish (primary language). Ensure bilingual SLP or interpreter-mediated therapy. Evaluate for receptive and expressive language \u2014 right MCA stroke can affect pragmatic language and prosody.\n- **Swallowing**: Confirm safe oral intake. Modified diet if needed based on clinical swallow evaluation.\n\n**4. Sickle Cell-Specific Considerations**:\n- **Hydration**: Maintain excellent hydration (1.5-2x maintenance IV fluids or oral equivalent) to prevent sickling. Monitor I&O.\n- **Temperature regulation**: Avoid cold therapy. Keep room warm. Cold can trigger vaso-occlusive crises.\n- **Pain monitoring**: Use age-appropriate pain scale (Wong-Baker FACES). Daily assessment for vaso-occlusive pain.\n- **Infection prevention**: Ensure vaccination status is current (especially pneumococcal). Penicillin prophylaxis (standard for SCD children \u2014 check if she is on it).\n- **Transfusion schedule**: Coordinate rehabilitation around transfusion days (every 3-4 weeks). She may be fatigued on transfusion days.\n\n**5. Psychological/Developmental Support**:\n- **Child life specialist**: Daily visits. Therapeutic play to process the hospitalization experience. Normalize hospital environment.\n- **Psychology**: Assessment for adjustment disorder. Trauma-informed play therapy. Address separation anxiety (cries when mother leaves).\n- **Education**: Hospital-based teacher to maintain academic progress during rehabilitation stay. IEP (Individualized Education Program) planning for school reentry.\n- **Family-centered care**: Include parents in all therapy sessions. Teach them exercises to continue at home. Provide education materials in Spanish.\n\n**6. Discharge Planning**:\n- **Equipment**: Pediatric wheelchair (may be temporary), AFO, bath chair, adaptive utensils.\n- **Home therapy**: Outpatient PT, OT, SLP \u2014 ensure providers accept emergency Medicaid and are Spanish-speaking or have interpreter services.\n- **School reentry**: Occupational therapist to visit school, recommend modifications (left-sided desk setup, extra time, aide for mobility). 504 plan or IEP.\n- **Follow-up**: Hematology (transfusion clinic), PM&R, neurosurgery, neuropsychology.")
B.b26=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","This family faces significant barriers to follow-up care: no insurance beyond emergency Medicaid, limited English, and fear of immigration enforcement. How do you address these systems-level barriers?")
B.b1d=new A.c("+ How do you ensure continuity of care for a child with sickle cell disease requiring lifelong transfusion therapy when the family is uninsured and fears immigration enforcement?","**Addressing Healthcare Access Barriers**:\n\n**1. Insurance and Coverage**:\n- **Emergency Medicaid**: Currently covering the inpatient stay. This covers emergency services but NOT routine outpatient care, chronic transfusions, or rehabilitation in most states.\n- **CHIP (Children's Health Insurance Program)**: The child, if born in the US, is a **US citizen** regardless of parental immigration status and is eligible for full Medicaid or CHIP. If born abroad, CHIP eligibility varies by state. Verify her citizenship/birth status.\n- **State-specific programs**: Many states offer full Medicaid to children regardless of immigration status (e.g., California, New York, Illinois, Washington, Oregon). Social worker must determine state-specific eligibility.\n- **Federally Qualified Health Centers (FQHCs)**: Required to serve all patients regardless of insurance or immigration status on a sliding-fee scale. Identify the nearest FQHC for primary care.\n\n**2. Immigration Concerns**:\n- **Hospitals are sensitive locations**: ICE (Immigration and Customs Enforcement) policy designates hospitals, schools, and churches as sensitive locations where enforcement actions are generally avoided.\n- **HIPAA protections**: Patient information cannot be shared with immigration authorities without a court order.\n- **Public charge rule**: As of current policy, Medicaid use by US citizen children does NOT count against parents in public charge determinations. The social worker should provide this information clearly.\n- **Connect with immigrant advocacy organization**: Legal aid organizations that specialize in immigrant healthcare access can provide the family with written assurance of their rights.\n\n**3. Transfusion Therapy Access**:\n- The child needs **lifelong monthly transfusions** \u2014 this is a life-or-death treatment. Missing transfusions means recurrent stroke risk.\n- **Sickle cell comprehensive centers**: Federally funded sickle cell treatment centers provide comprehensive care and often have social workers dedicated to navigating access barriers.\n- **Hospital financial assistance/charity care**: Most hospitals have charity care programs for uninsured patients. Apply proactively.\n- **Sickle Cell Disease Association of America (SCDAA)**: Provides resources, advocacy, and case management support.\n\n**4. Language Access**:\n- **Professional medical interpreters**: Required by law (Title VI of the Civil Rights Act) for all clinical encounters. Do not rely on the child or family members as interpreters.\n- **Written materials in Spanish**: Discharge instructions, medication guides, follow-up schedules, and home exercise programs.\n- **Spanish-speaking providers**: When possible, match the family with Spanish-speaking therapists and physicians for continuity.\n\n**5. Transportation**:\n- **Non-emergency medical transportation (NEMT)**: Covered by Medicaid in most states. Arrange for monthly transfusion appointments.\n- **Hospital-based van services or partnerships with rideshare programs**: Many children's hospitals offer transportation assistance.\n\n**6. Care Coordination**:\n- Assign a **bilingual social worker or patient navigator** as the family's single point of contact.\n- Create a **written care calendar** in Spanish with all appointment dates, locations, and contact numbers.\n- **Hematology-PM&R communication**: Ensure both teams are aligned on the schedule so rehabilitation appointments do not conflict with transfusion days.")
B.aRR=new A.c(u.j,'During a family meeting (with a Spanish interpreter present), the father says quietly: "Doctor, we missed the blood appointment because we were afraid. Our neighbor was taken by ICE at a hospital. If we keep coming here, will they take us? Our daughter needs us. If they deport us, she has no one." The mother is crying. The 6-year-old is watching her parents, looking frightened.')
B.aUD=new A.c("+ How do you address this family's fear of immigration enforcement while ensuring their child receives the lifelong medical care she needs?",'**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Acknowledge the fear directly and validate it.**\n[Through the interpreter, speaking slowly and making eye contact with both parents]\n"I hear you, and I want you to know that your fear is understandable. What happened to your neighbor is real, and I am not going to tell you it is nothing to worry about. You are trying to protect your family, and that is exactly what good parents do."\n\n**2. Provide concrete factual reassurance about hospital safety.**\n"I want to tell you something important about this hospital. This is a safe place. Immigration authorities have a policy of not conducting enforcement at hospitals, schools, or churches. We do not ask about immigration status. We do not share your information with immigration. Your medical records are protected by federal privacy law \u2014 no one outside this hospital can access them without a court order. I want you to hear that clearly: coming to this hospital for your daughter\'s care will not put you at risk."\n\n**3. Address the specific fear that caused the missed appointment.**\n"I understand that fear is what caused you to miss the blood transfusion appointment. I am not blaming you for that \u2014 I blame a system that made you feel unsafe seeking medical care for your child. But I need to be honest with you about what that missed appointment means. Your daughter\'s blood test three months ago showed that she was at very high risk for a stroke. The transfusions we wanted to start would have reduced that risk dramatically. She had her stroke because we did not get the chance to protect her. That is not your fault \u2014 it is a tragedy that should not have happened. But now, going forward, she needs these transfusions every month for the rest of her life to prevent another stroke. If she has another stroke, she could lose the ability to walk, to talk, or worse."\n\n**4. Address the child\'s distress.**\n[Look at the daughter and smile gently. Speak to her in simple terms through the interpreter]\n"Sweetie, your mommy and daddy are talking to the doctor because they want to make sure you get better. Everything is okay. Do you want to color while we finish talking? [Offer crayons and paper] Your mommy and daddy love you very much, and we are all going to take care of you."\n\n**5. Offer concrete protections and connect to resources.**\n[Back to parents] "Here is what I am going to do. I am going to connect you with a social worker who speaks Spanish and who helps families in your situation every day. She can connect you with a lawyer \u2014 free of charge \u2014 who specializes in immigrant rights and healthcare access. She can explain your legal protections in detail. We are also going to help you apply for insurance for your daughter \u2014 she may qualify for full coverage that pays for everything she needs. And if transportation is a barrier, we will arrange rides to every appointment."\n\n**6. Reframe: their presence is essential to their daughter\'s survival.**\n"You said that if you are deported, your daughter has no one. You are right. She needs you. And she needs you to be brave enough to bring her to her appointments, because those transfusions are what will keep her alive and healthy. I know that is a lot to ask when you are afraid. But I promise you that this medical team will do everything in our power to make sure seeking care for your daughter is safe for your family."\n\n**7. Establish a trusting relationship.**\n"I want to be your daughter\'s doctor for a long time. I want to watch her get stronger, go back to school, and go back to dance class. To do that, I need you to trust me enough to keep coming back. Can we make a plan together?"\n\n**Key Principles**: Validate fear without dismissing it. Provide factual reassurance about hospital safety policies. Be honest about the medical consequences of the missed appointment without blaming. Offer concrete resources (social worker, legal aid, insurance navigation, transportation). Address the child\'s emotional experience. Build a long-term therapeutic alliance grounded in trust.')
B.aFS=s([B.aVY,B.aWp,B.aRb,B.aPq,B.aPc,B.aWP,B.aPy,B.b26,B.b1d,B.aRR,B.aUD],t.E)
B.R8=new A.a5("stroke-pediatric-sickle-cell","Pediatric Stroke in Sickle Cell Disease",B.aFS,!1)
B.aN2=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 28-year-old male is transferred to the acute inpatient rehabilitation unit on post-operative day 5 following a T6 burst fracture sustained in a high-speed motorcycle collision. He underwent posterior spinal fusion with instrumentation from T4 to T8. The patient is alert, oriented, and hemodynamically stable on midodrine. He reports no sensation below his chest and complete inability to move his lower extremities. Nursing staff notes episodes of symptomatic orthostatic hypotension during upright positioning and the patient has an indwelling Foley catheter in place.")
B.aTU=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **Mechanism of Injury**: High-speed motorcycle collision with ejection; helmeted\n- **Surgical History**: Posterior T4-T8 instrumented fusion on day of injury; no intraoperative complications\n- **Neurologic Baseline**: Complete loss of motor and sensory function below T6 since presentation; no voluntary anal contraction; no deep anal pressure\n- **Autonomic**: Episodes of systolic BP dropping to 70s/40s with upright positioning; resting HR 55-60 (loss of sympathetic cardiac innervation)\n- **Bladder**: Indwelling Foley catheter draining clear yellow urine; no attempts at intermittent catheterization yet\n- **Bowel**: No bowel movement since admission; abdomen mildly distended; bowel sounds present but diminished\n- **Skin**: Sacral skin intact; mild erythema over bilateral heels (non-blanchable)\n- **Psychosocial**: Single, works as an electrician; lives alone in a second-floor apartment; no prior psychiatric history; mother is primary support\n- **DVT Prophylaxis**: Currently on enoxaparin 40 mg daily; bilateral SCDs in place\n- **Pain**: Reports a band-like burning sensation at the T6 level (at-level neuropathic pain)")
B.aQP=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 98/62 supine (drops to 72/48 sitting), HR 58, RR 16, Temp 37.0C, SpO2 98% on RA\n\n**ASIA/ISNCSCI Examination**:\n- **Motor**: Upper extremities 5/5 throughout (C5-T1 myotomes). Lower extremities 0/5 throughout (L2-S1 myotomes bilaterally). Total motor score: 50/100.\n- **Sensory (Light Touch)**: Intact C2-T6 bilaterally; absent T7-S4-5 bilaterally. Light touch score: 56/112.\n- **Sensory (Pin Prick)**: Intact C2-T6 bilaterally; absent T7-S4-5 bilaterally. Pin prick score: 56/112.\n- **Sacral Sparing**: No voluntary anal contraction (VAC absent). No deep anal pressure (DAP absent). No S4-S5 sensory function.\n- **Neurological Level of Injury (NLI)**: T6\n- **ASIA Impairment Scale**: **A (Complete)**\n\n**Musculoskeletal**: Upper extremities with full active ROM and normal strength. Spine with midline posterior surgical incision, clean/dry/intact with staples.\n**Respiratory**: Clear lung fields bilaterally; strong cough with upper abdominal support; reduced forced vital capacity (FVC) measured at 65% predicted (loss of intercostal/abdominal muscle innervation).\n**Abdomen**: Mildly distended, non-tender; bowel sounds hypoactive.\n**Skin**: Stage 1 pressure injury (non-blanchable erythema) bilateral heels. Sacrum intact.\n**Tone**: Lower extremities flaccid (consistent with spinal shock phase).")
B.aXj=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The patient's ASIA exam confirms a T6 ASIA A complete spinal cord injury. On day 5 post-injury, he remains in spinal shock with flaccid paralysis below the level of injury.")
B.aNm=new A.c("+ Classify this injury using the ASIA Impairment Scale and explain the distinction between neurogenic shock and spinal shock.","**ASIA Classification**:\nThis is an **ASIA Impairment Scale A (Complete)** injury. The criteria for AIS A are:\n1. No motor function preserved in the sacral segments S4-S5 (no VAC)\n2. No sensory function preserved in S4-S5 (no DAP, no LT/PP at S4-S5)\nThe **Neurological Level of Injury (NLI)** is T6 -- the most caudal segment with intact motor and sensory function bilaterally.\n\n**Neurogenic Shock vs. Spinal Shock**:\n- **Neurogenic Shock** is a *hemodynamic* condition caused by loss of sympathetic tone below the level of injury. In injuries at T6 and above, disruption of the sympathetic chain (T1-L2) leads to **hypotension** (loss of vascular tone) and **bradycardia** (unopposed vagal tone). Treatment includes IV fluids, vasopressors (norepinephrine, phenylephrine), and midodrine for chronic orthostatic hypotension. This patient's BP of 72/48 sitting with HR 58 is classic.\n- **Spinal Shock** is a *neurological* phenomenon characterized by temporary areflexia, flaccidity, and loss of reflexes below the injury level. It does NOT mean hemodynamic instability. Resolution is marked by return of the **bulbocavernosus reflex** (first reflex to return, typically within 24-72 hours). Only after spinal shock resolves can the ASIA exam be considered reliable for long-term prognostication. Upper motor neuron signs (spasticity, hyperreflexia) develop over weeks to months.\n\n**Imaging Interpretation**: CT/MRI would show a T6 burst fracture with retropulsed fragments and likely complete cord signal abnormality (edema/hemorrhage) at the T6 level. Post-surgical imaging confirms hardware placement T4-T8 with adequate decompression.")
B.aT5=new A.c("+ What is the expected prognosis for motor recovery in ASIA A thoracic injuries?","**Prognosis for ASIA A Thoracic SCI**:\n- The probability of neurological improvement (conversion from AIS A to a less severe grade) is **very low**, approximately **2-5%** at one year.\n- The vast majority of patients with thoracic ASIA A injuries will remain ASIA A at follow-up.\n- This contrasts with **cervical ASIA A** injuries, which have a somewhat higher conversion rate (~10-15%), and **ASIA B** injuries, which have substantially better prognosis (~30-40% convert to ASIA C or D).\n- **Key Prognostic Factors**: Preservation of pin prick sensation (more favorable than light touch only), younger age, and early MRI findings (hemorrhagic contusion carries worse prognosis than edema alone).\n- The **zone of partial preservation (ZPP)** is only applicable to complete (AIS A) injuries and describes dermatomes/myotomes below the NLI that retain some function. A wider ZPP may suggest potential for limited recovery in those segments.\n- Functional outcomes: With comprehensive rehabilitation, patients with T6 paraplegia should achieve **independence in wheelchair mobility, transfers, self-care, driving with hand controls**, and return to community living and employment.")
B.aSI=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You are now managing this patient's acute rehabilitation program. He has been medically stable for 48 hours and is participating in 3 hours of therapy daily.")
B.b0H=new A.c("+ Outline your comprehensive inpatient rehabilitation management plan.","**1. Bowel Program**:\n- Initiate a **reflexic (upper motor neuron) bowel program** once spinal shock resolves (injury above the conus medullaris).\n- Schedule: Every other day, 30 minutes after a meal (to utilize the gastrocolic reflex).\n- Components: Chemical stimulant (bisacodyl suppository) + digital stimulation to trigger reflex evacuation.\n- Oral medications: Docusate sodium 100 mg BID (stool softener) + senna 2 tabs at bedtime.\n- Adequate hydration (2-3L/day) and dietary fiber (15-20 g/day).\n\n**2. Bladder Program**:\n- Transition from indwelling Foley to **intermittent catheterization (IC)** program.\n- Schedule: Every 4-6 hours; target catheterization volumes <500 mL.\n- Monitor fluid intake (1500-2000 mL/day initially to manage volumes).\n- Urodynamics at 3-6 months to evaluate detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia.\n- Anticholinergic medication (oxybutynin) if detrusor overactivity develops.\n\n**3. Skin Care Protocol**:\n- **Pressure relief**: Weight shifts every 15-30 minutes in wheelchair; reposition in bed every 2 hours.\n- Address Stage 1 heel injuries: offload heels with pillows/heel suspension boots.\n- Skin checks twice daily (patient trained to use a long-handled mirror).\n- Pressure-mapping cushion evaluation for wheelchair seating.\n\n**4. DVT Prophylaxis**:\n- Continue **LMWH (enoxaparin 40 mg SQ daily)** for 8-12 weeks post-injury (high-risk period).\n- Bilateral SCDs when in bed.\n- Monitor for signs of DVT (unilateral leg swelling, warmth); low threshold for duplex ultrasound.\n\n**5. Orthostatic Hypotension Management**:\n- Progressive upright positioning (tilt table initially, then wheelchair with recline).\n- **Abdominal binder** and **compression stockings** (thigh-high or waist-high).\n- Midodrine 5-10 mg TID (30 min before upright activity).\n- Fludrocortisone 0.1 mg daily if refractory.\n\n**6. Pain Management**:\n- At-level neuropathic pain: First-line **gabapentin** (titrate 300 mg to 3600 mg/day) or **pregabalin**.\n- Avoid opioids for neuropathic pain; consider low-dose amitriptyline at bedtime if sleep disruption.\n\n**7. Therapy Goals**:\n- PT: Wheelchair mobility, transfer training (sliding board initially, then depression transfers), strengthening of trunk and UE, standing frame program.\n- OT: ADL independence, wheelchair skills, adaptive equipment training, home modification assessment.\n- Psychology: Adjustment counseling, coping skills, peer mentoring referral.\n\n**8. Autonomic Dysreflexia Education**:\n- Although T6 is at the threshold, educate patient and staff on recognition and emergency management of AD (pounding headache, HTN, flushing/sweating above level, bradycardia). Most common triggers: bladder distension, bowel impaction, skin breakdown.")
B.aY9=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The insurance case manager questions whether this patient meets criteria for acute inpatient rehabilitation and requests justification for the anticipated length of stay.")
B.b3z=new A.c("+ Justify acute inpatient rehabilitation admission and expected length of stay.","**Acute Inpatient Rehabilitation Admission Criteria** (CMS/CARF):\nThis patient meets admission criteria based on:\n1. **Medical necessity**: Requires physician-directed, interdisciplinary rehabilitation care that cannot be provided at a lower level of care.\n2. **Functional impairment**: Complete paraplegia requiring new learning of mobility, ADLs, bowel/bladder management, and skin care.\n3. **Tolerance**: Can participate in and benefit from **3 hours of therapy per day, 5-7 days per week** (met per documentation).\n4. **Medical complexity**: Active management of orthostatic hypotension, DVT prophylaxis, bowel/bladder program initiation, neuropathic pain, and autonomic dysreflexia risk.\n5. **Interdisciplinary need**: Requires PT, OT, psychology, rehabilitation nursing, case management, and recreation therapy.\n6. **Diagnosis**: Spinal cord injury is one of the **CMS 13 qualifying diagnoses** for IRF admission under the 60% rule.\n\n**Expected Length of Stay**:\n- For T6 complete paraplegia, the **SCIM-based and national benchmark** length of stay in acute inpatient rehabilitation is approximately **30-45 days**.\n- The expected FIM efficiency (functional gain per day) for paraplegia is approximately 1.5-2.0 FIM points/day.\n- Discharge planning: Will require accessible housing (first-floor or elevator access), wheelchair-accessible bathroom modifications, outpatient therapy continuation, and follow-up with SCI medicine, urology, and orthopedic surgery.\n- Goals at discharge: Independent wheelchair mobility on all surfaces, independent transfers, independent self-care, independent bowel/bladder program, demonstrated skin inspection technique, and community reintegration planning.")
B.b33=new A.c(u.j,'During a family meeting, the patient becomes tearful and asks directly: "Doc, just tell me the truth -- will I ever walk again?"')
B.aZ1=new A.c("+ Model your response using empathic, honest communication.","**Model Response**:\n\"I can hear how important this question is to you, and I want to be completely honest with you because you deserve that. Based on your examination, you have what we call a 'complete' spinal cord injury at the T6 level. What that means is that right now, there are no signals getting through below that point.\n\nI want to be straightforward: for injuries like yours, the chance of regaining the ability to walk is very small -- in the range of 2-5%. I know that is incredibly difficult to hear, and I wish I had different news.\n\nBut here is what I also want you to know: your life is not over. Not even close. People with your level of injury go on to live full, independent, meaningful lives. You will be able to drive, work, have relationships, play sports, travel -- all of it. Our goal in rehab is to help you become as strong and independent as possible.\n\nThere is also active research happening in spinal cord injury, including clinical trials for regenerative therapies and epidural stimulation. I want you to have realistic expectations, but I also don't want you to lose hope entirely.\n\nRight now, the most important thing you can do is throw yourself into your rehabilitation. The stronger and more skilled you become, the better your quality of life will be regardless of what happens with walking. I am going to be with you every step of this journey, and so is your entire team.\n\nWould it be helpful if we connected you with someone who has been through this -- a peer mentor who has a similar injury and is living a great life?\"")
B.aD4=s([B.aN2,B.aTU,B.aQP,B.aXj,B.aNm,B.aT5,B.aSI,B.b0H,B.aY9,B.b3z,B.b33,B.aZ1],t.E)
B.Qg=new A.a5("sci-t6-complete-paraplegia","Acute T6 Complete Paraplegia (ASIA A)",B.aD4,!1)
B.b2Y=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 45-year-old female with a history of C7 ASIA A spinal cord injury sustained 2 years ago in a motor vehicle collision presents to the outpatient SCI clinic with complaints of worsening constipation over the past 3 months and three urinary tract infections in the past 6 months. She reports her bowel program now takes over 2 hours to complete, she frequently has episodes of incontinence between scheduled programs, and she has not had a satisfactory evacuation in over a week. She is frustrated and admits she has been skipping her bowel program due to the prolonged time and embarrassment when her home health aide assists.")
B.aUE=new A.c("History & Systems Review:",'- **SCI History**: C7 ASIA A (motor complete, sensory complete); motor level C7 bilaterally; independent in wheelchair mobility and most ADLs with adaptive equipment\n- **Current Bowel Program**: Every other day; bisacodyl suppository + digital stimulation; reports program duration has increased from 45 minutes to >2 hours\n- **Diet**: Limited fluid intake (reports ~1L/day "to avoid catheterizing too often"); low fiber diet (mostly processed foods); admits to frequent fast food\n- **Medications**: Oxybutynin 5 mg TID (for neurogenic bladder), baclofen 20 mg TID, gabapentin 600 mg TID, docusate 100 mg BID\n- **Bladder Program**: Intermittent catheterization every 4-6 hours; recurrent UTIs (E. coli, Klebsiella) -- last UTI treated 3 weeks ago with ciprofloxacin\n- **Bowel Symptoms**: Hard stools (Bristol Stool Scale Type 1-2), abdominal bloating, loss of appetite, episodes of stool incontinence between programs\n- **Review of Systems**: Denies fever, nausea, vomiting. Reports worsening headaches and sweating episodes during attempted bowel programs (concerning for **autonomic dysreflexia**). No rectal bleeding.\n- **Psychosocial**: Works part-time from home as a graphic designer; lives with her partner; reports avoiding social activities due to fear of bowel accidents')
B.aN8=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 108/72 (seated), HR 64, Temp 36.8C, Weight 72 kg (up 5 kg in 6 months)\n\n**Abdomen**: Distended, tympanic to percussion in all quadrants. Palpable stool in left lower quadrant. Bowel sounds present but decreased. Non-tender (patient lacks sensation below injury level).\n\n**Digital Rectal Exam**: \n- Resting anal tone: **Increased** (tight sphincter consistent with upper motor neuron bowel)\n- Rectal vault: Packed with hard stool\n- Bulbocavernosus reflex: **Present** (confirms intact sacral reflex arc)\n- Anocutaneous (anal wink) reflex: **Present**\n- No palpable rectal masses; no blood on gloved finger\n\n**Skin**: Intact perineal skin; no perianal excoriation. Ischial skin intact bilaterally. No sacral breakdown.\n\n**Upper Extremities**: Full strength C5-C7 myotomes bilaterally; C8/T1 0/5 (consistent with C7 motor level). Tenodesis grasp functional.\n\n**Lower Extremities**: Spastic (Modified Ashworth 3 bilateral hip adductors, knee extensors). Bilateral ankle clonus. No edema.")
B.aUJ=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","This patient demonstrates a failing upper motor neuron bowel program with fecal loading, prolonged program times, and incontinence episodes. The recurrent UTIs may be related to both chronic constipation (compressing the bladder/urethra) and inadequate fluid intake.")
B.b11=new A.c("+ Differentiate upper motor neuron (UMN) bowel from lower motor neuron (LMN) bowel and explain why this distinction is critical for management.","**Upper Motor Neuron (Reflexic) Bowel** (injury above conus medullaris, typically T12 and above):\n- Intact sacral reflex arc (S2-S4)\n- **Increased** anal sphincter tone\n- Bulbocavernosus reflex **present**\n- Stool is retained due to spastic external anal sphincter\n- Management: **Digital stimulation** triggers reflex peristalsis and relaxation of the internal anal sphincter; chemical stimulants (bisacodyl/glycerin suppositories) augment reflex evacuation\n- This patient has UMN bowel (C7 injury, intact reflexes, increased tone)\n\n**Lower Motor Neuron (Areflexic) Bowel** (injury at or below conus medullaris/cauda equina):\n- Damaged sacral reflex arc\n- **Decreased/absent** anal sphincter tone (patulous anus)\n- Bulbocavernosus reflex **absent**\n- Risk of continuous stool leakage due to lax sphincter\n- Management: **Manual evacuation** (digital disimpaction) is the primary technique; stimulant suppositories are INEFFECTIVE (no reflex arc); high-fiber diet to form bulky stool; scheduled programs more frequently\n\n**Why This Matters**: Using the wrong bowel program is a common cause of failure. Giving digital stimulation to an LMN bowel patient is futile (no reflex to trigger). Conversely, relying on manual evacuation alone for a UMN bowel patient ignores the powerful reflex mechanism.\n\n**Workup for Constipation Complications**:\n- **Abdominal X-ray (KUB)**: Assess stool burden, rule out ileus or obstruction\n- **Basic metabolic panel**: Evaluate electrolytes, calcium (hypercalcemia can cause constipation), renal function\n- **Thyroid function**: Hypothyroidism is common and can worsen constipation\n- **Medication review**: Oxybutynin (anticholinergic) significantly slows bowel motility; gabapentin can also contribute\n- **If refractory**: Colonic transit study (Sitzmarker study) to assess motility; consider anorectal manometry")
B.aY5=new A.c("+ This patient reports sweating and headaches during bowel programs. What is your concern and how do you address it?","**Concern: Autonomic Dysreflexia (AD)**\nThis patient has a C7 injury (above T6 threshold) and is experiencing classic AD symptoms triggered by bowel distension/stimulation.\n\n**Pathophysiology**: Noxious stimulus below the injury level (fecal impaction, digital stimulation) causes massive sympathetic discharge that cannot be modulated by descending inhibitory pathways. This results in:\n- **Hypertension** (can be severe, >200 mmHg systolic)\n- Pounding headache\n- Diaphoresis and flushing above the level of injury\n- Piloerection above the level\n- Reflex bradycardia (baroreceptor-mediated)\n\n**Immediate Management**:\n1. **Sit the patient upright** (orthostatic reduction of BP)\n2. Loosen clothing, abdominal binder, leg bag straps\n3. **Check BP** immediately\n4. **Identify and remove the trigger**: In this case, fecal impaction is the likely trigger\n5. If BP remains >150 mmHg systolic: Apply **2% lidocaine jelly** to the rectum before any rectal intervention; administer **nitroglycerin paste 1 inch** above the level of injury or **nifedipine 10 mg bite-and-swallow**\n6. Monitor BP every 2-5 minutes until resolved\n\n**Prevention During Bowel Care**:\n- Pre-treat with lidocaine jelly intrarectally 5-10 minutes before digital stimulation\n- Optimize bowel program to prevent excessive impaction\n- Consider prophylactic nifedipine 10 mg 30 minutes before bowel program if recurrent AD")
B.b3n=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You determine that this patient needs a comprehensive bowel program overhaul. Her current program is failing due to multiple modifiable factors.")
B.aSG=new A.c("+ Design an optimized neurogenic bowel management program for this patient.","**Comprehensive Bowel Program Optimization**:\n\n**1. Timing and Schedule**:\n- Perform bowel program **every day** (transition from QOD) until program is reliable, then can trial QOD again\n- Schedule **30 minutes after the largest meal** of the day to capitalize on the **gastrocolic reflex**\n- Consistent timing daily is critical for bowel retraining\n\n**2. Dietary Modifications**:\n- Increase dietary fiber to **15-25 g/day** (gradually to prevent bloating): whole grains, fruits, vegetables, psyllium fiber supplement\n- **Increase fluid intake to 2-2.5 L/day** -- this is essential and directly contradicts her current fluid restriction. Address her concern about catheterization frequency by adjusting IC schedule rather than restricting fluids (dehydration worsens both constipation AND UTI risk)\n- Limit constipating foods: cheese, white bread, processed foods\n- Consider warm beverage (coffee or warm water) 30 minutes before bowel program\n\n**3. Medication Adjustments**:\n- **Switch oxybutynin** (highly anticholinergic, major constipation contributor) to **mirabegron 25-50 mg daily** (beta-3 agonist with no anticholinergic effects) -- this single change may significantly improve bowel motility\n- Continue docusate 100 mg BID\n- **Add senna 2 tabs at bedtime** (stimulant laxative taken night before program)\n- Consider **polyethylene glycol (MiraLAX) 17 g daily** as an osmotic agent\n\n**4. Bowel Program Technique (UMN Protocol)**:\n- Insert bisacodyl suppository (10 mg) against the rectal wall (not into stool) -- **contact with mucosa is essential for absorption**\n- Wait 15-20 minutes for suppository to take effect\n- Perform **digital stimulation**: gentle circular motion of gloved, lubricated finger against the rectal wall for 15-20 seconds at a time; repeat every 5-10 minutes until evacuation is complete\n- Pre-treat with 2% lidocaine jelly to prevent autonomic dysreflexia\n- Use **abdominal massage** (right to left, following colon path) to facilitate movement\n- Total program time goal: **< 60 minutes**\n\n**5. Positioning**:\n- Perform bowel program on a **padded commode chair** if possible (gravity-assisted)\n- If in bed, use **left side-lying position** (descending colon is gravity-dependent)\n\n**6. Rescue Plan for Refractory Constipation**:\n- If no results after 3 days: **mini-enema** (docusate liquid enema)\n- If impaction suspected: manual disimpaction with lidocaine pre-treatment\n- If chronic failure despite optimization: consider **transanal irrigation system** (Peristeen) or gastroenterology referral for evaluation of colonic inertia\n- Last resort: **Malone antegrade continence enema (MACE/ACE)** procedure or colostomy for severe refractory neurogenic bowel\n\n**7. UTI Prevention Strategy**:\n- Adequate hydration (addresses both constipation and UTI risk)\n- Strict clean intermittent catheterization technique review\n- Cranberry supplements (limited evidence but low risk)\n- Avoid prophylactic antibiotics unless >3 symptomatic UTIs per year despite behavioral modifications\n- Ensure complete bladder emptying; check post-void residuals\n- Treat only **symptomatic** UTIs (not asymptomatic bacteriuria, which is expected in SCI)")
B.aWU=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient asks about getting help at home with her bowel program since her current home health aide only comes 3 times per week and she needs daily assistance during the transition period.")
B.b1N=new A.c("+ How do you coordinate home health services and DME for neurogenic bowel management?","**Home Health Coordination**:\n1. **Prescribe increased home health aide visits**: Write an order for daily home health aide visits (at least during the bowel program retraining period of 4-6 weeks), then reassess frequency\n2. **Skilled nursing visits**: Order weekly skilled nursing visits for bowel program assessment, education reinforcement, and AD monitoring\n3. **Prior authorization**: Work with case manager to obtain insurance authorization; document medical necessity -- failed bowel program, recurrent UTIs related to constipation, AD episodes requiring professional monitoring\n4. **Caregiver training**: If partner is willing and able, train them on bowel program technique to reduce reliance on home health services long-term\n\n**DME for Bowel Care**:\n- **Padded commode/shower chair**: If not already in place, prescribe a tilt-in-space commode chair with adequate trunk support for safe, gravity-assisted bowel care\n- **Supplies**: Ensure prescription covers suppositories, gloves, lubricant, lidocaine jelly, underpads, and enema supplies\n- **Abdominal binder**: Assists with intra-abdominal pressure during evacuation\n\n**Follow-Up Plan**:\n- Phone check-in at 1 week after program changes\n- Clinic visit at 4 weeks to reassess: bowel diary review (frequency, consistency, duration, accidents), AD episodes, UTI recurrence\n- Urodynamics referral if not performed recently (given recurrent UTIs)\n- Nutrition referral for dietary counseling and weight management\n- Consider psychology referral for quality-of-life impact and social isolation")
B.aOs=new A.c(u.j,"During the clinic visit, you notice the patient avoids eye contact when discussing bowel care. When you ask about her bowel program in detail, she says quietly: \"I just... I hate talking about this. It's the most humiliating part of my injury. I'd rather deal with not walking than this.\"")
B.aT8=new A.c("+ Demonstrate how you maintain the patient's dignity while obtaining necessary clinical information about bowel function.","**Model Response**:\n\"Thank you for sharing that with me. I hear you, and I want you to know that what you're feeling is completely valid. Many of my patients tell me that bowel management is the single hardest part of living with a spinal cord injury -- even more than the mobility challenges. You are not alone in feeling this way.\n\nI also want you to know that in this clinic, we talk about bowel function the same way we talk about blood pressure or breathing -- it is a medical issue, not something to be ashamed of. There is nothing embarrassing about your body doing what bodies do.\n\nThe reason I need to ask these detailed questions is that your bowel program directly affects your UTIs, your comfort, your ability to go out and enjoy your life, and even your safety given the autonomic dysreflexia episodes. Getting this right can genuinely transform your quality of life.\n\nSo here is what I'd like to do: I'm going to ask you some specific questions, and I'll keep them clinical and straightforward. If at any point you need a break or feel uncomfortable, just let me know and we'll pause. There are no wrong answers.\n\nAlso, I want to bring up something: would you be interested in connecting with our SCI peer support group? There are other women with spinal cord injuries who have navigated these exact same challenges, and many of them say it was incredibly helpful to talk openly with someone who truly understands.\n\nYour dignity is important to me. Everything we discuss stays between us and your care team, and our only goal is to make your life better.\"")
B.azK=s([B.b2Y,B.aUE,B.aN8,B.aUJ,B.b11,B.aY5,B.b3n,B.aSG,B.aWU,B.b1N,B.aOs,B.aT8],t.E)
B.PS=new A.a5("sci-neurogenic-bowel","Neurogenic Bowel Management in Chronic SCI",B.azK,!1)
B.aSe=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 32-year-old male with a T10 ASIA A spinal cord injury sustained 6 weeks ago in a fall from a construction scaffold presents with progressive left hip stiffness noted by his physical therapist during range of motion exercises. The therapist reports that passive hip flexion has decreased from 120 degrees to 85 degrees over the past 2 weeks. Nursing staff have observed warmth and swelling around the left hip and proximal thigh. The patient denies pain (lacks sensation) but reports worsening spasticity in his left lower extremity and a low-grade fever over the past 5 days.")
B.aZk=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **SCI History**: T10 ASIA A sustained 6 weeks ago; posterior fusion T8-T12; uncomplicated post-operative course; admitted to acute inpatient rehab at week 2\n- **Current Complaints**: Decreasing left hip ROM over 2 weeks; left thigh swelling and warmth; worsening left lower extremity spasticity; intermittent low-grade fevers (up to 38.2C)\n- **Risk Factors for HO**: Complete SCI (ASIA A), young male, traumatic etiology, spasticity, immobilization -- this patient has **multiple high-risk features**\n- **Medications**: Enoxaparin 40 mg SQ daily (DVT prophylaxis, week 6), baclofen 20 mg TID, gabapentin 300 mg TID, docusate/senna\n- **Bowel/Bladder**: Bowel program established (QOD, bisacodyl + digital stimulation); intermittent catheterization every 4-6 hours\n- **Therapy Progress**: Was making good progress with transfers and wheelchair mobility until hip stiffness limited positioning\n- **Review of Systems**: Low-grade fevers (38.0-38.2C); no cough, dysuria, or wound drainage; no chest pain or shortness of breath; no calf swelling noted\n- **Psychosocial**: Single, construction worker, lives with parents; motivated in therapy; concerned about the setback")
B.b0Y=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 112/68, HR 78, Temp 38.1C, RR 14, SpO2 99% on RA\n\n**Left Hip**:\n- Palpable firm, warm, non-fluctuant mass in the anterior and medial left thigh/groin region\n- Passive ROM: Flexion 85 degrees (was 120), extension -10 degrees (10-degree flexion contracture developing), abduction 15 degrees (was 40), internal/external rotation significantly limited\n- No erythema, no open wounds, no drainage\n\n**Right Hip**: Full passive ROM maintained; no warmth or swelling\n\n**Lower Extremities**: Increased spasticity left > right; Modified Ashworth Scale 3 left hip flexors/adductors (was 1+ two weeks ago). No asymmetric calf swelling. Bilateral lower extremities are insensate below T10.\n\n**Surgical Wound**: T8-T12 posterior midline incision well-healed, no signs of infection.\n\n**Abdomen**: Soft, non-distended, bowel sounds present.\n\n**Skin**: Intact sacrum, heels, ischial tuberosities. No pressure injuries.")
B.b__=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The clinical findings of progressive hip stiffness, periarticular warmth and swelling, increased spasticity, and low-grade fever in a patient 6 weeks post-SCI are highly suspicious for heterotopic ossification.")
B.aXN=new A.c("+ What is your differential diagnosis, and how do you distinguish HO from other conditions?","**Differential Diagnosis for Warm, Swollen Extremity with Limited ROM Post-SCI**:\n\n1. **Heterotopic Ossification (HO)** -- Most likely\n - Timing: Typically develops 1-6 months post-SCI (peak at 2-3 months)\n - Presentation: Progressive ROM loss, periarticular warmth/swelling, firm mass, increased spasticity, low-grade fever\n - Location: Hip is the most common site (70-80%), followed by knee, shoulder, elbow\n - Risk factors: Complete SCI, spasticity, pressure ulcers, UTI, DVT -- this patient has several\n\n2. **Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT)** -- Must rule out\n - Can present with limb swelling, warmth, low-grade fever\n - However, DVT typically causes diffuse limb edema (not a firm periarticular mass) and does NOT cause ROM limitation\n - Patient is on LMWH prophylaxis but DVT can still occur\n\n3. **Fracture** (pathologic/insufficiency)\n - SCI patients develop rapid osteoporosis below the level of injury\n - Can occur with minimal trauma (transfers, ROM exercises)\n - Would present acutely, not progressively over 2 weeks\n - Typically involves long bones, not periarticular regions\n\n4. **Abscess/Cellulitis**\n - Would show erythema, fluctuance, more systemic signs of infection\n - No open wounds or recent injections in the area\n\n5. **Septic Arthritis**\n - Less likely without systemic sepsis, prior joint instrumentation, or bacteremia source\n\n**Diagnostic Workup**:\n- **Triple-Phase Bone Scan**: **Gold standard for early detection**. Phase 1 (flow) and Phase 2 (blood pool) show increased uptake within days of HO onset. Phase 3 (delayed) shows focal periarticular uptake. Positive before X-ray changes are visible.\n- **Alkaline Phosphatase (ALP)**: **Elevated** in active HO (often 2-3x normal). Serial levels track disease activity. Normal ALP does not rule out HO but declining levels suggest maturation.\n- **Plain Radiographs**: May be **normal in early HO** (first 2-4 weeks). Eventually show periarticular calcification/ossification. Useful for monitoring progression.\n- **CT Scan**: Better anatomic detail than X-ray; helpful for surgical planning if excision is considered later\n- **Doppler Ultrasound of Left Lower Extremity**: To **rule out concurrent DVT** (HO and DVT can coexist and HO can compress venous structures causing secondary DVT)\n- **CBC, ESR, CRP**: Inflammatory markers are elevated in HO but help distinguish from infection (very high WBC with left shift suggests infection)\n- **Serum Calcium/Phosphate**: Rule out hypercalcemia of immobilization (which can also occur in this timeframe)")
B.aPB=new A.c("+ The triple-phase bone scan shows intense uptake at the left hip in all three phases. Alkaline phosphatase is 285 U/L (normal 44-147). X-ray shows faint periarticular calcification medial to the left hip. What is your interpretation?","**Interpretation**:\nThe findings confirm **active heterotopic ossification of the left hip**, specifically:\n- **Triple-phase bone scan positive in all 3 phases**: Indicates active bone formation with increased blood flow, blood pooling, and osteoblastic activity. This is early/immature HO still in the active formation phase.\n- **Elevated ALP at 285 U/L** (nearly 2x normal): Confirms active osteoblastic activity. ALP is the most useful serum marker for monitoring HO activity; levels will decline as HO matures.\n- **Faint calcification on X-ray**: The fact that calcification is visible on X-ray at 6 weeks suggests the process has been ongoing for at least 2-4 weeks (X-ray lags behind bone scan by several weeks).\n- **Location (medial hip)**: This is the most common location for HO in SCI -- the medial and anterior aspects of the hip, involving the iliopsoas and adductor regions.\n\n**Maturity Assessment**: This HO is **immature/active** based on:\n- Positive Phase 1 and 2 on bone scan (blood flow/pooling = active inflammation)\n- Rising alkaline phosphatase\n- Clinical symptoms of warmth and swelling\n\nMaturity is important because **surgical excision should only be performed on mature HO** (typically 12-18 months post-onset) to minimize recurrence risk. Mature HO shows: normalized ALP, cold on Phase 1/2 of bone scan (only Phase 3 positive), and well-corticated bone on CT.")
B.aVt=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","With confirmed active HO of the left hip, you must now establish a comprehensive treatment plan to limit progression and preserve function.")
B.aRd=new A.c("+ Outline your management plan for active heterotopic ossification.","**Medical Management**:\n\n**1. NSAID Prophylaxis/Treatment -- Indomethacin**:\n- **Indomethacin 75 mg daily** (extended release) for 6 weeks is the first-line pharmacologic treatment\n- Mechanism: Inhibits prostaglandin-mediated osteoblast differentiation, slowing new bone formation\n- Must use GI prophylaxis: omeprazole 20 mg daily (indomethacin has high GI bleed risk)\n- Monitor renal function (BMP at 2 and 4 weeks)\n- Alternative: Celecoxib 200 mg BID (fewer GI side effects, similar efficacy in some studies)\n- **Contraindications to consider**: Active GI bleed, renal insufficiency, concomitant anticoagulation (weigh DVT prophylaxis overlap carefully)\n\n**2. Etidronate Disodium (Didronel)**:\n- 20 mg/kg/day for 2 weeks, then 10 mg/kg/day for 10 weeks\n- Bisphosphonate that inhibits hydroxyapatite crystal formation\n- Historically used but less favored currently due to osteomalacia risk and limited evidence compared to NSAIDs\n- May be considered if NSAIDs are contraindicated\n\n**3. Range of Motion Exercises**:\n- **Continue gentle passive ROM** within pain-free/resistance-free range\n- **DO NOT perform aggressive or forceful stretching** -- this can worsen HO by causing microtrauma and further inflammation\n- Goal: Maintain current functional ROM; prevent further contracture\n- Low-load, prolonged-duration stretching is preferred\n- Serial goniometry weekly to track ROM changes\n\n**4. Radiation Therapy (for Prophylaxis/Early Treatment)**:\n- **Single fraction radiation 700-800 cGy** to the affected hip\n- Most effective as **prophylaxis** (pre- or post-operatively) or in very early HO\n- Mechanism: Destroys osteoprogenitor cells that are rapidly dividing\n- Can be considered in this case given the active/early nature\n- Requires radiation oncology referral\n- Risk: Long-term malignancy risk (low but non-zero, especially in a 32-year-old)\n\n**5. Spasticity Management**:\n- Optimize baclofen (may need dose increase given worsening spasticity)\n- Spasticity can both contribute to and be exacerbated by HO\n- Consider intrathecal baclofen evaluation if oral medications are insufficient\n\n**6. Surgical Excision -- Timing and Indications**:\n- **NOT indicated now** -- only for mature HO causing significant functional limitation\n- Timing: **12-18 months** after onset, when ALP has normalized and bone scan shows only Phase 3 uptake\n- Indications: Significant functional impairment (inability to sit, transfer, or maintain hygiene), skin breakdown over bony prominence, neurovascular compression\n- Pre-operative radiation (single dose, 24 hours pre-op) + post-operative indomethacin (6 weeks) to prevent recurrence\n- Recurrence rate: 15-25% even with prophylaxis\n\n**7. Monitoring Plan**:\n- Serial ALP every 2-4 weeks (trending down = good response)\n- Repeat plain X-rays every 6-8 weeks to assess progression\n- Clinical ROM measurements weekly\n- Repeat bone scan at 3-6 months to assess maturity\n- Doppler ultrasound if any signs of DVT (HO can compress femoral vein)")
B.aYc=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","To manage this patient's HO comprehensively, you need to coordinate across multiple specialties and ensure timely access to diagnostic and therapeutic services.")
B.b3O=new A.c("+ Describe the multidisciplinary coordination required for HO management in the rehabilitation setting.","**Multidisciplinary Coordination**:\n\n**1. Orthopedic Surgery Consultation**:\n- Referral for: assessment of HO severity, discussion of surgical candidacy timeline, pre-operative planning\n- Timing: Early consultation to establish relationship; surgical excision would not be performed for at least 12-18 months\n- Coordinate: Serial imaging review, maturity assessment criteria\n\n**2. Radiation Oncology Referral**:\n- Referral for: consideration of single-fraction prophylactic/therapeutic radiation\n- Urgency: Sooner is better if radiation is to be used for active HO\n- Coordinate: Timing relative to NSAID therapy, shielding of gonads (important in this 32-year-old male -- discuss fertility preservation)\n- Insurance authorization: May require prior authorization; document functional impact and failed conservative measures\n\n**3. Physical Therapy Modification**:\n- Communicate ROM restrictions: gentle ROM only, no aggressive stretching\n- Modify therapy plan: focus on upper body strengthening, wheelchair skills, transfer techniques that minimize hip stress\n- Serial goniometry documentation for medical record and insurance justification\n\n**4. Rehabilitation Nursing**:\n- Monitor for signs of DVT (daily limb measurements)\n- Positioning adjustments to accommodate limited hip ROM\n- Skin surveillance: Limited hip flexion may alter sitting posture and increase pressure injury risk at ischial tuberosities\n\n**5. Seating and Positioning**:\n- Reassess wheelchair seating: A developing hip flexion contracture changes the seating geometry\n- May need tilt-in-space modification, custom cushion, or seat depth adjustment\n- Pressure mapping reassessment given altered posture\n\n**6. Laboratory Monitoring**:\n- Coordinate serial ALP draws (every 2-4 weeks)\n- Renal function monitoring while on indomethacin\n- CBC if concern for GI bleeding\n\n**7. Insurance and Authorization**:\n- Triple-phase bone scan and radiation therapy both require prior authorization from most insurers\n- Document medical necessity: functional decline (ROM measurements), failed conservative management, impact on rehabilitation goals\n- Appeal process may be needed for radiation therapy (sometimes denied as it is perceived as an oncology treatment)")
B.aQO=new A.c(u.j,"You need to inform the patient and his parents about the diagnosis of heterotopic ossification. The patient's mother is visibly anxious and asks, \"What is growing in his hip? Is it cancer? He's already been through so much.\"")
B.aP0=new A.c("+ Explain HO to the patient and family in understandable terms while addressing their fear.","**Model Response**:\n\"I completely understand your concern, and I want to reassure you right away -- this is **not cancer**. What is happening is something called heterotopic ossification, which I know is a mouthful. Let me explain it simply.\n\nAfter a spinal cord injury, the body sometimes gets confused about where to make bone. Instead of keeping bone formation in the skeleton where it belongs, new bone starts growing in the soft tissues around a joint -- in your son's case, around his left hip. Think of it like bone growing in a place it shouldn't be.\n\nThis is actually a well-known complication of spinal cord injury. It happens in about 20-30% of people with complete injuries like his. We caught it relatively early because his therapist noticed his hip was getting stiffer during exercises.\n\nHere is what this means practically: the extra bone is limiting how much his hip can move, which affects his ability to sit comfortably and transfer. If we don't manage it, it could continue to restrict his motion.\n\nThe good news is that we have a clear treatment plan:\n- We are starting a medication called indomethacin that helps slow down the abnormal bone growth\n- We are going to continue gentle range of motion exercises -- but not forceful stretching\n- We are referring to a couple of specialists to discuss whether radiation treatment could help\n- We will monitor with regular blood tests and imaging\n\nThe not-so-good news is that we need to be patient with this. The extra bone typically takes 12-18 months to fully mature and stop growing. If it causes significant problems after it has matured, it can be surgically removed, but we cannot do that safely while it is still actively forming.\n\nI know this feels like another setback on top of everything he has been dealing with, and that is completely understandable. But this is manageable, and it does not change his overall rehabilitation potential. We are going to adjust his therapy program and keep pushing forward.\n\nDo you have any questions? I want to make sure you both understand what we are dealing with and what to expect.\"")
B.aFl=s([B.aSe,B.aZk,B.b0Y,B.b__,B.aXN,B.aPB,B.aVt,B.aRd,B.aYc,B.b3O,B.aQO,B.aP0],t.E)
B.Q_=new A.a5("sci-heterotopic-ossification","Heterotopic Ossification Post-SCI",B.aFl,!1)
B.b06=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 55-year-old male with a T4 ASIA A spinal cord injury sustained 5 years ago presents to the SCI outpatient clinic with a worsening sacral wound. His home health nurse noted increasing wound size and malodorous drainage over the past 3 weeks. The patient reports he has been spending more time in his wheelchair recently (12-14 hours/day) because his wife left 2 months ago and he has been struggling with depression and self-care. He admits to not performing regular pressure relief weight shifts and has been sleeping in his wheelchair some nights.")
B.aRr=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **SCI History**: T4 ASIA A, traumatic (MVA), 5 years post-injury; uses manual wheelchair; previously independent in all ADLs\n- **Wound History**: First noticed a red area on sacrum approximately 6 weeks ago; progressed to open wound; has been applying over-the-counter gauze dressings at home without professional guidance\n- **Pressure Relief**: Admits to minimal weight shifts; sitting 12-14 hours/day; sleeping in wheelchair 3-4 nights per week; old wheelchair cushion (>3 years, never replaced, foam is compressed)\n- **Nutrition**: Poor oral intake; mostly canned soup and crackers; skipping meals; weight has dropped from 82 kg to 71 kg over 3 months (13% weight loss)\n- **Medications**: Baclofen 20 mg TID, gabapentin 600 mg TID, oxybutynin 10 mg BID, docusate/senna, multivitamin\n- **Medical History**: Prior Stage 2 pressure injury right ischial tuberosity (healed 2 years ago); recurrent UTIs; neurogenic bowel/bladder managed with IC and bowel program\n- **Psychosocial**: Recently separated from wife; lives alone now; two adult children live out of state; has been isolating; missed last 3 clinic appointments; screening positive for depression (PHQ-9 score 19 -- severe)\n- **Review of Systems**: Low-grade fevers (37.8-38.3C) for 1 week; malaise; decreased appetite; no rigors or chills; foul-smelling wound drainage")
B.aNx=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 102/64 (seated), HR 88, Temp 38.1C, RR 16, SpO2 97% on RA, Weight 71 kg, BMI 22.3\n\n**General**: Thin-appearing male in manual wheelchair; appears fatigued and withdrawn.\n\n**Wound Assessment (Sacral)**:\n- **Location**: Sacrum/coccyx, midline\n- **Size**: 8 cm x 6 cm x 4 cm deep\n- **Stage**: **NPUAP/EPUAP Stage 4** -- Full-thickness tissue loss with **exposed bone** (sacral bone visible at wound base), undermining of 2 cm at 3 o'clock and 5 o'clock positions\n- **Wound Bed**: 40% necrotic (yellow slough), 30% granulation tissue (pale/dusky, not healthy red), 30% exposed bone\n- **Drainage**: Copious, purulent, malodorous (green-yellow)\n- **Periwound Skin**: Macerated 2 cm circumferentially; erythema extending 3 cm beyond wound margins; warm to touch\n- **Tunneling**: 3 cm tunnel at 6 o'clock position (toward coccyx)\n\n**Nutritional Assessment**:\n- BMI 22.3 (down from 25.8 three months ago)\n- Temporal wasting present\n- Serum albumin: 2.4 g/dL (low; normal 3.5-5.0)\n- Prealbumin: 11 mg/dL (low; normal 18-45; most sensitive marker of acute nutritional status)\n- Total protein: 5.2 g/dL (low)\n\n**Labs**:\n- WBC: 14,200 (elevated)\n- ESR: 78 mm/hr (elevated)\n- CRP: 45 mg/L (elevated)\n- Hemoglobin: 10.2 g/dL (anemia of chronic disease)\n- Basic metabolic panel: Within normal limits\n\n**Remainder of Exam**: Chest clear; abdomen soft; bilateral lower extremities with chronic spasticity, no acute swelling; skin otherwise intact at ischial tuberosities and bilateral trochanters.")
B.b_W=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","This patient has a Stage 4 sacral pressure injury with exposed bone, purulent drainage, and systemic inflammatory signs. The combination of exposed bone and elevated inflammatory markers raises concern for osteomyelitis.")
B.aX7=new A.c("+ Describe the NPUAP pressure injury staging system and explain your workup for osteomyelitis.","**NPUAP Pressure Injury Staging**:\n\n- **Stage 1**: Non-blanchable erythema of intact skin. Skin is not broken. May be warmer/cooler, firmer/softer than surrounding tissue.\n- **Stage 2**: Partial-thickness skin loss involving epidermis and/or dermis. Presents as a shallow open ulcer with red-pink wound bed, or intact/ruptured blister. **No slough or granulation tissue**.\n- **Stage 3**: Full-thickness skin loss. Subcutaneous fat may be visible, but bone, tendon, and muscle are NOT exposed. May include undermining and tunneling.\n- **Stage 4**: Full-thickness tissue loss with **exposed bone, tendon, or muscle**. Slough or eschar may be present. Often includes undermining and tunneling. **This patient's wound is Stage 4** -- sacral bone is visible.\n- **Unstageable**: Full-thickness tissue loss with wound base obscured by slough (yellow, tan, gray, green, brown) or eschar (tan, brown, black). Cannot be staged until debrided.\n- **Deep Tissue Pressure Injury (DTPI)**: Intact or non-intact skin with localized area of persistent non-blanchable deep red, maroon, purple discoloration or epidermal separation revealing a dark wound bed. Damage originated at the bone-muscle interface.\n\n**Osteomyelitis Workup**:\nWith exposed sacral bone, purulent drainage, fever, and elevated inflammatory markers (WBC 14.2K, ESR 78, CRP 45), osteomyelitis must be ruled out:\n\n1. **MRI of the Sacrum** (with and without gadolinium): **Gold standard imaging** for osteomyelitis. Sensitivity 90%, specificity 80%. Findings: bone marrow edema (T1 low, T2/STIR high signal), cortical destruction, periosteal reaction, rim-enhancing fluid collections.\n\n2. **Bone Biopsy**: **Definitive diagnostic test**. Percutaneous or intraoperative bone biopsy with culture and histopathology. Surface wound cultures are unreliable (colonized with multiple organisms). Bone biopsy provides organism identification and antibiotic sensitivities.\n\n3. **Blood Cultures**: If systemic sepsis is suspected (fever, tachycardia).\n\n4. **Probe-to-Bone Test**: At bedside, insert a sterile metal probe into the wound. If bone is palpated (positive probe-to-bone), the **positive predictive value for osteomyelitis is 89%**. In this patient, bone is already visible, making osteomyelitis very likely.\n\n5. **Nuclear Medicine**: Labeled WBC scan (Indium-111 or Technetium-99m HMPAO) if MRI is contraindicated or equivocal. Triple-phase bone scan is sensitive but not specific (cannot distinguish infection from reactive changes).\n\n6. **Inflammatory Markers for Monitoring**: Serial ESR and CRP to track treatment response. Procalcitonin if bacteremia suspected.")
B.aVX=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","MRI confirms osteomyelitis of the sacrum with cortical erosion and bone marrow edema. Bone biopsy grows MSSA (methicillin-sensitive Staphylococcus aureus). You must now develop a comprehensive wound management plan.")
B.aQe=new A.c("+ Develop a comprehensive treatment plan for this Stage 4 pressure injury with confirmed osteomyelitis.","**1. Osteomyelitis Treatment**:\n- **IV antibiotics**: Nafcillin or cefazolin (for MSSA) x 6 weeks via PICC line\n- Infectious disease consultation for antibiotic management\n- Serial inflammatory markers (ESR/CRP) every 2 weeks to monitor response\n- Transition to oral suppressive therapy may be considered after IV course\n- **Note**: Antibiotics alone will not cure osteomyelitis if necrotic bone is not debrided\n\n**2. Surgical Debridement**:\n- **Urgent sharp debridement** of necrotic tissue and infected bone by plastic surgery or wound care specialist\n- Remove all slough and non-viable tissue to expose healthy, bleeding wound bed\n- Debride infected sacral bone back to viable bleeding bone\n- Culture bone intraoperatively for definitive organism identification\n- Serial bedside debridements may be needed every 1-2 weeks\n\n**3. Wound Care Protocol**:\n- **After debridement**: Negative pressure wound therapy (wound VAC) at 75-125 mmHg continuous\n  - Promotes granulation tissue formation, reduces wound volume, manages exudate\n  - Change VAC dressing every 48-72 hours\n- **Dressing progression**: As wound granulates and becomes smaller, transition to appropriate moisture-retentive dressings:\n  - Wound with heavy drainage: Calcium alginate or hydrofiber dressings\n  - Moderate drainage: Foam dressings\n  - Wound bed preparation: Collagenase (Santyl) for enzymatic debridement of residual slough\n- **Wound measurements**: Weekly documentation of length, width, depth, undermining, tunneling\n\n**4. Nutritional Optimization** (Critical for wound healing):\n- **Caloric goal**: 30-35 kcal/kg/day (2100-2500 kcal/day for this patient)\n- **Protein goal**: 1.5-2.0 g/kg/day (106-142 g protein/day) -- wounds cannot heal without adequate protein\n- Oral nutritional supplements (Ensure, Boost) TID between meals\n- **Vitamin C**: 500 mg BID (collagen synthesis)\n- **Zinc sulfate**: 220 mg daily (cell proliferation and immune function)\n- **Correct anemia**: Iron supplementation; consider erythropoietin if anemia persists\n- Dietitian referral for meal planning and monitoring\n- Weekly weight checks; repeat albumin/prealbumin every 2 weeks\n\n**5. Pressure Offloading** (Most important modifiable factor):\n- **Complete bed rest** with pressure redistribution mattress (alternating pressure or low-air-loss mattress) during acute healing phase\n- **NO sitting on the wound** until significant healing has occurred (may take weeks to months)\n- Repositioning schedule: Turn every 2 hours; 30-degree lateral tilt positions\n- When sitting is gradually reintroduced: Start with 15 minutes, inspect wound, slowly increase\n- New wheelchair cushion: ROHO or JAY pressure-relieving cushion with pressure mapping evaluation\n\n**6. Surgical Reconstruction (Flap Surgery)**:\n- **Myocutaneous flap closure** (gluteal rotation flap or V-Y advancement flap) should be considered once:\n  - Osteomyelitis is adequately treated (minimum 6 weeks IV antibiotics)\n  - Wound bed is clean with healthy granulation tissue\n  - Nutritional status is optimized (albumin >3.0)\n  - Patient is committed to post-operative pressure relief protocol (minimum 6-8 weeks of strict bed rest post-flap)\n- Plastic surgery consultation for surgical planning\n- **Post-flap protocol**: 6-8 weeks strict bed rest, progressive sitting program (starting at 15 min, increase by 15 min/day as tolerated with wound checks)\n\n**7. Depression Treatment**:\n- PHQ-9 score of 19 indicates severe depression -- this is driving his self-neglect\n- Start **SSRI** (sertraline 50 mg daily -- first-line, favorable side effect profile in SCI)\n- Psychiatry or psychology referral for therapy\n- Address social isolation: SCI peer support, community reintegration\n- **This is not optional** -- untreated depression will undermine all wound healing efforts")
B.b_9=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","This patient requires coordinated multidisciplinary wound care involving multiple services, equipment, and home modifications. He also needs a comprehensive seating evaluation and social support system.")
B.b3g=new A.c("+ Describe the systems-based approach to comprehensive wound care and prevention of recurrence.","**Wound Care Team Coordination**:\n1. **Physiatrist (Team Leader)**: Coordinates overall rehabilitation plan, manages comorbidities, prescribes DME, oversees wound prevention strategy\n2. **Wound Care Specialist/Plastic Surgery**: Debridement, wound care protocol, flap surgery planning\n3. **Infectious Disease**: Osteomyelitis antibiotic management, PICC line monitoring\n4. **Dietitian**: Nutritional optimization, calorie/protein tracking, supplement management\n5. **Rehabilitation Nursing**: Wound dressing changes, turning schedule compliance, patient/caregiver education\n6. **Physical/Occupational Therapy**: Pressure relief technique retraining, wheelchair mobility, adaptive equipment\n7. **Psychology/Psychiatry**: Depression treatment, behavioral activation, motivation\n8. **Social Work/Case Management**: Insurance authorization, home health coordination, community resources\n\n**Seating and Positioning Evaluation**:\n- Comprehensive **wheelchair seating clinic** evaluation\n- **Pressure mapping** to identify peak pressure areas and guide cushion selection\n- Replace current 3-year-old foam cushion with an appropriate pressure-relieving cushion (ROHO or equivalent)\n- Assess wheelchair frame, backrest, and positioning for optimal pressure distribution\n- Consider power wheelchair with **tilt-in-space** function for automated pressure relief if patient cannot reliably perform weight shifts\n\n**Home Modification and DME**:\n- **Pressure redistribution mattress** for home (alternating pressure or low-air-loss)\n- **PICC line care supplies** and home infusion pharmacy coordination\n- **Wound VAC rental** if continued at home\n- Home health nursing: daily wound care, PICC line dressing changes, antibiotic infusion\n- Home health aide: assist with ADLs during bed rest period\n\n**Prevention of Recurrence** (Long-term):\n- Lifetime skin inspection program (twice daily, with long-handled mirror)\n- Annual wheelchair seating evaluation with pressure mapping\n- Nutritional monitoring (maintain adequate weight, protein intake)\n- Mental health follow-up (depression is a modifiable risk factor for pressure injury recurrence)\n- Regular cushion replacement (every 1-3 years depending on type)\n- Community reintegration to combat isolation and improve self-care motivation\n- SCI clinic follow-up every 3-6 months with comprehensive skin assessment")
B.b_G=new A.c(u.j,"When you explain the severity of the wound and the need for weeks of bed rest, the patient becomes defensive: \"I know I should be doing my weight shifts, but you don't understand what it's been like since my wife left. I just don't care anymore. What's the point?\"")
B.b1p=new A.c("+ Demonstrate motivational interviewing and trauma-informed care to address non-adherence and depression.",'**Model Response Using Motivational Interviewing Principles**:\n\n"I hear you, and I want you to know that I am not here to lecture you or make you feel guilty. You are dealing with a tremendous amount right now -- the separation, being on your own, and now this wound on top of everything. That is a lot for anyone.\n\nLet me be honest with you about where things stand medically: this wound is serious. There is infection in your bone, and if we do not treat it aggressively, it could enter your bloodstream and become life-threatening. I am not saying that to scare you -- I am saying it because I respect you enough to be straight with you.\n\nBut I also want to address what you just said -- that you don\'t care anymore. That tells me something important. That is not laziness; that is depression, and it is a real medical condition, just like your spinal cord injury. It is treatable, and treating it is just as important as treating your wound.\n\nCan I ask you something? Before things got this hard -- back when things were going well -- what did your days look like? What mattered to you?"\n\n*[Allow patient to reflect and respond -- this is the motivational interviewing technique of evoking the patient\'s own values and motivation]*\n\n"Those things you just described -- that is still who you are. The depression is telling you otherwise right now, but it is lying to you.\n\nHere is what I would like to propose: We tackle this together, as a team. I am going to treat your wound, get you the right antibiotics, fix your nutrition, and get you a proper cushion. But I also want to start you on medication for depression and connect you with a counselor. And I want to get someone to your house to help -- not because you cannot do it, but because right now you need support, and there is no shame in that.\n\nYou do not have to do this alone. What do you think -- are you willing to let us help you?"')
B.aCU=s([B.b06,B.aRr,B.aNx,B.b_W,B.aX7,B.aVX,B.aQe,B.b_9,B.b3g,B.b_G,B.b1p],t.E)
B.QR=new A.a5("sci-pressure-injury-stage4","Stage 4 Sacral Pressure Injury in Chronic SCI",B.aCU,!1)
B.b3F=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 38-year-old female is admitted to the acute rehabilitation unit 10 days after sustaining a stab wound to the left posterolateral neck at the C5 level. She underwent emergent surgical exploration with wound repair and was stabilized in the trauma ICU. On transfer, she demonstrates left upper and lower extremity weakness with preserved but altered sensation. She reports inability to feel temperature on her right side and describes a burning, tingling pain in her left arm. She is alert, cooperative, but tearful and reports difficulty sleeping due to fear of her attacker.")
B.b_p=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **Mechanism**: Stabbing assault by a known assailant (domestic partner); knife penetrated left posterolateral neck at approximately C5 level\n- **Surgical**: Emergent wound exploration, debridement, and repair; no vascular injury identified; vertebral artery intact on CT angiography\n- **Neurological Course**: Deficits noted immediately post-injury and have been stable\n- **Pain**: Left arm/hand burning dysesthesia (neuropathic character); rates 7/10; worse at night; also left-sided neck pain at surgical site\n- **Bladder**: Initially required Foley; transitioned to voiding with elevated post-void residuals (150-200 mL); no current incontinence\n- **Bowel**: Having bowel movements but reports mild constipation\n- **Functional Status**: Requires moderate assist for transfers; ambulating 50 feet with rolling walker and moderate assist (left leg weakness)\n- **Psychosocial**: History of domestic violence (this is the third assault by this partner); has a protective order that was violated; two children ages 8 and 12 living with her mother currently; works as a school teacher; reports hypervigilance, nightmares, difficulty sleeping, and startle responses since the assault\n- **Medications**: Gabapentin 300 mg TID (started for neuropathic pain), acetaminophen 1000 mg Q6H PRN, docusate 100 mg BID, enoxaparin 40 mg daily")
B.aTo=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 128/78, HR 82, Temp 37.0C, RR 14, SpO2 99% on RA\n\n**ASIA/ISNCSCI Examination**:\n- **Motor (Left -- Ipsilateral to injury)**:\n  - C5: Biceps 3/5\n  - C6: Wrist extensors 3/5\n  - C7: Triceps 4-/5\n  - C8: Finger flexors 4/5\n  - T1: Finger abductors 4/5\n  - L2: Hip flexors 3/5\n  - L3: Knee extensors 3/5\n  - L4: Ankle dorsiflexors 3/5\n  - L5: Great toe extensors 2/5\n  - S1: Ankle plantarflexors 3/5\n- **Motor (Right -- Contralateral)**: 5/5 throughout (C5-T1, L2-S1)\n\n- **Sensory (Light Touch)**:\n  - Left: Intact throughout (C2-S4/5)\n  - Right: Intact throughout (C2-S4/5)\n- **Sensory (Pin Prick)**:\n  - Left: Intact C2-C4; **reduced** C5-C8; intact T1 and below\n  - Right: Intact C2-C4; **absent** C5 and below (contralateral spinothalamic tract disruption)\n- **Deep Pressure/Proprioception (Left)**: **Impaired** -- reduced position sense in left great toe and ankle (ipsilateral dorsal column disruption)\n- **Proprioception (Right)**: Intact\n\n- **Sacral Sparing**: VAC present (weak); DAP present; S4-S5 light touch present bilaterally\n- **ASIA Impairment Scale**: **C** (Motor incomplete -- more than half of key muscles below NLI are < grade 3)\n\n**Tone**: Left upper and lower extremity -- mild spasticity (MAS 1+) in elbow flexors and knee extensors; right side normal tone.\n**DTRs**: Left side hyperreflexic (3+); positive Babinski left; right side 2+ throughout.\n**Gait**: Left leg circumduction pattern; left foot drop; requires AFO and rolling walker with moderate assist.\n**Neck**: Left posterolateral surgical wound healing well with sutures; no drainage or erythema.")
B.b3p=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","This patient demonstrates the classic Brown-Sequard pattern with ipsilateral motor loss and proprioceptive deficit, and contralateral pain/temperature loss. Understanding the neuroanatomical basis is essential.")
B.aSa=new A.c("+ Explain the neuroanatomical basis of Brown-Sequard Syndrome and the specific tract involvement producing this patient's findings.","**Brown-Sequard Syndrome -- Spinal Cord Hemisection**:\n\nThis syndrome results from damage to one half of the spinal cord, producing a characteristic pattern of deficits:\n\n**Ipsilateral Findings (Left -- same side as injury)**:\n1. **Motor weakness/paralysis (ipsilateral)**: Due to disruption of the **lateral corticospinal tract**, which has already decussated at the medullary pyramids. This patient's left-sided weakness (C5-S1) reflects damage to the left corticospinal tract at C5.\n2. **Loss of proprioception and vibration (ipsilateral)**: Due to disruption of the **dorsal columns** (fasciculus gracilis and cuneatus), which ascend ipsilaterally and do not decussate until the medulla. This patient has impaired position sense on the left.\n3. **Hyperreflexia and Babinski sign (ipsilateral)**: Upper motor neuron signs from corticospinal tract damage.\n4. **At-level pain/sensory changes**: Damage to the dorsal horn at the level of injury can produce ipsilateral dermatomal pain and reduced pin prick at C5-C8 (zone of injury).\n\n**Contralateral Findings (Right -- opposite side)**:\n1. **Loss of pain and temperature sensation (contralateral)**: Due to disruption of the **lateral spinothalamic tract**, which has already decussated at the anterior white commissure 1-2 levels below the dorsal root entry zone. This patient has absent pin prick on the right below C5.\n2. **Preserved light touch and proprioception (contralateral)**: The right dorsal columns are intact, and light touch is carried by multiple pathways (including the anterior spinothalamic tract on the contralateral side).\n\n**Prognosis -- Key Differentiator**:\nBrown-Sequard syndrome has the **best prognosis of all incomplete SCI patterns**:\n- **75-90%** of patients regain functional ambulation\n- The ipsilateral (weak) leg typically recovers enough strength for ambulation because some corticospinal fibers remain on the contralateral side (anterior corticospinal tract, ~10-15% of fibers, uncrossed)\n- Motor recovery begins proximally and progresses distally\n- Contralateral pain/temperature sensation often partially recovers via ipsilateral spinothalamic fibers\n- This is in stark contrast to anterior cord syndrome (worst motor prognosis) and central cord syndrome (variable)\n\n**ASIA Classification**: This patient is **AIS C** -- motor incomplete with sacral sparing (VAC present) and more than half of key muscles below the NLI grade <3. As she recovers, she may convert to AIS D (majority of muscles grade 3 or above).")
B.b3K=new A.c("+ How do you evaluate and manage her neuropathic pain?","**Neuropathic Pain Classification in SCI**:\n- **At-level neuropathic pain**: Burning, tingling pain in the C5-C8 dermatomes (left arm) -- at or within 3 levels of the NLI. Caused by dorsal horn damage and nerve root injury at the level of the lesion.\n- **Below-level neuropathic pain**: Diffuse burning, shooting pain below the NLI. Caused by central sensitization and spinothalamic tract disruption.\n\n**Evaluation**:\n- Neuropathic pain screening: DN4 questionnaire or LANSS scale\n- Characterize: burning, electric, tingling, squeezing -- distinguish from nociceptive/musculoskeletal pain\n- Rule out other causes: syringomyelia (MRI if pain pattern changes), nerve root compression, complex regional pain syndrome\n- Assess impact: sleep disruption, mood, function (Brief Pain Inventory)\n\n**Pharmacologic Management (Stepwise)**:\n1. **First-line**: Gabapentin (titrate to 1800-3600 mg/day in divided doses) or **pregabalin** (150-600 mg/day) -- already started; needs uptitration\n2. **Second-line**: Add or switch to **duloxetine** (60-120 mg/day) or **amitriptyline** (25-75 mg at bedtime) -- dual benefit for pain and comorbid depression/anxiety\n3. **Third-line**: Tramadol (for mixed pain); topical agents (lidocaine 5% patch for at-level pain)\n4. **Avoid**: Long-term opioids for neuropathic pain (poor efficacy, high risk in SCI population)\n\n**Non-Pharmacologic**:\n- Cognitive behavioral therapy for pain management\n- Transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation (TENS) at or above level of injury\n- Mirror therapy (emerging evidence in SCI pain)\n- Desensitization techniques for allodynia\n- Address comorbid PTSD (see Domain E) -- trauma amplifies pain perception")
B.aSb=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","With her favorable prognosis for ambulation, you design a comprehensive rehabilitation program targeting functional recovery while managing her complex pain and psychosocial needs.")
B.b0Z=new A.c("+ Outline the rehabilitation program for this patient with Brown-Sequard Syndrome.","**Rehabilitation Program**:\n\n**1. Physical Therapy -- Gait and Mobility**:\n- **Gait training**: Progress from parallel bars to rolling walker to single-point cane as strength improves\n- **Left AFO**: Articulating ankle-foot orthosis for left foot drop (L5 weakness); reassess need as dorsiflexion recovers\n- **Body-weight-supported treadmill training**: Excellent modality for incomplete SCI gait retraining; utilize locomotor training principles to reinforce normal gait patterns\n- **Strengthening**: Progressive resistance exercises for left-sided weakness (C5-S1 myotomes); focus on hip flexors, knee extensors, and ankle dorsiflexors\n- **Balance training**: Impaired left-side proprioception affects balance; incorporate proprioceptive retraining (wobble board, tandem stance, eyes-closed exercises)\n- **Transfer training**: Progress from sliding board to stand-pivot transfers\n\n**2. Occupational Therapy**:\n- Left upper extremity fine motor and grip strengthening (C5-C6 weakness at 3/5)\n- Adaptive techniques for one-handed tasks during recovery phase\n- Left hand dexterity exercises (putty, pegboard, functional task practice)\n- Home safety evaluation and modification recommendations\n- Return-to-work assessment (teacher -- evaluate handwriting, computer use, classroom mobility)\n\n**3. Bladder Management**:\n- Monitor post-void residuals; currently elevated (150-200 mL)\n- If PVRs remain >150 mL: intermittent catheterization program or timed voiding with double-void technique\n- Urodynamics at 3 months to assess detrusor function\n- Expect improvement as neurologic recovery occurs (incomplete injury)\n\n**4. Spasticity Management**:\n- Currently mild (MAS 1+); monitor as may worsen or improve\n- Stretching program for left upper and lower extremity\n- If functionally limiting: baclofen 5 mg TID and titrate; tizanidine as alternative\n- Note: Some spasticity in the left leg may be functionally helpful for standing/transfers\n\n**5. Pain Management** (as detailed in Domain B):\n- Uptitrate gabapentin; consider adding duloxetine for dual pain/mood benefit\n- TENS, desensitization, CBT for pain\n\n**6. DVT Prophylaxis**:\n- Continue enoxaparin for 8-12 weeks post-SCI (standard for incomplete injuries with reduced mobility)\n- Bilateral SCDs when in bed\n\n**7. Functional Prognosis and Goals**:\n- **Short-term (discharge from inpatient rehab)**: Modified independent ambulation with AFO and rolling walker for household distances; independent transfers; independent ADLs with adaptive equipment as needed\n- **Long-term (6-12 months)**: Community ambulation with cane or independent; return to work as teacher; independent bladder management; pain controlled to allow function and sleep")
B.aWc=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","This patient sustained her spinal cord injury as a result of domestic violence. The assailant is a known partner who violated a protective order. She has two school-age children who are currently with her mother.")
B.aYC=new A.c("+ Describe the systems-based approach to managing a violence-related SCI, including mandatory reporting, safety planning, and victim advocacy.","**Violence-Related SCI -- Systems-Based Approach**:\n\n**1. Mandatory Reporting and Legal Obligations**:\n- This assault has already been reported to law enforcement (through the trauma center)\n- Verify that a **police report has been filed** and that the existing protective order violation is documented\n- Document injuries thoroughly in the medical record with precise descriptions (location, size, mechanism) -- this medical documentation may be critical for criminal prosecution\n- If children are at risk, **mandatory report to Child Protective Services** (even if children were not directly harmed, exposure to domestic violence is reportable in most states)\n\n**2. Social Work Involvement** (Immediate):\n- Consult social work on admission to rehabilitation\n- **Safety assessment**: Is the patient safe? Does the assailant know her location? Is there active law enforcement involvement?\n- **Safety planning**: Develop a personalized safety plan for discharge including safe housing, emergency contacts, escape plan if needed\n- **Victim advocacy**: Connect with local domestic violence advocacy organization for legal aid, counseling, housing assistance, and court accompaniment\n- **Children**: Assess custody situation, ensure children are safe and receiving support\n\n**3. Legal Resources**:\n- Facilitate access to a **victim advocate** who can assist with:\n  - Strengthening the protective order\n  - Victim compensation funds (for medical expenses, lost wages)\n  - Criminal case coordination\n  - Immigration assistance if applicable\n- Document that injuries are consistent with reported assault mechanism (important for legal proceedings)\n\n**4. Discharge Planning Considerations**:\n- **Safe housing**: Cannot discharge patient to home if assailant has access; may need domestic violence shelter with accessible accommodations (ADA compliant)\n- Coordinate with domestic violence shelter to ensure wheelchair accessibility, aide services, and medical follow-up access\n- Address financial security: patient may not be able to work during recovery; connect with disability benefits, crime victim compensation, FMLA\n- School arrangements for children\n\n**5. Staff Safety and Awareness**:\n- Flag the chart that this is a violence-related injury\n- Restrict visitor access; verify visitor identity\n- If assailant attempts to visit, notify security and law enforcement immediately\n- Educate rehabilitation staff about domestic violence dynamics (do not judge patient for prior relationship history)\n\n**6. Long-term Follow-up**:\n- Continued domestic violence counseling\n- PTSD treatment (see Domain E)\n- Re-assess safety at every outpatient visit\n- Community resources: support groups for DV survivors, children's counseling services")
B.aNf=new A.c(u.j,"During a therapy session, the patient freezes and becomes tearful when a male staff member approaches from behind. She later tells you: \"I can't stop thinking about it. Every time someone comes near me, I think he's back. I haven't slept more than 2 hours at a time since it happened. I don't feel safe anywhere.\"")
B.aOO=new A.c("+ Demonstrate PTSD screening and trauma-informed care in your response.",'**Model Response -- Trauma-Informed Care**:\n\n"Thank you for telling me this. What you are experiencing -- the fear, the flashbacks, the difficulty sleeping, the feeling of being on guard all the time -- these are completely normal responses to what happened to you. Your brain is trying to protect you, even though you are safe right now.\n\nWhat you are describing sounds like it may be post-traumatic stress disorder, which is very common after a violent assault. I want to screen you formally so we can get you the right support. Would it be okay if I asked you a few specific questions?"\n\n*[Administer the **PC-PTSD-5** (Primary Care PTSD Screen for DSM-5) or **PCL-5** (PTSD Checklist for DSM-5)]*\n\n**Key Screening Questions (PC-PTSD-5)**:\n1. Have you had nightmares or thought about the attack when you did not want to?\n2. Have you tried hard not to think about it or gone out of your way to avoid situations that remind you?\n3. Have you been constantly on guard, watchful, or easily startled?\n4. Have you felt numb or detached from people, activities, or your surroundings?\n5. Have you felt guilty or unable to stop blaming yourself?\n\n*[Score of 3+ suggests probable PTSD; this patient will likely screen positive]*\n\n**Continued Response**:\n"Based on what you have told me, I am concerned that you are experiencing PTSD, and I want to make sure we treat this just as seriously as we treat your physical injury. Here is what I would like to do:\n\nFirst, I am going to make some changes to your therapy environment to help you feel safer. I will talk to your therapy team about always approaching you from the front, announcing themselves, and giving you control over your space. If at any point you need a break or feel overwhelmed, you can always say so -- that is completely acceptable.\n\nSecond, I want to refer you to our psychologist who specializes in trauma. The most effective treatments for PTSD are **Cognitive Processing Therapy** and **Prolonged Exposure Therapy** -- both have strong evidence. We can also consider starting medication. The SSRI I am recommending for your depression, sertraline, is also FDA-approved for PTSD and can help with the nightmares and hyperarousal.\n\nFor sleep specifically, we can try **prazosin** -- it is a medication that helps reduce trauma-related nightmares. Many of my patients find it makes a significant difference.\n\nI also want you to know: what happened to you is not your fault. You did nothing to deserve this. And you are showing incredible strength by being here and working on your recovery.\n\nIs there anything we can do right now to help you feel safer on this unit?"')
B.aDC=s([B.b3F,B.b_p,B.aTo,B.b3p,B.aSa,B.b3K,B.aSb,B.b0Z,B.aWc,B.aYC,B.aNf,B.aOO],t.E)
B.PE=new A.a5("sci-brown-sequard-syndrome","Brown-Sequard Syndrome (Spinal Cord Hemisection)",B.aDC,!1)
B.aYH=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 22-year-old male is transferred to the ventilator-capable acute inpatient rehabilitation unit 3 weeks after sustaining a C3 ASIA A spinal cord injury from a diving accident in a shallow lake. He underwent emergent intubation at the scene, posterior cervical fusion C2-C5, and tracheostomy placement on post-injury day 7. He is currently ventilator-dependent 24 hours per day via a cuffed #8 Shiley tracheostomy on assist-control mode (TV 700 mL, RR 14, FiO2 0.30, PEEP 5). He is alert, communicates by mouthing words and using eye gaze, and appears engaged but anxious.")
B.aT7=new A.c("History & Systems Review:",'- **Mechanism**: Dove headfirst into shallow water (estimated 3-4 feet deep) at a lake party; immediate quadriplegia and apnea; bystander initiated rescue breathing\n- **Surgical**: Posterior C2-C5 fusion with lateral mass screws; tracheostomy day 7 (failed extubation attempt day 5 due to absent respiratory drive)\n- **Neurological**: C3 ASIA A -- no motor or sensory function below C3; no sacral sparing; only preserved function is neck flexion/extension (C1-C2), shoulder shrug (C3-C4 partial on right only, trapezius 2/5 right, 0/5 left)\n- **Respiratory**: Completely ventilator-dependent; no spontaneous respiratory effort observed; diaphragm paralysis confirmed (C3-C5 phrenic nerve involvement)\n- **Current Ventilator Settings**: AC mode, TV 700 mL, RR 14, FiO2 0.30, PEEP 5; ABG: pH 7.41, PaCO2 38, PaO2 95\n- **Complications**: Two episodes of mucus plugging requiring emergent suctioning; one episode of right lower lobe atelectasis (resolved with aggressive pulmonary toilet)\n- **Bowel/Bladder**: Indwelling Foley catheter; bowel program not yet established (UMN bowel pattern expected)\n- **Skin**: Intact; on alternating pressure mattress; turned every 2 hours\n- **Psychosocial**: College junior studying engineering; star athlete (swimming and water polo); parents are divorced; mother and father both involved but in conflict; younger sister is very close to patient. Patient has been mouthing "I want to die" to the night nurse on two occasions\n- **Medications**: Enoxaparin 40 mg daily, famotidine 20 mg BID, docusate 100 mg daily (via PEG -- placed day 10), albuterol nebulizer Q4H, normal saline nebulizer Q4H')
B.b1b=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 94/58 (supine), HR 56, RR 14 (ventilator), Temp 37.1C, SpO2 98% on FiO2 0.30\n\n**General**: Alert young male in hospital bed, ventilator-dependent via tracheostomy. Communicates by mouthing words. Appears fatigued.\n\n**ASIA Examination**:\n- **Motor**: C5-T1 bilateral: 0/5; L2-S1 bilateral: 0/5. Right trapezius 2/5 (C3-C4 partial innervation). Left trapezius 0/5. Total motor score: 0/100.\n- **Sensory**: Light touch and pin prick intact C2-C3 bilaterally; absent C4 and below bilaterally (C4 light touch 1/2 on right only).\n- **Sacral Sparing**: None (VAC absent, DAP absent, S4-S5 absent).\n- **NLI**: C3\n- **AIS**: A (Complete)\n\n**Respiratory Examination**:\n- **Chest wall**: No visible respiratory excursion with ventilator disconnection (brief trial)\n- **Diaphragm**: No palpable or visible diaphragmatic excursion\n- **Cough**: Absent voluntary cough; requires suctioning for secretion management\n- **Lung auscultation**: Diminished bases bilaterally; scattered rhonchi clearing with suctioning; no wheezing or crackles\n- **Chest X-ray**: Low lung volumes, mild bibasilar atelectasis, no consolidation\n\n**Phrenic Nerve Assessment**:\n- **Diaphragm fluoroscopy (sniff test)**: No diaphragmatic excursion bilaterally (paradoxical movement noted -- negative sniff test)\n- **Phrenic nerve conduction study**: Bilateral phrenic nerve latency absent (no compound motor action potential recorded from either hemidiaphragm)\n- Note: Phrenic nerve studies at 3 weeks may reflect neurapraxia vs. axonotmesis; repeat at 3-6 months to reassess\n\n**Autonomic**: Resting HR 56 (loss of sympathetic cardiac innervation); BP 94/58 supine requiring midodrine 10 mg TID; poikilothermy present.\n**Skin**: Intact. Bilateral heel offloading boots in place. Sacrum intact on pressure redistribution mattress.\n**Tone**: Flaccid throughout (spinal shock).")
B.aVi=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","This patient has a C3 ASIA A injury with complete ventilator dependence. Respiratory management is the critical medical issue that determines survival, quality of life, and discharge disposition.")
B.aRX=new A.c("+ Explain the respiratory physiology of high cervical SCI and evaluate this patient's candidacy for diaphragm pacing.","**Respiratory Physiology in High Cervical SCI**:\n\n**Key Respiratory Muscles and Innervation**:\n- **Diaphragm (C3-C5)**: Primary muscle of inspiration, responsible for ~65-75% of resting tidal volume. In C3 injury, bilateral diaphragm paralysis occurs -- this is why the patient is ventilator-dependent.\n- **Accessory muscles**: Sternocleidomastoid (CN XI/C2-C3) and upper trapezius (CN XI/C3-C4) -- may be partially preserved in this patient (right trapezius 2/5). These provide minimal tidal volume (~200-300 mL) and cannot sustain ventilation.\n- **Intercostals (T1-T11)**: Paralyzed. Loss of chest wall expansion and stabilization.\n- **Abdominals (T6-T12)**: Paralyzed. Loss of forced expiration and effective cough.\n- **Scalenes (C3-C8)**: Paralyzed in C3 injury.\n\n**Consequences**:\n- Complete loss of voluntary ventilation\n- Ineffective cough (expiratory muscles paralyzed) leading to secretion retention, atelectasis, pneumonia\n- Reduced lung compliance over time (chest wall stiffness from immobility)\n- V/Q mismatch from dependent atelectasis\n- **Pneumonia is the #1 cause of death** in chronic high cervical SCI\n\n**Diaphragm Pacing Candidacy**:\n\nDiaphragm pacing (also called phrenic nerve pacing) uses implanted electrodes to electrically stimulate the phrenic nerves, causing diaphragm contraction and inspiration. This can allow ventilator-free breathing in selected patients.\n\n**Candidacy Criteria**:\n1. **Intact lower motor neurons**: The phrenic nerve anterior horn cells (C3-C5) must be intact. This is the case in **upper motor neuron** lesions (injury above C3) or injuries where the phrenic nerve cell bodies are spared.\n2. **Phrenic nerve conduction**: Must demonstrate an intact phrenic nerve-to-diaphragm pathway. **This patient currently has absent phrenic nerve responses at 3 weeks -- but this is TOO EARLY to determine candidacy.** Early absence may reflect:\n   - **Neurapraxia**: Temporary conduction block from edema/contusion at the C3 level; may recover as swelling resolves\n   - **Axonotmesis/Neurotmesis**: Axonal damage at the anterior horn cell level; will NOT recover\n3. **Repeat phrenic nerve studies at 3-6 months** post-injury to allow time for neurapraxia to resolve and Wallerian degeneration to complete\n4. **If phrenic nerve is intact on repeat testing**: Patient IS a candidate for phrenic nerve pacing\n5. **If phrenic nerve remains absent**: Patient is NOT a candidate for standard phrenic pacing; may be considered for:\n   - **Intercostal-to-phrenic nerve transfer** (emerging surgical technique)\n   - **Direct diaphragm pacing** (intramuscular electrodes placed laparoscopically into the diaphragm -- NeuRx system)\n\n**Benefits of Diaphragm Pacing**:\n- Ventilator-free breathing for hours per day (some patients achieve 24-hour independence)\n- Improved speech quality (airflow-driven phonation)\n- Enhanced smell and taste\n- Reduced respiratory infections\n- Greater mobility (portable, no ventilator circuit)\n- Improved quality of life and independence\n- Reduced caregiver burden")
B.aZO=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","While awaiting repeat phrenic nerve studies, you must manage this patient's ventilator weaning trials, tracheostomy care, and comprehensive pulmonary rehabilitation program.")
B.aYI=new A.c("+ Describe your ventilator weaning protocol and pulmonary management plan for this patient.","**Ventilator Weaning Protocol for High Cervical SCI**:\n\n**1. Progressive Ventilator-Free Breathing (PVFB) Trials**:\n- Although C3 ASIA A patients rarely achieve full ventilator independence, some patients develop accessory muscle breathing capacity sufficient for brief periods off the ventilator\n- **Prerequisites**: Medically stable, afebrile, no acute pulmonary issues, FiO2 \u22640.40, PEEP \u22645\n- **Protocol**:\n  - Start with **tracheostomy collar trials** (humidified O2 via trach collar, ventilator disconnected)\n  - Initial trial: 1-5 minutes with continuous SpO2 and ETCO2 monitoring\n  - Abort criteria: SpO2 <92%, HR >120 or <50, RR >35, patient distress, PaCO2 >50\n  - If tolerated: Increase by 5-minute increments per session, twice daily\n  - Goal: Maximize ventilator-free time for quality of life (speech, eating, mobility) even if full weaning is not achievable\n  - **Realistic expectation for C3 ASIA A**: May achieve 15-60 minutes of ventilator-free breathing using accessory muscles; 24-hour ventilator independence is unlikely without diaphragm pacing\n\n**2. Tracheostomy Management**:\n- **Cuff management**: Deflate cuff during the day when possible to allow:\n  - Above-cuff airflow for **speech** (one-way speaking valve -- Passy-Muir valve)\n  - Improved swallowing function\n  - Reduced tracheal mucosal damage\n- **Passy-Muir Valve (PMV)**: Trial with cuff fully deflated; requires adequate airway around the trach tube\n  - Benefits: Voice production, improved swallowing, better secretion management, psychological benefit\n  - Monitor: SpO2, work of breathing, patient comfort\n- **Tracheostomy downsizing**: Consider downsizing from #8 to #6 Shiley if ventilation is adequate (allows more airflow around tube)\n- **Trach site care**: Daily cleaning, inner cannula changes, monitor for granulation tissue\n\n**3. Secretion Management and Pneumonia Prevention**:\n- **Mechanical insufflation-exsufflation (MI-E / CoughAssist)**: 4-6 sessions daily; delivers positive pressure insufflation followed by rapid negative pressure exsufflation to simulate a cough\n  - Settings: +40/-40 cmH2O, 3-5 cycles per treatment\n  - **Single most important device for secretion clearance** in high SCI\n- **Suctioning**: PRN via tracheostomy; gentle technique to minimize mucosal trauma; pre-oxygenate before suctioning\n- **Chest physiotherapy**: Manual percussion and vibration; postural drainage (modified for ventilator patient)\n- **Nebulizers**: Albuterol Q4-6H for bronchodilation; normal saline nebulizers to thin secretions\n- **Incentive spirometry**: Not applicable (no voluntary breathing effort)\n- **Pneumonia prevention bundle**:\n  - Head of bed elevation \u226530 degrees\n  - Oral care with chlorhexidine BID\n  - Aspiration precautions (swallow evaluation before any oral intake)\n  - Flu and pneumococcal vaccination\n  - Hand hygiene and respiratory precautions for all staff/visitors\n\n**4. Nutritional Support**:\n- PEG tube feeds for primary nutrition (swallowing evaluation pending)\n- High-protein formula to support respiratory muscle maintenance and prevent catabolism\n- Monitor for aspiration; advance to oral diet only after formal swallow study with SLP\n\n**5. Additional Pulmonary Considerations**:\n- **Glossopharyngeal breathing (GPB/frog breathing)**: Teach this technique -- patient uses tongue and pharyngeal muscles to gulp air into lungs; can provide emergency ventilation if ventilator disconnects; provides psychological sense of control\n- **Abdominal binder**: Improves diaphragm mechanics if any residual function is present; also helps with venous return and orthostatic management\n- **Respiratory muscle training**: If any accessory muscle function returns, progressive strengthening with resistive devices\n- **Pulmonary function monitoring**: Serial FVC, NIF (negative inspiratory force), and peak cough flow measurements")
B.aUm=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","As discharge planning begins, the team must decide between discharge to a long-term acute care facility (LTAC), a skilled nursing facility (SNF) with ventilator capability, or home with a home ventilator program. Each option has significant implications for insurance coverage, caregiver burden, and quality of life.")
B.b0J=new A.c("+ Compare discharge disposition options for a ventilator-dependent SCI patient and outline the requirements for safe home ventilator discharge.","**Discharge Disposition Options**:\n\n**Option 1: Long-Term Acute Care (LTAC)**:\n- Appropriate for: Patients still requiring active medical management, ongoing ventilator weaning attempts, or treatment of complications\n- Pros: 24/7 nursing with ventilator expertise; respiratory therapist on-site; physician availability\n- Cons: Institutional setting; limited rehabilitation services; not a long-term solution; insurance coverage typically limited (average stay 25-30 days)\n- Role: Transitional step if patient is not yet stable enough for home or SNF\n\n**Option 2: Skilled Nursing Facility (Ventilator Unit)**:\n- Appropriate for: Patients who are medically stable but cannot be safely managed at home\n- Pros: 24/7 nursing care; ventilator management; structured environment\n- Cons: Institutional living (devastating for a 22-year-old); limited autonomy; high infection risk; high depression rates; very limited community access; **significantly worse quality of life outcomes** compared to home discharge\n- Cost: $200,000-$400,000+ per year\n\n**Option 3: Home Ventilator Program** (PREFERRED if feasible):\n- Appropriate for: Medically stable, motivated patient with adequate caregiver support and funding\n- Pros: Best quality of life outcomes; greatest autonomy; lowest infection rates; ability to return to school/work; family proximity\n- Cons: Requires extensive caregiver training, home modification, equipment, and 24/7 skilled attendant care; high upfront costs; caregiver burnout risk\n- Cost: $150,000-$300,000+ per year (but often less than institutional care)\n\n**Requirements for Safe Home Ventilator Discharge**:\n\n**1. Equipment**:\n- **Two ventilators** (primary and backup -- mandatory for life-sustaining ventilation)\n- **Backup generator** (power failure plan)\n- **Portable suction machine** (battery-operated)\n- **CoughAssist device (MI-E)**\n- **Pulse oximeter with alarms**\n- **Manual resuscitation bag (Ambu bag)** at bedside at all times\n- **Tracheostomy supplies**: replacement trach tubes (same size and one size smaller), ties, suction catheters, saline\n- **Hospital bed** with pressure redistribution mattress\n- **Power wheelchair** with ventilator tray, head/chin control\n- **Patient lift system** (ceiling track or portable Hoyer)\n- **Environmental control unit (ECU)**: Allows patient to control lights, TV, phone, computer, door locks via head/mouth controls\n\n**2. Caregiver Training** (minimum 2-3 weeks in-hospital training):\n- Trained caregivers must be available **24/7** (minimum 2 trained caregivers)\n- Training includes: ventilator operation and troubleshooting, tracheostomy care and emergency change, suctioning, CoughAssist use, emergency manual ventilation (Ambu bag), skin care and turning, bowel/bladder program, medication administration, recognizing autonomic dysreflexia\n- Competency must be documented and assessed before discharge\n\n**3. Home Modifications**:\n- Wheelchair-accessible entrance (ramp), doorways (36+ inches), bathroom\n- Adequate electrical capacity for ventilator, suction, and medical equipment\n- Backup power source (generator or battery backup)\n- Notify local power company and fire department of ventilator-dependent resident\n\n**4. Insurance and Funding**:\n- Private insurance, Medicaid waiver programs (home and community-based services), or combination\n- Prior authorization for: home ventilator, backup ventilator, nursing hours (typically 16-24 hours/day of skilled nursing), respiratory therapy visits, DME\n- Medicaid HCBS waiver may cover attendant care services that allow home living\n- Vocational rehabilitation referral for return-to-school support (assistive technology, note-takers, accommodations)\n\n**5. Emergency Plan**:\n- Written emergency protocol posted at bedside and on refrigerator\n- Local EMS notified of ventilator-dependent patient at address\n- Emergency contact chain (caregiver, family, physician, 911)\n- Regular emergency drills with caregivers (ventilator failure, trach decannulation, power outage)")
B.b24=new A.c(u.j,'During a family meeting, the patient\'s parents and the rehabilitation team are discussing goals of care. The mother states emphatically: "We want everything done. Every treatment, every surgery, every option -- we want him to have the best chance." Meanwhile, the night nurse privately reports that the patient has been mouthing "let me go" and "I don\'t want to live like this" on two occasions when family is not present.')
B.aQR=new A.c("+ Navigate this ethical dilemma involving patient autonomy, surrogate decision-making, and end-of-life wishes in a young ventilator-dependent patient.","**Ethical Framework and Approach**:\n\nThis is a conflict between **patient autonomy** (the patient's own expressed wishes) and **surrogate/family wishes**. The foundational bioethical principle is that a **competent patient's wishes take precedence** over family wishes, even when the patient is ventilator-dependent.\n\n**Step 1: Assess Decision-Making Capacity**:\nBefore acting on either the family's or patient's statements, I must formally assess the patient's decision-making capacity:\n- Does he understand his medical condition and prognosis?\n- Does he understand the consequences of withdrawing ventilatory support (death)?\n- Can he reason about the decision and weigh risks/benefits?\n- Is his decision consistent over time (not just a momentary expression of distress)?\n- Is he free from undue influence, delirium, or untreated depression?\n\n**Critical consideration**: This patient is 22 years old and only 3 weeks post-injury. **Acute adjustment disorder and depression are extremely common** in early SCI and can profoundly affect decision-making. His statements may reflect transient despair rather than a settled, informed wish to die.\n\n**Step 2: Private Conversation with the Patient**:\n\"I want to talk to you about something important, just the two of us. The nurses have shared with me that you have expressed some thoughts about not wanting to continue living. I take that very seriously, and I want to understand what you are feeling.\n\nCan you help me understand what you mean when you say you want to let go? Are you in pain? Are you afraid of what your life will look like? Are you feeling hopeless right now?\n\nI am asking because I want to help you, not because I am going to dismiss what you are saying. Your feelings are valid. At the same time, I want to make sure you have all the information you need and that we are addressing any suffering that we can address.\n\nIt is also important for you to know that it has only been 3 weeks. Many people with injuries like yours have told me they felt exactly the way you feel right now, and that those feelings changed significantly over time -- especially once they were out of the hospital, back in their community, and connected to others living with similar injuries.\n\nI am not going to make any promises about what your life will look like. But I will promise you this: I will be honest with you, I will treat your pain and suffering, and I will respect your decisions. I would like to connect you with a psychologist who specializes in adjustment after spinal cord injury, and also with a peer mentor -- someone close to your age who has a high cervical injury and is living in the community.\"\n\n**Step 3: Address Family Separately**:\n\"I understand how much you love your son and want to fight for him. That is exactly what a parent should do. I need to share with you that he has expressed some distress and ambivalence about his situation. This is very common in early spinal cord injury and does not necessarily mean he has made a decision about his care.\n\nWhat I want to do is make sure we are treating any depression and giving him time, support, and information before any major decisions are made. I also want to involve our ethics team and a psychologist.\n\nI need you to understand something important: if your son is competent to make decisions, his wishes ultimately guide his care, even if they differ from yours. That is his legal and ethical right. But right now, we are not at a point where anyone needs to make that decision. We are at a point where we need to support him, treat his suffering, and give him time.\"\n\n**Step 4: Action Plan**:\n1. **Psychiatry consultation**: Formal capacity assessment and evaluation for major depression, adjustment disorder, and acute suicidality\n2. **Psychology referral**: Begin regular sessions focused on coping, grief processing, and adjustment\n3. **Peer mentoring**: Connect patient with a high-cervical SCI peer who lives in the community (this is one of the most powerful interventions for early SCI despair)\n4. **Ethics committee consultation**: Document the situation and engage the institutional ethics committee proactively\n5. **Palliative care consultation**: Not for end-of-life planning necessarily, but for holistic symptom management, goals-of-care conversations, and supporting the patient and family through this process\n6. **Time**: Do not rush any decisions. Research shows that most SCI patients who express early desire to die do NOT maintain that wish long-term when adequately supported. Longitudinal studies show quality of life in high cervical SCI is rated surprisingly high by patients living in the community.\n7. **Documentation**: Document all conversations, capacity assessments, and the patient's expressed wishes carefully in the medical record\n8. **Reassess regularly**: Revisit goals of care at regular intervals as the patient stabilizes emotionally and progresses through rehabilitation")
B.aDM=s([B.aYH,B.aT7,B.b1b,B.aVi,B.aRX,B.aZO,B.aYI,B.aUm,B.b0J,B.b24,B.aQR],t.E)
B.QN=new A.a5("sci-high-cervical-ventilator","High Cervical SCI with Ventilator Dependence (C3 ASIA A)",B.aDM,!1)
B.b_2=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'You are called emergently to the inpatient rehabilitation unit where a 30-year-old male with T4 ASIA A spinal cord injury (3 months post motor vehicle collision) is found with a blood pressure of 210/120 mmHg, pounding headache, profuse diaphoresis above the nipple line, and facial flushing. His heart rate is 48 bpm. He is anxious and says, "This has happened before \u2014 it\'s my blood thing, dysreflexia. Please help me, nobody here knows what to do." The nursing staff appears uncertain about the protocol. His baseline blood pressure is 90/60 mmHg.')
B.b2x=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **SCI Details**: T4 ASIA A complete paraplegia from MVC 3 months ago. Burst fracture T4 with posterior spinal cord compression. Underwent T3-T5 posterior fusion with decompression. Neurologically stable since surgery.\n- **Prior AD Episodes**: Two prior episodes during acute hospitalization \u2014 both triggered by urinary retention when catheter was kinked. Resolved with catheter unkinking and bladder drainage. BP peaked at 190/110 during worst episode.\n- **Current Medications**: Oxybutynin 5 mg TID (neurogenic bladder), docusate 100 mg BID, senna 8.6 mg QHS, gabapentin 300 mg TID, enoxaparin 40 mg SQ daily.\n- **Bladder Program**: Intermittent catheterization every 4-6 hours. Last catheterized 5 hours ago by nursing. Patient reports feeling 'full' (he has some visceral awareness).\n- **Bowel Program**: Digital stimulation bowel program every other day. Last bowel program was yesterday \u2014 patient states it was 'incomplete' and he still feels bloated.\n- **Skin**: Stage 2 pressure injury on left ischial tuberosity \u2014 being managed with foam dressing, changed daily.\n- **Timeline**: Symptoms began approximately 20 minutes ago. Patient asked nursing to check his catheter, but the day nurse is new to the SCI unit and was not sure what autonomic dysreflexia was. She took his vital signs, saw the high BP, and called a rapid response instead of initiating the AD protocol.\n- **PMH**: No other medical history. No hypertension prior to SCI.\n- **Social**: Former electrician. Single. Lives alone in an apartment (pre-injury). Parents are involved and supportive. Has been engaged and motivated in rehabilitation.")
B.aXL=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP **210/120 mmHg** (baseline 90/60), HR **48 bpm** (bradycardic), RR 22, SpO2 98% on RA, Temp 37.0C.\n\n**General**: Anxious male sitting upright in wheelchair. **Profuse diaphoresis above the nipple line**. Face and neck flushed. Goosebumps (piloerection) visible on arms. Skin below nipple line is pale, cool, and dry.\n\n**HEENT**: Pounding headache (patient reports 9/10). No nasal congestion currently. Pupils equal and reactive.\n\n**Cardiovascular**: Bradycardic at 48 bpm. Regular rhythm. No murmurs. Peripheral pulses strong.\n\n**Abdomen**: Distended in the suprapubic region \u2014 palpable bladder to the umbilicus. Mild diffuse distension beyond the bladder. No rebound or guarding (though abdominal exam is unreliable below T4 level).\n\n**Neurological**: T4 ASIA A \u2014 no motor or sensory function below T4. Upper extremities 5/5 throughout. Intact sensation above T4 (confirms level). No sensation below T4 to pinprick or light touch.\n\n**Skin**: Stage 2 pressure injury on left ischial tuberosity \u2014 foam dressing in place, appears clean and intact from visual inspection (do not remove dressing during crisis). No other skin breakdown noted on rapid inspection.\n\n**Rectal**: Not performed yet during this acute event \u2014 will need to check for fecal impaction after bladder is addressed (rectal exam can itself worsen AD if performed before applying anesthetic lubricant).\n\n**Foley/Catheter**: Patient is on intermittent catheterization program \u2014 no indwelling catheter currently in place. Bladder is markedly distended.")
B.aPN=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","You are managing an acute autonomic dysreflexia emergency. Describe the pathophysiology, systematic identification and elimination of triggers, and pharmacologic management if the trigger is not immediately correctable.")
B.aVS=new A.c("+ Explain the pathophysiology of autonomic dysreflexia, the systematic approach to identifying and eliminating the trigger, and pharmacologic treatment options.","**Pathophysiology of Autonomic Dysreflexia (AD)**:\n- Occurs in SCI at **T6 and above** (this patient is T4 \u2014 at risk).\n- A **noxious stimulus below the level of injury** triggers a massive, unmodulated sympathetic response through intact thoracolumbar sympathetic outflow (T5-L2).\n- The sympathetic surge causes widespread **vasoconstriction below the injury level**, resulting in severe hypertension.\n- The brain detects the hypertension via intact baroreceptors (carotid sinus, aortic arch) and attempts compensation via **parasympathetic activation** (vagus nerve) \u2014 causing bradycardia, vasodilation, and sweating **above** the injury level.\n- However, the descending inhibitory signals from the brainstem **cannot pass the spinal cord lesion** to reach the thoracolumbar sympathetics, so the vasoconstriction below the lesion persists.\n- Result: **Severe hypertension with reflex bradycardia**. The BP can exceed 250/130 mmHg \u2014 risking **hemorrhagic stroke, seizure, retinal hemorrhage, myocardial ischemia, and death**.\n\n**Immediate Management Algorithm**:\n\n**Step 1: Sit the patient upright** (if not already)\n- Lowers BP by approximately 15-20 mmHg through orthostatic effect\n- Lower legs off bed if possible\n- Loosen any tight clothing, abdominal binders, compression stockings\n\n**Step 2: Monitor BP every 2-3 minutes continuously**\n\n**Step 3: Systematic trigger identification and elimination (in order of frequency)**:\n\n**Urological (80-85% of causes)**:\n1. Check for bladder distension \u2014 this patient has a palpable bladder to umbilicus\n2. If indwelling catheter: check for kinks, blockage, or displacement. Irrigate gently. Replace if not draining.\n3. If no catheter (this patient): **Catheterize immediately**. Use **2% lidocaine jelly** instilled into urethra 2-3 minutes before catheterization (urethral instrumentation without anesthetic can worsen AD). Drain slowly \u2014 rapid decompression can cause hypotension.\n4. If catheterization fails: suprapubic aspiration or consider UTI as trigger.\n\n**Gastrointestinal (next most common)**:\n5. If bladder drainage does not resolve AD: check for **fecal impaction**. Apply **2% lidocaine gel to the rectum and perianal area**, wait 2-3 minutes, then perform a gentle digital rectal exam. If impaction is present, gently disimpact.\n6. Assess for acute abdomen \u2014 appendicitis, cholecystitis, bowel obstruction.\n\n**Integumentary**:\n7. Check skin thoroughly \u2014 ingrown toenails, pressure injuries, burns, tight clothing, restrictive dressings, sharp objects in shoe or wheelchair cushion.\n\n**Other triggers**: Urinary tract infection, epididymitis, kidney stones, fractures below the injury level, heterotopic ossification, DVT, labor (in females), surgical procedures.\n\n**Step 4: Pharmacologic management** (if BP remains >150 systolic after trigger elimination, or while searching for trigger):\n\n1. **Nitropaste (2% nitroglycerin ointment)**: Apply 1-inch strip above the injury level. Rapid onset (minutes). Easy to remove if hypotension occurs. First-line pharmacologic agent.\n2. **Nifedipine immediate-release 10 mg**: Bite and swallow (NOT sublingual \u2014 absorption is unpredictable). Onset 10-15 minutes. Caution: can cause precipitous hypotension in patients with baseline low BP.\n3. **Captopril 25 mg sublingual**: Alternative to nifedipine. Onset 15-30 minutes.\n4. **Hydralazine 10-20 mg IV**: For severe refractory AD. Onset 10-20 minutes.\n5. **Labetalol 20 mg IV**: Caution \u2014 can worsen bradycardia (use only if HR is not already significantly low).\n\n**For This Patient**:\n- He is already upright in wheelchair\n- Bladder distension is the most likely trigger (last catheterized 5 hours ago, palpable bladder)\n- **Immediate action**: Catheterize with lidocaine jelly, drain slowly\n- Apply nitropaste while preparing for catheterization\n- BP should drop within minutes of bladder drainage\n- If BP remains elevated after drainage, check for fecal impaction (he reported incomplete bowel program)\n- Monitor for 2 hours after resolution (triggers can recur)")
B.aUS=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","After treating the acute episode, develop a prevention strategy for recurrent autonomic dysreflexia and a long-term management plan for this patient.")
B.aWX=new A.c("+ Outline a comprehensive AD prevention strategy and long-term management plan.","**AD Prevention Strategy**:\n\n**1. Bladder Management Optimization**:\n- Review IC schedule \u2014 currently every 4-6 hours. Tighten to **every 4 hours** maximum. If volumes consistently exceed 400-500 mL, increase frequency.\n- Fluid intake monitoring \u2014 avoid bolus intake; spread fluids throughout the day\n- Oxybutynin dose may need adjustment \u2014 assess for adequate detrusor relaxation. Consider urodynamic study to evaluate bladder compliance and capacity.\n- If recurrent AD from bladder causes, consider **botulinum toxin injection** into detrusor muscle or switching to indwelling suprapubic catheter.\n- Monitor for UTIs \u2014 a common AD trigger. Prophylactic measures: adequate hydration, proper catheterization technique, consider cranberry products.\n\n**2. Bowel Management Optimization**:\n- Current bowel program appears inadequate (patient reports 'incomplete' evacuation). Increase stimulant laxative (senna). Consider adding polyethylene glycol (MiraLAX) daily.\n- Ensure bowel program is performed on a consistent schedule.\n- Pre-treat with rectal lidocaine gel before digital stimulation to minimize AD risk during bowel care.\n- Diet: adequate fiber (25-30 g/day) and fluid intake.\n\n**3. Skin Management**:\n- Stage 2 pressure injury is a potential AD trigger. Accelerate wound healing: offload with pressure relief cushion, optimize nutrition (protein 1.2 g/kg/day, vitamin C, zinc), consider wound care nurse consultation.\n- Prevent new pressure injuries: pressure relief every 15-30 minutes in wheelchair, inspect skin twice daily, proper cushion (ROHO or Jay)\n\n**4. Patient Education and AD Emergency Kit**:\n- Provide the patient with a **wallet card** describing AD: symptoms, BP threshold, emergency management steps.\n- **AD emergency kit**: Nitropaste (2% NTG ointment), BP cuff, and written instructions for emergency responders.\n- Educate that AD is a **medical emergency** \u2014 if symptoms occur and trigger cannot be found/resolved, go to ED immediately.\n- Educate about triggers: tight clothing, sunburn, ingrown toenails, sexual activity (ejaculation can trigger AD), temperature extremes.\n\n**5. Outpatient Monitoring**:\n- **24-hour ambulatory BP monitoring**: Consider to detect silent AD episodes (can occur without headache or symptoms).\n- Follow-up urodynamics at 6 months and annually.\n- Annual renal ultrasound (monitor for hydronephrosis from neurogenic bladder).\n\n**6. Pre-procedural Planning**:\n- Any procedure below the injury level (cystoscopy, urodynamics, bowel procedures, wound debridement) carries AD risk.\n- Pre-treat with nifedipine 10 mg PO 30 minutes before elective procedures.\n- Have nitropaste and IV antihypertensives immediately available.\n- For surgery: spinal or epidural anesthesia can prevent AD during procedures below the lesion level.")
B.aN_=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The nursing staff on the rehabilitation unit was unfamiliar with the autonomic dysreflexia protocol, leading to a delay in treatment. How do you address this systems-level failure?")
B.aSo=new A.c("+ What systems-level changes should be implemented to ensure all rehabilitation unit staff can recognize and manage autonomic dysreflexia promptly?","**Systems-Level Improvements**:\n\n**1. Unit-Wide AD Protocol**:\n- Develop a **standardized AD management protocol** posted at every nursing station and in every patient room on the SCI unit.\n- Include a clear, visual **algorithm flowchart**: symptoms -> sit up -> check bladder -> catheterize -> check bowel -> pharmacologic treatment -> call physician.\n- Laminated copies at bedside of every SCI patient with injury at T6 or above.\n- **AD orders entered automatically** in the EHR for all qualifying patients (standing order for nitropaste, catheterization supplies, lidocaine jelly at bedside).\n\n**2. Mandatory Staff Education**:\n- **Annual competency requirement**: All nursing staff, nursing aides, and therapy staff on the SCI/rehabilitation unit must complete AD recognition and management training.\n- Simulation exercises: Quarterly mock AD drills (similar to code blue drills) where staff practice the protocol in real-time.\n- Include AD in orientation for all new nurses to the unit \u2014 it should be covered in the first week.\n\n**3. Patient-Specific Communication**:\n- **EHR banner alert**: Any patient at risk for AD (SCI T6 and above) should have a visible alert in the medical record that triggers when any provider opens the chart.\n- **Bedside signage**: Signs at the head of the bed for at-risk patients: 'AUTONOMIC DYSREFLEXIA RISK \u2014 SEE PROTOCOL.'\n- Handoff communication: AD risk and individual triggers must be included in every nursing shift handoff (SBAR format).\n\n**4. Equipment Accessibility**:\n- **AD kit at bedside**: Pre-assembled kit containing: BP cuff, catheterization supplies, 2% lidocaine jelly, nitropaste (2% NTG ointment), protocol card. Checked and restocked daily.\n- Ensure IC supplies are always stocked in the patient's room \u2014 delay in locating a catheter kit cost valuable time.\n\n**5. Rapid Response Team Education**:\n- The rapid response team that arrived may not have been familiar with AD. Educate rapid response/code teams about AD \u2014 it can be mismanaged if treated as a standard hypertensive emergency (e.g., beta-blockers can worsen bradycardia).\n- Consider adding AD protocol to rapid response team training curriculum.\n\n**6. Quality Improvement**:\n- Track AD events as a **patient safety metric** on the SCI unit.\n- Time from symptom onset to BP normalization should be monitored.\n- Root cause analysis for this event: identify the breakdowns (new nurse unfamiliarity, no standing orders, no bedside supplies) and implement corrective actions.\n- Report as a near-miss or adverse event in the hospital safety reporting system.")
B.aZl=new A.c(u.j,"After the AD episode is resolved and BP normalizes, the patient is visibly angry. He says: \"This is the third time this has happened, and every time I have to tell the staff what to do. That nurse didn't even know what dysreflexia was. I could have had a stroke and died because she didn't know her job. I want to file a complaint. This place is supposed to be a rehab hospital for spinal cord injuries, and the nurses don't even know the most basic emergency.\" His anger is justified.")
B.b0L=new A.c("+ How do you respond to a patient whose justified anger about a near-miss event reflects genuine systems failures, while maintaining the therapeutic alliance and addressing the safety concern?",'**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Do not get defensive. His anger is justified \u2014 validate it fully.**\n"You are right. I am not going to argue with you or make excuses, because you are right. You should never have to be the one teaching your nurse about autonomic dysreflexia on a spinal cord injury rehabilitation unit. That is our job, and today we fell short."\n\n**2. Acknowledge the danger he was in.**\n"Your blood pressure was 210 over 120. Your baseline is 90 over 60. That is a dangerous level \u2014 you were at risk for a stroke, a seizure, or worse. The fact that it took 20 minutes before anyone started treating the actual problem is not acceptable. I understand your anger, and frankly, I share it."\n\n**3. Validate that his self-advocacy may have saved his life.**\n"I also want to say something about what you did. You recognized your own symptoms, you knew the diagnosis, and you told the staff what needed to happen. That kind of knowledge about your own body is what kept you safe today. You should not have had to do that, but the fact that you could may have prevented a catastrophic outcome."\n\n**4. Take responsibility as the physician leader of the team.**\n"As your physiatrist and the physician responsible for this unit, I take responsibility for this. It is my job to make sure every member of this team knows how to manage the conditions our patients have. Today showed me that we have a gap, and I am going to fix it."\n\n**5. Describe specific actions you will take \u2014 not vague promises.**\n"Here is what is going to change, starting today. First, I am placing an AD emergency kit at your bedside right now \u2014 it will have a blood pressure cuff, catheter supplies, lidocaine jelly, and nitropaste. It is not leaving your room. Second, I am writing standing orders in your chart so that any nurse can initiate the AD protocol immediately without waiting for a physician. Third, I am requesting a meeting with nursing leadership this week to implement mandatory AD training for every staff member on this unit. And fourth, I am going to make sure your nurse today \u2014 and I am not criticizing her personally, because she was not given the training she needed \u2014 receives one-on-one education about AD before she cares for another SCI patient."\n\n**6. Support his right to file a complaint.**\n"You said you want to file a complaint, and you absolutely should. Patient safety reports are how hospitals identify and fix problems. I will help you do that if you want, or I can connect you with patient relations. I will also be filing an internal safety report on my end. Your experience today should lead to changes that protect you and every future SCI patient on this unit."\n\n**7. Rebuild trust.**\n"I know today shook your trust in us, and I understand that. I cannot undo what happened, but I can promise you that I heard you, I believe you, and I am going to act on it. You deserve to feel safe in this hospital, and I am going to work to earn that trust back."\n\n**Key Principles**: Validated justified anger without defensiveness. Acknowledged the genuine safety threat. Took institutional responsibility. Offered specific, actionable solutions (not platitudes). Supported the patient\'s right to formal complaint. Separated individual nurse education gaps from personal blame (systems failure, not character failure). Committed to follow-through.')
B.aFu=s([B.b_2,B.b2x,B.aXL,B.aPN,B.aVS,B.aUS,B.aWX,B.aN_,B.aSo,B.aZl,B.b0L],t.E)
B.Qa=new A.a5("sci-autonomic-dysreflexia","Autonomic Dysreflexia Emergency",B.aFu,!1)
B.aS2=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 62-year-old right-handed male is admitted to the inpatient rehabilitation unit 12 days after a fall down 4 stairs at home, resulting in a hyperextension injury to the cervical spine. MRI revealed C4-5 severe spinal stenosis with cord signal change consistent with central cord syndrome. He was managed conservatively (no surgery) with a cervical collar and observation. He presents with bilateral upper extremity weakness greater than lower extremity weakness, more pronounced distally, with hand function severely affected. He can walk with a rolling walker but cannot button his shirt, hold a fork, or write. He is a retired mechanic who was the primary caregiver for his wife, who was diagnosed with metastatic breast cancer 6 months ago and is currently undergoing chemotherapy. He is tearful and says, "She takes care of me now instead of me taking care of her. I am supposed to be her rock."')
B.aOg=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **Injury Mechanism**: Fall down 4 stairs at home. Struck chin on railing, causing forceful cervical hyperextension. No loss of consciousness. Immediate bilateral hand and arm weakness with preserved ability to walk.\n- **Imaging**: MRI C-spine: severe degenerative stenosis at C4-5 (AP canal diameter 7 mm), disc osteophyte complex, ligamentum flavum hypertrophy. T2 hyperintense signal within the central cord at C4-5 level (no hemorrhage on gradient echo \u2014 pure edema pattern, favorable prognostic sign). CT: no fracture.\n- **Hospital Course**: Managed conservatively per neurosurgery \u2014 rigid cervical collar x 6 weeks. No surgical decompression recommended at this time (debatable \u2014 will discuss). Neurological exam has been slowly improving since admission.\n- **Current Functional Status**: Ambulatory with rolling walker on level surfaces. Requires supervision on stairs. Bilateral hand weakness severely limits ADLs \u2014 cannot button clothes, manipulate zippers, hold utensils, write, or open containers. Can feed himself with built-up utensil handles. Bladder: mild urgency and frequency, no retention (post-void residual 60 mL). Bowel: continent, mild constipation.\n- **Pain**: Neck pain 4/10 (muscular). Bilateral burning dysesthesia in hands 5/10 \u2014 worse at night.\n- **PMH**: Cervical spondylosis (known for years \u2014 never symptomatic until now), hypertension, type 2 diabetes (A1c 7.8%), benign prostatic hyperplasia, mild osteoarthritis bilateral knees.\n- **Wife's Condition**: Stage IV breast cancer with bone metastases (spine, pelvis, ribs). Currently receiving palliative chemotherapy (capecitabine). Requires assistance with medication management, meal preparation, transportation to oncology appointments, and emotional support. Previously, the patient performed all these tasks.\n- **Social**: Retired auto mechanic, age 62. Married 35 years. One adult son lives 3 hours away. Medicare insurance. Modest fixed income (Social Security). Single-story home with 4 exterior steps (where the fall occurred).\n- **Medications**: Lisinopril 10 mg daily, metformin 500 mg BID, tamsulosin 0.4 mg QHS, gabapentin 300 mg TID (started for hand dysesthesia), acetaminophen 1000 mg Q8H PRN.")
B.aOr=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 142/88 mmHg, HR 74 bpm, RR 14, SpO2 97% on RA, Temp 36.7C, BMI 28.\n\n**General**: Alert, cooperative male in rigid cervical collar. Tearful intermittently. Makes eye contact. Hands rest in lap \u2014 does not gesture.\n\n**Neurological**:\n- **Mental Status**: Alert, oriented x4. Affect sad but appropriate. Cognition grossly intact.\n- **Motor (Upper Extremity)**: **Deltoids** 4/5 bilaterally. **Biceps** 4/5 bilaterally. **Wrist extensors** 3+/5 bilaterally. **Finger extensors** 2/5 bilaterally. **Finger flexors** 2/5 bilaterally. **Intrinsic hand muscles** (interossei, lumbricals): **1/5 bilaterally** \u2014 essentially no functional hand grip. **Triceps** 3/5 bilaterally.\n- **Motor (Lower Extremity)**: **Hip flexors** 4+/5 bilaterally. **Knee extensors** 4+/5 bilaterally. **Ankle dorsiflexors** 4/5 bilaterally. **Ankle plantarflexors** 4+/5 bilaterally. \u2014 Consistent with central cord pattern: UE >> LE weakness, distal > proximal.\n- **Sensory**: Diminished pinprick C6-T1 bilaterally (cape-like distribution). Light touch preserved throughout. Proprioception intact. Burning dysesthesia in bilateral hands (C7-C8 distribution).\n- **Reflexes**: Upper extremity hyperreflexia (3+). Lower extremity hyperreflexia (3+). Bilateral Hoffman signs positive. No Babinski. No clonus.\n- **Tone**: Mild spasticity bilateral upper extremities (MAS 1+ at elbows and wrists).\n\n**Functional Assessment**:\n- **Grip strength**: R 2 kg, L 1.5 kg (normal for age ~40 kg). Essentially non-functional grip.\n- **Pinch strength**: Unable to perform lateral or tip-to-tip pinch.\n- **Fine motor**: Cannot pick up a penny, button a shirt, turn a key, or hold a pen. Can grasp large objects (water bottle) with bilateral palmar grasp using wrist extension assist.\n- **Gait**: Ambulatory with rolling walker. Mildly spastic gait pattern. Walks 200 feet before fatigue. Independent on level surfaces. Requires supervision and railing on stairs.\n\n**Musculoskeletal**: No shoulder pathology. Cervical collar limits exam. Bilateral knee crepitus with ROM.")
B.aWN=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","Discuss the pathophysiology of central cord syndrome, its typical recovery pattern, and the controversy surrounding early surgical decompression versus conservative management.")
B.aSZ=new A.c("+ Explain the pathophysiology and recovery prognosis of central cord syndrome, and discuss the surgical versus conservative management debate.","**Pathophysiology of Central Cord Syndrome**:\n- **Most common incomplete SCI pattern** (accounting for ~50% of incomplete injuries).\n- Classically occurs in **older adults with pre-existing cervical stenosis** after a **hyperextension** injury (fall, MVC). The narrowed canal provides no buffer \u2014 even mild hyperextension compresses the cord between the anterior disc-osteophyte complex and the posterior ligamentum flavum.\n- **Somatotopic organization of the corticospinal tract**: The cervical (upper extremity) fibers run centrally/medially in the lateral corticospinal tract, while the sacral and lumbar (lower extremity) fibers run laterally/peripherally. Central cord edema/hemorrhage affects the medial (UE) fibers first and most severely, sparing the peripheral (LE) fibers.\n- **Additional factors**: Central gray matter (anterior horn cells at C5-T1) is highly metabolically active and vulnerable to ischemia. Damage to anterior horn cells at the level of injury causes **LMN weakness in the hands** (flaccid, atrophic), while corticospinal tract damage above causes **UMN signs below** (spasticity, hyperreflexia).\n- This patient demonstrates the classic pattern: severely weak hands (combined LMN + UMN), moderately weak proximal arms, relatively preserved legs.\n\n**Recovery Pattern and Prognosis**:\n- Central cord syndrome has the **best prognosis among SCI syndromes** for functional recovery.\n- **Typical recovery sequence**: (1) Lower extremity strength recovers first; (2) Bladder function recovers next; (3) Proximal upper extremity strength follows; (4) **Hand function recovers last and often incompletely**.\n- **Prognostic factors**: (a) Younger age \u2014 better recovery; (b) Absence of hemorrhage on MRI (this patient has edema only \u2014 favorable); (c) Initial severity \u2014 less initial impairment predicts better outcome; (d) Early improvement \u2014 improvement in first 2 weeks is a positive sign.\n- This patient: age 62 (moderate prognosis), edema-only pattern (favorable), ambulatory within 2 weeks (favorable), hands 1-2/5 (concerning \u2014 hand recovery may be incomplete).\n- **Expected outcome**: Will likely regain functional ambulation without assistive device. Hand function expected to improve but may have permanent fine motor deficits \u2014 may never fully regain intrinsic hand function.\n\n**Surgical vs. Conservative Management Controversy**:\n\n**Arguments for Early Surgical Decompression**:\n- STASCIS trial and subsequent studies suggest that **early surgery (<24 hours)** in cervical SCI is associated with better neurological outcomes (>=2 grade AIS improvement).\n- The OSCIS registry data specifically in central cord syndrome showed improved outcomes with early surgery.\n- Ongoing compression from stenosis may limit recovery potential.\n- **AO Spine guidelines (2017)**: Recommend consideration of early surgical decompression for central cord syndrome with continued compression, particularly if no improvement within 2-4 weeks.\n\n**Arguments for Conservative Management (this patient's current approach)**:\n- Historic teaching: Many central cord syndrome patients improve significantly with conservative management alone.\n- Surgical risks in elderly patients: anesthesia, perioperative complications, adjacent segment disease.\n- The natural history often includes significant spontaneous improvement.\n- Some surgeons advocate **delayed surgery** (6-8 weeks) if recovery plateaus \u2014 allows initial edema to resolve.\n\n**Current Consensus**: Trending toward **early surgical decompression** for central cord syndrome with documented cord compression and persistent or worsening deficits. This patient should be re-evaluated by neurosurgery if hand function does not improve over the next 2-4 weeks, as delayed decompression may still provide benefit.")
B.aRn=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Develop a rehabilitation plan focused on maximizing upper extremity and hand function recovery while addressing the unique caregiving crisis this couple faces.")
B.aP2=new A.c("+ Outline a comprehensive rehabilitation plan addressing hand function, the cervical collar, and the dual-patient caregiving challenge.","**Hand and Upper Extremity Rehabilitation**:\n\n**1. OT-Directed Hand Rehabilitation (Priority #1)**:\n- **Active-assisted ROM**: Therapist assists finger flexion/extension through available range. Prevent contractures \u2014 tendons and joints stiffen quickly without active use.\n- **Electrical stimulation**: Neuromuscular electrical stimulation (NMES) to finger extensors and intrinsic hand muscles. Facilitates motor recruitment and prevents atrophy during recovery. FES (functional electrical stimulation) combined with task practice.\n- **Progressive strengthening**: As motor return occurs \u2014 theraputty, grip exercises, pinch exercises. Grade resistance carefully.\n- **Fine motor retraining**: Button boards, peg boards, coin manipulation, key turning \u2014 practice the exact tasks he needs for ADLs and caregiving.\n- **Adaptive equipment (bridge strategy)**: While hand function recovers \u2014 built-up utensils, universal cuff, rocker knife, button hook, zipper pull, key holder, jar opener, adaptive writing device. These are not permanent \u2014 they maintain independence during recovery.\n- **Splinting**: Resting hand splints at night (prevent intrinsic-minus clawing posture). Functional wrist splint during the day if wrist extensors are weak (tenodesis grasp augmentation).\n\n**2. Spasticity Management**:\n- Bilateral UE spasticity (MAS 1+) \u2014 currently mild. Monitor for progression.\n- Stretching program: Wrist and finger flexor/extensor stretches 3x daily.\n- If spasticity impairs function or worsens: baclofen 5 mg TID, titrate. Tizanidine at bedtime if spasticity disrupts sleep.\n- Targeted botulinum toxin if focal spasticity impairs hand function (e.g., flexor spasticity preventing grasp release).\n\n**3. Neuropathic Pain Management**:\n- Gabapentin currently 300 mg TID \u2014 may increase to 600 mg TID if tolerated.\n- Add topical lidocaine 5% patches to bilateral hands at night (when dysesthesia is worst).\n- Consider duloxetine 30-60 mg for dual pain/mood benefit.\n\n**4. Cervical Collar Management**:\n- Currently in rigid collar x 6 weeks per neurosurgery. At 6 weeks: flexion/extension X-rays to confirm stability.\n- Begin cervical isometrics (pain-free) while in collar. After collar removal: progressive cervical ROM and strengthening program with PT.\n- Educate about fall prevention \u2014 another hyperextension injury with this degree of stenosis could be catastrophic.\n\n**5. Gait and Mobility**:\n- Continue ambulation with rolling walker. Progress to straight cane as balance improves.\n- Lower extremity strengthening \u2014 will likely recover to independent ambulation.\n- Stair training with railing \u2014 critical for home access (4 exterior steps).\n- Home modification: handrails on both sides of exterior steps, grab bars in bathroom.\n\n**6. Addressing the Caregiving Crisis**:\n- **Social work consultation immediately**: This couple has a dual-patient situation with no local support.\n- **Home health services**: Arrange home health aide for minimum 4 hours daily initially \u2014 assist with meal preparation, medication management for both the patient and his wife, light housekeeping.\n- **Community resources**: Meals on Wheels, volunteer driver programs for wife's oncology appointments, Area Agency on Aging services.\n- **Son involvement**: Family meeting with son (by phone/video) to discuss increased involvement \u2014 even temporary relocation or periodic visits.\n- **Occupational therapy focus on caregiving tasks**: Train the patient specifically in tasks needed to care for his wife \u2014 medication pill box setup (adaptive pill organizer), meal preparation using adaptive equipment, dialing phone for emergencies.")
B.aTC=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","This patient and his wife are both medically complex, on a fixed income with Medicare, and have limited social support. Describe the systems-level coordination needed.")
B.aOb=new A.c("+ How do you coordinate care for a dual-patient household where the rehabilitation patient is also the primary caregiver for a spouse with terminal cancer?","**Systems-Level Care Coordination**:\n\n**1. Dual-Patient Care Conference**:\n- Organize a multidisciplinary meeting that includes: PM&R physician, oncologist (or oncology nurse navigator), social worker, OT, PT, discharge planner, palliative care team, and the patient and wife (by video if she cannot attend).\n- Goal: Align the rehabilitation plan with the oncology treatment schedule and establish a sustainable home care model.\n\n**2. Medicare Coverage and Benefits**:\n- **Inpatient rehabilitation**: Covered under Medicare Part A. Ensure medical necessity documentation supports continued stay.\n- **Home health services post-discharge**: Medicare Part A covers skilled nursing, PT, OT, SLP, home health aide under a plan of care. Requires homebound status and skilled need. He qualifies.\n- **Durable medical equipment**: Rolling walker, adaptive equipment, grab bars \u2014 covered under Medicare Part B (80% after deductible).\n- **Wife's home health**: She may independently qualify for home health services under her own Medicare/insurance based on her cancer diagnosis and functional needs.\n\n**3. Palliative Care Integration**:\n- His wife has stage IV cancer \u2014 palliative care should be actively involved.\n- If her condition deteriorates, hospice services provide comprehensive in-home support (nursing, aide, medications, equipment, respite care) that would also indirectly support him.\n- Discuss advance care planning for BOTH patients \u2014 ensure both have healthcare proxies and documented wishes.\n\n**4. Caregiver Role Transition**:\n- He was her caregiver. Now he needs a caregiver. This role reversal causes psychological distress for both.\n- As his hand function recovers, gradually restore his caregiving role \u2014 this is therapeutic for his rehabilitation AND his psychological well-being.\n- OT should specifically train caregiving tasks as rehabilitation goals (functional, meaningful activities).\n\n**5. Financial Assistance**:\n- Social Security benefits review \u2014 is he receiving maximum benefit? Does his wife qualify for disability?\n- Pharmaceutical assistance programs for wife's chemotherapy medications if copays are burdensome.\n- Local charitable organizations, faith community support (many churches have meal trains, transportation volunteers).\n- Area Agency on Aging: respite care, home modification assistance, caregiver support services.\n\n**6. Fall Prevention**:\n- The original injury was from a fall on home stairs. Prevent recurrence.\n- Home safety evaluation by OT before discharge.\n- Install bilateral handrails on exterior steps, non-slip treads, adequate lighting.\n- Remove tripping hazards inside home.\n- Consider a personal emergency response system (PERS) \u2014 medical alert pendant \u2014 for both patients.")
B.aQZ=new A.c(u.j,'During a therapy session, the patient breaks down and says: "My wife has cancer. She is dying. And now I cannot even hold her hand properly. I was supposed to take care of her through this. Instead, she has to get up and make me breakfast because I cannot hold a pan. She should be resting, and instead she is taking care of me. I feel like I have failed the one person who matters most to me."')
B.b1w=new A.c("+ How do you address this patient's guilt about needing care from his terminally ill wife and his grief over the role reversal?",'**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Stop the therapy session. This moment is more important than exercises.**\n[Sit down at his level. Give him your full attention.]\n"I want to hear this. This is important. Tell me more about what you are feeling."\n\n**2. Validate the grief and guilt without rushing to fix it.**\n"Thirty-five years of marriage, and for the last 6 months, you have been her protector through the worst diagnosis a person can face. You drove her to chemo. You made her meals. You held her when she was afraid. And then in one second, on 4 stairs, that role was taken from you. Of course you feel like you have failed her. That feeling makes complete sense. But I want to gently push back on the word \'failed.\' "\n\n**3. Reframe the narrative \u2014 he has not failed; he has been injured.**\n"You did not fail your wife. You fell. You have an injury to your spinal cord. This is not a character flaw or a moral failing \u2014 it is a medical condition, and it is one that improves. You are not the same person today that you were 12 days ago, and you will not be the same person 3 months from now. Your hands are going to get stronger. You are going to hold that pan again. You are going to hold her hand again."\n\n**4. Acknowledge the specific pain of not being able to physically care for her.**\n"I think the hardest part of this for you is not the buttons or the forks \u2014 it is that you cannot do the things that show your wife you love her. Cooking for her, driving her to appointments, being the strong one. Those are acts of love, and when you cannot do them, it feels like you have lost the way you express love. Am I hearing that right?"\n[Wait for response]\n\n**5. Help him see that caregiving is not the only form of love.**\n"Your wife is getting up to make you breakfast even though she is on chemotherapy. She is not doing that because she has to \u2014 she is doing that because she loves you. And I suspect that if I asked her, she would tell me that taking care of you does not feel like a burden. It feels like love. The same way it felt like love when you were taking care of her. You have not stopped being her rock. The rock just needs a little support right now."\n\n**6. Connect his rehabilitation to his caregiving goal.**\n"Here is what I want you to understand about your rehabilitation. Every exercise you do with your hands is not just about holding a fork or buttoning a shirt. It is about getting back to the man who takes care of his wife. That is your goal, and it is the most meaningful rehabilitation goal I have heard. We are going to build your therapy program around exactly that \u2014 the tasks you need to do to care for her."\n\n**7. Offer concrete help for the immediate crisis.**\n"In the meantime, while your hands are recovering, we are going to get help at home. A home health aide can take over the cooking and the tasks your wife should not be doing right now. We are going to get meals delivered. We are going to arrange transportation for her oncology appointments. Your wife should be resting, and you are right about that \u2014 so let us make sure she can. You are not failing her by accepting help. You are loving her by making sure she is taken care of even when you cannot do it yourself."\n\n**8. Offer to include his wife in a conversation.**\n"Would it help if we talked to your wife together? I suspect she has feelings about this too, and it might help both of you to hear each other\'s perspective with some support in the room."\n\n**Key Principles**: Recognized that the emotional crisis is as important as the physical rehabilitation. Validated guilt without reinforcing it. Reframed the injury as medical, not moral. Acknowledged that caregiving is an expression of love and its loss is a form of grief. Connected rehabilitation goals to his deepest motivation. Offered practical help to relieve the immediate burden. Invited dyadic communication.')
B.aDU=s([B.aS2,B.aOg,B.aOr,B.aWN,B.aSZ,B.aRn,B.aP2,B.aTC,B.aOb,B.aQZ,B.b1w],t.E)
B.Q2=new A.a5("sci-central-cord-syndrome","Central Cord Syndrome",B.aDU,!1)
B.b3y=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 45-year-old female is referred to outpatient PM&R 6 weeks after emergent L4-5 laminectomy and discectomy for cauda equina syndrome. She presented to the ED with acute low back pain radiating bilaterally into her legs, saddle anesthesia, and urinary retention (post-void residual 800 mL). Surgery was performed within 12 hours of symptom onset. She now has residual bilateral lower extremity weakness (left > right), persistent saddle hypoesthesia, neurogenic bladder requiring intermittent catheterization, and neurogenic bowel. She walks with bilateral Lofstrand crutches. She is a 45-year-old married mother of two teenage children and works as a high school physical education teacher. She appears embarrassed and hesitant during the interview, and near the end of the visit, quietly asks: "Doctor, will I ever be able to... be intimate with my husband again? We have not been able to since this happened, and he says it is fine, but I can see it is not."')
B.aTK=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **Presentation**: 6 weeks ago, acute onset severe low back pain while lifting weights at the gym. Progressive bilateral leg weakness over 12 hours. Noticed inability to urinate and numbness in the perineal region. Husband drove her to ED.\n- **Imaging**: MRI L-spine: massive L4-5 central disc herniation with severe cauda equina compression. Near-complete obliteration of the thecal sac.\n- **Surgical Course**: Emergent L4-5 laminectomy and discectomy performed 12 hours after symptom onset. Intraoperatively, the cauda equina was severely compressed with edematous nerve roots. Surgeon noted the roots were discolored but not transected.\n- **Post-operative Recovery**: Partial improvement. Leg strength improved from initial 1-2/5 bilaterally to current levels. Saddle numbness improved but persists. Urinary retention persists \u2014 started on clean intermittent catheterization (CIC) every 4-6 hours. Neurogenic bowel \u2014 requires scheduled bowel program.\n- **Current Functional Status**: Ambulatory with bilateral Lofstrand crutches. Can walk 500 feet before fatigue. Requires AFO on left (foot drop). Stairs with railing and supervision. Drives (automatic transmission, left foot weak but right foot functional for gas/brake). Independent in ADLs except needs extra time for toileting/catheterization.\n- **Bladder**: Areflexic bladder (LMN pattern). No sensation of fullness. CIC every 4-6 hours. Post-void residual >500 mL without catheterization. On tamsulosin 0.4 mg daily.\n- **Bowel**: Areflexic bowel (LMN pattern). No rectal sensation. Requires manual evacuation every other day. Uses stool softeners and bulk-forming agents.\n- **Sexual Function**: Complete loss of genital sensation. No clitoral sensation. No orgasm since injury. Reports vaginal dryness. Has not attempted intercourse since injury.\n- **PMH**: Previously healthy. Regular gym-goer. No prior back problems.\n- **Psychosocial**: Married 20 years. Two children ages 15 and 13. PE teacher \u2014 physically demanding job. Enjoys running, hiking, CrossFit (pre-injury). PHQ-9 score 14 (moderate depression).\n- **Medications**: Gabapentin 300 mg TID, tamsulosin 0.4 mg daily, docusate 100 mg BID, psyllium fiber daily, acetaminophen PRN.")
B.aSc=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 118/72 mmHg, HR 68 bpm, RR 14, SpO2 99% on RA, Temp 36.6C, BMI 24.\n\n**General**: Athletic-appearing female. Walks into clinic with bilateral Lofstrand crutches and left AFO. Appears anxious and avoids eye contact when discussing bladder/bowel/sexual topics.\n\n**Neurological**:\n- **Motor**: **Right**: Hip flexion 4/5, knee extension 4+/5, ankle dorsiflexion 4/5, ankle plantarflexion 4/5, great toe extension 4/5, hip abduction 4/5. **Left**: Hip flexion 3+/5, knee extension 4/5, ankle dorsiflexion 2/5, ankle plantarflexion 3/5, great toe extension 1/5, hip abduction 3/5. Bilateral gluteal atrophy noted.\n- **Sensory**: Saddle hypoesthesia \u2014 diminished sensation S2-S5 bilaterally (perineum, perianal area, posterior thighs). Can detect deep pressure but not light touch or pinprick in the saddle region. Diminished sensation L5-S1 distribution left foot.\n- **Reflexes**: Bilateral ankle reflexes absent. Bilateral knee reflexes 1+. Bulbocavernosus reflex absent. Anal wink absent.\n- **Tone**: Flaccid \u2014 no spasticity (LMN injury pattern).\n\n**Rectal Exam**: Decreased perianal sensation. Decreased resting anal sphincter tone. No voluntary anal contraction.\n\n**Gait**: Ambulates with bilateral Lofstrand crutches. Left foot drop \u2014 compensated by AFO. Trendelenburg sign left (weak hip abductors). Gait endurance limited to approximately 500 feet.\n\n**Musculoskeletal**: Lumbar incision well-healed. Mild lumbar tenderness. Full lumbar ROM except for guarding at end-range flexion. Negative bilateral straight-leg raise.")
B.aUa=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","Discuss the pathophysiology of cauda equina syndrome, the significance of timing to surgery, and the prognosis for bladder and sexual function recovery.")
B.b2j=new A.c("+ Explain the pathophysiology, surgical timing implications, and prognosis for neurogenic bladder and sexual function in cauda equina syndrome.","**Pathophysiology of Cauda Equina Syndrome (CES)**:\n- The cauda equina consists of the nerve roots L2-S5 descending through the lumbar spinal canal below the conus medullaris (which terminates at approximately L1-L2).\n- **CES results from compression of these peripheral nerve roots**, most commonly by a massive central disc herniation (as in this case), but also by tumor, abscess, hematoma, or spinal stenosis.\n- Because these are **peripheral nerves (LMN)**, the injury pattern differs fundamentally from spinal cord injury (UMN): **flaccid weakness, areflexic bladder and bowel, absent bulbocavernosus reflex, no spasticity**.\n- The sacral nerve roots (S2-S4) are particularly vulnerable due to their central position in the thecal sac \u2014 they are compressed first and most severely by central disc herniations. This explains why bladder, bowel, and sexual function are affected disproportionately.\n\n**Surgical Timing and Prognosis**:\n- CES is a **surgical emergency**. The debate centers on the optimal window:\n- **<24 hours**: Traditional teaching. Meta-analyses show improved outcomes compared to >48 hours.\n- **<48 hours**: Some studies show equivalent outcomes to <24 hours, but most experts recommend surgery as soon as possible.\n- **This patient**: Surgery at 12 hours \u2014 within the optimal window, but she presented with **CES with retention (CES-R)** \u2014 the most severe form, which carries a worse prognosis than CES with incomplete symptoms.\n\n**Bladder Recovery Prognosis**:\n- Bladder function recovers in approximately **40-70% of CES patients** operated on within 24-48 hours.\n- CES-R (this patient's presentation) has **worse bladder recovery** than CES-incomplete (30-50% recovery rate).\n- Recovery can continue for **up to 2-3 years** as peripheral nerves regenerate (approximately 1 mm/day).\n- **Unfavorable prognostic signs**: Absent bulbocavernosus reflex at 6 weeks (this patient), bilateral saddle anesthesia, absent anal contraction.\n- **This patient**: At 6 weeks post-surgery, still requiring CIC with no sensation of bladder fullness \u2014 likely to have permanent neurogenic bladder, though some improvement may still occur.\n\n**Sexual Function Prognosis**:\n- S2-S4 nerve roots mediate genital sensation (pudendal nerve), clitoral engorgement (parasympathetic), vaginal lubrication, and orgasm.\n- **Women with CES**: Loss of genital sensation is the primary sexual complaint. Orgasmic dysfunction is common. Vaginal dryness due to impaired parasympathetic innervation.\n- Recovery parallels bladder recovery \u2014 if S2-S4 roots recover, sexual sensation and function may improve.\n- Unlike male sexual dysfunction post-CES (where PDE5 inhibitors can assist with erectile function), **female sexual dysfunction after CES has fewer pharmacologic treatment options**, making counseling and adaptive strategies particularly important.\n- Prognosis for this patient: Guarded. Some return of sensation is possible over 1-2 years but complete recovery is unlikely given the severity of initial presentation.")
B.aYD=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Develop a comprehensive rehabilitation plan addressing this patient's motor recovery, neurogenic bladder and bowel, and return to work as a PE teacher.")
B.aSg=new A.c("+ Outline a rehabilitation plan for cauda equina syndrome with neurogenic bladder and bowel, including vocational considerations.","**Comprehensive Rehabilitation Plan**:\n\n**1. Lower Extremity Strengthening and Gait Training**:\n- **Progressive strengthening**: Peripheral nerve recovery may continue for 18-24 months. Focus on weak muscle groups \u2014 left ankle dorsiflexion (2/5), left great toe extension (1/5), bilateral hip abductors and gluteals.\n- **Electrical stimulation**: NMES to left tibialis anterior and peroneal muscles to maintain muscle bulk and facilitate nerve recovery.\n- **AFO optimization**: Current left AFO for foot drop. As dorsiflexion recovers, may transition to a less rigid design or discontinue.\n- **Gait retraining**: Progress from bilateral Lofstrand crutches to single crutch, then cane, then independent as strength allows. Address left Trendelenburg with hip abductor strengthening and gait cueing.\n- **Endurance training**: Stationary cycling (low resistance), aquatic therapy, progressive walking program.\n- **Balance training**: Single-leg stance, dynamic balance activities, perturbation training.\n\n**2. Neurogenic Bladder Management**:\n- **Continue CIC every 4-6 hours**: Teach optimal technique \u2014 clean (not sterile) technique, adequate lubrication, comfortable positioning. She must become fully independent and efficient.\n- **Urodynamics**: Order at 3 months post-injury to characterize bladder dynamics \u2014 capacity, compliance, detrusor function, leak point pressure. This guides long-term management.\n- **Monitor renal function**: Annual renal ultrasound to screen for hydronephrosis. Annual serum creatinine.\n- **UTI prevention**: Adequate hydration (2L/day). Proper CIC technique. Do NOT use prophylactic antibiotics routinely. Treat symptomatic UTIs only (not asymptomatic bacteriuria, which is common with CIC).\n- **Medications**: Continue tamsulosin (reduces urethral resistance, facilitating drainage). If urodynamics show poor compliance, consider anticholinergics cautiously.\n- **Workplace considerations**: She needs private bathroom access and scheduled breaks for CIC as a PE teacher.\n\n**3. Neurogenic Bowel Management**:\n- **LMN bowel pattern**: Areflexic, flaccid external sphincter. Risk of passive stool leakage rather than constipation.\n- **Program**: Scheduled manual evacuation every other day at the same time. Valsalva maneuver or abdominal massage to assist. Maintain stool consistency with fiber and stool softeners.\n- **Diet**: High fiber (25-30 g/day) with adequate water. Avoid gas-producing foods.\n- **Continence strategies**: Predictable evacuation schedule to prevent accidents. May need incontinence pad as backup for PE teaching (physically active, cannot always get to bathroom quickly).\n\n**4. Pain Management**:\n- Gabapentin 300 mg TID \u2014 optimize if neuropathic pain persists. Consider pregabalin for better tolerability.\n- Core stabilization exercises for residual low back pain.\n- Avoid heavy lifting permanently \u2014 re-herniation risk.\n\n**5. Return to Work as PE Teacher**:\n- **Functional capacity evaluation**: At 3-4 months, assess her ability to demonstrate exercises, supervise activities, manage a gym/field environment.\n- **Accommodations needed**: Access to private bathroom for CIC (scheduled breaks), modified duty initially (no heavy lifting or demonstration of high-impact activities), possible aide assistance.\n- **Gradual return**: Part-time schedule initially, progressing to full-time.\n- **ADA protections**: Her condition qualifies as a disability under the ADA. Employer must provide reasonable accommodations.\n- **Job modification**: May need to shift from full physical demonstration to coaching/instruction model. Can use student demonstrators for exercises she cannot perform.")
B.aQA=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","This patient requires long-term management of a neurogenic bladder and bowel with lifelong follow-up. How do you organize this longitudinal care?")
B.aYx=new A.c("+ Describe the long-term follow-up protocol and care coordination for a patient with neurogenic bladder and bowel from cauda equina syndrome.","**Longitudinal Follow-Up Protocol**:\n\n**Year 1 (Active Recovery Phase)**:\n- PM&R follow-up every 4-6 weeks for first 6 months, then every 3 months\n- PT/OT 2-3x weekly initially, tapering as strength plateaus\n- **Urodynamics** at 3 months and 12 months\n- Renal ultrasound at 12 months\n- EMG/NCS at 6 months if considering prognosis for ongoing recovery (can assess for reinnervation potentials)\n- Neuropsychological screening if depression persists\n- Vocational counseling for return to work planning\n\n**Annual Ongoing (Lifelong)**:\n- **Renal ultrasound annually**: Screen for hydronephrosis, stones\n- **Serum creatinine and urinalysis annually**: Monitor renal function\n- **Urodynamics every 2-3 years** or if symptoms change\n- **PM&R annual visit**: Reassess function, equipment needs, pain, bowel/bladder program effectiveness\n- **Urology co-management**: For complex bladder issues, recurrent UTIs, stones\n- **Gynecology**: Annual exam \u2014 discuss sexual function, pelvic floor health\n\n**Complication Surveillance**:\n- **UTIs**: Common with CIC. Educate on symptoms. Treat promptly.\n- **Renal deterioration**: The primary long-term threat. High-pressure bladder can cause reflux and kidney damage. Urodynamics detect this.\n- **Skin breakdown**: Saddle hypoesthesia means she may not feel pressure or irritation in the perineal area. Educate on skin inspection.\n- **Falls**: Ongoing fall risk from leg weakness. Annual reassessment of ambulatory aids and home safety.\n- **Psychological**: Depression, body image issues, sexual dysfunction \u2014 screen annually with PHQ-9. Offer counseling and support group referral.\n\n**Care Coordination Structure**:\n- **Physiatrist as medical home**: Coordinates all specialists, manages the rehabilitation plan, serves as longitudinal provider.\n- **Urology**: Bladder management, urodynamics, stone management.\n- **Colorectal/GI**: If bowel program becomes ineffective or incontinence worsens.\n- **Psychology/Psychiatry**: For depression and sexual health counseling.\n- **PCP**: Ongoing general health maintenance. Ensure PCP is educated about neurogenic bladder/bowel management.\n- **Employer/Occupational health**: ADA accommodations, return-to-work planning.")
B.aQz=new A.c(u.j,'Near the end of the visit, the patient hesitantly asks: "Doctor, will I ever be able to... be intimate with my husband again? I have no feeling down there. We have not been together since this happened. He says it does not matter, but I can tell he is frustrated. I feel broken. I used to feel attractive and confident, and now I wear pads and catheterize myself. How can anyone want this?"')
B.aXm=new A.c("+ How do you sensitively counsel this patient about sexual function after cauda equina syndrome, addressing her physical concerns, body image, and relationship dynamics?",'**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Thank her for raising the topic \u2014 it takes courage.**\n"I am really glad you brought this up. Sexual health is a vital part of your recovery, and it is something many patients are afraid to ask about. You are not broken for feeling the way you do, and this is exactly the kind of thing I am here to help with."\n\n**2. Normalize the experience.**\n"What you are going through \u2014 the loss of sensation, the changes in your body, the catheterization, the pads \u2014 these are medical consequences of your nerve injury. They do not define your attractiveness or your worth as a partner. But I understand why they feel that way right now, because our culture ties so much of a woman\'s identity to her body, and your body has changed in ways you did not choose."\n\n**3. Provide honest, specific medical information about sexual function after CES.**\n"Let me explain what is happening physically and what may change. The nerves that provide sensation to your genital area \u2014 the S2, S3, and S4 nerve roots \u2014 were compressed during your injury. Right now, those nerves are damaged, which is why you have lost sensation. Because these are peripheral nerves, not spinal cord, they have the ability to regenerate. Recovery is slow \u2014 it can take 12 to 24 months \u2014 and it may be partial rather than complete. Some women in your situation regain enough sensation for pleasurable sexual experiences, including orgasm, though it may feel different than before. I cannot promise full recovery, but I can tell you that this is not necessarily permanent."\n\n**4. Discuss specific adaptations and strategies.**\n"In the meantime, there are things we can do:\n- **Lubrication**: Vaginal dryness from nerve damage is treatable with water-based lubricants. This makes intercourse comfortable even without full sensation.\n- **Sensory mapping**: You may discover that areas above your level of numbness \u2014 your inner thighs, lower abdomen, breasts \u2014 have become more sensitive. Many women find that with exploration, they develop new pathways to arousal and pleasure.\n- **Vibration**: Higher-intensity vibration devices can sometimes stimulate deeper nerve pathways that are still intact. This is a legitimate medical recommendation.\n- **Timing with catheterization**: Catheterize before intimacy to empty the bladder. This reduces anxiety about leakage and allows you to focus on the experience.\n- **Positioning**: Depending on your leg strength and balance, certain positions may be more comfortable. An OT or physical therapist who specializes in sexual health can help with this.\n- **Emotional intimacy**: Physical closeness, massage, skin-to-skin contact \u2014 these are powerful and do not require genital sensation. They can rebuild the intimate connection while physical recovery continues."\n\n**5. Address the relationship directly.**\n"You mentioned your husband says it does not matter, but you sense his frustration. Can I suggest something? The frustration you are sensing may not be about sex specifically. It may be about not knowing how to help you, or feeling like he cannot express his own grief about these changes without adding to your burden. Consider having an honest conversation with him \u2014 and I can recommend a couples therapist who specializes in sexual health after injury or illness. This is not about \'fixing\' your marriage \u2014 it is about giving you both a safe space to talk about something that is hard to talk about."\n\n**6. Address her body image directly.**\n"You said, \'How can anyone want this?\' I want to challenge that. You are a 45-year-old woman who survived a surgical emergency, who is walking again 6 weeks later, who is fighting to get back to teaching. The catheterization and the pads are tools \u2014 like crutches. They are part of your recovery, not part of your identity. Your husband married you for who you are, not for the absence of medical devices. But I understand that feeling attractive starts from the inside, and right now, you are grieving the body you had. That grief is real and it deserves attention."\n\n**7. Offer referrals.**\n"I would like to refer you to a pelvic floor physical therapist \u2014 they specialize in exactly this type of rehabilitation and can help with both continence and sexual function. I can also recommend a psychologist who works with women experiencing sexual health changes after injury. And if you are open to it, couples counseling could be very helpful for you and your husband."\n\n**Key Principles**: Created a safe, non-judgmental space. Provided specific medical facts about prognosis. Offered concrete strategies (not just reassurance). Addressed body image as a legitimate psychological concern. Recommended professional resources (pelvic floor PT, sex therapist, couples counseling). Normalized the conversation about sex as a medical topic.')
B.aDH=s([B.b3y,B.aTK,B.aSc,B.aUa,B.b2j,B.aYD,B.aSg,B.aQA,B.aYx,B.aQz,B.aXm],t.E)
B.Qo=new A.a5("sci-cauda-equina-syndrome","Cauda Equina Syndrome",B.aDH,!1)
B.aPm=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 28-year-old female with T8 ASIA A complete paraplegia (4 years post motor vehicle collision) presents to your PM&R clinic at 20 weeks gestation for pregnancy management counseling. She was referred by her OB/GYN who has limited experience managing pregnancy in SCI patients. She is excited about the pregnancy \u2014 it was planned. She uses a manual wheelchair independently and is fully independent in all ADLs. She lives with her husband in an accessible ground-floor apartment. Her mother-in-law, who is also present at the visit, has been vocal about her disapproval. As the patient describes her birth plan, the mother-in-law interjects: "I love my daughter-in-law, but I have to say what everyone is thinking. How is a woman in a wheelchair going to take care of a baby? It is not fair to the child. She cannot even walk. What if there is an emergency?"')
B.aYG=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **SCI History**: T8 ASIA A complete paraplegia from MVC 4 years ago. Surgically stabilized T7-T9 posterior fusion. Neurologically stable. Independent manual wheelchair user. Drives with hand controls.\n- **Pregnancy Details**: G1P0 at 20 weeks. Planned pregnancy with fertility specialist consultation pre-conception. IVF was not needed \u2014 conceived naturally. Prenatal labs and anatomy scan normal. Fetus growing appropriately.\n- **Current SCI Management**: Neurogenic bladder \u2014 CIC every 4-6 hours (will need to increase frequency as uterus grows). Neurogenic bowel \u2014 digital stimulation program every other day. Skin \u2014 no history of pressure injuries. No spasticity issues.\n- **SCI-Specific Pregnancy Concerns**:\n  - **Autonomic dysreflexia**: T8 level \u2014 at risk for AD (T6 and above is classic, but some T7-T8 patients can experience AD). Uterine contractions are a potent trigger for AD.\n  - **UTIs**: Increased risk during pregnancy due to urinary stasis, increased progesterone (ureteral dilation), and CIC.\n  - **Pressure injuries**: Weight gain and altered sitting posture change pressure distribution. Wheelchair cushion may need reassessment.\n  - **DVT**: Already elevated risk from SCI; pregnancy further increases risk.\n  - **Respiratory**: T8 \u2014 respiratory function generally adequate but growing uterus may compromise diaphragmatic excursion.\n  - **Labor detection**: She will not feel uterine contractions below T8. Must rely on other signs (uterine palpation, abdominal tightening, increased spasticity, AD symptoms).\n- **Pre-pregnancy planning**: Met with maternal-fetal medicine (MFM) specialist and PM&R before conceiving. Stopped oxybutynin (pregnancy category C). Adjusted bowel medications. Folic acid supplementation started pre-conception.\n- **Psychosocial**: Married 3 years. Husband is supportive and enthusiastic about the baby. Patient works part-time as a graphic designer (remote). Mother-in-law lives nearby and has been involved but increasingly critical about the pregnancy.\n- **PMH**: No other medical history besides SCI.\n- **Medications**: Prenatal vitamins, iron supplement, docusate 100 mg BID. Stopped gabapentin and oxybutynin pre-conception.")
B.b1m=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 102/64 mmHg (normal for SCI \u2014 low baseline), HR 82 bpm, RR 16, SpO2 99% on RA, Temp 36.8C, Weight 62 kg (pre-pregnancy 56 kg), BMI 23.\n\n**General**: Alert, pleasant female in manual wheelchair. Visible 20-week pregnant abdomen. Appears healthy and well-nourished. Good affect. Mother-in-law sitting with arms crossed.\n\n**Neurological**: T8 ASIA A \u2014 unchanged from baseline. Upper extremities 5/5 throughout. No motor or sensory function below T8. Intact sensation above T8.\n\n**Obstetric**: Fundal height appropriate for 20 weeks (at umbilicus). Fetal heart tones 148 bpm by Doppler. No vaginal bleeding or discharge.\n\n**Musculoskeletal**: Good upper body strength. Able to perform depression transfers independently. Shoulders \u2014 no impingement signs. Sitting balance excellent.\n\n**Skin**: No pressure injuries. Wheelchair cushion assessed \u2014 ROHO cushion with appropriate inflation. Ischial skin intact bilaterally. Skin over sacrum intact.\n\n**Respiratory**: Chest expansion symmetric. Forced vital capacity not measured today but was 85% predicted 6 months ago.\n\n**Functional**: Independent in all wheelchair-level ADLs. Demonstrates ability to transfer to exam table independently. Reports increasing difficulty with floor-to-wheelchair transfers as abdomen grows.")
B.aTr=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","Discuss the SCI-specific medical risks during pregnancy and labor, particularly autonomic dysreflexia during labor, and the multidisciplinary management approach.")
B.aOS=new A.c("+ What are the SCI-specific risks during pregnancy and labor, and how do you manage autonomic dysreflexia risk during delivery?","**SCI-Specific Pregnancy Risks**:\n\n**1. Autonomic Dysreflexia During Labor (Most Dangerous Risk)**:\n- **Any patient with SCI at T6 or above is at risk**. T7-T8 patients may also experience AD, particularly with the intense noxious stimulus of uterine contractions.\n- Uterine contractions are the strongest visceral stimulus \u2014 can trigger severe, life-threatening AD with BP >200 mmHg.\n- **The patient will not feel contractions** (below T8), so AD symptoms (headache, flushing, sweating above the level) may be the **first sign of labor**.\n- **Management**: Epidural anesthesia is the treatment of choice \u2014 it blocks the afferent limb of the AD reflex. Must be placed early \u2014 ideally at the onset of labor, before contractions intensify.\n- **Anesthesia team must be aware**: Pre-operative planning with the obstetric anesthesiologist well before delivery. Document the AD risk prominently in the chart.\n- **If AD occurs before epidural placement**: Sit upright, immediate BP monitoring. Nitroglycerin paste or IV hydralazine for acute management. Short-acting antihypertensives \u2014 avoid long-acting agents that could cause sustained hypotension (her baseline BP is already 102/64).\n\n**2. Unrecognized Preterm Labor**:\n- She will not feel contractions. Preterm labor could progress undetected.\n- **Monitoring**: Teach self-palpation of uterine tightening. Monitor for increased spasticity (a sign of noxious stimuli below the level). Weekly cervical checks starting at 28 weeks. Home uterine activity monitoring may be considered.\n- Educate on non-pain signs of labor: bloody show, rupture of membranes, rhythmic abdominal tightening, back spasms above T8, AD symptoms.\n\n**3. Urinary Tract Infections**:\n- UTI risk is markedly increased: SCI baseline risk + pregnancy-related urinary stasis + ureteral dilation + growing uterus compressing bladder.\n- **CIC frequency**: Increase as pregnancy progresses. May need every 3-4 hours in the third trimester as bladder capacity decreases.\n- **Screening**: Monthly urine cultures. Treat asymptomatic bacteriuria in pregnancy (unlike in non-pregnant SCI patients).\n- UTIs can trigger preterm labor and AD.\n\n**4. Venous Thromboembolism**:\n- SCI + pregnancy = very high DVT/PE risk.\n- **Prophylaxis**: Low-molecular-weight heparin (enoxaparin) throughout pregnancy. Compression stockings. Frequent position changes.\n- Plan anticoagulation bridge for delivery.\n\n**5. Pressure Injury Risk**:\n- Weight gain (expected 25-35 lbs) alters pressure distribution in wheelchair.\n- Reassess wheelchair cushion quarterly during pregnancy.\n- Increase weight shifts (every 15 minutes).\n- If bed rest is ever required, aggressive pressure prevention protocol.\n\n**6. Respiratory**:\n- T8 \u2014 respiratory muscles mostly intact (diaphragm C3-5, intercostals). However, growing uterus elevates diaphragm.\n- Monitor FVC if respiratory symptoms develop. Incentive spirometry.\n\n**Delivery Planning**:\n- **Route**: Vaginal delivery is possible and preferred in most SCI patients. Cesarean section reserved for obstetric indications.\n- She will not feel contractions \u2014 will need coaching for pushing. Abdominal muscle paralysis (below T8) means she may have weak expulsive effort. Assisted vaginal delivery (vacuum or forceps) may be needed.\n- **Epidural anesthesia placed early** \u2014 for AD prevention, not pain control.\n- **Deliver at a tertiary center** with MFM, obstetric anesthesia, and PM&R available.\n- **Post-partum**: Monitor for AD during uterine involution. DVT prophylaxis. Reassess bladder and bowel programs. Breastfeeding is possible and encouraged \u2014 no contraindication from SCI.")
B.aOA=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Develop a trimester-by-trimester management plan and coordinate the multidisciplinary team for this patient's pregnancy.")
B.aUy=new A.c("+ Outline a comprehensive pregnancy management plan for a T8 ASIA A patient, including parenting preparation and postpartum rehabilitation.","**Trimester-by-Trimester Plan**:\n\n**Second Trimester (Current \u2014 20-28 weeks)**:\n- **MFM co-management**: Monthly visits with maternal-fetal medicine. Growth scans.\n- **PM&R visits**: Every 4-6 weeks to monitor SCI-specific issues.\n- **Bladder**: Increase CIC frequency from every 6 hours to every 4-5 hours as bladder capacity decreases. Monthly urine cultures.\n- **DVT prophylaxis**: Start enoxaparin 40 mg SQ daily (if not already). Compression stockings.\n- **Wheelchair/seating**: OT reassessment of cushion pressure mapping with increased weight. May need cushion adjustment or upgrade.\n- **Equipment planning**: Research adaptive parenting equipment \u2014 begin ordering now. Include: side-opening crib, wheelchair-accessible changing table, baby carrier adapted for wheelchair use (e.g., wrap-style carriers that secure baby to wheelchair user's chest).\n- **Anesthesia consultation**: Meet with obstetric anesthesiologist to discuss epidural planning for delivery.\n\n**Third Trimester (28-40 weeks)**:\n- **Increase monitoring**: MFM visits every 2 weeks from 28 weeks, weekly from 36 weeks.\n- **Cervical checks**: Weekly starting at 28 weeks to detect silent cervical change.\n- **Uterine monitoring**: Teach self-palpation. Consider home uterine activity monitoring.\n- **CIC**: Increase to every 3-4 hours. May become technically more difficult \u2014 larger abdomen makes positioning challenging. OT can help problem-solve technique.\n- **Respiratory**: Monitor for dyspnea. FVC if symptomatic.\n- **Wheelchair mobility**: May need tilt-in-space wheelchair temporarily if sitting tolerance decreases.\n- **Hospital bag**: Include catheterization supplies, bowel program supplies, wheelchair cushion, and a written care plan for nursing staff unfamiliar with SCI management.\n- **Preterm labor education**: Review signs \u2014 uterine tightening, increased spasticity, AD symptoms, back tightness, rupture of membranes.\n\n**Delivery Plan**:\n- Scheduled admission at 39 weeks (or earlier if indicated).\n- Epidural placed at onset of active labor or scheduled induction.\n- Accessible delivery room \u2014 wheelchair access, accessible bed.\n- PM&R physician available for AD management.\n- Neonatology on standby (standard).\n- Written AD protocol posted at bedside.\n\n**Postpartum Plan**:\n- **Bladder**: Reassess as uterus involutes \u2014 CIC schedule may return to pre-pregnancy frequency over 6-8 weeks.\n- **Bowel**: Restart bowel program medications that were held during pregnancy.\n- **DVT prophylaxis**: Continue for 6 weeks postpartum.\n- **Breastfeeding**: Encourage \u2014 milk production is hormonally driven and unaffected by SCI. Lactation consultant referral. Positioning assistance \u2014 side-lying or adapted wheelchair positioning.\n- **Mood**: Screen for postpartum depression at 2, 6, and 12 weeks. Baseline depression risk is elevated in SCI patients.\n- **Parenting OT**: Occupational therapy focused specifically on adaptive parenting \u2014 bathing the baby, changing diapers from wheelchair, wheelchair transfers while holding baby, car seat management.\n- **Home health**: Home nursing visit in first week to assess safety and provide support.\n\n**Adaptive Parenting Equipment**:\n- **Side-opening crib**: Allows wheelchair-level access without reaching over a railing.\n- **Wheelchair-height changing table**: Or use bed/couch for changing.\n- **Baby carrier**: Front-carry system that secures baby to chest with straps around wheelchair back. Keeps hands free for wheelchair propulsion.\n- **Stroller attachment**: Devices that attach a baby stroller to the wheelchair frame.\n- **Bath support**: Baby bathtub placed on a table at wheelchair height.")
B.aWe=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","This pregnancy requires coordination between PM&R, MFM, obstetric anesthesia, OT, and primary OB. Many providers have limited experience with SCI and pregnancy. How do you organize this care?")
B.aQp=new A.c("+ How do you ensure coordinated, expert care when most team members have never managed a pregnant SCI patient?","**Care Coordination Strategy**:\n\n**1. Designate PM&R as Co-Lead**:\n- The physiatrist should serve as **co-lead with MFM** throughout the pregnancy.\n- PM&R provides the SCI expertise that OB/MFM lacks; MFM provides the obstetric expertise that PM&R lacks.\n- Every decision should be made collaboratively.\n\n**2. Create a Shared Written Care Plan**:\n- A single document accessible to all providers (in the EHR as a shared care plan or problem list note) containing:\n  - SCI level, ASIA classification, baseline BP and vitals\n  - AD risk, triggers, and acute management protocol\n  - Bladder program (CIC schedule, supplies)\n  - Bowel program\n  - Skin management (pressure prevention)\n  - DVT prophylaxis plan\n  - Labor detection plan (she cannot feel contractions)\n  - Delivery plan (early epidural, AD monitoring)\n  - Postpartum plan\n- This document should be updated at each visit and available to any provider or nurse who sees the patient.\n\n**3. Multidisciplinary Planning Conference**:\n- At 28-30 weeks, convene a planning meeting: PM&R, MFM, OB, obstetric anesthesia, labor and delivery nursing, OT, and the patient/husband.\n- Review the delivery plan in detail. Address questions. Run through AD scenarios.\n- This meeting ensures everyone is aligned and no assumptions are made.\n\n**4. Labor and Delivery Staff Education**:\n- Many L&D nurses have never managed an SCI patient. Provide targeted education:\n  - AD recognition and management (this is an emergency \u2014 they must know the protocol)\n  - Neurogenic bladder \u2014 continue CIC during labor, do not assume she can void\n  - She will not report contraction pain \u2014 use tocodynamometer and palpation\n  - Skin: Reposition every 2 hours during labor. Use pressure-relieving mattress overlay.\n  - Transfer assistance \u2014 she can direct her own transfers\n- Laminated AD protocol card at bedside during labor.\n\n**5. Anesthesia Preparation**:\n- The anesthesiologist must understand that epidural is for **AD prevention**, not just pain control.\n- Epidural should be placed early, before active labor intensifies.\n- Have IV labetalol and nitroglycerin immediately available.\n- Post-anesthesia: Monitor for AD during uterine involution (24-48 hours postpartum).\n\n**6. Patient as Expert**:\n- This patient knows her body and her SCI better than any of her providers. Include her as an active participant in all planning. Her self-knowledge is a clinical asset.\n\n**7. Documentation for Future Pregnancies/Patients**:\n- After delivery, document the entire experience (complications, what worked, what did not) as a case report or institutional protocol.\n- This builds institutional knowledge for future SCI pregnancies.")
B.aWh=new A.c(u.j,'The mother-in-law interjects during the visit: "I love my daughter-in-law, but someone has to say what everyone is thinking. How is a woman in a wheelchair going to take care of a baby? She cannot walk. She cannot run after a toddler. What if there is a fire and she has to carry the baby down stairs? It is not fair to bring a child into this situation. My son should have married someone who can give him a normal family." The patient looks crushed. Her husband says, "Mom, stop," but looks uncomfortable.')
B.aR2=new A.c("+ How do you respond to a family member who openly questions a disabled woman's right to become a mother, while supporting the patient and preserving family relationships?",'**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Address the patient first \u2014 she was just hurt, and she is your patient.**\n[Turn to the patient and make eye contact]\n"Before I respond to what was just said, I want to say something directly to you. You are going to be a wonderful mother. Your ability to parent has nothing to do with whether you can walk. I work with parents with disabilities every day, and I have seen extraordinary parenting from people who use wheelchairs, who are blind, who are deaf, and who have every kind of physical limitation you can imagine. The qualities that make someone a good parent \u2014 love, patience, creativity, dedication \u2014 have nothing to do with legs."\n\n**2. Turn to the mother-in-law with respect, not hostility.**\n"Mrs. [Name], I can hear that you are coming from a place of love and concern \u2014 both for your son and for your future grandchild. Those concerns deserve a response, not dismissal. So let me address them specifically."\n\n**3. Address each concern with facts, not emotion.**\n"You asked how a woman in a wheelchair will take care of a baby. The answer is: with planning, adaptive equipment, and the same problem-solving skills she uses to manage everything else in her life. There are specially designed cribs that open from the side at wheelchair height. There are baby carriers made for wheelchair users. There are changing tables, bath supports, and feeding setups all designed for parents with disabilities. We have an occupational therapist who will work with her specifically on every parenting task \u2014 from diaper changes to bath time to car seat transfers. She will not do things the same way a standing parent does, but she will do them."\n\n"You asked about emergencies \u2014 what if there is a fire. That is a fair question, and it is one we plan for. We develop emergency evacuation plans with every family. And I would point out that her apartment is ground-floor and accessible \u2014 she chose it intentionally."\n\n**4. Gently but firmly challenge the underlying assumption.**\n"But I want to address something deeper in what you said. You said it is not fair to bring a child into this situation, and that your son should have married someone who could give him a normal family. I say this respectfully, but I disagree with that premise. Research consistently shows that children of parents with disabilities develop normally. In fact, many studies show they develop greater empathy, resilience, and problem-solving skills. A \'normal\' family is a family where a child is loved, safe, and supported. This child will have all of those things."\n\n**5. Name the legal and ethical reality without being adversarial.**\n"I also want to mention that the right to have children is a fundamental human right. The Americans with Disabilities Act and reproductive rights law are very clear: disability does not disqualify someone from parenthood. Your daughter-in-law\'s decision to have this baby is her decision, supported by her husband, and it is a decision I wholeheartedly support as her physician."\n\n**6. Invite the mother-in-law into the solution rather than the opposition.**\n"Mrs. [Name], your grandchild is coming in about 20 weeks. That child is going to need a grandmother who believes in this family. Instead of worrying about what your daughter-in-law cannot do, I would love to see you channel that energy into helping her prepare. Help her set up the nursery. Come to an OT session and see how the adaptive equipment works. I think if you see her in action, your worries will ease. She is one of the most capable people I have met."\n\n**7. Check in with the husband.**\n"[Husband\'s name], I want to make sure you are heard too. How are you feeling about all of this?"\n[Allow him to express support for his wife, which reinforces the family unit.]\n\n**8. Return to the patient and reaffirm.**\n"[Patient\'s name], you planned this pregnancy. You prepared for it. You are managing it beautifully. Twenty weeks from now, you are going to hold your baby, and all of this worry will become love. I am here to make sure you have every resource and every tool you need. Let us get back to planning."\n\n**Key Principles**: Prioritized the patient\'s emotional well-being first. Addressed concerns with specific facts rather than dismissing them. Challenged ableist assumptions respectfully but firmly. Cited evidence about outcomes for children of disabled parents. Named reproductive rights without being legalistic. Invited the mother-in-law to become part of the solution. Affirmed the husband\'s role. Returned focus to the patient\'s agency and competence.')
B.aG3=s([B.aPm,B.aYG,B.b1m,B.aTr,B.aOS,B.aOA,B.aUy,B.aWe,B.aQp,B.aWh,B.aR2],t.E)
B.QT=new A.a5("sci-pregnancy","SCI and Pregnancy",B.aG3,!1)
B.b12=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 32-year-old female is admitted to the inpatient rehabilitation unit with T10 ASIA A complete paraplegia resulting from a fall down two flights of stairs 3 weeks ago. She underwent T9-T11 posterior spinal fusion. The trauma surgery note states she \'fell,\' but nursing staff have noted inconsistencies in her account. She initially told the ED she tripped, but a social worker documented that she whispered, "He pushed me," before retracting the statement when her partner arrived. Law enforcement was notified per hospital policy but the patient declined to press charges, stating, "It was an accident." She is quiet, avoids eye contact, and flinches when male staff enter the room. Her partner, a 36-year-old male, has been calling the nursing station daily demanding to visit. He has not been allowed on the unit per a temporary safety hold placed by the acute care social worker, which expires in 2 days.')
B.aZt=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **Injury Details**: Found at the bottom of interior stairwell of her apartment building by a neighbor. Neighbor reported hearing yelling before the fall. Paramedics noted she was crying and saying \"he pushed me.\" CT/MRI showed T10 burst fracture with complete cord compression.\n- **Prior Presentations**: Medical records review reveals 3 ED visits in the past 18 months: (1) 'fell against countertop' \u2014 rib fracture; (2) 'walked into a door' \u2014 orbital contusion; (3) 'burned herself cooking' \u2014 second-degree burns to forearm. Different hospitals each time.\n- **Hospital Course**: T9-T11 posterior fusion. Post-operatively, she told the social worker her partner pushed her during an argument, then recanted when the partner arrived at the hospital demanding to see her.\n- **Current Status**: T10 ASIA A complete paraplegia. Neurogenic bladder (CIC program). Neurogenic bowel (digital stimulation program). No pressure injuries. Emotionally withdrawn. Minimal engagement in therapy \u2014 nursing reports she cries at night.\n- **Partner History**: Together 5 years, unmarried. He is controlling \u2014 she has no independent bank account, no cell phone in her name, no car. He calls the nursing station 3-4 times daily asking when she will be discharged, states he will 'take care of her at home.'\n- **Social**: Previously worked as a nail technician. No income currently. No family nearby \u2014 parents are in another state. One close friend she has been 'not allowed' to see for the past 2 years. No children. No insurance \u2014 application for emergency Medicaid in process.\n- **PMH**: Depression (untreated). No other medical history.\n- **Medications**: Enoxaparin 40 mg SQ daily, docusate 100 mg BID, senna 8.6 mg QHS, acetaminophen 1000 mg Q8H PRN, sertraline 50 mg daily (started during acute hospitalization).")
B.aRA=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:",'**Vitals**: BP 108/70 mmHg, HR 92 bpm, RR 16, SpO2 99% on RA, Temp 36.7C, BMI 22.\n\n**General**: Thin female in hospital bed. Avoids eye contact. Speaks in whispered monosyllables. Flinches when door opens unexpectedly. Cooperative but passive \u2014 does not initiate any interaction.\n\n**Neurological**: T10 ASIA A \u2014 no motor or sensory function below T10. Upper extremities 5/5 throughout. Intact sensation above T10. Complete paralysis below.\n\n**Musculoskeletal**: Surgical incision T9-T11 healing well, sutures removed. Upper extremity strength good. No shoulder pathology. Full upper body ROM.\n\n**Skin**: No pressure injuries. Healed burns noted on left forearm (linear, 6 cm, consistent with prior visit). Faded bruising on right upper arm (finger-pattern ecchymoses, approximately 3-4 weeks old). Small circular scar on left hand dorsum (cigarette burn appearance).\n\n**Abdominal**: Soft, non-tender. Bladder not palpable (recently catheterized). Bowel sounds present.\n\n**Psychological Observation**: Flat affect. Minimal verbal responses. PHQ-9 administered \u2014 score 22 (severe depression). PTSD screen positive. When asked about home, says, "He is not a bad person. It was my fault. I should not have made him angry."')
B.aXh=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","Identify the clinical indicators of intimate partner violence (IPV) in this case and discuss the medical team's assessment and documentation responsibilities.")
B.aPX=new A.c("+ What clinical findings in this case are consistent with intimate partner violence, and what are your documentation and assessment obligations?","**Clinical Indicators of Intimate Partner Violence**:\n\n**1. Injury Pattern**:\n- **Inconsistent mechanism**: A fall down stairs causing a T10 burst fracture is plausible, but the patient initially reported being pushed, then recanted \u2014 classic IPV retraction pattern.\n- **Prior injuries with implausible mechanisms**: Three ED visits in 18 months \u2014 rib fracture ('fell against countertop'), orbital contusion ('walked into door'), forearm burns ('cooking accident'). Each at a different hospital ('hospital shopping' to avoid detection).\n- **Physical examination findings**: (a) Finger-pattern ecchymoses on the upper arm \u2014 virtually pathognomonic for gripping/grabbing. (b) Cigarette burn on hand \u2014 circular, uniform depth, consistent with deliberate burn. (c) Linear forearm burn \u2014 may be consistent with being held against a hot surface.\n\n**2. Behavioral Indicators**:\n- Avoids eye contact, whispered speech, flinches with male staff \u2014 hypervigilance consistent with chronic abuse.\n- Minimal therapy engagement, crying at night.\n- Self-blame: \"It was my fault. I should not have made him angry\" \u2014 internalizing the abuser's narrative.\n- Retracted initial truthful disclosure when partner arrived \u2014 fear of retaliation.\n\n**3. Pattern of Coercive Control**:\n- No independent bank account, phone, or car \u2014 financial and logistical control.\n- Isolated from friend for 2 years \u2014 social isolation is a hallmark of coercive control.\n- Partner calls nursing station 3-4 times daily, demands to visit, states he will 'take care of her' \u2014 controlling behavior extending into the medical setting.\n\n**Documentation Obligations**:\n\n1. **Document findings objectively and in detail**:\n- Describe injuries precisely: location, size, shape, color, pattern. Use body diagrams.\n- Quote the patient's statements verbatim when possible ('patient states ___').\n- Record the inconsistency between stated mechanism and injury pattern.\n- Document prior ED visits and injury patterns.\n- **Do NOT write 'domestic violence' as speculation** \u2014 document observable findings that raise concern and the patient's direct statements.\n\n2. **Photography**: With patient consent, photograph all injuries (including old scars and bruises) with a ruler for scale. These become part of the medical record and may be critical evidence if she later decides to pursue legal action.\n\n3. **Safety assessment**: Document current danger \u2014 is the patient safe in the hospital? Is the abuser attempting to access her? What is the discharge safety plan?\n\n4. **Mandatory reporting considerations**: Varies by state:\n- **All states**: Healthcare providers are mandatory reporters for child abuse and elder abuse.\n- **IPV in adults**: Only some states mandate reporting for adult IPV. Check state law. In states that do not mandate reporting, the patient's autonomy to decline reporting must be respected \u2014 but support and resources must be offered.\n- **This case**: She has declined to press charges. In a non-mandatory reporting state, document this decision. In a mandatory reporting state, report as required and inform the patient.\n- Regardless of reporting requirements, the **injuries were caused by an assault** (she reported being pushed), and assault is a crime that may trigger mandatory reporting of violent injuries in many states.")
B.aMx=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Develop a rehabilitation plan that addresses her physical rehabilitation needs while creating a safety plan for her discharge.")
B.aSC=new A.c("+ Outline a rehabilitation and safety plan for an IPV survivor with a new SCI, including safe discharge planning.","**Integrated Rehabilitation and Safety Plan**:\n\n**1. Physical Rehabilitation (Standard T10 ASIA A)**:\n- **Mobility**: Wheelchair skills training, transfer training (bed, toilet, car), wheelchair propulsion on varied surfaces.\n- **Strengthening**: Upper body progressive resistance program. Core stability with intact musculature.\n- **Neurogenic bladder**: CIC training \u2014 she must be fully independent. Every 4-6 hours. Teach her to recognize UTI symptoms.\n- **Neurogenic bowel**: Digital stimulation bowel program training. Schedule, dietary management, medications.\n- **Skin**: Pressure relief every 15-30 minutes in wheelchair. Daily skin inspection with mirror. Appropriate cushion.\n- **ADL independence**: Dressing, bathing, grooming, cooking from wheelchair level \u2014 all critical for independent living away from the abuser.\n\n**2. Psychological Trauma Treatment**:\n- **Trauma-informed care**: All staff educated \u2014 do not require detailed retelling of abuse, ask permission before touching, explain all procedures in advance, allow her to maintain control of interactions.\n- **Individual therapy**: Begin trauma-focused therapy with a psychologist experienced in IPV. CBT or CPT for PTSD. Start with safety and stabilization before processing trauma.\n- **Sertraline**: Continue and optimize dosing (currently 50 mg \u2014 therapeutic range for PTSD is 100-200 mg). Titrate as tolerated.\n- **Group therapy**: If available, IPV survivor group can be powerful. She has been isolated \u2014 connection with other survivors counters the abuser's narrative.\n\n**3. Safety Planning (Critical)**:\n\n**Immediate (during hospitalization)**:\n- **Restrict partner access**: Extend the safety hold. Work with security and hospital administration to ensure the partner cannot access the unit. He is NOT listed as next of kin or healthcare proxy.\n- **Separate phone line**: Provide her with a hospital phone that the partner does not know about. Help her contact her parents and her friend.\n- **Legal consultation**: Domestic violence advocate and legal aid referral. Discuss protection order options. She may not be ready \u2014 do not pressure, but ensure she knows her options.\n\n**Discharge (DO NOT DISCHARGE TO THE PARTNER)**:\n- **Accessible domestic violence shelter**: Work with DV advocacy organization to identify an accessible shelter (wheelchair-accessible). This is a significant challenge \u2014 many DV shelters are not accessible. Start planning early.\n- **If no accessible shelter available**: Accessible transitional housing, family placement (parents in another state may be an option if housing is accessible), or extended rehabilitation stay while housing is arranged.\n- **Financial**: Emergency Medicaid, SSI/SSDI application (she now qualifies with complete paraplegia), SNAP benefits, emergency cash assistance.\n- **Phone**: Obtain a prepaid phone in her name that the partner does not know about. Program crisis hotline numbers.\n- **Safety plan document**: Developed with DV advocate \u2014 specific steps if the partner finds her, emergency contacts, escape plan.\n\n**4. Independence as Protection**:\n- **Her rehabilitation IS her safety plan.** The more independent she is \u2014 in mobility, ADLs, finances, housing \u2014 the harder it is for the abuser to control her.\n- OT should specifically train: money management, phone use, emergency calls, online resources for DV survivors with disabilities.\n- **Driver rehabilitation**: Once she is ready, hand control driving evaluation. A car equals freedom and escape capability.\n\n**5. Disability-Specific DV Resources**:\n- Women with disabilities experience IPV at **2-3 times the rate** of non-disabled women.\n- Post-injury, she is MORE vulnerable \u2014 she may believe she cannot survive without the abuser.\n- **National Domestic Violence Hotline**: 1-800-799-7233 (has TTY and accessible options).\n- **Abused Deaf Women's Advocacy Services (ADWAS)** and **Disability Rights organizations** provide specialized DV resources.\n- **Independent Living Centers**: Can provide housing, advocacy, and peer support for women with new disabilities.")
B.aQ_=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The partner is calling the nursing station multiple times daily and has now shown up at the hospital front desk demanding to see her. The temporary safety hold expires in 2 days. How do you manage this at the institutional level?")
B.b_X=new A.c("+ How do you manage an abusive partner's attempts to access a patient within the hospital system, and what institutional protections exist?","**Institutional Safety Protocols**:\n\n**1. Visitor Restriction and Extension**:\n- **Extend the safety hold**: Work with social work and hospital administration to extend the visitor restriction. This typically requires documentation of safety concern and the patient's expressed or implied wish not to have contact.\n- **Patient consent**: If the patient consents to restricting the partner, this is straightforward. If she is ambivalent (common in IPV), document the clinical basis for restriction (safety concern based on documented abuse pattern).\n- **Hospital security notification**: Provide security with the partner's name, physical description, and photograph if available. He should not be permitted past the front desk.\n- **Code word system**: Establish a code word with the patient \u2014 if the partner somehow reaches her by phone or in person, she can use the word to alert staff she needs help.\n\n**2. Legal Protections**:\n- **Protection order**: The DV advocate can help file an emergency protection order (EPO) or temporary restraining order (TRO). If granted, the partner is legally barred from contact.\n- **Hospital as private property**: The hospital can issue a trespass warning to the partner. If he returns after being warned, he can be arrested.\n- **Information restriction**: Place the patient on a 'confidential' or 'no information' status in the hospital system. If anyone calls asking if she is a patient, the hospital responds that they have no information. Her room number should not be accessible to non-clinical staff.\n\n**3. Communication Protocol**:\n- **Nursing station instructions**: All staff informed \u2014 do NOT provide any information about the patient to callers. Do not confirm or deny she is a patient. Do not accept gifts or deliveries from the partner.\n- **Chart access restriction**: Ensure the partner cannot access medical records (he is not the healthcare proxy). HIPAA protections apply.\n- **Caller ID**: If the partner's phone number is known, flag it at the nursing station.\n\n**4. Discharge Safety Systems**:\n- **Confidential discharge**: Do not include discharge destination in any records the partner could access. Use 'confidential address' or 'safe location' in discharge planning notes.\n- **Transportation**: Arrange discharge transportation that the partner cannot track \u2014 not a vehicle he would recognize.\n- **Medical records**: Ensure new address/phone (at shelter or safe location) is not linked to records at the old address.\n\n**5. Multidisciplinary Safety Team**:\n- Convene: PM&R physician, social worker, DV advocate, hospital security, nursing leadership, patient (when she is ready).\n- Develop a written safety plan that covers the remainder of her hospitalization and the transition to a safe discharge location.\n- Assign one social worker as the primary coordinator \u2014 too many contacts increases the risk of information leaks.\n\n**6. Staff Safety**:\n- Abusive partners sometimes become aggressive toward staff who block access. Security presence when he is known to be at the hospital.\n- De-escalation protocols for staff who interact with him by phone or in person.\n- Staff should NOT engage in confrontation \u2014 refer all interactions to security.")
B.aZ2=new A.c(u.j,'The patient\'s partner calls the nursing station and says: "I am her boyfriend. I need to come see her. She needs me \u2014 she cannot even go to the bathroom by herself now. Who is going to take care of her when she gets out? She has nobody. I have been taking care of her for 5 years. Tell the doctor to call me so I can discuss her discharge plan. I am picking her up when she is done there." The nurse transfers the call to you.')
B.aRY=new A.c("+ How do you handle a phone call from the patient's abusive partner who is asserting his role as her caregiver and requesting involvement in her discharge?",'**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Do not confirm or deny the patient is at the hospital.**\n"Thank you for calling. Due to patient privacy laws, I am unable to confirm or deny whether any individual is a patient at this facility, and I cannot discuss any patient\'s medical information without their written authorization."\n\n**2. If he presses (which he will):**\n"I understand your concern. Our policy applies to all patients and all callers. If someone you know is receiving care here and wishes to include you in their care, they can authorize that through their care team. I cannot provide any further information."\n\n**3. Do not engage with his narrative.**\n- He will say she needs him, she has no one else, he has been taking care of her.\n- Do NOT argue, correct, or challenge him on the phone. Do not say "we know what you did" or "she told us you pushed her." This could escalate his behavior and endanger the patient.\n- Do not provide any information about her condition, her injury, her discharge date, or her location.\n\n**4. If he becomes threatening or aggressive:**\n"I hear that you are upset. If you feel you need to speak with someone about a patient concern, you can contact our Patient Relations department at [number]. If you come to the hospital, please check in at the front desk. I am going to end this call now."\n- Document the call, including his exact words, tone, and any threats.\n- Notify hospital security immediately.\n- Notify the social worker and DV advocate.\n\n**5. After the call \u2014 document and act:**\n- Document the call verbatim in the medical record.\n- Notify the patient that her partner called and what he said \u2014 she has the right to know. Ask her how she wants to proceed.\n- Inform the care team \u2014 this is an escalation. His language (\'picking her up,\' \'she has nobody,\' \'who will take care of her\') frames her disability as a reason she needs him. This is **weaponizing her disability as a tool of control** \u2014 a recognized IPV tactic when a partner becomes disabled.\n- Increase security awareness. Consider whether the call pattern constitutes harassment (repeated unwanted calls) that could support a protection order.\n\n**6. Discuss with the patient (separately, privately):**\n"I want to tell you about a phone call I received from [partner\'s name]. He called asking about you and said he wants to be involved in your discharge. He said you need him because of your injury. I want to ask you: Is that what you want? Because I want you to know something \u2014 you are learning to do everything independently. You do not need anyone to take care of you. You are going to be able to catheterize yourself, manage your bowel program, transfer, dress, cook, and live on your own. Your injury does not make you dependent on him or anyone else. You have options, and I will help you see every one of them."\n\n**Key Principles**: HIPAA compliance \u2014 do not confirm or deny the patient is present. Do not engage with the abuser\'s framing. Do not provide information that could be used to control or locate the patient. Document everything. Inform the patient of all contacts. Counter the narrative that her disability makes her dependent on him. Safety of the patient and staff is paramount.')
B.aEk=s([B.b12,B.aZt,B.aRA,B.aXh,B.aPX,B.aMx,B.aSC,B.aQ_,B.b_X,B.aZ2,B.aRY],t.E)
B.QF=new A.a5("sci-domestic-violence","SCI from Domestic Violence",B.aEk,!1)
B.aZw=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 42-year-old right-hand-dominant male construction worker is referred to your outpatient brain injury clinic 3 months after a fall from scaffolding (approximately 12 feet). Emergency medical services documented a GCS of 10 at the scene with a brief loss of consciousness lasting approximately 5 minutes. CT head at presentation showed a small left frontal contusion with trace subarachnoid hemorrhage. He was admitted to the ICU for 48 hours with GCS improving to 14 by hospital day 2. He now reports persistent difficulty concentrating, irritability, word-finding problems, and inability to manage his work duties. His wife reports personality changes with increased impulsivity and short temper.")
B.b3H=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","A thorough assessment should include:\n- **Injury Characterization**: GCS at scene (10), duration of LOC (~5 min), duration of PTA (reported as 3 days by inpatient records), imaging findings (left frontal contusion, trace SAH) \u2014 these collectively confirm a **moderate TBI** (GCS 9-12).\n- **Cognitive Complaints**: Attention and concentration deficits, executive dysfunction (difficulty planning tasks, multitasking), word-finding difficulties, reduced processing speed.\n- **Behavioral/Emotional**: Irritability, impulsivity, low frustration tolerance, depressive symptoms (PHQ-9 score of 14).\n- **Physical Symptoms**: Intermittent headaches (3-4x/week, tension-type pattern), occasional dizziness with positional changes, fatigue by early afternoon.\n- **Sleep**: Fragmented sleep with early morning awakening, sleeping 10-11 hours but feeling unrefreshed.\n- **Vocational History**: 18 years as a construction foreman; duties include reading blueprints, supervising crews, operating heavy machinery, working at heights.\n- **Substance Use**: Previously 2-3 beers on weekends; none since injury. No illicit drug use.\n- **Social/Support**: Married with 2 children (ages 10 and 14). Wife has taken FMLA leave to assist. Workers' compensation claim is active.")
B.aYm=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Vitals**: BP 132/78 mmHg, HR 74 bpm, RR 14, Temp 98.4F, BMI 28.\n- **General**: Alert, oriented x4. Appears fatigued. Speech is fluent but with occasional word-finding pauses.\n- **Cranial Nerves**: Intact II-XII. Mild decreased olfaction bilaterally (CN I).\n- **Motor**: 5/5 strength throughout all extremities. Normal tone and bulk.\n- **Sensory**: Intact to light touch, pinprick, proprioception throughout.\n- **Coordination**: Finger-to-nose and heel-to-shin intact bilaterally. No dysmetria.\n- **Gait**: Normal station and gait. Tandem gait intact.\n- **Balance**: Romberg negative. Single-leg stance maintained 15 seconds bilaterally.\n- **Cognitive Screen (MoCA)**: Score 22/30 \u2014 deficits in attention (serial 7s), delayed recall (1/5 words), and executive function (Trail Making B errors, poor clock draw).\n- **Neuropsychological Testing Summary** (performed 2 weeks ago): Impaired sustained attention (CPT-3), impaired working memory (Digit Span backward at 5th percentile), executive dysfunction (Wisconsin Card Sorting Test \u2014 elevated perseverative errors), processing speed at 12th percentile. Language and visuospatial function within normal limits.")
B.aRv=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The patient's neuropsychological profile demonstrates genuine cognitive deficits consistent with his moderate TBI, particularly in the domains of attention, executive function, and processing speed \u2014 functions mediated by the frontal lobe, which is the site of his documented contusion.")
B.b0C=new A.c("+ Differentiate post-concussive syndrome from moderate TBI sequelae in this patient. What role does neuropsychological testing play?","Key distinctions:\n- **Post-Concussive Syndrome (PCS)**: Typically follows mild TBI (GCS 13-15). Symptoms are often subjective and may persist beyond expected recovery (3 months). Neuroimaging is usually normal. Neuropsychological testing may show inconsistent effort or symptom over-reporting.\n- **Moderate TBI Sequelae**: This patient's injury meets criteria for **moderate TBI** (GCS 9-12, LOC > 30 min or PTA 1-7 days, positive neuroimaging). His deficits are:\n  1. Consistent with the **lesion location** (left frontal contusion correlating with executive and attention deficits)\n  2. Corroborated by **objective neuropsychological testing** showing genuine impairment with adequate effort (validated by embedded performance validity tests)\n  3. Supported by **structural imaging** findings\n\n**Role of Neuropsychological Testing**:\n1. **Establishes baseline** cognitive functioning and quantifies deficits\n2. **Validates effort** through embedded and standalone performance validity tests (e.g., TOMM, RDS) \u2014 critical in medicolegal and workers' compensation settings\n3. **Guides rehabilitation** by identifying specific cognitive targets for therapy\n4. **Tracks recovery** through serial reassessment (typically at 6 and 12 months)\n5. **Informs vocational planning** by identifying which job demands exceed current cognitive capacity")
B.aZv=new A.c("+ What additional workup should be considered at 3 months post-injury?","1. **MRI Brain with susceptibility-weighted imaging (SWI)**: More sensitive than CT for detecting diffuse axonal injury (DAI), microhemorrhages, and evolution of contusions.\n2. **Endocrine Screening**: Post-traumatic hypopituitarism occurs in 25-50% of moderate-severe TBI. Screen **cortisol (AM), TSH/free T4, testosterone (in males), prolactin, IGF-1** at 3-6 months.\n3. **Sleep Study (Polysomnography)**: If sleep complaints persist despite sleep hygiene interventions \u2014 post-traumatic hypersomnia and sleep apnea are common after TBI.\n4. **Vestibular Evaluation**: Videonystagmography (VNG) if dizziness persists \u2014 to evaluate for BPPV, central vestibular dysfunction, or cervicogenic dizziness.\n5. **Depression Screening**: PHQ-9 is already elevated (14, moderate). Consider structured psychiatric evaluation for comorbid PTSD (PCL-5) given traumatic mechanism.")
B.b3b=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","With objective documentation of cognitive deficits, you now develop a comprehensive rehabilitation and return-to-work plan.")
B.aVc=new A.c("+ Outline the cognitive rehabilitation and pharmacological management plan, including vocational rehabilitation.","**Cognitive Rehabilitation**:\n1. **Speech-Language Pathology**: Targeted treatment for attention training (Attention Process Training - APT), compensatory strategy instruction (external memory aids, checklists, smartphone reminders), and metacognitive strategy training.\n2. **Occupational Therapy**: Executive function retraining through graded task complexity, time management, and multitasking practice in functional contexts.\n3. **Neuropsychology Follow-up**: Serial reassessment at 6 months to track recovery trajectory.\n\n**Pharmacological Management**:\n1. **Methylphenidate** 5 mg BID (morning and noon) \u2014 first-line for post-TBI attention and processing speed deficits. Titrate to 10 mg BID based on response. Monitor BP and HR.\n2. **Sertraline** 50 mg daily for comorbid depression/irritability \u2014 preferred SSRI in TBI population (avoids seizure threshold lowering seen with bupropion in the acute period).\n3. **Melatonin** 3-5 mg at bedtime for sleep-wake cycle regulation.\n4. **Avoid**: Benzodiazepines, typical antipsychotics, and anticholinergics \u2014 all impair cognitive recovery.\n\n**Vocational Rehabilitation**:\n1. **Functional Capacity Evaluation (FCE)**: Formal assessment of physical and cognitive demands vs. current abilities.\n2. **Job Site Analysis**: Detailed evaluation of his foreman duties to identify essential vs. marginal functions.\n3. **Graded Return-to-Work Plan**: Begin with modified duty (office-based planning tasks, 4 hours/day) with gradual increase in complexity and hours over 8-12 weeks.\n4. **Cognitive Accommodations**: Written task lists, reduced multitasking demands, scheduled rest breaks, prohibition from working at heights or operating heavy machinery until cleared.\n5. **Vocational Counselor**: Coordinate with workers' compensation vocational rehabilitation specialist for job coaching and workplace modifications.")
B.aTO=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient's workers' compensation case manager contacts your office requesting a timeline for full return to work and asks whether the patient will reach Maximum Medical Improvement (MMI).")
B.b1Y=new A.c("+ Discuss the workers' compensation process, vocational evaluation, and job site modification considerations.","**Workers' Compensation Considerations**:\n1. **Documentation**: Maintain detailed, objective medical records linking cognitive deficits to the work injury. Neuropsychological testing provides the strongest objective evidence.\n2. **Maximum Medical Improvement (MMI)**: For moderate TBI, most cognitive recovery occurs within 12-18 months. MMI determination should not occur before 12 months post-injury at minimum.\n3. **Impairment Rating**: At MMI, use the AMA Guides (6th Edition) to assign a permanent partial impairment rating based on residual cognitive deficits.\n\n**Vocational Evaluation**:\n1. **Transferable Skills Analysis**: Identify whether his existing skills (blueprint reading, crew management, scheduling) can be applied to a modified or alternative position.\n2. **Situational Assessment**: Trial period in the actual workplace to assess real-world performance with and without accommodations.\n3. **Earning Capacity Assessment**: If he cannot return to his previous role, quantify the difference in earning capacity for the compensation claim.\n\n**Job Site Modifications**:\n1. **Restricted Duties**: No working at heights, no heavy machinery operation, no solo supervision of safety-critical tasks until neuropsychological testing normalizes.\n2. **Environmental Modifications**: Quiet workspace for planning tasks, written checklists for daily job assignments, buddy system for complex tasks.\n3. **Schedule Modifications**: Gradual return from half-days to full-days; cognitive fatigue management with scheduled breaks.")
B.aND=new A.c(u.j,"The patient's employer calls your office directly: 'I need to know when Mike can come back. We're short-staffed and I need him on the job site. Can you just tell me what's wrong with him and when he'll be cleared?'")
B.aVF=new A.c("+ How do you handle this call while maintaining HIPAA compliance and patient confidentiality?","I would respond: 'I appreciate you calling and understand the staffing challenges you're facing. However, I am legally prohibited under HIPAA and patient confidentiality laws from disclosing any medical information about my patient to you without his explicit written authorization. I cannot confirm or deny that he is even my patient, nor can I discuss his diagnosis, treatment, prognosis, or timeline for return to work.\n\nHere is what I recommend:\n1. **Have the patient sign a Release of Information (ROI)** specifically authorizing me to communicate with you about his work status. The ROI must specify what information can be shared and with whom.\n2. Once I have that authorization, I can provide a **work status letter** outlining functional restrictions and capabilities without disclosing specific medical diagnoses.\n3. All communication regarding return-to-work planning should be coordinated through the **workers' compensation case manager**, who serves as the appropriate intermediary.\n4. If the patient consents, I would recommend a **three-way meeting** between the patient, employer, and vocational counselor to discuss workplace accommodations.'\n\n**Key Principles**:\n- Never release information without a signed, specific ROI from the patient\n- Provide **functional limitations**, not diagnostic details, to employers\n- Direct employer inquiries through appropriate channels (WC case manager)\n- Document this contact attempt in the medical record and inform the patient")
B.awt=s([B.aZw,B.b3H,B.aYm,B.aRv,B.b0C,B.aZv,B.b3b,B.aVc,B.aTO,B.b1Y,B.aND,B.aVF],t.E)
B.Qf=new A.a5("tbi-moderate-return-work","Moderate TBI & Return to Work",B.awt,!1)
B.aYE=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 17-year-old male high school varsity football player is brought to your sports medicine clinic the day after sustaining a hit during a Friday night game. His coach reports that during the third quarter, the patient collided helmet-to-helmet with an opposing player. He appeared dazed, asked the same question repeatedly ("What quarter is it?"), and had difficulty maintaining balance walking to the sideline. He was immediately removed from play per the school\'s concussion protocol. He did not lose consciousness. He now reports persistent headache (7/10), difficulty concentrating in class, sensitivity to light and noise, and feeling "foggy." He slept 12 hours last night but still feels tired.')
B.aZo=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","A comprehensive concussion evaluation should include:\n- **Mechanism & On-Field Signs**: Helmet-to-helmet contact, post-impact confusion, repetitive questioning (anterograde amnesia), balance difficulties \u2014 all observable signs consistent with sport-related concussion (SRC).\n- **Symptom Inventory (SCAT6 Symptom Checklist)**: Headache 7/10, pressure in head 5/10, dizziness 4/10, feeling slowed down 6/10, difficulty concentrating 7/10, fatigue 6/10, photophobia 5/10, phonophobia 4/10, feeling foggy 7/10, drowsiness 5/10. **Total symptom severity score: 56/132.**\n- **Concussion History**: One prior concussion 2 years ago (resolved in 10 days). No history of prolonged recovery.\n- **Red Flags to Exclude**: No LOC, no seizure activity, no worsening headache, no repeated vomiting, no double vision, no neck pain, no weakness/tingling in extremities, no increasing confusion.\n- **Pre-Injury Baseline**: Had pre-season ImPACT testing performed 4 months ago. Baseline symptom score was 2/132.\n- **Past Medical History**: ADHD (managed with behavioral strategies, no medications), no history of migraines, no prior surgeries.\n- **Academic Status**: Junior year, honors student, reports difficulty reading and completing homework since the injury.\n- **Medications**: None currently. No blood thinners.")
B.aZu=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Vitals**: BP 118/72 mmHg, HR 68 bpm, RR 14, Temp 98.2F, O2 Sat 99%.\n- **General**: Alert, oriented x4. Appears uncomfortable under fluorescent clinic lights, squinting.\n- **Neurological**: Cranial nerves II-XII intact. Pupils equal, round, reactive. No nystagmus at rest. Strength 5/5 throughout. Sensation intact. Reflexes 2+ and symmetric.\n- **Cervical Spine**: Full ROM without pain. No midline tenderness. Negative Spurling's test.\n- **Vestibular/Oculomotor Screen (VOMS)**:\n  - Smooth pursuits: Provokes headache (increase of 2 points)\n  - Saccades: Provokes headache and dizziness (increase of 3 points)\n  - Near Point of Convergence: 12 cm (abnormal; normal <5 cm)\n  - VOR: Provokes headache and nausea (increase of 3 points)\n- **Balance (Modified BESS)**: Errors on single-leg stance (4 errors) and tandem stance on foam (5 errors). Total BESS errors: 15 (baseline was 6).\n- **Cognitive Screen**: Orientation and immediate memory intact on SCAT6. Delayed recall 3/5 words (below baseline of 5/5). Concentration (months backward): 1 error.\n- **Provocative Exertion**: Not performed today \u2014 patient is symptomatic at rest.")
B.aPj=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","This presentation is consistent with an acute sport-related concussion based on the mechanism, witnessed on-field signs (confusion, amnesia, balance impairment), and persistent symptoms at 24 hours. The VOMS findings suggest vestibulo-ocular dysfunction, and the elevated BESS errors indicate persistent balance impairment.")
B.aXE=new A.c("+ Does this patient need a CT scan? Discuss the decision-making framework and concussion grading.","**CT Decision \u2014 Canadian CT Head Rule (adapted for sport)**:\nThis patient does **NOT** meet criteria for emergent CT because he has:\n- No LOC\n- No worsening or thunderclap headache\n- No vomiting\n- No signs of skull fracture\n- No focal neurological deficits\n- No seizure activity\n- No dangerous mechanism (high-velocity impact, fall from height)\n- GCS 15 at presentation\n\n**Important**: CT is normal in >95% of concussions. It should only be ordered to rule out **structural pathology** (hemorrhage, fracture), not to diagnose concussion.\n\n**Concussion Grading**:\n- The outdated Cantu and Colorado grading systems (Grades 1-3) are **no longer recommended**. The current **Berlin Consensus (6th International Conference on Concussion in Sport, 2023)** does not use severity grades at initial presentation.\n- Instead, the approach is **individualized symptom-based management** with serial reassessment.\n- **Prognostic modifiers** that predict prolonged recovery include: prior concussion history, younger age, high initial symptom burden (this patient's score of 56 is significant), ADHD, vestibulo-ocular abnormalities on VOMS, and female sex.\n\n**Baseline Comparison**:\n- Pre-season ImPACT baseline symptom score: 2/132; current: 56/132 \u2014 a clinically significant increase confirming the diagnosis.\n- Formal post-injury ImPACT testing should be deferred until symptoms improve at rest (typically 48-72 hours minimum) to avoid symptom exacerbation.")
B.aQo=new A.c("+ What are the concerning red flags that would warrant emergent neuroimaging or escalation of care?","**Red Flags Requiring Emergent CT/Transfer**:\n1. **Deteriorating consciousness** or GCS drop\n2. **Worsening or severe headache** not responding to acetaminophen\n3. **Repeated vomiting** (>2 episodes)\n4. **Seizure** activity\n5. **Focal neurological deficit** (weakness, sensory loss, speech difficulty)\n6. **Battle sign or raccoon eyes** suggesting basilar skull fracture\n7. **CSF otorrhea or rhinorrhea**\n8. **Increasing confusion or agitation** over time\n9. **Anticoagulant use** (not applicable here but important to screen)\n10. **Lucid interval** followed by rapid deterioration (suggests epidural hematoma)\n\n**Specific to This Patient**: His ADHD history may complicate symptom interpretation (attention deficits at baseline). However, the witnessed on-field signs and acute symptom onset clearly distinguish this as a concussion. If symptoms worsen over the next 24-48 hours despite rest, neuroimaging should be reconsidered.")
B.b2q=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You diagnose the patient with a sport-related concussion and now must outline the management plan including the stepwise return-to-play protocol.")
B.aX0=new A.c("+ Detail the acute management and the Berlin Consensus stepwise return-to-play protocol.",'**Acute Management (First 24-48 Hours)**:\n1. **Relative Rest**: Brief period (24-48 hours) of relative cognitive and physical rest. **Not** complete "cocooning" \u2014 light activity as tolerated (walking, light household tasks) is now recommended.\n2. **Symptom Management**:\n   - Headache: Acetaminophen preferred. **Avoid NSAIDs** in the first 48 hours (theoretical bleeding risk). Avoid opioids.\n   - Sleep: Encourage regular sleep schedule. Melatonin 1-3 mg if needed.\n   - Screen time: Limit to tolerance; brief periods with breaks are acceptable.\n3. **Education**: Reassure that most concussions resolve within 2-4 weeks in adolescents. Provide written discharge instructions.\n\n**Berlin Consensus 6-Stage Return-to-Sport Protocol**:\nEach stage requires a **minimum of 24 hours** without symptom exacerbation before progressing:\n\n1. **Stage 1 \u2014 Symptom-Limited Activity**: Daily activities that do not provoke symptoms. Walking, light reading.\n2. **Stage 2 \u2014 Light Aerobic Exercise**: Walking, stationary cycling at 70% max HR for 15-20 minutes. **No resistance training.**\n3. **Stage 3 \u2014 Sport-Specific Exercise**: Running drills, skating drills. **No head-impact activities.**\n4. **Stage 4 \u2014 Non-Contact Training Drills**: Progression to more complex drills, may begin resistance training. Coordinate with athletic trainer.\n5. **Stage 5 \u2014 Full-Contact Practice**: Following **medical clearance**, participate in normal practice with full contact.\n6. **Stage 6 \u2014 Return to Competition**: Full game participation.\n\n**Critical Rules**:\n- If symptoms recur at any stage, the athlete **drops back to the previous asymptomatic stage** and rests 24 hours before re-attempting.\n- **Medical clearance is required before Stage 5** \u2014 this must be provided by a physician trained in concussion management.\n- In adolescents, the protocol should be extended (more conservative) compared to adults.\n- Minimum timeline: Even in best-case scenarios, an adolescent should not return to full contact in less than **10-14 days**.')
B.aSU=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient is a junior in high school and is struggling academically since the concussion. He has midterm exams next week and reports that reading for more than 10 minutes triggers headaches.")
B.b1C=new A.c("+ Discuss school accommodations, state concussion laws, and clearance protocols for this student-athlete.","**School Accommodations (Return-to-Learn Protocol)**:\nAcademic accommodations should be implemented **before** return-to-sport and follow a similar stepwise approach:\n1. **Stage 1**: Home rest with minimal cognitive activity\n2. **Stage 2**: Gradual re-introduction of cognitive activity at home (15-20 minute sessions with breaks)\n3. **Stage 3**: Part-day school attendance with accommodations\n4. **Stage 4**: Full-day school attendance with accommodations\n5. **Stage 5**: Full academic workload without accommodations\n\n**Section 504 Plan Accommodations**:\n- Extended time for assignments and exams (1.5-2x normal)\n- Reduced homework/coursework load\n- Preferential seating away from windows and fluorescent lights\n- Permission to wear sunglasses indoors\n- Breaks during extended reading or screen use\n- Audio recordings of lectures in lieu of note-taking\n- Postponement of high-stakes testing (midterms should be deferred)\n- Excusal from noisy environments (cafeteria, assemblies, band)\n\n**State Concussion Laws** (present in all 50 states plus DC):\n- **Immediate removal from play** when concussion is suspected (fulfilled here)\n- **Written clearance from a licensed healthcare provider** trained in concussion management required before return to play\n- **Education requirements**: Athletes, parents, and coaches must receive annual concussion education and sign acknowledgment forms\n- **No same-day return to play** regardless of symptom resolution\n\n**Clearance Protocol**:\n1. Must be **asymptomatic at rest** and have completed the full return-to-sport protocol\n2. **Neurocognitive testing** (ImPACT) should return to within reliable change indices of baseline\n3. **VOMS and balance testing** should normalize\n4. Written clearance provided by the managing physician \u2014 not the athletic trainer, coach, or parent\n5. Document clearance in the athlete's school medical file")
B.aP9=new A.c(u.j,"The patient's father is in the exam room and becomes visibly agitated: 'Doc, the biggest game of the season is this Saturday \u2014 college scouts are going to be there. He needs to play. He says he feels fine now. Can't you just clear him? He had a concussion before and was fine. I don't want him to miss this opportunity.'")
B.b19=new A.c("+ How do you respond to the father while advocating for the patient's safety?","I would respond: 'Mr. [Name], I completely understand how important this game is \u2014 for both of you. I can see how much you care about your son's future, and I want to support that future too. That is exactly why I cannot clear him to play this Saturday.\n\nHere is why: Your son sustained a concussion less than 24 hours ago. His symptom score is significantly elevated, his balance testing is abnormal, and his eye-tracking examination shows dysfunction that needs time to heal. The minimum safe timeline for return to contact sports in an adolescent is at least 10-14 days, and only after he has completed every step of a graduated protocol without symptoms returning.\n\nThe most important thing I can tell you is this: if your son takes another hit to the head before his brain has fully recovered, he is at risk for **Second Impact Syndrome** \u2014 a rare but potentially fatal condition where the brain loses its ability to regulate blood flow, leading to catastrophic swelling. This risk is highest in adolescents.\n\nI know missing one game feels enormous right now, but protecting his brain protects his entire athletic career, his academics, and his long-term health. If scouts are interested in him, they will see him play again. I would be happy to provide documentation to the school and to any college programs explaining the medical situation.\n\nWhat I can promise you is that we will get him back on the field as quickly and safely as possible. I will see him again early next week to reassess, and if he is progressing well, we will start the return-to-play protocol right away.'")
B.aEG=s([B.aYE,B.aZo,B.aZu,B.aPj,B.aXE,B.aQo,B.b2q,B.aX0,B.aSU,B.b1C,B.aP9,B.b19],t.E)
B.Qu=new A.a5("tbi-concussion-return-play","Sport-Related Concussion & Return to Play",B.aEG,!1)
B.aOU=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 35-year-old female is seen in your brain injury rehabilitation clinic for a 6-month follow-up after a severe TBI sustained in a motor vehicle collision. Initial GCS was 6, and she required a 3-week ICU stay including 10 days of mechanical ventilation. CT showed bifrontal contusions and a small left temporal epidural hematoma managed conservatively. She completed 4 weeks of acute inpatient rehabilitation and has been receiving outpatient therapies. Her motor recovery has been excellent \u2014 she is ambulating independently and performing ADLs without assistance. However, she now presents with new complaints: progressive fatigue despite sleeping 10-12 hours nightly, unintentional weight gain of 18 pounds over 3 months despite no change in diet, amenorrhea (no menses since the accident), decreased libido, cold intolerance, constipation, dry skin, and depressed mood.")
B.aZV=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","A systematic assessment should address:\n- **Endocrine Symptom Mapping**:\n  - **Thyroid axis**: Cold intolerance, weight gain, constipation, dry skin, fatigue, depressed mood \u2014 suggestive of **hypothyroidism**\n  - **Gonadal axis**: Amenorrhea (6 months), decreased libido \u2014 suggestive of **hypogonadism**\n  - **Adrenal axis**: Fatigue, orthostatic lightheadedness, difficulty with physiological stress \u2014 screen for **adrenal insufficiency**\n  - **Growth hormone axis**: Fatigue, increased body fat, decreased lean mass, impaired cognition, reduced quality of life \u2014 suggestive of **GH deficiency**\n- **Injury Severity**: GCS 6 (severe TBI), bifrontal contusions, temporal EDH \u2014 basilar skull fractures and severe TBI carry highest risk for pituitary damage due to proximity of the pituitary stalk to bony structures and vulnerability to shearing forces.\n- **Timeline**: Symptoms have been gradually worsening over months, consistent with the evolution of post-traumatic hypopituitarism (which can present acutely or develop over 6-12 months post-injury).\n- **Menstrual History**: Previously regular 28-day cycles. Last menstrual period was 2 weeks before the accident. Not currently on hormonal contraception. Pregnancy test negative.\n- **Medications**: Sertraline 100 mg daily, melatonin 5 mg nightly, acetaminophen PRN.\n- **Psychiatric**: PHQ-9 score of 16 (moderately severe depression). Patient attributes this to frustration with ongoing symptoms.")
B.aOf=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Vitals**: BP 98/62 mmHg (orthostatic drop to 88/58 standing), HR 58 bpm, Temp 97.2F, BMI 29 (was 24 pre-injury).\n- **General**: Appears fatigued, with periorbital puffiness and pallid complexion. Speech is slow but coherent.\n- **Skin**: Dry, cool skin. Coarse hair. Lateral third of eyebrows appears thinned bilaterally.\n- **Thyroid**: Non-enlarged, no nodules palpable.\n- **Cardiac**: Bradycardia, regular rhythm, no murmurs.\n- **Neurological**: Alert, oriented x4. Cranial nerves intact. Visual fields full to confrontation. Strength 5/5 throughout. Reflexes 2+ with **delayed relaxation phase** (suggestive of hypothyroidism).\n- **Body Composition**: Increased central adiposity compared to prior clinic photos. Decreased muscle bulk in proximal extremities.\n- **Cognitive**: MoCA 25/30 \u2014 mild deficits in attention and delayed recall (improved from 20/30 at 3 months).\n- **Affect**: Flat, with psychomotor slowing. Reports anhedonia.")
B.aOV=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The constellation of fatigue, weight gain, amenorrhea, cold intolerance, orthostatic hypotension, and bradycardia in the setting of severe TBI raises high suspicion for **post-traumatic hypopituitarism** \u2014 a condition estimated to affect 25-50% of moderate-to-severe TBI survivors, yet frequently underdiagnosed.")
B.aPY=new A.c("+ Describe the pathophysiology of post-traumatic hypopituitarism and outline the systematic hormonal workup.","**Pathophysiology**:\n- The pituitary gland and its stalk are vulnerable to injury during TBI due to:\n  1. **Shearing forces** on the hypophyseal portal vessels along the pituitary stalk during acceleration-deceleration injury\n  2. **Direct compression** from edema, hemorrhage, or elevated ICP\n  3. **Ischemic injury** to the anterior pituitary (supplied by a low-pressure portal system with limited collateral blood supply)\n  4. **Basilar skull fracture** can directly damage the sella turcica\n- The **anterior pituitary** is most commonly affected. The posterior pituitary (ADH/oxytocin) has a direct arterial blood supply and is less commonly involved in chronic dysfunction.\n- Hormone axes are affected in order of vulnerability (most to least): **GH > Gonadotropins (LH/FSH) > ACTH > TSH** > ADH\n\n**Systematic Hormonal Workup**:\n1. **Adrenal Axis (MOST CRITICAL \u2014 screen first, as deficiency is life-threatening)**:\n   - AM cortisol (8 AM fasting): <3 mcg/dL is diagnostic; >18 mcg/dL excludes deficiency; 3-18 mcg/dL requires dynamic testing\n   - Cosyntropin (ACTH) stimulation test if AM cortisol is equivocal\n2. **Thyroid Axis**:\n   - **Free T4 and TSH**: In central (secondary) hypothyroidism, expect **low free T4 with low or inappropriately normal TSH** (unlike primary hypothyroidism where TSH is elevated)\n3. **Gonadal Axis**:\n   - LH, FSH, estradiol (in females) or testosterone (in males)\n   - In central hypogonadism: low estradiol/testosterone with low or inappropriately normal LH/FSH\n4. **Growth Hormone Axis**:\n   - IGF-1 as screening (may be low-normal even in GH deficiency)\n   - Definitive testing requires a **stimulation test** (glucagon stimulation test or insulin tolerance test) \u2014 do NOT test in the acute phase; defer to >6-12 months post-injury\n5. **Prolactin**: May be elevated (pituitary stalk effect) or low (anterior pituitary destruction)\n6. **Posterior Pituitary**: Serum sodium, serum and urine osmolality if any concern for diabetes insipidus (polyuria, polydipsia)\n7. **MRI Pituitary with dedicated thin-cut sella protocol**: Evaluate for pituitary atrophy, hemorrhage, empty sella, or stalk abnormalities")
B.b1i=new A.c("+ The labs return. Interpret these results and identify which axes are affected.","**Lab Results**:\n- AM Cortisol: 12 mcg/dL (equivocal \u2014 needs dynamic testing)\n- ACTH stimulation test: Peak cortisol 16 mcg/dL (suboptimal; normal >18 mcg/dL)\n- TSH: 1.2 mIU/L (normal range 0.5-4.5) \u2014 **inappropriately normal**\n- Free T4: 0.5 ng/dL (low; normal 0.8-1.8)\n- LH: 1.8 mIU/mL (low; follicular phase normal 2-15)\n- FSH: 2.1 mIU/mL (low; follicular phase normal 3-10)\n- Estradiol: 12 pg/mL (low; follicular phase normal 30-120)\n- Prolactin: 32 ng/mL (mildly elevated; normal <25)\n- IGF-1: 68 ng/mL (low for age; normal 115-340)\n- Sodium: 141 mEq/L (normal)\n- MRI Pituitary: Partial empty sella with thinning of the pituitary stalk\n\n**Interpretation**:\n1. **Central Hypothyroidism CONFIRMED**: Low free T4 with inappropriately normal TSH. The pituitary is failing to respond to low thyroid hormone levels.\n2. **Central Hypogonadism CONFIRMED**: Low estradiol with low LH and FSH. The pituitary is not producing adequate gonadotropins.\n3. **Secondary Adrenal Insufficiency PROBABLE**: Suboptimal cosyntropin stimulation response (peak 16 mcg/dL). This represents partial ACTH deficiency.\n4. **Growth Hormone Deficiency SUSPECTED**: Low IGF-1 warrants formal GH stimulation testing.\n5. **Mild Hyperprolactinemia**: Elevated prolactin likely reflects **stalk effect** (loss of dopaminergic inhibition from hypothalamus), consistent with MRI showing stalk thinning.\n6. **Posterior Pituitary INTACT**: Normal sodium argues against diabetes insipidus.")
B.aUN=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You now have a diagnosis of post-traumatic panhypopituitarism. The patient needs hormone replacement and ongoing rehabilitation coordination.")
B.b16=new A.c("+ Outline the hormone replacement hierarchy and discuss implications for rehabilitation.","**Hormone Replacement \u2014 ORDER MATTERS**:\nThe replacement hierarchy is critical because initiating thyroid hormone before cortisol replacement can precipitate an **adrenal crisis**.\n\n1. **FIRST \u2014 Glucocorticoid Replacement**:\n   - Hydrocortisone 15-20 mg daily in divided doses (10 mg AM, 5 mg afternoon)\n   - Educate on **stress dosing**: Double or triple the dose during illness, surgery, or physiological stress\n   - Provide a **medical alert bracelet** and emergency injection kit (Solu-Cortef 100 mg IM)\n   - **Do NOT start thyroid replacement until cortisol is replaced**\n\n2. **SECOND \u2014 Thyroid Replacement**:\n   - Levothyroxine 50-75 mcg daily (start low, titrate)\n   - Monitor with **free T4 only** (TSH is unreliable in central hypothyroidism)\n   - Target free T4 in the upper half of normal range\n\n3. **THIRD \u2014 Gonadal Replacement**:\n   - Estrogen/progesterone replacement therapy for bone health, cardiovascular protection, and symptom management\n   - If future fertility is desired, refer to **reproductive endocrinology** for pulsatile GnRH therapy or gonadotropin injections\n   - DEXA scan to assess bone mineral density (hypogonadism accelerates osteoporosis)\n\n4. **FOURTH \u2014 Growth Hormone** (after formal stimulation testing confirms deficiency):\n   - Recombinant GH (somatropin) starting at low dose (0.2 mg/day SQ), titrate based on IGF-1 levels\n   - Benefits in TBI: Improved body composition, cognitive function, exercise capacity, quality of life, and fatigue\n\n**Rehabilitation Implications**:\n- **Fatigue management**: Expect significant improvement in therapy participation once thyroid and cortisol are replaced. Adjust therapy intensity accordingly.\n- **Exercise tolerance**: Adrenal insufficiency limits exercise stress response \u2014 therapists must be educated on symptoms of adrenal crisis (hypotension, confusion, nausea)\n- **Cognitive recovery**: GH deficiency independently impairs cognition. Some patients show cognitive improvement with GH replacement.\n- **Body composition**: Hormone replacement combined with progressive resistance training can reverse the catabolic body composition changes.\n- **Bone health**: Initiate calcium (1200 mg/day) and vitamin D (2000 IU/day). Fall prevention program given osteoporosis risk.")
B.aT1=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","This patient now requires coordination between multiple specialists (endocrinology, brain injury medicine, psychiatry, gynecology) in addition to her ongoing rehabilitation team. She lives 90 minutes from the nearest academic medical center.")
B.aRW=new A.c("+ Discuss the systems-based approach to long-term endocrine follow-up coordination in TBI survivors.","**Long-Term Monitoring Schedule**:\n1. **Endocrine labs every 3-6 months** in the first year of replacement, then annually once stable:\n   - Free T4 (NOT TSH) for thyroid monitoring\n   - AM cortisol before daily hydrocortisone dose (assess adequacy)\n   - Estradiol levels and menstrual regularity\n   - IGF-1 if on GH replacement\n2. **DEXA scan** at baseline, then every 2 years to monitor bone mineral density\n3. **Annual screening** for new pituitary deficiencies \u2014 axes can fail progressively over years\n\n**Care Coordination Strategies**:\n1. **Designate a primary coordinator**: The PM&R brain injury specialist should serve as the medical home, coordinating referrals and synthesizing recommendations across specialties.\n2. **Telemedicine**: Endocrine follow-up can often be conducted via telehealth, reducing travel burden for a patient living 90 minutes from the academic center. Labs can be drawn locally.\n3. **Shared Electronic Health Record**: Ensure all providers have access to a unified medical record or receive copies of all specialist notes.\n4. **Patient Education & Self-Management**:\n   - Teach the patient and a family member to recognize signs of adrenal crisis\n   - Provide written sick-day rules for glucocorticoid stress dosing\n   - Ensure she carries medical alert identification at all times\n5. **Transition Planning**: Establish a clear long-term follow-up schedule before discharging from active rehabilitation. Ensure primary care provider is educated on post-traumatic hypopituitarism.\n\n**Quality Improvement Opportunity**:\n- Implement a **standardized endocrine screening protocol** for all moderate-severe TBI patients at 3-6 months and 12 months post-injury in your rehabilitation program.\n- This is a widely underdiagnosed condition \u2014 studies suggest fewer than 10-15% of eligible TBI patients receive appropriate hormonal screening.")
B.aZ8=new A.c(u.j,"During the visit, the patient becomes tearful and says: 'I feel like I will never be done dealing with this accident. Every time I think I'm getting better, something new goes wrong. First my brain, then my balance, now my hormones. I'm gaining weight, I have no energy, and I don't even feel like a woman anymore. When does this end?'")
B.aSJ=new A.c("+ Provide an empathetic response that validates her frustration while providing a realistic and hopeful framework.","I would respond: 'I hear you, and your frustration is completely valid. You have been through an incredibly difficult experience, and I want you to know that what you are feeling right now is not a sign that things are getting worse \u2014 it is actually a sign that we are getting closer to understanding the full picture of your recovery.\n\nHere is what I want you to understand: the fatigue, the weight gain, the changes to your cycle \u2014 these are not random setbacks. They have a specific, identifiable medical cause. Your brain injury affected your pituitary gland, which is the master hormone regulator of your body. Now that we have identified this, we can treat it directly.\n\nMany of the symptoms that have been making your life so difficult \u2014 the exhaustion, the weight changes, the mood symptoms, even some of the cognitive fogginess \u2014 are likely being driven in large part by these hormone deficiencies. Once we begin replacing these hormones in the correct order, most patients experience significant improvement in energy, mood, and body composition over the next several weeks to months.\n\nI am not going to tell you this journey has a simple endpoint, because recovery from a brain injury is a process. But I will tell you three things: First, you have already made remarkable physical recovery. Second, we now have a treatable explanation for your current symptoms. Third, you are not facing this alone \u2014 we have an entire team working together on your behalf.\n\nLet us start treatment today and set a follow-up in 6 weeks. I expect you will notice meaningful differences by then. And at every step, I want you to tell me how you are feeling, because your experience matters to how we adjust this plan.'")
B.aDd=s([B.aOU,B.aZV,B.aOf,B.aOV,B.aPY,B.b1i,B.aUN,B.b16,B.aT1,B.aRW,B.aZ8,B.aSJ],t.E)
B.QC=new A.a5("tbi-pituitary-dysfunction","TBI-Induced Pituitary Dysfunction",B.aDd,!1)
B.aXs=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 25-year-old male is transferred to your inpatient rehabilitation unit 3 weeks after sustaining a gunshot wound (GSW) to the right frontal lobe during an altercation. The bullet entered through the right frontal bone and lodged in the right frontal white matter; neurosurgery performed an emergent craniotomy with debridement and dural repair. Post-operatively, he developed right-sided cerebral edema requiring a decompressive craniectomy with a bone flap stored in an abdominal subcutaneous pocket. CT head on transfer shows encephalomalacia in the right orbitofrontal and dorsolateral prefrontal cortex with residual metallic fragments. He is now medically stable but exhibiting severe behavioral disturbances including impulsive aggression toward staff, sexually inappropriate comments to female nurses, perseverative profanity, and inability to follow the therapy schedule. He has pulled out his IV line twice and attempted to leave the unit. His GCS at admission is E4V4M6 (14). Rancho Los Amigos Level V (Confused, Inappropriate, Non-Agitated alternating with Level IV episodes of agitation).")
B.aSM=new A.c("History & Systems Review:",'**Injury Details & Neurosurgical Course**:\n- **Mechanism**: GSW, low-velocity handgun round (9mm) at close range, right frontal entry wound\n- **Initial GCS**: 7 (E1V2M4) in the field\n- **Surgical interventions**: Emergent craniotomy with debridement and dural repair (day 0), decompressive craniectomy (day 2) for refractory ICP elevation\n- **ICU course**: 10 days in neuro-ICU, required intracranial pressure monitoring (peak ICP 32 mmHg), started on levetiracetam 500 mg BID for seizure prophylaxis\n- **Infections**: CSF cultures grew coagulase-negative Staphylococcus on day 5, treated with 14-day course of IV vancomycin (completed)\n- **Imaging**: CT on transfer shows right frontal encephalomalacia, retained metallic fragments (non-ferromagnetic per neurosurgery), absent right frontal bone flap\n\n**Pre-Injury History**:\n- No prior TBI or neurological history\n- Employed as an auto mechanic, high school diploma\n- **Substance use**: Occasional marijuana, social alcohol (2-3 beers on weekends), denies IV drug use\n- **Psychiatric history**: None documented, though family reports he was "always hot-headed"\n- **Social**: Lives with girlfriend and 2-year-old daughter in an apartment. Girlfriend is present and engaged but visibly frightened by his behavior changes\n\n**Current Medications**: Levetiracetam 500 mg BID, acetaminophen 650 mg PRN, docusate sodium 100 mg BID, melatonin 3 mg QHS, haloperidol 2 mg IM PRN for acute agitation (given 3 times in last 48 hours)\n\n**Systems Review**:\n- **Neuro/Behavioral**: Disinhibited, impulsive, sexually inappropriate verbalizations, low frustration tolerance with rapid escalation to physical aggression, poor insight into deficits, confabulation\n- **Cognition**: Disoriented to time and situation, follows 1-step commands inconsistently, attention span <2 minutes, severe executive dysfunction\n- **Sleep**: Fragmented sleep-wake cycle, sundowning behavior\n- **GI**: Tolerating regular diet, intermittent constipation\n- **GU**: Voiding independently but with frequent incontinence episodes due to behavioral rather than neurogenic causes')
B.aNp=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:",'- **Vitals**: BP 138/82 mmHg, HR 92 bpm, RR 16, Temp 98.9F, O2 Sat 98% on room air\n- **General**: Agitated young male in wheelchair with helmet in place over craniectomy site. Intermittently yelling profanities. Right frontal scalp wound well-healed. Palpable cranial defect over right frontal region.\n- **HEENT**: Right frontal craniectomy site \u2014 soft, concave, well-healed incision without erythema or drainage. Abdominal incision over right lower quadrant (bone flap pocket) clean, dry, intact.\n- **Neurological**:\n  - **Mental status**: Alert but confused. Oriented to self only. Speech is fluent but content is tangential and frequently inappropriate. Confabulates when asked about his injury ("I got in a fight and won").\n  - **Cranial nerves**: Left visual field cut (left homonymous hemianopia). Pupils 3mm bilaterally, reactive. Remainder intact.\n  - **Motor**: Left upper extremity 4/5 throughout (mild hemiparesis), left lower extremity 4+/5. Right side 5/5.\n  - **Sensation**: Decreased light touch on left upper extremity. Left-sided extinction on double simultaneous stimulation.\n  - **Tone**: Mildly increased tone in left upper extremity (Modified Ashworth 1+).\n  - **Coordination**: Unable to assess formally due to behavioral non-compliance.\n  - **Reflexes**: 3+ left biceps, triceps, brachioradialis; 2+ on right. Left Babinski positive.\n- **Skin**: Multiple ecchymoses on bilateral forearms from self-inflicted striking against wheelchair armrests. No pressure injuries.')
B.b0s=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","This patient presents with a penetrating traumatic brain injury (pTBI) with right frontal lobe damage resulting in a neurobehavioral syndrome characterized by disinhibition, impulsive aggression, and executive dysfunction \u2014 the classic orbitofrontal syndrome. The penetrating mechanism introduces unique considerations compared to closed TBI.")
B.aQQ=new A.c("+ Discuss the key differences between penetrating and closed TBI and the specific risks in this case.","**Penetrating vs. Closed TBI \u2014 Key Differences**:\n\n1. **Pathophysiology**:\n   - Penetrating TBI causes **focal tissue destruction** along the missile tract with surrounding contusion and edema, whereas closed TBI typically produces diffuse axonal injury with coup-contrecoup contusions\n   - Penetrating injuries create direct communication between the intracranial space and the external environment, dramatically increasing **infection risk**\n   - Retained metallic fragments create a **chronic foreign body reaction** and serve as a seizure focus\n\n2. **Seizure Risk**:\n   - Penetrating TBI carries a **30-50% lifetime risk** of post-traumatic epilepsy compared to 5-10% for severe closed TBI\n   - Risk factors include: retained metallic fragments, involvement of the motor cortex, dural penetration, intracranial hemorrhage, and frontal/temporal lobe injury \u2014 this patient has multiple risk factors\n   - **Seizure prophylaxis**: Per the Brain Trauma Foundation, levetiracetam or phenytoin is recommended for 7 days post-injury for early seizure prevention. For penetrating TBI specifically, **extended prophylaxis is often continued** beyond 7 days given the higher risk, though evidence for long-term prophylactic AEDs is debated\n   - This patient should continue levetiracetam and be monitored closely; conversion to long-term AED therapy is warranted if seizures develop\n\n3. **Infection Risk**:\n   - **CNS infection** rates in penetrating TBI range from 5-23%\n   - This patient already had early CSF infection treated with vancomycin\n   - Ongoing risks include: brain abscess (may present weeks later), subdural empyema, meningitis, osteomyelitis of the cranial bone flap\n   - Monitor for fever, altered mental status changes (difficult in this patient), new neurological deficits, wound drainage\n   - Retained fragments are a nidus for late infection\n\n4. **Craniectomy Considerations**:\n   - The cranial defect requires **helmet use at all times** until cranioplasty\n   - Cranioplasty is typically performed 3-6 months after injury once edema has resolved and infection is cleared\n   - **Syndrome of the trephined**: progressive neurological decline due to atmospheric pressure on exposed brain \u2014 may present as worsening cognition, mood changes, and motor decline. Can mimic behavioral plateau in rehab.\n\n5. **Behavioral Localization**:\n   - Right orbitofrontal damage explains disinhibition, poor social judgment, and sexually inappropriate behavior\n   - Right dorsolateral prefrontal damage explains executive dysfunction, impaired working memory, and poor behavioral regulation\n   - Left homonymous hemianopia localizes to the right optic radiation involvement")
B.b18=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT",'The nursing staff reports escalating difficulty managing this patient. He struck a physical therapy aide yesterday, makes sexually explicit comments to female staff every session, and has threatened to "walk out of here." The haloperidol PRN is being used frequently but causes excessive sedation that impairs his therapy participation.')
B.aX5=new A.c("+ Outline a comprehensive behavioral management strategy including pharmacological and non-pharmacological approaches, and discuss restraint alternatives.","**Non-Pharmacological Management (FIRST LINE)**:\n\n1. **Structured Environment**:\n   - Consistent daily schedule posted visually at bedside\n   - Low-stimulation room (private, reduced noise, dimmed lights when possible)\n   - Limit visitors to 1-2 familiar people at a time\n   - Redirect rather than confront \u2014 avoid power struggles\n   - Use calm, simple, repetitive instructions\n\n2. **Behavioral Strategies**:\n   - **Antecedent management**: Identify and eliminate triggers (overstimulation, fatigue, pain, hunger, need to toilet)\n   - **Structured therapy sessions**: Short (15-20 minutes), predictable, with built-in breaks\n   - **Positive reinforcement**: Reward calm behavior with preferred activities or items\n   - **De-escalation protocol**: Train all staff in CPI (Crisis Prevention Institute) techniques\n   - **1:1 sitter** during high-risk periods (evenings, transitions)\n\n3. **Staff Safety Strategies**:\n   - Assign male staff for direct care when possible (given sexual disinhibition toward female staff)\n   - Two-person assist for transfers and ADL training\n   - Remove potential projectiles from the room\n   - Establish clear team communication with behavioral tracking sheets (ABC data \u2014 Antecedent, Behavior, Consequence)\n\n**Pharmacological Management**:\n\n1. **Discontinue haloperidol** \u2014 First-generation antipsychotics impair dopaminergic neurotransmission critical for neuroplasticity and cognitive recovery after TBI. They are associated with worse functional outcomes and should be avoided as maintenance agents.\n\n2. **Beta-blocker \u2014 Propranolol** (first-line for TBI agitation):\n   - Start propranolol 20 mg TID, titrate up to 60 mg TID as tolerated\n   - Monitor HR (hold if <50 bpm) and BP\n   - Evidence supports efficacy for aggression in TBI without the cognitive dulling of antipsychotics\n   - Contraindicated in asthma, severe bradycardia, or hypotension\n\n3. **Second-line agents**:\n   - **Amantadine** 100 mg BID (also promotes arousal and cognitive recovery \u2014 dual benefit)\n   - **Valproic acid** if seizures co-occur \u2014 mood stabilization and seizure control\n   - **Buspirone** 10-15 mg TID for anxiety-driven agitation\n   - **Trazodone** 50-100 mg QHS for sleep fragmentation\n\n4. **Agents to AVOID in TBI**:\n   - Typical antipsychotics (haloperidol) \u2014 impair recovery\n   - Benzodiazepines \u2014 paradoxical disinhibition, impair cognition and neuroplasticity\n   - Anticholinergics \u2014 worsen confusion\n\n5. **PRN Acute Agitation** (when non-pharmacological strategies fail):\n   - Olanzapine 2.5-5 mg IM (preferred over haloperidol if antipsychotic needed acutely)\n   - Short-term use only with plan to taper\n\n**Restraint Alternatives**:\n- Physical restraints are **last resort** and should be avoided \u2014 they increase agitation, cause injury, and violate patient autonomy\n- Alternatives: 1:1 sitter, enclosed low bed, padded side rails, alarm-equipped wheelchair, secured unit with wanderguard system\n- If restraints are absolutely necessary for imminent safety threat: use least restrictive method, physician order required, reassess every 1-2 hours, document ongoing need")
B.aOD=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient's behavioral dysregulation is severe enough that the therapy team is struggling to provide 3 hours of daily therapy required for acute inpatient rehabilitation. His FIM scores are stagnant. The insurance case manager is requesting justification for continued acute rehab stay.")
B.aXa=new A.c("+ Discuss the systems-level considerations including level of care decisions, legal issues with involuntary treatment, and appropriate referrals.","**Level of Care Decision**:\n- If the patient cannot participate in the **3-hour rule** (minimum 3 hours of multidisciplinary therapy per day, 5 days per week) required for acute inpatient rehabilitation, alternative settings must be considered\n- **Options**:\n  1. **Neurobehavioral specialty unit**: Dedicated TBI behavioral units (e.g., programs at major TBI centers) are specifically designed for patients with severe post-TBI agitation. Staff are trained in behavioral management, environments are secured, and therapy is adapted to the patient's level. This is the **ideal next step** if available.\n  2. **Subacute/skilled nursing facility with brain injury experience**: If a neurobehavioral unit is not available, a SNF with TBI experience can provide a lower-intensity program while behavioral management is optimized\n  3. **Psychiatric unit**: Generally NOT appropriate \u2014 psychiatric units lack TBI expertise and may use medications harmful to brain recovery\n\n**Insurance Justification Strategy**:\n- Document that behavioral dysregulation is a **direct consequence of the brain injury** and constitutes a medical condition requiring specialized rehabilitation\n- Show a clear pharmacological optimization plan with expected timeline (propranolol titration typically shows effect within 1-2 weeks)\n- Request authorization for neurobehavioral specialty program if available\n- Demonstrate that without continued rehabilitation, the patient is at high risk for institutionalization, creating far greater long-term cost\n\n**Legal Considerations**:\n- This patient lacks **decisional capacity** due to his brain injury (disoriented, poor insight, unable to understand risks/benefits of treatment)\n- **Surrogate decision-maker**: The girlfriend is not a legal surrogate unless designated by healthcare proxy or court order. Identify next of kin hierarchy per state law (typically parents if unmarried)\n- **Involuntary treatment**: If the patient attempts to leave AMA and lacks capacity, an **emergency petition** or physician hold may be warranted if he poses danger to himself or others\n- Consult hospital ethics committee and legal counsel for guidance on capacity determination and guardianship proceedings\n- **Criminal case implications**: If law enforcement is involved, ensure HIPAA protections are maintained. Do not release medical records without proper legal authorization.")
B.aMY=new A.c(u.j,"During a therapy session, the patient makes sexually explicit comments to a female occupational therapist, describing in graphic detail what he wants to do to her. She is visibly shaken and leaves the room. She later tells you she does not feel safe treating him and is considering filing a complaint.")
B.aTD=new A.c("+ How do you address this situation \u2014 supporting the staff member, managing the patient's behavior, and maintaining a therapeutic alliance?","**Immediate Response to the Staff Member**:\n\n'I want you to know that what happened is completely unacceptable, and your safety and well-being are a priority. You have every right to feel upset, and I take this very seriously. Let me be clear \u2014 you are not obligated to continue treating him, and I will never ask you to work in a situation where you feel unsafe.\n\nI also want to help you understand the medical context, not as an excuse, but because it is important for processing what happened. His right frontal lobe injury has destroyed the brain circuits responsible for social inhibition, impulse control, and behavioral regulation. Before his injury, he may not have had these behaviors. What you experienced is a direct manifestation of his brain damage \u2014 similar to how a stroke patient cannot move a paralyzed limb, he currently cannot inhibit these impulses.\n\nThat said, this explanation does not diminish your experience. We will make the following changes: I am reassigning his OT sessions to a male therapist for now, I am adjusting his behavioral medication regimen today, and I am implementing a formal behavioral protocol with clear de-escalation steps. If you want to file a formal incident report, I fully support that, and I will ensure our documentation reflects the neurological basis of the behavior.'\n\n**Addressing the Patient** (during a calm period):\n\n'[Patient name], I need to talk to you about something important. During your therapy session today, you said things to your therapist that were not appropriate. I know your brain injury makes it very hard to control what you say right now, and we are going to keep working on that. But we need to have some rules \u2014 when you feel the urge to say something like that, I need you to try to stop and take a breath. We are going to practice this together. Your therapy team is here to help you, and we need everyone to feel safe so we can keep helping you get better.'\n\n**Systemic Changes**:\n- Implement a formal **behavioral contract** with visual cues in the therapy gym\n- Assign male therapists for 1:1 sessions until pharmacological management takes effect\n- Schedule team debriefing to discuss behavioral plan and ensure all staff are trained on de-escalation\n- Document the incident thoroughly as a neurobehavioral event with clear clinical context\n- Ensure the staff member has access to employee assistance program (EAP) resources")
B.aws=s([B.aXs,B.aSM,B.aNp,B.b0s,B.aQQ,B.b18,B.aX5,B.aOD,B.aXa,B.aMY,B.aTD],t.E)
B.R2=new A.a5("tbi-penetrating-behavioral","Penetrating TBI with Behavioral Dysregulation",B.aws,!1)
B.b2n=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 40-year-old female is undergoing acute inpatient rehabilitation for a severe traumatic brain injury sustained 3 months ago in a motor vehicle collision (unrestrained driver, frontal impact at highway speed). Her initial GCS was 6, and CT showed bifrontal contusions, a small right temporal epidural hematoma (managed conservatively), and diffuse subarachnoid hemorrhage. She underwent ICP monitoring for 5 days in the neuro-ICU. She was started on levetiracetam 500 mg BID for early seizure prophylaxis, which was discontinued at 7 days per protocol. She has been progressing well in rehabilitation \u2014 Rancho Level VII, FIM motor score improving from 42 to 68 over the past 3 weeks. Today, during a physical therapy session on the balance beam, the PT observes the patient suddenly stop mid-stride, stare blankly for approximately 5 seconds, then develop bilateral tonic stiffening of all extremities followed by rhythmic clonic jerking lasting approximately 90 seconds. She falls off the balance beam (padded surface, 6 inches above ground). Post-ictally, she is unresponsive for 4 minutes, then gradually regains awareness over 15 minutes with confusion and left-sided weakness that resolves over 30 minutes (Todd's paralysis).")
B.aNz=new A.c("History & Systems Review:",'**Seizure Event Details**:\n- **Witnessed by PT**: Onset with behavioral arrest and staring (likely focal onset with impaired awareness) followed by bilateral tonic-clonic activity \u2014 classified as **focal to bilateral tonic-clonic seizure**\n- **Duration**: Approximately 90 seconds of convulsive activity\n- **Post-ictal**: Unresponsive for 4 minutes, confused for 15 minutes, left-sided Todd\'s paralysis lasting 30 minutes (lateralizing to right hemisphere seizure focus, consistent with right temporal contusion)\n- **Injury during seizure**: Fall from balance beam \u2014 low height, padded surface. No apparent head strike, no lacerations. Tongue not bitten. No urinary incontinence noted.\n- **Prior seizure history**: None witnessed during this admission. However, nursing reports 2 episodes in the past week where the patient "zoned out" for 10-15 seconds during conversation \u2014 likely unrecognized focal seizures with impaired awareness.\n\n**TBI-Related History**:\n- **Injury**: MVC, initial GCS 6, bifrontal contusions, right temporal EDH, diffuse SAH\n- **Post-traumatic amnesia**: 21 days (severe TBI by PTA criteria)\n- **Current Rancho Level**: VII (Automatic-Appropriate)\n- **Cognitive status**: Improving executive function, mild word-finding difficulty, moderate impairment in complex attention and working memory\n- **Motor status**: Independent ambulation with standby assist for balance, left UE fine motor deficits improving\n\n**Past Medical History**: Migraines (well-controlled, last episode 2 years ago), anxiety (managed with sertraline 50 mg daily), G2P2 (two healthy children, ages 8 and 12)\n\n**Social History**: Divorced, single mother, works full-time as an elementary school teacher. Her mother has been caring for the children during her hospitalization. She drives herself and the children to school daily \u2014 this is essential for her return to independent living.\n\n**Current Medications**: Sertraline 50 mg daily, acetaminophen 650 mg PRN, melatonin 3 mg QHS, docusate sodium 100 mg BID, multivitamin daily')
B.aSS=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Vitals** (post-ictal, 30 minutes after seizure): BP 148/88 mmHg, HR 98 bpm, RR 18, Temp 99.1F, O2 Sat 97% on room air, Glucose 142 mg/dL\n- **General**: Awake, drowsy, oriented to self and place but not date. Speaking in short sentences. No acute distress.\n- **HEENT**: No external signs of head trauma from the fall. No tongue laceration. Well-healed right temporal craniotomy scar from ICP monitor placement.\n- **Neurological**:\n  - **Mental status**: Post-ictal confusion resolving. Following 2-step commands. Naming 2/3 objects. Short-term recall 1/3 at 5 minutes.\n  - **Cranial nerves**: Intact. Visual fields full to confrontation.\n  - **Motor**: Left upper extremity 4+/5 (near baseline \u2014 was 4+/5 prior to seizure). Left lower extremity 5/5. Right side 5/5. Todd's paralysis has resolved.\n  - **Sensation**: Intact to light touch throughout.\n  - **Reflexes**: 2+ throughout, symmetric. No Babinski.\n  - **Coordination**: Finger-to-nose mildly dysmetric on left (baseline finding).\n- **Skin**: No injuries from the fall. No incontinence-related skin breakdown.\n- **Cardiac**: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs.")
B.aTv=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","This patient has had her first witnessed generalized tonic-clonic seizure 3 months after a severe TBI. This constitutes a late post-traumatic seizure, which has significant implications for diagnosis, management, and long-term prognosis.")
B.b0R=new A.c("+ Differentiate early vs. late post-traumatic seizures, discuss EEG utility, and address the Engel classification.","**Early vs. Late Post-Traumatic Seizures (PTS)**:\n\n1. **Early PTS** (within 7 days of injury):\n   - Occur in 4-25% of TBI patients depending on severity\n   - Mechanism: Acute cellular injury, ionic shifts, glutamate excitotoxicity, blood-brain barrier disruption\n   - **Do NOT necessarily indicate epilepsy** \u2014 may be provoked/acute symptomatic seizures\n   - Prophylaxis with levetiracetam or phenytoin for 7 days is **recommended** by the Brain Trauma Foundation for severe TBI\n   - This patient received appropriate 7-day prophylaxis\n\n2. **Late PTS** (beyond 7 days post-injury):\n   - This patient's seizure at 3 months is classified as a **late post-traumatic seizure**\n   - Mechanism: Epileptogenesis \u2014 gliosis, iron deposition from hemosiderin, cortical reorganization, neuronal loss creating abnormal circuitry\n   - **Late PTS = Post-Traumatic Epilepsy (PTE)**: A single unprovoked seizure >7 days post-TBI establishes the diagnosis, as the recurrence risk exceeds 80%\n   - Risk factors for PTE in this patient: severe TBI (GCS <10), cortical contusions, subdural/epidural hematoma, subarachnoid hemorrhage, prolonged PTA, early PTS (not documented here), and frontal/temporal lobe involvement\n   - This patient has **multiple high-risk features** \u2014 her risk of recurrence is extremely high\n\n**EEG Evaluation**:\n- **Routine EEG** (20-minute) should be obtained but has limited sensitivity (~30-50%) for detecting epileptiform discharges interictally\n- **Prolonged video EEG monitoring** (24-72 hours) is preferred if available, especially to:\n  - Confirm seizure type and localization (right temporal focus suspected given Todd's paralysis lateralization)\n  - Detect subclinical seizures (the \"zoning out\" episodes may be frequent focal seizures with impaired awareness)\n  - Rule out non-epileptic events\n- Expected findings: Right temporal or bifrontal epileptiform discharges (sharp waves, spikes) consistent with her contusion locations\n- A normal interictal EEG does NOT rule out epilepsy\n\n**Engel Classification** (Used for Surgical Outcomes, not initial classification):\n- **Class I**: Seizure-free (or auras only)\n- **Class II**: Rare disabling seizures\n- **Class III**: Worthwhile improvement (>75% reduction)\n- **Class IV**: No worthwhile improvement\n- This classification is primarily used to report outcomes after epilepsy surgery, not for initial diagnosis. For PTE that becomes medically refractory, the Engel classification guides assessment of surgical candidacy and outcomes.\n\n**ILAE Seizure Classification** (more relevant here):\n- This seizure is classified as: **Focal onset, with impaired awareness, evolving to bilateral tonic-clonic**\n- The focal onset (staring, behavioral arrest) followed by bilateral spread is consistent with a structural epilepsy due to known cortical lesions")
B.aSX=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You must now initiate antiepileptic drug therapy, modify her rehabilitation program for safety, and address the significant functional implications including driving restrictions.")
B.aYl=new A.c("+ Discuss AED selection, driving restrictions, and necessary modifications to the rehabilitation program.",'**Antiepileptic Drug (AED) Selection**:\n\n1. **First-line: Levetiracetam (Keppra)**:\n   - Restart at 500 mg BID, titrate to 750-1000 mg BID over 1-2 weeks\n   - **Advantages in TBI**: No hepatic enzyme induction (important for drug interactions), no effect on bone density, weight-neutral, rapid titration, IV formulation available, does not impair cognitive recovery\n   - **Disadvantages**: Psychiatric side effects \u2014 irritability, depression, behavioral changes in up to 10-15% of patients. Given her pre-existing anxiety and the cognitive effects of TBI, monitor closely\n   - If psychiatric side effects develop, consider adding pyridoxine (vitamin B6) 50 mg daily (some evidence for mitigating levetiracetam-related irritability) or switch agents\n\n2. **Alternative agents**:\n   - **Lamotrigine**: Excellent cognitive profile, mood-stabilizing properties. Limitation is very slow titration (8 weeks to therapeutic dose) \u2014 not ideal acutely\n   - **Lacosamide**: Good tolerability, minimal cognitive effects, can be titrated faster than lamotrigine\n   - **Oxcarbazepine**: Effective for focal epilepsy but risk of hyponatremia\n\n3. **Agents to AVOID in TBI rehabilitation**:\n   - **Phenytoin**: Impairs cognitive recovery, complex pharmacokinetics, induces hepatic enzymes, causes cerebellar toxicity\n   - **Phenobarbital**: Severe cognitive sedation, impairs neuroplasticity\n   - **Topiramate**: Significant cognitive side effects ("Dopamax"), word-finding difficulty, would worsen her existing deficits\n   - **Benzodiazepines**: Impair memory, cause sedation, tolerance develops\n\n**Driving Restrictions**:\n- **Most states require a seizure-free interval** before resuming driving, typically 3-12 months depending on the state (most commonly 6 months)\n- Physician has an **obligation to inform the patient** of driving restrictions\n- Some states require **mandatory physician reporting** to the DMV (California, Delaware, Nevada, New Jersey, Oregon, Pennsylvania); others rely on self-reporting by the patient\n- This patient must be informed today that she **cannot drive** until she meets the seizure-free interval required by her state\n- Given she is a single mother who drives her children \u2014 transportation planning is critical for discharge\n\n**Rehabilitation Program Modifications**:\n1. **Fall precautions**: Upgrade to high fall risk. No unassisted balance activities, no elevated surfaces (balance beam contraindicated), no aquatic therapy unsupervised\n2. **Seizure precautions**: All therapy staff trained in seizure first aid. Keep suction and oxygen at bedside. Time seizures and document semiology.\n3. **Therapy scheduling**: Avoid scheduling high-risk activities when seizures are most likely (often early morning or with fatigue/sleep deprivation)\n4. **Helmet use**: If craniectomy site is present or skull integrity is compromised, helmet use is mandatory. In this patient with intact skull, a helmet is recommended during balance/gait training until seizure control is established\n5. **Supervision level**: Increase to contact-guard assist minimum for all mobility activities\n6. **AED blood level monitoring**: Levetiracetam levels (therapeutic range 12-46 mcg/mL) to ensure compliance and adequate dosing')
B.b3l=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient is devastated to learn she cannot drive. She says, \"I'm a single mom. I drive my kids to school. I drive to work. If I can't drive, I can't do anything. How long is this going to last?\"")
B.aMW=new A.c("+ Discuss state driving laws post-seizure, physician reporting obligations, and the systems you would mobilize to support this patient.","**State Driving Laws Post-Seizure**:\n- Driving laws vary significantly by state but generally fall into two categories:\n  1. **Mandatory reporting states** (6 states): Physicians are legally required to report seizure diagnoses to the DMV. Failure to report may expose the physician to liability.\n  2. **Voluntary reporting states** (majority): Physicians may report but are not required to. The patient is expected to self-report. However, the physician has an **ethical and legal obligation** to clearly document the counseling provided about driving restrictions.\n- **Seizure-free intervals** typically range from **3-12 months** (most states require 6 months)\n- Some states allow exceptions with neurologist certification if seizures are reliably nocturnal-only, occur only during medication changes, or have a consistent prolonged aura\n- **Documentation is critical**: Note the exact date of seizure, date driving restrictions counseled, and that the patient verbalized understanding. This protects both patient and physician.\n\n**Systems to Mobilize**:\n\n1. **Transportation**:\n   - Social work consultation for paratransit/medical transportation enrollment\n   - Coordinate with school district \u2014 many offer bus transportation that can be arranged mid-year for her children\n   - Explore ride-share program subsidies through local brain injury association\n   - Family support: Engage her mother (already helping with children) for transportation assistance\n\n2. **Return to Work**:\n   - As an elementary school teacher, she will need clearance to return with accommodations\n   - Contact the school district's human resources department regarding FMLA extension if needed\n   - ADA reasonable accommodations may include: modified schedule, avoidance of seizure-triggering environments (flashing lights, extreme fatigue), buddy system with a colleague trained in seizure first aid\n   - Discuss with neuropsychology whether her cognitive status supports safe return to teaching\n\n3. **Epilepsy Resources**:\n   - Referral to comprehensive epilepsy center/epileptologist for ongoing management\n   - Epilepsy Foundation resources: seizure action plans, support groups, legal advocacy for employment protection\n   - Medical alert bracelet recommendation\n\n4. **Childcare & Family Support**:\n   - Brain Injury Association of America (BIAA) local chapter for family resources\n   - Identify emergency childcare plan in the event of a seizure while caring for children\n   - Safety-proof the home: no unsupervised bathing, avoid cooking on open flame when alone with children, install child safety locks")
B.aRf=new A.c(u.j,"Two days after the seizure, the patient pulls you aside during rounds. She is tearful and says quietly: \"Dr. [Name], I need to tell you something, but you have to promise not to tell anyone. I had another one of those staring episodes this morning, but I didn't tell the nurses. If my employer finds out I have epilepsy, they'll fire me. I'm a teacher \u2014 they'll say I'm a danger to the kids. I can't lose my job. Please just keep this between us.\"")
B.aY8=new A.c("+ How do you navigate the ethical tension between patient confidentiality, safety obligations, and the patient's fear of workplace discrimination?","**Response**:\n\n'Thank you for trusting me enough to tell me this. I can hear how frightened you are, and I want you to know that I take your concerns about your job very seriously. Let me address several things.\n\nFirst, I need to be honest with you \u2014 I cannot promise to keep the seizure a secret from your treatment team. Your nurses and therapists need to know so they can keep you safe. If you have a seizure during therapy and we haven't prepared for it, you could be seriously hurt. Reporting this to your team is a medical safety issue, not a judgment about you.\n\nHowever, I want to be very clear about something else: your medical information is **protected by HIPAA**. Your employer does not have automatic access to your medical records. I will never contact your school district about your diagnosis without your written consent. Your epilepsy diagnosis is confidential medical information.\n\nNow, about your job \u2014 I understand your fear, but I want to give you some reassurance. **Epilepsy is a protected condition under the Americans with Disabilities Act.** Your employer cannot fire you because you have epilepsy. They are legally required to provide reasonable accommodations. And there are many teachers across this country who teach successfully with epilepsy. You are not a danger to your students.\n\nWhat I would like to do is this: First, let me document this morning's event so we can optimize your medication \u2014 these breakthrough seizures tell me your current dose may not be adequate. Second, I am going to connect you with our social worker who specializes in ADA protections and return-to-work planning. And third, when the time comes, we can work with your school district's occupational health office to develop a seizure action plan that protects both you and your students \u2014 on your terms, when you are ready.\n\nThe staring episodes are actually very important information. They tell me your seizures are not yet controlled, which means we need to adjust your medication. Hiding seizures does not make them go away \u2014 it just means we cannot treat them properly. Can we work together on this?'\n\n**Key Ethical Principles**:\n- **Duty to document**: The seizure must be documented in the medical record. Concealing medical events compromises care and creates legal liability.\n- **HIPAA protections**: Reassure the patient that her diagnosis is confidential and will not be shared with her employer without consent.\n- **ADA protections**: Educate her that employment discrimination based on epilepsy is illegal.\n- **Mandatory reporting**: In most states, a physician is not required to report seizures to an employer. If the state mandates DMV reporting, that obligation must be fulfilled but is separate from employer notification.\n- **Therapeutic alliance**: Validate her fear, provide concrete reassurance, and partner with her rather than dictating.")
B.aGf=s([B.b2n,B.aNz,B.aSS,B.aTv,B.b0R,B.aSX,B.aYl,B.b3l,B.aMW,B.aRf,B.aY8],t.E)
B.PJ=new A.a5("tbi-post-traumatic-seizures","Post-Traumatic Seizures & Anticonvulsant Management",B.aGf,!1)
B.b_V=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 33-year-old male is admitted to your acute inpatient rehabilitation unit 2 weeks after a severe traumatic brain injury sustained when he drove his pickup truck into a concrete highway median at approximately 70 mph. Emergency responders found him unresponsive with a GCS of 5 (E1V1M3). Blood alcohol level at the scene was 0.28 g/dL (legal limit 0.08). CT head revealed a large right frontotemporal contusion with 8mm of midline shift, right temporal bone fracture, and traumatic subarachnoid hemorrhage. He underwent emergent right frontotemporal craniotomy for contusion evacuation. His ICU course was complicated by alcohol withdrawal syndrome requiring a 6-day benzodiazepine taper per CIWA protocol, aspiration pneumonia treated with antibiotics, and a percutaneous gastrostomy tube placed on day 8. He is now tolerating a mechanical soft diet by mouth, the PEG is clamped, and he is at Rancho Level VI (Confused-Appropriate). He is oriented to self and place but not date. He admits to the injury but minimizes the role of alcohol: "I only had a couple beers. The road was wet."')
B.aPF=new A.c("History & Systems Review:",'**Substance Use History** (obtained from patient, family, and medical records):\n- **Alcohol**: Patient reports "social drinking" \u2014 2-3 beers a few times per week. However, his brother (primary support person) reports the patient has been drinking heavily since age 19, typically consuming a 12-pack of beer daily on weekdays and more on weekends. He has had 2 prior DUI arrests (ages 24 and 29). He was mandated to attend a DUI education program after the second arrest but did not complete it.\n- **AUDIT Score**: 28/40 (obtained from brother\'s collateral \u2014 score >20 indicates likely alcohol dependence)\n- **CAGE Questionnaire**: Positive for all 4 items \u2014 has felt need to **C**ut down, been **A**nnoyed by criticism about drinking, felt **G**uilty about drinking, and used alcohol as an **E**ye-opener in the morning\n- **Marijuana**: Daily use for "back pain" \u2014 smokes 2-3 joints per day\n- **Other substances**: Denies cocaine, opioids, methamphetamine use. UDS on admission was positive for alcohol and THC only.\n- **Withdrawal history**: Two prior episodes of alcohol withdrawal with tremors. No history of withdrawal seizures or delirium tremens prior to this admission.\n\n**TBI-Related History**:\n- **Injury severity**: GCS 5, PTA ongoing (estimated >14 days), severe TBI by all criteria\n- **Surgical**: Right frontotemporal craniotomy, bone flap replaced at time of surgery\n- **Current cognitive status**: Rancho VI, following 2-step commands, attention span 10-15 minutes, significant executive dysfunction, poor insight, memory consolidation impairment\n- **Motor**: Left hemiparesis \u2014 UE 3+/5, LE 4/5; ambulatory with rolling walker and min assist\n\n**Pre-Injury Social History**:\n- Employed intermittently as a construction worker \u2014 frequently missed work due to hangovers. Currently unemployed for 4 months prior to injury.\n- Single, never married. Has a 6-year-old son from a prior relationship; does not have custody (mother has full custody, cites his drinking)\n- Lives with his brother in a rented house\n- Education: Completed 10th grade, obtained GED\n- Legal: 2 prior DUIs, current DUI charges pending from this crash. License was suspended at time of crash.\n\n**Family History**: Father died of alcoholic cirrhosis at age 52. Mother has depression. Paternal uncle with alcohol use disorder.\n\n**Current Medications**: Levetiracetam 750 mg BID, thiamine 100 mg daily, folic acid 1 mg daily, multivitamin daily, famotidine 20 mg BID, docusate 100 mg BID, sertraline 50 mg daily (started for post-TBI depression)')
B.aYt=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Vitals**: BP 132/78 mmHg, HR 76 bpm, RR 14, Temp 98.4F, O2 Sat 98% on room air\n- **General**: Thin male (BMI 20.1, weight 148 lbs, height 5'11\") appearing older than stated age. Right frontotemporal craniotomy scar well-healed. PEG tube in place, clamped, site clean.\n- **HEENT**: Mild bilateral parotid gland enlargement. Dental examination shows multiple caries and gum recession. Spider angiomata noted on upper chest (3 lesions).\n- **Neurological**:\n  - **Mental status**: Alert, oriented to self and place. Confabulates about the date (\"It's probably Tuesday\"). Attention: serial 7s \u2014 completes 2 before losing track. Naming intact. Delayed recall 1/3 objects at 5 minutes.\n  - **Cranial nerves**: Left visual field deficit (left inferior quadrantanopia). Otherwise intact.\n  - **Motor**: Right UE and LE 5/5. Left UE: shoulder flexion 3+/5, elbow flexion 4/5, grip 3/5, finger extension 3/5. Left LE: hip flexion 4/5, knee extension 4+/5, ankle dorsiflexion 4/5.\n  - **Tone**: Left UE Modified Ashworth 1+ at elbow flexors and wrist flexors. Left LE tone normal.\n  - **Sensation**: Decreased light touch left hand and forearm. Proprioception impaired at left great toe.\n  - **Gait**: Ambulatory with rolling walker, min assist for balance. Left foot drop requiring AFO. Circumduction pattern on left.\n  - **Reflexes**: 3+ left biceps, 2+ right. Left Babinski positive.\n- **Abdomen**: Hepatomegaly \u2014 liver palpable 3 cm below right costal margin, non-tender. PEG tube site without erythema.\n- **Extremities**: Mild bilateral lower extremity edema. Palmar erythema bilateral. Dupuytren's contracture early changes in right 4th and 5th digits.\n- **Laboratory** (recent):\n  - AST 72 U/L (elevated), ALT 45 U/L (mildly elevated), GGT 128 U/L (elevated \u2014 marker of chronic alcohol use)\n  - MCV 102 fL (macrocytic \u2014 consistent with chronic alcohol use and/or folate deficiency)\n  - Albumin 3.1 g/dL (mildly low \u2014 nutritional compromise)\n  - Platelets 128,000 (mildly low \u2014 possible early portal hypertension)\n  - Thiamine and folate levels pending")
B.b_m=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","This case involves the intersection of two conditions that compound each other: traumatic brain injury and alcohol use disorder. Understanding how chronic alcohol exposure affects the brain \u2014 and how TBI further compromises it \u2014 is essential for rehabilitation planning.")
B.aOW=new A.c("+ Discuss the screening tools for alcohol use disorder and the compounding cognitive effects of chronic alcohol use combined with TBI.","**Screening Tools for Alcohol Use Disorder (AUD)**:\n\n1. **CAGE Questionnaire** (4 items):\n   - Positive if >=2/4 items endorsed. Sensitivity 75-95% for dependence.\n   - This patient scored 4/4 \u2014 highly suggestive of alcohol dependence\n   - Limitation: Does not quantify severity; may miss hazardous drinking without dependence\n\n2. **AUDIT (Alcohol Use Disorders Identification Test)** (10 items):\n   - Score 0-7: Low risk\n   - Score 8-15: Hazardous drinking\n   - Score 16-19: Harmful drinking\n   - Score 20+: Likely dependence\n   - This patient scored 28 \u2014 consistent with severe AUD\n   - More comprehensive than CAGE; WHO-developed; assesses quantity, frequency, and consequences\n\n3. **AUDIT-C** (abbreviated 3-item version): Useful for screening in rehab settings when cognitive impairment limits lengthy assessment\n\n4. **DSM-5 Criteria for Alcohol Use Disorder**: Requires 2+ of 11 criteria within 12 months. Severity: Mild (2-3), Moderate (4-5), Severe (6+). This patient likely meets criteria for **severe AUD** based on collateral history.\n\n**Compounding Cognitive Effects of Chronic Alcohol + TBI**:\n\n1. **Pre-existing Alcohol-Related Brain Damage**:\n   - Chronic heavy alcohol use causes: cerebellar degeneration (ataxia, balance impairment), frontal lobe volume loss (executive dysfunction), hippocampal atrophy (memory impairment), corpus callosum thinning, and peripheral neuropathy\n   - **Wernicke-Korsakoff Spectrum**: Thiamine deficiency from chronic alcohol causes Wernicke encephalopathy (confusion, ataxia, ophthalmoplegia) acutely, and Korsakoff syndrome (severe anterograde amnesia, confabulation) chronically. The confabulation seen in this patient could reflect Korsakoff overlay on TBI.\n   - This patient's hepatomegaly, macrocytosis, elevated GGT, spider angiomata, and palmar erythema confirm end-organ damage from chronic alcohol use\n\n2. **TBI Superimposed on Alcohol-Damaged Brain**:\n   - The brain has **reduced cognitive reserve** \u2014 the ability to compensate for injury is diminished when baseline function is already compromised\n   - Chronic alcohol users have **worse TBI outcomes**: longer PTA, more severe cognitive impairment, slower recovery trajectory, and higher mortality\n   - Both conditions affect **overlapping neural systems** \u2014 frontal executive networks, hippocampal memory circuits, and cerebellar motor coordination\n   - Differentiating TBI-related cognitive deficits from alcohol-related deficits can be extremely difficult and requires serial neuropsychological testing over time\n\n3. **Relapse Risk**:\n   - TBI-related executive dysfunction and impulsivity dramatically increase relapse risk\n   - Patients with TBI + AUD have **relapse rates of 25-50%** within the first year after injury\n   - The combination is bidirectional: alcohol worsens TBI recovery, and TBI-related impulsivity worsens alcohol control")
B.aPM=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You need to develop a dual-diagnosis treatment plan that addresses both TBI rehabilitation and alcohol use disorder simultaneously, as treating one without the other will lead to failure in both.")
B.aUi=new A.c("+ Outline a dual-diagnosis treatment approach including pharmacological options and rehabilitation-integrated substance abuse treatment.",'**Integrated Dual-Diagnosis Treatment Plan**:\n\n1. **Motivational Interviewing (MI) \u2014 Cornerstone Approach**:\n   - MI is the preferred counseling approach for AUD in TBI because it is non-confrontational, patient-centered, and effective even with cognitive impairment\n   - Key principles: Express empathy, develop discrepancy (between current behavior and goals), roll with resistance, support self-efficacy\n   - Example for this patient: Help him connect alcohol use to the loss of his son\'s custody, his DUI history, and this life-threatening injury \u2014 without lecturing or judging\n   - Adapt for cognitive deficits: use simple language, visual aids, repetition, shorter sessions (15-20 minutes), written summaries\n   - This patient is currently in the **pre-contemplation/contemplation stage** of change (minimizes alcohol role) \u2014 confrontational approaches will backfire\n\n2. **Pharmacological Treatment for AUD**:\n   - **Naltrexone** (preferred agent):\n     - 50 mg oral daily (start after ensuring no opioid use for 7-10 days)\n     - Opioid antagonist that reduces alcohol craving and "reward" from drinking\n     - **Contraindication**: Active hepatic failure (AST/ALT >3-5x normal) \u2014 this patient\'s liver enzymes are only mildly elevated, so naltrexone can be used with monitoring\n     - Monitor LFTs monthly for the first 3 months\n     - Alternative: IM naltrexone (Vivitrol) 380 mg monthly \u2014 better for adherence in patients with cognitive impairment who may forget daily pills\n   - **Acamprosate** (alternative):\n     - 666 mg TID \u2014 reduces glutamate excitotoxicity and modulates GABA\n     - Safe in liver disease (renally eliminated)\n     - Limitation: TID dosing is challenging for patients with memory impairment\n   - **Disulfiram (Antabuse)**: Generally **NOT recommended** in TBI \u2014 requires intact executive function to understand consequences of drinking while on medication. Hepatotoxic. Risk of severe reaction in a patient who may impulsively drink.\n\n3. **Nutritional Rehabilitation**:\n   - Continue **thiamine 100 mg daily** (lifelong in chronic alcohol users) \u2014 critical for preventing Wernicke-Korsakoff\n   - **Folic acid** 1 mg daily for macrocytic anemia\n   - **Nutritional supplementation**: High-calorie, high-protein diet. Albumin of 3.1 indicates nutritional depletion. Target BMI >22.\n   - Dietitian consultation for meal planning that supports both brain recovery and hepatic health\n\n4. **Rehabilitation-Integrated Substance Abuse Treatment**:\n   - **Psychology**: Weekly individual sessions using MI and CBT adapted for TBI\n   - **Group therapy**: Modified for cognitive accessibility \u2014 shorter sessions, visual aids, concrete examples, repetition\n   - **AA/12-step referral**: Introduce the concept but recognize that traditional AA may be challenging for TBI patients due to cognitive demands. Consider **brain injury-specific support groups** if available.\n   - **Education**: Provide TBI-adapted education about alcohol\'s effects on the healing brain using simple, concrete language with visual materials\n\n5. **Relapse Prevention Planning**:\n   - Identify triggers (stress, social situations, boredom, pain)\n   - Develop a concrete, written relapse prevention plan with the patient\n   - Involve the brother as a sober support person\n   - Plan for environmental modifications: remove alcohol from the home, avoid bars and drinking friends\n   - Establish a "crisis plan" with emergency numbers if craving becomes overwhelming')
B.b1o=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","As discharge approaches, the patient will need ongoing dual-diagnosis support. His brother is committed to helping but has limited resources. The patient has no insurance (he lost his Medicaid eligibility 6 months ago when he failed to recertify).")
B.aQ2=new A.c("+ Discuss the discharge planning for a dual-diagnosis TBI/AUD patient with limited resources, including sober living, outpatient programs, and community supports.","**Discharge Planning for Dual-Diagnosis TBI/AUD**:\n\n1. **Insurance & Benefits**:\n   - Re-apply for **Medicaid** immediately \u2014 social work should initiate this during the inpatient stay. His TBI with functional impairments will likely qualify him under disability criteria.\n   - Apply for **Social Security Disability Insurance (SSDI)** if he has sufficient work credits, or **Supplemental Security Income (SSI)** if not\n   - Explore **Medicaid waiver programs** for TBI (available in many states) which provide home and community-based services\n\n2. **Residential Options**:\n   - **Sober living facility**: A structured, substance-free residential environment is ideal. However, most sober living homes are not designed for TBI patients with cognitive impairment.\n   - **TBI-specific transitional living programs**: Some states and brain injury organizations offer supervised residential programs for TBI survivors. These are the optimal setting \u2014 they provide both structure and rehabilitation support.\n   - **Brother's home**: If residential programs are unavailable, discharge to brother's home with clear conditions: alcohol-free household, structured daily schedule, brother trained in behavioral and seizure management\n\n3. **Outpatient Substance Abuse Programs**:\n   - **Intensive outpatient program (IOP)**: 9-12 hours per week of group and individual therapy. Seek programs experienced with dual diagnosis.\n   - **SAMHSA Treatment Locator** (findtreatment.gov): Federal resource to identify local substance abuse treatment programs, many of which offer sliding-scale fees\n   - **State-funded treatment**: Most states have public substance abuse treatment funded through block grants \u2014 available regardless of insurance status\n   - Adapt program for TBI: shorter sessions, repetition, written materials, cognitive accommodations\n\n4. **Outpatient TBI Rehabilitation**:\n   - Outpatient PT/OT/speech therapy \u2014 coordinate with substance abuse treatment schedule\n   - Neuropsychological re-evaluation at 6 months and 12 months post-injury\n   - TBI clinic follow-up for seizure management, cranioplasty planning, and cognitive monitoring\n\n5. **Community Supports**:\n   - **Brain Injury Association** local chapter \u2014 peer mentoring, support groups, resource navigation\n   - **AA meetings** \u2014 identify meetings near brother's home, consider having brother attend with him initially for cognitive support\n   - **Vocational rehabilitation**: State VR services for eventual return-to-work assessment and supported employment\n   - **Legal aid**: Connect with legal services for pending DUI charges \u2014 a brain injury may be relevant to legal proceedings\n\n6. **Monitoring & Follow-Up**:\n   - Establish a care coordinator/case manager to oversee the transition\n   - Random alcohol testing through outpatient program\n   - LFT monitoring every 3 months while on naltrexone\n   - Clear plan for intervention if relapse occurs \u2014 not punitive but a return to more intensive treatment")
B.b1q=new A.c(u.j,"The patient's brother and elderly mother visit during family conference. When you begin discussing the alcohol use disorder and the need for substance abuse treatment, the mother interrupts: \"Doctor, please. He's been through enough. He almost died. Can't you just let him have a beer now and then? It's the only thing that relaxes him. He's not an alcoholic \u2014 his father was an alcoholic. [Patient] just likes to have a good time. Don't take that away from him too.\"")
B.aP_=new A.c("+ How do you respond to the family's enabling while maintaining empathy and a therapeutic alliance?",'**Response**:\n\n\'Mrs. [Name], I can see how much you love your son, and I understand that after nearly losing him, the last thing you want is for him to suffer more restrictions. You have been through an incredibly difficult time as a family, and I respect that.\n\nI need to share some things with you that are important, and I ask you to hear me out because this comes from a place of wanting the best outcome for [patient name].\n\nYour son\'s blood alcohol level when the paramedics found him was 0.28 \u2014 that is more than three times the legal limit. At that level, most people would be severely impaired. He was driving at 70 miles per hour. He very nearly died that night, and the brain injury he sustained has changed his life. I say this not to blame him but because we cannot help him recover if we do not address what led to this injury.\n\nI also need to be medically direct with you: alcohol is toxic to a healing brain. After a traumatic brain injury, even small amounts of alcohol can worsen cognitive recovery, increase seizure risk, impair balance and coordination, and interact dangerously with his medications. This is not about punishment or taking something away \u2014 it is about protecting the brain recovery we have all been working so hard for.\n\nI know you said his father struggled with alcohol. I want you to know that alcohol use disorder has a strong genetic component. What I see in your son\'s medical history \u2014 the daily drinking, the two prior DUIs, the withdrawal symptoms he experienced in the ICU \u2014 these tell me that his relationship with alcohol is more than "having a good time." And that is not a moral failing. It is a medical condition, just like his brain injury.\n\nWhat I am asking for is not forever \u2014 it is for right now. His brain needs every possible advantage to heal. And the best thing your family can do for him is to support a sober environment. That means no alcohol in the home, not offering him a drink, and gently redirecting if he asks for one.\'\n\n*Turning to the brother*: \'[Brother\'s name], I know you are going to be his primary support at home. What are your thoughts about keeping the home alcohol-free during his recovery?\'\n\n**Key Communication Strategies Used**:\n- **Validated the mother\'s emotions** before delivering difficult information\n- **Used objective medical data** (BAL 0.28) rather than judgments to counter minimization\n- **Framed sobriety as medical treatment** rather than punishment or deprivation\n- **Addressed the genetic component** gently since the mother raised the father\'s alcoholism herself\n- **Avoided the label "alcoholic"** \u2014 used "alcohol use disorder" and "medical condition"\n- **Engaged the brother** as the practical ally for discharge planning\n- **Set a time frame** ("right now") rather than demanding permanent abstinence, which is less threatening and more achievable in the change process')
B.aEf=s([B.b_V,B.aPF,B.aYt,B.b_m,B.aOW,B.aPM,B.aUi,B.b1o,B.aQ2,B.b1q,B.aP_],t.E)
B.Ra=new A.a5("tbi-substance-abuse","TBI with Co-Occurring Substance Use Disorder",B.aEf,!1)
B.b2K=new A.c("Initial Presentation","An 8-month-old male infant is referred to your pediatric rehabilitation consultation service from the pediatric ICU. He was brought to the emergency department 10 days ago by his mother's boyfriend with a chief complaint of \"the baby won't stop crying and seems sleepy.\" The boyfriend reported that the infant rolled off a couch (approximately 2 feet) onto a carpeted floor about 6 hours prior. On arrival, the infant was lethargic with a bulging anterior fontanelle, GCS 9 (E2V2M5), and bilateral retinal hemorrhages noted on fundoscopic examination. CT head revealed bilateral subdural hematomas (SDH) of different densities \u2014 a large acute right-sided SDH with 6mm midline shift and a smaller chronic left-sided SDH \u2014 plus diffuse cerebral edema. He underwent emergent right-sided craniotomy for SDH evacuation. MRI brain performed on day 3 showed extensive bilateral cortical and subcortical restricted diffusion consistent with diffuse axonal injury and hypoxic-ischemic injury. A skeletal survey revealed healing posterior rib fractures at ribs 6-8 on the left (estimated 2-4 weeks old) and a healing metaphyseal corner fracture of the left distal tibia (estimated 3-6 weeks old). Ophthalmology confirmed extensive bilateral multilayered retinal hemorrhages extending to the periphery. Child Protective Services (CPS) has been notified, and the boyfriend has been arrested. The mother is present at bedside.")
B.aUb=new A.c("History & Systems Review:",'**Birth & Developmental History** (obtained from mother):\n- **Birth**: Full-term (39 weeks), vaginal delivery, birth weight 3.4 kg, Apgars 8 and 9. No NICU stay.\n- **Pre-injury development**: Mother reports he was meeting milestones \u2014 sitting independently, babbling ("mama," "dada"), reaching and grasping toys, eating pureed foods. Pediatrician records confirm normal developmental screening at 6-month well-child visit.\n- **Immunizations**: Up to date through 6 months.\n- **Prior medical visits**: One ER visit at age 4 months for "fussiness" \u2014 discharged with diagnosis of colic. In retrospect, this may represent an earlier episode of abusive injury.\n\n**Family & Social History**:\n- Mother is a 22-year-old single parent, works part-time at a retail store. She separated from the infant\'s biological father during pregnancy due to domestic violence.\n- Mother\'s boyfriend (27-year-old male) moved in 3 months ago. He is unemployed and has been the primary caregiver during the day while mother works.\n- Mother denies any knowledge of abuse and is visibly distraught. She states, "He was always so good with the baby. I don\'t understand what happened."\n- No other children in the home.\n- Maternal grandmother lives nearby and is involved.\n- No family history of bleeding disorders, bone diseases, or metabolic conditions.\n\n**Hospital Course**:\n- **Neurosurgery**: Right craniotomy for SDH evacuation (day 0). Post-operative ICP monitoring for 4 days (peak ICP 28 mmHg).\n- **Seizures**: Two witnessed clinical seizures on day 2 (right-sided focal clonic activity for 45 seconds each). Continuous EEG showed right hemispheric epileptiform discharges and 3 additional electrographic seizures over 48 hours. Started on levetiracetam.\n- **Respiratory**: Required intubation for 5 days due to decreased consciousness. Successfully extubated on day 5.\n- **Nutrition**: NPO for 5 days, then gradually advanced. Currently tolerating breast milk and stage 2 purees via spoon-feeding with some coughing episodes (swallow evaluation recommended).\n- **Current status** (day 10): GCS 11 (E3V3M5), intermittent visual tracking, inconsistent response to name, increased tone in all extremities, frequent irritability.\n\n**Current Medications**: Levetiracetam 20 mg/kg/day divided BID, acetaminophen 15 mg/kg PRN, oral vitamin D supplementation')
B.aUX=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Vitals**: HR 142 bpm, RR 32, Temp 98.8F, O2 Sat 98% on room air, Weight 8.1 kg (25th percentile \u2014 down from 50th percentile at 6-month visit)\n- **General**: Irritable infant in crib, intermittently consolable. Right frontal craniotomy incision well-healed with Steri-Strips. Anterior fontanelle open, soft, slightly full.\n- **HEENT**: Right parietal craniotomy site healing well. No new bruising on face or scalp. Bilateral periorbital ecchymoses (fading, approximately 10 days old).\n- **Eyes**: Ophthalmology re-examination shows bilateral multilayered retinal hemorrhages \u2014 flame-shaped hemorrhages in all quadrants extending to ora serrata, with intraretinal hemorrhages. No retinoschisis. Visual fixation inconsistent \u2014 intermittently tracks a large bright toy to midline but does not track past midline. Cortical visual impairment suspected.\n- **Neurological**:\n  - **Alertness**: Arouses to stimulation, intermittent visual tracking, inconsistent response to name and voice. No social smile observed.\n  - **Cranial nerves**: Pupils 3mm bilateral, sluggishly reactive. Inconsistent blink to threat. Gag intact. Facial movement symmetric with crying.\n  - **Motor**: Increased extensor tone in all extremities (Modified Ashworth 2 in bilateral upper and lower extremities). Fisting of both hands. Spontaneous movement present but limited \u2014 predominantly extension posturing when agitated. No purposeful reaching observed.\n  - **Reflexes**: 3+ throughout. Bilateral ankle clonus (sustained, >10 beats). Bilateral Babinski positive.\n  - **Primitive reflexes**: Asymmetric tonic neck reflex (ATNR) strongly present. Moro present but asymmetric (diminished on right). Palmar grasp diminished bilaterally.\n- **Skin**: Fading bruises on bilateral upper arms (fingertip pattern) and left flank. No new bruising. No burns. Diaper area clean without lesions.\n- **Musculoskeletal**: No palpable deformities at rib or lower extremity fracture sites. No swelling or tenderness on gentle palpation. Full passive ROM at all joints without crepitus.\n- **Abdomen**: Soft, non-tender. No hepatosplenomegaly.")
B.b2k=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING",'This infant presents with the classic triad of abusive head trauma (AHT), formerly called "shaken baby syndrome." The constellation of findings is highly specific for non-accidental trauma and must be evaluated systematically.')
B.aYo=new A.c("+ Discuss the diagnostic evaluation for abusive head trauma, the significance of each finding, and the differential diagnosis.","**Classic Triad of Abusive Head Trauma (AHT)**:\n\n1. **Subdural Hematomas (SDH)**:\n   - Bilateral SDH of different ages is **highly specific** for AHT \u2014 indicates repeated episodes of trauma\n   - Mechanism: Rotational acceleration-deceleration forces cause tearing of bridging veins between the dural surface and brain\n   - The reported mechanism (2-foot fall from couch onto carpet) is **biomechanically inconsistent** with this injury pattern. Short falls from <3 feet rarely cause any intracranial injury and virtually never cause bilateral SDH.\n\n2. **Retinal Hemorrhages**:\n   - Bilateral, multilayered, extending to the periphery (ora serrata) \u2014 this pattern is **almost pathognomonic** for AHT\n   - Mild, isolated retinal hemorrhages can occur from birth trauma, CPR, or accidental head injury \u2014 but extensive multilayered hemorrhages to the periphery are seen almost exclusively in abusive trauma\n   - Mechanism: Vitreoretinal traction from repetitive acceleration-deceleration forces\n\n3. **Encephalopathy/Diffuse Brain Injury**:\n   - MRI showing diffuse axonal injury with hypoxic-ischemic changes indicates severe, widespread brain damage\n   - Mechanism: Shearing of axons from rotational forces plus secondary hypoxic-ischemic injury from apnea and cerebral edema\n\n**Additional Evidence of Non-Accidental Trauma**:\n- **Healing posterior rib fractures**: Classic metaphyseal fractures from squeezing the chest \u2014 these are considered **highly specific** for abuse. They require significant force and are not seen in normal handling or CPR.\n- **Metaphyseal corner fracture** (bucket-handle/corner fracture) of distal tibia: Another **highly specific** fracture for abuse \u2014 caused by twisting/pulling forces on extremities\n- **Multiple fractures of different ages**: Indicates repeated episodes of trauma over weeks\n- **Fingertip pattern bruising**: On upper arms and flank \u2014 consistent with forceful gripping\n- **Growth faltering**: Weight dropped from 50th to 25th percentile \u2014 may indicate neglect or chronic stress\n\n**Essential Workup**:\n1. **Skeletal survey** (completed) \u2014 AP and lateral views of all long bones, hands, feet, skull, spine, ribs, pelvis. Repeat in 2 weeks to detect healing fractures not visible acutely.\n2. **Ophthalmology consultation** (completed) \u2014 dilated fundoscopic examination with documentation\n3. **MRI brain** (completed) \u2014 more sensitive than CT for diffuse axonal injury, parenchymal contusions, and dating of injuries\n4. **Laboratory studies**: CBC with differential, coagulation panel (PT, PTT, INR, fibrinogen), Factor VIII and IX levels, Von Willebrand panel \u2014 to rule out bleeding diathesis. Also metabolic panel, hepatic panel (screening for occult abdominal trauma), urinalysis\n5. **Continuous EEG monitoring** (completed) \u2014 for seizure detection\n\n**Differential Diagnosis to Exclude**:\n- **Bleeding disorders**: Factor deficiencies, Von Willebrand disease \u2014 must be ruled out with lab work\n- **Glutaric aciduria type I**: Metabolic condition causing macrocephaly and SDH \u2014 screen with urine organic acids\n- **Osteogenesis imperfecta**: Can cause multiple fractures \u2014 but does NOT cause SDH or retinal hemorrhages. Blue sclerae, family history, and collagen studies can differentiate.\n- **Benign enlargement of subarachnoid spaces**: Can predispose to SDH from minor trauma but does not explain retinal hemorrhages, fractures, or bruising pattern\n- **Birth trauma**: Only relevant in the immediate neonatal period, not at 8 months")
B.aY_=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","The infant has sustained severe, life-altering brain injury. You must now develop a comprehensive neurodevelopmental rehabilitation plan while managing the complex medical and social needs of this patient.")
B.aYO=new A.c("+ Outline the neurodevelopmental monitoring plan, early intervention referral, and seizure management for this infant.","**Neurodevelopmental Monitoring & Rehabilitation**:\n\n1. **Prognosis**: This infant has sustained severe brain injury with diffuse axonal injury and hypoxic-ischemic damage. The prognosis is guarded. Expected long-term outcomes may include:\n   - Cerebral palsy (spastic quadriparesis pattern based on current examination)\n   - Intellectual disability\n   - Cortical visual impairment (CVI)\n   - Epilepsy\n   - Feeding difficulties\n   - Communication impairment\n\n2. **Acute Rehabilitation Interventions** (inpatient):\n   - **Physical therapy**: Positioning program to counteract extensor posturing. Gentle ROM to prevent contractures. Supported upright positioning to promote head control. Parent training in handling techniques.\n   - **Occupational therapy**: Sensory stimulation program (visual, auditory, tactile). Hand splinting to address fisting and prevent contractures. Feeding therapy \u2014 assess oral motor function and coordinate with speech therapy.\n   - **Speech therapy**: Swallow evaluation (modified barium swallow or FEES if indicated given coughing with feeds). Oral motor stimulation. Early communication assessment.\n   - **Tone management**: Serial monitoring of tone. Consider oral baclofen if tone significantly interferes with positioning, care, or comfort. Avoid botulinum toxin in infants this young.\n\n3. **Early Intervention (EI) Referral**:\n   - **Federal mandate** under IDEA Part C: Infants and toddlers (0-3 years) with established developmental delays or conditions with high probability of delay are entitled to early intervention services\n   - This infant qualifies automatically based on the diagnosis of brain injury with documented developmental regression\n   - **EI services include**: Developmental therapy, PT, OT, speech therapy, vision services, family training and support \u2014 all provided in the natural environment (typically the home)\n   - Referral should be made **prior to hospital discharge** \u2014 the EI evaluation must occur within 45 days of referral\n   - Develop an **Individualized Family Service Plan (IFSP)** \u2014 the family-centered plan of care under Part C\n\n4. **Seizure Management**:\n   - Continue levetiracetam 20 mg/kg/day divided BID\n   - Target seizure freedom \u2014 if breakthrough seizures occur, titrate to 40-60 mg/kg/day\n   - If levetiracetam fails, second-line options for infantile epilepsy include: oxcarbazepine, phenobarbital (less preferred due to cognitive effects but sometimes necessary in refractory infantile seizures), or lacosamide\n   - Monitor for infantile spasms \u2014 this infant is in the age range (4-12 months) and the brain injury is a major risk factor. If clusters of brief flexion or extension spasms develop, obtain urgent EEG to evaluate for hypsarrhythmia. Infantile spasms require ACTH or vigabatrin as first-line treatment.\n   - **EEG follow-up**: Routine EEG at 1 month and 3 months post-injury, then as clinically indicated\n   - Continue seizure precautions: supervised sleep positioning, no unsupervised bathing\n\n5. **Vision Services**:\n   - Cortical visual impairment (CVI) assessment and grading (Roman-Lantzy CVI Range)\n   - Vision stimulation program: high-contrast toys, controlled lighting, preferred color identification (often red/yellow in CVI)\n   - Referral to teacher of the visually impaired through EI\n\n6. **Follow-Up Schedule**:\n   - Pediatric physiatrist: Monthly for the first 6 months, then quarterly\n   - Neurology/epilepsy: Monthly until seizures controlled, then every 3 months\n   - Ophthalmology: Every 3 months for CVI monitoring\n   - Developmental pediatrician: Every 6 months for comprehensive developmental assessment\n   - Repeat neuroimaging (MRI) at 6 months to assess for evolving encephalomalacia and hydrocephalus")
B.b0f=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The child abuse team has completed their investigation. The boyfriend has been charged with felony child abuse. CPS is now determining the infant's placement \u2014 the mother is being evaluated to determine if she can retain custody or if foster care is needed.")
B.aUV=new A.c("+ Discuss the mandatory reporting obligations, CPS coordination, and the medical home model for children in foster care.","**Mandatory Reporting of Child Abuse**:\n\n1. **Legal Obligation**: All healthcare providers are **mandated reporters** in all 50 states. The duty to report arises when there is **reasonable suspicion** of abuse \u2014 not certainty. Failure to report is a criminal offense (misdemeanor in most states, felony in some).\n\n2. **What to Report**:\n   - Report to CPS (Child Protective Services) and/or law enforcement per state-specific protocols\n   - The report should include: child's identifying information, nature and extent of injuries, discrepancy between reported mechanism and injury pattern, other indicators of abuse (prior injuries, growth faltering)\n   - **You do not need to determine who committed the abuse** \u2014 that is the role of CPS and law enforcement\n\n3. **Documentation Standards**:\n   - Thorough, objective medical documentation is essential and may be used in criminal proceedings\n   - Document injuries with **precise descriptions**: size, shape, color, location, pattern. Use body diagrams.\n   - **Photograph** all injuries with a ruler for scale (hospital protocol)\n   - Document the reported mechanism and the medical opinion regarding biomechanical plausibility\n   - Avoid using conclusory language like \"child abuse\" in isolation \u2014 instead: \"The constellation of findings (bilateral SDH of different ages, extensive retinal hemorrhages, healing rib fractures, and metaphyseal fractures) is consistent with abusive head trauma and is not explained by the reported mechanism of a short fall.\"\n\n4. **CPS Coordination During Hospitalization**:\n   - Attend CPS case conferences as the medical expert\n   - Provide written medical summary for the court if requested\n   - Coordinate with the child abuse pediatrician (CAP) \u2014 the medical specialist in forensic child abuse evaluation\n   - Advocate for the child's medical needs in custody and placement decisions\n\n**Foster Care Medical Home Model**:\n- If this infant is placed in foster care, he will need a **medical home** \u2014 a single coordinating primary care provider who manages all medical, developmental, and specialty care\n- The AAP recommends that children in foster care receive:\n  - Comprehensive medical evaluation within 72 hours of placement\n  - Developmental screening at every visit\n  - Mental health screening (relevant for older children)\n  - Coordination of specialty care appointments\n  - A portable medical record that travels with the child through placements\n- **Challenge**: Children in foster care experience fragmented care due to placement changes, Medicaid transitions, and multiple caseworkers. The physiatrist should ensure the rehabilitation plan is clearly documented and portable.\n- All children in foster care are eligible for **Medicaid** automatically \u2014 ensuring coverage for rehabilitation services\n\n**Advocacy Role of the Physiatrist**:\n- Provide clear documentation of the child's medical and functional needs to inform custody decisions\n- Recommend that any placement (mother, family member, or foster care) must be able to meet the child's complex medical needs: seizure management, therapy attendance, follow-up appointments, feeding safety\n- If the mother retains custody, recommend a safety plan and parenting support services\n- Testify as an expert witness if subpoenaed regarding the mechanism and extent of injuries")
B.b2H=new A.c(u.j,"During a family conference to discuss the infant's rehabilitation needs and discharge planning, the mother begins crying inconsolably. She says: \"I didn't know. I swear I didn't know he was hurting my baby. I trusted him. What kind of mother am I? They're going to take my baby away from me, aren't they? Please, doctor, tell them I'm a good mother. Tell them I would never let anyone hurt him.\"")
B.aSB=new A.c("+ How do you navigate the mandatory reporting process while supporting a parent who may be both a victim and a responsible party?","**Response**:\n\n'I can see how much pain you are in right now, and I want you to know that your feelings are completely valid. This is one of the most devastating situations a mother can face.\n\nI need to be honest with you about a few things. First \u2014 my role as your baby's doctor. My primary obligation is to [infant's name] and his safety. Everything I do is guided by what is best for him. That means I am required by law to report suspected child abuse, and that report has already been made. That is not a reflection of my judgment of you as a person \u2014 it is a legal obligation that every doctor has.\n\nSecond \u2014 I am not the one who decides custody. That is determined by CPS and the family court based on their full investigation. What I can tell you is that the court will be looking at whether [infant's name] will be safe and whether his complex medical needs can be met. And those needs are significant \u2014 he will require ongoing therapies, medication management, frequent medical appointments, and careful monitoring for potentially years to come.\n\nWhat I can do is this: I can document his medical needs clearly so that whoever is caring for him \u2014 whether that is you, a family member, or a foster family \u2014 knows exactly what he requires. If you are determined by CPS to be an appropriate caregiver, I will work with you closely to make sure you are trained and supported in managing his care.\n\nI also want to say something important. You mentioned trusting someone who hurt your child. Many parents in your situation carry tremendous guilt. I would strongly encourage you to connect with our hospital social worker, who can help you access counseling and support. This is not just for your sake \u2014 it is for [infant's name]. He needs a mother who is emotionally healthy and supported.\n\nRight now, the most important thing you can do for your son is to be present, to attend his therapy sessions, to learn his care needs, and to cooperate fully with the investigation. Those actions will speak louder than any words.'\n\n**Key Principles in This Conversation**:\n- **Do not offer opinions on custody or guilt** \u2014 the physician's role is medical, not legal\n- **Acknowledge the mother's distress** without validating or invalidating her claim of ignorance\n- **Maintain clear boundaries**: The child is the patient. The physician's advocacy is for the child.\n- **Avoid accusatory language**: Do not say \"you should have known\" or imply blame. CPS will determine the mother's role.\n- **Recognize the mother may be a victim too**: Domestic violence history with the biological father suggests a pattern of abusive relationships. The boyfriend may have been abusive to her as well.\n- **Provide concrete, actionable guidance**: Attend therapy, cooperate with investigation, seek counseling\n- **Document the conversation**: Note the mother's statements and emotional state in the medical record objectively, as they may be relevant to the CPS investigation")
B.aFn=s([B.b2K,B.aUb,B.aUX,B.b2k,B.aYo,B.aY_,B.aYO,B.b0f,B.aUV,B.b2H,B.aSB],t.E)
B.Qn=new A.a5("tbi-abusive-head-trauma","Pediatric TBI \u2014 Non-Accidental Trauma (Abusive Head Trauma)",B.aFn,!1)
B.b3t=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 78-year-old female is evaluated for admission to acute inpatient rehabilitation 2 days after open reduction internal fixation (ORIF) with a sliding hip screw for a right intertrochanteric hip fracture. She sustained a mechanical fall at home when she tripped over a throw rug in her hallway at night while walking to the bathroom. She was found on the floor by her daughter the next morning, having been unable to get up. She is a retired schoolteacher who lives alone in a two-story home with bedrooms upstairs. She was previously independent in all ADLs and IADLs and walked without an assistive device. Her daughter lives 20 minutes away and visits twice weekly.")
B.b_4=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","A comprehensive geriatric hip fracture assessment should include:\n- **Fracture & Surgical Details**: Right intertrochanteric fracture (stable, two-part pattern), treated with ORIF sliding hip screw 2 days ago. Surgeon has authorized **weight bearing as tolerated (WBAT)** on the right lower extremity.\n- **Fall Circumstances**: Mechanical fall at night (tripped on throw rug), unable to get up (prolonged lying on floor estimated 8-10 hours) \u2014 raises concern for rhabdomyolysis, pressure injury, dehydration, and hypothermia.\n- **Delirium Screening**: Pre-fracture cognitive status per daughter: independent, managing finances, driving. Currently intermittently confused, especially at night (sundowning). CAM (Confusion Assessment Method) is positive.\n- **Osteoporosis History**: No prior DEXA scan, no osteoporosis treatment. History of a distal radius (Colles') fracture 5 years ago \u2014 a fragility fracture sentinel event that was not evaluated.\n- **Comorbidities**: Hypertension (amlodipine 5 mg), type 2 diabetes (metformin 1000 mg BID, A1c 7.2%), hypothyroidism (levothyroxine 75 mcg), GERD (omeprazole 20 mg daily), osteoarthritis of both knees, insomnia (zolpidem 5 mg nightly).\n- **Pre-Admission Medications Review**: Zolpidem is a fall risk factor (Beers criteria). Omeprazole chronic use increases fracture risk.\n- **Nutrition**: BMI 21, albumin 3.0 g/dL (low), pre-albumin 12 mg/dL (low) \u2014 suggests protein-calorie malnutrition.\n- **Social/Functional**: Lives alone, 2-story home, no grab bars, throw rugs throughout. Daughter is the primary support person.")
B.aNL=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Vitals**: BP 142/78 mmHg supine, 118/68 standing (orthostatic positive \u2014 drop >20 systolic), HR 92 bpm, Temp 98.8F, O2 Sat 95% on RA.\n- **General**: Thin, elderly female, appears older than stated age. Intermittently drowsy but arousable. Oriented to person and place but not date.\n- **Delirium Assessment**: CAM positive (acute onset, fluctuating course, inattention, disorganized thinking). No hallucinations reported.\n- **Surgical Site**: Right hip incision with staples, clean and dry, mild ecchymosis, no signs of infection. Mild surrounding edema.\n- **Skin**: 2 cm x 2 cm Stage 2 pressure injury on the sacrum (likely from prolonged time on floor). No other breakdown.\n- **Musculoskeletal**: Right hip \u2014 limited active ROM due to pain and guarding. Passive ROM: flexion 70 degrees, abduction 20 degrees. Left hip \u2014 full ROM. Bilateral knee crepitus with mild effusions.\n- **Motor**: Right hip flexion 3/5, right knee extension 4-/5 (pain-limited), left LE 4+/5 throughout, UEs 4/5 bilaterally.\n- **Functional Assessment**: Requires maximal assist for bed mobility. Unable to sit to stand without assist of 2. Has not attempted ambulation yet. Transfers with sliding board and assist of 2.\n- **Labs**: Hgb 9.8 g/dL (post-operative anemia), WBC 11.2 (mildly elevated), BUN 28, Cr 1.3 (baseline 0.9), CK 1,840 U/L (elevated \u2014 rhabdomyolysis from prolonged immobilization on floor), albumin 3.0, vitamin D 18 ng/mL (insufficient), calcium 8.9.")
B.aVe=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","This patient presents with multiple interacting geriatric syndromes: fragility fracture, delirium, malnutrition, orthostatic hypotension, rhabdomyolysis from prolonged lying, pressure injury, and previously undiagnosed osteoporosis \u2014 all complicating her rehabilitation course.")
B.aSY=new A.c("+ Outline the systematic approach to fall risk assessment and delirium workup in this patient.","**Fall Risk Assessment \u2014 Multifactorial Approach**:\n1. **Intrinsic Factors**:\n   - Orthostatic hypotension (documented on exam)\n   - Visual impairment (needs formal screening \u2014 has she had an eye exam recently?)\n   - Vestibular/balance dysfunction (assess when delirious state resolves)\n   - Lower extremity weakness and bilateral knee OA\n   - Nocturia prompting nighttime ambulation\n   - Medications: **Zolpidem** (sedative-hypnotic \u2014 high fall risk, Beers criteria), amlodipine (can worsen orthostasis)\n   - Nutritional deficiency with sarcopenia (low BMI, low albumin)\n   - Vitamin D insufficiency (18 ng/mL)\n2. **Extrinsic Factors**:\n   - Throw rugs, no nightlights, no grab bars, stairs in the home\n   - No assistive device despite age and OA\n3. **Validated Tools**: Perform Timed Up and Go (TUG), Berg Balance Scale, and Morse Fall Scale once delirium resolves.\n\n**Delirium Workup**:\n1. **Identify and Treat Precipitants**:\n   - **Infection**: UA with culture, CXR, blood cultures, wound assessment\n   - **Metabolic**: BMP (elevated BUN/Cr \u2014 dehydration, rhabdomyolysis), glucose, thyroid function, calcium\n   - **Medications**: Zolpidem (anticholinergic/sedative \u2014 **discontinue immediately**), assess for opioid-related delirium from post-operative pain management\n   - **Pain**: Undertreated pain is a common delirium trigger in hip fracture patients\n   - **Hypoxia**: Check O2 sat (95% \u2014 borderline; consider PE given recent surgery and immobility)\n   - **Urinary retention**: Check post-void residual; Foley catheter is a delirium risk factor if present\n   - **Constipation/Fecal impaction**: Common precipitant in elderly\n2. **Non-Pharmacological Delirium Management (First-Line)**:\n   - Reorientation cues (clock, calendar, family photos)\n   - Sleep hygiene (lights off at night, minimize nighttime vital checks)\n   - Early mobilization\n   - Correct sensory deficits (ensure glasses and hearing aids are at bedside)\n   - Avoid physical restraints\n3. **Pharmacological** (only if safety concern from agitation): Low-dose haloperidol 0.5-1 mg or quetiapine 12.5-25 mg. Avoid benzodiazepines.")
B.aQT=new A.c("+ Discuss the osteoporosis evaluation and its significance in this clinical context.","**Osteoporosis Assessment**:\n- This patient has sustained **two fragility fractures** (Colles' fracture at age 73, now hip fracture at 78) \u2014 she meets criteria for osteoporosis treatment regardless of DEXA results per clinical diagnosis of osteoporosis.\n- **DEXA scan** should be obtained (ideally of the contralateral hip and lumbar spine) for baseline T-scores to guide treatment monitoring.\n- **FRAX Score**: Calculate 10-year fracture probability. Her risk factors include: age >70, female sex, prior fragility fracture, low BMI, vitamin D insufficiency.\n- **Lab Evaluation for Secondary Causes**:\n  - CBC, CMP (calcium, phosphorus, alkaline phosphatase)\n  - Vitamin D 25-OH (already low at 18)\n  - TSH (hypothyroidism can accelerate bone loss \u2014 already on levothyroxine, check if overtreated)\n  - Consider: Serum protein electrophoresis (rule out multiple myeloma in elderly with fragility fracture), 24-hour urine calcium, PTH\n\n**Significance**: Hip fractures in the elderly carry a **20-30% one-year mortality rate** and a **50% rate of permanent functional decline**. Only 25% of hip fracture patients return to their pre-fracture level of function. Identifying and treating osteoporosis is essential to prevent the contralateral hip fracture (which carries even higher mortality).")
B.aUH=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","The patient is medically stabilized. Her delirium is improving with non-pharmacological measures and correction of dehydration. She is appropriate for acute inpatient rehabilitation. You now develop the comprehensive rehab program.")
B.aRM=new A.c("+ Detail the rehabilitation program including therapy goals, weight-bearing progression, osteoporosis treatment, and fall prevention strategy.","**Rehabilitation Program**:\n1. **Physical Therapy**:\n   - **Weight Bearing**: WBAT on right LE as cleared by surgeon. Begin with sit-to-stand transfers using front-wheeled walker (FWW). Progress from parallel bars to walker ambulation.\n   - **Gait Training**: Emphasize heel-strike pattern, upright posture, and appropriate step length. Progress distance daily.\n   - **Strengthening**: Isometric quad sets and gluteal sets initially. Progress to active hip abduction, extension, and knee extension as tolerated. Include contralateral LE strengthening.\n   - **Balance Training**: Start with static standing balance, progress to weight shifting, tandem stance, and single-leg stance as able.\n   - **Stair Training**: Essential for discharge home (2-story house) \u2014 teach step-over-step or step-to-step pattern with railing.\n2. **Occupational Therapy**:\n   - ADL retraining (lower body dressing, bathing, toileting with adaptive equipment)\n   - Hip precaution education (avoid excessive flexion, adduction, internal rotation if applicable per surgeon)\n   - Kitchen and meal preparation tasks\n   - Home safety evaluation and recommendations\n3. **Osteoporosis Treatment**:\n   - **Vitamin D**: Load with 50,000 IU weekly x 8 weeks, then maintenance 2,000 IU daily\n   - **Calcium**: 1,200 mg daily (dietary + supplementation)\n   - **Bisphosphonate**: Initiate **zoledronic acid 5 mg IV annually** (preferred over oral bisphosphonates in this population due to better adherence and efficacy). First dose can be given 2 weeks post-operatively once vitamin D is replete (>30 ng/mL).\n   - **Consider anabolic agent**: Teriparatide or romosozumab may be appropriate given two fragility fractures.\n4. **Fall Prevention Bundle**:\n   - Discontinue zolpidem (Beers criteria). Use non-pharmacological sleep measures; consider melatonin or trazodone if needed.\n   - Manage orthostatic hypotension: Hydration, compression stockings, review amlodipine dose.\n   - Home modifications (remove throw rugs, install nightlights, grab bars in bathroom, handrails on all stairs)\n   - Assistive device: FWW for household ambulation initially\n5. **Nutrition**: High-protein diet (1.2-1.5 g/kg/day), nutritional supplementation, dietitian consultation.\n6. **Pressure Injury**: Sacral Stage 2 \u2014 moist wound care, pressure redistribution mattress, turn every 2 hours, nutritional optimization.\n7. **DVT Prophylaxis**: Enoxaparin 40 mg SQ daily for 28-35 days post-operatively per AAOS guidelines.")
B.aOY=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The utilization review nurse contacts you on day 2 of admission questioning whether this patient meets criteria for acute inpatient rehabilitation versus skilled nursing facility (SNF).")
B.aSy=new A.c("+ Justify acute inpatient rehabilitation admission and discuss the criteria differentiating acute rehab from SNF placement.","**Acute Inpatient Rehabilitation Justification (CMS Criteria)**:\nThis patient meets all criteria for acute inpatient rehabilitation:\n1. **Medical Complexity Requiring Physician Oversight**: Active delirium, rhabdomyolysis, orthostatic hypotension, anemia, pressure injury, and new osteoporosis diagnosis requiring medication initiation \u2014 these require daily physician management.\n2. **Rehabilitation Nursing Need**: 24-hour rehabilitation nursing for delirium management, fall prevention, wound care, and medication titration.\n3. **Therapy Intensity**: Patient can tolerate **3 hours of therapy per day** (PT/OT/SLP if cognitive deficits persist) \u2014 the hallmark requirement for acute rehab.\n4. **Prior Level of Function**: She was **fully independent** prior to the fracture with high rehabilitation potential.\n5. **Multidisciplinary Team Need**: Requires coordinated care from physiatrist, nursing, PT, OT, social work, dietitian, and pharmacist.\n\n**Acute Rehab vs. SNF Criteria**:\n| Factor | Acute Rehab | SNF |\n|--------|------------|-----|\n| Therapy hours | 3 hours/day minimum | 1-2 hours/day |\n| Physician visits | Daily | Weekly-biweekly |\n| Medical complexity | High | Low-moderate |\n| Prior function | Independent | May have been dependent |\n| Rehab potential | High | Moderate |\n| Typical LOS | 12-16 days | 20-30 days |\n| Nursing ratio | Higher | Lower |\n\n**Discharge Planning** (begin on admission):\n- Home safety evaluation by OT\n- DME ordering: FWW, raised toilet seat, shower bench, reacher, sock aid\n- Home health PT/OT upon discharge\n- Arrange daughter's schedule for first 2 weeks post-discharge\n- Consider personal emergency response system (medical alert device) given she lives alone\n- Outpatient physiatry follow-up at 2 weeks, orthopedic surgery follow-up at 6 weeks, DEXA scan, endocrinology referral if needed")
B.b_b=new A.c(u.j,"During a family meeting, the patient's daughter states: 'I've been thinking about this a lot. I don't think Mom should go back home. She was on the floor all night and nobody knew. I think it's time for a nursing home. It's the safest option.' The patient, whose delirium has cleared, responds firmly: 'I am NOT going to a nursing home. That is my house and I want to go home.'")
B.b3i=new A.c("+ Navigate this family conflict while respecting patient autonomy and addressing safety concerns.","I would respond: 'I appreciate both of you sharing your feelings, and I want you to know that both perspectives have merit. [To the daughter]: Your concern about safety is absolutely justified \u2014 what happened was frightening, and you want to make sure it does not happen again. [To the patient]: Your desire to live in your own home is a right that we fully respect, and our goal is to help you get back there.\n\nHere is what I would like to propose. Rather than making a permanent decision right now, let us take a step-by-step approach:\n\n1. **Right now**: We focus entirely on your rehabilitation. You are making daily progress, and we need to see how far your recovery goes over the next 10-14 days before making any decisions about living arrangements.\n\n2. **Before discharge**: Our occupational therapist will conduct a detailed home safety evaluation. Many of the hazards that led to this fall \u2014 the throw rugs, the lack of nightlights and grab bars \u2014 are fixable. We will create a specific modification plan for your home.\n\n3. **Safety net**: We will set you up with a personal emergency response system \u2014 a wearable button so that if you do fall, you can call for help immediately. We can also explore options like daily check-in calls or a home health aide for the first few weeks.\n\n4. **Trial period**: You go home with these modifications and supports in place. If after 4-6 weeks you and your daughter feel it is working safely, you continue. If new concerns arise, we revisit other options at that time \u2014 which might include assisted living, not necessarily a nursing home.\n\nThe goal is to maximize both your independence AND your safety. Those two things are not mutually exclusive, and I believe we can achieve both. Does this approach work for the two of you?'")
B.aFZ=s([B.b3t,B.b_4,B.aNL,B.aVe,B.aSY,B.aQT,B.aUH,B.aRM,B.aOY,B.aSy,B.b_b,B.b3i],t.E)
B.QW=new A.a5("geriatric-hip-fracture","Hip Fracture Rehabilitation (Intertrochanteric)",B.aFZ,!1)
B.aZa=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'An 82-year-old male is referred to your inpatient rehabilitation consultation service after a 12-day hospitalization for community-acquired pneumonia complicated by sepsis requiring 4 days in the ICU. Prior to admission, he was living independently in a single-story home with his wife, walking unaided to the mailbox daily, and driving to the grocery store weekly. He now requires moderate assistance for all transfers and is unable to ambulate. He reports generalized weakness, poor appetite, and feeling "wobbly" when sitting at the edge of bed. His wife reports that he has been declining functionally over the past 6 months with a 15-pound unintentional weight loss, but this hospitalization has accelerated his decline dramatically.')
B.aQG=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","A systematic assessment of the deconditioned geriatric patient should include:\n- **Pre-Admission Functional Trajectory**: Previously independent in ADLs but with gradual 6-month decline \u2014 suggests an underlying process (sarcopenia, occult malignancy, depression) beyond acute deconditioning.\n- **Hospital Course Contributors to Deconditioning**: 12-day hospitalization, 4 ICU days, prolonged bed rest, anorexia during acute illness, possible ICU-acquired weakness.\n- **Fall History**: Two falls in the past 6 months prior to admission \u2014 one while getting out of the shower, one while reaching for an item on a high shelf. No fractures sustained.\n- **Comorbidities**: Hypertension (lisinopril 20 mg, metoprolol 50 mg BID), type 2 diabetes (glipizide 10 mg BID), hyperlipidemia (atorvastatin 40 mg), BPH (tamsulosin 0.4 mg at bedtime), insomnia (lorazepam 0.5 mg nightly x 8 years), chronic pain (tramadol 50 mg TID for lumbar DDD), GERD (omeprazole 40 mg daily), depression (no current treatment, diagnosed 1 year ago).\n- **Polypharmacy**: Currently on **12 medications** \u2014 multiple fall-risk medications identified.\n- **Nutritional Status**: 15-pound weight loss over 6 months. BMI 20.5. Poor oral intake during hospitalization. Albumin 2.6 g/dL, pre-albumin 10 mg/dL.\n- **Cognitive Screen**: SLUMS score 20/30 (suggests mild cognitive impairment) \u2014 new finding per wife.\n- **Mood**: PHQ-2 positive (score 4/6). Reports loss of interest in activities and feeling like a burden.\n- **Social**: Lives with wife (age 79, has bilateral knee OA and uses a cane). Daughter lives out of state. Limited social engagement outside the home.")
B.b0h=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Vitals**: BP 148/82 mmHg supine, 112/68 mmHg standing at 1 minute (orthostatic positive, symptomatic \u2014 reports dizziness), HR 62 bpm supine (blunted by metoprolol), 68 standing, Temp 98.2F, O2 Sat 94% on RA.\n- **General**: Thin, elderly male with temporal wasting and loose-fitting hospital gown. Appears frail.\n- **Sarcopenia Assessment**: Calf circumference 29 cm (below 34 cm cutoff for sarcopenia screening). Grip strength by dynamometry: 18 kg (below 27 kg cutoff for males). SARC-F questionnaire score: 8/10 (high risk).\n- **Musculoskeletal**: Diffuse muscle atrophy, most prominent in quadriceps and gluteals bilaterally. Kyphotic posture. Bilateral knee crepitus without acute effusion.\n- **Motor Strength (MRC Scale)**: Hip flexors 3+/5 bilaterally, knee extensors 3/5, ankle dorsiflexors 4-/5, hip abductors 3/5. UEs: Deltoids 4-/5, grip 3+/5.\n- **Functional Testing**:\n  - **Timed Up and Go (TUG)**: 38 seconds with FWW and standby assist (>13.5 seconds = fall risk; >30 seconds = high fall risk)\n  - **Berg Balance Scale**: 26/56 (score <45 = fall risk; <36 = nearly 100% fall risk)\n  - **30-Second Chair Stand**: 3 repetitions with UE support (normal for age: >8)\n  - **4-Meter Gait Speed**: 0.5 m/s (below 0.8 m/s threshold for frailty)\n- **Neurological**: No focal deficits. Decreased vibratory sense at bilateral great toes (peripheral neuropathy). Romberg positive with sway.\n- **Skin**: Intact, no pressure injuries. Thin, fragile skin with ecchymoses on bilateral forearms.")
B.aOK=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","This patient demonstrates a classic geriatric syndrome of **acute-on-chronic deconditioning** superimposed on pre-existing sarcopenia and frailty. His functional assessments place him at very high risk for falls and loss of independence.")
B.b1h=new A.c("+ Perform a falls risk stratification with medication review and identify the role of orthostatic hypotension.","**Falls Risk Stratification \u2014 Multifactorial Analysis**:\n\n**High-Risk Medications (Beers Criteria & STOPP/START)**:\n1. **Lorazepam** (benzodiazepine): **HIGHEST PRIORITY for discontinuation.** Associated with a 2-5x increased fall risk in elderly. Causes sedation, impaired balance, slowed reaction time, and cognitive impairment. Has been on it for 8 years \u2014 will require gradual taper.\n2. **Tramadol** (opioid analgesic): CNS depression, dizziness, seizure risk. Beers criteria \u2014 avoid in elderly. Contributes to fall risk and cognitive impairment.\n3. **Glipizide** (sulfonylurea): Hypoglycemia risk, especially with poor oral intake \u2014 hypoglycemia is a common cause of falls in elderly diabetics.\n4. **Tamsulosin** (alpha-blocker): Exacerbates orthostatic hypotension, particularly with first-dose effect and in combination with other antihypertensives.\n5. **Metoprolol**: Blunts compensatory tachycardia during orthostasis, masking the heart rate response. May contribute to exercise intolerance.\n\n**Orthostatic Hypotension Analysis**:\n- Documented **36 mmHg systolic drop** with symptomatic dizziness \u2014 meets criteria for orthostatic hypotension (>20 mmHg systolic or >10 mmHg diastolic).\n- **Contributing Factors**: Dehydration (acute illness), tamsulosin, lisinopril, metoprolol, prolonged bed rest (loss of baroreceptor sensitivity), autonomic neuropathy from diabetes.\n- **Management**: IV/oral hydration, compression stockings (thigh-high), abdominal binder during ambulation, rise slowly with staged position changes (supine to seated x 2 minutes, then stand), review and reduce antihypertensive doses.\n\n**Additional Fall Risk Factors Identified**:\n- Peripheral neuropathy (decreased proprioception)\n- Positive Romberg (vestibular or proprioceptive impairment)\n- Sarcopenia with profound LE weakness\n- Cognitive impairment (SLUMS 20/30)\n- Untreated depression (impairs motivation, attention)\n- Vitamin D likely insufficient (needs level checked)\n- Visual impairment (needs formal assessment)")
B.b_U=new A.c("+ What screening tests should be ordered to evaluate the 6-month pre-admission decline?","The unintentional 15-pound weight loss and progressive functional decline over 6 months require investigation beyond acute deconditioning:\n\n1. **Malignancy Screening**: CBC with differential, CMP (including LDH, alkaline phosphatase), PSA, age-appropriate cancer screening (colonoscopy status, low-dose CT chest if smoking history), CT chest/abdomen/pelvis if high clinical suspicion.\n2. **Endocrine**: TSH, free T4, AM cortisol, testosterone (sarcopenia and frailty can be exacerbated by hypogonadism), HbA1c.\n3. **Nutritional**: Vitamin D 25-OH, B12, folate, iron studies, albumin/pre-albumin (already low).\n4. **Cognitive**: Full neuropsychological testing or at minimum a MoCA (SLUMS already suggests MCI). Consider MRI brain if cognitive decline is confirmed.\n5. **Depression**: Geriatric Depression Scale (GDS-15) \u2014 untreated depression causes anorexia, weight loss, and functional decline.\n6. **Inflammatory Markers**: ESR, CRP \u2014 to screen for occult inflammatory or infectious process.\n7. **Sleep Evaluation**: Chronic benzodiazepine use suggests long-standing sleep issues. Screen for obstructive sleep apnea (STOP-BANG questionnaire).")
B.aZf=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You determine the patient is appropriate for acute inpatient rehabilitation. You design a progressive mobility and reconditioning program while addressing his polypharmacy and fall risk.")
B.aTl=new A.c("+ Outline the progressive mobility program, balance training, and medication reconciliation plan.","**Progressive Mobility Program**:\n1. **Phase 1 (Days 1-3)**: Bed mobility training, seated edge-of-bed exercises (ankle pumps, seated marching, seated hip flexion), sit-to-stand training from elevated surface with UE support. Orthostatic conditioning with staged position changes.\n2. **Phase 2 (Days 4-7)**: Standing balance exercises at parallel bars, weight shifting, pre-gait activities (marching in place, sidestepping). Begin walker ambulation with appropriate level of assist. Start at 50-100 feet and progress.\n3. **Phase 3 (Days 8-14)**: Increase ambulation distance (goal: 300+ feet), introduce varied surfaces (carpet, tile), begin stair training if applicable. Progress from FWW to straight cane as tolerated. Functional task integration (reaching, carrying light objects while walking).\n4. **Resistance Training**: Progressive resistance exercises 3x/week: Seated leg press or wall sits, hip abduction with resistance band, heel raises, sit-to-stand without UE support (goal).\n\n**Balance Training Protocol**:\n- Static balance: Feet together, semi-tandem, tandem, single-leg (progress through each)\n- Dynamic balance: Reaching tasks, turning, obstacle navigation\n- Perturbation training: Gentle external challenges to balance recovery\n- Dual-task training: Walking while performing cognitive task (counting backward)\n- Goal: Berg Balance Scale >45 (from current 26) and TUG <15 seconds\n\n**Medication Reconciliation Plan**:\n1. **DISCONTINUE Lorazepam**: Gradual taper over 4-8 weeks (reduce by 0.125 mg every 1-2 weeks). Replace with sleep hygiene program, melatonin 1-3 mg, and consider trazodone 25-50 mg if needed.\n2. **DISCONTINUE Tramadol**: Transition to scheduled acetaminophen 650 mg TID and topical diclofenac for lumbar DDD. If pain persists, consider duloxetine (dual benefit for pain and depression).\n3. **SWITCH Glipizide to Metformin** (if renal function allows): Lower hypoglycemia risk. Liberalize glycemic targets (A1c <8% acceptable in frail elderly per ADA).\n4. **REDUCE Tamsulosin**: Consider dose reduction or switch to 5-alpha reductase inhibitor if BPH symptoms are controlled. Hold during acute orthostasis management.\n5. **REDUCE Antihypertensives**: Lower BP target to <150/90 mmHg per JNC-8 for age >60. Consider reducing metoprolol dose. Reassess lisinopril need.\n6. **ADD**: Vitamin D 2,000 IU daily (check level first), duloxetine 30 mg daily (for depression and chronic pain \u2014 dual indication).\n7. **REVIEW Omeprazole**: De-escalate to H2 blocker or discontinue if possible (chronic PPI increases fracture risk, C. difficile risk, and hypomagnesemia).")
B.b3J=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient is approaching discharge. You need to develop a comprehensive fall prevention program and coordinate community resources for this frail elderly couple.")
B.aOz=new A.c("+ Describe the fall prevention program, home modifications, and community resources for this patient.","**Home Modification Plan** (OT home evaluation):\n1. **Bathroom**: Install grab bars at toilet and shower/tub, raised toilet seat, shower bench or tub transfer bench, non-slip bath mats, handheld shower head.\n2. **Bedroom**: Bed rail or trapeze if needed, nightlight with motion sensor, telephone within reach from bed.\n3. **General**: Remove all throw rugs and loose cords, ensure adequate lighting in hallways and stairways, secure carpet edges, rearrange furniture for clear walking paths, install handrails on any steps.\n4. **Kitchen**: Move frequently used items to counter height, non-slip mat in front of sink.\n\n**DME Prescription**: Front-wheeled walker, shower bench, raised toilet seat with arms, reacher, long-handled shoe horn.\n\n**Community Resources & Services**:\n1. **Home Health**: PT (2-3x/week x 6-8 weeks), OT (1-2x/week x 4 weeks), home health aide (2 hours daily x 2 weeks for ADL assistance), skilled nursing for medication management and wound assessment.\n2. **Meals on Wheels**: Daily meal delivery to address nutritional deficiency \u2014 both patient and wife may benefit.\n3. **Area Agency on Aging**: Referral for comprehensive geriatric support services, transportation assistance, caregiver respite programs.\n4. **Community Fall Prevention Program**: Refer to evidence-based programs such as **Otago Exercise Program** (home-based, individually tailored strength and balance program shown to reduce falls by 35% in elderly) or **Tai Chi: Moving for Better Balance** (group class if available).\n5. **Personal Emergency Response System (PERS)**: Medical alert device (wearable pendant or wristband) for both patient and wife.\n6. **Outpatient Follow-Up**: Physiatry at 2 weeks, PCP within 1 week, ophthalmology for vision screening, geriatric psychiatry if depression does not improve with duloxetine.\n7. **Caregiver Assessment**: Wife is 79 with bilateral knee OA and uses a cane \u2014 she may not be able to serve as a safe assist for transfers. Assess her needs and consider additional home support.")
B.b1u=new A.c(u.j,"During physical therapy, the patient repeatedly refuses to use his front-wheeled walker. He tells you: 'I am not using that thing. My neighbor uses a walker and he looks like he's 100 years old. I have always been strong and independent. I don't want people to see me like that. I would rather just hold onto the furniture.'")
B.b3h=new A.c("+ How do you address the patient's refusal to use an assistive device while respecting his dignity?","I would respond: 'I hear you, and I want you to know that I respect your pride and your sense of independence \u2014 those qualities are a strength, not a weakness, and they are actually going to help you recover.\n\nBut I want to be honest with you about something. Right now, your balance and leg strength are significantly below where they need to be for safe walking without support. Your testing shows that you are at very high risk for falling, and at your age, a fall that causes a hip fracture could mean you never walk independently again. Using the furniture \u2014 what we call furniture surfing \u2014 is actually one of the most dangerous things you can do, because furniture moves, it tips, and it is not designed to support your weight.\n\nHere is how I would like you to think about the walker: it is not a sign of weakness \u2014 it is a **tool for recovery**, the same way a cast protects a broken bone while it heals. It is temporary. Our goal is to get you strong enough that you graduate from the walker to a cane, and eventually to walking on your own again. But we cannot get there if you fall and break something along the way.\n\nLet me also show you some options. Modern walkers are lightweight and slim. We can adjust it so it fits you properly and feels comfortable. Some patients prefer a rollator with a seat, which looks more like sporting equipment than a medical device.\n\nI will make you a deal: use the walker consistently for the next 4 weeks while we build your strength and balance. At your follow-up, we will retest you, and if your numbers improve to the safe range, we will talk about stepping down to a cane. Your independence is our shared goal \u2014 the walker is how we protect it while we rebuild it.'")
B.aDf=s([B.aZa,B.aQG,B.b0h,B.aOK,B.b1h,B.b_U,B.aZf,B.aTl,B.b3J,B.aOz,B.b1u,B.b3h],t.E)
B.Qt=new A.a5("geriatric-deconditioning","Deconditioning & Falls Prevention",B.aDf,!1)
B.aW7=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 70-year-old retired male postal worker is evaluated on postoperative day 1 following a right total knee arthroplasty (TKA) for severe tricompartmental osteoarthritis. He reports 8 years of progressive right knee pain that had become refractory to conservative management including physical therapy, NSAIDs, intra-articular corticosteroid injections (3 over 2 years), viscosupplementation, and an unloader brace. Preoperative standing X-rays showed near-complete medial joint space loss with varus deformity of 8 degrees. He underwent a cemented posterior-stabilized TKA through a medial parapatellar approach. The surgeon reports a well-balanced knee with good patellar tracking. He lives with his wife in a single-story home with 3 steps at the front entrance with a railing.")
B.aYk=new A.c("History & Systems Review:",'A comprehensive post-TKA assessment should include:\n- **Surgical Details**: Cemented posterior-stabilized (PS) TKA, medial parapatellar approach, well-balanced in flexion and extension, no intraoperative complications. Weight bearing as tolerated (WBAT) immediately.\n- **Pain Assessment**: Current pain 6/10 at rest (on multimodal pain regimen), 8/10 with initial PT session this morning. Describes pain as sharp and aching in the surgical knee.\n- **Pre-Operative Functional Status**: Independent ambulator without assistive device. Active lifestyle \u2014 walked 3-5 miles daily, played recreational golf 2x/week, gardened regularly. Drove independently.\n- **Comorbidities**: Hypertension (amlodipine 10 mg, lisinopril 20 mg), type 2 diabetes (metformin 1000 mg BID, A1c 7.4%), obesity (BMI 32), obstructive sleep apnea (uses CPAP nightly), prior DVT in left leg 15 years ago (completed 6 months of warfarin at that time).\n- **DVT Risk Assessment**: High risk \u2014 age >60, BMI >30, prior VTE history, lower extremity surgery. Rivaroxaban 10 mg daily has been started per orthopedic protocol.\n- **Preoperative ROM**: Right knee flexion 95 degrees (limited by osteophytes and pain), extension -5 degrees (mild flexion contracture).\n- **Expectations Assessment**: Patient states he wants to be playing golf in 2 weeks and tennis by 6 weeks. He says his friend had a TKA and was "back to normal in 10 days."')
B.aSh=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Vitals**: BP 138/82 mmHg, HR 88 bpm, Temp 99.1F (low-grade, expected POD1), O2 Sat 96% on RA, BMI 32.\n- **Surgical Site**: Midline anterior incision with surgical staples, covered by dry sterile dressing. Moderate perioperative edema and ecchymosis extending from mid-thigh to proximal calf. Surgical drain removed this morning, output was 180 mL.\n- **Neurovascular**: Right foot is warm, well-perfused. Dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses palpable. Sensation intact in all dermatomes (saphenous, superficial/deep peroneal, tibial, sural). Able to dorsiflex and plantarflex right ankle \u2014 **no peroneal nerve palsy**.\n- **ROM (POD1)**: Right knee passive flexion 72 degrees (limited by pain and swelling), passive extension 0 degrees (full). Left knee: flexion 120 degrees, extension 0 degrees.\n- **Strength**: Right quadriceps \u2014 able to perform straight leg raise without extensor lag (4-/5). Right hamstrings 4/5. Hip flexion/abduction 4/5. Left LE 5/5 throughout. UEs 5/5.\n- **Functional Status**: Transferred from bed to chair with moderate assist x1. Ambulated 40 feet with front-wheeled walker and contact guard assist. Gait pattern antalgic with decreased right stance phase and limited knee flexion during swing.\n- **Hemoglobin**: 10.2 g/dL (from 14.1 pre-op). No transfusion indicated per restrictive threshold.\n- **Pain Regimen**: Femoral nerve block catheter in situ (ropivacaine infusion), acetaminophen 1g TID scheduled, celecoxib 200 mg BID, gabapentin 300 mg TID, oxycodone 5 mg Q6H PRN (used 2 doses in 24 hours).")
B.aYu=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The patient's postoperative course is progressing as expected. The key clinical challenges are optimizing pain control to facilitate therapy participation, monitoring for complications, and establishing realistic functional expectations.")
B.aNr=new A.c("+ Outline the expected recovery timeline after TKA and identify the key complications to monitor for.","**Expected Recovery Timeline**:\n- **POD 0-1**: Mobilize out of bed, begin PT (transfers, gait training with walker). Goal: safe sit-to-stand and short-distance ambulation.\n- **POD 1-3**: Progress ambulation distance. Stair training before discharge. Achieve \u226580 degrees flexion. Discharge home (typical LOS 1-2 days for uncomplicated TKA).\n- **Weeks 1-6**: Outpatient PT 2-3x/week. Wean from walker to cane (typically 2-4 weeks). Progress ROM toward \u2265110 degrees flexion. Return to driving (typically 4-6 weeks for right TKA once off opioids). Light ADLs independent.\n- **Weeks 6-12**: Continue strengthening. Most patients return to low-impact activities (golf at 8-12 weeks, swimming, cycling). ROM plateau typically reached by 10-12 weeks.\n- **3-12 Months**: Continued strength gains. Maximum improvement by 12 months. Full functional recovery for most ADLs and recreational activities.\n\n**Key Complications to Monitor**:\n1. **Deep Vein Thrombosis/Pulmonary Embolism**: Highest risk in first 2-6 weeks. This patient is high risk (prior DVT, obesity, surgery). Monitor for calf pain/swelling, dyspnea, tachycardia. Continue anticoagulation (rivaroxaban) for 14-35 days per AAOS/ACCP guidelines.\n2. **Infection**: Superficial (2-4%) or deep periprosthetic joint infection (1-2%). Monitor for increasing erythema, warmth, purulent drainage, fever >101.5F persisting beyond POD3. Risk factors: diabetes (A1c >7), obesity, prolonged surgical time.\n3. **Arthrofibrosis/Stiffness**: Failure to achieve \u226590 degrees flexion by 6 weeks is concerning. May require manipulation under anesthesia (MUA) if \u226490 degrees at 6-12 weeks post-op.\n4. **Peroneal Nerve Palsy**: Reported in 0.3-1.3% of TKA, especially with valgus correction. Manifests as foot drop. This patient's neurological exam is intact.\n5. **Extensor Mechanism Disruption**: Inability to perform SLR (quadriceps tendon or patellar tendon rupture) \u2014 this patient can perform SLR, which is reassuring.\n6. **Periprosthetic Fracture**: Risk with falls, osteoporosis. Educate on fall prevention.\n7. **Wound Complications**: Dehiscence, hematoma (risk increased with anticoagulation). Monitor wound daily.")
B.aU5=new A.c("+ Discuss the current evidence on Continuous Passive Motion (CPM) machines and cryotherapy after TKA.","**Continuous Passive Motion (CPM)**:\n- **Historical Use**: CPM machines were routinely prescribed after TKA to prevent adhesions and improve ROM.\n- **Current Evidence**: Multiple systematic reviews and meta-analyses (including Cochrane review) demonstrate **NO clinically significant benefit** of CPM over standard PT in terms of final ROM, function, pain, or length of stay.\n- **AAOS Clinical Practice Guidelines**: CPM is given a **moderate-strength recommendation AGAINST** routine use after TKA.\n- **Current Practice**: CPM has been largely abandoned in most high-volume TKA centers. The focus has shifted to early active mobilization, aggressive PT, and multimodal pain management to facilitate therapy participation.\n- **Exception**: Some surgeons still use CPM in select cases (e.g., patients with pre-operative stiffness, revision TKA, or MUA) \u2014 defer to surgeon preference if prescribed.\n\n**Cryotherapy**:\n- **Mechanism**: Local cold application reduces tissue temperature, decreases metabolic rate, and provides analgesia through reduced nerve conduction velocity.\n- **Evidence**: Moderate evidence supports cryotherapy for **short-term pain reduction and decreased opioid consumption** after TKA. Continuous-flow cryotherapy devices (e.g., circulating ice water cuffs) appear modestly more effective than ice packs alone.\n- **Practical Recommendation**: Use cryotherapy as an **adjunct to multimodal analgesia**. Apply for 20 minutes on/20 minutes off. Use a barrier to prevent skin injury. Particularly helpful before and after PT sessions to optimize pain control.\n- **Compression + Cryotherapy**: Devices combining compression with cold therapy (e.g., Game Ready system) show promising results for edema control and pain reduction but add significant cost.")
B.aUs=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","The patient is being discharged home on POD 2. You finalize the rehabilitation protocol and discharge plan.")
B.aVx=new A.c("+ Detail the outpatient PT progression protocol and multimodal pain management strategy.","**Outpatient Physical Therapy Protocol**:\n\n**Phase 1 \u2014 Acute Recovery (Weeks 0-2)**:\n- Goals: Independent transfers, household ambulation with walker, \u226590 degrees flexion, full extension, independent SLR\n- Exercises: Quad sets, SLR in 4 planes, ankle pumps, heel slides for ROM, seated knee extension\n- Gait: WBAT with FWW, normalize gait pattern, emphasize heel-strike and full extension in stance\n- Modalities: Cryotherapy, NMES to quadriceps (if significant quad inhibition)\n\n**Phase 2 \u2014 Intermediate Recovery (Weeks 2-6)**:\n- Goals: \u2265110 degrees flexion, full extension maintained, wean to cane, community ambulation\n- Exercises: Mini squats, step-ups (4-6 inch), stationary bike (once \u2265105 degrees flexion achieved), leg press, hamstring curls, standing hip abduction\n- Balance: Single-leg stance, tandem walking, perturbation training\n- Functional: Stair training (reciprocal pattern), car transfers, return-to-driving assessment (right TKA \u2014 typically cleared at 4-6 weeks once off opioids and knee flexion >100 degrees)\n\n**Phase 3 \u2014 Advanced Recovery (Weeks 6-12)**:\n- Goals: \u2265120 degrees flexion, full strength, return to recreational activities\n- Exercises: Advanced closed-chain strengthening (squats, lunges, lateral step-downs), proprioceptive training on unstable surfaces, progressive resistance training\n- Sport-Specific: Golf \u2014 practice putting and chipping at 8 weeks, full swing at 10-12 weeks with gradual progression. Walking 18 holes typically 12-16 weeks.\n- Impact Precautions: Encourage low-impact activities (golf, swimming, cycling, walking). Discourage high-impact (running, jumping, singles tennis). Doubles tennis may be considered at 4-6 months.\n\n**Multimodal Pain Management (Opioid-Sparing Protocol)**:\n1. **Scheduled (Around-the-Clock)**:\n   - Acetaminophen 1000 mg TID (avoid in liver disease; max 3g/day if >65)\n   - Celecoxib 200 mg BID x 2-4 weeks (monitor renal function with diabetes; hold if GFR <30)\n   - Gabapentin 300 mg TID (neuropathic pain component, taper off by 4-6 weeks)\n2. **PRN/Rescue**: Oxycodone 5 mg Q6H PRN for breakthrough pain. Goal: eliminate opioid use by 2-4 weeks post-op.\n3. **Cryotherapy**: 20 min on/off, especially before and after PT sessions.\n4. **Elevation**: Elevate the surgical leg above heart level when resting to manage edema.\n5. **Peripheral Nerve Block**: Femoral catheter will be removed before discharge. Consider adductor canal block for quadriceps-sparing analgesia if needed.")
B.aUT=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","You are planning discharge and must determine the appropriate rehabilitation setting and address pre-surgical optimization considerations for future joint replacement candidates.")
B.aYP=new A.c("+ Compare home health PT vs. outpatient PT after TKA, and discuss the role of pre-surgical optimization (prehabilitation).","**Home Health PT vs. Outpatient PT**:\n\n| Factor | Home Health PT | Outpatient PT |\n|--------|---------------|---------------|\n| Setting | Patient's home | PT clinic |\n| Frequency | 2-3x/week | 2-3x/week |\n| Equipment | Limited (bands, portable) | Full gym (bike, leg press, pool) |\n| Best for | Patients with transportation barriers, high fall risk, early post-op (weeks 0-2) | Most patients once safe for community mobility (typically by week 2-3) |\n| Insurance | Requires homebound status for Medicare coverage | No homebound requirement |\n| Evidence | Adequate for early recovery | Superior equipment and outcomes data for achieving maximal ROM and strength |\n\n**Recommendation for This Patient**: Begin with **home health PT for weeks 1-2** (manages early post-op needs, stair training in actual home environment), then **transition to outpatient PT by week 2-3** once he can safely leave home and tolerate car transfers. This hybrid approach maximizes both early safety and long-term outcomes.\n\n**DME Needs**: FWW (transition to straight cane at 2-4 weeks), raised toilet seat with arms, shower bench, reacher/grabber, long-handled shoe horn, elastic shoelaces.\n\n**Pre-Surgical Optimization (Prehabilitation)**:\nWhile this patient has already had surgery, prehabilitation principles should be documented for future joint replacement candidates:\n1. **Exercise Prehab**: 4-6 weeks of structured PT prior to surgery focusing on quad/hamstring/hip strengthening, ROM optimization, and aerobic conditioning. Evidence shows faster recovery and shorter LOS.\n2. **Weight Optimization**: BMI >40 is a relative contraindication to TKA due to increased complication rates. This patient's BMI of 32 is acceptable but weight management counseling is appropriate.\n3. **Glycemic Control**: A1c >8% is associated with increased infection rates. This patient's 7.4% is borderline \u2014 intensive management prior to elective surgery is recommended. Some surgeons require A1c <7.5-8%.\n4. **Smoking Cessation**: Minimum 4-6 weeks before surgery (not applicable here).\n5. **Anemia Correction**: Iron supplementation or EPO if pre-operative Hgb is low.\n6. **Mental Health Optimization**: Depression and catastrophizing are the strongest predictors of poor TKA outcomes. Screen and treat pre-operatively.\n7. **Expectation Management**: Pre-surgical education on realistic recovery timeline reduces dissatisfaction \u2014 this patient's unrealistic expectations should have been addressed pre-operatively.")
B.aNe=new A.c(u.j,"During your POD1 visit, the patient says: 'My buddy Jim had his knee done 3 months ago and he was back on the golf course in 10 days. I have a foursome scheduled 2 weeks from now and I already paid my entry fee for a charity tournament. Also, I told my wife I would be playing tennis with her by 6 weeks. When can I get cleared for all that?'")
B.aQv=new A.c("+ Address the patient's unrealistic expectations while maintaining rapport and motivation.","I would respond: 'I love your enthusiasm and your motivation to get back to the activities you enjoy \u2014 that positive attitude is actually one of the best predictors of a good outcome after knee replacement. So let us channel that energy in the right direction.\n\nI want to give you honest information so you can plan appropriately. Your friend Jim's timeline of 10 days to golf is genuinely unusual and not the standard recovery. Everyone's body heals at a different pace, and pushing too hard too fast can actually set you back significantly \u2014 we are talking about an implant that needs bone to grow into it and soft tissues that need time to heal.\n\nHere is a realistic timeline based on what the evidence tells us and what I see in my practice:\n\n**Golf**: Putting and chipping on a practice green \u2014 you may be able to start around 6-8 weeks. Full swing with irons \u2014 typically 10-12 weeks. Walking 18 holes \u2014 most of my patients achieve this around 3-4 months. So the charity tournament in 2 weeks \u2014 I would strongly recommend sitting that one out or participating in a non-playing role. However, I would be optimistic that you could be playing comfortable rounds by this summer.\n\n**Tennis**: This is a more important conversation. Singles tennis involves a lot of pivoting, sudden direction changes, and impact that can accelerate wear on a knee replacement. Most orthopedic surgeons recommend transitioning to doubles tennis, which is lower impact. Doubles can typically be started around 4-6 months post-op. I would recommend discussing this specific activity with your surgeon at your 6-week follow-up.\n\n**Driving**: For a right knee replacement, most patients safely return to driving at 4-6 weeks, once you are off opioid pain medications and can demonstrate adequate reaction time.\n\nWhat I can promise you is that if you commit to your physical therapy program and follow the recovery plan, you will get back to an active lifestyle. Let us set some milestone goals together so you have targets to work toward rather than arbitrary dates.'")
B.aCN=s([B.aW7,B.aYk,B.aSh,B.aYu,B.aNr,B.aU5,B.aUs,B.aVx,B.aUT,B.aYP,B.aNe,B.aQv],t.E)
B.Qe=new A.a5("geriatric-tka-rehab","Total Knee Arthroplasty Rehabilitation",B.aCN,!1)
B.aYS=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 75-year-old female is admitted to your acute inpatient rehabilitation unit after a fall at home resulting in a right hip contusion and sacral insufficiency fracture. She tripped while walking to the kitchen, striking her right hip against the counter. X-rays showed no femoral fracture, but subsequent MRI revealed a non-displaced sacral insufficiency fracture. She was managed conservatively with pain control and is now medically stable but functionally declined. Prior to admission, she lived with her husband in a single-story home and was independent in ADLs using a straight cane for community ambulation. She brings a bag containing 14 medication bottles from 4 different prescribers. She reports difficulty keeping track of her medications and sometimes takes extra doses when she cannot remember if she already took them.")
B.aYv=new A.c("History & Systems Review:",'A comprehensive medication-focused assessment should include:\n- **Complete Medication List (14 medications)**:\n  1. Metoprolol succinate 100 mg daily (cardiologist) \u2014 for hypertension/rate control\n  2. Lisinopril 20 mg daily (PCP) \u2014 hypertension\n  3. Amlodipine 10 mg daily (PCP) \u2014 hypertension\n  4. Furosemide 40 mg daily (cardiologist) \u2014 HFpEF\n  5. Potassium chloride 20 mEq daily (PCP) \u2014 furosemide-related\n  6. Atorvastatin 80 mg daily (cardiologist) \u2014 hyperlipidemia\n  7. Metformin 1000 mg BID (PCP) \u2014 type 2 diabetes\n  8. Glipizide 10 mg BID (endocrinologist) \u2014 type 2 diabetes\n  9. Diazepam 5 mg nightly (PCP) \u2014 insomnia, prescribed 12 years ago\n  10. Diphenhydramine 50 mg nightly (OTC, self-prescribed) \u2014 insomnia\n  11. Oxybutynin 5 mg TID (urologist) \u2014 overactive bladder\n  12. Omeprazole 40 mg daily (PCP) \u2014 GERD\n  13. Cyclobenzaprine 10 mg TID (pain clinic) \u2014 chronic low back pain\n  14. Aspirin 81 mg daily (PCP) \u2014 primary prevention\n- **Symptom Review Attributable to Medications**: Daytime drowsiness, dry mouth, constipation (last BM 5 days ago), cognitive fogginess ("I feel like I\'m in a cloud"), urinary retention symptoms (weak stream despite OAB medication), frequent lightheadedness on standing, recurrent falls (3 in past 6 months), bilateral ankle edema.\n- **Anticholinergic Burden Score (ACB)**: Diphenhydramine (3) + Oxybutynin (3) + Cyclobenzaprine (2) = **Total ACB Score: 8** (high; scores \u22653 associated with increased cognitive impairment and falls).\n- **Cognitive Status**: MoCA 21/30. Husband reports progressive memory complaints over 2 years. Never formally evaluated \u2014 currently attributed to "aging."\n- **Comorbidities**: Hypertension, HFpEF (EF 55%), type 2 diabetes (A1c 6.8%), hyperlipidemia, GERD, overactive bladder, chronic low back pain, insomnia, osteoporosis (T-score -2.8 femoral neck).')
B.aOH=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Vitals**: BP 118/62 mmHg supine (on 3 antihypertensives + furosemide), **BP 92/54 mmHg standing** (significant orthostatic hypotension \u2014 symptomatic with dizziness), HR 56 bpm (bradycardic on metoprolol), Temp 97.8F, BMI 26.\n- **General**: Elderly female appearing tired. Speech slightly slurred. Dry mucous membranes. Pupils sluggishly reactive (anticholinergic effect).\n- **Cardiovascular**: Bradycardic, regular rhythm, no murmurs. 2+ bilateral ankle edema (amlodipine side effect vs. HFpEF). JVP not elevated.\n- **Abdominal**: Distended, tympanic. Palpable stool in LLQ (constipation from anticholinergic burden + opioid-sparing but still constipating medications). Bladder palpable on exam (urinary retention).\n- **Musculoskeletal**: Tenderness over right greater trochanter and sacrum. No focal deformity. Limited lumbar ROM due to pain. Bilateral knee mild crepitus.\n- **Neurological**: Alert but processing speed notably slow. MoCA 21/30 (deficits: trail-making, delayed recall 1/5, clock draw). Strength 4/5 in LEs bilaterally, 4+/5 UEs. Diminished vibratory sense bilateral feet. Romberg positive.\n- **Gait**: Antalgic, shuffling pattern with narrow base. Requires FWW and contact guard assist. TUG: 32 seconds (high fall risk).\n- **Post-Void Residual (bladder scan)**: 280 mL \u2014 significant urinary retention.\n- **Labs**: Na 132 (mildly low), K 3.6, BUN 32, Cr 1.4 (baseline 1.0), glucose 72 (borderline low on dual diabetes agents), A1c 6.8%, vitamin D 14 ng/mL.")
B.aX9=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","This patient demonstrates the classic cascade of polypharmacy complications: medications causing side effects that are treated with additional medications, leading to drug interactions, falls, cognitive impairment, and functional decline. The anticholinergic burden is critically high.")
B.b0B=new A.c("+ Identify the high-risk medications using Beers Criteria and anticholinergic burden assessment, and prioritize deprescribing targets.","**Beers Criteria Flagged Medications (2023 AGS Update)**:\n1. **Diazepam** (long-acting benzodiazepine): **AVOID** in elderly. Associated with cognitive impairment, delirium, falls, fractures, and motor vehicle accidents. Half-life in elderly is 40-100 hours with active metabolites. **Highest priority for deprescribing.**\n2. **Diphenhydramine** (first-generation antihistamine): **AVOID** in elderly. Highly anticholinergic \u2014 causes confusion, dry mouth, constipation, urinary retention, and falls. ACB score of 3.\n3. **Oxybutynin** (anticholinergic for OAB): **AVOID** in elderly, especially those with cognitive impairment. Crosses blood-brain barrier readily. Known to cause confusion, hallucinations. ACB score of 3.\n4. **Cyclobenzaprine** (skeletal muscle relaxant): **AVOID** in elderly. Anticholinergic, sedating, causes dizziness. Poorly tolerated with minimal efficacy for chronic pain. ACB score of 2.\n5. **Glipizide** (sulfonylurea): **Use with caution** \u2014 high hypoglycemia risk, especially with A1c already 6.8% (overtreatment of diabetes in a 75-year-old).\n6. **Aspirin 81 mg** (primary prevention): 2022 USPSTF no longer recommends aspirin for primary CV prevention in adults \u226560 years due to increased bleeding risk.\n\n**Drug Interaction Concerns**:\n- Triple antihypertensive therapy + furosemide + metoprolol \u2192 **severe orthostatic hypotension and bradycardia**\n- Diazepam + diphenhydramine + cyclobenzaprine \u2192 **additive CNS depression** (sedation, fall risk, cognitive impairment)\n- Anticholinergic burden (ACB 8) + oxybutynin for OAB \u2192 **paradox**: treating bladder overactivity while simultaneously causing urinary retention\n- Glipizide + borderline glucose of 72 \u2192 **hypoglycemia risk** contributing to falls\n\n**Deprescribing Priority Order**:\n1. **IMMEDIATE**: Discontinue diphenhydramine (OTC \u2014 can stop abruptly)\n2. **IMMEDIATE**: Discontinue cyclobenzaprine (can stop abruptly; ineffective for chronic pain)\n3. **URGENT**: Begin diazepam taper (CANNOT stop abruptly \u2014 seizure risk)\n4. **URGENT**: Discontinue oxybutynin (likely causing urinary retention that counteracts its own indication)\n5. **SOON**: Discontinue aspirin (no longer indicated for primary prevention at age 75)\n6. **SOON**: Discontinue or reduce glipizide (A1c overtreated; target A1c <8% in frail elderly per ADA)\n7. **EVALUATE**: Reduce antihypertensive burden (consider stopping amlodipine \u2014 causing ankle edema)")
B.aU_=new A.c("+ Describe the prescribing cascade identified in this patient's medication list.","A **prescribing cascade** occurs when a drug side effect is misidentified as a new medical condition and treated with an additional drug. This patient has multiple cascading prescriptions:\n\n1. **Cascade 1 \u2014 Amlodipine \u2192 Furosemide \u2192 KCl**:\n   - Amlodipine causes peripheral edema (dose-dependent, affects ~10% at 10 mg)\n   - Ankle edema was likely interpreted as fluid overload from HFpEF\n   - Furosemide was added to treat the edema\n   - Potassium chloride was added to replace furosemide-induced potassium wasting\n   - **Resolution**: Reduce or discontinue amlodipine; reassess whether edema resolves, allowing furosemide and KCl reduction.\n\n2. **Cascade 2 \u2014 Oxybutynin \u2192 Constipation \u2192 (likely future laxative)**:\n   - Oxybutynin's anticholinergic effects cause constipation and urinary retention\n   - Paradoxically, the oxybutynin is likely WORSENING her bladder function by causing retention (PVR 280 mL)\n   - **Resolution**: Discontinue oxybutynin. PVR will likely improve. If OAB symptoms persist, consider mirabegron (beta-3 agonist \u2014 non-anticholinergic alternative).\n\n3. **Cascade 3 \u2014 Multiple CNS depressants \u2192 Cognitive complaints \u2192 potential future cholinesterase inhibitor**:\n   - Diazepam + diphenhydramine + cyclobenzaprine collectively cause cognitive impairment\n   - MoCA of 21 may be significantly influenced by medication-induced cognitive dysfunction rather than true dementia\n   - **Resolution**: Deprescribe all three agents; repeat cognitive testing in 8-12 weeks when drug effects have cleared to determine if deficits are reversible.")
B.b3C=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You have identified the high-risk medications and prescribing cascades. Now you develop a systematic deprescribing plan with appropriate monitoring.")
B.aWB=new A.c("+ Detail the systematic deprescribing plan with taper schedules and monitoring parameters.","**Systematic Deprescribing Plan**:\n\n**Week 1 (Inpatient)**:\n1. **STOP Diphenhydramine**: Immediately. No taper needed. Educate patient it is causing confusion and urinary retention.\n2. **STOP Cyclobenzaprine**: Immediately. Replace with structured back pain program (PT, acetaminophen scheduled, topical diclofenac). Educate that muscle relaxants are ineffective for chronic pain.\n3. **STOP Oxybutynin**: Immediately. Recheck PVR in 72 hours (expect improvement). Monitor for OAB symptom return \u2014 if symptoms recur, consider mirabegron 25 mg daily.\n4. **BEGIN Diazepam taper**: Convert 5 mg diazepam nightly to equivalent lorazepam dose (more predictable metabolism in elderly). Start lorazepam 1 mg nightly, then reduce by 0.25 mg every 1-2 weeks. Total taper duration: 8-12 weeks.\n5. **REDUCE Glipizide**: Decrease from 10 mg BID to 5 mg BID. Monitor fingerstick glucose QID. Target fasting glucose 100-180 mg/dL and A1c <8% (relaxed target per ADA for frail elderly).\n6. **STOP Aspirin 81 mg**: Discontinue \u2014 no longer indicated for primary prevention.\n\n**Week 2-3 (Inpatient/Transition)**:\n7. **REDUCE Amlodipine**: Decrease from 10 mg to 5 mg. Monitor BP, ankle edema. If edema resolves and BP remains controlled, can discontinue entirely.\n8. **REASSESS Furosemide**: If edema resolves with amlodipine reduction, decrease furosemide to 20 mg daily or discontinue. Can then reduce KCl accordingly.\n9. **REDUCE Metoprolol**: If HR is persistently <60 bpm, reduce from 100 mg to 50 mg daily. Target resting HR 60-80 bpm.\n\n**Monitoring Parameters**:\n- **Vital signs** (including orthostatics): Daily during inpatient stay, then weekly as outpatient\n- **Fingerstick glucose**: QID during glipizide taper, then daily fasting once stable\n- **Electrolytes (Na, K, Cr)**: Weekly during diuretic adjustments\n- **Post-void residual**: Repeat at 72 hours and 1 week after oxybutynin discontinuation\n- **Cognitive reassessment (MoCA)**: Repeat at 8-12 weeks after anticholinergic medications cleared\n- **Sleep diary**: Track sleep quality during benzodiazepine taper\n- **Fall log**: Document any falls or near-falls during medication transitions\n- **Bowel function**: Monitor for constipation resolution (anticipate improvement within 1-2 weeks)\n\n**Non-Pharmacological Replacements**:\n- **Insomnia** (replacing diazepam/diphenhydramine): Sleep hygiene education, CBT-I referral, melatonin 1-3 mg, trazodone 25-50 mg if needed\n- **Back pain** (replacing cyclobenzaprine): PT for core stabilization, acetaminophen 650 mg TID scheduled, topical NSAID, heat therapy\n- **OAB** (replacing oxybutynin): Bladder training (timed voiding, urge suppression techniques), pelvic floor exercises, fluid management, caffeine reduction")
B.aPe=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","This patient has 14 medications from 4 different prescribers (PCP, cardiologist, endocrinologist, urologist), none of whom are aware of each other's full prescription lists. She also self-prescribes OTC medications.")
B.b31=new A.c("+ Discuss the systems-level approach to coordinating multiple prescribers, pharmacy consultation, and medication therapy management.","**Multi-Prescriber Coordination**:\n1. **Consolidated Medication List**: Create a single, comprehensive, reconciled medication list and distribute to ALL prescribers (PCP, cardiologist, endocrinologist, urologist, pain clinic). Identify the **PCP as the central coordinator** for all medication changes.\n2. **Communication**: Send a detailed letter to each prescriber outlining:\n   - The complete medication list and identified interactions\n   - Specific deprescribing recommendations with clinical justification\n   - Request for agreement or alternative suggestions\n   - Assignment of prescribing responsibility (which provider manages which medications)\n3. **Pharmacy Consultation**:\n   - **Single Pharmacy Policy**: Counsel the patient to use ONE pharmacy for all prescriptions to enable automatic drug interaction screening\n   - **Pharmacist-Led Medication Therapy Management (MTM)**: Medicare Part D covers MTM for patients with multiple chronic conditions, multiple medications, and high drug costs. This patient qualifies.\n   - MTM includes comprehensive medication review, creation of a personal medication list, and medication action plan\n4. **Medication Management Tools**:\n   - **Pill organizer**: Weekly pill box (AM/PM compartments) filled by pharmacy or caregiver\n   - **Medication synchronization**: Coordinate all refill dates to a single monthly pickup (Med Sync programs)\n   - **Simplified regimen**: Consolidate to once-daily dosing where possible (e.g., metformin XR instead of BID)\n   - **Large-print medication list**: Post on refrigerator and carry a copy in wallet\n5. **Follow-Up Structure**:\n   - PCP visit within 1 week of discharge for medication review\n   - Pharmacist MTM visit within 2 weeks\n   - Repeat orthostatic vital signs at every visit for 3 months\n   - Repeat basic metabolic panel at 2 and 4 weeks during taper period\n\n**Quality Improvement Considerations**:\n- Implement a **Brown Bag Review** at every outpatient visit \u2014 patient brings all medication bottles for reconciliation\n- Use a validated deprescribing tool such as the **MedStopper** or **Deprescribing.org** algorithms\n- Screen anticholinergic burden at every encounter using the ACB Calculator\n- Track outcomes: fall rate, cognitive scores, symptom burden, hospitalizations, and medication count over time\n- Advocate for integrated EHR with cross-provider visibility of prescriptions")
B.aZ5=new A.c(u.j,"When you discuss discontinuing the diazepam, the patient becomes visibly upset: 'Absolutely not. I have been taking that pill for 12 years and it is the ONLY thing that helps me sleep. Every time I have tried to stop, I cannot sleep at all and I feel terrible. My doctor said it was fine to keep taking it. You are not my regular doctor \u2014 why are you changing my medications?'")
B.aRi=new A.c("+ How do you address the patient's refusal to stop a high-risk medication while respecting her autonomy and building trust?","I would respond: 'I completely understand your concern, and I want you to know that I am not here to take away something that has helped you. You are right that you know your body, and your experience matters to me. I also want to be upfront \u2014 you are correct that I am not your regular doctor. But part of my job as your rehabilitation physician is to look at the complete picture of your medications, especially after a fall, and make sure everything is working together safely.\n\nLet me share what I am seeing, and then you and I can make a decision together.\n\nThe diazepam belongs to a class of medications called benzodiazepines. When you first started taking it 12 years ago, it probably worked well. But here is what we have learned since then: in people over 65, benzodiazepines actually change how they affect the body. They stay in your system much longer \u2014 for diazepam, that can be 3-4 days. So even though you take it at night, it is still affecting your brain during the day. That foggy feeling you described, the difficulty concentrating, the dizziness \u2014 those may be partially caused by this medication building up in your system.\n\nMore importantly, benzodiazepines are one of the strongest risk factors for falls in older adults. You have fallen 3 times in 6 months, and you are here now because of a fall. I am genuinely worried that if we do not address this, the next fall could result in a hip fracture.\n\nHere is what I am NOT suggesting: I am not suggesting we stop it cold turkey. That would be unsafe and would make you feel terrible \u2014 which is exactly what happened when you tried to stop before. Instead, I am proposing a very slow, gradual taper over 2-3 months, reducing by a tiny amount every 1-2 weeks. During this process, we will replace it with safer sleep strategies \u2014 including a non-addictive sleep medication if needed, relaxation techniques, and a structured sleep program that has strong evidence for long-term insomnia improvement.\n\nI would also like to involve your primary care doctor in this plan. Would you be comfortable with me reaching out to them to coordinate this together? That way you have your trusted doctor on board with every step.\n\nUltimately, this is your decision. I cannot and would not force you to change your medications. But I want to make sure you have all the information to make the best choice for your health and safety.'")
B.aDK=s([B.aYS,B.aYv,B.aOH,B.aX9,B.b0B,B.aU_,B.b3C,B.aWB,B.aPe,B.b31,B.aZ5,B.aRi],t.E)
B.Qx=new A.a5("geriatric-polypharmacy","Polypharmacy & Medication Reconciliation",B.aDK,!1)
B.b_Q=new A.c("Initial Presentation","An 80-year-old male with moderate Alzheimer's dementia is brought to your outpatient PM&R clinic by his 78-year-old wife. The stated reason for the visit is the patient's \"worsening mobility,\" but within minutes it becomes clear that the wife is the one in crisis. She has a bruise on her left forearm and is limping on her right leg. When asked about her own injuries, she admits she fell two days ago while trying to help her husband get out of the bathtub \u2014 he grabbed her arm as he lost his balance, pulling her down. She did not seek medical attention for herself. She appears gaunt, exhausted, and tearful. She has lost 15 pounds over the past 6 months. She says, \"I'm fine, doctor. We're here for him.\" The patient sits quietly in a wheelchair, intermittently calling his wife by his mother's name, and becomes agitated when she steps more than a few feet away from him.")
B.aYe=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","**Patient (80-year-old male)**:\n- **Alzheimer's Dementia**: Diagnosed 4 years ago. Progressive decline \u2014 currently moderate stage. Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA) score 12/30 at last neurology visit 6 months ago.\n- **Functional Status**: Requires assistance with all IADLs and most ADLs. His wife helps with bathing, dressing, toileting (frequent incontinence episodes), transfers, and feeding (needs cueing to eat, sometimes forgets how to use utensils). He can stand with assistance and take a few shuffling steps with a rolling walker, but his wife provides most of the physical support.\n- **Behavioral Symptoms**: Sundowning with evening agitation and restlessness. Wanders at night \u2014 wife has placed alarms on exterior doors after he left the house at 3 AM last month. Occasionally becomes verbally aggressive (\"You're not my wife! Get away from me!\"). Has struck his wife twice in the past month during bathing.\n- **Medications**: Donepezil 10 mg daily, memantine 10 mg BID, quetiapine 25 mg QHS (for sundowning), aspirin 81 mg daily, lisinopril 10 mg daily\n- **Medical History**: Hypertension, hyperlipidemia, benign prostatic hyperplasia, bilateral knee osteoarthritis\n\n**Wife/Caregiver (78-year-old female)**:\n- **Current Injuries**: Left forearm bruise (from patient grabbing her during fall), right knee pain and limp from the fall (no X-ray obtained)\n- **Medical History**: Osteoporosis (T-score -2.8 at lumbar spine), hypertension, type 2 diabetes (HbA1c was 6.8 one year ago but she has not checked in months), depression (diagnosed 2 years ago, on sertraline 50 mg but inconsistently taking it)\n- **Functional Decline**: Lost 15 lbs in 6 months (BMI now 19.2). Sleeping only 3-4 hours per night due to patient's nighttime wandering. Has not seen her own primary care physician in 14 months. Has not had a DEXA scan or mammogram in 3 years. Stopped attending her weekly church group 8 months ago.\n- **Social Isolation**: Their two adult children live out of state. Son calls weekly but has not visited in a year. Daughter visits twice yearly. No paid caregivers \u2014 wife provides 24/7 care alone. No respite care. Financial status: fixed income (Social Security and modest pension), Medicare as primary insurance.\n- **Caregiver Burden Assessment**: Zarit Burden Interview score 72/88 (severe caregiver burden \u2014 scores >24 indicate high burden)")
B.aVH=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Patient Examination**:\n- **Vitals**: BP 148/82 mmHg, HR 72 bpm, Weight 172 lbs\n- **General**: Pleasant-appearing elderly male in wheelchair. Poorly groomed \u2014 clothes stained, fingernails long and dirty, body odor noted. This represents a change from his historically meticulous appearance per wife.\n- **Neurological**: Oriented to self only. Follows simple 1-step commands inconsistently. Speech is fluent but with frequent word-finding pauses and paraphasic errors. Naming 1/5 objects. Unable to complete MoCA (estimated <10). Cranial nerves grossly intact. Motor: diffuse mild weakness (4/5 throughout), likely from deconditioning. Gait: shuffling, narrow-based, requires mod assist of 1 and rolling walker for 10 feet before fatigue. Increased rigidity bilateral upper extremities (may suggest Parkinson-overlap or Lewy body component).\n- **Skin**: Sacral erythema without breakdown (stage 1 pressure injury). Bilateral lower extremity skin dry and cracked.\n\n**Wife Examination** (with her consent):\n- **Vitals**: BP 158/92 mmHg (elevated \u2014 not well-controlled), HR 88 bpm, Weight 112 lbs (BMI 19.2)\n- **General**: Thin, fatigued-appearing woman. Dark circles under eyes. Tremulous hands. Tearful during examination.\n- **Left Forearm**: 4x6 cm ecchymosis on volar forearm, tender to palpation, consistent with grip-pattern bruise. No deformity or crepitus. ROM intact with pain.\n- **Right Knee**: Swelling, mild effusion. Tender over medial joint line. ROM 10-110 degrees (limited by pain). Weight-bearing with antalgic gait. No ligamentous instability.\n- **Screening Assessment**: PHQ-9 score: 18 (moderately severe depression). She endorses feeling hopeless, having no energy, sleeping poorly, losing interest in activities, and difficulty concentrating. She denies active suicidal ideation but says, \"Sometimes I think he'd be better off if I just weren't here anymore.\"")
B.b_N=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","This case presents a dual clinical challenge: the patient with progressive dementia and functional decline, and his wife who is experiencing severe caregiver burnout with medical, psychological, and functional consequences. Both require assessment and intervention.")
B.aQs=new A.c("+ Assess the caregiver's injury and fall risk, stage the patient's dementia, and evaluate his decisional capacity.","**Caregiver Injury Assessment**:\n- Right knee injury: Clinical presentation consistent with an acute meniscal injury or significant contusion/bone bruise. Given her osteoporosis (T-score -2.8), an **X-ray is mandatory** to rule out occult fracture (tibial plateau fracture from a ground-level fall in an osteoporotic patient). If X-ray is negative but symptoms persist, MRI should be obtained.\n- Left forearm bruise: Grip pattern consistent with the reported mechanism. However, given her osteoporosis, a forearm X-ray is prudent to rule out a distal radius fracture.\n- **Fall risk assessment**: She is at extremely high risk for further falls and fracture. Risk factors include: osteoporosis, deconditioning, sleep deprivation, depression (impairs attention and balance), weight loss with sarcopenia, and the physical demands of caregiving for a dependent adult.\n- Her passive suicidal ideation (\"he'd be better off without me\") requires immediate follow-up \u2014 this is a **safety concern** that must not be dismissed.\n\n**Dementia Staging**:\n- Using the **Global Deterioration Scale (GDS)/Reisberg Scale**:\n  - This patient is at **Stage 5-6** (Moderately Severe to Severe Cognitive Decline)\n  - Stage 5: Cannot recall current address, disoriented to time/place, requires assistance choosing clothing\n  - Stage 6: Largely unaware of recent events, requires assistance with ADLs, incontinence developing, behavioral changes including agitation and wandering\n  - His MoCA of 12 (and estimated <10 currently), incontinence, wandering, need for ADL assistance, and behavioral aggression place him solidly in moderate-to-severe dementia\n\n- **Functional Assessment Staging (FAST)**:\n  - FAST Stage 6d-6e: Requires assistance with toileting, incontinence developing\n  - This staging is important for hospice eligibility \u2014 **FAST Stage 7** and beyond may qualify for hospice services under Medicare\n\n**Capacity Evaluation**:\n- Decisional capacity is **decision-specific** and requires assessment of 4 elements:\n  1. **Understanding**: Can the patient understand the relevant information? \u2014 Likely impaired given MoCA <10\n  2. **Appreciation**: Can the patient appreciate how the information applies to his situation? \u2014 Impaired (does not recognize his wife, calls her by his mother's name)\n  3. **Reasoning**: Can the patient weigh options rationally? \u2014 Severely impaired given executive dysfunction\n  4. **Communication**: Can the patient consistently communicate a choice? \u2014 Variable\n- This patient almost certainly **lacks capacity** for complex medical and financial decisions\n- Determine if a **healthcare power of attorney** or **advance directive** exists. If not, the wife likely serves as surrogate decision-maker under state default surrogate laws. If guardianship is needed, it requires a court process.")
B.aVr=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You recognize that the caregiver crisis is as urgent as the patient's medical needs. The wife cannot continue providing 24/7 care alone without serious consequences to her own health \u2014 and ultimately to his care.")
B.aPW=new A.c("+ Outline a management plan that addresses both the patient's rehabilitation needs and the caregiver's health optimization, including respite care and adult day programs.","**Immediate Interventions for the Wife**:\n1. **Medical care**: Order right knee X-ray and left forearm X-ray today. Treat knee injury (ice, compression, acetaminophen, consider short course of topical NSAID given age). Refer to her PCP for urgent follow-up within 1 week.\n2. **Depression treatment**: Her PHQ-9 of 18 with passive suicidal ideation requires intervention. Increase sertraline to 100 mg daily (ensure she is actually taking it). Refer for counseling \u2014 caregiver-specific support if available. Perform a suicide safety assessment and develop a safety plan.\n3. **Osteoporosis management**: She needs a DEXA scan update and initiation of bisphosphonate therapy if not already on one. Calcium and vitamin D supplementation. Fall prevention program.\n4. **Diabetes management**: HbA1c needs to be rechecked. Her weight loss and poor self-care may be affecting glucose control in either direction.\n5. **Nutrition**: Refer to dietitian. High-calorie, high-protein diet. Consider nutritional supplements (Ensure or equivalent).\n\n**Respite Care Options**:\n1. **Adult Day Programs (Adult Day Health)**:\n   - Structured daytime program (typically 6-8 hours) providing socialization, activities, meals, medication management, and supervision for dementia patients\n   - Benefits: Gives wife 6-8 hours of daily respite, provides the patient with social stimulation and structured activity, may reduce behavioral symptoms\n   - Cost: $70-150/day depending on region. Some Medicaid waiver programs cover this. Some offer sliding-scale fees.\n   - Recommend starting with 2-3 days per week and increasing as tolerated\n\n2. **In-Home Respite Care**:\n   - Paid caregiver comes to the home to provide care while the wife rests, attends appointments, or runs errands\n   - Options: Home health aides (personal care), companion services, or skilled nursing for more complex needs\n   - The National Family Caregiver Support Program (under the Older Americans Act) provides grants for respite services through local Area Agencies on Aging\n\n3. **Short-Term Residential Respite**:\n   - The patient stays in a skilled nursing facility or assisted living memory care unit for a defined period (days to weeks) while the wife recovers\n   - Medicare does not typically cover respite unless the patient is enrolled in hospice (5 days of inpatient respite per benefit period)\n   - Some Medicaid waiver programs and veteran programs cover respite stays\n\n**Patient Rehabilitation Plan**:\n1. **Physical therapy**: Progressive mobility program \u2014 improve transfer safety, gait endurance, and balance. Reduce fall risk. Caregiver training in safe transfer techniques.\n2. **Occupational therapy**: ADL retraining with emphasis on structured routines. Environmental assessment of the home for safety modifications (grab bars, shower bench, raised toilet seat, door alarms).\n3. **Behavioral management**: Review quetiapine dosing for sundowning. Consider increasing to 50 mg QHS. Non-pharmacological strategies: consistent daily routine, increased daytime activity (adult day program), nightlight placement, calming music at bedtime.\n4. **Pressure injury prevention**: Address stage 1 sacral erythema with positioning schedule, cushioned wheelchair seat, moisture barrier cream, and nutritional optimization.")
B.b0y=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The wife is overwhelmed and does not know what resources are available. She says, \"We don't have a lot of money. Medicare pays for his doctor visits but not much else. I don't even know where to start.\"")
B.b2V=new A.c("+ Discuss the community resources including Area Agency on Aging, Medicaid waiver programs, and respite funding sources available to this couple.","**Community Resources & Funding**:\n\n1. **Area Agency on Aging (AAA)**:\n   - Federally mandated local agencies (over 600 nationwide) that coordinate services for older adults\n   - Services include: information and referral, case management, meals on wheels, transportation, legal assistance, caregiver support programs\n   - **National Family Caregiver Support Program** (through AAA): Provides counseling, support groups, respite care, supplemental services, and caregiver training specifically for family caregivers\n   - Contact through the **Eldercare Locator**: 1-800-677-1116 or eldercare.acl.gov\n\n2. **Medicaid Waiver Programs (Home and Community-Based Services)**:\n   - If the patient qualifies for Medicaid (income and asset tests vary by state), HCBS waivers can fund:\n     - Personal care aide services (bathing, dressing, toileting assistance)\n     - Adult day health programs\n     - Respite care (in-home and facility-based)\n     - Home modifications (ramps, grab bars)\n     - Care coordination\n   - Many states have specific **Aged and Disabled** or **Alzheimer's/Dementia** waivers\n   - Application is through the state Medicaid office \u2014 social work can initiate\n\n3. **Medicare Coverage**:\n   - Medicare covers: physician visits, home health (if homebound and skilled need exists), durable medical equipment (wheelchair, walker, hospital bed), and hospice (if FAST stage 7 or physician certifies 6-month prognosis)\n   - Medicare does **NOT** cover: custodial care (long-term ADL assistance), adult day programs, long-term nursing home care (beyond 100 days of skilled nursing after hospitalization), or respite care (except under hospice benefit)\n\n4. **Alzheimer's Association**:\n   - 24/7 Helpline: 1-800-272-3900\n   - Local chapters offer support groups, care consultation, education programs, and a safe return program (for wandering patients \u2014 GPS tracking/ID bracelets)\n   - Community resource navigator to help families identify local services\n\n5. **Veterans Benefits** (if applicable \u2014 should be screened):\n   - VA Aid and Attendance benefit: Up to $2,431/month for veterans needing assistance with ADLs\n   - VA caregiver support program\n\n6. **Long-Term Care Planning**:\n   - If the patient does not have long-term care insurance, Medicaid planning with an elder law attorney may be necessary to protect the wife's assets while qualifying the patient for Medicaid nursing facility coverage\n   - **Spousal impoverishment protection**: Federal law protects the community spouse (the wife) from losing all assets when the other spouse qualifies for Medicaid \u2014 she is entitled to retain a minimum monthly maintenance needs allowance and a community spouse resource allowance\n\n7. **Emergency Resources**:\n   - If the wife's health deteriorates acutely, the patient can be placed in an **emergency respite** through CPS/Adult Protective Services or through hospital social work emergency protocols\n   - If the wife is hospitalized, the patient would need immediate temporary placement \u2014 having this plan in place before a crisis is essential")
B.aQ5=new A.c(u.j,'After reviewing the options, you gently raise the possibility of memory care assisted living placement for the patient. The wife breaks down sobbing: "I promised him, doctor. Forty-two years ago, when we got married, we promised each other we would never put each other in a home. I cannot break that promise. He trusted me. If I put him in a home, what kind of wife am I? He would never have done that to me."')
B.aUg=new A.c("+ How do you support this caregiver while having an honest discussion about the trajectory of dementia and the possibility of placement?","**Response**:\n\n'Mrs. [Name], I need you to stop for a moment and really hear what I am about to say, because it comes from a place of deep respect for you and for the promise you made.\n\nForty-two years ago, when you and your husband made that promise, you were making it to each other as healthy, capable adults who could not have imagined this situation. The promise you made was rooted in love \u2014 and that love is still here. I have watched you today, and everything about you tells me you are a devoted wife. You have given everything you have to his care.\n\nBut I am worried about you. You have lost 15 pounds. You are sleeping 3 to 4 hours a night. Your blood pressure is not controlled. You fell and hurt your knee, and you did not even go to the doctor because you were too busy taking care of him. Your depression is severe. And you said something earlier that I cannot ignore \u2014 that he might be better off without you. Mrs. [Name], if something happens to you, who takes care of him?\n\nI want to reframe something for you. Keeping your promise to your husband does not mean you have to do everything yourself. It means making sure he is safe, comfortable, and cared for \u2014 even when that care is more than one person can provide. Right now, you are being asked to be his nurse, his physical therapist, his aide, and his wife \u2014 24 hours a day, 7 days a week. No human being can sustain that.\n\nI am not telling you to place him in memory care today. What I am asking is that we build a support system around both of you so that you can continue to be his wife \u2014 not his exhausted, injured caregiver who is running on empty. Let us start with an adult day program two or three days a week. Let us get a home health aide to help with bathing and transfers so you are not getting pulled down in the bathtub. Let us take care of your knee and your depression.\n\nAnd if there comes a time when his needs exceed what can safely be provided at home \u2014 even with support \u2014 then we will have that conversation together, with honesty and compassion. Choosing memory care, if it comes to that, is not abandoning him. It is making sure he gets the level of care he needs while preserving your health so you can be present with him for as long as possible. The spouses who visit every day, who bring photographs and hold hands and advocate fiercely for their loved one's care \u2014 they have not broken any promise. They have kept the deepest one of all.'\n\n**Key Communication Strategies**:\n- **Validated the promise** as meaningful rather than dismissing it\n- **Reframed the promise** from \"I will do everything myself\" to \"I will ensure he is safe and loved\"\n- **Named the medical reality** of her deteriorating health without being harsh\n- **Addressed the passive suicidal ideation** directly by connecting it to her husband's welfare\n- **Offered a graduated approach** rather than an ultimatum \u2014 start with community supports, not immediate placement\n- **Normalized the possibility of placement** as an act of love, not failure\n- **Avoided imposing a decision** \u2014 partnered with her in planning")
B.aCY=s([B.b_Q,B.aYe,B.aVH,B.b_N,B.aQs,B.aVr,B.aPW,B.b0y,B.b2V,B.aQ5,B.aUg],t.E)
B.QJ=new A.a5("geriatric-dementia-caregiver","Dementia Caregiver Crisis",B.aCY,!1)
B.aUY=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 72-year-old female presents to your PM&R clinic with acute severe mid-back pain that began 3 days ago while lifting a bag of groceries from the trunk of her car. She felt a sudden sharp pain "like something snapped" in her mid-back and had to sit down immediately. The pain has been unrelenting since then \u2014 she rates it 9/10, worse with any movement, standing, or walking, and slightly improved when lying flat. She has been essentially bed-bound for 3 days, getting up only to use the bathroom with her husband\'s assistance. She has tried over-the-counter acetaminophen and ibuprofen with minimal relief. She denies any fall, direct trauma, leg weakness, numbness, or bowel/bladder changes. She is otherwise an active, independent woman who walks 2 miles daily, drives, and volunteers at the local library. She is terrified that she will "end up hunched over like my mother" who had severe kyphosis and died after a hip fracture at age 81.')
B.aVk=new A.c("History & Systems Review:",'**Pain History**:\n- **Onset**: Acute, 3 days ago, while lifting approximately 15-lb bag of groceries\n- **Location**: Mid-thoracic spine, centered at the thoracolumbar junction, no radiation\n- **Quality**: Sharp, constant aching with severe exacerbations on movement\n- **Severity**: 9/10 with activity, 5/10 at rest lying flat\n- **Aggravating factors**: Any trunk flexion, standing, walking, coughing, sneezing\n- **Relieving factors**: Lying supine on firm surface, ice packs\n- **No red flags for cord compression**: No leg weakness, no saddle anesthesia, no bowel or bladder dysfunction, no progressive neurological symptoms\n\n**Past Medical History**:\n- **Osteoporosis**: Diagnosed 5 years ago after DEXA scan. Was prescribed alendronate 70 mg weekly but stopped it after 4 months due to GI side effects (esophageal irritation and nausea). Has not been on any osteoporosis treatment since.\n- **DEXA Results** (most recent, 2 years ago):\n  - Lumbar spine T-score: -3.1\n  - Femoral neck T-score: -2.7\n  - Total hip T-score: -2.4\n- **Prior fractures**: Distal radius (Colles\') fracture at age 65 from a fall on outstretched hand \u2014 treated with casting. This was the event that prompted the DEXA scan.\n- **GERD**: On omeprazole 20 mg daily for 8 years (notable: PPIs are associated with increased fracture risk with long-term use)\n- **Hypothyroidism**: Levothyroxine 75 mcg daily (over-replacement can accelerate bone loss)\n- **Hypertension**: Lisinopril 20 mg daily\n- **Vitamin D**: Was told her level was "low" 3 years ago, took supplements briefly but stopped\n\n**Social History**: Retired school administrator. Lives with husband in a two-story home (bedroom upstairs). Non-smoker. Occasional wine (1-2 glasses per week). No prior falls. Independent in all ADLs and IADLs until this event.\n\n**Family History**: Mother \u2014 severe osteoporosis with thoracic kyphosis, hip fracture at age 81, died from complications (pneumonia). Maternal aunt \u2014 vertebral fractures. No family history of malignancy.\n\n**Medications**: Acetaminophen 650 mg PRN (taking q6h), ibuprofen 400 mg TID (self-directed), omeprazole 20 mg daily, levothyroxine 75 mcg daily, lisinopril 20 mg daily, calcium 500 mg daily (inconsistent)')
B.aQr=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Vitals**: BP 152/88 mmHg (elevated, likely pain-related), HR 82 bpm, RR 16, Temp 98.4F, Height 5'4\" (reports she was 5'6\" at age 50 \u2014 2 inches of height loss), Weight 128 lbs, BMI 22.0\n- **General**: Anxious-appearing woman, moving very slowly and guarding her back. Arrived with husband supporting her by the arm.\n- **Spine**:\n  - **Inspection**: Mild thoracic kyphosis (increased compared to expected for age). No visible deformity, bruising, or skin changes.\n  - **Palpation**: Exquisite point tenderness at T12 spinous process. Paravertebral muscle spasm bilateral from T10-L2. No step-off deformity.\n  - **ROM**: Severely limited in all planes due to pain. Unable to flex forward more than 10 degrees. Extension, lateral bending, and rotation all provoke severe pain.\n  - **Percussion**: Positive percussion tenderness at T12.\n- **Neurological**:\n  - **Motor**: 5/5 hip flexion, knee extension, ankle dorsiflexion, and great toe extension bilaterally. No weakness.\n  - **Sensation**: Intact to light touch and pinprick in all dermatomes L1-S2 bilaterally. No saddle anesthesia.\n  - **Reflexes**: 2+ bilateral knee and ankle jerks. No Babinski. No clonus.\n  - **Gait**: Antalgic, slow, taking small steps with truncal guarding. Requires contact guard assist from husband. Refuses to attempt tandem gait due to pain.\n- **Imaging** (brought from urgent care visit yesterday):\n  - **Thoracolumbar X-ray**: T12 anterior wedge compression fracture with approximately 40% loss of anterior vertebral body height. No retropulsion of bone fragments into the spinal canal. Mild degenerative changes at L3-L4 and L4-L5. Diffuse osteopenia.\n  - No MRI obtained yet.")
B.aZY=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","This patient has an acute osteoporotic vertebral compression fracture (VCF) at T12 with significant pain and functional decline. The fracture occurred with minimal trauma (lifting a grocery bag), confirming the fragility nature of the fracture and the severity of her untreated osteoporosis.")
B.aQu=new A.c("+ Interpret the DEXA results, discuss the FRAX fracture risk assessment, and outline the secondary osteoporosis workup.","**DEXA Interpretation**:\n- **T-score criteria** (WHO Classification):\n  - Normal: T-score >= -1.0\n  - Osteopenia: T-score -1.0 to -2.5\n  - Osteoporosis: T-score <= -2.5\n  - Severe/Established Osteoporosis: T-score <= -2.5 **with** a fragility fracture\n- This patient meets criteria for **severe osteoporosis**: T-score -3.1 (lumbar spine) with two fragility fractures (Colles' fracture and now VCF)\n- The lumbar spine T-score may actually be **falsely elevated** by the compression fracture itself and degenerative changes \u2014 the femoral neck T-score (-2.7) may be more reliable for monitoring\n\n**FRAX Assessment** (Fracture Risk Assessment Tool):\n- FRAX is a WHO-developed algorithm that estimates the 10-year probability of major osteoporotic fracture and hip fracture based on clinical risk factors and femoral neck BMD\n- **This patient's risk factors for FRAX**: Age 72, female, prior fragility fracture, parental history of hip fracture, BMI 22 (low), no smoking, minimal alcohol\n- Treatment is recommended when: 10-year hip fracture risk >=3% or major osteoporotic fracture risk >=20%\n- This patient **does not need FRAX** to justify treatment \u2014 she has already sustained a fragility fracture with a T-score of -3.1. Treatment is **unequivocally indicated**.\n\n**Secondary Osteoporosis Workup**:\nBefore attributing her osteoporosis solely to postmenopausal/age-related causes, rule out secondary causes:\n1. **Laboratory studies**:\n   - **Calcium, phosphorus, albumin** (to detect hypercalcemia/hypocalcemia)\n   - **25-hydroxyvitamin D** (vitamin D deficiency is extremely common and worsens bone loss \u2014 she has a history of low levels)\n   - **PTH** (to rule out primary hyperparathyroidism)\n   - **TSH** (she is on levothyroxine \u2014 over-replacement with suppressed TSH accelerates bone loss)\n   - **Comprehensive metabolic panel** including creatinine (renal osteodystrophy)\n   - **CBC** (to screen for multiple myeloma, anemia)\n   - **Serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP)** and **urine protein electrophoresis** (rule out multiple myeloma \u2014 a common cause of pathologic VCF in this age group)\n   - **24-hour urine calcium** (to assess calcium absorption and excretion)\n   - **Celiac panel** (occult celiac disease causes malabsorption of calcium and vitamin D)\n2. **Medication review**:\n   - **Omeprazole** (8 years of PPI use): Associated with 30-40% increased fracture risk with prolonged use. Impairs calcium absorption. Should be deprescribed if possible.\n   - **Levothyroxine**: Check TSH to ensure not over-replaced\n3. **Consider bone turnover markers**: CTX (C-terminal telopeptide) to assess current resorption rate and later to monitor treatment response")
B.aW3=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You diagnose an acute T12 osteoporotic vertebral compression fracture. The patient is in severe pain, functionally debilitated, and has been untreated for osteoporosis for over 4 years. She needs both acute pain management and long-term skeletal health optimization.")
B.aYJ=new A.c("+ Detail the acute pain management strategy, bracing options, and kyphoplasty candidacy, and develop the long-term osteoporosis treatment plan.","**Acute Pain Management**:\n\n1. **Pharmacological**:\n   - **Acetaminophen** 1000 mg TID scheduled (not PRN) \u2014 foundational analgesic\n   - **Calcitonin nasal spray** (Miacalcin) 200 IU one nostril daily: Analgesic effect specific to vertebral fracture pain. Evidence supports pain reduction in acute VCF. Also has modest antiresorptive effect. Use for 2-4 weeks.\n   - **Short-term opioid**: Tramadol 50 mg q6h PRN or oxycodone 5 mg q6h PRN for breakthrough pain. Limit to 2 weeks maximum with clear taper plan. Counsel about constipation (add bowel regimen) and fall risk.\n   - **Muscle relaxant**: Cyclobenzaprine 5 mg TID PRN for paravertebral spasm (use lowest dose in elderly, nighttime dosing preferred, monitor for sedation and fall risk)\n   - **Discontinue ibuprofen**: NSAID use with this degree of pain will require higher doses and prolonged courses \u2014 risk of GI bleeding, renal injury, and cardiovascular events in elderly. The benefit-risk ratio does not favor NSAIDs here.\n   - **Topical analgesic**: Lidocaine 5% patch over paraspinal muscles for localized relief\n\n2. **Bracing**:\n   - **TLSO (Thoracolumbosacral Orthosis)** \u2014 Jewett hyperextension brace or cruciform anterior spinal hyperextension (CASH) brace\n   - Purpose: Limits flexion at the thoracolumbar junction, reduces load on the anterior vertebral body, provides pain relief through motion restriction\n   - Wear during all upright activities. Remove for sleeping.\n   - Duration: 6-8 weeks typically\n   - **Caution**: Prolonged bracing can cause deconditioning and skin breakdown \u2014 monitor skin under brace, encourage progressive weaning\n\n3. **Kyphoplasty/Vertebroplasty Candidacy**:\n   - **Indications**: Severe pain refractory to 4-6 weeks of conservative management, progressive kyphotic deformity, or pain preventing participation in rehabilitation\n   - **Kyphoplasty** (balloon-assisted): Preferred over vertebroplasty as it may restore some vertebral height and has lower cement extravasation risk\n   - **Timing**: If pain remains severe at 4-6 weeks despite conservative measures, refer for interventional evaluation. Some centers consider earlier intervention (2-3 weeks) for severe cases.\n   - **Prerequisite**: MRI to confirm marrow edema at the fracture level (confirms acute/subacute fracture) and to rule out retropulsion, cord compression, or malignancy\n   - **Contraindications**: Healed fracture without edema, retropulsed fragment with canal compromise, active infection, uncorrectable coagulopathy\n   - **Controversy**: The INVEST and VERTOS trials showed mixed results vs. sham procedure. However, for patients with severe pain and functional decline, the benefit is generally accepted.\n\n**Long-Term Osteoporosis Treatment**:\n\n1. **Bisphosphonate therapy** (once acute fracture management is initiated):\n   - Given her prior GI intolerance to oral alendronate, options include:\n     - **IV Zoledronic acid** (Reclast) 5 mg IV once yearly \u2014 preferred. Bypasses GI tract entirely. Most potent bisphosphonate. Can be initiated as early as 2 weeks after fracture.\n     - **Oral risedronate** (Actonel): Better GI tolerance profile than alendronate. If patient prefers oral.\n   - Alternative non-bisphosphonate: **Denosumab** (Prolia) 60 mg SQ every 6 months \u2014 if bisphosphonates contraindicated or not tolerated. Caution: rebound bone loss and vertebral fractures upon discontinuation.\n\n2. **Anabolic therapy** consideration:\n   - Given two fragility fractures and T-score -3.1, this patient is at **very high fracture risk**\n   - **Teriparatide** (Forteo) 20 mcg SQ daily or **Abaloparatide** (Tymlos) 80 mcg SQ daily \u2014 PTH analog that builds new bone (anabolic) rather than just preventing resorption\n   - **Romosozumab** (Evenity) 210 mg SQ monthly for 12 months \u2014 sclerostin inhibitor with dual anabolic/antiresorptive effect. Most potent reduction in fracture risk. Caution: cardiovascular risk (avoid in patients with recent MI or stroke)\n   - Current guidelines increasingly support **anabolic-first therapy** in very high-risk patients, followed by transition to bisphosphonate or denosumab\n\n3. **Calcium and Vitamin D**:\n   - Vitamin D: Check 25-OH vitamin D level. Target >30 ng/mL. Supplement with vitamin D3 1000-2000 IU daily (higher if deficient).\n   - Calcium: Total intake 1200 mg/day (diet + supplement). Prefer dietary calcium when possible. Limit supplements to 500 mg at a time for absorption.\n\n4. **Medication deprescribing**:\n   - Taper and discontinue omeprazole if possible (try H2 blocker or lifestyle modifications for GERD)\n   - Check TSH and adjust levothyroxine if over-replaced\n\n5. **Rehabilitation**:\n   - Once acute pain subsides (2-4 weeks), begin progressive exercise program:\n   - Weight-bearing exercise, resistance training (back extensors), balance training\n   - Avoid spinal flexion exercises (no sit-ups, no toe touches)\n   - Goal: Prevent further fractures through strength, balance, and fall prevention")
B.b39=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","During the visit, you recommend initiating osteoporosis treatment. You also discuss the importance of a fall prevention program to reduce her risk of additional fractures.")
B.aYd=new A.c("+ Discuss fall prevention program components and current osteoporosis screening guidelines.","**Fall Prevention Program**:\n\n1. **Multifactorial Fall Risk Assessment** (USPSTF Grade B recommendation for adults >=65 with known fall risk factors):\n   - **Gait and balance assessment**: Timed Up and Go (TUG) test, Berg Balance Scale, 30-second chair stand test \u2014 perform once acute fracture has healed\n   - **Medication review**: Identify fall-risk-increasing drugs (FRIDs) \u2014 sedatives, antihypertensives causing orthostatic hypotension, anticholinergics. Her current medications are relatively low-risk, but ongoing vigilance is needed.\n   - **Vision screening**: Annual eye exam. Correct refractive errors. Counsel about bifocal/progressive lens hazards on stairs.\n   - **Orthostatic vitals**: Check for orthostatic hypotension (present in up to 30% of elderly)\n   - **Footwear evaluation**: Sturdy, low-heeled shoes with non-slip soles\n   - **Cognitive screening**: Cognitive impairment is a major fall risk factor \u2014 screen with MoCA if any concerns\n\n2. **Home Safety Assessment**:\n   - OT home evaluation: Remove throw rugs, ensure adequate lighting (especially stairways and bathroom), install grab bars in bathroom, non-slip bath mat, night lights in hallway\n   - Bedroom is upstairs \u2014 consider temporary first-floor sleeping arrangement during recovery, and long-term planning if stairs become unsafe\n   - Stair safety: Ensure handrails on both sides, good lighting, non-slip strips on treads\n\n3. **Exercise-Based Fall Prevention**:\n   - **Tai Chi**: Strong evidence for fall reduction in older adults (30-40% reduction in fall risk)\n   - **Otago Exercise Program**: Home-based strength and balance program demonstrated to reduce falls by 35%\n   - **Progressive resistance training**: Targets sarcopenia and improves functional strength\n   - Begin after fracture healing \u2014 typically 8-12 weeks post-injury\n\n**Osteoporosis Screening Guidelines**:\n\n1. **USPSTF (2018, reaffirmed)**:\n   - Screen all women aged **>=65** with DEXA\n   - Screen postmenopausal women **<65** with risk factors (use FRAX to identify those with 10-year risk equivalent to a 65-year-old white woman \u2014 approximately 9.3%)\n   - **Insufficient evidence** to recommend screening in men (Grade I)\n\n2. **ISCD (International Society for Clinical Densitometry)**:\n   - Women >=65 and men >=70\n   - Younger postmenopausal women and men 50-69 with risk factors\n   - Anyone with a fragility fracture\n   - Anyone on medications associated with bone loss (glucocorticoids, aromatase inhibitors, androgen deprivation)\n\n3. **Monitoring**:\n   - Repeat DEXA every **1-2 years** during treatment to assess response\n   - Measure at the same site with the same machine for consistency\n   - Bone turnover markers (CTX) can assess treatment response earlier (3-6 months)\n\n4. **Fracture Liaison Service (FLS)**:\n   - An FLS is a systematic, coordinated model of care that identifies patients with fragility fractures and ensures they receive appropriate osteoporosis evaluation and treatment\n   - Studies show that without an FLS, up to **80% of fragility fracture patients never receive osteoporosis treatment** \u2014 a critical care gap\n   - If your institution has an FLS, this patient should be enrolled. If not, advocate for developing one.")
B.aTF=new A.c(u.j,"When you recommend restarting osteoporosis treatment, the patient becomes resistant: \"No, doctor. I am not taking that medication again. I read online that bisphosphonates cause your jawbone to rot and your thighbone to snap in half. My friend's dentist told her to stop taking it. I don't want to trade one problem for another.\"")
B.aYA=new A.c("+ How do you address the patient's medication concerns while respecting her autonomy and providing evidence-based counseling?",'**Response**:\n\n\'I appreciate you telling me your concerns. You have clearly done your research, and I respect that. I want to make sure you have the complete picture so you can make the best decision for yourself.\n\nThe conditions you are referring to are real \u2014 they are called **osteonecrosis of the jaw** and **atypical femoral fractures.** They do exist, and I take them seriously. But let me put them in perspective for you.\n\nOsteonecrosis of the jaw occurs in approximately **1 in 10,000 to 1 in 100,000** patients taking bisphosphonates for osteoporosis. The much higher rates you may have read about are in cancer patients receiving much larger IV doses \u2014 10 to 20 times the osteoporosis dose \u2014 for bone metastases. For osteoporosis treatment doses, the risk is extremely low. And it can be further minimized by maintaining good dental hygiene and having any major dental procedures completed before starting treatment.\n\nAtypical femoral fractures are also very rare \u2014 approximately **3-5 per 100,000 patient-years** in the first 3-5 years of treatment. The risk does increase with very prolonged use (beyond 5-7 years), which is why we typically reassess after 3-5 years and may recommend a "drug holiday."\n\nNow let me compare those rare risks to your current situation. You have a T-score of -3.1. You have already broken two bones \u2014 your wrist 7 years ago and your spine 3 days ago, from lifting a grocery bag. Without treatment, your risk of having another vertebral fracture within the next year is approximately **20%**. Your risk of a hip fracture \u2014 which is the one your mother had, and which carries a **20-30% mortality rate** in the year following the fracture in women your age \u2014 is significantly elevated.\n\nSo the question is not whether bisphosphonates have risks \u2014 they do. The question is whether a 1-in-10,000 risk of jaw problems outweighs a 1-in-5 risk of another broken bone in the next year. I believe the math is strongly in favor of treatment.\n\nThat said, I also have options that bypass the concerns you have about oral bisphosphonates. We could consider an annual IV infusion of zoledronic acid \u2014 just once a year, no GI side effects. Or we could consider an entirely different class of medication that actually builds new bone rather than just preventing loss. I would like to find the option that you are most comfortable with.\n\nWhat matters most to me is that you are protected. I do not want to see you back here with another fracture \u2014 or worse. Can we explore these options together?\'\n\n**Key Communication Strategies**:\n- **Acknowledged her concerns as legitimate** rather than dismissing them\n- **Provided specific risk data** to counter vague fears with concrete numbers\n- **Contextualized the risk** by comparing rare side effects to the very real risk of future fractures\n- **Used her personal history** (mother\'s hip fracture) to make the risk tangible\n- **Offered alternatives** to address the specific barriers (GI intolerance, fear of bisphosphonates)\n- **Respected autonomy** by framing the conversation as a partnership ("Can we explore these options together?")\n- **Did not use scare tactics** but was direct about the consequences of non-treatment')
B.aCL=s([B.aUY,B.aVk,B.aQr,B.aZY,B.aQu,B.aW3,B.aYJ,B.b39,B.aYd,B.aTF,B.aYA],t.E)
B.Qw=new A.a5("geriatric-vertebral-compression-fracture","Osteoporotic Vertebral Compression Fracture",B.aCL,!1)
B.aXX=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'An 85-year-old male is referred to your PM&R clinic by an orthopedic surgeon for "pre-surgical optimization" prior to a planned elective right total knee arthroplasty (TKA). The surgeon\'s referral note states: "Severe right knee OA, bone-on-bone on X-ray, failed conservative management. Patient and family requesting surgery. Please evaluate for prehabilitation and surgical fitness." Upon meeting the patient, you observe a thin, slow-moving elderly man who takes nearly 2 minutes to walk from the waiting room to the exam room (approximately 40 feet) using a front-wheeled walker. He is accompanied by his daughter and son-in-law, who do most of the talking. The daughter states emphatically: "Dad needs this surgery. He can barely walk anymore. We want him to have the best quality of life possible." The patient is quiet, nodding occasionally, and seems to defer to his family on all questions.')
B.aYf=new A.c("History & Systems Review:",'**Right Knee History**:\n- Severe tricompartmental osteoarthritis diagnosed 6 years ago\n- Prior conservative treatment: Physical therapy (3 courses over 5 years), acetaminophen, topical diclofenac, 3 corticosteroid injections (last one 8 months ago with minimal benefit), 1 course of viscosupplementation (no benefit), tried an unloader brace (poor tolerance)\n- Current function: Ambulatory with front-wheeled walker indoors. Uses wheelchair for distances >50 feet. Rates knee pain 7/10 with activity.\n\n**Frailty Assessment \u2014 Fried Criteria (5 domains)**:\n1. **Unintentional weight loss**: Lost 18 lbs over the past year (from 155 to 137 lbs, BMI now 19.8). Attributes it to "not being hungry" and difficulty preparing meals. **POSITIVE**\n2. **Self-reported exhaustion**: Endorses feeling exhausted most days. "Everything takes so much effort now." On the CES-D exhaustion items, responds positively to "I felt that everything I did was an effort" and "I could not get going" >=3 days per week. **POSITIVE**\n3. **Low physical activity**: Essentially sedentary. Only walks from bed to bathroom and to kitchen. No exercise program. Kilocalorie expenditure estimated <270 kcal/week by weighted score of activities. **POSITIVE**\n4. **Slow gait speed**: Timed 15-foot walk: 12.8 seconds (gait speed 0.36 m/s). Normal for age and height would be >0.8 m/s. **POSITIVE**\n5. **Weak grip strength**: Dominant hand grip (right): 16 kg. Below the threshold of 26 kg for males of his BMI. **POSITIVE**\n- **Fried Frailty Score: 5/5 \u2014 SEVERELY FRAIL**\n\n**Comprehensive Geriatric Assessment**:\n- **Cognition**: MoCA score 21/30 (borderline \u2014 impairment in visuospatial/executive 2/5, delayed recall 2/5, attention 5/6). Mild cognitive impairment suspected.\n- **Depression**: PHQ-9 score 11 (moderate depression). He endorses low mood, poor appetite, fatigue, poor concentration, and feeling like "a burden to my family."\n- **Nutrition**: BMI 19.8, albumin 3.0 g/dL (low), prealbumin 14 mg/dL (low \u2014 suggestive of protein-calorie malnutrition). Mini Nutritional Assessment Short Form (MNA-SF) score: 6/14 (malnourished).\n- **Functional status**: Katz ADL Index 4/6 (needs assistance with bathing and dressing). Lawton IADL 3/8 (needs help with cooking, laundry, shopping, transportation, finances).\n- **Polypharmacy**: 11 medications including metoprolol, lisinopril, amlodipine, atorvastatin, omeprazole, metformin, aspirin, acetaminophen, gabapentin, duloxetine, tamsulosin.\n- **Falls**: 3 falls in the past 6 months, 1 resulting in a rib contusion.\n\n**Past Medical History**: Hypertension, type 2 diabetes (HbA1c 7.2), coronary artery disease (stent placed 3 years ago), chronic kidney disease stage 3a (GFR 52), mild cognitive impairment, depression, BPH, GERD, osteoarthritis (bilateral knees, lumbar spine)\n\n**Social History**: Widower (wife died 2 years ago). Lives alone in a single-story home. Daughter lives 30 minutes away and visits 3 times per week. Son-in-law does yard work and home maintenance. Has a home health aide 3 mornings per week for bathing and light housekeeping. Drives \u2014 but daughter reports he has had 2 minor fender-benders in the past year.')
B.aSr=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","- **Vitals**: BP 128/72 mmHg, HR 64 bpm (on metoprolol), RR 14, Temp 97.8F, O2 Sat 96% RA, Weight 137 lbs, Height 5'10\", BMI 19.8\n- **General**: Thin, elderly male appearing older than stated age. Temporal wasting. Loose-fitting clothing. Moves slowly and deliberately.\n- **Musculoskeletal**:\n  - **Right knee**: Large effusion. Varus deformity (approximately 10 degrees). Crepitus through full ROM. ROM 5-100 degrees (15-degree flexion contracture). Tender over medial and lateral joint lines. Ligaments stable.\n  - **Left knee**: Moderate crepitus, ROM 0-115 degrees, mild varus. Less symptomatic.\n  - **Lumbar spine**: Diffuse tenderness, limited extension. No focal neurological deficits.\n  - **Upper extremities**: Grip strength 16 kg dominant (right), 14 kg non-dominant. Generalized muscle wasting \u2014 deltoids, biceps, quadriceps, gastrocnemius all visibly atrophied.\n- **Neurological**: Oriented x3 (slow to recall date). Cranial nerves intact. Motor: Generalized weakness \u2014 hip flexion 3+/5 bilateral, knee extension 3/5 right (painful), 4-/5 left, ankle dorsiflexion 4/5 bilateral. Sensation diminished to monofilament testing bilateral feet (diabetic neuropathy). Reflexes 1+ throughout. Romberg positive. Proprioception impaired at great toes bilaterally.\n- **Cardiovascular**: Regular rate, S4 gallop. No murmurs. Bilateral lower extremity edema 1+.\n- **Skin**: Thin, fragile skin with multiple ecchymoses on forearms. No pressure injuries.")
B.aMU=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","This patient presents with severe frailty by all standard measures. While he has legitimate severe knee OA that has failed conservative management, the question is whether elective TKA is appropriate \u2014 and whether prehabilitation can meaningfully reduce his surgical risk.")
B.b2I=new A.c("+ Discuss frailty assessment tools, prehabilitation evidence, and surgical risk stratification in frail elderly patients.",'**Frailty Assessment Tools**:\n\n1. **Fried Frailty Phenotype** (used in this case):\n   - 5 criteria: weight loss, exhaustion, low activity, slow gait, weak grip\n   - Frail = 3+/5, Pre-frail = 1-2/5, Robust = 0/5\n   - This patient scores 5/5 \u2014 the most severe classification\n   - **Limitation**: Does not capture cognitive impairment, depression, or polypharmacy\n\n2. **Clinical Frailty Scale (CFS)** (Rockwood):\n   - 9-point visual/descriptive scale from 1 (Very Fit) to 9 (Terminally Ill)\n   - This patient is approximately **CFS 6-7** (Moderately to Severely Frail) \u2014 has dependence in ADLs/IADLs, slow, falls, needs help with personal care\n   - Widely used in surgical risk assessment\n\n3. **Edmonton Frail Scale**: 17-point scale assessing cognition, general health, functional independence, social support, medication use, nutrition, mood, continence, and functional performance. Comprehensive but time-consuming.\n\n4. **FRAIL Scale**: 5-item questionnaire (Fatigue, Resistance, Ambulation, Illness, Loss of weight) \u2014 simpler screening tool\n\n**Surgical Risk in Frail Patients**:\n- Frailty is an **independent predictor of surgical complications** above and beyond age and comorbidities:\n  - Frail patients undergoing elective surgery have **2-3x higher rates** of postoperative complications, ICU admission, prolonged hospitalization, and discharge to skilled nursing facility\n  - **30-day mortality** after major surgery in frail patients is 2-5x higher than non-frail patients\n  - For TKA specifically: frail patients have higher rates of wound complications, infection, venous thromboembolism, delirium (especially with MCI), and hospital readmission\n  - **1-year functional outcomes** of TKA in frail patients are significantly worse \u2014 many do not achieve meaningful functional improvement\n\n- **ACS-NSQIP Surgical Risk Calculator**: Evidence-based tool that estimates complication risks based on procedure type, age, comorbidities, functional status, and ASA class. Should be used to provide specific risk percentages for shared decision-making.\n\n**Prehabilitation Evidence**:\n- **Definition**: Structured optimization program prior to surgery to improve functional reserve and reduce perioperative risk\n- **Components**:\n  1. **Exercise**: Progressive resistance training (especially quadriceps strengthening), aerobic conditioning, balance training. Duration: 6-8 weeks minimum.\n  2. **Nutrition**: Protein supplementation (1.2-1.5 g/kg/day), caloric optimization, vitamin D repletion, correction of anemia\n  3. **Cognitive optimization**: Depression treatment, cognitive engagement, delirium risk reduction strategies\n  4. **Medical optimization**: Medication review, glycemic control (HbA1c target <8% pre-surgery), anemia correction, smoking cessation (not applicable here)\n\n- **Evidence for prehab before TKA**:\n  - Moderate evidence that prehabilitation reduces length of stay, improves early postoperative function, and reduces discharge to rehabilitation facility\n  - Limited evidence that prehabilitation reverses frailty sufficiently to change the risk profile of a severely frail patient\n  - **Critical question**: Can 6-8 weeks of prehabilitation meaningfully improve a patient with 5/5 Fried frailty criteria, MCI, depression, malnutrition, and sarcopenia? The honest answer is that improvement is possible but the degree of improvement may not be sufficient to make surgery low-risk.\n\n- **Prehabilitation as a "test"**: If a patient cannot participate in or tolerate a prehabilitation program, they are unlikely to tolerate the demands of post-surgical rehabilitation. Prehabilitation serves as both intervention and prognostic tool.')
B.aW9=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You believe this patient's frailty makes elective TKA high-risk with uncertain functional benefit. However, his knee pain is real and limiting. You must develop a management plan that addresses the immediate surgical question and the broader frailty syndrome.")
B.aZD=new A.c("+ Design a prehabilitation program for this patient and define measurable benchmarks for surgical readiness.","**6-8 Week Prehabilitation Program**:\n\n1. **Physical Therapy (3x/week)**:\n   - **Quadriceps strengthening**: Start with isometric quad sets and straight leg raises, progress to resistance band exercises and mini-squats as tolerated\n   - **Hip abductor and extensor strengthening**: Critical for post-TKA gait recovery\n   - **Aerobic conditioning**: Start with seated exercises (arm ergometer, seated marching), progress to walking program. Goal: tolerate 10-15 minutes continuous activity.\n   - **Balance training**: Weight shifting, tandem stance, single-leg stance with support\n   - **ROM preservation**: Gentle knee ROM exercises to prevent further contracture progression\n\n2. **Nutritional Optimization**:\n   - **Protein**: 1.2-1.5 g/kg/day (approximately 75-90 g/day for this patient). Consider oral nutritional supplements (e.g., Ensure High Protein 2x/day between meals).\n   - **Caloric goal**: 30-35 kcal/kg/day to promote weight gain and muscle synthesis\n   - **Vitamin D**: Check level, supplement to target >30 ng/mL. Loading dose if deficient.\n   - **Correct anemia**: Check iron studies, B12, folate. Optimize hemoglobin before surgery (target >10 g/dL)\n   - **Albumin target**: >3.0 g/dL pre-surgery (wound healing correlates with nutritional status)\n   - **Dietitian referral**: Weekly sessions to develop meal plans, address barriers to eating\n\n3. **Depression and Cognitive Optimization**:\n   - Optimize duloxetine dosing (currently on duloxetine \u2014 assess dose adequacy. Titrate to 60 mg if currently on lower dose. Dual benefit: mood and pain.)\n   - Assess for social isolation and loneliness as contributors to depression\n   - Cognitive engagement: Puzzles, reading, social activities. Consider referral to neuropsychology for baseline cognitive testing pre-surgery (important for detecting post-operative cognitive decline).\n   - **Delirium prevention plan**: Minimize anticholinergic burden (review all medications), ensure hearing aids and glasses are available peri-operatively, maintain sleep-wake cycle, avoid benzodiazepines\n\n4. **Medication Review**:\n   - Consider deprescribing omeprazole (prolonged PPI use, low benefit in many elderly patients)\n   - Review gabapentin \u2014 may be contributing to fatigue, falls, and cognitive dulling. Consider dose reduction or switch to duloxetine monotherapy for neuropathic pain.\n   - Ensure aspirin management plan with surgeon (typically held 7 days pre-op)\n\n**Measurable Benchmarks for Surgical Readiness** (reassess at 6-8 weeks):\n1. **Gait speed**: Improvement from 0.36 m/s toward >0.6 m/s (below 0.6 m/s is associated with very poor surgical outcomes)\n2. **Grip strength**: Improvement from 16 kg toward >20 kg\n3. **Timed Up and Go (TUG)**: Achieve <20 seconds (currently would likely be >30 seconds)\n4. **Chair stand test**: Ability to perform 5 sit-to-stands from a standard chair without armrests in <15 seconds\n5. **Nutritional markers**: Albumin >3.0, weight gain of >=3 lbs, prealbumin >18\n6. **Depression**: PHQ-9 improvement to <10\n7. **Engagement**: Patient demonstrates ability to participate actively in therapy 3x/week without excessive fatigue or non-compliance\n\n**Decision Framework**:\n- If benchmarks are met: Proceed with TKA with enhanced perioperative care (geriatric co-management, delirium prevention, early mobilization protocol)\n- If benchmarks are partially met: Re-discuss risks and benefits with patient and family. Consider additional prehab time.\n- If benchmarks are not met: Recommend against elective TKA. Continue conservative management with optimized prehab gains as the treatment.\n- **If the patient cannot tolerate prehab**: This is strong evidence that he will not tolerate post-surgical rehabilitation. Surgery should not proceed.")
B.b1L=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE",'The orthopedic surgeon calls you after receiving your evaluation note. She says: "I see your concerns about frailty, but the family is really pushing for this surgery. The knee is bone-on-bone. Can you clear him for prehab and we\'ll plan the surgery for 8 weeks from now?"')
B.aRB=new A.c("+ Discuss the shared decision-making framework, surgical risk communication, and your role in the clearance process.",'**Response to the Surgeon**:\n\n\'I appreciate you involving me in this patient\'s care, and I share your goal of optimizing his quality of life. However, I have significant concerns about proceeding with elective TKA in this patient, and I want to discuss them openly.\n\nThis patient scores 5 out of 5 on the Fried Frailty criteria \u2014 he is severely frail. His gait speed is 0.36 meters per second, his BMI is 19.8 with sarcopenia, his albumin is 3.0, he has mild cognitive impairment, moderate depression, and he has fallen 3 times in 6 months. The published literature consistently shows that severely frail patients undergoing elective TKA have substantially higher complication rates and are less likely to achieve meaningful functional gains.\n\nI am not saying surgery is impossible. What I am saying is that we need a structured prehabilitation trial to answer two questions: Can we improve his physiological reserve enough to reduce risk? And can he demonstrate the capacity to participate in the intensive post-operative rehabilitation that TKA demands?\n\nI will design and oversee a 6 to 8 week prehabilitation program with specific, measurable benchmarks. At the end of that period, I will reassess him and provide you with objective data to inform the surgical decision \u2014 not just a subjective clearance.\'\n\n**Shared Decision-Making Framework**:\n- Shared decision-making (SDM) requires three elements:\n  1. **Clinical evidence**: The physician provides objective information about risks, benefits, and alternatives\n  2. **Patient values and preferences**: The patient expresses what matters most to them\n  3. **Deliberation**: The physician and patient discuss options and reach a decision together\n\n- **Critical issue in this case**: The family is driving the decision, but the patient has been quiet. It is unclear whether the patient himself wants surgery or is deferring to family pressure. This must be clarified.\n\n**Surgical Risk Communication**:\n- Use **absolute risk** rather than relative risk: "Out of 100 patients with your health profile undergoing this surgery, approximately X will have a serious complication" \u2014 more meaningful than "your risk is 2-3 times higher"\n- Use **visual aids**: Risk ladders, icon arrays, or pictographs help elderly patients understand probabilities\n- Present **realistic functional outcomes**: "Even with a successful surgery, your functional improvement may be limited by your other health conditions. You may not achieve the level of activity you had 10 years ago."\n- Discuss **alternatives to surgery**: Continued conservative management is not "doing nothing" \u2014 it includes optimized pain management, mobility aids, home modifications, and weight-bearing exercise\n\n**Role of the Physiatrist in Surgical Clearance**:\n- The physiatrist\'s role is not to "clear" or "deny" surgery \u2014 it is to provide a **comprehensive functional risk assessment** and recommendations\n- Document: frailty status, functional capacity, prehabilitation plan, benchmarks for reassessment, and specific risk concerns\n- The ultimate surgical decision is between the surgeon, the patient, and the anesthesiologist \u2014 but the physiatrist provides critical prognostic information\n- **Do not rubber-stamp clearance under family pressure** \u2014 this is a patient safety issue')
B.aSx=new A.c(u.j,"During the prehabilitation discussion, you ask to speak with the patient privately for a few minutes. The daughter reluctantly agrees and steps out. Once alone, you ask the patient directly how he feels about the surgery. He is quiet for a long moment, then says softly: \"Honestly, doc? I don't want the surgery. My knees hurt, sure, but I'm 85. I'm tired. After Mary died, I just... I don't have the fight in me anymore. But my daughter \u2014 she's convinced this will fix everything. She lost her mother, and she's terrified of losing me. I don't want to disappoint her. Can't you just tell her I'm not a good candidate?\"")
B.b0g=new A.c("+ How do you handle a situation where the patient's wishes conflict with the family's goals, and the patient is asking you to be the \"bad guy\"?","**Response to the Patient**:\n\n'Mr. [Name], thank you for being honest with me. What you just told me is incredibly important, and I want you to know that your voice is the one that matters most in this decision. This is your body and your life.\n\nI hear two things from you. First, that the surgery is not something you want. And second, that you love your daughter and do not want to hurt her. Both of those things can be true at the same time.\n\nI will tell you what I can and cannot do. I cannot lie to your daughter and tell her you are not a candidate if that is not the complete truth. But here is what I can do: I can help you have this conversation with her, and I can help frame it in a way that she can understand. Because the truth is, your medical concerns are real \u2014 you are frail, and surgery carries genuine risk. But more importantly, your preferences matter. Medicine should not be something that happens to you against your will.\n\nI would like to suggest that we bring your daughter back in and have this conversation together. I will start by presenting the medical picture \u2014 the frailty assessment, the risks, and the realistic outcomes. And then I will create space for you to share how you feel. You do not have to use the exact words you said to me, but I will help you. And if it gets hard, I will be right here.\n\nBefore we do that, I want to check on something you said \u2014 that you are tired and do not have the fight in you since Mary died. I understand that grief can feel that way. But I want to make sure we are addressing that too. Would you be open to talking with someone about how you have been feeling since your wife passed? Not a psychiatrist \u2014 maybe a counselor or a pastor, someone you would be comfortable with.'\n\n**Bringing the Family Back In**:\n\n'[Daughter's name], thank you for giving us that time. I want to share what I have found in my evaluation and then have an important discussion together.\n\nYour father is what we call severely frail. This means his body's reserves \u2014 strength, nutrition, endurance \u2014 are significantly depleted. When we operate on frail patients, the complication rates are much higher, and the functional improvement is often less than we would hope. This is not a reflection of his willpower or your family's support \u2014 it is a biological reality.\n\nI have designed a prehabilitation program to see if we can improve his fitness enough to make surgery safer. But I want to be transparent \u2014 even with prehab, the surgery carries meaningful risk for someone in his condition.\n\nI also want to make sure we are all hearing from the most important person in this room. [Patient's name], is there anything you would like to share with your daughter about how you are feeling about all of this?'\n\n**Key Principles**:\n- **Refused to be the \"bad guy\"** \u2014 this would undermine the patient's autonomy and the family relationship\n- **Empowered the patient** to express his own wishes with support\n- **Used the medical evaluation as a bridge** \u2014 the objective data creates a natural opening for the patient to express his preference without it feeling like a personal rejection of his daughter\n- **Screened for depression and grief** \u2014 his lack of motivation could reflect untreated bereavement/depression, which is treatable and might change his perspective\n- **Did not make the decision for anyone** \u2014 facilitated a conversation where the patient, family, and medical team can reach a shared understanding\n- **Maintained professional integrity** \u2014 did not misrepresent the medical assessment to serve as a social buffer")
B.aEe=s([B.aXX,B.aYf,B.aSr,B.aMU,B.b2I,B.aW9,B.aZD,B.b1L,B.aRB,B.aSx,B.b0g],t.E)
B.Qb=new A.a5("geriatric-frailty-surgical-risk","Frailty Syndrome & Surgical Risk",B.aEe,!1)
B.b1x=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 76-year-old female is referred to your outpatient PM&R clinic for \'generalized weakness and deconditioning\' by her primary care physician. She is brought to the appointment by her 52-year-old son, who does most of the talking. She appears underweight, frail, and has multiple bruises on her arms in various stages of healing. She is wearing a long-sleeved shirt in warm weather. She avoids eye contact and defers all questions to her son, saying, "He knows better than I do." When you ask her directly how she is doing, her son answers for her: "She is fine, she just needs physical therapy. She has been clumsy lately and falling." The patient flinches slightly when her son places his hand on her shoulder.')
B.b_q=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **Referral Reason**: PCP noted progressive weakness, weight loss (15 lbs over 4 months), and multiple bruises. Referred for 'deconditioning rehabilitation.'\n- **History from Son**: States mother has been 'declining' for 6 months. Has fallen 'several times.' Attributes bruises to falls and 'thin skin.' States she forgets to eat. Denies any other concerns. Becomes agitated when asked detailed questions: \"Can you just give her the therapy referral and we can go?\"\n- **PMH**: Hypertension, type 2 diabetes (A1c 9.2% \u2014 previously well-controlled at 6.8%), osteoarthritis bilateral knees, mild cognitive impairment (MMSE 24/30 one year ago \u2014 mild impairment but retains decision-making capacity), depression.\n- **Medications (per son)**: Lisinopril 10 mg daily, metformin 500 mg BID, acetaminophen PRN. Son states she 'does not need' the other medications her PCP prescribed (amlodipine, atorvastatin, sertraline) because 'they were not working.'\n- **Social History**: Widowed 3 years. Previously lived independently in her own home. Son moved in 2 years ago after losing his job. He now manages her finances (she signed power of attorney for finances 'because he said it would be easier'). She has not seen her daughter (who lives 2 hours away) in over a year \u2014 son states 'they had a falling out.' Previously attended church weekly, senior center activities \u2014 has not gone in 8 months ('she does not want to').\n- **Functional History**: Previously independent in all ADLs and IADLs. Now son reports she 'needs help with everything.' However, the PCP note from 6 months ago documented her as independent in all ADLs.\n- **Nutritional Concern**: Weight 6 months ago: 145 lbs. Current weight: 130 lbs. BMI dropped from 24.5 to 22.0. Albumin 2.8 g/dL (low).\n- **Financial Concern (from PCP records)**: PCP noted at last visit that patient mentioned 'not having money for groceries' despite receiving Social Security and a pension from her deceased husband.")
B.aQw=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:",'**Vitals**: BP 168/94 mmHg (poorly controlled \u2014 medications discontinued?), HR 88 bpm, RR 16, SpO2 97% on RA, Temp 36.5C, Weight 130 lbs, BMI 22.0.\n\n**General**: Frail-appearing elderly female. Poorly groomed \u2014 unwashed hair, dirty fingernails (previously described as well-groomed by PCP). Wearing long-sleeved shirt and pants despite 85-degree weather. Appears anxious. Does not speak unless spoken to directly and looks at son before answering.\n\n**Skin** (examined after asking son to step out \u2014 see Domain E):\n- **Upper Arms**: Multiple bruises in various stages \u2014 fresh purple bruising on left upper arm (grab pattern, 4 finger marks), yellow-green bruising on right upper arm (older, approximately 2-3 weeks), faded brown bruise on left forearm.\n- **Torso**: Two large bruises on left flank (4 cm and 6 cm). One bruise on right lower ribs.\n- **Wrists**: Circumferential bruising on both wrists consistent with being grabbed or restrained.\n- **Back**: Linear bruise across mid-back (consistent with being struck with a linear object).\n- **Pressure areas**: Intact. No pressure injuries.\n\n**Musculoskeletal**: Generalized muscle wasting consistent with malnutrition and disuse. Grip strength R 8 kg, L 7 kg (below normal for age). Bilateral knee crepitus. Gait \u2014 shuffling, short stride, uses furniture for support. Timed Up and Go: 22 seconds (elevated fall risk).\n\n**Neurological**: Alert, oriented x 3 (not to date). MMSE 22/30 (declined from 24 six months ago \u2014 may reflect depression or worsening cognition). Follows commands. Can express preferences when asked directly without son present. When asked if she feels safe at home, she pauses for 12 seconds and says, "I do not want to cause trouble."\n\n**Oral Exam**: Poor dentition. Dry mucous membranes (dehydration). One loose tooth.\n\n**Nutritional Assessment**: Temporal wasting. Loose-fitting clothing. Loss of subcutaneous fat in arms and legs.')
B.b2d=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","Identify the clinical indicators of elder abuse in this case and discuss the screening tools, types of elder abuse, and risk factors.")
B.aWS=new A.c("+ What clinical findings indicate elder abuse, what types of abuse are present, and what screening tools are available?","**Clinical Indicators of Elder Abuse in This Case**:\n\n**Physical Abuse Indicators**:\n- **Bruises in various stages of healing**: Indicates repeated trauma over weeks.\n- **Pattern injuries**: Grab-mark bruises on upper arms and wrists (finger-shaped). Linear bruise on back (struck with an object). These are NOT consistent with falls.\n- **Location**: Bruises on torso, flank, and back are highly suspicious \u2014 falls in elderly typically produce bruises on extremities, bony prominences, and face. Trunk bruises suggest inflicted injury.\n- **Concealment**: Long sleeves in warm weather to hide bruises.\n- **Patient's fear response**: Flinching when son touches her. Looking at son before answering. Hesitation when asked about safety.\n\n**Neglect Indicators**:\n- **Weight loss**: 15 lbs in 4 months with low albumin \u2014 malnutrition.\n- **Medication withholding**: Son discontinued 3 medications without medical guidance, including antihypertensive (BP now uncontrolled at 168/94), statin, and antidepressant.\n- **Poor hygiene**: Previously well-groomed, now unwashed with dirty fingernails.\n- **Dehydration**: Dry mucous membranes.\n- **Diabetes mismanagement**: A1c rose from 6.8% to 9.2% \u2014 suggesting dietary neglect and possible medication mismanagement.\n\n**Financial Exploitation Indicators**:\n- Patient has Social Security and a pension but reports 'no money for groceries.'\n- Son obtained financial power of attorney after moving in.\n- Son is unemployed and living in her home.\n\n**Psychological/Emotional Abuse Indicators**:\n- Social isolation: Cut off from daughter, church, senior center.\n- Loss of autonomy: Son speaks for her, makes all decisions.\n- Patient's fear and deference: 'He knows better than I do.'\n- Rapid functional decline inconsistent with medical conditions.\n\n**Types of Elder Abuse Present**:\n1. **Physical abuse**: Inflicted injuries (grab marks, linear bruise, restraint marks)\n2. **Neglect**: Malnutrition, dehydration, medication withholding, hygiene neglect\n3. **Financial exploitation**: Misappropriation of income, obtaining POA under questionable circumstances\n4. **Psychological abuse**: Isolation, intimidation, controlling behavior\n\n**Screening Tools**:\n- **Elder Abuse Suspicion Index (EASI)**: 6-question screening tool for primary care. Asks directly about fear, neglect, and exploitation.\n- **Hwalek-Sengstock Elder Abuse Screening Test (H-S/EAST)**: 15-item tool assessing vulnerability, potential abuse situations, and overt abuse.\n- **Vulnerability to Abuse Screening Scale (VASS)**: Self-report measure.\n- **Clinical judgment**: In this case, the physical findings alone are sufficient to raise high suspicion without formal screening tools.\n\n**Risk Factors Present**:\n- Social isolation (cut off from family and community)\n- Caregiver financial dependence (son is unemployed, living in her home)\n- Caregiver substance use (should be assessed \u2014 financial problems and job loss)\n- Cognitive impairment (mild \u2014 reduces ability to self-advocate)\n- Recent widowhood (lost the protective factor of a spouse)\n- Functional dependence (creates power imbalance)")
B.b1e=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","As a mandatory reporter, you must act. Outline the immediate medical management and the reporting process.")
B.aS1=new A.c("+ Describe the immediate medical management, mandatory reporting obligations, and safety planning for this patient.","**Immediate Medical Actions**:\n\n**1. Medical Evaluation and Treatment**:\n- **Document all injuries**: Detailed description with body diagram. Photograph all bruises with patient consent (with ruler for scale). Note the pattern, size, color (estimate age), and location of each injury.\n- **Laboratory workup**: CBC (anemia from malnutrition or occult bleeding), CMP (renal function, electrolytes \u2014 dehydration), albumin/prealbumin (nutritional status), coagulation studies (rule out bleeding disorder), thyroid function, vitamin D and B12 levels, urinalysis.\n- **Imaging**: Consider skeletal survey if concern for occult fractures (similar to child abuse workup). Rib X-rays given flank and rib bruising.\n- **Medication reconciliation**: Restart amlodipine (BP 168/94 \u2014 dangerously uncontrolled), atorvastatin, and sertraline. Her son's unilateral decision to stop medications is medical neglect.\n- **Nutrition**: Immediate dietary intervention \u2014 high-calorie, high-protein supplements (Ensure, Boost). Referral to dietitian. Consider oral nutritional supplementation.\n- **Cognitive assessment**: Repeat formal cognitive testing \u2014 worsening from MMSE 24 to 22 could reflect depression, medication changes, malnutrition, or genuine cognitive decline. Differentiate with geriatric psychiatry referral.\n- **Depression treatment**: Restart sertraline. PHQ-9 screening. Assess for suicidal ideation.\n\n**2. Mandatory Reporting**:\n- **Elder abuse is a mandatory reporting obligation in ALL 50 states** for healthcare providers.\n- **Report to Adult Protective Services (APS)**: Call the APS hotline. Provide: patient name, location, specific concerns (physical abuse \u2014 describe injuries, neglect \u2014 malnutrition/medication withholding, financial exploitation \u2014 missing income), alleged perpetrator (son).\n- **You are NOT required to prove abuse** \u2014 only to report reasonable suspicion. APS investigates.\n- **Reporting is not optional and cannot be deferred** because the patient is reluctant or because you want to 'gather more information.' You have sufficient evidence to report now.\n- **Document the report**: Date, time, APS reference number, name of person you spoke with.\n- **Inform the patient**: She needs to know you are making a report. Frame it as protection, not betrayal.\n\n**3. Immediate Safety Assessment**:\n- **Is she safe to go home with this son today?** If there is immediate danger, she should not be discharged to his care.\n- **Options**: (a) Admit to hospital for medical stabilization (malnutrition, uncontrolled hypertension) \u2014 this buys time for APS investigation. (b) Emergency placement in a safe environment (adult foster care, temporary shelter). (c) Contact the daughter \u2014 she may be unaware of the situation.\n- **APS may conduct an emergency investigation** and recommend immediate separation if danger is imminent.\n\n**4. Capacity Assessment**:\n- She has mild cognitive impairment but appears to retain decision-making capacity for basic choices.\n- If she has capacity and refuses intervention, she has the right to return home \u2014 but you must still report, document, and provide safety resources.\n- The financial POA should be reviewed by APS \u2014 if obtained through coercion, it may be invalid.\n\n**5. Safety Planning with the Patient**:\n- Emergency contact numbers (APS, 911, the daughter's phone number if she consents).\n- Code word with a neighbor or friend to signal she needs help.\n- Hide a small amount of cash and a pre-paid phone where the son will not find them.\n- Local elder abuse hotline number.\n- Discuss willingness to contact her daughter.")
B.aZe=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","Describe the systems-level response to elder abuse, including the role of Adult Protective Services, legal protections, and institutional responsibilities.")
B.aWu=new A.c("+ What happens after an APS report is filed, and what legal and institutional mechanisms protect this patient?","**APS Investigation Process**:\n\n1. **Intake and screening**: APS receives the report, screens for jurisdiction and urgency.\n2. **Investigation (within 24-72 hours for urgent cases)**: APS investigator visits the patient, interviews the patient (privately), assesses the living situation, interviews the alleged abuser, reviews financial records if financial exploitation is suspected.\n3. **Determination**: APS substantiates or does not substantiate the abuse allegation.\n4. **Intervention options if substantiated**:\n   - Voluntary services: Caregiver support, respite care, in-home services, counseling\n   - Involuntary interventions (if patient lacks capacity): Guardianship petition, emergency removal\n   - Criminal referral: If abuse constitutes criminal conduct (assault, theft), law enforcement is notified\n   - Protective orders: Court orders restricting the abuser's access\n\n**Legal Protections**:\n- **Adult Protective Services statutes**: Every state has laws authorizing APS investigation and intervention.\n- **Mandatory reporting laws**: Healthcare providers who fail to report suspected elder abuse face legal penalties (fines, license disciplinary action, criminal charges in some states).\n- **Reporting immunity**: Healthcare providers who report in good faith are immune from civil liability, even if the investigation does not substantiate abuse.\n- **Financial exploitation statutes**: Many states have specific elder financial exploitation laws with criminal penalties.\n- **Guardianship/conservatorship**: If the patient lacks capacity and the POA holder is the abuser, the court can appoint a guardian and revoke the POA.\n\n**Institutional Responsibilities**:\n\n1. **Hospital/clinic elder abuse protocol**: The institution should have a written protocol for suspected elder abuse, including: screening procedures, documentation standards, reporting procedures, and safety planning.\n2. **Social work involvement**: Automatic social work consultation when elder abuse is suspected.\n3. **Ethics committee**: Available for complex cases where patient autonomy conflicts with safety concerns.\n4. **Community partnerships**: Relationships with APS, law enforcement, Area Agency on Aging, legal aid, and elder abuse advocacy organizations.\n5. **Staff training**: Annual elder abuse recognition training for all clinical staff \u2014 physicians, nurses, therapists, front desk staff.\n6. **Follow-up tracking**: After APS report, the medical team should follow up with the patient \u2014 ensure she is seen within 1-2 weeks regardless of the APS outcome.\n\n**Resources for This Patient**:\n- **Eldercare Locator**: 1-800-677-1116 (national resource for elder care services)\n- **National Adult Protective Services Association (NAPSA)**\n- **Area Agency on Aging**: In-home services, Meals on Wheels, transportation, adult day programs\n- **Legal aid**: Free legal services for review of POA, protection orders\n- **Long-term care ombudsman**: If she is ultimately placed in a facility")
B.aPt=new A.c(u.j,'You need to examine this patient without her son present. When you ask the son to step out, he becomes agitated: "I am her healthcare proxy. I have the right to be in every appointment. She cannot answer your questions \u2014 she gets confused. I am not leaving." The patient looks at the floor.')
B.b1S=new A.c("+ How do you safely separate a suspected elder abuse victim from the suspected abuser in a clinical setting, and how do you then communicate with the patient?",'**Model Response**:\n\n**Phase 1: Separating the Patient from the Son**\n\n**1. Be firm but non-confrontational with the son.**\n"I appreciate that you are involved in your mother\'s care, and I understand you want to be helpful. It is standard practice in our clinic to examine patients privately for part of the visit. This is something we do with every patient, regardless of the circumstances. I need to do a complete physical examination, and hospital policy requires that examinations be conducted with the patient and the clinical team only."\n\n**2. If he resists:**\n"I understand your concern about her confusion, but I am able to communicate with her effectively, and if I need clarification, I will bring you back in afterward. This is a non-negotiable part of our clinical protocol. Our medical assistant will show you to the waiting area, and I will come get you when the examination is complete."\n\n**3. If he becomes aggressive or refuses:**\n"I hear that you feel strongly about this. I want to assure you that your mother is in good hands. However, I cannot proceed with the examination with anyone else in the room. If you would prefer, I can reschedule for another day, but this is our standard of care."\n[If he still refuses, do NOT back down. Signal the medical assistant to contact security if needed. The patient\'s safety is the priority.]\n\n**4. Have staff stay with the son.**\n- A medical assistant or social worker should remain in the waiting area to observe the son\'s behavior and ensure he does not attempt to re-enter the exam room.\n- If he leaves the building with the patient\'s belongings (coat, purse), this is a red flag for escalating control.\n\n**Phase 2: Communicating with the Patient Privately**\n\n**1. Create safety and calm.**\n[Sit at her level. Speak gently. Do not rush.]\n"Mrs. [Name], it is just you and me now. I want you to know that nothing you say in this room will be shared with your son without your permission. This is a safe and private conversation."\n\n**2. Assess safety with direct, non-judgmental questions.**\n"I am going to ask you some questions that I ask all of my patients. Please answer as honestly as you can.\n- Do you feel safe at home?\n- Has anyone hit you, kicked you, or hurt you physically?\n- Has anyone taken your money or belongings without your permission?\n- Are you getting enough to eat?\n- Has anyone stopped you from seeing people you want to see?\n- Are you afraid of anyone in your life right now?"\n[Pause after each question. Give her time to answer.]\n\n**3. If she discloses (or partially discloses):**\n"Thank you for telling me that. I believe you. What is happening to you is not okay, and it is not your fault. You deserve to be safe, to have enough food, and to see the people you love. I want to help you, and there are people whose job it is to protect people in your situation."\n\n**4. If she minimizes or denies:**\n"I understand. I want you to know that the bruises I see on your body are concerning to me. They are in patterns that are not typical of falls. I am not trying to get anyone in trouble \u2014 I am trying to make sure you are safe. If you ever feel unsafe or want help, you can call this number at any time [provide card]. You can also tell any nurse or doctor at any visit, and we will help you."\n\n**5. Explain mandatory reporting with compassion.**\n"Mrs. [Name], I want to be honest with you. As a doctor, when I see injuries and circumstances that concern me for a patient\'s safety, I am required by law to contact an agency called Adult Protective Services. This is not to punish you or your son \u2014 it is to make sure you are protected. They will come to talk with you, and their goal is to keep you safe. I am going to make that call today. Is there anything you want to tell me before I do?"\n\n**6. Empower her.**\n"You told me earlier that your son \'knows better\' than you. I have been talking to you for the last 20 minutes, and I want you to know \u2014 you are clear, you are thoughtful, and you know exactly what is happening in your life. You are not confused. You deserve to make your own decisions, and I am going to help you do that."\n\n**Key Principles**: Non-negotiable private examination (clinical standard of care \u2014 the abuser cannot override this). Direct, non-judgmental safety screening. Believed and validated the patient. Explained mandatory reporting transparently. Empowered the patient by affirming her cognitive capacity and autonomy. Did not confront the son directly about abuse (this could escalate danger to the patient after the visit).')
B.aDr=s([B.b1x,B.b_q,B.aQw,B.b2d,B.aWS,B.b1e,B.aS1,B.aZe,B.aWu,B.aPt,B.b1S],t.E)
B.Qi=new A.a5("geriatric-elder-abuse","Elder Abuse Identification",B.aDr,!1)
B.aY7=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 35-year-old right-handed female with a 2-year history of relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis (RRMS) presents to the outpatient PM&R clinic with a 5-day history of progressive right leg weakness and worsening fatigue. She was previously maintained on dimethyl fumarate with one prior relapse 18 months ago involving left optic neuritis that resolved with IV steroids. She reports her right leg now "drags" when walking, she has tripped twice this week, and her fatigue has become so severe she cannot complete a full workday as a middle school teacher. She denies fever, recent illness, or UTI symptoms but notes increased stress from a recent divorce.')
B.aW5=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","Key areas to assess:\n- **Relapse history**: Number, type, and recovery from prior relapses. Time since last relapse. Current disease-modifying therapy (DMT) adherence and tolerability.\n- **Fatigue characterization**: Onset, severity, pattern (primary MS fatigue vs. secondary causes). Use the Modified Fatigue Impact Scale (MFIS) or Fatigue Severity Scale (FSS). Screen for depression (PHQ-9) and sleep disorders.\n- **Bladder/Bowel function**: Urgency, frequency, hesitancy, nocturia, incontinence, constipation. These are common early MS symptoms often underreported.\n- **Cognitive function**: Assess processing speed, attention, and memory. Consider Symbol Digit Modalities Test (SDMT).\n- **Mood and psychosocial**: Screening for depression and anxiety. Impact on work, relationships, and daily activities.\n- **Reproductive history**: Menstrual cycle, contraception use, desire for future pregnancy \u2014 critical for DMT counseling.\n- **Heat sensitivity**: Uhthoff phenomenon \u2014 does heat exposure worsen symptoms?\n- **Fall history**: Frequency, circumstances, injuries sustained, fear of falling.\n- **Spasticity symptoms**: Stiffness, spasms, especially nocturnal. Impact on gait and ADLs.")
B.aOI=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 118/72 mmHg, HR 74 bpm, RR 14, SpO2 99% on room air, Temp 36.8C, BMI 24.\n\n**Neurologic Exam**:\n- **Mental Status**: Alert and oriented x4. Speech fluent, no dysarthria. Affect mildly flat. SDMT score 42 (low-normal, suggestive of mild processing speed deficit).\n- **Cranial Nerves**: Left relative afferent pupillary defect (Marcus Gunn pupil \u2014 residual from prior optic neuritis). Visual acuity 20/20 OD, 20/30 OS. Color desaturation noted OS. Extraocular movements intact. No facial weakness. No dysphagia.\n- **Motor**: Right hip flexion 4/5, right knee extension 4/5, right ankle dorsiflexion 4-/5, right hip abduction 4/5. Left lower extremity 5/5 throughout. Upper extremities 5/5 bilaterally.\n- **Tone**: Mildly increased tone in right lower extremity (Modified Ashworth Scale 1+) at the knee extensors and ankle plantarflexors. Bilateral ankle clonus \u2014 3 beats on the right, none on the left.\n- **Reflexes**: Right patellar 3+, left patellar 2+. Right Achilles 3+. Positive Babinski sign on the right. Positive Hoffman sign bilaterally.\n- **Sensation**: Diminished vibration at the right great toe. Light touch intact. Proprioception mildly impaired at the right ankle.\n- **Coordination**: Mild intention tremor on right heel-to-shin. Finger-to-nose intact bilaterally.\n- **Gait**: Spastic gait with right foot circumduction. Walks without assistive device but veers to the right. Tandem gait impaired. Timed 25-foot walk: 8.2 seconds (elevated).\n- **EDSS Estimation**: Approximately **4.0** \u2014 limited walking ability, fully ambulatory without aid, up and about 12 hours/day despite relatively severe disability.")
B.b00=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","You discuss the clinical picture with your attending and are asked to differentiate a true relapse from a pseudo-relapse and interpret diagnostic findings.")
B.aRl=new A.c("+ How do you differentiate a true MS relapse from a pseudo-relapse, and what diagnostic workup would you order?","**True Relapse vs. Pseudo-Relapse**:\n\nA **true relapse (exacerbation)** is defined as:\n- New or worsening neurologic symptoms lasting **>24 hours**\n- Occurring **>30 days** from the onset of the last relapse\n- **Not attributable** to infection, fever, or metabolic derangement\n- Typically develops over days and peaks within 2-4 weeks\n\nA **pseudo-relapse** involves:\n- Temporary worsening of existing or prior symptoms\n- Triggered by **infection** (especially UTI \u2014 most common in MS), fever, heat exposure (Uhthoff phenomenon), stress, sleep deprivation, or medication changes\n- Resolves when the triggering factor is treated\n- Does NOT represent new CNS inflammatory activity\n\n**In this patient**: The progressive onset over 5 days involving a new deficit (right leg weakness not previously affected) is concerning for a true relapse, but pseudo-relapse must be excluded first.\n\n**Diagnostic Workup**:\n1. **Urinalysis and urine culture** \u2014 UTI is the most common trigger for pseudo-relapse in MS\n2. **CBC with differential** \u2014 screen for infection or other hematologic abnormality\n3. **Comprehensive metabolic panel** \u2014 electrolytes, renal and hepatic function\n4. **MRI brain and cervical/thoracic spine with and without gadolinium** \u2014 look for new or enlarging T2/FLAIR lesions and gadolinium-enhancing lesions indicating active inflammation. A new enhancing lesion confirms a true relapse.\n5. **Vitamin D level (25-OH)** \u2014 low levels are associated with increased relapse risk\n6. **TSH** \u2014 hypothyroidism can mimic MS fatigue\n7. **JC virus antibody titer** \u2014 important for DMT risk stratification if therapy change is considered\n\n**MRI Findings Expected in True Relapse**:\n- New T2 hyperintense lesion(s) in the thoracic or cervical spinal cord (correlating with right leg weakness)\n- Gadolinium enhancement indicating active blood-brain barrier breakdown (enhancing lesions typically persist for 2-8 weeks)\n- Comparison with prior MRI to confirm new lesion burden")
B.aP8=new A.c("+ The MRI shows two new enhancing thoracic cord lesions. The patient's MFIS score is 52 (significant fatigue). How do you assess and classify her fatigue?","**MS Fatigue Assessment and Classification**:\n\nThe MFIS score of 52 (out of 84) indicates **moderate to significant fatigue** impacting physical, cognitive, and psychosocial function. Scores above 38 are considered clinically significant.\n\n**Primary MS Fatigue**:\n- Directly related to the disease process \u2014 immune-mediated inflammation, demyelination, and axonal injury disrupting neural circuits\n- Characteristically described as an overwhelming sense of exhaustion disproportionate to activity level\n- Often worse in the afternoon and exacerbated by heat\n- Distinct from normal tiredness \u2014 does not fully resolve with rest\n\n**Secondary Contributors to Evaluate**:\n1. **Depression** \u2014 prevalence 50% in MS; PHQ-9 screening essential. Depression-related fatigue overlaps significantly.\n2. **Sleep disorders** \u2014 insomnia, restless legs syndrome, nocturia disrupting sleep, obstructive sleep apnea\n3. **Medication side effects** \u2014 dimethyl fumarate can cause fatigue; baclofen, gabapentin, and sedating medications contribute\n4. **Deconditioning** \u2014 reduced physical activity worsens fatigue in a vicious cycle\n5. **Thyroid dysfunction** \u2014 check TSH\n6. **Anemia** \u2014 check CBC\n\n**Fatigue Assessment Tools**:\n- **Modified Fatigue Impact Scale (MFIS)**: 21-item questionnaire with physical (0-36), cognitive (0-40), and psychosocial (0-8) subscales. Total 0-84.\n- **Fatigue Severity Scale (FSS)**: 9-item scale, scores >4 indicate significant fatigue\n- **Visual Analog Scale for Fatigue**: Quick screening in clinic\n- **Epworth Sleepiness Scale**: If sleep disorder is suspected\n\n**EDSS Fatigue Correlation**: At EDSS 4.0, fatigue becomes one of the most disabling symptoms and is the primary reason many MS patients reduce work hours or leave employment.")
B.aO0=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","The workup confirms a true relapse with new enhancing thoracic cord lesions. Urinalysis is negative. You are asked to develop a comprehensive treatment plan.")
B.aPQ=new A.c("+ Outline your acute relapse treatment and long-term rehabilitation management plan.","**Acute Relapse Treatment**:\n1. **IV methylprednisolone 1000 mg daily for 3-5 days** \u2014 standard first-line treatment for acute MS relapse. Accelerates recovery but does not change the ultimate degree of recovery. Administer in the morning to minimize insomnia.\n2. **Oral taper** \u2014 consider a short oral prednisone taper (e.g., 60 mg x 5 days then taper over 1-2 weeks) though this is optional and practice varies.\n3. **Gastroprotection**: Proton pump inhibitor (omeprazole 20 mg daily) during steroid course.\n4. **Blood glucose monitoring**: Especially important as steroids cause hyperglycemia. Check fasting glucose daily during infusion.\n5. **If refractory to steroids**: Consider plasmapheresis (PLEX) \u2014 5-7 exchanges over 10-14 days. Indicated for severe relapses that do not respond to IV steroids.\n\n**Disease-Modifying Therapy Review**:\n- Patient has had a **breakthrough relapse on dimethyl fumarate** \u2014 this warrants discussion of escalating to a **higher-efficacy DMT** (e.g., natalizumab, ocrelizumab, or ofatumumab).\n- Check **JC virus antibody index** before considering natalizumab (risk of PML if JCV positive with index >1.5).\n- Discuss **treatment goals**: Achieving NEDA (No Evidence of Disease Activity) \u2014 no relapses, no new MRI lesions, no disability progression.\n\n**Fatigue Management**:\n1. **Pharmacologic**: Amantadine 100 mg BID (first-line for MS fatigue). Alternative: modafinil 100-200 mg each morning.\n2. **Non-pharmacologic**: Energy conservation techniques, pacing strategies, cooling vests for heat sensitivity, sleep hygiene counseling.\n3. **Exercise**: Aerobic exercise 30 minutes, 3-5 days per week at moderate intensity (60-75% max HR). Swimming or aquatic therapy excellent for heat-sensitive patients. Resistance training 2-3 days per week.\n\n**Spasticity Management**:\n- Stretching program for right lower extremity \u2014 daily hamstring, calf, and hip flexor stretches\n- Consider baclofen 5 mg TID, titrate as needed (max 80 mg/day). Monitor for weakness and sedation.\n- Ankle-foot orthosis (AFO) evaluation for right foot drop to improve gait safety\n\n**Rehabilitation Referrals**:\n- **Physical therapy**: Gait training, balance exercises, strengthening, aerobic conditioning\n- **Occupational therapy**: Energy conservation education, workplace modifications\n- **Neuropsychology**: Formal cognitive assessment given low SDMT score\n- **Psychology/psychiatry**: Mood support given recent divorce and disease burden")
B.aSQ=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient is a middle school teacher and reports she is struggling to get through the school day. She asks about disability options and resources.")
B.b2e=new A.c("+ How would you coordinate MS clinic resources and address disability accommodations for this patient?","**Comprehensive MS Care Coordination**:\n1. **MS specialty clinic model**: Ideally, this patient should receive care through a comprehensive MS center with neurology, PM&R, neuropsychology, social work, and therapy services co-located. Coordinate with her neurologist regarding DMT changes.\n2. **National MS Society resources**: Connect with local chapter for support groups, educational programs, financial assistance, and the MS Navigator program (free personalized guidance).\n3. **Peer support**: MS peer mentoring programs can help address the emotional and practical challenges of living with a chronic neurologic disease.\n\n**Workplace Accommodations (ADA)**:\n1. **Americans with Disabilities Act**: MS qualifies as a disability. The patient is entitled to **reasonable accommodations** from her employer.\n2. **Specific accommodations to recommend**:\n   - Schedule modifications \u2014 arrange teaching schedule to avoid afternoon fatigue peak\n   - Temperature control \u2014 ensure classroom has adequate cooling (Uhthoff phenomenon)\n   - Rest breaks \u2014 scheduled rest periods during the school day\n   - Seated teaching option \u2014 provide a stool or chair in the classroom\n   - Parking \u2014 handicap placard for closer parking to reduce walking distance\n   - Reduced workload \u2014 consider part-time schedule or teaching assistant support\n3. **FMLA**: Family and Medical Leave Act allows up to 12 weeks of unpaid, job-protected leave per year for medical treatment (including infusion therapy and relapses).\n\n**Disability Options**:\n- **Short-term disability**: For acute relapses requiring time off work\n- **Long-term disability/SSDI**: If disease progresses and she cannot sustain full-time employment. EDSS 4.0 is often a tipping point for employment challenges. Early consultation with a disability attorney or advocate may be helpful.\n\n**Long-Term Follow-Up Plan**:\n- MRI brain and spine every 6-12 months (or with new symptoms)\n- Annual EDSS and functional assessments\n- Regular ophthalmologic evaluation (OCT to monitor retinal nerve fiber layer thickness)\n- Bone density monitoring with recurrent steroid use\n- Annual vitamin D level check and supplementation (target 40-60 ng/mL)")
B.aWY=new A.c(u.j,"During the visit, the patient becomes tearful and says: 'My ex-husband and I were talking about starting a family before the divorce. I still want to have children. But I'm terrified that pregnancy will make my MS worse or that my medications will hurt the baby. Can I ever safely have a baby?'")
B.aPx=new A.c("+ Demonstrate how you would counsel this patient about DMT management and pregnancy planning with MS.","I would approach this sensitive conversation with empathy, medical accuracy, and shared decision-making:\n\n**1. Validate her emotions and desire**:\n'Thank you for sharing something so personal with me. Wanting to have children is a completely valid and important goal, and having MS does not mean you cannot be a mother. Let me share what we know about MS and pregnancy so you can make informed decisions.'\n\n**2. Reassure about pregnancy and MS**:\n'The good news is that research consistently shows pregnancy is actually **protective** during MS. Most women experience fewer relapses during pregnancy, especially in the second and third trimesters. This is thought to be related to the natural immune changes that occur to protect the baby. However, there is an increased risk of relapse in the first 3-6 months **postpartum**, so we plan ahead for that.'\n\n**3. Discuss DMT and pregnancy planning**:\n'Regarding your medication, dimethyl fumarate must be stopped before conception \u2014 it is not safe during pregnancy. The timing depends on which medication you are on. For dimethyl fumarate, we recommend stopping at least **one month before** attempting conception. If we switch you to a higher-efficacy therapy like ocrelizumab, the recommendation is to wait at least **6 months** after the last infusion before trying to conceive. Some women choose natalizumab, which can be continued closer to conception and even into early pregnancy in select cases to reduce relapse risk.'\n\n**4. Outline the planning process**:\n'Here is what I would recommend: First, let's stabilize your current relapse and optimize your disease control. Then, when you are ready to start planning, we would coordinate with your neurologist and an obstetrician experienced in high-risk pregnancies. We would create a timeline for safely transitioning off your DMT, plan for disease monitoring during pregnancy, and establish a postpartum plan \u2014 including potentially restarting DMT quickly after delivery and discussing breastfeeding options.'\n\n**5. Address breastfeeding and long-term concerns**:\n'Exclusive breastfeeding may have some protective effect against postpartum relapses, though the data is still evolving. Some newer DMTs are considered compatible with breastfeeding. Your child's risk of developing MS is slightly higher than the general population \u2014 about 2-5% if one parent has MS compared to 0.1% baseline \u2014 but the vast majority of children of parents with MS do not develop the disease.'\n\n**6. Empower and offer ongoing support**:\n'This is not a decision you need to make today. I want you to know that many women with MS have healthy pregnancies and healthy babies. We will work together as a team to find the safest path for you and your future child. Would it be helpful to connect you with other women with MS who have navigated this journey?'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Empathy, normalizing her desire, evidence-based reassurance, structured planning, multidisciplinary coordination, patient empowerment.")
B.aGj=s([B.aY7,B.aW5,B.aOI,B.b00,B.aRl,B.aP8,B.aO0,B.aPQ,B.aSQ,B.b2e,B.aWY,B.aPx],t.E)
B.PT=new A.a5("neuro-ms-relapsing-remitting","Multiple Sclerosis \u2014 Relapsing-Remitting",B.aGj,!1)
B.aR4=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 68-year-old right-handed retired postal worker presents to the PM&R clinic referred by his neurologist for rehabilitation evaluation. He was diagnosed with Parkinson disease 6 years ago, currently Hoehn and Yahr Stage 3. His chief complaints are increasing falls (4 falls in the past month, one resulting in a right wrist fracture), worsening freezing of gait at doorways and when turning, progressive difficulty with handwriting and speech volume, and trouble rising from chairs. His wife accompanies him and reports he has become more withdrawn and forgetful. Current medications include carbidopa-levodopa 25/100 mg TID, rasagiline 1 mg daily, and pramipexole 0.5 mg TID.")
B.aNE=new A.c("History & Systems Review:",'Key areas to assess:\n- **Motor symptoms**: Tremor (rest vs. action), rigidity, bradykinesia, postural instability. Determine "on" vs. "off" periods and their timing relative to medication doses.\n- **Freezing of gait (FOG)**: Triggers (doorways, turning, initiating gait, dual-tasking, crowded spaces). Duration of episodes. Fall history \u2014 circumstances, injuries, fear of falling.\n- **Non-motor symptoms**: Depression (GDS-15), anxiety, apathy, REM sleep behavior disorder, constipation, orthostatic hypotension, urinary urgency, excessive daytime sleepiness.\n- **Cognitive screening**: Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA) \u2014 PD dementia prevalence increases significantly with disease duration. Assess for hallucinations (medication-related vs. disease progression).\n- **Speech/Swallowing**: Hypophonia, monotone speech, drooling, dysphagia risk. Has he been screened recently?\n- **Medication timing**: Exact schedule of carbidopa-levodopa doses. Duration of benefit per dose. Presence of motor fluctuations (wearing off, on-off phenomenon) or dyskinesias.\n- **Functional status**: ADL independence \u2014 dressing (buttons, zippers), hygiene, feeding (tremor affecting utensil use), transfers, community mobility.\n- **Driving status**: Current driving status and safety concerns.\n- **UPDRS assessment**: Unified Parkinson\'s Disease Rating Scale \u2014 Part III (Motor Examination) is critical for documenting disease severity and tracking progression.')
B.aO9=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:",'**Vitals**: Seated BP 138/82 mmHg, HR 68 bpm. Standing BP 112/68 mmHg, HR 80 bpm (orthostatic hypotension present \u2014 >20 mmHg systolic drop). RR 14, Temp 36.9C.\n\n**General**: Masked facies (hypomimia). Reduced blink frequency. Stooped posture with anterior trunk flexion. Mild drooling.\n\n**Neurologic Exam**:\n- **Mental Status**: Alert, oriented x4. MoCA score 22/30 (deficits in visuospatial/executive function, delayed recall, and verbal fluency \u2014 suggestive of PD-MCI). No hallucinations reported.\n- **Speech**: Hypophonic, monotone. Reduced articulatory precision. Intelligible but requires listener effort.\n- **Motor**: Bilateral resting tremor (4-6 Hz), right greater than left. Rigidity \u2014 cogwheel rigidity at wrists bilaterally, lead-pipe rigidity at the neck. Bradykinesia \u2014 slow and decremental finger tapping, reduced amplitude of rapid alternating movements bilaterally.\n- **Strength**: 5/5 throughout when accounting for bradykinesia.\n- **Gait**: Short shuffling steps, reduced arm swing bilaterally (right > left). Festination noted. **Freezing of gait** triggered by turning 180 degrees and when approaching the exam room doorway. Difficulty initiating gait from standing. Retropulsion on pull test \u2014 takes 3 steps backward (abnormal).\n- **Postural Stability**: Positive pull test. Unable to perform tandem gait. Timed Up and Go (TUG): 18 seconds (elevated; >13.5 sec predicts fall risk in PD).\n- **Functional**: Requires armrest push-off to rise from standard chair. Micrographia noted on writing sample. Difficulty with fine motor tasks (buttoning shirt took 45 seconds).\n\n**UPDRS Part III (Motor) Score**: Estimated 38/132 (moderate motor disability) \u2014 scored during an "on" state, approximately 90 minutes after last levodopa dose.')
B.aQk=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The attending asks you to analyze this patient's motor fluctuations and determine if he may be a candidate for advanced therapies.")
B.b3f=new A.c("+ How do you evaluate medication timing and motor fluctuations, and what criteria would make this patient a candidate for deep brain stimulation?",'**Motor Fluctuation Assessment**:\n\n**Wearing-off phenomenon**: This patient is on carbidopa-levodopa TID. With disease progression, the duration of benefit from each dose shortens. Key questions:\n- How long does each dose last? (Typical early: 4-6 hours; wearing off: <4 hours)\n- Does he notice symptoms returning before the next dose?\n- Are there predictable "off" periods tied to medication timing?\n\n**Motor fluctuation types to identify**:\n1. **Wearing off**: Predictable return of symptoms before next dose \u2014 most common. This patient likely has this given H&Y Stage 3 after 6 years.\n2. **On-off phenomenon**: Unpredictable, sudden transitions between mobile ("on") and immobile ("off") states\n3. **Dose failure**: Occasional dose does not take effect \u2014 may relate to protein intake interfering with levodopa absorption\n4. **Peak-dose dyskinesias**: Involuntary choreiform movements at peak levodopa levels\n5. **Diphasic dyskinesias**: Occur at rising and falling levodopa levels\n\n**Medication diary**: Ask the patient and wife to complete a 3-day motor diary documenting "on," "off," "on with dyskinesia," and sleep times in 30-minute intervals.\n\n**Levodopa challenge test**: Administer 1.5x the usual morning dose after 12-hour medication washout. A >30% improvement in UPDRS-III confirms levodopa responsiveness \u2014 a prerequisite for DBS candidacy.\n\n**Deep Brain Stimulation (DBS) Candidacy Criteria**:\n1. **Idiopathic Parkinson disease** (not atypical parkinsonism \u2014 MSA, PSP, CBD)\n2. **Robust levodopa response** (>30% improvement on challenge test)\n3. **Motor fluctuations and/or dyskinesias** inadequately controlled with optimized medication\n4. **Disease duration >4 years** (to ensure correct diagnosis)\n5. **No significant cognitive impairment** \u2014 MoCA score of 22 is borderline. Formal neuropsychological testing is essential. DBS in patients with PD-MCI or dementia worsens cognitive outcomes.\n6. **No untreated psychiatric disease** (active psychosis, severe depression)\n7. **Realistic expectations** \u2014 DBS improves motor fluctuations, tremor, and dyskinesias but does NOT improve levodopa-unresponsive symptoms (freezing of gait in "on" state, postural instability, speech, cognitive decline)\n\n**For this patient**: His freezing of gait and postural instability are concerning because these are often **axial symptoms that may not respond to DBS**. The MoCA of 22 warrants formal neuropsychological evaluation before proceeding. He should be referred to a movement disorders center for multidisciplinary DBS evaluation.')
B.aW8=new A.c('+ The patient reports feeling confused and seeing "small animals" in the corner of the room at night. His wife confirms this. How do you evaluate this?',"**Evaluation of Visual Hallucinations in Parkinson Disease**:\n\nVisual hallucinations in PD are common (affecting 20-40% of patients) and represent a spectrum from benign to concerning:\n\n**Classification**:\n1. **Minor hallucinations (passage/presence)**: Sense of something passing in peripheral vision or feeling of a presence \u2014 common, usually benign\n2. **Formed visual hallucinations**: Seeing people, animals, or objects \u2014 as in this patient\n3. **Hallucinations with loss of insight**: Patient believes hallucinations are real \u2014 indicates PD psychosis\n\n**Differential Diagnosis**:\n1. **Medication-induced**: Dopamine agonists (pramipexole is the most likely culprit in this patient) > amantadine > levodopa > MAO-B inhibitors. Anticholinergics are high risk.\n2. **PD dementia progression**: Hallucinations with cognitive decline suggest evolving Lewy body spectrum disease\n3. **Delirium**: UTI, metabolic derangement, infection \u2014 always rule out acute causes\n4. **REM sleep behavior disorder**: May overlap with hypnagogic/hypnopompic hallucinations\n\n**Workup**:\n- CBC, CMP, urinalysis \u2014 rule out delirium\n- Medication reconciliation \u2014 timing of hallucinations relative to doses\n- Formal neuropsychological testing \u2014 distinguish PD-MCI from PD dementia\n- Ophthalmologic exam \u2014 rule out Charles Bonnet syndrome (visual loss-related hallucinations)\n\n**Management Strategy (Stepwise Medication Adjustment)**:\n1. **First**: Reduce or eliminate dopamine agonist (pramipexole) \u2014 this is the most common culprit and should be tapered first\n2. **Second**: Reduce rasagiline if hallucinations persist\n3. **Third**: If carbidopa-levodopa must be maintained for motor function, add **pimavanserin** (Nuplazid) \u2014 a selective serotonin inverse agonist (5-HT2A) FDA-approved specifically for PD psychosis. Does not worsen motor symptoms.\n4. **Alternative**: Low-dose quetiapine (12.5-50 mg at bedtime) \u2014 commonly used off-label. Avoid all other typical and atypical antipsychotics (haloperidol, olanzapine, risperidone) as they block D2 receptors and catastrophically worsen parkinsonism.\n5. **If dementia is confirmed**: Consider cholinesterase inhibitor (rivastigmine \u2014 the only one FDA-approved for PD dementia) which may also reduce hallucinations.")
B.aVv=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You are developing a comprehensive rehabilitation program for this patient. His neurologist has adjusted his carbidopa-levodopa to QID dosing and tapered pramipexole.")
B.aTH=new A.c("+ Outline your rehabilitation and fall prevention strategy, including evidence-based exercise interventions.",'**Evidence-Based Rehabilitation Program**:\n\n**1. LSVT BIG (Lee Silverman Voice Treatment \u2014 BIG)**:\n- Intensive amplitude-based physical therapy program specifically designed for PD\n- 16 sessions over 4 weeks (4 days/week, 1 hour/session)\n- Focuses on **high-amplitude movements** to counteract bradykinesia and hypokinesia\n- Proven to improve gait speed, step length, balance, and functional mobility\n- Should be initiated as a priority referral\n\n**2. LSVT LOUD**:\n- Intensive speech therapy program for PD hypophonia\n- Same schedule: 16 sessions over 4 weeks\n- Focuses on **loud phonation** to recalibrate vocal effort\n- Proven to improve vocal loudness by 8-10 dB and maintain gains at 2-year follow-up\n- Can be performed concurrently with LSVT BIG\n\n**3. Freezing of Gait Strategies**:\n- **External cueing**: Rhythmic auditory cueing (metronome, music with a steady beat), visual cues (laser pointer attached to walker projecting a line on the floor, colored tape on floor at doorways)\n- **Internal cueing**: Counting "1-2-3-march," thinking about taking large steps\n- **Attentional strategies**: Avoid dual-tasking when walking. Stop talking when walking through doorways.\n- **Weight-shifting technique**: Shift weight side to side before initiating gait\n- **Turning technique**: Use wide arc turns instead of pivoting. Avoid turning in narrow spaces.\n\n**4. Fall Prevention Program**:\n- **Orthostatic hypotension management**: Rise slowly from sitting/lying. Compression stockings (thigh-high, 20-30 mmHg). Elevate head of bed 30 degrees. Increase salt and fluid intake if BP tolerates. Consider midodrine 5 mg TID or droxidopa if symptomatic.\n- **Home safety evaluation**: Remove throw rugs, improve lighting (especially nightlights for nocturia), install grab bars in bathroom, raised toilet seat, bedside commode for nighttime use.\n- **Assistive device**: Consider a rolling walker with wheels \u2014 NOT a standard walker, which can worsen freezing. A walker with a laser line attachment can assist with freezing.\n- **Hip protectors**: Consider for patients with recurrent falls to reduce fracture risk.\n\n**5. Exercise Prescription**:\n- **Aerobic**: 150 minutes/week moderate intensity (cycling, treadmill, or dance). Emerging evidence supports vigorous exercise (70-85% max HR) for neuroprotective benefit.\n- **Resistance training**: 2-3 days/week targeting major muscle groups, emphasis on anti-gravity muscles and trunk extensors to address stooped posture.\n- **Balance training**: Tai chi (proven to reduce falls in PD), yoga, or structured balance exercises 2-3 days/week.\n- **Dual-task training**: Progressive cognitive-motor dual-tasking in a safe, supervised environment.\n- **Boxing/PWR! Moves**: Non-contact boxing programs designed for PD are growing in evidence and improve agility and confidence.\n\n**6. Additional Rehabilitation Interventions**:\n- **Occupational therapy**: Adaptive equipment for fine motor tasks (built-up utensils, button hooks, electric razor), handwriting exercises (large-amplitude letter practice), home modification assessment.\n- **Swallowing evaluation**: Refer for VFSS given drooling and disease severity \u2014 silent aspiration risk increases with PD progression.')
B.aMz=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient's wife asks about his driving safety. He insists he is still safe to drive. His wife privately tells you she is terrified of his driving and has noticed near-misses.")
B.aNU=new A.c("+ How do you approach driving assessment and community resource coordination for this patient?","**Driving Assessment in Parkinson Disease**:\n\n**Risk Factors Warranting Formal Evaluation (this patient has multiple)**:\n- Hoehn & Yahr Stage 3 (postural instability)\n- Cognitive impairment (MoCA 22 \u2014 executive dysfunction affects driving)\n- Freezing of gait (may correlate with \"freezing\" in driving decisions)\n- Recurrent falls (impaired reaction time)\n- Motor fluctuations (unpredictable \"off\" periods)\n- Orthostatic hypotension (risk of syncope)\n- Visual hallucinations (even if insight preserved)\n\n**Formal Driving Evaluation Process**:\n1. **Clinical pre-driving assessment**: Trail Making Test B, Useful Field of View test, and assessment of neck rotation, reaction time, and divided attention.\n2. **Certified Driver Rehabilitation Specialist (CDRS) evaluation**: Comprehensive on-road and off-road assessment. This is the gold standard and provides an objective third-party determination.\n3. **On-road assessment**: Evaluates actual driving ability in real-world conditions including lane keeping, turns, merging, and response to unexpected events.\n\n**Approach with the Patient**:\n- Frame driving assessment as a safety and independence tool, not as taking something away.\n- Emphasize: 'I want to help you stay driving as long as it is safe \u2014 a formal evaluation can identify areas where you are still strong and areas where adaptive strategies might help.'\n- If the evaluation determines driving cessation is necessary, provide the recommendation in writing and document in the chart.\n\n**State reporting obligations**: Laws vary by state. Some states require physician reporting of conditions that impair driving safety. Know your state's mandatory vs. voluntary reporting laws.\n\n**Community Resources and Support**:\n1. **Parkinson's Foundation**: Helpline (1-800-4PD-INFO), exercise programs, care partner resources, educational materials\n2. **Michael J. Fox Foundation**: Research updates, clinical trial finder, patient education\n3. **Local PD support groups**: Both for patients and for care partners (separate groups are important)\n4. **Transportation alternatives**: Paratransit services, ride-sharing programs, volunteer driver programs, Area Agency on Aging transportation resources\n5. **Exercise community**: PD-specific programs \u2014 Rock Steady Boxing, Dance for PD, PD-specific yoga and cycling classes\n6. **Caregiver resources**: Respite care referral, caregiver support groups, Area Agency on Aging services\n7. **Legal and financial planning**: Encourage advance directive completion and financial/healthcare power of attorney while the patient has capacity")
B.aWa=new A.c(u.j,"After the patient goes to the restroom, his wife breaks down crying. She says: 'Doctor, I am at my breaking point. I haven't slept through the night in months because he acts out his dreams and hits me in his sleep. I have to watch him every second so he doesn't fall. I can't leave the house. I love him but I don't know how much longer I can do this.'")
B.b3M=new A.c("+ Demonstrate how you would address caregiver burnout and provide support to this patient's wife.","I would respond with genuine empathy and a structured action plan:\n\n**1. Validate and acknowledge her experience**:\n'Thank you for telling me this. What you are describing is real, it is serious, and it is incredibly common among care partners of people with Parkinson disease. You are not failing \u2014 you are carrying an enormous burden, and it is completely understandable that you are exhausted. Your wellbeing matters just as much as his, and I want to help.'\n\n**2. Address the immediate safety concern (RBD)**:\n'What you are describing \u2014 acting out dreams and hitting you in his sleep \u2014 is called REM Sleep Behavior Disorder. It is very common in Parkinson disease and it is treatable. I am going to talk with his neurologist about starting melatonin at bedtime, which is the first-line treatment and is very safe. If that is not enough, clonazepam at a low dose can help. In the meantime, for your safety, please consider sleeping in a separate bed or placing a body pillow between you. This is not about separating from him emotionally \u2014 it is about keeping you safe.'\n\n**3. Develop a caregiver support plan**:\n'Here is what I want to put in place for you:\n- **Respite care**: I am going to connect you with our social worker who can arrange in-home respite care so you can have regular breaks \u2014 even a few hours a week to yourself can make a significant difference.\n- **Caregiver support group**: The Parkinson's Foundation runs support groups specifically for care partners. Being with people who truly understand what you are going through can be incredibly sustaining.\n- **Adult day program**: We can look into PD-specific adult day programs where he can participate in exercise and social activities while you have time for yourself.\n- **Home health aide**: Depending on your insurance, we may be able to get a home health aide for assistance with his daily care.'\n\n**4. Screen for caregiver depression and health**:\n'I also want to ask \u2014 when is the last time you saw your own doctor? Caregiver burnout puts you at high risk for depression, anxiety, and physical health problems. I strongly encourage you to schedule a visit for yourself. Your health matters.'\n\n**5. Discuss future planning**:\n'I know this is hard to think about, but as Parkinson disease progresses, the care demands will increase. It is important to have conversations now \u2014 together \u2014 about long-term care preferences, advance directives, and financial planning while he is still able to participate in those decisions. Our social worker can help facilitate these conversations.'\n\n**6. Provide immediate resources**:\n'Before you leave today, I am going to give you the Parkinson's Foundation Helpline number \u2014 they have counselors available who specialize in supporting care partners. You do not have to do this alone.'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Validating caregiver distress, addressing safety concerns (RBD), providing concrete actionable steps, screening for caregiver health, future planning, immediate resource provision.")
B.aFG=s([B.aR4,B.aNE,B.aO9,B.aQk,B.b3f,B.aW8,B.aVv,B.aTH,B.aMz,B.aNU,B.aWa,B.b3M],t.E)
B.R3=new A.a5("neuro-parkinsons-rehabilitation","Parkinson Disease Rehabilitation",B.aFG,!1)
B.aYY=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 62-year-old right-handed male presents to the PM&R clinic with a 2-year history of progressive new weakness in his left lower extremity and worsening generalized fatigue. He contracted paralytic poliomyelitis at age 4, affecting his left leg. He was treated with bracing and physical therapy as a child and made a significant recovery, walking independently without assistive devices since age 10. He worked as a maintenance supervisor for 35 years and was an avid recreational cyclist. Over the past 2 years, he has noticed his left leg "giving out" when climbing stairs, new difficulty walking more than two blocks, profound fatigue by midafternoon, and new onset of bilateral shoulder pain with overhead activities. He is frustrated because he has always prided himself on overcoming his childhood illness.')
B.b0o=new A.c("History & Systems Review:",'Key areas to assess:\n- **Original polio history**: Age at onset, muscles affected, extent of initial paralysis, recovery pattern, childhood treatments (bracing, surgery, rehabilitation).\n- **Stable period**: Duration of functional stability after initial recovery. What activities was he able to perform? This "plateau period" typically lasts 25-40+ years before post-polio symptoms emerge.\n- **New symptoms timeline**: Gradual onset of new weakness, fatigue, muscle pain, joint pain. Characterize each symptom.\n- **Fatigue assessment**: Distinguish **central fatigue** (overwhelming exhaustion, cognitive fog) from **peripheral fatigue** (rapid muscle fatigability with use). Both are common in post-polio syndrome (PPS). Use Fatigue Severity Scale.\n- **Pain characterization**: Muscle pain (overuse of compensatory muscles), joint pain (biomechanical stress from chronic asymmetry), or neuropathic pain.\n- **Functional decline**: Specific activities now difficult \u2014 stairs, walking distance, transfers, ADLs, work tasks. Quantify decline.\n- **Cold intolerance**: Common in PPS-affected limbs due to autonomic dysfunction.\n- **Respiratory function**: Any dyspnea, sleep-disordered breathing, or morning headaches? Respiratory muscle involvement may have been subclinical initially.\n- **Swallowing**: Bulbar polio involvement? New dysphagia?\n- **Weight changes**: Weight gain from reduced activity worsens biomechanical stress.\n- **Psychological impact**: Depression, grief over loss of hard-won function, identity as a "polio survivor."')
B.aZr=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 132/78 mmHg, HR 72 bpm, RR 16, SpO2 97% on room air, Temp 36.7C, BMI 28.\n\n**Musculoskeletal/Neurologic Exam**:\n- **Left Lower Extremity**: Moderate quadriceps atrophy. Left thigh circumference 3 cm less than right. Left hip flexion 3+/5, knee extension 3/5 (decreased from baseline of 4+/5 per prior records), ankle dorsiflexion 3/5, ankle plantarflexion 4/5. Mild genu recurvatum on standing (compensatory hyperextension at the knee due to quad weakness).\n- **Right Lower Extremity**: 5/5 throughout. Mild quadriceps hypertrophy \u2014 consistent with chronic compensatory overuse.\n- **Upper Extremities**: Left deltoid 4/5, right deltoid 4+/5. Left triceps 4-/5, right triceps 4+/5. Bilateral grip strength reduced (left 18 kg, right 24 kg by dynamometry). New weakness in muscles not previously thought to be affected \u2014 suggests subclinical involvement during original infection.\n- **Reflexes**: Left patellar absent, right patellar 1+. Left Achilles absent, right Achilles 1+. Bilateral upper extremity reflexes 1+. Plantar responses downgoing bilaterally (no upper motor neuron signs).\n- **Sensation**: Intact to light touch, pinprick, vibration, and proprioception throughout. (Polio is a pure motor neuron disease \u2014 sensory preservation is expected.)\n- **Tone**: Reduced tone in left lower extremity. No spasticity.\n- **Gait**: Antalgic gait favoring the left leg. Left quadriceps lurch (knee hyperextends in stance phase to compensate for quad weakness). Mild left Trendelenburg sign. Uses no assistive device. Endurance testing: reports significant fatigue after 6-minute walk test (distance 320 meters, below normal).\n- **Shoulders**: Bilateral supraspinatus tenderness. Painful arc 60-120 degrees bilaterally. Positive Neer impingement sign bilaterally \u2014 consistent with rotator cuff overuse from compensatory upper extremity weight-bearing (using arms to push up from chairs, pull on railings).\n\n**Pulmonary Function Testing**: FVC 78% predicted (mildly reduced). Maximum inspiratory pressure 65 cmH2O (mildly reduced). Suggest subclinical respiratory muscle weakness.")
B.aRs=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The attending asks you to confirm the diagnosis and discuss the key workup needed to differentiate post-polio syndrome from other progressive neuromuscular diseases.")
B.aVM=new A.c("+ What are the diagnostic criteria for post-polio syndrome, and how do you differentiate it from ALS? What would you expect on EMG/NCS?","**Post-Polio Syndrome Diagnostic Criteria** (March of Dimes criteria):\n1. **Confirmed history of paralytic poliomyelitis** with evidence of motor neuron loss (residual weakness, atrophy, areflexia on exam)\n2. **Period of partial or complete neurologic recovery** followed by a **stable interval** of at least 15 years (typically 25-40 years)\n3. **Gradual or sudden onset** of new progressive and persistent muscle weakness, with or without fatigue, muscle atrophy, or muscle/joint pain\n4. **Symptoms persist for at least 1 year**\n5. **Exclusion of other conditions** that could explain the symptoms\n\n**PPS is a diagnosis of exclusion**. The pathophysiology involves progressive dysfunction and dropout of enlarged motor units that were created during recovery from acute polio (through collateral sprouting and reinnervation). Over decades, these oversized motor units cannot be maintained.\n\n**Differentiating PPS from ALS** (critical distinction):\n\n| Feature | Post-Polio Syndrome | ALS |\n|---|---|---|\n| Progression | Very slow (years) | Rapid (months) |\n| Upper motor neuron signs | Absent | Present (hyperreflexia, Babinski, spasticity) |\n| Sensory findings | Absent | Absent |\n| Fasciculations | May be present (chronic) | Prominent, widespread |\n| Bulbar involvement | Only if prior bulbar polio | Common |\n| Distribution | Muscles affected by original polio and compensatory muscles | Diffuse, not limited to prior polio distribution |\n| Reflexes | Reduced/absent | Pathologically brisk |\n\n**This patient**: No upper motor neuron signs (no hyperreflexia, no Babinski, no spasticity), very slow progression over 2 years, weakness predominantly in prior-polio-affected muscles \u2014 consistent with PPS over ALS.\n\n**EMG/NCS Expected Findings in PPS**:\n- **Nerve conduction studies**: Motor amplitudes may be reduced in affected limbs. Sensory studies should be normal (polio does not affect sensory neurons). Normal conduction velocities.\n- **Needle EMG**: \n  - **Chronic denervation-reinnervation changes**: Large-amplitude, long-duration, polyphasic motor unit potentials (MUPs) \u2014 evidence of prior collateral sprouting\n  - **Fibrillation potentials and positive sharp waves**: Present in actively denervating muscles, indicating ongoing motor neuron dropout\n  - **Reduced recruitment**: Fewer motor units firing at higher rates\n  - **Fasciculation potentials**: May be present but are typically stable, not the rapidly progressive pattern seen in ALS\n  - **Key distinction from ALS**: In PPS, active denervation is confined primarily to muscles in the distribution of original polio involvement (and compensatory muscles). In ALS, active denervation is widespread across multiple myotomes and progresses rapidly.\n\n**Additional Workup**:\n- MRI cervical and lumbar spine \u2014 rule out radiculopathy, myelopathy, spinal stenosis\n- TSH, vitamin B12, CK \u2014 screen for metabolic and myopathic causes\n- Sleep study \u2014 if respiratory symptoms or sleep complaints (sleep-disordered breathing is common)\n- Pulmonary function tests \u2014 baseline and serial monitoring")
B.aRT=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","EMG/NCS confirms chronic denervation-reinnervation changes with scattered active denervation in the left lower extremity and bilateral upper extremities. MRI spine is unremarkable. You confirm the diagnosis of post-polio syndrome.")
B.aPL=new A.c("+ Outline your rehabilitation management plan emphasizing energy conservation and avoiding overuse.",'**Core Management Principles for PPS**:\nThe overarching philosophy is **"less is more"** \u2014 unlike most rehabilitation paradigms, patients with PPS must reduce physical stress on weakened motor units to preserve function.\n\n**1. Energy Conservation and Pacing**:\n- **Activity modification**: Distribute tasks throughout the day with scheduled rest periods. Use the "50% rule" \u2014 do only 50% of what you think you can do before resting.\n- **Pacing education**: Alternate strenuous and light activities. Plan the most demanding tasks for peak energy times (typically morning).\n- **Planned rest periods**: 15-20 minute rest breaks mid-morning and mid-afternoon. Even brief rest periods allow motor unit recovery.\n- **Weight management**: BMI 28 \u2014 even modest weight loss reduces biomechanical stress on weakened muscles and joints.\n- **Lifestyle restructuring**: May need to modify work duties, reduce hours, or consider vocational rehabilitation.\n\n**2. Exercise Prescription (Non-Fatiguing)**:\n- **DO prescribe**: Low-intensity, non-fatiguing exercise. Swimming or aquatic therapy (buoyancy unloads joints). Gentle cycling at low resistance. General conditioning at sub-maximal effort.\n- **DO NOT prescribe**: High-intensity strengthening, progressive resistive exercises for severely weakened muscles, or "no pain no gain" approaches. Overuse exercise accelerates motor unit dropout.\n- **Guidelines**: Exercise to the point of mild fatigue, NOT exhaustion. If a muscle is weaker the next day, the exercise was too intense. Allow 48 hours between strengthening sessions for the same muscle group.\n- **Stretching**: Gentle range of motion and stretching daily \u2014 safe and beneficial for maintaining flexibility.\n\n**3. Assistive Devices and Orthotics**:\n- **Knee-ankle-foot orthosis (KAFO) or knee orthosis**: Evaluate for a stance-control KAFO or carbon fiber AFO with anterior tibial shell for the left leg \u2014 address the genu recurvatum and reduce quadriceps demand.\n- **Cane or forearm crutch**: Left-side weakness warrants assistive device for community ambulation \u2014 use a cane in the right hand to unload the left leg.\n- **Motorized scooter or wheelchair**: For longer community distances to conserve energy. Many PPS patients resist this strongly \u2014 frame as an "energy conservation tool" that preserves function for priority activities.\n- **Adaptive equipment**: Reacher, shower bench, raised toilet seat, grab bars.\n\n**4. Pain Management**:\n- **Shoulder pain**: Modify compensatory upper extremity weight-bearing. Shoulder strengthening within non-fatiguing limits. Consider subacromial corticosteroid injection for impingement. Avoid overhead activities.\n- **Muscle pain**: Warm compresses, gentle massage, low-dose NSAIDs as needed. Avoid chronic NSAID use.\n- **Joint pain**: Biomechanical optimization with orthoses, weight management, activity modification.\n\n**5. Respiratory Monitoring**:\n- Serial pulmonary function tests every 1-2 years (FVC, MIP, MEP)\n- If FVC drops below 50% predicted or symptoms of nocturnal hypoventilation develop, initiate nocturnal non-invasive ventilation (BiPAP)\n- Pneumococcal and influenza vaccination essential\n\n**6. Pharmacology**:\n- No FDA-approved medication for PPS weakness or fatigue. Pyridostigmine has shown modest benefit in some studies. Modafinil may help central fatigue.\n- Avoid medications that worsen neuromuscular transmission (aminoglycosides, high-dose magnesium).')
B.aMO=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient needs a new orthotic and long-term follow-up. You discuss the care plan and durable medical equipment prescription process.")
B.aNP=new A.c("+ Describe the orthotic prescription process and long-term follow-up plan for this patient.","**Orthotic Prescription Process**:\n\n1. **Referral to certified orthotist**: Prescribe a custom orthotic evaluation for the left lower extremity. Include specific diagnosis (post-polio syndrome, left lower extremity weakness with genu recurvatum), functional goals (safe community ambulation, reduce compensatory gait deviations), and any biomechanical requirements.\n\n2. **Orthotic options to consider**:\n   - **Stance-control KAFO**: Locks at the knee during stance phase to prevent hyperextension, unlocks during swing to allow knee flexion for a more natural gait. Appropriate for quadriceps strength of 3/5.\n   - **Carbon fiber AFO with anterior tibial shell**: If ankle dorsiflexion weakness is the primary concern and quad weakness is mild-moderate.\n   - **Custom knee orthosis (Swedish knee cage)**: A lighter option if only knee hyperextension control is needed.\n\n3. **Insurance documentation**: Letter of medical necessity (LMN) must include diagnosis, functional limitation, why the device is medically necessary, and expected functional benefit. Include EMG findings and manual muscle testing to support medical necessity. PPS patients often face insurance denials \u2014 be prepared to appeal.\n\n4. **Fitting and follow-up**: Initial fitting, gait training with new device (coordinate with PT), 2-week follow-up for adjustments, then quarterly until optimized.\n\n**Long-Term Follow-Up Plan**:\n1. **PM&R follow-up**: Every 6 months initially, then annually once stable. Monitor for progressive weakness using standardized manual muscle testing and functional measures (6-minute walk test, timed stair climb).\n2. **Pulmonary function**: Annual PFTs (FVC, MIP, MEP). Refer to pulmonology if FVC drops below 70% predicted.\n3. **Orthotic and equipment review**: Annual assessment of brace fit, wear, and function. As weakness progresses, equipment needs change.\n4. **Bone density screening**: Post-polio limbs are at higher risk for osteoporosis due to reduced weight-bearing. DEXA scan at baseline and every 2 years.\n5. **Vocational rehabilitation**: If work duties become unsustainable, refer to vocational rehabilitation services for job modification, retraining, or disability evaluation.\n6. **Fall risk reassessment**: Annual fall screening with TUG test and fall diary.\n7. **Mental health monitoring**: Ongoing screening for depression and adjustment disorder given the psychological burden of progressive disability after decades of stability.")
B.aZb=new A.c(u.j,"When you present the diagnosis of post-polio syndrome and recommend bracing and activity modification, the patient becomes visibly upset. He says: 'Absolutely not. I beat polio when I was a kid. I worked my whole life. I'm not going to let this stop me now. I don't need a brace or a scooter \u2014 I just need to push harder. That's what got me through it the first time.'")
B.aMy=new A.c("+ Demonstrate how you would address this patient's denial and resistance to treatment recommendations.","I would approach this with deep respect for his identity and lived experience while providing honest medical guidance:\n\n**1. Honor his history and identity**:\n'I hear you, and I have enormous respect for what you accomplished. Recovering from polio as a child and living a full, active life as a maintenance supervisor and cyclist \u2014 that took incredible determination and strength. You absolutely did beat polio, and no one is taking that away from you.'\n\n**2. Reframe the current situation with honesty**:\n'What I need you to understand is that what is happening now is different from what happened when you were four years old. Back then, your body created new nerve connections to compensate for the ones the virus destroyed. Those compensating nerve cells have been doing the work of 5 or 10 cells for over 50 years. Now, after decades of extra duty, some of those nerve cells are wearing out. This is not a failure of willpower \u2014 it is biology, and pushing harder is actually the one thing that can make it worse faster.'\n\n**3. Use an analogy he can relate to**:\n'Think of it this way \u2014 if you had a machine at work running at triple its rated capacity for 50 years straight, you would not fix it by running it harder. You would reduce the load, do preventive maintenance, and use it more efficiently so it lasts as long as possible. That is exactly what we are doing here.'\n\n**4. Connect treatment to his goals**:\n'I do not want to take away your independence \u2014 I want to preserve it. If we use a brace to protect your knee now, you may keep walking independently for years longer than if we do nothing. A scooter for long distances is not giving up \u2014 it means you save your energy for the things that matter most to you. What activities are most important to you? Let's build a plan that keeps you doing those things.'\n\n**5. Offer graduated acceptance**:\n'We do not have to do everything at once. Let's start with one thing \u2014 would you be willing to try the brace for two weeks and see how your knee feels? If it does not help, we will talk about alternatives. And the exercise we prescribe will not be 'stop moving' \u2014 it will be smarter, more targeted exercise that protects your muscles instead of burning them out.'\n\n**6. Normalize the emotional response**:\n'Many polio survivors feel exactly the way you do when they hear this diagnosis. There is a grief in confronting this after so many years of doing well. If you are open to it, I can connect you with a support group of other polio survivors going through the same thing. Sometimes talking to someone who truly understands your experience can help.'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Respecting patient identity and autonomy, reframing medical reality without dismissing emotions, using relatable analogies, motivational interviewing approach, collaborative goal-setting, graduated acceptance, peer support referral.")
B.aEK=s([B.aYY,B.b0o,B.aZr,B.aRs,B.aVM,B.aRT,B.aPL,B.aMO,B.aNP,B.aZb,B.aMy],t.E)
B.QV=new A.a5("neuro-post-polio-syndrome","Post-Polio Syndrome",B.aEK,!1)
B.aOB=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 55-year-old right-handed male civil engineer presents to the PM&R clinic after EMG/NCS performed by the referring neurologist confirmed findings consistent with ALS. He was referred for rehabilitation evaluation and care coordination. His chief complaints are progressive right hand weakness over 6 months causing difficulty with drafting and typing, fasciculations in both arms and the right thigh, recent onset of tripping on uneven surfaces, and a 10-pound unintentional weight loss. He has not yet been told the diagnosis. The referring neurologist\'s note reads: "EMG shows diffuse denervation with fasciculations in three body regions. Nerve conduction studies show reduced CMAP amplitudes with normal sensory responses. Please discuss diagnosis and initiate multidisciplinary management." His wife and adult son accompany him. He appears anxious and states: "I\'ve been reading online, and I\'m terrified this is ALS."')
B.b38=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","Key areas to assess:\n- **Motor symptoms timeline**: Onset with right hand weakness (grip, pinch). Progressive involvement of intrinsic hand muscles, then forearm. Recent tripping suggests early lower extremity involvement. Ask about foot drop, stair difficulty.\n- **Bulbar function**: Speech changes (slurring, nasal quality), dysphagia (choking on liquids vs solids, meal duration changes, drooling), jaw fatigue when chewing. Weight loss may reflect early dysphagia or hypermetabolism.\n- **Respiratory symptoms**: Orthopnea, morning headaches, excessive daytime somnolence, reduced exercise tolerance \u2014 all early signs of diaphragmatic weakness. Sleep-disordered breathing. Ask about ability to count to 20 in a single breath.\n- **Fasciculations**: Distribution, timing, whether they preceded weakness.\n- **Negative symptoms (critical for excluding mimics)**: No sensory changes, no bowel/bladder dysfunction, no eye movement abnormalities, no pain (pain argues against pure ALS). No cognitive or behavioral changes (screen for frontotemporal involvement with brief cognitive/behavioral assessment).\n- **Family history**: Any family members with ALS, dementia, or neurodegenerative disease? Approximately 5-10% of ALS is familial.\n- **Functional status**: Handwriting deterioration, difficulty with buttons and zippers, opening jars, utensil use. Work status \u2014 still working? Able to perform job duties?\n- **Psychosocial**: Mood screen (PHQ-9). Current support system. Insurance status. Advance directive status. Financial planning concerns.\n- **ALS Functional Rating Scale-Revised (ALSFRS-R)**: Administer the 12-item scale (score 0-48). This is the single most important functional outcome measure in ALS and should be repeated at every visit to track disease progression.")
B.aUr=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 134/78 mmHg, HR 76 bpm, RR 16, SpO2 97% on room air, Weight 172 lbs (down from 182 lbs six months ago), BMI 24.1.\n\n**General**: Well-appearing male, alert, anxious. Normal body habitus with early thenar wasting noted bilaterally (right > left). No respiratory distress at rest.\n\n**Neurologic Exam**:\n- **Cranial Nerves**: II-XII grossly intact. No tongue fasciculations or atrophy. Normal palatal elevation. Normal extraocular movements (sparing of eye movements is characteristic of ALS). Speech clear without dysarthria at this stage. Jaw jerk mildly brisk.\n- **Motor \u2014 Upper Extremities**: Right hand intrinsic muscle atrophy (first dorsal interosseous, thenar eminence). Left thenar eminence early flattening. Fasciculations visible in bilateral deltoids and right forearm at rest. Right grip strength 18 kg (reduced), left grip 32 kg. Right finger abduction 3+/5, left 4+/5. Bilateral biceps 5/5, triceps 5/5, deltoids 5-/5. Fine motor: difficulty with rapid finger tapping on the right, impaired pincer grasp.\n- **Motor \u2014 Lower Extremities**: Right tibialis anterior 4/5, left 5/5. Right hip flexion 4+/5. Bilateral quadriceps 5/5. Fasciculations visible in right quadriceps and bilateral gastrocnemius.\n- **Tone**: No spasticity in extremities currently. Mildly increased tone at bilateral ankles on rapid dorsiflexion.\n- **Reflexes**: Biceps 3+ bilaterally, triceps 3+ bilaterally, brachioradialis 3+ bilaterally, patellar 3+ bilaterally, Achilles 3+ bilaterally. **Positive Hoffman sign bilaterally. Positive Babinski on the right.** The combination of upper motor neuron signs (hyperreflexia, Hoffman, Babinski) with lower motor neuron signs (atrophy, fasciculations, weakness) is the hallmark of ALS.\n- **Sensory**: Intact to light touch, pinprick, proprioception, and vibration throughout (normal sensory exam is expected in ALS).\n- **Gait**: Mild right foot drop with steppage gait pattern. Tandem gait impaired. No assistive device currently used.\n\n**Respiratory Assessment**: Forced vital capacity (FVC) sitting: 82% predicted. FVC supine: 74% predicted (>10% drop from sitting to supine suggests early diaphragmatic weakness). Maximal inspiratory pressure (MIP): -52 cmH2O (normal >-60). Sniff nasal inspiratory pressure (SNIP): 58 cmH2O.\n\n**ALSFRS-R Score**: 39/48 (deficits in handwriting, cutting food, dressing, walking, and climbing stairs).")
B.aR8=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The attending asks you to present the diagnostic criteria that support this patient's ALS diagnosis and discuss important mimics that must be excluded.")
B.b_M=new A.c("+ Explain the revised El Escorial criteria and the EMG/NCS patterns that confirm ALS. What mimics must be excluded?","**Revised El Escorial Criteria (Awaji-Shima Criteria)**:\n\nALS diagnosis requires:\n1. **Evidence of lower motor neuron (LMN) degeneration** by clinical, electrophysiologic, or neuropathologic examination\n2. **Evidence of upper motor neuron (UMN) degeneration** by clinical examination\n3. **Progressive spread** of symptoms within a region or to other regions\n4. **Absence of**: Electrophysiologic or pathologic evidence of other disease processes that explain the signs\n\n**Diagnostic Certainty Levels**:\n- **Clinically Definite ALS**: UMN + LMN signs in at least 3 body regions (bulbar, cervical, thoracic, lumbosacral)\n- **Clinically Probable ALS**: UMN + LMN signs in at least 2 regions with some UMN signs rostral to LMN signs\n- **Clinically Probable \u2014 Laboratory Supported**: UMN + LMN signs in only 1 region, OR UMN signs alone in 1 region with EMG evidence of LMN involvement in at least 2 regions\n- **Clinically Possible ALS**: UMN + LMN signs in only 1 region, or UMN signs in 2+ regions, or LMN signs rostral to UMN signs\n\n**This patient's classification**: He has UMN signs (hyperreflexia, Hoffman, Babinski) and LMN signs (atrophy, fasciculations, weakness) in at least 2 regions (cervical and lumbosacral), with EMG confirming LMN involvement in 3 regions. This meets **Clinically Probable or Definite ALS** depending on thoracic paraspinal EMG findings.\n\n**EMG/NCS Pattern in ALS**:\n- **Nerve conduction studies**: Reduced compound muscle action potential (CMAP) amplitudes reflecting motor axon loss. **Normal sensory nerve action potentials (SNAPs)** \u2014 this is critical. Conduction velocities are normal or mildly reduced (not below 70% of normal). No conduction block (conduction block suggests multifocal motor neuropathy, a treatable mimic).\n- **Needle EMG**: Active denervation (fibrillation potentials, positive sharp waves) and chronic reinnervation (large-amplitude, long-duration, polyphasic motor unit potentials with reduced recruitment) in at least 3 body regions. Fasciculation potentials are present. The Awaji criteria accept fasciculation potentials as equivalent to fibrillation potentials for diagnosing LMN involvement.\n\n**Critical Mimics to Exclude**:\n1. **Multifocal motor neuropathy with conduction block (MMN)**: Treatable with IVIG. Key differentiator: conduction block on NCS, absent UMN signs, often asymmetric distal upper extremity weakness. Anti-GM1 antibodies present in ~50%.\n2. **Cervical spondylotic myelopathy with radiculopathy**: Can produce UMN + LMN signs. MRI cervical spine is essential. However, cervical stenosis is common incidentally \u2014 the EMG pattern must show involvement beyond a single cervical root distribution.\n3. **Kennedy disease (X-linked bulbospinal muscular atrophy)**: Slowly progressive, LMN predominant, bulbar involvement, gynecomastia, sensory neuropathy on NCS. Genetic testing for CAG repeat expansion in androgen receptor gene.\n4. **Inclusion body myositis (IBM)**: Proximal and distal weakness, especially finger flexors and quadriceps. CK mildly elevated. EMG can mimic neurogenic disease. Muscle biopsy is diagnostic.\n5. **Hyperparathyroidism, lead toxicity, thyroid disease**: Metabolic causes of motor neuron dysfunction. Check calcium, PTH, lead level, TSH.\n6. **Primary lateral sclerosis (PLS)**: Pure UMN disease. Must observe for at least 4 years without LMN signs before diagnosing PLS over ALS.\n7. **Benign fasciculation syndrome**: Fasciculations without weakness, atrophy, or denervation on EMG.\n\n**Minimum workup to exclude mimics**: MRI brain and cervical spine, complete metabolic panel, TSH, PTH, B12, serum protein electrophoresis, GM1 antibodies, CK, and consideration of genetic testing if family history or atypical features.")
B.aXz=new A.c("+ The patient's ALSFRS-R drops 4 points over 3 months and his FVC falls to 68% predicted. Discuss respiratory management milestones.","**Respiratory Decline in ALS \u2014 Critical Milestones and Interventions**:\n\nRespiratory failure is the leading cause of death in ALS. Proactive monitoring and timely intervention directly impact survival and quality of life.\n\n**Monitoring Schedule**: FVC, MIP, SNIP, and nocturnal oximetry should be assessed every 1-3 months depending on rate of decline.\n\n**Milestone 1 \u2014 FVC <80% predicted or symptomatic orthopnea**:\n- Initiate **nocturnal noninvasive ventilation (NIV/BiPAP)**\n- Typical starting settings: IPAP 10, EPAP 4, backup rate 12\n- Begin with nocturnal use, gradually increase to daytime as needed\n- NIV has been shown to improve survival by approximately 7-11 months and significantly improve quality of life\n- This patient at 68% FVC should already be on NIV if not started at 82%\n\n**Milestone 2 \u2014 FVC <50% predicted**:\n- NIV should be used >16 hours/day at this point for many patients\n- **Cough augmentation**: Mechanical insufflation-exsufflation (CoughAssist) device \u2014 settings typically +40/-40 cmH2O. Critical for secretion clearance and pneumonia prevention.\n- **Lung volume recruitment**: Air stacking with bag-valve-mask to maintain chest wall compliance\n- Discuss **advance directives in detail** \u2014 specifically tracheostomy decision\n- Vaccinations: influenza, pneumococcal, COVID-19\n\n**Milestone 3 \u2014 FVC <30% or NIV failure (CO2 retention, inability to tolerate NIV)**:\n- **Decision point: invasive ventilation via tracheostomy vs comfort-focused care**\n- Tracheostomy ventilation can extend survival by years but requires 24-hour caregiver support and is associated with significant caregiver burden. Only ~5-10% of ALS patients in the US choose tracheostomy.\n- If the patient declines tracheostomy, transition to **hospice and palliative care**\n- Hospice eligibility: FVC <30%, dyspnea at rest, declining to use NIV, or critically impaired nutritional status\n\n**Rapid Progression Concern in This Patient**: An ALSFRS-R decline of 4 points in 3 months is faster than the average rate (approximately 1 point per month). This suggests a more aggressive disease course. Median survival from symptom onset in ALS is 2-5 years, but rapid decliners may have shorter prognosis. The delta FVC (82% to 68% in 3 months = 14% drop) also indicates accelerated respiratory decline.\n\n**Concurrent respiratory management**:\n- **Sleep study**: Evaluate for sleep-disordered breathing and nocturnal hypoventilation (transcutaneous CO2 monitoring is preferred)\n- **Secretion management**: Glycopyrrolate or hyoscyamine for sialorrhea. Nebulized saline and suction for thick secretions.\n- **Dyspnea management**: Low-dose opioids (morphine 2.5-5 mg q4h PRN) are safe and effective for dyspnea palliation in ALS and do not significantly accelerate death when used appropriately\n- **Anxiety management**: Lorazepam 0.5-1 mg for acute air hunger episodes")
B.aRU=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You are establishing this patient in a multidisciplinary ALS clinic. Discuss the comprehensive management plan including the timing of key interventions.")
B.aYQ=new A.c("+ Outline the multidisciplinary ALS clinic approach, including medication management, nutritional support with PEG timing, and adaptive equipment.","**Multidisciplinary ALS Clinic Model**:\n\nMultidisciplinary ALS clinics have been shown to improve survival by 7-12 months and significantly improve quality of life compared to general neurology care. The team includes:\n\n**Core Team Members and Their Roles**:\n1. **Physiatrist/Neurologist**: Disease monitoring (ALSFRS-R every visit), medication management, respiratory surveillance, coordination of care\n2. **Pulmonologist**: FVC, MIP, SNIP monitoring, NIV initiation and titration, cough augmentation\n3. **Speech-Language Pathologist**: Swallowing assessment (FEES or VFSS when indicated), speech augmentation devices, communication strategies\n4. **Dietitian/Nutritionist**: Caloric needs assessment (ALS increases metabolic rate 10-20%), diet texture modification, high-calorie supplements, PEG nutrition planning\n5. **Physical Therapist**: Mobility preservation, fall prevention, stretching/ROM program, gait aids, exercise prescription (moderate intensity, avoiding overwork weakness)\n6. **Occupational Therapist**: Upper extremity adaptive equipment, ADL modifications, home safety evaluation, splinting for wrist drop\n7. **Respiratory Therapist**: NIV fitting and adherence troubleshooting, CoughAssist training, suctioning education\n8. **Social Worker**: Insurance navigation, disability application (SSDI has compassionate allowance fast-track for ALS), financial counseling, caregiver support, advance care planning facilitation\n9. **Psychologist/Psychiatrist**: Depression screening and treatment, grief counseling, caregiver mental health support\n10. **Palliative Care**: Symptom management, advance directive completion, goals of care discussions\n\n**Pharmacologic Management**:\n- **Riluzole 50 mg BID**: The only oral medication proven to extend survival in ALS (by approximately 2-3 months). Mechanism: reduces glutamate excitotoxicity. Monitor LFTs at baseline and every 3 months. Most common side effects: nausea, fatigue, elevated LFTs.\n- **Edaravone (Radicava)**: IV free radical scavenger. May slow functional decline in a subset of patients (early ALS with preserved respiratory function). Administered in 14-day cycles with 14-day breaks. Insurance approval can be challenging. Now available as an oral formulation.\n- **Tofersen**: For patients with SOD1 mutations (approximately 2% of all ALS). Intrathecal antisense oligonucleotide. Genetic testing should be offered to all ALS patients.\n- **Symptomatic medications**: Baclofen or tizanidine for spasticity, glycopyrrolate or botulinum toxin to salivary glands for sialorrhea, dextromethorphan/quinidine (Nuedexta) for pseudobulbar affect, SSRIs or SNRIs for depression\n\n**PEG Tube Timing \u2014 Critical Decision**:\n- **Discuss PEG placement when FVC is still >50%**: The procedure carries higher risk once FVC drops below 50% due to sedation risk and poor respiratory reserve. Many ALS experts recommend prophylactic PEG discussion when there is 10% or more weight loss, meal duration exceeding 30 minutes, frequent choking, or declining oral caloric intake.\n- **This patient has already lost 10 lbs (5.5% body weight)**: PEG discussion should begin now, before respiratory decline makes the procedure higher risk.\n- PEG does not replace oral feeding initially \u2014 it supplements calories. Patients can continue to eat for pleasure as long as swallowing is safe.\n- Radiologically inserted gastrostomy (RIG) or per-oral image-guided gastrostomy (PIG) are alternatives if FVC is too low for standard PEG.\n\n**Adaptive Equipment Progression**:\n- **Current needs**: Right hand \u2014 wrist splint for functional positioning, built-up grip utensils, universal cuff, voice-to-text software for work, ergonomic keyboard modifications\n- **Mobility**: Right AFO for foot drop. Eventual transition to rollator walker, then manual wheelchair, then power wheelchair with tilt/recline for pressure relief and respiratory positioning\n- **Communication**: When speech declines \u2014 speech-generating devices (SGDs). Start with high-tech AAC evaluation early while the patient can still learn the device. Eye-gaze tracking technology for late-stage communication.\n- **Home modifications**: Hospital bed with adjustable head elevation, ceiling lift for transfers, accessible bathroom, ramp for wheelchair access\n\n**Exercise Guidelines in ALS**:\n- Moderate-intensity aerobic exercise and resistance training are safe and may preserve function temporarily\n- Avoid exhaustive or eccentric exercise \u2014 overwork weakness can accelerate motor neuron loss\n- Focus on ROM, stretching, and functional maintenance rather than strengthening weakened muscles\n- Aquatic therapy is excellent for maintaining mobility with reduced joint stress")
B.aQN=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient asks about financial resources and long-term planning. He is still employed but his functional decline is accelerating. His wife works part-time and they have two children in college.")
B.aVw=new A.c("+ Discuss ALS-specific resources, disability and insurance planning, and hospice eligibility criteria.",'**Disability and Insurance Planning**:\n\n**Social Security Disability Insurance (SSDI)**:\n- ALS is on the **Social Security Compassionate Allowances list**, which fast-tracks disability approval. Unlike most conditions that require a 5-month waiting period, **ALS patients receive SSDI benefits immediately upon approval** with no waiting period (per the ALS Disability Insurance Access Act).\n- **Medicare eligibility**: ALS patients become eligible for Medicare **immediately upon SSDI approval** \u2014 the usual 24-month waiting period is waived for ALS. This is critical for covering NIV, wheelchair, PEG supplies, and home health.\n- Application should be initiated NOW while the patient can participate in the process.\n\n**Employment Considerations**:\n- **FMLA**: The patient may qualify for intermittent FMLA leave for clinic visits and symptom management while still able to work\n- **ADA reasonable accommodations**: Voice-to-text software, ergonomic modifications, flexible scheduling, telework options\n- **Short-term disability**: Through employer policy \u2014 bridge to SSDI approval\n- **Long-term disability**: Private LTD policies \u2014 review "own occupation" vs "any occupation" definitions\n\n**Financial Planning**:\n- **ALS Association**: Provides equipment loan programs, support groups, care management, respite grants, and a national helpline (1-800-782-4747)\n- **ALS-specific equipment funding**: Many ALS Association chapters provide communication devices, hospital beds, and wheelchair assistance when insurance denies coverage\n- **Veterans**: If the patient has any military service, ALS is presumptively service-connected by the VA regardless of when or where the veteran served \u2014 this provides full VA benefits\n- **Estate planning**: Encourage the patient to establish power of attorney (financial and healthcare), update his will, and complete advance directives while he has full decision-making capacity. Refer to elder law attorney.\n\n**Hospice Eligibility Criteria for ALS**:\nPatients qualify for hospice when a physician certifies a prognosis of 6 months or less if the disease follows its expected course. Specific ALS criteria include:\n1. **FVC <30% predicted** (critically impaired respiratory function)\n2. **Patient declines further mechanical ventilation** (chooses comfort over tracheostomy)\n3. **Dyspnea at rest** despite optimal NIV\n4. **Critical nutritional impairment**: Declining to receive artificial nutrition (PEG), continued weight loss despite PEG, or inability to maintain hydration\n5. **Rapid progression**: ALSFRS-R declining at a rate suggesting less than 6 months survival\n6. **Life-threatening complications**: Recurrent aspiration pneumonia, sepsis, Stage 3-4 pressure injuries\n\n**Important**: Hospice does NOT mean giving up. Palliative care should run parallel to active ALS management from the time of diagnosis. Hospice is a transition of goals from disease management to comfort maximization. Patients can revoke hospice and return to active treatment at any time.\n\n**Concurrent care model**: Some payers allow concurrent hospice and disease-modifying treatment \u2014 this is ideal for ALS patients who may want to continue riluzole while receiving hospice services.')
B.b1g=new A.c(u.j,'The patient is sitting in front of you with his wife and adult son. He says: "Doctor, just tell me straight \u2014 is this ALS? And if it is, how long do I have?"')
B.aY1=new A.c("+ Demonstrate how you would deliver the ALS diagnosis and address the prognosis question with direct, compassionate communication.","**Breaking Bad News \u2014 SPIKES Framework Applied to ALS Diagnosis**:\n\nThis is one of the most difficult conversations in medicine. The approach must be direct without being brutal, honest without eliminating hope.\n\n**Setting and Preparation**:\n- Ensure a private, quiet room. Sit at eye level. No physical barriers between you. Phone silenced.\n- Confirm who the patient wants present \u2014 he has chosen his wife and son.\n- Assess the patient's understanding: he has already expressed fear of ALS, which indicates he has some preparation.\n\n**Delivering the Diagnosis**:\n\n'Thank you for asking me directly \u2014 I respect that, and I owe you a straightforward answer.\n\nBased on the electrical studies of your nerves and muscles, your examination today, and the pattern of your symptoms over the past six months, the diagnosis is amyotrophic lateral sclerosis \u2014 ALS.'\n\n**Pause. Allow silence. Do not fill the space with more words immediately.**\n\nWatch for the patient's reaction. He may cry, become silent, ask questions, or express anger. His wife and son will also be processing. Allow the emotional response to happen.\n\n**Responding to \"How long do I have?\"**:\n\n'That is a question I take very seriously, and I want to answer it honestly.\n\nThe truth is that ALS affects every person differently. For most people, the disease progresses over 2 to 5 years from when symptoms first start. Some people live significantly longer \u2014 10 years or more. A small percentage have a more rapid course.\n\nWhat I can tell you is that your breathing tests right now are in a reasonable range, your swallowing is intact, and we are catching this at a point where we can put a strong team around you. There are treatments that can help slow the disease, manage symptoms, and maintain your quality of life.\n\nI cannot give you an exact number of months or years \u2014 and honestly, anyone who does is guessing. What I can promise you is that I will be honest with you at every step. I will never withhold information from you. And I will work with you and your family to make sure you have the best possible quality of life for as long as possible.'\n\n**Addressing the family**:\n\nTurn to the wife and son: 'I know this is devastating for all of you. This diagnosis affects the entire family. I want you to know that we will support not just [patient name], but all of you through this.'\n\n**Establishing hope without false reassurance**:\n\n'I want to be clear about something important \u2014 this diagnosis changes your future, but it does not end it. People with ALS continue to work, to travel, to be with their families. The research in ALS is moving faster than at any time in history. There are clinical trials available, and I will make sure you know about anything that might be relevant to you.\n\nWhat matters now is what we do next. I am going to connect you with a team of specialists who work with ALS patients every day \u2014 respiratory therapists, physical therapists, speech therapists, social workers, and others. You are not going to face this alone.'\n\n**Before they leave**:\n- Schedule a follow-up within 1-2 weeks \u2014 the patient will have many questions after the shock has settled\n- Provide written information (ALS Association materials)\n- Ensure the patient has your contact information for questions that arise\n- Offer to call or meet with other family members who were not present\n- Begin advance directive conversation gently: 'At a future visit, when you are ready, I would like to talk about your wishes and preferences so that your care always reflects what matters most to you.'\n\n**Key communication principles**: Warning shot before diagnosis, direct and unambiguous language, allowing silence, honoring the prognosis question with honest ranges rather than specific numbers, grounding hope in concrete next steps, acknowledging the family, and providing a clear plan forward.")
B.aDi=s([B.aOB,B.b38,B.aUr,B.aR8,B.b_M,B.aXz,B.aRU,B.aYQ,B.aQN,B.aVw,B.b1g,B.aY1],t.E)
B.Q7=new A.a5("neuro-als-end-of-life","Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis \u2014 Breaking Bad News & End-of-Life Planning",B.aDi,!1)
B.b2A=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 38-year-old right-handed male high school mathematics teacher presents to the PM&R clinic referred by his PCP for evaluation of involuntary movements and cognitive concerns. He reports 8 months of progressive involuntary jerking movements in his arms and face that worsen with stress, increasing difficulty with lesson planning and grading that used to take him half the time, irritability and impulsive outbursts that have strained his marriage, and two recent falls when turning quickly in his classroom. His father was diagnosed with Huntington disease at age 42 and died at age 55. The patient has never undergone genetic testing. He is accompanied by his wife, who separately tells you he was nearly fired last semester for erratic behavior in the classroom. They have an 18-year-old daughter about to start college and a 15-year-old son. The patient states: "I know what this probably is. My father had the same thing. But I need to hear it from a doctor before I can deal with it."')
B.aOC=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","Key areas to assess:\n- **Movement disorder characterization**: Chorea \u2014 involuntary, non-repetitive, purposeless movements that flow from one body part to another. Distinguish from dystonia, tics, myoclonus, or tremor. Distribution (face, trunk, extremities), severity, impact on function, worsening factors (stress, fatigue, anxiety).\n- **Cognitive symptoms**: Executive dysfunction (planning, organizing, multitasking), processing speed decline, difficulty with mental flexibility. These are often the earliest and most disabling cognitive changes in HD and can precede motor symptoms by years. Assess impact on work performance.\n- **Psychiatric symptoms**: Depression (prevalence 30-50% in HD), irritability, apathy, impulsivity, obsessive-compulsive behaviors, psychosis. Suicidal ideation screening is **critical** \u2014 suicide rate in HD is 4-6 times the general population, with highest risk around the time of diagnosis and in early symptomatic disease.\n- **Behavioral changes**: Disinhibition, perseveration, poor judgment, inappropriate social behavior. Wife's report of erratic classroom behavior and near-termination is significant.\n- **Motor function**: Gait instability, falls, tandem walking difficulty, fine motor impairment (handwriting, typing), dysphagia screening.\n- **Family history details**: Father's age of onset (42), disease course, cause of death. Paternal vs maternal transmission matters \u2014 paternal transmission is associated with **anticipation** (earlier onset in successive generations due to CAG repeat expansion during spermatogenesis).\n- **Functional impact**: UHDRS (Unified Huntington's Disease Rating Scale) \u2014 Total Functional Capacity (TFC) score, independence scale. Current ability to manage finances, drive, work, perform household tasks.\n- **Weight**: HD causes hypermetabolism. Track weight loss, caloric intake. Dysphagia screening.\n- **Children**: The 18-year-old daughter and 15-year-old son each have a 50% chance of inheriting the gene. This will become a central issue.")
B.aMH=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:",'**Vitals**: BP 128/76 mmHg, HR 72 bpm, RR 14, Weight 168 lbs (down from 178 lbs one year ago per PCP records), BMI 23.4.\n\n**General**: Appears younger than stated age. Frequent involuntary movements of the face and upper extremities throughout the examination. Restless, difficulty sitting still. Intermittent grimacing.\n\n**Neurologic Exam**:\n- **Mental Status**: Alert, oriented x4. **Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA): 23/30** \u2014 deficits in trails (executive function), verbal fluency (generated only 8 words beginning with "F" in 60 seconds; normal >12), and delayed recall (2/5 words). No insight impairment currently.\n- **Motor \u2014 Chorea Assessment**: Moderate generalized chorea affecting face (grimacing, eyebrow elevation, lip pursing), bilateral upper extremities (random jerking movements of fingers, wrists, and shoulders), mild trunk involvement, and mild lower extremity involvement. Chorea worsens with conversation (dual-tasking) and when asked about his father.\n- **Tone**: Mildly reduced (hypotonia is typical in chorea-predominant HD; rigidity and bradykinesia emerge in later stages).\n- **Motor Impersistence**: Cannot maintain tongue protrusion for 10 seconds ("milkmaid\'s grip" also present \u2014 inability to maintain constant hand squeeze). This is characteristic of HD.\n- **Eye Movements**: Slowed saccadic initiation. Head thrusting to initiate saccades \u2014 the patient moves his head first, then eyes follow. Impaired smooth pursuit. These oculomotor findings are early and characteristic of HD.\n- **Gait**: Wide-based, irregular cadence. Choreiform movements of trunk and extremities during walking. Difficulty with tandem gait. No festination or freezing. Turns are unsteady.\n- **Reflexes**: 2+ throughout, symmetric. Toes downgoing bilaterally.\n- **Coordination**: Mild dysmetria on finger-to-nose (confounded by chorea). Impaired rapid alternating movements.\n- **Speech**: Mildly dysarthric with irregular rate and rhythm. Hypernasality not present.\n\n**Psychiatric Screen**: PHQ-9 score: 14 (moderate depression). Denies active suicidal ideation but acknowledges passive death wish: "Sometimes I think my family would be better off if I just weren\'t around." **This requires immediate further assessment.**\n\n**UHDRS Total Functional Capacity (TFC)**: 10/13 (Stage II \u2014 reduced capacity at work, reduced financial management, impaired driving).')
B.aSD=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The attending asks you to discuss the genetic testing approach and staging of Huntington disease in this patient.")
B.aN6=new A.c("+ Explain the genetic testing for HD including CAG repeat interpretation, and describe the HD staging system. What cognitive battery would you use?","**Genetic Testing for Huntington Disease**:\n\n**The HD Gene (HTT gene, chromosome 4p16.3)**: HD is caused by a CAG trinucleotide repeat expansion in the huntingtin gene. The test is a simple blood draw that measures the number of CAG repeats.\n\n**CAG Repeat Interpretation**:\n- **<27 repeats**: Normal. Will not develop HD. Cannot pass to children.\n- **27-35 repeats (Intermediate)**: Will NOT develop HD, but the repeat can expand during transmission to offspring (especially paternal). Genetic counseling recommended.\n- **36-39 repeats (Reduced Penetrance)**: May or may not develop HD. Incomplete penetrance. Variable onset and severity. Children at risk.\n- **\u226540 repeats (Full Penetrance)**: **Will develop HD** if they live long enough. 100% penetrance. Each child has 50% chance of inheriting the expanded allele.\n\n**Inverse Correlation**: Higher CAG repeat number correlates with earlier age of onset. Juvenile HD (onset <20 years) is associated with very high repeat numbers (typically >60) and usually transmitted paternally due to anticipation.\n\n**Anticipation**: The CAG repeat can expand during spermatogenesis, so paternal transmission may result in longer repeats and earlier onset in successive generations. This patient's father had onset at 42 \u2014 this patient's onset at 38 is consistent with anticipation.\n\n**Pre-test Genetic Counseling Requirements**:\nGenetic testing for HD should NEVER be ordered casually. International guidelines (HDSA) require:\n1. Pre-test genetic counseling by a certified genetic counselor\n2. Assessment of the patient's psychological readiness for the result\n3. Discussion of implications for employment, insurance, family members\n4. Suicide risk assessment (risk is highest around time of testing/diagnosis)\n5. A companion who can provide support\n6. A waiting period (typically 1 month between counseling and blood draw)\n7. Results delivered in person, never by phone or mail\n\n**For this patient**: Given his clinical presentation (chorea, cognitive decline, psychiatric symptoms, family history, age of onset), the diagnosis is clinically near-certain. However, genetic confirmation is still important for definitive diagnosis, family planning implications, and clinical trial eligibility.\n\n**Huntington Disease Staging (Shoulson-Fahn Staging based on TFC)**:\n- **Stage I (TFC 11-13)**: Fully functional at work and home. Able to manage finances and domestic responsibilities.\n- **Stage II (TFC 7-10)**: Functional at work but at reduced capacity. Can still handle domestic tasks but with difficulty. **This patient is Stage II (TFC 10).**\n- **Stage III (TFC 3-6)**: Unable to work. Requires assistance with finances. Can perform basic ADLs with some help.\n- **Stage IV (TFC 1-2)**: Dependent for most ADLs. Requires nursing-level care. Severe chorea or rigidity.\n- **Stage V (TFC 0)**: Total dependence. Bed-bound. Requires full nursing care.\n\n**Cognitive Assessment Battery for HD**:\n- **HD-CAB (Huntington Disease Cognitive Assessment Battery)**: Standardized battery specifically validated for HD. Includes Symbol Digit Modalities Test, Stroop Interference Test, Trail Making Test B, Paced Tapping, One Touch Stockings of Cambridge (planning), and Emotion Recognition.\n- **MoCA**: Useful for screening but not specific to HD. Executive function subtests are most relevant.\n- **Verbal Fluency Tests**: Category fluency and phonemic fluency \u2014 highly sensitive to HD-related cognitive decline.\n- **Neuropsychological testing**: Comprehensive evaluation to establish baseline and track progression. Essential for work capacity determination.")
B.b2R=new A.c("+ The patient's PHQ-9 is 14 and he endorses a passive death wish. How do you manage psychiatric symptoms in HD?",'**Psychiatric Emergency Management and HD Psychiatric Care**:\n\n**Immediate Suicide Risk Assessment for This Patient**:\nThe passive death wish statement ("my family would be better off without me") requires urgent evaluation:\n1. **Columbia Suicide Severity Rating Scale (C-SSRS)**: Administer immediately. Distinguish passive ideation from active ideation, plan, and intent.\n2. **Risk factors specific to HD**: Period around diagnosis is highest risk. Family history of HD-related suicide. Loss of functional roles (work impairment). Depression. Impulsivity (HD-related disinhibition lowers the threshold for acting on suicidal thoughts).\n3. **Means restriction**: Ask about firearm access, medications in the home. If firearms are present, counsel on safe storage or temporary removal.\n4. **Safety plan**: Develop a written safety plan with the patient identifying warning signs, coping strategies, people to contact, and crisis hotline numbers (988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline).\n5. **If active suicidal ideation with plan or intent**: Psychiatric emergency evaluation. Do not discharge from clinic.\n\n**This patient**: He has moderate depression, passive death wish without active ideation, and is in a high-risk period (peri-diagnostic). He needs same-week psychiatry referral, safety planning, and close follow-up. He should NOT be left alone until his wife confirms she understands the risk.\n\n**Psychiatric Management in HD**:\n\n**Depression (30-50% prevalence in HD)**:\n- **First-line**: SSRIs \u2014 citalopram or sertraline are preferred. These also help with irritability and obsessive symptoms.\n- **Second-line**: Venlafaxine or mirtazapine (mirtazapine has the advantage of appetite stimulation and sedation for insomnia)\n- **Avoid**: Bupropion \u2014 may lower seizure threshold and worsen chorea. TCAs \u2014 anticholinergic burden worsens cognition.\n- **Psychotherapy**: CBT adapted for cognitive impairment. Supportive therapy. Caregiver therapy.\n\n**Irritability and Aggression (most common behavioral complaint in HD)**:\n- **First-line**: SSRIs (citalopram, sertraline)\n- **Second-line**: Low-dose atypical antipsychotics (olanzapine, quetiapine) \u2014 these also suppress chorea. Valproic acid for mood stabilization.\n- **Behavioral strategies**: Structured routines, reduced environmental stimulation, caregiver education about triggers.\n\n**Apathy (distinct from depression \u2014 most disabling psychiatric symptom in later stages)**:\n- No well-established pharmacologic treatment. SSRIs may worsen apathy. Structured activity schedules and caregiver-led engagement are the primary strategies.\n- Differentiate apathy from depression \u2014 they have different mechanisms and treatments.\n\n**Psychosis (rare but occurs in 3-11% of HD)**:\n- Atypical antipsychotics: olanzapine or quetiapine preferred. Risperidone can worsen motor symptoms.\n- Avoid typical antipsychotics (haloperidol) \u2014 they suppress chorea but cause severe rigidity and swallowing impairment.\n\n**Obsessive-Compulsive Behaviors (common in HD)**:\n- SSRIs are first-line. Higher doses may be needed than for depression.\n\n**Sleep Disturbance**:\n- Melatonin for circadian rhythm disruption. Trazodone or mirtazapine for insomnia.\n- Avoid benzodiazepines \u2014 they worsen cognitive impairment and increase fall risk.')
B.aV5=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","The genetic test confirms 44 CAG repeats. Discuss your comprehensive rehabilitation and medical management plan.")
B.aUK=new A.c("+ Outline pharmacologic management of chorea, fall prevention, and the rehabilitation program for this patient.","**Chorea Management**:\n\n**Tetrabenazine (Xenazine)** \u2014 First-line pharmacologic treatment for HD chorea:\n- Mechanism: Vesicular monoamine transporter 2 (VMAT2) inhibitor \u2014 depletes presynaptic dopamine\n- Starting dose: 12.5 mg daily, titrate by 12.5 mg every week. Maximum 100 mg/day (in divided doses TID).\n- **CYP2D6 testing is required before exceeding 50 mg/day** \u2014 poor metabolizers have higher drug levels and increased side effect risk\n- Side effects: Depression (BLACK BOX WARNING \u2014 contraindicated in actively suicidal patients), sedation, parkinsonism, akathisia, dysphagia\n- **For this patient**: His depression and passive suicidal ideation are a relative contraindication. Must stabilize psychiatric symptoms before initiating or use an alternative.\n\n**Deutetrabenazine (Austedo)** \u2014 Preferred alternative:\n- Deuterated form of tetrabenazine with longer half-life, more stable plasma levels, less frequent dosing (BID vs TID), and **fewer psychiatric side effects**\n- Starting dose: 6 mg daily, titrate by 6 mg weekly to a maximum of 48 mg/day (in divided doses BID)\n- Still carries a depression/suicidality warning but appears to have lower risk than tetrabenazine\n- **Better choice for this patient given his psychiatric comorbidities**\n\n**Valbenazine (Ingrezza)**: FDA-approved for tardive dyskinesia, not HD chorea, but uses the same VMAT2 mechanism. Used off-label.\n\n**Atypical Antipsychotics**: Olanzapine or risperidone suppress chorea and may be useful if psychosis or severe aggression coexists.\n\n**Important**: Not all chorea requires treatment. If chorea is mild and not functionally limiting or dangerous, observation is appropriate. Suppressing chorea with VMAT2 inhibitors can unmask underlying bradykinesia and worsen gait.\n\n**Fall Prevention Program**:\n- **PT evaluation**: Balance assessment (Berg Balance Scale, TUG), gait analysis, identify chorea-related and cognitive-related fall risk\n- **Home safety**: Remove tripping hazards, install grab bars, adequate lighting, consider handrails on both sides of stairs\n- **Footwear**: Supportive, flat, non-slip shoes. Avoid loose slippers.\n- **Gait training**: Wide-based gait strategies, safe turning techniques, dual-task avoidance during mobility\n- **Assistive devices**: May be needed as disease progresses \u2014 weighted walker can help counteract chorea-induced instability. Wheelchair assessment for community mobility when falls become frequent.\n- **Hip protectors**: For patients with recurrent falls\n- **Driving assessment**: Given cognitive decline and chorea, formal driving evaluation by a CDRS is essential. His TFC of 10 suggests driving may already be unsafe.\n\n**Comprehensive Rehabilitation Program**:\n- **Physical therapy**: Aerobic exercise (shown to improve motor function in HD), balance training, gait training with cueing, flexibility and stretching\n- **Occupational therapy**: Workplace accommodations assessment, adaptive strategies for fine motor tasks, home modification, energy conservation\n- **Speech therapy**: Dysarthria management, swallowing assessment and monitoring (dysphagia is a leading cause of death in HD from aspiration), communication strategies as disease progresses\n- **Cognitive rehabilitation**: Compensatory strategies (external memory aids, structured schedules, simplified routines), vocational rehabilitation to maximize work tenure\n- **Nutritional counseling**: High-calorie diet (HD increases metabolic demands significantly). Monitor weight at every visit \u2014 weight loss correlates with disease progression. May require 3000-4000 calories/day to maintain weight.")
B.aWK=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient asks about his ability to continue working and his insurance. He is worried about being fired and losing coverage for his family.")
B.b2J=new A.c("+ Discuss employment protections (GINA, ADA), insurance implications of genetic testing, and genetic counseling referral for at-risk family members.","**Employment and Insurance Protections**:\n\n**Genetic Information Nondiscrimination Act (GINA, 2008)**:\n- **Title I**: Prohibits health insurance companies from using genetic information (including genetic test results, family history of genetic disease) to make eligibility, coverage, or premium decisions\n- **Title II**: Prohibits employers with 15+ employees from using genetic information in hiring, firing, job assignment, or promotion decisions\n- **GINA Limitations**: GINA does NOT protect against discrimination in **life insurance, disability insurance, or long-term care insurance**. This is critical \u2014 if this patient has not yet purchased these policies, he should be advised to do so BEFORE genetic testing is on his medical record.\n- **For this patient**: His employer cannot fire him based on his HD genetic status. However, if his job performance has deteriorated to the point of legitimate performance concerns (as suggested by the near-firing), the employer can act on documented performance issues.\n\n**Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA)**:\n- HD qualifies as a disability under the ADA once it substantially limits major life activities\n- The employer must provide **reasonable accommodations**: reduced workload, modified schedule, additional time for grading, quiet workspace, assistance with classroom management\n- The employer can require a fitness-for-duty evaluation if there are legitimate safety or performance concerns\n- Teaching may become untenable as cognitive decline progresses \u2014 but accommodations must be attempted first\n\n**Insurance Implications**:\n- **Health insurance**: Protected by GINA and the ACA (Affordable Care Act) \u2014 cannot be denied or charged more based on pre-existing conditions including HD\n- **Life insurance**: NOT protected by GINA. If the patient does not have life insurance, genetic test results could lead to denial. **Advise the patient to obtain life insurance before genetic test results are documented.**\n- **Long-term care insurance**: NOT protected by GINA. Same advice \u2014 purchase before testing.\n- **Disability insurance**: NOT protected by GINA. Obtain before testing.\n- **COBRA**: If the patient loses employment, COBRA provides 18 months of continued health insurance coverage\n- **SSDI/Medicare**: As HD progresses, the patient will qualify for SSDI. HD is on the Compassionate Allowances list (similar to ALS) for expedited processing.\n\n**Genetic Counseling for At-Risk Family Members**:\n- The patient's **18-year-old daughter and 15-year-old son** each have a **50% chance** of having inherited the HD gene mutation\n- **The patient should be the one to disclose his diagnosis to his children**, with guidance from a genetic counselor on how to have this conversation\n- Predictive genetic testing in **minors is generally not recommended** by the HDSA and the American Society of Human Genetics unless there is a clinical indication (juvenile HD symptoms). The 15-year-old should NOT be tested.\n- The 18-year-old can pursue predictive testing if she chooses \u2014 but only through a formal genetic counseling protocol with psychological assessment and support (see Domain E for the dilemma this creates).\n- **Referral to HD Society of America (HDSA) Center of Excellence** for comprehensive genetic counseling, family support, and clinical trial access\n\n**HDSA Resources**:\n- HDSA Centers of Excellence \u2014 multidisciplinary HD clinics (similar to ALS clinics)\n- HDSA Social Worker \u2014 assist with disability applications, insurance navigation, caregiver support\n- HDSA Youth Alliance \u2014 support program specifically for young people affected by HD in their families")
B.b3E=new A.c(u.j,"After the appointment, the patient's 18-year-old daughter calls the clinic and asks to speak with you privately. She says: \"My dad told me about his diagnosis. I'm about to go to college and I need to know if I'm going to get this disease. I want to get tested but I don't want my parents to know. Can you order the test for me?\"")
B.aU4=new A.c("+ How do you respond to the daughter's request for predictive genetic testing, addressing ethical boundaries, genetic counseling requirements, and the emotional dimensions of this situation?","**Navigating the Genetic Testing Request \u2014 Ethical and Communication Framework**:\n\nThis scenario tests multiple critical competencies: genetic counseling boundaries, patient autonomy vs beneficence, appropriate referral, and compassionate communication with a distressed young person.\n\n**My Response**:\n\n'Thank you for calling me, and I can only imagine how frightening this is for you. Learning about your father's diagnosis is an enormous thing to process, and I think it shows real strength that you are thinking about this proactively.\n\nI want to be completely honest with you about what I can and cannot do.\n\n**What I cannot do**: I cannot order the genetic test for you myself. This is not because I don't think you deserve to know \u2014 it is because predictive testing for Huntington disease is a life-changing decision with profound implications, and there is a well-established process designed to protect people like you.'\n\n**Explaining the Predictive Testing Protocol**:\n\n'Here is why the process matters: People who test positive for the HD gene learn that they will develop a serious, progressive illness at some point in their life. Research shows that how someone receives this information \u2014 the preparation, the support, the counseling beforehand \u2014 makes a major difference in how they cope with the result.\n\nThe international guidelines for HD predictive testing require:\n1. Meeting with a certified genetic counselor who specializes in HD\n2. A psychological readiness assessment\n3. Discussion of what a positive or negative result would mean for your life, your relationships, your career plans, your future decisions about having children\n4. A companion or support person identified before the test\n5. A waiting period to ensure the decision is not impulsive\n6. Results delivered in person with immediate support available\n\nThis process exists because decades of experience have taught us that people who go through genetic counseling before testing cope significantly better with the results \u2014 regardless of whether the result is positive or negative.'\n\n**Addressing the Secrecy Request**:\n\n'Regarding not telling your parents \u2014 I understand why you might want to keep this private. You are 18, and you have the legal right to make your own medical decisions.\n\nHowever, I want to encourage you to think carefully about going through this alone. Your parents are facing their own grief right now, but your father may actually want to be part of supporting you. And your mother could be an important source of strength.\n\nThat said, this is ultimately your decision. A genetic counselor can help you think through whether and how to involve your family. They will respect your confidentiality.'\n\n**Addressing the Urgency**:\n\n'I hear that you feel you need to know before starting college. I understand the desire for certainty. But I want you to know that there is no medical urgency here. If you carry the gene, it would likely be decades before symptoms would appear, given that your father's symptoms started at 38.\n\nSome people in your situation choose to get tested because not knowing feels unbearable. Others choose not to be tested \u2014 they prefer to live without that knowledge. Both choices are valid. A genetic counselor can help you explore which path is right for you, right now.'\n\n**Concrete Next Steps**:\n\n'What I can do for you today is refer you to a genetic counselor at the HDSA Center of Excellence nearest to your college. They will see you confidentially. They will not contact your parents unless you give permission. And they will walk you through this process at your pace.\n\nI am also going to give you the HDSA helpline number and their youth program \u2014 there are other young adults who have been exactly where you are now, and many of them have found it incredibly helpful to connect with peers.\n\nCan I set that referral up for you?'\n\n**Key Ethical Principles Demonstrated**:\n- **Autonomy**: Respecting her legal right to make medical decisions at 18\n- **Beneficence**: Ensuring she has proper support and counseling before testing\n- **Non-maleficence**: Protecting her from potential harm of receiving devastating results without adequate psychological preparation\n- **Scope of practice**: Recognizing that ordering predictive genetic tests without genetic counseling infrastructure is below the standard of care\n- **Appropriate referral**: Connecting her with the right specialist rather than exceeding your expertise\n- **Emotional attunement**: Validating her fear while providing structure and hope")
B.aG2=s([B.b2A,B.aOC,B.aMH,B.aSD,B.aN6,B.b2R,B.aV5,B.aUK,B.aWK,B.b2J,B.b3E,B.aU4],t.E)
B.Qc=new A.a5("neuro-huntingtons-genetic-counseling","Huntington Disease \u2014 Genetic Counseling Dilemma",B.aG2,!1)
B.aRu=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 50-year-old right-handed female former pediatric nurse presents to the PM&R clinic for mobility reassessment. She was diagnosed with relapsing-remitting MS at age 32 and transitioned to secondary progressive MS (SPMS) at age 44. Her current EDSS is 7.0 \u2014 she is essentially restricted to a wheelchair but can transfer and propel a manual wheelchair short distances. Over the past 6 months, she has become unable to walk even with a rolling walker, has fallen 6 times (sustaining a rib fracture from one fall), and is now using a borrowed manual wheelchair that is poorly fitted. She reports increasing upper extremity fatigue with manual wheelchair propulsion, worsening spasticity in both legs, neurogenic bladder requiring intermittent catheterization, and overwhelming fatigue that limits her to 3-4 productive hours per day. She was forced to leave her nursing career 3 years ago. She is divorced, lives alone in a two-story townhouse with the only bathroom upstairs, and her 22-year-old daughter lives across town. She enters the clinic in tears and says: "I used to take care of other people. Now I can\'t even take care of myself."')
B.aWJ=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","Key areas to assess:\n- **MS disease course**: Age of diagnosis, number of relapses during RRMS phase, when progressive decline began (transition to SPMS), current disease-modifying therapy status. Has she been evaluated for any active inflammation (gadolinium-enhancing lesions) that might indicate SPMS with activity?\n- **Mobility decline timeline**: When did she transition from cane to walker to wheelchair? Rate of decline (gradual vs stepwise). Is there any remaining ambulation capacity (household ambulation, therapeutic standing)?\n- **Upper extremity function**: Grip strength, fine motor coordination, sensation. This is critical because upper extremity preservation determines independence with self-care, wheelchair propulsion, and catheterization. Any recent changes in hand function?\n- **Spasticity assessment**: Distribution (adductors, hamstrings, ankle plantarflexors), severity (Modified Ashworth Scale), functional impact (does spasticity interfere with transfers, catheterization, positioning?), current antispasticity medications, painful spasms (especially nocturnal).\n- **Bladder management**: Type of neurogenic bladder (urodynamics results), catheterization schedule, UTI frequency, fluid management, continence status. Has she seen urology recently?\n- **Bowel function**: Neurogenic bowel program, constipation management, bowel accidents, time required for bowel care.\n- **Fatigue**: Modified Fatigue Impact Scale (MFIS). Timing of fatigue (worse in afternoon, with heat). Current fatigue management strategies. Sleep quality \u2014 does nocturia disrupt sleep?\n- **Pain**: Neuropathic pain (burning, electric sensations), musculoskeletal pain from wheelchair use (shoulders, neck), spasticity-related pain.\n- **Cognitive function**: Processing speed, memory, executive function \u2014 cognitive impairment affects 40-70% of MS patients. Impact on medication management, appointment scheduling, self-care routines.\n- **Mood**: Depression screen (PHQ-9). Social isolation \u2014 she lives alone, lost her career, lost her marriage. This is a high-risk psychosocial profile.\n- **Skin integrity**: Pressure injury risk assessment \u2014 she is now primarily wheelchair-seated. Current cushion type and condition.\n- **Home environment**: Two-story townhouse with only bathroom upstairs \u2014 this is an immediate barrier to safety and independence.")
B.aOQ=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 122/74 mmHg, HR 68 bpm, RR 14, Temp 36.8C, Weight 156 lbs, BMI 27.2.\n\n**General**: Appears fatigued. Seated in a standard hospital-loaned manual wheelchair that is too wide, with a flat foam cushion. Posture shows mild thoracic kyphosis and left lateral lean. No pressure injuries visible.\n\n**Neurologic Exam**:\n- **Mental Status**: Alert, oriented x4. Affect is tearful but cooperative. Cognition grossly intact at bedside \u2014 formal neuropsychological testing recommended. Processing speed appears mildly reduced.\n- **Cranial Nerves**: Internuclear ophthalmoplegia (INO) on left lateral gaze (chronic, previously documented). Visual acuity 20/30 OS (history of optic neuritis). Remaining cranial nerves intact.\n- **Motor \u2014 Upper Extremities**: Deltoids 4+/5 bilaterally, biceps 5/5, triceps 4+/5, wrist extensors 4+/5, grip strength 22 kg right, 19 kg left. Fine motor: slightly impaired rapid finger movements. Intact ability to perform intermittent catheterization independently.\n- **Motor \u2014 Lower Extremities**: Hip flexors 2/5 bilaterally, knee extensors 2/5 right, 3-/5 left, ankle dorsiflexion 1/5 bilaterally, plantarflexion 2/5 bilaterally. Unable to perform straight leg raise against gravity on the right.\n- **Tone**: Modified Ashworth Scale \u2014 bilateral hip adductors 2, bilateral hamstrings 2, bilateral ankle plantarflexors 3. Bilateral ankle clonus (sustained, >10 beats). Spasticity interferes with passive ROM at the ankles and complicates transfer positioning.\n- **Sensory**: Reduced proprioception at bilateral ankles and toes. Reduced light touch and temperature sensation below the knees bilaterally. Intact sensation in upper extremities and trunk.\n- **Reflexes**: 3+ in bilateral lower extremities with bilateral Babinski. 2+ in upper extremities.\n- **Coordination**: Mild intention tremor bilateral hands. Impaired heel-to-shin (confounded by weakness).\n\n**Functional Assessment**:\n- **Transfers**: Moderate assist x1 for stand-pivot transfer. Can perform lateral sliding board transfer with supervision and setup.\n- **Wheelchair Mobility**: Propels current manual wheelchair approximately 100 feet before upper extremity fatigue. Unable to propel up any incline. Cannot propel over outdoor surfaces (gravel, grass, broken sidewalk).\n- **ADLs**: Independent with upper body dressing. Requires assistance with lower body dressing due to spasticity and weakness. Independent with self-catheterization but reports increasing difficulty with positioning.\n- **EDSS**: 7.0 (restricted to wheelchair, able to transfer, can self-propel manual wheelchair limited distance).\n\n**Skin**: No pressure injuries. Mild erythema noted over bilateral ischial tuberosities \u2014 concerning for Stage 1 pressure injury or pre-injury. Current cushion is completely inadequate.")
B.aXb=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The attending asks you to differentiate SPMS from PPMS and discuss how you determine when a patient should transition from ambulatory aids to a wheelchair.")
B.aWE=new A.c("+ Differentiate SPMS from PPMS, and explain the clinical reasoning for determining when wheelchair transition is appropriate.","**SPMS vs PPMS \u2014 Key Distinctions**:\n\n**Secondary Progressive MS (SPMS)**:\n- Begins as relapsing-remitting MS (RRMS) with clearly defined relapses and remissions\n- Transitions to progressive accumulation of disability independent of relapses\n- Transition is often gradual and recognized retrospectively \u2014 there is no definitive biomarker for the RRMS-to-SPMS transition\n- Can be classified as **SPMS with activity** (continued relapses or new MRI lesions superimposed on progressive decline) or **SPMS without activity**\n- This patient's course: RRMS diagnosed at 32, progressive decline without relapses beginning at 44 = SPMS\n- **Treatment**: Siponimod (Mayzent) is FDA-approved specifically for SPMS with active disease. Ocrelizumab may be considered. If no inflammatory activity, disease-modifying therapies have limited benefit and focus shifts to symptomatic management and rehabilitation.\n\n**Primary Progressive MS (PPMS)**:\n- Progressive accumulation of disability from onset \u2014 no initial relapsing phase\n- Approximately 10-15% of all MS patients\n- Tends to present later (average onset age 40s), more common equal male-to-female ratio (unlike RRMS which is 3:1 female)\n- Spinal cord involvement predominates (progressive myelopathy) with less brain lesion burden on MRI\n- **Treatment**: Ocrelizumab (Ocrevus) is the only FDA-approved disease-modifying therapy for PPMS\n\n**Clinical Determination of Wheelchair Transition**:\n\nThis is not a single decision point \u2014 it is a **spectrum of mobility aid progression** that should be assessed regularly:\n\n**Indicators that wheelchair evaluation is needed**:\n1. **Falls**: This patient has fallen 6 times in 6 months with a fracture \u2014 the risk-benefit ratio of continued ambulation has shifted toward danger. Recurrent falls are the clearest indicator.\n2. **Energy cost of ambulation**: When walking requires so much energy that the patient has no reserve for meaningful activities (work, social engagement, self-care), walking becomes an exercise rather than functional mobility.\n3. **Functional ambulation**: Can the patient ambulate safely within the home, in the community, and in time-critical situations (getting to the bathroom)? If not, ambulation is no longer functional.\n4. **EDSS 6.5-7.0**: This range typically marks the transition. EDSS 6.5 = constant bilateral assistance required. EDSS 7.0 = essentially wheelchair-dependent.\n5. **Upper extremity decline**: If UE function is declining, early power wheelchair provision preserves shoulder integrity for self-care and transfers rather than exhausting it on wheelchair propulsion.\n\n**Critical Reframing**: The wheelchair is not a sign of giving up \u2014 it is a **tool for expanded independence and safety**. Patients who resist wheelchair transition often experience accelerating decline from falls, fractures, deconditioning, and social isolation because they stop going places they cannot walk to.\n\n**For this patient**: She has clearly crossed the threshold. She cannot safely ambulate, she has sustained injuries from falls, and she is using a poorly fitted manual wheelchair that is causing fatigue and skin concerns. She needs a comprehensive wheelchair evaluation including power wheelchair assessment.")
B.aOP=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Develop a comprehensive management plan addressing her wheelchair needs, spasticity, fatigue, and home modification requirements.")
B.aV3=new A.c("+ Outline the power wheelchair evaluation, home modification plan, upper body preservation program, and fatigue management strategy.","**Power Wheelchair Evaluation and Prescription**:\n\n**Seating and Mobility Evaluation by ATP (Assistive Technology Professional)**:\n- Must be performed by a certified ATP in conjunction with a physiatrist or PT\n- **Power wheelchair with tilt-in-space and recline**: Essential for pressure redistribution, spasticity management (positioning), fatigue management, and postural support\n- **Seat width**: Custom-fit to patient \u2014 current wheelchair is too wide, causing lateral lean and poor posture\n- **Seat cushion**: Pressure-mapping assessment to select appropriate cushion. ROHO or Jay-series cushion with adequate pressure redistribution for ischial protection\n- **Back support**: Custom-molded or off-the-shelf with lateral trunk supports to correct lateral lean and thoracic kyphosis\n- **Footrests**: Angle-adjustable to accommodate ankle plantarflexor spasticity\n- **Headrest**: If fatigue causes head drop or for tilt/recline positioning\n- **Joystick control**: Standard hand joystick is appropriate given her current UE function. Contingency plan for alternative drive controls (head array, sip-and-puff) if UE function declines.\n- **Additional features**: Elevating legrests for edema management, seat elevator for counter-height access, USB charger port\n\n**Manual Wheelchair**: Retain a lightweight manual wheelchair for situations where power wheelchair cannot be used (small spaces, transport, backup). Ultralight rigid frame with ergonomic pushrim for short-distance propulsion.\n\n**Home Modification Plan \u2014 Immediate Priorities**:\n1. **Bathroom access crisis**: She lives in a two-story townhouse with the only bathroom upstairs \u2014 this is unsafe and unsustainable. Options:\n   - **First floor bathroom addition** (if structurally feasible)\n   - **Stairlift installation** (temporary solution, but does not work with wheelchair)\n   - **Relocation to an accessible single-story unit** \u2014 may be the most practical long-term solution\n2. **Roll-in shower**: With shower wheelchair or tub bench with handheld showerhead\n3. **Grab bars**: Throughout bathroom, at bed, at exterior doors\n4. **Door widening**: Minimum 32 inches for wheelchair clearance (36 inches preferred)\n5. **Ramp**: For exterior entry\n6. **Kitchen modifications**: Lowered countertop section, accessible storage, side-opening oven\n7. **Hospital bed or adjustable bed**: For positioning, transfers, and skin protection\n\n**Upper Body Preservation Program \u2014 Critical Priority**:\nHer upper extremities are her lifeline for independence. Shoulder injury from manual wheelchair overuse is preventable.\n- **Rotator cuff strengthening**: External rotation, scapular stabilization exercises, resistance bands\n- **Ergonomic propulsion training**: If using manual wheelchair, long smooth strokes (semicircular pattern) rather than short choppy strokes to reduce repetitive stress\n- **Stretching**: Pectoralis, anterior shoulder, neck \u2014 prevent forward-rolled shoulder posture from wheelchair seating\n- **Minimize manual wheelchair propulsion**: The power wheelchair prescription will be the most important intervention for shoulder preservation\n- **Weight management**: Every pound impacts wheelchair propulsion difficulty and transfer effort\n\n**Fatigue Management \u2014 Multimodal Approach**:\n1. **Energy conservation training**: OT-led program teaching pacing, prioritization, and planning. Break activities into segments with rest periods. Schedule demanding tasks during peak energy hours (usually morning for MS).\n2. **Cooling strategies**: MS fatigue worsens dramatically with heat (Uhthoff phenomenon). Cooling vest, air-conditioned environment, cool beverages.\n3. **Sleep optimization**: Address nocturia (timed fluid restriction, bedside catheterization), treat restless legs if present, assess for sleep apnea.\n4. **Pharmacologic**: Amantadine 100 mg BID (first-line for MS fatigue). Modafinil 200 mg each morning (second-line). Avoid stimulants that disrupt sleep.\n5. **Exercise**: Paradoxically, structured low-to-moderate intensity exercise (aquatic therapy is ideal) improves MS fatigue over time.\n6. **Depression treatment**: Fatigue and depression overlap significantly \u2014 treating depression often improves fatigue.\n\n**Spasticity Management**:\n- **Current regimen review**: Optimize oral medications \u2014 baclofen (titrate to 20 mg TID if tolerated, monitor sedation), add tizanidine 4 mg at bedtime for nocturnal spasms\n- **Stretching program**: Daily passive ROM, especially hip adductors, hamstrings, ankle plantarflexors. Caregiver or self-directed with straps.\n- **Standing program**: If a standing frame is tolerable, 30-60 minutes daily reduces spasticity and maintains bone density\n- **Botulinum toxin**: For focal spasticity interfering with function (ankle clonus impeding footrest positioning, adductor spasticity impeding catheterization)\n- **Intrathecal baclofen pump**: Consider if oral medications are insufficient or side effects are limiting. ITB is highly effective for lower extremity spasticity in MS and can dramatically improve comfort, positioning, and catheterization access.")
B.aXp=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE",'The patient\'s insurance has denied the power wheelchair request, citing "insufficient documentation of medical necessity." Discuss the funding pathways and appeals process.')
B.b0G=new A.c("+ Explain the wheelchair funding process, insurance appeals strategy, and home accessibility resources.",'**Power Wheelchair Insurance Funding \u2014 Medicare and Private Insurance**:\n\n**Medicare Coverage Criteria for Power Mobility Devices (PMDs)**:\nMedicare requires a comprehensive, multi-step process:\n1. **Face-to-face examination**: Must be performed by the treating physician (or NP/PA) within 45 days before the wheelchair prescription. Must document that the patient\'s condition is expected to last at least 3 years and that a power wheelchair is needed for use in the home.\n2. **Seven-element order**: Written prescription with specific elements including diagnosis, wheelchair type, accessories, and reason the patient cannot use a manual wheelchair (or why a manual wheelchair would be insufficient).\n3. **Specialty evaluation**: Performed by PT/OT who is a certified ATP (Assistive Technology Professional). This seating and mobility evaluation generates a detailed report documenting functional limitations and wheelchair specifications.\n4. **Medical necessity documentation**: Must demonstrate that the patient (a) has a mobility limitation that significantly impairs participation in mobility-related ADLs in the home, (b) the limitation cannot be resolved by a cane, walker, or manual wheelchair, and (c) the patient has the cognitive and physical ability to safely operate a power wheelchair.\n\n**Common Reasons for Denial and How to Address Them**:\n- **"Insufficient documentation"**: This is the most common denial. The physician letter must specifically state how the patient\'s condition prevents safe use of a manual wheelchair (upper extremity fatigue, fall risk, shoulder preservation) and how a power wheelchair will improve function IN THE HOME (Medicare focuses on home use, not community mobility).\n- **"Patient can use a manual wheelchair"**: Document that she can only propel 100 feet before fatigue, cannot manage inclines or rough surfaces, and continued manual propulsion risks shoulder injury that would render her unable to transfer or self-catheterize.\n- **"Scooter would be sufficient"**: Document that a scooter lacks tilt/recline for pressure management, does not provide adequate postural support for her trunk weakness and lateral lean, and does not accommodate her spasticity management needs.\n\n**Appeals Process**:\n1. **First-level appeal (Redetermination)**: Submit within 120 days. Include additional documentation \u2014 a detailed letter from the physiatrist, the ATP evaluation, photographs of current ill-fitting wheelchair, documentation of falls and injuries.\n2. **Second-level appeal (Reconsideration by QIC)**: If redetermination is denied. Include peer-reviewed evidence on power wheelchair outcomes in progressive MS.\n3. **Third-level appeal (ALJ Hearing)**: Administrative Law Judge hearing. The patient and physician can testify. Success rates at ALJ hearings are significantly higher than lower-level appeals.\n4. **Congressional inquiry**: Contact the patient\'s congressional representative \u2014 constituent services offices can advocate with CMS.\n\n**Home Accessibility Funding Resources**:\n- **State Assistive Technology Programs**: Every state has a federally funded AT program that provides equipment loans, financial assistance, and information\n- **National MS Society**: Home modification grants, equipment assistance, financial assistance for medical costs\n- **VA Specially Adapted Housing (SAH) grants**: If the patient has any military service history\n- **USDA Rural Development grants**: Section 504 Home Repair Program for rural homeowners\n- **State vocational rehabilitation**: May fund home modifications as part of independent living plan\n- **Habitat for Humanity**: Some chapters offer home modification programs for people with disabilities\n- **Community Development Block Grants**: Through local government \u2014 some municipalities fund accessibility modifications\n- **Tax deductions**: Medical expense deductions for home modifications that are medically necessary (IRS Publication 502)\n\n**Transition Planning**:\n- The patient may need to relocate to an accessible unit. Social work referral to assist with accessible housing search, Section 8 housing vouchers if income-eligible, and local accessible housing registries.')
B.aTf=new A.c(u.j,"During the wheelchair evaluation, the patient begins crying and says: \"This wheelchair means I've lost. I'll never walk again. My life as I knew it is over. Why should I even bother with any of this?\"")
B.aQM=new A.c("+ Demonstrate how you would address her grief over loss of walking while maintaining engagement in rehabilitation.","**Responding to Grief Over Mobility Loss**:\n\nThis is a pivotal moment. The patient is expressing genuine grief \u2014 the loss of walking is experienced as a death of her former identity. Dismissing this grief or rushing to \"fix it\" with positive reframing will damage the therapeutic alliance. But allowing her to remain in despair without offering a path forward would be equally harmful.\n\n**Step 1 \u2014 Sit with the grief. Do not rush past it.**\n\nI would pull my chair close, make eye contact, and allow silence before speaking.\n\n'I hear you. And I want you to know that what you are feeling right now is not weakness \u2014 it is a completely natural response to a very real loss. Losing the ability to walk is a loss, and it deserves to be grieved. You do not have to pretend this is okay.'\n\n**Step 2 \u2014 Acknowledge her identity loss specifically.**\n\n'You told me when we started that you used to take care of other people \u2014 that was your career, your identity. And now you feel like you cannot take care of yourself. I imagine that shift is one of the most painful parts of all of this.'\n\n**Allow her to respond. Listen without interrupting.**\n\n**Step 3 \u2014 Gently challenge the narrative without invalidating the grief.**\n\n'I want to push back gently on one thing you said \u2014 that you have lost. Because from where I am sitting, I see a woman who has fought this disease for 18 years. Who adapted when she had to give up nursing. Who lives independently. Who manages her own catheterization, her own medications, her own daily life. That is not someone who has lost.\n\nThe wheelchair is not the end of a fight. It is a different tool for continuing one. I have seen patients in your situation go from being trapped in their homes \u2014 afraid to go anywhere because of falls \u2014 to getting out in the community, traveling, volunteering, re-engaging with life, because a properly fitted power wheelchair gave them the freedom to do things they had stopped doing.'\n\n**Step 4 \u2014 Reframe the wheelchair as independence, not dependence.**\n\n'Right now, you are borrowing a wheelchair that does not fit you, that exhausts your arms, and that cannot get you where you need to go. That is not a wheelchair \u2014 that is a cage. What we are going to get you is a tool that gives you back your independence \u2014 that protects your arms so you can keep doing your own care, that takes you where you want to go without falling, and that fits your body so you are comfortable and safe.'\n\n**Step 5 \u2014 Address the \"why bother\" with concrete, meaningful goals.**\n\n'You asked why you should bother. Let me ask you something \u2014 if you could go to your daughter's apartment for dinner without worrying about falling, would that matter to you? If you could go to the grocery store, or to a park, or to a movie, on your own schedule \u2014 would that matter?\n\nBecause those are the things we are working toward. Not walking \u2014 I cannot promise that. But living. Fully, safely, on your terms.'\n\n**Step 6 \u2014 Offer professional support for grief processing.**\n\n'I also want to connect you with a psychologist who works with people navigating exactly this kind of transition. Grief does not have to be carried alone, and having someone to talk to who understands what this kind of loss feels like can make a real difference.\n\nThe National MS Society also has peer support programs \u2014 connecting you with other women with MS who have been through this same transition and come out the other side. Would you be open to that?'\n\n**Step 7 \u2014 Establish partnership.**\n\n'Here is what I need from you: I need you to show up. To the wheelchair evaluation, to PT, to the home assessment. You do not have to feel hopeful every day. You do not have to pretend this does not hurt. But I need you to keep showing up, because the work we do together only works if you are in it with me. Can we agree on that?'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Validating grief without wallowing, acknowledging identity loss, gently challenging the catastrophic narrative with evidence of her strength, reframing the wheelchair as freedom rather than defeat, providing concrete motivating goals, offering professional mental health support, and establishing a collaborative partnership.")
B.aCW=s([B.aRu,B.aWJ,B.aOQ,B.aXb,B.aWE,B.aOP,B.aV3,B.aXp,B.b0G,B.aTf,B.aQM],t.E)
B.Qy=new A.a5("neuro-progressive-ms-wheelchair","Progressive MS \u2014 Wheelchair Transition",B.aCW,!1)
B.aNg=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 50-year-old right-hand-dominant female accountant presents to the PM&R clinic with a 4-month history of progressive right shoulder stiffness and pain. The onset was insidious with no preceding trauma. She initially noticed difficulty reaching behind her back to fasten her bra and has progressively lost the ability to reach overhead. The pain is a deep, constant ache that worsens at night, frequently disrupting her sleep when she rolls onto the right side. She has a 12-year history of type 2 diabetes mellitus (HbA1c 8.2%) and hypothyroidism. She took ibuprofen without significant relief. She is increasingly frustrated because she cannot perform basic work tasks such as filing overhead and has difficulty washing and styling her hair.")
B.aS9=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","Key areas to assess:\n- **Onset and progression**: Insidious vs. traumatic onset. Duration of symptoms. Which movements were lost first? Night pain severity.\n- **Diabetes history**: Duration, glycemic control (HbA1c), complications. Diabetes is the strongest risk factor for adhesive capsulitis \u2014 prevalence 10-36% in diabetics vs. 2-5% in general population. Bilateral involvement more common in diabetics.\n- **Other risk factors**: Thyroid disease (present), prior shoulder surgery or immobilization, Dupuytren contracture, cardiovascular disease, autoimmune conditions.\n- **Contralateral shoulder**: History of frozen shoulder on the left? Bilateral involvement occurs in 20-40% of cases (even higher with diabetes). Having one frozen shoulder increases risk of contralateral involvement.\n- **Red flags to exclude**: Night sweats, weight loss, history of cancer (rule out Pancoast tumor). Fever (septic arthritis). Progressive neurologic symptoms.\n- **Cervical spine symptoms**: Neck pain, radiculopathy symptoms \u2014 cervical pathology can refer pain to the shoulder and coexist.\n- **Functional impact**: ADLs (dressing, hygiene, grooming), work limitations (overhead filing, computer use), sleep quality, and psychological impact.\n- **Prior treatments**: Physical therapy, injections, medications tried and their effects.")
B.b0m=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:",'**Vitals**: BP 128/82 mmHg, HR 76 bpm, Temp 36.8C, BMI 31.\n\n**Right Shoulder Examination**:\n- **Inspection**: No visible atrophy, swelling, or ecchymosis. Normal scapular position at rest.\n- **Active ROM** (compared to left/normal):\n  - Forward flexion: 95 degrees (left: 170 degrees)\n  - Abduction: 80 degrees (left: 175 degrees)\n  - External rotation (arm at side): 15 degrees (left: 60 degrees)\n  - Internal rotation: Hand to lateral thigh only (left: hand to T10 spinous process)\n- **Passive ROM**: Essentially identical to active ROM \u2014 passive motion does NOT significantly exceed active motion. This is the hallmark finding of adhesive capsulitis. Firm, "rubbery" end-feel in all directions.\n- **Capsular pattern present**: External rotation most limited, then abduction, then flexion, then internal rotation.\n- **Impingement tests**: Neer test \u2014 limited by ROM restriction, not clearly positive. Hawkins-Kennedy \u2014 limited by ROM restriction. **Negative** for classical impingement pain pattern.\n- **Rotator cuff strength** (tested within available ROM): Supraspinatus (empty can) \u2014 4+/5, pain-limited. External rotation \u2014 4+/5. Internal rotation \u2014 5/5. These are relatively preserved, arguing against significant rotator cuff tear.\n- **Scapular motion**: Excessive scapulothoracic substitution noted during attempted abduction (early shrug sign) \u2014 compensating for glenohumeral restriction.\n- **Cervical spine**: Full ROM. Spurling test negative. No radicular symptoms.\n- **Left shoulder**: Full painless ROM. No signs of contralateral involvement at this time.\n- **Neurovascular**: Distal pulses intact. Sensation intact in C5-T1 distributions. No signs of thoracic outlet syndrome.')
B.aZi=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The attending asks you to discuss the staging, differential diagnosis, and role of imaging in this presentation.")
B.aMw=new A.c("+ Describe the stages of adhesive capsulitis, the key differential diagnoses, and when imaging is indicated.","**Stages of Adhesive Capsulitis** (Reeves Classification):\n\n**Stage 1 \u2014 Freezing (Painful Phase)**: Duration 2-9 months\n- Gradual onset of diffuse shoulder pain, often worse at night\n- Progressive loss of ROM begins\n- Pain is the predominant symptom\n- Arthroscopy: Diffuse synovitis with hypervascular synovium\n\n**Stage 2 \u2014 Frozen (Stiffening Phase)**: Duration 4-12 months\n- Pain may gradually decrease\n- Stiffness becomes the predominant complaint\n- ROM reaches its nadir\n- Arthroscopy: Dense, fibrotic capsular contracture with less synovitis\n\n**Stage 3 \u2014 Thawing (Resolution Phase)**: Duration 12-42 months\n- Gradual spontaneous improvement in ROM\n- Pain minimal\n- Recovery may be incomplete, especially in diabetics\n\n**This patient** is in the **late Stage 1/early Stage 2 transition** \u2014 she has significant pain AND progressive stiffness over 4 months.\n\n**Total natural history**: 1-3 years to resolution. However, in diabetics, recovery is often longer and less complete \u2014 up to 20% have permanent residual stiffness.\n\n**Key Differential Diagnoses**:\n1. **Rotator cuff tear**: Active ROM may be limited, but passive ROM is typically preserved (unlike adhesive capsulitis). Weakness on strength testing. Positive impingement signs.\n2. **Glenohumeral osteoarthritis**: Crepitus on exam. Bone-on-bone end-feel vs. capsular end-feel. Radiographic joint space narrowing and osteophytes.\n3. **Calcific tendinitis**: Acute onset pain, calcification on radiograph. May coexist with adhesive capsulitis.\n4. **Cervical radiculopathy**: Neck pain, dermatomal symptoms, positive Spurling test. Shoulder ROM is typically preserved.\n5. **Pancoast tumor**: Shoulder pain in a smoker, Horner syndrome, neurologic deficits \u2014 must exclude especially in patients with risk factors.\n6. **Posterior shoulder dislocation**: Locked internal rotation. Often missed. Best seen on axillary view radiograph.\n\n**Imaging Indications**:\n- **Plain radiographs (AP and axillary lateral)**: First-line. Usually normal in adhesive capsulitis but essential to rule out fracture, arthritis, calcific tendinitis, dislocation, and tumor. Mild osteopenia of the humeral head may be seen.\n- **MRI**: Not routinely required for diagnosis but indicated if rotator cuff tear is suspected, symptoms are atypical, or there is no improvement after 3-6 months of appropriate treatment. In adhesive capsulitis, MRI may show thickened inferior glenohumeral ligament, thickened axillary recess, and coracohumeral ligament thickening.\n- **Ultrasound**: Can evaluate rotator cuff integrity and guide injections. Doppler may show hypervascularity in the rotator interval in early stages.\n- **Arthrography**: Historically diagnostic (reduced joint capsule volume <10 mL, normal is 20-30 mL). Now rarely performed for diagnosis alone but used therapeutically in hydrodilatation.")
B.aZ0=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Radiographs are normal. You confirm the clinical diagnosis of adhesive capsulitis and develop a treatment plan.")
B.aME=new A.c("+ Outline your comprehensive treatment plan including physical therapy, injection options, and indications for procedural interventions.","**Treatment Plan \u2014 Staged Approach**:\n\n**1. Patient Education (Critical Foundation)**:\n- Explain the natural history \u2014 this condition WILL improve, but the timeline is months to years, not weeks.\n- Set expectations: Pain control is the priority in the freezing phase. Aggressive stretching during the painful phase can worsen inflammation and pain.\n- Reassure that most patients recover meaningful function.\n\n**2. Physical Therapy Program**:\n- **Freezing phase (current)**: Gentle ROM within pain tolerance. Pendulum exercises (Codman exercises). Passive and active-assisted ROM in pain-free range. Scapular stabilization exercises. Heat before therapy, ice after.\n- **Frozen phase**: Progressive stretching program \u2014 sustained low-load stretching at end-range. Wall slides, table slides, behind-the-back towel stretches, cross-body adduction stretch, sleeper stretch for internal rotation. Hold stretches 30-60 seconds, repeat 3-5 times, perform 2-3 times daily.\n- **Thawing phase**: Progressive strengthening as ROM improves. Rotator cuff and scapular stabilizer strengthening. Functional activity retraining.\n- **Frequency**: PT 2-3 times per week with daily home exercise program.\n\n**3. Pharmacologic Management**:\n- **NSAIDs**: Meloxicam 15 mg daily or naproxen 500 mg BID with meals \u2014 for pain and inflammation. Monitor renal function given diabetes. Limit to 2-4 weeks.\n- **Short course oral corticosteroid**: Prednisolone 30 mg daily x 3 weeks with taper \u2014 may provide rapid pain relief in the freezing phase, but blood glucose will need close monitoring given HbA1c 8.2%.\n- **Neuropathic adjuvant**: Gabapentin 300 mg at bedtime if night pain is severely disrupting sleep (titrate up as needed).\n\n**4. Injection Therapy**:\n- **Intra-articular glenohumeral corticosteroid injection**: First-line procedural intervention. Triamcinolone 40 mg with 4 mL lidocaine via posterior approach (or ultrasound-guided). Most effective in Stage 1 (freezing). Provides 4-6 weeks of pain relief to facilitate PT. Limit to 3 injections total. Warn patient of glucose elevation for 48-72 hours post-injection.\n- **Suprascapular nerve block**: Alternative or adjunct \u2014 blocks 70% of shoulder pain sensation. Useful for severe pain limiting participation in PT.\n- **Hydrodilatation (distension arthrography)**: Injection of saline (20-30 mL) and corticosteroid under fluoroscopic or ultrasound guidance to distend and rupture the contracted capsule. Evidence supports short-term ROM improvement. Consider if not responding to standard injection and PT after 3-4 months.\n\n**5. Indications for More Aggressive Intervention**:\n- **Manipulation under anesthesia (MUA)**: Consider if no improvement after 6+ months of conservative management. Performed under general anesthesia with controlled sequential stretching. Risk of fracture (especially in osteoporotic or diabetic patients), labral tear, rotator cuff tear, and nerve injury. Typically followed by immediate aggressive PT.\n- **Arthroscopic capsular release**: Alternative to MUA with better visualization and controlled release of the contracted capsule. Preferred in diabetic patients (who have thicker capsular fibrosis). Combined with MUA in some protocols.\n\n**6. Home Program**:\n- Pendulum exercises 3 times daily\n- Self-stretching with wand/cane exercises\n- Moist heat before stretching\n- Ice after exercise if painful\n- Sleep positioning: Avoid lying on the affected side. Pillow support under the affected arm.")
B.aSu=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient asks about the impact of her diabetes on recovery and whether she needs to modify her work duties.")
B.b1k=new A.c("+ How does diabetes management affect frozen shoulder outcomes, and what workplace modifications would you recommend?","**Diabetes and Adhesive Capsulitis**:\n\n**Impact on Disease Course**:\n- Diabetic patients have **longer symptom duration**, **more severe stiffness**, and **less complete recovery** than non-diabetic patients.\n- HbA1c >7.5% (this patient is 8.2%) is associated with worse outcomes and higher recurrence rates.\n- Advanced glycation end-products (AGEs) accumulate in collagen tissue, increasing capsular fibrosis and stiffness.\n- Response to corticosteroid injections is often less robust and shorter-lasting in diabetics.\n- Bilateral involvement occurs in up to 40% of diabetic patients \u2014 monitor the left shoulder.\n\n**Diabetes Optimization Recommendations**:\n1. **Coordinate with endocrinologist/PCP**: Emphasize that improving glycemic control may directly improve shoulder outcomes. Target HbA1c <7.0%.\n2. **Monitor glucose around treatments**: Intra-articular steroid injections and oral steroids will elevate blood glucose significantly for 48-72 hours. Advise more frequent glucose monitoring and may need temporary insulin dose adjustment.\n3. **Steroid use considerations**: Limit oral steroid use given diabetes. Intra-articular injection is preferred (less systemic glucose effect). If oral course is used, coordinate with her diabetes provider for medication adjustment.\n\n**Workplace Modifications**:\n1. **Ergonomic assessment**: Request formal evaluation of her workstation.\n2. **Specific accommodations** (under ADA as a temporary disability):\n   - Relocate files from overhead shelving to desk-height or below-shoulder-height locations\n   - Ergonomic keyboard tray and mouse positioning to avoid shoulder elevation\n   - Reduce repetitive overhead reaching tasks \u2014 reassign to colleague temporarily\n   - Allow scheduled breaks for stretching exercises (10 minutes, 2-3 times per workday)\n   - Modified keyboard/mouse position to reduce shoulder strain\n3. **Temporary work restrictions**: No overhead lifting >5 pounds with right arm. No sustained reaching above shoulder height.\n4. **Documentation**: Provide a work capacity evaluation letter specifying temporary restrictions and expected duration (3-6 months minimum).\n5. **Gradual return to full duties**: As ROM improves through the thawing phase, progressively increase work demands. Do not return to full overhead activities until at least 140 degrees of forward flexion and 40 degrees of external rotation are achieved.")
B.aVb=new A.c(u.j,"At her 4-week follow-up, the patient is visibly frustrated. She says: 'I have been doing the exercises religiously and it is getting WORSE, not better. It has been 4 months now and you told me it would get better. I think something else is going on. Maybe I need an MRI or a second opinion.'")
B.aSw=new A.c("+ Demonstrate how you would address this patient's frustration with the slow pace of recovery.","I would respond with empathy, validation, and clear communication about expectations:\n\n**1. Validate her frustration**:\n'I completely understand your frustration, and I appreciate that you have been so diligent with your exercises. Doing everything right and still feeling worse is incredibly discouraging, and I take your concerns seriously.'\n\n**2. Explain the expected disease course honestly**:\n'Here is the reality of adhesive capsulitis \u2014 and I want to be completely transparent. You are currently in what we call the freezing phase, where the shoulder is still actively tightening. During this phase, it is common and expected for symptoms to worsen despite appropriate treatment. This is not because the treatment is failing \u2014 it is because the disease has not yet run its course. The treatments we are doing now are preventing it from getting even worse and will speed your recovery once the condition turns the corner into the thawing phase.'\n\n**3. Provide a realistic timeline**:\n'The total course of frozen shoulder is typically 1 to 3 years, and in patients with diabetes it can take longer. I know that is not what you want to hear. The good news is that the vast majority of patients \u2014 over 90% \u2014 do recover meaningful function. We are likely several months away from the thawing phase where you will start to see real improvement.'\n\n**4. Address her request for imaging thoughtfully**:\n'Regarding an MRI \u2014 I do not dismiss your instinct at all. Based on my examination today, your strength testing is reassuring and the pattern of stiffness is consistent with adhesive capsulitis. However, given that it has been 4 months and you are appropriately concerned, I am willing to order an MRI to confirm there is nothing else contributing. I want you to feel confident in the diagnosis.'\n\n**5. Offer treatment escalation**:\n'In the meantime, I would like to offer a corticosteroid injection into your shoulder joint. This is the most effective treatment we have for the painful phase, and research shows it can significantly reduce your pain and improve your ability to participate in therapy. We do need to watch your blood sugar closely afterward. Would you like to try that today?'\n\n**6. Reinforce the partnership**:\n'I am not giving up on you, and I do not want you to give up on the process. If you would like a second opinion, I fully support that \u2014 I can refer you to a shoulder specialist. My goal is for you to feel confident in your care plan. Let us adjust our approach and continue working together through this.'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Empathy, validation without defensiveness, honest prognostication, willingness to investigate patient concerns, treatment escalation, collaborative decision-making, openness to second opinion.")
B.aDe=s([B.aNg,B.aS9,B.b0m,B.aZi,B.aMw,B.aZ0,B.aME,B.aSu,B.b1k,B.aVb,B.aSw],t.E)
B.Q4=new A.a5("msk-adhesive-capsulitis","Adhesive Capsulitis (Frozen Shoulder)",B.aDe,!1)
B.aZL=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 58-year-old right-hand-dominant male warehouse manager presents to the PM&R clinic 10 days after falling onto his outstretched right hand at work. He reports immediate onset of severe right shoulder pain and has been unable to lift his arm above shoulder height since the injury. He has significant nighttime pain preventing sleep on his right side. He denies numbness or tingling. His primary care physician obtained an MRI which shows a full-thickness supraspinatus tendon tear measuring 2.5 cm in retraction with mild fatty infiltration of the supraspinatus muscle (Goutallier Grade 1). There is also a small joint effusion and mild subacromial bursitis. He is anxious and states he needs to get back to work as soon as possible.")
B.b_c=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","Key areas to assess:\n- **Mechanism of injury**: Fall on outstretched hand (FOOSH) \u2014 acute traumatic tear vs. acute-on-chronic. Did he have any pre-existing shoulder symptoms? Chronic degenerative tears are common at this age.\n- **Workers' compensation status**: Was this an on-the-job injury? Workers' comp claim filed? This will affect treatment pathway, authorization, and return-to-work planning.\n- **Dominant hand involvement**: Right-dominant with right shoulder tear \u2014 higher functional impact.\n- **Occupational demands**: Warehouse manager \u2014 overhead lifting, carrying, pushing/pulling requirements. Quantify physical demands (sedentary, light, medium, heavy, very heavy per DOT classification).\n- **Prior shoulder history**: Any previous injury, injection, or treatment to the right shoulder. Prior imaging for comparison.\n- **Smoking status**: Smoking impairs tendon healing and increases re-tear rates. Critical for surgical planning.\n- **Medical comorbidities**: Diabetes (impairs healing), rheumatoid arthritis, osteoporosis (anchor fixation concerns), anticoagulation.\n- **Functional limitations**: Specific ADL limitations \u2014 dressing, bathing, grooming, sleeping, driving, and work activities.\n- **Pain management**: Current pain medications, effectiveness, any opioid use since injury.\n- **Patient expectations and preferences**: Understanding of diagnosis, desire for surgery vs. conservative management, timeline expectations.")
B.aTX=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 140/86 mmHg, HR 78 bpm, Temp 36.9C, BMI 29.\n\n**Right Shoulder Examination**:\n- **Inspection**: Mild swelling over the anterolateral shoulder. No visible atrophy (acute tear \u2014 atrophy takes weeks to develop). Guarding with any shoulder movement.\n- **Active ROM**: Forward flexion 90 degrees (pain-limited), abduction 80 degrees with painful arc 60-120 degrees, external rotation 30 degrees, internal rotation to buttock level. Significant pain with all active movements.\n- **Passive ROM**: Forward flexion 160 degrees, abduction 150 degrees, external rotation 50 degrees. Passive ROM significantly exceeds active ROM \u2014 consistent with a rotator cuff tear (NOT adhesive capsulitis).\n- **Strength Testing**:\n  - Empty can test (supraspinatus): 3/5, pain and weakness. Positive for supraspinatus pathology.\n  - External rotation (infraspinatus/teres minor): 4/5, mild pain.\n  - Belly press (subscapularis): 5/5, no pain.\n  - External rotation lag sign: Positive \u2014 arm drifts into internal rotation when placed in external rotation and released. Indicates significant rotator cuff compromise.\n  - Drop arm test: Positive \u2014 cannot slowly lower arm from 90 degrees abduction. Arm drops.\n- **Special Tests**: Neer impingement sign positive. Hawkins-Kennedy positive. Speed test (biceps) mildly positive.\n- **Cervical Spine**: Full ROM. Spurling test negative bilaterally.\n- **Neurovascular**: Distal pulses intact. Sensation intact in axillary nerve distribution (lateral deltoid \u2014 important to check after trauma). Deltoid strength 4/5 (pain-limited but intact innervation).")
B.aTI=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The attending asks you to interpret the MRI findings and discuss tear classification and surgical indications.")
B.aQL=new A.c("+ Classify this rotator cuff tear and discuss the indications for surgical versus conservative management.","**Rotator Cuff Tear Classification**:\n\n**By Thickness**:\n- **Partial-thickness**: Articular-sided, bursal-sided, or intratendinous. Graded by depth (<25%, 25-50%, >50% of tendon thickness).\n- **Full-thickness**: Complete disruption from bursal to articular surface. This patient has a full-thickness tear.\n\n**By Size (full-thickness, based on retraction)**:\n- Small: <1 cm\n- Medium: 1-3 cm\n- Large: 3-5 cm\n- Massive: >5 cm or involving 2+ tendons\n- **This patient**: 2.5 cm retraction \u2014 **medium-sized** full-thickness tear\n\n**By Chronicity**:\n- Acute: Traumatic event, no pre-existing changes\n- Chronic: Degenerative, fatty infiltration, muscle atrophy\n- **Acute-on-chronic**: Pre-existing degeneration with acute traumatic extension \u2014 likely in this 58-year-old given mild fatty infiltration\n\n**Goutallier Classification (Fatty Infiltration on MRI/CT)**:\n- Grade 0: No fat\n- Grade 1: Some fatty streaks (this patient)\n- Grade 2: Fat < muscle\n- Grade 3: Fat = muscle\n- Grade 4: Fat > muscle\n- Grades 3-4 predict poor surgical outcomes and irreversible muscle changes.\n\n**Indications FAVORING Surgical Repair**:\n1. **Acute traumatic full-thickness tear** in a functional, active individual \u2014 strongest indication\n2. Full-thickness tear with significant functional impairment\n3. Failure of 6-12 weeks of conservative management\n4. Younger, active patient (<65) with high physical demands\n5. Tear retraction <3 cm with Goutallier Grade 0-2 (repairable with good tissue quality)\n6. Workers' compensation with heavy labor demands\n\n**Indications FAVORING Conservative Management**:\n1. Partial-thickness tears (especially <50% depth)\n2. Chronic, massive, retracted tears with Grade 3-4 fatty infiltration (poor surgical outcomes)\n3. Low-demand, elderly patients (>70-75) with minimal functional impairment\n4. Significant medical comorbidities making surgery high-risk\n5. Patient preference after informed discussion\n6. Intact or compensated shoulder function despite imaging findings\n\n**For This Patient**: Multiple factors favor surgical referral \u2014 acute traumatic mechanism, dominant extremity, medium-sized tear with minimal fatty infiltration (good tissue quality, better prognosis), high occupational demands, and age (58 \u2014 reasonable surgical candidate). However, a trial of conservative management for 6-12 weeks is still reasonable if the patient prefers, as some full-thickness tears compensate well with rehabilitation. The key is early surgical consultation so the option is not delayed \u2014 chronic retraction and progressive fatty infiltration reduce surgical success.")
B.aNu=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You discuss options with the patient. He wants to understand both pathways before deciding. The orthopedic surgeon also wants your recommendation on pre-operative optimization and post-operative rehabilitation.")
B.aZR=new A.c("+ Outline both conservative and post-surgical rehabilitation protocols for this patient.","**Conservative Management Protocol (if chosen)**:\n\n**Phase 1 \u2014 Acute (Weeks 0-6)**:\n- Sling for comfort (not immobilization) \u2014 wean as tolerated by 2-4 weeks\n- Pain management: Ice, acetaminophen, short course of NSAIDs, tramadol if needed. Avoid opioids beyond 1-2 weeks.\n- Gentle pendulum exercises and PROM beginning immediately\n- Scapular retraction and postural exercises\n\n**Phase 2 \u2014 Recovery (Weeks 6-12)**:\n- Progressive AROM and stretching\n- Isometric rotator cuff strengthening, progressing to isotonic\n- Scapular stabilization exercises\n- Closed kinetic chain exercises (wall push-ups)\n\n**Phase 3 \u2014 Strengthening (Weeks 12-24)**:\n- Progressive resistance training for rotator cuff and scapular stabilizers\n- Functional movement retraining for work tasks\n- Deltoid compensation training (anterior deltoid can partially compensate for supraspinatus)\n\n**Reassessment**: If no meaningful improvement by 12 weeks of compliant conservative care, recommend surgical repair before further retraction and fatty infiltration occur.\n\n**Post-Surgical Rehabilitation Protocol** (after arthroscopic rotator cuff repair):\n\n**Phase 1 \u2014 Maximum Protection (Weeks 0-6)**:\n- Sling immobilization for 6 weeks (critical for tendon-to-bone healing)\n- Only passive ROM exercises performed by therapist: forward flexion to 120 degrees, external rotation to 30 degrees\n- Pendulum exercises\n- Elbow, wrist, and hand AROM to prevent stiffness\n- Scapular isometrics (retraction, depression)\n- Cryotherapy for pain and swelling\n- **No active shoulder movement. No lifting. No driving.**\n\n**Phase 2 \u2014 Active Motion (Weeks 6-12)**:\n- Wean from sling\n- Progress to active-assisted then active ROM\n- Isometric rotator cuff exercises in neutral\n- Light resistance band exercises beginning at week 8-10\n- Scapular stabilization exercises\n- May begin driving when off sling and able to safely control the steering wheel\n\n**Phase 3 \u2014 Strengthening (Weeks 12-20)**:\n- Progressive isotonic rotator cuff strengthening\n- Resistance band and light dumbbell exercises\n- Closed and open kinetic chain exercises\n- Sport/work-specific movement patterns\n- Core and kinetic chain strengthening\n\n**Phase 4 \u2014 Return to Activity (Weeks 20-26+)**:\n- Full unrestricted ROM expected\n- Strength at least 80-90% of contralateral side\n- Work hardening program for return to warehouse duties\n- Functional capacity evaluation (FCE) before return to heavy labor\n\n**Key Milestones**: Full PROM by week 8. Full AROM by week 12. Return to desk work by week 8-10. Return to heavy labor by 6-9 months (with FCE clearance). Full recovery up to 12 months.")
B.aTY=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient confirms this was a work injury and asks about his workers' compensation options and when he can return to work.")
B.aVE=new A.c("+ How do you navigate workers' compensation requirements and determine appropriate surgical referral criteria?","**Workers' Compensation Management**:\n\n**Documentation Requirements**:\n1. **First report of injury**: Ensure this has been filed with the employer and workers' comp carrier. Include date, time, mechanism, and witnessed status.\n2. **Medical records**: Thorough documentation of mechanism (acute traumatic event at work), objective exam findings, imaging results, and functional limitations. This establishes causation and medical necessity.\n3. **Work status reports**: At each visit, document specific work restrictions using standard terminology (sedentary, light, medium, heavy). Provide modified duty restrictions when possible.\n\n**Work Status for This Patient**:\n- **Currently**: Sedentary duty only. No use of right upper extremity for lifting, carrying, pushing, or pulling. If modified duty is available (administrative tasks), he can work. If not, temporary total disability.\n- **If conservative**: Light duty by 6-8 weeks. Medium duty by 12-16 weeks. Full duty pending FCE at 20-24 weeks.\n- **If surgical**: No work for 6-8 weeks. Sedentary/light duty by 8-12 weeks. Medium duty by 16-20 weeks. Full duty pending FCE at 6-9 months.\n\n**Surgical Referral Criteria**:\n1. Full-thickness tear confirmed on MRI\n2. Functional limitation preventing return to pre-injury work duties\n3. Good tissue quality (Goutallier Grade 0-2) suggesting repairable tear\n4. Reasonable surgical candidate (medically fit)\n5. Workers' comp authorization may require a trial of conservative management (typically 6 weeks) before approving surgery \u2014 varies by jurisdiction.\n\n**Authorization Process**:\n- Submit pre-authorization request to workers' comp carrier with supporting documentation (MRI report, exam findings, failed conservative care if applicable)\n- Independent medical examination (IME) may be requested by the carrier\n- Utilization review may question surgical necessity \u2014 be prepared to support with evidence-based guidelines (ACOEM Practice Guidelines)\n- Peer-to-peer review may be needed if initially denied\n\n**Return-to-Work Planning**:\n- **Functional Capacity Evaluation (FCE)**: Order before full-duty return. Objective measurement of the patient's ability to meet the physical demands of his specific job.\n- **Work hardening/conditioning program**: If FCE reveals gap between current capacity and job demands, 2-4 week structured program to bridge the gap.\n- **Permanent impairment rating**: If residual deficits remain at maximum medical improvement (MMI), calculate impairment rating per AMA Guides (6th edition). This determines any permanent partial disability settlement.")
B.aZH=new A.c(u.j,"The patient returns after seeing the orthopedic surgeon, who recommended 3 months of conservative management before considering surgery. The patient is upset: 'The surgeon said I should just do physical therapy! But you said surgery was a good option. I need to get back to work \u2014 my family depends on my income. Why can't the doctors agree?'")
B.aVP=new A.c("+ Demonstrate how you would mediate between the patient's expectations and the differing provider recommendations.","I would approach this as an opportunity to educate, align expectations, and advocate for the patient:\n\n**1. Acknowledge his frustration and financial concerns**:\n'I hear you, and I understand this is not just a medical issue \u2014 it is affecting your ability to provide for your family. That stress is real, and I want to help you navigate this as efficiently as possible.'\n\n**2. Clarify the apparent disagreement**:\n'I want to reassure you that the surgeon and I are actually not in disagreement. Both surgery and conservative management are legitimate evidence-based options for your type of tear. What I told you was that your tear has features that make it a good candidate for surgical repair if conservative management does not work. What the surgeon is recommending is a reasonable and common first step \u2014 trying physical therapy before surgery. Many patients with full-thickness tears recover good function without surgery.'\n\n**3. Explain the reasoning**:\n'The surgeon likely wants to accomplish several things: first, see if your shoulder can compensate without surgery, which is possible even with a full-thickness tear. Second, pre-rehabilitation before surgery actually improves surgical outcomes \u2014 patients who enter surgery with better motion and less inflammation tend to recover faster. Third, insurance and workers' comp often require a documented trial of conservative care before authorizing surgery.'\n\n**4. Provide a clear action plan**:\n'Here is what I recommend: let us commit fully to the conservative program for 6 to 8 weeks. You will do physical therapy twice a week, your home exercises daily, and I will see you every 2-3 weeks to track your progress. If at 8 weeks you are not making meaningful gains in strength and function, I will personally advocate with the surgeon and your workers' comp carrier for surgical authorization. We will not let this drag on unnecessarily.'\n\n**5. Address the financial concern directly**:\n'Regarding your income \u2014 workers' compensation should cover your lost wages if you cannot perform modified duty. I am going to write specific work restrictions today. If your employer can offer modified duty within those restrictions, you can return to work immediately in a limited capacity. If not, you are entitled to temporary total disability benefits. Our social worker can help you navigate the claims process.'\n\n**6. Offer to coordinate with the surgeon**:\n'If you would like, I am happy to call the surgeon's office so we can align our recommendations and make sure we are all on the same page. You should not have to manage conflicting advice \u2014 that is our job.'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Validating concerns, clarifying perceived provider disagreement, educating about shared decision-making, providing a concrete timeline with milestones, addressing psychosocial concerns (financial stress), offering direct inter-provider communication.")
B.a7M=s([B.aZL,B.b_c,B.aTX,B.aTI,B.aQL,B.aNu,B.aZR,B.aTY,B.aVE,B.aZH,B.aVP],t.E)
B.QG=new A.a5("msk-rotator-cuff-tear","Rotator Cuff Tear \u2014 Surgical vs Conservative Management",B.a7M,!1)
B.aOp=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 22-year-old female collegiate soccer player presents to the PM&R sports medicine clinic 2 weeks after left ACL reconstruction using a bone-patellar tendon-bone (BTB) autograft. The injury occurred during a non-contact pivoting mechanism while changing direction in a game 4 weeks ago. She had immediate swelling and inability to bear weight. MRI confirmed a complete ACL tear with an associated lateral meniscus tear (posterior horn) that was repaired at the time of ACL reconstruction. She is currently in a hinged knee brace locked in extension, using crutches for ambulation, and performing basic quad sets at home. She is a Division I scholarship athlete and her coach has already asked when she will be ready for preseason in 6 months.")
B.aRj=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","Key areas to assess:\n- **Surgical details**: Graft type (BTB autograft confirmed), fixation method, concomitant procedures (meniscal repair \u2014 critical as this affects weight-bearing and ROM restrictions), surgeon's specific post-operative protocol and restrictions.\n- **Pre-injury level of play**: Starter vs. reserve, position, years of competitive play, prior knee injuries. She is a D1 scholarship athlete \u2014 high-level athlete with strong motivation to return.\n- **Injury mechanism**: Non-contact pivoting \u2014 this is the classic ACL tear mechanism. Female soccer players are at 2-4x higher ACL injury risk compared to males.\n- **Contralateral knee history**: Prior ACL injury on the right? Young female athletes have a 15-25% risk of second ACL injury (ipsilateral re-tear or contralateral tear) within 2 years of return to sport.\n- **Current symptoms**: Pain level (VAS), swelling, knee ROM, ability to perform quad sets, sleep quality, appetite.\n- **Psychological status**: Anxiety about recovery, fear of re-injury, mood changes, loss of athletic identity. Screen with ACL-RSI (Return to Sport after Injury) scale.\n- **Menstrual history**: Oligomenorrhea or amenorrhea? Female athlete triad/RED-S screening (low energy availability, menstrual dysfunction, low bone density).\n- **Nutritional status**: Adequate protein and caloric intake for healing? Is she restricting intake due to reduced activity?\n- **Academic impact**: Missing classes for appointments, difficulty concentrating, eligibility concerns.\n- **Social support**: Family, teammates, athletic training staff involvement.")
B.aXn=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:",'**Vitals**: BP 112/68 mmHg, HR 62 bpm (athlete), Temp 36.7C, BMI 22.\n\n**Left Knee Examination (2 weeks post-op)**:\n- **Inspection**: Surgical incisions clean, dry, and well-approximated. Mild-moderate effusion. Ecchymosis tracking distally to the proximal calf. Patellar tendon harvest site midline \u2014 mild tenderness to palpation.\n- **Range of Motion**: Active extension 0 degrees (achieves full extension \u2014 critical early goal). Active flexion 72 degrees (target at 2 weeks is 60-90 degrees). Passive flexion 80 degrees with guarding.\n- **Quadriceps Activation**: Visible quad contraction with quad set, but lag present \u2014 cannot perform straight leg raise without a 10-degree extensor lag. This indicates quad inhibition, not necessarily quad weakness.\n- **Patellar mobility**: Mildly restricted in medial and superior glide \u2014 monitor for developing patellar fibrosis.\n- **Effusion**: Moderate (positive ballotement test). Grade 2+ effusion.\n- **Gait**: Ambulating with bilateral crutches, weight-bearing as tolerated per surgeon protocol (partial weight-bearing due to meniscal repair). Heel-to-toe pattern with crutches. No knee hyperextension thrust.\n- **Contralateral knee**: Stable, full ROM, no effusion. Lachman test negative. No laxity.\n- **Hip/Core screening**: Mild bilateral hip abductor weakness (4/5) and core instability on single-leg stance \u2014 pre-existing neuromuscular risk factors for ACL injury. These will be addressed in rehabilitation.\n\n**Psychological Assessment**: Patient tearful during the visit. Reports difficulty sleeping, loss of appetite, and feeling "disconnected from the team." ACL-RSI score: 28/100 (low psychological readiness \u2014 significant fear of re-injury and emotional disturbance).')
B.aUP=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The attending asks you to discuss the graft healing biology and how to recognize early complications.")
B.aUA=new A.c("+ Describe the BTB autograft healing timeline and how you would recognize arthrofibrosis and other complications.",'**BTB Autograft Healing Timeline (Ligamentization Process)**:\n\n**Phase 1 \u2014 Avascular Necrosis (Weeks 0-4)**:\n- Graft is essentially "dead tissue" \u2014 relies on diffusion for nutrients\n- Central graft necrosis with preservation of outer shell\n- Bone plugs begin incorporating into tunnels\n- **Graft is at its weakest during this period** \u2014 must protect\n\n**Phase 2 \u2014 Revascularization and Cellular Repopulation (Weeks 4-12)**:\n- New blood vessels infiltrate the graft from the periphery\n- Host cells (fibroblasts) migrate into the graft matrix\n- Graft mechanical strength is at its nadir around 6-8 weeks ("ligamentization valley")\n- **Highest risk period for graft failure if overloaded**\n\n**Phase 3 \u2014 Remodeling (Months 3-12)**:\n- Progressive collagen remodeling and maturation\n- Graft gradually approximates native ACL properties\n- Bone plug integration typically solid by 8-12 weeks\n- By 6 months, graft has approximately 50-60% of native ACL strength\n- Full ligamentization may take 12-24+ months\n\n**BTB-Specific Considerations**: Bone-to-bone healing at fixation sites is faster and stronger than soft tissue graft healing (hamstring graft). However, BTB grafts have donor site morbidity \u2014 anterior knee pain and kneeling pain in up to 30-40% of patients.\n\n**Recognizing Complications**:\n\n**1. Arthrofibrosis (Stiffness)**:\n- Loss of extension is the most concerning \u2014 >5 degrees of extension loss compared to contralateral side at 4-6 weeks is a red flag\n- Flexion <90 degrees at 4-6 weeks should raise concern\n- Risk factors: Pre-operative ROM deficit, early surgery (<3 weeks from injury), excessive inflammation, poor compliance with extension exercises\n- **In this patient**: At 2 weeks, she has 0 degrees extension (excellent) and 72 degrees flexion (on track). Monitor closely at 4 and 6 weeks.\n- **Treatment if detected**: Aggressive extension protocol, low-load long-duration stretching, patellar mobilization. If persistent, lysis of adhesions arthroscopically.\n\n**2. Graft Failure/Re-tear**:\n- Incidence: 5-15% depending on age, sport, and graft type\n- Early failure (<6 months): Usually traumatic or protocol non-compliance\n- Signs: Recurrent instability, giving way episodes, positive Lachman/pivot shift on exam, new effusion\n- Highest risk period for re-tear: First 12 months after return to sport\n\n**3. Infection**:\n- Incidence: 0.5-1%. Presents with increasing pain, warmth, erythema, fever, persistent effusion\n- Requires urgent surgical irrigation and debridement with IV antibiotics\n\n**4. Cyclops Lesion**:\n- Fibrous nodule anterior to the graft causing mechanical block to terminal extension\n- Presents as inability to achieve full extension with a firm endpoint\n- Diagnosed on MRI, treated with arthroscopic excision\n\n**5. Patellar Fracture or Tendon Rupture** (BTB-specific):\n- Rare but devastating. Patellar fracture risk at donor site. Avoid heavy resisted knee extension in early rehabilitation.\n\n**6. Meniscal Repair Failure**:\n- This patient had a lateral meniscus repair \u2014 compliance with protected weight-bearing is essential. Deep flexion >90 degrees and squatting are restricted for 4-6 weeks to protect the repair.')
B.aRo=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You develop a phased rehabilitation protocol in coordination with the surgical team and athletic training staff.")
B.aQc=new A.c("+ Outline the phased rehabilitation protocol from the current 2-week mark through return to sport.","**Phase 1 \u2014 Protection and Early Motion (Weeks 0-6)**:\n- **Goals**: Protect graft and meniscal repair, achieve full extension (0 degrees), flexion to 90-100 degrees by week 4 and 120 degrees by week 6, minimize effusion, restore quad activation.\n- **Weight-bearing**: Partial weight-bearing with crutches for first 4-6 weeks (meniscal repair protocol \u2014 standard ACL without meniscal repair is WBAT immediately).\n- **Brace**: Hinged knee brace locked in extension for walking, unlock 0-90 degrees for exercises. Discontinue brace at 4-6 weeks per surgeon.\n- **Exercises**: Quad sets, straight leg raises (all planes), patellar mobilization, ankle pumps, prone hangs for extension, heel slides for flexion, stationary bike (when 100+ degrees flexion achieved), pool walking (when incisions healed).\n- **Modalities**: Cryotherapy, NMES to quadriceps (reduce quad inhibition), game-ready compression.\n\n**Phase 2 \u2014 Progressive Strengthening (Weeks 6-12)**:\n- **Goals**: Full ROM (0-135+ degrees), normalize gait without assistive device, improve quad strength (target 60% of contralateral), progress closed kinetic chain (CKC) exercises.\n- **Weight-bearing**: Full weight-bearing, wean crutches.\n- **Exercises**: Mini-squats (0-60 degrees), leg press (limited arc), step-ups (4-inch progressing to 8-inch), hamstring curls, hip strengthening (abductors, external rotators \u2014 critical for neuromuscular control), stationary bike with progressive resistance, elliptical trainer, aquatic jogging.\n- **Core and hip**: Initiate comprehensive core stability and hip strengthening program \u2014 address the pre-existing hip abductor weakness identified on exam.\n- **Proprioception**: Double-leg balance exercises on unstable surfaces, weight-shifting drills.\n\n**Phase 3 \u2014 Advanced Strengthening and Neuromuscular Training (Months 3-6)**:\n- **Goals**: Quad strength >70% of contralateral, begin running progression, advanced neuromuscular control.\n- **Running initiation criteria** (typically 3-4 months): No effusion, full ROM, quad strength >70%, single-leg squat with good mechanics, no pain with hopping.\n- **Running progression**: Walk-jog intervals on flat surface, progress distance and speed over 6-8 weeks. Forward running only initially.\n- **Exercises**: Full-depth squats, single-leg exercises (Bulgarian split squats, single-leg deadlifts), plyometric preparation (double-leg jump landing drills), lateral band walks, perturbation training on wobble board.\n- **Neuromuscular training**: ACL prevention program principles (landing mechanics, deceleration training, hip-dominant movement patterns). This reduces re-injury risk and addresses the biomechanical factors that contributed to the original injury.\n\n**Phase 4 \u2014 Sport-Specific Training (Months 6-9)**:\n- **Goals**: Quad and hamstring strength >90% of contralateral, pass functional tests, progress to cutting and pivoting, psychological readiness.\n- **Exercises**: Agility drills (ladder, cones), cutting progressions (45-degree cuts, then 90-degree cuts, then reactive cutting), plyometrics (single-leg hops, box jumps, depth jumps), sport-specific drills (soccer \u2014 dribbling, passing, shooting progressions without contact).\n- **On-field progression**: Individual drills, then team drills without contact, then controlled scrimmaging, then full contact practice.\n\n**Phase 5 \u2014 Return to Competition (Months 9-12+)**:\n- Must meet ALL return-to-sport criteria before clearance (see Domain D)\n- Gradual reintroduction to full competition\n- Maintenance of neuromuscular prevention program indefinitely")
B.aZ7=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","At 7 months post-surgery, the patient is progressing well and her coach begins pressuring her to return for the fall season. You discuss return-to-sport criteria.")
B.aMZ=new A.c("+ What objective return-to-sport criteria must be met, and how do you assess psychological readiness?","**Objective Return-to-Sport Criteria** (ALL must be met):\n\n**1. Time-Based Minimum**:\n- Minimum **9 months** post-surgery for BTB autograft ACL reconstruction. Many sports medicine specialists now recommend 12+ months based on evidence that every month of delay in return to sport (up to 9 months) reduces re-injury risk by 51%.\n- With concomitant meniscal repair, 9-12 months is appropriate.\n\n**2. Strength Testing**:\n- **Quadriceps Limb Symmetry Index (LSI) >90%**: Isokinetic testing at 60 degrees/sec and 180 degrees/sec. Both concentric quadriceps and hamstring strength must be >90% of the contralateral limb.\n- **Hamstring-to-quadriceps ratio >60%** on the operative leg.\n- **Hip abductor and external rotator strength**: Symmetric bilaterally. Deficit increases dynamic valgus risk.\n\n**3. Functional Hop Testing Battery (LSI >90% on all)**:\n- **Single-leg hop for distance**: Single maximum hop, compare limb symmetry\n- **Triple hop for distance**: Three consecutive single-leg hops\n- **Crossover hop for distance**: Three hops crossing over a line\n- **Timed 6-meter hop**: Single-leg hopping for speed\n- All four tests must achieve >90% LSI to pass\n\n**4. Movement Quality Assessment**:\n- **Single-leg squat test**: Observe for dynamic knee valgus, trunk lean, Trendelenburg\n- **Drop vertical jump (DVJ)**: Video analysis for knee valgus angle on landing. Acceptable landing mechanics without medial knee collapse.\n- **Landing Error Scoring System (LESS)**: Standardized assessment of jump-landing biomechanics\n- **Y-Balance Test**: Composite reach score >95% of contralateral limb\n\n**5. Sport-Specific Functional Testing**:\n- Successful completion of progressive agility and cutting drills without pain, effusion, or apprehension\n- Sport-specific drills at game speed (for soccer: sprinting, cutting, heading, kicking)\n- Full practice participation for at least 2-4 weeks without setback\n\n**6. Psychological Readiness** (Often overlooked but critical):\n- **ACL-RSI Scale**: Score >56 out of 100 associated with successful return. Her initial score was 28 \u2014 this needs significant improvement.\n- **Tampa Scale of Kinesiophobia**: Assess fear of movement and re-injury.\n- Subjective confidence: Patient should self-report >80% confidence in the knee.\n- Components assessed: Confidence in knee function, absence of fear during sport-specific activities, re-injury anxiety level, readiness to accept risk.\n\n**For this patient at 7 months**: She is NOT ready. Minimum time criteria not met (9 months). Must complete all functional testing. Her low ACL-RSI score suggests she needs sports psychology support before return is appropriate. It would be premature and unsafe to clear her at this time, regardless of coach pressure.")
B.aWg=new A.c(u.j,"The patient reports that her head coach has been texting her daily asking when she will be cleared. She says: 'Coach told me that if I'm not back by preseason, they might give my scholarship to someone else. I know I'm not ready but I can't afford to lose my scholarship. Can you clear me?'")
B.b35=new A.c("+ Demonstrate how you would advocate for patient safety while navigating the coach's pressure and the athlete's financial concerns.","I would address this with a clear stance on patient safety while actively problem-solving her legitimate concerns:\n\n**1. Affirm her bravery in being honest**:\n'I am glad you told me about this pressure. Being honest with me about what you are feeling \u2014 both physically and about the situation with your coach \u2014 takes courage, and it helps me help you.'\n\n**2. State the medical position clearly and unequivocally**:\n'I need to be direct with you: I cannot and will not clear you to return to competition until you meet objective safety criteria. Returning too early with an ACL graft that has not fully healed puts you at significant risk of re-tearing the graft or injuring your other knee. The research is very clear \u2014 athletes who return before 9 months have a re-injury rate that is dramatically higher. A second ACL surgery would set you back another 9-12 months and the outcomes are never as good as the first reconstruction.'\n\n**3. Validate her fear and reframe the situation**:\n'Your scholarship concerns are completely valid, and I do not take them lightly. But I want you to consider this: if you return early, re-tear the graft, and need a revision surgery, that could end your career entirely. Protecting your knee now is protecting your athletic future.'\n\n**4. Offer concrete advocacy steps**:\n'Here is what I am prepared to do for you:\n- First, I will write a formal letter to the athletic department and compliance office documenting that you are recovering from a major knee reconstruction and that premature return poses unacceptable medical risk. NCAA rules prohibit retaliation against athletes for following medical advice.\n- Second, I will contact the team physician and athletic trainer directly to align our timeline and communicate your progress.\n- Third, I would like to involve our athletic department's Student Athlete Advisory Committee or compliance officer. Threatening a scholarship based on recovery from a sport-related injury is not appropriate, and there are protections in place.'\n\n**5. Address the psychological component**:\n'I also want to talk about how you are feeling emotionally. Your ACL-RSI score tells me you have significant fear about returning, and that is completely normal. Research shows that athletes who return before they are psychologically ready are at higher risk of re-injury. I want to connect you with a sports psychologist who specializes in working with injured athletes. This is not about mental weakness \u2014 elite athletes use sports psychology as a performance tool.'\n\n**6. Provide a realistic and hopeful timeline**:\n'Based on where you are now at 7 months, I am optimistic about your recovery. If we continue on this trajectory, we can target functional testing at 9 months and, if you pass all criteria, a graduated return to full competition between 9 and 12 months. That gives us a clear goal to work toward together.'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Unwavering patient advocacy, refusal to compromise medical safety under external pressure, addressing financial/scholarship concerns with concrete institutional resources, psychological support referral, inter-provider communication, empowerment through knowledge.")
B.aGe=s([B.aOp,B.aRj,B.aXn,B.aUP,B.aUA,B.aRo,B.aQc,B.aZ7,B.aMZ,B.aWg,B.b35],t.E)
B.R5=new A.a5("msk-acl-reconstruction-rehab","ACL Reconstruction Rehabilitation",B.aGe,!1)
B.aX3=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 48-year-old right-hand-dominant male software engineer presents to the PM&R clinic with a 6-week history of right-sided neck pain radiating into his right arm and hand. The pain began insidiously and has progressively worsened. He describes a burning, electric sensation that travels from his right lateral neck down the lateral arm and forearm into his thumb and index finger. The pain is worse when looking up and turning his head to the right. He has noticed his right arm feels "heavy" and he is dropping objects. He works 10-12 hours per day at a computer and has a sedentary lifestyle. He has been taking ibuprofen 600 mg TID with minimal relief and is sleeping in a recliner because lying flat worsens the pain. He asks about getting opioid pain medication.')
B.aTE=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","Key areas to assess:\n- **Pain characterization**: Dermatomal distribution (C6 pattern: lateral arm, forearm, thumb, index finger). Quality (burning, shooting \u2014 radicular/neuropathic). Provocative factors (extension, ipsilateral rotation, Valsalva). Alleviating factors (abduction relief sign \u2014 resting hand on head reduces traction on nerve root).\n- **Neurologic symptoms**: Numbness, tingling, weakness \u2014 which specific muscles affected? Any bilateral symptoms or lower extremity symptoms that would suggest myelopathy?\n- **Red flags (myelopathy)**: Gait disturbance, bilateral hand clumsiness, bowel/bladder dysfunction, Lhermitte sign (electric sensation down spine with neck flexion). These change the diagnosis and urgency dramatically.\n- **Red flags (other)**: Fever, weight loss, night sweats (infection, malignancy). History of cancer. IV drug use. Recent trauma.\n- **Occupational history**: Prolonged computer use with poor ergonomics. Repetitive neck positions. This is highly relevant for both etiology and management.\n- **Prior cervical spine history**: Previous episodes, imaging, treatments, injections, or surgery.\n- **Psychosocial factors**: Work stress, sleep disruption, catastrophizing, fear-avoidance beliefs. These predict chronicity and should be addressed early.\n- **Medications**: NSAID use, acetaminophen, any prior opioid prescriptions, gabapentinoids, muscle relaxants tried.\n- **Functional impact**: Work productivity, driving, ADLs, exercise, sleep quality.")
B.b25=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 134/84 mmHg, HR 74 bpm, Temp 36.8C, BMI 30.\n\n**Cervical Spine and Upper Extremity Examination**:\n- **Inspection**: Mild loss of cervical lordosis. Forward head posture. Rounded shoulders. No visible atrophy.\n- **Cervical ROM**: Flexion 40 degrees (mildly limited), extension 30 degrees (moderately limited, reproduces arm pain), right lateral flexion 25 degrees (limited, painful), right rotation 45 degrees (limited, reproduces radiating arm pain). Left ROM relatively preserved.\n- **Palpation**: Paravertebral muscle spasm right > left at C5-C6 level. Tenderness over right C6 facet joint.\n\n**Provocative Tests**:\n- **Spurling test**: Positive on the right \u2014 axial compression with extension and right lateral flexion reproduces shooting pain into the right thumb. This is the hallmark exam finding for cervical radiculopathy (specificity 93%, sensitivity 30-50%).\n- **Shoulder abduction relief sign**: Positive \u2014 placing the right hand on top of the head reduces the radiating arm pain by relieving traction on the C6 nerve root.\n- **Neck distraction test**: Positive \u2014 manual axial distraction reduces arm pain.\n- **Lhermitte sign**: Negative (important \u2014 argues against myelopathy).\n\n**Neurologic Exam**:\n- **Motor**: Right biceps 4/5 (C5-C6), right wrist extensors (extensor carpi radialis) 4/5 (C6), right triceps 5/5 (C7), right grip 5/5. Left upper extremity 5/5 throughout. Lower extremities 5/5 bilaterally.\n- **Reflexes**: Right biceps (C5-C6) 1+ (diminished compared to left 2+). Right brachioradialis 1+. Triceps 2+ bilaterally. Patellar and Achilles reflexes 2+ bilaterally. No pathologic reflexes (Hoffman negative bilaterally, Babinski negative bilaterally).\n- **Sensation**: Decreased light touch and pinprick over the right lateral forearm and thumb (C6 dermatome). Intact in all other distributions.\n- **Coordination**: Intact finger-to-nose bilaterally. No upper extremity drift. Rapid alternating movements normal.\n- **Gait**: Normal. Tandem gait normal. No spasticity. (Myelopathy screening negative.)\n\n**Shoulder examination**: Full ROM bilaterally, negative impingement signs, negative rotator cuff weakness \u2014 important to rule out shoulder pathology masquerading as cervical radiculopathy.")
B.aT6=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The attending asks you to localize the nerve root level and discuss the diagnostic workup approach.")
B.aUd=new A.c("+ How do you localize this to a C6 radiculopathy, and when would you order imaging and electrodiagnostic studies?","**Localization to C6 Nerve Root**:\n\nThe clinical picture is consistent with a **right C6 radiculopathy** based on the convergence of:\n\n| Finding | C6 Root |\n|---|---|\n| Pain distribution | Lateral arm, lateral forearm, thumb, index finger |\n| Sensory deficit | Lateral forearm, thumb |\n| Motor weakness | Biceps (elbow flexion), wrist extensors (extensor carpi radialis) |\n| Reflex diminished | Biceps, brachioradialis |\n| Provocative test | Spurling reproduces C6 dermatomal pain |\n\n**Comparison with Adjacent Levels**:\n- **C5**: Lateral arm/deltoid. Deltoid weakness. Biceps reflex. No hand symptoms.\n- **C6**: Lateral forearm, thumb, index finger. Biceps and wrist extensor weakness. Biceps/brachioradialis reflex.\n- **C7**: Posterior arm/forearm, middle finger. Triceps and wrist flexor weakness. Triceps reflex.\n- **C8**: Medial forearm, ring/small fingers. Grip and intrinsic hand weakness. No reliable reflex.\n\n**Most likely etiology**: C5-C6 disc herniation (most common level for cervical radiculopathy) or C5-C6 foraminal stenosis from spondylosis.\n\n**Imaging Approach**:\n1. **Cervical radiographs (AP, lateral, oblique)**: Reasonable initial study. May show disc space narrowing, foraminal narrowing on oblique views, osteophytes, loss of lordosis. Cannot visualize disc herniation or neural compression.\n2. **MRI cervical spine WITHOUT contrast**: Gold standard for soft tissue visualization. Indicated when:\n   - Neurologic deficit is present (this patient has weakness and reflex change \u2014 MRI is indicated)\n   - Red flags are present\n   - Symptoms persist beyond 4-6 weeks of conservative management\n   - Surgical intervention is being considered\n   - **For this patient**: MRI is indicated NOW given objective neurologic deficit (weakness, reflex change) at 6 weeks duration.\n3. **CT myelogram**: Alternative if MRI is contraindicated (pacemaker, severe claustrophobia). Superior for bony foraminal stenosis.\n\n**Electrodiagnostic Studies (EMG/NCS)**:\n- **Timing**: NOT useful in the first 2-3 weeks. Fibrillation potentials take 2-3 weeks to develop after axonal injury. Optimal timing is **3-6 weeks** after symptom onset. This patient at 6 weeks is in the ideal window.\n- **Indications**: Confirm radiculopathy, localize level, assess severity (axonal loss), rule out peripheral neuropathy or other diagnoses (brachial plexopathy, median neuropathy/CTS).\n- **Expected NCS findings**: Typically normal sensory studies (the lesion is proximal to the DRG, so peripheral sensory fibers remain intact). Motor amplitudes may be reduced if significant axonal loss. Normal distal latencies and conduction velocities.\n- **Expected Needle EMG findings**: \n  - Fibrillation potentials and positive sharp waves in C6-innervated muscles (biceps, brachioradialis, pronator teres, extensor carpi radialis) AND the cervical paraspinal muscles\n  - Reduced recruitment in affected muscles\n  - Large polyphasic MUPs if chronic reinnervation is occurring\n  - Normal findings in non-C6 muscles (deltoid \u2014 C5, triceps \u2014 C7) helps confirm the level\n  - **Key**: Paraspinal fibrillations localize the lesion to the root level rather than a more distal plexus or peripheral nerve lesion.")
B.aMS=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","MRI confirms a right C5-C6 posterolateral disc herniation with moderate right C6 foraminal stenosis. The patient asks about treatment options.")
B.aZM=new A.c("+ Outline your conservative management plan, indications for epidural injection, and surgical referral criteria.","**Conservative Management (First-Line \u2014 resolves 80-90% of cases)**:\n\n**1. Pharmacologic Pain Management**:\n- **NSAIDs**: Continue meloxicam 15 mg daily (preferable to ibuprofen TID \u2014 once-daily dosing, better compliance, less GI risk). If insufficient, consider a 7-10 day course of a methylprednisolone dose pack (Medrol Dosepak) for acute radicular inflammation.\n- **Neuropathic pain agent**: Gabapentin 300 mg at bedtime, titrate by 300 mg every 3-5 days to 300 mg TID (target 900-1800 mg/day divided TID). Alternative: pregabalin 75 mg BID, titrate to 150 mg BID. Duloxetine 30-60 mg daily is another option with dual mechanism.\n- **Muscle relaxant**: Cyclobenzaprine 5-10 mg at bedtime for paravertebral spasm \u2014 short course (2-4 weeks). Avoid prolonged use.\n- **Topical**: Lidocaine 5% patch over the trapezius/paracervical region for local pain relief without systemic effects.\n- **Regarding opioid request** (addressed in Domain E): NOT indicated as first-line for cervical radiculopathy. Multimodal approach is far more effective and safer.\n\n**2. Physical Therapy**:\n- **Manual therapy**: Cervical traction (intermittent mechanical traction, 10-15 lbs, 15 minutes) \u2014 evidence supports traction for radiculopathy with foraminal stenosis. Opens the neuroforamen and reduces nerve root compression.\n- **Therapeutic exercise**: Cervical retraction exercises (chin tucks), deep neck flexor strengthening, scapular stabilization, upper thoracic extension mobility.\n- **Nerve gliding exercises**: Median nerve and C6 nerve root glides to reduce adhesions and improve neural mobility.\n- **Posture retraining**: Correct forward head posture and rounded shoulders. Ergonomic education.\n- **Modalities**: Heat, TENS for pain relief. Avoid cervical manipulation (thrust techniques) in the setting of disc herniation.\n- **Frequency**: 2-3 times per week for 6-8 weeks.\n\n**3. Activity Modifications**:\n- Avoid prolonged neck extension and ipsilateral rotation.\n- Cervical pillow for sleep (maintain neutral alignment).\n- Frequent position changes during computer work (every 30-45 minutes).\n\n**Epidural Steroid Injection (ESI) Indications**:\n- Persistent moderate-severe radicular pain despite 4-6 weeks of conservative management\n- Pain limiting participation in physical therapy\n- Patient prefers to avoid or delay surgery\n- **Approach**: Cervical transforaminal ESI at C5-C6 under fluoroscopic guidance is most targeted. Alternative: interlaminar approach. Use non-particulate steroid (dexamethasone) for transforaminal approach to minimize risk of particulate embolism.\n- **Expected outcomes**: 60-70% of patients report significant short-term relief (4-12 weeks). May be repeated up to 3 times per year.\n- **Risks to discuss**: Rare but serious \u2014 epidural hematoma, spinal cord injury, infection, dural puncture. Transient steroid effects (flushing, glucose elevation, insomnia).\n\n**Surgical Referral Criteria (Urgent or Elective)**:\n\n**Urgent surgical referral**:\n- Progressive neurologic deficit (worsening weakness)\n- Myelopathy signs (gait disturbance, bilateral hand dysfunction, hyperreflexia, Babinski)\n- Bowel/bladder dysfunction\n\n**Elective surgical referral**:\n- Failure of 6-12 weeks of comprehensive conservative management\n- Persistent significant pain and functional impairment\n- MRI-confirmed structural pathology correlating with clinical findings\n- Patient preference after informed discussion\n\n**Surgical options**: Anterior cervical discectomy and fusion (ACDF) is the standard for single-level disc herniation. Cervical disc arthroplasty is an alternative that preserves motion. Posterior foraminotomy for primarily foraminal pathology.")
B.aQx=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient works 10-12 hours per day at a computer. He asks about workplace modifications and whether he should take disability leave.")
B.aY6=new A.c("+ What ergonomic modifications would you recommend, and how do you approach disability documentation?","**Ergonomic Workplace Assessment and Modifications**:\n\n**Workstation Evaluation** (refer to occupational therapist or ergonomic consultant):\n1. **Monitor position**: Top of screen at eye level or slightly below. Distance approximately arm's length (20-26 inches). Position directly in front \u2014 avoid turning head to view monitor. Consider dual-monitor setup positioned symmetrically if using two screens.\n2. **Chair**: Adjustable height so feet are flat on floor, thighs parallel to ground. Lumbar support maintaining natural lordosis. Armrests at elbow height to reduce shoulder/trapezius strain.\n3. **Keyboard and mouse**: At elbow height, elbows at approximately 90 degrees. Wrists in neutral position. Consider ergonomic keyboard (split keyboard) and vertical mouse to reduce forearm pronation strain.\n4. **Document holder**: Positioned between keyboard and monitor to avoid repeated neck flexion/rotation when referencing papers.\n5. **Headset**: If frequent phone use, a headset eliminates neck cradling the phone.\n\n**Behavioral Modifications**:\n- **Micro-breaks**: Stand and move every 30-45 minutes. Set a timer. Perform cervical retraction exercises during breaks.\n- **20-20-20 rule**: Every 20 minutes, look at something 20 feet away for 20 seconds (reduces eye strain which contributes to forward head posture).\n- **Work hours**: 10-12 hour days are a significant contributing factor. Recommend reducing to 8 hours during active treatment. If not possible, ensure 2-3 extended breaks per day.\n- **Standing desk**: Alternating sit-stand desk allows position changes. Aim for 30-50% standing time.\n\n**Disability Documentation**:\n\n**Approach \u2014 Modified Duty First**:\n- Most patients with cervical radiculopathy can continue working with modifications rather than full disability leave.\n- Document specific restrictions: No lifting >10 lbs with right arm. Avoid sustained overhead work. Ergonomic workstation required. Limit continuous computer work to 45-minute intervals with breaks.\n\n**If Modified Duty is Insufficient**:\n- Short-term disability may be appropriate during acute flare or post-procedure (ESI or surgery).\n- Duration: Typically 1-2 weeks for ESI recovery. 4-6 weeks for ACDF surgery.\n- Documentation requirements: Objective findings (exam, MRI, EMG), functional limitations, specific job demands that cannot be met, expected duration of disability, treatment plan.\n\n**Return-to-Work Planning**:\n- Graduated return: Start at reduced hours (4-6 hours) and increase by 2 hours per week.\n- Ergonomic modifications must be in place BEFORE returning to full duties.\n- Follow-up work capacity assessment at 4 and 8 weeks after return.\n- If permanent restrictions are needed (rare with radiculopathy), vocational counseling for job modification or role change.")
B.b2u=new A.c(u.j,"During the visit, the patient becomes increasingly insistent about pain medication. He says: 'Doctor, I really need something stronger. The ibuprofen does nothing. I haven't slept in weeks. My friend gave me some of his Percocet and it was the only thing that helped. Can't you just prescribe me a small amount?'")
B.aPD=new A.c("+ Demonstrate how you would address this patient's request for opioids while maintaining the therapeutic relationship and implementing a multimodal pain management strategy.","I would approach this with empathy, clear boundaries, and a strong alternative plan:\n\n**1. Validate the pain experience**:\n'I believe you when you say your pain is severe. Not sleeping for weeks, not being able to work comfortably \u2014 that is miserable, and I take it seriously. I want to get your pain under control, and I have a plan to do that.'\n\n**2. Educate about why opioids are not the right choice here**:\n'I understand the Percocet helped, and that makes sense \u2014 opioids are powerful pain relievers. But here is why they are not the right treatment for your condition: Cervical radiculopathy pain is primarily nerve pain \u2014 burning, shooting, electrical. Opioids are actually poor at treating nerve pain. They mask the sensation temporarily but do not address the underlying nerve inflammation. More importantly, opioids have significant risks \u2014 dependence can develop in as little as 5-7 days of regular use, they cause sedation that would impair your work as a software engineer, and they can actually make chronic pain worse over time through a process called opioid-induced hyperalgesia.'\n\n**3. Present the multimodal alternative with confidence**:\n'Here is what I want to do instead, and I am confident this will be more effective:\n- **Tonight**: I am starting you on gabapentin, which specifically targets nerve pain. It also helps with sleep \u2014 many patients notice improved sleep within the first few nights.\n- **This week**: I am prescribing a short course of an oral steroid (Medrol Dosepak) to reduce the inflammation around the compressed nerve root. Many patients get significant relief within 48-72 hours.\n- **For muscle spasm**: Cyclobenzaprine at bedtime will address the muscle tightness AND help you sleep.\n- **This month**: I am referring you for physical therapy with cervical traction, which physically opens the space where the nerve is being compressed.\n- **If needed**: A cervical epidural steroid injection can deliver anti-inflammatory medication directly to the compressed nerve root \u2014 this is the most targeted treatment available.'\n\n**4. Address the borrowed medication directly but without judgment**:\n'I appreciate you being honest with me about taking your friend's medication. I am not here to judge you \u2014 you were desperate for relief. However, I do need to let you know that taking someone else's prescription medication is unsafe because it was not prescribed for you, your medical history, or your other medications. Going forward, I need you to take only medications that I or your other doctors prescribe.'\n\n**5. Set expectations and follow-up**:\n'I want to see you back in 2 weeks so we can assess how the gabapentin and other treatments are working. If we are not getting adequate pain control, I will escalate the treatment \u2014 but with targeted interventions, not with opioids. My goal is to treat the cause of your pain, not just cover it up. Does this plan make sense to you?'\n\n**6. If the patient continues to insist**:\n'I hear your frustration, and I respect that you are asking for what you think will help. But prescribing opioids for this condition would not be good medicine \u2014 it would be doing you a disservice. I would rather give you treatments that actually work for nerve pain than prescribe something that provides temporary relief but creates new problems. Let us give this plan 2 weeks, and if you are still in significant pain, we will adjust \u2014 but I will always be steering you toward the safest and most effective options.'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Empathy without capitulation, evidence-based education, comprehensive multimodal alternative, non-judgmental response to borrowed medication, clear boundaries with maintained therapeutic alliance, specific follow-up plan.")
B.are=s([B.aX3,B.aTE,B.b25,B.aT6,B.aUd,B.aMS,B.aZM,B.aQx,B.aY6,B.b2u,B.aPD],t.E)
B.Qh=new A.a5("msk-cervical-radiculopathy-c6","Cervical Radiculopathy (C6)",B.are,!1)
B.aWf=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 40-year-old right-hand-dominant female graphic designer presents to the PM&R clinic with an 8-month history of chronic right-sided neck and shoulder pain. She describes a constant deep ache in her right upper trapezius and periscapular region with intermittent sharp pain that "shoots" up the right side of her neck into the base of her skull, causing tension-type headaches 3-4 times per week. The pain worsens throughout the workday and with stress. She works 8-10 hours per day at a computer using a drawing tablet. She has tried ibuprofen, heating pads, and a brief course of physical therapy (6 visits) without lasting improvement. She is convinced she has "a pinched nerve" and has been requesting an MRI from her primary care physician, who referred her to PM&R instead.')
B.aXu=new A.c("History & Systems Review:",'Key areas to assess:\n- **Pain characteristics**: Location (right upper trapezius, levator scapulae, suboccipital region). Quality (deep ache with referred pain pattern). Temporal pattern (worse with sustained postures, stress, and by end of day). Aggravating factors (computer work, emotional stress, cold exposure, poor sleep). Relieving factors (heat, massage, position change, weekends/vacations).\n- **Referred pain pattern**: Upper trapezius trigger points classically refer pain to the posterolateral neck, temporal region, and behind the eye \u2014 "question mark" pattern. Does her headache pattern match this?\n- **Sleep quality**: Poor sleep is both a consequence and perpetuating factor of MPS. Assess sleep position, quality, and duration. Non-restorative sleep is common.\n- **Psychosocial assessment**: Stress level, anxiety (GAD-7), depression (PHQ-9), work satisfaction, perfectionism traits, catastrophizing (Pain Catastrophizing Scale). These are critical perpetuating factors.\n- **Widespread pain screening**: Does she have pain in other body regions? More than 3 months? Widespread Pain Index and Symptom Severity Scale \u2014 screen for fibromyalgia if pain is diffuse.\n- **Prior PT details**: What was done in the 6 sessions? Was trigger point therapy specifically addressed? Was postural correction included? Brief, non-specific PT is often inadequate for MPS.\n- **Ergonomic history**: Workstation setup, breaks, posture habits, use of drawing tablet (asymmetric arm positioning).\n- **Exercise habits**: Current activity level, fitness, stretching routine.\n- **Cervical spine symptoms**: Any radicular symptoms (numbness, tingling, weakness in a dermatomal pattern)? Important to differentiate from radiculopathy.')
B.aRy=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 122/76 mmHg, HR 68 bpm, Temp 36.8C, BMI 23.\n\n**Postural Assessment**:\n- Forward head posture (ear anterior to shoulder line by approximately 3 cm)\n- Rounded shoulders bilaterally, right worse than left\n- Mild right scapular protraction with winging\n- Right shoulder elevated approximately 1 cm compared to left (upper trapezius hypertonicity)\n\n**Cervical Spine Examination**:\n- **ROM**: Flexion 45 degrees (normal), extension 50 degrees (normal), right lateral flexion 30 degrees (mildly limited by tightness, not pain), left lateral flexion 40 degrees (normal), rotation symmetric at 70 degrees bilaterally.\n- **Spurling test**: Negative bilaterally \u2014 no radicular symptoms provoked.\n- **Lhermitte sign**: Negative.\n\n**Trigger Point Examination** (the key diagnostic finding):\n- **Right upper trapezius**: Palpable taut band with a discrete, exquisitely tender **trigger point** (TrP) in the mid-belly of the muscle. Firm sustained pressure reproduces her typical neck pain AND her headache pattern (referred pain to the right temporal region). Local twitch response elicited on snapping palpation.\n- **Right levator scapulae**: Active TrP at the superior angle of the scapula. Referred pain to the right lateral neck.\n- **Right suboccipital muscles**: Active TrP in the right rectus capitis posterior major. Referred pain to the right occipital region \u2014 consistent with her tension headache pattern.\n- **Right infraspinatus**: Latent TrP (tender on palpation but does not spontaneously cause pain).\n- **Left upper trapezius**: Mild taut bands without active TrPs.\n\n**Neurologic Exam**:\n- **Motor**: 5/5 throughout bilateral upper and lower extremities. No weakness in any myotomal distribution.\n- **Reflexes**: 2+ and symmetric throughout (biceps, triceps, brachioradialis, patellar, Achilles). No pathologic reflexes.\n- **Sensation**: Intact to light touch, pinprick, and vibration in all dermatomes. No dermatomal sensory loss.\n\n**Shoulder exam**: Full ROM bilaterally. Negative impingement signs. Rotator cuff strength 5/5 bilaterally.")
B.aZB=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The attending asks you to confirm the diagnosis and differentiate myofascial pain syndrome from other conditions.")
B.aY3=new A.c("+ How do you differentiate myofascial pain syndrome from fibromyalgia and cervical radiculopathy? What are the diagnostic criteria for MPS?","**Myofascial Pain Syndrome (MPS) Diagnostic Features**:\n\nMPS is a clinical diagnosis based on physical examination findings. There is no confirmatory blood test or imaging study. The key diagnostic features are:\n\n1. **Taut band**: A palpable, ropy, tense band of muscle fibers within a skeletal muscle\n2. **Trigger point (TrP)**: A hyperirritable spot within the taut band that is painful on compression\n3. **Referred pain pattern**: Pressure on the TrP reproduces the patient's recognized pain pattern in a predictable, non-dermatomal distribution\n4. **Local twitch response**: A visible or palpable brief contraction of the taut band when the TrP is snapped or needled\n5. **Restricted ROM**: Due to taut band shortening of the muscle\n\n**Active vs. Latent Trigger Points**:\n- **Active TrP**: Spontaneously painful and reproduces the patient's clinical pain complaint. Causes referred pain at rest or with movement.\n- **Latent TrP**: Painful only when palpated. Does not cause spontaneous pain but may restrict ROM and cause weakness. Can become active under stress.\n\n**Differentiating MPS from Fibromyalgia**:\n\n| Feature | Myofascial Pain Syndrome | Fibromyalgia |\n|---|---|---|\n| Pain distribution | Regional (one or few muscles) | Widespread (all four body quadrants) |\n| Key finding | Trigger points in taut bands | Widespread allodynia, tender points (though 2010 criteria use WPI/SSS instead) |\n| Referred pain | Yes \u2014 specific patterns | Not a primary feature |\n| Twitch response | Present | Absent |\n| Fatigue | Mild, localized | Severe, central |\n| Sleep disturbance | Secondary to pain | Primary (non-restorative sleep is a core feature) |\n| Cognitive symptoms | Minimal | \"Fibro fog\" \u2014 prominent |\n| Central sensitization | Mild/absent | Prominent |\n| Prognosis | Usually resolves with treatment | Chronic, waxing-waning |\n\n**However**: MPS and fibromyalgia can coexist. Chronic untreated MPS with persistent nociceptive input may lead to central sensitization and evolve into fibromyalgia in susceptible individuals.\n\n**Differentiating MPS from Cervical Radiculopathy**:\n\n| Feature | Myofascial Pain Syndrome | Cervical Radiculopathy |\n|---|---|---|\n| Pain pattern | Non-dermatomal, follows TrP referral maps | Dermatomal (follows specific nerve root) |\n| Numbness/tingling | Absent | Present in dermatomal distribution |\n| Weakness | Muscle shortening/guarding (not true neurologic weakness) | True motor weakness in myotomal pattern |\n| Reflexes | Normal | Diminished at involved level |\n| Spurling test | Negative | Positive |\n| EMG/NCS | Normal | Abnormal (fibrillations, reduced recruitment) |\n| Exam finding | Trigger points with referred pain | Radicular signs (weakness, sensory loss, reflex change) |\n\n**This patient**: Regional pain (right neck/shoulder), trigger points with characteristic referred pain patterns reproducing her symptoms, normal neurologic exam, negative Spurling test \u2014 classic MPS, not radiculopathy. An MRI is NOT indicated.\n\n**When WOULD imaging be indicated in MPS patients**:\n- Neurologic deficits on exam\n- Positive Spurling or radicular signs\n- Red flags (trauma, cancer history, progressive symptoms)\n- Failure to respond to 8-12 weeks of appropriate MPS-targeted treatment")
B.aMC=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You confirm the diagnosis of myofascial pain syndrome and develop a comprehensive treatment plan.")
B.aVm=new A.c("+ Outline your treatment plan including trigger point interventions, physical therapy, and stress management.",'**Comprehensive MPS Treatment Plan**:\n\n**1. Trigger Point Interventions (In-Office)**:\n\n**Trigger Point Injection (TPI)**:\n- **Technique**: Identify TrP by palpation. Insert 25-gauge needle into the TrP using a fast-in, fan-out technique to elicit local twitch responses. Inject 1-2 mL per TrP site.\n- **Injectate options**:\n  - **Lidocaine 1% (without epinephrine)**: Most commonly used. Provides immediate pain relief and breaks the pain cycle. Low risk.\n  - **Bupivacaine 0.25%**: Longer-acting local anesthetic.\n  - **Botulinum toxin type A**: Reserved for refractory cases. Provides longer-lasting relief (3-4 months) by reducing muscle contracture. Not first-line due to cost.\n- **Today\'s plan**: Inject the right upper trapezius and right levator scapulae active TrPs with 1% lidocaine. Treat 2-3 TrPs per session maximum.\n\n**Dry Needling**:\n- Insertion of a solid filament needle (acupuncture needle) into the TrP without injectate\n- Elicits local twitch responses that release the taut band\n- Evidence suggests dry needling is equally effective to wet needling for many patients\n- Often performed by specially trained physical therapists (scope varies by state)\n\n**Post-injection protocol**: Apply moist heat or ice. Perform gentle stretching of the treated muscle immediately. Avoid strenuous use of the muscle for 24-48 hours. TPI can be repeated every 2-4 weeks for 3-4 sessions.\n\n**2. Physical Therapy Program (MPS-Specific)**:\n- **Manual therapy**: Myofascial release, ischemic compression of TrPs, strain-counterstrain technique.\n- **Stretching**: Upper trapezius stretch (lateral neck flexion with scapular depression), levator scapulae stretch (chin toward axilla), suboccipital release stretches. Hold 30 seconds, 3-5 repetitions, 3 times daily.\n- **Postural correction**: Chin tuck exercises (deep neck flexor strengthening), scapular retraction and depression exercises, thoracic extension mobilization. Address the forward head posture and rounded shoulders.\n- **Strengthening**: Lower and middle trapezius strengthening (prone Y, T, W exercises), serratus anterior activation, deep neck flexor endurance training. The goal is to shift the load away from the overactive upper trapezius.\n- **Aerobic exercise**: 30 minutes of moderate aerobic exercise 5 days per week. Walking, cycling, or swimming. Aerobic exercise raises endorphin levels, improves sleep, and reduces central pain sensitization.\n- **Frequency**: 2 times per week for 8-12 weeks with emphasis on daily home exercise program.\n\n**3. Stress Management (Critical Perpetuating Factor)**:\n- **Cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT)**: Address catastrophizing, fear-avoidance beliefs, and pain-related anxiety. Reframe her belief that she has a "pinched nerve" (see Domain E).\n- **Relaxation techniques**: Progressive muscle relaxation (PMR), diaphragmatic breathing, mindfulness-based stress reduction (MBSR).\n- **Biofeedback**: Surface EMG biofeedback can teach the patient to reduce upper trapezius muscle tension during rest and work. Highly effective for MPS.\n- **Sleep hygiene**: Consistent sleep/wake times, cool dark room, no screens 1 hour before bed, limit caffeine after noon. If sleep remains poor, consider low-dose amitriptyline (10-25 mg at bedtime) which also has analgesic properties for chronic pain.\n\n**4. Pharmacologic Adjuncts**:\n- **Topical therapy**: Diclofenac gel 1% applied to the upper trapezius 4 times daily. Minimal systemic absorption.\n- **Muscle relaxant**: Tizanidine 2-4 mg at bedtime \u2014 reduces muscle tone centrally and promotes sleep. Preferable to cyclobenzaprine for chronic use.\n- **Low-dose tricyclic**: Amitriptyline 10-25 mg at bedtime if sleep disruption and daily headaches are prominent. Addresses pain, sleep, and headache simultaneously.\n- **Avoid**: Chronic NSAID use (GI, renal, cardiovascular risks), opioids (not effective for MPS, risk of dependence), benzodiazepines.\n\n**5. Self-Management Tools**:\n- Foam roller and tennis ball for self-myofascial release\n- Theracane or similar self-massage device for reaching upper trapezius\n- Hot pack or microwavable heat wrap for home use\n- Stretching app or video for daily guided stretching routine')
B.b3k=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient works long hours at a computer with a drawing tablet. You discuss workplace modifications and the broader biopsychosocial approach to her chronic pain.")
B.b_e=new A.c("+ How would you implement ergonomic modifications and a biopsychosocial model for this patient's management?",'**Ergonomic Consultation**:\n\n1. **Referral**: Request a formal ergonomic assessment of her workstation through occupational therapy or a certified ergonomic consultant. Many employers will cover this, especially if documented as medically necessary.\n\n2. **Drawing Tablet-Specific Modifications**:\n   - Position the drawing tablet directly in front of her at desk height (not off to one side \u2014 asymmetric positioning creates unilateral trapezius overload).\n   - Tilt the tablet at a slight angle using a tablet stand to reduce neck flexion.\n   - Alternate between tablet and mouse/keyboard tasks to reduce sustained static postures.\n   - Consider a larger tablet that allows elbow-driven movements rather than wrist-only movements.\n\n3. **General Workstation Modifications**:\n   - Monitor at eye level, directly in front\n   - Chair with adequate lumbar support and adjustable armrests\n   - Standing desk option for postural variation (alternate sitting and standing every 30-45 minutes)\n   - Headset for phone use\n   - Document holder at monitor level\n\n4. **Behavioral Workplace Interventions**:\n   - Timed micro-breaks every 30 minutes (stretch, walk, change position)\n   - Workplace stretching program posted at her desk\n   - Stress awareness \u2014 recognize when trapezius is tensing during stressful tasks and consciously relax\n\n**Biopsychosocial Model Implementation**:\n\nChronic MPS is a prototypical biopsychosocial condition where biological, psychological, and social factors all perpetuate the pain cycle. A purely biomedical approach (injections alone) will fail without addressing all three domains.\n\n**Biological Factors**:\n- Peripheral muscle dysfunction (trigger points, taut bands)\n- Postural biomechanics (forward head posture, scapular dyskinesis)\n- Physical deconditioning\n- Sleep disruption\n- Treatment: TPI, PT, exercise, ergonomics, pharmacology\n\n**Psychological Factors**:\n- Pain catastrophizing ("I have a pinched nerve \u2014 something is seriously wrong")\n- Fear-avoidance beliefs (avoiding movement for fear of worsening damage)\n- Anxiety and work-related stress\n- Perfectionism traits (common in graphic designers \u2014 may resist taking breaks)\n- Loss of self-efficacy (feeling helpless about the pain)\n- Treatment: CBT, pain neuroscience education, relaxation training, biofeedback, sports psychology or pain psychology referral\n\n**Social Factors**:\n- Work demands (long hours, deadlines, sustained postures)\n- Work culture (is taking breaks acceptable? supportive supervisor?)\n- Financial pressure to work through pain\n- Reduced social and recreational activities due to pain\n- Treatment: Ergonomic modifications, workplace advocacy, activity pacing, social reconnection\n\n**Interdisciplinary Team**:\n- PM&R physician: Overall management, injections, medical coordination\n- Physical therapist: Manual therapy, exercise prescription, postural correction\n- Psychologist: CBT, stress management, biofeedback\n- Occupational therapist: Ergonomic assessment, activity modification\n- Primary care: Coordinate general health, diabetes screening (BMI 23 is fine here, but address general wellness)\n\n**Outcome Measures to Track**:\n- Visual Analog Scale (VAS) for pain\n- Neck Disability Index (NDI)\n- Headache frequency diary\n- PHQ-9 and GAD-7 for mood\n- Sleep quality (Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index)\n- Work productivity measures\n- Patient-Specific Functional Scale (PSFS)')
B.aVy=new A.c(u.j,"After your examination, you explain the diagnosis of myofascial pain syndrome. The patient looks skeptical and says: 'With all due respect, I feel like you are not taking this seriously. I have real pain \u2014 I can barely work. My friend had similar pain and it turned out to be a herniated disc. I really think I need an MRI. Are you sure this is just muscle pain?'")
B.aXd=new A.c("+ Demonstrate how you would redirect this patient's expectations about her diagnosis while validating her concerns.","I would respond with respect for her perspective while firmly and kindly educating:\n\n**1. Validate her pain and her concern**:\n'I want to be very clear about something \u2014 I am taking your pain extremely seriously. Eight months of pain that is affecting your ability to work and causing headaches multiple times per week is significant, and you deserve a thorough evaluation and effective treatment. That is exactly what we are doing today.'\n\n**2. Demonstrate the evidence from the exam**:\n'Let me show you what I found on your exam and why I am confident in this diagnosis. When I pressed on this spot in your trapezius muscle \u2014 right here \u2014 it reproduced the exact pain you have been experiencing, including the headache. That is called a trigger point, and the fact that pressing on a specific muscle point recreated your typical pain pattern tells us where the pain generator is. Your neurologic exam is completely normal \u2014 your reflexes are symmetric, your strength is full, you have no numbness or tingling in a nerve pattern, and the Spurling test that specifically provokes pinched nerves was negative. If you had a pinched nerve, I would expect to find abnormalities in at least one of those areas.'\n\n**3. Reframe \"just muscle pain\" respectfully**:\n'I also want to address the idea that this is \"just\" muscle pain. Myofascial pain syndrome is a legitimate, well-studied medical condition \u2014 it is not the same as a simple muscle strain that goes away in a few days. Trigger points involve dysfunctional muscle tissue that develops its own cycle of pain, spasm, and referred symptoms. The headaches you are having are caused by these trigger points referring pain to your head. This is real pathology, and it requires real treatment.'\n\n**4. Address the MRI request directly**:\n'Regarding the MRI \u2014 I completely understand why you want one. An MRI feels like a definitive answer, and your friend's experience makes you worry you might have the same thing. Here is my honest assessment: based on your normal neurologic exam, negative Spurling test, and the fact that your pain is clearly reproduced by trigger point palpation rather than nerve provocation, an MRI right now would very likely be normal \u2014 or it might show common age-related findings that look abnormal but are not causing your symptoms. In fact, studies show that 30-40% of people your age with NO neck pain have disc herniations on MRI. An incidental finding could actually lead us down the wrong treatment path.'\n\n**5. Offer a conditional plan**:\n'Here is what I propose: let us treat the condition we have identified with targeted interventions \u2014 trigger point injections, proper physical therapy focused on your specific trigger points and posture, ergonomic changes, and stress management. If after 8-12 weeks of this comprehensive approach you are not significantly better, I will absolutely order an MRI at that point. That is not dismissing your concern \u2014 it is applying a logical diagnostic and treatment sequence.'\n\n**6. Empower her with understanding**:\n'I would actually like you to learn more about myofascial pain syndrome. When you understand the mechanism \u2014 how these trigger points develop from sustained postures and stress, and how they refer pain in predictable patterns \u2014 it often gives patients a sense of control over their condition. Unlike a disc herniation where you might feel helpless, MPS is a condition where your active participation in stretching, posture correction, stress management, and ergonomics can make a dramatic difference.'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Validating pain severity, demonstrating exam findings as evidence, reframing \"muscle pain\" as legitimate pathology, educating about MRI limitations, offering a conditional plan that honors her concern, empowering through knowledge, avoiding defensive posturing.")
B.aDE=s([B.aWf,B.aXu,B.aRy,B.aZB,B.aY3,B.aMC,B.aVm,B.b3k,B.b_e,B.aVy,B.aXd],t.E)
B.QX=new A.a5("msk-myofascial-pain-syndrome","Myofascial Pain Syndrome (Upper Trapezius)",B.aDE,!1)
B.aT3=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 68-year-old right-handed retired firefighter presents to the PM&R clinic with a 2-year history of progressive bilateral leg pain and heaviness that worsens with walking and prolonged standing. He describes the pain as a deep aching and burning sensation in both buttocks, thighs, and calves that begins after walking approximately 1-2 blocks and forces him to stop and sit down or lean forward on a shopping cart for relief. He notes that walking uphill is easier than walking downhill and that riding a stationary bicycle is painless. He has tried over-the-counter NSAIDs with minimal relief. He also reports mild low back pain but emphasizes that the leg symptoms are far more bothersome. His PCP ordered an MRI showing moderate-to-severe central canal stenosis at L3-4 and L4-5 with ligamentum flavum hypertrophy and facet arthropathy. He is a Jehovah\'s Witness and states upfront: "My faith does not allow blood transfusions, and I need you to respect that no matter what treatment we discuss." He is accompanied by his wife.')
B.aOm=new A.c("History & Systems Review:",'Key areas to assess:\n- **Claudication characterization**: Onset distance (how far can he walk before symptoms start), maximum walking distance, time to symptom relief with sitting (neurogenic claudication typically resolves within minutes of sitting or flexion). Positional relief pattern \u2014 does leaning on a shopping cart (flexed posture) extend walking distance? This is the classic "shopping cart sign."\n- **Neurogenic vs vascular claudication**: Critical differential. Ask about peripheral vascular disease risk factors (smoking history as a firefighter, hypertension, diabetes, hyperlipidemia). Palpate peripheral pulses. Key distinguishing features:\n  - Neurogenic: Relief with flexion (sitting, leaning forward), worse walking downhill (extension), bicycle riding painless, pulses present\n  - Vascular: Relief with standing still (does not require sitting), worse walking uphill, bicycle riding also painful, pulses diminished\n- **Red flag symptoms**: Saddle anesthesia, bowel/bladder dysfunction (cauda equina syndrome), progressive neurologic deficit, fever, unexplained weight loss, history of cancer.\n- **Back pain component**: Mechanical vs inflammatory pattern. Worse with extension (stenosis) vs flexion (disc). Morning stiffness duration.\n- **Neurologic symptoms**: Numbness, tingling, weakness in specific dermatomal/myotomal patterns. Foot drop. Balance changes.\n- **Functional impact**: Walking tolerance, stairs, community mobility, recreational activities, sleep disruption. Swiss Spinal Stenosis Questionnaire.\n- **Prior treatments**: PT (what type \u2014 flexion-based vs extension-based matters), injections, chiropractic, medications.\n- **Surgical history**: Any prior lumbar surgery. This affects surgical planning if surgery becomes indicated.\n- **Religious beliefs and treatment boundaries**: Jehovah\'s Witness faith \u2014 blood transfusion refusal is a sincere, protected religious belief. This has important implications for surgical planning and informed consent.')
B.aPn=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 142/86 mmHg, HR 74 bpm, RR 14, Weight 198 lbs, BMI 28.5.\n\n**General**: Well-developed male, walks into clinic with mildly antalgic gait. No acute distress at rest.\n\n**Lumbar Spine**:\n- Inspection: Mild loss of lumbar lordosis. No scoliosis.\n- ROM: Extension limited to 10 degrees and reproduces bilateral buttock and posterior thigh pain. Flexion 60 degrees with mild LBP only. Lateral bending mildly limited bilaterally.\n- Palpation: Tenderness over L3-5 spinous processes and bilateral paraspinal muscles. Facet loading sign positive bilaterally.\n\n**Neurologic Exam**:\n- **Motor**: Bilateral hip flexion 5/5, knee extension 5/5, ankle dorsiflexion 4+/5 bilaterally (subtle weakness), EHL 4+/5 bilaterally, ankle plantarflexion 5/5. Single heel raise: can perform bilaterally but fatigues after 5 repetitions (abnormal for age).\n- **Sensory**: Mildly reduced light touch sensation in bilateral L5 dermatomes (dorsal foot). Intact L4 and S1 dermatomes.\n- **Reflexes**: Patellar 2+ bilaterally, Achilles 1+ bilaterally (diminished). No pathologic reflexes. Babinski absent bilaterally.\n- **Straight Leg Raise**: Negative bilaterally (SLR tests nerve root tension from disc herniation \u2014 expected to be negative in stenosis).\n- **Provocative testing**: **Stoop test**: Patient walks until symptoms reproduce (approximately 3 minutes of walking in the hallway), then flexes forward \u2014 symptoms improve within 30 seconds. This is pathognomonic for neurogenic claudication.\n\n**Vascular Exam**: Dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses 2+ bilaterally. No femoral bruits. Ankle-brachial index (ABI) 1.1 bilaterally (normal >0.9). Skin warm, no trophic changes. Hair distribution normal.\n\n**Hip Exam**: Full ROM bilaterally. No groin pain with flexion and internal rotation (FADIR negative). Hip pathology excluded as a pain generator.\n\n**MRI Findings (reviewed)**: Moderate-to-severe central canal stenosis at L3-4 and L4-5 secondary to ligamentum flavum hypertrophy, facet arthropathy with synovial cysts, and mild disc bulging. Thecal sac AP diameter <10 mm at both levels (moderate stenosis 10-12 mm, severe <10 mm). No disc herniation. Mild foraminal stenosis bilaterally at L4-5. Conus terminates at L1 level (normal).")
B.aWo=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The attending asks you to explain the pathophysiology of neurogenic claudication, the role of EMG in lumbar stenosis, and how you distinguish this from other causes of leg pain with walking.")
B.aSn=new A.c("+ Explain the pathophysiology of neurogenic claudication, when EMG is indicated, and the complete differential diagnosis.","**Pathophysiology of Neurogenic Claudication**:\n\nNeurogenic claudication results from dynamic compression of the cauda equina nerve roots within a narrowed spinal canal. The mechanism involves:\n\n1. **Positional stenosis**: Lumbar extension narrows the central canal by 9-15% (thickening of ligamentum flavum, facet override, disc bulging posteriorly). Flexion opens the canal. This is why walking downhill (extension) is worse and cycling (flexion) is painless.\n2. **Venous congestion hypothesis**: Canal stenosis compresses epidural veins, causing venous congestion and impaired arterial inflow to nerve roots. With walking (which increases neural metabolic demand), the nerve roots become ischemic. This explains the exercise-induced symptom onset and rest-related relief.\n3. **Mechanical compression**: Direct mechanical compression of nerve roots causes demyelination and axonal injury, producing the neurologic deficits (weakness, numbness).\n4. **Inflammatory component**: Stenosis creates a local inflammatory environment with elevated levels of IL-6, TNF-alpha, and substance P around compressed nerve roots.\n\n**Role of EMG/NCS in Lumbar Stenosis**:\n\nEMG is NOT required for diagnosis when clinical presentation and MRI are concordant. However, EMG is indicated when:\n1. **Clinical-radiographic mismatch**: Symptoms do not correlate with MRI findings (e.g., MRI shows mild stenosis but symptoms are severe, or MRI shows multilevel stenosis but clinical picture suggests a specific level)\n2. **Peripheral neuropathy coexists**: Common in this age group \u2014 diabetic neuropathy or other polyneuropathies can mimic or coexist with stenosis. EMG helps distinguish radiculopathy from neuropathy.\n3. **Surgical planning**: To identify which levels are clinically significant when multilevel stenosis is present\n4. **Baseline documentation**: Before intervention, to measure against post-treatment results\n\n**Expected EMG findings in lumbar stenosis**:\n- Needle EMG: Denervation (fibrillation potentials, positive sharp waves) in a multi-root distribution (L3-S1 myotomes) \u2014 polyradiculopathy pattern\n- Paraspinal fibrillations are common\n- Nerve conduction studies: May show reduced CMAP amplitudes in affected motor nerves (peroneal, tibial). H-reflex may be prolonged or absent bilaterally. **Normal sensory NCS helps distinguish from peripheral neuropathy (where SNAPs would be reduced).**\n\n**Complete Differential Diagnosis of Leg Pain with Walking**:\n\n1. **Vascular claudication**: Peripheral arterial disease. Key differences from neurogenic: pain with uphill walking (not downhill), relief with standing still (does not require sitting/flexion), worsened by cycling, diminished pulses, abnormal ABI (<0.9). This patient's normal ABI and pulses exclude vascular claudication.\n2. **Hip osteoarthritis**: Groin pain with walking, limited ROM (especially internal rotation), pain reproduced by FADIR test. Excluded by normal hip exam.\n3. **Trochanteric bursitis / gluteal tendinopathy**: Lateral hip pain, worse with side-lying, positive lateral hip tenderness.\n4. **Peripheral neuropathy**: Symmetric stocking-glove sensory loss, burning/tingling, not positional, not relieved by flexion. EMG/NCS distinguishes.\n5. **Chronic exertional compartment syndrome**: Younger patients, exercise-induced anterior leg tightness and pain, resolves with rest, compartment pressure testing diagnostic.\n6. **Lumbar disc herniation**: Usually unilateral radicular pain in a single dermatome, positive SLR, typically younger patients. MRI shows disc herniation rather than canal stenosis.\n7. **Spondylolisthesis**: Can cause stenosis (degenerative spondylolisthesis, especially L4-5). Standing lateral X-ray assesses stability.\n8. **Cauda equina syndrome**: Acute emergency \u2014 saddle anesthesia, urinary retention, bilateral leg weakness. Must be excluded if acute neurologic changes occur.")
B.b2y=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Develop the conservative and interventional management plan, including discussion of when surgical decompression is indicated.")
B.aPi=new A.c("+ Outline the conservative treatment pathway, epidural injection approach, and criteria for surgical referral.","**Conservative Management \u2014 First-Line Approach**:\n\n**Physical Therapy (Flexion-Based Program)**:\n- **Flexion-based exercises are the cornerstone**: Williams flexion exercises, posterior pelvic tilts, knee-to-chest stretches, lumbar flexion mobilizations. These open the spinal canal and reduce nerve root compression.\n- **Avoid extension-based programs**: McKenzie extension exercises (used for disc herniation) will WORSEN stenosis symptoms.\n- **Aerobic conditioning**: Stationary bicycle (flexion-based, painless for this patient), aquatic therapy (buoyancy offloads spine, water resistance provides conditioning), recumbent elliptical\n- **Core stabilization**: Abdominal bracing, transversus abdominis activation, multifidus training \u2014 in a neutral-to-flexion bias\n- **Gait training**: Walking with a rollator walker with forearm supports maintains slight lumbar flexion during ambulation and may extend walking distance\n- **Neural mobilization**: Gentle nerve gliding exercises for the lower extremities\n- **Duration**: 6-12 weeks of supervised PT before reassessment\n\n**Pharmacologic Management**:\n- **NSAIDs**: Trial of prescription-strength (meloxicam 15 mg daily or naproxen 500 mg BID) \u2014 he has only tried OTC doses. GI protection with PPI given his age.\n- **Gabapentin or pregabalin**: For neuropathic component (burning, tingling). Start gabapentin 300 mg at bedtime, titrate to 300 mg TID or higher as tolerated. Evidence is mixed for stenosis but reasonable for neuropathic symptoms.\n- **Calcitonin**: Limited evidence for neurogenic claudication. Some studies show modest benefit. Can be considered as adjunct.\n- **Acetaminophen**: Adjunctive for mild pain. Avoid opioids.\n- **Muscle relaxants**: Short-term if significant paraspinal spasm contributes.\n\n**Epidural Steroid Injections (ESIs)**:\n- **Approach**: Interlaminar or transforaminal epidural injection at L3-4 and/or L4-5 under fluoroscopic guidance\n- **For central stenosis**: Interlaminar approach delivers medication to the central canal where the compression is located\n- **Evidence**: Moderate evidence for short-to-intermediate-term pain relief (weeks to months) in lumbar stenosis. Does NOT change the natural history of stenosis. Role is primarily as a bridge \u2014 to improve function enough for effective PT participation, or to temporize while deciding on surgery.\n- **Protocol**: Reasonable to try a series of up to 3 injections over 6-12 months. If no benefit after 2 injections, additional injections are unlikely to help.\n- **Risks**: Infection, bleeding, dural puncture, transient worsening, rare neurologic injury. In this patient who refuses blood transfusion, the extremely low risk of epidural hematoma should be discussed and documented, though the risk is negligible with standard technique.\n\n**Surgical Referral Criteria**:\n1. **Failed adequate conservative treatment** (minimum 3-6 months of PT, medications, and/or injections)\n2. **Progressive neurologic deficit** (worsening weakness, foot drop, cauda equina symptoms \u2014 these are urgent/emergent indications regardless of conservative trial)\n3. **Intolerable functional limitation** despite conservative care \u2014 the patient defines what is intolerable\n4. **Concordance between imaging and clinical presentation** \u2014 the surgical level must match the clinical syndrome\n\n**Surgical Options**:\n- **Laminectomy/decompression**: Standard posterior decompression. Removes lamina and ligamentum flavum to open the canal. Most common procedure for stenosis. No fusion required unless instability is present.\n- **Laminotomy**: Less invasive, preserves more bony architecture. Bilateral decompression through a unilateral approach.\n- **Interspinous process device (X-STOP)**: Limits extension and maintains canal opening. Less invasive but higher reoperation rate. Limited by insurance coverage.\n- **Laminectomy with fusion**: Required if spondylolisthesis, scoliosis, or iatrogenic instability from wide decompression.\n\n**SPORT Trial Evidence**: The Spine Patient Outcomes Research Trial showed that surgical decompression provided significantly greater improvement than conservative care for lumbar stenosis at 2 and 4 years, but the conservative group also improved, and crossover between groups was high. Shared decision-making is essential.")
B.aZh=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient is interested in surgical decompression but wants to understand the decision-making process given both his clinical situation and his religious convictions. How do you approach shared decision-making?")
B.b0F=new A.c("+ Discuss the shared decision-making framework for surgical vs conservative treatment, incorporating the patient's values and religious beliefs.","**Shared Decision-Making Framework for Lumbar Stenosis Surgery**:\n\nLumbar spinal stenosis is a condition where surgery is elective (absent progressive neurologic deficit or cauda equina syndrome). This makes it an ideal candidate for shared decision-making \u2014 there is no single \"right\" answer, and the patient's values, preferences, and life circumstances should heavily influence the decision.\n\n**Step 1 \u2014 Present the Options Clearly**:\n'There are two reasonable paths forward:\n\n**Path 1 \u2014 Continued conservative care**: Physical therapy focused on flexion exercises, medications, and possibly epidural injections. The evidence shows that many patients with stenosis improve or stabilize with conservative treatment, though it may not fully resolve your symptoms.\n\n**Path 2 \u2014 Surgical decompression**: Removes the bone and tissue compressing your nerves. Research shows that surgery provides greater improvement in pain and walking ability compared to conservative care in the first 2-4 years, but conservative care also improves over time. Surgery has risks including infection, nerve injury, and the need for repeat surgery.'\n\n**Step 2 \u2014 Use Decision Aids**:\n- Validated decision aids for lumbar stenosis exist (e.g., Ottawa Decision Guide adapted for spinal stenosis)\n- Present outcome probabilities: approximately 60-70% of surgical patients report meaningful improvement at 2 years vs 30-45% of conservatively managed patients\n- Discuss that improvement with conservative care is slower but real, and some patients deteriorate with either approach\n\n**Step 3 \u2014 Explore the Patient's Values**:\n'What matters most to you? Is it getting back to longer walks as quickly as possible? Is it avoiding surgery altogether? Is it minimizing risk? Your answer helps us figure out which path is right for you.'\n\n**Step 4 \u2014 Address the Blood Transfusion Issue Directly and Respectfully**:\n\n'I want to address your faith conviction about blood transfusions because it is important to your surgical planning and I want to make sure we handle it correctly.\n\nThe good news is that lumbar decompression surgery for stenosis is not a high blood-loss procedure. Typical blood loss for a standard laminectomy is 100-300 mL, and blood transfusion is rarely needed. However, there is always a small risk of unexpected bleeding.\n\nIf you decide to pursue surgery, here is what we would do to respect your wishes and maximize your safety:\n\n1. **Bloodless surgery planning**: Refer to a surgeon experienced with Jehovah's Witness patients or a hospital with a bloodless surgery program. Many major medical centers have dedicated programs.\n2. **Preoperative optimization**: Check hemoglobin and iron stores. If anemic, treat with IV iron and/or erythropoietin (EPO) before surgery to build up your blood count.\n3. **Intraoperative techniques**: Cell saver (some Jehovah's Witnesses accept this because the blood remains in a continuous circuit \u2014 this is an individual decision of conscience), meticulous hemostasis, tranexamic acid to reduce surgical bleeding, controlled hypotension.\n4. **Advance directive**: A detailed surgical consent and advance directive specifying exactly which blood products you will and will not accept. Some Jehovah's Witnesses accept certain blood fractions (albumin, clotting factors, immunoglobulins) \u2014 this is a personal decision. We would have this conversation in detail before surgery.\n5. **Anesthesia consultation**: Preoperative anesthesia planning specifically addressing the no-transfusion protocol.'\n\n**Step 5 \u2014 Document the Decision-Making Process**:\n- Document that the patient was informed of all options, risks, and benefits\n- Document his religious beliefs and the specific blood product restrictions\n- Ensure the surgical advance directive is completed, signed, and accessible in the medical record and the operating room\n- Include a witnessed conversation that the patient understands the risk of refusing transfusion in the rare event of significant hemorrhage\n\n**Step 6 \u2014 Respect the Decision**:\nRegardless of which path the patient chooses \u2014 continued conservative care, surgery with a bloodless protocol, or a hybrid approach \u2014 the physician's role is to provide information, ensure understanding, respect autonomy, and support the decision. The patient's religious conviction is not a barrier to be overcome but a value to be incorporated into the treatment plan.")
B.b_5=new A.c(u.j,'The patient decides to pursue surgery. During the preoperative visit, you are asked by the surgical team to discuss the blood transfusion refusal. The anesthesiologist pulls you aside and says: "If he starts hemorrhaging on the table and refuses transfusion, we could lose him. Can we get a court order to override his wishes?"')
B.aVz=new A.c("+ Address the anesthesiologist's question about overriding the patient's blood transfusion refusal, and demonstrate how you would facilitate the preoperative conversation.","**Responding to the Anesthesiologist**:\n\n'I understand your concern \u2014 you want to protect the patient's life, and that instinct is what makes you a good physician. But the answer is no, we should not seek a court order, and I want to explain why.\n\nThis patient is a competent adult who has made an informed decision based on a deeply held religious belief. The legal and ethical framework is clear:\n\n1. **Patient autonomy**: A competent adult has the legal right to refuse any medical treatment, including life-saving treatment. This is protected by the First Amendment, the Patient Self-Determination Act, and decades of case law.\n2. **Informed refusal**: He has been informed of the risks of refusing blood transfusion, including the possibility of death from hemorrhage. He understands and accepts this risk. His decision is documented.\n3. **Court orders**: Courts have consistently upheld the right of competent adult Jehovah's Witness patients to refuse blood transfusion, even when refusal may result in death. Seeking a court order against a competent patient's clearly expressed wishes would be ethically inappropriate and legally unlikely to succeed.\n4. **The distinction that matters**: Court orders are sometimes sought for minors or for patients who lack decision-making capacity. Neither applies here.\n\nOur obligation is to provide the best possible surgical care within his stated boundaries \u2014 not to override those boundaries.'\n\n**Facilitating the Preoperative Conversation with the Patient**:\n\nI would bring together the patient, his wife, the surgeon, and the anesthesiologist for a structured preoperative conference:\n\n'[Patient name], we are meeting today to make sure everyone on your surgical team understands your wishes and that we have a clear plan that respects your beliefs while giving you the safest surgery possible.\n\nI want to walk through this together so there is no confusion on the day of surgery.'\n\n**Step 1 \u2014 Clarify specific boundaries**:\n'Every Jehovah's Witness patient I have worked with has slightly different personal decisions about what they will and will not accept. I want to make sure we understand yours specifically.'\n\nReview a detailed blood product consent form:\n- **Whole blood**: Refused (universal among Jehovah's Witnesses)\n- **Packed red blood cells**: Refused\n- **Plasma**: Refused\n- **Platelets**: Refused\n- **Albumin**: Conscience decision \u2014 some accept, some decline\n- **Clotting factor concentrates**: Conscience decision\n- **Immunoglobulins**: Conscience decision\n- **Erythropoietin**: Generally accepted (not a blood product)\n- **Cell saver with continuous circuit**: Conscience decision \u2014 many accept this\n- **Hemodilution (pre-surgical blood draw kept in continuous circuit)**: Conscience decision\n\n**Step 2 \u2014 Confirm understanding of risks**:\n'I want to make sure you understand that while the risk of significant bleeding during this surgery is low, if unexpected hemorrhage occurs and your hemoglobin drops to a critically low level, we would not be able to give you blood products per your wishes. In an extreme scenario, this could be life-threatening. Do you understand and accept this risk?'\n\n**Step 3 \u2014 Confirm consistency under pressure**:\n'I also want to ask \u2014 and this is important \u2014 if you are in the operating room and the situation becomes serious, your wishes as documented today will be followed. We will not ask your wife to override your decision while you are under anesthesia. Is that consistent with what you want?'\n\n**Step 4 \u2014 Document thoroughly**:\n- Detailed advance directive specifying each blood product category\n- Signed by patient, witnessed by two staff members\n- Copy to surgeon, anesthesiologist, OR nursing, medical record\n- Hospital Jehovah's Witness liaison (many hospitals have one) should be notified\n\n**Step 5 \u2014 Address the wife**:\n'Mrs. [name], I want to make sure you are also comfortable with this plan and that you understand your husband's wishes. If a situation arises during surgery where a decision needs to be made, we will follow his advance directive. Is there anything you want to discuss?'\n\n**Key principles**: Respecting autonomy, informed refusal, team communication, thorough documentation, involving family, and providing the best possible care within the patient's stated boundaries.")
B.aEx=s([B.aT3,B.aOm,B.aPn,B.aWo,B.aSn,B.b2y,B.aPi,B.aZh,B.b0F,B.b_5,B.aVz],t.E)
B.PP=new A.a5("msk-lumbar-spinal-stenosis","Lumbar Spinal Stenosis \u2014 Neurogenic Claudication",B.aEx,!1)
B.aWw=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 42-year-old right-handed female elementary school teacher presents to the PM&R clinic with severe right hand and wrist pain, swelling, and color changes 3 months after an uncomplicated closed reduction and casting of a right distal radius fracture sustained from a fall on an outstretched hand. She reports that the pain never improved after cast removal \u2014 instead, it dramatically worsened. She describes constant burning, throbbing pain rated 8-9/10 that is disproportionate to any remaining injury. She cannot tolerate clothing sleeves touching her forearm, water from the shower feels like fire on her hand, and she is unable to use her dominant hand for writing, typing, or self-care. She has not been able to return to teaching. Her employer's HR department sent her a letter requesting she return to work or provide documentation of disability, and her workers' compensation adjuster has questioned the legitimacy of her claim, stating: \"The fracture healed normally on X-ray \u2014 there is no reason she can't work.\" She is tearful and frustrated: \"Everyone thinks I'm faking this. My own family doesn't believe me. The pain is real.\"")
B.aR6=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","Key areas to assess:\n- **Pain characterization**: Burning, throbbing quality. Spontaneous pain without provocation. Allodynia (pain from normally non-painful stimuli \u2014 clothing, light touch, air movement). Hyperalgesia (exaggerated pain response to mildly painful stimuli). Pain distribution \u2014 does it follow a dermatomal pattern (it should NOT in CRPS \u2014 it is regional and non-dermatomal).\n- **Vasomotor changes**: Color changes (red, blue, mottled, pale) \u2014 ask if they fluctuate. Temperature asymmetry \u2014 does the hand feel warmer or cooler than the other side? Can change over time (warm early, cool later).\n- **Sudomotor changes**: Sweating asymmetry \u2014 increased or decreased sweating compared to the unaffected hand.\n- **Edema**: Onset, distribution, does it fluctuate. Non-pitting edema is common in CRPS.\n- **Motor/trophic changes**: Weakness (not from a specific nerve or root lesion), reduced ROM, tremor, dystonia. Trophic changes: altered hair growth, nail changes (brittle, ridged, discolored), skin changes (shiny, thin, or thickened).\n- **Timeline**: Symptoms should follow a precipitating event (fracture in this case) and be disproportionate to the inciting event. The 3-month timeline is typical for CRPS presentation.\n- **Psychological screening**: CRPS does NOT equal malingering or somatization \u2014 but comorbid anxiety, depression, and PTSD are common and affect outcomes. Screen with PHQ-9 and GAD-7. Assess pain catastrophizing (PCS).\n- **Functional impact**: Dominant hand involvement in a teacher \u2014 cannot write, type, carry materials, or manage a classroom. Impact on ADLs: dressing, hygiene, cooking, driving.\n- **Prior treatments**: Has she received any treatment for CRPS specifically, or has it been managed as persistent fracture pain? Many CRPS cases are delayed in diagnosis because the condition is not recognized.\n- **Workers' compensation status**: Work injury? Claim status? IME scheduled? Adversarial interactions with insurance?")
B.b0I=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 138/84 mmHg (may reflect pain), HR 82 bpm, RR 16, Temp 36.9C.\n\n**General**: Alert, guarding the right hand and wrist. Holds the right upper extremity protectively against her body. Appears distressed. Tearful during examination.\n\n**Right Upper Extremity Examination**:\n- **Inspection**: Diffuse edema of the right hand and wrist extending to mid-forearm. Skin is erythematous with mottled discoloration. Shiny, taut appearance of the dorsal hand skin. Nail changes \u2014 right fingernails are ridged and grow faster than the left. Increased hair growth on the right dorsal hand compared to the left.\n- **Temperature**: Right hand palpably warmer than the left. Infrared thermometry: right dorsal hand 34.2C, left dorsal hand 31.8C (>1C asymmetry is significant; this is >2C asymmetry).\n- **Edema**: Non-pitting edema of right hand and fingers. Figure-of-eight measurement: right hand 2.3 cm greater than left.\n- **Allodynia testing**: Light touch with cotton wisp to the right dorsal hand elicits severe pain (rated 7/10). Light brush stroke across the right forearm causes withdrawal and grimacing. Allodynia extends beyond the original fracture site to involve the entire hand, wrist, and distal forearm.\n- **Hyperalgesia**: Pinprick with Wartenberg wheel \u2014 dramatically amplified pain response on right hand compared to left.\n- **Motor**: Right grip strength 8 kg (left 28 kg). Right pinch strength <1 kg (left 6 kg). Active ROM of right wrist: flexion 20 degrees (left 70), extension 15 degrees (left 65). Finger flexion limited by pain and edema. **Weakness is NOT in a specific nerve distribution** \u2014 it is global and pain-limited, consistent with CRPS rather than nerve injury.\n- **Tremor**: Fine resting tremor of the right hand.\n- **Sudomotor**: Right palm notably more moist than left on palpation.\n- **Left upper extremity**: Completely normal examination.\n\n**Radiographs (right hand/wrist)**: Healed distal radius fracture in anatomic alignment. **Patchy periarticular osteopenia** of the carpals, metacarpals, and phalanges on the right \u2014 this is characteristic of CRPS (Sudeck atrophy).\n\n**Screening Exam**: No cervical radiculopathy signs. Negative Tinel at carpal tunnel and cubital tunnel. No thoracic outlet syndrome signs. Shoulder exam normal \u2014 rules out shoulder-hand syndrome variant.")
B.aYg=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The attending asks you to apply the diagnostic criteria for CRPS and discuss the role of diagnostic studies.")
B.aXo=new A.c("+ Apply the Budapest criteria to this patient and discuss the utility of three-phase bone scan, thermography, and other diagnostic studies.","**Budapest Criteria for CRPS (2010 IASP Revision)**:\n\nThe Budapest criteria replaced the earlier IASP criteria to improve specificity while maintaining sensitivity. Diagnosis requires ALL four of the following:\n\n**1. Continuing pain disproportionate to the inciting event** \u2014 MET\nHer fracture healed normally, yet she has severe burning pain rated 8-9/10 with allodynia \u2014 this is grossly disproportionate to a healed distal radius fracture.\n\n**2. Must report at least ONE symptom in THREE of the four categories**:\n- **Sensory**: Hyperesthesia (increased sensitivity) and/or allodynia \u2014 **MET** (she reports clothing, water, and light touch causing severe pain)\n- **Vasomotor**: Temperature asymmetry and/or skin color changes \u2014 **MET** (she reports color changes and the hand feels different temperature)\n- **Sudomotor/edema**: Edema and/or sweating changes \u2014 **MET** (she reports swelling)\n- **Motor/trophic**: Decreased ROM, weakness, tremor, dystonia, and/or trophic changes \u2014 **MET** (she reports inability to use the hand, weakness)\n\n**Result: Reports symptoms in 4/4 categories \u2014 criterion met.**\n\n**3. Must display at least ONE sign at time of evaluation in TWO or more categories**:\n- **Sensory**: Evidence of allodynia (cotton wisp test) and hyperalgesia (pinprick) \u2014 **MET**\n- **Vasomotor**: Temperature asymmetry >1C (measured at >2C) and visible color changes (erythema, mottling) \u2014 **MET**\n- **Sudomotor/edema**: Visible edema (2.3 cm difference) and increased sweating \u2014 **MET**\n- **Motor/trophic**: Decreased ROM, weakness, tremor, nail changes, hair changes, skin changes \u2014 **MET**\n\n**Result: Signs present in 4/4 categories \u2014 criterion met.**\n\n**4. No other diagnosis better explains the signs and symptoms** \u2014 **MET**\nCervical radiculopathy, peripheral nerve injury, carpal tunnel, thoracic outlet syndrome, and complex fracture have been excluded.\n\n**This patient meets ALL Budapest criteria for CRPS Type I (formerly Reflex Sympathetic Dystrophy).**\n\nCRPS Type I (no identified nerve injury) vs Type II (formerly causalgia \u2014 identifiable nerve injury). Her fracture did not involve a major named nerve, making this Type I.\n\n**Diagnostic Studies \u2014 Role and Limitations**:\n\n**Three-Phase Bone Scan (Technetium-99m)**:\n- **Classic finding**: Increased uptake in the affected extremity on all three phases \u2014 especially the delayed (third) phase showing diffuse periarticular uptake in the affected hand\n- **Sensitivity/Specificity**: Sensitivity 50-75%, specificity 80-90%. A positive bone scan supports the diagnosis but a NEGATIVE bone scan does NOT exclude CRPS. Sensitivity decreases with longer disease duration (the scan is most useful in the first 6 months).\n- **For this patient**: At 3 months, a bone scan may still be positive and can be useful for documentation purposes, especially for the workers' compensation dispute.\n\n**Infrared Thermography**:\n- Measures temperature asymmetry objectively. Asymmetry >1C between affected and contralateral extremity is significant.\n- Can document vasomotor instability over time (serial measurements)\n- Useful for objective documentation but not diagnostic in isolation\n\n**Quantitative Sudomotor Axon Reflex Test (QSART)**:\n- Measures sudomotor (sweating) function. Can document autonomic dysfunction objectively.\n- Available at specialized autonomic testing laboratories.\n\n**Plain Radiographs**:\n- Already show patchy periarticular osteopenia \u2014 consistent with CRPS. This finding (Sudeck atrophy) takes weeks to develop and is relatively specific when present in the context of other CRPS features.\n\n**MRI**:\n- May show bone marrow edema and soft tissue enhancement in the affected region. Not routinely needed for diagnosis if Budapest criteria are met, but can help exclude other pathology.\n\n**Sympathetic Block (Stellate Ganglion Block)**:\n- Historically used as a diagnostic test for CRPS \u2014 pain relief after sympathetic block was considered confirmatory. This is now considered unreliable as a diagnostic test because (1) many CRPS patients do not have sympathetically maintained pain, and (2) the block has non-specific analgesic effects.\n- Better used therapeutically than diagnostically.\n\n**Key Point**: CRPS is a **clinical diagnosis** based on the Budapest criteria. Diagnostic studies are supportive and useful for documentation but are NOT required. No single test confirms or excludes CRPS.")
B.aPU=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Develop the comprehensive treatment plan using an interdisciplinary approach.")
B.aMT=new A.c("+ Outline the multimodal treatment approach including graded motor imagery, desensitization, interventional options, and the role of spinal cord stimulation.","**CRPS Treatment \u2014 Multimodal Interdisciplinary Approach**:\n\nTreatment must address pain, function, and psychological comorbidities simultaneously. A purely pharmacologic approach is insufficient.\n\n**Tier 1 \u2014 Rehabilitation-Based Interventions (Foundation of Treatment)**:\n\n**Graded Motor Imagery (GMI) \u2014 Three-Stage Program**:\nGMI is the most evidence-based rehabilitation intervention for CRPS. It works by gradually retraining the brain's cortical representation of the affected limb.\n1. **Stage 1 \u2014 Laterality Recognition**: Patient views images of left and right hands and identifies which side each image represents. This activates the premotor cortex without movement. Use the Recognise app or flash cards. Practice 3 times daily for 2 weeks.\n2. **Stage 2 \u2014 Imagined Movements**: Patient imagines moving the affected hand through various positions WITHOUT actually moving it. This activates motor planning networks. Practice 3 times daily for 2 weeks.\n3. **Stage 3 \u2014 Mirror Therapy**: Place a mirror between the hands. The patient moves the unaffected hand while watching its reflection, creating the visual illusion that the affected hand is moving painlessly. Gradually introduce movements of the affected hand behind the mirror. 10-15 minutes, 3-5 times daily.\n\n**Desensitization Program (OT-led)**:\n- Systematic graded exposure to tactile stimuli on the affected hand\n- Begin with textures the patient can tolerate (soft cotton, silk) and progressively advance to coarser textures (terrycloth, denim, Velcro)\n- Includes contrast baths (alternating warm and cool water immersion), fluidotherapy, vibration\n- Must be patient-directed \u2014 never force exposure beyond tolerance\n\n**Functional Restoration PT/OT**:\n- Stress-loading program: scrubbing (alternating compression) and carrying (sustained traction) exercises for the affected hand\n- Active ROM within pain tolerance \u2014 gentle, progressive\n- Edema management: retrograde massage, compression glove, elevation\n- Splinting: Resting splint for night use to maintain wrist in neutral. Dynamic splinting if joint contractures develop.\n\n**Tier 2 \u2014 Pharmacologic Management**:\n\n**Neuropathic Pain Medications**:\n- **Gabapentin**: Titrate to 1800-3600 mg/day in divided doses. First-line.\n- **Pregabalin**: Alternative to gabapentin. 75 mg BID, titrate to 150-300 mg BID.\n- **Duloxetine**: 60 mg daily \u2014 addresses both neuropathic pain and comorbid depression.\n- **Amitriptyline or nortriptyline**: 10-75 mg at bedtime. Analgesic and sleep benefit.\n\n**Anti-inflammatory/Bone Agents**:\n- **Bisphosphonates**: Alendronate or IV pamidronate \u2014 evidence supports use in CRPS for reducing pain and edema, especially in the first year. Mechanism may relate to inhibiting bone resorption and modulating inflammation.\n- **Oral corticosteroids**: Short course of prednisone (40 mg daily taper over 2-3 weeks) can be effective in early inflammatory CRPS with significant edema.\n\n**Topical Agents**:\n- **Topical ketamine/gabapentin/clonidine compound cream**: Applied to the affected area. Avoids systemic side effects.\n- **Lidocaine 5% patch**: Over the most painful area.\n- **Capsaicin 8% patch (Qutenza)**: Applied in-clinic under supervision. Can provide weeks of pain relief.\n\n**Avoid Opioids**: Opioids are generally ineffective for CRPS and carry risks of dependence, hyperalgesia, and functional impairment. If used at all, only short-term for severe flares.\n\n**Tier 3 \u2014 Interventional Procedures**:\n\n**Sympathetic Nerve Blocks**:\n- **Stellate ganglion block** (for upper extremity CRPS): Injection of local anesthetic near the stellate ganglion at C6-7 under fluoroscopic guidance. If the first block provides significant but temporary relief, a series of 3-6 blocks may produce cumulative benefit.\n- Response to sympathetic block helps classify the pain as sympathetically maintained (SMP) vs sympathetically independent (SIP) \u2014 this guides further treatment.\n\n**Spinal Cord Stimulation (SCS)**:\n- **Indication**: CRPS refractory to 3-6 months of conservative and pharmacologic treatment\n- **Mechanism**: Electrical stimulation of dorsal columns modulates pain signaling via the gate control theory and supraspinal mechanisms\n- **Evidence**: CRPS is one of the strongest indications for SCS. The KEMLER trial showed sustained improvement in pain and quality of life at 5 years. Newer high-frequency (10 kHz) and dorsal root ganglion (DRG) stimulation show particular promise for CRPS.\n- **DRG Stimulation**: Targets the dorsal root ganglion at the affected spinal level. More focal coverage than traditional SCS. FDA-approved for CRPS. May be superior to conventional SCS for extremity pain.\n- **Process**: Trial stimulation (percutaneous leads for 5-7 days) \u2192 if >50% pain relief \u2192 permanent implant.\n\n**Tier 4 \u2014 Advanced/Refractory Options**:\n- Intrathecal drug delivery (baclofen, ziconotide, opioid)\n- IV ketamine infusion (subanesthetic doses \u2014 emerging evidence, specialized centers)\n- Amputation is NOT recommended for CRPS \u2014 phantom limb pain and CRPS recurrence in the stump are common")
B.aUL=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The workers' compensation adjuster is requesting an independent medical examination (IME) and has suggested the patient is exaggerating. Discuss disability determination and advocacy.")
B.aUF=new A.c('+ How do you approach disability documentation for CRPS, and how do you advocate for a patient with an "invisible disability" in a workers\' compensation setting?',"**CRPS as an \"Invisible Disability\" \u2014 Challenges and Advocacy**:\n\nCRPS is one of the most commonly disbelieved chronic pain conditions because (1) the fracture has healed on X-ray, (2) objective findings can fluctuate, (3) the pain appears \"disproportionate,\" and (4) many clinicians and adjusters are unfamiliar with the condition.\n\n**Disability Documentation Strategy**:\n\n**1. Establish the Diagnosis with Rigorous Documentation**:\n- Apply and document the Budapest criteria explicitly in the medical record \u2014 check each criterion and state whether it is met\n- Record objective examination findings at every visit: temperature asymmetry (measured with thermometer), edema (measured circumferentially), ROM (goniometer), grip strength (dynamometer), allodynia testing results\n- Photographs at each visit documenting color changes, edema, trophic changes\n- Supportive diagnostic studies: three-phase bone scan, thermography, radiographic osteopenia\n\n**2. Functional Capacity Documentation**:\n- Functional Capacity Evaluation (FCE) by a certified evaluator \u2014 documents objective physical capabilities and limitations\n- Specific functional limitations: grip strength, pinch strength, sustained hand use, repetitive hand use, lifting capacity, fine motor tasks, typing speed and duration\n- Consistency testing within the FCE (measures of effort and reliability)\n\n**3. Medical Narrative Report**:\nWrite a comprehensive report for the workers' compensation claim that includes:\n- Mechanism of injury and causal relationship to the workplace fall\n- Diagnosis with Budapest criteria application\n- Objective examination findings with measurements\n- Diagnostic study results\n- Treatment to date and response\n- Current functional limitations as they relate to her specific job demands (elementary school teacher)\n- Work capacity assessment: can she teach? With modifications? What restrictions?\n- Prognosis and expected future treatment needs\n- Statement of medical probability (more likely than not that CRPS resulted from the work injury)\n\n**4. Responding to the IME**:\n- The patient has the right to know who the IME examiner is and to have an observer present in some jurisdictions\n- Review the IME report when available \u2014 if it disputes the CRPS diagnosis, prepare a detailed rebuttal citing the Budapest criteria, objective findings, and supporting literature\n- The treating physician's opinion often carries significant weight because of the longitudinal treatment relationship\n\n**Advocacy for the Patient**:\n\n**Educating the Adjuster and Employer**:\n- Provide the workers' compensation adjuster with educational materials about CRPS (IASP fact sheets, published clinical guidelines)\n- A letter from the physiatrist explaining that CRPS is a recognized, objective medical condition \u2014 not a psychological or malingered condition \u2014 carries clinical authority\n- Cite the Budapest criteria's specificity: the criteria were developed specifically to reduce over-diagnosis, so meeting them is meaningful\n\n**Addressing the \"Normal X-Ray\" Argument**:\n'The fracture has healed, but CRPS is not a fracture. It is a disorder of the nervous system that was triggered by the fracture. The X-ray showing a healed bone is expected. CRPS is diagnosed by clinical criteria, not by X-ray, and this patient meets every diagnostic criterion.'\n\n**Workplace Accommodations if Return to Work is Possible**:\n- Modified duty: reduced writing demands, use of left hand or voice-to-text, reduced classroom hours initially\n- Ergonomic assessment of the classroom and teaching materials\n- Gradual return-to-work plan with defined milestones\n- Job restructuring: can an aide handle tasks requiring dominant hand use?\n\n**If Unable to Return to Teaching**:\n- Vocational rehabilitation assessment for alternative employment\n- SSDI application if disability is expected to last >12 months\n- ADA protections for disability discrimination\n\n**Key Message**: The physiatrist serves as both the treating physician and the patient's medical advocate. Documenting CRPS rigorously and communicating the diagnosis in clear, objective terms is the most powerful advocacy tool.")
B.aUC=new A.c(u.j,"During a follow-up visit, the patient is distraught. She says: \"My boss called me at home and said they don't believe I'm really disabled. He said I should be ashamed of myself for faking an injury. My own sister told me it's all in my head. How can I prove to people that this is real?\"")
B.aVT=new A.c("+ Demonstrate how you validate this patient's experience, address the stigma of an invisible disability, and empower her to advocate for herself.","**Addressing Delegitimization of Pain \u2014 A Core Clinical Skill**:\n\nDelegitimization \u2014 when others question or deny the reality of a patient's suffering \u2014 is one of the most psychologically damaging experiences for people with chronic pain. It compounds the pain with shame, isolation, and self-doubt.\n\n**Step 1 \u2014 Unequivocal Validation**:\n\n'I want to say something clearly, and I want you to hear it: your pain is real. CRPS is a medical condition recognized by every major medical organization in the world. It is not in your head, it is not exaggerated, and it is not something you are making up.\n\nI can see CRPS on your examination \u2014 the swelling, the color changes, the temperature difference, the way your hand reacts to light touch. These are objective, measurable findings. No one can fake a 2-degree temperature difference between their hands. No one can fake bone loss on an X-ray.\n\nYou have nothing to be ashamed of. Your boss is wrong. And your sister, as much as she may care about you, does not have the medical knowledge to make that judgment.'\n\n**Step 2 \u2014 Educate to Empower**:\n\n'Part of what makes CRPS so frustrating is that other people cannot see your pain. Your hand may look relatively normal to someone who does not know what to look for. But as your physician, I know exactly what I am seeing, and I want to give you tools to help the people in your life understand.\n\nI am going to do three things:\n1. Write a clear medical letter that you can share with your employer and your family explaining your diagnosis, what CRPS is, and what the objective medical findings are. This is not a letter asking for sympathy \u2014 it is a medical document stating facts.\n2. Give you educational materials from the International Association for the Study of Pain that explain CRPS in accessible language. You can share these with your sister, your friends, or anyone who needs to understand.\n3. Connect you with a CRPS support group \u2014 online or in person. Being around other people who share this experience can be incredibly validating.'\n\n**Step 3 \u2014 Address the Workplace Issue Specifically**:\n\n'What your boss did \u2014 calling you at home and accusing you of faking \u2014 may constitute workplace harassment of a person with a disability. You have legal protections under the Americans with Disabilities Act and under your state's workers' compensation laws.\n\nI am going to recommend that you speak with a workers' compensation attorney if you do not already have one. Your employer is not permitted to harass you for filing a legitimate claim. In the meantime, I will document this interaction in your medical record and include it in any future reports, because it is relevant to your psychological wellbeing and recovery.'\n\n**Step 4 \u2014 Address the Self-Doubt**:\n\n'I suspect that hearing these things from people you trust has made you start to wonder yourself \u2014 \"Am I overreacting? Is it really that bad?\" I want to name that feeling because it is incredibly common in people with invisible disabilities.\n\nYou are not overreacting. CRPS is rated as one of the most painful conditions in medicine \u2014 more painful than childbirth, more painful than amputation without anesthesia, in published pain severity rankings. The fact that you are functioning at all right now is a testament to your resilience, not evidence that the pain is not real.'\n\n**Step 5 \u2014 Psychological Support**:\n\n'I am also going to refer you to a psychologist who specializes in chronic pain \u2014 not because this is a psychological problem, but because dealing with constant pain AND being disbelieved by the people around you is an enormous psychological burden. You deserve professional support for both.'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Unequivocal medical validation, providing objective evidence the patient can share, empowering self-advocacy with education and documentation, addressing workplace harassment, naming and countering self-doubt, and providing psychological support without pathologizing.")
B.aCG=s([B.aWw,B.aR6,B.b0I,B.aYg,B.aXo,B.aPU,B.aMT,B.aUL,B.aUF,B.aUC,B.aVT],t.E)
B.PR=new A.a5("msk-crps-invisible-disability","Complex Regional Pain Syndrome (CRPS)",B.aCG,!1)
B.b23=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 50-year-old right-handed male auto mechanic presents to the PM&R clinic with 12 months of persistent right lateral elbow pain. The pain began insidiously without a specific injury and has progressively worsened despite 6 months of physical therapy (stretching and strengthening), a counterforce brace, two corticosteroid injections (the second injection 4 months ago provided only 2 weeks of relief), and activity modification. He describes sharp pain localized to the lateral epicondyle that radiates into his forearm extensors. The pain is worst when gripping tools, turning wrenches, and lifting heavy car parts. He cannot grip a coffee mug without pain. He drops objects frequently. His grip strength has declined significantly on the right. He is the sole provider for his family with three children, works 50-60 hours per week, and his employer does not offer light-duty options. He states: \"I need you to put me on disability. I can't do anything with this arm anymore. My life is over if I can't work.\" His workers' compensation claim was filed 8 months ago and has been contentious.")
B.aXZ=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","Key areas to assess:\n- **Pain characterization**: Location (lateral epicondyle, common extensor origin), radiation (into the forearm extensor mass), aggravating factors (gripping, wrist extension against resistance, lifting with palm down), alleviating factors (rest, avoidance). Night pain? (suggests significant tendinopathy or alternative diagnosis).\n- **Mechanism and timeline**: Insidious onset in a mechanic \u2014 repetitive gripping, wrist extension, and forearm pronation/supination are classic occupational risk factors. 12-month duration classifies this as chronic lateral epicondylitis (typical natural history is 12-18 months with eventual resolution in 80-90% of cases).\n- **Treatment history in detail**: What type of PT was performed? Eccentric exercises specifically? Were the corticosteroid injections ultrasound-guided or landmark-based? Steroid injection provides short-term relief but is associated with WORSE long-term outcomes compared to watchful waiting \u2014 the recurrence after his second injection is consistent with this evidence.\n- **Red flags and differential diagnosis symptoms**: Neck pain or radicular symptoms (cervical radiculopathy at C6 can mimic lateral epicondylitis), numbness/tingling in the hand (radial tunnel syndrome, posterior interosseous nerve syndrome), locking or catching (loose bodies, osteochondritis dissecans of the capitellum), medial elbow pain (medial epicondylitis), instability sensation.\n- **Bilateral symptoms**: Is the left elbow also symptomatic? Bilateral epicondylitis suggests a systemic or ergonomic component.\n- **Occupational details**: Specific job tasks, frequency of gripping and lifting, tool types, vibratory tool exposure, postures. Has an ergonomic assessment been performed?\n- **Psychosocial factors**: Workers' compensation claim duration and disputes, financial stress (sole provider, 3 children), job satisfaction, fear-avoidance beliefs, catastrophizing. These are strong predictors of chronic disability and must be addressed.\n- **Functional impact**: DASH (Disabilities of the Arm, Shoulder and Hand) score. Specific activities impaired \u2014 work, self-care, recreation.")
B.aUR=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 136/82 mmHg, HR 76 bpm, Weight 195 lbs, BMI 27.8.\n\n**General**: Well-appearing male. Favors the right arm. No acute distress at rest.\n\n**Right Elbow Examination**:\n- **Inspection**: No visible swelling or ecchymosis. No muscle wasting. Elbow carrying angle normal.\n- **Palpation**: Exquisite point tenderness over the lateral epicondyle at the common extensor origin, specifically over the extensor carpi radialis brevis (ECRB) origin. Tenderness extends 1-2 cm distal along the extensor mass.\n- **Range of Motion**: Full elbow flexion and extension. Full pronation and supination. No mechanical block.\n- **Provocative Tests**:\n  - **Cozen test (resisted wrist extension with forearm pronated and elbow extended)**: Strongly positive \u2014 reproduces his exact pain at the lateral epicondyle\n  - **Mill test (passive wrist flexion with forearm pronated and elbow extended)**: Positive \u2014 stretching the common extensors reproduces pain\n  - **Maudsley test (resisted middle finger extension)**: Positive \u2014 isolates the ECRB/EDC origin\n  - **Chair lift test (lifting a chair with forearm pronated)**: Positive\n- **Grip strength (Jamar dynamometer)**: Right 24 kg, Left 45 kg (47% reduction on the right). Pain-free grip strength on the right: 12 kg.\n\n**Differential Diagnosis Examination**:\n- **Radial tunnel syndrome**: Tenderness 3-4 cm distal to the lateral epicondyle over the radial tunnel \u2014 mildly tender (this is concerning for coexistent radial tunnel syndrome, which occurs in up to 5% of cases). Resisted forearm supination \u2014 mildly painful.\n- **Cervical spine**: Full ROM, no radicular signs. Spurling test negative. Normal C6 myotome and dermatome.\n- **Posterior interosseous nerve (PIN)**: No finger drop. Resisted finger extension intact. PIN compression less likely.\n- **Elbow instability**: Varus and valgus stress at 30 degrees of flexion \u2014 stable. Posterolateral rotatory instability (pivot shift) test negative.\n- **Medial epicondyle**: Non-tender. No medial epicondylitis.\n\n**Diagnostic Ultrasound (performed in clinic)**:\n- **ECRB tendon**: Thickened, hypoechoic, with loss of normal fibrillar echotexture. No full-thickness tear. Mild increased vascularity on power Doppler (neovascularity \u2014 consistent with tendinopathy). No calcification. No cortical irregularity of the lateral epicondyle.\n- **Common extensor tendon**: Partial-thickness undersurface tear of the ECRB origin (<50% thickness).\n- **Radial nerve**: Normal caliber at the radial tunnel. No compression or swelling.\n\n**DASH Score**: 62/100 (significant disability \u2014 scores >50 indicate severe functional limitation).")
B.aU3=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The attending asks you to discuss the differential diagnosis and the significance of the ultrasound findings.")
B.aNt=new A.c("+ Discuss the differential diagnosis for chronic lateral elbow pain and interpret the ultrasound findings in the context of failed conservative treatment.",'**Comprehensive Differential Diagnosis for Chronic Lateral Elbow Pain**:\n\n**1. Lateral Epicondylitis (Epicondylalgia) \u2014 Primary Diagnosis**:\n- Degenerative tendinopathy of the common extensor origin, predominantly the extensor carpi radialis brevis (ECRB)\n- Histopathology shows **angiofibroblastic tendinosis** \u2014 NOT inflammation. The term "tendinitis" is a misnomer. The pathology is collagen disorganization, neovascularization, and failed healing response.\n- The ultrasound findings (hypoechoic thickening, neovascularity, partial-thickness undersurface tear) confirm the diagnosis and quantify severity.\n\n**2. Radial Tunnel Syndrome \u2014 Must Be Excluded**:\n- Compression of the posterior interosseous nerve (PIN) within the radial tunnel (between the radial head and the supinator muscle)\n- Can mimic or coexist with lateral epicondylitis. Up to 5% of lateral epicondylitis cases have concurrent radial tunnel syndrome.\n- Key differentiator: tenderness is more DISTAL (4 fingerbreadths distal to the lateral epicondyle over the mobile wad) rather than directly over the epicondyle. Pain with resisted supination is more suggestive of radial tunnel than epicondylitis.\n- This patient has mild tenderness over the radial tunnel \u2014 this warrants monitoring. If his lateral epicondylitis treatment fails, occult radial tunnel syndrome may be contributing.\n- EMG/NCS: Often normal in radial tunnel syndrome (a clinical diagnosis). EMG is more useful for excluding PIN syndrome (which causes motor deficit without pain).\n\n**3. Cervical Radiculopathy (C5-C6)**:\n- Can refer pain to the lateral elbow. C6 radiculopathy may present with lateral arm pain, weakness of wrist extension, and reduced brachioradialis reflex.\n- Excluded in this patient by normal cervical exam, negative Spurling, and pain that is precisely localized to the epicondyle with positive provocative tests.\n\n**4. Osteochondritis Dissecans (OCD) of the Capitellum**:\n- More common in young athletes (gymnasts, throwers) but can present in adults\n- Lateral elbow pain with locking, catching, or mechanical symptoms\n- Diagnosed by X-ray or MRI showing capitellar lesion\n- Excluded by absence of mechanical symptoms and normal ultrasound of the capitellum.\n\n**5. Posterolateral Rotatory Instability (PLRI)**:\n- Instability of the lateral ulnar collateral ligament. Can follow trauma, dislocation, or iatrogenic injury (e.g., from repeated corticosteroid injections, which weaken ligamentous structures).\n- Two corticosteroid injections raise mild concern for this complication. Pivot shift test was negative.\n\n**6. Intra-articular Pathology**: Loose bodies, synovial plica, radiocapitellar arthritis. Excluded by normal ROM and absence of mechanical symptoms.\n\n**Ultrasound Findings in Context**:\n\nThe ultrasound confirms **moderate tendinopathy with a partial-thickness undersurface tear (<50%)**. Key implications:\n- Tendon thickening and hypoechogenicity indicate chronic degenerative change, not acute inflammation \u2014 this explains why anti-inflammatory medications and corticosteroid injections have been ineffective.\n- Neovascularity (Doppler signal) is associated with pain generation. Some treatments specifically target neovascularity (sclerosing injections, high-volume injections).\n- The partial tear is significant \u2014 partial tears >50% thickness or complete tears may warrant surgical consideration. This tear at <50% is still within the range of non-operative management.\n- Post-injection tendon quality: Repeated corticosteroid injections are associated with tendon degeneration, collagen necrosis, and weakening. This patient\'s failed recovery may be partly iatrogenic from the steroid injections.')
B.b_g=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Given 12 months of failed conservative treatment, discuss the next steps in management including regenerative options and surgical indications.")
B.aZn=new A.c("+ Compare PRP vs corticosteroid injection evidence, outline eccentric exercise prescription, and discuss when surgery is indicated.","**Management of Recalcitrant Lateral Epicondylitis**:\n\n**Eccentric Exercise \u2014 The Evidence-Based Foundation**:\n- Despite 6 months of PT, the question is whether his program specifically included a **structured eccentric loading protocol**, which is the most evidence-based exercise intervention for tendinopathy.\n- **Protocol**: Tyler Twist exercise using a FlexBar (rubber bar). The patient holds the bar with the affected wrist in extension, then slowly allows the wrist to move into flexion (eccentric load on the extensors). 3 sets of 15 repetitions, twice daily, for 8-12 weeks.\n- The Tyler Twist protocol showed a 91% improvement rate in chronic lateral epicondylitis in the original RCT.\n- If the patient's prior PT did NOT include structured eccentric loading, this should be tried before advancing to injections or surgery.\n\n**PRP vs Corticosteroid Injection \u2014 Evidence Review**:\n\n**Corticosteroid Injection**:\n- Short-term benefit (2-6 weeks) in pain reduction\n- **Long-term outcomes are WORSE than placebo** \u2014 multiple RCTs and systematic reviews demonstrate that corticosteroid injection is associated with higher recurrence rates, delayed healing, and worse outcomes at 6-12 months compared to watchful waiting\n- Mechanism of harm: corticosteroids inhibit tenocyte proliferation, reduce collagen synthesis, and promote tendon degeneration\n- This patient has already had two injections with diminishing returns \u2014 further steroid injections are contraindicated and potentially harmful\n\n**Platelet-Rich Plasma (PRP)**:\n- Rationale: PRP delivers concentrated growth factors (PDGF, TGF-beta, VEGF) to the degenerative tendon to stimulate a healing response\n- **Evidence**: Multiple RCTs show PRP is superior to corticosteroid injection at 6-12 months for lateral epicondylitis. The Mishra (2014) and Gosens (2011) RCTs showed significant improvement in DASH scores and pain with PRP vs corticosteroid at 1-2 year follow-up.\n- PRP appears more effective than placebo in chronic cases, though the evidence is still debated in systematic reviews.\n- **Technique**: Ultrasound-guided injection of leukocyte-rich PRP (LR-PRP) directly into the area of tendinopathy. Typically a single injection with possible repeat at 6-8 weeks if partial response.\n- **Post-injection protocol**: Relative rest for 1 week, then graduated eccentric loading program. NSAIDs should be AVOIDED for 2-4 weeks post-injection (they inhibit the inflammatory healing cascade that PRP is designed to stimulate).\n- **Limitations**: Not consistently covered by insurance or workers' compensation. Patient may need to pay out-of-pocket ($500-1500).\n\n**Other Injection Options**:\n- **Autologous whole blood injection**: Similar rationale to PRP but simpler and less expensive. Evidence is modest.\n- **Prolotherapy (hypertonic dextrose)**: Stimulates a healing response through controlled irritation. Some evidence of benefit.\n- **Botulinum toxin injection**: Into the ECRB to reduce tension on the tendon. Temporary finger extension weakness is a side effect. Limited evidence.\n- **High-volume injection**: Saline + local anesthetic injected under ultrasound guidance to disrupt neovascularity and adhesions. Emerging evidence.\n\n**Surgical Indications**:\nSurgery is considered when:\n1. Symptoms persist for >6-12 months despite comprehensive conservative treatment\n2. Patient has failed structured eccentric exercise, at least one regenerative injection (PRP), and activity modification\n3. The functional limitation is significant (DASH >50, unable to perform essential job functions)\n4. Imaging shows structural pathology correlating with symptoms (partial tear on ultrasound/MRI)\n\n**Surgical Options**:\n- **Open debridement (Nirschl procedure)**: Identification and excision of the angiofibroblastic tissue within the ECRB origin. Gold standard with >85% good-to-excellent outcomes.\n- **Arthroscopic debridement**: Less invasive, faster recovery, allows evaluation of intra-articular pathology. Similar outcomes to open.\n- **Percutaneous tenotomy (TENEX/Tenjet)**: Ultrasound-guided percutaneous debridement using a needle-like device. Performed under local anesthesia in the clinic. Faster recovery than open surgery. Growing evidence base.\n\n**For This Patient**: He has failed conservative PT (assuming it included eccentric loading) and two corticosteroid injections. The next appropriate step is either (1) a structured eccentric loading trial if not previously attempted, AND/OR (2) ultrasound-guided PRP injection. If these fail after 3-6 additional months, surgical referral is indicated.")
B.aXD=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient insists on being placed on permanent disability. His workers' compensation case is adversarial and he is frustrated with the system.")
B.aW4=new A.c("+ Discuss workers' compensation for repetitive strain injury, ergonomic assessment, and your approach to the disability request.","**Workers' Compensation for Repetitive Strain \u2014 Key Concepts**:\n\n**Establishing Work-Relatedness**:\n- Lateral epicondylitis from repetitive gripping, wrist extension, and tool use in an auto mechanic is a well-recognized occupational injury\n- Causation: the mechanism (repetitive forceful gripping, vibration exposure from power tools, sustained forearm pronation) is biomechanically consistent with the ECRB tendinopathy\n- The workers' compensation system requires documentation that the condition arose out of and in the course of employment \u2014 the repetitive occupational exposure is the proximate cause\n\n**Ergonomic Assessment \u2014 Essential and Often Overlooked**:\nAn ergonomic assessment of his workplace should have been conducted early in the claim but may not have been. Key elements:\n- **Tool assessment**: Size and weight of tools, grip diameter (tools with grips <3 cm or >5 cm increase grip force requirements). Are anti-vibration tools available? Torque-limiting tools?\n- **Posture analysis**: Sustained wrist extension, forceful gripping frequency, repetition rate. Overhead work requiring sustained elbow extension.\n- **Workstation modification**: Adjustable workbench height, rotating platforms to reduce awkward postures, tool balancers for suspended tools\n- **Work practice changes**: Task rotation, microbreaks, two-handed lifting techniques, power tool substitution for manual tools\n- **Personal protective equipment**: Anti-vibration gloves, ergonomic tool grips\n\nIf the patient returns to work without ergonomic modifications, the condition will recur regardless of treatment.\n\n**Approaching the Disability Request**:\n\nThis is a critical communication moment. The patient is requesting permanent disability for lateral epicondylitis \u2014 a condition that resolves in 80-90% of cases and has effective surgical options. Simply granting or denying the request would be a disservice.\n\n**Framework**:\n\n1. **Acknowledge his frustration and financial pressure**: His distress is real \u2014 he is the sole provider, he has been in pain for a year, the workers' compensation system has been adversarial, and he feels hopeless.\n\n2. **Assess for yellow flags**: Fear-avoidance beliefs, catastrophizing (\"my life is over\"), passive coping strategies (seeking disability rather than active treatment), adversarial workers' compensation claim. These are strong predictors of chronic disability and are potentially modifiable.\n\n3. **Be honest about the disability question**: Permanent total disability for lateral epicondylitis would be medically difficult to justify when treatment options (PRP, surgery) have not been exhausted. An IME examiner would identify this. Premature disability determination can harm the patient by removing motivation for treatment and locking him into a disabled identity for a treatable condition.\n\n4. **Reframe the path forward**: The goal is not disability \u2014 it is return to function. Present a clear treatment plan with defined milestones:\n   - Temporary total disability: appropriate NOW while undergoing active treatment\n   - PRP injection and structured eccentric exercise program: 3-month trial\n   - If insufficient: surgical referral with expected 3-6 month post-surgical recovery\n   - Ergonomic workplace assessment before return to work\n   - Graduated return-to-work with ergonomic modifications\n   - If all treatments fail AND surgery fails: THEN permanent impairment rating and disability determination is appropriate\n\n5. **Impairment Rating**: If permanent impairment exists after maximal medical improvement (MMI), rate according to AMA Guides to the Evaluation of Permanent Impairment (6th edition). Lateral epicondylitis with residual functional deficit would be rated using the upper extremity regional grid (class 1-4 based on clinical findings and functional loss).\n\n6. **Legal representation**: If the workers' compensation claim is adversarial, ensure the patient has an attorney. The physiatrist's role is medical treatment and documentation \u2014 not legal advocacy.")
B.aVq=new A.c(u.j,"The patient becomes visibly angry when you explain that permanent disability is not appropriate at this time. He says: \"You're just like the insurance company. You don't believe me either. I can't do anything with this arm and you want me to just suck it up and go back to work.\"")
B.b_s=new A.c("+ Demonstrate how you manage the patient's anger, set realistic expectations, and maintain the therapeutic alliance.","**Managing Patient Anger and Setting Realistic Expectations**:\n\nThis situation requires de-escalation, empathy, and honest redirection without capitulation or confrontation. The patient is not angry at you \u2014 he is angry at the situation, the pain, the insurance system, and the feeling of being disbelieved. The goal is to transform this adversarial moment into a collaborative one.\n\n**Step 1 \u2014 Do not become defensive. Absorb the anger.**\n\nI would pause, maintain open body language, and respond calmly:\n\n'I hear you, and I understand why you are angry. You have been in pain for a year, the system has fought you at every turn, and now it feels like I am taking their side. I want to address that directly because it is important to me that you know where I stand.'\n\n**Step 2 \u2014 Restate your position clearly and without apology.**\n\n'I believe your pain is real. I can see it on the ultrasound \u2014 your tendon is damaged. I can measure it with your grip strength \u2014 you have lost nearly half your strength on the right. This is a legitimate injury and you are not faking it.\n\nBut here is where I need to be honest with you, because being honest with you is more important to me than telling you what you want to hear: putting you on permanent disability right now would not be the right thing to do for you.'\n\n**Step 3 \u2014 Explain WHY in terms of his interests, not medical policy.**\n\n'Here is why: you are 50 years old. If I write you off as permanently disabled right now, you are looking at 15-17 years before retirement age on a workers' compensation check that is a fraction of what you earn. Your three kids are still growing up. Your identity as a mechanic \u2014 as someone who works with his hands and provides for his family \u2014 that matters to you. I do not want to take that away from you for a condition that I believe we can treat.\n\nThere are treatments we have not tried yet. PRP \u2014 an injection using your own blood to stimulate healing \u2014 has strong evidence for exactly your condition. If that does not work, there is a surgery with an 85% success rate. And if we get you back to work, we are going to make sure your workplace is set up so this does not happen again.'\n\n**Step 4 \u2014 Validate the financial fear and provide concrete support.**\n\n'I also hear the financial pressure you are under, and I do not take that lightly. Here is what I AM going to do:\n- I am placing you on temporary total disability for the next 3 months while we pursue active treatment. You will receive workers' compensation wage replacement during this time.\n- I am prescribing a structured rehabilitation program.\n- I am scheduling you for a PRP injection.\n- I am requesting an ergonomic assessment of your workplace for when you are ready to return.\n\nIf after we have exhausted these options you still cannot work, I will be the first person to document that. But I owe it to you to try to get you better first.'\n\n**Step 5 \u2014 Re-establish the alliance.**\n\n'I know the insurance company has made you feel like the enemy is everyone on the medical side. I am not the insurance company. I am your doctor. My job is to get you out of pain and back to the life you want. I cannot do that if we are fighting each other. Can we work together on this?'\n\n**Step 6 \u2014 Check in.**\n\nWait for his response. If he remains angry, acknowledge it again: 'I understand this is not what you wanted to hear today. You do not have to agree with me right now. But I want you to think about what I have said, and I will see you next week. I am not going anywhere.'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: De-escalation through active listening, refusing to become adversarial, honest communication that serves the patient's long-term interests, reframing disability as a last resort rather than a first option, providing concrete financial protection (temporary disability), and explicitly rebuilding the therapeutic alliance.")
B.aFg=s([B.b23,B.aXZ,B.aUR,B.aU3,B.aNt,B.b_g,B.aZn,B.aXD,B.aW4,B.aVq,B.b_s],t.E)
B.PI=new A.a5("msk-lateral-epicondylitis-failed-conservative","Lateral Epicondylitis \u2014 Failed Conservative Treatment",B.aFg,!1)
B.aT0=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 55-year-old female presents to your PM&R clinic with bilateral heel pain for 2 years that has progressively worsened despite multiple treatment attempts. She works as an elementary school teacher and stands 6-8 hours per day. She reports severe first-step pain in the morning that takes 20-30 minutes to resolve, and escalating pain throughout the workday such that she is limping by afternoon. She has tried over-the-counter insoles, stretching (intermittently), and two courses of oral NSAIDs with minimal relief. She received one corticosteroid injection in each heel 6 months ago with temporary improvement lasting 3 weeks. Her BMI is 38, and she is aware of her weight. She says, "I know you are going to tell me to lose weight. Every doctor tells me that. But I cannot exercise because my feet hurt too much to walk."')
B.aOE=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **Pain Duration**: 2 years bilateral heel pain. Right worse than left. Onset insidious \u2014 no specific injury.\n- **Pain Character**: Sharp, stabbing pain at the medial calcaneal tubercle bilaterally. Worst with first steps in the morning (9/10 right, 7/10 left). Improves to 5/10 with walking, then worsens to 8/10 after prolonged standing. Better with rest and non-weight-bearing.\n- **Aggravating Factors**: Prolonged standing (teaching), walking on hard surfaces, end of the day, barefoot walking.\n- **Relieving Factors**: Rest, ice, elevation, cushioned shoes.\n- **Failed Treatments**: (1) Over-the-counter gel insoles \u2014 minimal relief; (2) Stretching \u2014 'tried it for a couple weeks, did not help'; (3) Ibuprofen 600 mg TID x 2 weeks \u2014 mild improvement while taking, returned after stopping; (4) Corticosteroid injection bilateral heels 6 months ago \u2014 3 weeks relief then pain returned.\n- **Functional Impact**: Limping by afternoon. Had to request a chair in her classroom (never sat before). Cannot walk her dog. Cannot exercise. Has gained 20 lbs since pain began (was BMI 34, now 38). Cannot keep up with students on field trips. Has missed 12 work days in the past year due to pain.\n- **PMH**: Obesity (BMI 38), type 2 diabetes (A1c 8.1%), hypertension, depression, bilateral knee osteoarthritis.\n- **Social**: Divorced. Two adult children. Lives alone. Elementary school teacher x 25 years. Enjoys (or enjoyed) walking, gardening, cooking.\n- **Weight History**: Struggled with weight since her 30s. Has tried multiple diets. Lost 40 lbs 5 years ago on a structured program but regained when she could no longer exercise due to foot pain. Feels defeated about weight management.\n- **Medications**: Lisinopril 20 mg daily, metformin 1000 mg BID, sertraline 100 mg daily, acetaminophen 1000 mg PRN.")
B.b1P=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 138/86 mmHg, HR 78 bpm, RR 14, SpO2 98% on RA, Temp 36.7C, BMI 38, Weight 220 lbs.\n\n**General**: Overweight female who limps into the exam room favoring the right foot. Removes shoes slowly with discomfort. Pleasant and cooperative.\n\n**Feet**:\n- **Inspection**: Bilateral pes planus (flat feet). Mild hallux valgus bilaterally. Callousing of medial heel bilaterally. No erythema or swelling. Intact skin.\n- **Palpation**: Exquisite point tenderness at the medial calcaneal tubercle bilaterally (right > left). Pain reproduced with deep palpation. Tenderness extends along the medial band of the plantar fascia on the right. Windlass test positive bilaterally (dorsiflexion of great toe reproduces heel pain).\n- **ROM**: Ankle dorsiflexion with knee extended \u2014 5 degrees bilaterally (normal >10 degrees \u2014 tight gastrocnemius-soleus complex). Dorsiflexion with knee flexed \u2014 10 degrees (improved \u2014 confirms gastrocnemius tightness as primary contributor).\n- **Strength**: Ankle plantarflexion 5/5 bilaterally. Ankle dorsiflexion 5/5 bilaterally. Toe flexors 4+/5 bilaterally. Intrinsic foot muscles \u2014 mild weakness bilaterally.\n- **Arch**: Collapses with weight-bearing. Navicular drop test positive (>10 mm drop bilaterally).\n\n**Gait**: Antalgic gait favoring right. Reduced push-off bilaterally. Overpronation throughout stance phase. Short stride length.\n\n**Knees**: Bilateral crepitus with ROM. Mild medial joint line tenderness bilaterally. No effusion. ROM 0-130 degrees bilaterally.\n\n**Neurovascular**: Dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses 2+ bilaterally. Sensation intact to monofilament (important \u2014 diabetic foot screening). No signs of peripheral neuropathy.\n\n**Imaging (brought by patient)**: Bilateral foot X-rays from 6 months ago \u2014 bilateral calcaneal spurs (incidental finding \u2014 not the cause of pain). No fractures. No evidence of stress fracture.")
B.b1A=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","This patient has failed initial conservative treatment for bilateral chronic plantar fasciitis. Discuss the biomechanical contributors, the differential diagnosis of chronic heel pain, and the evidence-based treatment algorithm.")
B.aWL=new A.c("+ Discuss the biomechanical factors contributing to her chronic plantar fasciitis, the differential diagnosis, and the stepwise treatment approach.","**Biomechanical Contributors**:\n1. **Obesity (BMI 38)**: Strong association with plantar fasciitis. Increased mechanical load \u2014 the plantar fascia bears approximately 2-3x body weight during walking. At 220 lbs, her fascia experiences approximately 440-660 lbs of force with each step.\n2. **Pes planus (flat feet)**: Excessive pronation increases tensile strain on the plantar fascia along its medial band.\n3. **Gastrocnemius tightness**: Dorsiflexion limited to 5 degrees (need >10 degrees for normal gait). Tight calf muscles force compensatory pronation and increase plantar fascia strain. This is one of the **most important modifiable factors**.\n4. **Prolonged standing**: 6-8 hours/day on hard surfaces. Cumulative microtrauma.\n5. **Inadequate footwear**: Teacher \u2014 may be wearing non-supportive shoes.\n\n**Differential Diagnosis of Chronic Heel Pain**:\n1. **Plantar fasciitis** (most likely \u2014 classic presentation): First-step pain, medial calcaneal tubercle tenderness, positive Windlass test.\n2. **Calcaneal stress fracture**: Consider if pain is not strictly at the medial tubercle. Squeeze test (medial-lateral calcaneal compression) would be positive. MRI to evaluate.\n3. **Tarsal tunnel syndrome**: Tibial nerve entrapment behind medial malleolus. Burning, tingling in the sole. Tinel sign at tarsal tunnel. EMG/NCS for confirmation.\n4. **Fat pad atrophy**: Central heel pain (not medial). More common in elderly. Reduced subcutaneous tissue on palpation.\n5. **Baxter neuropathy (inferior calcaneal nerve entrapment)**: First branch of the lateral plantar nerve. Pain at medial heel, can mimic plantar fasciitis. Consider if typical treatment fails.\n6. **Calcaneal spur**: Present on X-ray but NOT the cause of pain \u2014 calcaneal spurs are present in 15-25% of the asymptomatic population. The spur is at the origin of the flexor digitorum brevis, not the plantar fascia.\n7. **Plantar fascia rupture**: Especially after steroid injection. Acute worsening of pain with palpable defect.\n8. **Seronegative spondyloarthropathy**: Bilateral heel pain in a woman \u2014 consider ankylosing spondylitis or reactive arthritis. Ask about back pain, morning stiffness. Check HLA-B27, ESR, CRP if clinical suspicion.\n\n**Evidence-Based Treatment Algorithm**:\n\n**Tier 1 \u2014 Correct the Biomechanics (she has not done this adequately)**:\n1. **Custom orthotic insoles**: NOT over-the-counter gel insoles. Custom semi-rigid orthotics with medial arch support and cushioned heel post. Address her pes planus and overpronation. This is the most important intervention she has not received.\n2. **Gastrocnemius stretching program** (the single most evidence-supported exercise): Wall stretch with knee extended, 30-second holds, 3 repetitions, 3 times daily. Compliance is key \u2014 she did it for 2 weeks. Need sustained 6-8 week commitment. Also: eccentric calf stretches on a step.\n3. **Plantar fascia-specific stretching**: Cross-leg stretch \u2014 cross affected foot over opposite knee, pull toes into dorsiflexion, stretching the fascia. Hold 10 seconds, 10 repetitions before first steps in the morning.\n4. **Footwear modification**: Supportive shoes with cushioned heel and arch support worn at ALL times \u2014 including in the house (no barefoot walking).\n5. **Night splint**: Dorsiflexion night splint (maintains ankle at 0-5 degrees dorsiflexion overnight, preventing fascial contracture). Compliance is challenging but evidence supports efficacy.\n\n**Tier 2 \u2014 Advanced Conservative**:\n6. **Extracorporeal shockwave therapy (ESWT)**: Strong evidence for chronic plantar fasciitis failing 6 months of conservative treatment. Radial or focused shockwave. Typically 3-5 sessions. NNT ~4 for significant improvement.\n7. **Physical therapy \u2014 targeted program**: Intrinsic foot strengthening (towel curls, marble pickups), progressive loading of the plantar fascia (heel raises progressing to single-leg), and manual therapy.\n8. **Corticosteroid injection**: She had one round \u2014 repeat injections increase risk of plantar fascia rupture and fat pad atrophy. Use sparingly.\n\n**Tier 3 \u2014 Advanced Interventional**:\n9. **Platelet-rich plasma (PRP) injection**: Emerging evidence \u2014 may be superior to corticosteroid for long-term outcomes.\n10. **Ultrasound-guided percutaneous tenotomy (Tenex)**: Minimally invasive debridement of degenerative fascia tissue.\n11. **Surgical plantar fasciotomy**: Last resort \u2014 partial plantar fascia release. Reserved for failure of all conservative measures after 12 months. Risk of arch collapse.")
B.aN5=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Develop a comprehensive treatment plan that addresses her chronic plantar fasciitis, the biomechanical contributors, and the barriers to exercise created by her foot pain.")
B.aRE=new A.c("+ Create an integrated management plan addressing foot pain, weight management, and the exercise-pain cycle.","**Comprehensive Treatment Plan**:\n\n**1. Break the Pain-Inactivity-Weight Cycle**:\n- Her central problem: foot pain prevents exercise, lack of exercise causes weight gain, weight gain worsens foot pain. We must intervene at every point simultaneously.\n\n**2. Foot Pain Treatment (Aggressive Multi-Modal)**:\n- **Custom orthotics**: Prescription for custom semi-rigid foot orthotics from a certified orthotist. Medial arch support with deep heel cup and cushioned posting. Wear in all shoes.\n- **Gastrocnemius stretching**: Demonstrate in clinic. Written home program. 30-second holds, 3 reps, 3 times daily. Set phone reminders. This must be done for minimum 8 weeks consistently.\n- **Plantar fascia-specific stretch**: Before getting out of bed every morning \u2014 10 repetitions of cross-leg toe dorsiflexion stretch.\n- **Night splint**: Prescribe dorsiflexion night splint. Explain that the first week is uncomfortable but most patients adapt. Use nightly for 3 months.\n- **ESWT referral**: Given 2 years of symptoms and failed conservative treatment, she qualifies for extracorporeal shockwave therapy. Refer to a center offering this.\n- **Footwear prescription**: Supportive athletic shoes (Brooks, ASICS, New Balance \u2014 brands with pronation control). Wear indoors and outdoors. No barefoot, no flip-flops, no flat shoes.\n- **Ice**: Frozen water bottle rolling under arch for 10-15 minutes after prolonged standing.\n\n**3. Non-Weight-Bearing Exercise Program (Start Immediately)**:\n- **Aquatic exercise**: Pool walking, water aerobics. Water eliminates ground reaction force \u2014 she can exercise without foot pain. 2-3 times weekly.\n- **Stationary cycling**: Minimal plantar fascia loading. Start 15 minutes at low resistance, progress.\n- **Seated upper body exercises**: Resistance bands, arm ergometer. Burns calories without foot loading.\n- **Chair yoga or seated fitness classes**: Maintain flexibility and core strength.\n- Goal: 150 minutes/week of moderate-intensity exercise through non-weight-bearing modalities while treating the foot pain.\n\n**4. Weight Management Referral**:\n- Refer to a multidisciplinary weight management program (dietitian, behavioral psychologist, exercise physiologist).\n- Discuss pharmacologic options: GLP-1 receptor agonists (semaglutide, tirzepatide) have dual benefit \u2014 weight loss AND improved diabetes control (her A1c is 8.1%). Coordinate with PCP.\n- Set realistic goals: 5-10% body weight loss (11-22 lbs) would significantly reduce plantar fascia loading and improve diabetes.\n\n**5. Workplace Modifications**:\n- **Anti-fatigue mat**: Standing mat at her teaching station.\n- **Seating**: Alternating sit-stand throughout the day (sit for 15 min every hour).\n- **Supportive shoes at work**: Must wear orthotics and supportive footwear during teaching.\n- **Brief stretch breaks**: 30-second calf stretch between classes.\n\n**6. Follow-Up Timeline**:\n- 4 weeks: Reassess pain, orthotic fit, stretching compliance, exercise tolerance.\n- 8 weeks: Evaluate response to conservative program. If insufficient, proceed with ESWT.\n- 3 months: Reassess comprehensively. If still failing, consider PRP injection or advanced interventions.\n- Ongoing: Weight management follow-up, diabetes monitoring.")
B.aQY=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","This patient has failed initial treatment partly due to fragmented care \u2014 she received a steroid injection without a comprehensive biomechanical plan. How should the system manage chronic musculoskeletal conditions like this?")
B.aXy=new A.c("+ What systems-level changes would improve outcomes for chronic musculoskeletal conditions like plantar fasciitis?","**Systems-Level Approach**:\n\n**1. Structured Treatment Pathways**:\n- Implement evidence-based **clinical pathways** for common MSK conditions (plantar fasciitis, rotator cuff, low back pain) in primary care and orthopedic settings.\n- Pathway for plantar fasciitis should mandate: biomechanical assessment, appropriate orthotic prescription, stretching program education, and a minimum 6-8 week trial of comprehensive conservative treatment before injection.\n- This patient received an injection as a second visit intervention without ever receiving orthotics or a supervised stretching program \u2014 a common failure of fragmented care.\n\n**2. Physiatry as MSK Medical Home**:\n- PM&R physicians are ideally positioned to manage chronic MSK conditions because we address biomechanics, function, exercise, and pain holistically.\n- Referral to physiatry early in the course (not after 2 years of failed treatment) would have prevented chronicity.\n\n**3. Integrated Weight Management**:\n- Obesity is a modifiable risk factor for plantar fasciitis, knee OA, diabetes, and many other conditions. Healthcare systems should co-locate or integrate weight management services with MSK care.\n- The exercise-pain paradox (cannot exercise because of pain, cannot lose weight without exercise) should be recognized as a systems problem requiring multi-modal solutions.\n\n**4. Insurance Coverage Gaps**:\n- Custom orthotics: Often not covered or require excessive prior authorization. Advocate for coverage \u2014 orthotics are cost-effective compared to surgery.\n- ESWT: Variable coverage. May require appeal with documentation of failed conservative treatment.\n- Weight management programs: GLP-1 agonists are expensive. Ensure insurance covers evidence-based weight loss interventions as medical necessities.\n- Pool/aquatic therapy: May require specific prescription language to be covered.\n\n**5. Compliance Support**:\n- Stretching programs fail because patients are given a handout and sent home. Systems should include: video instruction, app-based exercise tracking, follow-up PT visits to verify technique, and accountability check-ins.\n- Night splint compliance is low \u2014 systems should include a follow-up call at 1 week to troubleshoot comfort issues.\n\n**6. Outcome Tracking**:\n- Track patient-reported outcomes (FAAM \u2014 Foot and Ankle Ability Measure) at each visit.\n- Monitor compliance with stretching, orthotic wear, and exercise program.\n- Use data to refine treatment pathways.")
B.aXJ=new A.c(u.j,'You need to discuss weight as a contributing factor to her plantar fasciitis and overall health. She preemptively says: "I know you are going to tell me to lose weight. Every doctor says that. They look at me and see a fat person, not a patient. I have tried everything. I lost 40 pounds once and gained it all back. I am tired of being judged. Can we just talk about my feet?"')
B.aTJ=new A.c("+ How do you discuss weight as a modifiable risk factor for plantar fasciitis without shaming the patient, while maintaining clinical honesty?",'**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Acknowledge her experience and frustration first.**\n"I hear you, and I am sorry that has been your experience. Being told to \'just lose weight\' without a plan, without support, and without anyone addressing the reason you came in \u2014 that is not helpful medicine. It is lazy medicine. And you deserve better than that."\n\n**2. Start with her chief complaint \u2014 respect her request.**\n"So let us start with your feet, because that is why you are here, and I have a specific plan for your heel pain that does not begin or end with weight loss. I want to talk about orthotics, stretching, footwear, and a treatment called shockwave therapy that can help heal the damaged tissue. These interventions can improve your pain regardless of your weight. Let me walk you through them."\n[Present the foot treatment plan in detail first \u2014 show her you are taking her complaint seriously.]\n\n**3. Then transition to weight with honesty and partnership.**\n"Now, I want to be honest with you about something, and I am going to say it differently than you may have heard before. Your weight is not the cause of your plantar fasciitis \u2014 plenty of thin people get it too. But at your current weight, the forces on your plantar fascia with every step are approximately 500 pounds. That is a lot of mechanical stress on a structure that is only a few millimeters thick. Reducing that load, even by 10-15%, would meaningfully reduce your pain and improve your response to treatment. I am not saying this because I am judging you. I am saying it because the physics of it matters, and I would not be doing my job if I did not mention it."\n\n**4. Acknowledge the cycle she is trapped in.**\n"But here is what I think every doctor who has told you to lose weight has missed: you cannot exercise because your feet hurt. So telling you to lose weight through exercise is telling you to do something that is physically impossible right now. That is not a failure of willpower \u2014 it is a failure of medical planning. We need to treat your feet AND create exercise options that do not involve standing on them."\n\n**5. Offer concrete, non-judgmental exercise alternatives.**\n"Here is my plan. While we treat your feet, I want you to start exercising in a pool. Water takes the weight off your feet completely. You can swim, walk in the pool, or join a water aerobics class \u2014 and you will get a cardiovascular workout without a single ounce of pressure on your heels. I also want you on a stationary bike, which puts almost no load on the plantar fascia. The goal is not punishment or a crash diet. The goal is to break the cycle: treat the pain so you can move, move so you can lose weight, lose weight so the pain improves further."\n\n**6. Address the 40-pound loss and regain without judgment.**\n"You lost 40 pounds once. That tells me you are capable of extraordinary discipline. The fact that you regained it does not mean you failed \u2014 it means the support system was not sustainable. Weight regain after loss is the norm, not the exception. It is biology, not willpower. If you are open to it, I would like to refer you to a weight management program that includes medical options \u2014 there are newer medications that can help with appetite and blood sugar control simultaneously. This is not about judgment. It is about giving you tools that work with your biology instead of against it."\n\n**7. Reaffirm her autonomy.**\n"Ultimately, this is your body and your decision. I am going to treat your feet aggressively regardless of what you decide about weight management. But I would not be a good doctor if I did not tell you that addressing weight will help your feet, your knees, your diabetes, and your energy. I am here to partner with you on whatever you are ready to do \u2014 no judgment, no pressure, just options."\n\n**Key Principles**: Led with the chief complaint \u2014 treated her as a patient with heel pain, not a patient with obesity. Acknowledged prior experiences of weight stigma in healthcare. Separated weight from moral failure. Explained the biomechanics factually without shaming. Identified the exercise-pain trap and offered solutions. Praised her past success rather than focusing on regain. Offered pharmacologic and programmatic support for weight management. Affirmed her autonomy and maintained the therapeutic relationship.')
B.aE9=s([B.aT0,B.aOE,B.b1P,B.b1A,B.aWL,B.aN5,B.aRE,B.aQY,B.aXy,B.aXJ,B.aTJ],t.E)
B.Qm=new A.a5("msk-plantar-fasciitis-chronic","Chronic Plantar Fasciitis",B.aE9,!1)
B.aQ0=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 19-year-old male college football player (Division I offensive lineman) is referred to your sports medicine PM&R clinic 3 days after experiencing transient quadriplegia during a game. After making a head-down block, he experienced immediate bilateral upper and lower extremity tingling, burning, and weakness lasting approximately 8 minutes. He was immobilized on the field and transported to the ED, where his neurological exam was completely normal by arrival. MRI of the cervical spine revealed developmental cervical stenosis with a Torg-Pavlov ratio of 0.70 at C4-5 (normal >0.80) and no cord signal abnormality. He is accompanied by his father (a former college football player) and his mother. The family is here to get clearance to return to play. The father says, "This is his dream. He has NFL scouts watching him. This was just a stinger \u2014 he was fine in 10 minutes."')
B.aXS=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **Event**: During a game, made a head-down block (spearing technique). Immediately felt 'electric shock' down all four extremities followed by bilateral arm and leg weakness/numbness. Could not move arms or legs for approximately 3-4 minutes. Motor function returned gradually over 5-8 minutes \u2014 arms first, then legs. By the time paramedics arrived, he had full strength but reported residual tingling in both hands for another 30 minutes.\n- **ED Evaluation**: Neurological exam normal by arrival. Full motor strength 5/5 throughout. Sensation intact. Reflexes normal. MRI cervical spine: developmental canal stenosis C3-C6, most severe at C4-5 (Torg-Pavlov ratio 0.70). No disc herniation. No cord edema or hemorrhage. No ligamentous injury.\n- **Prior Episodes**: Reports one prior episode 8 months ago \u2014 bilateral arm tingling and weakness lasting 'about 30 seconds' after a tackle. Did not report it to training staff. Continued playing. \"It went away, so I figured it was nothing.\"\n- **Current Status**: Completely neurologically normal. No pain. No residual symptoms. Feels '100% fine' and wants to play this Saturday.\n- **Athletic History**: Started football at age 8. Offensive lineman since high school. Starter since freshman year of college. Second-team All-Conference last year. Projected late-round NFL draft pick if he has a strong junior season.\n- **Family**: Father played college football (safety), no history of cervical issues. Mother is an accountant \u2014 appears anxious and has been quiet. Younger brother (16) also plays football.\n- **PMH**: No other medical history. No prior neck pain or injury.\n- **Medications**: None.\n- **Social**: Scholarship athlete. Sports management major. Family has significant financial hope tied to potential NFL career.")
B.aNC=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 124/76 mmHg, HR 58 bpm (athlete's bradycardia), RR 14, SpO2 99% on RA, Temp 36.7C, Height 6'4\", Weight 295 lbs, BMI 35.9.\n\n**General**: Large, muscular male. Appears anxious but cooperative. Wearing team gear.\n\n**Cervical Spine**:\n- **Inspection**: Thick, muscular neck. No deformity.\n- **Palpation**: No tenderness over spinous processes or paraspinals. No step-off.\n- **ROM**: Full active ROM \u2014 flexion, extension, lateral flexion, rotation. No pain.\n- **Spurling test**: Negative bilaterally.\n- **Special tests**: Lhermitte sign negative. Hoffmann sign negative bilaterally.\n\n**Neurological**:\n- **Motor**: 5/5 throughout upper and lower extremities bilaterally. No focal weakness.\n- **Sensory**: Intact to light touch, pinprick, proprioception throughout.\n- **Reflexes**: 2+ throughout. No Babinski. No Hoffmann. No clonus.\n- **Coordination**: Finger-to-nose, heel-to-shin normal.\n\n**Gait**: Normal. Tandem gait normal.\n\n**Musculoskeletal**: Full ROM all joints. No upper extremity weakness. Good grip strength bilaterally.\n\n**Imaging Review**: MRI C-spine \u2014 sagittal T2 images show narrowed AP canal diameter at C3-C6. Torg-Pavlov ratio 0.70 at C4-5 (measured as ratio of sagittal canal diameter to vertebral body diameter; normal >0.80). No cord compression on axial images. No cord signal change. No disc herniation. MRI functional reserve (CSF space around the cord) is diminished but cord is not deformed.")
B.aN0=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","Discuss the pathophysiology of cervical cord neurapraxia (CCN), the significance of developmental cervical stenosis, and the criteria for return-to-play decisions.")
B.b3j=new A.c("+ Explain cervical cord neurapraxia, the role of the Torg ratio, and the evidence regarding return-to-play after transient quadriplegia.","**Cervical Cord Neurapraxia (CCN) \u2014 Transient Quadriplegia**:\n\n**Pathophysiology**:\n- **CCN** is a transient episode of bilateral sensory and/or motor dysfunction in the upper and lower extremities following cervical hyperextension, hyperflexion, or axial loading.\n- Caused by **transient compression of the cervical spinal cord** within a developmentally narrowed spinal canal.\n- The cord is temporarily compressed between the posterior vertebral body/disc and the ligamentum flavum, causing ischemia or mechanical distortion.\n- Symptoms resolve as the neck returns to neutral and compression is relieved \u2014 typically within minutes to hours.\n- **DISTINCT from a 'stinger' (burner)**: Stingers are unilateral brachial plexus traction injuries (one arm only). CCN is bilateral with involvement of arms AND legs \u2014 this indicates spinal cord, not peripheral nerve, involvement.\n\n**Significance of the Torg-Pavlov Ratio**:\n- Ratio = sagittal spinal canal diameter / sagittal vertebral body diameter (measured on lateral X-ray or MRI).\n- **Normal**: >0.80. **Stenotic**: <0.80.\n- This patient: 0.70 \u2014 significant developmental stenosis.\n- **Limitations**: The Torg ratio has high sensitivity (~90%) but low positive predictive value (~12%) for CCN. Many athletes with low Torg ratios never experience CCN. The ratio alone does not dictate return-to-play decisions.\n- **Functional stenosis** (MRI assessment of CSF space around the cord) is more clinically relevant than the Torg ratio. Loss of CSF buffer (functional reserve) means the cord has less protection during dynamic motion.\n\n**Recurrence Risk**:\n- Athletes with one episode of CCN and developmental stenosis have a **56% recurrence rate** if they return to contact sports (Torg et al.).\n- This patient has had **TWO episodes** (one unreported) \u2014 his recurrence risk is even higher.\n- Each episode carries the risk of **incomplete recovery** \u2014 one episode may result in permanent spinal cord injury.\n\n**Return-to-Play Criteria and Controversy**:\n\n**Absolute Contraindications to Return (consensus)**:\n1. **Cord signal change on MRI** (edema, hemorrhage, myelomalacia)\n2. **Ligamentous instability**\n3. **Residual neurological deficit**\n4. **Recurrent episodes** (this patient \u2014 two episodes)\n\n**Relative Contraindications**:\n1. Single episode with complete resolution and normal MRI cord signal, but developmental stenosis persists (the canal does not change)\n2. Significant functional stenosis (reduced CSF space) even without cord signal change\n\n**For This Patient**:\n- Two episodes of transient quadriplegia with developmental stenosis (Torg 0.70) = **strong recommendation against return to contact sports**.\n- The risk of permanent spinal cord injury with continued play is unacceptably high.\n- This is a **career-ending recommendation** \u2014 one of the most difficult conversations in sports medicine.")
B.b2_=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Outline the comprehensive management including further workup, activity restrictions, and long-term follow-up for this athlete.")
B.aWW=new A.c("+ What additional evaluation is needed, and what are the activity restrictions and long-term management recommendations?","**Additional Evaluation**:\n\n1. **Dynamic flexion/extension MRI or CT**: Assess for dynamic stenosis \u2014 the canal may narrow further with extension (the position of injury). This strengthens the case for disqualification if cord compression worsens with motion.\n2. **Somatosensory evoked potentials (SSEPs)**: Baseline SSEPs can detect subclinical conduction abnormalities even with a normal clinical exam. Abnormal SSEPs in a clinically normal patient indicate subclinical cord compromise and are an additional contraindication.\n3. **CT cervical spine**: Better osseous detail than MRI. Confirm no occult fracture or congenital anomaly contributing to stenosis.\n4. **Neurosurgical consultation**: For a second opinion on anatomic risk and to discuss whether surgical decompression could theoretically allow return to play. (Note: There is NO consensus that surgery allows safe return to contact sports with developmental stenosis.)\n\n**Activity Restrictions**:\n- **No return to football or any contact/collision sport**: This is the primary recommendation.\n- **Non-contact sports**: Permitted after full evaluation confirms no residual cord compromise. Swimming, cycling, weight training (with cervical-safe technique), running.\n- **Cervical strengthening**: Aggressive cervical musculature strengthening program \u2014 strong neck muscles provide dynamic stabilization. While this does not change bony anatomy, it reduces the risk during activities of daily living.\n- **Avoid high-risk activities**: No diving into water, no roller coasters, no trampolines, no wrestling. Anything that could cause sudden cervical hyperextension or axial loading.\n\n**Long-Term Management**:\n- **Annual follow-up**: Neurological exam and symptom review. Any new symptoms (hand clumsiness, gait unsteadiness, bowel/bladder changes) warrant urgent MRI to evaluate for myelopathy from progressive stenosis.\n- **MRI surveillance**: Consider repeat MRI in 2-3 years or if symptoms develop. Degenerative changes may worsen the stenosis over time.\n- **Cervical myelopathy screening**: Developmental stenosis at age 19 may lead to symptomatic cervical myelopathy decades earlier than the general population, especially with superimposed degenerative changes.\n- **Education**: The patient must understand the long-term implications \u2014 he has a narrow spinal canal that he will live with forever. Even a minor car accident or fall could cause cord injury that a person with a normal canal would tolerate without issue.\n\n**Academic and Career Planning**:\n- If he loses his athletic scholarship, the university's student-athlete services, NCAA insurance programs, and academic advisors must be engaged.\n- NCAA Catastrophic Injury Insurance may provide coverage if he is medically disqualified.\n- Career counseling \u2014 coaching, sports management, athletic training, broadcasting. Many former athletes find fulfilling careers in sports without playing.")
B.aUI=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","Discuss the systems-level considerations in medical disqualification of a scholarship athlete, including conflicts of interest and institutional pressures.")
B.aTt=new A.c("+ What systems-level challenges exist when a team physician recommends disqualification of a high-profile athlete, and how are athletes protected?","**Systems-Level Challenges**:\n\n**1. Conflicts of Interest**:\n- **Team physicians** employed by or contracted with the university may face pressure (explicit or implicit) from coaching staff and athletic departments to clear athletes to play.\n- Financial incentives: A winning football program generates millions in revenue. Losing a starting offensive lineman affects the team's competitive position.\n- The physician's primary obligation is ALWAYS to the patient, not the team. This must be documented and understood by all parties.\n\n**2. Independent Medical Evaluation**:\n- For career-ending decisions, an **independent physician** (not employed by the university) should confirm the recommendation.\n- The athlete has the right to a second opinion. Encourage this \u2014 it protects both the athlete and the recommending physician.\n- Document that the recommendation is based on medical evidence, not opinion.\n\n**3. NCAA Protections**:\n- **NCAA Constitution**: Requires that the well-being of student-athletes is a core commitment.\n- **Medical disqualification policies**: Most conferences require that the team physician's medical decision is final and cannot be overridden by coaches or administrators.\n- **Scholarship protection**: NCAA rules generally protect medically disqualified athletes' scholarships for the remainder of their degree. The university cannot revoke the scholarship because of the injury.\n- **NCAA Catastrophic Injury Insurance Program**: Provides coverage for athletes with life-altering injuries, including lost earning potential.\n\n**4. Documentation Requirements**:\n- Detailed medical record documenting: diagnosis, imaging findings, recurrence risk, evidence supporting the recommendation, discussion with the patient and family.\n- Written disqualification letter with specific activities contraindicated.\n- Documentation that the athlete was informed of risks and the basis for the decision.\n\n**5. Second-Opinion Seeking**:\n- The family may seek out physicians willing to clear him. 'Doctor shopping' for return-to-play clearance is a known phenomenon.\n- If another physician clears him against your recommendation, document your disagreement and the basis for it. The liability risk shifts but does not disappear.\n\n**6. Younger Brother Consideration**:\n- The 16-year-old brother also plays football. Cervical stenosis has a heritable component. He should be screened with cervical spine imaging before continuing contact sports.\n- This is a family health issue, not just an individual one.")
B.aPR=new A.c(u.j,'You have determined that this athlete should not return to contact sports. The father says: "This is his ticket to the NFL. We are talking about millions of dollars and a career he has worked for since he was 8 years old. You are going to take that away based on one MRI? He feels fine. I played with stingers every game and I am fine. Find a doctor who will clear him if you will not." The athlete is staring at the floor. The mother is crying quietly.')
B.aOX=new A.c("+ How do you deliver a career-ending recommendation to a young athlete whose family has significant emotional and financial investment in his career, while addressing the father's resistance?",'**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Pause. Address the father\'s emotions before his arguments.**\n"Mr. [Name], I hear you. I can see how much this means to you and to your son. You have watched him work for this since he was 8 years old. You have driven him to practices, sat in the stands, and dreamed about this with him. This is not just his loss \u2014 it is yours too. And I am sorry."\n\n**2. Turn to the athlete \u2014 he is the patient, not his father.**\n"[Player\'s name], I want to talk to you directly, because this is your body and your life. I need you to hear what I am about to say, and I need you to understand that I am saying it because I care about your future \u2014 all of it, not just the next football season."\n\n**3. Explain the medical situation in clear, non-technical terms.**\n"Your spinal canal \u2014 the tunnel that protects your spinal cord \u2014 is narrower than normal. You were born this way. It is not something you did wrong. But it means that your spinal cord has less room and less protection than a person with a normal-sized canal. When you made that head-down block, your spinal cord was briefly pinched. That is why you could not move your arms or legs for 8 minutes. This has now happened twice."\n\n**4. Be direct about the risk. Do not soften it.**\n"Here is what concerns me. Each time this happens, you are rolling the dice. The next time, the pinching could be more severe, and instead of 8 minutes of paralysis, it could be permanent. I am talking about a wheelchair for the rest of your life. I am talking about not being able to hold your children, not being able to walk across the stage at graduation, not being able to live independently. I am not trying to scare you. I am trying to make sure you understand what is at stake."\n\n**5. Address the father\'s comparison to his own experience.**\n[Turn to father] "Mr. [Name], you said you played with stingers and you are fine. I believe you. Stingers are a completely different injury \u2014 they involve the nerves in the shoulder area, and they affect one arm. What happened to your son is different. He lost function in all four limbs. That means his spinal cord, not a peripheral nerve, was involved. That is the difference between a muscle cramp and a heart attack. They are not the same thing."\n\n**6. Acknowledge the NFL dream directly \u2014 do not dismiss it.**\n"I understand that this recommendation may mean the end of his football career. I understand the financial implications. I understand what the NFL represents for your family. I did not make this decision lightly, and I am not making it casually. But I would rather he be alive and healthy at 30 than paralyzed at 20. No amount of money is worth his spinal cord."\n\n**7. Address the mother \u2014 she has been silent but is clearly distressed.**\n"Mrs. [Name], you have been very quiet. I suspect you are feeling what every mother feels when she hears this \u2014 relieved and heartbroken at the same time. Am I right?"\n[Allow her to speak. She may be the ally in this conversation \u2014 many mothers in this situation privately want their son to stop playing but feel they cannot say so in front of a father invested in the athletic career.]\n\n**8. Offer a path forward \u2014 not just a closed door.**\n"[Player\'s name], football is not your only identity, even though it might feel that way right now. You are a Division I athlete with a scholarship, a sports management degree in progress, and a network that most 19-year-olds would envy. Coaches, scouts, broadcasters, trainers, managers \u2014 there are hundreds of careers in football that do not require you to risk your spinal cord. And your scholarship is protected \u2014 the university cannot take it away because of a medical disqualification."\n\n**9. Offer a second opinion \u2014 and mean it.**\n"I also want you to know that you have the right to a second opinion, and I encourage you to get one. I am confident in my recommendation, but this decision is too important for you to wonder \'what if.\' I can refer you to an independent spine specialist or a sports medicine physician at another institution. If they disagree with me, we will talk about it. But I believe they will tell you the same thing."\n\n**10. End with what matters most.**\n"In 20 years, I want you to be coaching your own kids\' football team, or running a sports agency, or calling games on TV. I want you to look back and be grateful that you walked away with your health. I know this is not what you wanted to hear today. But this is the right decision, and I think deep down, you know it."\n\n**Key Principles**: Acknowledged the emotional and financial weight of the decision. Spoke directly to the athlete (the patient). Used clear, non-technical language. Did not soften the risk \u2014 honest about potential permanent paralysis. Addressed the father\'s stinger comparison with specific medical distinction. Included the mother. Offered alternative paths forward. Encouraged a second opinion to build trust and reduce the feeling of a unilateral decision. Ended with a vision of a fulfilling future beyond football.')
B.aFp=s([B.aQ0,B.aXS,B.aNC,B.aN0,B.b3j,B.b2_,B.aWW,B.aUI,B.aTt,B.aPR,B.aOX],t.E)
B.QO=new A.a5("msk-cervical-neurapraxia","Cervical Spinal Cord Neurapraxia",B.aFp,!1)
B.aQa=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 52-year-old male warehouse worker presents to the outpatient PM&R clinic with a 2-year history of worsening low back pain. He reports constant, dull aching across the lumbosacral region with intermittent radiation into the bilateral buttocks, rated 7/10 at baseline. He has failed conservative treatment including multiple courses of physical therapy, NSAIDs, and two epidural steroid injections with transient relief. He is currently taking oxycodone 10 mg TID (prescribed by his primary care physician) and reports escalating use, now occasionally taking an extra dose. He has been on modified duty at work but is now out on workers' compensation leave for the past 3 months. He reports difficulty sleeping, low mood, and increasing social isolation.")
B.aWI=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","Key areas to assess in chronic low back pain:\n- **Red flags**: Assess for cauda equina syndrome (saddle anesthesia, bowel/bladder dysfunction, progressive motor weakness), malignancy (unexplained weight loss, history of cancer, night pain unrelieved by rest), infection (fever, IV drug use, recent procedure), and fracture (osteoporosis, trauma, steroid use).\n- **Yellow flags (psychosocial)**: Catastrophizing, fear-avoidance beliefs, depression/anxiety, job dissatisfaction, ongoing litigation/compensation claims, passive coping strategies.\n- **Pain characteristics**: Mechanical vs. inflammatory pattern. Worse with flexion (disc) vs. extension (facet/stenosis). Radicular symptoms (dermatomal distribution, positive neural tension signs).\n- **Opioid assessment**: Current dose in morphine milligram equivalents (MME), signs of opioid use disorder (SOAPP-R, ORT screening), aberrant behaviors (early refills, lost prescriptions, multiple prescribers).\n- **Functional status**: ADL limitations, sleep quality (Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index), mood screening (PHQ-9, GAD-7), ability to perform job duties.\n- **Prior treatments**: Document all failed interventions with duration and response. Review imaging already obtained.")
B.aWF=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 142/88 mmHg, HR 82 bpm, BMI 34.2 kg/m2.\n\n**Inspection**: Mildly antalgic gait with shortened stride length bilaterally. Flattened lumbar lordosis. No visible deformity or skin changes.\n\n**Palpation**: Diffuse paraspinal tenderness L3-S1 bilaterally, greater on the left. No step-off deformity. SI joint compression test mildly positive on left.\n\n**Range of Motion**: Lumbar flexion limited to 40 degrees (pain-limited). Extension limited to 10 degrees with reproduction of bilateral buttock pain. Lateral bending reduced bilaterally.\n\n**Neurologic**: Strength 5/5 in bilateral lower extremities. Sensation intact to light touch in all dermatomes. Deep tendon reflexes 2+ at patella and Achilles bilaterally. No clonus.\n\n**Special Tests**: Straight leg raise negative bilaterally. FABER (Patrick) test positive on left (SI joint). Waddell signs: 2/5 positive (superficial tenderness, pain with axial loading), suggesting possible non-organic overlay.\n\n**Behavioral observations**: Patient grimaces disproportionately during exam maneuvers. Guarded movement patterns throughout.")
B.aOj=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","Given the 2-year history of pain with psychosocial complexity, opioid dependence, and failed conservative measures, this patient requires a comprehensive biopsychosocial reassessment before proceeding with further interventions.")
B.aXW=new A.c("+ Discuss the concept of central sensitization in chronic low back pain and how it influences your diagnostic approach. When is repeat imaging appropriate?",'**Central Sensitization**:\nCentral sensitization is a maladaptive neuroplastic process in which the central nervous system amplifies nociceptive signaling, leading to:\n- **Allodynia**: Pain from normally non-painful stimuli (e.g., light touch causes pain)\n- **Hyperalgesia**: Exaggerated pain response to mildly painful stimuli\n- **Expanded receptive fields**: Pain spreading beyond the original injury site\n- **Temporal summation**: Pain that worsens with repetitive stimulation (wind-up)\n\nClinical indicators in this patient include: diffuse bilateral tenderness beyond anatomic structures, pain disproportionate to exam findings, 2 years of escalating pain despite treatment, and comorbid sleep disturbance and mood changes (which both lower pain thresholds).\n\n**Quantitative Sensory Testing (QST)** can objectively measure altered pain processing but is primarily a research tool.\n\n**Imaging Appropriateness**:\n- **Repeat imaging is NOT indicated** unless new red flags emerge (progressive neurologic deficit, new bowel/bladder symptoms, trauma, suspected malignancy).\n- Prior imaging should be reviewed. MRI findings of disc degeneration, bulges, and facet arthropathy are **highly prevalent in asymptomatic individuals** (Brinjikji et al. \u2014 50% of 40-year-olds have disc degeneration on MRI).\n- Over-imaging leads to **nocebo effect** \u2014 patients told they have "degenerative disc disease" catastrophize more and have worse outcomes.\n- The clinical picture is consistent with **chronic nociplastic pain** (pain arising from altered nociception without clear nociceptive or neuropathic etiology, per IASP 2017 terminology).')
B.aNs=new A.c("+ What psychosocial factors must be addressed, and how do you screen for them?","**Critical Psychosocial Factors (Yellow Flags)**:\n\n1. **Depression and Anxiety**: Screen with PHQ-9 and GAD-7. This patient reports low mood, social isolation, and sleep disturbance \u2014 all concerning. Depression is the strongest predictor of chronic pain disability.\n\n2. **Fear-Avoidance Beliefs**: Assess with the Fear-Avoidance Beliefs Questionnaire (FABQ). Patients who believe activity will worsen their pain become deconditioned, perpetuating the pain cycle. His guarded movements and work avoidance suggest high fear-avoidance.\n\n3. **Pain Catastrophizing**: Screen with the Pain Catastrophizing Scale (PCS). Catastrophizing (rumination, magnification, helplessness) predicts poor surgical and rehabilitative outcomes.\n\n4. **Workers' Compensation and Secondary Gain**: Ongoing compensation claims are associated with delayed recovery. This is NOT malingering \u2014 the system itself creates incentive misalignment. Assess job satisfaction, relationship with employer, and desire to return to work.\n\n5. **Opioid Use Disorder Screening**: Administer the Screener and Opioid Assessment for Patients with Pain (SOAPP-R). His current dose is oxycodone 10 mg TID = 30 mg/day oxycodone x 1.5 conversion factor = **45 MME/day**. CDC guidelines recommend caution above 50 MME and avoiding above 90 MME. While below the 90 MME threshold, he is approaching the caution zone and showing aberrant behaviors. His extra dosing is an aberrant behavior requiring further evaluation.\n\n6. **Sleep disturbance**: Poor sleep amplifies central sensitization. Screen for obstructive sleep apnea (high BMI, STOP-BANG questionnaire) and treat aggressively.\n\n7. **Social support**: Isolation worsens outcomes. Assess family dynamics, recreational activities, and community engagement.")
B.aWO=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT",'You decide to enroll the patient in an interdisciplinary chronic pain rehabilitation program. He asks why he needs "more physical therapy" when it has not worked before.')
B.aNv=new A.c("+ Design a comprehensive interdisciplinary pain management program for this patient, including medication optimization and opioid taper strategy.",'**Interdisciplinary Pain Rehabilitation Program (IPRP)**:\nThis differs fundamentally from standard PT. It is a structured, typically 3-4 week intensive program (4-6 hours/day) with coordinated team-based care.\n\n**Team Components**:\n1. **Physical Therapy**: Graded exercise therapy using quota-based (not pain-contingent) progression. Focus on aerobic conditioning, core stabilization, and functional task training. Goal: break the fear-avoidance cycle. Start with aquatic therapy if needed to reduce load.\n2. **Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT)**: Weekly individual and group sessions addressing catastrophizing, fear-avoidance, pain neuroscience education, and coping strategies. Evidence shows CBT reduces pain intensity by 30-50% in chronic LBP.\n3. **Occupational Therapy**: Ergonomic assessment and job simulation. Activity pacing and energy conservation strategies. Return-to-work planning with graded exposure.\n4. **Psychology/Psychiatry**: Formal depression and anxiety treatment. Consider SSRI/SNRI (duloxetine 60 mg daily has dual benefit for pain and depression).\n5. **Pain Neuroscience Education (PNE)**: Explain central sensitization in patient-friendly terms. Reframe pain as a "volume knob turned too high" rather than ongoing tissue damage. This is evidence-based and improves outcomes.\n\n**Medication Optimization**:\n- **Start duloxetine 30 mg daily, titrate to 60 mg** \u2014 FDA-approved for chronic musculoskeletal pain and treats comorbid depression.\n- **Add topical diclofenac** for localized flares as needed.\n- **Consider low-dose tricyclic antidepressant (amitriptyline 10-25 mg at bedtime)** for sleep and pain modulation.\n- **Discontinue chronic NSAID use** if present (GI and renal risk with long-term use).\n\n**Opioid Taper Strategy**:\n1. Have an honest conversation about risks of chronic opioids (hyperalgesia, hormonal effects, immune suppression, overdose risk).\n2. Taper slowly: reduce by **10% of original dose every 1-2 weeks**. Faster tapers increase withdrawal and relapse risk.\n3. Provide adjunctive support: clonidine for autonomic symptoms, hydroxyzine for anxiety, loperamide for GI symptoms.\n4. Monitor with urine drug screens and prescription drug monitoring program (PDMP).\n5. Consider buprenorphine if opioid use disorder is diagnosed.\n6. **Never abruptly discontinue** \u2014 forced rapid tapers are associated with suicide and illicit opioid use.')
B.b_n=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The workers' compensation case manager requests a Functional Capacity Evaluation (FCE) to determine if the patient can return to his warehouse job, which requires frequent lifting of 50+ pounds.")
B.b3r=new A.c("+ Discuss the role of FCE in disability evaluation, workers' compensation considerations, and how you determine return-to-work status.","**Functional Capacity Evaluation (FCE)**:\n- An FCE is a standardized, objective assessment of a patient's ability to perform work-related tasks (lifting, carrying, pushing, pulling, standing, sitting tolerance, etc.).\n- It typically takes 4-6 hours over 1-2 days and is performed by a certified evaluator (usually PT or OT).\n- **Validity measures** are built in to assess consistency of effort (coefficient of variation, heart rate correlation with exertion, grip strength patterns).\n- Results are compared to the **Dictionary of Occupational Titles (DOT)** physical demand categories: sedentary, light, medium, heavy, very heavy.\n- His warehouse job likely falls under **heavy** physical demands (lifting 50-100 lbs occasionally, 25-50 lbs frequently).\n\n**Workers' Compensation Considerations**:\n1. **Maximum Medical Improvement (MMI)**: The point at which the patient's condition has stabilized and is unlikely to improve further with treatment. Once MMI is reached, a permanent impairment rating is assigned.\n2. **Impairment Rating**: Use the **AMA Guides to the Evaluation of Permanent Impairment (6th Edition)**. For lumbar spine, this involves the Diagnosis-Based Impairment (DBI) method with grade modifiers for functional history, physical exam, and clinical studies.\n3. **Disability vs. Impairment**: Impairment is a medical determination (loss of function). Disability is an administrative/legal determination (inability to perform specific job tasks). The physician determines impairment; the system determines disability.\n4. **Return-to-Work Options**: If the FCE shows the patient cannot meet heavy demands, options include: modified duty, transitional work program, vocational rehabilitation for job retraining, or permanent partial disability settlement.\n\n**Physician's Role**:\n- Provide objective, unbiased medical opinions. You are an advocate for medical truth, not for or against any party.\n- Document all findings thoroughly. Use standardized outcome measures (Oswestry Disability Index, visual analog scale).\n- Coordinate with the case manager, employer, and vocational counselor to explore all return-to-work pathways before declaring permanent disability.")
B.aR9=new A.c(u.j,"During the visit, you discuss the opioid taper plan. The patient becomes visibly angry and says: \"So you're just going to take away my pain medicine? That's the only thing that helps me get through the day. You doctors don't care about my pain \u2014 you just care about the government rules.\"")
B.aU1=new A.c("+ Demonstrate how you would respond to this patient's anger about opioid tapering while maintaining the therapeutic alliance.",'I would use a structured, empathetic approach that validates the patient\'s experience while providing honest medical guidance:\n\n**1. Pause and acknowledge the emotion**:\n"I hear your frustration, and I want you to know that your pain is absolutely real and I take it seriously. You are not being punished, and I am not taking anything away to be difficult."\n\n**2. Validate the experience**:\n"You have been dealing with severe pain for two years, and oxycodone has been the one thing that felt like it was helping. I completely understand why you would feel angry at someone suggesting we change that. If I were in your shoes, I might feel the same way."\n\n**3. Provide medical education without being condescending**:\n"What I want to share with you is that research has shown us something important \u2014 when opioids are used for chronic pain over a long period, the body actually adapts in a way that can make pain worse. It is called opioid-induced hyperalgesia. The medication that helped you initially may now be contributing to your pain sensitivity. This is not your fault. It is your nervous system responding to the medication."\n\n**4. Reframe the taper as treatment, not punishment**:\n"I am not proposing we take away your pain relief. I am proposing we replace it with treatments that work better for chronic pain and do not carry the same risks. We will do this slowly, at a pace you can tolerate, and we will add new medications and therapies before and during the taper so you are never left without support."\n\n**5. Emphasize shared decision-making and partnership**:\n"This is your body and your life. I will not force anything on you. But I would be failing you as your doctor if I did not tell you the truth about what the evidence shows. Can we work together on a plan that you feel comfortable with? If at any point the taper feels too fast, we slow down. You are in control of this process."\n\n**6. Set a follow-up plan**:\n"I want to see you every two weeks during this process. You will have my direct line if you are struggling between visits. We are a team, and I am committed to helping you get your life back."\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Motivational interviewing, empathy, patient autonomy, honest medical communication, maintaining therapeutic alliance under duress.')
B.aDk=s([B.aQa,B.aWI,B.aWF,B.aOj,B.aXW,B.aNs,B.aWO,B.aNv,B.b_n,B.b3r,B.aR9,B.aU1],t.E)
B.Q9=new A.a5("pain-chronic-low-back","Chronic Low Back Pain \u2014 Multidisciplinary Approach",B.aDk,!1)
B.aXY=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 45-year-old female elementary school teacher presents to the PM&R clinic with a 3-year history of widespread musculoskeletal pain, profound fatigue, and cognitive difficulties she describes as "brain fog." She reports pain "all over" that fluctuates in intensity but never fully resolves, rated 6-8/10 on average. She sleeps 8-9 hours per night but wakes feeling unrefreshed. She has difficulty concentrating at work and has made errors in lesson planning that are atypical for her. She has seen multiple specialists including rheumatology, neurology, and orthopedics without a definitive diagnosis. She is frustrated and tearful, stating that previous doctors have told her that her labs are normal and "nothing is wrong."')
B.b20=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","Key areas to assess in suspected fibromyalgia:\n- **Pain distribution**: Ask about pain in all four quadrants of the body plus axial skeleton. Use a body pain diagram to map affected areas.\n- **Associated symptoms**: Non-restorative sleep, fatigue (distinguish from sleepiness), cognitive dysfunction (memory, concentration, word-finding), headaches (tension or migraine), irritable bowel symptoms, interstitial cystitis symptoms, temporomandibular dysfunction.\n- **Symptom modifiers**: Worsened by stress, weather changes, poor sleep, overexertion. Improved by warmth, gentle activity, stress reduction.\n- **Sleep history**: Screen for obstructive sleep apnea (STOP-BANG), restless legs syndrome, and sleep hygiene habits. Alpha-delta sleep intrusion is characteristic.\n- **Psychiatric comorbidities**: Screen for depression (PHQ-9), anxiety (GAD-7), PTSD, and history of childhood adverse experiences (ACE score).\n- **Medication history**: Current and prior analgesics, response to NSAIDs (typically poor in fibromyalgia), any prior trials of pregabalin, duloxetine, or milnacipran.\n- **Functional impact**: Work performance, social participation, exercise habits, ADL limitations.\n- **Family history**: Fibromyalgia has familial aggregation \u2014 ask about first-degree relatives with chronic pain, fibromyalgia, or chronic fatigue syndrome.")
B.b2b=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 118/72 mmHg, HR 76 bpm, BMI 27.8 kg/m2, Temp 37.0C.\n\n**General**: Well-appearing female in no acute distress but appears fatigued. Affect is mildly flat.\n\n**Musculoskeletal**: No joint swelling, erythema, or warmth. Full range of motion in all joints without crepitus. No synovitis detected.\n\n**Tender Point Examination (Historical Reference)**: Tenderness to 4 kg/cm2 palpation at 14 of 18 classic tender point sites (bilateral: occiput, low cervical, trapezius, supraspinatus, second rib, lateral epicondyle, gluteal, greater trochanter, medial knee).\n\n**ACR 2010/2016 Criteria Assessment**:\n- **Widespread Pain Index (WPI)**: 14/19 areas (patient endorses pain in bilateral jaw, bilateral shoulder girdle, bilateral upper arm, bilateral lower arm, chest, abdomen, upper back, lower back, bilateral hip, bilateral upper leg, bilateral lower leg).\n- **Symptom Severity Scale (SSS)**: Fatigue 3/3, waking unrefreshed 3/3, cognitive symptoms 2/3, headaches (+), IBS symptoms (+), depression (+) = Total SSS 11/12.\n- WPI >= 7 AND SSS >= 5 = **Criteria met for fibromyalgia diagnosis**.\n\n**Neurologic**: Strength 5/5 throughout. Sensation intact. Reflexes 2+ and symmetric. No focal deficits. Allodynia noted to light touch in bilateral trapezius regions.\n\n**Skin**: No rashes, livedo reticularis, or Raynaud phenomenon.")
B.aNH=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","Before confirming the diagnosis of fibromyalgia, several important conditions must be excluded as they can mimic or coexist with fibromyalgia.")
B.aNl=new A.c("+ Outline your differential diagnosis and the targeted laboratory workup needed to exclude mimics of fibromyalgia.","**Key Differential Diagnoses to Exclude**:\n\n1. **Hypothyroidism**: Fatigue, cognitive dysfunction, myalgias, and weight gain can all mimic fibromyalgia. Order **TSH and free T4**. Even subclinical hypothyroidism (elevated TSH with normal T4) can cause symptoms.\n\n2. **Inflammatory Arthritis (Rheumatoid Arthritis, SLE, Sjogren syndrome)**: Despite normal joint exam, early inflammatory arthritis may present with pain before visible synovitis. Order **ESR, CRP, ANA, RF, anti-CCP antibodies**. Sjogren syndrome is particularly important as it frequently coexists with fibromyalgia (ask about dry eyes and dry mouth).\n\n3. **Obstructive Sleep Apnea (OSA)**: Non-restorative sleep and fatigue are hallmarks of both. BMI 27.8 and cognitive dysfunction warrant screening. **STOP-BANG questionnaire** \u2014 if score >= 3, refer for polysomnography.\n\n4. **Vitamin D Deficiency**: Widespread musculoskeletal pain and fatigue. Extremely common and easily treatable. Order **25-hydroxyvitamin D level**.\n\n5. **Anemia**: Fatigue and cognitive changes. Order **CBC with differential**.\n\n6. **Hepatitis C**: Associated with widespread pain and fatigue. Order **Hepatitis C antibody** if risk factors present.\n\n7. **Multiple Sclerosis**: Fatigue, cognitive fog, and paresthesias in a middle-aged female. Her neurologic exam is normal, but this warrants consideration if symptoms progress.\n\n8. **Polymyalgia Rheumatica**: If patient were over 50 with elevated ESR. Less likely given age but worth considering.\n\n**Recommended Lab Panel**:\n- CBC with differential\n- Comprehensive metabolic panel (CMP)\n- TSH, free T4\n- ESR, CRP\n- ANA, RF, anti-CCP\n- 25-hydroxyvitamin D\n- HbA1c (diabetes can cause neuropathic pain)\n- Ferritin (iron deficiency can cause fatigue and restless legs)\n\n**Important**: Extensive additional testing (MRI of every painful area, EMG/NCS in the absence of neurologic deficits) is NOT recommended and can reinforce illness behavior and medicalization.")
B.b0l=new A.c('+ The patient asks: "Could this all be in my head?" How do you explain the pathophysiology of fibromyalgia?',"**Patient-Friendly Explanation**:\n\"Absolutely not \u2014 fibromyalgia is a real, physical condition and we understand the biology behind it much better now. Let me explain what is happening in your body.\n\nYour nervous system has a 'volume control' for pain signals. In fibromyalgia, that volume control is turned up too high. Normal signals from your muscles and joints that should not be painful are being amplified by your brain and spinal cord, making them feel painful. This is called **central sensitization**.\"\n\n**Pathophysiology for the Examiner**:\n1. **Central sensitization**: Augmented central pain processing with increased excitatory neurotransmitters (substance P elevated 3x in CSF, glutamate elevated in insular cortex on MR spectroscopy) and decreased descending inhibitory pathways (reduced serotonin, norepinephrine, dopamine).\n\n2. **Functional neuroimaging abnormalities**: fMRI studies demonstrate increased activation in the pain matrix (anterior cingulate cortex, insular cortex, somatosensory cortex) in response to non-painful stimuli.\n\n3. **Small fiber neuropathy**: Emerging evidence shows reduced intraepidermal nerve fiber density on skin biopsy in approximately 40-50% of fibromyalgia patients, providing a peripheral nociceptive driver.\n\n4. **Hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis dysregulation**: Blunted cortisol response to stress, disrupted diurnal cortisol rhythm.\n\n5. **Sleep architecture abnormalities**: Alpha wave intrusion during stage N3 (slow-wave) sleep, reducing restorative sleep quality. This creates a vicious cycle: poor sleep lowers pain thresholds, and pain disrupts sleep.\n\n6. **Neuroinflammation**: PET imaging with TSPO ligands demonstrates activated glial cells in the brain of fibromyalgia patients.\n\n7. **Genetic predisposition**: Polymorphisms in genes encoding COMT (catechol-O-methyltransferase), serotonin transporter (5-HTTLPR), and adrenergic receptors increase vulnerability.")
B.aSv=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Labs return and are entirely normal (TSH 2.1, ANA negative, ESR 8, CRP 0.3, vitamin D 32, CBC normal). You confirm the diagnosis of fibromyalgia and discuss a comprehensive treatment plan.")
B.aRH=new A.c("+ Design an evidence-based multimodal treatment program for fibromyalgia, including pharmacologic and non-pharmacologic interventions.","**Non-Pharmacologic Interventions (First-Line)**:\n\n1. **Exercise** (strongest evidence base in fibromyalgia):\n- **Aerobic exercise**: Start low, go slow. Begin with 10-15 minutes of low-impact activity (walking, cycling, aquatic exercise) 3x/week and gradually increase to 30-45 minutes, 5x/week. Target 60-70% of age-predicted max heart rate initially.\n- **Aquatic therapy**: Warm-water pool exercise (thermoneutral, 33-34C) is particularly effective due to buoyancy reducing joint stress and warmth reducing pain sensitivity.\n- **Resistance training**: Light weights, 2x/week, emphasizing major muscle groups. Start with 40-50% of 1-repetition max.\n- **Key principle**: Patients will initially feel worse with exercise (post-exertional malaise). Educate that this is expected and will improve with consistency over 4-6 weeks.\n\n2. **Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT)**: Addresses catastrophizing, fear-avoidance, sleep behaviors, and coping strategies. Recommended weekly sessions for 12-16 weeks. NNT of approximately 4 for clinically meaningful pain reduction.\n\n3. **Sleep Hygiene Optimization**: Fixed sleep/wake times, no screens 1 hour before bed, cool/dark room, limit caffeine after noon. If OSA suspected, polysomnography and CPAP if positive. Treat restless legs if present (check ferritin, consider gabapentin).\n\n4. **Mindfulness-Based Stress Reduction (MBSR)**: 8-week structured program with demonstrated efficacy for fibromyalgia pain and fatigue.\n\n5. **Patient Education**: Pain neuroscience education \u2014 explaining the biology of fibromyalgia reduces threat perception and improves self-efficacy.\n\n**Pharmacologic Interventions**:\n\n1. **First-line options** (FDA-approved for fibromyalgia):\n- **Duloxetine 60 mg daily**: SNRI that addresses pain, fatigue, and comorbid depression. Start at 30 mg x 1 week, then increase. Particularly beneficial in this patient given depressive symptoms.\n- **Pregabalin 150-225 mg BID**: Alpha-2-delta calcium channel ligand. Improves pain and sleep. Start at 75 mg at bedtime, titrate over 2-4 weeks. Side effects: weight gain, dizziness, peripheral edema.\n- **Milnacipran 50 mg BID**: SNRI alternative. Better for fatigue-predominant fibromyalgia. Start 12.5 mg daily, titrate over 4 weeks.\n\n2. **Adjunctive agents**:\n- **Low-dose amitriptyline (10-25 mg at bedtime)**: Improves sleep quality and pain. Older but effective. Avoid in elderly or cardiac disease.\n- **Cyclobenzaprine 5-10 mg at bedtime**: Structurally similar to amitriptyline; improves sleep architecture.\n\n3. **Medications to AVOID**:\n- **Opioids**: No evidence of benefit in fibromyalgia and may worsen central sensitization (opioid-induced hyperalgesia). Strongly NOT recommended.\n- **Corticosteroids**: Ineffective and harmful long-term.\n- **Benzodiazepines**: Suppress stage N3 sleep, worsen fatigue, risk of dependence.")
B.aV8=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient asks you to complete disability paperwork. She reports that she cannot continue teaching due to fatigue and cognitive dysfunction but her employer is requesting medical documentation to justify leave.")
B.b_6=new A.c("+ Discuss the challenges of disability documentation in fibromyalgia and your approach to patient advocacy while maintaining objectivity.",'**Challenges of Disability Documentation in Fibromyalgia**:\n\n1. **Subjective nature of symptoms**: Fibromyalgia lacks objective biomarkers (no lab test, no imaging finding). Disability examiners often discount conditions without objective pathology. This creates a significant barrier for patients.\n\n2. **Inconsistent recognition**: Some insurers and disability systems have historically classified fibromyalgia as a "subjective" or "disputed" diagnosis, although this has improved since the ACR criteria formalization and growing neuroimaging evidence.\n\n3. **Social Security Disability (SSDI)**: SSA Ruling 12-2p acknowledges fibromyalgia as a medically determinable impairment. It can be established using either 1990 ACR criteria (tender points) or 2010 criteria (WPI + SSS). However, the claimant must still demonstrate that symptoms cause functional limitations precluding substantial gainful activity.\n\n**Approach to Documentation**:\n\n1. **Use validated outcome measures**: Document Fibromyalgia Impact Questionnaire-Revised (FIQR), Widespread Pain Index, Symptom Severity Scale, and functional assessments at every visit. These provide objective tracking over time.\n\n2. **Functional limitations focus**: Document specific functional deficits: "Patient cannot sustain attention for more than 20 minutes," "Patient requires rest breaks every 45 minutes due to fatigue," "Patient reports inability to stand for more than 30 minutes." Avoid vague statements.\n\n3. **Cognitive testing**: Refer for formal neuropsychological testing if cognitive dysfunction is prominent. Objective cognitive data strengthens disability claims significantly.\n\n4. **Treatment adherence documentation**: Show that the patient has engaged in and failed appropriate treatments. Payers are more likely to approve disability if the patient has pursued all reasonable therapeutic options.\n\n5. **Physician advocacy role**: As the treating physiatrist, you can provide a **Medical Source Statement** detailing the patient\'s residual functional capacity (RFC). Be honest and evidence-based \u2014 do not exaggerate or minimize. Your credibility as an expert is your most valuable tool for the patient.\n\n6. **Consider partial disability or accommodations first**: Before full disability, explore FMLA leave, reduced work hours, ergonomic modifications, cognitive accommodations (written instructions, reduced multitasking), and a gradual return-to-work plan. Full disability should be a last resort as it often worsens outcomes in fibromyalgia (deconditioning, social isolation, loss of purpose).')
B.aY4=new A.c(u.j,"The patient breaks down crying during the visit and says: \"Nobody believes my pain is real. My husband thinks I'm lazy. My last doctor told me to just exercise more. I feel like I'm going crazy.\"")
B.b14=new A.c("+ Demonstrate how you would validate this patient's experience and build a therapeutic alliance.",'I would use a structured empathetic response that prioritizes validation before education:\n\n**1. Create space for the emotion \u2014 do not rush past it**:\n*Pause. Allow a moment of silence. Offer a tissue if needed.*\n"Take your time. This is a safe space, and I am here to listen."\n\n**2. Validate explicitly and directly**:\n"I want to say something very clearly, and I need you to hear this: Your pain is real. It is not in your head, it is not laziness, and you are not going crazy. What you have is a recognized medical condition with a real biological basis, and I believe you."\n\n**3. Normalize the experience of being dismissed**:\n"Unfortunately, what you have experienced \u2014 being told nothing is wrong because lab tests are normal \u2014 is something many patients with fibromyalgia go through. It does not mean nothing is wrong. It means the standard tests do not capture what is happening in your nervous system. That is a limitation of the tests, not a limitation of your experience."\n\n**4. Name the diagnosis with confidence**:\n"Based on your symptoms, your examination, and the criteria we use in our field, you meet the criteria for fibromyalgia. This is a condition where the central nervous system processes pain signals differently, essentially amplifying normal signals. It also affects sleep, cognition, and energy. It is well-studied and there are effective treatments."\n\n**5. Address the family dynamic**:\n"You mentioned your husband thinks you are lazy. That must be incredibly hurtful and isolating. Would you be open to having him come to a future appointment? Sometimes when a family member hears the explanation directly from a physician and understands the biology, it changes their perspective. I would be happy to educate him."\n\n**6. Instill hope with a concrete plan**:\n"Here is what I want you to know: fibromyalgia is treatable. It may not be curable, but we can significantly improve your pain, your sleep, and your ability to function. We are going to build a plan together \u2014 starting today \u2014 and I will be with you through this process. You are not alone in this anymore."\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Validation, empathy, diagnostic confidence, patient education, family engagement, therapeutic hope, continuity of care.')
B.a7L=s([B.aXY,B.b20,B.b2b,B.aNH,B.aNl,B.b0l,B.aSv,B.aRH,B.aV8,B.b_6,B.aY4,B.b14],t.E)
B.R_=new A.a5("pain-fibromyalgia","Fibromyalgia",B.a7L,!1)
B.aPk=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 60-year-old male is referred to the outpatient PM&R amputee clinic 3 months after a right transtibial (below-knee) amputation secondary to peripheral vascular disease and non-healing diabetic foot ulcer. He reports severe, constant burning and cramping sensations in his "missing right foot," rated 8/10 in intensity. He also describes intermittent electric shock-like sensations in the phantom toes that wake him from sleep. He has been unable to progress with prosthetic fitting because the pain is so debilitating. He has become increasingly withdrawn, stopped attending church, and admits to feeling hopeless. His medical history includes type 2 diabetes, peripheral arterial disease, hypertension, and coronary artery disease with prior stent placement.')
B.b1M=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","Critical distinctions and assessments in post-amputation pain:\n- **Phantom limb pain (PLP)**: Pain perceived in the amputated limb. Described as burning, cramping, shooting, stabbing, or twisting. Present in 50-80% of amputees. Onset usually within the first week but can occur months later.\n- **Phantom limb sensation (PLS)**: Non-painful awareness of the missing limb (tingling, itching, sense of position). Present in nearly all amputees. Telescoping (perception that the phantom limb is shrinking) is common.\n- **Residual limb pain (RLP)**: Pain in the remaining stump. Causes include neuroma, bone spur, skin breakdown, infection, ischemia, heterotopic ossification, and poorly fitting prosthetic socket.\n- **Differentiation is essential**: Ask specifically where the pain is \u2014 in the remaining limb or in the missing part? What is the quality?\n- **Pre-amputation pain history**: Patients with severe pre-amputation pain are at higher risk for PLP (pain memory imprinting theory).\n- **Vascular status**: Assess contralateral limb viability. Check for signs of progressive PVD (claudication, rest pain, skin changes in the remaining foot). ABI of contralateral limb.\n- **Diabetes management**: Current HbA1c, glucose control, neuropathy symptoms in the remaining foot.\n- **Psychosocial assessment**: Screen for depression (PHQ-9), PTSD, grief/loss, social isolation, body image disturbance. Amputees have a 2-3x higher rate of depression than the general population.\n- **Functional goals**: Ambulatory expectations (K-level classification), prior mobility level, home accessibility, social support system.")
B.aWy=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 138/82 mmHg, HR 74 bpm, BMI 29.5 kg/m2, Temp 36.8C, HbA1c 7.8% (recent lab).\n\n**Residual Limb Examination**:\n- **Shape**: Cylindrical, mildly edematous distally. Not yet fully matured for prosthetic fitting.\n- **Skin**: Surgical incision well-healed. No open wounds, erythema, or signs of infection. Mild discoloration consistent with PVD.\n- **Soft tissue**: Adequate soft tissue padding over the distal tibia. No adherent scar tissue.\n- **Palpation**: Point tenderness at the distal lateral residual limb near the fibular transection site. Positive Tinel sign at this location radiating into the phantom foot, suggesting possible **neuroma formation**.\n- **Range of Motion**: Right knee extension to 0 degrees (no flexion contracture). Knee flexion to 120 degrees.\n- **Contralateral Limb**: Left foot with diminished pedal pulses (dorsalis pedis 1+, posterior tibial absent). Monofilament testing reveals loss of protective sensation at 3 of 10 plantar sites. Early Charcot changes not present but foot at high risk.\n\n**General Neurologic**: Bilateral stocking-pattern sensory loss consistent with diabetic peripheral neuropathy. Reflexes 1+ at bilateral knees. Absent left Achilles reflex.\n\n**Functional Assessment**: Independent in wheelchair mobility. Transfers with stand-pivot using a walker with moderate assist. Has not attempted ambulation with a prosthesis due to pain.")
B.b1_=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The patient asks you to explain why he still feels pain in a foot that is no longer there. He finds this deeply distressing and fears it means something is wrong with his brain.")
B.aNc=new A.c("+ Explain the neurophysiologic mechanisms of phantom limb pain and differentiate it from neuroma-related residual limb pain in this patient.",'**Mechanisms of Phantom Limb Pain**:\n\n1. **Peripheral mechanisms**:\n- After nerve transection, the proximal nerve stump forms a **neuroma** \u2014 a disorganized tangle of regenerating axons. These generate spontaneous ectopic discharges (abnormal firing without stimulation).\n- Upregulation of sodium channels (Nav1.3, Nav1.8) in the dorsal root ganglion increases neuronal excitability.\n- This patient\'s positive Tinel sign at the fibular transection site suggests a clinically significant neuroma contributing to both RLP and PLP.\n\n2. **Spinal cord mechanisms**:\n- Loss of afferent input from the amputated limb causes **dorsal horn reorganization**. Wide dynamic range neurons become hyperexcitable.\n- Loss of inhibitory interneuron activity leads to central sensitization at the spinal level.\n- Wind-up phenomenon: repeated C-fiber input from the neuroma amplifies spinal cord responses.\n\n3. **Cortical reorganization (most important)**:\n- The somatosensory cortex undergoes **maladaptive neuroplasticity**. The cortical area that previously represented the amputated foot is invaded by adjacent representations (e.g., the residual limb, genitals per Penfield homunculus).\n- **Ramachandran\'s theory**: The degree of cortical reorganization correlates with the intensity of phantom limb pain. The brain "searches" for the missing limb\'s input and misinterprets adjacent signals as pain.\n- **Neuromatrix theory (Melzack)**: The brain contains an innate body schema (neuromatrix) that generates a perception of the body independent of peripheral input. When the expected input is absent, the neuromatrix generates abnormal sensory experiences including pain.\n\n4. **Pain memory**: Pre-amputation pain patterns can be "imprinted" in the CNS and replayed as phantom pain. This patient had chronic diabetic foot pain before amputation, which may be contributing.\n\n**Differentiating PLP from Neuroma-Related RLP**:\n- The **burning and cramping in the phantom foot** = phantom limb pain (cortical and spinal mechanisms)\n- The **point tenderness with positive Tinel sign at the fibular transection site** = residual limb pain from neuroma (peripheral mechanism)\n- These often coexist and both require treatment. The neuroma is likely driving peripheral input that maintains central sensitization.')
B.aXT=new A.c("+ What additional diagnostic studies would you consider?","**Diagnostic Studies**:\n\n1. **Ultrasound of the residual limb**: High-resolution ultrasound can identify neuroma formation at the nerve transection sites. Neuromas appear as hypoechoic bulbous enlargements of the nerve stump. This is the preferred first-line imaging modality.\n\n2. **Diagnostic nerve block**: Lidocaine injection at the site of the suspected fibular neuroma. If it eliminates both the local tenderness AND reduces the phantom pain, this confirms the neuroma as a significant peripheral driver of the PLP. If phantom pain persists despite block, central mechanisms predominate.\n\n3. **MRI of the residual limb**: If ultrasound is inconclusive, MRI can identify neuroma, heterotopic ossification, abscess, or bone spur. Also assess for residual osteomyelitis given diabetic history.\n\n4. **Vascular studies**: Arterial duplex of the residual limb to ensure adequate perfusion for healing and prosthetic use. ABI of the contralateral limb to assess progression of PVD.\n\n5. **X-ray of the residual limb**: Rule out bone spur, heterotopic ossification, or retained foreign body.\n\n6. **Do NOT order**: EMG/NCS of the residual limb is not helpful for neuroma diagnosis and is unnecessarily painful. Brain imaging is not indicated for phantom limb pain in the absence of other neurologic findings.")
B.aMv=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You confirm the diagnosis of phantom limb pain with a contributing residual limb neuroma. The patient is eager for relief so he can begin prosthetic training.")
B.aUo=new A.c("+ Outline a comprehensive, evidence-based treatment plan for phantom limb pain including pharmacologic, non-pharmacologic, and interventional approaches.",'**Pharmacologic Management**:\n\n1. **First-line \u2014 Gabapentinoids**:\n- **Gabapentin**: Start 300 mg at bedtime, titrate by 300 mg every 3-5 days to target dose of 900-1800 mg TID. Address his sleep disruption and neuropathic pain simultaneously.\n- **Pregabalin**: Alternative at 75 mg BID, titrate to 150-300 mg BID. Better bioavailability and less variable absorption than gabapentin.\n\n2. **Adjunctive \u2014 Tricyclic Antidepressant or SNRI**:\n- **Nortriptyline 25-75 mg at bedtime**: Effective for neuropathic pain and sleep, lower anticholinergic side effects than amitriptyline. However, use with caution given his CAD history (check baseline ECG for QTc).\n- **Duloxetine 60 mg daily**: Safer cardiovascular profile, also addresses depressive symptoms.\n\n3. **Topical agents for residual limb**:\n- **Lidocaine 5% patch**: Apply over the neuroma site for 12 hours on/12 hours off.\n- **Capsaicin 8% patch**: Single application by a trained provider, can provide 3 months of relief by desensitizing peripheral nociceptors.\n\n4. **Avoid**: Opioids are generally not effective for phantom limb pain and carry significant risks in this population.\n\n**Non-Pharmacologic Interventions**:\n\n1. **Mirror therapy** (strongest evidence for PLP):\n- Patient places the intact left foot in front of a mirror positioned at midline. Observing the mirror reflection moving creates a visual illusion of the missing right foot moving. This "fools" the cortex into receiving the expected visual-proprioceptive feedback, reducing maladaptive cortical reorganization.\n- Protocol: 15-20 minutes daily for a minimum of 4 weeks. Home mirror box can be constructed inexpensively.\n\n2. **Graded motor imagery (GMI)**: Three-stage approach \u2014 laterality recognition, explicit motor imagery (imagining moving the phantom limb), then mirror therapy. Addresses cortical reorganization systematically.\n\n3. **TENS (Transcutaneous Electrical Nerve Stimulation)**: Apply electrodes to the residual limb. Provides competing sensory input to modulate spinal gating. Use conventional high-frequency (80-100 Hz) TENS for 30-60 minutes, 2-3 times daily.\n\n4. **Desensitization program**: Progressive tactile stimulation of the residual limb (tapping, rubbing with various textures, vibration) to normalize sensory input and reduce allodynia.\n\n5. **Prosthetic fitting**: Wearing a well-fitting prosthesis itself reduces PLP in many patients by providing proprioceptive feedback to the cortex. Prioritize prosthetic training once pain is manageable.\n\n**Interventional Options** (if conservative measures fail):\n\n6. **Targeted neuroma injection**: Corticosteroid + local anesthetic injection at the fibular neuroma under ultrasound guidance.\n\n7. **Peripheral nerve stimulation**: Implanted electrode adjacent to the nerve proximal to the neuroma.\n\n8. **Targeted Muscle Reinnervation (TMR)**: Surgical procedure that transfers the severed nerve endings to nearby motor nerve branches, giving them a target to innervate. This prevents neuroma formation and has strong emerging evidence for both treatment and prevention of PLP.\n\n9. **Dorsal root ganglion (DRG) stimulation**: Neuromodulation targeting the DRG at the relevant spinal levels for refractory cases.')
B.b2p=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient is ready for prosthetic fitting. His insurance is Medicare Part B. He asks about the process for getting a prosthesis and whether it will be covered.")
B.aOl=new A.c("+ Describe the prosthetic prescription process, K-level classification, and insurance coverage considerations. If this patient were a veteran, what additional resources would be available?","**Prosthetic Prescription Process**:\n\n1. **Physician prescription**: The physiatrist provides a detailed prescription including the type of prosthesis and specific componentry. The prescription must be based on the patient's functional potential, not just current ability.\n\n2. **K-Level Classification (Medicare Functional Classification Level)**:\n- **K0**: Does not have the ability or potential to ambulate or transfer. Prosthesis does not enhance quality of life (no prosthesis justified).\n- **K1**: Has the ability or potential to use a prosthesis for transfers or household ambulation on level surfaces (limited household ambulator).\n- **K2**: Has the ability or potential for limited community ambulation (community ambulator with environmental barriers).\n- **K3**: Has the ability or potential for ambulation with variable cadence, including community activities beyond simple locomotion (active community ambulator).\n- **K4**: Has the ability or potential for prosthetic ambulation that exceeds basic ambulation, including high-impact activities (athlete, active adult).\n\nThis patient's K-level: Given his PVD, diabetes, CAD, and age, he is likely a **K2 ambulator** with potential for K3 depending on rehabilitation progress.\n\n3. **Componentry based on K-level**:\n- K2 transtibial: PTB or TSB socket, pelite liner or gel liner with pin lock, SACH or single-axis foot, endoskeletal pylon.\n- If he demonstrates K3 potential: dynamic energy-storing foot (e.g., Flex-Foot) may be justified.\n\n**Medicare Part B Coverage**:\n- Medicare covers **80%** of the approved prosthetic device. The patient is responsible for the 20% coinsurance (or supplemental insurance covers it).\n- **Requires**: Physician prescription with documented medical necessity, K-level justification, prosthetist evaluation.\n- **Prior authorization** may be required for higher-cost components.\n- Medicare covers **5 prosthetic socks** per year and socket replacements as needed with documentation of poor fit.\n- Repair and replacement are covered when medically necessary.\n\n**VA Benefits (if veteran)**:\nIf this patient were a service-connected veteran:\n1. **VA Prosthetics and Sensory Aids Service**: Provides prosthetic devices at no cost for service-connected conditions.\n2. **No K-level restrictions**: The VA is generally more generous than Medicare in prosthetic componentry. Veterans may receive microprocessor-controlled knees, specialized sport prostheses, and multiple prostheses for different activities.\n3. **Amputation System of Care**: Specialized VA Regional Amputation Centers with dedicated interdisciplinary teams.\n4. **Lifetime prosthetic care**: Ongoing replacements, repairs, and upgrades at no cost.\n5. **Additional benefits**: Adaptive housing grants, automobile adaptation grants, clothing allowance for prosthesis-related wear, caregiver support.\n6. **Peer support**: VA-sponsored peer visitation programs connecting new amputees with experienced amputee mentors.")
B.aRI=new A.c(u.j,"During the visit, the patient becomes tearful and says: \"I don't see the point of any of this. I can't work, I can't drive, I can barely leave my house. My wife does everything for me now. I used to be a man who provided for his family, and now I'm just a burden. Sometimes I think they'd be better off without me.\"")
B.aOe=new A.c("+ This patient is expressing hopelessness and possible suicidal ideation. Demonstrate how you would screen for suicide risk and connect him to appropriate resources.",'**Immediate Response \u2014 Take the Statement Seriously**:\n\n"I appreciate you trusting me with how you are feeling. What you just said about your family being better off without you \u2014 I want to make sure I understand what you mean. Are you having thoughts of ending your life or harming yourself?"\n\n**If he endorses suicidal ideation, assess further using the Columbia Suicide Severity Rating Scale (C-SSRS) framework**:\n1. **Ideation**: "Have you actually been thinking about killing yourself?" (Active vs. passive ideation)\n2. **Plan**: "Have you thought about how you would do it?"\n3. **Intent**: "Do you have any intention of acting on these thoughts?"\n4. **Means**: "Do you have access to firearms, medications, or other means?" (Amputees have increased access to prescription medications)\n5. **Preparatory behaviors**: "Have you done anything to prepare, like giving away possessions or writing notes?"\n6. **Protective factors**: "What has kept you from acting on these thoughts?" (Family, faith, fear of consequences)\n\n**Risk Factors Present in This Patient**:\n- Male sex (higher completion rate)\n- Chronic pain\n- Recent major disability/amputation\n- Loss of independence and role identity\n- Social isolation (stopped attending church)\n- Hopelessness\n- Depression (likely \u2014 needs formal screening)\n\n**Management Based on Risk Level**:\n\n**If imminent risk (active plan with intent and means)**:\n- Do not leave the patient alone\n- Activate emergency psychiatric evaluation (call 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline or 911)\n- Means restriction counseling (secure medications, firearms)\n\n**If moderate risk (ideation without imminent plan)**:\n- Administer PHQ-9 formally (score likely elevated)\n- Initiate antidepressant: **sertraline 50 mg daily** (safe with his cardiac history)\n- Urgent referral to psychiatry or psychology within 1-2 weeks\n- Establish a **safety plan** (Stanley-Brown Safety Planning): identify warning signs, coping strategies, people to contact, crisis resources\n- Provide crisis numbers: **988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline**, Crisis Text Line (text HOME to 741741)\n\n**Regardless of Risk Level \u2014 Address Isolation and Peer Support**:\n"I want to connect you with people who understand exactly what you are going through. There is an amputee support group that meets at our hospital every other Thursday. Many of the members felt exactly like you do now, and they have found ways to rebuild meaningful lives. Would you be willing to try one meeting?"\n\n**Additional resources**: Amputee Coalition of America peer support network, online forums, recreational therapy programs, adaptive sports programs.\n\n**Follow-up**: Schedule a return visit within 1-2 weeks. Document suicide risk assessment thoroughly. Communicate with PCP and any mental health providers. Consider involving the patient\'s wife in a future session to address caregiver burden and relationship dynamics.\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Direct and compassionate suicide screening, structured risk assessment, evidence-based intervention, crisis resource provision, peer support engagement, coordinated follow-up.')
B.aFK=s([B.aPk,B.b1M,B.aWy,B.b1_,B.aNc,B.aXT,B.aMv,B.aUo,B.b2p,B.aOl,B.aRI,B.aOe],t.E)
B.Qd=new A.a5("pain-phantom-limb","Phantom Limb Pain",B.aFK,!1)
B.b1F=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 50-year-old male former electrician presents to the PM&R pain clinic referred by his primary care physician for worsening axial low back pain and bilateral lower extremity pain. He has undergone three lumbar surgeries over the past 6 years: an L4-5 microdiscectomy (2020), an L4-5 posterior lumbar interbody fusion with pedicle screw fixation (2022), and a revision fusion extending to L3-S1 (2024). Despite these surgeries, his pain is now worse than his pre-surgical baseline. He rates axial pain at 9/10 and bilateral leg pain at 7/10, describing a constant burning and aching quality with electric shock-like sensations radiating into both calves. He is currently taking oxycodone 30 mg QID, gabapentin 800 mg TID, cyclobenzaprine 10 mg TID, and alprazolam 1 mg BID prescribed by multiple providers. His calculated opioid burden is 180 MME/day. He has a PHQ-9 score of 22 (severe depression), has not worked in 3 years, and is applying for Social Security Disability. He is divorced, lives alone in a second-floor apartment, and his only social contact is a weekly phone call with his adult daughter. He reports passive suicidal ideation without plan or intent, stating, "I just don\'t see the point anymore."')
B.b36=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","**Surgical History Review**:\n- Document exact procedures, approach, levels, hardware placed, and intraoperative complications for each surgery\n- Review operative notes for incidental durotomy (risk factor for arachnoiditis)\n- Assess interval between surgeries and indication for each reoperation\n- Prior imaging reviewed: most recent MRI shows post-surgical changes at L3-S1, posterior hardware in place, epidural fibrosis at L4-5, mild central stenosis at L2-3 above the fusion\n\n**Pain Characterization**:\n- **Axial mechanical pain**: Possible adjacent segment disease (L2-3), hardware failure, pseudoarthrosis, sacroiliac joint dysfunction from altered biomechanics\n- **Radicular/neuropathic pain**: Epidural fibrosis, arachnoiditis, recurrent disc herniation, foraminal stenosis from hardware\n- **Widespread pain**: Assess for central sensitization \u2014 pain beyond dermatomal distribution, allodynia, temporal summation\n- **Central Sensitization Inventory (CSI)**: Administer to quantify central sensitization; scores >40 suggest clinically significant central sensitization\n\n**Red Flags**:\n- Cauda equina syndrome: saddle anesthesia, urinary retention, fecal incontinence\n- Hardware failure: new onset mechanical instability, audible clicking\n- Infection: fever, wound drainage, elevated ESR/CRP\n\n**Psychosocial Assessment**:\n- Severe depression with passive suicidal ideation \u2014 requires immediate safety assessment and psychiatric referral\n- Social isolation, loss of occupational identity, financial stress\n- Polypharmacy with opioid-benzodiazepine combination (high overdose risk)\n- Screen for opioid use disorder: SOAPP-R, check PDMP, urine drug screen\n- Assess for PTSD related to surgical trauma and perceived medical failure\n\n**Functional Assessment**:\n- Walking tolerance, sitting tolerance, sleep quality\n- ADL limitations, fall risk assessment (sedating medications)\n- PROMIS-29 or Brief Pain Inventory for comprehensive functional baseline")
B.aWV=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 148/92 mmHg, HR 76 bpm, BMI 31.8 kg/m2. Appears older than stated age, disheveled, flat affect.\n\n**Inspection**: Well-healed midline lumbar scar from L3-S1. Mild thoracolumbar kyphosis. Significant paraspinal muscle atrophy bilaterally. Antalgic gait with shortened stride length and reduced arm swing.\n\n**Palpation**: Diffuse tenderness over bilateral paraspinals L1-S1, extending into bilateral gluteals. Hardware palpable beneath scar without crepitus or step-off. SI joint tenderness bilaterally, left greater than right.\n\n**Range of Motion**: Lumbar flexion 20 degrees (severely limited, pain-guarded). Extension 5 degrees with reproduction of bilateral leg pain. Lateral bending limited to 10 degrees bilaterally.\n\n**Neurologic Examination**:\n- **Motor**: Bilateral tibialis anterior 4/5, EHL 4-/5, gastrocnemius 4+/5. Bilateral hip flexors 4/5. Grip strength reduced (deconditioning vs. medication effect)\n- **Sensory**: Decreased pinprick sensation in bilateral L5 and S1 dermatomes. Allodynia to light touch over bilateral lumbar paraspinals and into upper gluteal region (beyond surgical field, suggesting central sensitization). Vibratory sense decreased at bilateral great toes\n- **Reflexes**: Achilles reflexes absent bilaterally. Patellar reflexes 1+ bilaterally. No clonus. Babinski downgoing bilaterally\n\n**Special Tests**:\n- Straight leg raise positive bilaterally at 30 degrees with reproduction of calf pain\n- Seated slump test positive bilaterally\n- FABER test positive bilaterally (SI joint involvement)\n- Gaenslen test positive on left\n- Quantitative sensory testing: lowered pressure pain thresholds in remote regions (forearm), temporal summation present \u2014 consistent with central sensitization\n\n**Gait**: Slow, guarded, wide-based. Uses single-point cane in right hand. Unable to tandem walk. Heel and toe walking weak bilaterally.\n\n**Psychological Observations**: Monotone speech, poor eye contact, slow psychomotor responses. Tearful when discussing functional decline.")
B.aZU=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING",'The patient has been told by his last surgeon that "there is nothing more we can do surgically." He wants to know what is causing his pain and whether there are any options left.')
B.aWm=new A.c("+ Develop a systematic diagnostic framework for failed back surgery syndrome. How do you differentiate the multiple potential pain generators?",'**Failed Back Surgery Syndrome (FBSS) \u2014 Diagnostic Framework**:\nFBSS is not a single diagnosis but a clinical scenario with multiple potential etiologies requiring systematic evaluation.\n\n**Structural/Surgical Causes**:\n1. **Pseudoarthrosis (failed fusion)**: Incidence 5-35% after lumbar fusion. Diagnosed with dynamic flexion-extension radiographs showing motion at fused segment, or CT with fine cuts showing lucency around hardware. May require revision surgery if clinically significant.\n2. **Adjacent segment disease**: Accelerated degeneration above or below fusion (occurs in 30% within 10 years). This patient\'s L2-3 stenosis above fusion is suspicious. Diagnostic selective nerve root block at L2-3 can confirm.\n3. **Hardware failure**: Screw loosening, rod fracture, cage migration. Diagnosed with CT reconstruction; plain films may miss subtle failure.\n4. **Recurrent disc herniation**: At operated or adjacent levels. MRI with gadolinium differentiates recurrent disc (no enhancement) from epidural fibrosis (enhances on contrast).\n5. **Epidural fibrosis/arachnoiditis**: Post-surgical scar tissue encasing nerve roots. MRI shows clumping of nerve roots in the thecal sac (arachnoiditis) or epidural scar tissue. No surgical treatment for arachnoiditis.\n6. **Foraminal stenosis**: Hardware or bony overgrowth compressing exiting nerve roots. May be missed on standard MRI; CT myelogram may be needed.\n7. **Sacroiliac joint dysfunction**: Altered biomechanics after fusion increase SI joint stress. Diagnosed with combination of 3 or more provocative physical exam tests (FABER, Gaenslen, thigh thrust, compression, distraction) and diagnostic SI joint injection (>75% pain relief).\n\n**Non-Structural Causes**:\n1. **Central sensitization/nociplastic pain**: As evidenced by allodynia in remote areas, temporal summation, and CSI score. This represents altered central pain processing and will not respond to further surgical intervention.\n2. **Myofascial pain**: Paraspinal deconditioning and trigger points from chronic guarding.\n3. **Opioid-induced hyperalgesia**: Paradoxical increased pain sensitivity from chronic high-dose opioids. Differentiated from tolerance by pain that is diffuse, extends beyond original distribution, and worsens with dose escalation.\n\n**Diagnostic Workup**:\n- CT lumbar spine with fine cuts through hardware \u2014 assess fusion status and hardware integrity\n- MRI lumbar spine with and without gadolinium \u2014 differentiate disc from fibrosis, evaluate adjacent segments\n- Flexion-extension radiographs \u2014 assess stability at fused segments\n- EMG/NCS bilateral lower extremities \u2014 characterize radiculopathy vs. peripheral neuropathy\n- Diagnostic selective nerve root blocks at L2-3 if adjacent segment disease suspected\n- Diagnostic SI joint injections bilaterally (image-guided)\n- Laboratory: ESR, CRP (rule out infection), CBC, metabolic panel, vitamin D, testosterone (opioid endocrinopathy)\n- PDMP query, urine drug screen with confirmatory testing\n\n**Key Principle**: Multiple pain generators typically coexist in FBSS. Treatment must address all contributors simultaneously, not seek a single "source."')
B.aP3=new A.c("+ What role does opioid-induced hyperalgesia play in this presentation, and how do you distinguish it from opioid tolerance?","**Opioid-Induced Hyperalgesia (OIH) vs. Tolerance**:\n\n**Opioid Tolerance**:\n- Decreased analgesic effect from the same dose over time\n- Pain character and distribution remain the same\n- Pain improves with dose escalation\n- Pharmacologic mechanism: mu-receptor downregulation and desensitization\n\n**Opioid-Induced Hyperalgesia**:\n- Paradoxical increased pain sensitivity caused by opioid use\n- Pain becomes more diffuse, extends beyond original injury\n- Pain quality may change (often described as more diffuse, burning)\n- Pain worsens with dose escalation (key distinguishing feature)\n- Allodynia and hyperalgesia in areas unrelated to the surgical site\n- Mechanism: NMDA receptor activation, descending facilitation from the rostral ventromedial medulla, glial cell activation, increased dynorphin in the spinal cord\n\n**Clinical Evidence in This Patient Supporting OIH**:\n- Pain worse than pre-surgical baseline despite 180 MME/day\n- Allodynia extending beyond surgical field into upper lumbar and gluteal regions\n- Diffuse quality inconsistent with any single structural cause\n- Temporal summation on quantitative sensory testing\n- Escalating opioid doses without benefit\n\n**Evaluation Strategy**:\n- A cautious opioid dose reduction trial can help differentiate: if pain improves with dose reduction, OIH is likely; if pain worsens, tolerance is the primary mechanism\n- This must be done slowly (5-10% reduction every 2-4 weeks) with close monitoring\n- Consider rotating to a partial agonist (buprenorphine) which has lower OIH risk and NMDA antagonist properties\n- Ketamine infusion (sub-anesthetic dose, 0.1-0.3 mg/kg/hr) in a monitored setting can rapidly reverse OIH through NMDA antagonism and may serve as both diagnostic and therapeutic\n\n**Safety Concern**: This patient is on concurrent opioids and benzodiazepines (oxycodone + alprazolam). This combination dramatically increases overdose risk and must be addressed as a priority. CDC guidelines strongly caution against co-prescribing.")
B.b_T=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Diagnostic workup reveals intact hardware without pseudoarthrosis, mild adjacent segment stenosis at L2-3, epidural fibrosis at L4-5 without significant compression, and bilateral SI joint dysfunction. EMG shows chronic bilateral L5-S1 radiculopathy without acute denervation. You determine this is a complex case with multiple pain generators and significant central sensitization.")
B.aZN=new A.c("+ Outline a comprehensive management strategy including neuromodulation options, interdisciplinary rehabilitation, and medication optimization.","**Phase 1 \u2014 Safety and Stabilization (Weeks 1-4)**:\n- **Psychiatric emergency**: Refer for same-day psychiatric evaluation given passive suicidal ideation. Initiate antidepressant \u2014 duloxetine 30 mg titrated to 60 mg (dual pain/depression benefit). Safety plan documentation.\n- **Benzodiazepine taper**: Begin gradual alprazolam taper (reduce by 0.25 mg every 2-4 weeks) \u2014 never abruptly discontinue. This is the most dangerous co-prescription and must be addressed before opioid taper.\n- **PDMP review**: Verify all controlled substance prescribers and consolidate prescribing to a single provider.\n- **Naloxone prescription**: Given high MME and concurrent benzodiazepine, prescribe naloxone rescue kit and educate the patient and his daughter.\n\n**Phase 2 \u2014 Targeted Interventional Procedures (Weeks 4-12)**:\n- **SI joint injections**: Fluoroscopy-guided bilateral SI joint corticosteroid injections. If >75% relief, consider SI joint radiofrequency ablation (lateral branch neurotomy) for durable benefit.\n- **L2-3 transforaminal epidural steroid injection**: If radicular component correlates with adjacent segment disease. Diagnostic and therapeutic.\n- **Medial branch blocks at L2-3, L3-4**: If facetogenic component suspected at adjacent segment. If positive (>80% relief on 2 separate occasions), proceed with radiofrequency ablation.\n\n**Phase 3 \u2014 Neuromodulation Evaluation (Months 3-6)**:\n- **Spinal cord stimulation (SCS)**: FBSS is an FDA-approved indication. This patient is a candidate given neuropathic leg pain, failed conservative treatment, and no untreated psychiatric instability.\n  - Modern options: high-frequency (10 kHz) stimulation (Senza), burst stimulation (BurstDR), dorsal root ganglion stimulation (for focal neuropathic pain)\n  - **Trial period**: 5-7 day percutaneous trial. Success defined as >50% pain reduction in target area.\n  - Pre-implant requirements: psychiatric clearance, psychological evaluation (Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory-2), stable on medications, no active substance use disorder\n  - **PROCESS trial**: Level 1 evidence showing SCS superior to conventional medical management for FBSS\n- **Intrathecal drug delivery system (IDDS)**: Consider if SCS trial fails. Allows targeted delivery of opioid (morphine or hydromorphone) and/or ziconotide at a fraction of systemic doses. Requires successful intrathecal trial and psychological clearance. Risk of granuloma formation at catheter tip with chronic intrathecal opioids.\n\n**Phase 4 \u2014 Intensive Interdisciplinary Pain Rehabilitation (Concurrent)**:\n- Enroll in 3-4 week intensive program (4-6 hours daily)\n- **Physical therapy**: Aquatic therapy progressing to land-based. Quota-based graded activity. Core stabilization, cardiovascular reconditioning. Goal: functional restoration independent of pain level.\n- **Cognitive behavioral therapy**: Pain catastrophizing reduction, activity pacing, sleep hygiene. Address fear-avoidance and kinesiophobia (Tampa Scale for Kinesiophobia).\n- **Pain neuroscience education**: Explain central sensitization, neural plasticity, and the biopsychosocial model\n- **Vocational counseling**: Even if full return to electrician work is not possible, explore alternative employment or volunteer activity for occupational identity.\n\n**Medication Optimization**:\n- Duloxetine 60 mg daily (initiated in Phase 1)\n- Gabapentin dose optimization: ensure at adequate dose (up to 1200 mg TID) or consider switch to pregabalin for improved bioavailability\n- Low-dose naltrexone (4.5 mg nightly): emerging evidence for central sensitization through glial cell modulation\n- Gradual opioid taper (10% every 2-4 weeks) after stabilization. Target: lowest effective dose or complete discontinuation\n- Consider buprenorphine transition if opioid use disorder identified or if OIH confirmed\n- Topical agents: lidocaine patches to axial spine, capsaicin 8% patch to areas of allodynia")
B.aS8=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE",'The patient is awaiting a Social Security Disability determination. He asks you for a letter supporting his disability claim. Simultaneously, his attorney requests all medical records and asks you to state that the patient is "totally and permanently disabled due to surgical negligence."')
B.aU6=new A.c("+ Navigate the disability evaluation process and discuss the ethical considerations of disability certification versus functional restoration.",'**Social Security Disability (SSDI) Process**:\n- SSDI uses a 5-step sequential evaluation: (1) Is the applicant working at substantial gainful activity level? (2) Is the impairment severe? (3) Does it meet or equal a listed impairment? (4) Can they do past relevant work? (5) Can they do any other work?\n- **Listing 1.04 (Disorders of the Spine)**: Requires evidence of nerve root compression, spinal arachnoiditis, or lumbar stenosis resulting in pseudoclaudication with inability to ambulate effectively\n- The physician provides objective medical evidence; the adjudicator makes the disability determination\n\n**Responding to the Disability Letter Request**:\n- Provide an objective, factual medical report documenting: diagnoses, surgical history, exam findings, functional limitations, treatment history, and prognosis\n- Use standardized outcome measures (Oswestry Disability Index, 6-Minute Walk Test results) to support functional limitations\n- **Do not** state the patient is "totally and permanently disabled" \u2014 this is a legal/administrative determination, not a medical one\n- **Do not** opine on surgical negligence \u2014 this is outside your role as a treating physician and creates a conflict of interest\n\n**Responding to the Attorney**:\n- Release records only with signed patient authorization (HIPAA)\n- Decline to provide opinions on surgical negligence. State clearly: "As the treating physician, it is not appropriate for me to render opinions on the standard of care provided by other physicians. I am happy to provide objective documentation of the patient\'s current condition and functional status."\n- If asked to testify, this would require a separate agreement as an expert witness, not as a treating physician\n\n**Disability vs. Functional Restoration \u2014 The Tension**:\n- Studies show that patients who receive disability determination often have worse pain outcomes than those who pursue functional restoration\n- However, some patients with severe FBSS genuinely cannot return to competitive employment, and denying this reality creates harm\n- **Balanced approach**: Support the disability application with objective evidence while simultaneously pursuing maximum functional restoration. These are not mutually exclusive.\n- Frame the goal as maximizing quality of life, independence, and community participation regardless of employment status\n\n**Second Opinion Considerations**:\n- The patient has a right to seek additional surgical opinions\n- Refer to a spine surgeon at a different institution to avoid perceived bias\n- Provide all imaging and operative notes to the consulting surgeon\n- Prepare the patient that additional surgery is unlikely to help and carries significant risk in a multiply-operated spine\n\n**Insurance and Authorization**:\n- SCS and IDDS require extensive prior authorization documentation\n- Most payers require evidence of failed conservative treatment (6+ months), psychological clearance, and successful trial\n- Appeal process for denials: peer-to-peer review with medical director, citing PROCESS trial and NICE guidelines supporting SCS for FBSS')
B.b0Q=new A.c(u.j,"During a follow-up visit, the patient becomes agitated and states: \"My last surgeon ruined my life. He botched every single surgery. I'm going to sue him for everything he's worth, and you need to write in my chart that he committed malpractice. If you don't help me, I'll sue you too.\"")
B.aZg=new A.c("+ Demonstrate how you maintain professional boundaries while responding to a patient threatening litigation against a colleague and against you.",'**Step 1 \u2014 Remain Calm and De-escalate**:\n"I can see how frustrated and angry you are right now, and I understand why. You have been through three surgeries and are in more pain than when you started. That is incredibly demoralizing, and your feelings are completely valid."\n\n**Step 2 \u2014 Acknowledge the Suffering Without Validating the Legal Claim**:\n"What I can tell you with certainty is that your pain is real, your suffering is real, and you deserve better than what you have been experiencing. I am here to help you get to a better place, and that is my sole focus as your physician."\n\n**Step 3 \u2014 Set a Clear Professional Boundary**:\n"However, I must be transparent with you about what I can and cannot do. As your treating physician, it is not within my role to evaluate whether another surgeon met the standard of care. I was not present for those surgeries, and making that determination requires a formal expert review process that is separate from clinical care. Writing that in your chart would not be accurate and could actually harm your legal case if it were deemed unfounded."\n\n**Step 4 \u2014 Address the Threat Directly but Without Defensiveness**:\n"I also want to address your comment about suing me. I understand you are in pain and frustrated, and sometimes we say things in those moments that come from a place of desperation. My commitment to you is to provide the best care I can based on the evidence. If at any point you feel I am not meeting your needs, we can discuss alternative providers. But I want to continue working with you because I believe we can improve your quality of life."\n\n**Step 5 \u2014 Redirect to Actionable Steps**:\n"What I can do is document your current condition thoroughly and objectively, provide a comprehensive functional assessment, and ensure your medical records accurately reflect your clinical course. If you choose to pursue legal action, your attorney can request those records and retain an independent medical expert for opinions on standard of care."\n\n**Step 6 \u2014 Reaffirm the Therapeutic Relationship**:\n"Right now, the most important thing is your health and your recovery. I am proposing treatments that have strong evidence for improving function and reducing pain. Can we focus our energy on that plan? I believe there is still meaningful improvement possible for you, and I want to help you get there."\n\n**Documentation**:\n- Document the patient\'s statements verbatim in the medical record\n- Document your response and the boundaries set\n- Note the clinical rationale for declining to opine on another physician\'s care\n- Consider notifying risk management if the patient explicitly threatens litigation against you\n- Continue providing standard of care treatment regardless of litigation threats')
B.aFa=s([B.b1F,B.b36,B.aWV,B.aZU,B.aWm,B.aP3,B.b_T,B.aZN,B.aS8,B.aU6,B.b0Q,B.aZg],t.E)
B.R7=new A.a5("pain-failed-back","Failed Back Surgery Syndrome & Central Sensitization",B.aFa,!1)
B.aUp=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 58-year-old female retired schoolteacher is referred to outpatient PM&R for functional optimization and pain management. She was diagnosed with pancreatic adenocarcinoma 8 months ago, initially Stage III locally advanced, now with metastatic disease to the liver and peritoneum identified on restaging CT 6 weeks ago. She has completed 4 cycles of FOLFIRINOX chemotherapy with partial response but has declined further cycles due to toxicity. Her oncologist estimates a prognosis of 4-8 months. She presents with severe epigastric and mid-back pain rated 8/10, described as deep, constant, gnawing abdominal pain with radiation through to the thoracolumbar spine. She also reports a burning, shooting quality in the left flank suggesting neuropathic involvement of the celiac plexus. Current medications include extended-release morphine 60 mg BID, immediate-release morphine 15 mg Q4H PRN (using 4-6 breakthrough doses daily), ondansetron 4 mg TID, senna-docusate BID, and dexamethasone 4 mg daily. Despite these medications, she rates pain control as "poor." She has lost 28 pounds over 8 months (current weight 112 lbs, BMI 19.2). She is widowed and lives with her 30-year-old daughter. Her daughter\'s wedding is planned in 11 weeks. The patient states that walking her daughter down the aisle is her most important goal.')
B.b1y=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","**Cancer Pain Classification (Essential Framework)**:\n- **Nociceptive somatic pain**: Abdominal wall involvement, peritoneal carcinomatosis causing diffuse abdominal aching\n- **Nociceptive visceral pain**: Pancreatic tumor mass effect, hepatic capsular distension from metastases \u2014 deep, poorly localized, gnawing quality\n- **Neuropathic pain**: Celiac plexus invasion \u2014 burning, shooting, lancinating quality in left flank and back. May involve splanchnic nerves and retroperitoneal nerve plexus.\n- **Cancer-related bone pain**: Assess for osseous metastases (not yet identified but must be surveilled)\n- **Treatment-related pain**: Chemotherapy-induced peripheral neuropathy from oxaliplatin component of FOLFIRINOX\n\n**WHO Analgesic Ladder (Modified for Cancer Pain)**:\n- Step 1: Non-opioids (acetaminophen, NSAIDs)\n- Step 2: Weak opioids (tramadol, codeine) \u2014 often bypassed in moderate-severe cancer pain\n- Step 3: Strong opioids (morphine, oxycodone, hydromorphone, fentanyl, methadone)\n- Step 4 (modern addition): Interventional procedures (nerve blocks, intrathecal pumps, neurolysis)\n- **Adjuvant agents at every step**: Antidepressants, anticonvulsants, corticosteroids, bisphosphonates\n\n**Functional Assessment**:\n- **ECOG Performance Status**: Currently 2 (ambulatory, capable of self-care, unable to carry out work activities, up and about >50% of waking hours)\n- **Karnofsky Performance Scale**: 60% (requires occasional assistance but cares for most personal needs)\n- **Edmonton Symptom Assessment System (ESAS)**: Pain 8/10, fatigue 7/10, nausea 5/10, appetite 2/10, drowsiness 6/10, depression 5/10\n- **Nutritional status**: Significant cancer cachexia, albumin 2.8 g/dL, prealbumin 12 mg/dL\n- **Gait and mobility**: Walks with rolling walker, limited to 150 feet before needing to rest. Difficulty with stairs (bedroom and bathroom are on second floor).\n- **ADLs**: Independent in basic ADLs with modified technique but reports increasing fatigue limiting IADLs\n\n**Systems Review**:\n- Assess for bowel obstruction symptoms (nausea, vomiting, distension) \u2014 common in peritoneal carcinomatosis\n- Screen for depression and anxiety (PHQ-9, GAD-7). Assess existential distress and spiritual needs.\n- Evaluate sleep quality \u2014 pain disrupting sleep is an emergency in palliative care\n- Review for opioid side effects: constipation (present, on bowel regimen), nausea, sedation, myoclonus (can occur at higher morphine doses due to M3G accumulation)")
B.aNi=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 108/64 mmHg, HR 92 bpm, BMI 19.2 kg/m2, temperature 37.1C. Appears cachectic and fatigued but alert and engaged.\n\n**General**: Thin woman appearing older than stated age. Temporal wasting. Mild jaundice of sclerae. Dry mucous membranes. No peripheral edema.\n\n**Abdomen**: Mildly distended. Diffuse tenderness to palpation, most severe in epigastrium and left upper quadrant. Hepatomegaly palpable 4 cm below costal margin. No rebound, no guarding. Bowel sounds present but hypoactive.\n\n**Back**: Tenderness to palpation over T10-L1 spinous processes and bilateral paraspinals. No step-off deformity. Percussion tenderness at T11-T12.\n\n**Neurologic**:\n- **Motor**: Bilateral upper extremities 4+/5 globally (deconditioning). Lower extremities: hip flexors 4/5, knee extensors 4+/5, ankle dorsiflexors 5/5, plantarflexors 5/5\n- **Sensory**: Decreased sensation to pinprick in a stocking distribution to mid-calf bilaterally (oxaliplatin neuropathy). Hyperalgesia in left T10-T12 dermatome (celiac plexus involvement). Allodynia over left flank.\n- **Reflexes**: 2+ upper extremities, 1+ bilateral Achilles (neuropathy), 2+ patellar. No pathologic reflexes.\n- **Coordination**: Finger-to-nose intact. Tandem gait impaired (weakness and deconditioning)\n\n**Musculoskeletal**: Significant proximal muscle wasting (deltoids, quadriceps, gluteals). Reduced grip strength bilaterally (12 kg right, 10 kg left; expected >20 kg). Bilateral shoulder ROM full but weak at end range. No contractures.\n\n**Functional Mobility**: Sit-to-stand requires one upper extremity assist. Gait with rolling walker \u2014 slow, short stride, mild Trendelenburg bilaterally. 6-Minute Walk Test: 180 meters (predicted >400 meters for age). Timed Up and Go: 18 seconds (fall risk >13.5 seconds).\n\n**Skin**: No pressure injuries. Surgical scar from prior Whipple attempt (aborted due to vascular involvement). Port-a-cath site left subclavian, clean.")
B.b2B=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The patient's pain is inadequately controlled on her current regimen. She is drowsy from the morphine but still in severe pain. You must optimize her cancer pain management.")
B.aU2=new A.c("+ Discuss the approach to opioid rotation in cancer pain and the role of interventional procedures. When would you consider a celiac plexus block?","**Opioid Rotation \u2014 Rationale and Method**:\nThis patient has a poor response to morphine with significant side effects (drowsiness) \u2014 classic indication for opioid rotation due to incomplete cross-tolerance between opioid receptor subtypes.\n\n**Current Opioid Burden**:\n- ER morphine 60 mg BID = 120 mg/day\n- IR morphine 15 mg x 5 doses average = 75 mg/day\n- Total oral morphine equivalent = 195 mg/day\n\n**Rotation Options**:\n1. **Hydromorphone**: Convert using equianalgesic table (oral morphine 30 mg = oral hydromorphone 6 mg). 195 mg morphine = 39 mg hydromorphone. Apply 25-50% dose reduction for incomplete cross-tolerance = 20-30 mg oral hydromorphone daily. Consider ER hydromorphone (Exalgo) with IR hydromorphone for breakthrough.\n2. **Methadone**: Potent for neuropathic pain (NMDA antagonist properties), long half-life, inexpensive. However, complex pharmacokinetics and QTc prolongation risk. Conversion ratios are NOT linear \u2014 for >200 mg oral morphine, ratio is approximately 8-12:1. Requires inpatient monitoring for initiation. Consider if neuropathic component dominant.\n3. **Transdermal fentanyl**: Useful if oral route compromised (nausea, dysphagia, bowel obstruction). Convert: oral morphine 195 mg/day divided by 2 = approximately 100 mcg/hr patch. Apply 25% reduction = 75 mcg/hr. Provide IR breakthrough (oral morphine or fentanyl lozenge).\n\n**Recommended rotation**: Hydromorphone given her renal function is adequate, nausea concerns favor ER formulation, and it has less neuroexcitatory metabolite accumulation (no M3G equivalent).\n\n**Adjuvant Medications**:\n- **Neuropathic component**: Add pregabalin 75 mg BID, titrate to 150 mg BID (better bioavailability than gabapentin and less pill burden in polypharmacy)\n- **Continue dexamethasone**: Reduces peritumoral edema, improves appetite, anti-emetic properties\n- **Acetaminophen 650 mg QID**: Additive analgesia with opioids, opioid-sparing\n- **Consider ketamine**: Sub-anesthetic oral ketamine 10-25 mg TID for refractory neuropathic pain (off-label but used in palliative care)\n\n**Celiac Plexus Block/Neurolysis**:\n- **Indication**: Visceral abdominal pain from pancreatic cancer unresponsive to pharmacologic management \u2014 this is one of the strongest indications in all of interventional pain medicine\n- **Technique**: CT or fluoroscopy-guided, posterior transcrural or anterior approach. Diagnostic block with local anesthetic first; if >50% relief, proceed with neurolysis using absolute alcohol or phenol.\n- **Evidence**: Randomized trials show celiac plexus neurolysis provides superior pain relief to pharmacologic management alone in pancreatic cancer, with reduced opioid requirements and improved quality of life\n- **Timing**: Should be offered early rather than as a last resort. In this patient with severe visceral pain and neuropathic features suggestive of celiac plexus involvement, this should be pursued within 1-2 weeks.\n- **Risks**: Transient diarrhea (30-40%), orthostatic hypotension (10-20%), rare but serious complications include retroperitoneal hemorrhage, paraplegia (artery of Adamkiewicz), pneumothorax\n- **Expected benefit**: 70-90% of pancreatic cancer patients achieve good-to-excellent pain relief lasting weeks to months")
B.aSR=new A.c("+ How do you design a palliative rehabilitation program that balances function with realistic prognosis?",'**Palliative Rehabilitation \u2014 Principles**:\nPalliative rehabilitation is not about "getting better" in the traditional sense. It is about maximizing function, dignity, and quality of life within the constraints of a life-limiting illness. The goal shifts from restorative to **adaptive and supportive**.\n\n**Rehabilitation Goal Framework (Time-Based)**:\n- **Short-term (2-4 weeks)**: Optimize pain control, improve home safety, maintain current ADL independence, address fall risk\n- **Medium-term (4-12 weeks)**: Build enough endurance and strength to walk her daughter down the aisle in 11 weeks. This is a concrete, meaningful, achievable goal.\n- **Long-term (ongoing)**: Maintain comfort, adapt environment as function declines, prepare for increasing dependency, advance care planning\n\n**Exercise Prescription in Advanced Cancer**:\n- **Evidence base**: Exercise in advanced cancer improves fatigue (strongest evidence), physical function, mood, and sleep. It does NOT accelerate disease progression.\n- **Aerobic training**: Start at current tolerance (she can walk 150 feet). Progress by 10% per week. Use RPE (Borg scale 3-4/10). Walking program in the home, progressing to outdoors. Target: sustained walking of 100-150 feet continuously (approximate aisle length plus standing time)\n- **Resistance training**: Light resistance (1-2 lb weights, resistance bands). Focus on sit-to-stand, hip extensors, and quadriceps for gait stability. 2-3 sets of 8-10 repetitions, 3 days per week.\n- **Balance training**: Static standing balance progressing to dynamic. Tandem stance, single-leg stance with support. Critical for fall prevention and confidence walking the aisle.\n- **Prehabilitation for the wedding**: Practice walking the expected distance in the expected shoes. Practice standing for the ceremony duration. Develop a backup plan (wheelchair nearby, seated option).\n\n**Nutritional Optimization**:\n- Dietitian consultation for cancer cachexia management\n- High-calorie, high-protein supplements (goal: 1.2-1.5 g protein/kg/day)\n- Megestrol acetate or dronabinol for appetite stimulation (though evidence is limited)\n- Address nausea aggressively to enable intake\n\n**Home Modifications**:\n- First-floor bedroom and bathroom setup (hospital bed, bedside commode) to eliminate stairs\n- Shower bench, grab bars, raised toilet seat\n- Remove throw rugs and clutter (fall prevention)\n- Rollator walker optimization \u2014 ensure correct height, consider a rollator with a seat for rest breaks\n\n**Psychosocial Support**:\n- Social work referral for advance care planning, POLST/MOLST completion, power of attorney, healthcare proxy\n- Psychology or chaplain referral for existential distress\n- Caregiver burden assessment for the daughter (who is simultaneously planning a wedding and caring for a dying parent)\n- Support groups for patients with advanced cancer\n\n**Monitoring**:\n- Weekly reassessment of ECOG status and ESAS\n- Titrate exercise intensity to current functional status (may need to scale back if disease progresses)\n- Reassess goals regularly \u2014 if prognosis changes, goals must adapt')
B.aXq=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE",'The patient\'s daughter asks about hospice, but the patient says she is "not ready to give up." The oncologist has recommended stopping chemotherapy. The daughter asks if her mother can receive rehab and hospice at the same time.')
B.b08=new A.c("+ Clarify the distinction between hospice and palliative care, and discuss whether concurrent hospice and rehabilitation services are possible.",'**Hospice vs. Palliative Care \u2014 Critical Distinctions**:\n\n**Palliative Care**:\n- Appropriate at any stage of serious illness, regardless of prognosis\n- Can be concurrent with curative or disease-modifying treatment\n- Focuses on symptom management, quality of life, and psychosocial support\n- Provided by a specialty team (physician, NP, social worker, chaplain) in any setting\n- Covered by standard insurance as a consultative service\n- No requirement to forgo curative treatment\n\n**Hospice**:\n- A specific model of palliative care for patients with estimated prognosis of 6 months or less if the disease follows its expected course\n- Medicare Hospice Benefit requires two physician certifications of terminal prognosis\n- Patient must elect the benefit and agree to forgo curative treatment for the terminal diagnosis\n- Provides comprehensive services: nursing, aide, social work, chaplain, bereavement support, medications related to the terminal diagnosis, DME\n- Settings: home, inpatient hospice facility, nursing home, hospital\n- Can be revoked at any time if patient changes mind\n\n**Concurrent Hospice and Rehabilitation**:\n- This is a common source of confusion. The hospice benefit covers services that are **palliative in nature**, which CAN include rehabilitation.\n- **PT, OT, and SLP** can be provided under hospice if they are aimed at comfort, safety, and maintaining function (not restoration to a prior level)\n- Examples: PT for fall prevention, safe transfers, and gait training to maintain mobility; OT for energy conservation and adaptive equipment; SLP for safe swallowing\n- The key distinction is **intent**: rehabilitation under hospice must be documented as comfort-directed or safety-directed, not restorative\n- Many hospice agencies have therapists on staff or contract with rehabilitation providers\n\n**For This Patient**:\n- She meets prognostic criteria for hospice (4-8 month estimated survival, declining functional status, stopped chemotherapy)\n- However, she has a time-limited, concrete rehabilitation goal (walking down the aisle in 11 weeks)\n- **Recommended approach**: Initiate palliative care consultation now. Discuss hospice enrollment with the patient and daughter. If she enrolls in hospice, negotiate with the hospice agency for therapy services directed at her wedding goal.\n- Alternatively, she can receive outpatient rehabilitation now (covered by standard Medicare Part B) and transition to hospice when she is ready or when her function declines further.\n- Reframe for the patient: "Hospice is not giving up. It is choosing to focus all of our energy on what matters most to you \u2014 being present and comfortable for your daughter\'s wedding and the time you have together. Hospice brings more support into your home, not less."\n\n**Advance Care Planning**:\n- POLST/MOLST form completion\n- Healthcare proxy designation (likely daughter)\n- Discussion of resuscitation preferences\n- Goals of care documentation in the medical record\n- Ensure oncology, PM&R, palliative care, and primary care are aligned on the care plan')
B.aTs=new A.c(u.j,'During your visit, the patient tearfully states: "I need to walk my daughter down the aisle. Her father died when she was twelve, and I promised I would be there. Please tell me I can do this."')
B.aPu=new A.c("+ Respond to this emotionally charged request, setting achievable goals while preserving hope in the context of a terminal prognosis.",'**Step 1 \u2014 Honor the Emotion and the Meaning**:\n"Thank you for sharing that with me. I can hear how deeply important this is to you, and I want you to know that I take this goal as seriously as you do. This is not just about walking \u2014 this is about a promise you made to your daughter, and it matters."\n\n**Step 2 \u2014 Be Honest Without Crushing Hope**:\n"I want to be completely honest with you, because you deserve that. Your body has been through a lot, and the cancer and the treatments have taken a real toll on your strength. Eleven weeks from now, I cannot guarantee exactly how you will feel. But what I can tell you is that there are things we can do right now to give you the best possible chance of being there the way you want to be."\n\n**Step 3 \u2014 Present a Concrete Plan**:\n"Here is what I am going to do. We are going to start a rehabilitation program specifically designed around that walk down the aisle. We are going to work on your endurance so you can walk the distance. We are going to work on your balance so you feel steady. We are going to get your pain under better control so you are not suffering through this. And we are going to build in rest strategies and backup plans so that no matter what happens, you are there for your daughter on that day."\n\n**Step 4 \u2014 Normalize Adaptation Without Framing It as Failure**:\n"Now, I want us to think about this together. What does \'walking her down the aisle\' look like to you? Some of my patients have done this with a walker and felt proud. Some have been in a wheelchair and held their daughter\'s hand the whole way. The love and the meaning are the same whether you are walking independently or being wheeled. What matters is that you are there, present, and as comfortable as possible."\n\n**Step 5 \u2014 Empower Shared Goal-Setting**:\n"Can we plan for the best-case scenario while also having a backup that feels okay to you? I would like to involve your daughter in this conversation too, if you are comfortable with that, so we can all work toward the same goal."\n\n**Step 6 \u2014 Address the Unspoken**:\n"I also want you to know that whatever happens in the weeks and months ahead, the work we are doing now is not just about the wedding. It is about giving you the most comfortable, meaningful time possible with your daughter and the people you love. That is worth fighting for, and I am in this with you."\n\n**Key Communication Principles**:\n- **Hope and honesty are not opposites** \u2014 you can maintain hope while being truthful about uncertainty\n- **Goal concordance**: The physician\'s treatment plan is built around the patient\'s stated goal, not abstract medical outcomes\n- **Adaptive planning**: Offering a continuum of success (walking independently, walking with a device, wheelchair participation) ensures the patient does not feel like a failure if full ambulation is not possible\n- **Avoid prognostic precision**: Do not say "you have 4-8 months" \u2014 frame uncertainty honestly without destroying hope\n- **Include the family**: The daughter is both caregiver and central to the patient\'s primary goal; her involvement is essential')
B.aEE=s([B.aUp,B.b1y,B.aNi,B.b2B,B.aU2,B.aSR,B.aXq,B.b08,B.aTs,B.aPu],t.E)
B.PO=new A.a5("pain-cancer-palliative","Cancer Pain Management \u2014 Palliative Rehabilitation",B.aEE,!1)
B.aZm=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 14-year-old girl is referred to outpatient pediatric PM&R after 6 months of progressive bilateral lower extremity weakness resulting in wheelchair dependence. The onset was subacute, beginning with difficulty climbing stairs and progressing over 2 weeks to inability to stand or walk. She was admitted to the pediatric neurology service twice. Extensive workup including brain and full-spine MRI, lumbar puncture, EMG/NCS, anti-ganglioside antibodies, anti-AQP4 and anti-MOG antibodies, CK, aldolase, ANA panel, vitamin B12, TSH, heavy metals, and genetic testing for hereditary neuropathies have all been unremarkable. She was treated empirically with a 5-day course of IV methylprednisolone and 5 days of IVIG with no improvement. She has been using a manual wheelchair full-time for 4 months. She reports bilateral leg pain rated 6/10, described as "heavy and tingling." She has missed 4 months of school and has been receiving homebound tutoring for 3 hours per week. Prior to onset, she was a competitive gymnast training 15 hours per week. She lives with her mother and stepfather. Her biological father is incarcerated. Her mother reports that the girl\'s best friend moved away 2 months before symptom onset, and she had been bullied at school for several weeks before the weakness began. Her mother is visibly anxious and has brought a binder of medical records and internet research suggesting possible rare mitochondrial disorders.')
B.aRF=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","**Functional Neurological Disorder (FND) \u2014 Key Historical Features**:\n- Acute or subacute onset of neurological symptoms that are incompatible with recognized neurological disease\n- Symptoms often follow a stressor (bullying, loss of friend, family disruption)\n- Extensive negative workup despite significant disability\n- History of high-achieving or high-performance background (competitive gymnast)\n- La belle indifference may or may not be present (its absence does not rule out FND)\n\n**Psychosocial Assessment (Critical in Pediatric FND)**:\n- **Precipitating stressors**: Best friend moved away, bullying at school, biological father incarcerated \u2014 multiple adverse childhood experiences (ACEs)\n- **Family dynamics**: Mother-stepfather relationship, custody situation, maternal anxiety and hypervigilance about medical diagnoses\n- **School performance**: Prior academic history, social relationships, any learning disabilities\n- **Psychiatric screening**: Assess for depression (PHQ-A), anxiety (GAD-7 or SCARED), PTSD, history of abuse or neglect\n- **Symptom modeling**: Has the patient observed wheelchair use or significant disability in family members or peers?\n- **Secondary gain assessment**: What has the wheelchair provided? (escape from school bullying, increased parental attention, avoidance of gymnastics pressure)\n- Important: secondary gain does not mean the patient is faking. FND is a genuine neurological disorder of brain network function, not malingering.\n\n**Pain Assessment**:\n- Bilateral leg heaviness and tingling \u2014 assess with Pediatric Pain Questionnaire\n- Pain catastrophizing in adolescents (Pain Catastrophizing Scale \u2014 Child version)\n- Functional Disability Inventory (FDI) \u2014 validated measure of pain-related disability in pediatrics\n- Sleep assessment: insomnia and pain amplification are commonly comorbid\n\n**Prior Treatment Review**:\n- Document all negative testing thoroughly (this will be important for parent education)\n- Review response to empiric immunologic treatment (none, supporting non-inflammatory etiology)\n- Assess for iatrogenic harm: unnecessary procedures, reinforcement of sick role through prolonged wheelchair use, homebound instruction reinforcing disability identity\n\n**Red Flags to Reassess**:\n- Progressive symptoms despite rehabilitation (consider re-evaluation)\n- New neurologic signs not consistent with FND (upper motor neuron signs, true muscle atrophy, optic neuritis)\n- Weight loss, fevers, night sweats\n- Bowel or bladder incontinence that is persistent and cannot be explained by functional patterns")
B.b1H=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:",'**General**: Well-appearing, well-nourished 14-year-old girl seated in manual wheelchair. Appears comfortable at rest. Good eye contact. Appropriately interactive but becomes quiet when asked about school.\n\n**Vitals**: BP 110/68 mmHg, HR 78 bpm, BMI 21.5 kg/m2 (50th percentile). Afebrile.\n\n**Neurologic Examination \u2014 Positive Clinical Signs of FND**:\n- **Hoover sign (POSITIVE)**: When asked to extend the left hip against resistance, the hip extensors appear 2/5 strength. However, when asked to flex the right hip against resistance, the left hip extends with full force involuntarily (synergistic activation). Same finding on the right side. This is a validated positive clinical sign of functional weakness.\n- **Hip abductor sign (POSITIVE)**: When testing hip abduction, the contralateral hip (supposedly weak) demonstrates full abduction synergy. Inconsistent with organic weakness.\n- **Give-way weakness**: When testing knee extension, the patient initially demonstrates normal strength then suddenly releases. This "collapsing" pattern differs from true upper or lower motor neuron weakness patterns.\n- **Tremor entrainment**: When asked to tap a rhythm with her right hand, a bilateral lower extremity tremor that was present at rest ceases. This entrainment phenomenon is a positive sign of functional tremor.\n- **Drift without pronation**: When arms are extended with eyes closed, there is downward drift of the bilateral upper extremities without pronation. Organic upper motor neuron drift is accompanied by pronation.\n\n**Neurologic Examination \u2014 Conventional Findings**:\n- **Motor (voluntary testing)**: Bilateral hip flexors 2/5, hip extensors 2/5, knee extensors 3/5, knee flexors 3/5, ankle dorsiflexors 3/5, plantarflexors 3/5 (note: effort-dependent and inconsistent between trials)\n- **Functional motor**: Despite the above, when distracted during transfers, she was observed to briefly bear full weight through both legs before catching herself and sitting back down\n- **Sensory**: Reports decreased sensation to light touch in a non-dermatomal pattern (entire left leg to the groin crease, "splitting the midline" \u2014 a finding inconsistent with organic pathology as the midline has bilateral innervation)\n- **Reflexes**: 2+ and symmetric throughout. No clonus. Babinski downgoing bilaterally.\n- **Coordination**: Finger-to-nose intact. No cerebellar signs.\n- **Cranial nerves**: Intact throughout.\n\n**Musculoskeletal**: No contractures despite 6 months of wheelchair use (suggesting she is moving her legs more than reported). No muscle atrophy. Muscle bulk appears normal and symmetric.\n\n**Gait Assessment**: Patient states she cannot stand. With bilateral support and encouragement, she is able to stand with significant visible effort and truncal sway but does not collapse. Takes 3 steps with a slow, deliberate, effortful pattern that is not consistent with any specific neurologic gait pattern (not spastic, not ataxic, not steppage).')
B.aSH=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING",'The neurologist has documented "functional neurological disorder" in the chart but told the family that "all the tests are normal." The parents are not satisfied with this explanation and want more testing.')
B.aS6=new A.c('+ Explain the diagnostic criteria for FND, the significance of positive clinical signs, and why this is a "rule-in" diagnosis rather than a "rule-out" diagnosis.','**Functional Neurological Disorder (FND) \u2014 Diagnostic Framework**:\n\n**DSM-5 Criteria (Conversion Disorder/FND)**:\n1. One or more symptoms of altered voluntary motor or sensory function\n2. Clinical findings provide evidence of **incompatibility** between the symptom and recognized neurological or medical conditions\n3. The symptom is not better explained by another medical or mental disorder\n4. The symptom causes clinically significant distress or impairment\n\n**Critical Concept \u2014 FND is a "Rule-In" Diagnosis**:\n- FND is NOT a diagnosis of exclusion. It is diagnosed based on **positive clinical signs** that demonstrate internal inconsistency.\n- Simply having a negative workup is insufficient and leads to diagnostic insecurity for patients and families\n- The positive signs found in this patient are validated, reliable clinical markers:\n\n**Positive Clinical Signs Found (and Their Significance)**:\n1. **Hoover sign**: Sensitivity 63%, specificity 100% for functional leg weakness. The involuntary synergistic activation of the "weak" hip extensors during contralateral hip flexion demonstrates that the motor pathways are intact and the weakness is not due to structural damage.\n2. **Hip abductor sign**: Same principle \u2014 contralateral synergistic activation during ipsilateral testing\n3. **Give-way (collapsing) weakness**: Inconsistent with pyramidal or lower motor neuron patterns. True weakness shows consistent force generation.\n4. **Tremor entrainment**: Disappearance of tremor during a distraction task proves the tremor is not generated by a fixed oscillator (as in Parkinson disease or essential tremor)\n5. **Midline splitting of sensory loss**: The midline has bilateral innervation; organic lesions do not cause sharp midline sensory boundaries\n6. **Drift without pronation**: Organic corticospinal tract dysfunction causes pronation with drift; drift without pronation suggests functional etiology\n7. **Observed weight-bearing during distracted transfer**: Demonstrates motor capability that is not accessible during formal testing\n\n**Why Further Testing is NOT Indicated**:\n- All appropriate testing has been completed and is negative\n- Positive clinical signs CONFIRM the diagnosis \u2014 they do not merely suggest it by exclusion\n- Additional testing carries risks: reinforcement of the sick role, iatrogenic anxiety, false-positive findings leading to inappropriate treatments, radiation exposure, procedural complications\n- Each additional test communicates to the patient: "We still think something might be structurally wrong"\n\n**Important Nuance**:\n- FND is a disorder of brain network function, not "imagined" or "faked"\n- fMRI studies show altered connectivity in the right temporoparietal junction and supplementary motor area in FND patients\n- It is as "real" as any other neurological condition and requires specialized treatment\n- Malingering is a conscious, deliberate fabrication of symptoms for external gain \u2014 FND is involuntary')
B.b21=new A.c("+ What is the prognosis for pediatric FND, and what factors predict good vs. poor outcomes?","**Prognosis for Pediatric FND**:\n- Generally better than adult FND, with higher rates of full recovery\n- Approximately 60-80% of pediatric patients with motor FND achieve significant improvement or full resolution with appropriate treatment within 12 months\n- Early diagnosis and rapid initiation of rehabilitation are the strongest predictors of good outcome\n- Prolonged symptom duration (>12 months) before treatment is associated with poorer outcomes\n\n**Favorable Prognostic Factors**:\n- Younger age at onset\n- Shorter duration of symptoms before diagnosis and treatment\n- Identifiable precipitating stressor (present: bullying, friend loss)\n- Acute onset (present: progressed over 2 weeks)\n- Good premorbid function (present: competitive gymnast, good student)\n- Acceptance of the diagnosis by patient and family\n- Absence of comorbid personality disorders\n- Intact family support system\n- No ongoing litigation or secondary gain\n\n**Unfavorable Prognostic Factors in This Patient**:\n- 6 months of wheelchair dependence (significant but not yet beyond the window for good recovery)\n- Homebound schooling reinforcing disability identity\n- Mother's anxiety and medical hypervigilance may perpetuate illness behavior\n- Multiple ACEs (incarcerated father, bullying, social loss)\n- Identity as a competitive gymnast has been lost, creating a vacuum\n- Two prior hospitalizations may have reinforced medical model thinking\n\n**Risk of Recurrence**:\n- Even with full recovery, FND symptoms may recur under future stressors\n- Relapse prevention is a critical component of treatment: identifying personal triggers, developing coping strategies, early intervention plan if symptoms recur\n- Family must understand that recurrence does not mean the diagnosis was wrong")
B.b2U=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You plan to admit the patient to the inpatient pediatric rehabilitation unit for an intensive 2-3 week functional restoration program. Design the treatment approach.")
B.aPb=new A.c("+ Outline the interdisciplinary rehabilitation program for pediatric FND, including the critical first step of diagnosis communication.",'**Step 1 \u2014 Diagnosis Communication (The Most Important Step)**:\n- This conversation sets the trajectory for the entire treatment course. Done well, it creates buy-in. Done poorly, it alienates the family.\n- Meet with the patient and both parents together\n- Use language that is validating and explanatory, not dismissive:\n  - "Your daughter\'s weakness is real. Her brain is sending the wrong signals to her legs, like a software problem rather than a hardware problem. All of the hardware \u2014 the nerves, the muscles, the spinal cord \u2014 is working perfectly. But the signals are getting scrambled."\n  - "This is called Functional Neurological Disorder. We see this in young people, especially high-achieving, strong young people like your daughter. It is the brain\'s response to overwhelming stress."\n  - "The good news is that because the hardware is intact, the brain can relearn how to send the right signals. That is what rehabilitation is for, and children your daughter\'s age respond very well to this treatment."\n- Show the positive clinical signs to the family: demonstrate the Hoover sign in real time so they can see the legs working involuntarily\n- Acknowledge the stressors without forcing the patient to discuss them in detail: "Sometimes the brain protects us from overwhelming experiences by redirecting the stress into the body"\n\n**Step 2 \u2014 Inpatient Rehabilitation Program (2-3 Weeks)**:\n\n**Physical Therapy (Primary Treatment Modality)**:\n- Sessions: 60 minutes, 2x daily, 5 days per week\n- **Graded motor retraining**: Begin with the movements the patient can do (demonstrated during Hoover sign and distracted transfer). Build from automatic/involuntary movements to voluntary control.\n- **Distraction-based techniques**: Perform dual tasks (cognitive tasks, ball-catching, music) while walking to bypass the voluntary motor interference\n- **Treadmill training with body-weight support**: Allows the patient to walk with reduced fear of falling. Gradually decrease support as confidence builds.\n- **Mirror therapy and visual feedback**: Use mirrors to provide visual input of leg movement during involuntary activation\n- **Goal-setting**: Collaborative, patient-directed. Break walking into micro-goals (standing 30 seconds, 1 step, 5 steps, hallway, gym). Celebrate each milestone.\n- **Critical principle**: NEVER confront the patient about inconsistency. Never say, "We saw you move your legs, so we know you can walk." This is counterproductive and damages trust.\n\n**Psychology/Psychiatry (Concurrent)**:\n- CBT adapted for adolescents: cognitive restructuring of catastrophic thoughts about disability, behavioral activation\n- Process trauma and stressors: bullying, friend loss, father\'s incarceration. Trauma-informed care approach.\n- Address perfectionism and high-achievement pressure (common in gymnasts with FND)\n- Family therapy: address maternal anxiety, family dynamics with stepfather, communication patterns\n- Screen for and treat comorbid depression and anxiety (SSRI if indicated \u2014 fluoxetine is first-line in adolescents)\n\n**Occupational Therapy**:\n- ADL retraining progressing from wheelchair level to standing and ambulatory\n- Energy conservation and activity pacing\n- School task simulation (sitting at a desk, carrying books, navigating hallways)\n\n**School Reintegration Planning**:\n- Begin during inpatient stay with education liaison\n- Develop a graduated return-to-school plan (start with 2 hours, increase weekly)\n- 504 Plan or IEP accommodations: elevator access, extra transition time, rest breaks, counseling access\n- Coordinate with school counselor regarding bullying intervention\n\n**Recreational Therapy**:\n- Reintroduce physical activity in a low-pressure context\n- Group activities with peers on the rehab unit\n- Explore new interests if return to competitive gymnastics creates pressure\n\n**Pain Management**:\n- Avoid opioids \u2014 treat pain with CBT, PT, and non-pharmacologic strategies\n- Consider amitriptyline 10-25 mg at bedtime for pain and sleep\n- TENS unit for comfort during therapy\n\n**Discharge Criteria**:\n- Ambulating independently or with minimal device for household distances\n- Patient and family understand the diagnosis and the relapse prevention plan\n- Outpatient therapy arranged (PT 2x/week, psychology weekly)\n- School reintegration plan in place with specific dates\n- Follow-up with PM&R in 2 weeks, then monthly')
B.b_O=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE",'The school district is reluctant to allow the patient back without "medical clearance" and a "diagnosis that explains her wheelchair use." They are offering continued homebound instruction as an alternative.')
B.b3c=new A.c("+ Discuss the school reintegration process for a child with FND and how you navigate institutional barriers.",'**School Reintegration \u2014 The Make-or-Break Factor**:\n- Prolonged school absence is one of the strongest perpetuating factors in pediatric FND\n- Every week of continued homebound instruction reinforces disability identity and social isolation\n- Return to school is therapeutic, not contingent on full symptom resolution\n\n**Medical Documentation for the School**:\n- Provide a clear letter on institutional letterhead stating:\n  1. The diagnosis: Functional Neurological Disorder (a recognized neurological condition)\n  2. The treatment plan: Intensive rehabilitation with graduated return to full school participation\n  3. The medical recommendation: Return to in-person school is a critical component of treatment and is medically necessary\n  4. Accommodations needed: Specific, time-limited, with a plan for fading\n- Avoid language that reinforces disability: Do not write "patient cannot walk" \u2014 instead: "Patient is transitioning from wheelchair to ambulatory mobility and may require wheelchair access for distances initially"\n\n**504 Plan Accommodations**:\n- Elevator access (if applicable)\n- Extra time between classes\n- Permission to use wheelchair or walker as needed during transition\n- Preferential seating near the door\n- Access to school counselor as needed\n- Modified PE participation (graded return, not exemption)\n- Option for rest breaks in the nurse\'s office (with a time limit to prevent extended avoidance)\n- Reduced homework during initial reintegration, with a plan to return to full load\n\n**IEP vs. 504 Plan**:\n- 504 Plan is appropriate if the student does not have a qualifying learning disability and only needs accommodations\n- IEP may be needed if the prolonged absence has caused significant academic gaps requiring specialized instruction\n- FND may qualify under "Other Health Impairment" for an IEP if needed\n\n**Graduated Return Schedule**:\n- Week 1: 2 hours per day (attend favorite classes)\n- Week 2: Half day (4 hours)\n- Week 3: Full day with rest breaks\n- Week 4: Full day with accommodations\n- Month 2-3: Begin fading accommodations\n- Monitor and adjust based on response\n\n**Addressing the School\'s Concerns**:\n- Offer to participate in a school re-entry meeting (physician, parent, school counselor, teachers, principal)\n- Educate school staff about FND: it is a real neurological condition, the student is not faking, and return to school is prescribed as part of treatment\n- Provide the school with a brief FND information handout\n- Establish a communication protocol between the school nurse, parents, and the medical team\n\n**Avoiding Unnecessary Medical Procedures**:\n- The school may request additional testing or specialist clearance. Firmly state that the diagnosis has been established and additional testing is not medically indicated.\n- If the school insists on independent evaluation, offer a team conference rather than more tests\n- Document in writing that prolonged homebound instruction is contraindicated and contributes to poorer outcomes\n\n**Anti-Bullying Intervention**:\n- The precipitating bullying must be addressed before school return\n- Work with the school counselor to ensure a safe environment\n- Consider whether the patient should return to the same school or transfer if the bullying environment is not resolvable\n- The patient should not be forced to explain her condition to peers unless she chooses to')
B.b1j=new A.c(u.j,"The patient's mother becomes tearful and frustrated during the family meeting. She says: \"You're telling me nothing is wrong with my daughter? She can't walk! I've been researching mitochondrial disorders and I think she needs a muscle biopsy. You doctors have been dismissing us for six months. If this was a rare disease, you would have found it by now if you had just looked harder.\"")
B.aWx=new A.c("+ Respond to the mother's distress and demand for more testing without dismissing her concerns, while firmly redirecting toward rehabilitation.",'**Step 1 \u2014 Validate the Mother\'s Experience and Advocacy**:\n"I hear you, and I want to start by saying this: you are an incredible advocate for your daughter. The fact that you have organized all of her records, researched her symptoms, and fought for answers shows how much you love her. I do not want you to stop being her advocate. I need you to be her advocate."\n\n**Step 2 \u2014 Directly Address the Fear of Being Dismissed**:\n"I also want to be clear about something. I am not telling you that nothing is wrong. Something is very wrong \u2014 your daughter cannot walk, she is in pain, she has missed four months of school, and she has lost her sport and her social life. That is serious, and I am taking it seriously. What I am telling you is that we now understand what is causing it, and it is treatable."\n\n**Step 3 \u2014 Explain the Diagnosis in a Way That Validates the Symptoms**:\n"Functional Neurological Disorder means that the wiring in her brain \u2014 the connections between the parts that plan movement and the parts that execute it \u2014 is not communicating properly. It is like a software glitch in a perfectly good computer. The hardware \u2014 her muscles, her nerves, her spinal cord \u2014 has been tested thoroughly, and it is all healthy. That is actually very good news, because it means her body has the ability to recover."\n\n**Step 4 \u2014 Address the Testing Request Specifically**:\n"Regarding the muscle biopsy and mitochondrial testing: I understand why you are thinking about that, and it is a reasonable question. Here is what I want you to consider. Your daughter has had two MRIs, a spinal tap, an EMG, blood work for every inflammatory and genetic condition that could explain her symptoms, and two courses of immune therapy. All of that was appropriate and thorough. A muscle biopsy is an invasive procedure with risks, and mitochondrial disorders present with a very different clinical picture \u2014 progressive weakness, exercise intolerance, and specific laboratory findings like elevated lactate, none of which your daughter has. More importantly, your daughter has positive clinical signs that confirm the diagnosis of FND. Let me show you."\n\n**Step 5 \u2014 Demonstrate a Positive Sign in Real Time**:\n"Watch what happens when I test her left leg. When I push down on her left leg and ask her to resist, it appears weak. But now watch \u2014 when I ask her to lift her right leg, her left leg pushes down with full force without her even trying. That tells me the pathway from her brain to her leg is working perfectly. The problem is in the voluntary control signal, not the nerve or muscle. This is a positive diagnostic test, not just a negative workup."\n\n**Step 6 \u2014 Reframe Testing as Potentially Harmful**:\n"I worry that more testing would actually set your daughter back. Every new test sends a message to her brain: the doctors still think something might be structurally wrong. That message keeps her stuck in the pattern. What her brain needs right now is the opposite message: we know what this is, we know how to treat it, and we believe in her ability to recover."\n\n**Step 7 \u2014 Propose a Collaborative Path Forward**:\n"Here is what I am asking of you. Give us three weeks. Let your daughter come into our rehabilitation program where we have a team that specializes in exactly this. If she is not making meaningful progress in three weeks, I will sit down with you and we will reassess together. But I have seen young people just like your daughter walk out of our program. I believe she can too. Can we try this together?"\n\n**Key Principles**:\n- Never say "it\'s all in her head" or "there\'s nothing wrong"\n- Demonstrating positive signs provides tangible evidence that is more convincing than verbal explanation\n- Setting a time-limited trial gives the anxious parent a concrete endpoint and reduces the pressure of an open-ended commitment\n- Partnering with the parent rather than opposing them is essential for treatment success')
B.ajK=s([B.aZm,B.aRF,B.b1H,B.aSH,B.aS6,B.b21,B.b2U,B.aPb,B.b_O,B.b3c,B.b1j,B.aWx],t.E)
B.QD=new A.a5("pain-pediatric-fnd","Pediatric Chronic Pain \u2014 Functional Neurological Symptom Disorder",B.ajK,!1)
B.aR1=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 22-year-old Black female with sickle cell disease (HbSS) is referred to your PM&R pain management clinic for chronic daily pain between vaso-occlusive crises. She reports constant baseline pain in her bilateral hips, lower back, and bilateral shoulders rated 6-7/10 daily, with crisis-level exacerbations (10/10) requiring ED visits approximately every 6-8 weeks. She has been on daily opioids (oxycodone 15 mg QID) prescribed by her hematologist for the past 3 years. She is tearful and angry during the visit, stating: \"Every time I go to the ER in a crisis, they treat me like I am faking it. They make me wait for hours. The last time, a nurse said to another nurse \u2014 loud enough for me to hear \u2014 'She is probably just drug-seeking.' I am so tired of being treated like a criminal for being sick.\"")
B.aOJ=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **SCD History**: Diagnosed at birth. Genotype HbSS. Baseline hemoglobin 7.0-7.5 g/dL. History of multiple vaso-occlusive crises (3-5 per year since age 15). Two episodes of acute chest syndrome (ages 16 and 19). Avascular necrosis (AVN) of bilateral femoral heads (diagnosed age 20). Chronic hemolytic anemia.\n- **Chronic Pain Pattern**: Constant baseline pain began at approximately age 17-18. Multifocal: bilateral hips (AVN), lumbar spine, bilateral shoulders (early AVN), bilateral knees. Pain is present every day \u2014 never below 4/10. Described as deep, aching, with intermittent sharp exacerbations. Worse with activity, cold, stress, and menses.\n- **Crisis Pain vs. Chronic Pain**: She clearly distinguishes the two. Chronic pain is 'the background noise I live with.' Crisis pain is 'like someone lit my bones on fire' \u2014 typically builds over hours, peaks over 1-2 days, with associated fevers, swelling, and inability to move.\n- **Opioid History**: Started on hydrocodone at age 16 for crises only. Transitioned to daily oxycodone at age 19 when chronic pain became constant. Current: oxycodone 15 mg QID (60 mg daily = 90 MME). She is tolerant \u2014 current dose provides 30-40% pain relief at baseline. During crises, she requires significantly higher doses in the ED.\n- **ED Experiences**: 8 ED visits in the past year for crises. Reports: (1) Average wait time of 3-4 hours before receiving pain medication; (2) Repeated requests for 'proof' that she has sickle cell; (3) Given doses of morphine 'that would not even help a child'; (4) Observed receiving less attention than other patients in distress; (5) Overheard staff make dismissive comments; (6) On two occasions, drug-tested without her knowledge.\n- **Functional Impact**: Missed 40 days of college last year (junior, pre-med biology major). Cannot sit in lectures for >1 hour without pain escalating. Cannot exercise due to hip AVN. Uses a cane on 'bad days.' Sleep disrupted \u2014 wakes 2-3 times nightly from pain.\n- **Psychological**: PHQ-9 score 16 (moderately severe depression). Reports feeling 'hopeless about pain ever getting better.' Anxiety about ED visits \u2014 sometimes delays seeking care during crises because 'it is not worth the humiliation.'\n- **PMH**: SCD (HbSS), AVN bilateral hips, chronic hemolytic anemia (baseline Hgb 7.0-7.5), iron overload (on deferasirox), cholelithiasis (pigmented stones).\n- **Medications**: Hydroxyurea 1500 mg daily, oxycodone 15 mg QID, deferasirox 20 mg/kg daily, folic acid 1 mg daily, ibuprofen 600 mg PRN (limited use \u2014 renal concerns).\n- **Social**: College junior, pre-med biology. Lives with roommate in campus housing. Parents supportive but live 3 hours away. Single. No substance use. Does not drink alcohol.")
B.b2T=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 118/74 mmHg, HR 90 bpm, RR 14, SpO2 98% on RA, Temp 36.8C, BMI 20 (thin habitus typical of SCD).\n\n**General**: Thin young woman. Appears fatigued. Scleral icterus (chronic hemolysis). Walks with an antalgic gait favoring the right hip. Grimaces with transfers.\n\n**HEENT**: Scleral icterus bilaterally. Pale conjunctivae (anemia).\n\n**Musculoskeletal**:\n- **Hips**: Bilateral limited ROM \u2014 right hip flexion 90 degrees (pain at end range), internal rotation 10 degrees (severely limited, pain). Left hip flexion 100 degrees, internal rotation 15 degrees. Pain with logroll test bilaterally. Consistent with bilateral AVN.\n- **Lumbar Spine**: Tenderness over bilateral paraspinals. ROM mildly limited (guarded). No radiculopathy.\n- **Shoulders**: Bilateral pain with overhead activities. ROM full but painful at end range. No instability.\n- **Knees**: Mild bilateral effusions. Crepitus. ROM full.\n\n**Neurological**: Intact. No focal deficits.\n\n**Skin**: Chronic leg ulcer over right lateral malleolus (2 cm, shallow, healing). Scattered small scars from prior IV access on bilateral arms and hands.\n\n**Abdominal**: Soft. Splenomegaly not palpable (likely auto-infarcted). No hepatomegaly.")
B.aW_=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","Discuss the unique pathophysiology of chronic pain in sickle cell disease, the overlap between nociceptive, neuropathic, and central sensitization pain mechanisms, and the approach to evaluating AVN.")
B.aR7=new A.c("+ Explain the complex pain physiology in sickle cell disease and the evaluation of avascular necrosis as a contributor to chronic pain.","**Pain Pathophysiology in SCD \u2014 A Complex, Multi-Mechanism Problem**:\n\n**1. Nociceptive Pain (tissue damage)**:\n- **Avascular necrosis (AVN)**: Caused by sickling-induced microvascular occlusion of end-arteriolar blood supply to bone. The femoral head is most commonly affected (50-70% of HbSS patients by age 35). Causes progressive bone death, articular surface collapse, and secondary osteoarthritis. This patient has bilateral hip AVN \u2014 a major source of mechanical, activity-related pain.\n- **Bone marrow infarction**: Repeated microinfarctions in the medullary bone of vertebrae, pelvis, and long bones cause chronic deep bone pain.\n- **Joint effusions**: Inflammatory response to microinfarction causes synovitis.\n\n**2. Neuropathic Pain**:\n- SCD patients develop **peripheral neuropathy** from chronic ischemic nerve damage. Prevalence up to 30%.\n- **Central sensitization**: Repeated severe pain episodes (crises since childhood) cause neuroplastic changes in the dorsal horn and brain, lowering pain thresholds and amplifying pain signals. This explains why chronic pain persists even between crises and why patients describe pain as worsening over years despite stable disease.\n- **Hyperalgesia**: Studies show SCD patients have lower pain thresholds and tolerance to experimental pain stimuli, confirming central sensitization.\n\n**3. Inflammatory Pain**:\n- Chronic hemolysis releases free hemoglobin, which scavenges nitric oxide, causing endothelial dysfunction, inflammation, and pain signaling.\n- Elevated substance P, calcitonin gene-related peptide (CGRP), and other inflammatory mediators.\n- Mast cell activation \u2014 increasingly recognized in SCD pain.\n\n**AVN Evaluation**:\n- **Staging (Ficat classification)**:\n  - Stage I: Normal X-ray, abnormal MRI (bone marrow edema)\n  - Stage II: Sclerosis/cysts on X-ray, femoral head shape preserved\n  - Stage III: Subchondral collapse (crescent sign) \u2014 femoral head flattening begins\n  - Stage IV: Acetabular involvement \u2014 secondary osteoarthritis\n- **This patient**: Order AP and frog-leg lateral hip X-rays. If Stage III-IV, total hip arthroplasty (THA) consultation is appropriate. THA in SCD patients is technically challenging (hard, sclerotic bone, increased infection risk, perioperative crisis risk) but can dramatically improve pain and function.\n- **MRI of bilateral hips**: If X-rays are equivocal or to assess extent of necrosis for surgical planning.\n- **Shoulder AVN**: Emerging \u2014 order shoulder X-rays and MRI if clinical suspicion is high.\n\n**Key Concept**: This patient's chronic pain is NOT simply 'drug-seeking behavior.' It reflects a biologically complex condition with identifiable structural disease (AVN), peripheral nerve damage, central sensitization from years of repeated severe pain, and chronic inflammatory mediators. The pain is REAL, MEASURABLE, and UNDERTREATED.")
B.aON=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Develop a comprehensive chronic pain management plan that addresses her multiple pain mechanisms, optimizes her opioid regimen, and integrates non-pharmacologic strategies.")
B.aQt=new A.c("+ Outline a multimodal pain management plan for chronic SCD pain, including opioid optimization and non-opioid strategies.","**Multimodal Pain Management Plan**:\n\n**1. Opioid Optimization (NOT Elimination)**:\n- She is on 90 MME daily with 30-40% relief. This is a moderate dose and she has clear structural disease justifying opioid use.\n- **Do NOT taper her opioids** without a clear alternative for pain control. Forced opioid reduction in SCD patients increases ED utilization, undertreated crises, and suffering.\n- **Convert to long-acting opioid**: Switch from oxycodone IR 15 mg QID to extended-release oxycodone (OxyContin) 30 mg BID for stable baseline coverage, plus oxycodone IR 10-15 mg Q4-6H PRN for breakthrough. This provides more consistent plasma levels and may improve pain control.\n- **Individualized crisis plan**: Written crisis action plan \u2014 at onset of crisis, increase opioid dose by 50-100%, add acetaminophen, hydrate aggressively, and present to ED if not improving in 6-8 hours. This empowers her to manage early crises at home.\n\n**2. Non-Opioid Pharmacologic Additions**:\n- **Duloxetine 60 mg daily**: SNRI for central sensitization component and comorbid depression. Dual benefit.\n- **Gabapentin 300 mg TID** (titrate to 600 mg TID): For neuropathic component (burning, tingling pain).\n- **Acetaminophen 1000 mg TID scheduled**: Adjuvant analgesic \u2014 reduces overall opioid need.\n- **NSAIDs**: Use cautiously \u2014 renal concerns in SCD. Short courses during crises if renal function is normal.\n- **Topical agents**: Diclofenac gel to bilateral hips for localized AVN pain. Lidocaine patches to lumbar spine.\n\n**3. AVN Management**:\n- **Orthopedic referral**: If AVN is Stage III-IV, total hip arthroplasty should be discussed. Bilateral hip replacement in a 22-year-old is a significant decision but may be the single most impactful intervention for her pain.\n- **If early-stage AVN**: Protected weight-bearing (cane use), physical therapy for hip strengthening and ROM preservation, bisphosphonates (limited evidence in SCD-related AVN).\n- **Activity modification**: Low-impact exercise \u2014 swimming, stationary cycling. Avoid impact loading.\n\n**4. Non-Pharmacologic Strategies**:\n- **Physical therapy**: Aquatic therapy for hip pain (buoyancy reduces joint loading). Core strengthening. Gentle hip ROM. Should NOT be prescriptive exercise that increases pain.\n- **CBT for pain**: Cognitive behavioral therapy specifically adapted for SCD pain \u2014 address catastrophizing, develop coping strategies, manage depression and anxiety. Evidence supports CBT as adjunct in SCD pain.\n- **Mindfulness-based stress reduction (MBSR)**: Moderate evidence for chronic pain management. Available as app-based programs for accessibility.\n- **Acupuncture**: Some evidence for chronic pain; may be worth trialing.\n- **Heat therapy**: Warm compresses to painful areas. Heated blankets during crises. Avoid cold (triggers sickling).\n\n**5. SCD Disease Modification**:\n- **Optimize hydroxyurea**: Check current dose and response. Target MCV >100 fL and HbF >20% if possible. Higher HbF reduces sickling and crisis frequency.\n- **Crizanlizumab (Adakveo)**: Anti-P-selectin antibody that reduces vaso-occlusive crises. Consider if she is having >3 crises/year.\n- **L-glutamine (Endari)**: FDA-approved for SCD. Reduces crisis frequency by approximately 25%.\n- **Chronic transfusion therapy**: Consider if crises remain frequent despite hydroxyurea optimization.\n\n**6. Mental Health**:\n- Depression treatment: Duloxetine (as above \u2014 addresses pain and depression). Psychology referral for CBT.\n- Address healthcare-related trauma: Her experiences of medical racism have caused healthcare avoidance and mistrust. This needs specific attention.\n- Academic support: Connect with disability services at her university for accommodations (flexible attendance, extended test time, rest breaks).")
B.aQn=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","This patient's ER experiences reflect a well-documented pattern of racial bias in pain management for sickle cell patients. How should the healthcare system address this?")
B.aMP=new A.c("+ Describe the systems-level interventions needed to address the documented racial disparities in pain management for sickle cell disease patients.","**Systemic Racial Bias in SCD Pain Management \u2014 The Evidence**:\n\n- Multiple studies document that **Black patients receive less pain medication**, longer wait times, and more scrutiny in EDs than white patients with similar complaints.\n- SCD patients specifically face **'drug-seeking' labeling** at disproportionate rates. A landmark study (Haywood et al.) found that 69% of SCD patients reported being treated as drug-seekers in the ED.\n- **Analgesic dosing**: Emergency physicians prescribe lower opioid doses for SCD pain than clinical guidelines recommend. NHLBI guidelines recommend initiating opioids within 30 minutes of ED arrival \u2014 the average actual time is 60-90 minutes.\n- **The false belief**: Studies show that a significant percentage of medical trainees and practicing physicians endorse false beliefs about biological differences in pain perception between Black and white patients.\n\n**Systems-Level Interventions**:\n\n**1. SCD-Specific ED Protocols**:\n- Implement a **standardized SCD acute pain protocol** in every ED:\n  - Triage as **ESI Level 2** (emergent) \u2014 vaso-occlusive crises are medical emergencies.\n  - **IV opioid analgesic within 30 minutes** of arrival per NHLBI guidelines.\n  - Weight-based dosing using the patient's established effective dose (documented in the EHR or an individualized pain plan).\n  - Avoid repeated 'prove you have SCD' requests \u2014 verify via medical records, not interrogation.\n- Post the protocol visibly in the ED.\n\n**2. Individualized Pain Plans in the EHR**:\n- Every SCD patient should have an **individualized pain management plan** accessible to any ED provider in the health system.\n- The plan should include: diagnosis confirmation, baseline medications, effective crisis dose (based on prior visits), medication allergies, hydroxyurea compliance, and patient-reported preferences.\n- This eliminates the need for the patient to 'prove' their condition and advocating for appropriate doses while in severe pain.\n\n**3. Implicit Bias Training**:\n- **Mandatory annual implicit bias training** for all ED staff \u2014 physicians, nurses, triage staff.\n- Include SCD-specific scenarios that address the 'drug-seeking' myth.\n- Use evidence: present data on racial disparities in analgesic prescribing and SCD-specific undertreated pain.\n\n**4. Patient Experience Monitoring**:\n- Track SCD patient satisfaction, wait times, and pain management metrics as a quality measure.\n- Compare time-to-analgesic for SCD patients vs. other pain conditions (renal colic, fractures) \u2014 disparities will be visible.\n- Patient advisory board: Include SCD patients in hospital quality improvement committees.\n\n**5. SCD Day Hospital/Infusion Center**:\n- Establish dedicated SCD acute care units or infusion centers where patients can receive crisis management without going through the ED.\n- Staffed by providers who understand SCD pain, provide rapid IV access and opioid administration, and avoid the bias-laden ED environment.\n- This is the single most impactful systems intervention \u2014 studies show SCD day hospitals reduce ED visits, admissions, and patient distress.\n\n**6. Physician Advocacy**:\n- As the treating physiatrist, you have a responsibility to **advocate for your patient within the system**.\n- Write letters to ED medical directors describing appropriate dosing for this patient.\n- Provide the patient with a written pain plan she can present at any ED.\n- If she reports mistreatment, help her file a formal complaint. Offer to corroborate the medical basis for her pain and medication needs.")
B.aNM=new A.c(u.j,"The patient is crying and says: \"The last time I went to the ER, I was in the worst crisis of my life. I was screaming in pain. The nurse said to another nurse, loud enough for me to hear, 'She is probably just drug-seeking. They all are.' I laid there for 3 hours before they gave me anything. I am a pre-med student. I have a 3.8 GPA. I have never used drugs in my life. But because I am young and Black and I ask for pain medication, I am a drug addict to them. Tell me, doctor \u2014 how do I get treated like a human being when I am in pain?\"")
B.b_y=new A.c("+ How do you respond to a patient describing racial bias in her pain management, validate her experience, and commit to actionable advocacy?",'**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Stop. Bear witness. Do not rush past this.**\n[Put down your pen. Make eye contact. Let her see that you are fully present.]\n"I hear you. And I believe you. What happened to you in that ER was wrong. Not just because they made you wait, not just because the dose was inadequate \u2014 but because a healthcare professional reduced your humanity to a stereotype. That is not care. That is harm."\n\n**2. Name what happened without euphemism.**\n"What you described has a name. It is racial bias in pain management, and it is one of the most well-documented health disparities in American medicine. Black patients \u2014 and sickle cell patients specifically \u2014 receive less pain medication, wait longer for treatment, and are subjected to more suspicion than white patients with equivalent conditions. This is not your perception. This is measured, published, replicated fact. You are not imagining it, and you are not oversensitive."\n\n**3. Acknowledge her as a whole person.**\n"You are a 22-year-old pre-med student with a 3.8 GPA who has been living with one of the most painful conditions in medicine since the day she was born. You did not choose this disease. You did not choose to need opioids. You take your medication as prescribed, you manage your health, and you are pursuing a career in medicine \u2014 while living with pain every single day. The fact that you have to justify your pain to anyone is a failure of the system, not a failure of you."\n\n**4. Validate the rage AND the exhaustion.**\n"You asked me how to be treated like a human being when you are in pain. You should not have to have a strategy for that. The fact that you do \u2014 that every time you go to an ER, you have to think about how to present yourself so they believe you \u2014 that is an additional burden on top of your disease. And it is exhausting. And it is unfair. And you have every right to be angry."\n\n**5. Commit to specific actions \u2014 not just sympathy.**\n"Here is what I am going to do for you. Not vague promises \u2014 specific actions.\n\nFirst, I am writing you an individualized pain management plan. It will be a document you can carry or present at any ER that says: your diagnosis, your current medications, your effective crisis dose, and my contact number. Any emergency physician who reads it will know exactly how to treat you.\n\nSecond, I am going to call the ED medical director at the hospital where this happened. I am going to describe what you experienced and request that their SCD pain protocol be reviewed. I will advocate for triage standards that get you treated within 30 minutes.\n\nThird, I am going to connect you with a sickle cell advocacy organization that specifically addresses healthcare bias. You should not have to fight this alone, and there is strength in collective voice.\n\nFourth, I am going to make sure this clinic is a place where you are always believed, always respected, and always treated with the urgency your condition demands. You will never have to prove your pain to me."\n\n**6. Acknowledge that the system needs to change \u2014 not her.**\n"I want to say one more thing. The problem is not that you need to learn how to present yourself better in the ER. The problem is that the ER needs to learn how to treat you better. You should not have to be calm and articulate while your bones feel like they are on fire in order to be believed. You should not have to mention your GPA to be taken seriously. The standard of care should be the same whether you are a pre-med student or a person without any education at all. Pain is pain. And yours has been undertreated for too long."\n\n**7. End with affirmation.**\n"You said you want to go to medical school. I hope you do. Because medicine needs physicians who know what it is like to be the patient in that ER bed. You will change this system from the inside. But right now, let us make sure the system takes care of you."\n\n**Key Principles**: Believed her unconditionally. Named racial bias explicitly \u2014 did not use euphemisms or passive language. Cited evidence to validate her experience as systemic, not personal. Acknowledged her whole identity beyond her disease. Committed to specific, actionable advocacy steps. Placed the burden of change on the system, not the patient. Affirmed her future and her agency.')
B.aCV=s([B.aR1,B.aOJ,B.b2T,B.aW_,B.aR7,B.aON,B.aQt,B.aQn,B.aMP,B.aNM,B.b_y],t.E)
B.PW=new A.a5("pain-sickle-cell-chronic","Chronic Pain in Sickle Cell Disease",B.aCV,!1)
B.b_x=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 3-month-old male infant is referred to the pediatric PM&R clinic by his pediatrician for evaluation of a "limp right arm" noted since birth. Birth history is significant for vaginal delivery complicated by shoulder dystocia requiring suprapubic pressure and McRoberts maneuver. Birth weight was 4.5 kg (9 lbs 15 oz). The mother is a 34-year-old G2P2 with gestational diabetes controlled with insulin. APGAR scores were 7 at 1 minute and 9 at 5 minutes. The mother reports that the right arm hangs limply at his side and he does not reach for toys with it, though he moves his right hand and fingers well. She is distraught and wants to know if her son will recover.')
B.aV4=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","Key areas to assess in neonatal brachial plexus palsy (NBPP):\n- **Birth details**: Mode of delivery, shoulder dystocia, maneuvers used, birth weight, maternal diabetes. Shoulder dystocia is the strongest risk factor.\n- **Onset and progression**: Was the arm limp immediately at birth? Any improvement since? Spontaneous recovery in the first 2-4 weeks suggests neurapraxia (favorable prognosis).\n- **Hand and finger function**: Active finger flexion and extension suggest intact C8-T1 (lower trunk/medial cord). This helps distinguish Erb palsy (upper trunk) from total plexus palsy.\n- **Associated injuries**: Clavicle fracture (palpate for callus), humeral fracture, torticollis (from SCM injury during delivery), phrenic nerve palsy (C3-C5 \u2014 respiratory distress, elevated hemidiaphragm), Horner syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis \u2014 indicates C8-T1/stellate ganglion involvement, very poor prognosis).\n- **Feeding**: Can the infant bring hand to mouth? Asymmetric arm use during breastfeeding?\n- **Developmental milestones**: Tracking for age-appropriate milestones (social smile by 6 weeks, cooing by 2 months, head control developing).\n- **Family concerns**: Parental guilt is extremely common \u2014 address this directly.")
B.aQ8=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:",'**General**: Alert, well-nourished 3-month-old male. Weight 6.8 kg (90th percentile). No respiratory distress.\n\n**Right Upper Extremity Examination**:\n- **Resting posture**: Classic **"waiter\'s tip" position** \u2014 shoulder adducted and internally rotated, elbow extended, forearm pronated, wrist slightly flexed.\n- **Active Movement Scale (AMS)**:\n  - Shoulder abduction: 1 (contraction, no movement) = severely impaired\n  - Shoulder external rotation: 0 (no contraction) = absent\n  - Shoulder flexion: 1 (contraction, no movement)\n  - Elbow flexion: 2 (movement with gravity eliminated)\n  - Elbow extension: 5 (full movement against gravity)\n  - Forearm supination: 1 (contraction, no movement)\n  - Wrist extension: 5 (full movement)\n  - Finger flexion/extension: 7 (full movement) = intact\n\n- **Reflexes**: Moro reflex **absent on right** (asymmetric \u2014 right arm does not abduct and extend). Present and normal on left. Biceps reflex absent on right. Grasp reflex intact bilaterally (confirms intact C8-T1).\n\n**Assessment for Associated Injuries**:\n- **Clavicle**: No palpable callus or crepitus bilaterally.\n- **Horner syndrome**: Pupils equal and reactive. No ptosis. No anhidrosis noted. **Horner syndrome ABSENT** \u2014 this is a favorable prognostic sign.\n- **Respiratory**: Symmetric chest rise. No tachypnea. Phrenic nerve palsy unlikely but chest X-ray should confirm.\n- **Torticollis**: No head tilt or SCM tightness noted.\n\n**Left upper extremity**: Normal active movement throughout. Normal tone and reflexes.')
B.b2L=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The clinical picture is consistent with a right upper trunk brachial plexus injury (Erb palsy, C5-C6). You now need to determine the severity of nerve injury and establish a monitoring and decision-making plan.")
B.aW6=new A.c("+ Classify the types of brachial plexus birth injury, explain the relevant nerve root levels, and discuss your approach to prognostication including the role of imaging and electrodiagnostics.",'**Classification of Neonatal Brachial Plexus Palsy**:\n\n1. **Erb Palsy (C5-C6 \u2014 Upper Trunk)**: Most common (approximately 70-80% of cases). Affects shoulder abduction, external rotation, elbow flexion, forearm supination. The "waiter\'s tip" posture. Prognosis is generally favorable \u2014 70-90% recover spontaneously if recovery begins by 2-3 months.\n\n2. **Extended Erb Palsy (C5-C6-C7 \u2014 Upper and Middle Trunk)**: Adds wrist and finger extension weakness to the Erb pattern. More guarded prognosis.\n\n3. **Klumpke Palsy (C8-T1 \u2014 Lower Trunk)**: Rare in isolation (< 1%). Affects hand intrinsic muscles, finger flexors, wrist flexors. "Claw hand" deformity. Often associated with Horner syndrome if T1 sympathetic fibers are involved.\n\n4. **Total Plexus Palsy (C5-T1)**: Complete flail arm. Worst prognosis. Often involves avulsion injuries. May have Horner syndrome and phrenic nerve palsy.\n\n**This Patient**: Right C5-C6 (Erb palsy). Intact hand function (C8-T1 spared). No Horner syndrome. Favorable pattern.\n\n**Prognostication**:\n- **Most important prognostic indicator**: Recovery of **antigravity biceps function (elbow flexion) by 3-6 months of age**. If biceps recovers by 3 months, prognosis for good functional outcome is excellent. If no biceps recovery by 5-6 months, microsurgical exploration is indicated.\n- **Toronto Test Score**: At 3 months, assess AMS scores across multiple muscle groups. A total score helps predict who will and will not recover.\n\n**Imaging**:\n- **Chest X-ray**: Rule out phrenic nerve palsy (elevated right hemidiaphragm) and clavicle fracture.\n- **MRI neurography of the brachial plexus**: Can identify nerve root avulsion (pseudomeningocele = pathognomonic for avulsion), but requires sedation/anesthesia in an infant. Reserve for surgical planning if no recovery by 3-6 months.\n- **Ultrasound**: Emerging role for visualizing neuroma-in-continuity. Non-invasive and does not require sedation.\n\n**Electrodiagnostics (EMG/NCS)**:\n- Can be helpful at 3-4 weeks to differentiate neurapraxia (conduction preserved distally) from axonotmesis/neurotmesis (Wallerian degeneration with fibrillation potentials).\n- Presence of **motor unit recruitment in biceps and deltoid** is favorable.\n- Absence of motor units at 3 months with fibrillations = poor spontaneous recovery likely.\n- Technically challenging in infants and requires an experienced pediatric electromyographer.')
B.aUG=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You establish a treatment plan with the family and outline the timeline for monitoring recovery and potential surgical intervention.")
B.aQh=new A.c("+ Outline the comprehensive rehabilitation and medical management plan for this infant, including criteria for surgical referral.",'**Immediate Rehabilitation Program (0-3 months)**:\n\n1. **Gentle passive range of motion (PROM)**: Parents taught by a pediatric therapist to perform gentle PROM of the right shoulder (especially external rotation and abduction), elbow (flexion/extension), and forearm (supination) multiple times daily. **Critical to prevent contracture** \u2014 internal rotation contracture is the most common secondary deformity.\n\n2. **Positioning**: Encourage midline orientation. Place toys to the right side to stimulate visual tracking toward the affected arm. Avoid traction on the arm (careful with dressing, bathing, lifting under arms).\n\n3. **Sensory stimulation**: Tactile input to the affected arm (gentle massage, textured toys) to maintain cortical representation and promote neuroplasticity.\n\n4. **Parent education**: Teach safe handling techniques \u2014 **never pull the affected arm**. Demonstrate proper lifting (support under trunk, not under arms).\n\n**Monitoring Protocol (3-9 months)**:\n- **Monthly clinic visits** with Active Movement Scale (AMS) scoring to track recovery objectively.\n- **Critical decision point at 3 months**: If biceps function is absent (AMS < 3 for elbow flexion), refer to a brachial plexus microsurgeon for evaluation.\n- **Cookie test (Tassin test)**: At 3 months, observe if the infant can bring the hand to the mouth. Inability to flex the elbow against gravity is the strongest indicator for surgical intervention.\n- **If recovery is progressing**: Continue conservative management with therapy.\n\n**Surgical Intervention (if indicated at 3-6 months)**:\n- **Microsurgical nerve reconstruction**: Nerve grafting (sural nerve autograft), nerve transfer (Oberlin transfer \u2014 ulnar nerve fascicle to biceps motor nerve for elbow flexion), or neurolysis of neuroma-in-continuity.\n- Optimal surgical window is **3-9 months of age**. Earlier surgery allows reinnervation before motor endplate degeneration (which occurs by approximately 12-18 months).\n\n**Splinting**:\n- **Shoulder abduction splint**: Consider if developing internal rotation contracture. "Sully" or custom-molded orthosis to maintain the shoulder in neutral to slight external rotation and abduction.\n\n**Later Interventions (if residual deficits)**:\n- **Botulinum toxin (age 1-4 years)**: To the internal rotators (pectoralis major, subscapularis) if muscle imbalance develops, to allow external rotators to strengthen. Combine with intensive OT.\n- **Secondary orthopedic procedures (age 2-8 years)**: Tendon transfers (latissimus dorsi/teres major transfer to rotator cuff for external rotation), humeral osteotomy for fixed internal rotation contracture, subscapularis release.')
B.aSF=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The family asks about additional services available for their son and what to expect regarding developmental progress.")
B.aYw=new A.c("+ Discuss early intervention services, developmental coordination, and the multidisciplinary team approach for this infant.","**Early Intervention (EI) Services**:\n- Under the **Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) Part C**, this infant qualifies for early intervention services from birth to age 3. These are **federally mandated and provided at no cost** to the family regardless of income.\n- Referral process: The physician refers to the local EI program. An Individualized Family Service Plan (IFSP) is developed within 45 days of referral.\n- **Services through EI**: Pediatric occupational therapy (primary), physical therapy, developmental monitoring, family training and counseling.\n- Services are provided in the **natural environment** (home-based), which is ideal for parent coaching and integration into daily routines.\n\n**Multidisciplinary Team**:\n1. **Pediatric physiatrist**: Medical lead, coordinates care, monitors recovery, makes surgical referral decisions.\n2. **Pediatric occupational therapist**: Primary therapist for upper extremity rehabilitation, ROM, strengthening, functional training.\n3. **Pediatric physical therapist**: Gross motor development, balance, bilateral coordination as the child grows.\n4. **Brachial plexus microsurgeon**: Surgical consultation if no biceps recovery by 3-6 months.\n5. **Developmental pediatrician**: Monitor global developmental milestones. Children with NBPP may have subtle fine motor delays even on the unaffected side.\n6. **Pediatric orthopedist**: Later involvement if skeletal deformities develop (glenohumeral dysplasia, posterior shoulder subluxation).\n\n**Developmental Considerations**:\n- Asymmetric arm function can affect milestone achievement: rolling (typically 4-6 months), sitting with arm support (6-8 months), crawling (may use asymmetric patterns), and bilateral hand play (9-12 months).\n- Monitor for developmental handedness \u2014 early strong hand preference (before 12 months) suggests significant impairment of the affected side.\n- School-age concerns: PE participation, handwriting (if dominant arm affected), sports adaptation, psychosocial impact of visible asymmetry.")
B.aQD=new A.c(u.j,'The mother becomes tearful and says: "This is my fault, isn\'t it? I should have had a C-section. I knew he was big but my doctor said vaginal delivery was fine. Did I do this to my baby?"')
B.aQE=new A.c("+ Demonstrate how you would address this mother's guilt while providing honest information and support.",'I would respond with empathy, validation, and clear medical information:\n\n**1. Address the emotion immediately and directly**:\n"I can see how much pain you are carrying with this, and I want to help you with that right now. This is NOT your fault. I want to say that clearly \u2014 you did nothing wrong."\n\n**2. Provide medical context without blame**:\n"Brachial plexus injuries can happen during deliveries that go perfectly by the book. Even with the best obstetric care, shoulder dystocia cannot always be predicted or prevented. Large birth weight and gestational diabetes are risk factors, but they do not guarantee this outcome, and many large babies are delivered without any injury. The decision about delivery method was made by your medical team based on the information available at the time."\n\n**3. Redirect from guilt to empowerment**:\n"What I can tell you is that you are here, right now, doing exactly the right thing for your son. You noticed the problem, you brought him in for evaluation, and you are going to be the most important part of his recovery. The exercises and stimulation you will do at home every day matter more than anything we do in this clinic."\n\n**4. Provide realistic hope**:\n"The majority of babies with this type of injury \u2014 the upper trunk pattern \u2014 make significant recoveries. Your son has a very encouraging sign: his hand and fingers are working well, which means the lower nerves are intact. We are going to monitor him very closely over the next few months, and if his arm does not recover on its own within a certain timeframe, there are surgical options with excellent outcomes."\n\n**5. Normalize the grief and offer support**:\n"It is completely normal for parents to feel guilt and grief after a birth injury. If you find that these feelings are overwhelming or affecting your ability to bond with your baby, please tell me. We can connect you with a counselor who specializes in this, and there are parent support groups through the United Brachial Plexus Network where you can talk to other families who have been through the same experience."\n\n**6. Establish partnership**:\n"We are going to get through this together as a team \u2014 you, your son, our therapists, and me. I will see you every month, and you can call my office any time with questions between visits. You are already being an excellent mother by advocating for him."\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Direct absolution of guilt, medical education, parental empowerment, realistic prognostication, psychosocial support resources, continuity of care.')
B.aDW=s([B.b_x,B.aV4,B.aQ8,B.b2L,B.aW6,B.aUG,B.aQh,B.aSF,B.aYw,B.aQD,B.aQE],t.E)
B.PX=new A.a5("peds-brachial-plexus","Brachial Plexus Birth Injury (Erb Palsy)",B.aDW,!1)
B.aXk=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'An 8-year-old female with a history of repaired lumbosacral myelomeningocele (L4 level, repaired on day of life 1) and VP shunt (placed at 2 weeks of age for hydrocephalus) presents to the pediatric PM&R clinic with a 3-month history of progressive back pain and declining gait function. Her mother reports that she was previously ambulating with bilateral AFOs and Lofstrand crutches for community distances but now can only manage household distances before fatigue and pain force her to stop. She has also developed new urinary incontinence after having been dry during the day for the past 2 years with a clean intermittent catheterization (CIC) program. Her mother additionally notices that her right foot seems "more curved" than before. The child appears anxious and quieter than her usual personality.')
B.aOt=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","Key areas to assess in a child with myelomeningocele and new neurologic changes:\n- **Tethered cord symptoms**: New or worsening back pain (especially with flexion), progressive motor weakness, changes in gait pattern, new or worsening scoliosis, increasing foot deformity (cavovarus, equinus), deteriorating bladder or bowel function. Tethered cord occurs in 20-50% of children with repaired myelomeningocele.\n- **VP shunt function**: Headaches, nausea/vomiting, lethargy, vision changes, sunset eyes, personality changes \u2014 shunt malfunction can also cause lower extremity deterioration via brainstem compression.\n- **Syringomyelia**: Can coexist with or mimic tethered cord. Upper extremity weakness, scoliosis, pain, sensory changes.\n- **Chiari II malformation status**: Present in nearly all patients with myelomeningocele. Can become symptomatic with swallowing difficulty, stridor, apnea, upper extremity weakness.\n- **Bladder function baseline**: Establish the previous CIC schedule, volume per catheterization, continence status, UTI frequency. Urodynamics should be done regularly (baseline, then annual or with changes).\n- **Bowel program**: Constipation is common. Current bowel regimen (timed toileting, stimulant, Malone antegrade continence enema?).\n- **Orthopedic history**: Prior surgeries, current bracing, spinal deformity, hip stability (hip subluxation/dislocation common at L3-L4 level).\n- **Skin**: Insensate skin areas. Any pressure injuries from braces or wheelchair?\n- **Latex allergy**: All myelomeningocele patients should be treated as latex-sensitive (high prevalence due to repeated surgeries).\n- **Developmental and academic function**: Grade level, school performance, social participation.")
B.aZc=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 100/62 mmHg, HR 88 bpm, Weight 28 kg (50th percentile), Height 120 cm (10th percentile \u2014 short stature common in myelomeningocele).\n\n**Spine**: Well-healed midline lumbosacral scar from primary closure. No hairy patch, dimple, or sinus tract above the scar. Mild lumbar kyphosis. Tenderness to palpation at the lumbosacral junction. No palpable step-off.\n\n**VP Shunt**: Palpable tubing along the right lateral neck and anterior chest wall. Reservoir compresses and refills appropriately.\n\n**Lower Extremity Motor Exam** (compared to baseline from 6 months ago):\n- Right hip flexion: 4/5 (was 4+/5) \u2014 subtle decline\n- Right knee extension: 4/5 (was 4+/5)\n- Right ankle dorsiflexion: 2/5 (was 3+/5) \u2014 **significant decline**\n- Right ankle plantarflexion: 1/5 (was 2/5)\n- Left hip flexion: 4/5 (unchanged)\n- Left knee extension: 4/5 (unchanged)\n- Left ankle dorsiflexion: 3/5 (unchanged)\n- Left ankle plantarflexion: 2/5 (unchanged)\n\n**Sensation**: Decreased light touch and pin prick from L4 distally bilaterally (baseline). New decreased sensation at L3 dermatome on the right (previously intact) \u2014 **ascending sensory level is a red flag**.\n\n**Reflexes**: Absent at bilateral ankles (baseline). Right patellar reflex diminished compared to left (new finding).\n\n**Foot Examination**: Right foot with progressive **cavovarus deformity** \u2014 increased arch height and hindfoot varus, worse than previous clinic notes describe. Left foot with mild equinovarus, stable.\n\n**Gait**: Ambulates with bilateral AFOs and Lofstrand crutches with a crouched, lurching pattern. Right foot drop worse than baseline. Increased trunk sway. Endurance markedly reduced.\n\n**Skin**: No pressure injuries. Perianal sensation diminished bilaterally (baseline).")
B.b_B=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The combination of progressive right lower extremity weakness, ascending sensory level, worsening foot deformity, and deteriorating bladder function in a child with repaired myelomeningocele is highly concerning for tethered spinal cord.")
B.aMD=new A.c("+ Describe the workup for suspected tethered cord syndrome, including how to differentiate it from other causes of neurologic deterioration in myelomeningocele.","**Primary Workup \u2014 MRI of the Spine**:\n- **MRI of the entire spine with and without contrast** is the study of choice.\n- Findings suggestive of tethered cord: low-lying conus medullaris (below L2 in children over 12 weeks), thickened filum terminale (>2 mm), dorsal displacement of the cord against the posterior thecal sac at the repair site, scar tissue tethering the cord to the dura.\n- **Important caveat**: In patients with repaired myelomeningocele, the MRI will ALWAYS show an anatomically low conus and tethered appearance. The diagnosis of **symptomatic** re-tethering is therefore **clinical** \u2014 based on progressive neurologic deterioration, NOT imaging alone.\n- MRI also evaluates for **syringomyelia** (fluid-filled cavity within the spinal cord), which can cause similar progressive deficits and may coexist with tethering.\n\n**Additional Studies**:\n1. **MRI Brain**: Evaluate VP shunt function (ventricle size compared to prior imaging), Chiari II malformation status. **Shunt malfunction can mimic tethered cord** because elevated intracranial pressure can cause brainstem compression affecting lower extremity function.\n2. **CT head (rapid shunt series)**: If acute shunt malfunction is suspected, CT is faster than MRI. Compare ventricle size to baseline.\n3. **Urodynamics**: Compare to baseline. Findings suggestive of neurologic deterioration include increasing detrusor pressures, new detrusor overactivity, decreased bladder compliance, increasing post-void residuals.\n4. **Spine X-rays**: Evaluate scoliosis progression (Cobb angle measurement), hip stability, progressive foot deformity.\n\n**Differential Diagnosis of Neurologic Deterioration**:\n1. **Tethered cord** (most likely) \u2014 progressive, subacute onset, pain + motor + bladder changes\n2. **VP shunt malfunction** \u2014 can be insidious; assess for headaches, papilledema, compare ventricle size\n3. **Symptomatic syringomyelia** \u2014 can cause upper extremity weakness, scoliosis, dissociated sensory loss; seen on MRI\n4. **Symptomatic Chiari II** \u2014 brainstem signs (swallowing, stridor, apnea), upper extremity findings\n5. **Orthopedic causes** \u2014 hip subluxation, progressive scoliosis causing pain and functional decline (but would not explain bladder changes or ascending sensory level)\n6. **Normal growth-related stretching** \u2014 growth spurts can unmask subclinical tethering\n\n**The ascending sensory level and new bladder incontinence in this patient make tethered cord the leading diagnosis and require urgent neurosurgical evaluation.**")
B.aWl=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","MRI confirms re-tethering of the spinal cord at the repair site with a small associated syrinx at T12. Neurosurgery is consulted and recommends surgical untethering.")
B.aZz=new A.c("+ Outline the pre-operative preparation, post-operative rehabilitation plan, and ongoing bladder management strategy.","**Pre-Operative Preparation**:\n1. **Latex-free protocol**: Mandatory for all surgical procedures in myelomeningocele patients. Alert anesthesia, OR staff, and all providers. Use non-latex gloves, tubing, and equipment throughout.\n2. **Baseline documentation**: Record detailed motor exam, sensory levels, urodynamic findings, and functional status (ambulation distance, FIM/WeeFIM scores) for post-operative comparison.\n3. **Neurosurgical planning**: Intraoperative neurophysiologic monitoring (SSEP, MEP, EMG) is recommended during untethering to minimize risk of iatrogenic nerve injury.\n4. **Anesthesia considerations**: Evaluate for difficult airway (Chiari malformation), VP shunt positioning, cardiac evaluation if needed.\n5. **Child Life preparation**: Age-appropriate surgical preparation \u2014 use dolls, picture books, and tours of the surgical area to reduce anxiety. This 8-year-old is old enough to understand simple explanations.\n\n**Post-Operative Rehabilitation**:\n1. **Positioning**: Flat bed rest for 48-72 hours post-operatively to allow dural healing and reduce CSF leak risk. Log-roll precautions.\n2. **Pain management**: Multimodal approach (acetaminophen, ketorolac, low-dose opioid PCA or nurse-controlled analgesia). Avoid constipation aggressively (docusate, senna) as straining increases intrathecal pressure.\n3. **Wound monitoring**: Daily assessment for CSF leak (clear drainage on dressing), wound dehiscence, and infection.\n4. **Mobilization**: Gradual progression from supine to seated (day 3-5), then to upright and ambulation per neurosurgical clearance. Orthotics refitted as needed.\n5. **Motor monitoring**: Serial lower extremity exams compared to pre-operative baseline. Some patients experience transient worsening before improvement.\n6. **Outpatient therapy**: Intensive PT and OT for 8-12 weeks post-operatively to regain functional mobility. Goal: return to pre-decline ambulatory status (community ambulation with AFOs and crutches).\n\n**Bladder Management**:\n1. **Continue CIC program**: Typically Q4-6 hour schedule. Adjust catheterization frequency based on post-operative urodynamics.\n2. **Post-operative urodynamics**: Repeat at 3-6 months after surgery to assess for improvement in bladder compliance and capacity.\n3. **Anticholinergic medication**: If detrusor overactivity persists, continue or initiate **oxybutynin** (0.2 mg/kg/dose BID-TID) or **solifenacin** (dose adjusted for weight).\n4. **Renal protection**: Maintain detrusor leak point pressure below 40 cmH2O to protect the upper urinary tracts. Annual renal ultrasound.\n5. **UTI prevention**: Adequate fluid intake, complete bladder emptying with CIC, cranberry prophylaxis (limited evidence). Antibiotic prophylaxis only if recurrent febrile UTIs.\n6. **Bowel program**: Continue timed toileting or bowel program. Consider antegrade continence enema (Malone/MACE procedure) if bowel incontinence is significantly impacting quality of life.")
B.b2v=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The child's mother asks about school accommodations. Her daughter has been missing school and falling behind due to medical appointments, fatigue, and the upcoming surgery.")
B.b0_=new A.c("+ Discuss school accommodations, IEP/504 planning, and adaptive PE for a child with myelomeningocele. When should wheelchair assessment be considered?",'**School Accommodations (IDEA and Section 504)**:\n\n1. **Individualized Education Program (IEP)** under IDEA: If the child qualifies for special education services based on a disability that adversely affects educational performance. Myelomeningocele qualifies under "Orthopedic Impairment" or "Other Health Impairment."\n- IEP provides: specialized instruction, related services (school-based OT, PT, nursing), accommodations, and modifications.\n- This child likely needs: school nurse for CIC during school hours, accessible classroom (first floor or elevator), extended time for transitions, modified PE, rest breaks for fatigue.\n\n2. **504 Plan**: If the child does not qualify for special education but needs accommodations to access education equally. Many children with myelomeningocele are cognitively intact and perform at grade level but need accommodations.\n- Examples: preferential seating, extra time between classes, bathroom access without restrictions, assistive technology for writing if hand function is affected.\n\n**Adaptive Physical Education**:\n- The child should NOT be excused from PE entirely \u2014 physical activity is critical for cardiovascular health, weight management (obesity is common in myelomeningocele), and psychosocial development.\n- Adaptive PE modifications: wheelchair sports (basketball, tennis), swimming (excellent for this population), handcycling, seated activities. Avoid contact sports with high fall risk.\n- The school must provide an adapted PE program per the IEP if the child cannot safely participate in standard PE.\n\n**Wheelchair Assessment**:\n- This child is currently a community ambulator with AFOs and crutches. However, with her declining function, she may need a **wheelchair for distance mobility** while maintaining ambulatory function for short distances and therapeutic benefit.\n- **Part-time wheelchair use does NOT mean abandoning ambulation**. Frame it as a "mobility tool" that increases independence, reduces energy expenditure, and enables participation in activities that would otherwise be impossible.\n- Assessment by a seating and mobility specialist (ATP \u2014 Assistive Technology Professional) is indicated. Consider a lightweight manual wheelchair with proper seating and pressure-relieving cushion (ROHO or Jay).\n- If she recovers after untethering surgery, the wheelchair may become backup only.\n\n**Additional School Needs**:\n- Skin protection: Insensate skin requires pressure relief. Wheelchair cushion, repositioning schedule, ability to do weight shifts during class.\n- Psychosocial support: School counselor involvement, peer education about disability (with family permission), anti-bullying awareness.\n- Emergency plan: Written health plan for seizures (if applicable), shunt malfunction signs, allergic reaction protocol (latex allergy \u2014 ensure school is latex-free in her environment).')
B.b0u=new A.c(u.j,"Before the surgery, the child herself says to you: \"I'm scared about the surgery. Will it hurt? What if I can't walk anymore after?\"")
B.aUx=new A.c("+ Demonstrate age-appropriate counseling for this 8-year-old patient about her upcoming surgery.",'I would kneel or sit at her eye level and speak directly to her using simple, honest, age-appropriate language:\n\n**1. Validate her fear**:\n"It is totally okay to feel scared. That is a very brave thing to tell me, and I am glad you did. A lot of kids \u2014 and even grown-ups \u2014 feel scared before surgery. That is completely normal."\n\n**2. Explain the surgery simply**:\n"Do you know how sometimes when your hair gets tangled and you have to gently untangle it so it does not pull? That is kind of what is happening inside your back. There is something called your spinal cord \u2014 it is like a superhighway that sends messages from your brain to your legs. Right now, it is stuck to some scar tissue and getting pulled, which is making your legs weaker and your back hurt. The surgeon is going to very carefully unstick it so it can move freely again."\n\n**3. Address pain honestly**:\n"You will be asleep the whole time during the surgery, so you will not feel anything during it. When you wake up, your back will be sore \u2014 kind of like when you bump yourself, but we will have special medicine to make sure the pain stays small. The nurses will ask you how you are feeling using a faces chart, and if you say it hurts, they will give you more medicine right away. You are in charge of telling us."\n\n**4. Address the walking fear directly and honestly**:\n"I understand you are worried about walking. The whole reason we are doing this surgery is to help your legs get stronger again, not weaker. The surgeon does this operation a lot and is very good at it. Most kids who have this surgery feel better afterward, and our goal is to get you back to walking the way you were before your back started hurting."\n\n**5. Give her some control**:\n"Is there anything special that would make you feel better? You can bring your favorite stuffed animal or blanket to the hospital. And your mom will be right there when you wake up. Would you like to meet the surgeon before the day of surgery so you can ask them questions too?"\n\n**6. End with encouragement**:\n"You know what I think? I think you are really tough. You have been dealing with your back hurting and your legs being tired, and you still come to your appointments and work hard in therapy. That takes a lot of strength. I am going to be here for you the whole way through this."\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Age-appropriate language, use of analogies, honest but non-frightening explanation, validation of emotions, giving the child a sense of control, parental reassurance, developmental appropriateness for an 8-year-old\'s cognitive level.')
B.aD_=s([B.aXk,B.aOt,B.aZc,B.b_B,B.aMD,B.aWl,B.aZz,B.b2v,B.b0_,B.b0u,B.aUx],t.E)
B.Qp=new A.a5("peds-tethered-cord","Tethered Cord Syndrome",B.aD_,!1)
B.aS7=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 12-year-old female is referred to the pediatric PM&R clinic by her pediatrician for evaluation of bilateral knee swelling and pain persisting for 6 months. She reports morning stiffness lasting 60-90 minutes that improves with activity throughout the day. The knee swelling began insidiously in the left knee and then involved the right knee 2 months later. She has also noted intermittent pain in both wrists. She was previously an active soccer player but has stopped participating due to pain and stiffness. She is in 7th grade with good academic performance but has been missing gym class. There is no history of preceding illness, tick bite, trauma, or rash. Family history is notable for a maternal aunt with rheumatoid arthritis.")
B.b0j=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","Key areas to assess in suspected juvenile idiopathic arthritis:\n- **Joint symptoms**: Number and pattern of involved joints, duration of symptoms (must be > 6 weeks for JIA diagnosis), morning stiffness duration (>30 minutes suggests inflammatory arthritis), gelling phenomenon (stiffness after prolonged sitting).\n- **Systemic features**: Fever pattern (quotidian spiking fevers suggest systemic JIA), rash (evanescent salmon-colored rash in systemic JIA), lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, serositis.\n- **Ocular symptoms**: Chronic anterior uveitis is a major complication of JIA (especially oligoarticular and ANA-positive). Often asymptomatic initially \u2014 ask about eye redness, pain, photophobia, visual changes. **Routine slit-lamp screening is mandatory**.\n- **Growth and development**: Assess height velocity \u2014 chronic inflammation and corticosteroid use can impair linear growth. Localized overgrowth (leg length discrepancy from asymmetric knee involvement) can also occur.\n- **Functional impact**: School attendance, physical activity, peer relationships, ADL difficulties (writing, dressing, stairs). Use the Childhood Health Assessment Questionnaire (CHAQ).\n- **Psychosocial**: Screen for depression, anxiety, and social isolation. Chronic illness in adolescents significantly impacts self-esteem and peer relationships.\n- **Red flags for malignancy**: Bone pain out of proportion, night sweats, weight loss, severe fatigue, unexplained cytopenias \u2014 must rule out leukemia before diagnosing JIA.")
B.b1W=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 105/68 mmHg, HR 78 bpm, Temp 37.0C, Weight 45 kg (50th percentile), Height 152 cm (50th percentile). Tanner stage III.\n\n**Musculoskeletal Examination**:\n- **Bilateral knees**: Moderate warm effusions bilaterally, left greater than right. Positive bulge sign on left. Positive ballottement test bilaterally. Flexion limited to 120 degrees bilaterally (normal 140). Extension lacks 5 degrees on left (early flexion contracture). No ligamentous laxity.\n- **Bilateral wrists**: Mild dorsal swelling. Slightly decreased extension. Grip strength mildly reduced bilaterally.\n- **Other joints**: No swelling or tenderness in ankles, hips, shoulders, elbows, or small joints of hands and feet. TMJ: no tenderness, no decreased opening, no deviation.\n- **Spine**: Full ROM. No tenderness.\n\n**Growth Assessment**: Leg lengths equal. No limb length discrepancy at this time. Tanner staging appropriate for age.\n\n**Gait**: Antalgic gait with reduced stance phase on left. Decreased knee flexion during swing phase bilaterally. No Trendelenburg sign.\n\n**Eyes**: External exam normal. No conjunctival injection. (Slit-lamp exam by ophthalmology pending.)\n\n**Skin**: No rashes, psoriatic plaques, nail pitting, or nodules. No enthesitis (tenderness at Achilles insertion, plantar fascia, or iliac crest).\n\n**Lymph Nodes**: No significant lymphadenopathy. No hepatosplenomegaly.")
B.aRh=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","This 12-year-old has a 6-month history of chronic, symmetric polyarticular joint involvement with prolonged morning stiffness and no systemic features, making polyarticular juvenile idiopathic arthritis the leading diagnosis.")
B.aR_=new A.c("+ Classify the subtypes of JIA, identify the most likely subtype in this patient, and outline the differential diagnosis with appropriate laboratory workup.","**ILAR Classification of JIA Subtypes** (International League of Associations for Rheumatology):\n\n1. **Oligoarticular JIA** (most common, approximately 50%): 1-4 joints in the first 6 months. Two subtypes: persistent (remains <=4 joints) and extended (spreads to >4 joints after 6 months). Peak age 2-4 years, female predominant, highest ANA positivity, highest risk for uveitis.\n\n2. **Polyarticular JIA, RF-negative** (approximately 20%): >=5 joints in first 6 months, RF negative. Bimodal age distribution (2-4 years and 6-12 years). Symmetric involvement common.\n\n3. **Polyarticular JIA, RF-positive** (approximately 5%): >=5 joints, RF positive. Essentially childhood-onset RA. Older girls (late childhood/adolescence). More aggressive course, erosive disease.\n\n4. **Systemic JIA (Still disease)** (approximately 10%): Quotidian (daily) spiking fevers plus arthritis. Evanescent salmon-colored rash, lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, serositis. Equal sex distribution. Risk of macrophage activation syndrome (MAS).\n\n5. **Enthesitis-related arthritis** (approximately 10%): Arthritis plus enthesitis. Older boys. HLA-B27 associated. May progress to ankylosing spondylitis. Acute (not chronic) uveitis.\n\n6. **Psoriatic arthritis** (approximately 5%): Arthritis plus psoriasis or dactylitis/nail pitting plus family history.\n\n7. **Undifferentiated arthritis**: Does not fit one category or fits more than one.\n\n**This Patient**: Most likely **Polyarticular JIA, RF-negative or RF-positive** \u2014 she has >=5 joints involved (bilateral knees + bilateral wrists = 4 large joints, meeting polyarticular threshold if one more joint qualifies), 6-month duration, symmetric, adolescent female, family history of RA.\n\n**Differential Diagnosis**:\n1. **Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)**: Polyarthritis in an adolescent female. Check ANA, anti-dsDNA, complement levels (C3, C4), CBC, urinalysis for proteinuria.\n2. **Lyme disease**: Oligoarticular (especially knee), but can be polyarticular. Geographic exposure? Check Lyme serology (ELISA, confirm with Western blot).\n3. **Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL)**: Must exclude in any child with joint pain. Night pain, bone pain, limp, cytopenias. Check CBC with differential, LDH, uric acid, peripheral smear.\n4. **Reactive arthritis**: Post-infectious (GI or GU). Usually oligoarticular, asymmetric, lower extremity predominant.\n5. **Inflammatory bowel disease-associated arthritis**: GI symptoms? Growth failure? Check inflammatory markers, albumin.\n\n**Laboratory Workup**:\n- CBC with differential (rule out leukemia, assess for anemia of chronic disease)\n- ESR and CRP (elevated in active inflammatory arthritis)\n- Comprehensive metabolic panel (baseline before starting medications)\n- ANA (positive in 40-50% of polyarticular JIA; helps stratify uveitis risk)\n- Rheumatoid Factor (RF) and anti-CCP antibodies (subtype classification, prognosis)\n- HLA-B27 (if enthesitis-related arthritis suspected)\n- Lyme serology (if endemic area)\n- LDH, uric acid, peripheral smear (if leukemia concern)\n- Urinalysis (screen for SLE nephritis)\n- Ophthalmology referral for slit-lamp exam (screen for uveitis \u2014 can be asymptomatic)")
B.aNW=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Labs return: ESR 42, CRP 2.8, ANA 1:160 positive, RF negative, anti-CCP negative, CBC normal, Lyme negative. The diagnosis is confirmed as polyarticular JIA, RF-negative. Ophthalmology slit-lamp exam shows no uveitis.")
B.aZW=new A.c("+ Design a comprehensive rehabilitation and medical management plan including exercise prescription, joint protection, and coordination with rheumatology for DMARD and biologic therapy.","**Rehabilitation Program**:\n\n1. **Exercise Prescription** (critical and evidence-based in JIA):\n- **Aerobic exercise**: 30-45 minutes of moderate-intensity activity at least 5 days/week. Low-impact preferred: swimming (excellent \u2014 buoyancy reduces joint stress, warm water reduces stiffness), cycling, walking. Start at tolerated intensity and gradually increase.\n- **Strengthening**: Focus on quadriceps (protect the knee), hip stabilizers, and grip strength. Open-chain exercises initially if joints are inflamed (leg extensions, straight leg raises), progressing to closed-chain (squats, lunges) as inflammation is controlled.\n- **Flexibility**: Daily stretching, especially for hamstrings and quadriceps to address the developing knee flexion contracture. Prone lying for 20 minutes daily to stretch knee into extension.\n- **Key message**: Exercise does NOT damage joints in JIA. Children should be as active as possible. Deconditioning and muscle atrophy from inactivity worsen outcomes.\n\n2. **Joint Protection Strategies**:\n- Activity pacing: alternate periods of activity and rest during flares.\n- Wrist splints (resting splints at night if wrist involvement is symptomatic) to maintain alignment and reduce morning stiffness.\n- Proper backpack use (<=10% body weight, both straps).\n- Ergonomic school setup: supportive chair, appropriate desk height, keyboard/mouse positioning.\n\n3. **Physical Therapy**: Referral for 2-3x/week supervised therapy initially, then transition to a home exercise program. Focus on knee ROM restoration (address flexion contracture), quadriceps strengthening, gait normalization.\n\n4. **Occupational Therapy**: Assess hand function, adaptive devices if needed (built-up pen grips for writing), energy conservation education.\n\n**Pharmacologic Management** (coordinated with Pediatric Rheumatology):\n\n1. **NSAIDs** (first-line, symptom control): Naproxen 10-15 mg/kg/day divided BID (maximum 1000 mg/day). Common first NSAID for JIA due to BID dosing and good safety profile. Allow 4-6 weeks for full effect.\n\n2. **Intra-articular corticosteroid injection**: Triamcinolone hexacetonide (preferred in JIA \u2014 longer duration of action than acetonide) into the most symptomatic knee(s). Can provide months of relief and is a bridge while DMARDs take effect. May require sedation in a 12-year-old.\n\n3. **DMARDs** (disease-modifying, if insufficient response to NSAIDs within 2-3 months):\n- **Methotrexate**: First-line DMARD for polyarticular JIA. 10-15 mg/m2/week (subcutaneous preferred for better bioavailability). Requires folic acid supplementation, baseline and periodic monitoring of CBC, liver enzymes, and renal function.\n\n4. **Biologic therapy** (if inadequate response to methotrexate after 3-6 months):\n- **TNF inhibitors**: Etanercept (0.8 mg/kg/week SQ, max 50 mg) or adalimumab (24 mg/m2 every 2 weeks SQ). Well-studied in pediatric JIA with excellent efficacy.\n- **IL-6 inhibitor**: Tocilizumab (IV or SQ) \u2014 alternative if TNF inhibitor fails.\n- Requires screening for tuberculosis (PPD or QuantiFERON Gold) and hepatitis B before starting biologics.\n\n5. **Uveitis screening schedule**: ANA-positive polyarticular JIA requires slit-lamp exam every 3-6 months. Frequency depends on age, ANA status, and disease duration.")
B.b_7=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient is frustrated about missing gym class and soccer. Her mother asks about school PE modifications and what sports are safe. They also want to know about planning for when she eventually transitions to adult rheumatology care.")
B.b1v=new A.c("+ Discuss school PE modifications, safe sports participation, and transition planning from pediatric to adult rheumatology.",'**School PE Modifications**:\n- The child should have a **504 plan** documenting her medical condition and PE modifications. She should NOT be excused from PE entirely \u2014 physical activity is therapeutic.\n- Modifications: excused from timed runs or high-impact activities during flares, allowed warm-up time for morning stiffness, permitted to self-pace activities, option to swim instead of run, ice packs available after class.\n- Communicate directly with the PE teacher (with parental consent) about JIA and activity guidelines.\n- Provide a written note specifying: "May participate in modified PE. Avoid high-impact repetitive loading during active flares. Allow self-pacing and rest breaks as needed."\n\n**Safe Sports Participation**:\n- **Encouraged**: Swimming (best overall exercise for JIA), cycling, yoga, walking, elliptical, dance, martial arts (non-contact), golf.\n- **With caution/modification**: Soccer (her sport of choice) \u2014 can participate when disease is well-controlled on medication. Use appropriate bracing if needed. Monitor for flares after games. High-level competitive play may need to be modified during active disease.\n- **Generally avoid during active disease**: Sports with high joint impact (gymnastics tumbling, distance running on hard surfaces) or high collision risk (football, rugby, wrestling).\n- **Key message for this adolescent**: The goal of treatment is to allow her to return to the activities she loves. With modern JIA therapy (biologics), the majority of children achieve inactive disease or remission and can participate in sports.\n\n**Transition Planning (Pediatric to Adult Rheumatology)**:\nTransition planning should begin by age 12-14 and complete by 18-21:\n\n1. **Self-management education** (start now at age 12):\n- Teach the patient about her diagnosis, medications, and side effects.\n- Gradually shift responsibility from parent to patient for scheduling appointments, refilling medications, and recognizing flares.\n- Use the **Got Transition** program readiness assessments annually.\n\n2. **Healthcare literacy** (ages 14-16):\n- Introduce concepts of insurance, medication coverage, prior authorizations.\n- Teach her to communicate directly with her medical team.\n- Create a portable medical summary she can carry.\n\n3. **Transfer of care** (ages 16-21):\n- Identify an adult rheumatologist and schedule an overlap visit (joint appointment with pediatric and adult providers).\n- Transfer medical records, medication history, and treatment response.\n- **Critical risk period**: The transition gap between pediatric and adult care is a known period of disease flare and medication non-adherence. Proactive planning reduces this risk.\n\n4. **Reproductive health** (by age 14-16):\n- Methotrexate is teratogenic. Discuss contraception with adolescent females on methotrexate.\n- Pre-conception planning should include stopping methotrexate 3 months before conception.\n- Biologics have variable safety in pregnancy (discuss when appropriate).')
B.b_w=new A.c(u.j,"After the discussion about diagnosis and treatment, the patient says: \"I just want to be normal. My friends all play sports and I'm stuck on the sidelines. I don't even want to tell anyone I have arthritis because they'll think I'm old or weird. Why can't I just be like everyone else?\"")
B.aVU=new A.c("+ Demonstrate how you would counsel this adolescent about living with a chronic illness while supporting her identity and peer relationships.",'I would speak directly to the patient (not through her mother), maintaining eye contact and treating her as the primary participant in the conversation:\n\n**1. Validate her frustration without minimizing it**:\n"I hear you, and I am not going to tell you that this is easy or that you should just \'be positive\' about it. You are 12 years old, and you want to play soccer and hang out with your friends and not think about medications and doctor appointments. That is completely fair, and it is okay to be upset about it."\n\n**2. Normalize her experience**:\n"You are not alone in feeling this way. I have many patients your age with JIA who have told me almost exactly the same thing. It is frustrating when your body does not cooperate with the life you want to live. And it is normal to not want to feel different from your friends."\n\n**3. Reframe the narrative \u2014 she is more than her diagnosis**:\n"Arthritis is something you have, but it is not who you are. You are a soccer player, a student, a friend, a daughter \u2014 and you happen to also have a condition that we are going to manage together. The good news is that the treatments we have today are really effective. Our goal is not for you to sit on the sidelines. Our goal is to get you back on the field."\n\n**4. Address the disclosure concern pragmatically**:\n"You get to decide who knows about your diagnosis \u2014 that is completely your choice, and there is no right or wrong answer. Some kids find it helpful to tell their close friends because then their friends understand when they are having a bad day. Other kids prefer to keep it private. What feels right to you is what matters."\n\n**5. Provide peer connection**:\n"Would you be interested in connecting with other kids your age who have JIA? There are programs like the Arthritis Foundation\'s camps and online communities where teenagers with arthritis support each other. A lot of kids tell me it is the first time they felt truly understood."\n\n**6. Set a concrete, motivating goal**:\n"Here is what I want to work toward with you: getting you back to playing soccer. Let us set that as our goal, and I will work with your rheumatologist and your physical therapist to make it happen. It might take a few months to get the inflammation fully controlled, but I believe we can get there. Will you work with me on that?"\n\n**7. Invite her to be an active partner in her care**:\n"From now on, I want you to be part of the decisions about your treatment. If something is not working for you \u2014 if a medication makes you feel bad or a therapy exercise is not realistic \u2014 tell me. This is your body and your life, and your voice matters in this process."\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Adolescent-centered communication, validation without toxic positivity, autonomy and agency, peer support resources, goal-setting, shared decision-making appropriate to developmental stage.')
B.aFH=s([B.aS7,B.b0j,B.b1W,B.aRh,B.aR_,B.aNW,B.aZW,B.b_7,B.b1v,B.b_w,B.aVU],t.E)
B.R0=new A.a5("peds-jia","Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis",B.aFH,!1)
B.aVl=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 5-year-old boy is referred to the pediatric PM&R spasticity clinic for progressive difficulty walking and increasing toe-walking. He was born at 28 weeks gestation weighing 1,100 grams, with a NICU course complicated by Grade II bilateral intraventricular hemorrhage and periventricular leukomalacia seen on head ultrasound. He was diagnosed with spastic diplegic cerebral palsy at 18 months when he failed to achieve independent sitting. He achieved independent ambulation at age 3.5 years using a posterior walker. He currently walks with bilateral hinged AFOs and a posterior walker at home but uses a manual wheelchair for community distances. His parents report worsening toe-walking over the past 6 months with increasing difficulty getting his heels to the ground, frequent tripping, and new scissoring pattern. He receives school-based PT twice weekly and private outpatient PT once weekly. His last botulinum toxin injections to bilateral gastrocnemius muscles were 8 months ago with diminishing benefit compared to prior injections. He is cognitively age-appropriate and attends a regular kindergarten classroom. His father is a physical therapist at another facility and has been researching selective dorsal rhizotomy (SDR). His mother is a stay-at-home parent who is anxious about surgical intervention. The parents are in significant conflict about the treatment approach.")
B.aSk=new A.c("History & Systems Review:",'**Cerebral Palsy Classification**:\n- **Type**: Spastic diplegia (bilateral spastic CP predominantly affecting lower extremities)\n- **GMFCS Level**: Currently Level III (walks with hand-held mobility device indoors, uses wheeled mobility outdoors and in the community). Important to note: GMFCS level can appear to worsen during growth spurts even without true neurological deterioration.\n- **MACS (Manual Ability Classification System)**: Level I (handles objects easily and successfully)\n- **CFCS (Communication Function Classification System)**: Level I (effective sender and receiver with familiar and unfamiliar partners)\n\n**Spasticity Assessment**:\n- **Modified Ashworth Scale (MAS)**: Quantify tone at each joint (hip adductors, hamstrings, gastrocnemius/soleus, tibialis posterior)\n- **Modified Tardieu Scale**: More specific for spasticity \u2014 measures the angle of catch (R1) vs. passive ROM (R2). The difference (R2-R1) indicates the dynamic component amenable to spasticity treatment. If R1 is close to R2, the contracture is fixed.\n- **Key distinction**: Spasticity (velocity-dependent increase in tone) vs. dystonia (sustained or intermittent involuntary muscle contractions). Both can coexist in CP. Dystonia is not effectively treated with SDR or botulinum toxin targeting spasticity.\n\n**Growth and Development Concerns**:\n- Children with CP often experience functional decline during growth spurts because bone grows faster than spastic muscles, worsening contractures (the "birthday syndrome")\n- This patient\'s worsening equinus at age 5 is consistent with a growth-related decline\n- Puberty will bring another major period of risk\n\n**Current Treatment Review**:\n- Botulinum toxin injections with diminishing returns suggest either antibody formation, progressive contracture (fixed vs. dynamic), or dosing/targeting issues\n- PT 3x/week is appropriate but may need goal reassessment\n- AFO design should be reviewed \u2014 is the current AFO accommodating his equinus or correcting it?\n\n**Gait Analysis**:\n- Instrumented gait analysis (3D motion capture, EMG, force plates) is the gold standard for surgical decision-making in ambulatory CP\n- Should be performed before any orthopedic or neurosurgical intervention\n- Identifies specific gait deviations, muscle firing patterns, and which muscles are contributing to dysfunction\n\n**Comorbidity Screening**:\n- Hip surveillance: AP pelvis radiograph annually until skeletal maturity. Migration percentage >33% requires close monitoring. GMFCS III patients have 40% risk of hip displacement.\n- Spine: Monitor for scoliosis\n- Vision and hearing: Periventricular leukomalacia associated with cortical visual impairment\n- Nutritional status: Growth charts for CP (Brooks 2011)\n- Bone health: Increased fracture risk from decreased weight-bearing, consider DXA if prior fracture')
B.aQd=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: Weight 18 kg (25th percentile for CP), Height 107 cm (10th percentile). Well-appearing, cooperative, interactive boy.\n\n**Neurologic \u2014 Tone Assessment**:\n- **Hip adductors**: MAS 2 bilaterally, Modified Tardieu R1 = 20 degrees abduction (catch), R2 = 45 degrees (full passive ROM). Dynamic component present (R2-R1 = 25 degrees).\n- **Hamstrings**: MAS 1+ bilaterally, popliteal angle 45 degrees bilaterally (normal >60). Fixed component present.\n- **Gastrocnemius**: MAS 3 bilaterally, Modified Tardieu R1 = -10 degrees dorsiflexion with knee extended (catch at 10 degrees plantar flexion), R2 = 5 degrees dorsiflexion. Significant dynamic component but limited passive range.\n- **Soleus**: With knee flexed, dorsiflexion to 10 degrees passively. This indicates the gastrocnemius is the primary contributor to equinus (two-joint muscle tighter than single-joint soleus).\n- **Tibialis posterior**: Mild spasticity contributing to varus in swing phase\n- **No dystonia detected**: Tone is clearly velocity-dependent, increases with speed, and has a catch. No sustained posturing at rest.\n\n**Musculoskeletal**:\n- Bilateral dynamic equinus contractures (correctable to 5 degrees DF with knee flexed but not beyond -10 degrees with knee extended)\n- Mild bilateral hamstring tightness\n- Hip flexion contractures: Thomas test positive at 10 degrees bilaterally\n- Femoral anteversion: Internal rotation 60 degrees bilaterally (increased), external rotation 20 degrees (decreased) \u2014 lever arm dysfunction\n- No hip click or instability. Galeazzi sign negative.\n\n**Gait Observation**:\n- Bilateral equinus gait with forefoot initial contact\n- Scissoring (hip adductor spasticity) during swing phase\n- Crouched posture at the knees in midstance\n- Increased lumbar lordosis (hip flexor tightness compensation)\n- Requires bilateral handhold support, unable to walk without posterior walker\n- Falls 3-4 times daily per parent report\n\n**Functional Assessment**:\n- Gross Motor Function Measure (GMFM-66): 58% (consistent with GMFCS III)\n- Transfers: Sit-to-stand with pull-to-stand on furniture. Floor-to-stand requires furniture assist.\n- Stairs: Ascends and descends with one rail and one hand held, step-to pattern\n- Self-care: Independent in feeding, upper body dressing. Requires help with lower body dressing, toileting assistance for balance.\n\n**Imaging**: AP pelvis radiograph: bilateral Reimer migration percentage 22% (within normal limits, <33%). No subluxation.")
B.aOF=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The father asks specifically about selective dorsal rhizotomy (SDR) and states he has reviewed the literature showing excellent outcomes. He wants a referral to the neurosurgery team.")
B.aMN=new A.c("+ Discuss SDR candidacy criteria, compare it to other spasticity management options, and interpret gait lab data in decision-making.","**Selective Dorsal Rhizotomy (SDR) \u2014 Candidacy Criteria**:\nSDR involves selective sectioning of dorsal (sensory) nerve rootlets at L1-S1 that contribute most to spasticity, based on intraoperative EMG response to stimulation.\n\n**Ideal SDR Candidate**:\n- Spastic diplegia (bilateral spastic CP predominantly in lower extremities)\n- GMFCS Level II or III (ambulatory with or without device)\n- Age 4-8 years (before fixed contractures develop)\n- Predominantly spasticity (not dystonia or ataxia)\n- Good underlying strength (can generate voluntary movement when spasticity is temporarily reduced, e.g., after botulinum toxin)\n- Adequate selective motor control\n- Good cognitive function and ability to participate in intensive post-operative rehabilitation\n- Supportive family who can commit to 6-12 months of intensive post-operative therapy\n- No significant fixed contractures (contractures will not be corrected by SDR and may require orthopedic surgery)\n- Gait lab data showing spasticity as the primary driver of gait abnormality\n\n**This Patient's SDR Candidacy**:\n- Favorable: Spastic diplegia, GMFCS III, age 5, pure spasticity (no dystonia), good cognition, motivated family\n- Concerns: Developing fixed contracture at the ankle (R2 only 5 degrees DF with knee extended), hamstring tightness (may need concurrent or staged orthopedic surgery), GMFCS III (most data for SDR is in GMFCS II; outcomes in level III are more variable)\n- **Gait lab is essential before proceeding** \u2014 must confirm spasticity is the primary gait deviation driver and assess whether the crouch and equinus are primarily dynamic (spasticity) or structural (contracture/bony)\n\n**Comparison of Spasticity Management Options**:\n\n1. **Botulinum Toxin (BoNT-A)**:\n   - Focal, reversible, outpatient procedure\n   - Duration: 3-6 months per cycle\n   - Best for focal spasticity in 1-4 muscle groups\n   - This patient has diminishing benefit \u2014 possible reasons: antibody formation (consider switching from onabotulinumtoxinA to abobotulinumtoxinA), progressive contracture making spasticity treatment alone insufficient, or dosing issues\n   - Maximum dose: 16-20 units/kg body weight per session (depending on formulation)\n\n2. **Serial Casting**:\n   - Short-stretch fiberglass casts applied progressively to improve ROM\n   - Often combined with botulinum toxin (inject, then cast 1-2 weeks later)\n   - Can gain 10-15 degrees of dorsiflexion over 4-6 weeks of casting\n   - Good option to maximize ROM before considering SDR\n\n3. **Intrathecal Baclofen (ITB)**:\n   - Delivers baclofen directly to the spinal cord via implanted pump\n   - Best for diffuse bilateral spasticity in GMFCS IV-V\n   - Generally not first-line for ambulatory patients (risk of excessive weakness)\n   - Requires surgical implantation and refills every 3-6 months\n   - Complication risks: catheter malfunction, infection, withdrawal syndrome (medical emergency)\n\n4. **Oral Medications**:\n   - Baclofen, diazepam, tizanidine, dantrolene\n   - Systemic side effects (sedation, cognitive dulling) limit utility in children\n   - Generally adjunctive, not primary treatment in ambulatory CP\n\n5. **SDR**:\n   - Permanent, irreversible spasticity reduction\n   - Does NOT address contractures, bony deformities, or weakness\n   - Requires 6-12 months of intensive PT post-operatively (3-5 sessions per week)\n   - Risk of new weakness, sensory changes, bladder dysfunction (transient in most), spinal complications\n   - Long-term outcomes: 20-year follow-up studies show sustained spasticity reduction, but many patients still require orthopedic surgery later\n\n6. **Orthopedic Surgery (Single-Event Multilevel Surgery \u2014 SEMLS)**:\n   - Addresses fixed contractures and bony deformities in one surgical session\n   - May include: gastrocnemius recession or lengthening, hamstring lengthening, femoral derotation osteotomy, adductor release\n   - Should be guided by gait lab data\n   - Can be done instead of or in addition to SDR\n\n**Recommended Approach for This Patient**:\n1. Obtain instrumented gait analysis\n2. Trial of BoNT-A with serial casting to bilateral gastrocnemius (combined approach to maximize ROM)\n3. Based on gait lab results, discuss SDR vs. SEMLS vs. combined approach with the neurosurgery and orthopedic teams\n4. The gait lab data will determine whether spasticity alone (SDR) or spasticity plus contracture/bony problems (SEMLS +/- SDR) is the best strategy")
B.aPl=new A.c("+ How do you differentiate spasticity from dystonia in this patient, and why does it matter for treatment selection?",'**Spasticity vs. Dystonia \u2014 Clinical Differentiation**:\n\n**Spasticity**:\n- Velocity-dependent increase in tonic stretch reflexes\n- Has a "catch" at a specific angle (Modified Tardieu R1)\n- Increases with faster passive stretch\n- Clasp-knife quality (resistance then sudden release)\n- Worse with anxiety, cold, pain\n- Diminishes during sleep\n- Involves antigravity muscles preferentially\n- Upper motor neuron sign (hyperreflexia, clonus, Babinski)\n\n**Dystonia**:\n- Sustained or intermittent involuntary muscle contractions causing abnormal postures or repetitive movements\n- NOT velocity-dependent \u2014 present regardless of speed of passive movement\n- May be triggered by voluntary movement (action dystonia) or present at rest\n- Can involve agonist and antagonist muscles simultaneously (co-contraction)\n- May have a directional preference (twisting, turning)\n- Can worsen with fatigue, stress, and voluntary effort\n- May improve with sensory tricks (geste antagoniste)\n- Does NOT have the clasp-knife quality\n\n**Why It Matters**:\n- **SDR only treats spasticity** \u2014 it sections sensory rootlets that drive the spastic reflex arc. If the predominant movement disorder is dystonia, SDR will unmask weakness without addressing the abnormal posturing.\n- **Botulinum toxin treats both** spasticity and dystonia but through different mechanisms\n- **ITB can treat both** but is more effective for spasticity\n- **Oral baclofen**: Primarily anti-spasticity\n- **Trihexyphenidyl (Artane)**: Anti-dystonia medication (anticholinergic). Would not help pure spasticity.\n- **Deep brain stimulation**: For severe dystonia, not spasticity\n\n**Assessment in This Patient**:\n- The velocity-dependent catch on Modified Tardieu testing\n- The presence of clonus and hyperreflexia\n- The clasp-knife quality of resistance\n- The absence of sustained posturing at rest\n- All confirm that spasticity, not dystonia, is the predominant movement disorder, making him a reasonable SDR candidate from a neurological standpoint\n\n**Hypertonia Assessment Tool (HAT)**:\n- Validated tool to differentiate spasticity, dystonia, and rigidity in children with CP\n- Particularly useful when both spasticity and dystonia coexist (mixed CP)\n- Should be administered by experienced clinicians')
B.aYy=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Gait analysis confirms spasticity-driven equinus gait with dynamic valgus and mild crouch. You are proceeding with a combined botulinum toxin and serial casting trial while the family considers SDR.")
B.aMQ=new A.c("+ Design the botulinum toxin injection plan and serial casting protocol, and outline the ongoing spasticity management strategy.","**Botulinum Toxin Injection Plan**:\n\n**Preparation**:\n- Discuss goals with family: improve heel contact, reduce scissoring, improve gait efficiency\n- Consider switching from onabotulinumtoxinA (Botox) to abobotulinumtoxinA (Dysport) if diminishing response suggests antibody formation (different formulations, partial cross-reactivity)\n- Use ultrasound or electrical stimulation guidance for accurate targeting\n\n**Injection Targets and Doses (using onabotulinumtoxinA)**:\n- **Bilateral medial gastrocnemius**: 4 units/kg per leg (primary equinus driver)\n- **Bilateral lateral gastrocnemius**: 2 units/kg per leg\n- **Bilateral hip adductors (adductor longus)**: 2 units/kg per leg (for scissoring)\n- **Bilateral medial hamstrings (semitendinosus)**: 2 units/kg per leg (for crouch and hamstring tightness)\n- Total dose: approximately 20 units/kg body weight per session (360 units at 18 kg body weight), within safe limits (maximum 20 units/kg or 400 units total, whichever is less)\n- Inject under sedation (procedural sedation or nitrous oxide) given the patient's age and number of injection sites\n\n**Serial Casting Protocol**:\n- Begin 10-14 days after botulinum toxin injections (when toxin effect is maximal)\n- Apply short-stretch fiberglass casts to bilateral lower legs in maximum tolerated dorsiflexion\n- Change casts weekly for 4-6 weeks, progressively increasing dorsiflexion\n- Goal: achieve at least 10 degrees of passive dorsiflexion with knee extended\n- Monitor skin integrity at each cast change\n- Patient may continue to walk in casts (fiberglass walking casts with rocker bottoms)\n\n**Post-Casting AFO Prescription**:\n- Fabricate new bilateral solid AFOs (or hinged AFOs with plantarflexion stop) immediately after final cast removal to maintain gains\n- Custom-molded, full-length footplate, anterior trim line allowing adequate dorsiflexion\n- Review shoe fit and ensure AFOs are compatible with school shoes\n\n**Ongoing Spasticity Management Strategy**:\n- **PT optimization**: Transition from maintenance PT to goal-directed, task-specific training. Strengthen hip extensors, knee extensors, and ankle dorsiflexors. Work on single-limb stance and dynamic balance.\n- **Home exercise program**: Daily stretching of gastrocnemius, hamstrings, and hip flexors. Standing frame program (45-60 minutes daily for hip development and stretching)\n- **Botulinum toxin cycles**: Every 4-6 months as needed. Track response systematically with GMFM scores and gait parameters.\n- **Annual hip surveillance**: AP pelvis radiograph until skeletal maturity\n- **SDR consideration**: Continue family education. Arrange visit to SDR center for pre-surgical evaluation. Decision is not urgent \u2014 window extends to age 8-10.\n- **Orthopedic surgery planning**: If contractures progress despite botulinum toxin and casting, SEMLS (gastrocnemius recession, possible hamstring lengthening, femoral derotation) may be needed. Typically performed around age 7-10 based on gait lab data.\n\n**Monitoring and Follow-up**:\n- Spasticity clinic visits every 4-6 months\n- GMFM-66 assessment every 6-12 months\n- Gait analysis annually or before any surgical intervention\n- Growth monitoring with CP-specific growth charts\n- Bone health assessment if fracture history or prolonged non-weight-bearing")
B.b2E=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The family is navigating insurance coverage for botulinum toxin, DME (wheelchair and AFOs), and potentially SDR surgery. The school is also involved in providing therapy.")
B.aX4=new A.c("+ Discuss the systems-based issues in managing a child with CP, including insurance, school-based services, Medicaid waivers, and transition planning.",'**Insurance and Coverage Issues**:\n\n**Botulinum Toxin**:\n- Most private insurers and Medicaid cover botulinum toxin for spasticity in CP\n- Prior authorization typically requires documentation of spasticity severity, functional limitation, and failed conservative treatment\n- Some insurers limit the number of injection sessions per year (typically 3-4)\n- Appeal process: physician letter of medical necessity, peer-to-peer review, citing American Academy of Neurology/American Academy of PM&R guidelines\n\n**DME (Durable Medical Equipment)**:\n- Custom AFOs: Covered under most plans with a prescription and documentation of medical necessity. Replacement every 12-18 months in growing children.\n- Manual wheelchair: Covered for community mobility in GMFCS III. Requires prescription, PT evaluation for fitting, and letter of medical necessity.\n- Standing frame: Often denied as "not medically necessary." Appeal with evidence for hip development, bone density, bowel function, and spasticity management. Cite Paleg et al. systematic review supporting standing programs in CP.\n- Gait trainer/posterior walker: Covered as DME. Replacement as the child grows.\n\n**Medicaid Waivers (Katie Beckett / TEFRA)**:\n- Children with significant disabilities may qualify for Medicaid regardless of parental income through home and community-based waivers\n- Covers services not typically covered by private insurance: extended therapy, respite care, personal care assistants, home modifications\n- Wait lists can be years long depending on the state\n- Application requires documentation of functional limitations and care needs\n\n**SDR Surgery Coverage**:\n- Increasingly covered by major insurers for appropriate candidates\n- Requires comprehensive pre-surgical evaluation including gait lab, psychological assessment, and neurosurgical consultation\n- Some families travel to specialized SDR centers (St. Louis, Alder Hey, etc.), which may be out-of-network\n- Out-of-network appeals may require documentation that the service is not available in-network at the required quality level\n\n**School-Based Therapy vs. Outpatient Therapy**:\n- **School-based PT/OT**: Provided under the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA). Goals are educationally relevant (accessing classroom, navigating school, writing). Frequency is often limited (1-2x per week for 30 minutes).\n- **Outpatient PT/OT**: Medically prescribed. Goals are functionally and medically relevant (gait improvement, contracture prevention, post-surgical rehabilitation). Can be more intensive.\n- **These are complementary, not duplicative.** The child should receive both. Document different goals for each to justify coverage.\n- Common insurance denial: "Patient is already receiving PT at school." Appeal: School-based and medical PT have different mandates, goals, and legal frameworks.\n\n**Transition Planning (Even at Age 5, Begin Thinking Ahead)**:\n- Discuss long-term trajectory: GMFCS III patients who walk at age 5 may or may not maintain ambulation into adulthood. Approximately 50% of GMFCS III individuals use a wheelchair as their primary mobility in adulthood.\n- Begin discussing the concept of mobility as a spectrum (walking is not inherently superior to wheeled mobility if it is inefficient and exhausting)\n- Transition to adult care will occur at age 18-21 \u2014 plan for transferring medical records, identifying adult CP-experienced physicians, and addressing guardianship if cognitively impaired (not an issue in this patient)\n\n**Family Resources**:\n- United Cerebral Palsy (UCP) \u2014 local chapters provide family support, advocacy, and resources\n- Shriners Hospitals for Children \u2014 free orthopedic and rehabilitation care for children with CP\n- Easter Seals \u2014 therapy services, respite care, adaptive recreation\n- CP Research Network \u2014 connect families with clinical trials and research opportunities')
B.aWk=new A.c(u.j,"During the family meeting, the father says firmly: \"I've read the studies. SDR has the best outcomes. I want my son to have every chance at normal walking. We should do the surgery now.\" The mother, tearful, responds: \"I can't put him through that. He's only five. What if something goes wrong? I don't want him to be worse.\" They look to you to resolve the disagreement.")
B.aNT=new A.c("+ Mediate the parental conflict about SDR while maintaining both parents' trust and ensuring shared decision-making.",'**Step 1 \u2014 Acknowledge Both Perspectives as Valid**:\n"I want to thank both of you for being so invested in your son\'s care. The fact that you are both passionate about this shows how much you love him. And I want to be honest with you \u2014 you are both right, in a sense. There is strong evidence that SDR can improve outcomes in the right candidate, and there are real risks to any surgery in a five-year-old. These are not contradictory positions. They are two sides of the same careful decision."\n\n**Step 2 \u2014 Normalize the Disagreement**:\n"I see families struggle with this exact decision regularly, and it would be unusual if you both felt the same way about it. Different parents bring different perspectives, and that is actually healthy for the decision-making process. You are checking each other\'s reasoning."\n\n**Step 3 \u2014 Address the Father\'s Perspective with Nuance**:\n"[Father\'s name], I respect the research you have done, and you are right that the evidence for SDR in well-selected candidates is strong. There are studies showing sustained improvement in gait and spasticity reduction 20 years after the procedure. However, I want to make sure we are looking at the complete picture. SDR works best when spasticity is the primary driver of gait difficulty and there are minimal fixed contractures. Right now, your son has some contracture component developing at his ankles that SDR alone will not address. We need the gait lab data to tell us exactly how much of his walking difficulty is spasticity versus contracture versus bony alignment. If the gait lab shows that spasticity is dominant, SDR becomes a stronger option. If contractures and bony issues are significant, orthopedic surgery may be needed with or without SDR."\n\n**Step 4 \u2014 Address the Mother\'s Fear with Specific Information**:\n"[Mother\'s name], your concerns are absolutely valid and shared by many parents. Let me be specific about the risks so we are working with real numbers rather than fear of the unknown. The most common complications are transient: temporary bladder dysfunction in about 10-20% that usually resolves within weeks, temporary sensory changes, and temporary increased weakness during recovery. Serious permanent complications are rare \u2014 less than 1-2% in experienced centers. The biggest challenge is the rehabilitation commitment: 6-12 months of intensive therapy, 3-5 times per week. It is a marathon, and the whole family has to be ready for it."\n\n**Step 5 \u2014 Provide a Shared Decision-Making Framework**:\n"Here is what I recommend as a path forward that respects both of your positions. First, we proceed with the botulinum toxin and serial casting trial \u2014 this addresses the immediate contracture problem and buys us time. Second, we get the instrumented gait analysis \u2014 this gives us objective data that takes the decision out of opinion and into evidence. Third, we schedule a consultation with the neurosurgery SDR team \u2014 you can meet the surgeon, ask questions, and hear their assessment without committing to anything. After all of that, we sit down together, review the data, and make a decision as a team. There is no rush. The window for SDR extends to age 8-10. Your son is five. We have time to get this right."\n\n**Step 6 \u2014 Center the Child**:\n"One more thing. Your son is five, and while he cannot make this decision, his experience matters. He is going to do better with whatever treatment his parents are aligned on. If one of you feels coerced into a decision you are not comfortable with, that stress will affect the whole family and his recovery. The goal is for both of you to feel confident, not for one person to win and the other to lose."\n\n**Key Principles**:\n- Do not take sides \u2014 you are a facilitator, not an arbiter\n- Use data (gait lab) to depersonalize the decision\n- Provide specific risk numbers rather than vague reassurances\n- Create a sequential plan that addresses both parents\' needs (caution and action)\n- Affirm that taking time to decide is not the same as doing nothing')
B.aEm=s([B.aVl,B.aSk,B.aQd,B.aOF,B.aMN,B.aPl,B.aYy,B.aMQ,B.b2E,B.aX4,B.aWk,B.aNT],t.E)
B.Qr=new A.a5("peds-cp-spasticity","Cerebral Palsy \u2014 Spasticity Decision-Making",B.aEm,!1)
B.aT4=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'An 11-year-old boy with Duchenne muscular dystrophy (DMD) presents to the PM&R neuromuscular clinic for routine follow-up. He was diagnosed at age 4 after his preschool teacher noted frequent falls and difficulty keeping up with peers. Genetic testing confirmed an exon 45-50 deletion in the dystrophin gene. He has been on deflazacort 0.9 mg/kg/day since age 5. He transitioned from independent ambulation to using a gait trainer 18 months ago and now reports that walking has become "too tiring" even with the device. He fell twice last week at school, sustaining a knee abrasion and significant embarrassment in front of peers. His last 6-Minute Walk Test (3 months ago) was 180 meters, down from 260 meters one year ago. His FVC on pulmonary function testing was 72% predicted, down from 85% one year ago. His most recent echocardiogram (4 months ago) showed normal left ventricular ejection fraction at 58% with no wall motion abnormalities. He lives with both parents and a 9-year-old sister who does not carry the mutation. His mother is a known carrier. The family home is a two-story colonial with the bedrooms upstairs and a half bathroom on the main floor. The boy is an avid video gamer, loves Marvel movies, and has a close group of 3 friends at school. He has been increasingly withdrawn over the past month, refusing to go to birthday parties and skipping recess. During today\'s visit, when his mother steps out to use the restroom, he quietly tells you: "I don\'t want to be in a wheelchair. My friends will make fun of me."')
B.aY0=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","**DMD Disease Course and Current Stage**:\n- DMD follows a predictable trajectory: early ambulatory (diagnosis to ~7 years), late ambulatory (7-12 years), early non-ambulatory (12-15 years), late non-ambulatory (>15 years)\n- This patient is in the **late ambulatory to early non-ambulatory transition** \u2014 a critical and emotionally charged period\n- Loss of ambulation typically occurs between ages 9-13 (deflazacort extends this by 2-3 years on average)\n- His declining 6-Minute Walk Test and increasing falls indicate this transition is imminent\n\n**Corticosteroid Management**:\n- **Deflazacort 0.9 mg/kg/day** is standard of care \u2014 prolongs ambulation, delays scoliosis, preserves pulmonary and cardiac function\n- Side effects to monitor: weight gain (deflazacort has less weight gain than prednisone), cataracts (annual ophthalmology screening), growth deceleration, vertebral compression fractures, adrenal suppression, behavioral changes, glucose intolerance\n- **Never abruptly discontinue** \u2014 risk of adrenal crisis. Stress-dose steroids needed for illness or surgery.\n- Continue corticosteroids into non-ambulatory phase \u2014 ongoing benefits for cardiac and pulmonary function\n\n**Cardiac Monitoring**:\n- Cardiomyopathy is universal in DMD, typically developing by age 10-15\n- Current LVEF 58% is at the lower end of normal \u2014 requires close monitoring\n- **Standard**: Echocardiogram and ECG every 12 months (every 6 months after age 10 or if LVEF <55%)\n- **Cardiac MRI**: More sensitive than echocardiogram for early fibrosis. Consider if echo shows borderline function.\n- **ACE inhibitor or ARB**: Initiate prophylactically by age 10 or at first sign of dysfunction (lisinopril or losartan). This patient should be started on prophylactic cardiac protection now.\n- **Beta-blocker**: Add if LVEF declines below 55%\n\n**Pulmonary Monitoring**:\n- FVC 72% predicted indicates early restrictive lung disease\n- **Standard**: Spirometry every 6 months once FVC <80% predicted\n- **Cough assessment**: Peak cough flow <270 L/min indicates inadequate cough and need for assisted cough device\n- **Sleep study**: Obtain when FVC <50% or if symptoms of nocturnal hypoventilation (morning headaches, daytime somnolence, vivid dreams)\n- **Nocturnal BiPAP**: Initiate when FVC <50% predicted, sleep study shows hypoventilation, or daytime hypercapnia develops\n- **Mechanical insufflation-exsufflation (CoughAssist)**: Prescribe when peak cough flow <270 L/min or during respiratory illnesses\n\n**Musculoskeletal Assessment**:\n- **Contractures**: Assess ankle dorsiflexion (equinus contractures common), knee flexion contractures, hip flexion contractures, IT band tightness\n- **Scoliosis**: Monitor closely during non-ambulatory transition. Progression is rapid without corticosteroids. Surgical spinal fusion considered when Cobb angle >20-30 degrees and before FVC drops below 30-40% predicted.\n- **Fractures**: Low bone density from corticosteroids, immobility, and vitamin D deficiency. Vertebral compression fractures may be asymptomatic. Consider lateral spine radiograph and DXA scan.\n- **Bone health**: Vitamin D supplementation (target 25-OH vitamin D >30 ng/mL), calcium supplementation, weight-bearing as tolerated\n\n**Functional Assessment**:\n- North Star Ambulatory Assessment (NSAA): standardized functional scale for ambulatory DMD\n- Brooke Upper Extremity Scale and Vignos Lower Extremity Scale for longitudinal monitoring\n- Performance of Upper Limb (PUL): Important as upper extremity function becomes primary concern in non-ambulatory phase")
B.aYp=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: Weight 42 kg (75th percentile \u2014 steroid-related weight gain), Height 136 cm (10th percentile \u2014 growth deceleration from steroids), BMI 22.7 (>85th percentile). Cushingoid facies. Moon facies.\n\n**General**: Alert, interactive but subdued affect. Makes eye contact with examiner but avoids eye contact with mother when DMD is discussed.\n\n**Musculoskeletal**:\n- **Pseudohypertrophy**: Prominent bilateral calf enlargement (fibrofatty replacement)\n- **Proximal weakness pattern**: Gowers sign positive \u2014 uses hands on thighs to rise from floor (takes 12 seconds, labored)\n- **Shoulder girdle**: Scapular winging bilaterally. Difficulty raising arms above head against gravity.\n- **Contractures**: Bilateral ankle dorsiflexion -5 degrees (5 degree equinus contracture), bilateral knee extension to -10 degrees (mild flexion contractures), bilateral hip flexion contractures (Thomas test positive at 15 degrees), bilateral IT band tightness (Ober test positive)\n- **Spine**: Increased lumbar lordosis (compensatory for hip extensor weakness and hip flexion contractures). No scoliosis palpable. Forward bend test negative.\n\n**Neurologic**:\n- **Motor (MRC grading)**:\n  - Neck flexors: 3/5\n  - Shoulder abduction: 3+/5 bilaterally\n  - Elbow flexion: 4/5 bilaterally\n  - Wrist extension: 4+/5 bilaterally\n  - Grip: 4/5 bilaterally\n  - Hip flexion: 3/5 bilaterally\n  - Hip extension: 2+/5 bilaterally\n  - Knee extension: 3/5 bilaterally\n  - Knee flexion: 3-/5 bilaterally\n  - Ankle dorsiflexion: 3/5 bilaterally\n  - Ankle plantarflexion: 3+/5 bilaterally\n- **Reflexes**: Absent at Achilles bilaterally. Trace at patella. 1+ biceps. No pathologic reflexes.\n- **Sensory**: Intact throughout (DMD does not affect sensory nerves)\n\n**Gait**: Waddling (Trendelenburg) gait with exaggerated lumbar lordosis. Toe-walking bilaterally. Wide base of support. Unable to run. Requires 2 attempts to rise from chair. Unable to climb stairs without bilateral rail support.\n\n**Pulmonary**: Spirometry today: FVC 68% predicted (declining), FEV1/FVC ratio normal. Peak cough flow 240 L/min (below threshold of 270). Mild intercostal retractions at rest.\n\n**Functional**: NSAA score 12/34 (significant decline from 22/34 one year ago). Unable to stand from floor without furniture. Unable to climb 4 steps within 12 seconds.")
B.b0M=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The family needs guidance on the timing and approach to wheelchair prescription, as well as ongoing medical monitoring during this transition phase.")
B.b0c=new A.c("+ Discuss the evidence-based approach to power wheelchair prescription in DMD, including standing frame use and the monitoring schedule during the non-ambulatory transition.",'**Power Wheelchair Prescription \u2014 Timing and Approach**:\n\n**When to Introduce a Power Wheelchair**:\n- The wheelchair should be framed as a tool for independence, NOT as "giving up"\n- Evidence supports early introduction \u2014 before complete loss of ambulation \u2014 so the child can learn to operate it while still ambulatory\n- This patient\'s indicators for immediate wheelchair prescription: 6-Minute Walk Test <300 meters and declining, frequent falls (safety issue), functional mobility limiting school and social participation, declining NSAA score\n- **Dual mobility approach**: Use the power wheelchair for community and school distances while encouraging walking for short household distances as long as safe and not exhausting\n\n**Power Wheelchair Specifications for DMD**:\n- **Power tilt-in-space**: Essential for pressure redistribution, respiratory support, and positioning as the disease progresses\n- **Power recline**: For pressure relief and comfort\n- **Elevating legrests**: For edema management and contracture positioning\n- **Seat elevator**: Allows the child to reach counters, talk at eye level with peers, and access school desks \u2014 critical for psychosocial development\n- **Custom seating**: Contoured seat and back cushions, lateral trunk supports (will become critical as scoliosis develops), headrest (will need as neck flexor weakness progresses)\n- **Expandable frame**: The chair must accommodate growth and disease progression for 3-5 years. Consider a frame that can be reconfigured as needs change.\n- **Joystick control**: Standard hand joystick initially. Plan for alternative drive controls (mini-joystick, head array, sip-and-puff) as upper extremity weakness progresses in later stages.\n- **Mounting hardware**: For tablet/phone, ventilator tray (future), communication device if needed\n- **Environmental controls**: Consider integration with smart home technology (voice-activated devices)\n\n**Standing Frame**:\n- Prescribe a supine or prone standing frame for daily use (45-60 minutes daily)\n- Benefits: bone density preservation, contracture management, bowel and bladder function, psychological well-being\n- May need a sit-to-stand frame or a power standing wheelchair if the family can obtain one\n- Continue as long as the patient can tolerate it, typically into the mid-teens\n\n**Monitoring Schedule During Non-Ambulatory Transition**:\n\n| Assessment | Frequency |\n|---|---|\n| PM&R/Neuromuscular clinic | Every 3-4 months |\n| Pulmonary function tests | Every 6 months |\n| Echocardiogram + ECG | Every 6-12 months (every 6 months if LVEF <55%) |\n| Cardiac MRI | Every 2 years or if echo concerning |\n| Spine radiograph (scoliosis) | Every 6-12 months in non-ambulatory phase |\n| DXA scan | Every 1-2 years |\n| Ophthalmology (cataracts) | Annually |\n| Endocrine (growth, glucose, adrenal) | Annually |\n| Sleep study | When FVC <50% or symptoms present |\n| Lateral spine radiograph | If back pain (vertebral fractures) |\n| Vitamin D level | Every 6-12 months |\n\n**Respiratory Interventions to Initiate Now**:\n- Peak cough flow is 240 L/min (<270 threshold): Prescribe mechanical insufflation-exsufflation (CoughAssist) for home use\n- Train the family in assisted cough techniques (manual and device-assisted)\n- Lung volume recruitment (breath stacking) with a manual resuscitation bag \u2014 daily practice to maintain chest wall compliance\n- Influenza and pneumococcal vaccinations annually\n- Aggressive treatment of respiratory infections \u2014 early antibiotic therapy, chest physiotherapy, CoughAssist use with illness\n\n**Cardiac Intervention**:\n- Start ACE inhibitor (lisinopril 2.5-5 mg daily) or ARB (losartan) now \u2014 evidence supports prophylactic cardioprotection even with normal LVEF in DMD after age 10\n- Monitor blood pressure and renal function after initiation')
B.aXM=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You prescribe a power wheelchair and standing frame. The family also needs guidance on scoliosis management, respiratory muscle training, and preparation for the next stages of the disease.")
B.aSq=new A.c("+ Design a comprehensive management plan addressing musculoskeletal, respiratory, and cardiac needs during the non-ambulatory transition.","**Musculoskeletal Management**:\n\n**Contracture Prevention**:\n- Daily stretching program (15-20 minutes): ankle dorsiflexion, knee extension, hip extension, IT band, hip abduction\n- Night splints: bilateral ankle-foot orthoses worn during sleep to maintain ankle dorsiflexion range\n- Standing frame: 45-60 minutes daily\n- Aquatic therapy: Warm water pool (88-92 degrees F) provides buoyancy for active ROM and gentle strengthening without eccentric loading. **Eccentric exercise is contraindicated in DMD** as it accelerates muscle damage.\n- PT: 1-2x per week outpatient, focused on ROM maintenance, functional transfers, and respiratory muscle training. Avoid aggressive strengthening \u2014 submaximal, concentric-only, functional movements.\n\n**Scoliosis Monitoring and Management**:\n- Progressive scoliosis is nearly universal in non-ambulatory DMD without corticosteroid treatment (90%+). Deflazacort reduces this to approximately 30%.\n- AP and lateral spine radiographs every 6-12 months\n- **Bracing**: Not effective for neuromuscular scoliosis in DMD \u2014 does not prevent progression and may compromise respiratory function by restricting chest wall movement. Not recommended.\n- **Surgical fusion**: Indicated when Cobb angle reaches 20-30 degrees and is progressive. Timing is critical \u2014 must be done before FVC drops below 30-40% predicted (below this, surgical risk is substantially higher). In this patient with FVC 68%, there is a window for surgery if scoliosis develops.\n- Posterior spinal fusion from T2 to pelvis is standard. Growing rod constructs may be considered in younger patients.\n\n**Respiratory Muscle Training**:\n- Inspiratory muscle training (IMT) with a threshold device: emerging evidence for delaying respiratory decline\n- Lung volume recruitment (breath stacking): 2-3 times daily with manual resuscitation bag. Patient takes successive breaths without exhaling to achieve maximal lung inflation. Maintains chest wall compliance.\n- CoughAssist training: Family must be proficient in device use. Settings: +40/-40 cmH2O, 5 cycles, 3 sets with rest between. Use during respiratory infections and daily for secretion management.\n- Airway clearance techniques: High-frequency chest wall oscillation vest can be added if secretion management becomes difficult\n\n**Cardiac Management**:\n- Initiate ACE inhibitor or ARB now\n- If LVEF declines below 55%: Add beta-blocker (carvedilol)\n- If LVEF declines below 45%: Consider eplerenone (aldosterone antagonist), cardiology-guided advanced heart failure management\n- Avoid dehydration \u2014 cardiomyopathy patients are preload-dependent\n- Stress-dose steroids for any illness or procedure (adrenal suppression from chronic deflazacort)\n\n**Emerging Therapies**:\n- **Exon-skipping therapies**: Eteplirsen (exon 51), golodirsen (exon 53), viltolarsen (exon 53), casimersen (exon 45). This patient has an exon 45-50 deletion \u2014 check eligibility for exon-skipping agents (exon 51 skipping would be needed for a 45-50 deletion to restore reading frame)\n- **Gene therapy**: Micro-dystrophin gene therapy (delandistrogene moxeparvovec/Elevidys) \u2014 FDA approved with accelerated approval for DMD ages 4-5, trials ongoing for older children. Discuss with neurology.\n- **Cardiac gene therapy**: In clinical trials for DMD cardiomyopathy\n- These therapies aim to slow progression, not cure, and work best when started early. The PM&R physician should ensure families are connected to a comprehensive DMD center for access to trials.\n\n**Nutrition**:\n- Dietitian consultation: Balance adequate nutrition for growth with weight management (excess weight accelerates functional decline and complicates wheelchair seating)\n- Monitor for corticosteroid-related metabolic effects: glucose intolerance, dyslipidemia\n- Calcium 1000-1300 mg/day + Vitamin D 1000-2000 IU/day for bone health")
B.aNd=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The family needs help navigating resources, school accommodations, and planning for the future. The mother asks about connecting with other DMD families.")
B.aTS=new A.c("+ Discuss DMD-specific resources, school accommodations, genetic counseling considerations, and long-term transition planning.","**DMD-Specific Resources**:\n- **Muscular Dystrophy Association (MDA)**: Care center network, summer camps, equipment loan programs, advocacy. MDA Care Centers provide multidisciplinary DMD clinics with neurology, PM&R, pulmonology, cardiology, PT/OT, social work in one visit.\n- **Parent Project Muscular Dystrophy (PPMD)**: Family support, research advocacy, educational webinars, annual conference connecting families with leading researchers\n- **CureDuchenne**: Research funding, patient registry, care guidelines\n- **Team Gleason (Steve Gleason Foundation)**: Technology (communication devices, eye-tracking, smart home) and adventure programs for neuromuscular diseases\n- **Jett Foundation**: Financial assistance, programs for boys with DMD\n\n**School Accommodations (IEP/504 Plan)**:\n- **Physical access**: Elevator access, accessible classroom and bathroom, wheelchair-accessible desk, permission to use power wheelchair throughout school\n- **Adapted PE**: Modified physical education with appropriate activities (swimming, adapted sports, light activities). Avoid eccentric exercise and contact sports.\n- **Fatigue management**: Extra time between classes, reduced homework if fatigue impacts completion, option for rest periods\n- **Writing support**: As upper extremity weakness progresses, typing may replace handwriting. Voice-to-text software, extended time for written assignments.\n- **Emergency evacuation plan**: Specific plan for evacuating a wheelchair-dependent student during fire or emergency\n- **Health plan**: Medication administration at school (deflazacort timing), CoughAssist access, trained aide for respiratory emergencies\n- **Psychosocial support**: Regular access to school counselor, anti-bullying protocols, peer education about DMD (with the student's permission)\n\n**Genetic Counseling**:\n- **For the mother**: She is a confirmed carrier. Risk of cardiomyopathy in female carriers \u2014 echocardiogram recommended for all female carriers. May benefit from genetic counselor to discuss implications for future pregnancies.\n- **For the sister**: She has a 50% chance of being a carrier. Genetic testing can be offered when she is old enough to consent (typically adolescence, though practice varies). Must be handled sensitively.\n- **For the patient's future**: DMD males are typically infertile, but with advancing medical therapies and longer survival, fertility counseling may become relevant. Sperm banking can be discussed in adolescence if appropriate.\n- **Family planning for the parents**: If they desire more children, preimplantation genetic diagnosis or prenatal testing is available\n\n**Long-Term Transition Planning**:\n- **Life expectancy**: With modern care (corticosteroids, cardiac management, non-invasive ventilation), survival now extends into the 30s and beyond for many patients\n- **Education and vocation**: Many DMD patients complete college and pursue careers in fields compatible with physical limitations. Encourage academic excellence and career exploration.\n- **Independent living**: Increasingly possible with personal care assistants, smart home technology, and power wheelchair with environmental controls\n- **Transition to adult care**: Begin planning at age 14-16. Identify adult neuromuscular, cardiology, pulmonology, and PM&R providers. Transfer medical records and care summaries.\n- **Guardianship and decision-making**: DMD does not typically affect cognition (though some patients have learning differences related to dystrophin isoforms in the brain). Full decision-making capacity is expected.\n- **Advance care planning**: Should be discussed in late adolescence/early adulthood. Preferences for mechanical ventilation (invasive vs. non-invasive), resuscitation status, and end-of-life care.")
B.aXf=new A.c(u.j,"When his mother has briefly left the room, the boy looks at you and says quietly: \"I don't want to be in a wheelchair. My friends will make fun of me. I'd rather stay home than go to school in a wheelchair.\"")
B.b_A=new A.c("+ Provide age-appropriate emotional support to an 11-year-old boy facing wheelchair transition, addressing body image, peer relationships, and self-worth.",'**Step 1 \u2014 Create a Safe Space and Validate His Feelings**:\n"Thank you for telling me that. It takes courage to say what you are feeling, and I am really glad you trust me enough to share it. Can I tell you something? What you are feeling right now \u2014 that worry about what your friends will think \u2014 that is completely normal. Any kid your age would feel the same way. You are not being weak or silly. You are being honest, and that matters."\n\n**Step 2 \u2014 Explore His Specific Fears**:\n"Can you tell me more about what you are worried about? Is it what your three close friends would think, or is it more about the other kids at school? Have any of them said anything about your walking lately?"\n[Listen actively. Let him lead the conversation. Do not rush to reassure.]\n\n**Step 3 \u2014 Reframe the Wheelchair as Empowerment, Not Loss**:\n"I want to tell you something that might sound weird, but hear me out. Right now, you are spending all of your energy just trying to walk to class and back. You told me walking is exhausting, and you have been skipping recess and birthday parties because of it. The wheelchair is not going to take something away from you. It is going to give you something back \u2014 energy. Energy to play video games with your friends at their houses. Energy to go to birthday parties. Energy to focus in class instead of being tired. It is like giving Iron Man his suit. Tony Stark is a genius without the suit, but the suit lets him do more, not less."\n\n**Step 4 \u2014 Address Peer Relationships Practically**:\n"About your friends \u2014 your three close friends already know you. They have watched you use a walker. They know who you are. I think you might be surprised by how they react. And here is an idea: what if you were the one to tell them about the wheelchair before they see it? You could even let them try driving it. A power wheelchair with a joystick and seat elevator is actually pretty cool. Some of my patients\' friends have been jealous."\n\n**Step 5 \u2014 Offer Connection with Peers Who Understand**:\n"There are also other boys your age who have gone through exactly what you are going through right now. MDA has programs and camps where you can meet them. Some of them are doing incredible things \u2014 playing adaptive sports, streaming video games, going to school and college. Would you be interested in meeting some of them? Sometimes it helps to talk to someone who actually knows what it feels like."\n\n**Step 6 \u2014 Empower His Autonomy**:\n"This is your wheelchair, your life, and your decision about how you want to handle it at school. We can work together on a plan. You get to decide who you tell, when you start using it, and how you want to introduce it. You are the boss of this."\n\n**Step 7 \u2014 Address What to Tell His Parents**:\n"I also want to check with you \u2014 is it okay if I share what you told me with your parents? They love you so much, and I think they would want to know how you are feeling. But if you want to talk to them yourself, or if you want me to help you talk to them, we can do that too."\n\n**Step 8 \u2014 Offer Ongoing Support**:\n"This is not a one-time conversation. I want you to know that every time you come see me, you can talk about this. If you are having a bad day, if someone says something hurtful, if you are feeling sad \u2014 you can call us. And if you want, we can connect you with a counselor who is really good at helping kids work through big changes like this. Not because something is wrong with you, but because everyone needs someone to talk to."\n\n**Key Principles**:\n- Speak directly to the child, not through the parents\n- Use age-appropriate language and references (Marvel analogies work for this patient)\n- Validate emotions before problem-solving\n- Offer practical strategies for peer management\n- Empower autonomy and choice\n- Connect with peer mentors who share the experience\n- Offer ongoing psychological support without pathologizing normal grief\n- Consider formal psychology or counseling referral for adjustment support')
B.aEc=s([B.aT4,B.aY0,B.aYp,B.b0M,B.b0c,B.aXM,B.aSq,B.aNd,B.aTS,B.aXf,B.b_A],t.E)
B.Q0=new A.a5("peds-duchenne","Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy \u2014 Transition to Non-Ambulatory",B.aEc,!1)
B.aQ4=new A.c("Initial Presentation","An 18-year-old female with L3 myelomeningocele presents to the PM&R transition clinic for establishment of adult care. She was followed in the pediatric spina bifida clinic since birth and has \"aged out\" of the program. Her surgical history includes myelomeningocele repair at 1 day of life, VP shunt placement at 2 weeks (revised twice \u2014 most recently at age 8 for proximal obstruction), bilateral hip osteotomies at age 6, and a Mitrofanoff appendicovesicostomy at age 10 for bladder management. She ambulates with bilateral KAFOs and Lofstrand crutches for household distances and uses a lightweight manual wheelchair for community mobility. She performs clean intermittent catheterization (CIC) via the Mitrofanoff channel every 4 hours and uses a timed bowel program with digital stimulation and bisacodyl suppository every other day. She is a senior in high school, has been accepted to a state university 3 hours from home, and plans to study graphic design. She has a 3.5 GPA, has passed her driver's test using hand controls, and has an active social media presence where she advocates for disability awareness. Her parents, particularly her father, are deeply involved in her care and have accompanied her to every medical appointment. During today's visit, she arrives independently in her wheelchair. Her parents are in the waiting room and ask to come back with her. She tells the front desk staff: \"I'd like to go back alone first.\" When you meet with her privately, she says: \"I need to figure out how to do this on my own. My parents still treat me like I'm twelve, and they don't want me to go away for college. My dad says it's too dangerous. I love them, but I need my own life.\"")
B.aN4=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","**Transition-Specific Assessment (Comprehensive)**:\n\n**Medical Complexity in Adult Spina Bifida**:\n- **Tethered cord syndrome**: Occurs in up to 30% of repaired myelomeningocele patients, can present at any age with new neurologic symptoms. Monitor for new back pain, change in gait, worsening lower extremity strength, change in bladder function, progressive scoliosis, or new foot deformity.\n- **VP shunt malfunction**: Lifelong risk. Symptoms in adults may be subtle: headaches, cognitive changes, vision changes, personality changes, nausea. Adult emergency departments frequently miss shunt malfunction because they are less familiar with it than pediatric EDs.\n- **Latex allergy**: Present in up to 50-70% of spina bifida patients due to early and repeated surgical exposure. Must be documented prominently in all medical records. Life-threatening anaphylaxis risk. All medical procedures must be performed in latex-free environment.\n- **Chiari II malformation**: Present in virtually all myelomeningocele patients. May be asymptomatic or cause headaches, dysphagia, sleep apnea, upper extremity weakness. MRI if new symptoms.\n\n**Bladder Management Review**:\n- CIC via Mitrofanoff every 4 hours \u2014 assess technique, compliance, and independence\n- Renal ultrasound annually \u2014 monitor for hydronephrosis, renal calculi, renal scarring\n- Urodynamics every 1-2 years \u2014 assess bladder compliance, detrusor pressures (>40 cmH2O risks upper tract damage), capacity, and continence\n- Anticholinergic medication review: Is she on oxybutynin or solifenacin? Side effects (dry mouth, constipation, cognitive effects)\n- Continence status: Social continence is critical for college life. Any incontinence episodes?\n- UTI frequency: Recurrent UTIs are common. Distinguish colonization (asymptomatic bacteriuria, common and does NOT require treatment) from true infection (new symptoms: fever, increased spasticity, foul-smelling urine, abdominal pain, leukocytosis)\n\n**Bowel Management Review**:\n- Current program effectiveness: continent between programs? Time to complete? Any skin breakdown from incontinence?\n- Transition to independent bowel program management: Can she perform digital stimulation and suppository insertion independently?\n- Dietary fiber and fluid intake optimization\n- Consider transanal irrigation (Peristeen system) if current program is inefficient for college schedule\n\n**Skin and Pressure Injury Risk**:\n- L3 myelomeningocele: Sensation absent below L3 (absent over lateral leg, foot, perianal region)\n- Insensate skin over buttocks, perineum, and feet \u2014 lifetime risk of pressure injuries\n- Wheelchair cushion assessment: pressure mapping to optimize cushion selection\n- Skin check routine: Does she perform daily skin checks of all insensate areas? Use a mirror for areas she cannot see directly?\n- Foot care: Insensate feet are at risk in KAFOs \u2014 inspect feet after each use\n\n**Musculoskeletal**:\n- L3 level motor function: Hip flexion and adduction preserved (L1-L3). Knee extension partially preserved (L3-L4). Absent: ankle dorsiflexion, plantarflexion, hip abduction, hip extension, knee flexion\n- KAFO condition: Inspect for wear, fit, and alignment\n- Upper extremity overuse injuries: Shoulder impingement, rotator cuff pathology, carpal tunnel syndrome \u2014 common in long-term crutch and wheelchair users\n- Obesity: Weight management is critical \u2014 limited lower extremity exercise capacity makes weight gain common\n\n**Psychosocial and Sexual Health**:\n- Depression and anxiety screening (PHQ-9, GAD-7)\n- Sexual health counseling: Sensation, fertility, safe sex practices, gynecologic care. Women with spina bifida have normal fertility. Pregnancy is possible but high-risk (folic acid supplementation critical, obstetric consultation needed).\n- Relationship history and body image assessment\n- Substance use screening (CRAFFT for adolescents)")
B.aUZ=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 118/72 mmHg, HR 74 bpm, Weight 72 kg, Height 160 cm (measured supine), BMI 28.1 kg/m2 (overweight). Well-healed lumbar scar from myelomeningocele repair.\n\n**General**: Well-appearing young woman, articulate, confident demeanor. Arrives independently in lightweight manual wheelchair.\n\n**Neurologic**:\n- **Motor**: Hip flexors 4/5 bilaterally, hip adductors 4/5, knee extensors 3+/5 bilaterally, knee flexors 1/5 bilaterally, tibialis anterior 0/5, gastrocnemius/soleus 0/5, EHL 0/5, hip abductors 2/5, hip extensors 1/5\n- **Sensory**: Intact sensation to L3 dermatome (anterior thigh). Absent sensation below L3: lateral thigh (L3-4 border), below knee, feet, perianal region, perineum\n- **Reflexes**: Patellar trace bilaterally. Achilles absent. Bulbocavernosus reflex absent.\n- **Tone**: Flaccid bilateral lower extremities below L3\n\n**Musculoskeletal**:\n- Bilateral KAFOs in good condition, mild wear on medial uprights\n- Mild bilateral knee flexion contractures (lacks 10 degrees of full extension)\n- Bilateral ankle plantigrade in AFO, no significant equinus\n- Mild thoracolumbar levoscoliosis (Cobb angle ~15 degrees, stable per prior imaging)\n- Bilateral shoulder examination: Mild bilateral supraspinatus tenderness, positive Neer impingement sign on left, full ROM but painful arc on left. Likely early rotator cuff tendinopathy from wheelchair propulsion.\n\n**Skin**: No pressure injuries. Well-healed scars over bilateral hips (osteotomy), lumbar spine, abdomen (Mitrofanoff). Mitrofanoff stoma in right lower quadrant, clean and patent, no granulation tissue.\n\n**Abdominal**: Soft, non-distended. VP shunt tubing palpable in right abdomen. No tenderness.\n\n**Wheelchair Assessment**: Lightweight rigid-frame manual wheelchair, 16-inch seat width, gel cushion (no pressure mapping data available), ROHO back support. Propulsion mechanics: mild forward trunk lean, asymmetric push pattern (right dominant). Wheelchair is 3 years old and showing frame wear.\n\n**Functional Mobility**:\n- Wheelchair-to-mat transfer: Independent, sliding board not used\n- Floor-to-wheelchair: Independent using step-up technique against furniture\n- Ambulation with bilateral KAFOs and Lofstrand crutches: Swing-through gait pattern, energy-intensive. Walks approximately 100 feet before requiring rest. Uses ambulation primarily at home.\n- Car transfer: Independent. Drives with hand controls independently.")
B.b2F=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","This patient is transitioning from pediatric to adult care. You must establish a comprehensive adult monitoring plan and identify the key medical concerns specific to adult spina bifida.")
B.aPE=new A.c("+ Outline the essential components of an adult spina bifida transition care plan, including the monitoring schedule and common adult complications.",'**Adult Spina Bifida \u2014 Transition Care Plan**:\n\n**Medical Passport Document**:\nCreate a comprehensive medical summary that the patient carries and provides to all new providers. This is critical because most adult providers have limited experience with spina bifida. Include:\n- Complete surgical history with dates\n- VP shunt type, location, and revision history\n- Current level of lesion and functional status\n- Latex allergy status (PROMINENTLY marked)\n- Bladder and bowel program details\n- Baseline neurologic examination\n- Current medications\n- Allergies\n- Emergency contacts and key providers\n- Baseline imaging (most recent brain MRI, spinal MRI, renal ultrasound)\n\n**Annual Monitoring Schedule**:\n\n| System | Assessment | Frequency |\n|---|---|---|\n| Renal | Ultrasound, BUN/Creatinine, GFR | Annually |\n| Bladder | Urodynamics | Every 1-2 years |\n| Neurologic | Comprehensive exam, compare to baseline | Annually |\n| Shunt | Baseline brain imaging (CT or MRI), then PRN if symptoms | Baseline + PRN |\n| Skin | Full body skin check, pressure mapping | Annually |\n| Musculoskeletal | Joint ROM, KAFO/wheelchair assessment, scoliosis | Annually |\n| Metabolic | BMI, lipids, glucose, vitamin D | Annually |\n| Gynecologic | Pap smear, pelvic exam (adapted positioning) | Per guidelines |\n| Psychological | Depression/anxiety screening | Annually |\n| Upper extremity | Shoulder and wrist assessment for overuse | Annually |\n\n**Common Adult Complications to Monitor**:\n\n1. **Tethered cord**: New symptoms warranting emergent MRI: worsening back pain, new lower extremity weakness, change in bladder or bowel function, progressive scoliosis, new foot deformity. Surgical untethering may be needed. Maintain a documented baseline neurologic exam for comparison.\n\n2. **VP shunt malfunction**: Annual baseline brain imaging (CT or fast MRI) to document stable ventricle size. If symptoms develop (headache, cognitive change, nausea, vision changes), compare to baseline. Adult EDs must be educated about this patient\'s shunt. Provide a "shunt card" with shunt details and neurosurgery contact information.\n\n3. **Renal deterioration**: Leading historical cause of death in spina bifida before modern urologic management. Monitor upper tracts vigilantly. Detrusor pressures >40 cmH2O on urodynamics require intervention (medication adjustment, botulinum toxin to detrusor, bladder augmentation).\n\n4. **Obesity**: Affects up to 50% of adults with spina bifida. Limited exercise capacity, lower basal metabolic rate. Dietitian referral. Explore accessible exercise options: hand cycling, wheelchair sports, swimming.\n\n5. **Pressure injuries**: Insensate skin + sitting + limited mobility = high risk. Pressure mapping every 1-2 years. Wheelchair cushion replacement every 2-3 years or sooner if worn. Weight shifts every 15-30 minutes.\n\n6. **Upper extremity overuse**: Rotator cuff tendinopathy, CTS, elbow pathology from wheelchair propulsion and crutch use. May eventually limit manual wheelchair use \u2014 consider power assist wheels or power wheelchair if upper extremity pain limits function.\n\n7. **Depression and social isolation**: Rates of depression are 2-3x higher than general population. Transition from structured pediatric care to fragmented adult care is a high-risk period. Screen actively.\n\n**Sexual and Reproductive Health**:\n- Women with L3 myelomeningocele typically have intact fertility\n- Pregnancy is possible but high-risk: folic acid 4 mg/day (high-dose for neural tube defect history), shunt monitoring during pregnancy, cesarean delivery often needed, latex precautions during delivery\n- Sensation: L3 level means absent genital sensation. Sexual counseling should address alternative erogenous zones, communication with partners, and the emotional aspects of intimacy.\n- Gynecologic care: May need adapted positioning for pelvic exams (side-lying). Pap smears per standard guidelines.\n- Contraception counseling: Avoid estrogen-containing methods if immobile (DVT risk). Progesterone-only, IUD, or barrier methods preferred.')
B.aQC=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","The patient is preparing for college 3 hours from home. She needs a comprehensive plan for managing her medical needs independently.")
B.b_H=new A.c("+ Develop a practical college readiness plan addressing medical self-management, emergency preparedness, and healthcare access at the college location.","**College Readiness \u2014 Medical Self-Management Plan**:\n\n**Self-Management Skills Checklist** (assess and train before departure):\n- [ ] Independent CIC via Mitrofanoff on schedule, including in unfamiliar settings (public restrooms, dorm bathroom)\n- [ ] Independent bowel program execution including suppository insertion and digital stimulation\n- [ ] Ordering and managing catheter and bowel supply inventory (insurance, DME company contact)\n- [ ] Recognizing UTI symptoms vs. asymptomatic bacteriuria (when to treat vs. when to monitor)\n- [ ] Daily skin checks of all insensate areas with hand mirror\n- [ ] Medication management (refills, pharmacy transfer, insurance authorization)\n- [ ] Recognizing VP shunt malfunction symptoms (headache, nausea, vision changes, cognitive decline)\n- [ ] Weight management strategies and accessible exercise options\n- [ ] Wheelchair maintenance: tire inflation, caster replacement, frame adjustment\n- [ ] KAFO inspection and minor troubleshooting\n\n**Emergency Preparedness Plan**:\n- Identify the nearest Level 1 trauma center and neurosurgery service (for shunt emergencies)\n- Identify a urologist near campus familiar with neurogenic bladder\n- Medical alert identification (bracelet or app-based) listing: latex allergy (life-threatening), VP shunt, myelomeningocele, neurogenic bladder\n- Emergency wallet card with medical passport summary, neurosurgery contact, and family emergency contact\n- Educate roommate on latex allergy recognition and epinephrine auto-injector use (prescribe EpiPen if latex allergy confirmed)\n- Register with campus security/disability services for emergency evacuation assistance\n\n**Healthcare Access at College**:\n- Establish with an adult PM&R physician, urologist, and primary care physician near campus BEFORE departure\n- Transfer medical records and baseline imaging to new providers\n- Ensure insurance coverage extends to the college location (network considerations)\n- Campus health center: Visit before classes start, provide medical passport, educate staff on spina bifida, confirm latex-free exam rooms\n- Pharmacy: Transfer prescriptions, ensure specialty supplies (catheters, suppositories) can be delivered to campus address\n\n**Disability Services Office**:\n- Register early (ideally spring before enrollment)\n- Accommodations to request: accessible dormitory room (roll-in shower, grab bars, adjustable bed height, proximity to accessible entrance), accessible classroom assignment, extended time for exams if bathroom breaks needed, priority course registration (to secure accessible classroom locations), accessible parking permit\n- Housing: Single room or accessible suite preferred. Ideally ground floor. Ensure bathroom accommodates wheelchair and has space for bowel program supplies.\n\n**Vocational and Academic Planning**:\n- Graphic design is an excellent career choice \u2014 compatible with physical limitations, can be done remotely\n- Vocational Rehabilitation (VR) services: State VR agency can provide funding for tuition, adaptive technology, transportation, and other supports for individuals with disabilities pursuing education or employment\n- Assistive technology assessment: Adaptive computer workstation (wheelchair-height desk, ergonomic setup to protect shoulders and wrists)\n\n**Social Integration**:\n- Encourage connecting with campus disability advocacy groups\n- Adaptive sports and recreation programs (wheelchair basketball, hand cycling club)\n- Counseling center: Establish contact early for ongoing support during transition\n- Social media advocacy: Her existing platform is a strength \u2014 connect with college disability community online\n\n**Supply Management**:\n- Catheter supplies: Order 3-month supply before departure. Establish auto-delivery. Keep 2-week emergency stock.\n- Bowel program supplies: Same approach\n- Backup wheelchair (if possible) or identify local wheelchair repair service\n- KAFO: Identify orthotist near campus for adjustments and repairs")
B.aUe=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient asks about financial support for college and whether she qualifies for disability benefits. She also wants to know about vocational rehabilitation services.")
B.aXi=new A.c("+ Discuss SSI/SSDI eligibility, vocational rehabilitation services, and college disability services for a young adult with spina bifida.","**SSI/SSDI and Financial Support**:\n\n**Supplemental Security Income (SSI)**:\n- Needs-based program for individuals with limited income and resources who are disabled\n- Children with spina bifida often receive SSI, but eligibility is re-evaluated at age 18 using adult criteria\n- **Age-18 redetermination**: At 18, SSA applies adult disability standards (ability to engage in substantial gainful activity). Many pediatric SSI recipients lose benefits at this redetermination if their functional status allows employment.\n- This patient is high-functioning (drives, attends college, independent in ADLs). She may not meet adult SSI disability criteria.\n- **Important**: SSI income limits may affect eligibility. If she works part-time, earnings above the substantial gainful activity threshold ($1,620/month in 2025) could disqualify her.\n\n**Social Security Disability Insurance (SSDI)**:\n- Requires sufficient work history (earnings credits). An 18-year-old typically has insufficient work history.\n- **SSDI as an adult child**: If a parent receives SSDI or has died, a disabled adult child (disability onset before age 22) may qualify for benefits on the parent's record. This patient's onset is clearly before age 22.\n- Worth exploring with a Social Security benefits counselor\n\n**Plan to Achieve Self-Support (PASS)**:\n- An SSA work incentive that allows SSI recipients to set aside income and resources for a work goal (such as education)\n- Could allow her to attend college and work part-time without losing all SSI benefits\n- Requires a written PASS plan approved by SSA\n\n**Vocational Rehabilitation (VR) Services**:\n- State-federal program that assists individuals with disabilities in achieving employment\n- **Services available**: Career counseling, job training, college tuition assistance, adaptive equipment, job placement, transportation, on-the-job support\n- **Eligibility**: Must have a disability that results in an impediment to employment and must be able to benefit from VR services in terms of employment outcome\n- This patient should be referred to VR as early as possible \u2014 ideally age 16 (transition planning under IDEA), but 18 is still appropriate\n- VR can fund: tuition not covered by financial aid, adaptive computer equipment, ergonomic workstation, transportation to campus, other disability-related educational expenses\n- **Individual Plan for Employment (IPE)**: Developed collaboratively between the VR counselor and the client. Sets the employment goal (graphic designer) and the services needed to get there.\n\n**College Disability Services**:\n- **Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act** and **Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA)**: Federal laws requiring colleges to provide reasonable accommodations to students with documented disabilities\n- The student must self-identify and provide documentation (medical records, functional limitation statements)\n- The college does NOT have to provide the same level of support as K-12 (no IEP equivalent). The student is responsible for self-advocacy.\n- Common accommodations: accessible housing, classroom accessibility, exam accommodations, note-taking assistance, priority registration\n\n**Financial Aid**:\n- FAFSA: File for federal financial aid. Disability does not directly increase aid, but medical expenses may affect the Expected Family Contribution.\n- Scholarships: Numerous disability-specific scholarships exist (National Federation of the Blind, Spina Bifida Association scholarships, Organization for Autism Research, etc.)\n- Spina Bifida Association of America: Scholarship program specifically for students with spina bifida\n\n**Health Insurance**:\n- If currently on parent's insurance: ACA allows dependent coverage until age 26\n- If on Medicaid: Check state rules for continued eligibility during college. Some states have Medicaid buy-in programs for working individuals with disabilities.\n- College student health insurance: Evaluate whether it covers specialty care, DME, and prescriptions needed. May need to maintain parent's insurance as primary.")
B.aYU=new A.c(u.j,"After your private conversation with the patient, you invite her parents into the room. Her father immediately says: \"She is not ready to go away to college. She has never been on her own. What if her shunt malfunctions? What if she gets a UTI and can't get to a doctor? I've been taking care of her since the day she was born. It's too dangerous.\" The patient looks at you with pleading eyes.")
B.aPr=new A.c("+ Navigate the tension between an overprotective parent and an adult patient seeking independence, while preserving the family relationship.",'**Step 1 \u2014 Acknowledge the Father\'s Love and Sacrifice**:\n"[Father\'s name], before I say anything else, I want to acknowledge what you have done for your daughter. Eighteen years of medical appointments, surgeries, therapy, advocating with schools and insurance companies, learning how to manage a catheter program, sitting in hospital waiting rooms \u2014 you have been an extraordinary father. The fact that she is sitting here today, accepted to college with a 3.5 GPA, driving a car, and advocating for herself, is a direct result of the foundation you built. You should be incredibly proud."\n\n**Step 2 \u2014 Validate the Fear**:\n"I also want to say that your concerns are medically legitimate. Shunt malfunctions happen. UTIs happen. Pressure injuries happen. These are real risks, and you are not being irrational. You know better than most parents what is at stake because you have lived it."\n\n**Step 3 \u2014 Reframe the Risk Equation**:\n"But I want to offer you a different way to think about this. The greatest risk to your daughter\'s health and well-being over the next 50 years is not a shunt malfunction or a UTI. Those are manageable medical events. The greatest risk is dependency \u2014 not developing the skills and confidence to manage her own health, navigate her own emergencies, and build her own life. Every young adult with spina bifida who thrives in adulthood went through a scary transition period where their parents had to step back and let them practice being independent. And the parents who did that \u2014 even though it was terrifying \u2014 gave their children the greatest gift after their initial care."\n\n**Step 4 \u2014 Give the Patient Her Voice**:\n"[Patient\'s name], I think your father needs to hear from you. Can you tell him what you told me \u2014 what you want and what you are prepared to do?"\n[Allow her to speak. She has demonstrated the maturity and self-advocacy to do this. Your role is to create the space.]\n\n**Step 5 \u2014 Present the Safety Net**:\n"Let me tell you what we are going to do to make this as safe as possible. We are going to establish her with a neurosurgeon, a urologist, and a PM&R physician near campus before she leaves. She will have a medical alert identification for her latex allergy and shunt. She will have a medical passport that any emergency room can reference. Her roommate will be educated about emergencies. She will have a supply stockpile. We will do a self-management skills assessment and fill any gaps before she goes. She will not be starting from zero \u2014 she will be starting with 18 years of training that you gave her."\n\n**Step 6 \u2014 Propose a Graduated Approach**:\n"Would it help if we thought about this in stages? What if she spends the summer practicing fully independent medical self-management at home \u2014 you step back completely from her catheter schedule, her bowel program, her skin checks, her medication management? You are there as a safety net but not doing it for her. If she manages the summer independently, that builds confidence for both of you. And when she goes to college, we set up weekly check-in calls between her and our clinic for the first semester, just to make sure everything is on track."\n\n**Step 7 \u2014 Address the Unspoken Loss**:\n"I also want to gently say this: letting go of a caregiving role you have held for 18 years is a loss. It changes your identity as a parent. That is real, and it is okay to grieve that even as you celebrate her independence. Some parents find it helpful to talk to a counselor during this transition \u2014 not because something is wrong, but because it is a big change for the whole family."\n\n**Step 8 \u2014 Unite the Family**:\n"You are both on the same team. You both want her to be safe, healthy, and happy. The question is not whether she goes to college \u2014 she has earned that. The question is how we prepare her and support her so that she succeeds. Let us build that plan together."\n\n**Key Principles**:\n- The patient is a legal adult at 18 \u2014 her decisions are hers. But destroying the family relationship in the process of asserting autonomy serves no one.\n- Honor the parent\'s history while redirecting their energy from protection to preparation\n- Frame independence as the ultimate outcome of good parenting, not a rejection of it\n- Provide concrete safety measures that address specific fears\n- Offer a graduated approach that respects both the patient\'s autonomy and the parent\'s need for reassurance\n- Acknowledge the parent\'s grief in the role transition')
B.aD3=s([B.aQ4,B.aN4,B.aUZ,B.b2F,B.aPE,B.aQC,B.b_H,B.aUe,B.aXi,B.aYU,B.aPr],t.E)
B.QP=new A.a5("peds-spina-bifida-transition","Spina Bifida \u2014 Transition to Adult Care",B.aD3,!1)
B.aN7=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 65-year-old male retired construction worker is referred to the outpatient pulmonary rehabilitation program by his pulmonologist. He has a 45-pack-year smoking history (quit 2 years ago but recently relapsed, currently smoking half a pack per day). He was diagnosed with COPD 8 years ago and classified as GOLD Stage III (severe). He was hospitalized 6 weeks ago for an acute exacerbation requiring a 5-day course of systemic corticosteroids and antibiotics. Since discharge, he reports persistent dyspnea with activities such as walking to the mailbox or climbing 5 stairs. He rates his dyspnea as MRC (Medical Research Council) Grade 3 \u2014 "stops for breath after walking about 100 yards or after a few minutes on level ground." He has become sedentary and anxious about leaving home, fearing another exacerbation. He uses tiotropium, fluticasone/salmeterol, and albuterol as rescue. He has home oxygen prescribed at 2 L/min with activity but admits he rarely uses it because it "makes him look sick."')
B.b_C=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","Key areas to assess before initiating pulmonary rehabilitation:\n- **COPD severity**: GOLD classification (based on post-bronchodilator FEV1), COPD Assessment Test (CAT) score, exacerbation history (2+ moderate or 1+ severe exacerbation in past year = high risk).\n- **Symptom burden**: MRC dyspnea scale, Borg dyspnea scale with activity, dyspnea on ADLs (bathing, dressing, eating).\n- **Exercise limitation**: Deconditioning vs. ventilatory limitation vs. cardiac limitation vs. musculoskeletal limitation. Often multifactorial in COPD.\n- **Comorbidities**: Screen for cardiovascular disease (CAD, heart failure, pulmonary hypertension), osteoporosis (steroid use, smoking), anxiety and depression (40% prevalence in COPD), skeletal muscle wasting/sarcopenia, malnutrition (BMI, albumin).\n- **Nutritional status**: Unintentional weight loss is a poor prognostic indicator in COPD. Calculate BMI and assess dietary intake. Cachexia is common in severe COPD.\n- **Smoking status**: Current use, readiness to quit, prior quit attempts, nicotine replacement therapy history. Active smoking is NOT a contraindication to pulmonary rehab but cessation counseling must be integrated.\n- **Oxygen use**: Compliance, prescribed flow rate, documented desaturation on prior testing. Address barriers to use.\n- **Vaccination status**: Influenza (annual), pneumococcal (PCV15 or PCV20, PPSV23), COVID-19, Tdap.\n- **Advance care planning**: Has the patient considered goals of care? COPD has a progressive, terminal trajectory \u2014 discuss early while functional.")
B.aPw=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 134/78 mmHg, HR 92 bpm, RR 22 at rest, SpO2 91% on room air at rest (94% on 2 L/min O2), Temp 36.9C, BMI 22.1 kg/m2.\n\n**General**: Thin male, barrel chest, using accessory muscles of respiration at rest (SCM, scalene prominence). Pursed-lip breathing spontaneously. Speaking in 4-5 word sentences before pausing to breathe.\n\n**Pulmonary**: Decreased breath sounds bilaterally with prolonged expiratory phase. Scattered expiratory wheezes. No crackles. Hyperresonant to percussion. Diaphragmatic excursion reduced bilaterally.\n\n**Cardiovascular**: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs. No peripheral edema. No elevated JVP.\n\n**Musculoskeletal**: Generalized muscle wasting, particularly quadriceps and deltoids. Grip strength reduced bilaterally (22 kg right, 20 kg left \u2014 below age/sex norms). Thoracic kyphosis present.\n\n**Pulmonary Function Tests** (recent):\n- FEV1: 38% predicted (GOLD Stage III \u2014 severe)\n- FEV1/FVC ratio: 0.52\n- DLCO: 45% predicted (reduced gas transfer)\n- Total lung capacity: 128% predicted (hyperinflation)\n- Residual volume: 180% predicted (air trapping)\n\n**6-Minute Walk Test (6MWT)**: Distance 245 meters (predicted approximately 550 meters for age/height = 45% predicted). SpO2 dropped from 92% to 84% on room air during the test. With 3 L/min O2, SpO2 maintained at 89%. Borg dyspnea 7/10 at end of test. Required 2 rest stops.")
B.aVg=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The 6MWT reveals significant exercise-induced desaturation and markedly reduced functional capacity. You need to evaluate his exercise capacity further and determine supplemental oxygen needs.")
B.b0V=new A.c("+ Discuss the evaluation of exercise capacity in COPD, contraindications to exercise in pulmonary rehabilitation, and the criteria for supplemental oxygen prescription.","**Exercise Capacity Evaluation**:\n\n1. **6-Minute Walk Test (6MWT)**: The most commonly used and practical test for pulmonary rehab. Measures submaximal exercise capacity. Normal distance is approximately 400-700 meters depending on age, sex, and height. A **minimum clinically important difference (MCID) is 30 meters** for COPD.\n\n2. **Incremental Shuttle Walk Test (ISWT)**: Externally paced, progressive test. Patient walks between two cones 10 meters apart at increasing speeds signaled by audio beeps. More reproducible than 6MWT and better correlates with VO2 max. MCID is 47.5 meters.\n\n3. **Cardiopulmonary Exercise Testing (CPET)**: Gold standard for differentiating ventilatory vs. cardiac vs. musculoskeletal limitation. Measures VO2 peak, anaerobic threshold, VE/VCO2 slope, heart rate response, oxygen pulse. Indicated when the cause of exercise limitation is unclear or when cardiac disease is suspected.\n\n**This patient's limitations are likely multifactorial**: severe ventilatory limitation (FEV1 38%), deconditioning (skeletal muscle wasting), exercise-induced hypoxemia (desaturation to 84%), and anxiety/fear-avoidance behavior.\n\n**Contraindications to Exercise in Pulmonary Rehab**:\n- **Absolute**: Unstable angina, recent MI (within 4 weeks), uncontrolled heart failure, active pulmonary embolism, resting SpO2 < 85% on supplemental O2, uncontrolled arrhythmia, severe pulmonary hypertension (mPAP > 55 mmHg), aortic dissection.\n- **Relative**: Orthopedic limitations that prevent exercise, uncontrolled hypertension (SBP > 200), significant cognitive impairment that limits participation.\n- **Active smoking is NOT a contraindication** \u2014 smokers benefit from pulmonary rehab and smoking cessation should be incorporated.\n\n**Supplemental Oxygen Criteria (CMS/Medicare)**:\n- **Continuous O2 (Group I)**: PaO2 <= 55 mmHg OR SpO2 <= 88% at rest on room air.\n- **Continuous O2 (Group II)**: PaO2 56-59 mmHg OR SpO2 89% WITH evidence of end-organ hypoxia (polycythemia with Hct > 56%, pulmonary hypertension on echo, cor pulmonale with peripheral edema).\n- **Ambulatory/Exercise O2**: Demonstrated desaturation to SpO2 <= 88% during ambulation or exercise with documented improvement with O2.\n\n**This patient qualifies for supplemental O2**: SpO2 91% at rest (borderline) but desaturates to 84% during exercise. He needs **3 L/min O2 with activity** (titrated to maintain SpO2 >= 88-90% during the 6MWT). He should also have a repeat resting ABG to determine if he meets criteria for continuous O2.\n\n**Oxygen titration during rehab**: Adjust flow rate to maintain SpO2 >= 88-90% during exercise. Use pulse oximetry continuously during exercise sessions. Reassess oxygen needs every 60-90 days and after exacerbations.")
B.aX1=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You enroll the patient in your pulmonary rehabilitation program. He asks what exactly pulmonary rehab involves and whether it will really make a difference given the severity of his disease.")
B.aOk=new A.c("+ Design a comprehensive pulmonary rehabilitation program for this patient including exercise prescription, education, and self-management components.","**Pulmonary Rehabilitation Program Structure**:\n- **Duration**: 12-16 weeks (minimum 24 sessions for Medicare coverage), typically 2-3 sessions per week.\n- **Session length**: 60-90 minutes per session.\n- **Setting**: Outpatient hospital-based or freestanding pulmonary rehab center.\n- **Team**: Pulmonologist (medical director), physiatrist or rehab physician, respiratory therapist, exercise physiologist, physical therapist, occupational therapist, dietitian, psychologist/social worker.\n\n**Exercise Prescription**:\n\n1. **Endurance (Aerobic) Training** (core component):\n- **Modalities**: Treadmill walking, stationary cycling (upright or recumbent), NuStep (combined upper and lower body).\n- **Intensity**: Start at 60-80% of peak work rate from ISWT or baseline 6MWT. Use modified Borg dyspnea scale (target 4-6 out of 10) for intensity guidance.\n- **Duration**: Start at 10-15 minutes continuous (or interval training if unable to sustain continuous), progress to 30-45 minutes.\n- **Interval training**: For patients unable to sustain continuous exercise. Alternate 1-2 minutes of higher intensity with 1-2 minutes of rest. Equally effective as continuous training in COPD.\n- **Supplemental O2**: 3 L/min via nasal cannula during exercise, titrate to maintain SpO2 >= 88%.\n\n2. **Resistance (Strength) Training**:\n- Address peripheral muscle weakness (quadriceps, deltoids, biceps) which is an independent predictor of mortality in COPD.\n- Start at 40-60% of 1-RM for upper and lower body. 2-3 sets of 8-12 repetitions. Progress by 5-10% when the patient can complete 3 sets of 12 comfortably.\n- Key muscle groups: quadriceps (leg press, knee extension), hamstrings, hip abductors, biceps, deltoids, latissimus dorsi.\n\n3. **Breathing Retraining**:\n- **Pursed-lip breathing (PLB)**: Inhale through nose for 2 counts, exhale through pursed lips for 4-6 counts. Reduces respiratory rate, increases tidal volume, prevents dynamic hyperinflation, and reduces dyspnea.\n- **Diaphragmatic breathing**: Place hand on abdomen, focus on diaphragmatic excursion. Limited evidence but may help some patients.\n- **Active expiratory technique**: During exercise, coordinate breathing with exertion \u2014 exhale during the effort phase.\n\n4. **Flexibility and Balance**:\n- Chest wall stretching (pectoralis, intercostal stretching) to improve thoracic mobility.\n- Balance training to reduce fall risk (COPD patients have 2-3x fall risk vs. age-matched controls due to deconditioning, medications, and hypoxia).\n\n**Education and Self-Management**:\n5. **Disease education**: COPD pathophysiology, medication technique (inhaler review \u2014 up to 50% of patients use inhalers incorrectly), oxygen therapy rationale.\n6. **Action plan for exacerbations**: Written plan for recognizing early symptoms (increased sputum, color change, increased dyspnea) and initiating antibiotics + corticosteroids per standing prescription.\n7. **Energy conservation and pacing**: OT-led sessions on ADL modification, activity pacing, work simplification.\n8. **Nutritional counseling**: Address sarcopenia with protein optimization (1.2-1.5 g/kg/day), caloric supplementation if underweight, small frequent meals (large meals worsen dyspnea by elevating the diaphragm).\n9. **Anxiety and depression management**: CBT, relaxation techniques, mindfulness. Consider pharmacotherapy (SSRI) if significant \u2014 avoid benzodiazepines (respiratory depression risk).\n\n**Expected Outcomes of Pulmonary Rehab** (to share with patient):\n- Reduced dyspnea (strongest evidence)\n- Improved exercise capacity (30-50 meter improvement in 6MWD)\n- Improved quality of life (SGRQ, CAT scores)\n- Reduced hospital readmissions and emergency visits\n- Improved depression and anxiety\n- Improved survival when combined with optimal medical therapy\n- **Pulmonary rehab does NOT improve FEV1** \u2014 but it dramatically improves the patient's experience of living with COPD.")
B.b2P=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient's wife calls your office asking about insurance coverage for pulmonary rehab. She is also concerned because he has started smoking again and wants to know what resources are available.")
B.b_R=new A.c("+ Discuss Medicare coverage criteria for pulmonary rehabilitation and evidence-based smoking cessation strategies.",'**Medicare Coverage for Pulmonary Rehabilitation**:\n\n1. **Qualifying diagnoses** (CMS-approved):\n- Moderate to very severe COPD (GOLD II-IV, FEV1 < 80% predicted) \u2014 this patient qualifies\n- Asthma (under specific criteria)\n- Interstitial lung disease\n- Other chronic lung diseases causing dyspnea\n\n2. **Coverage specifics**:\n- Medicare Part B covers up to **36 sessions** of pulmonary rehab.\n- An additional **36 sessions** (total 72) may be approved if the physician documents continued medical necessity and functional improvement.\n- Sessions must be supervised by a physician and provided by qualified staff.\n- Requires a physician prescription and documented face-to-face encounter.\n- Patient pays the standard 20% coinsurance (or supplemental insurance covers it).\n- Sessions must include: exercise training, education, psychosocial assessment, outcomes assessment.\n\n3. **Documentation requirements**: Physician must document: diagnosis with supporting PFTs, treatment goals, individualized plan, progress notes after each session or at minimum every 30 days.\n\n4. **Maintenance programs**: After completing formal pulmonary rehab, maintenance exercise is critical but NOT covered by Medicare. Patients must self-fund gym memberships or home equipment. This is a significant barrier.\n\n**Smoking Cessation Strategies**:\n\n1. **Brief Intervention (5 A\'s framework)**:\n- **Ask**: Document smoking status at every visit.\n- **Advise**: "Quitting smoking is the single most important thing you can do for your lungs. I strongly advise you to quit."\n- **Assess**: Readiness to quit (Prochaska stages of change). This patient relapsed \u2014 assess triggers and barriers.\n- **Assist**: Provide resources (see below).\n- **Arrange**: Follow-up within 1-2 weeks of quit date.\n\n2. **Pharmacotherapy** (first-line):\n- **Combination nicotine replacement therapy (NRT)**: Long-acting (patch \u2014 21 mg/day for 4 weeks, then taper) PLUS short-acting (nicotine gum 2 mg or lozenge PRN for cravings). Combination NRT is more effective than single-agent.\n- **Varenicline (Chantix)**: Most effective single agent. Partial nicotine receptor agonist. Start 0.5 mg daily x 3 days, then 0.5 mg BID x 4 days, then 1 mg BID for 12 weeks. Start 1-2 weeks before quit date.\n- **Bupropion SR**: 150 mg daily x 3 days, then 150 mg BID for 12 weeks. Especially useful if comorbid depression. Can be combined with NRT.\n\n3. **Behavioral support**:\n- Individual or group counseling (even brief 10-minute counseling increases quit rates).\n- **Quitline**: 1-800-QUIT-NOW (free coaching, NRT samples).\n- **SmokeFreeTXT**: Text-based support program.\n- Pulmonary rehab itself provides a supportive environment for cessation.\n\n4. **Medicare coverage for cessation**: Medicare covers 2 quit attempts per year, each including up to 4 counseling sessions (8 total sessions/year). Pharmacotherapy (varenicline, bupropion, NRT) covered under Part D.\n\n5. **Motivational interviewing approach for relapse**: "I know quitting is hard \u2014 the average person tries 7-10 times before quitting for good. The fact that you quit for 2 years tells me you can do it. That was not a failure; it was practice. What do you think triggered the relapse, and how can we plan for that trigger next time?"')
B.aTm=new A.c(u.j,"During a pulmonary rehab session, you observe the patient coughing and smelling of cigarette smoke. When you ask about it, he says: \"I know, I know. I tried to quit but I just can't. My wife nags me about it every day and it just makes me want to smoke more. What's the point anyway \u2014 my lungs are already destroyed.\"")
B.aOv=new A.c("+ Demonstrate motivational interviewing techniques for smoking cessation in a patient who has relapsed and feels hopeless.",'I would use motivational interviewing principles \u2014 express empathy, develop discrepancy, roll with resistance, and support self-efficacy:\n\n**1. Express empathy and avoid confrontation**:\n"I am not here to lecture you or nag you \u2014 you get enough of that at home. I can see you are frustrated, and I want you to know that I am not judging you. Quitting smoking is one of the hardest things a person can do, and the fact that you were able to quit for two years tells me something important about you \u2014 you have done it before."\n\n**2. Roll with resistance (do NOT argue)**:\n"You said what is the point because your lungs are already damaged. I understand why you might feel that way. Can I share some information with you that might change that perspective, or would you rather not hear it right now?"\n*Wait for permission \u2014 this respects autonomy.*\n\n**3. Develop discrepancy (if he agrees to hear it)**:\n"Here is something I want you to consider. You are right that we cannot undo the damage that has been done. But continuing to smoke accelerates the decline of your remaining lung function by about 50-60 mL of FEV1 per year, compared to about 30 mL per year if you quit. That difference is the difference between being independent at home for years versus needing more and more oxygen and potentially ending up in the hospital again. Quitting now still makes a meaningful difference for your future."\n\n**4. Explore ambivalence**:\n"It sounds like part of you wants to quit \u2014 otherwise you would not have tried or felt guilty about it. What are the things you liked about not smoking during those two years?"\n*Listen actively. Reflect back what he says.*\n\n**5. Support self-efficacy**:\n"Two years is a long time to be smoke-free. That is not a failure \u2014 that is a success. What we need to figure out is what triggered the relapse and how to prepare you for that trigger next time. Research shows that the average person quits 7-10 times before it sticks permanently. You are not starting over; you are continuing a process."\n\n**6. Offer concrete help without pressure**:\n"When you are ready \u2014 and only when you are ready \u2014 I can offer you tools that make quitting significantly easier. There are medications like varenicline that reduce cravings and make cigarettes less satisfying. We can also connect you with a free quit coach through the national quitline. But this is your decision. I will support you whether you quit today, next month, or next year. My job is to be honest with you and to help you when you are ready."\n\n**7. Address the family dynamic**:\n"You mentioned your wife nagging you. That usually comes from a place of love and fear \u2014 she is scared of losing you. But I understand that pressure can backfire. Would it be helpful if I spoke with her about how to be supportive without adding pressure? Sometimes when we reframe the approach, it takes the tension out of it."\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Motivational interviewing (OARS \u2014 open questions, affirmations, reflections, summaries), rolling with resistance instead of confronting, respecting patient autonomy, accurate medical information delivered without judgment, practical support resources.')
B.aE7=s([B.aN7,B.b_C,B.aPw,B.aVg,B.b0V,B.aX1,B.aOk,B.b2P,B.b_R,B.aTm,B.aOv],t.E)
B.R1=new A.a5("cardiopulm-copd-rehab","Pulmonary Rehabilitation (COPD)",B.aE7,!1)
B.aUn=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 58-year-old male is referred to outpatient cardiac rehabilitation (Phase II) 6 weeks after coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG, 3-vessel). His pre-operative course was significant for a non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI) with cardiac catheterization revealing severe three-vessel coronary artery disease and an ejection fraction (EF) of 35% on echocardiogram, consistent with ischemic cardiomyopathy and NYHA Class III heart failure. His post-surgical course was complicated by atrial fibrillation (now rate-controlled on amiodarone) and a left pleural effusion requiring thoracentesis. He was discharged home 10 days post-operatively. He now reports exertional dyspnea walking more than one block, fatigue climbing stairs, difficulty sleeping flat (uses 2 pillows), and 5-pound weight gain over the past week. He admits to being terrified of exercising, stating: "I don\'t want to have another heart attack." His medications include metoprolol, lisinopril, spironolactone, furosemide, atorvastatin, aspirin, amiodarone, and apixaban.')
B.aYW=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","Key areas to assess before initiating Phase II cardiac rehabilitation:\n- **Cardiac history detail**: Type and date of CABG, number of grafts, conduits used (LIMA, SVG), pre-operative EF, post-operative complications, current medications and optimization status.\n- **Heart failure management**: Daily weights, sodium and fluid restriction adherence, volume status assessment. His 5-pound weight gain and orthopnea suggest volume overload requiring diuretic adjustment.\n- **Symptom assessment**: NYHA functional classification, Canadian Cardiovascular Society angina class, dyspnea on exertion threshold, orthopnea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea, lower extremity edema.\n- **Risk stratification**: AHA risk class for cardiac rehab (low, moderate, high risk). EF < 40% and NYHA Class III place him in the **high-risk** category requiring telemetry monitoring during rehab sessions.\n- **Surgical wound status**: Sternal incision healing, sternal precautions (typically no lifting > 10 lbs for 8-12 weeks, no driving for 4-6 weeks), saphenous vein harvest site healing.\n- **Comorbidities**: Diabetes, hypertension, hyperlipidemia, peripheral arterial disease, renal function (CKD from cardiorenal syndrome), depression (30-40% post-CABG).\n- **Psychosocial**: Fear of exercise, anxiety, depression screening (PHQ-9), caregiver support, return-to-work timeline, sexual activity counseling.\n- **Advance care planning**: Given EF 35%, discuss goals of care, advance directives, and code status if not already addressed.")
B.b0w=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 118/72 mmHg, HR 72 bpm (regular on amiodarone), RR 18, SpO2 95% on room air, Weight 98 kg (up from 93 kg at discharge = +5 kg), BMI 31.2.\n\n**General**: Mildly dyspneic with conversation. Deconditioned appearance. Speaking in full sentences but pauses occasionally.\n\n**Cardiovascular**: Regular rate and rhythm. S1, S2 present. S3 gallop heard at apex (indicative of volume overload/heart failure). Grade 2/6 systolic murmur at apex (mitral regurgitation, secondary to LV dilation). JVP elevated to 10 cm.\n\n**Pulmonary**: Bibasilar crackles in the lower one-third bilaterally. Decreased breath sounds at the left base (residual effusion vs. atelectasis). No wheezes.\n\n**Extremities**: Bilateral pitting edema to mid-shin (2+). Saphenous vein harvest site on the left leg well-healed with mild residual edema.\n\n**Sternal wound**: Midline sternotomy incision well-healed. No sternal click, tenderness, or wound drainage. Mild sternal discomfort with palpation.\n\n**Musculoskeletal**: Generalized deconditioning. Quadriceps atrophy bilaterally. Grip strength reduced.\n\n**Pre-Rehab Exercise Testing** (Symptom-limited graded exercise test performed at cardiac rehab intake):\n- Protocol: Modified Bruce\n- Duration: 5 minutes 30 seconds (Stage 2)\n- Peak HR: 108 bpm (73% age-predicted max)\n- Peak BP: 152/82 mmHg\n- Reason for termination: Dyspnea (Borg 8/10) and fatigue\n- ECG: Sinus rhythm, no ST changes, no arrhythmias\n- Estimated METs: 4.2\n- No angina")
B.b_l=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","This patient presents with decompensated heart failure (volume overload) at his cardiac rehab intake visit. His medical issues must be optimized before beginning the exercise program.")
B.aUQ=new A.c("+ Perform risk stratification for cardiac rehabilitation and identify contraindications to initiating exercise. How do you manage his acute volume overload?","**AHA Risk Stratification for Cardiac Rehabilitation**:\n\nThis patient is **HIGH RISK** based on the following:\n- EF < 40% (EF 35%)\n- NYHA Class III heart failure\n- Post-CABG with recent atrial fibrillation\n- Abnormal hemodynamics with exercise (limited to 4.2 METs)\n- Current volume overload\n\n**High-risk patients require**:\n- **Continuous ECG telemetry monitoring** during all exercise sessions (minimum 18 sessions or until stable)\n- **Physician availability** on-site or immediately accessible\n- **Blood pressure and SpO2 monitoring** before, during, and after exercise\n- **Daily weight check** before each session (>2 lb gain in 24 hours or >5 lbs in a week warrants medical evaluation)\n\n**Contraindications to Exercise** (must be resolved before starting):\n- **Absolute contraindications**: Unstable angina, decompensated heart failure (THIS PATIENT \u2014 currently volume overloaded), acute PE, acute myocarditis/pericarditis, symptomatic severe aortic stenosis, uncontrolled arrhythmia, acute aortic dissection, resting SBP > 200 or DBP > 110.\n- **Relative contraindications**: Moderate valvular stenosis, known electrolyte abnormalities, uncontrolled metabolic disease, resting HR > 120 bpm.\n\n**Management of Acute Volume Overload** (before starting rehab):\n1. **Increase furosemide dose**: Double the current dose (e.g., furosemide 40 mg to 80 mg daily, or add an afternoon dose). Target net negative fluid balance of 1-2 liters/day until euvolemic.\n2. **Sodium restriction**: Reinforce < 2 grams/day. Dietary counseling referral.\n3. **Fluid restriction**: 1.5-2 liters/day for patients with hyponatremia or persistent congestion.\n4. **Daily weights**: Patient must weigh himself every morning before eating, after voiding, in the same clothing. Report weight gain > 2 lbs in 24 hours.\n5. **Electrolyte monitoring**: Check BMP within 48-72 hours of diuretic adjustment (risk of hypokalemia, hyponatremia, worsening renal function with aggressive diuresis).\n6. **Contact cardiologist/heart failure team**: Communicate the volume overload findings. May need IV diuretics if oral adjustment fails.\n7. **Delay exercise initiation** until volume overload is resolved (typically 1-2 weeks with appropriate diuretic management). He can attend educational sessions during this time.\n\n**Guideline-Directed Medical Therapy (GDMT) Review**:\nEnsure the patient is on optimal heart failure medications:\n- Beta-blocker (metoprolol \u2014 confirm target dose: metoprolol succinate 200 mg daily)\n- ACEi/ARB or ARNI (lisinopril \u2014 consider switching to sacubitril/valsartan for EF <= 40% per ACC/AHA guidelines)\n- Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist (spironolactone \u2014 already on)\n- SGLT2 inhibitor (empagliflozin or dapagliflozin \u2014 **should be added** per current guidelines for HFrEF regardless of diabetes status)\n- Diuretic for volume management\n- Consider ICD evaluation if EF remains <= 35% after 3 months of GDMT (primary prevention of sudden cardiac death)")
B.aXx=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","After 2 weeks of diuretic optimization, the patient is euvolemic (weight 93 kg, no edema, crackles resolved, JVP normalized). He is now medically cleared to begin the exercise program.")
B.aVp=new A.c("+ Design an evidence-based Phase II cardiac rehabilitation exercise prescription for this patient with HFrEF, including intensity, duration, progression, and safety parameters.",'**Exercise Prescription for Phase II Cardiac Rehabilitation (HFrEF)**:\n\n**Aerobic Training**:\n1. **Frequency**: 3 sessions/week in supervised setting. Encourage 2 additional low-intensity home sessions (walking) as tolerated.\n\n2. **Intensity** \u2014 Use multiple methods for safety:\n- **Heart Rate Reserve (HRR/Karvonen method)**: Start at 40-60% HRR, progress to 60-80% HRR.\n  - HRR = (Peak HR on GXT - Resting HR) x target % + Resting HR\n  - His values: (108 - 72) x 0.40 + 72 = 86 bpm (low end), x 0.60 + 72 = 94 bpm (high end)\n  - **Starting target HR range: 86-94 bpm**\n- **RPE (Rating of Perceived Exertion)**: Target Borg 11-14 ("fairly light" to "somewhat hard") on the 6-20 scale.\n- **METs**: Start at 2-3 METs (slow walking, stationary cycling at low resistance), progress toward his peak of 4.2 METs and beyond.\n- **Talk test**: Should be able to carry on a conversation during exercise. If unable to speak in sentences, intensity is too high.\n\n3. **Duration**: Start with 15-20 minutes of continuous aerobic exercise (or interval training if unable to sustain). Progress by 5 minutes per session every 1-2 weeks to a goal of 30-45 minutes.\n\n4. **Modalities**: Treadmill walking (preferred \u2014 most functional), recumbent bicycle (easier on sternum), arm ergometer (after sternal precautions cleared, typically 8-12 weeks), NuStep.\n\n5. **Warm-up and cool-down**: 5-10 minutes each. Gradual HR elevation and reduction. Critical in heart failure to avoid orthostatic hypotension (beta-blocker and diuretic effects).\n\n**Resistance Training** (begin after 3-4 weeks of aerobic training and sternal clearance):\n- Light resistance: 40-60% of 1-RM, 10-15 repetitions, 1-2 sets.\n- Focus on major muscle groups: quadriceps, hamstrings, biceps, latissimus.\n- **Avoid Valsalva maneuver** \u2014 exhale during exertion, inhale during relaxation.\n- Sternal precautions must be observed: no heavy lifting (>10 lbs), no chest fly or bench press until full sternal healing confirmed (typically 8-12 weeks).\n\n**Safety Parameters \u2014 When to STOP Exercise**:\n- SBP drop > 10 mmHg from baseline with increasing workload\n- SBP > 200 mmHg or DBP > 110 mmHg\n- New ST depression > 1 mm or ST elevation > 1 mm\n- New arrhythmia (VT, rapid AF, new heart block)\n- SpO2 < 88%\n- Borg dyspnea or angina > 6/10\n- Dizziness, pallor, cyanosis, confusion\n- Weight gain > 2 lbs from previous session\n\n**Progression Strategy**:\n- Weeks 1-4: Low intensity (40-50% HRR), 15-20 minutes, focus on building confidence\n- Weeks 5-8: Moderate intensity (50-60% HRR), 20-30 minutes, add resistance training\n- Weeks 9-12: Progress toward 60-70% HRR, 30-40 minutes, increase resistance\n- Weeks 13-36: Full program, target 30-45 minutes at 60-80% HRR\n\n**Evidence for Cardiac Rehab in Heart Failure** (HF-ACTION trial):\n- Modest reduction in all-cause mortality and hospitalization (11% reduction)\n- Significant improvement in quality of life, functional capacity, and depression\n- Safe \u2014 exercise training in HFrEF does NOT increase adverse events\n- The benefit is dose-dependent: higher adherence = better outcomes')
B.b3v=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient is nearing completion of his 36-session Phase II cardiac rehabilitation program. His wife asks about what happens next and whether insurance will cover ongoing exercise.")
B.aSt=new A.c("+ Discuss the Phase II to Phase III cardiac rehab transition, insurance coverage, and strategies for long-term exercise maintenance.","**Phases of Cardiac Rehabilitation**:\n\n1. **Phase I (Inpatient)**: Begins during hospitalization. Early mobilization, patient education, risk factor counseling. Typically 3-7 days.\n\n2. **Phase II (Outpatient, Supervised)**: This is what the patient is completing. 36 sessions (up to 72 with documented medical necessity). ECG-monitored, supervised exercise. Covered by Medicare Part B (80% coverage after deductible).\n\n3. **Phase III (Maintenance/Community-Based)**: Ongoing independent exercise program. Typically at a gym, community center, or home. May or may not include periodic monitoring. **NOT covered by Medicare or most commercial insurance** \u2014 this is a major barrier.\n\n4. **Phase IV (Lifelong Maintenance)**: Self-directed, independent exercise. No supervision.\n\n**Insurance Coverage Details**:\n- **Medicare Part B**: Covers Phase II cardiac rehab for qualifying diagnoses (post-MI, post-CABG, stable angina, heart failure with EF <= 35%, post-heart transplant, post-valve repair/replacement). Patient pays 20% coinsurance.\n- **36 initial sessions**: Approved with physician referral and documented plan.\n- **Additional 36 sessions (up to 72 total)**: May be approved with documentation of continued medical necessity (ongoing symptoms, need for monitoring, continued functional improvement).\n- **Intensive Cardiac Rehab (ICR)**: 72 sessions with enhanced lifestyle modification components. Covered by Medicare for eligible programs (Pritikin, Ornish, Benson-Henry).\n\n**Transition to Phase III \u2014 Key Strategies**:\n\n1. **Graduated independence**: In the final weeks of Phase II, reduce monitoring frequency. Teach the patient to self-monitor (HR by palpation or wearable device, RPE, symptom awareness, daily weights).\n\n2. **Written home exercise prescription**: Provide a detailed, individualized exercise plan including:\n- Type: Walking 30-45 minutes (or cycling)\n- Intensity: HR range 86-108 bpm, RPE 11-14\n- Frequency: 5 days/week aerobic, 2-3 days/week resistance\n- Progression: Increase duration by 5 minutes every 2 weeks\n\n3. **Community resources**: Connect with YMCA cardiac wellness programs (many offer reduced rates for cardiac rehab graduates), Silver Sneakers (Medicare-sponsored gym access \u2014 check eligibility), local heart failure support groups.\n\n4. **Telehealth/remote monitoring**: Emerging evidence supports home-based cardiac rehab with remote ECG monitoring and video check-ins. May improve access for patients in rural areas.\n\n5. **Ongoing medical follow-up**: Quarterly visits with cardiology/heart failure clinic. Repeat echocardiogram at 3-6 months to reassess EF (if EF improves to > 35%, may not need ICD). Annual exercise stress testing to guide prescription updates.\n\n6. **Red flag education for the patient and family**: Seek immediate care for: new chest pain, increasing dyspnea, rapid weight gain (>2 lbs/day), new edema, syncope, palpitations, ICD discharge (if implanted).")
B.b_a=new A.c(u.j,"On his first day of supervised exercise in cardiac rehab, the patient steps onto the treadmill and immediately becomes visibly anxious. He grips the handrails tightly, his heart rate rises to 105 before starting, and he says: \"I can't do this. What if my heart can't handle it? I don't want to die on a treadmill. My friend had a heart attack at the gym.\"")
B.aXt=new A.c("+ Demonstrate how you would address this patient's exercise-related fear and cardiac anxiety to enable participation in rehabilitation.",'I would immediately recognize this as cardiac anxiety (kinesiophobia/fear-avoidance specific to cardiac patients), which is extremely common post-CABG and a major barrier to rehabilitation adherence:\n\n**1. Pause and create safety \u2014 do not push through**:\n"Let us step off the treadmill for a moment. There is no rush, and nothing bad is happening right now. You are safe. Let us sit down and talk."\n*Allow his heart rate to come down. Offer water.*\n\n**2. Validate the fear**:\n"What you are feeling is completely normal, and I want you to know that. Almost every patient I see after heart surgery feels the same way when they first start exercising. Your body went through something major, and your mind is trying to protect you. That fear is your brain doing its job \u2014 but in this case, it is giving you a false alarm."\n\n**3. Provide concrete safety reassurance**:\n"I want to explain what makes this different from exercising on your own. Right now, you are wearing a heart monitor that lets us see your heart rhythm in real time on that screen over there. If anything unusual happens \u2014 and it almost never does \u2014 we will know before you do. There is a physician available, a defibrillator on the wall, and every person in this room is trained in advanced cardiac life support. You are literally in the safest place in the world to exercise."\n\n**4. Reframe exercise as medicine, not risk**:\n"Here is the part that might surprise you: NOT exercising is actually more dangerous for your heart than exercising. Your heart is a muscle, and like any muscle, it gets weaker without use. The research on cardiac rehab after CABG is very strong \u2014 patients who complete the program have fewer heart attacks, fewer hospital visits, and live longer than those who do not. Exercise is not going to hurt your heart. It is going to help it heal."\n\n**5. Start small and give him control**:\n"Here is what I propose: let us start with just walking on the treadmill at a very slow speed \u2014 like walking through your living room. No incline. Just 5 minutes. You can stop at any time. You are in control. If you feel okay after 5 minutes, we can keep going. If not, we stop and try again next time. There is no failure here \u2014 just showing up is a victory."\n\n**6. Address the friend\'s story**:\n"I am sorry about your friend. That must have been frightening and I can see why it is on your mind. What I can tell you is that the risk of a cardiac event during supervised cardiac rehabilitation is extremely low \u2014 approximately 1 event per 117,000 patient-hours of exercise. You are far safer exercising here under monitoring than you are being sedentary at home."\n\n**7. Build confidence over time**:\n"Each session, we will gradually increase the intensity. By the end of this program, most patients tell me they cannot believe they were ever afraid. You are going to get stronger. And I will be right here with you every step of the way."\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Acute anxiety management, validation, concrete safety data, patient education, patient autonomy/control, graduated exposure (behavioral technique), building self-efficacy, therapeutic alliance.')
B.aG7=s([B.aUn,B.aYW,B.b0w,B.b_l,B.aUQ,B.aXx,B.aVp,B.b3v,B.aSt,B.b_a,B.aXt],t.E)
B.Qj=new A.a5("cardiopulm-cardiac-rehab","Heart Failure & Cardiac Rehab (Phase II)",B.aG7,!1)
B.aXR=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 48-year-old female elementary school teacher presents to the PM&R clinic 4 months after hospitalization for severe COVID-19 pneumonia. She was hospitalized for 3 weeks, including 5 days in the ICU requiring high-flow nasal cannula (peak FiO2 80%) but was not intubated. Since discharge, she reports persistent debilitating fatigue that does not improve with rest, exertional dyspnea with activities she previously performed easily (walking up one flight of stairs, carrying groceries), cognitive difficulties described as "brain fog" (difficulty concentrating, word-finding problems, inability to multitask), widespread myalgias, intermittent palpitations with positional changes, and disrupted sleep. She attempted to return to teaching 8 weeks after discharge but lasted only 3 days before her symptoms became unmanageable. She is currently on short-term disability leave. She was previously healthy with no chronic medical conditions and ran 3-4 miles three times weekly.')
B.aTn=new A.c("History & Systems Review:",'Key areas to assess in suspected post-COVID condition (Long COVID):\n- **Timeline and symptoms**: WHO defines post-COVID condition as symptoms persisting or developing 3+ months after confirmed or probable SARS-CoV-2 infection, lasting at least 2 months, not explained by an alternative diagnosis. Most common symptoms: fatigue (58%), cognitive dysfunction (32%), dyspnea (26%), pain (20%).\n- **Post-Exertional Malaise (PEM)**: Hallmark feature. Worsening of symptoms 12-72 hours after physical or cognitive exertion that is disproportionate to the effort. Ask specifically: "Do your symptoms get worse a day or two after you try to push through activity?" This is critical because it changes the exercise approach.\n- **Autonomic dysfunction**: Orthostatic intolerance, postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome (POTS), palpitations, temperature dysregulation, GI dysmotility. Ask about dizziness with standing, racing heart, exercise intolerance out of proportion to deconditioning.\n- **Cognitive assessment**: Memory complaints, executive dysfunction, processing speed, attention. Screen with Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA).\n- **Psychiatric screening**: Depression (PHQ-9), anxiety (GAD-7), PTSD (PCL-5 \u2014 ICU stay is a risk factor for PTSD). Sleep quality assessment.\n- **Functional impact**: Prior level of function vs. current. Use validated tools: Post-COVID Functional Status Scale, Functional Assessment of Chronic Illness Therapy-Fatigue (FACIT-F).\n- **Prior COVID treatment**: Severity of illness, ICU stay, ventilatory support, medications received (steroids, antivirals, immunomodulators), vaccination status at time of infection.\n- **Comorbidities**: While she was previously healthy, new-onset conditions can emerge after COVID (new diabetes, thyroid dysfunction, autoimmune conditions).')
B.aP1=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**:\n- Supine: BP 118/74 mmHg, HR 72 bpm\n- Standing (3 minutes): BP 112/78 mmHg, HR 108 bpm (increase of 36 bpm) \u2014 **positive for POTS criteria** (HR increase >= 30 bpm within 10 minutes of standing without orthostatic hypotension)\n- RR 16, SpO2 98% on room air at rest, Temp 36.8C, BMI 24.5.\n\n**General**: Well-appearing female who appears fatigued. Affect is flat. No acute distress at rest.\n\n**Pulmonary**: Clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, crackles, or diminished breath sounds. Chest X-ray from 2 weeks ago: resolved bilateral infiltrates, no residual consolidation.\n\n**Cardiovascular**: Regular rate and rhythm at rest. No murmurs. No peripheral edema. Tachycardic on standing.\n\n**Neurologic**: Alert and oriented x 4. Cranial nerves intact. Strength 5/5 in all extremities. Sensation intact. Reflexes 2+ and symmetric. No focal deficits. Coordination intact.\n\n**Cognitive Screen (MoCA)**: Score 24/30 (normal >= 26). Lost points on: delayed recall (2/5), serial 7s (attention), and verbal fluency. These deficits are consistent with post-COVID cognitive impairment.\n\n**6-Minute Walk Test**: Distance 380 meters (predicted approximately 550 meters = 69% predicted). SpO2 maintained >= 95% throughout. HR rose to 132 bpm within 2 minutes (exaggerated chronotropic response). Borg dyspnea 6/10, fatigue 7/10 at completion. Patient reported significant symptom exacerbation the following day (post-exertional malaise confirmed).\n\n**Musculoskeletal**: Diffuse myalgias with palpation but no focal tenderness, swelling, or weakness. Full ROM in all joints.")
B.b2i=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","This patient presents with a classic post-COVID syndrome constellation: fatigue with post-exertional malaise, cognitive dysfunction, autonomic dysfunction (POTS), and exertional dyspnea. A systematic workup is needed to exclude treatable causes.")
B.b_t=new A.c("+ Define the diagnostic criteria for post-COVID condition, outline your differential diagnosis, and describe the targeted workup including cardiac evaluation.","**Post-COVID Condition (WHO Definition, October 2021)**:\n- Occurs in individuals with a history of probable or confirmed SARS-CoV-2 infection\n- Usually 3 months from onset of COVID-19\n- Symptoms last at least 2 months\n- Cannot be explained by an alternative diagnosis\n- Symptoms may be new onset after initial recovery or persist from acute illness\n- May fluctuate or relapse over time\n\n**Differential Diagnosis** (must exclude before attributing entirely to post-COVID):\n\n1. **Cardiac causes of dyspnea/exercise intolerance**:\n- Myocarditis (post-COVID myocardial inflammation \u2014 reported in 1-4% of hospitalized patients)\n- Pericarditis\n- Pulmonary embolism (COVID increases VTE risk)\n- New-onset heart failure\n\n2. **Pulmonary causes**:\n- Organizing pneumonia\n- Pulmonary fibrosis (post-ARDS)\n- Pulmonary embolism\n- Reactive airway disease\n\n3. **Endocrine/metabolic**:\n- New-onset hypothyroidism or hyperthyroidism (thyroiditis post-COVID is described)\n- Adrenal insufficiency\n- New-onset diabetes mellitus\n- Anemia\n\n4. **Other**:\n- Deconditioning alone (3 weeks of hospitalization causes significant muscle and cardiovascular deconditioning)\n- Depression/anxiety (can cause fatigue, cognitive complaints, and somatic symptoms)\n- Obstructive sleep apnea (can worsen post-COVID fatigue)\n\n**Targeted Workup**:\n\n1. **Laboratory studies**:\n- CBC (anemia), CMP (renal/hepatic function), TSH/free T4 (thyroid), HbA1c (new diabetes), CRP/ESR (residual inflammation), ferritin (elevated in post-COVID, also iron status), D-dimer (if PE suspected), BNP/NT-proBNP (heart failure), cortisol (if adrenal insufficiency suspected)\n\n2. **Cardiac workup** (critical given dyspnea and tachycardia):\n- **ECG**: Assess for arrhythmia, ST changes, prolonged QTc\n- **Echocardiogram**: Assess EF, wall motion abnormalities, pericardial effusion, right heart strain (suggestive of PE or pulmonary hypertension)\n- **Cardiac MRI**: If myocarditis suspected (elevated troponin, new wall motion abnormality, chest pain). Can detect myocardial inflammation, edema, and fibrosis. Gold standard for post-COVID myocarditis diagnosis.\n- **Holter monitor**: If palpitations reported \u2014 evaluate for arrhythmia burden\n\n3. **Pulmonary workup**:\n- **Pulmonary function tests (PFTs)**: Spirometry, DLCO. Reduced DLCO is the most common PFT abnormality post-COVID (impaired gas transfer).\n- **CT chest** (if PFTs abnormal): Evaluate for organizing pneumonia, pulmonary fibrosis, PE\n- **CT pulmonary angiography**: If D-dimer elevated or high clinical suspicion for PE\n\n4. **Autonomic testing**:\n- **Active standing test** (done \u2014 positive for POTS)\n- **Tilt-table test**: Confirmatory if needed. Monitors HR and BP during 45-degree head-up tilt for 10-45 minutes.\n\n5. **Cognitive testing**:\n- **Neuropsychological testing**: Formal evaluation if MoCA is abnormal or cognitive complaints are significantly impacting function. Can quantify deficits in processing speed, attention, memory, and executive function.")
B.aX2=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Workup returns: ECG normal sinus rhythm, echocardiogram shows normal EF (60%) with no wall motion abnormalities, PFTs show mild restrictive pattern with DLCO at 72% predicted, D-dimer normal, labs normal except ferritin mildly elevated at 280 and CRP 4.2 (mildly elevated). No PE, no myocarditis. The picture is consistent with post-COVID syndrome with autonomic dysfunction (POTS), post-exertional malaise, cognitive impairment, and residual pulmonary limitation.")
B.aPz=new A.c("+ Design a comprehensive rehabilitation program for this patient, addressing the unique challenges of post-exertional malaise, POTS management, and cognitive rehabilitation.",'**Critical Principle**: Traditional graded exercise therapy (progressive overload) can be HARMFUL in patients with post-exertional malaise. The rehabilitation approach must be modified to respect the PEM threshold.\n\n**Exercise Management \u2014 Pacing-Based Approach**:\n\n1. **Identify the PEM threshold**: Start with very low-intensity activity and monitor for symptom exacerbation 24-48 hours later. Her 6MWT triggered PEM the next day, so her current threshold is below that effort level.\n\n2. **Activity pacing ("stop-rest-pace" strategy)**:\n- The "energy envelope" concept: total daily activity must stay within 70-80% of the PEM threshold.\n- Alternate activity periods (15-20 minutes) with mandatory rest periods (10-15 minutes).\n- Use a heart rate monitor to stay below the PEM trigger HR. Starting target: stay below **HR 110 bpm** during activity (approximately 15-20 bpm below the HR where she developed PEM symptoms).\n\n3. **Exercise prescription (START LOW, GO SLOW)**:\n- **Phase 1 (Weeks 1-4)**: Supine or recumbent exercises only (recumbent bike, supine exercises, aquatic therapy). 5-10 minutes per session, 3-4 days/week. Intensity: RPE 2-3/10 (very light). NO post-exercise symptom exacerbation is the goal.\n- **Phase 2 (Weeks 5-8)**: If Phase 1 tolerated without PEM, gradually increase to 15-20 minutes. Begin upright activities (seated bike, slow walking).\n- **Phase 3 (Weeks 9-12+)**: Gradual transition to upright exercise. Increase duration before intensity. Goal: 20-30 minutes of low-moderate activity.\n- **Key rule**: If PEM occurs, reduce activity by 50% for the next week, then reattempt previous level. Never push through a PEM episode.\n\n**POTS/Dysautonomia Management**:\n\n1. **Non-pharmacologic**:\n- Increase fluid intake to 2-3 liters/day\n- Increase sodium intake to 3-5 grams/day (if no hypertension or heart failure)\n- Compression garments (abdominal binder > compression stockings for POTS)\n- Avoid prolonged standing, hot environments, and large meals\n- Sleep with the head of bed elevated 10-15 degrees (reduces nocturnal diuresis)\n- Counter-pressure maneuvers (leg crossing, calf muscle pumping) when symptomatic\n\n2. **Pharmacologic** (if non-pharmacologic measures insufficient):\n- **Fludrocortisone 0.1-0.2 mg daily**: Volume expansion. Monitor for hypokalemia and supine hypertension.\n- **Midodrine 5-10 mg TID**: Peripheral vasoconstrictor. Avoid within 4 hours of bedtime (supine hypertension).\n- **Low-dose propranolol 10-20 mg TID**: If hyperadrenergic (resting tachycardia, palpitations). Controls HR without excessive blood pressure reduction.\n- **Ivabradine 5-7.5 mg BID**: Selective sinus node inhibitor. Reduces HR without affecting blood pressure. Emerging evidence in post-COVID POTS.\n\n**Cognitive Rehabilitation**:\n1. **Compensatory strategies**: External memory aids (phone reminders, written lists, structured routines), reduce multitasking, break complex tasks into smaller steps.\n2. **Cognitive exercises**: Computer-based cognitive training programs (attention, processing speed, working memory). 20-30 minutes daily.\n3. **Sleep optimization**: Poor sleep worsens cognitive dysfunction. Sleep hygiene counseling. Consider melatonin 3-5 mg if insomnia present. Rule out sleep apnea if not already done.\n4. **Speech-language pathology referral**: For structured cognitive-linguistic therapy if cognitive deficits are significantly impacting function.\n\n**Fatigue Management**:\n- Energy conservation and activity modification training (OT-led)\n- Structured daily routine with planned rest periods\n- Avoid "boom-bust" cycling (overdoing on good days, crashing for days after)\n- Consider low-dose naltrexone (LDN) 1.5-4.5 mg at bedtime (emerging evidence for neuroinflammation-driven fatigue, off-label)\n\n**Mental Health**:\n- Validate the psychological impact of functional loss\n- CBT for illness adjustment, anxiety management\n- PTSD screening (PCL-5) given ICU stay\n- Consider SSRI if depression or anxiety is clinically significant')
B.aVD=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient's school principal contacts her, expressing frustration that she has been on leave for 4 months. The principal says: \"We need you back. Plenty of people had COVID and went back to work. Are you sure this isn't just anxiety?\" The patient is devastated and asks you for help.")
B.b0K=new A.c("+ Discuss return-to-work accommodations for post-COVID syndrome, disability documentation, and how you advocate for the patient when the legitimacy of symptoms is questioned.",'**Patient Advocacy \u2014 Establishing Medical Legitimacy**:\n\n1. **Post-COVID condition is a recognized medical diagnosis**:\n- ICD-10 code: U09.9 (Post-COVID-19 condition, unspecified)\n- Recognized by WHO, CDC, NIH, and major medical organizations\n- The Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) may cover Long COVID if it substantially limits one or more major life activities (which it clearly does in this patient: working, concentrating, breathing, walking)\n\n2. **Objective documentation for the employer**:\n- Provide a letter documenting the medical diagnosis with objective findings: abnormal MoCA (24/30), positive POTS criteria on standing test, reduced 6MWD (69% predicted), documented post-exertional malaise, mildly reduced DLCO (72%), and elevated CRP.\n- Include functional limitations in specific, measurable terms: "Patient is unable to sustain cognitive activity for more than 30-40 minutes without significant symptom exacerbation," "Patient experiences post-exertional malaise 24-48 hours after sustained activity requiring 1-2 days of recovery."\n\n**Return-to-Work Accommodations (under ADA/FMLA)**:\n\n1. **Graduated return-to-work plan**:\n- Week 1-2: 50% schedule (half days, 4 hours)\n- Week 3-4: 60-75% schedule (6 hours, with built-in rest breaks)\n- Week 5-8: Progress to full days if tolerated\n- If PEM occurs, reduce and retry at a slower pace\n\n2. **Specific workplace accommodations**:\n- **Cognitive**: Reduced class size or teaching load initially, written lesson plans prepared in advance, colleague support for complex tasks, reduced meetings\n- **Physical**: Seated teaching option (stool at front of class), elevator access, accessible parking, no recess duty or after-school activities initially\n- **Schedule**: Flexible start time (fatigue is often worse in the morning), scheduled rest breaks every 60-90 minutes, option to work from home on days with symptom flares\n- **Environmental**: Temperature control (heat worsens POTS), hydration access in classroom (water bottle)\n\n3. **FMLA protections**: If she has been employed for 12+ months and her employer has 50+ employees, she is entitled to up to 12 weeks of unpaid, job-protected leave under FMLA. She may also use FMLA intermittently for flare days.\n\n4. **Short-term vs. long-term disability**: If she cannot return to work even with accommodations, document for long-term disability. Include formal neuropsychological testing results, functional capacity assessment, and specialist letters supporting the diagnosis.\n\n**Disability Documentation Tips**:\n- Use objective measures (MoCA scores, 6MWT, CPET results, tilt table results, PFTs) rather than subjective symptoms alone\n- Document treatment adherence and response\n- Describe specific functional limitations in work-relevant terms ("cannot sustain standing for > 20 minutes," "requires rest breaks every 45 minutes")\n- Provide clear medical opinion: "It is my professional medical opinion that this patient is currently unable to perform the essential functions of her job as a full-time classroom teacher without the accommodations listed above."')
B.aVA=new A.c(u.j,"The patient is in your office, clearly distressed after the phone call from her principal. She says: \"My boss basically said I'm faking it. My own family is starting to wonder if I'm just depressed or lazy. I used to run 3 miles and teach 30 kids all day. Now I can barely make it through a trip to the grocery store. What is wrong with me? Am I ever going to get better?\"")
B.aRx=new A.c("+ Demonstrate how you would validate this patient's experience, address the stigma of post-COVID syndrome, and provide honest prognostic counseling.",'I would recognize that this patient is experiencing a combination of medical symptoms and social invalidation that is compounding her suffering:\n\n**1. Start with direct, unequivocal validation**:\n"I want to tell you something clearly, and I need you to believe it: what you are experiencing is real, it is medical, and it is not your fault. You are not lazy, you are not depressed about returning to work, and you are not making this up. I have objective test results that prove your body is not functioning the way it should \u2014 your heart rate, your cognitive testing, your exercise capacity, and your inflammatory markers all confirm that something biological is happening."\n\n**2. Acknowledge the pain of being disbelieved**:\n"Being told that your symptoms are not real \u2014 especially by people you care about and depend on \u2014 is one of the most isolating and hurtful experiences a person can go through. I am sorry that is happening to you. You deserve better, and I want you to know that in this office, you will always be believed and taken seriously."\n\n**3. Explain the condition in empowering terms**:\n"What happened is that the COVID infection triggered a cascade of changes in your body \u2014 your autonomic nervous system, which controls your heart rate and blood pressure, is not regulating properly. Your immune system is still in an activated state even though the virus is gone. And your brain is recovering from inflammation that affected the areas responsible for concentration and memory. These are real, physical processes. You would not tell someone with a broken leg to just walk it off, and this is no different."\n\n**4. Address prognosis honestly**:\n"Here is the honest answer about recovery: the majority of patients with post-COVID syndrome do improve over time. Studies show that most people see significant improvement over 6-12 months, and many continue to improve out to 2 years. But recovery is often not linear \u2014 you will have good days and bad days, and that is normal. Some people recover fully, and some are left with residual symptoms that they learn to manage effectively. I cannot predict exactly where you will land, but I can tell you that the rehabilitation program we are building is designed to maximize your recovery."\n\n**5. Address the employer situation concretely**:\n"Regarding your boss, that is something I can help with directly. I will write a detailed medical letter documenting your diagnosis, your objective test results, and the specific accommodations you need to return to work safely. Your principal does not need to understand the neuroscience \u2014 they need to understand that this is a legitimate medical condition with legal protections under the ADA, and that you are actively in treatment working toward recovery."\n\n**6. Provide peer support and community**:\n"You are not alone in this. Millions of people are dealing with the same thing. There are online communities like Body Politic and Survivor Corps where people with Long COVID support each other, share strategies, and advocate together. Sometimes hearing from someone who has walked this path \u2014 and improved \u2014 is the most powerful medicine."\n\n**7. Commit to the partnership**:\n"We are going to get through this step by step. I am here for the long haul. We have a plan for your body, your cognition, your autonomic system, and your return to work. It will take time and patience, but I believe you will regain function. And the fact that you were a runner and a dedicated teacher before this tells me you have the determination to do it."\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Unequivocal validation, acknowledgment of medical gaslighting, objective evidence-based reassurance, honest prognostication, concrete advocacy, peer support resources, therapeutic commitment.')
B.aD6=s([B.aXR,B.aTn,B.aP1,B.b2i,B.b_t,B.aX2,B.aPz,B.aVD,B.b0K,B.aVA,B.aRx],t.E)
B.QM=new A.a5("cardiopulm-post-covid","Post-COVID Rehabilitation",B.aD6,!1)
B.aNj=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 45-year-old male is admitted to the acute rehabilitation unit 3 weeks after orthotopic heart transplant for end-stage non-ischemic dilated cardiomyopathy. He had been listed as Status 4 on the transplant waitlist for 14 months, during which he was supported with a left ventricular assist device (LVAD) as bridge to transplant. His post-transplant course was complicated by acute cellular rejection (Grade 2R) treated with pulse methylprednisolone on post-operative day 10, now resolved on surveillance biopsy. He is profoundly deconditioned \u2014 unable to stand for more than 2 minutes, requires moderate assist for transfers, and becomes dyspneic walking 30 feet. He was previously an avid competitive cyclist who completed century rides (100 miles), and his primary question to the rehab team is: "When can I race again?"')
B.aV0=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **Cardiac History**: Non-ischemic dilated cardiomyopathy diagnosed age 38, etiology unclear (negative genetic testing, negative viral serologies). Progressive heart failure despite optimal medical therapy. LVEF declined from 25% to 12% over 2 years. HeartMate 3 LVAD implanted 14 months ago as bridge to transplant. Orthotopic heart transplant 3 weeks ago (bicaval technique).\n- **Post-Transplant Course**: Uncomplicated surgery. Extubated POD 1. Acute cellular rejection Grade 2R on POD 10 surveillance biopsy \u2014 treated with IV methylprednisolone pulse x 3 days, repeat biopsy POD 17 showed resolution (Grade 0R). Current LVEF 55% on echocardiogram.\n- **Functional Status Pre-Illness**: Elite recreational cyclist (century rides, 5000+ miles/year). Worked as a software engineer (desk job). Independent in all activities.\n- **Current Functional Status**: FIM score 78/126. Requires moderate assist for transfers, supervision for bed mobility. Ambulates 30 feet with rolling walker and contact guard assist before needing to rest (dyspnea and leg fatigue). Cannot climb stairs. ADLs require setup assistance.\n- **Medications**: Tacrolimus 3 mg BID (target trough 10-15 ng/mL), mycophenolate mofetil 1000 mg BID, prednisone 20 mg daily (taper planned), valganciclovir 900 mg daily (CMV prophylaxis), trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole DS 3x/week (PCP prophylaxis), pantoprazole 40 mg daily, magnesium oxide 400 mg BID (tacrolimus-induced hypomagnesemia), insulin glargine 15 units nightly (steroid-induced hyperglycemia)\n- **Psychosocial**: Married, 2 children ages 10 and 13. Wife works part-time as a teacher. He has been unable to work for 18 months. Describes cycling as 'my identity' and is anxious about losing it permanently.\n- **Review of Systems**: Fatigue, proximal muscle weakness, mild tremor (tacrolimus), frequent loose stools (mycophenolate), hyperglycemia, difficulty sleeping in hospital")
B.aP4=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 132/78 mmHg, HR 98 bpm (resting \u2014 note: denervated heart), RR 18, SpO2 97% on RA, Temp 36.7C, Weight 78 kg, BMI 25.5.\n\n**Cardiovascular**:\n- **Resting HR**: 98 bpm \u2014 elevated baseline consistent with denervated donor heart (loss of vagal tone). Regular rhythm.\n- **Heart sounds**: Normal S1/S2, no murmurs, no S3/S4.\n- **Sternotomy incision**: Well-healed, stable, no drainage or erythema.\n- **JVP**: Not elevated. No peripheral edema.\n\n**Pulmonary**: Clear to auscultation bilaterally. No pleural effusion on recent CXR. Mild dyspnea with sustained conversation.\n\n**Musculoskeletal**:\n- **Proximal weakness**: Hip flexors 3+/5 bilaterally, knee extensors 4-/5 bilaterally, shoulder flexors 4/5 bilaterally. Distal strength 4+/5 throughout.\n- **Muscle atrophy**: Visible quadriceps and gluteal wasting. Reduced calf circumference bilaterally.\n- **ROM**: Full throughout. Sternal precautions in effect.\n- **Grip strength**: R 28 kg, L 26 kg (reduced from estimated baseline of 45+ kg).\n\n**Neurologic**: Alert, oriented. Fine tremor bilateral hands (tacrolimus). Sensation intact. Reflexes 2+ and symmetric. No focal deficits.\n\n**Skin**: Sternotomy scar healing well. LVAD driveline exit site (left abdomen) \u2014 healing, no infection signs. Multiple ecchymoses on arms (anticoagulation/steroid-related).\n\n**Functional Testing**:\n- **6-Minute Walk Test**: 125 meters (predicted >550 meters for age \u2014 severely deconditioned)\n- **30-second chair stand**: 3 repetitions with armrest use (severely impaired)\n- **Modified Borg Dyspnea Scale**: 5/10 after 6MWT\n- **Heart rate response during 6MWT**: Resting 98 bpm, peak 108 bpm (blunted chronotropic response \u2014 denervated heart)")
B.aUz=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The rehabilitation team needs to understand the unique physiology of the denervated transplanted heart to design a safe exercise program.")
B.aNh=new A.c("+ Explain the physiology of the denervated transplanted heart and its implications for exercise prescription. How does the heart rate response differ from a normally innervated heart?","**Denervated Heart Physiology**:\n\nDuring orthotopic heart transplant, all autonomic nerve connections (sympathetic and parasympathetic) to the donor heart are severed. This creates a **denervated heart** with fundamentally altered exercise physiology:\n\n**1. Resting Heart Rate**:\n- Normal heart: ~60-70 bpm (vagal tone keeps rate low)\n- Denervated heart: **90-110 bpm** (loss of vagal restraint; intrinsic SA node rate)\n- This patient's resting HR of 98 is typical and NOT tachycardia requiring treatment\n\n**2. Heart Rate Response to Exercise**:\n- Normal heart: Rapid HR increase via sympathetic activation within seconds of exercise onset\n- Denervated heart: **Delayed, blunted HR response**. HR increases slowly (over 3-6 minutes) via **circulating catecholamines** (epinephrine/norepinephrine from adrenal medulla), NOT direct neural stimulation. Peak HR is typically **20-25% lower** than age-predicted maximum.\n- Denervated heart peak HR: ~130-150 bpm (versus predicted max of ~175 bpm for age 45)\n\n**3. Heart Rate Recovery**:\n- Normal heart: Rapid HR decline after exercise (vagal reactivation)\n- Denervated heart: **Prolonged HR recovery** \u2014 HR remains elevated for 5-10+ minutes after exercise cessation because catecholamine clearance is slow. This requires **extended cool-down periods**.\n\n**4. Cardiac Output Augmentation**:\n- In the denervated heart, cardiac output increases primarily through the **Frank-Starling mechanism** (increased venous return leads to increased stroke volume via increased preload) in the early phase of exercise, with catecholamine-mediated contractility and HR increase providing additional augmentation later.\n- This is why a **prolonged warm-up** is essential \u2014 the heart needs time to transition.\n\n**5. Response to Medications**:\n- Denervated hearts do NOT respond to **atropine** (no vagal nerve to block)\n- DO respond to **direct-acting agents**: isoproterenol, epinephrine, dobutamine (act directly on cardiac receptors)\n- Do NOT respond to **indirect-acting agents**: digoxin (vagal-mediated effects are absent)\n\n**6. Reinnervation**:\n- Partial sympathetic reinnervation may occur over **1-5 years** in some patients\n- Evidence: improved HR response, HR variability, and anginal sensation\n- Typically incomplete; exercise prescriptions should assume denervation for at least the first 1-2 years\n\n**Implications for Exercise Prescription**:\n- **Extended warm-up** (10-15 minutes minimum) to allow catecholamine-mediated HR response\n- **Extended cool-down** (10-15 minutes minimum) to allow catecholamine clearance\n- **RPE-based intensity monitoring** (Borg scale) rather than target heart rate, since HR response is unreliable\n- **Avoid sudden high-intensity activity** \u2014 the heart cannot rapidly increase output\n- **No isometric Valsalva maneuvers** early post-transplant (sternal precautions + preload sensitivity)")
B.b07=new A.c("+ What are the signs of allograft rejection, and how does immunosuppression affect the rehabilitation plan?",'**Allograft Rejection \u2014 Recognition in Rehabilitation**:\n\n**Types of Rejection**:\n1. **Hyperacute**: Occurs within minutes to hours. Pre-formed antibodies. Managed intraoperatively. Not a rehabilitation concern.\n2. **Acute Cellular Rejection (ACR)**: Most common. T-cell mediated. Highest risk in first 3-6 months. Graded 0R to 3R on endomyocardial biopsy. This patient had Grade 2R (moderate) \u2014 now resolved.\n3. **Antibody-Mediated Rejection (AMR)**: B-cell/antibody mediated. Can be acute or chronic. Detected by immunohistochemistry.\n4. **Chronic Allograft Vasculopathy (CAV)**: Progressive coronary artery disease in the transplanted heart. Leading cause of late graft failure. Develops over years.\n\n**Signs of Rejection That Rehabilitation Staff Must Recognize**:\n- **New or worsening fatigue** out of proportion to activity level\n- **Declining exercise tolerance** \u2014 failure to progress or regression in therapy\n- **New arrhythmias** \u2014 atrial fibrillation, ventricular ectopy (denervated hearts are prone to atrial arrhythmias with rejection)\n- **Unexplained weight gain** (fluid retention)\n- **New peripheral edema, elevated JVP, or S3 gallop**\n- **Low-grade fever** without other source\n- **Hypotension** or signs of low cardiac output\n- **Important**: Because the heart is denervated, patients will **NOT experience angina** even with significant rejection or CAV. Ischemia is "silent."\n\n**Action**: Any of these signs should prompt immediate notification of the transplant team and consideration of holding therapy pending evaluation. Endomyocardial biopsy is the gold standard for diagnosis.\n\n**Immunosuppression Implications for Rehabilitation**:\n\n1. **Infection Risk** (highest in first 3-6 months):\n- **Gym hygiene**: Wipe all equipment before and after use. Avoid shared mats. Hand hygiene strictly enforced.\n- **Avoid group exercise classes** with many participants in the early post-transplant period.\n- **Avoid gardening or soil contact** (Aspergillus, Nocardia risk).\n- **Monitor for fever**: Low threshold for infectious workup. Temperature >38C should prompt notification of transplant team.\n\n2. **Steroid Myopathy** (prednisone):\n- Proximal muscle weakness is exacerbated by corticosteroids. This patient\'s proximal weakness (hip flexors 3+/5) is likely multifactorial \u2014 deconditioning + steroid myopathy.\n- As prednisone is tapered, strength should improve. Resistance training accelerates recovery.\n\n3. **Osteoporosis Risk**:\n- Corticosteroids + tacrolimus both increase bone loss. DEXA scan recommended at 6-12 months post-transplant.\n- Weight-bearing exercise is protective. Calcium and vitamin D supplementation.\n\n4. **Tremor** (tacrolimus):\n- Fine tremor may affect hand function and balance. Monitor during therapy.\n\n5. **Renal Function**:\n- Tacrolimus is nephrotoxic. Monitor creatinine. Ensure adequate hydration during exercise.\n- Avoid NSAIDs (further nephrotoxicity).\n\n6. **Diabetes/Hyperglycemia**:\n- Tacrolimus and prednisone both cause hyperglycemia. Monitor glucose before and after exercise. Exercise will improve insulin sensitivity.')
B.aOw=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Design an exercise rehabilitation program for this patient during inpatient rehabilitation and outline the outpatient progression plan.")
B.b2D=new A.c("+ Outline a phased exercise rehabilitation program for this heart transplant recipient, including inpatient and outpatient components.",'**Phase I \u2014 Inpatient Rehabilitation (Current, Weeks 3-6 Post-Transplant)**:\n\n*Aerobic Training*:\n- **Warm-up**: 10-15 minutes of slow walking or seated cycling at very low resistance. Essential for catecholamine-mediated HR increase.\n- **Exercise**: Walk or cycle 10-20 minutes at RPE 11-13 (Borg 6-20 scale = "fairly light" to "somewhat hard"). Start with interval format: 3-5 minutes exercise / 2 minutes rest. Progress to continuous exercise.\n- **Cool-down**: 10-15 minutes of slow walking tapering to seated rest. Allow HR to return toward resting level (prolonged recovery expected).\n- **Frequency**: 2 sessions daily (AM and PM), 5-6 days/week.\n- **Monitoring**: HR, BP, RPE, SpO2 before, during, and after exercise. Telemetry monitoring recommended for first 2 weeks of rehabilitation.\n\n*Resistance Training*:\n- **Sternal precautions**: No lifting >5 lbs with upper extremities, no pushing/pulling, no bilateral arm elevation above shoulders for 6-8 weeks post-sternotomy.\n- **Lower extremity focus initially**: Seated knee extensions, hip flexion, ankle pumps, standing hip abduction with support. 1-2 sets of 10-15 reps at low resistance.\n- **After sternal precautions lifted (6-8 weeks)**: Add upper extremity resistance training starting with resistance bands, progressing to light weights.\n\n*Functional Training*:\n- Progressive transfers: bed to chair, chair to standing, sit-to-stand repetitions\n- Gait training: advance from rolling walker to single-point cane to independent\n- Stair training: start with 4-6 steps, progress to full flight\n- ADL training with OT: bathing, dressing, meal preparation\n\n*Discharge Criteria*: Independent transfers, ambulating 500+ feet independently, climbing one flight of stairs with rail, independent ADLs, FIM score >100.\n\n**Phase II \u2014 Outpatient Cardiac Rehabilitation (Weeks 6-18 Post-Transplant)**:\n\n*Structure*: Formal cardiac rehab program 3 days/week, ECG-monitored sessions.\n- **Aerobic**: Progress to 30-45 minutes continuous exercise. Modalities: treadmill, cycling, elliptical. RPE-guided intensity (RPE 12-14, "somewhat hard").\n- **Resistance**: After sternal clearance, progressive resistance training 2-3 days/week. All major muscle groups. 2-3 sets of 10-15 reps. Progress load by 5% when completing 15 reps easily.\n- **Flexibility**: Daily stretching, especially chest/shoulder to counteract sternal healing posture.\n\n**Phase III \u2014 Community/Independent Exercise (Months 4-12+)**:\n\n- Transition to independent exercise with ongoing physiatrist or cardiologist oversight.\n- **Aerobic goal**: 150-300 minutes/week moderate intensity. Cycling can be reintroduced.\n- **Resistance goal**: 2-3 sessions/week, progressive loading targeting functional goals.\n- **Reassessment**: Cardiopulmonary exercise testing (CPET) at 6 and 12 months to objectively measure VO2 peak and guide exercise intensity.\n\n**Regarding Competitive Cycling**:\n- Transplant recipients CAN return to recreational cycling and even organized events (Transplant Games of America include cycling).\n- **Timeline**: Typically 6-12 months post-transplant before return to vigorous sustained activity.\n- **Limitations**: VO2 peak in transplant recipients is typically **60-70% of age-predicted normal** even with optimal training, due to chronotropic incompetence and peripheral factors.\n- **Century rides (100 miles)**: Possible but will require adjusted expectations for pace and completion time. Hydration and thermoregulation are critical (denervated heart has altered thermoregulatory responses).\n- **Competition**: Encourage Transplant Games rather than open competition \u2014 more appropriate peer group and safety infrastructure.')
B.aWb=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient is eager to return to work and cycling. He asks about the long-term follow-up requirements and how rehabilitation fits into the transplant continuum of care.")
B.b2S=new A.c("+ Describe transplant team coordination, lifelong follow-up requirements, and return-to-work planning for heart transplant recipients.","**Lifelong Transplant Follow-Up**:\n\n1. **Endomyocardial Biopsy Schedule**: Weekly for month 1, biweekly months 2-3, monthly months 3-6, every 2-3 months for year 1, then annually or as indicated. This is the gold standard for rejection surveillance.\n2. **Cardiac Catheterization/Angiography**: Annual to screen for cardiac allograft vasculopathy (CAV). May include intravascular ultrasound (IVUS) for early detection.\n3. **Echocardiography**: Serial echocardiograms to monitor graft function (LVEF, diastolic function, valve competence).\n4. **Laboratory Monitoring**: Tacrolimus trough levels (weekly initially, then monthly), renal function (BUN/Cr, GFR), CBC, hepatic panel, lipid panel, glucose/HbA1c, CMV/EBV viral loads.\n5. **Cancer Screening**: Immunosuppressed patients have 2-4x increased cancer risk. Annual dermatology skin exams (skin cancer), age-appropriate cancer screenings, vigilance for post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder (PTLD).\n6. **Bone Health**: DEXA scan at 1 year, then every 2 years. Osteoporosis prevention and treatment.\n7. **Vaccination**: Annual influenza (inactivated only \u2014 no live vaccines). Pneumococcal, hepatitis B. No live vaccines (no MMR, varicella, live influenza, or shingles live vaccine; Shingrix recombinant is acceptable).\n\n**Medication Adherence \u2014 Critical Issue**:\n- **Non-adherence to immunosuppression is the leading preventable cause of graft loss**.\n- Discuss medication management at every rehabilitation visit.\n- Pill organizers, phone alarms, pharmacy auto-refill, family involvement.\n- Address cost barriers \u2014 transplant medications can cost $2,000-3,000/month. Social work referral for copay assistance programs (manufacturer programs, NeedyMeds, Patient Access Network Foundation).\n\n**Transplant Team Coordination**:\n- **Transplant cardiologist**: Primary coordinator of medical management, rejection surveillance, immunosuppression adjustment.\n- **Physiatrist/Cardiac Rehab**: Exercise prescription, functional recovery, return to activity guidance.\n- **Transplant coordinator**: Scheduling biopsies, labs, imaging. Patient's primary point of contact.\n- **Social worker**: Insurance navigation, financial counseling, disability applications, return-to-work coordination.\n- **Psychologist**: Adjustment to transplant, body image (scar, weight changes), guilt about donor, anxiety about rejection.\n- **Dietitian**: Weight management, diabetic diet, renal diet if needed.\n\n**Return-to-Work Planning**:\n- **Timeline**: Most transplant recipients who were working before illness can return to work within 3-6 months post-transplant. This patient's desk job (software engineering) has low physical demands.\n- **Considerations**: Frequent medical appointments (biopsies, labs) require flexible scheduling. Infection risk in first 6 months \u2014 discuss workplace accommodations (private office vs open plan, hand hygiene access).\n- **Disability**: He has been unable to work for 18 months (LVAD period + transplant). If on SSDI, Ticket to Work program allows trial work period without losing benefits.\n- **ADA protections**: Transplant recipients are protected under the Americans with Disabilities Act. Reasonable accommodations include flexible scheduling for medical appointments and modified work environment during high immunosuppression periods.")
B.aZs=new A.c(u.j,'During the initial rehabilitation evaluation, the patient says: "Before I got sick, I was averaging 200 miles a week on my bike. I did the Death Ride twice. Cycling is who I am. I need you to tell me straight \u2014 will I ever race again, or is that life over?"')
B.b17=new A.c("+ How would you manage this patient's expectations about returning to competitive cycling while maintaining his motivation for rehabilitation?","**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Acknowledge how central cycling is to his identity**:\n'I can see how important cycling is to you \u2014 it is not just a hobby, it is part of who you are. Completing the Death Ride twice puts you in an elite category of endurance athletes. I respect that, and I want to work with you, not against that identity.'\n\n**2. Give an honest, direct answer \u2014 he asked for straight talk**:\n'You asked me to be straight with you, so I will be. The physiology of a transplanted heart is different from what you had before. Your new heart does not respond to your nervous system the way your original heart did. It relies on circulating hormones to speed up, which means slower acceleration, a lower peak heart rate, and a longer recovery. Most transplant recipients achieve about 60 to 70 percent of their predicted maximum exercise capacity, even with excellent training.'\n\n**3. Reframe what is possible rather than dwelling on limitations**:\n'Here is the good news. Heart transplant recipients absolutely ride bikes \u2014 some ride long distances. There are transplant recipients who have completed centuries, triathlons, and even multi-day cycling tours. The Transplant Games of America have competitive cycling events. You will ride again. But your racing will likely look different \u2014 you may not match your pre-illness speeds or power output, and your warm-up needs to be longer because of how the transplanted heart responds to exercise.'\n\n**4. Create a progressive goal framework**:\n'Rather than focusing on century rides right now, let us build a ladder of goals:\n- **Month 1-2**: Walk independently, climb stairs, begin stationary cycling in cardiac rehab.\n- **Month 3-4**: Ride a stationary bike for 30-45 minutes continuously. Begin outdoor cycling on flat, safe terrain.\n- **Month 6**: Complete a 20-30 mile ride at your own pace.\n- **Month 9-12**: Attempt a 50-mile ride. Consider entering a Transplant Games cycling event.\n- **Year 2+**: Evaluate century ride feasibility based on CPET results and training progression.\nEach goal builds on the last. We will celebrate every milestone.'\n\n**5. Address the deeper fear \u2014 loss of identity**:\n'I think what you are really asking is whether you will still be you after this transplant. And the answer is yes. You are still a cyclist. You are still someone who takes on the Death Ride. The numbers on the speedometer may change, but the drive and discipline that got you to those starting lines is still there. That mental toughness is exactly what will make you successful in rehabilitation.'\n\n**6. Provide a role model**:\n'I would like to connect you with another transplant recipient who is an endurance athlete. Hearing from someone who has been where you are and is back on the bike can be more motivating than anything I can say. Would you be open to that?'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Honest communication as requested, respecting the patient's identity, realistic goal-setting with progressive milestones, reframing limitations as adaptations rather than losses, offering peer mentorship, maintaining motivation while managing expectations.")
B.aFx=s([B.aNj,B.aV0,B.aP4,B.aUz,B.aNh,B.b07,B.aOw,B.b2D,B.aWb,B.b2S,B.aZs,B.b17],t.E)
B.PQ=new A.a5("cardiopulm-heart-transplant","Heart Transplant Rehabilitation",B.aFx,!1)
B.aZ9=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 38-year-old female attorney is referred to the pulmonary rehabilitation clinic 6 months after a massive bilateral pulmonary embolism with right ventricular strain and hemodynamic instability requiring catheter-directed thrombolysis. She was on oral contraceptive pills at the time and was found to be heterozygous for Factor V Leiden. She reports persistent dyspnea on exertion \u2014 she becomes breathless walking up one flight of stairs and cannot keep up with her 5-year-old daughter at the playground. She was an avid runner (half-marathons) before the PE and has not exercised since the event because she is terrified that exercise will trigger another clot. She has lost her two largest clients because she cannot sustain the stamina for full-day depositions. She breaks down crying during the intake, saying: "I feel like I am 80 years old. I am afraid to move, afraid to breathe hard, afraid to live my life."')
B.aTa=new A.c("History & Systems Review:",'- **PE History**: Massive bilateral PE 6 months ago. Presented with acute dyspnea, pleuritic chest pain, syncope. CT angiography showed bilateral main pulmonary artery emboli with saddle component. RV/LV ratio 1.4 (RV strain). Troponin elevated. BNP 890 pg/mL. Hemodynamically unstable (SBP 78 mmHg, HR 130). Treated with catheter-directed tPA in the IR suite. ICU stay 4 days, total hospitalization 8 days.\n- **Thrombophilia Workup**: Heterozygous Factor V Leiden. No other thrombophilia identified. Antiphospholipid antibodies negative. No prior VTE history. Was taking combined oral contraceptive pills (OCPs) \u2014 now discontinued.\n- **Current Anticoagulation**: Rivaroxaban 20 mg daily. Hematology recommends lifelong anticoagulation given unprovoked massive PE + Factor V Leiden.\n- **Persistent Symptoms**: Dyspnea with moderate exertion (walking uphill, stairs, carrying groceries). Exercise tolerance estimated at 50% of pre-PE baseline. Intermittent sharp right-sided chest pain with deep breathing. Fatigue requiring afternoon rest. Decreased stamina throughout the day.\n- **Functional Impact**: Unable to run. Avoids all vigorous activity. Uses elevator exclusively. Cannot sustain 8-hour workdays. Lost 2 major clients. Considering part-time practice.\n- **Psychological**: PTSD symptoms \u2014 flashbacks to the syncopal episode, avoidance of activities associated with elevated heart rate, hypervigilance to any chest sensation, catastrophic thinking ("every twinge means another PE"). PHQ-9 score 14 (moderate depression). GAD-7 score 16 (severe anxiety). Insomnia \u2014 wakes multiple times fearing she will die in her sleep.\n- **PMH**: Otherwise healthy. No prior cardiac or pulmonary disease.\n- **Medications**: Rivaroxaban 20 mg daily, escitalopram 10 mg daily (started 4 months ago, only partial response), melatonin 5 mg QHS, ibuprofen PRN for chest pain\n- **Social**: Married, 5-year-old daughter. Partner is supportive but frustrated by activity avoidance. Previous half-marathon runner (1:52 PR). Law firm partner (litigation).\n- **Review of Systems**: Dyspnea on exertion, fatigue, intermittent pleuritic chest pain, insomnia, anxiety, palpitations (especially with exertion or anxiety)')
B.b2W=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 118/74 mmHg, HR 82 bpm, RR 16, SpO2 98% on RA at rest, Temp 36.8C, BMI 24.2.\n\n**Cardiovascular**: Regular rate and rhythm. Normal S1/S2. No murmurs. No RV heave. JVP not elevated. No peripheral edema. No calf tenderness or asymmetry.\n\n**Pulmonary**: Clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezing, crackles, or pleural rub. Symmetric chest expansion.\n\n**Musculoskeletal**: No joint abnormalities. Full ROM throughout. Mild generalized deconditioning. No muscle atrophy.\n\n**Neurologic**: Intact. No focal deficits.\n\n**Functional Testing**:\n- **6-Minute Walk Test**: 410 meters (predicted >550 meters for age \u2014 moderately reduced). SpO2 remained >95% throughout. HR: resting 82, peak 138, recovery at 2 min 105. Stopped at 6 minutes due to dyspnea and anxiety (Borg dyspnea 6/10). No desaturation.\n- **30-second chair stand**: 12 repetitions (low-normal)\n- **Grip strength**: R 26 kg, L 24 kg (normal for age)\n\n**Recent Investigations**:\n- **Echocardiogram** (5 months post-PE): LVEF 60%. RV mildly dilated. RVSP estimated 42 mmHg (mildly elevated \u2014 normal <35). Mild tricuspid regurgitation.\n- **CT Pulmonary Angiography** (4 months post-PE): Near-complete resolution of bilateral PE. Residual small filling defects in right lower lobe segmental arteries. No evidence of chronic organized thrombus.\n- **BNP**: 48 pg/mL (normal, down from 890 at presentation)\n- **CBC, CMP, TSH**: Normal. Hemoglobin 13.1 g/dL.")
B.aOd=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The pulmonologist asks you to evaluate whether this patient's persistent exercise limitation is from post-PE syndrome, deconditioning, or chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension (CTEPH), and to design a rehabilitation program.")
B.b3_=new A.c("+ Differentiate post-PE syndrome from CTEPH and deconditioning. What further workup is indicated?","**Post-PE Syndrome**:\nA recognized clinical entity affecting **30-50% of patients after acute PE**. Characterized by persistent dyspnea, exercise intolerance, and reduced quality of life despite adequate anticoagulation and resolution of acute clot burden. Pathophysiology includes:\n- Residual pulmonary vascular obstruction (even small residual clot)\n- Pulmonary vascular remodeling and endothelial dysfunction\n- RV dysfunction/remodeling (even if LVEF is normal)\n- Deconditioning from prolonged activity avoidance\n- Psychological factors (PTSD, fear-avoidance, anxiety)\n\n**CTEPH \u2014 Must Be Ruled Out**:\nChronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension develops in **2-4% of PE survivors**. Key features:\n- Mean pulmonary artery pressure >=20 mmHg on right heart catheterization after >=3 months of anticoagulation\n- Organized, fibrotic thrombus causing mechanical obstruction (vs acute clot)\n- Progressive exertional dyspnea that worsens over time\n- Signs of RV failure (elevated JVP, edema, RV heave)\n- **Treatment**: Pulmonary thromboendarterectomy (PTE) is potentially curative \u2014 a surgical emergency to diagnose\n\n**This Patient's Risk for CTEPH**:\n- RVSP 42 mmHg (mildly elevated) at 5 months \u2014 concerning\n- Residual filling defects on CTA \u2014 could represent organized thrombus or resolving acute clot\n- Large initial clot burden is a CTEPH risk factor\n\n**Recommended Workup**:\n\n1. **V/Q Scan**: **Most sensitive screening test for CTEPH** \u2014 more sensitive than CTA for detecting chronic thromboembolic disease. A normal V/Q scan essentially excludes CTEPH. Mismatched perfusion defects suggest CTEPH.\n\n2. **Cardiopulmonary Exercise Testing (CPET)**: Gold standard for objective exercise limitation assessment. Will differentiate:\n- **Cardiac limitation**: Reduced VO2 peak, reduced O2 pulse, ventilatory equivalents suggest pulmonary vascular disease\n- **Deconditioning**: Low VO2 peak with normal cardiovascular parameters, early lactate threshold, high submaximal HR\n- **Ventilatory limitation**: Reduced breathing reserve, expiratory flow limitation\n- **Psychological/symptom amplification**: Stopping well before physiologic limits, hyperventilation pattern, normal physiologic parameters at peak exercise\n\n3. **Right Heart Catheterization**: If V/Q scan is abnormal or RVSP remains elevated, proceed to RHC for definitive hemodynamic assessment. This is the diagnostic gold standard for pulmonary hypertension.\n\n4. **Repeat Echocardiogram**: Reassess RV size and function, RVSP trend. If RVSP is normalizing, less concern for CTEPH.\n\n**Most Likely Diagnosis in This Patient**: Post-PE syndrome with significant deconditioning and psychologically-driven exercise avoidance (fear-avoidance behavior). However, CTEPH must be formally excluded given the mildly elevated RVSP.")
B.aNw=new A.c("+ CPET results show VO2 peak of 18 mL/kg/min (65% predicted), low anaerobic threshold, normal ventilatory equivalents, and hyperventilation at peak exercise. V/Q scan shows matched defects only. Interpret these findings.","**CPET Interpretation**:\n\n- **VO2 peak 18 mL/kg/min (65% predicted)**: Moderately reduced. For a prior half-marathon runner, expected VO2 peak would be 38-42 mL/kg/min. Current level is consistent with a sedentary, mildly impaired individual.\n- **Low anaerobic threshold**: Early transition to anaerobic metabolism, consistent with deconditioning. Peripheral muscles are detrained and reach lactate threshold at lower workloads.\n- **Normal ventilatory equivalents (VE/VCO2)**: Argues AGAINST significant pulmonary vascular disease. In CTEPH or pulmonary hypertension, VE/VCO2 slope is typically >36 (elevated dead space ventilation). Normal value here is reassuring.\n- **Hyperventilation at peak exercise**: Breathing frequency disproportionately high relative to metabolic demand. Pattern consistent with **anxiety-driven hyperventilation** rather than physiologic limitation.\n- **V/Q scan \u2014 matched defects only**: **Effectively rules out CTEPH**. CTEPH produces mismatched perfusion defects (perfusion worse than ventilation). Matched defects are nonspecific.\n\n**Integrated Diagnosis**:\nThis patient has **post-PE syndrome** with two dominant components:\n1. **Cardiovascular deconditioning**: 6 months of activity avoidance has caused VO2 peak to drop from likely 40+ to 18 mL/kg/min. This is the primary driver of her functional limitation.\n2. **Anxiety-mediated exercise avoidance (fear-avoidance cycle)**: PTSD symptoms, catastrophic thinking, and hyperventilation pattern suggest a significant psychological barrier to exercise. Her perceived limitation exceeds her physiologic limitation.\n\n**CTEPH is excluded** based on normal VE/VCO2 and matched V/Q scan. The mildly elevated RVSP (42 mmHg) likely represents residual RV remodeling from the massive PE that is expected to improve with exercise and time.\n\n**Prognostic Significance**: Both deconditioning and anxiety-driven avoidance are **highly treatable** with graded exercise rehabilitation and cognitive behavioral therapy. This is an excellent candidate for pulmonary rehabilitation.")
B.b3e=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","CTEPH has been excluded. The primary diagnoses are post-PE syndrome with deconditioning and anxiety-driven exercise avoidance. Design a comprehensive rehabilitation program.")
B.aQB=new A.c("+ Outline a graded exercise rehabilitation program for this patient, including anticoagulation considerations and psychological support.",'**Graded Exercise Rehabilitation Program for Post-PE Syndrome**:\n\n**Phase 1 \u2014 Supervised Introduction (Weeks 1-4)**:\nGoal: Break the fear-avoidance cycle. Demonstrate that exercise is safe.\n\n*Aerobic Training*:\n- **Setting**: Supervised cardiac/pulmonary rehab with ECG and SpO2 monitoring (reassurance function as much as medical necessity)\n- **Modality**: Walking on treadmill or cycling (cycling may feel safer initially \u2014 lower impact, seated position)\n- **Intensity**: Start BELOW anaerobic threshold. RPE 10-12 (Borg scale) = "light" to "fairly light." HR target: 60-70% of peak HR from CPET (~95-110 bpm).\n- **Duration**: 15-20 minutes continuous, with permission to stop if needed (patient controls the pace).\n- **Frequency**: 3 sessions/week supervised + 2 home walking sessions (20 minutes at comfortable pace)\n- **Key intervention**: After each session, explicitly debrief \u2014 "You just exercised for 20 minutes. Your oxygen level stayed normal. Your heart rhythm was perfect. Nothing bad happened. Let us notice that together."\n\n*Resistance Training*:\n- Begin bodyweight exercises: squats, lunges, wall push-ups, seated rows with band. 2 sets of 12-15 reps.\n- Focus on lower extremity and core to rebuild running foundation.\n\n**Phase 2 \u2014 Progressive Loading (Weeks 5-12)**:\n- **Aerobic**: Progress to 30-45 minutes at RPE 12-14 ("somewhat hard"). Introduce interval training: 3-minute moderate / 1-minute vigorous alternating. Add incline walking.\n- **Reintroduce running**: Week 6-8, begin walk/jog intervals on treadmill (1 min jog / 2 min walk x 10 cycles). Progress jog duration as tolerated.\n- **Resistance**: Progress to moderate-weight dumbbell or machine exercises. 3 sets of 10-12 reps.\n- **Monitoring**: Transition from continuous ECG to periodic SpO2 spot-checks as confidence builds.\n\n**Phase 3 \u2014 Return to Running (Weeks 12-24)**:\n- Follow structured return-to-running program (C25K-style progression).\n- Outdoor running introduction with GPS watch for pacing.\n- Progressive long runs: build by 10% weekly, with cutback weeks.\n- **Half-marathon timeline**: If progressing well, realistic to target a half-marathon at 9-12 months post-PE onset (6 months from now).\n\n**Anticoagulation Management During Exercise**:\n1. **Rivaroxaban timing**: Take with the largest meal of the day for absorption. Exercise timing does not need to change.\n2. **Contact/collision sports**: Avoid sports with significant bleeding risk (boxing, football, hockey). Running, cycling, swimming are safe.\n3. **Fall risk activities**: Caution with trail running (fall risk \u2192 bruising/bleeding). Road running and treadmill preferred initially.\n4. **NSAID interaction**: Discontinue ibuprofen \u2014 increased bleeding risk with rivaroxaban. Switch to acetaminophen for chest wall pain.\n5. **Medical alert**: Wear medical alert identification listing anticoagulant use.\n6. **Menstrual considerations**: Monitor for heavy menstrual bleeding on rivaroxaban (common in premenopausal women). Discuss with hematology and gynecology.\n\n**Psychological Support \u2014 Critical Component**:\n1. **Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT)** for health anxiety and PTSD:\n- **Cognitive restructuring**: Challenge catastrophic thoughts ("chest tightness = another PE" reframed as "chest tightness can be normal with exercise after deconditioning")\n- **Graded exposure**: Systematic desensitization to feared stimuli (elevated HR, breathlessness, chest sensations) through progressive exercise\n- **Interoceptive exposure**: Intentionally induce elevated HR and breathlessness in a controlled setting to reduce fear response\n2. **SSRI optimization**: Escitalopram 10 mg has only partially helped. Consider increasing to 20 mg or switching to sertraline. Add short-acting anxiolytic (hydroxyzine 25 mg PRN) for acute anxiety episodes during exercise.\n3. **Trauma processing**: EMDR or prolonged exposure therapy for PTSD symptoms related to the syncopal/near-death experience.\n4. **Partner involvement**: Include partner in 1-2 sessions to address relationship strain from activity avoidance and discuss how to supportively encourage activity without pressuring.')
B.aSz=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient asks about returning to full-time work and whether pulmonary rehabilitation is covered by insurance for her condition.")
B.aRP=new A.c("+ Discuss return-to-work planning for this patient and insurance/access considerations for post-PE rehabilitation.",'**Return-to-Work Planning**:\n\n1. **Current Limitation**: Cannot sustain 8-hour workdays due to fatigue and exercise intolerance. Has lost clients due to inability to perform full-day depositions.\n2. **Modified Work Plan**:\n- **Weeks 1-4**: Continue reduced schedule (part-time, 4-5 hours/day). Request accommodations: scheduled rest breaks, ability to sit during depositions, reduced travel.\n- **Weeks 5-12**: Progressive increase to 6-hour days. Alternate heavy deposition days with lighter administrative days.\n- **Weeks 12+**: Target full-time return as exercise tolerance improves.\n3. **ADA Considerations**: Post-PE syndrome with documented functional limitations may qualify as a disability under the ADA. Reasonable accommodations include modified schedule, rest breaks, and ergonomic workspace (standing desk to alternate positions).\n4. **Documentation**: Provide detailed functional capacity documentation to employer \u2014 frame in terms of specific limitations and expected recovery timeline rather than diagnosis alone.\n5. **Litigation stamina strategy**: For full-day depositions, advise frequent brief standing breaks (every 30-45 minutes), adequate hydration, and avoidance of heavy meals that provoke post-prandial fatigue.\n\n**Insurance and Access for Post-PE Rehabilitation**:\n\n1. **Pulmonary rehabilitation coverage**: Traditional pulmonary rehab is CMS-covered for COPD, ILD, and pulmonary hypertension \u2014 but **post-PE syndrome is NOT a standard-approved diagnosis for pulmonary rehab under Medicare**. Coverage varies by commercial payer.\n2. **Cardiac rehabilitation coverage**: If the patient has documented RV dysfunction (which she does \u2014 mildly dilated RV, elevated RVSP), cardiac rehab may be authorized under heart failure/cardiomyopathy diagnoses. Some programs classify massive PE with RV strain as qualifying.\n3. **Alternative coverage strategies**:\n- Code as physical therapy for deconditioning/functional impairment (ICD-10: R53.81, R26.89, Z87.821)\n- Psychology visits coded under PTSD/anxiety diagnoses (F43.10, F41.1)\n- Frame each discipline under its specific benefit category rather than bundled as "post-PE rehab"\n4. **Advocacy**: Post-PE rehabilitation is an emerging field. The PERT (Pulmonary Embolism Response Team) Consortium and PE research networks are advocating for formal recognition of post-PE syndrome as a rehabilitative diagnosis. Document outcomes to contribute to the evidence base.\n\n**Hematology Coordination**:\n- Lifelong anticoagulation monitoring (annual reassessment of bleeding risk vs recurrence risk)\n- Genetic counseling for Factor V Leiden (autosomal dominant \u2014 50% chance of inheritance by daughter)\n- Contraception counseling: OCPs are contraindicated. Progestin-only methods or non-hormonal options recommended.\n- Pre-pregnancy planning if desired: requires transition from rivaroxaban to LMWH (rivaroxaban is teratogenic)')
B.aPC=new A.c(u.j,"Before the first supervised exercise session, the patient freezes at the treadmill. Her eyes fill with tears and she says: \"I can't do this. What if I get another clot? The last time my heart was beating fast, I passed out and almost died. I can't go through that again. My daughter needs her mother.\"")
B.aPs=new A.c("+ How would you address this patient's exercise-related fear and PTSD response to help her take the first step?","**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Stop. Do not push. Step away from the treadmill.**\n'Let us step away from the machine for a moment. There is no pressure to get on it right now. Let us sit down and talk.'\n[Move to a chair. The physical act of stepping away from the trigger reduces the acute anxiety response and shows her she has control.]\n\n**2. Validate the fear as rational, not irrational**:\n'What you are feeling is not weakness and it is not irrational. Six months ago, your heart was racing and you collapsed and nearly died. Your brain learned from that experience that a fast heart rate means danger. That is your survival instinct doing exactly what it is supposed to do. The problem is that it cannot tell the difference between a dangerous fast heart rate and a healthy one from exercise.'\n\n**3. Provide the medical reassurance she needs to hear**:\n'I want to share some medical facts with you. First, your CTEPH workup is negative \u2014 you do not have chronic clot disease. Second, you are on rivaroxaban, which is actively preventing new clots right now. Third, exercise actually reduces your risk of another PE \u2014 it improves blood flow and reduces stasis, which is one of the main causes of clots. Sitting still out of fear is physiologically more dangerous than moving.'\n\n**4. Normalize the PTSD response**:\n'What you are experiencing has a name. It is called a fear-avoidance response, and it is very common after life-threatening medical events. About one-third of PE survivors develop significant anxiety or PTSD symptoms. You are not alone in this, and it is treatable.'\n\n**5. Offer an achievable first step \u2014 not the treadmill**:\n'Here is what I would like to try instead of the treadmill today. Can you walk with me to the end of this hallway and back? Just a walk. At your pace. I will be right beside you, and we will watch your oxygen level on this monitor the entire time. You are in complete control \u2014 you can stop any time. Can we try that?'\n[Start with the least threatening activity. Success breeds confidence.]\n\n**6. Use interoceptive labeling in real time**:\n[During the walk]: 'Notice your heart rate is 95 right now. How does your body feel? What thoughts are coming up? Your oxygen is 98 percent \u2014 completely normal. Your heart is doing exactly what it should be doing. Let us keep walking and just notice the sensations without judging them.'\n\n**7. Celebrate the success and plan next steps**:\n'You just walked 200 feet. Your heart rate went up and came back down. Your oxygen stayed perfect. Nothing bad happened. I know that might not feel like much compared to half-marathons, but this was the hardest step \u2014 the first one. Can we agree to come back on Wednesday and try again? Maybe a little further?'\n\n**8. Address the core fear \u2014 being there for her daughter**:\n'You said your daughter needs her mother. She does. And the best thing you can do for her is to rebuild your health so you can be active and present for her. Right now, fear is taking you away from her just as much as illness would. Let us fight for that together.'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Removing the trigger, validating the PTSD response as adaptive, providing medical evidence for safety, normalizing the experience, offering graded exposure starting below the fear threshold, real-time interoceptive processing, celebrating success, connecting to core values (daughter), collaborative therapeutic alliance.")
B.aD2=s([B.aZ9,B.aTa,B.b2W,B.aOd,B.b3_,B.aNw,B.b3e,B.aQB,B.aSz,B.aRP,B.aPC,B.aPs],t.E)
B.Q5=new A.a5("cardiopulm-post-pe-syndrome","Pulmonary Embolism Recovery \u2014 Post-PE Syndrome",B.aD2,!1)
B.b_z=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 55-year-old right-handed female with Stage IIB invasive ductal carcinoma of the left breast is referred to the cancer rehabilitation clinic after completing cycle 10 of 12 of dose-dense paclitaxel (taxane-based chemotherapy). She reports progressive bilateral numbness and tingling in her feet that began after cycle 6 and has now spread to her ankles and fingertips. She describes a "walking on cotton" sensation and has tripped twice in the past month. She is having difficulty buttoning her blouse and reports dropping kitchen utensils. She is anxious about these symptoms but has not yet told her oncologist, fearing that her chemotherapy will be discontinued before completion.')
B.aZX=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **Oncologic History**: Left breast invasive ductal carcinoma, ER+/PR+/HER2-, s/p lumpectomy and sentinel lymph node biopsy (1/3 nodes positive), currently on dose-dense AC-T regimen (completed adriamycin/cyclophosphamide, now on paclitaxel)\n- **Neuropathy Timeline**: Onset after cycle 6 of paclitaxel; progressive worsening with each cycle\n- **Symptom Character**: Bilateral symmetric stocking-glove distribution numbness, tingling, burning in feet > hands; worse at night\n- **Functional Impact**: Difficulty with fine motor tasks (buttoning, writing), unsteady gait, two near-falls, now avoids stairs\n- **Pain**: Rates burning foot pain 5/10, interfering with sleep\n- **Prior Neuropathy Risk Factors**: No pre-existing diabetes, no alcohol use disorder, no prior neuropathy, no B12 deficiency history\n- **Medications**: Paclitaxel, ondansetron, dexamethasone, tamoxifen (to start after chemo), acetaminophen PRN\n- **Psychosocial**: Married, works as an accountant (desk job requiring extensive keyboard use), independent in all ADLs prior to chemo\n- **Review of Systems**: No proximal weakness, no bowel/bladder dysfunction, no autonomic symptoms")
B.b0W=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 128/76 mmHg, HR 74 bpm, RR 14, SpO2 99% on RA, Temp 36.8C, BMI 27.\n\n**Neurologic Exam**:\n- **Mental Status**: Alert, oriented, cognition intact. Reports mild 'chemo brain' with word-finding difficulty.\n- **Cranial Nerves**: Intact II-XII.\n- **Motor**: 5/5 strength throughout upper and lower extremities. No proximal weakness. Grip strength mildly reduced bilaterally (18 kg R, 16 kg L on dynamometry; baseline estimated 25-28 kg).\n- **Sensation**: Diminished light touch and pin prick in stocking distribution to mid-calf bilaterally and in glove distribution to the wrists bilaterally. Vibration sense absent at bilateral great toes, reduced at medial malleoli. Proprioception impaired at bilateral great toes (3/5 correct).\n- **Reflexes**: Ankle jerks absent bilaterally. Knee jerks 1+ bilaterally. Upper extremity reflexes 2+ and symmetric. Plantar responses flexor bilaterally.\n- **Coordination**: Mild bilateral finger dysmetria. Difficulty with rapid alternating movements of the hands.\n- **Gait**: Wide-based gait. Positive Romberg sign. Unable to perform tandem gait (3 steps then sidesteps). Mild bilateral foot slap.\n\n**Musculoskeletal**: No joint swelling or deformity. Full ROM throughout.\n**Skin**: Surgical scar left breast well-healed. No lymphedema noted.")
B.aMM=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The oncology team asks you to evaluate the severity of this patient's neuropathy and provide recommendations regarding her remaining chemotherapy cycles.")
B.b_1=new A.c("+ Grade this patient's CIPN using the CTCAE scale. What electrodiagnostic findings would you expect, and what are the key differential diagnoses?","**CTCAE (Common Terminology Criteria for Adverse Events) Grading for Peripheral Neuropathy**:\n- **Grade 1**: Asymptomatic; loss of deep tendon reflexes or paresthesia only\n- **Grade 2**: Moderate symptoms; limiting instrumental ADLs (this patient's difficulty with buttons, keyboard use, and gait unsteadiness fits here)\n- **Grade 3**: Severe symptoms; limiting self-care ADLs; assistive device indicated\n- **Grade 4**: Life-threatening consequences; urgent intervention indicated\n\n**This patient is CTCAE Grade 2**, approaching Grade 3 given the fall risk and progressive trajectory.\n\n**Expected Electrodiagnostic (EDX) Findings**:\n- **Nerve conduction studies (NCS)**: Reduced or absent **sensory nerve action potential (SNAP) amplitudes** in sural, superficial peroneal, and median/ulnar sensory nerves bilaterally. Sensory conduction velocities may be mildly reduced. Motor conduction studies are typically **normal or near-normal** (taxane neuropathy is predominantly sensory axonal). Compound muscle action potential (CMAP) amplitudes are usually preserved.\n- **EMG**: Typically **normal** needle EMG unless severe. May show mild denervation (fibrillations, positive sharp waves) in distal intrinsic foot muscles in advanced cases.\n- **Pattern**: Length-dependent, symmetric, sensory-predominant **axonal** neuropathy.\n\n**Key Differential Diagnoses**:\n1. **Diabetic neuropathy** \u2014 check HbA1c, fasting glucose\n2. **B12 deficiency** \u2014 especially with concurrent metformin use or poor nutrition during chemo\n3. **Paraneoplastic neuropathy** \u2014 anti-Hu antibodies; would expect more asymmetric or rapidly progressive course\n4. **Compression neuropathy** \u2014 carpal tunnel or tarsal tunnel syndrome superimposed\n5. **Hypothyroidism** \u2014 check TSH\n6. **Alcohol-related neuropathy** \u2014 obtain alcohol use history\n\n**Recommended workup**: Vitamin B12, methylmalonic acid, HbA1c, TSH, serum protein electrophoresis, and consider EDX to document baseline severity and guide dose modifications.")
B.aQU=new A.c("+ The oncologist asks whether she should dose-reduce or discontinue the remaining 2 cycles of paclitaxel. How do you advise?",'**Recommendations for Chemotherapy Modification**:\n\nThis decision requires **shared decision-making** between the oncologist, the patient, and the rehabilitation team. Key considerations:\n\n1. **Do NOT automatically discontinue**: At Grade 2, chemotherapy can often continue with dose modification. Grade 3 typically warrants discontinuation or significant dose reduction.\n\n2. **Dose reduction protocol**: A **20-25% dose reduction** of paclitaxel is a standard first step for Grade 2 CIPN. If symptoms stabilize or improve, the remaining cycles may be completed at reduced dose.\n\n3. **Risk-benefit analysis**: With 10 of 12 cycles completed, the patient has received the **majority of her treatment benefit**. The marginal benefit of the final 2 cycles must be weighed against the risk of progressive, potentially irreversible neuropathy. The oncologist should estimate the absolute benefit of completing all cycles versus stopping at 10.\n\n4. **"Coasting" phenomenon**: CIPN from taxanes can **worsen for weeks to months after discontinuation** before stabilizing or improving. This means her current Grade 2 symptoms may progress to Grade 3 even if chemotherapy is stopped now.\n\n5. **Prognostic counseling**: Approximately **30-40% of patients** have persistent CIPN at 6 months post-treatment. Some patients have permanent symptoms. Earlier intervention (dose reduction or cessation) is associated with better long-term outcomes.\n\n6. **Documentation**: Formally document the CIPN grade, functional limitations, and recommendations in the medical record. This provides medicolegal protection and supports the shared decision.')
B.aVI=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","The patient completes her final chemotherapy cycle at a reduced dose. She is now 6 weeks post-chemotherapy and her neuropathy symptoms have not improved. She continues to have difficulty with balance and hand function.")
B.b0x=new A.c("+ Outline a comprehensive rehabilitation management plan for persistent CIPN.","**Pharmacologic Management**:\n1. **Duloxetine 60 mg daily** \u2014 the ONLY medication with Level 1 evidence (ASCO guidelines) for CIPN treatment. Start at 30 mg daily for 1 week, then increase to 60 mg. Also addresses her anxiety and neuropathic pain.\n2. **Gabapentin 300 mg TID** (titrate up) \u2014 second-line for neuropathic pain if duloxetine insufficient or not tolerated.\n3. **Topical treatments**: Menthol 1% cream or lidocaine 5% patches for localized foot pain, especially at night.\n4. **Avoid**: Opioids are not indicated for CIPN pain.\n\n**Balance and Gait Training (Physical Therapy)**:\n5. **Proprioceptive retraining**: Progressive balance exercises on foam pads, BOSU balls, single-leg stance with eyes closed.\n6. **Vestibular and sensory integration**: Tai chi or yoga-based balance programs (evidence supports benefit in CIPN).\n7. **Strengthening**: Ankle dorsiflexor and intrinsic foot muscle strengthening; consider ankle-foot orthosis if foot drop progresses.\n8. **Fall prevention program**: Home safety assessment, remove throw rugs, install grab bars, nightlights; consider assistive device (single-point cane) for community ambulation.\n\n**Hand Function (Occupational Therapy)**:\n9. **Fine motor retraining**: Theraputty exercises, pegboard activities, button/zipper practice.\n10. **Adaptive equipment**: Built-up handles for utensils, button hook, ergonomic keyboard, pen grips for writing.\n11. **Desensitization program**: Graded texture exposure for hypersensitivity.\n12. **Workplace modifications**: Ergonomic assessment for her accounting work \u2014 voice-to-text software, wrist rests, modified keyboard.\n\n**Exercise Program**:\n13. **Aerobic exercise**: 150 minutes/week of moderate-intensity activity (walking, cycling, swimming) \u2014 evidence shows exercise improves CIPN symptoms and cancer-related fatigue.\n14. **Resistance training**: 2-3 days/week focusing on lower extremity and core strengthening.\n\n**Monitoring**:\n15. Reassess CIPN grade and functional status every 4-6 weeks. Serial grip strength measurements. Repeat EDX at 6 months if no improvement to document trajectory.")
B.aNI=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient asks about ongoing rehabilitation support. She has been told her oncology visits will become less frequent now that active treatment is over.")
B.aTN=new A.c("+ How would you structure cancer survivorship rehabilitation care for this patient? Discuss coordination with oncology and available service models.","**Cancer Survivorship Rehabilitation Model**:\n\n1. **Survivorship Care Plan**: Per ASCO/ACS guidelines, a formal survivorship care plan should be created documenting her treatment history, known side effects (CIPN), surveillance schedule, and rehabilitation needs. This is shared with the patient, oncologist, and primary care physician.\n\n2. **Cancer Rehabilitation Continuum**:\n- **Preventive (prehabilitation)**: Ideally, baseline functional assessment and exercise counseling would have occurred before chemotherapy started.\n- **Restorative**: Current phase \u2014 active therapy to restore function lost to CIPN.\n- **Supportive**: Ongoing management of persistent impairments.\n- **Palliative**: Not applicable here but part of the continuum.\n\n3. **Prospective Surveillance Model (PSM)**: Standardized screening for impairments at regular intervals (baseline, mid-treatment, end of treatment, 6 months, 12 months). Uses validated tools such as the FACT-Taxane subscale, CIPN-20 questionnaire, or Brief Pain Inventory.\n\n4. **Multidisciplinary Coordination**:\n- **Oncology**: Ongoing tamoxifen management, cancer surveillance, communication about CIPN trajectory.\n- **PM&R/Cancer Rehab**: Quarterly functional assessments, therapy prescriptions, pharmacologic management of CIPN.\n- **PT/OT**: Goal-directed therapy programs with clear discharge criteria and home exercise program.\n- **Psychology**: Screen for cancer-related distress, anxiety, fear of recurrence.\n- **Primary Care**: Long-term cardiovascular monitoring (anthracycline exposure), bone health (tamoxifen effects).\n\n5. **Insurance and Access**: Cancer rehabilitation services are covered under standard therapy benefits. Advocate for therapy referrals with specific cancer rehabilitation diagnoses (G62.0 \u2014 drug-induced polyneuropathy) to ensure appropriate authorization.")
B.aT9=new A.c(u.j,"During the initial evaluation, the patient becomes tearful and admits: 'I've been hiding these symptoms from my oncologist for two months. I was terrified she would stop my chemo and the cancer would come back. Did I make a mistake by not speaking up sooner?'")
B.aVO=new A.c("+ How would you address this patient's fear and counsel her about the importance of symptom reporting?","**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Validate her emotions and normalize the fear**:\n'I really appreciate you sharing that with me, and I want you to know that your fear is very common. Many patients worry that reporting side effects will lead to stopping treatment. That concern comes from a place of wanting to do everything possible to fight the cancer, and that shows incredible strength.'\n\n**2. Gently reframe the misconception**:\n'I want to reassure you that reporting symptoms does not automatically mean stopping chemotherapy. In fact, the opposite is true \u2014 when your oncologist knows about side effects early, she has more options to adjust your treatment while keeping you on track. Dose reductions, schedule changes, and supportive medications can often allow treatment to continue safely.'\n\n**3. Address the guilt without blame**:\n'You did not make a mistake. You made the best decision you could with the information you had. The important thing is that we are addressing this now, and there are effective treatments and therapies that can help improve your symptoms.'\n\n**4. Educate about the consequences of delayed reporting**:\n'What I do want you to take away from this is that early reporting of side effects actually protects your health AND your ability to complete treatment. Neuropathy that is caught early can sometimes be managed with smaller dose adjustments, whereas waiting can lead to more significant changes that are harder to reverse.'\n\n**5. Establish a collaborative symptom-monitoring plan**:\n'Going forward, I would like to set up a simple system. We will use a brief questionnaire at each visit to track your symptoms, and I will communicate directly with your oncologist. You are part of this team \u2014 your observations about your body are critical information that helps us give you the best care.'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Empathy, normalization of fear, non-judgmental counseling, patient empowerment, reframing misconceptions about treatment modification, collaborative communication.")
B.aEj=s([B.b_z,B.aZX,B.b0W,B.aMM,B.b_1,B.aQU,B.aVI,B.b0x,B.aNI,B.aTN,B.aT9,B.aVO],t.E)
B.Rb=new A.a5("cancer-cipn","Chemotherapy-Induced Peripheral Neuropathy",B.aEj,!1)
B.aMX=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 62-year-old male with a known history of Stage IV non-small cell lung cancer (adenocarcinoma) with bone metastases is brought to the emergency department by his wife with a 5-day history of progressively worsening mid-back pain and 48 hours of bilateral lower extremity weakness. The back pain is severe (9/10), localized to the thoracic region, worse when lying flat, and not relieved by over-the-counter analgesics. Over the past two days, he has developed difficulty standing from a chair and now requires a walker. He reports new urinary hesitancy and a sensation of incomplete bladder emptying since yesterday. His wife notes he has seemed increasingly unsteady.")
B.aNn=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **Oncologic History**: Diagnosed 14 months ago with Stage IV NSCLC (EGFR mutation-negative, PD-L1 50%); treated with carboplatin/pemetrexed x 4 cycles, then pembrolizumab maintenance. Known bone metastases to ribs and pelvis. Last imaging 3 months ago showed stable disease.\n- **Pain History**: New thoracic back pain x 5 days, rapidly progressive, worse at night and supine, not relieved by position change \u2014 classic mechanical instability pattern vs. epidural compression\n- **Neurologic Symptoms**: Progressive bilateral LE weakness x 48 hours; urinary hesitancy x 24 hours; no saddle anesthesia reported but not specifically assessed\n- **Functional Decline**: Was community ambulatory with a cane 1 week ago; now requires walker and maximal assist\n- **Medications**: Pembrolizumab q3 weeks, dexamethasone 2 mg daily (for bone pain), oxycodone 10 mg q6h PRN, omeprazole, ondansetron\n- **Comorbidities**: COPD (former 30 pack-year smoker, quit at diagnosis), hypertension, coronary artery disease s/p stent 5 years ago\n- **Psychosocial**: Retired engineer, married, lives in single-story home with wife; two adult children nearby; full code status\n- **Performance Status**: ECOG 2 prior to this presentation")
B.b1U=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 152/94 mmHg, HR 92 bpm, RR 18, SpO2 94% on RA, Temp 37.2C.\n\n**General**: Cachectic-appearing male in moderate distress from pain, lying in lateral decubitus position.\n\n**Spine**: Severe point tenderness over the T7-T8 spinous processes. Percussion tenderness present. No visible deformity. Paraspinal muscle spasm bilaterally.\n\n**Neurologic Exam**:\n- **Mental Status**: Alert, oriented x 4, cognition intact.\n- **Cranial Nerves**: Intact.\n- **Motor**: Upper extremities 5/5 throughout. Lower extremities: hip flexion 3/5 bilaterally, knee extension 4-/5 bilaterally, ankle dorsiflexion 4/5 bilaterally, great toe extension 3/5 bilaterally, ankle plantarflexion 4/5 bilaterally.\n- **Tone**: Mildly increased in bilateral lower extremities (MAS 1+).\n- **Sensation**: Decreased pin prick and light touch below approximately T8 dermatome bilaterally. Proprioception intact at great toes.\n- **Reflexes**: Patellar 3+ bilaterally with 3 beats of clonus at right ankle. Babinski sign positive bilaterally.\n- **Rectal Exam**: Decreased but present perianal sensation. Voluntary anal contraction present but weak. Anal wink present.\n- **ASIA Classification**: T8 ASIA D (incomplete \u2014 motor function preserved below the neurological level with at least half of key muscles grading >= 3).\n\n**Post-void residual**: 280 mL (elevated \u2014 suggestive of neurogenic bladder).")
B.aTc=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","This patient presents with a clinical picture consistent with metastatic epidural spinal cord compression (MESCC). Urgent evaluation and management decisions must be made.")
B.aTG=new A.c("+ What is the urgency of imaging, and how do you use the Tokuhashi Prognostic Score to guide treatment decisions?","**Imaging Urgency**:\n- MESCC is an **oncologic emergency**. MRI of the **entire spine** (with and without gadolinium) must be obtained within **24 hours** of clinical suspicion \u2014 ideally within hours. Whole-spine imaging is essential because **10-38% of patients have multilevel disease**.\n- If MRI is contraindicated, CT myelography is an alternative.\n- Do NOT delay imaging for other workup. Do NOT wait for plain radiographs (sensitivity only 60-70%).\n\n**Expected MRI Findings**: Epidural soft tissue mass at T7-T8 with spinal cord compression and possible signal change within the cord (edema or myelomalacia). Vertebral body involvement with pathologic fracture is common.\n\n**Bilsky Epidural Spinal Cord Compression Scale**:\n- Grade 0: Bone-only disease\n- Grade 1a-c: Epidural impingement without cord compression\n- Grade 2: Cord compression with visible CSF\n- Grade 3: Cord compression without visible CSF\nThis patient likely has **Grade 2-3** compression given his clinical findings.\n\n**Tokuhashi Revised Prognostic Scoring System** (total 0-15 points):\n- General condition (KPS): 1 (KPS 40-70%)\n- Number of extraspinal bone metastases: 1 (1-2 sites)\n- Number of vertebral body metastases: 1 (1-2)\n- Major internal organ metastases: 1 (no major organ mets currently)\n- Primary cancer site: 1 (lung \u2014 poor prognosis primary)\n- Spinal cord palsy: 1 (incomplete \u2014 Frankel C-D)\n- **Estimated total: 6/15**\n\n**Tokuhashi Interpretation**:\n- 0-8 points: Predicted survival <6 months \u2192 palliative/non-operative treatment (radiation)\n- 9-11 points: Predicted survival 6-12 months \u2192 consider palliative surgery or radiation\n- 12-15 points: Predicted survival >12 months \u2192 consider excisional surgery\n\nWith a score of approximately 6, **palliative radiation therapy** would be the primary recommendation, though surgical decompression should be considered given his ambulatory status and incomplete deficit.")
B.aVC=new A.c("+ The spine surgeon and radiation oncologist are consulted. How does the presence of ambulatory function at presentation affect the treatment decision?","**Critical Prognostic Principle**: The single strongest predictor of post-treatment ambulatory function in MESCC is **pre-treatment neurological status**. Patients who are ambulatory at the time of treatment have a **70-90% chance** of maintaining ambulation. Those who are paraplegic have only a **10-15% chance** of regaining ambulation.\n\n**Treatment Decision Framework**:\n\n1. **Immediate Dexamethasone**: Start **high-dose dexamethasone** immediately \u2014 loading dose of **10 mg IV**, followed by **4 mg IV/PO every 6 hours**. This reduces vasogenic edema around the compressed cord and may provide rapid neurological improvement. Taper over 2-4 weeks after definitive treatment.\n\n2. **Surgery vs. Radiation \u2014 Patchell Trial (2005) Evidence**:\n- Landmark RCT showed that **surgery (decompressive laminectomy) followed by radiation** was superior to radiation alone for MESCC with single-level compression\n- Surgery group: 84% maintained ambulation vs. 57% radiation alone\n- Surgery group had longer duration of ambulation (122 vs. 13 days)\n\n3. **This patient's case for surgery**:\n- He is **still ambulatory** (critical window)\n- Single-level compression (T7-T8)\n- Life expectancy likely >3 months\n- ECOG 2 with reasonable surgical fitness\n- Has **incomplete** neurological deficit (ASIA D \u2014 better prognosis)\n\n4. **Surgical contraindications to consider**: Multiple-level compression, radiosensitive tumor, very poor life expectancy (<3 months), medically unfit for surgery, prior radiation to the same field.\n\n5. **NSCLC is relatively radioresistant**, which further supports surgical decompression followed by postoperative radiation (conventional fractionation or SBRT).\n\n**Recommendation**: Advocate for **urgent surgical decompression within 24-48 hours** followed by postoperative radiation therapy, given his preserved ambulatory function and incomplete deficit.")
B.aTj=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","The patient undergoes posterior decompressive laminectomy at T7-T8 with instrumented fusion. Postoperatively, his pain improves significantly. Motor exam shows improvement to 4/5 hip flexion bilaterally. He is transferred to acute inpatient rehabilitation on postoperative day 4.")
B.aV9=new A.c("+ Design a rehabilitation plan for this patient with MESCC in the context of advanced cancer.","**Rehabilitation in the Palliative/Oncologic Context**:\n\n1. **Goal Setting**: Use the **SMART framework** adapted for prognosis. Primary goals are **maintaining ambulation, pain control, and maximizing independence** for the time he has. Avoid overly aggressive or unrealistic goals, but do NOT withhold rehabilitation based on cancer diagnosis alone.\n\n2. **Physical Therapy**:\n- Progressive mobility: bed mobility \u2192 sit-to-stand \u2192 ambulation with assistive device\n- Strengthening: Focus on hip flexors, knee extensors, and ankle dorsiflexors\n- Gait training with appropriate assistive device (rolling walker initially, progress to single-point cane)\n- Endurance training adapted to his COPD and deconditioning\n- Spinal precautions per surgical protocol\n\n3. **Occupational Therapy**:\n- ADL training with energy conservation techniques (cancer-related fatigue)\n- Home safety assessment and equipment recommendations\n- Bathroom equipment (shower chair, grab bars, raised toilet seat)\n\n4. **Pain Management**:\n- Continue dexamethasone taper (steroid-sparing approach)\n- Opioid optimization: Convert PRN oxycodone to scheduled long-acting formulation + breakthrough\n- Neuropathic pain component: Add gabapentin if radicular symptoms persist\n- Non-pharmacologic: Positioning, TENS unit, heat therapy\n\n5. **Bladder Management**:\n- Post-void residual monitoring; if PVR >200 mL, initiate intermittent catheterization program\n- Consider urodynamics if bladder dysfunction persists\n- Urology referral if neurogenic bladder confirmed\n\n6. **DVT Prophylaxis**: High risk (cancer + surgery + immobility) \u2014 LMWH for at least 4 weeks postoperatively.\n\n7. **Nutritional Support**: High-protein diet, nutritional supplementation for cancer cachexia. Albumin and prealbumin monitoring. Dietitian consultation.\n\n8. **Bone Health**: Evaluate for bisphosphonate or denosumab therapy for skeletal metastases. Ensure calcium/vitamin D supplementation. Fall prevention protocol.\n\n9. **Psychosocial**: Chaplaincy, social work, psychology referrals. Cancer support group. Advance care planning discussion initiation.")
B.aMV=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The case manager asks whether this patient is appropriate for acute inpatient rehabilitation versus a skilled nursing facility, given his advanced cancer diagnosis. Additionally, the patient's wife asks about hospice and what would happen if the cancer progresses further.")
B.aWi=new A.c("+ Discuss the framework for the hospice versus rehabilitation decision and goals of care in MESCC.",'**Rehabilitation vs. Hospice \u2014 Not Mutually Exclusive**:\n\n1. **Acute Inpatient Rehabilitation Justification**:\n- He meets the 3-hour therapy tolerance rule\n- SCI is a CMS-13 qualifying diagnosis (even when metastatic)\n- He has realistic, achievable functional goals (ambulation with device, independence in ADLs)\n- Requires physician-directed multidisciplinary care\n- Medical complexity (pain management, neurogenic bladder, DVT risk, immunotherapy decisions)\n\n2. **Goals of Care Framework**:\n- Use the **"REMAP" framework**: Reframe, Expect emotion, Map values, Align, Plan\n- Distinguish between **disease-directed treatment** (immunotherapy continuation) and **comfort-focused care** (hospice)\n- This patient\'s ECOG status and preserved function suggest he is NOT a hospice candidate at this moment\n- Hospice eligibility generally requires estimated prognosis of **6 months or less** with physician certification\n\n3. **Concurrent Care/Open Access Hospice**:\n- Some insurers and Medicare Advantage plans offer **concurrent care** models allowing rehabilitation AND hospice-type services simultaneously\n- Medicare traditional hospice benefit requires **forgoing curative treatment** for the hospice diagnosis\n- Important to discuss these options early so the family understands the transition pathway\n\n4. **Advance Directive Discussion**:\n- Ensure advance directives are documented (healthcare proxy, living will)\n- Discuss code status in context of spinal cord compromise (CPR effectiveness, realistic expectations)\n- POLST form completion if appropriate for the state\n\n5. **Discharge Planning**: Anticipate need for home health PT/OT, durable medical equipment (hospital bed, wheelchair as backup, rolling walker), and outpatient oncology follow-up for radiation therapy and immunotherapy decisions.')
B.aVW=new A.c(u.j,"You are meeting with the patient and his wife to discuss the overall prognosis. His wife has been tearful, and the patient says quietly: 'Doc, just tell me straight \u2014 am I going to be in a wheelchair for the rest of my life? How much time do I have?'")
B.aWs=new A.c("+ Demonstrate how you would deliver prognostic information while maintaining hope for quality of life.","**Model Response Using SPIKES Protocol Adapted for Rehabilitation**:\n\n**1. Set up the environment**: Ensure privacy, sit at eye level, ensure both the patient and wife are comfortable. Put pager on silent.\n\n**2. Assess Perception**: 'Before I share my thoughts, can you tell me what the oncology team has told you about where things stand with the cancer and what you understand about this hospitalization?'\n\n**3. Obtain Invitation**: 'You've asked me to be straight with you, and I respect that. I want to give you honest information. Is it okay if I share what I know, including some things that may be hard to hear?'\n\n**4. Provide Knowledge (with empathy and hope)**:\n'Here is what I can tell you with confidence: The surgery was successful in relieving the pressure on your spinal cord, and we are already seeing your strength improving. The fact that you still have movement in your legs is a very positive sign. With the rehabilitation program we have planned, there is a good chance \u2014 I would say better than 70-80% \u2014 that you will be able to walk again with an assistive device like a cane or walker.'\n\n**Pause. Allow the information to settle.**\n\n'Regarding the bigger picture with the cancer \u2014 I want to be honest that this is a serious situation. The spine involvement tells us the cancer is active, and your oncologist will discuss what treatment options remain. I am not able to give you a specific timeline because every patient is different, and there are treatments available that may help.'\n\n**5. Address Emotions empathetically**:\n'What I CAN promise you is that our goal right now is to get you as strong and as independent as possible so that you can spend your time doing the things that matter most to you. Whether that is being home with your wife, seeing your children, or sitting on your porch \u2014 we are going to work toward that together.'\n\n**6. Summarize and Strategize**:\n'Let us take this one step at a time. Right now, your job is rehabilitation. My job is to help you get the most out of every day. We will have ongoing conversations as things evolve, and you can ask me anything at any time. You are not alone in this.'\n\n**Key principles**: Ask-Tell-Ask method, truth-telling balanced with realistic hope, focusing on function and quality of life, providing a concrete plan, emotional attunement to both patient and family.")
B.aEs=s([B.aMX,B.aNn,B.b1U,B.aTc,B.aTG,B.aVC,B.aTj,B.aV9,B.aMV,B.aWi,B.aVW,B.aWs],t.E)
B.Qk=new A.a5("cancer-metastatic-spinal-cord-compression","Metastatic Epidural Spinal Cord Compression",B.aEs,!1)
B.aO2=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 60-year-old right-handed male is referred to the cancer rehabilitation clinic 6 weeks after total laryngectomy with bilateral selective neck dissection (levels II-IV) for T3N2aM0 squamous cell carcinoma of the larynx. He communicates via writing on a whiteboard and appears frustrated by the slowness of this method. He has a permanent tracheostomy with a heat-moisture exchanger (HME) in place. He completed concurrent chemoradiation (cisplatin + 70 Gy) prior to surgery and has significant radiation fibrosis of the anterior neck. He reports difficulty swallowing anything thicker than thin liquids, a stiff and painful right shoulder that he cannot raise above shoulder level, and progressive social withdrawal. His wife notes he has stopped attending church and refuses phone calls from friends. He lost 14 kg over the past 4 months and remains dependent on supplemental tube feeds via PEG.")
B.aP6=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **Oncologic History**: Laryngeal SCC, T3N2aM0 (stage IVA). 40 pack-year smoking history, quit 6 months ago at diagnosis. 20-year history of heavy alcohol use, currently abstinent 6 months. Prior treatment: concurrent chemoradiation (cisplatin x 3 cycles, 70 Gy to primary + bilateral neck), then salvage total laryngectomy with bilateral selective neck dissection for persistent disease.\n- **Surgical Details**: Total laryngectomy with permanent end tracheostomy. Primary tracheoesophageal puncture (TEP) placed at time of surgery. Bilateral selective neck dissection levels II-IV with sacrifice of right spinal accessory nerve (intraoperative finding of tumor encasement).\n- **Nutrition**: PEG tube placed pre-operatively. Currently tolerating thin liquids by mouth in small sips. Supplemental tube feeds providing 60% of caloric needs. Weight 68 kg (pre-illness weight 82 kg). Prealbumin 14 mg/dL.\n- **Swallowing**: Frequent coughing with thin liquids, sensation of food 'sticking' at the base of tongue, avoids all solid foods. No prior modified barium swallow since surgery.\n- **Voice/Communication**: TEP in place but patient has not been trained on use. Currently communicating via whiteboard and text-to-speech phone app.\n- **Right Shoulder**: Progressive difficulty raising right arm since neck dissection. Pain rated 6/10 in right trapezius region and shoulder. Unable to reach overhead.\n- **Psychosocial**: Married, two adult children. Retired long-haul truck driver (CDL holder). Wife is primary caregiver. Reports depressed mood, anhedonia, poor sleep. PHQ-9 score 18 (moderately severe depression).\n- **Medications**: Gabapentin 300 mg TID (radiation neuropathy), omeprazole 40 mg daily, metoclopramide 10 mg QID, oxycodone 5 mg Q6H PRN, multivitamin, nicotine patch 14 mg\n- **Review of Systems**: Xerostomia, thick saliva, trismus (interincisal opening 22 mm), hypothyroidism (on levothyroxine since radiation), hearing loss bilateral (cisplatin ototoxicity)")
B.b32=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 134/82 mmHg, HR 78 bpm, RR 16, SpO2 97% via tracheostomy on RA, Temp 36.9C, BMI 22.1.\n\n**General**: Thin male with permanent tracheostomy and HME device in place. Communicates via whiteboard. Appears withdrawn with flat affect.\n\n**Head and Neck**:\n- **Tracheostomy**: Permanent end stoma, well-healed, patent. HME in place. No peristomal skin breakdown.\n- **Neck**: Significant radiation fibrosis \u2014 woody, non-pliable tissue throughout anterior and lateral neck bilaterally. Bilateral neck dissection scars well-healed. Limited cervical ROM: flexion 30 degrees, extension 20 degrees, rotation 30 degrees bilaterally.\n- **Oral**: Xerostomia with thick ropey saliva. Trismus with interincisal distance 22 mm (normal >40 mm). Oral mucosa atrophic with radiation changes. No visible fistula.\n- **TEP Site**: Tracheoesophageal puncture site patent, prosthesis in place. No leakage around prosthesis noted.\n\n**Right Shoulder/Upper Extremity**:\n- **Inspection**: Right scapular winging prominent. Right trapezius visibly atrophied compared to left.\n- **ROM**: Right shoulder \u2014 active flexion 85 degrees, passive 140 degrees; active abduction 70 degrees, passive 130 degrees. Left shoulder full ROM.\n- **Strength**: Right upper trapezius 2/5, right middle trapezius 3-/5, right lower trapezius 3/5. Right deltoid 4/5, right serratus anterior 4/5. Left upper extremity 5/5 throughout.\n- **Special Tests**: Positive scapular winging with forward flexion. Pain with right shoulder active abduction 60-90 degrees.\n\n**Neurologic**: CN XI (spinal accessory) \u2014 right trapezius and SCM weakness. Remaining cranial nerves grossly intact. Hearing diminished bilaterally (unable to hear whispered voice at 2 feet). Peripheral sensation intact.\n\n**Respiratory**: Breathing comfortably through tracheostomy. Lungs clear to auscultation. Peak cough flow reduced secondary to laryngectomy (loss of glottic closure for effective cough).")
B.aX_=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The head and neck surgery team asks you to co-manage this patient's swallowing dysfunction and assess his candidacy for alaryngeal speech rehabilitation.")
B.b01=new A.c("+ Describe the swallowing evaluation approach in a post-laryngectomy patient. What are the key risks, and how does swallowing physiology differ from pre-laryngectomy anatomy?","**Post-Laryngectomy Swallowing Physiology**:\n\nAfter total laryngectomy, the airway and digestive tracts are **permanently separated**. The trachea is sutured to the skin as a permanent stoma, and the pharynx is reconstructed as a closed tube (neopharynx) connecting the oropharynx to the esophagus. This means:\n- **Aspiration risk is eliminated** in an intact laryngectomy (no connection between airway and food passage)\n- Dysphagia is caused by **stricture, fibrosis, or impaired pharyngeal propulsion** rather than airway protection failure\n- The reconstructed neopharynx may have altered compliance and peristalsis\n\n**Swallowing Evaluation Approach**:\n1. **Modified barium swallow (MBS)**: Assess neopharyngeal transit, detect stricture at the pharyngoesophageal (PE) segment, evaluate base-of-tongue retraction (often impaired after radiation), and identify pooling or residue.\n2. **Esophagram**: Evaluate for distal esophageal stricture if symptoms suggest obstruction below the PE segment.\n3. **Fiberoptic endoscopic evaluation**: Via the stoma, can visualize the neopharynx and PE segment from above.\n\n**Key Risks and Complications**:\n1. **Pharyngocutaneous fistula**: Abnormal communication between the neopharynx and skin \u2014 presents as saliva or food leaking through the neck wound. Risk factors include prior radiation, malnutrition (low prealbumin as in this patient), diabetes, and hypothyroidism. **This is the most feared early complication** \u2014 requires NPO status, wound care, and possible surgical repair.\n2. **Pharyngoesophageal stricture**: Narrowing at the reconstruction site. Common after chemoradiation + surgery. Presents as progressive solid food dysphagia. May require serial dilation.\n3. **Radiation fibrosis**: Causes reduced pharyngeal contraction, base-of-tongue retropulsion weakness, and impaired PE segment opening. Progressive and may worsen for months to years after radiation.\n4. **Pseudo-epiglottic fold**: Tissue fold at the base of tongue that can impede bolus transit.\n\n**This Patient's Dysphagia Workup**:\n- MBS to evaluate neopharyngeal transit and PE segment patency\n- Assess base-of-tongue driving force (likely weakened from radiation)\n- Evaluate for stricture requiring dilation\n- Nutritional optimization (prealbumin 14 is suboptimal \u2014 target >20)\n- Trismus management to improve oral preparatory phase (interincisal opening only 22 mm)")
B.b3s=new A.c("+ The patient's TEP prosthesis is in place but he has not been trained. Describe the three main alaryngeal speech options, their mechanisms, and candidacy factors.","**Alaryngeal Speech Options After Total Laryngectomy**:\n\n**1. Tracheoesophageal Puncture (TEP) Speech** \u2014 Gold Standard:\n- **Mechanism**: A one-way valve prosthesis is placed in a surgically created fistula between the posterior tracheal wall and the anterior esophageal wall. When the patient occludes the stoma (with a finger or hands-free valve), exhaled air is shunted through the prosthesis into the esophagus and vibrates the PE segment (neovibratory source), producing voice.\n- **Advantages**: Most natural-sounding voice, highest speech intelligibility (80-90%), fastest rehabilitation, adequate volume and phrase length.\n- **Candidacy**: Requires adequate pulmonary function to generate airflow, intact PE segment vibration (no spasm or hypotonicity), manual dexterity to occlude stoma (or hands-free valve), and motivation for prosthesis care.\n- **Maintenance**: Prosthesis requires replacement every 3-6 months due to candidal colonization. Patient or caregiver must learn cleaning and troubleshooting.\n- **Complications**: Prosthesis leakage (through or around), candidal colonization, granulation tissue, aspiration through the puncture site if prosthesis dislodges.\n\n**2. Electrolarynx (Artificial Larynx)**:\n- **Mechanism**: A battery-powered handheld device that generates vibration. Placed against the neck or cheek, the vibrations are transmitted through tissue into the oral cavity. The patient articulates normally to shape the sound into speech.\n- **Advantages**: Easy to learn, immediately available, no surgical procedure needed, inexpensive.\n- **Disadvantages**: Mechanical, robotic voice quality; requires one hand to hold device (limits function); can be difficult to use with radiation fibrosis (poor sound transmission through fibrotic tissue \u2014 a significant issue for this patient); reduced intelligibility in noisy environments.\n- **Candidacy**: Good option for immediate communication while learning TEP speech, or as backup when TEP is non-functional.\n\n**3. Esophageal Speech**:\n- **Mechanism**: The patient learns to insufflate air into the upper esophagus and then release it in a controlled belch. The vibrating PE segment produces sound that is articulated into speech.\n- **Advantages**: No device or prosthesis needed, both hands free.\n- **Disadvantages**: Most difficult to learn (only 25-30% achieve functional fluency), quiet voice, short phrase length (4-5 syllables per air charge), long learning curve (months to years).\n- **Candidacy**: Intact PE segment, strong motivation, adequate cognitive function, good SLP support.\n\n**Recommendation for This Patient**:\n- **Primary**: TEP speech training \u2014 prosthesis already in place. Refer to SLP experienced in alaryngeal speech rehabilitation. Start with digital stoma occlusion technique, progress to hands-free HME valve if successful.\n- **Interim**: Electrolarynx for immediate communication needs. May have reduced effectiveness due to radiation fibrosis \u2014 try intraoral adapter if neck placement is ineffective.\n- **Backup**: Text-to-speech apps on smartphone/tablet for complex communication needs.")
B.aTP=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","The MBS reveals moderate pharyngeal residue with thin liquids and severe residue with purees. No stricture is identified but base-of-tongue driving force is significantly reduced. The patient is also struggling with right shoulder dysfunction and worsening radiation fibrosis.")
B.aYF=new A.c("+ Develop a comprehensive rehabilitation plan addressing this patient's swallowing, shoulder dysfunction, radiation fibrosis, and nutritional needs.","**1. Swallowing Rehabilitation (SLP-Directed)**:\n- **Tongue base exercises**: Effortful swallow, Masako (tongue-hold) maneuver, and tongue base retraction exercises to strengthen propulsive force.\n- **Mendelsohn maneuver**: To improve PE segment opening duration and bolus transit.\n- **Diet progression**: Advance from thin liquids to nectar-thick, then purees as residue decreases. Track percentage of oral intake versus tube feeds weekly.\n- **PEG tube management**: Maintain PEG for supplemental feeds until patient consistently meets >75% of caloric needs orally. Do NOT remove prematurely.\n- **Oral care**: Aggressive xerostomia management \u2014 saliva substitutes, frequent water sips, pilocarpine 5 mg TID if tolerated. Fluoride trays for dental protection.\n\n**2. Trismus Management**:\n- **Therabite or Dynasplint jaw device**: Progressive passive stretch protocol 3 times daily for 5-10 minutes. Goal: interincisal opening >35 mm.\n- **Tongue depressor stacking**: Simple home exercise supplement.\n- **Monitor**: Weekly measurements of interincisal distance.\n\n**3. Right Shoulder \u2014 Spinal Accessory Nerve Palsy Rehabilitation**:\n- **Phase 1 (Current \u2014 6 weeks post-op)**: PROM to maintain glenohumeral and scapulothoracic mobility. Scapular stabilization exercises within pain-free range. Pendulum exercises. Ice and NSAID for pain management.\n- **Phase 2 (8-12 weeks)**: Active-assisted and active scapular retraction, protraction, elevation. Serratus anterior and rhomboid strengthening to compensate for trapezius weakness. Progressive resistance band exercises.\n- **Phase 3 (3-6 months)**: If no accessory nerve recovery, focus on compensatory strengthening of levator scapulae, rhomboids, serratus anterior. Consider EMG at 3-4 months to assess reinnervation potential.\n- **Surgical option**: If no nerve recovery by 9-12 months, consider Eden-Lange muscle transfer (transfer of levator scapulae and rhomboids to replace trapezius function) or nerve grafting if identified early.\n- **Pain management**: Gabapentin continuation, topical lidocaine patches to trapezius region, trigger point injections PRN.\n\n**4. Radiation Fibrosis Syndrome Management**:\n- **Manual therapy**: Myofascial release, soft tissue mobilization of fibrotic neck tissues by therapist trained in oncology rehabilitation.\n- **Cervical ROM exercises**: Gentle active and passive neck ROM 3-4 times daily.\n- **Pentoxifylline 400 mg TID + Vitamin E 1000 IU daily**: Evidence-based combination for radiation fibrosis (PENTOCLO protocol). Discuss with oncologist.\n- **Hyperbaric oxygen therapy**: Consider referral if fibrosis is progressive and refractory to conservative measures.\n\n**5. Nutritional Optimization**:\n- **Caloric goal**: 30-35 kcal/kg/day (2040-2380 kcal/day) with 1.5 g protein/kg/day.\n- **Dietitian-guided plan**: Optimize oral intake with calorie-dense, easy-to-swallow foods (smoothies, protein shakes, soups). Supplement remainder via PEG.\n- **Monitor**: Weekly weights, monthly prealbumin (goal >20 mg/dL).\n\n**6. Depression Management**:\n- PHQ-9 of 18 warrants treatment. Start SSRI (sertraline 50 mg daily \u2014 avoid duloxetine with hepatic concerns given alcohol history).\n- Psycho-oncology referral for adjustment disorder with depressed mood.\n- Screen for suicidal ideation (head/neck cancer patients have among the highest suicide rates of all cancer diagnoses).")
B.b2o=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient asks how all these therapies will be coordinated and whether his insurance will cover speech rehabilitation devices. He also wants to know about smoking cessation support since he is struggling with cravings.")
B.aUl=new A.c("+ Describe the multidisciplinary coordination model for post-laryngectomy rehabilitation, insurance considerations for communication devices, and smoking cessation integration.","**Multidisciplinary Team Coordination**:\n1. **Speech-Language Pathologist (SLP)**: Central role \u2014 TEP speech training, swallowing therapy, trismus exercises, electrolarynx fitting. Should be SLP with **head and neck cancer specialization**. Frequency: 2-3 sessions/week initially.\n2. **Physiatrist (PM&R)**: Overall rehabilitation plan coordination, shoulder dysfunction management, radiation fibrosis treatment, medication management, functional outcome tracking.\n3. **Head & Neck Surgeon/ENT**: Ongoing cancer surveillance (every 2-3 months year 1), TEP prosthesis replacement, stricture management, fistula monitoring.\n4. **Medical Oncologist**: Surveillance imaging, management of treatment sequelae.\n5. **Radiation Oncologist**: Monitoring radiation late effects (fibrosis, hypothyroidism, carotid stenosis screening).\n6. **Dietitian**: Nutritional optimization, PEG feed management, oral diet advancement guidance.\n7. **Psychologist/Psychiatrist**: Depression treatment, adjustment counseling, body image support.\n8. **Physical Therapist**: Shoulder rehabilitation, cervical ROM, general deconditioning.\n9. **Audiologist**: Cisplatin ototoxicity monitoring, hearing aid evaluation.\n\n**Cancer Survivorship Program**: Integrate into a formal survivorship clinic with a survivorship care plan documenting all treatments received, ongoing side effects, surveillance schedule, and rehabilitation needs.\n\n**Insurance and Device Coverage**:\n- **TEP prosthesis**: Covered as a prosthetic device under most insurance plans (Medicare Part B covers as DME). Replacement prostheses typically covered every 3-6 months.\n- **Electrolarynx**: Covered as a speech-generating device under Medicare and most commercial plans. Requires SLP prescription and documentation of medical necessity.\n- **HME/hands-free speaking valves**: Often require prior authorization. Document medical necessity (pulmonary hygiene, hands-free communication for safety).\n- **Speech therapy**: Covered under rehabilitation benefits. May require pre-authorization. Document specific alaryngeal speech rehabilitation goals.\n- **Therabite/trismus device**: Often covered under DME benefit with documentation of radiation-induced trismus.\n\n**Smoking Cessation Integration**:\n- **Current status**: Abstinent 6 months on nicotine patch, but reporting cravings.\n- **Continue pharmacotherapy**: Nicotine patch taper. Consider adding nicotine lozenge for breakthrough cravings. Discuss varenicline or bupropion if cravings persist.\n- **Behavioral counseling**: Refer to tobacco cessation counselor or quitline (1-800-QUIT-NOW). Cognitive behavioral techniques for craving management.\n- **Motivation**: Continued smoking after laryngectomy increases risk of stomal recurrence, second primary cancers, and impaired wound healing. Frame cessation as integral to rehabilitation success.\n- **Support group**: Laryngectomee support groups often reinforce cessation (International Association of Laryngectomees, WebWhispers online community).")
B.aWH=new A.c(u.j,"During the rehabilitation evaluation, the patient writes on his whiteboard: 'Nobody will understand me anymore. I can't talk, I can't eat, I can barely move my arm. I'm useless to my family. What is the point of all this rehab?' His wife, sitting beside him, begins to cry.")
B.aNV=new A.c("+ How would you address this patient's profound sense of loss and hopelessness while also supporting his wife as caregiver?","**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Create space for the grief and validate both patient and caregiver**:\n'I hear you, and I want you to know that what you are feeling makes complete sense. You have been through an enormous ordeal \u2014 losing your natural voice, struggling to eat, dealing with pain and physical changes. These are real losses, and you have every right to grieve them. [Turning to wife] And I can see how much this is affecting you too. You are carrying a heavy burden as well.'\n\n**2. Acknowledge the voice loss specifically \u2014 it is an identity loss**:\n'Your voice was part of who you are. It is how you connected with your wife, your children, your friends. Losing it is not just a physical change \u2014 it is a loss of something deeply personal. I do not want to minimize that.'\n\n**3. Offer realistic hope without false promises**:\n'What I can tell you is that you are only six weeks out from surgery, and you have a tracheoesophageal prosthesis already in place. Many patients who feel exactly the way you feel right now go on to communicate very effectively with TEP speech. It sounds different from your original voice, but most people who learn it say they can have normal conversations \u2014 on the phone, at dinner, even in groups. I have seen patients return to social lives they thought were over.'\n\n**4. Reframe \"useless\" with concrete evidence**:\n'You said you are useless to your family. But your wife is here beside you right now, and she is here because you matter to her \u2014 not because of what you can do physically, but because of who you are. The fact that you showed up to this appointment and are engaging in this conversation tells me you still have fight in you, even when it does not feel that way.'\n\n**5. Address the suicidality screen directly**:\n'When you say, \"what is the point,\" I want to make sure I understand what you mean. Are you having thoughts of not wanting to be here, or of harming yourself?' [Screen for active suicidal ideation. Head and neck cancer patients have elevated suicide risk. If positive, escalate immediately with psychiatry consultation and safety planning.]\n\n**6. Create a tangible near-term goal**:\n'Rather than thinking about everything at once, I would like to focus on one thing together this week. Let us start speech therapy so you can try your TEP voice. If we can get you speaking again \u2014 even a few words \u2014 I think it will change how you feel about everything else. Would you be willing to try that?'\n\n**7. Support the caregiver**:\n'[To wife] I also want to make sure you have support. Caregiving for someone after laryngectomy is exhausting, and it is not something you should do alone. We have a social worker and a caregiver support group I would like to connect you with. Your wellbeing matters too \u2014 you cannot pour from an empty cup.'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Grief validation for both patient and caregiver, direct screening for suicidal ideation in a high-risk population, realistic hope grounded in evidence, reframing self-worth, concrete goal-setting for agency, caregiver support integration.")
B.WV=s([B.aO2,B.aP6,B.b32,B.aX_,B.b01,B.b3s,B.aTP,B.aYF,B.b2o,B.aUl,B.aWH,B.aNV],t.E)
B.Qv=new A.a5("cancer-head-neck-laryngectomy","Head & Neck Cancer \u2014 Post-Laryngectomy Rehabilitation",B.WV,!1)
B.aSW=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 52-year-old right-hand-dominant female is referred to the lymphedema clinic with progressive right upper extremity swelling 2 years after right modified radical mastectomy with axillary lymph node dissection (ALND, 18 of 22 nodes removed, 4 positive) and adjuvant radiation to the right chest wall and axilla for Stage IIIA (T2N2M0) invasive ductal carcinoma. She noticed mild hand swelling 6 months ago that she attributed to warm weather. Over the past 3 months, the swelling has progressed to involve the entire right arm and is now constantly present. She has had two episodes of cellulitis in the past year requiring oral antibiotics. She reports heaviness, tightness, and intermittent aching in the arm. She has stopped attending her book club and social gatherings, telling her husband she is embarrassed by the appearance of her arm. She works as a high school art teacher and is having difficulty demonstrating painting techniques.")
B.aYR=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **Oncologic History**: Right breast invasive ductal carcinoma, ER+/PR-/HER2+, Stage IIIA. Treated with neoadjuvant AC-TH (doxorubicin/cyclophosphamide, then docetaxel/trastuzumab), right MRM with ALND (18 nodes removed, 4/18 positive), adjuvant chest wall and regional nodal radiation (50 Gy), completing trastuzumab, and now on letrozole (aromatase inhibitor). Last imaging (PET/CT) 3 months ago showed no evidence of recurrence.\n- **Lymphedema Timeline**: First noticed right hand puffiness at 18 months post-surgery. Progressive involvement of forearm, then entire arm. No precipitating event (no injury, infection, or air travel at onset).\n- **Infections**: Two episodes of right arm cellulitis \u2014 treated with cephalexin each time. No hospitalizations for cellulitis.\n- **Functional Impact**: Difficulty with overhead reaching, carrying objects >5 lbs with right arm, prolonged painting/drawing causes increased swelling. Has modified teaching to rely more on verbal instruction.\n- **BMI**: 31.2 (obese). Weight has increased 10 kg since completing chemotherapy.\n- **Comorbidities**: Hypertension, prediabetes (HbA1c 6.2%), osteopenia (letrozole-related), chemotherapy-induced amenorrhea\n- **Medications**: Letrozole 2.5 mg daily, lisinopril 10 mg daily, metformin 500 mg BID, calcium/vitamin D, acetaminophen PRN\n- **Previous Lymphedema Treatment**: Wore an off-the-shelf compression sleeve (20-30 mmHg) for 2 months \u2014 discontinued because it was uncomfortable and did not seem to help. No formal CDT.\n- **Psychosocial**: Married, 2 teenage children. Works full-time as art teacher. Previously very active socially. Now avoids sleeveless tops, avoids pool parties, stopped wearing her wedding ring (does not fit right hand). PHQ-9 score 12 (moderate depression).")
B.aPp=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 138/84 mmHg, HR 72 bpm, RR 14, SpO2 99% on RA, Temp 36.7C, BMI 31.2.\n\n**Right Upper Extremity**:\n- **Inspection**: Diffuse swelling from hand to axilla. Loss of normal anatomic contours at wrist and elbow. Skin thickened with peau d'orange texture over dorsum of hand and forearm. Positive **Stemmer sign** (inability to pinch and lift a skin fold at the base of the second toe/finger \u2014 in this case, unable to tent skin at base of right 2nd digit). No skin breakdown. Well-healed mastectomy scar. No erythema or warmth to suggest active cellulitis.\n- **Circumferential Measurements** (compared to left):\n  - Hand (metacarpal): R 22.5 cm, L 19.0 cm (+3.5 cm)\n  - Wrist: R 19.0 cm, L 16.5 cm (+2.5 cm)\n  - Forearm (10 cm below olecranon): R 30.0 cm, L 25.5 cm (+4.5 cm)\n  - Elbow: R 31.0 cm, L 27.0 cm (+4.0 cm)\n  - Upper arm (10 cm above olecranon): R 38.0 cm, L 32.0 cm (+6.0 cm)\n- **Volume**: Estimated right arm volume excess approximately 35-40% compared to left (by truncated cone formula).\n- **Tissue Consistency**: Pitting edema present at hand and forearm with slow rebound (>10 seconds). Non-pitting, fibrotic tissue at upper arm with firm induration.\n- **ROM**: Right shoulder flexion 140 degrees (limited by tissue bulk), abduction 120 degrees. Elbow, wrist, and hand ROM functionally full but limited by swelling.\n- **Strength**: Right grip 18 kg (left 28 kg). Right shoulder and elbow strength 4+/5 (limited by heaviness and fatigue).\n\n**Left Upper Extremity**: Normal. No swelling.\n\n**Chest Wall**: Right mastectomy scar well-healed. Mild chest wall lymphedema with thickened skin in right axillary fold. Radiation skin changes (hyperpigmentation, telangiectasia) over right anterior chest.\n\n**Vascular**: Radial and ulnar pulses 2+ bilaterally. No axillary or supraclavicular lymphadenopathy. No venous distension.\n\n**Neurologic**: Intact sensation bilaterally. No signs of brachial plexopathy.")
B.aOn=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The referring oncologist wants you to stage the lymphedema, rule out recurrence or venous disease, and establish a treatment plan.")
B.aXc=new A.c("+ Stage this patient's lymphedema using the ISL classification. What diagnostic workup should be performed, and what are the key differential diagnoses?","**International Society of Lymphology (ISL) Staging**:\n- **Stage 0 (Subclinical)**: Lymphatic damage present but no visible swelling. Detectable by bioimpedance or lymphoscintigraphy.\n- **Stage I (Spontaneously reversible)**: Early accumulation of protein-rich fluid. Pitting edema that reduces with elevation. Subsides with overnight rest.\n- **Stage II Early**: Limb elevation alone rarely reduces swelling. Pitting is present.\n- **Stage II Late**: Progressive tissue fibrosis. May or may not pit. Skin changes beginning.\n- **Stage III (Lymphostatic elephantiasis)**: Pitting may be absent. Skin changes \u2014 papillomas, hyperkeratosis, massive volume increase.\n\n**This Patient: ISL Stage II Late** \u2014 She has a combination of pitting edema (hand/forearm) and non-pitting fibrotic tissue (upper arm), positive Stemmer sign, peau d'orange skin changes, and the swelling does not resolve with elevation or overnight rest. She is transitioning toward Stage III without intervention.\n\n**Severity by Volume**: >40% volume excess = **severe** lymphedema.\n\n**Required Diagnostic Workup**:\n1. **Duplex ultrasound of right upper extremity**: Rule out **deep vein thrombosis (DVT)** \u2014 essential before initiating compression therapy. Upper extremity DVT can occur in cancer patients (especially with central lines, radiation).\n2. **Cross-sectional imaging**: Review recent PET/CT for any evidence of axillary or supraclavicular nodal recurrence that could cause lymphatic obstruction. If >6 months since last scan, consider updated imaging.\n3. **Bioimpedance spectroscopy (L-Dex)**: Measures extracellular fluid volume. L-Dex >10 suggests significant lymphedema. Useful for ongoing monitoring and documenting treatment response.\n4. **Lymphoscintigraphy**: If diagnosis is uncertain or to plan surgical intervention. Shows delayed or absent lymphatic transport in the affected limb.\n5. **Basic labs**: CBC (rule out infection), CMP, albumin (hypoalbuminemia causes generalized edema), TSH (hypothyroidism can cause edema).\n\n**Key Differential Diagnoses**:\n1. **Cancer recurrence** with lymphatic obstruction \u2014 most critical to rule out\n2. **Deep vein thrombosis** \u2014 can coexist with lymphedema\n3. **Cellulitis** \u2014 recurrent in lymphedema; no current signs\n4. **Radiation-induced brachial plexopathy** with autonomic changes \u2014 no neurologic signs here\n5. **Medication-induced edema** \u2014 review medication list\n6. **Chronic venous insufficiency** \u2014 typically lower extremity, but can contribute\n7. **Heart failure or nephrotic syndrome** \u2014 bilateral edema expected, not unilateral\n8. **Lipedema** \u2014 bilateral, symmetric, spares hands and feet, non-pitting")
B.b1z=new A.c("+ The patient had a bioimpedance reading of L-Dex 42 at today's visit. Her baseline pre-surgical L-Dex was 2. Explain bioimpedance spectroscopy and its role in lymphedema surveillance.","**Bioimpedance Spectroscopy (BIS) \u2014 L-Dex Technology**:\n\n**Mechanism**: BIS measures the impedance (resistance) of tissue to a small, painless electrical current passed between electrodes placed on the hands and feet. Extracellular fluid (edema) has low impedance, while intracellular fluid has higher impedance. By measuring at multiple frequencies, BIS can **differentiate extracellular fluid from total body water**, providing an objective measure of lymphedema.\n\n**L-Dex Score Interpretation**:\n- Compares the affected limb's impedance ratio to the contralateral limb\n- **Normal range**: -10 to +10\n- **L-Dex >10**: Subclinical or clinical lymphedema likely\n- **L-Dex >20**: Established lymphedema\n- **This patient's L-Dex of 42**: Severe lymphedema with marked extracellular fluid accumulation, consistent with ISL Stage II late\n\n**Role in Prospective Surveillance Model (PSM)**:\n1. **Pre-surgical baseline**: Measure L-Dex before any breast cancer surgery (this patient's baseline was 2 \u2014 normal).\n2. **Serial monitoring**: Measure at each follow-up visit (every 3-6 months post-surgery). An increase of >10 from individual baseline triggers early intervention.\n3. **Early detection advantage**: BIS can detect subclinical lymphedema (Stage 0/I) **before clinically visible swelling**, allowing early compression intervention that can prevent progression.\n4. **Treatment response tracking**: Serial L-Dex measurements objectively document response to CDT, compliance with compression, and detect early recurrence.\n\n**Limitations**:\n- Requires contralateral limb for comparison (bilateral lymphedema cannot be assessed)\n- Affected by body composition changes, hydration status, and implanted devices (pacemakers are a contraindication)\n- Does not distinguish lymphedema from other causes of edema\n\n**Evidence**: The PREVENT trial demonstrated that prospective BIS surveillance with early compression intervention reduced the rate of clinical lymphedema progression by approximately 50% compared to standard follow-up. This has shifted the paradigm toward **prospective surveillance rather than reactive treatment**.")
B.aWQ=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Duplex ultrasound is negative for DVT. Recent PET/CT shows no recurrence. You confirm ISL Stage II Late lymphedema with severe volume excess. The patient asks what treatment options are available.")
B.b3L=new A.c("+ Outline the phases of Complete Decongestive Therapy (CDT) and discuss advanced interventions for refractory lymphedema.","**Complete Decongestive Therapy (CDT) \u2014 Standard of Care for Moderate-Severe Lymphedema**:\n\n**Phase I \u2014 Intensive Reduction Phase (2-6 weeks)**:\n1. **Manual Lymphatic Drainage (MLD)**: Specialized light-pressure massage technique that redirects lymph fluid from congested areas to functional lymph node basins. Performed by a **certified lymphedema therapist (CLT)** 4-5 days/week. Starts proximally (clearing trunk and unaffected quadrants first), then progresses distally.\n2. **Short-stretch compression bandaging**: Multi-layer bandaging applied after each MLD session. Uses **short-stretch bandages** (low resting pressure, high working pressure) \u2014 NOT elastic ACE wraps. Includes foam padding for even pressure distribution. Worn 23 hours/day, rewrapped daily.\n3. **Remedial exercises**: Performed while bandaged to enhance lymphatic pumping. Includes diaphragmatic breathing, shoulder pumps, fist clenching, arm elevation exercises. Low-intensity, repetitive movements.\n4. **Meticulous skin care**: Daily moisturizing with low-pH lotion. Inspection for cuts, fungal infection, or signs of cellulitis. Nail care to prevent ingrown nails. Goal: prevent cellulitis, which worsens lymphedema with each episode.\n\n**Expected Phase I Outcome**: 40-60% reduction in excess limb volume over 2-6 weeks.\n\n**Phase II \u2014 Maintenance Phase (lifelong)**:\n1. **Custom compression garment**: Flat-knit, custom-measured compression sleeve + gauntlet/glove. **30-40 mmHg** (Class II) or **40-50 mmHg** (Class III) depending on severity. Worn during all waking hours. Replaced every 6 months.\n2. **Self-MLD**: Patient trained to perform simplified MLD daily.\n3. **Compression garment wear**: Daytime compression sleeve + nighttime compression bandaging or alternative nighttime garment system (e.g., Solaris or JoViPak).\n4. **Exercise**: Regular aerobic and resistance exercise WITH compression garment. Progressive resistance training is safe and does NOT worsen lymphedema (ACOSOG Z0011, PAL trial).\n5. **Skin care**: Ongoing daily moisturizing and skin inspection.\n6. **Weight management**: Critical \u2014 obesity is a modifiable risk factor for lymphedema progression.\n\n**Adjunctive Therapies**:\n- **Intermittent pneumatic compression (IPC)**: Multi-chamber sequential pump used 30-60 minutes daily at home. Supplements CDT but does NOT replace it. Covered by most insurance with prior authorization.\n- **Low-level laser therapy**: May reduce limb volume and tissue fibrosis. 2-3 sessions/week during active treatment.\n- **Kinesiology taping**: Decongestive taping applied between CDT sessions for mild additional fluid movement.\n\n**Surgical Options for Refractory Cases**:\n1. **Lymphovenous anastomosis (LVA)**: Microsurgical procedure connecting lymphatic channels directly to venules. Best results in early-stage lymphedema with functioning lymphatics. Minimally invasive, can be performed under local anesthesia.\n2. **Vascularized lymph node transfer (VLNT)**: Harvest functional lymph nodes (commonly from groin or submental region) and transplant to the affected axilla. Promotes lymphangiogenesis. Best for patients without functional proximal lymphatics.\n3. **Suction-assisted protein lipectomy (SAPL/liposuction)**: For chronic Stage II-III lymphedema where excess tissue is primarily adipose/fibrotic rather than fluid. Requires lifelong strict compression garment use after surgery.\n\n**For This Patient**: Start Phase I CDT immediately (4-5 sessions/week for 4-6 weeks), then transition to Phase II maintenance. Add IPC for home use. Refer to a microsurgeon experienced in lymphatic surgery for evaluation if CDT response is suboptimal after 6 months.")
B.aVh=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient is concerned about the cost of treatment. She asks about insurance coverage for compression garments and whether there are certified therapists near her rural home 90 minutes from the clinic.")
B.b1J=new A.c("+ Discuss CLT certification requirements, insurance coverage challenges for lymphedema care, and how to address access barriers.","**Certified Lymphedema Therapist (CLT) Requirements**:\n- Must be a licensed healthcare professional (PT, OT, RN, MD, or massage therapist in some states)\n- Complete a minimum **135-hour certification course** accredited by LANA (Lymphology Association of North America) or equivalent body\n- Curriculum covers: anatomy and pathophysiology of the lymphatic system, CDT techniques (MLD, bandaging, exercise, skin care), assessment and measurement, compression garment selection, and clinical practicum\n- **LANA certification**: Voluntary board certification exam. CLT-LANA designation is the gold standard. Requires recertification every 6 years.\n- **Why this matters**: Non-certified providers may apply incorrect techniques (standard massage is contraindicated, elastic wraps are ineffective). Refer ONLY to CLT-certified therapists.\n\n**Insurance Coverage \u2014 Current Landscape**:\n1. **CDT therapy sessions**: Generally covered under outpatient PT/OT benefits. Document diagnosis G97.2 (postprocedural lymphedema) and specific functional limitations. May require prior authorization.\n2. **Compression garments**: This is a **major coverage gap**. Medicare historically does NOT cover compression garments (no benefit category). Many commercial plans also exclude them. However, the **Lymphedema Treatment Act (signed into law 2022, effective 2024)** now requires Medicare to cover compression supplies including garments, bandaging systems, and pneumatic devices.\n3. **Pneumatic compression devices**: Covered by Medicare under DME benefit with documentation of medical necessity (trial of CDT, measurements showing persistent edema). Requires prior authorization and face-to-face evaluation documentation.\n4. **Custom garments cost**: $200-800 per garment (sleeve + gauntlet/glove), needed bilaterally if both arms affected, replaced every 6 months. Financial burden for uninsured or underinsured patients.\n\n**Addressing Access Barriers for This Patient**:\n1. **Telehealth hybrid model**: Intensive Phase I CDT requires in-person visits. Consider concentrated 4-week daily treatment block (patient stays near clinic or arranges temporary housing). Then transition to telehealth monitoring for Phase II maintenance with periodic in-person reassessments.\n2. **Training a local therapist**: If a local PT/OT is interested, provide mentorship while they pursue CLT certification. The patient's community benefits long-term.\n3. **Self-management education**: Invest heavily in Phase II self-MLD training, self-bandaging, and garment donning/doffing education. Ensure patient is independent before discharge from intensive phase.\n4. **Patient advocacy resources**: National Lymphedema Network (NLN), Lymphatic Education & Research Network (LE&RN), and the Lymphedema Advocacy Group offer financial assistance programs, garment banks, and peer support.\n5. **Survivorship clinic integration**: Embed lymphedema screening and management into the cancer survivorship program. Prospective surveillance with BIS at every oncology follow-up to catch recurrence or worsening early.")
B.aVa=new A.c(u.j,"Near the end of the evaluation, the patient becomes tearful and says: 'I can't wear anything nice with this arm. I haven't worn my wedding ring in months. My husband says he doesn't care, but I see him look away. I stopped going to book club because I don't want people to see me like this. I feel like cancer took my body and now lymphedema is taking my life.'")
B.aQK=new A.c("+ How would you address this patient's body image distress, social isolation, and psychosocial impact of lymphedema?","**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Validate the emotional weight of lymphedema as a visible, chronic condition**:\n'What you are describing is something I hear from many of my lymphedema patients, and I want you to know that your feelings are completely valid. Lymphedema is not just swelling \u2014 it is a constant, visible reminder of everything you have been through, and it affects how you see yourself and how you move through the world. That is a real and legitimate burden.'\n\n**2. Acknowledge the specific losses she named**:\n'Not being able to wear your wedding ring, changing how you dress, pulling away from your book club \u2014 each of those is a loss. You are not being vain or dramatic. These are the things that make up your daily life and identity, and lymphedema has intruded on all of them.'\n\n**3. Offer concrete hope tied to the treatment plan**:\n'Here is what I want you to know: with proper treatment, we typically see a 40 to 60 percent reduction in arm volume during the intensive phase. That means your arm can get significantly smaller. Many of my patients are able to wear their rings again, wear short sleeves comfortably, and return to activities they had given up. I cannot promise it will go back to exactly how it was, but I can tell you there is a lot of room for improvement from where you are today.'\n\n**4. Address the relationship concern with sensitivity**:\n'What you said about your husband looking away \u2014 that must be painful. Sometimes our partners do not know how to respond, and their discomfort may come from not wanting to say the wrong thing rather than from how they feel about you. Would you be open to having him come to one of your therapy sessions? It can help partners understand what you are going through and learn how they can support you. Couples often feel closer after that.'\n\n**5. Create a pathway back to social engagement**:\n'Regarding your book club \u2014 I understand the instinct to pull away, but isolation tends to make everything feel worse. As we start treatment and you see your arm respond, I would like you to set a goal for when you will go back. It does not have to be next week, but having that target gives us something to work toward together. In the meantime, there are also lymphedema support groups where you can connect with women going through the same thing.'\n\n**6. Address depression formally**:\n'Your PHQ-9 score today suggests moderate depression, which is not surprising given everything you are carrying. I would like to refer you to a psychologist who specializes in working with cancer survivors. Treatment for depression will also help you engage more fully in your lymphedema therapy and overall recovery.'\n\n**7. Practical wardrobe solutions**:\n'There are also some practical things that can help right now. Compression garment companies make fashionable sleeves in various patterns and colors. Some women find that wearing a garment they feel good about actually helps them feel more confident. I can connect you with resources for that.'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Validation of body image distress without minimizing, concrete hope tied to evidence-based treatment outcomes, addressing relationship dynamics, graduated social re-engagement planning, formal depression screening and treatment, practical problem-solving for daily dignity.")
B.aDB=s([B.aSW,B.aYR,B.aPp,B.aOn,B.aXc,B.b1z,B.aWQ,B.b3L,B.aVh,B.b1J,B.aVa,B.aQK],t.E)
B.PF=new A.a5("cancer-lymphedema-breast","Breast Cancer Lymphedema \u2014 Complex Decongestive Therapy",B.aDB,!1)
B.aNy=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 65-year-old right-handed male is referred to the cancer rehabilitation clinic by his colorectal surgeon 6 weeks prior to planned open right hemicolectomy for a newly diagnosed T3N1M0 (Stage III) adenocarcinoma of the ascending colon. He was found to have the mass during a screening colonoscopy prompted by 3 months of fatigue and unintentional 7 kg weight loss. He describes profound exhaustion \u2014 he can barely walk to his mailbox without needing to rest and has stopped walking his dog, an activity he previously enjoyed daily. He sleeps 10-11 hours per night but wakes unrefreshed. He is a retired mechanical engineer whose wife died of pancreatic cancer 18 months ago. He lives alone and his only child lives 4 hours away. When the surgeon mentioned rehabilitation before surgery, the patient responded: "Why bother exercising if I might die anyway?"')
B.b2m=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **Oncologic History**: Ascending colon adenocarcinoma T3N1M0, diagnosed 2 weeks ago via colonoscopy with biopsy. CT chest/abdomen/pelvis shows no distant metastases. CEA elevated at 8.4 ng/mL. Planned for open right hemicolectomy in 6 weeks, followed by adjuvant FOLFOX chemotherapy.\n- **Fatigue Characterization**: Onset 3 months ago, progressive. BFI (Brief Fatigue Inventory) worst fatigue 8/10, average fatigue 7/10, interference with daily activities 8/10. Not relieved by rest. Worse in afternoon. No clear exacerbating factors beyond activity.\n- **Weight Loss**: 82 kg to 75 kg over 3 months (8.5% loss). Decreased appetite. Eating 1-2 small meals/day. No nausea or vomiting.\n- **Functional Decline**: 6-minute walk test equivalent \u2014 estimates he can walk about 5 minutes on flat ground before needing to stop. Previously walked 30-45 minutes daily with his dog. Stopped 2 months ago. Difficulty climbing the 14 stairs to his bedroom \u2014 now sleeps on the couch some nights. Grip strength declining \u2014 has trouble opening jars.\n- **Sleep**: 10-11 hours/night, unrefreshed. No snoring per self-report (lives alone). Denies restless legs. Takes diphenhydramine 50 mg nightly for sleep (started after wife's death).\n- **Mood**: PHQ-9 score 15 (moderately severe depression). GAD-7 score 14 (moderate anxiety). Grief from wife's death 18 months ago \u2014 reports it was 'the same cancer' (she had pancreatic adenocarcinoma). Fears the same outcome.\n- **PMH**: Hypertension, type 2 diabetes (HbA1c 7.8%), hyperlipidemia, mild COPD (former smoker, 30 pack-years, quit 10 years ago), osteoarthritis bilateral knees\n- **Medications**: Metformin 1000 mg BID, amlodipine 5 mg daily, atorvastatin 40 mg daily, diphenhydramine 50 mg QHS, tiotropium inhaler daily\n- **Social History**: Widower, lives alone in two-story house. One adult daughter (nurse) lives 4 hours away. Retired. Limited social network since wife's death \u2014 stopped attending their couples' social groups. Has a dog (golden retriever) he describes as 'my only companion.'\n- **Review of Systems**: Fatigue, anorexia, 7 kg weight loss, depressed mood, anxiety, knee pain with stairs, mild dyspnea on exertion, occasional loose stools")
B.aQF=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 142/86 mmHg, HR 88 bpm, RR 16, SpO2 94% on RA, Temp 36.8C, BMI 25.0 (was 27.3 three months ago).\n\n**General**: Thin, fatigued-appearing male. Appears older than stated age. Cooperative but low energy.\n\n**Cardiopulmonary**: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs. Lungs with mild diffuse expiratory wheezes. Mild dyspnea with conversation during prolonged sentences.\n\n**Abdominal**: Soft, non-distended. Mild right-sided fullness (known mass). No hepatomegaly. Well-healed midline scar from prior cholecystectomy.\n\n**Musculoskeletal**:\n- **Grip strength**: R 24 kg, L 22 kg (below age-sex norms of ~38-42 kg; meets criteria for low grip strength per sarcopenia definition)\n- **Bilateral knees**: Mild crepitus, ROM 0-120 degrees bilaterally, no effusion. Pain with prolonged standing and stair climbing.\n- **General**: Reduced muscle mass visually apparent in upper extremities and thighs. No edema.\n\n**Functional Assessment**:\n- **30-second chair stand test**: 6 repetitions (below normal of >12 for age/sex \u2014 indicates lower extremity weakness)\n- **Timed Up and Go (TUG)**: 14.2 seconds (normal <12 seconds \u2014 mildly impaired)\n- **6-Minute Walk Test**: 285 meters (predicted >450 meters for age \u2014 significantly reduced cardiopulmonary fitness)\n- **Gait**: Slow, deliberate gait. No assistive device. Mild antalgic component favoring knees.\n\n**Neurologic**: Alert, oriented. Grossly intact. Mild deconditioning-related generalized weakness (4/5 proximally, 4+/5 distally).\n\n**Laboratory (recent)**:\n- Hemoglobin 10.2 g/dL (mild anemia \u2014 likely iron deficiency from GI blood loss)\n- Iron 35 mcg/dL (low), ferritin 18 ng/mL (low), TIBC 420 mcg/dL (elevated)\n- Albumin 3.2 g/dL (low), prealbumin 14 mg/dL (low)\n- TSH 6.8 mIU/L (mildly elevated \u2014 subclinical hypothyroidism)\n- HbA1c 7.8%, fasting glucose 162 mg/dL\n- Vitamin D 18 ng/mL (deficient)")
B.aXH=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The surgeon asks you to evaluate this patient's fatigue and functional status, and determine whether prehabilitation might improve his surgical outcomes.")
B.aVu=new A.c("+ Construct a systematic differential diagnosis for this patient's cancer-related fatigue, identifying reversible contributors.","**Cancer-Related Fatigue (CRF) \u2014 Systematic Differential**:\n\nCRF is multifactorial. The NCCN guidelines recommend screening for **treatable contributing factors** before attributing fatigue solely to the cancer itself.\n\n**Reversible Contributors Identified in This Patient**:\n\n1. **Anemia** (Hgb 10.2 g/dL):\n- Iron deficiency anemia from chronic GI blood loss (colon cancer). Low iron, low ferritin, elevated TIBC confirm iron deficiency.\n- **Treatment**: IV iron infusion (ferric carboxymaltose or iron sucrose) \u2014 preferred over oral iron given malabsorption risk and time pressure before surgery. Target Hgb >12 before surgery if possible.\n\n2. **Depression** (PHQ-9 = 15):\n- Moderately severe depression with complicated grief (wife's death from cancer). Fatigue, hypersomnia, anorexia, and social withdrawal are overlapping symptoms.\n- **Treatment**: SSRI initiation (sertraline or escitalopram), grief counseling referral, consider psychiatry consultation given severity.\n\n3. **Deconditioning/Sarcopenia**:\n- 6MWT 285 m (severely reduced), grip strength below sarcopenia threshold, chair stand test impaired. Progressive functional decline over 3 months from inactivity cycle (fatigue leads to less activity, which worsens fatigue).\n- **Treatment**: Structured exercise program (the core of prehabilitation).\n\n4. **Subclinical Hypothyroidism** (TSH 6.8):\n- TSH mildly elevated. While mild, hypothyroidism exacerbates fatigue. In the context of surgery, treating is reasonable.\n- **Treatment**: Start low-dose levothyroxine 25-50 mcg daily. Recheck TSH in 6 weeks.\n\n5. **Malnutrition/Cachexia**:\n- 8.5% weight loss in 3 months, low albumin (3.2), low prealbumin (14), reduced caloric intake. Meets criteria for cancer cachexia (>5% weight loss in 6 months with underlying disease).\n- **Treatment**: Nutritional optimization (see management plan).\n\n6. **Vitamin D Deficiency** (18 ng/mL):\n- Contributes to fatigue, weakness, and mood disturbance.\n- **Treatment**: Vitamin D3 50,000 IU weekly x 8 weeks, then 2000 IU daily maintenance.\n\n7. **Medication Side Effect**:\n- **Diphenhydramine 50 mg nightly** \u2014 anticholinergic, causes next-day sedation and cognitive dulling. Major contributor to unrefreshed sleep and daytime fatigue in older adults.\n- **Treatment**: Discontinue. Replace with sleep hygiene education or low-dose melatonin.\n\n8. **Uncontrolled Diabetes** (HbA1c 7.8%):\n- Hyperglycemia contributes to fatigue. Perioperative glycemic control also reduces surgical complications.\n- **Treatment**: Optimize metformin, consider adding a second agent. Endocrinology consultation.\n\n9. **Sleep Disorder**:\n- Hypersomnia (10-11 hours) with unrefreshed sleep. Rule out obstructive sleep apnea (BMI now 25, but may have had higher BMI previously, male, age). Consider sleep study if time permits.\n\n10. **COPD/Mild Hypoxia** (SpO2 94%):\n- Borderline oxygen saturation at rest. May desaturate further with exertion. Ensure tiotropium compliance, consider pulmonary function testing.\n\n**Cancer Itself**: Tumor-related cytokine activation (IL-6, TNF-alpha) causes central fatigue. This is the non-reversible component that will improve after tumor resection.")
B.aZA=new A.c("+ What is the evidence base for cancer prehabilitation, and how does it differ from standard preoperative optimization?",'**Cancer Prehabilitation \u2014 Evidence Base**:\n\n**Definition**: Prehabilitation is a **proactive, multimodal intervention** initiated between cancer diagnosis and treatment (surgery, chemotherapy, or radiation) designed to optimize physical and psychological function, build physiologic reserve, and improve treatment tolerance and recovery.\n\n**Key Distinction from Standard Preoperative Optimization**:\n- Standard preoperative care focuses on medical optimization (controlling comorbidities, correcting labs, NPO protocols)\n- Prehabilitation adds **structured exercise, nutritional supplementation, and psychological support** as formal therapeutic interventions in the pre-treatment window\n\n**Evidence for Colorectal Cancer Prehabilitation**:\n\n1. **Functional Capacity**: Multiple RCTs demonstrate that 4-6 weeks of prehabilitation improves 6MWT distance by 25-40 meters and VO2 peak by 1.5-2.0 mL/kg/min compared to controls. Patients who enter surgery with higher fitness levels recover faster.\n\n2. **Postoperative Complications**: Systematic reviews and meta-analyses show a **30-50% reduction in overall postoperative complications** (particularly pulmonary complications, wound infections, and ileus) in prehabilitated patients undergoing major abdominal surgery.\n\n3. **Length of Stay**: Prehabilitation is associated with **1.5-2.5 day shorter hospital stays** after colorectal surgery.\n\n4. **Functional Recovery**: Prehabilitated patients return to baseline function **4-6 weeks faster** than controls. Without prehabilitation, many patients never return to their pre-diagnosis function level.\n\n5. **The "Dip and Recovery" Model**: Surgery causes a predictable decline in function (the "dip"). Patients who start at a higher functional level (through prehabilitation) dip to a less severe nadir and recover to a higher plateau. Patients who are deconditioned at surgery have less reserve, dip below the threshold for complications, and may never fully recover.\n\n**Multimodal Prehabilitation Components (Triad)**:\n1. **Exercise**: Aerobic + resistance training (most critical component)\n2. **Nutrition**: Protein supplementation + caloric optimization\n3. **Psychological**: Anxiety reduction + coping skills + motivation\n\n**Cost-Effectiveness**: Multiple analyses show prehabilitation is cost-effective due to reduced complications, shorter ICU and hospital stays, and faster return to independence. The estimated savings are $2,000-5,000 per patient in reduced acute care costs.')
B.aOi=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You decide to initiate a 6-week prehabilitation program. The patient is skeptical but agrees to try. Design the program.")
B.aO7=new A.c("+ Outline a comprehensive 6-week prehabilitation program for this patient addressing exercise, nutrition, and psychological preparation.",'**6-Week Multimodal Prehabilitation Program**:\n\n**EXERCISE PRESCRIPTION** (supervised 3x/week + home program):\n\n*Aerobic Component*:\n- **Weeks 1-2**: Walk 10-15 minutes at moderate intensity (RPE 3-4/10 or "can talk but not sing"). Start with flat terrain. Include 3-minute warm-up and cool-down. Daily frequency encouraged, even in 5-minute bouts.\n- **Weeks 3-4**: Progress to 20-25 minutes. Introduce inclines or increased pace. Consider stationary cycling to reduce knee stress. Target RPE 4-5/10.\n- **Weeks 5-6**: 30 minutes moderate intensity. Include interval training: 2-minute moderate / 1-minute vigorous alternating. Monitor SpO2 with exertion \u2014 supplemental O2 if desaturates below 88%.\n- **Goal**: Improve 6MWT by 30+ meters and reduce dyspnea with daily activities.\n\n*Resistance Component*:\n- **Weeks 1-2**: Bodyweight exercises \u2014 sit-to-stand (progress to chair squats), wall push-ups, seated rows with resistance band, standing hip abduction. 2 sets of 8-10 repetitions. Rest 60-90 seconds between sets.\n- **Weeks 3-4**: Add light dumbbells (2-5 lbs) or increase resistance band tension. Include leg press, chest press, lat pulldown, bicep curls. 2-3 sets of 10-12 repetitions.\n- **Weeks 5-6**: Progress to moderate resistance. Focus on functional movements \u2014 carrying weighted bags (grocery simulation), stair climbing with progression, floor-to-stand transfers.\n- **Goal**: Improve grip strength by 4-5 kg, chair stand test >10 repetitions.\n\n*Inspiratory Muscle Training*:\n- Threshold IMT device at 30% of maximal inspiratory pressure (MIP).\n- 30 breaths, 2 times daily.\n- Reduces postoperative pulmonary complications by 50% in abdominal surgery patients.\n\n**NUTRITIONAL OPTIMIZATION** (dietitian-directed):\n\n1. **Caloric needs**: 30-35 kcal/kg/day (2250-2625 kcal/day). Emphasize calorie-dense foods given poor appetite.\n2. **Protein**: 1.5 g/kg/day (112 g/day). Whey protein supplement 20-30 g twice daily between meals. Leucine-enriched supplements preferred for muscle protein synthesis.\n3. **Iron repletion**: IV ferric carboxymaltose 750 mg x 2 doses (day 1 and day 7). Recheck Hgb at week 4.\n4. **Vitamin D**: 50,000 IU weekly x 6 weeks.\n5. **Oral nutritional supplements**: Ensure Plus or equivalent 1-2 cans daily between meals if not meeting caloric goals.\n6. **Immunonutrition**: Consider arginine/omega-3/nucleotide-enriched formula (Impact Advanced Recovery) for 5 days pre-surgery \u2014 evidence shows reduced infection rates in colorectal surgery.\n7. **Glycemic optimization**: Optimize diabetes management. Reduce HbA1c as much as possible in 6 weeks. Perioperative glucose <180 mg/dL target.\n\n**PSYCHOLOGICAL PREPARATION**:\n\n1. **Cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT)**: Weekly sessions addressing health anxiety, grief processing, and fear of cancer death. Specifically address his equation of his diagnosis with his wife\'s death (different cancer, different prognosis).\n2. **Discontinue diphenhydramine**: Replace with sleep hygiene protocol (consistent sleep/wake times, no screens 1 hour before bed, cool dark room) and melatonin 3 mg if needed.\n3. **Start SSRI**: Sertraline 50 mg daily (helps both depression and anxiety; no significant surgical interaction).\n4. **Relaxation training**: Diaphragmatic breathing, progressive muscle relaxation, guided imagery. Practice daily \u2014 will also use perioperatively for anxiety management.\n5. **Social reconnection**: Encourage resumption of one social activity per week. Walking the dog counts \u2014 both exercise and mood benefit.\n6. **Preoperative teaching**: Familiarize with what to expect post-surgery (pain management, early mobilization protocol, stoma education if applicable). Reduces anxiety and improves compliance.\n\n**MONITORING AND REASSESSMENT**:\n- 6MWT, grip strength, chair stand, TUG at baseline, week 3, and week 6 (pre-surgery)\n- Weekly weights, biweekly labs (Hgb, prealbumin, glucose)\n- PHQ-9 and GAD-7 at weeks 3 and 6\n- Communicate results to surgical team \u2014 if significant functional improvement, advocate for proceeding with surgery. If functional decline, alert team to higher perioperative risk.')
B.b1f=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient asks whether his insurance will cover 6 weeks of exercise therapy before surgery, and the surgeon wants to know the evidence supporting delaying surgery for prehabilitation.")
B.aRK=new A.c("+ Address insurance justification for prehabilitation, the safety of a 6-week surgical delay for prehabilitation, and the emerging prehabilitation service delivery models.","**Insurance Justification**:\n\n1. **Diagnosis coding**: Use ICD-10 codes that support medical necessity \u2014 R53.83 (other fatigue), M62.84 (sarcopenia), E44.1 (mild protein-calorie malnutrition), Z51.89 (encounter for other specified aftercare), and the cancer diagnosis (C18.2, malignant neoplasm of ascending colon).\n2. **Therapy justification**: Frame as medical rehabilitation for documented functional impairments (reduced 6MWT, impaired grip strength, abnormal TUG, sarcopenia) rather than \"exercise.\" Impairment-based documentation with specific functional goals and measurable outcomes.\n3. **Prior authorization strategy**: Include surgeon's letter stating that functional optimization is medically necessary prior to major abdominal surgery. Cite complication reduction data and reduced length-of-stay evidence.\n4. **Coverage reality**: Most commercial insurance covers PT/OT for documented functional impairments. Medicare covers therapy with documented medical necessity. Cancer prehabilitation as a formal program is gaining recognition but is not yet a universally covered benefit category. Individual therapy sessions (PT, OT, psychology) are covered under existing benefit structures.\n\n**Safety of 6-Week Surgical Delay**:\n\n1. **Oncologic safety**: For Stage III colon cancer, the standard recommendation is surgery within 4-8 weeks of diagnosis. A 6-week prehabilitation window falls within standard surgical scheduling timelines and does not represent a delay beyond normal practice.\n2. **Evidence**: Multiple studies show that a 4-6 week delay for prehabilitation in colorectal cancer does NOT worsen oncologic outcomes (no difference in overall survival or disease-free survival).\n3. **Risk-benefit**: The patient's current functional status (6MWT 285 m, sarcopenia, anemia, malnutrition) places him at HIGH risk for postoperative complications. Prehabilitation to even modestly improve these parameters likely reduces complication risk more than any potential harm from a standard-timeline delay.\n4. **Communication with surgeon**: Present objective data \u2014 baseline functional tests, anemia severity, nutritional parameters \u2014 and projected improvement trajectory. Frame as a shared goal of getting the patient to surgery in the best possible condition.\n\n**Emerging Prehabilitation Service Delivery Models**:\n\n1. **Hospital-based prehabilitation programs**: Dedicated clinics within cancer centers (e.g., McGill Prehabilitation Program model). Multidisciplinary team (physiatrist, PT, dietitian, psychologist) under one roof.\n2. **Home-based/hybrid programs**: Initial in-person assessment, then supervised home exercise via telehealth with wearable activity monitors. Particularly useful for patients in rural areas (like this patient's daughter's suggestion).\n3. **Community-based partnerships**: Partner with local gyms, YMCAs (LIVESTRONG at the YMCA program), or community centers for supervised exercise.\n4. **Digital prehabilitation**: App-based exercise programs with remote monitoring. Emerging evidence supports feasibility and adherence, though less robust than supervised programs.\n5. **Prehabilitation as a quality metric**: Some institutions are incorporating prehabilitation into Enhanced Recovery After Surgery (ERAS) protocols as a standard preoperative pathway, increasing institutional support and funding.")
B.aVR=new A.c(u.j,'When you present the prehabilitation plan, the patient stares at the floor and says quietly: "My wife had cancer too. She did everything right \u2014 all the treatments, all the exercises they told her to do. And she still died in eight months. So tell me, doctor, why should I bother exercising when I might die anyway?"')
B.aYN=new A.c("+ How do you respond to this patient's existential distress and motivate engagement in prehabilitation while honoring his grief?","**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Pause. Do not rush past this moment.**\n[Sit quietly for a moment. Make eye contact. This is not a question that needs an immediate clinical answer \u2014 it is an expression of grief and existential fear.]\n\n**2. Honor his wife's memory and his loss**:\n'I am truly sorry about your wife. Losing your partner to cancer \u2014 and now facing a cancer diagnosis yourself \u2014 is an extraordinary burden. I cannot imagine how that must feel. Would you tell me a little about her?'\n[Allow him to share. This validates that she matters in this conversation and that you see him as a person, not just a patient.]\n\n**3. Acknowledge the fear without dismissing it**:\n'Your fear makes perfect sense. You watched the person you love most go through treatment and not survive, and now you are being asked to do similar things. It would be strange if you were not afraid. I would be afraid too.'\n\n**4. Gently differentiate without minimizing**:\n'I want to be honest with you. Pancreatic cancer and colon cancer are very different diseases. Your wife's cancer is one of the most aggressive cancers we know. Your colon cancer, caught at Stage III, has a very different trajectory. With surgery and chemotherapy, the majority of patients with your stage of colon cancer \u2014 roughly 70 percent \u2014 are alive and well at five years. I do not want to make promises I cannot keep, but the odds here are genuinely in your favor.'\n\n**5. Reframe exercise not as a cure, but as an investment in his experience**:\n'Here is how I think about prehabilitation. Even if I cannot guarantee you a specific outcome \u2014 and no doctor can \u2014 what I can offer you is this: patients who are stronger going into surgery recover faster, spend less time in the hospital, have fewer complications, and feel better sooner. Exercise will not change the biology of your cancer, but it will change how you experience the next six months of your life. That matters whether you live five more years or fifty.'\n\n**6. Connect to what he still loves**:\n'You mentioned your dog \u2014 your golden retriever. You said he is your companion. Right now, you cannot walk him like you used to, and that is a loss too. What if we started there? What if the first goal of prehabilitation is simply getting you back to walking your dog for 20 minutes? Not because of surgery, not because of cancer \u2014 just because it is something that brings you joy.'\n\n**7. Offer the relationship, not just the program**:\n'I am not going to force you into anything. But I would like you to come back next week \u2014 just to talk, and maybe try a few exercises. If it feels pointless after two or three sessions, we will have an honest conversation about that. But I think you owe it to yourself to try before deciding it is not worth it. And I will be here either way.'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Allowing silence and emotional space, honoring the deceased spouse, differentiating prognosis without dismissing fear, reframing motivation from survival to quality of experience, connecting to intrinsic values (the dog), offering unconditional therapeutic relationship, avoiding false reassurance while providing realistic hope.")
B.aEi=s([B.aNy,B.b2m,B.aQF,B.aXH,B.aVu,B.aZA,B.aOi,B.aO7,B.b1f,B.aRK,B.aVR,B.aYN],t.E)
B.PU=new A.a5("cancer-prehab-fatigue","Cancer-Related Fatigue \u2014 Prehabilitation Before Surgery",B.aEi,!1)
B.b_S=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 30-year-old right-handed male is transferred to the acute rehabilitation unit from the burn intensive care unit 3 weeks after sustaining 40% total body surface area (TBSA) burns in a house fire. He has deep partial-thickness and full-thickness burns involving both upper extremities (circumferential), anterior chest, and face. He was intubated for 14 days due to inhalation injury and has been extubated for 5 days with stable respiratory status. He underwent split-thickness skin grafting to both forearms, dorsal hands, and anterior chest. He is alert and oriented but has significant pain with movement and has been largely immobile in bed. Nursing reports he refuses to look at himself in a mirror and becomes agitated during dressing changes.")
B.aN9=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **Burn Details**: 40% TBSA \u2014 bilateral upper extremities (circumferential, 20%), anterior chest/abdomen (12%), face/neck (8%); mixture of deep partial-thickness and full-thickness\n- **Inhalation Injury**: Grade 2 inhalation injury on bronchoscopy; intubated x 14 days; currently on room air with SpO2 96%\n- **Surgical History**: Tangential excision and split-thickness skin graft (STSG) to bilateral forearms, dorsal hands, and anterior chest at days 7 and 12; donor sites bilateral thighs\n- **Pain**: Reports 8/10 with movement, 5/10 at rest; currently on scheduled acetaminophen, gabapentin, and PRN IV hydromorphone for procedural pain\n- **Nutrition**: Receiving oral diet with high-protein supplements; weight loss of 8 kg since admission; prealbumin 12 mg/dL (low)\n- **Edema**: Significant bilateral upper extremity edema, worse in hands\n- **ROM Limitations**: Unable to fully extend elbows, wrists in flexion posture, MCP joints stiffening in extension\n- **Psychological**: Avoids looking at burned areas, nightmares about the fire, hypervigilance to alarms\n- **PMH**: Previously healthy, no medications, worked as a carpenter\n- **Social**: Single, lives alone, no children; parents live nearby")
B.aZI=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 118/72 mmHg, HR 96 bpm, RR 18, SpO2 96% on RA, Temp 37.4C, Weight 74 kg (pre-injury 82 kg).\n\n**General**: Thin, anxious-appearing male with extensive bandaging to bilateral upper extremities and chest.\n\n**Burn/Wound Assessment**:\n- **Face**: Healed superficial partial-thickness burns with pink, hyperpigmented skin. No eyelid contracture. Lips dry but intact ROM.\n- **Neck**: Anterior burn with early band-like scar contracture limiting neck extension to neutral (0 degrees).\n- **Chest**: Grafted anterior chest \u2014 grafts 90% taken, pink, adherent. Early hypertrophic scarring at graft margins.\n- **Upper Extremities**: Circumferential grafted forearms \u2014 grafts well-healed. Dorsal hand grafts intact. Significant bilateral hand and forearm edema. Early web space contracture between thumb and index finger bilaterally.\n\n**Range of Motion** (Active/Passive):\n- Shoulders: Flexion 110/130 degrees bilaterally (limited by axillary scar bands), abduction 90/110 degrees\n- Elbows: Extension lacking 25 degrees bilaterally, flexion 120 degrees\n- Wrists: Extension 10/25 degrees, flexion 40/50 degrees\n- MCPs: Extension 0 degrees, flexion 50/70 degrees (significant stiffness)\n- Grip strength: 8 kg R, 6 kg L (severely reduced)\n\n**Neurologic**: Intact sensation in non-burned areas. Diminished light touch over grafted regions. Motor strength 4/5 throughout UE (limited by pain and edema). Lower extremities 5/5.\n\n**Respiratory**: Mild expiratory wheezes bilaterally. Cough productive of scant clear sputum. Peak cough flow reduced.\n\n**Psychological**: Makes minimal eye contact. Becomes visibly distressed when bandages are removed. Cooperative but withdrawn.")
B.b_u=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The rehabilitation team needs to establish treatment priorities and understand the wound healing trajectory to guide therapy intensity.")
B.b1l=new A.c("+ Classify the burn depths present in this patient and explain the timeline for contracture development. What signs indicate compartment syndrome in circumferential burns?","**Burn Depth Classification**:\n- **Superficial (first-degree)**: Epidermis only (face \u2014 now healed). Painful, erythematous, no blistering. Heals in 5-7 days without scarring.\n- **Superficial partial-thickness (second-degree)**: Epidermis + superficial dermis. Moist, pink, blistering, blanches with pressure, very painful. Heals in 10-14 days. Low scar risk.\n- **Deep partial-thickness (second-degree)**: Epidermis + deep dermis (into reticular layer). Mottled pink/white, less blanching, reduced sensation. Heals in 3-6 weeks but with significant scarring. Often requires grafting.\n- **Full-thickness (third-degree)**: Entire dermis destroyed. Leathery, waxy, insensate. Cannot heal by re-epithelialization \u2014 requires STSG. **This patient has deep partial and full-thickness burns on extremities and chest.**\n\n**Contracture Development Timeline**:\n- **Acute phase (0-2 weeks)**: Edema and wound contraction begin. Anti-contracture positioning is critical.\n- **Proliferative phase (2 weeks - 6 months)**: **Highest risk for contracture**. Active wound contraction, myofibroblast activity, collagen deposition. This is the current phase for this patient.\n- **Remodeling/Maturation phase (6 months - 2 years)**: Collagen reorganization. Scars soften and flatten. Continued risk of contracture without ongoing management.\n\n**Key Contracture Risk Areas in This Patient**:\n- **Axillary contracture** (shoulder adduction/IR posture)\n- **Antecubital contracture** (elbow flexion)\n- **Wrist flexion contracture**\n- **MCP hyperextension contracture** (claw deformity) \u2014 MCPs should be positioned in 70-90 degrees of flexion\n- **First web space contracture** (thumb adduction)\n- **Neck flexion/anterior contracture**\n\n**Compartment Syndrome in Circumferential Burns** (acute phase concern):\n- **Signs**: Pain out of proportion, pain with passive stretch of distal muscles, tense/firm compartment, paresthesias, diminished pulses (late finding), compartment pressures >30 mmHg\n- **Management**: Emergent **escharotomy** (incision through burn eschar to release pressure) \u2014 does not require anesthesia in full-thickness burns (insensate)\n- In extremities, escharotomy is performed along medial and lateral aspects\n- **Fasciotomy** may be needed if pressure remains elevated after escharotomy")
B.aRt=new A.c('+ What is the "anti-deformity" positioning protocol for this patient\'s burn pattern?','**Anti-Deformity Positioning Protocol**:\n\nThe fundamental principle is to position **opposite to the expected contracture** \u2014 burned surfaces are maintained in a stretched/elongated position.\n\n| **Body Region** | **Contracture Tendency** | **Anti-Deformity Position** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Neck (anterior burn) | Flexion | Extension \u2014 no pillow, neck extension splint or conforming orthosis |\n| Chest/Axilla | Shoulder adduction, IR | Shoulders abducted 90 degrees on arm troughs or airplane splints |\n| Elbow (anterior) | Flexion | Full extension \u2014 elbow extension splints, arm boards |\n| Wrist (dorsal burn) | Extension | Wrist in 20-30 degrees extension (functional position) |\n| MCP joints | Hyperextension (claw) | MCPs flexed 70-90 degrees \u2014 "intrinsic plus" or "safe" position splint |\n| IP joints | Flexion | Full extension |\n| Thumb web space | Adduction | C-bar or web spacer splint maintaining abduction |\n\n**Splinting Schedule**:\n- Splints worn at **all times except during therapy and exercise** in the early post-graft period\n- After graft stabilization (7-10 days post-surgery), splints worn at night and during rest periods\n- Dynamic splinting introduced once wounds are closed to provide sustained low-load stretch\n\n**Additional Positioning**:\n- Elevate bilateral upper extremities above heart level to reduce edema\n- Foot boards for ankle neutral positioning (even though LE not burned \u2014 prevents contracture from immobility)\n- No pillow under neck to prevent neck flexion contracture\n- Bed positioning: supine preferred with arms abducted on arm troughs')
B.aRQ=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","The patient is now 4 weeks post-burn. Grafts are well-healed, and he is cleared for full active and passive ROM exercises. He continues to have significant pain with therapy and his nutritional status remains suboptimal.")
B.aUw=new A.c("+ Develop a comprehensive burn rehabilitation management plan including scar management, nutrition, and therapy.","**1. Aggressive ROM and Stretching Program**:\n- Active and active-assisted ROM exercises **at minimum 3 times daily** for all affected joints\n- Sustained end-range stretching (low-load, prolonged stretch) for 20-30 minutes at end range\n- Serial static splinting to progressively increase ROM at night\n- Joint-specific targets: shoulder flexion/abduction >150 degrees, full elbow extension, wrist extension >30 degrees, MCP flexion >80 degrees, full first web space opening\n\n**2. Scar Management Program** (critical during proliferative phase):\n- **Pressure garments**: Custom-fitted compression garments at **25 mmHg** worn **23 hours/day**. Order custom garments now (interim use Tubigrip or Coban wrapping until custom garments arrive in 2-3 weeks).\n- **Silicone therapy**: Silicone gel sheets or silicone-based cream applied under pressure garments. Shown to reduce hypertrophic scar formation.\n- **Scar massage**: Deep friction massage perpendicular to scar bands, 10 minutes per area, 2-3 times daily after wound closure. Helps remodel collagen alignment.\n- **Moisturization**: Fragrance-free moisturizer applied multiple times daily \u2014 healed burn skin lacks sebaceous glands.\n\n**3. Pain Management for Therapy Participation**:\n- Pre-medicate 30-45 minutes before therapy sessions (short-acting opioid for breakthrough)\n- Transition from IV to oral pain regimen: scheduled extended-release opioid + oral breakthrough\n- Continue gabapentin for neuropathic/itching component (pruritus is extremely common)\n- Non-pharmacologic: music therapy during dressing changes, relaxation breathing, distraction techniques\n- Begin opioid taper plan as wounds heal and pain improves\n\n**4. Nutritional Support**:\n- **Caloric needs**: Curreri formula: 25 kcal/kg/day + 40 kcal/%TBSA/day = ~3450 kcal/day for this patient\n- **Protein**: 1.5-2.0 g/kg/day (110-148 g/day) for wound healing and lean mass preservation\n- **Micronutrients**: Vitamin C 500 mg BID, Zinc 220 mg daily, Multivitamin with iron\n- **Monitoring**: Weekly prealbumin (goal >20 mg/dL), weekly weights, calorie counts\n- Dietitian consultation for meal planning; oral supplements between meals\n\n**5. Respiratory Rehabilitation**:\n- Incentive spirometry 10 repetitions every hour while awake\n- Progressive aerobic conditioning (recumbent bike, walking program)\n- Chest wall stretching to prevent restrictive defect from anterior chest scarring\n\n**6. Functional Training**:\n- Progressive ADL training: feeding, grooming, dressing\n- Adaptive equipment as needed (built-up handles, long-handled devices)\n- Upper extremity strengthening program beginning with light resistance and progressing\n- Vocational assessment \u2014 carpentry requires full hand function; discuss timeline and alternatives")
B.aQV=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient is approaching discharge from inpatient rehabilitation. The social worker raises concerns about his readiness for community reintegration given his visible facial scarring and psychological state.")
B.aWA=new A.c("+ Describe the burn center multidisciplinary team model and community reintegration planning for this patient.","**Burn Rehabilitation Multidisciplinary Team**:\n- **Physiatrist/Burn Surgeon**: Medical direction, scar/contracture management, surgical planning for reconstructive procedures\n- **Physical Therapist**: ROM, strengthening, functional mobility, aerobic conditioning\n- **Occupational Therapist**: ADLs, splinting, scar management, adaptive equipment, vocational rehabilitation\n- **Psychologist/Psychiatrist**: Adjustment disorder, PTSD screening and treatment, body image counseling, coping skills\n- **Social Worker**: Discharge planning, financial resources, housing, support groups\n- **Recreational Therapist**: Community outing practice, leisure skill adaptation, social reintegration activities\n- **Dietitian**: Nutritional optimization for wound healing and recovery\n- **Orthotist**: Custom splinting and pressure garment fabrication\n- **Vocational Rehabilitation Counselor**: Return-to-work planning, job modification assessment\n\n**Community Reintegration Plan**:\n1. **Gradual community exposure**: Supervised community outings before discharge (grocery store, restaurant) with recreational therapy to practice social interaction skills.\n2. **Burn survivor support group**: Connect with the Phoenix Society for Burn Survivors or local burn support network. Peer mentoring with a burn survivor who is further in recovery.\n3. **Body image counseling program**: Structured sessions addressing visible difference, developing coping scripts for social interactions, practicing responses to staring or questions.\n4. **Camouflage/cosmetic options**: Dermatology referral for scar camouflage products; laser therapy discussion for hypertrophic scars when mature.\n5. **Outpatient therapy continuum**: Plan for 3-5 days/week outpatient PT/OT for minimum 6-12 months. Scar management continues for 12-24 months.\n6. **Work reintegration**: Vocational rehabilitation referral. Modified duty initially. Carpentry may require full hand recovery \u2014 explore timeline and alternative work if needed.\n7. **Fire safety and prevention education**: Home safety plan, working smoke detectors, fire extinguisher training.\n8. **Long-term follow-up**: Burn clinic every 3-6 months for scar assessment, contracture monitoring, reconstructive surgery planning (likely will need scar revision or release procedures).")
B.aZ_=new A.c(u.j,"During a therapy session, the occupational therapist reports that the patient refused to look in the mirror during facial scar care. He said: 'I don't want to see what I look like. I'm never going to look normal again. No one is going to want to be around me.'")
B.aU8=new A.c("+ How would you address this patient's body image distress and avoidance behavior?","**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Acknowledge and validate the distress**:\n'Thank you for telling me how you feel. What you are going through is incredibly difficult, and your feelings are a normal and expected response to a major change in your appearance. You do not have to pretend this is easy.'\n\n**2. Normalize the experience**:\n'Almost every burn survivor I have worked with has had a period where they avoided looking at their scars. It is a natural protective response. The fact that you are talking about it is actually a sign of strength, not weakness.'\n\n**3. Provide honest reassurance without minimizing**:\n'I am not going to tell you that your scars do not matter or that everything will go back to the way it was. What I can tell you is that burn scars change dramatically over the first one to two years. They become softer, flatter, and less red. There are also treatments \u2014 pressure garments, silicone, laser therapy, and eventually reconstructive surgery \u2014 that can make a significant difference in appearance.'\n\n**4. Address the social fear directly**:\n'You said no one will want to be around you. I understand why it feels that way right now. But I have seen many burn survivors build and maintain rich relationships \u2014 friendships, careers, and romantic partnerships. There are strategies and even practice sessions we can do to help you feel more confident in social situations. Would you be open to meeting with our psychologist to work on some of those skills?'\n\n**5. Introduce mirror exposure gradually**:\n'Looking in the mirror does not have to happen all at once. We can start small \u2014 maybe just looking at your hands first, then working up gradually at your own pace. This approach is called graded exposure, and it is evidence-based for helping people adjust to changes in appearance. You control the pace.'\n\n**6. Offer peer support**:\n'I also know a burn survivor who went through something very similar and is willing to talk to patients who are going through this. Sometimes hearing from someone who has been where you are can make a real difference. Would you be interested in connecting with him?'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Validation without patronizing, honest communication about prognosis, graded exposure approach, peer support referral, psychological referral, patient-controlled pace of recovery.")
B.aG6=s([B.b_S,B.aN9,B.aZI,B.b_u,B.b1l,B.aRt,B.aRQ,B.aUw,B.aQV,B.aWA,B.aZ_,B.aU8],t.E)
B.PG=new A.a5("burn-major-rehab-40-tbsa","Major Burn Rehabilitation \u2014 40% TBSA",B.aG6,!1)
B.b0v=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 26-year-old right-handed male active-duty soldier is admitted to the inpatient polytrauma rehabilitation program at a VA medical center 4 weeks after sustaining injuries from an improvised explosive device (IED) blast during a foot patrol in a combat zone. He sustained a right transtibial amputation (performed in theater), a mild traumatic brain injury with loss of consciousness estimated at 10 minutes, bilateral tympanic membrane perforations, and multiple soft tissue fragment wounds to the left lower extremity. He was evacuated through Landstuhl Regional Medical Center and has arrived at the stateside VA. He is ambulatory with crutches, wearing a rigid removable dressing on his residual limb, and reports constant bilateral ringing in his ears, headaches, difficulty concentrating, and nightmares about the blast.")
B.aRN=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **Mechanism of Injury**: IED blast during dismounted patrol; thrown approximately 5 meters; LOC estimated 10 minutes per buddy statement; GCS 14 (E3V5M6) on initial field assessment\n- **Surgical History**: Right transtibial amputation at junction of middle and distal thirds (performed in combat theater); revision and wound closure at Landstuhl; multiple debridements of left LE fragment wounds (now healing by secondary intention)\n- **TBI Classification**: Mild TBI (mTBI) \u2014 LOC <30 min, GCS 13-15, PTA <24 hours; CT head negative for intracranial hemorrhage\n- **Audiologic**: Bilateral tympanic membrane perforations (right > left); bilateral high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss; constant bilateral tinnitus rated 7/10 severity\n- **Current Symptoms**: Daily headaches (bifrontal, pressure-type, 5/10), difficulty concentrating on reading for >15 minutes, irritability, insomnia (sleeps 3-4 hours/night due to nightmares), photosensitivity, dizziness with quick head movements\n- **Residual Limb**: Right transtibial, 15 cm from tibial tubercle; wound healed, mild distal edema, phantom limb sensation present, phantom pain 4/10 (burning, intermittent)\n- **Pain**: Headaches 5/10, phantom limb pain 4/10, left LE wound sites 3/10\n- **Psychosocial**: Single, from rural Kansas; infantry MOS; this was his second deployment; close friend killed in the same blast\n- **Pre-injury Baseline**: Excellent physical fitness, no prior medical or psychiatric history, high school education, no substance use")
B.aYi=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 132/78 mmHg, HR 84 bpm, RR 16, SpO2 99% on RA, Temp 36.9C.\n\n**General**: Well-built, muscular young male appearing his stated age. Cooperative but guarded. Makes intermittent eye contact.\n\n**Cognitive Screen (MOCA)**: 24/30 \u2014 lost points on delayed recall (3 points), attention/serial 7s (1 point), verbal fluency (1 point), abstraction (1 point). Effort appeared good.\n\n**Residual Limb Exam (Right Transtibial)**:\n- Length: 15 cm from tibial tubercle (appropriate for prosthetic fitting)\n- Incision: Well-healed posterior myocutaneous flap, no dehiscence\n- Shape: Cylindrical with mild distal edema\n- Skin: Intact, no adherent scar, mobile soft tissue\n- Sensitivity: Mild allodynia at distal end; positive Tinel sign at the peroneal nerve\n- Bony prominences: Fibular head non-tender, tibial crest with mild prominence\n- ROM: Knee flexion 130 degrees, full extension (no contracture)\n- Hip: Full ROM, strength 5/5 throughout\n\n**Left Lower Extremity**: Three healing fragment wounds (1-3 cm) on anterolateral calf. Granulating well. Sensation intact. Full ROM. Strength 5/5.\n\n**Neurologic**: Cranial nerves intact except decreased hearing bilaterally (Weber lateralizes left). No nystagmus at rest. Positive head impulse test bilaterally. Balance: Romberg positive with eyes closed. Single-leg stance on left: 8 seconds (reduced).\n\n**Psychiatric Screening**: PHQ-9: 14 (moderate depression). PCL-5: 48 (above threshold of 33 for probable PTSD). AUDIT-C: 0 (no alcohol use).")
B.b2w=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","This patient presents with multiple concurrent diagnoses requiring prioritization and coordinated management.")
B.aVL=new A.c("+ How do you prioritize the assessment and treatment of this polytrauma patient? What is the significance of the dual diagnosis of TBI and amputation?","**Polytrauma Priority Assessment Framework**:\n\nThe VA Polytrauma System of Care uses a **concurrent treatment model** rather than sequential treatment. All conditions are addressed simultaneously by an integrated team. However, conditions that threaten safety or impede rehabilitation participation take priority:\n\n**Priority 1 \u2014 Safety and Medical Stability**:\n- **Pain control**: Uncontrolled pain limits therapy participation. Address headaches, phantom pain, and wound pain with a multimodal approach.\n- **Sleep**: 3-4 hours/night is critically insufficient for cognitive and physical recovery. Must address immediately.\n- **PTSD screening/safety**: PCL-5 of 48 is significant. Screen for suicidal ideation (Columbia Suicide Severity Rating Scale). Nightmares and hypervigilance are active symptoms requiring treatment.\n\n**Priority 2 \u2014 Cognitive and Sensory Function**:\n- **TBI recovery monitoring**: MOCA 24/30 at 4 weeks post-mTBI \u2014 most mTBI patients recover within 3 months. Serial cognitive testing to document trajectory.\n- **Vestibular dysfunction**: Dizziness with head movement and positive head impulse test suggest **blast-related vestibular injury** \u2014 common in IED blasts even without temporal bone fracture.\n- **Hearing**: Audiology evaluation for hearing aids and tinnitus management.\n\n**Priority 3 \u2014 Prosthetic Rehabilitation**:\n- Residual limb maturation and prosthetic fitting\n- Gait training and functional mobility\n\n**Dual Diagnosis Significance (TBI + Amputation)**:\n- **Cognitive deficits impair prosthetic training**: Learning to use a prosthesis requires attention, motor planning, and memory \u2014 all potentially impaired by TBI.\n- **Balance impairment is compounded**: TBI-related vestibular dysfunction + amputation-related loss of proprioceptive input creates significantly greater fall risk.\n- **Therapy sessions may need modification**: Shorter sessions, more repetition, written/visual instructions, reduced environmental distractions.\n- **Pain syndromes overlap**: Post-concussive headaches + phantom limb pain require careful pharmacologic management to avoid polypharmacy and sedation.\n- **Psychological burden is multiplied**: Loss of limb + cognitive changes + combat trauma = extremely high risk for depression, PTSD, and substance use.")
B.b0k=new A.c("+ What prosthetic prescription would you recommend, and what is the expected timeline for prosthetic fitting?","**Prosthetic Rehabilitation Timeline**:\n\n1. **Pre-prosthetic phase (current \u2014 weeks 2-6 post-amputation)**:\n- Residual limb shaping: Elastic shrinker sock or rigid removable dressing (already in place)\n- Desensitization program: Progressive texture exposure, tapping, massage\n- Phantom limb pain management: Mirror therapy, gabapentin/pregabalin, TENS\n- Contralateral limb strengthening and core stability\n- Upper extremity strengthening for crutch/walker use\n- Hip and knee ROM maintenance (prevent flexion contracture)\n\n2. **Preparatory/diagnostic prosthesis (weeks 6-8)**:\n- Once residual limb wound is fully healed, edema stabilized, and limb shape maturing\n- **Initial prosthetic prescription** for a young, active military patient:\n  - **Socket**: Total surface bearing (TSB) with gel liner and pin/lock suspension\n  - **Foot**: Energy-storing carbon fiber foot (e.g., dynamic response category) \u2014 appropriate for this patient's age and activity goals\n  - **K-level**: Anticipate **K4** (high activity, active athlete/military) given age, fitness level, and motivation\n- Initial gait training in parallel bars, progressing to open floor\n\n3. **Definitive prosthesis (months 3-6)**:\n- After residual limb volume stabilizes (typically 6-12 months)\n- May upgrade to sport-specific or running prosthesis for physical fitness goals\n- Consider microprocessor knee only if transfemoral (not needed for transtibial)\n\n4. **Advanced training (months 3-12)**:\n- Running program, agility training, sport-specific activities\n- Driving evaluation and training\n- Military-specific functional testing if return to duty is a goal\n\n**Special Considerations for This Patient**:\n- TBI-related balance issues may slow prosthetic training progression\n- Vestibular rehabilitation should occur concurrently\n- Shorter training sessions with more frequent breaks (cognitive fatigue)\n- Bilateral tinnitus may interfere with verbal cuing during gait training \u2014 use visual/tactile cues")
B.aQf=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","The patient is 6 weeks post-injury and has been fitted with a preparatory prosthesis. He is making progress with gait training but continues to have significant headaches, difficulty concentrating during therapy, vestibular symptoms, and worsening nightmares.")
B.b2M=new A.c("+ Outline the concurrent management plan addressing TBI rehabilitation, prosthetic training, vestibular rehabilitation, and PTSD screening.","**Concurrent Rehabilitation Plan**:\n\n**1. TBI Management**:\n- **Headache treatment**: Amitriptyline 25 mg at bedtime (treats headache AND insomnia AND neuropathic pain) OR topiramate if amitriptyline poorly tolerated. Avoid opioids for headaches. Acute: NSAIDs with caution for medication-overuse headache.\n- **Cognitive rehabilitation**: Speech-language pathology for attention, memory, and executive function training. Compensatory strategies (calendar system, alarms, checklists). Neuropsychological testing at 3 months to document recovery.\n- **Return-to-activity progression**: Graduated increase in cognitive and physical demands following Buffalo Concussion Treadmill Test protocol if needed.\n\n**2. Vestibular Rehabilitation**:\n- **Diagnosis**: Likely blast-related peripheral vestibular injury (bilateral). Obtain VNG/ENG and audiometry.\n- **Treatment**: Vestibular physical therapy \u2014 gaze stabilization exercises (VOR x 1 and x 2), habituation exercises for motion sensitivity, balance retraining on various surfaces.\n- **Integration with prosthetic training**: Balance activities during gait training serve double duty for vestibular and prosthetic rehab.\n\n**3. Prosthetic Training (continued)**:\n- Progressive gait training: level surfaces \u2192 inclines \u2192 stairs \u2192 uneven terrain\n- Weight-shifting and balance activities on prosthesis\n- Donning/doffing independence\n- Limb care and skin inspection education\n- Distance and endurance building\n\n**4. PTSD and Mental Health**:\n- **Immediate referral**: Psychology/psychiatry for PCL-5 score of 48 and active PTSD symptoms\n- **Evidence-based treatment**: Cognitive Processing Therapy (CPT) or Prolonged Exposure (PE) therapy \u2014 gold standard for combat PTSD\n- **Pharmacotherapy**: Prazosin 1-2 mg at bedtime for trauma-related nightmares (titrate to effect). SSRI (sertraline \u2014 FDA-approved for PTSD) if symptoms warrant.\n- **Sleep hygiene**: Consistent sleep-wake schedule, limit caffeine, dark/cool room, white noise machine for tinnitus\n- **Grief support**: His close friend was killed \u2014 address traumatic grief specifically\n- **Suicide risk monitoring**: Regular safety assessments given risk factors (male, combat veteran, TBI, PTSD, chronic pain, isolation)\n\n**5. Tinnitus Management**:\n- Audiology: Hearing aids with tinnitus masking features for bilateral SNHL\n- Tinnitus Retraining Therapy (TRT) or CBT-based tinnitus management\n- Sound enrichment at night (white noise) for sleep\n\n**6. Phantom Limb Pain**:\n- Mirror therapy: 15 minutes daily\n- Gabapentin 300-600 mg TID (also helps with TBI-related neuropathic symptoms)\n- TENS to residual limb\n- Graded motor imagery program")
B.aXP=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient is being treated at a VA Polytrauma Rehabilitation Center. His mother asks about the VA system of care and what benefits he is entitled to.")
B.aQS=new A.c("+ Describe the VA Polytrauma System of Care and the benefits/transition resources available to this veteran.","**VA Polytrauma System of Care (PSC) \u2014 Four-Tiered Model**:\n\n1. **Polytrauma Rehabilitation Centers (PRC)** \u2014 5 sites nationally (Tampa, Richmond, Minneapolis, Palo Alto, San Antonio). Provide acute comprehensive inpatient polytrauma rehabilitation. **This patient is at this level.**\n2. **Polytrauma Network Sites (PNS)** \u2014 23 sites. Provide post-acute rehabilitation, consultative services.\n3. **Polytrauma Support Clinic Teams (PSCT)** \u2014 At every VA medical center. Outpatient follow-up and ongoing case management.\n4. **Polytrauma Points of Contact (PPOC)** \u2014 Every VA facility. Screening and referral coordination.\n\n**Key VA Benefits for This Patient**:\n- **Service-connected disability rating**: Each condition (amputation, TBI, hearing loss, tinnitus, PTSD) will receive a separate VA disability rating. Combined rating will likely exceed 80-100%.\n- **Prosthetic services**: VA provides ALL prosthetic limbs and components at no cost to the veteran \u2014 including sport/recreation prostheses, backup limbs, and lifetime replacement.\n- **Traumatic Servicemembers Group Life Insurance (TSGLI)**: One-time lump sum payment for qualifying traumatic injuries (up to $100,000 for amputation).\n- **Specially Adapted Housing (SAH)**: Grants for home modifications if rated for specific service-connected disabilities.\n- **Automobile Adaptive Equipment**: VA provides vehicle modifications and may provide a one-time automobile purchase grant.\n- **Vocational Rehabilitation (Chapter 31)**: Education and job training benefits if he cannot return to prior military duty.\n- **Caregiver Support Program**: If his mother or future spouse provides caregiving, stipend and support services are available.\n\n**Military Transition Resources**:\n- **Medical Evaluation Board (MEB)/Physical Evaluation Board (PEB)**: Military disability evaluation process that will determine fitness for continued service vs. medical retirement.\n- **Integrated Disability Evaluation System (IDES)**: Joint DoD/VA process allowing simultaneous military separation and VA disability rating.\n- **Warrior Transition Unit**: Provides structured support during recovery and transition.\n- **Purple Heart eligibility**: Awarded for combat-related injuries; entitles veteran to priority VA care.\n\n**Psychosocial Transition Support**:\n- Peer mentoring programs (Operation Comfort, Wounded Warrior Project)\n- Adaptive sports programs (VA Adaptive Sports, Paralympic military program)\n- Vet Center services for readjustment counseling\n- Suicide prevention coordinator engagement")
B.aQX=new A.c(u.j,"At the weekly team meeting, the psychologist reports that the patient has been isolating in his room, skipping group therapy sessions, and declining to interact with other patients on the unit. He told the nursing staff: 'Group therapy is pointless. Nobody understands what I've been through. I just want to be left alone.'")
B.aV1=new A.c("+ How would you engage this veteran who is isolating and refusing group therapy?","**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Meet the patient in his space (do not force the group setting)**:\nVisit him in his room rather than summoning him to an office. Sit down, demonstrate unhurried availability.\n\n'I heard you've been spending more time in your room lately and that group therapy hasn't felt helpful. I wanted to check in with you and hear your perspective.'\n\n**2. Validate without agreeing to discontinue treatment**:\n'I want you to know that what you are feeling \u2014 wanting to pull away, not wanting to talk about it \u2014 that is actually one of the most common responses after what you have been through. Avoidance and withdrawal are part of how the brain tries to protect itself after trauma. You are not broken, and you are not being difficult.'\n\n**3. Address the \"nobody understands\" statement**:\n'You said nobody understands what you've been through. You may be right that nobody has had your exact experience. But I will say this \u2014 there are three other guys on this unit right now who have been through IED blasts, and two of them felt exactly the way you feel now when they first got here. One of them told me last week that the thing that helped him most was having someone who got it to talk to. I am not going to force you, but I think you might be surprised.'\n\n**4. Reframe group therapy and offer alternatives**:\n'Group therapy is not about sitting in a circle sharing your feelings \u2014 I know that image is not appealing. There are different types of groups: adaptive sports, art therapy, cooking class, and peer mentoring. Some of these may feel less clinical and more natural. Would you be willing to try just one session of adaptive sports this week? If it is not for you, I will not push.'\n\n**5. Leverage his strengths and identity**:\n'You are an infantry soldier. You did not get through two deployments by quitting when things got hard. I am not asking you to feel better \u2014 I am asking you to show up and give it a chance, the same way you would push through a tough training day. The discipline you already have is your greatest asset in this recovery.'\n\n**6. Establish a concrete, small commitment**:\n'Here is what I would propose: this week, try one adaptive sports session and one individual meeting with your psychologist. That is it. After that, we talk again and you tell me honestly how it went. Deal?'\n\n**Key principles**: Meet the patient where they are, validate military culture and identity, avoid coercion, use motivational interviewing techniques, offer choice and autonomy, reframe therapy in non-stigmatizing terms, leverage peer connection, establish small achievable commitments.")
B.aFQ=s([B.b0v,B.aRN,B.aYi,B.b2w,B.aVL,B.b0k,B.aQf,B.b2M,B.aXP,B.aQS,B.aQX,B.aV1],t.E)
B.QZ=new A.a5("polytrauma-blast-tbi-amputation","Polytrauma \u2014 Blast Injury with TBI and Transtibial Amputation",B.aFQ,!1)
B.aXe=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 28-year-old female is referred to the burn rehabilitation clinic 4 months after sustaining severe facial, neck, and bilateral hand burns from a deliberate acid attack by a former partner. She underwent emergent irrigation and debridement in the ED, followed by three staged reconstructive surgeries including split-thickness skin grafting to her bilateral hands and neck, and a full-thickness skin graft from her groin to her left cheek. Additional reconstructive procedures are planned, including nasal reconstruction and revision of oral commissure contracture. She has not left her parents' home since hospital discharge 2 months ago. She wears a scarf covering her entire face when anyone other than her parents is present, including during this clinic visit. Her mother accompanies her and reports the patient has been sleeping 16 hours per day, eating very little, and has made statements about 'not wanting to be here anymore.'")
B.aTi=new A.c("History & Systems Review:",'- **Injury Details**: Deliberate acid attack (concentrated sulfuric acid) by ex-boyfriend 4 months ago. Burns to face (forehead spared by hairline, full-thickness burns to bilateral cheeks, nose, lips, chin), anterior neck (circumferential deep partial to full-thickness), and bilateral dorsal hands (deep partial-thickness \u2014 defensive injuries).\n- **Surgical History**: (1) ED irrigation and debridement day 0; (2) STSG bilateral dorsal hands and anterior neck, day 7; (3) FTSG from left groin to left cheek, day 21; (4) Oral commissure release and Z-plasty, day 45. Planned: nasal reconstruction with paramedian forehead flap, right cheek revision, eyelid reconstruction.\n- **Current Wound Status**: All grafts healed. Active hypertrophic scarring throughout. Developing microstomia (oral aperture 25 mm, premorbid estimated 45-50 mm). Left nasal alar collapse with partial nasal airway obstruction. Right lower eyelid ectropion requiring taping.\n- **Functional Impact**: Cannot open mouth wide enough to eat solid foods \u2014 on soft/puree diet. Difficulty with oral hygiene. Cannot apply makeup (previously important to her). Bilateral hand scarring limits fine motor \u2014 cannot button clothes or open containers independently.\n- **Pain**: Neuropathic burning in facial scars 7/10. Severe pruritus of neck and hand scars. Currently on gabapentin 600 mg TID and oxycodone 10 mg Q6H PRN.\n- **Psychological**: PHQ-9 score 24 (severe depression). PTSD Checklist (PCL-5) score 62 (clinical PTSD; cutoff 33). Reports nightmares 4-5 nights/week of the attack. Hypervigilance \u2014 startles at loud noises, cannot be in same room as men other than her father. Suicidal ideation \u2014 passive ("I don\'t want to be here") without active plan. Refusing psychiatric medication \u2014 "pills won\'t fix my face."\n- **Social**: Single. Previously worked as a dental hygienist \u2014 cannot return due to facial scarring and hand dysfunction. Living with parents. No income. Has not spoken to friends since the attack. Victim protection order in place against the attacker, who is incarcerated awaiting trial.\n- **PMH**: Previously healthy. No prior psychiatric history.\n- **Medications**: Gabapentin 600 mg TID, oxycodone 10 mg Q6H PRN, diphenhydramine 25 mg PRN pruritus, melatonin 5 mg QHS')
B.b0t=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 110/68 mmHg, HR 76 bpm, RR 14, SpO2 99% on RA, Temp 36.7C, BMI 19.2 (pre-injury BMI 23).\n\n**General**: Thin female wearing a full-face scarf. Reluctantly removes scarf after reassurance. Avoids eye contact. Flat affect. Whispered responses.\n\n**Face and Neck**:\n- **Forehead**: Spared above hairline. Mild scarring at hairline border.\n- **Bilateral cheeks**: Full-thickness grafted areas with raised, erythematous, firm hypertrophic scars. Left cheek FTSG is a different color and texture than surrounding skin. Right cheek has contracted, irregular scar tissue pending revision.\n- **Nose**: Significant alar destruction bilaterally. Left nostril collapsed. Breathing through mouth primarily. Nasal reconstruction planned.\n- **Lips**: Scarring of both lips with loss of vermillion border definition. **Oral commissure contracture** \u2014 microstomia with oral aperture measuring **25 mm** (functional minimum for eating is approximately 35-40 mm). Z-plasty sites healing but contracture recurrence beginning.\n- **Eyelids**: Right lower eyelid ectropion \u2014 scleral show 3 mm, incomplete eye closure with forced blink. Taping in place. Left eyelids intact but with scar banding inferiorly.\n- **Neck**: Circumferential hypertrophic scarring. Band-like anterior scar contracture limiting neck extension to 10 degrees (normal 45-60). Chin-to-chest distance limited.\n- **Ears**: Right ear \u2014 loss of superior helix from acid exposure. Left ear intact.\n\n**Bilateral Hands**:\n- **Dorsal surface**: Grafted, healed. Active hypertrophic scarring with web space contracture between thumb and index finger bilaterally. First web space angle 25 degrees (normal 45-60 degrees).\n- **ROM**: MCP flexion 60/80 degrees (limited by dorsal scar tightness). Grip strength R 10 kg, L 8 kg (severely reduced; pre-injury estimated 25-30 kg). Fine motor: unable to pick up a penny, difficulty with buttons, cannot hold a toothbrush effectively.\n- **Sensation**: Hypersensitivity of grafted areas. Pain with light touch over dorsal hands.\n\n**Psychological Observation**: Does not initiate conversation. Answers in 1-2 word whispers. Becomes tearful when scarf is removed. Flinches when the (male) medical assistant enters the room. Mother provides most of the history.")
B.b2l=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The burn surgeon asks you to evaluate scar contracture severity and develop a rehabilitation plan prioritizing function while additional reconstructive surgeries are planned.")
B.aZT=new A.c("+ Assess this patient's microstomia and oral commissure contracture. What splinting and prevention strategies are indicated, and how do you grade scar contracture severity?","**Microstomia Assessment**:\n- **Normal oral aperture**: 45-50 mm (intercommissural distance)\n- **Functional minimum**: 35-40 mm (required for eating utensils, oral hygiene, dental care)\n- **This patient**: 25 mm \u2014 severely restricted. Cannot insert a standard toothbrush. Cannot eat with a fork. Cannot undergo standard dental examination or intubation.\n- **Cause**: Circumoral burn scar contracture pulling the commissures medially. Z-plasty provided temporary improvement but contracture is recurring (expected \u2014 scars contract during the proliferative/maturation phase for 12-18 months).\n\n**Scar Contracture Grading (Modified Vancouver Scar Scale components)**:\n- **Vascularity**: 0 (normal) to 3 (purple) \u2014 this patient's facial scars are erythematous (score 2)\n- **Pliability**: 0 (normal) to 5 (contracture) \u2014 firm to banding (score 4)\n- **Height**: 0 (flat) to 3 (>5mm) \u2014 raised 2-4 mm (score 2)\n- **Pigmentation**: 0 (normal) to 3 (mixed) \u2014 hypopigmented grafts with hyperpigmented scar borders (score 2)\n\n**Microstomia Prevention and Management**:\n\n1. **Oral Commissure Splint (Microstomia Prevention Appliance \u2014 MPA)**:\n- Custom-fabricated horizontal mouth splint that provides sustained outward stretch to the commissures\n- Worn for **prolonged periods** \u2014 initially 2 hours on/1 hour off during the day, and throughout the night\n- Must be comfortable enough for adherence; adjust gradually\n- Types: Dynamic (spring-loaded \u2014 provides active stretch) vs static (maintains position)\n- **TheraBite or Dynasplint oral system**: Commercially available option for progressive stretch\n\n2. **Oral Stretching Exercises**:\n- Active mouth opening exercises (maximal opening, hold 10 seconds, relax, repeat 10x) performed 4-6 times daily\n- Tongue depressor stacking for progressive stretch\n- Oral motor exercises: exaggerated vowel sounds, lip stretching, cheek puffing\n- **Goal**: Maintain >35 mm aperture to preserve functional eating and oral hygiene\n\n3. **Silicone Inserts**:\n- Custom-molded silicone conformers placed inside the oral commissures to provide sustained pressure and stretch\n- Worn under the splint or independently\n- Help maintain surgical gains between reconstructive procedures\n\n4. **Scar Management for Perioral Area**:\n- Silicone gel sheeting cut to conform around lips (difficult area \u2014 adherence is challenging)\n- Scar massage: Deep friction massage perpendicular to scar bands, 5-10 minutes, 3-4 times daily\n- Intralesional corticosteroid injection (triamcinolone 10-40 mg/mL) for hypertrophic bands \u2014 performed by burn surgeon\n\n5. **Coordination with Reconstructive Surgery**:\n- Each surgical procedure (commissuroplasty, flap reconstruction) will temporarily improve aperture\n- Aggressive splinting and therapy must resume within days of surgery to maintain gains\n- Communicate rehabilitation goals to the surgical team: adequate aperture for nutrition, hygiene, and eventually dental care and employment")
B.aRZ=new A.c("+ The patient's right lower eyelid ectropion is worsening. Describe the assessment and management approach.","**Eyelid Ectropion Assessment**:\n- **Definition**: Outward turning of the eyelid margin away from the globe, caused by scar contracture pulling the lid inferiorly\n- **Severity grading**: Mild (1-2 mm scleral show), moderate (2-4 mm), severe (>4 mm with corneal exposure)\n- **This patient**: 3 mm scleral show = moderate ectropion\n- **Complications if untreated**: Corneal exposure keratitis, corneal ulceration, vision loss, chronic tearing (paradoxically \u2014 exposed eye tears reflexively), chronic discomfort\n\n**Conservative Management (current \u2014 bridge to surgery)**:\n1. **Eyelid taping**: Steri-strips or surgical tape applied to support the lower lid in a closed or near-closed position. Particularly important at night.\n2. **Lubricating eye drops**: Artificial tears every 2-3 hours during the day. Lubricating ointment (preservative-free) at bedtime.\n3. **Moisture chamber**: Clear plastic eye shield at night to reduce corneal drying.\n4. **Scar massage and silicone**: Gentle vertical massage of the infraorbital scar to stretch the contracting tissue. Silicone gel applied to the scar band.\n5. **Ophthalmology referral**: Regular corneal examinations to monitor for keratitis. Urgent referral if patient reports eye pain, redness, decreased vision, or photophobia.\n\n**Surgical Management (planned)**:\n1. **Full-thickness skin graft reconstruction**: Release the scar contracture and place FTSG (typically from postauricular or supraclavicular donor site for color match) to reconstruct the lower eyelid.\n2. **Timing**: Should be prioritized in the surgical reconstruction plan given the risk of corneal damage. This is a **functional priority** over cosmetic reconstruction.\n3. **Post-surgical rehabilitation**: Resume eyelid taping, scar management, and splinting immediately post-operatively to prevent recurrence.\n\n**Key Teaching Point**: In facial burn rehabilitation, **eyelid protection is the highest functional priority** \u2014 corneal damage can cause permanent vision loss. Eyelid reconstruction should take precedence over other facial reconstructive procedures if resources or timing are limited.")
B.aQj=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Develop a comprehensive rehabilitation plan addressing this patient's functional deficits, scar management, pain, and psychosocial needs while coordinating with the planned reconstructive surgeries.")
B.aXU=new A.c("+ Outline a holistic burn rehabilitation plan for this patient, integrating functional restoration, scar management, and psychological treatment.",'**Comprehensive Burn Rehabilitation Plan**:\n\n**1. Facial Scar Management**:\n- **Custom facial pressure garment (transparent face orthosis \u2014 TFO)**: Clear thermoplastic mask custom-molded to the patient\'s face providing 25 mmHg continuous pressure. Worn 23 hours/day. More effective than fabric face masks for facial contours.\n- **Silicone inserts**: Placed under the TFO at concave areas (nasolabial folds, perioral area) to enhance contact pressure.\n- **Scar massage**: 3-4 times daily, 5 minutes per region. Deep cross-friction technique. Use cocoa butter or silicone-based cream.\n- **Laser therapy referral**: Pulsed dye laser for erythematous hypertrophic scars \u2014 reduces vascularity, thickness, and itch. Fractional CO2 laser to improve scar pliability. Typically 3-6 sessions spaced 4-6 weeks apart. Coordinate with surgical plan.\n\n**2. Hand Rehabilitation (OT-directed)**:\n- **Web space splinting**: Bilateral C-bar or thumb spica splints maintaining first web space in maximal abduction. Worn at night and during rest.\n- **Active ROM exercises**: Composite fist making, individual finger flexion/extension, thumb opposition exercises, 4-6 times daily.\n- **Progressive strengthening**: Theraputty graded resistance exercises, grip strengthening, pinch strengthening. Goal: restore functional grip for ADLs.\n- **Desensitization program**: Graded texture exposure for dorsal hand hypersensitivity \u2014 start with soft textures (cotton, silk), progress to rougher textures over 4-6 weeks.\n- **Functional retraining**: Practice buttons, zippers, utensil use, toothbrushing, writing.\n- **Adaptive equipment**: Built-up handles for utensils and toothbrush, button hook, jar opener until grip strength improves.\n\n**3. Neck Contracture Management**:\n- **Neck extension splint/conformer**: Custom cervical orthosis maintaining neck in extension. Worn at night and during rest periods.\n- **Active cervical ROM exercises**: Neck extension, rotation, lateral flexion stretches 4-6 times daily.\n- **No pillow at night**: Prevents neck flexion contracture.\n\n**4. Oral Function**:\n- **Microstomia splint**: As detailed in Domain B. Progressive wear schedule.\n- **SLP referral**: Oral motor exercises, articulation therapy if speech is affected by lip scarring.\n- **Dietary progression**: Work with dietitian to advance diet as oral aperture improves. High-calorie, high-protein soft diet for weight restoration (BMI 19.2 \u2014 underweight).\n\n**5. Pain Management**:\n- **Neuropathic pain**: Optimize gabapentin (consider increasing to 800 mg TID or switching to pregabalin for better tolerability). Add duloxetine 60 mg daily for dual benefit (pain + depression).\n- **Opioid taper**: Patient on oxycodone 40 mg daily equivalent \u2014 develop taper plan. Target 10% reduction every 1-2 weeks. Replace with non-opioid alternatives.\n- **Pruritus**: Cetirizine 10 mg BID (less sedating than diphenhydramine). Add topical menthol 1% cream or pramoxine lotion. Massage and moisturizing reduce itch.\n\n**6. Psychological Treatment \u2014 Critical Priority**:\n- **Immediate safety planning**: Passive suicidal ideation requires safety assessment. Establish safety plan (crisis contacts, coping strategies, lethal means restriction). If ideation escalates to active plan, psychiatric hospitalization.\n- **Trauma-focused therapy**: Prolonged Exposure (PE) therapy or Cognitive Processing Therapy (CPT) for PTSD \u2014 both have strong evidence in assault survivors.\n- **Pharmacotherapy**: Patient refuses medication. Explore her resistance with empathy. Validate that "pills will not fix my face" while explaining that medication can reduce nightmares, improve sleep, and give her the emotional bandwidth to engage in therapy and rehabilitation. Prazosin 1-2 mg QHS for nightmares specifically (if she agrees). SSRI for depression/PTSD.\n- **Graded social exposure**: Structured, therapist-guided program to progressively reintroduce social contact \u2014 start with one trusted friend, progress to small groups, then community outings.\n- **Body image therapy**: Mirror exposure therapy (graded), self-compassion exercises, cognitive restructuring of appearance-related beliefs.')
B.aZx=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient has no income, cannot return to her previous job as a dental hygienist, and has ongoing legal proceedings against her attacker. Her parents are absorbing all costs. She needs comprehensive support services.")
B.aYV=new A.c("+ Describe the victim advocacy, legal support, vocational rehabilitation, and community reintegration resources available to this patient.","**Victim Advocacy and Legal Support**:\n\n1. **Victim advocate coordinator**: Assign a dedicated victim advocate (available through the DA's office or community-based organizations) who can:\n- Navigate the criminal justice process (restraining orders, court appearances, victim impact statements)\n- Connect to victim compensation funds (Crime Victims Compensation \u2014 available in all 50 states, covers medical expenses, mental health treatment, lost wages, and rehabilitation costs not covered by insurance)\n- Coordinate with the prosecution team regarding the patient's medical status and ongoing treatment needs\n\n2. **Crime Victims Compensation**: State-funded program that can cover:\n- Medical and rehabilitation costs (copays, deductibles, uncovered services)\n- Mental health treatment\n- Lost wages\n- Prescription medications\n- Transportation to medical appointments\n- Application should be filed as soon as possible (time limits vary by state, typically 1-3 years from the crime)\n\n3. **Legal aid**: Pro bono legal services for civil protection orders, civil lawsuit for damages, and disability benefits applications.\n\n4. **Safe housing**: Confirm current living arrangement is secure. If the attacker makes bail or is released, reassess safety plan immediately. Coordinate with law enforcement.\n\n**Vocational Rehabilitation**:\n\n1. **State vocational rehabilitation (VR) services**: Referral to state VR agency. As a victim of violent crime with acquired disability, she is a priority candidate.\n2. **Career assessment**: Dental hygienist role requires close face-to-face patient contact and fine manual dexterity \u2014 may not be feasible in the near-term. Explore:\n- Dental hygiene education/teaching (less direct patient contact)\n- Healthcare administration or dental office management\n- Telehealth or remote work opportunities in healthcare\n3. **Retraining funding**: VR services can fund retraining, education, assistive technology, and job placement services.\n4. **SSDI/SSI application**: If unable to work for >12 months (likely given planned surgeries and recovery), apply for Social Security disability. Burn injury with documented functional limitations and psychological disability qualifies.\n\n**Community Reintegration Program**:\n\n1. **Phoenix Society for Burn Survivors**: National organization providing peer support, community programs, and the World Burn Congress \u2014 an annual conference where burn survivors connect.\n2. **Burn survivor peer mentor**: Match with a female burn survivor who has navigated similar challenges \u2014 particularly someone who experienced facial burns. Peer support is one of the strongest predictors of successful community reintegration.\n3. **Changing Faces (international) / AboutFace (North America)**: Organizations specifically supporting individuals with facial differences. Offer social skills workshops, community events, and advocacy.\n4. **Gradual community re-entry plan**:\n- Week 1-2: Brief outing with mother only (drive-through, park with few people)\n- Week 3-4: Brief interaction with a store clerk or librarian\n- Month 2: Attend a small burn support group meeting\n- Month 3: Visit a trusted friend's home\n- Month 4+: Public spaces with increasing social complexity\n5. **Social media and online community**: Many burn survivors find initial social reconnection through online communities before in-person contact. This can be a bridge.\n\n**Financial Safety Net**:\n- **Crime victims compensation** (immediate)\n- **SSDI application** (3-6 month process, retroactive once approved)\n- **State VR services** (ongoing)\n- **Hospital social work**: Charity care applications for ongoing surgical costs\n- **Community fundraising**: With patient's consent, a victim advocacy organization can help coordinate community support campaigns")
B.aZS=new A.c(u.j,'After the physical examination, with the scarf still off, the patient whispers: "People stare at me. Children point. A woman at the grocery store pulled her daughter away from me like I was a monster. I will never look normal again. Why would anyone want to be around me?"')
B.aMF=new A.c("+ How would you address this patient's profound body image distress and fear of social interaction following a violent, disfiguring attack?","**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Sit with the pain. Do not rush to reassure.**\n[Pause. Maintain eye contact. Let her see that you are not looking away from her face.]\n'I am sitting here looking at you right now, and I want you to know that I see you \u2014 not your scars. I see a 28-year-old woman who survived something that should never have happened to her. And I am not looking away.'\n\n**2. Validate the experiences she described without minimizing**:\n'The staring, the pointing, the woman pulling her child away \u2014 those things happened, and they are painful. I am not going to tell you that people will not notice your scars, because they will. Some people will react with fear or discomfort because they do not know how to respond to something unfamiliar. That is their limitation, not yours. But it still hurts, and you do not deserve it.'\n\n**3. Acknowledge that she is not responsible for what happened to her**:\n'I want to say something important. What happened to you was a crime. It was done to you. You did not cause this, and you do not deserve to be punished for it by hiding from the world. The person who did this has already taken enough from you. I do not want him to take your whole life.'\n\n**4. Provide honest framing about appearance and reconstruction**:\n'Your surgical team has a plan for nasal reconstruction, eyelid repair, and scar revision. Over the next one to two years, your appearance will continue to change. Scars soften and flatten. Color fades. Reconstructive surgery can make a significant difference. You will not look like you did before \u2014 I want to be honest about that \u2014 but you will not look like you do right now either. This is the worst it will be.'\n\n**5. Introduce coping strategies for social encounters**:\n'There is something I want to offer you. We work with a psychologist who specializes in helping people with visible differences develop confidence in social situations. She teaches specific strategies \u2014 how to respond when someone stares, how to put a curious child at ease, how to walk into a room and own it. These are skills, and they can be learned. Some of the most socially confident people I know are burn survivors who have learned these techniques.'\n\n**6. Introduce the idea of peer connection**:\n'I also know a woman \u2014 a burn survivor \u2014 who had facial burns similar to yours. She was exactly where you are two years ago. She told me once that the turning point for her was meeting another survivor who understood. Would you be willing to talk to her? It could be by phone or text first \u2014 you do not have to meet in person until you are ready.'\n\n**7. Gently challenge the \"monster\" narrative**:\n'You said you feel like people see you as a monster. I want to challenge that word. A monster is someone who throws acid on another person's face. A monster is not the person who survived it. You are not a monster. You are a survivor. And when you are ready, your story has the power to help other survivors too.'\n\n**8. End with immediate next steps \u2014 keep it small**:\n'For right now, I am not asking you to go to the grocery store or face a crowd. I am asking you to come back next week, let our hand therapist work with you on your grip so you can hold your own toothbrush again, and meet our psychologist for one conversation. That is it. Can we start there?'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Non-verbal presence (not looking away), validation without false reassurance, naming the crime and redirecting shame from victim to perpetrator, honest surgical prognosis, skills-based social confidence building, peer support, challenging internalized stigma, minimal actionable first steps to avoid overwhelming.")
B.aFL=s([B.aXe,B.aTi,B.b0t,B.b2l,B.aZT,B.aRZ,B.aQj,B.aXU,B.aZx,B.aYV,B.aZS,B.aMF],t.E)
B.PZ=new A.a5("burn-facial-acid-attack","Facial Burn with Psychosocial Reconstruction",B.aFL,!1)
B.b05=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 35-year-old male electrician is referred to outpatient PM&R 8 weeks after sustaining a high-voltage electrical burn injury (13,800 volts) while working on a power line. He suffered entry wounds on his right hand and exit wounds on both feet. He developed bilateral forearm compartment syndrome requiring emergent fasciotomies. He also suffered right median nerve injury at the wrist and left ulnar nerve injury at the elbow. He now presents with bilateral forearm surgical scars, limited hand function, neuropathic pain, and inability to work. He is the sole financial provider for his wife and three children (ages 2, 5, and 8).")
B.aTM=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **Mechanism**: High-voltage (13,800V) electrical contact while working on a power line without proper lockout/tagout. Right hand contacted the live wire (entry wound). Current passed through the body and exited through both feet (exit wounds on plantar surfaces). Brief cardiac arrest at scene \u2014 resuscitated with 1 shock from AED by coworkers.\n- **Acute Course**: Admitted to burn ICU. Cardiac monitoring x 72 hours (no arrhythmias after resuscitation). Rhabdomyolysis (peak CK 45,000 U/L) managed with aggressive IV fluids. Bilateral forearm compartment syndrome developed within 6 hours \u2014 emergent 4-compartment fasciotomies bilateral forearms. Fasciotomy wounds closed with split-thickness skin grafts at day 10.\n- **Nerve Injuries**: EMG/NCS at 6 weeks: Right median nerve \u2014 complete conduction block at wrist (no motor response, absent sensory). Left ulnar nerve \u2014 severe axonotmesis at elbow (fibrillations in FDI and ADM, no voluntary MUPs).\n- **Burns**: Entry wound right palm \u2014 deep partial/full thickness, healed with contracture. Exit wounds bilateral plantar feet \u2014 healed but with neuropathic pain on weight-bearing. Bilateral forearm fasciotomy scars \u2014 hypertrophic, pruritic, restricting ROM.\n- **Current Function**: Cannot grip objects with right hand (no thumb opposition, no finger flexion of index/middle). Left hand \u2014 weak grip, cannot spread fingers, drops small objects. Cannot return to electrician work. Cannot open jars, button shirts, type, or hold tools. Wife does all cooking and most childcare.\n- **Pain**: Right hand burning dysesthesia 8/10 constant. Bilateral plantar foot burning with weight-bearing 6/10. Bilateral forearm scar tightness and itching.\n- **Psychological**: PHQ-9 score 16. Reports flashbacks of the electrical shock. Nightmares. Avoids anything electrical \u2014 will not change a lightbulb. Financial stress \u2014 family has no income. Workers compensation claim filed but not yet approved.\n- **PMH**: Previously healthy. Non-smoker. No prior injuries.\n- **Medications**: Gabapentin 300 mg TID, oxycodone 5 mg Q6H PRN, ibuprofen 600 mg TID, melatonin 5 mg QHS.")
B.b0r=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 138/88 mmHg, HR 76 bpm, RR 14, SpO2 99% on RA, Temp 36.5C.\n\n**Bilateral Forearms**: Well-healed fasciotomy scars \u2014 hypertrophic, erythematous, raised, adherent to underlying tissue. Forearm ROM: Right wrist extension 30 degrees (limited by scar), flexion 40 degrees. Left wrist extension 45 degrees, flexion 55 degrees. Bilateral forearm supination limited to 60 degrees (scar contracture).\n\n**Right Hand (Entry Wound)**:\n- Deep scar on palm \u2014 contracted, limiting finger extension.\n- Thenar atrophy (median nerve). No thumb opposition. No FPL function.\n- Index and middle finger flexion 0/5 (FDS/FDP \u2014 median innervated).\n- Ring and small finger flexion 4/5 (ulnar innervated FDP intact).\n- Sensation: Absent in median distribution (thumb, index, middle, radial ring). Intact in ulnar distribution.\n- Tinel sign positive at wrist over median nerve.\n\n**Left Hand (Ulnar Nerve Injury)**:\n- Hypothenar atrophy. First dorsal interosseous atrophy.\n- Froment sign positive. Wartenberg sign positive.\n- Finger abduction 1/5. Finger adduction 2/5.\n- Small finger DIP flexion absent (FDP to small finger).\n- Sensation: Diminished in ulnar distribution (small finger, ulnar ring).\n- Clawing of ring and small fingers.\n\n**Bilateral Feet**: Healed exit wounds on plantar surfaces. Allodynia to light touch on bilateral soles. Gait: Antalgic, avoids full weight-bearing on forefeet.\n\n**Cardiac**: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs. EKG in chart: normal sinus rhythm, no arrhythmias.")
B.aNB=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","This patient has a complex presentation with electrical burn injuries, bilateral nerve injuries, compartment syndrome sequelae, and neuropathic pain.")
B.b0b=new A.c("+ Explain the unique pathophysiology of high-voltage electrical injuries and the prognosis for nerve recovery.",'**Electrical Burn Pathophysiology**:\n\n1. **Electrical vs Thermal Burns**: Electrical burns cause deep tissue injury along the current pathway that is far more extensive than visible surface burns. The "iceberg" analogy \u2014 surface wounds underestimate internal damage. Current follows the path of least resistance: nerves > blood vessels > muscle > skin > fat > bone.\n\n2. **Tissue Damage Mechanisms**:\n- **Joule heating**: Current passing through tissue generates heat proportional to resistance. Nerves and vessels are preferentially damaged because current concentrates there.\n- **Electroporation**: Cell membrane disruption from electrical field \u2014 causes delayed cell death even in tissue that initially appears viable.\n- **Vascular damage**: Thrombosis of small and medium vessels causes delayed tissue necrosis. This is why compartment syndrome develops hours after injury.\n\n3. **Compartment Syndrome**: Massive muscle edema from rhabdomyolysis and direct electrical damage causes compartment pressures to rise. Bilateral forearm compartments are classic for hand-to-foot electrical pathway. Fasciotomy must occur within 6 hours to prevent irreversible ischemic damage.\n\n4. **Nerve Injury Prognosis**:\n- **Right median nerve (complete block at wrist)**: Entry wound was at the right palm \u2014 direct electrical and thermal injury to the nerve. Prognosis is guarded. If the nerve is in continuity, recovery may occur over 6-18 months (axonal regeneration at 1mm/day). EMG at 3-4 months looking for nascent MUPs is key. If no recovery by 6 months, surgical exploration with nerve grafting or tendon transfers should be considered.\n- **Left ulnar nerve (axonotmesis at elbow)**: Current pathway caused thermal injury. Axonotmesis (Sunderland Grade II-III) has better prognosis than neurotmesis. Expect some recovery over 6-12 months. Serial EMG every 3 months to monitor reinnervation.\n\n5. **Delayed Complications of Electrical Injury**:\n- Cataracts (can develop months to years later \u2014 needs ophthalmology screening)\n- Cardiac arrhythmias (rare after initial 48 hours but monitor)\n- Neuropsychological changes (attention, memory, processing speed)\n- Peripheral neuropathy (can be progressive)\n- Heterotopic ossification at joints near current pathway')
B.aWn=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Develop a comprehensive rehabilitation plan for this patient's bilateral hand dysfunction, pain, scars, and psychological injuries.")
B.aXO=new A.c("+ Outline a rehabilitation plan addressing hand function, scar management, pain, and psychological recovery.","**Comprehensive Rehabilitation Plan**:\n\n**1. Hand Therapy (Critical Priority)**:\n- **Certified Hand Therapist (CHT)** 3-5x weekly.\n- **Right hand**: Custom resting hand splint maintaining thumb in opposition and wrist in neutral. ROM exercises for all joints. Tendon gliding exercises for intact tendons. If median nerve recovers \u2014 progressive strengthening. If no recovery by 6 months \u2014 tendon transfer surgery consultation (opponensplasty using ring finger FDS, tendon transfers for index/middle finger flexion).\n- **Left hand**: Anti-claw splint (MCP flexion block) to prevent clawing progression. Intrinsic muscle substitution exercises. If ulnar nerve recovers \u2014 progressive strengthening. If incomplete recovery \u2014 consider tendon transfers for claw correction.\n- **Adaptive equipment**: Built-up utensil handles, button hooks, universal cuff for tools. Start functional training with adapted tools immediately \u2014 do not wait for nerve recovery.\n\n**2. Scar Management**:\n- **Pressure garments**: Custom-fitted Jobst garments for bilateral forearms \u2014 wear 23 hours/day for 12-18 months.\n- **Silicone sheets**: Apply under pressure garments to hypertrophic scars.\n- **Scar massage**: Deep friction massage 10 minutes 3x daily to break adhesions.\n- **ROM exercises**: Aggressive stretching to prevent scar contracture progression, especially forearm supination and wrist extension.\n- **Palmar scar contracture**: May require surgical release with Z-plasty or skin grafting if conservative measures fail.\n\n**3. Pain Management**:\n- **Gabapentin**: Increase to 600-900 mg TID for neuropathic pain.\n- **Opioid taper**: Begin structured taper of oxycodone \u2014 replace with duloxetine 60 mg daily (neuropathic pain + depression benefit).\n- **Desensitization program**: Progressive tactile stimulation of hypersensitive areas (plantar feet, right palm).\n- **TENS**: Bilateral forearms and feet.\n- **Mirror therapy**: For right hand \u2014 may help with cortical reorganization.\n- **Plantar foot pain**: Custom orthotics with metatarsal offloading. Gel insoles. Gradual weight-bearing progression.\n\n**4. PTSD and Depression**:\n- **Psychiatry referral**: Optimize antidepressant (sertraline or switch to duloxetine for dual benefit).\n- **Trauma-focused therapy**: CPT or EMDR for electrical injury PTSD.\n- **Prazosin**: Consider for nightmares if trazodone insufficient.\n- **Avoidance behavior**: His fear of anything electrical is functionally impairing \u2014 this needs specific desensitization in therapy.\n\n**5. Cardiac Surveillance**:\n- Repeat EKG at 6 months and 1 year.\n- Ophthalmology exam for cataracts at 6 months and annually for 3 years.")
B.aU9=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","This patient is the sole breadwinner for a family of five and cannot return to his previous occupation. His workers compensation claim is pending.")
B.aTu=new A.c("+ How do you navigate the workers compensation process and vocational rehabilitation for this patient?",'**Workers Compensation and Vocational Planning**:\n\n1. **Workers Compensation Claim**:\n- **OSHA Reporting**: This injury must be reported to OSHA as a workplace injury. The employer is required to report electrical injuries resulting in hospitalization.\n- **Temporary Total Disability (TTD)**: Patient should be receiving TTD wage replacement while unable to work. If claim is pending, social work should assist with emergency financial resources.\n- **Documentation**: Provide detailed functional limitations, work restrictions, and expected recovery timeline to the WC carrier. Be specific: "Cannot grip objects >1 lb with right hand. Cannot perform fine motor tasks bilaterally. Cannot safely work around electrical equipment."\n\n2. **Maximum Medical Improvement (MMI)**:\n- Cannot be determined yet at 8 weeks \u2014 nerve recovery continues for 12-18 months.\n- Serial EMG every 3 months guides prognosis.\n- Do NOT declare MMI prematurely \u2014 this patient\'s function may significantly improve.\n\n3. **Vocational Rehabilitation**:\n- **Cannot return to electrician work**: High-voltage electrical work requires bilateral grip strength, fine motor dexterity, and working at heights \u2014 all permanently impacted.\n- **Transferable skills assessment**: Evaluate what skills from his electrician training transfer to other work (construction management, electrical inspection, teaching, safety officer).\n- **Retraining**: WC may fund vocational retraining if he cannot return to any gainful employment in his field.\n- **ADA considerations**: If he transitions to desk work, accommodations needed (adaptive keyboard, voice dictation, modified tools).\n\n4. **Immediate Financial Crisis**:\n- Social work referral for emergency assistance (rent, utilities, food).\n- Apply for Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP).\n- Contact local charities, burn survivor foundations (Phoenix Society), and union emergency funds (if union member).\n- Children may qualify for free/reduced school meals.\n\n5. **Impairment Rating** (when at MMI):\n- Use AMA Guides to the Evaluation of Permanent Impairment, 6th edition.\n- Rate bilateral upper extremity impairment (nerve injuries, ROM loss, grip strength loss).\n- Rate bilateral lower extremity impairment (plantar neuropathic pain).\n- Combine for whole-person impairment rating.')
B.aXV=new A.c(u.j,'During a follow-up visit, the patient breaks down: "I cannot hold my 2-year-old daughter. She reaches for me and I cannot pick her up because my hands don\'t work. My 8-year-old asked me to help with his homework and I cannot hold a pencil. My wife is working double shifts at Walmart because I cannot provide. We are about to lose our house. I was supposed to protect my family and instead I am destroying them. What is the point of all this therapy if I cannot even be a father?"')
B.aNY=new A.c("+ How do you address this patient's financial crisis, fatherhood grief, and hopelessness while maintaining therapeutic engagement?",'**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Acknowledge the crisis is real \u2014 do not minimize.**\n"I hear you, and I want you to know that what you are feeling is not an exaggeration. Your family is in a financial crisis, your hands are not working the way they need to, and you are watching the people you love most struggle because of something that happened to you. That is an enormous burden, and I am not going to pretend that positive thinking fixes it."\n\n**2. Separate the fixable from the grievable.**\n"There are two things happening right now, and they need different responses. First, there is a financial emergency \u2014 and that is something we can act on today. Second, there is grief \u2014 grief about not being able to hold your daughter, help with homework, provide for your family the way you used to. The grief is real and it deserves space. But the financial crisis needs action right now, so let me start there."\n\n**3. Take immediate action on the financial crisis.**\n"Before you leave today, I am going to have our social worker sit down with you. We are going to: file an emergency appeal on your workers comp claim for temporary disability payments. Apply for emergency assistance through the Phoenix Society burn survivor fund. Connect your wife with our patient family support services. Check if your children qualify for additional school-based resources. This is not charity \u2014 these are programs that exist for exactly this situation."\n\n**4. Address the fatherhood grief specifically.**\n"Now let me talk about your daughter. You said you cannot hold her. Right now, that is true for a two-handed lift. But can you sit in a chair and have her placed in your lap? Can you wrap your arms around her even if your grip is weak? Can you read to her, sing to her, make her laugh? Being a father is not about grip strength. Your daughter does not need you to pick her up \u2014 she needs you to be present, and you are."\n\n**5. Reframe the homework situation.**\n"Your 8-year-old asked for help with homework. You cannot hold a pencil yet. But can you sit next to him and talk him through problems? Can you use voice-to-text to type? We can get you adapted pencil grips, built-up pens, or a tablet with a stylus that is easier to hold. These are not giving up \u2014 they are adapting so you can do the thing that matters, which is being there for your son."\n\n**6. Connect therapy goals to what he cares about.**\n"You asked what is the point of all this therapy. The point is your daughter. The point is homework with your son. Every exercise we do with your hands is not abstract \u2014 it is about getting you back to the things that make you a father. So let me make you a deal: you tell me the three things you most want to be able to do with your kids, and I will build your entire therapy program around those three things."\n\n**7. Safety check.**\n"One more thing. You said \'what is the point.\' I need to ask you directly: are you having thoughts of harming yourself?"\n[Assess and respond appropriately based on answer]\n\n**Key Principles**: Validated the crisis as real. Took concrete action on the financial emergency (social work referral). Separated practical problems from grief. Reframed fatherhood around presence rather than physical capability. Connected rehabilitation goals to his deepest motivation. Performed suicide risk assessment.')
B.aFc=s([B.b05,B.aTM,B.b0r,B.aNB,B.b0b,B.aWn,B.aXO,B.aU9,B.aTu,B.aXV,B.aNY],t.E)
B.PM=new A.a5("burn-electrical-injury","Electrical Burn with Compartment Syndrome Sequelae",B.aFc,!1)
B.b10=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 45-year-old right-handed female is referred to the PM&R/neuromuscular clinic by her primary care physician with a 3-month history of intermittent drooping of both eyelids and double vision that worsens throughout the day. She initially noticed the ptosis in the evening while watching television, but it has become progressively more frequent. Over the past month, she has developed jaw fatigue while chewing dinner and occasional difficulty swallowing solid foods. She reports that her symptoms are minimal in the morning but become significantly worse by late afternoon. She denies limb weakness, shortness of breath, or difficulty holding her head up. She has no prior neurologic history.")
B.aSi=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **Symptom Pattern**: Fluctuating ptosis (bilateral, right worse than left), diplopia (horizontal, worse with sustained gaze), jaw fatigue with prolonged chewing, mild dysphagia for solids, all worse with fatigue and later in the day, improved with rest\n- **Negative Symptoms**: No limb weakness, no respiratory difficulty, no neck weakness (head drop), no nasal speech, no difficulty climbing stairs or rising from a chair\n- **Onset**: Insidious over 3 months with progressive worsening\n- **Aggravating Factors**: Prolonged activity, heat exposure, stress, late in the day\n- **Relieving Factors**: Rest, sleep, cool temperatures\n- **PMH**: Hypothyroidism (on levothyroxine), well-controlled; no other autoimmune diseases\n- **Medications**: Levothyroxine 75 mcg daily, calcium/vitamin D supplement\n- **Family History**: Mother has rheumatoid arthritis; no family history of neuromuscular disease\n- **Social**: High school teacher, married, two children; never smoked; social alcohol only\n- **Review of Systems**: No weight change, no rash, no joint pain, no Raynaud phenomenon, no dry eyes/mouth")
B.b3o=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 122/74 mmHg, HR 72 bpm, RR 14, SpO2 99% on RA, Temp 36.7C.\n\n**General**: Well-appearing female in no acute distress.\n\n**Neurologic Exam**:\n- **Mental Status**: Alert, oriented, cognition intact.\n- **Cranial Nerves**: Bilateral ptosis, right > left, with approximately 3 mm of lid droop on the right at rest. Sustained upgaze for 60 seconds produces worsening bilateral ptosis (fatigable ptosis \u2014 right lid covers pupil by 60 seconds). Extraocular movements full initially but sustained lateral gaze produces diplopia at 30 seconds. Pupils equal and reactive. Facial strength intact. Jaw closure 5/5 initially but fatigues to 4/5 with sustained jaw clench for 30 seconds. Palate elevates symmetrically. Tongue midline with normal bulk.\n- **Ice Pack Test**: Application of an ice pack to the right eyelid for 2 minutes produces **measurable improvement in ptosis** (lid droop decreases from 3 mm to 1 mm). **Positive ice pack test.**\n- **Motor**: Bilateral upper and lower extremity strength 5/5 in all muscle groups. No fatigable limb weakness with repetitive testing (10 repetitions of shoulder abduction, hip flexion).\n- **Sensation**: Intact throughout.\n- **Reflexes**: 2+ and symmetric throughout. Plantar responses flexor.\n- **Coordination**: Intact finger-to-nose and heel-to-shin bilaterally.\n\n**Pulmonary**: Clear to auscultation. Negative inspiratory force (NIF) -65 cmH2O (normal >-60). Forced vital capacity 3.2 L (92% predicted).\n\n**Neck**: No thyromegaly. No lymphadenopathy.")
B.aO5=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The clinical presentation is highly suggestive of myasthenia gravis. A systematic diagnostic workup is needed to confirm the diagnosis and evaluate for associated conditions.")
B.aUq=new A.c("+ What serological and electrodiagnostic tests would you order to confirm MG? Why is CT chest essential?","**Serological Testing**:\n1. **Acetylcholine receptor (AChR) antibodies**: Positive in **85% of generalized MG** and **50% of ocular MG**. This is the first-line serological test. Includes binding, blocking, and modulating antibodies.\n2. **Muscle-specific kinase (MuSK) antibodies**: Order if AChR antibodies are negative. Positive in **40-50% of AChR-seronegative** patients. MuSK-positive MG has distinct clinical features (prominent bulbar and facial weakness, muscle atrophy).\n3. **LRP4 antibodies**: Newer test for double-seronegative MG (both AChR and MuSK negative). Available at specialized labs.\n4. **Striational (anti-striated muscle) antibodies**: If positive, increases concern for **thymoma** (present in 75-85% of thymoma-associated MG).\n\n**Electrodiagnostic Studies (EDX)**:\n1. **Repetitive nerve stimulation (RNS)**: Low-frequency stimulation (2-3 Hz) of a motor nerve while recording CMAP. A **decremental response of \u226510%** between the first and fourth/fifth response is diagnostic. Test proximal muscles (spinal accessory-trapezius, facial nerve-nasalis) for higher sensitivity in ocular/bulbar MG. Sensitivity: 75% for generalized MG, only 30-50% for ocular MG.\n2. **Single-fiber EMG (SFEMG)**: Most sensitive test for MG (sensitivity 92-99%). Measures **jitter** (variability in time interval between firing of two muscle fibers in the same motor unit). Increased jitter and **blocking** are characteristic. Performed in orbicularis oculi for ocular MG or extensor digitorum communis for generalized.\n\n**CT Chest (with contrast)**:\n- **Essential** in all newly diagnosed MG patients to evaluate for **thymoma**.\n- **10-15%** of MG patients have thymoma; conversely, **30-50%** of thymoma patients develop MG.\n- **Thymic hyperplasia** is present in **60-70%** of AChR-positive MG patients (especially younger women).\n- Thymoma is an indication for **thymectomy regardless of MG severity**.\n- If CT is equivocal, MRI chest can provide better characterization.\n\n**Additional Workup**:\n- TSH (thyroid disease coexists in 10-15% of MG patients \u2014 she already has hypothyroidism)\n- ANA, RF (screen for coexisting autoimmune diseases)\n- Baseline CBC, CMP, LFTs (before starting immunosuppression)\n- Pulmonary function tests with NIF and FVC as baseline")
B.aZ3=new A.c("+ AChR antibodies return positive. CT chest shows an anterior mediastinal mass. How does this change your management?","**Anterior Mediastinal Mass \u2014 Presumed Thymoma**:\n\nThis finding significantly changes management priorities:\n\n1. **Thymoma staging and surgical planning**: Refer to thoracic surgery for **thymectomy**. Thymoma is an indication for surgical resection regardless of MG severity. Surgical approach: median sternotomy or video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS) for complete thymectomy.\n\n2. **Masaoka staging** (determined pathologically post-resection):\n- Stage I: Encapsulated, no invasion\n- Stage II: Microscopic or macroscopic invasion into capsule or fat\n- Stage III: Invasion into surrounding structures (pericardium, lung, great vessels)\n- Stage IV: Pleural/pericardial dissemination or distant metastases\n\n3. **Pre-operative MG optimization**: The patient must be medically optimized before surgery to reduce the risk of **post-operative myasthenic crisis**.\n- Start pyridostigmine (acetylcholinesterase inhibitor) \u2014 see management plan\n- Consider IVIG or plasmapheresis pre-operatively if bulbar or respiratory symptoms develop\n- Anesthesia must be aware: avoid neuromuscular blocking agents (succinylcholine is contraindicated; non-depolarizing agents should be used at reduced doses with careful monitoring)\n\n4. **Post-thymectomy outcomes**: Improvement in MG symptoms occurs in **70-80%** of patients, but **complete stable remission** may take **2-5 years**. MG may transiently worsen post-operatively.\n\n5. **Adjuvant therapy**: Radiation therapy may be indicated for incompletely resected or advanced-stage thymoma (Stage III-IV).\n\n6. **Long-term surveillance**: Thymoma can recur; serial CT chest imaging recommended.")
B.b0e=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","The patient undergoes successful thymectomy. Pathology confirms a Stage II thymoma (WHO type B2). Post-operatively, her ptosis and diplopia persist, and she develops new mild proximal upper extremity weakness (deltoids 4+/5 bilaterally). She is now classified as MGFA Class IIa.")
B.aYs=new A.c("+ Outline the pharmacologic management of MG and the exercise/rehabilitation guidelines.","**Pharmacologic Management (Stepwise Approach)**:\n\n1. **Pyridostigmine (Mestinon)**: First-line symptomatic treatment.\n- Starting dose: 30 mg TID, titrate to 60 mg every 4-6 hours as needed (max 120 mg every 4 hours)\n- Mechanism: Acetylcholinesterase inhibitor \u2014 increases ACh availability at the NMJ\n- Side effects: Cholinergic (diarrhea, cramping, increased secretions, bradycardia). Use glycopyrrolate for GI side effects.\n- **Cholinergic crisis**: Overdosing causes weakness PLUS excessive secretions, miosis, bradycardia \u2014 distinguish from myasthenic crisis.\n\n2. **Corticosteroids**: If pyridostigmine alone insufficient.\n- Prednisone starting at 10-20 mg daily, gradually increase to 40-60 mg daily\n- **Caution**: Rapid initiation of high-dose steroids can cause transient worsening \u2014 must start low and titrate slowly in an outpatient setting or initiate in hospital if bulbar/respiratory symptoms present\n- Taper once remission achieved; chronic low-dose may be needed\n\n3. **Steroid-sparing immunosuppressants** (add if steroid-dependent or intolerant):\n- **Mycophenolate mofetil** 1000 mg BID or **Azathioprine** 2-3 mg/kg/day (check TPMT before starting)\n- Onset of benefit: 6-12 months for azathioprine, 6 months for mycophenolate\n- Monitoring: CBC and LFTs every 1-3 months\n\n4. **Rescue therapies** (for exacerbations or crisis):\n- **IVIG** (2 g/kg over 2-5 days) or **Plasmapheresis** (5 exchanges over 10-14 days)\n- Used for myasthenic crisis, pre-operative optimization, or severe exacerbations\n\n5. **Newer agents**: Eculizumab (complement inhibitor), efgartigimod (FcRn inhibitor) \u2014 for refractory generalized AChR-positive MG.\n\n**Exercise and Rehabilitation Guidelines**:\n- **Exercise is safe and beneficial** in stable MG when properly supervised\n- **Aerobic exercise**: Low-to-moderate intensity, 20-30 minutes, 3-5 times/week. Avoid exercising in extreme heat.\n- **Resistance training**: Low-to-moderate intensity, avoid exhaustive exercise. Use RPE scale (keep at 12-14/20). Rest between sets.\n- **Timing**: Exercise in the morning when strength is best (after pyridostigmine dose has taken effect, typically 1-2 hours after dosing)\n- **Monitoring**: Teach patient to recognize signs of overexertion (increased ptosis, difficulty swallowing, voice changes). Stop exercise if these occur.\n- **Pulmonary rehabilitation**: Inspiratory muscle training may improve respiratory function\n- **Avoid**: Extreme heat exposure, exhaustive exercise, exercise late in the day when symptoms peak")
B.b_I=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient is stabilized on pyridostigmine and low-dose prednisone. She asks about medications she should avoid and what to do in an emergency.")
B.aO4=new A.c("+ What medications are contraindicated or require caution in MG? Describe the protocol for MG crisis prevention and management.","**Medications to AVOID or Use with Extreme Caution in MG**:\n\n1. **Antibiotics**: Aminoglycosides (gentamicin, tobramycin), fluoroquinolones (ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin), macrolides (azithromycin) \u2014 all can worsen neuromuscular transmission\n2. **Cardiac medications**: Beta-blockers (propranolol, metoprolol), calcium channel blockers (verapamil), procainamide, quinidine\n3. **Neuromuscular blocking agents**: Succinylcholine (prolonged paralysis), non-depolarizing agents (exaggerated response \u2014 use only with careful monitoring)\n4. **Other**: Magnesium sulfate (blocks NMJ transmission \u2014 dangerous), D-penicillamine (can induce MG), botulinum toxin, quinine, statins (rare reports of worsening), immune checkpoint inhibitors (pembrolizumab, nivolumab \u2014 can trigger or worsen MG)\n\n**Patient should carry a medical alert card/bracelet listing the MG diagnosis and these precautions.**\n\n**MG Crisis Prevention and Management**:\n\n**Myasthenic Crisis Definition**: Respiratory failure requiring intubation/mechanical ventilation due to MG exacerbation. Mortality 3-5% with modern ICU care.\n\n**Prevention**:\n- Regular monitoring of NIF and FVC (crisis risk increases when FVC <15-20 mL/kg or NIF weaker than -20 cmH2O)\n- Medication adherence counseling\n- Avoid known triggers: infection, surgery, medication changes, emotional stress, heat\n- Pre-operative IVIG/PLEX for any planned surgery\n- Annual influenza and pneumococcal vaccination (avoid live vaccines if on immunosuppression)\n\n**Management Protocol (20/30/40 Rule)**:\n- FVC <20 mL/kg \u2014 consider ICU admission\n- NIF weaker than -30 cmH2O \u2014 prepare for possible intubation\n- FVC declining >30% from baseline \u2014 strong indication for intubation\n\n**Treatment of Crisis**:\n1. ICU admission with serial respiratory monitoring (NIF, FVC every 4-6 hours)\n2. **Plasmapheresis** or **IVIG** \u2014 both equally effective; PLEX may have faster onset\n3. Hold or reduce pyridostigmine (excess cholinergic secretions complicate respiratory management)\n4. Identify and treat trigger (infection most common)\n5. Avoid medications that worsen MG in the ICU setting\n6. Intubation if needed (use non-depolarizing agents at 50% dose with careful monitoring)")
B.aSd=new A.c(u.j,"At a follow-up visit, the patient is visibly anxious. She says: 'I had a really scary episode last week where I felt like I couldn't swallow and my breathing felt tight at a parent-teacher conference. I don't know when this disease is going to act up. I'm afraid to go anywhere or do anything because I don't know if it will happen again.'")
B.aTW=new A.c("+ How would you counsel this patient about coping with the unpredictability of MG symptoms?","**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Validate the fear and assess the episode**:\n'That sounds like a very frightening experience, and I completely understand why it has made you anxious. Before we talk about coping strategies, I want to make sure I understand exactly what happened. Can you walk me through the episode? How long did it last? Did you take your pyridostigmine on time that day? Were you under extra stress?'\n\n**2. Educate about triggers and patterns**:\n'One thing that can actually help reduce the unpredictability is learning your personal triggers. For many patients, stress, heat, fatigue, and missed medications are the most common triggers. It sounds like the parent-teacher conference may have involved stress and possibly a long evening when your symptoms are naturally at their worst. Understanding these patterns gives you more control than you might realize.'\n\n**3. Create an action plan (reduces anxiety about the unknown)**:\n'I want you to have a clear plan so that if this happens again, you know exactly what to do. First, always carry an extra dose of pyridostigmine with you. If you notice worsening swallowing or breathing, take your medication, sit down, and rest. If symptoms do not improve within 20-30 minutes or are getting worse, go to the emergency room and present your MG medical alert card. I am going to write a letter for you to carry that explains your diagnosis and emergency management to any ER physician.'\n\n**4. Address the avoidance behavior**:\n'I hear you saying that you are avoiding activities because of the fear. That is a very natural response, but I am concerned that avoiding the things you enjoy will actually make you feel worse \u2014 not better. Avoidance tends to grow over time. Would you be open to working with a psychologist who specializes in helping people with chronic conditions manage anxiety? Cognitive behavioral therapy has been shown to be very effective for exactly this kind of worry.'\n\n**5. Empower with self-monitoring tools**:\n'I would also like you to start a simple symptom diary \u2014 just a few notes each day about how your energy was, whether you had any episodes, what you were doing, and whether you took your medications on time. Over a few weeks, this will help us both see patterns and make adjustments to your medication timing that might prevent these flares.'\n\n**6. Reassure about the treatment trajectory**:\n'I want to remind you that we are still early in your treatment. The thymectomy benefits continue to build over months to years, and we have several medication options to adjust if your current regimen is not providing adequate control. Most patients with MG live full, active lives once we find the right treatment balance. We will get there.'\n\n**Key principles**: Thorough symptom assessment, pattern identification to reduce perceived unpredictability, concrete action plan, avoidance prevention, CBT referral, empowerment through self-monitoring, realistic reassurance.")
B.aDy=s([B.b10,B.aSi,B.b3o,B.aO5,B.aUq,B.aZ3,B.b0e,B.aYs,B.b_I,B.aO4,B.aSd,B.aTW],t.E)
B.R9=new A.a5("nm-myasthenia-gravis","Myasthenia Gravis \u2014 Fluctuating Weakness",B.aDy,!1)
B.b3x=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 28-year-old male is referred to the neuromuscular rehabilitation clinic by his primary care physician for evaluation of progressive difficulty walking and frequent ankle sprains. He reports being considered "clumsy" his entire life and was always the slowest runner in school. Over the past 3-4 years, he has noticed worsening difficulty lifting his feet, tripping on uneven surfaces, and cramping in his calves after walking long distances. He also reports progressive difficulty with fine motor tasks such as buttoning shirts and opening jars. His father reportedly had "high arches and weak ankles" but was never formally diagnosed with a neurologic condition. The patient works as a graphic designer and is concerned about his ability to continue working.')
B.aTR=new A.c("History & Systems Review:",'- **Symptom Timeline**: Lifelong "clumsiness" with progressive worsening of distal weakness over the past 3-4 years; slow, insidious course since childhood\n- **Motor Complaints**: Bilateral foot drop (worse on right), frequent ankle sprains (3-4 per year), difficulty with stairs (compensates by lifting knees high), hand weakness with grip and pinch\n- **Sensory Complaints**: Numbness in toes bilaterally; decreased awareness of foot position; cold sensitivity in feet and hands\n- **Pain**: Calf cramping with prolonged walking; aching in feet at end of day\n- **No "Red Flag" Symptoms**: No bowel/bladder dysfunction, no respiratory difficulty, no swallowing problems, no cognitive changes, no rapid progression\n- **Family History**: Father \u2014 high arches, weak ankles, "clumsy" (never evaluated by neurologist); paternal grandfather used ankle braces; no other known family members affected\n- **PMH**: No significant medical history; no surgeries\n- **Medications**: None\n- **Social**: Graphic designer (computer-based work), lives with girlfriend in a second-floor apartment (no elevator), enjoys hiking (increasingly difficult)')
B.aRS=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:",'**Vitals**: BP 118/68 mmHg, HR 66 bpm, RR 14, SpO2 99% on RA.\n\n**General**: Thin, well-appearing male. Notably thin lower legs.\n\n**Musculoskeletal**:\n- **Feet**: Bilateral pes cavus (high arches) with hammer toes (claw toe deformity of toes 2-5). Calluses under metatarsal heads bilaterally. Right foot with mild hindfoot varus.\n- **Lower Legs**: Marked bilateral calf atrophy with "inverted champagne bottle" appearance (normal proximal thigh bulk with distal wasting).\n- **Hands**: Mild thenar and hypothenar atrophy bilaterally. Early intrinsic hand muscle wasting with mild first dorsal interosseous hollowing.\n\n**Neurologic Exam**:\n- **Motor**: Bilateral ankle dorsiflexion 3/5 (right) and 3+/5 (left); EHL 2/5 bilaterally; ankle eversion 3/5 bilaterally; ankle plantarflexion 4/5 bilaterally; knee extension 5/5; hip flexion 5/5. Upper extremity: hand intrinsics 4/5 bilaterally; grip strength 22 kg R, 20 kg L (reduced for age); wrist extension 5/5; biceps/deltoid 5/5.\n- **Sensation**: Diminished vibration at toes and ankles bilaterally (absent at great toes, reduced at medial malleoli). Reduced pin prick to mid-calf bilaterally. Proprioception impaired at great toes (2/5 correct). Light touch decreased in feet and fingertips.\n- **Reflexes**: Ankle jerks absent bilaterally. Knee jerks 1+ bilaterally. Upper extremity reflexes 2+. Babinski absent.\n- **Gait**: Bilateral steppage gait (exaggerated hip and knee flexion to clear feet). Foot slap at heel strike. Unable to walk on heels. Able to walk on toes with difficulty. Wide-based. Tandem gait impaired.\n- **Palpation**: Bilateral peroneal nerves enlarged and palpable at the fibular heads. Bilateral ulnar nerves thickened at the elbows.\n\n**Spine**: No scoliosis. No spinal tenderness.')
B.aNX=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The clinical presentation of a young male with lifelong progressive distal weakness, pes cavus, palpably enlarged nerves, and a positive family history is classic for Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease. Electrodiagnostic and genetic testing are needed for classification.")
B.b1r=new A.c("+ Describe the EMG/NCS findings expected in CMT and explain how to classify CMT subtypes. How do you differentiate CMT from acquired neuropathies?","**Expected Electrodiagnostic Findings**:\n\nGiven the palpably enlarged nerves and clinical presentation, this patient most likely has **CMT Type 1 (demyelinating)**:\n\n**Nerve Conduction Studies**:\n- **Motor NCS**: Uniformly slowed conduction velocities in ALL nerves. Median motor conduction velocity typically **<38 m/s** (often 15-30 m/s). Distal motor latencies prolonged. CMAP amplitudes may be reduced (secondary axonal loss). **Key feature: uniform slowing** \u2014 all nerves affected symmetrically, unlike acquired demyelinating neuropathies.\n- **Sensory NCS**: SNAP amplitudes markedly reduced or absent in lower extremities; reduced in upper extremities. Conduction velocities slowed.\n- **No conduction block or temporal dispersion** \u2014 these features suggest acquired demyelinating neuropathy (e.g., CIDP), NOT CMT.\n\n**EMG (Needle Exam)**:\n- Chronic denervation and reinnervation in distal muscles: large-amplitude, long-duration, polyphasic motor unit potentials (MUPs)\n- Reduced recruitment in distal muscles\n- Fibrillation potentials may be present in severely affected muscles\n\n**CMT Classification**:\n| **Type** | **Pathology** | **Median Motor CV** | **Inheritance** | **Common Gene** |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CMT1A | Demyelinating | <38 m/s | AD | PMP22 duplication (70-80% of CMT1) |\n| CMT1B | Demyelinating | <38 m/s | AD | MPZ (myelin protein zero) |\n| CMT2 | Axonal | >38 m/s | AD | MFN2 (mitofusin 2) most common |\n| CMTX | X-linked | Intermediate (30-40 m/s in males) | X-linked | GJB1 (connexin 32) |\n| CMT4 | Demyelinating | <38 m/s | AR | Various |\n\n**Differentiating CMT from Acquired Neuropathies**:\n1. **CIDP**: Acquired demyelinating neuropathy with **conduction block, temporal dispersion, and non-uniform slowing** across nerve segments. Elevated CSF protein. Responds to IVIG/steroids. CMT has **uniform slowing without conduction block**.\n2. **Diabetic neuropathy**: Axonal, acquired; check HbA1c. No pes cavus or family history.\n3. **GBS**: Acute onset, ascending weakness, areflexia. Self-limited with treatment.\n4. **Hereditary neuropathy with liability to pressure palsies (HNPP)**: PMP22 deletion (vs. duplication in CMT1A). Episodic focal neuropathies at compression sites.\n\n**Genetic Testing**: Order **PMP22 duplication analysis** first (highest yield for CMT1). If negative, proceed to CMT gene panel.")
B.aV_=new A.c("+ Genetic testing confirms PMP22 duplication consistent with CMT1A. What is the natural history and prognosis?","**Natural History of CMT1A**:\n\n1. **Progression**: CMT1A is **slowly progressive over decades**. Most patients develop symptoms in the first or second decade, though diagnosis is often delayed. Weakness and sensory loss progress distally to proximally over many years.\n\n2. **Functional milestones**:\n- Most patients remain ambulatory throughout life, though many require AFOs and walking aids\n- Wheelchair dependence is uncommon in CMT1A (more common in CMT2 and CMT4)\n- Upper extremity involvement typically follows lower extremity by years to decades\n- Life expectancy is **normal**\n\n3. **Key complications to monitor**:\n- Progressive foot deformity (pes cavus, claw toes) \u2014 may require surgical intervention\n- Ankle instability and recurrent sprains\n- Hip dysplasia (rare, in severe cases)\n- Scoliosis (monitor with serial spine exams)\n- Chronic pain (present in 50-70% of CMT patients \u2014 often underrecognized)\n- Fatigue (significant and often underappreciated)\n- Peripheral neuropathic pain\n\n4. **No disease-modifying therapy exists**: Despite extensive research, no pharmacologic treatment has been proven to alter the course of CMT1A. High-dose ascorbic acid (vitamin C) showed promise in animal models but **failed in human clinical trials**. PXT3003 and other agents are under investigation.\n\n5. **Pregnancy considerations**: CMT1A is autosomal dominant \u2014 **50% chance of passing to each child**. Pregnancy itself may temporarily worsen CMT symptoms in some women.")
B.aWZ=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","The patient returns for a comprehensive rehabilitation plan. His bilateral foot drop is limiting his walking distance and causing safety concerns at work and in the community.")
B.aMJ=new A.c("+ Develop a rehabilitation management plan including orthotic prescription, strengthening program, and surgical considerations.","**1. Orthotic Prescription (AFO)**:\n- **Bilateral ankle-foot orthoses (AFOs)** are indicated for foot drop management\n- **Recommended type**: Custom-molded **carbon fiber posterior leaf spring (PLS) AFO** or similar lightweight energy-storing design\n- Rationale: Young, active patient needs lightweight, low-profile AFO that fits in shoes and provides ankle dorsiflexion assist without restricting plantarflexion excessively\n- **Avoid**: Heavy double-upright metal braces (too bulky for this age group); rigid AFOs that eliminate ankle motion unnecessarily\n- **Shoe modifications**: Extra-depth shoes with rocker sole to accommodate hammer toes and AFOs; custom molded foot orthoses within shoes to redistribute pressure from metatarsal heads\n- Consider trial of off-the-shelf dorsiflexion assist devices (e.g., posterior elastic strap AFO) while custom AFOs are fabricated\n\n**2. Strengthening and Exercise Program**:\n- **Moderate-intensity strengthening**: Focus on remaining functional muscles \u2014 hip flexors, quadriceps, hamstrings, ankle plantarflexors. Evidence supports that **moderate resistance training is safe and beneficial** in CMT. Avoid exhaustive eccentric exercise.\n- **Aerobic conditioning**: Low-impact aerobic exercise (cycling, swimming, aquatic therapy) 30 minutes, 3-5 days/week. Helps with fatigue and cardiovascular fitness.\n- **Stretching program**: Daily Achilles tendon and plantar fascia stretching to prevent further equinus contracture. Toe stretching for claw toe deformity.\n- **Balance training**: Proprioceptive retraining exercises given sensory impairment. Standing balance on foam pad, eyes open progressing to eyes closed.\n- **Hand therapy**: Intrinsic hand muscle exercises, adaptive grip training. Ergonomic assessment for computer work.\n\n**3. Surgical Referral Considerations**:\n- **Orthopedic foot/ankle surgery referral** for evaluation of progressive pes cavus and hindfoot varus\n- Common procedures in CMT:\n  - **Plantar fascia release** for rigid cavus\n  - **Posterior tibial tendon transfer** (to dorsum of foot) for foot drop\n  - **Calcaneal osteotomy** (Dwyer) for hindfoot varus\n  - **Hammer toe correction** (PIP arthroplasty or arthrodesis)\n  - **Triple arthrodesis** for severe, rigid deformity (last resort \u2014 eliminates subtalar motion)\n- Surgery should be considered when bracing alone is insufficient or deformity is progressive and rigid\n\n**4. Pain Management**:\n- Gabapentin or pregabalin for neuropathic pain\n- Properly fitted footwear to reduce mechanical foot pain\n- NSAID use cautiously for musculoskeletal pain\n- Physical modalities: TENS, warm water therapy\n\n**5. Fatigue Management**:\n- Energy conservation education\n- Activity pacing throughout the day\n- Ensure adequate sleep and screen for sleep-disordered breathing")
B.aNK=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient's genetic testing has confirmed CMT1A. He asks about genetic counseling and long-term disability planning for what he now understands is a lifelong, progressive condition.")
B.aPI=new A.c("+ Discuss genetic counseling referral and disability/vocational planning for a progressive hereditary neuropathy.","**Genetic Counseling Referral**:\n1. **Mandatory referral**: All patients with genetically confirmed hereditary neuropathy should be referred to a genetic counselor for comprehensive education.\n2. **Inheritance pattern**: CMT1A is **autosomal dominant** \u2014 each child has a **50% chance** of inheriting the PMP22 duplication.\n3. **Genetic counselor roles**:\n- Explain inheritance, penetrance (high but variable expressivity), and reproductive options\n- Discuss prenatal testing options (chorionic villus sampling, amniocentesis) and preimplantation genetic testing (PGT) with IVF\n- Address family screening \u2014 recommend his father be evaluated formally\n- Provide emotional support for the psychological impact of a genetic diagnosis\n4. **Cascade testing**: Offer genetic testing to at-risk family members (siblings, father's siblings)\n\n**Disability and Vocational Planning**:\n1. **Current employment assessment**: As a graphic designer, his work is primarily computer-based \u2014 this is favorable. However, hand weakness may eventually affect fine motor tasks (mouse use, drawing tablet).\n2. **Workplace accommodations (ADA)**:\n- Ergonomic workstation assessment (ergonomic mouse, keyboard, wrist supports)\n- Voice recognition software (Dragon NaturallySpeaking) for when typing becomes difficult\n- Accessible workspace (first floor or elevator access, accessible parking)\n- Flexible schedule for fatigue management\n3. **State vocational rehabilitation services**: Referral for vocational counseling, assistive technology funding, and job modification support.\n4. **Long-term disability insurance**: Encourage the patient to obtain or review long-term disability coverage now while he is still working, as it becomes increasingly difficult to obtain after a progressive diagnosis.\n5. **Social Security Disability**: Generally not needed in CMT1A unless the patient has severe disease limiting all employment. Most CMT1A patients maintain gainful employment with accommodations.\n6. **Driving assessment**: Currently safe to drive. Monitor as foot weakness progresses \u2014 may eventually need hand controls.\n7. **Housing**: Second-floor apartment without elevator is currently manageable but should plan for accessible housing as disease progresses. Occupational therapy home assessment recommended.")
B.b2t=new A.c(u.j,"At the follow-up visit, the patient is visibly distressed. He says: 'My girlfriend and I are talking about getting married and having kids. But now I find out this disease is genetic. What if I pass this on to my children? Would it be irresponsible to have kids knowing they might have this?'")
B.b1V=new A.c("+ How would you counsel this patient about the genetic implications for his future children?","**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Acknowledge the emotional weight of the question**:\n'This is one of the most important and personal questions you can ask, and I am glad you are bringing it up. The fact that you are thinking about this shows how much you care. This is not a question with a simple right or wrong answer, and I want to walk through it with you so you can make the decision that feels right for you and your girlfriend.'\n\n**2. Provide clear genetic information**:\n'Here is what the genetics tell us: CMT1A is autosomal dominant, which means that each child you have would have a 50% chance of inheriting the gene change. However \u2014 and this is important \u2014 inheriting the gene does not mean they will have the exact same experience as you. CMT1A has what we call variable expressivity, meaning some people with the same gene change have very mild symptoms that barely affect their daily life, while others have more significant difficulties. Even within the same family, the severity can be quite different.'\n\n**3. Challenge the framing without being dismissive**:\n'You used the word irresponsible, and I want to gently push back on that. Many people with CMT live full, productive, meaningful lives \u2014 including you. Having a condition is not the same as having a life that is not worth living. That said, your concern is valid, and there are options available to you.'\n\n**4. Present reproductive options**:\n'If having a child without CMT is important to you, there are medical options. Preimplantation genetic testing with in vitro fertilization allows embryos to be tested for the PMP22 duplication before pregnancy, so only unaffected embryos are transferred. Prenatal testing during pregnancy is also available. And of course, adoption is an option that some families choose. A genetic counselor can walk you through all of these options in detail \u2014 including costs, success rates, and what each process involves.'\n\n**5. Offer support and referral**:\n'I would strongly recommend that you and your girlfriend meet with a genetic counselor together. They specialize in helping families navigate exactly these decisions. They will not tell you what to do \u2014 they will help you understand your options so you can make a decision that is right for your family. Would you like me to arrange that referral?'\n\n**6. Normalize the emotional process**:\n'Whatever you decide, there is no wrong answer. Take the time you need, talk it through together, and know that we are here to support you every step of the way.'\n\n**Key principles**: Non-directive genetic counseling approach, accurate genetic education, challenging internalized stigma, presenting all reproductive options neutrally, referral to genetic counseling, supporting patient autonomy in deeply personal decisions.")
B.aDn=s([B.b3x,B.aTR,B.aRS,B.aNX,B.b1r,B.aV_,B.aWZ,B.aMJ,B.aNK,B.aPI,B.b2t,B.b1V],t.E)
B.Q6=new A.a5("nm-charcot-marie-tooth","Charcot-Marie-Tooth Disease",B.aDn,!1)
B.aQ3=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 35-year-old right-handed male software programmer presents to the PM&R electrodiagnostic clinic with a 4-month history of progressive right hand weakness and numbness in the ring and small fingers. He first noticed tingling in the small finger after long coding sessions and initially attributed it to "sleeping on his arm." The numbness has become constant over the past 6 weeks, and he now reports difficulty typing accurately, dropping his coffee mug, and trouble turning keys in locks. He has noticed that his symptoms worsen significantly when he talks on the phone (holding elbow flexed) or sleeps with his arms bent. He denies neck pain, shoulder symptoms, or similar symptoms in the left hand.')
B.b_J=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **Symptom Onset**: 4 months ago, initially intermittent paresthesias in the right small finger; progressed to constant numbness in small and ulnar half of ring finger; weakness developed over past 6 weeks\n- **Aggravating Factors**: Prolonged elbow flexion (phone calls, sleeping with arms bent, sustained typing posture), direct pressure on medial elbow (leaning on desk)\n- **Functional Impact**: Difficulty with fine motor tasks (typing accuracy decreased, drops objects, can't open jars well), weakness with grip, trouble with pinch\n- **No Neck Pain**: No radiculopathy symptoms, no Spurling sign provocation, no shoulder/proximal arm symptoms\n- **Work History**: Software programmer for 10 years; works 10-12 hours/day at desk; recently started a new project requiring intensive coding with ergonomically poor workstation setup\n- **PMH**: No diabetes, no thyroid disease, no prior elbow fractures or dislocations, no rheumatologic conditions\n- **Medications**: None\n- **Social**: Non-smoker, social drinker, recreational rock climbing (grip-intensive), right-hand dominant\n- **Review of Systems**: No weight loss, no constitutional symptoms, no other neurologic complaints")
B.aYZ=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 120/72 mmHg, HR 68 bpm, RR 14, SpO2 99% on RA.\n\n**Right Upper Extremity Exam**:\n- **Inspection**: Visible atrophy of the **first dorsal interosseous muscle** (hollowing between thumb and index finger metacarpals). Mild hypothenar atrophy. No intrinsic muscle wasting on the left for comparison.\n- **Tinel Sign**: Positive at the **right cubital tunnel** (elbow medial epicondyle/ulnar groove) \u2014 reproduces tingling into the ring and small fingers. Negative at Guyon canal (wrist).\n- **Elbow Flexion Test**: Sustained elbow flexion with wrist extension for 60 seconds reproduces paresthesias in ulnar distribution. Positive.\n- **Froment Sign**: **Positive** \u2014 patient compensates for adductor pollicis weakness by flexing the thumb IP joint (using FPL, median-innervated) when attempting to hold a piece of paper between thumb and index finger. Indicates ulnar nerve motor deficit.\n- **Wartenberg Sign**: **Positive** \u2014 small finger abducts away from ring finger at rest due to unopposed extensor digiti minimi (inability of 3rd palmar interosseous to adduct).\n\n**Motor Exam (Right)**:\n- First dorsal interosseous: 3+/5 (weak index finger abduction)\n- Abductor digiti minimi: 4-/5\n- Adductor pollicis: 3+/5 (Froment positive)\n- Flexor carpi ulnaris: 5/5 (intact \u2014 suggests the lesion may spare FCU branch)\n- FDP to small finger: 4+/5 (subtle weakness of distal phalanx flexion)\n- All median, radial, and proximal muscles: 5/5\n\n**Sensory Exam**: Decreased light touch and pin prick in the right small finger and ulnar half of the ring finger, extending onto the dorsal ulnar hand. Sensation in the dorsal ulnar hand (dorsal ulnar cutaneous nerve territory) is decreased \u2014 this localizes the lesion to the **elbow** (Guyon canal lesion spares the dorsal hand).\n\n**Cervical Spine**: Full ROM without pain. Spurling test negative bilaterally. No myelopathic signs.\n\n**Left Upper Extremity**: Normal throughout.")
B.aPK=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The clinical examination strongly suggests ulnar neuropathy at the elbow. Electrodiagnostic studies are needed to confirm the localization, assess severity, and guide treatment decisions.")
B.aPf=new A.c("+ Describe the expected EMG/NCS findings for ulnar neuropathy at the elbow. How do you differentiate this from C8 radiculopathy and ulnar neuropathy at the wrist (Guyon canal)?","**Expected Nerve Conduction Study (NCS) Findings**:\n\n1. **Ulnar motor NCS** (recording abductor digiti minimi):\n- **Focal slowing across the elbow**: Conduction velocity across the elbow segment <50 m/s (or >10 m/s slower than the forearm segment). This is the hallmark finding.\n- **CMAP amplitude drop** across the elbow of >20% suggests conduction block or focal demyelination at the elbow.\n- Distal CMAP amplitude may be reduced if there is secondary axonal loss (as expected with first dorsal interosseous atrophy).\n- Record with **short-segment (inching) technique** across the elbow in 2 cm increments to precisely localize the site of compression.\n\n2. **Ulnar sensory NCS**:\n- **Reduced SNAP amplitude** of the ulnar sensory nerve (recording 5th digit). Compare side-to-side (>50% asymmetry is abnormal).\n- **Dorsal ulnar cutaneous (DUC) nerve**: Abnormal SNAP confirms lesion is at or proximal to the elbow (DUC branches off proximal to Guyon canal \u2014 it is NORMAL in Guyon canal lesions).\n\n3. **Medial antebrachial cutaneous nerve**: Should be normal (localizes lesion to ulnar nerve, not medial cord or lower trunk).\n\n**Needle EMG Findings**:\n- **Denervation** (fibrillations, positive sharp waves) in **ulnar-innervated hand intrinsics** (FDI, ADM)\n- Possible denervation in FDP-4,5 and FCU (elbow-level innervated muscles)\n- **Normal**: Non-ulnar C8/T1 muscles \u2014 APB (median C8/T1), EIP (radial C8), FPL (median C8). If these are abnormal, consider C8 radiculopathy or lower trunk/medial cord plexopathy.\n- **Cervical paraspinals**: Normal (rules out radiculopathy)\n\n**Differential Diagnosis and Localization**:\n\n| **Feature** | **Ulnar at Elbow** | **Ulnar at Wrist (Guyon)** | **C8 Radiculopathy** |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Dorsal ulnar hand sensation | Abnormal | **Normal** | Abnormal |\n| FCU weakness | Possible | Normal | Normal |\n| FDI weakness | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n| APB weakness (median) | No | No | **Yes** |\n| EIP weakness (radial) | No | No | **Yes** |\n| DUC SNAP | Abnormal | **Normal** | Normal |\n| MABC SNAP | Normal | Normal | Normal |\n| Paraspinal EMG | Normal | Normal | **May be abnormal** |\n| Neck pain/Spurling | No | No | **Often yes** |")
B.b2X=new A.c("+ EDX confirms moderate ulnar neuropathy at the elbow with axonal loss. What clinical grading systems guide treatment decisions?","**McGowan Classification of Ulnar Neuropathy Severity**:\n- **Grade I (Mild)**: Intermittent paresthesias, no weakness or atrophy, subjective complaints only\n- **Grade II (Moderate)**: Constant paresthesias, measurable weakness, early muscle wasting. **This patient is McGowan Grade II** (weakness of FDI 3+/5, positive Froment, visible atrophy).\n- **Grade III (Severe)**: Marked intrinsic atrophy, severe weakness, claw deformity\n\n**Dellon Modification**:\n- Adds subclassification of Grade II into IIA (fair muscle strength) and IIB (poor muscle strength)\n\n**EMG Severity Assessment**:\n- **Mild/demyelinating**: Conduction slowing or block across elbow, normal SNAP amplitude, no denervation on EMG\n- **Moderate**: Conduction slowing PLUS reduced CMAP amplitude, reduced SNAP, fibrillations in intrinsics\n- **Severe**: Absent or very low CMAP, absent SNAP, dense denervation in multiple ulnar muscles, reduced recruitment\n\n**Treatment Decision Based on Severity**:\n- **Mild (McGowan I)**: Conservative management first (activity modification, splinting) for 3-6 months\n- **Moderate (McGowan II) with axonal loss on EDX**: This is a clinical inflection point. If symptoms are progressive and EDX shows active denervation, **surgical consultation is warranted** because axonal loss indicates ongoing nerve damage that may not reverse with conservative measures alone.\n- **Severe (McGowan III)**: Surgical decompression indicated, though outcomes are less favorable once severe atrophy has developed.\n\n**Prognostic Factors for Recovery**:\n- Shorter duration of symptoms \u2192 better outcomes\n- Demyelinating (conduction block) \u2192 better prognosis than axonal loss\n- Younger age \u2192 better nerve regeneration potential\n- McGowan Grade I-II \u2192 significantly better surgical outcomes than Grade III")
B.b0X=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You decide to initiate a 3-month trial of conservative management given that this is his first presentation, while simultaneously preparing the patient for possible surgery if conservative measures fail.")
B.b1Q=new A.c("+ Outline conservative management for cubital tunnel syndrome and the indications for surgical intervention.","**Conservative Management Protocol**:\n\n**1. Elbow Splinting**:\n- **Night splint**: Elbow extension splint (or elbow pad with extension block) worn during sleep to prevent sustained elbow flexion >30-45 degrees\n- Can use a towel wrap or commercial padded elbow brace to limit flexion\n- Wear consistently every night for minimum 3 months\n\n**2. Activity Modification**:\n- Avoid prolonged elbow flexion: use speakerphone or headset for phone calls\n- Avoid direct pressure on the medial elbow: do not lean on desk\n- Take regular breaks during coding sessions (5 minutes every 30-45 minutes)\n- Avoid sleeping with elbows fully flexed (pillow between arms may help)\n\n**3. Ergonomic Workstation Modifications**:\n- Keyboard positioned so elbows are at 70-90 degrees of flexion (not more)\n- Padded elbow/forearm rest at desk height\n- Ergonomic keyboard (split keyboard) to reduce ulnar wrist deviation\n- Mouse positioned close to body to reduce reaching\n- Monitor at eye level to reduce forward head posture\n- Standing desk option to vary positions throughout the day\n\n**4. Nerve Gliding Exercises**:\n- Ulnar nerve gliding/flossing exercises prescribed by occupational therapy\n- Gentle, progressive \u2014 avoid aggressive stretching that could irritate the nerve\n\n**5. Anti-inflammatory Measures**:\n- Short course of NSAIDs for acute inflammation if present\n- Avoid corticosteroid injection at the cubital tunnel (risk of nerve injury, limited evidence)\n\n**Indications for Surgical Intervention** (refer if ANY of the following):\n- **Failure of conservative management after 3-6 months** of consistent compliance\n- **Progressive weakness or atrophy** despite conservative measures\n- **EDX evidence of progressive axonal loss** on repeat testing\n- **Severe presentation at onset** (McGowan Grade III, dense denervation)\n- **Structural abnormality** (subluxing ulnar nerve over medial epicondyle, ganglion cyst, osteophyte)\n\n**Surgical Options**:\n1. **In-situ decompression**: Release of the cubital tunnel retinaculum without moving the nerve. Simplest procedure, good outcomes for mild-moderate cases.\n2. **Medial epicondylectomy**: Removal of the medial epicondyle to decompress the nerve.\n3. **Anterior transposition** (subcutaneous, intramuscular, or submuscular): Moves the nerve anterior to the medial epicondyle. Used when nerve subluxes or when in-situ decompression fails. Submuscular transposition may have best outcomes for severe cases.\n\n**Post-surgical rehabilitation**: Immobilization for 1-3 weeks depending on procedure, then progressive ROM and strengthening. Nerve recovery follows Seddon/Sunderland classification \u2014 expect 1 mm/day (1 inch/month) for axonal regeneration. Full recovery may take 6-12 months.")
B.aXl=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient expresses concern about his job. He asks: 'I can barely type for an hour without my hand going numb. My boss is already noticing mistakes in my code. What if I can't do my job anymore?'")
B.aYK=new A.c("+ Discuss ergonomic workplace modification and disability accommodation for this patient.","**Workplace Accommodation (Americans with Disabilities Act)**:\n\n1. **ADA Protections**: Cubital tunnel syndrome causing substantial limitation of hand function may qualify as a disability under the ADA Amendments Act (2008), which broadened the definition. The employer is required to provide **reasonable accommodations** unless it creates an undue hardship.\n\n2. **Reasonable Accommodations to Request**:\n- **Ergonomic workstation assessment** by a certified ergonomist (employer responsibility)\n- **Adaptive equipment**: Ergonomic split keyboard, vertical mouse, voice recognition software (Dragon NaturallySpeaking), forearm supports, adjustable desk\n- **Modified work schedule**: Shorter coding sprints with mandatory breaks; flexible scheduling for medical appointments and therapy\n- **Modified duties**: During acute phase, reduce intensive typing requirements; assign code review, design documentation, or mentoring tasks\n- **Telecommuting**: May allow better control of workstation ergonomics at home\n\n3. **Process for Requesting Accommodations**:\n- Patient initiates the interactive process by informing HR of the medical condition\n- Physiatrist provides a letter documenting the diagnosis, functional limitations, and specific accommodation recommendations (NOT disclosing full medical details)\n- Employer is required to engage in good-faith interactive process to identify effective accommodations\n\n4. **Workers' Compensation Considerations**:\n- Cubital tunnel syndrome may be a **compensable work-related injury** if it is predominantly caused by or aggravated by work activities (repetitive elbow flexion, keyboard use)\n- Document the temporal relationship between work activities and symptom onset\n- Workers' comp claim may provide coverage for medical treatment, time off, and modified duty\n\n5. **Disability Insurance**:\n- If unable to perform essential job functions despite accommodations, short-term disability may cover recovery period, especially if surgery is needed\n- Long-term disability is unlikely to be needed for cubital tunnel syndrome with appropriate treatment\n\n6. **Vocational Rehabilitation**:\n- Referral to vocational rehabilitation counselor if job modification is insufficient\n- Technology solutions: Voice-to-text programming tools, eye-tracking interfaces, specialized input devices\n- Most software programmers can return to full productivity with appropriate accommodations and treatment")
B.aXK=new A.c(u.j,"At the 3-month follow-up, conservative management has not improved his symptoms significantly. EMG shows progressive axonal loss compared to the initial study. Surgery is recommended. The patient becomes upset: 'I'm terrified of surgery on my hand. What if it makes things worse? My entire career depends on my hands. I'd rather live with the numbness than risk losing more function.'")
B.aZC=new A.c("+ How would you counsel this patient about the risks and benefits of surgery while respecting his autonomy?","**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Validate the fear**:\n'I completely understand your concern. Your hands are essential to your career and your identity as a programmer. The idea of surgery near the nerve that controls your hand function is understandably scary. I want to make sure you have all the information you need to make the best decision for you.'\n\n**2. Present the risk of NOT having surgery (informed refusal)**:\n'Here is what concerns me about waiting: your repeat nerve test today shows that the nerve damage is getting worse compared to three months ago. The muscle between your thumb and index finger has lost more bulk, and the electrical signals in the nerve are weaker. If we continue to watch and the nerve continues to deteriorate, the damage may reach a point where it cannot fully recover, even with surgery later. The general rule is that the longer the nerve is compressed and the more axonal damage occurs, the less likely a full recovery becomes.'\n\n**3. Provide honest surgical outcome data**:\n'The good news is that surgery for cubital tunnel syndrome is generally very successful. About 80-90% of patients with your severity level experience significant improvement in symptoms. The risk of surgery making things worse is approximately 5% or less in experienced hands. Complications like infection, wound healing problems, or nerve irritation are uncommon. I would refer you to a peripheral nerve surgeon who does this procedure regularly.'\n\n**4. Address the specific fear about his career**:\n'I want to be direct with you: the biggest risk to your career right now is NOT having surgery \u2014 it is allowing the nerve damage to progress to the point where your hand function deteriorates further. Most patients return to keyboard work within 4-6 weeks after surgery, and many notice improvement in numbness and grip within the first few months.'\n\n**5. Respect autonomy while ensuring informed decision-making**:\n'Ultimately, this is your decision, and I will support whatever you choose. But I would not be doing my job if I did not tell you that the window for the best surgical outcomes is narrowing as the nerve damage progresses. Here is what I would suggest: meet with the surgeon for a consultation. Ask every question you have. You are not committing to surgery by having a conversation. After that, you can make a fully informed decision.'\n\n**6. Offer a compromise and timeline**:\n'If you are truly not ready for surgery, I understand. What I would ask is that we repeat the nerve test in 6-8 weeks. If the nerve is stable, we have some time. If it is getting worse, I would strongly encourage you to reconsider. Can we agree on that plan?'\n\n**Key principles**: Informed consent AND informed refusal, honest prognosis presentation, respecting patient autonomy, addressing specific fears rather than generic reassurance, offering incremental steps (consultation vs. commitment), establishing a monitoring plan as a safety net.")
B.aEr=s([B.aQ3,B.b_J,B.aYZ,B.aPK,B.aPf,B.b2X,B.b0X,B.b1Q,B.aXl,B.aYK,B.aXK,B.aZC],t.E)
B.QE=new A.a5("nm-cubital-tunnel-ulnar-neuropathy","Ulnar Neuropathy at the Elbow \u2014 Cubital Tunnel Syndrome",B.aEr,!1)
B.aS_=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 55-year-old male is referred to PM&R for profound generalized weakness after a 3-week ICU stay for septic shock secondary to perforated diverticulitis. He required mechanical ventilation, vasopressors, continuous IV corticosteroids (methylprednisolone 60 mg daily for 14 days), and a 5-day course of cisatracurium for ventilator dyssynchrony. He has failed three spontaneous breathing trials. Nursing reports he cannot lift his extremities off the bed. He is alert and follows commands but appears frustrated and tearful. His wife and two adult children are at bedside daily asking when he can come home.")
B.b3a=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","**Past Medical History:** Hypertension, type 2 diabetes mellitus (A1c 8.2%), hyperlipidemia, obesity (BMI 34). No prior neuromuscular disease.\n\n**Hospital Course:** Admitted with peritonitis and septic shock. Required emergent colostomy. Complicated by ARDS requiring prone positioning, acute kidney injury (resolved), and ICU delirium (resolved). Received high-dose corticosteroids for refractory shock and neuromuscular blocking agents for severe ARDS.\n\n**Current Complaints:** Cannot move arms or legs meaningfully, unable to grip objects, cannot sit unsupported, persistent ventilator dependence, diffuse muscle pain, profound fatigue.\n\n**Medications:** Insulin sliding scale, metoprolol, atorvastatin, famotidine, subcutaneous heparin, acetaminophen PRN.\n\n**Social History:** Construction foreman, married, two adult children. Primary breadwinner. No tobacco, occasional alcohol. Home is a single-story ranch with 2 steps to enter.")
B.b2Q=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**General:** Alert, oriented x3, cachectic appearance with significant muscle wasting, tracheostomy in place on pressure support ventilation (PS 10/PEEP 5, FiO2 0.35).\n\n**Neurological:** Cranial nerves intact. Mental status normal. Sensation intact to light touch and pinprick throughout (important distinguishing feature). Deep tendon reflexes diffusely reduced (1+ throughout). No fasciculations.\n\n**Motor Exam (MRC Scale):**\n- Shoulder abduction: 2/5 bilateral\n- Elbow flexion: 2/5 bilateral\n- Wrist extension: 2/5 bilateral\n- Grip: 1/5 bilateral\n- Hip flexion: 1/5 bilateral\n- Knee extension: 2/5 bilateral\n- Ankle dorsiflexion: 1/5 bilateral\n\n**Functional Status:** Dependent for all bed mobility, unable to sit at edge of bed unsupported, total assist transfers, no standing tolerance.\n\n**Skin:** Stage 2 sacral pressure injury (2 cm x 1.5 cm), healed abdominal surgical wound. Diffuse edema in extremities.\n\n**Labs:** CK elevated at 1,200 IU/L (was 4,500 at peak 10 days ago). Albumin 2.1. Prealbumin 8 mg/dL. Glucose poorly controlled (180-300 range).")
B.aWC=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","**Primary Diagnosis:** Critical illness myopathy (CIM) \u2014 ICU-acquired weakness in the setting of prolonged corticosteroid and neuromuscular blocking agent exposure.\n\n**Key Diagnostic Reasoning:**\n- **CIM vs CIP distinction:** CIM presents with preserved sensory nerve action potentials (SNAPs) on NCS, reduced compound muscle action potential (CMAP) amplitudes, and myopathic motor unit potentials on EMG. CIP shows reduced SNAPs AND CMAPs with neuropathic recruitment. Mixed CIM/CIP is common.\n- **EMG/NCS findings expected:** Low CMAP amplitudes with normal SNAPs, myopathic short-duration polyphasic MUPs, early recruitment, possible fibrillation potentials and positive sharp waves in proximal and distal muscles. Direct muscle stimulation showing reduced muscle-to-nerve CMAP ratio is highly specific for CIM.\n- **Medication culprits:** Synergistic toxicity of corticosteroids + neuromuscular blocking agents is the classic combination causing CIM. Steroids cause selective thick filament (myosin) loss. NMBAs prolong exposure.\n- **CK elevation pattern:** Elevated CK supports myopathic process; trending down from peak suggests resolving acute phase.\n- **Differential diagnosis:** Must exclude pre-existing myopathy, prolonged NMB effect (train-of-four testing), spinal cord pathology, Guillain-Barr\xe9 syndrome, and rhabdomyolysis.\n- **Ventilator weaning failure:** Diaphragmatic myopathy from CIM is a major contributor \u2014 assess with diaphragm ultrasound or transdiaphragmatic pressure measurement.")
B.b_K=new A.c("+ Diagnostic question","**Q: How would you differentiate CIM from CIP on electrodiagnostic testing, and does it matter clinically?**\n\n**A:** CIM shows low CMAPs with **preserved SNAPs**, myopathic MUPs (short duration, polyphasic, early recruitment), and elevated CK. CIP shows low CMAPs AND **low SNAPs** with neuropathic MUPs (large amplitude, reduced recruitment). Direct muscle stimulation is the gold standard: in CIM, direct muscle stimulation produces a lower CMAP than nerve stimulation (reduced muscle excitability), while in CIP the ratio is preserved. The distinction matters prognostically \u2014 **CIM generally recovers faster and more completely than CIP** (weeks to months vs months to years). Pure CIP with axonal loss has slower recovery dependent on reinnervation. Mixed forms are most common (up to 50% of cases) and carry intermediate prognosis. Both require aggressive rehabilitation, but CIP patients may need longer LTAC stays and have higher rates of persistent disability at 1 year.")
B.b0z=new A.c("+ Challenge question","**Q: The ICU team wants to restart dexamethasone for possible adrenal insufficiency. How do you advise?**\n\n**A:** This requires a careful risk-benefit analysis. First, confirm adrenal insufficiency with a **cosyntropin stimulation test** before empirically restarting steroids. If true adrenal insufficiency is confirmed, use the **lowest effective replacement dose** (hydrocortisone 15-25 mg/day in divided doses rather than high-dose dexamethasone). Avoid dexamethasone specifically as it has the highest myopathic potential among corticosteroids. Emphasize that ongoing steroid exposure is the single most modifiable risk factor for worsening CIM. If steroids are necessary, implement concurrent aggressive rehabilitation, nutritional optimization, and glycemic control to mitigate further myopathic damage. Document the discussion and shared decision-making with the ICU team.")
B.aNN=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","**ICU-Based Early Mobilization Protocol:**\n- Begin with passive ROM and electrical muscle stimulation while still ventilator-dependent\n- Progressive mobility ladder: in-bed exercises \u2192 sitting at edge of bed \u2192 tilt table \u2192 standing frame \u2192 assisted ambulation\n- Diaphragm strengthening: progressive ventilator weaning (reduce PS by 2 cmH2O as tolerated), inspiratory muscle training once able to tolerate tracheostomy collar trials\n- Target: increase tracheostomy collar trial duration by 30 minutes per day\n\n**Nutritional Optimization (Critical):**\n- Protein supplementation: 1.5-2.0 g/kg/day to support muscle recovery\n- Caloric goal: 25-30 kcal/kg/day, adjusted for obesity\n- Correct albumin/prealbumin deficiency \u2014 consider enteral supplementation\n- Tight glycemic control (target 140-180 mg/dL) \u2014 hyperglycemia worsens CIM\n- Vitamin D level check and supplementation (common deficiency in ICU patients)\n\n**Pressure Injury Management:**\n- Offloading protocol, specialty mattress, repositioning every 2 hours\n- Wound care nurse consultation, nutrition optimization supports healing\n\n**Rehabilitation Pharmacology:**\n- Avoid medications that worsen weakness (aminoglycosides, additional steroids, statins \u2014 consider holding atorvastatin temporarily given elevated CK)\n- Pain management: acetaminophen, gabapentin for neuropathic component if CIP coexists\n- VTE prophylaxis: continue subcutaneous heparin given immobility")
B.aVN=new A.c("+ Management question","**Q: The patient fails his 5th spontaneous breathing trial. What is your ventilator weaning strategy incorporating rehabilitation?**\n\n**A:** Implement a **rehabilitation-integrated weaning protocol**:\n1. **Assess diaphragm function:** Bedside diaphragm ultrasound measuring thickening fraction (normal >30%; <20% predicts weaning failure). Consider phrenic nerve conduction studies if diaphragm dysfunction suspected.\n2. **Inspiratory muscle training (IMT):** Once patient tolerates brief tracheostomy collar trials, use threshold IMT device starting at 30% of maximal inspiratory pressure, progressing to 50-60%. Perform 5 sets of 6 breaths, twice daily.\n3. **Progressive tracheostomy collar trials:** Start at whatever duration he tolerates (even 5 minutes), increase by 15-30 minutes per session. Use a structured sprint-and-rest protocol rather than continuous weaning.\n4. **Upright positioning during weaning trials:** Sitting position improves diaphragm mechanics vs supine.\n5. **Concurrent whole-body strengthening:** Peripheral muscle weakness correlates with diaphragm weakness \u2014 total body rehabilitation supports weaning.\n6. **Monitor closely:** Heart rate, respiratory rate, SpO2, tidal volumes, subjective dyspnea. Abort trial for HR >140, RR >35, SpO2 <88%, or patient distress.\n7. **Nutritional adequacy:** Malnourished patients cannot wean \u2014 ensure protein and caloric goals are met.\n8. **Timeline:** If no progress in 2-3 weeks with aggressive rehabilitation, consider LTAC transfer for prolonged weaning facility.")
B.b_d=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","**LTAC vs Acute Inpatient Rehab Triage Decision:**\n- **LTAC criteria (appropriate now):** Ventilator dependence is primary barrier, requires >6 hours/day of nursing-level respiratory care, unable to participate in 3 hours of therapy daily, medical complexity with ongoing ICU-level needs.\n- **Acute rehab criteria (not yet met):** Must be ventilator-free or on stable nocturnal-only ventilation, able to tolerate 3 hours of therapy daily, medically stable, and making functional gains.\n- **Recommended pathway:** Transfer to LTAC for ventilator weaning + early rehab \u2192 reassess for acute rehab transfer once ventilator-free and able to participate in intensive therapy.\n\n**ICU-Based Rehab Team Model:**\n- Early PT/OT/SLP involvement in ICU reduces ICU length of stay by 2-3 days and improves functional outcomes\n- Cost-effectiveness data supports ICU mobility programs: reduced ventilator days, fewer LTAC transfers, lower 30-day readmission rates\n- Barriers: staffing, safety concerns (lines, tubes), cultural resistance from ICU staff\n\n**Financial/Insurance Considerations:**\n- Prolonged hospitalization + LTAC + acute rehab could exceed $500,000\n- Workers compensation does not apply (non-occupational illness)\n- Short-term disability through employer \u2014 coordinate with social work\n- Family Medical Leave Act (FMLA) protection for job \u2014 but construction foreman role may not be feasible long-term\n- Long-term disability planning if recovery incomplete")
B.aP7=new A.c("+ Systems question","**Q: The insurance company denies the LTAC transfer, saying the patient should go to a SNF with ventilator capabilities. How do you advocate?**\n\n**A:** Draft a **peer-to-peer appeal** emphasizing:\n1. **Medical necessity:** Patient requires physician-directed ventilator weaning protocol with daily adjustments, which SNF ventilator units typically cannot provide. SNF ventilator care is custodial maintenance, not active weaning.\n2. **Rehabilitation potential:** Active CIM with expected recovery \u2014 this patient needs aggressive, coordinated weaning + rehabilitation, not long-term custodial ventilator care.\n3. **Outcome data:** LTAC ventilator weaning success rates are 50-75% vs significantly lower in SNF settings. Failed weaning in SNF leads to permanent ventilator dependence and higher long-term costs.\n4. **Cost argument:** Successful LTAC weaning ($50,000-80,000 for 3-4 weeks) prevents permanent ventilator dependence in SNF ($150,000+/year indefinitely).\n5. **Documentation:** Include EMG evidence of CIM (not permanent neuropathy), CK trending down (active recovery), intact cognition (can participate in weaning protocol), and premorbid functional independence.\n6. If peer-to-peer fails, escalate to **external review** and involve hospital case management and patient advocacy.")
B.aUk=new A.c(u.j,'**Scenario:** The patient\'s wife and adult children ask during a family meeting: "Doctor, will he ever be normal again? Will he walk? Will he work? Please just tell us the truth."\n\n**Recommended Approach:**\n\n**Acknowledge the emotional weight:** "I can see how exhausted and worried you all are. You\'ve been through an incredibly difficult three weeks, and I want to give you the most honest information I can."\n\n**Provide honest prognostic framework with appropriate uncertainty:**\n"Based on what we know about his specific condition \u2014 critical illness myopathy \u2014 I can tell you that this type of muscle weakness does tend to recover, which is the encouraging part. The electrical tests of his muscles show a pattern that has a better recovery trajectory than some alternatives.\n\nHere is what I can say with confidence: Most patients with this condition do regain the ability to walk, usually within 3 to 6 months with intensive rehabilitation. However, getting back to full pre-illness strength \u2014 especially for a physically demanding job like construction \u2014 is less certain, and full recovery can take 12 months or longer.\n\nWhat I cannot predict with certainty is exactly how far his recovery will go. Some patients return to nearly full function, others have lasting fatigue and reduced endurance."\n\n**Address the ventilator concern directly:** "The most immediate priority is getting him off the ventilator. We have a specific plan for that, and I\'m cautiously optimistic \u2014 but I want to be transparent that it may take several more weeks."\n\n**Provide a structured recovery timeline:**\n- Next 2-4 weeks: Focus on ventilator weaning at LTAC\n- Months 1-3: Intensive inpatient rehabilitation, targeting basic mobility\n- Months 3-6: Outpatient rehabilitation, progressive strengthening\n- 6-12 months: Reassess vocational capacity\n\n**Invite ongoing dialogue:** "I know this is a lot of uncertainty to sit with. I commit to updating you regularly as we see how he responds to treatment. Your questions are always welcome \u2014 there are no wrong questions in this situation."')
B.b0S=new A.c("+ Communication question",'**Q: His wife breaks down crying and says, "The ICU doctor told us he\'d be fine once the infection cleared. Nobody told us about this." How do you respond?**\n\n**A:** This requires empathetic validation without blaming colleagues:\n\n"I hear your frustration and I am truly sorry that the reality has been different from what you expected. That disconnect between what you were told and what is happening now must feel like a betrayal of trust, and your feelings are completely valid.\n\nI cannot speak to what was communicated previously, but I can tell you that ICU-acquired weakness sometimes develops gradually and may not have been fully apparent when those earlier conversations happened. The focus during the acute illness was appropriately on saving his life \u2014 and they succeeded at that.\n\nWhat I can promise you is that from this point forward, I will be straightforward with you about what we know, what we don\'t know, and what our plan is. I will not give you false reassurance, but I will also make sure to share good news as progress happens.\n\nWould it be helpful to set up a weekly family meeting so you always have a scheduled time to ask questions and hear updates? Many families find that structure helps during a long recovery."\n\nThis response validates the family\'s distress, avoids undermining the ICU team, takes ownership of future communication, and offers a concrete supportive structure.')
B.arb=s([B.aS_,B.b3a,B.b2Q,B.aWC,B.b_K,B.b0z,B.aNN,B.aVN,B.b_d,B.aP7,B.aUk,B.b0S],t.E)
B.R4=new A.a5("nm-icu-weakness","Critical Illness Myopathy / ICU-Acquired Weakness",B.arb,!1)
B.aZP=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 48-year-old female is referred by her primary care physician for bilateral hand numbness and weakness that has progressively worsened over 2 years. She works as a meat packer at a poultry processing plant, performing repetitive cutting and gripping motions 10 hours per day. She reports dropping objects multiple times daily, difficulty buttoning shirts, inability to open jars, and waking nightly with hand numbness requiring vigorous shaking. She has noticed visible wasting of the muscle at the base of both thumbs over the past 6 months. She is right-hand dominant. She has been wearing over-the-counter wrist splints at night with minimal relief. She is anxious because her supervisor told her she must file a workers' compensation claim or risk termination for poor job performance.")
B.aNJ=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","**Past Medical History:** Type 2 diabetes mellitus (A1c 7.8%), hypothyroidism (on levothyroxine), obesity (BMI 32), osteoarthritis of bilateral knees.\n\n**Prior Treatment:** OTC wrist splints for 4 months, ibuprofen PRN, no prior injections or surgical consultations.\n\n**Current Complaints:** Bilateral hand numbness (thumb, index, middle fingers, and radial half of ring finger), worse at night and with sustained gripping. Weakness of thumb opposition and pinch grip. Pain radiating from wrists to forearms. Dropping objects \u2014 broke three plates last week. Cannot feel texture of fabric when sewing (her hobby).\n\n**Medications:** Metformin 1000 mg BID, levothyroxine 75 mcg daily, ibuprofen 400 mg PRN.\n\n**Social History:** Immigrated from Guatemala 12 years ago. Primary language is Spanish with limited English proficiency. Lives with husband and three children (ages 10, 14, 17). Husband works at the same plant. Family is sole income dependent on her continued employment. No health insurance outside of workers' compensation if claim is approved. 17-year-old daughter serves as interpreter for medical appointments.")
B.aPZ=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Hands/Wrists:**\n- Visible thenar atrophy bilateral, right worse than left\n- Thumb opposition: 3/5 right, 4-/5 left (cannot oppose to 5th digit on right)\n- Thumb abduction (APB): 3/5 right, 3+/5 left\n- Grip strength by dynamometer: 12 kg right, 18 kg left (expected ~30 kg)\n- Pinch strength: 2 kg right, 3 kg left (expected ~7 kg)\n- Positive Tinel sign bilateral (right > left)\n- Positive Phalen test bilateral at 10 seconds\n- Positive Durkan compression test bilateral\n- Two-point discrimination: 8 mm right index, 6 mm left index (normal <6 mm)\n- Semmes-Weinstein monofilament: diminished in median nerve distribution bilateral\n\n**Cervical Spine:** Full ROM without pain. Spurling test negative bilateral. No neck tenderness.\n\n**Upper Extremity Neurological:** Biceps, triceps, brachioradialis reflexes symmetric and normal. No intrinsic hand muscle wasting outside thenar eminence. Ulnar-innervated intrinsics normal. No Hoffmann sign.\n\n**Vascular:** Radial pulses 2+ bilateral. Allen test normal. No Adson maneuver reproduction of symptoms. No supraclavicular fullness.")
B.b1O=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","**Primary Diagnosis:** Bilateral severe carpal tunnel syndrome (CTS) with thenar atrophy \u2014 likely combined occupational and metabolic etiology.\n\n**EMG/NCS Severity Grading (Expected Findings):**\n- **Sensory NCS:** Absent or severely prolonged median sensory distal latency (>4.5 ms) and reduced/absent SNAP amplitude bilateral. Comparative studies (median vs ulnar to ring finger) will show >0.5 ms difference.\n- **Motor NCS:** Prolonged median motor distal latency (>6.0 ms expected given severity), reduced CMAP amplitude from APB (reflecting axonal loss/thenar atrophy). Right worse than left.\n- **Needle EMG of APB:** Fibrillation potentials, positive sharp waves (active denervation), chronic neurogenic changes (large amplitude, long duration, polyphasic MUPs), reduced recruitment \u2014 all indicating severe, chronic median neuropathy with axonal loss.\n- **Severity classification:** Stevens/AANEM severe grade \u2014 motor and sensory axonal loss with denervation. This is beyond the moderate stage where conservative management alone would suffice.\n\n**Critical Differential Diagnosis:**\n- **Cervical radiculopathy (C6-C7):** Excluded by negative Spurling, normal reflexes, normal non-median innervated muscles, no neck pain. EMG should sample C6-C7 myotomes (pronator teres, FCR, triceps) to confirm.\n- **Thoracic outlet syndrome:** No vascular symptoms, negative Adson, no supraclavicular pathology. But should be considered given bilateral symptoms in a manual laborer.\n- **Diabetic polyneuropathy:** Must check distal lower extremity NCS. If sural SNAP is also reduced, superimposed polyneuropathy is likely contributing. Diabetes also increases CTS risk by 2-3x.\n- **Proximal median neuropathy (pronator syndrome):** No forearm tenderness, no weakness of FPL/FDP to index (anterior interosseous branch). Ruled out clinically.")
B.aYM=new A.c("+ Diagnostic question","**Q: Her EMG shows absent bilateral median SNAPs and motor axonal loss. Her sural nerve study is also mildly abnormal. How does this change your management?**\n\n**A:** The abnormal sural nerve study confirms **superimposed diabetic sensorimotor polyneuropathy**, which is critical for several reasons:\n1. **Prognostic implications for surgery:** Carpal tunnel release in patients with concurrent polyneuropathy has **lower success rates** for sensory recovery. The patient should be counseled that numbness may only partially improve after surgery because some sensory loss is from the polyneuropathy, not just median nerve compression.\n2. **Thenar motor recovery:** Despite polyneuropathy, surgical decompression should still be pursued for motor recovery \u2014 ongoing compression will only worsen the thenar atrophy. However, recovery of APB strength may be incomplete given the chronic denervation.\n3. **Medical optimization:** Tighter glycemic control (target A1c <7%) may slow polyneuropathy progression and improve surgical outcomes. Refer to endocrinology.\n4. **Workers' compensation implications:** The employer/insurer may argue the polyneuropathy is the primary cause, not occupational exposure. The physiatrist must document that CTS is a distinct, superimposed compressive neuropathy with occupational contribution, and that polyneuropathy is a predisposing factor, not the sole cause.\n5. **Bilateral approach:** Given severity on both sides with axonal loss, staged bilateral carpal tunnel release is indicated \u2014 operate on the worse (right/dominant) side first.")
B.aQq=new A.c("+ Challenge question",'**Q: The patient asks if cortisone injections could help her avoid surgery. What is your evidence-based response?**\n\n**A:** In her case, corticosteroid injection is **unlikely to provide lasting benefit** and surgery should not be delayed:\n1. **Evidence for injections:** Corticosteroid injections provide short-term relief (1-3 months) in mild-to-moderate CTS. A Cochrane review shows significant improvement at 1 month but no sustained benefit at 6 months compared to placebo.\n2. **Severity matters:** For severe CTS with thenar atrophy and axonal loss on EMG, injections are **not recommended as definitive treatment**. Ongoing axonal loss is occurring \u2014 every month of delay risks further irreversible motor damage.\n3. **Role of injection:** A single injection could be offered as a **diagnostic/therapeutic bridge** while awaiting surgical scheduling or workers\' comp authorization. If symptoms improve dramatically, it confirms CTS as the primary generator.\n4. **Risks:** Repeated injections (>3) may cause tendon damage and median nerve injury. Given her diabetes, post-injection glucose spikes should be anticipated.\n5. **Bottom line:** "I understand wanting to avoid surgery, and an injection may help temporarily. But given the muscle wasting in your thumbs, I strongly recommend surgical release to prevent further permanent weakness. Delaying surgery in your situation carries real risk of irreversible damage."')
B.aVo=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","**Surgical Planning:**\n- **Carpal tunnel release indicated bilaterally** \u2014 severe CTS with axonal loss and thenar atrophy is a clear surgical indication\n- **Open vs endoscopic:** Both have equivalent long-term outcomes. Open release has slightly higher pillar pain and longer return to manual work. Endoscopic has slightly higher risk of incomplete release and nerve injury. For a manual laborer, open release may be preferred for complete visualization and lower revision rate.\n- **Staged approach:** Operate on right (dominant/worse) side first, left side 4-6 weeks later once right hand functional for ADLs.\n\n**Post-Operative Rehabilitation:**\n- Week 1-2: Wound care, edema management, gentle finger ROM. Wrist splint in neutral for comfort (not mandatory).\n- Week 2-4: Progressive wrist ROM, scar mobilization/desensitization, light grip strengthening.\n- Week 4-8: Progressive strengthening, work simulation tasks, power grip and pinch strengthening.\n- Week 8-12: Work hardening program if returning to manual labor.\n\n**Thenar Strengthening Program:**\n- Opponens exercises, thumb abduction against resistance, pinch strengthening with therapy putty\n- Realistic expectation: with chronic denervation and atrophy, full APB recovery is unlikely. Some reinnervation expected over 6-12 months.\n\n**Metabolic Optimization:**\n- Glycemic control optimization \u2014 A1c target <7%\n- TSH monitoring \u2014 hypothyroidism contributes to CTS\n- Weight management counseling\n\n**Splinting:** Continue nighttime neutral wrist splints bilateral until surgery to minimize nocturnal symptoms.")
B.aVs=new A.c("+ Management question","**Q: Six months after bilateral carpal tunnel release, her grip strength has improved but she still has significant numbness in her right index finger. What are your next steps?**\n\n**A:** Persistent numbness 6 months post-release requires systematic evaluation:\n1. **Repeat EMG/NCS:** Compare to pre-operative baseline. If median motor latency has improved and CMAP amplitude increased, decompression was successful for the motor component. Persistent sensory loss may reflect **irreversible axonal damage from prolonged compression** or contribution from diabetic polyneuropathy.\n2. **Assess for incomplete release:** If median motor latency is unchanged or worse, consider ultrasound evaluation for incomplete transverse carpal ligament release or post-surgical scar/fibrosis compressing the nerve.\n3. **Evaluate polyneuropathy contribution:** If sural and lower extremity NCS have worsened, the polyneuropathy is progressing and contributing to ongoing sensory loss. Optimize glycemic control.\n4. **Sensory re-education program:** Desensitization and cortical re-mapping techniques (texture discrimination training, Semmes-Weinstein monofilament progression) can improve functional sensory use even without full nerve recovery.\n5. **Adaptive strategies:** Textured grips on tools, visual compensation for diminished protective sensation, skin inspection routine to prevent unnoticed injuries.\n6. **Counsel the patient:** Motor improvement (grip, pinch) is the primary surgical goal and has been achieved. Sensory recovery is slower (up to 18 months) and may be permanently incomplete given the severity and concurrent polyneuropathy.")
B.aSj=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","**Workers' Compensation Considerations:**\n- **Occupational causation:** Repetitive gripping, forceful pinching, and vibration exposure in meat packing is a well-established CTS risk factor. Her job meets OSHA criteria for repetitive motion injury.\n- **Complicating factors:** Pre-existing diabetes and hypothyroidism are **predisposing conditions**, not exclusions. Workers' comp typically covers conditions that are **aggravated or accelerated** by occupational exposure, even with pre-existing risk factors. Document temporal relationship between occupational exposure and symptom onset.\n- **Independent Medical Examination (IME):** Anticipate employer/insurer requesting IME. Ensure thorough documentation of occupational exposure history, job demands analysis, and EMG severity.\n- **Maximum Medical Improvement (MMI):** Typically reached 6-12 months post-surgery. Impairment rating per AMA Guides 6th Edition using grip/pinch strength loss and sensory deficit grading.\n\n**Ergonomic Consultation:**\n- Formal job demands analysis of the meat packing position\n- Ergonomic modifications: tool redesign (padded handles, reduced grip force requirements), job rotation to reduce repetitive exposure, mandatory rest breaks\n- If returning to the same position without modifications, recurrence risk is significant\n\n**Disability Accommodations:**\n- If unable to return to meat packing, vocational rehabilitation referral through workers' comp\n- ADA reasonable accommodations: modified duties, job reassignment to less manual position\n- Consider permanent partial disability rating if persistent deficits at MMI")
B.aPJ=new A.c("+ Systems question","**Q: The workers' compensation insurer denies the claim, stating CTS is due to diabetes, not work. How do you respond as the treating physician?**\n\n**A:** Write a detailed **causation letter** addressing:\n1. **Occupational contribution is well-established:** Cite NIOSH criteria \u2014 her job involves repetitive forceful gripping, vibration exposure, and sustained wrist postures for 10 hours/day, all recognized CTS risk factors. Meta-analyses show 2-3x increased CTS risk in meat processing workers.\n2. **Diabetes is a predisposing factor, not the cause:** Diabetes increases susceptibility to compressive neuropathy but does not independently cause the degree of focal median neuropathy seen here. Her diabetic polyneuropathy is mild (sural SNAP only mildly reduced) while her median neuropathy is severe \u2014 this disproportion argues for a compressive etiology superimposed on a mild systemic neuropathy.\n3. **Temporal relationship:** Symptoms began and progressed during employment. No CTS symptoms prior to starting this job 8 years ago.\n4. **Bilateral severity pattern:** Consistent with occupational bilateral hand use rather than systemic disease, which would typically present symmetrically with polyneuropathy pattern first.\n5. **Legal standard:** Most workers' comp jurisdictions use \"substantial contributing factor\" standard, not sole cause. Occupational exposure need only be a material contributing factor.\n6. If denied again, refer the patient to a **workers' compensation attorney** and support with comprehensive medical records and opinion letter.")
B.aU0=new A.c(u.j,'**Scenario:** The patient speaks limited English. Her 17-year-old daughter has been serving as interpreter for all medical appointments. You need to discuss complex surgical decisions, workers\' compensation implications, and prognosis.\n\n**Critical Issue:** Using a family member \u2014 especially a minor child \u2014 as a medical interpreter is **inadequate and potentially harmful** for complex medical decision-making.\n\n**Recommended Approach:**\n\n**Step 1 \u2014 Arrange professional interpretation:** "I appreciate your daughter helping you communicate, and I can see she cares about you very much. However, for the medical decisions we need to discuss today, I need to use a certified medical interpreter to make sure every detail is communicated accurately. This is for your protection \u2014 medical and legal terms can be very difficult to translate, and I want to make sure you have complete and accurate information."\n\n**Step 2 \u2014 Use qualified interpreter services:**\n- Request in-person certified medical interpreter in Spanish (preferred for complex discussions)\n- If unavailable, use telephone or video interpreter service (mandated by Title VI of the Civil Rights Act and Section 1557 of the ACA)\n- Ensure interpreter is familiar with medical terminology\n- Speak directly to the patient, not to the interpreter ("Tell her..." is incorrect framing)\n\n**Step 3 \u2014 Address the daughter\'s role sensitively:**\n- Do not dismiss the daughter or make her feel excluded\n- "Your daughter is welcome to stay and listen, but I want the professional interpreter to handle the medical translation so there is no burden on her to get complex terms exactly right"\n- The daughter should not bear the emotional weight of interpreting bad news or complex prognosis\n\n**Step 4 \u2014 Ensure comprehension through teach-back:**\n- After explaining surgical options, ask the patient (through interpreter) to explain back her understanding\n- Provide written materials in Spanish \u2014 surgical consent, post-op instructions, workers\' comp forms\n- Schedule follow-up with interpreter availability confirmed\n\n**Step 5 \u2014 Document interpreter use:** Note in medical record the name/ID of certified interpreter used, language of service, and that the patient demonstrated understanding.')
B.aMK=new A.c("+ Communication question",'**Q: Despite your recommendation, the patient insists her daughter interpret because "she understands me best." How do you handle this?**\n\n**A:** Respect the patient\'s preference while ensuring safety and legal compliance:\n\n"I completely understand that your daughter knows you best and you feel most comfortable with her. I respect that, and she is welcome to be here. However, I have a responsibility to make sure that the medical and legal information we discuss today \u2014 especially about surgery and workers\' compensation \u2014 is translated with complete accuracy.\n\nHere is what I suggest as a compromise: I will use the professional interpreter for the medical discussion to ensure every detail is accurate. Your daughter can listen to everything and add clarification if she feels the interpreter missed something about how you are feeling or what you are experiencing. After we finish, your daughter can discuss everything with you at home in whatever way feels most natural.\n\nThis way, you get the best of both \u2014 the accuracy of a professional interpreter for the medical and legal terms, and the comfort and trust of your daughter\'s presence."\n\n**If the patient still refuses the professional interpreter:**\n- Document that professional interpreter was offered and declined\n- Document that risks of using a family member interpreter were explained\n- Proceed with the daughter but **supplement with written materials in Spanish** and schedule a follow-up with a professional interpreter for surgical consent specifically\n- **Surgical consent MUST be obtained with a certified interpreter** per institutional policy and legal requirements \u2014 this is non-negotiable regardless of patient preference\n- Consult risk management if the patient refuses certified interpreter for consent')
B.aFV=s([B.aZP,B.aNJ,B.aPZ,B.b1O,B.aYM,B.aQq,B.aVo,B.aVs,B.aSj,B.aPJ,B.aU0,B.aMK],t.E)
B.Q1=new A.a5("nm-carpal-tunnel","Carpal Tunnel Syndrome \u2014 Bilateral Severe with Thenar Atrophy",B.aFV,!1)
B.aTT=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 45-year-old male is transferred to acute inpatient rehabilitation 8 weeks after onset of Guillain-Barr\xe9 syndrome (acute inflammatory demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy \u2014 AIDP). He presented initially with ascending weakness following a Campylobacter jejuni gastroenteritis, progressed to respiratory failure requiring intubation by day 5, and received 5 days of IV immunoglobulin (IVIg). He was ventilator-dependent for 4 weeks and weaned successfully 2 weeks ago. He has been deconditioned in the ICU and step-down unit. He was a competitive marathon runner who completed 3 marathons annually and identifies strongly as an athlete. He is a high school physical education teacher. His wife reports he has become withdrawn and irritable since learning about the expected prolonged recovery.")
B.aWM=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","**Past Medical History:** No significant prior medical history. Excellent baseline fitness \u2014 ran 50 miles/week. No prior neurological conditions.\n\n**GBS Course:** Campylobacter gastroenteritis 10 days before weakness onset. Ascending weakness over 3 days from feet to respiratory muscles. CSF showed albuminocytologic dissociation (protein 185 mg/dL, WBC 2). Anti-ganglioside antibodies negative. Treated with IVIg 0.4 g/kg x 5 days. Nadir at week 2 \u2014 ventilator-dependent, no voluntary limb movement. Began recovery of proximal upper extremity movement at week 5.\n\n**Current Complaints:** Severe weakness in all extremities (proximal > distal early recovery pattern), bilateral foot drop, neuropathic pain in feet and hands (burning, electric), profound fatigue disproportionate to activity, orthostatic dizziness, constipation, difficulty sleeping due to pain.\n\n**Medications:** Gabapentin 300 mg TID, acetaminophen, docusate, melatonin, subcutaneous heparin.\n\n**Social History:** Married, two children (ages 8 and 12). Wife is a part-time nurse. Home is a two-story colonial \u2014 bedroom and bathroom upstairs. Identified as a marathon runner since age 25 \u2014 running community is his primary social network.")
B.aOa=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**General:** Thin, deconditioned male. Alert, oriented, affect flat. Visible muscle wasting in distal extremities.\n\n**Neurological:**\n- Cranial nerves: Mild bilateral facial weakness (residual CN VII involvement). Normal swallowing and voice.\n- Sensation: Reduced light touch and pinprick in glove-and-stocking distribution to mid-forearms and mid-calves. Proprioception impaired at toes bilateral.\n\n**Motor Exam (MRC Scale):**\n- Shoulder abduction: 3+/5 bilateral\n- Elbow flexion: 4-/5 bilateral\n- Wrist extension: 3/5 bilateral\n- Grip: 2+/5 bilateral\n- Finger abduction: 2/5 bilateral\n- Hip flexion: 3/5 bilateral\n- Knee extension: 3+/5 bilateral\n- Ankle dorsiflexion: 1/5 bilateral\n- Ankle plantarflexion: 2/5 bilateral\n\n**Reflexes:** Absent throughout (areflexia).\n\n**Functional Status:** Moderate assist for bed mobility, maximal assist for sit-to-stand, unable to ambulate (bilateral foot drop + proximal weakness). Can self-feed with adaptive utensils. Requires assist for grooming, dressing.\n\n**Autonomic:** Resting HR 95, orthostatic drop (supine 120/75 \u2192 sitting 90/55 with lightheadedness). Constipation (no BM x 4 days). Anhidrosis noted in distal extremities.\n\n**Pain:** Reports 7/10 burning neuropathic pain in both feet, worse at night. Allodynia to light touch on soles.")
B.aNR=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","**Diagnosis Confirmation:** AIDP variant of Guillain-Barr\xe9 syndrome, recovery phase (8 weeks post-onset), with residual severe weakness, neuropathic pain, and autonomic dysfunction.\n\n**EMG Prognostic Factors (Critical for Recovery Prediction):**\n- **Favorable prognostic indicators:** Primarily demyelinating pattern (prolonged distal latencies, conduction block, temporal dispersion, prolonged F-waves) with preserved CMAP amplitudes \u2014 suggests remyelination potential with good recovery.\n- **Unfavorable prognostic indicators:** Low distal CMAP amplitudes (<20% of lower limit of normal), inexcitable nerves, widespread denervation on needle EMG \u2014 suggests axonal loss requiring slower reinnervation.\n- **Mixed pattern:** Most AIDP patients have secondary axonal degeneration. Recovery depends on the ratio of demyelination to axonal loss.\n- **Timeline markers:** If CMAPs remain absent at 3 months, prognosis for complete recovery is poor. Early CMAP recovery (by 3 months) predicts good functional outcomes.\n\n**GBS Variants to Consider:**\n- **AIDP (this patient):** Most common in North America. Demyelinating. Best recovery potential.\n- **AMAN (acute motor axonal neuropathy):** Pure motor, axonal. Associated with Campylobacter and anti-GM1 antibodies. Can have rapid recovery or prolonged course.\n- **AMSAN:** Motor and sensory axonal. Worst prognosis.\n- **Miller Fisher:** Ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, areflexia. Anti-GQ1b antibodies. Excellent recovery.\n\n**CIDP Surveillance:**\n- Monitor for treatment-related fluctuations (TRF): deterioration within 8 weeks of IVIg suggesting CIDP rather than GBS\n- If patient deteriorates after initial improvement, or improves then worsens 3+ times, reclassify as CIDP\n- CIDP requires ongoing immunotherapy (IVIg or plasma exchange maintenance)\n- Re-evaluate at 3-month and 6-month marks for any progressive or relapsing course")
B.b2g=new A.c("+ Diagnostic question","**Q: His repeat EMG at 8 weeks shows absent tibial and peroneal CMAPs bilaterally but recovering median and ulnar CMAPs with conduction block. What does this tell you about prognosis?**\n\n**A:** This is a **mixed prognostic picture** with important implications:\n- **Upper extremities (encouraging):** Recovering median and ulnar CMAPs with conduction block indicate **demyelination with remyelination in progress**. Conduction block is reversible \u2014 as remyelination occurs, these nerves should recover well. Predicts good hand function recovery over 3-6 months.\n- **Lower extremities (concerning):** Absent tibial and peroneal CMAPs at 8 weeks suggest either **severe conduction block distally** or **axonal loss**. Must repeat at 3-4 months to differentiate. If CMAPs remain absent at 3 months, significant axonal degeneration has occurred, and lower extremity recovery will be slow (6-18 months) and potentially incomplete.\n- **Clinical correlation:** Explains his pattern of proximal > distal recovery and persistent bilateral foot drop. The foot drop may be the longest-lasting deficit.\n- **Rehabilitation implications:** Upper extremity therapy can be progressive and goal-oriented. Lower extremity plan should include bilateral AFOs for mobility and a prolonged gait training timeline. Do not set expectations for running at this stage.\n- **Recommend repeat EMG at 3 months** to reassess lower extremity CMAP recovery \u2014 this will be the most important prognostic timepoint.")
B.b0n=new A.c("+ Challenge question","**Q: At week 10, the patient develops new weakness in his arms after two weeks of improvement. Should you treat this as a GBS relapse or CIDP?**\n\n**A:** This clinical scenario requires urgent differentiation:\n\n**GBS treatment-related fluctuation (TRF):** Occurs in approximately 10% of GBS patients. Defined as clinical worsening within 8 weeks of IVIg or plasma exchange. Thought to represent ongoing immune attack outlasting the initial treatment. Management: **re-treat with IVIg** (same 0.4 g/kg x 5 days protocol). Typically one episode, self-limited.\n\n**CIDP reclassification criteria:** If the patient experiences 3 or more fluctuations, or continues to worsen beyond 8 weeks from onset, CIDP must be considered. CIDP requires ongoing maintenance immunotherapy.\n\n**Immediate steps:**\n1. Repeat EMG/NCS to look for new demyelination or conduction block compared to prior study\n2. Repeat CSF analysis if considering CIDP\n3. Re-treat with IVIg given he is within 10 weeks of onset \u2014 this is likely TRF\n4. Monitor closely for respiratory decline (serial FVC measurements every 6 hours initially)\n5. If improvement after retreatment followed by another decline, strong suspicion for CIDP \u2014 initiate maintenance IVIg every 4 weeks\n6. Consider plasma exchange if IVIg retreatment fails\n\nThis clinical scenario highlights why GBS patients in rehab need ongoing neuromuscular surveillance \u2014 the physiatrist must be vigilant for immune reactivation.")
B.aNa=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","**Intensive Inpatient Rehabilitation Program:**\n- PT 2x daily: progressive mobility training, tilt table for orthostatic reconditioning, seated and standing balance, pre-gait activities, bilateral AFO fitting and gait training\n- OT 2x daily: ADL retraining, fine motor recovery exercises, adaptive equipment (built-up utensils, button hook), energy conservation strategies\n- Focus on proximal-to-distal recovery pattern \u2014 prioritize proximal strengthening while monitoring for distal nerve recovery\n\n**Orthotic Prescription:**\n- Bilateral custom-molded AFOs for foot drop \u2014 start with posterior leaf spring design for lightweight support during gait training\n- Consider carbon fiber AFOs once ambulatory for more dynamic energy return\n- Serial reassessment: as ankle dorsiflexion recovers, transition to lighter orthoses and eventually discontinue\n- Wrist cock-up splints for nighttime to maintain wrist extension positioning\n\n**Neuropathic Pain Management:**\n- Gabapentin: titrate from 300 mg TID to 600-900 mg TID as tolerated (monitor sedation and balance effects)\n- If inadequate: add duloxetine 30-60 mg daily (dual benefit for pain and mood)\n- Topical lidocaine 5% patches to feet for localized allodynia\n- Avoid opioids \u2014 will worsen constipation and impair rehabilitation participation\n- Non-pharmacological: TENS unit, desensitization techniques, sleep hygiene optimization\n\n**Fatigue Management (Major GBS Issue):**\n- Fatigue in GBS is disproportionate to weakness level and persists long-term in up to 80% of patients\n- Energy conservation education: pacing, prioritization, positioning\n- Structured rest periods between therapy sessions\n- Graded exercise: avoid overexertion which can worsen fatigue paradoxically\n- Screen for depression as contributor to fatigue\n- Consider low-dose amantadine if fatigue is debilitating and refractory\n\n**Autonomic Management:**\n- Orthostatic hypotension: compression stockings, abdominal binder, gradual position changes, hydration, salt supplementation. Consider midodrine 5 mg TID if refractory.\n- Constipation: bowel program with scheduled docusate + senna, adequate hydration, fiber supplementation\n- Monitor for cardiac dysrhythmias (autonomic GBS involvement) \u2014 telemetry if resting HR consistently >120 or irregular")
B.aUM=new A.c("+ Management question","**Q: The patient is making good progress but hits a plateau at week 12 of rehab. He can walk 100 feet with bilateral AFOs and a rolling walker but is not improving further. What is your approach?**\n\n**A:** A plateau at 12 weeks of rehab (20 weeks post-onset) is not unexpected in GBS but requires systematic evaluation:\n\n1. **Rule out treatable causes:** Repeat EMG to assess for ongoing denervation vs reinnervation. Check for CIDP conversion. Screen for depression (major cause of functional plateau). Evaluate nutritional status and sleep quality.\n\n2. **Reassess rehabilitation intensity:** Consider whether current program intensity is appropriate \u2014 GBS patients can be **overexercised**, leading to paradoxical functional decline. Eccentric overloading of partially reinnervated muscles causes damage. Reduce high-intensity resistance and focus on **submaximal, high-repetition functional training**.\n\n3. **Modify goals and timeline:** Reframe the plateau as a consolidation phase, not an endpoint. GBS motor recovery continues for 12-18 months. Transition from acute rehab to intensive outpatient rehabilitation (3-5 days/week).\n\n4. **Technology augmentation:** Body-weight supported treadmill training for gait endurance. Functional electrical stimulation (FES) cycling for lower extremity strengthening and cardiovascular conditioning.\n\n5. **Community reintegration focus:** Shift goals from impairment-based (strength) to participation-based (return to teaching, community mobility, driving).\n\n6. **Discharge planning:** He is appropriate for discharge home with outpatient rehab. Ensure home modifications (stair rail, shower bench), DME (rolling walker, wheelchair for community distances), and home exercise program.")
B.aZQ=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","**Acute Rehab vs SNF Disposition:**\n- **Acute rehab criteria (met):** Medically stable, able to tolerate 3 hours of therapy daily, requires multidisciplinary coordinated care (PT, OT, SLP for facial weakness, nursing for autonomic management), needs physician-directed rehabilitation, expected to make functional gains.\n- **SNF would be inappropriate:** His complexity (autonomic dysfunction, neuropathic pain management, potential for CIDP conversion, need for serial EMG monitoring) requires physiatrist-directed care.\n- **Insurance justification:** Document functional gains weekly with objective measures (FIM scores, distance walked, grip strength). Insurance typically authorizes 2-4 weeks initially with extensions based on documented progress.\n\n**Disability Planning for Prolonged Recovery:**\n- **Short-term disability:** Through employer \u2014 PE teacher role requires significant physical capacity. Likely unable to return for 6-12+ months.\n- **FMLA protection:** 12 weeks of job protection. If recovery extends beyond FMLA, work with employer on extended leave or ADA accommodations.\n- **Return-to-work planning:** PE teacher role may need modifications initially \u2014 classroom-based health education, reduced physical demonstration requirements. Gradual return with vocational rehabilitation support.\n- **Social Security Disability Insurance (SSDI):** If recovery is prolonged beyond 12 months, GBS is a listed impairment under SSA Blue Book (11.14 \u2014 peripheral neuropathy) if functional limitations meet criteria.\n\n**Long-Term Follow-Up Structure:**\n- Physiatry: every 2-4 weeks during active recovery, then quarterly\n- EMG: repeat at 3 months, 6 months, 12 months to track reinnervation\n- Neurology: co-management for CIDP surveillance\n- Psychology: ongoing for adjustment disorder and identity-related grief\n- Community resources: GBS/CIDP Foundation International for peer support")
B.aWd=new A.c("+ Systems question","**Q: Insurance denies continued acute rehab at week 3, stating the patient has plateaued. He is walking 50 feet with a walker and bilateral AFOs. How do you appeal?**\n\n**A:** This denial reflects a misunderstanding of GBS recovery trajectory. Appeal with:\n\n1. **Medical necessity documentation:** GBS recovery is non-linear with expected plateaus followed by continued improvement. A 3-week snapshot does not capture the recovery trajectory. Cite literature showing GBS patients continue making significant gains for 6-12 months.\n\n2. **Objective functional gains:** Even if ambulation distance has plateaued, document improvements in other domains \u2014 transfers, ADLs, wheelchair mobility, pain management, orthostatic tolerance. Use FIM scores showing aggregate improvement.\n\n3. **Medical complexity requiring physician oversight:** Autonomic dysfunction management, neuropathic pain titration, CIDP surveillance, and orthotic prescription adjustments require physiatrist-directed care not available in SNF.\n\n4. **Cost-effectiveness argument:** Premature discharge to SNF will result in slower recovery, higher readmission risk, and longer total disability duration. Intensive rehab reduces long-term disability costs and accelerates return to work (as a taxpaying teacher).\n\n5. **Peer-reviewed support:** Cite ACRM and AAN guidelines supporting intensive inpatient rehabilitation for moderate-to-severe GBS. Reference studies showing superior outcomes in acute rehab vs SNF for GBS patients.\n\n6. **Request peer-to-peer review** with the insurance company medical director. If denied, escalate to state insurance commission external review.")
B.aNS=new A.c(u.j,'**Scenario:** During a therapy session, the patient stops exercising, puts his head down, and says: "I ran my last marathon in 3 hours and 15 minutes. Now I can\'t walk to the bathroom. Who am I if I can\'t run? How long until I can run a marathon again?"\n\n**This Requires Navigating Identity Loss \u2014 Not Just Prognosis:**\n\n**Step 1 \u2014 Validate the grief:** "I hear you, and I want you to know that what you are feeling is completely understandable. Running was not just exercise for you \u2014 it was part of who you are, your community, your identity. Losing that, even temporarily, is a real loss, and it makes sense that you are grieving it."\n\n**Step 2 \u2014 Honest prognosis without destroying hope:**\n"Here is what I can honestly tell you about recovery from Guillain-Barr\xe9:\n- Most people with your condition do regain the ability to walk independently \u2014 that is the most likely outcome.\n- Recovery of nerve function continues for 12 to 18 months, sometimes longer.\n- Your athletic baseline and fitness level before this illness are actually advantages \u2014 your cardiovascular system and your brain\'s motor patterns are intact. Your nerves need to heal.\n- Returning to competitive marathon running is a possibility I cannot guarantee. Some GBS survivors do return to high-level athletics, but many experience lasting fatigue and endurance limitations.\n- Setting a marathon as the ultimate goal is fine, but we need intermediate milestones first \u2014 walking to the bathroom, walking the hallway, walking outside, walking a mile."\n\n**Step 3 \u2014 Reframe identity without dismissing it:**\n"You said \'who am I if I can\'t run?\' I think you are still the same person \u2014 disciplined, competitive, goal-oriented. Those qualities are exactly what will drive your recovery. Right now, your marathon is getting from this bed to the end of the hallway. That takes the same mental toughness."\n\n**Step 4 \u2014 Connect to peer support:**\n"I would like to connect you with the GBS/CIDP Foundation. They have a peer mentorship program with people who have been exactly where you are now \u2014 including some who returned to running. Hearing from someone who has been through it can be more powerful than anything I can say."\n\n**Step 5 \u2014 Screen for clinical depression:**\n"I also want to check in on how you are doing emotionally overall \u2014 not just about running, but sleep, appetite, motivation, any feelings of hopelessness. What you are going through would be difficult for anyone, and getting support for the emotional side is just as important as the physical therapy."')
B.b15=new A.c("+ Communication question",'**Q: His wife pulls you aside and says, "He used to be so positive. Now he won\'t talk to the kids, refuses to video call his running group, and told me last night he feels like a burden. I\'m scared." How do you respond?**\n\n**A:** This raises concern for clinical depression requiring immediate assessment:\n\n"Thank you for telling me this \u2014 what you are describing is important, and I take it seriously. The changes you are seeing \u2014 withdrawal from family, isolation from his social network, feelings of being a burden \u2014 these can be signs of depression, which is very common after a serious illness like this. Up to 30% of GBS patients develop clinical depression during recovery.\n\nHere is what I would like to do:\n1. I will arrange for a formal psychiatric or psychology evaluation this week. Depression in rehabilitation is treatable and, importantly, untreated depression directly slows physical recovery.\n2. I am going to speak with him about this \u2014 not in a way that puts you in the middle, but because as his physician, screening for depression is part of my job.\n3. If he is diagnosed with depression, we will likely recommend counseling and possibly medication. Some antidepressants like duloxetine can actually help with both his mood and his nerve pain.\n\nFor you \u2014 I also want to check in on how you are doing. Caregiver burnout is real. Are you getting any support? The social worker on our team can connect you with caregiver resources and family counseling.\n\nOne more thing \u2014 him refusing to video call his running group concerns me because social isolation accelerates depression. Could we gently encourage him to reconnect, even if it is just texting? Sometimes people withdraw because they are ashamed of how they look or where they are functionally. His running friends may be exactly the motivation he needs, but he may need permission to show vulnerability."\n\nDocument this conversation and initiate formal depression screening (PHQ-9) and psychology referral the same day.')
B.aEM=s([B.aTT,B.aWM,B.aOa,B.aNR,B.b2g,B.b0n,B.aNa,B.aUM,B.aZQ,B.aWd,B.aNS,B.b15],t.E)
B.PN=new A.a5("nm-gbs-recovery","Guillain-Barr\xe9 Syndrome \u2014 AIDP Recovery Phase",B.aEM,!1)
B.aSK=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 40-year-old male with a C6 ASIA A spinal cord injury sustained 10 years ago in a diving accident presents to the seating and mobility clinic for a new wheelchair prescription. His current ultralight manual wheelchair is 8 years old and showing significant frame fatigue with loose axle components. He has been using it independently for community mobility but reports progressive bilateral shoulder pain over the past 18 months that is limiting his transfers and propulsion endurance. He rates his shoulder pain 6/10 during transfers and 4/10 during flat-ground propulsion. He also reports a healed Stage 2 sacral pressure injury from 6 months ago and is concerned about his current cushion.")
B.aWr=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **SCI Level/Classification**: C6 ASIA A (complete) x 10 years; preserved wrist extension (tenodesis grasp functional)\n- **Current Equipment**: Ultralight rigid-frame manual wheelchair (Ti-Lite ZRA, 8 years old); Jay J2 cushion (5 years old, showing wear); standard sling back upholstery\n- **Functional Status**: Independent in wheelchair mobility on flat and gentle inclines; independent in lateral sliding board transfers; independent in self-care with adaptive equipment; drives with hand controls\n- **Shoulder Pain History**: Bilateral shoulder pain x 18 months, right worse than left; pain with overhead reaching, transfers, and prolonged propulsion; diagnosed with bilateral supraspinatus tendinopathy on ultrasound 6 months ago\n- **Pressure Injury History**: Stage 2 sacral pressure ulcer healed 6 months ago after 3 months of wound care; attributed to worn-out cushion and increased sitting time during a work project\n- **Weight**: 78 kg (has gained 8 kg over past 2 years \u2014 reduced activity due to shoulder pain)\n- **Employment**: Works as an IT consultant from home; drives independently\n- **Home**: Accessible single-level home with roll-in shower\n- **Psychosocial**: Married, two young children; actively involved in wheelchair basketball (limited recently by shoulder pain)")
B.aNQ=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 110/68 mmHg (seated), HR 64 bpm, SpO2 99% on RA.\n\n**Upper Extremity Exam**:\n- **Shoulders**: Bilateral positive Neer and Hawkins impingement signs. Painful arc 80-120 degrees of abduction bilaterally. Supraspinatus strength 4/5 bilaterally (pain-limited). External rotation 4+/5. No instability. ROM: flexion 160 degrees, abduction 150 degrees, ER 80 degrees, IR to L3 bilaterally.\n- **Biceps**: 5/5 bilaterally\n- **Wrist extensors**: 5/5 bilaterally (key muscle for C6)\n- **Triceps**: 0/5 bilaterally (absent \u2014 below level of injury)\n- **Hand function**: Tenodesis grasp functional bilaterally for ADLs\n\n**Seating Assessment (in current wheelchair)**:\n- **Posture**: Posterior pelvic tilt, mild thoracic kyphosis, slight left lateral lean\n- **Wheelchair fit**: Seat width appears adequate; seat depth slightly short (2 cm gap behind knees \u2014 inadequate thigh support); back height low with no lateral trunk support\n- **Cushion**: Jay J2 foam/gel \u2014 visible compression set in foam, gel pack shifted anteriorly, cover worn and smooth\n\n**Pressure Mapping** (seated on current cushion):\n- Peak pressure over right ischial tuberosity: 185 mmHg (excessive \u2014 goal <60 mmHg)\n- Peak pressure over left IT: 160 mmHg\n- Sacral/coccygeal pressure elevated at 140 mmHg\n- Asymmetric loading with right > left\n\n**Skin**: Healed Stage 2 sacral pressure injury \u2014 area of hyperpigmented, thinned skin over sacrum. No current breakdown. Bilateral ischial tuberosities with mild callus formation.")
B.aYT=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","This patient requires a comprehensive wheelchair and seating prescription that addresses his shoulder pain, pressure injury prevention, postural correction, and functional mobility needs.")
B.b3P=new A.c("+ Compare wheelchair options for this patient: ultralight manual vs. power-assist vs. full power wheelchair. What factors guide the selection?","**Wheelchair Type Selection Analysis**:\n\n**Option 1: Ultralight Manual Wheelchair (Rigid Frame)**\n- **Pros**: Lightest option (typically 12-18 lbs); best energy efficiency for propulsion; folding or rigid frame options; fits in car independently; preserves upper extremity strength and cardiovascular fitness; familiar to patient\n- **Cons**: Does NOT address the fundamental problem \u2014 continued shoulder overuse; shoulder pain will likely progress without intervention\n- **Indication**: If shoulder pain resolves with treatment and wheelchair optimization\n\n**Option 2: Power-Assist Wheels (e.g., SmartDrive, E-Motion, Twion)**\n- **Pros**: Reduces propulsion effort by 30-50%; maintains manual wheelchair skills and body; fits on existing ultralight frame; allows manual propulsion when desired (exercise, sport) with power assist for long distances/inclines; preserves car transfer independence\n- **Cons**: Adds weight to wheelchair (5-15 lbs depending on system); battery management; cost ($5,000-8,000); may not be sufficient if shoulder pain is severe\n- **Indication**: **Best option for this patient** \u2014 reduces shoulder demand while preserving his independence, community mobility, and wheelchair basketball participation\n\n**Option 3: Full Power Wheelchair**\n- **Pros**: Eliminates propulsion-related shoulder stress; tilt-in-space and recline for pressure relief; power seat elevation available\n- **Cons**: Heavy (250-350+ lbs); cannot be self-loaded into car; requires van modification or wheelchair-accessible vehicle ($50,000+); changes his functional identity; may accelerate deconditioning; psychological impact\n- **Indication**: If shoulder pain progresses to the point where manual propulsion is not feasible even with power assist; or if bilateral rotator cuff tears develop\n\n**Recommended Approach for This Patient**:\n1. **New ultralight rigid-frame manual wheelchair** (properly fitted) with **power-assist system** (e.g., SmartDrive MX2+) for daily use\n2. Keep an ultralight manual chair for wheelchair basketball and short-distance use\n3. Optimize wheelchair configuration to reduce shoulder demand (see below)\n4. Concurrent shoulder rehabilitation program\n\n**Key Configuration Optimizations to Reduce Shoulder Stress**:\n- **Axle position**: Move rear axle forward (center of gravity closer to rear wheels) to improve push efficiency \u2014 each push covers more distance with less force\n- **Seat height**: Optimize so that with hands resting on push rims at top dead center, elbow flexion is 100-120 degrees\n- **Camber**: 3-5 degrees of camber for improved ergonomic hand position and stability\n- **Push rim**: Coated/ergonomic push rims (natural-fit or surge) for improved grip with less force\n- **Weight**: Minimize total chair weight (titanium frame, lightweight components)")
B.b2O=new A.c("+ What seating system (cushion and back) would you prescribe to address pressure injury prevention and postural support?","**Cushion Selection for Pressure Injury Prevention**:\n\nGiven the history of Stage 2 sacral pressure injury and current elevated pressure mapping values:\n\n1. **Air-based cushion** (e.g., ROHO Quadtro Select or ROHO High Profile):\n- Best pressure redistribution \u2014 air cells conform to body contours\n- Adjustable inflation for individualized pressure optimization\n- **ROHO Quadtro Select**: 4-zone adjustability allows targeted pressure reduction under ITs and sacrum\n- Peak pressures typically reduced to <40-60 mmHg\n- Requires ongoing maintenance (daily inflation check)\n\n2. **Hybrid cushion** (e.g., Jay Xtreme Active, Varilite):\n- Combines foam base with air/fluid overlay\n- Good pressure redistribution with more postural stability than pure air\n- Less maintenance than ROHO\n- Consider if patient finds pure air cushion unstable for transfers\n\n**Recommended**: ROHO Quadtro Select \u2014 best match for his pressure injury history and elevated IT pressures. Repeat pressure mapping with new cushion to verify adequate pressure redistribution.\n\n**Back Support System**:\n- Current sling back provides inadequate postural support \u2192 contributing to posterior pelvic tilt and kyphosis\n- **Prescription**: Rigid or semi-rigid back support (e.g., Jay J3 back or Ride Designs custom back)\n- Features needed: Adjustable lateral trunk supports to correct left lean; contoured posterior pelvic shelf to prevent posterior pelvic tilt; appropriate back height (below inferior scapular angle for C6 \u2014 preserves shoulder mobility for propulsion)\n\n**Additional Seating Components**:\n- **Anti-thrust seat**: Slight seat angle (dump) of 2-3 degrees to reduce posterior pelvic sliding\n- **Lap belt**: Two-point lap belt at 45-degree angle over pelvis (not abdomen) for pelvic positioning\n- **Armrests**: Desk-length, swing-away armrests for transfer clearance; consider removing for sports use\n\n**Pressure Relief Education Reinforcement**:\n- Weight shifts every 15-30 minutes (lean forward, lateral lean, or tilt)\n- With power-assist wheelchair: can potentially add tilt-in-space feature if future power chair conversion needed\n- Skin inspection twice daily with long-handled mirror\n- Cushion inflation check daily (for ROHO)")
B.b_8=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","In addition to the wheelchair prescription, the patient needs a comprehensive shoulder preservation program to prevent further rotator cuff damage and maintain his independence.")
B.aRp=new A.c("+ Outline a shoulder preservation protocol for this SCI patient and the broader upper extremity preservation strategy.","**Shoulder Preservation Protocol (Based on PVA/CSCM Clinical Practice Guidelines)**:\n\n**1. Rotator Cuff and Scapular Stabilization**:\n- **Strengthening**: External rotators (infraspinatus, teres minor) and scapular stabilizers (lower trapezius, serratus anterior, rhomboids). Use resistance bands or light free weights.\n- **Stretching**: Pectoral stretching (horizontal abduction), posterior capsule stretching (cross-body adduction). These address the typical SCI posture of protracted shoulders and tight anterior structures.\n- **Frequency**: 3 sets of 15 reps, 3-4 times/week. Low weight, high repetition to build endurance.\n- **Avoid**: Overhead exercises, heavy bench press, dips, and exercises that impinge the supraspinatus.\n\n**2. Propulsion Biomechanics Optimization**:\n- **Semicircular propulsion pattern**: Long, smooth strokes with hands dropping below the push rim on recovery (NOT the arc or pumping pattern which increases shoulder impingement)\n- **Reduce stroke frequency**: Fewer, more powerful strokes are less damaging than many short strokes\n- **Glove use**: Padded wheelchair gloves reduce grip force needed on push rims\n\n**3. Transfer Technique Modification**:\n- **Sliding board transfers**: Head-hips technique to reduce upper extremity loading\n- **Avoid**: Depression transfers (tricep-dip style) as they maximally load the shoulder in impingement position. Since this patient lacks triceps (C6), he already uses sliding board \u2014 reinforce technique.\n- **Transfer board**: Ensure smooth, low-friction board; consider Beasy board (curved, rotating) to reduce shoulder torque\n\n**4. Weight Management**:\n- Address the 8 kg weight gain \u2014 every kilogram of excess body weight increases shoulder loading during transfers and propulsion\n- Nutritional counseling for SCI-specific metabolism (reduced basal metabolic rate, increased visceral fat)\n- Accessible exercise: Arm ergometry, wheelchair basketball (modified intensity), swimming\n\n**5. Pain Management**:\n- Physical therapy for rotator cuff tendinopathy: eccentric exercises, manual therapy, modalities\n- NSAIDs for acute flares (short courses)\n- Subacromial corticosteroid injection if PT fails (limit to 3 per year)\n- Consider PRP injection if refractory\n\n**6. Long-Term Upper Extremity Preservation**:\n- Annual shoulder screening exam\n- Median nerve protection (carpal tunnel is common in chronic SCI \u2014 avoid prolonged wrist hyperextension)\n- Ergonomic desk/computer setup for work\n- Electric door openers, reaching aids, and other environmental modifications to reduce overhead reaching")
B.aX8=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE",'You have completed the seating evaluation and are ready to submit the wheelchair prescription. The patient\'s preferred ultralight wheelchair model with power-assist wheels has a total cost of $14,500. The insurance company sends a prior authorization denial stating that a "standard wheelchair" is sufficient.')
B.aTx=new A.c("+ How do you justify the wheelchair prescription for insurance approval? Discuss the letter of medical necessity, ATP certification, and Medicare/SADMERC requirements.","**Letter of Medical Necessity (LMN) \u2014 Key Components**:\n\n1. **Diagnosis and medical history**: C6 ASIA A SCI x 10 years, bilateral shoulder tendinopathy, healed Stage 2 sacral pressure injury\n2. **Functional limitations**: The patient's current wheelchair is 8 years old with frame fatigue. A standard wheelchair does NOT meet his needs because:\n- Standard wheelchair weight (35-50 lbs) would accelerate shoulder deterioration\n- Standard wheelchair lacks proper axle adjustability for propulsion efficiency\n- Standard sling seat/back causes posterior pelvic tilt \u2192 pressure injury recurrence risk\n3. **Medical necessity for ultralight**: Ultralight classification (\u226430 lbs) is medically necessary to reduce propulsion forces on damaged rotator cuffs. Literature supports that ultralight wheelchairs reduce stroke frequency by 10-15% compared to standard wheelchairs.\n4. **Medical necessity for power assist**: Progressive bilateral rotator cuff tendinopathy despite conservative management. Power-assist reduces propulsion effort by 30-50%, documented to slow progression of upper extremity deterioration in SCI.\n5. **Seating system justification**: Prior Stage 2 sacral pressure injury with pressure mapping demonstrating peak pressures >180 mmHg on current cushion. ROHO Quadtro Select provides superior pressure redistribution, documented on repeat pressure mapping.\n\n**Assistive Technology Professional (ATP) Certification**:\n- **CMS requires** that complex rehabilitation technology (Group 3 power wheelchairs, custom seating) be prescribed with involvement of a **RESNA-certified ATP**\n- The ATP conducts the seating evaluation, recommends specific products, ensures proper fit, and provides documentation supporting medical necessity\n- The ATP and physician must independently document the evaluation\n\n**Medicare/CMS Requirements for Complex Rehabilitation Technology**:\n- **Face-to-face evaluation**: Within 6 months of prescription by the treating physician\n- **Detailed Product Description (DPD)**: Specific product codes (HCPCS), features, and justification for each component\n- **Functional mobility assessment**: Document why a lower-level device is insufficient\n- **SADMERC (Statistical Analysis Durable Medical Equipment Regional Carrier)**: Previously handled coding verification; now replaced by **Pricing, Data Analysis and Coding (PDAC)** contractor. Each wheelchair and component must have a valid HCPCS code verified by PDAC.\n- **Competitive Bidding**: Complex rehabilitation technology is currently exempt from Medicare competitive bidding (advocated by NRRTS and industry groups)\n\n**Appeal Process for Denial**:\n1. **First-level appeal**: Submit detailed LMN with pressure mapping data, shoulder imaging reports, and clinical documentation. Cite peer-reviewed literature supporting ultralight wheelchair and power-assist for shoulder preservation.\n2. **Peer-to-peer review**: Request to speak directly with the insurance medical director. Physiatrist-to-physician communication is often effective.\n3. **External review/state insurance commissioner**: If internal appeals fail.\n4. **Patient advocacy**: Connect with United Spinal Association or state disability advocacy organizations for assistance navigating appeals.")
B.aSA=new A.c(u.j,"After the insurance denial, the patient is frustrated: 'I specifically need the TiLite Aero Z wheelchair because I've tried it and it fits perfectly. The insurance company says I can only have the cheaper model. I don't care what they say \u2014 I need that chair. Can't you just tell them I need it?'")
B.aS4=new A.c("+ How would you navigate the patient's preference for a specific wheelchair brand when insurance coverage is limited?","**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Validate the frustration**:\n'I hear your frustration, and I share it. You know your body and your needs better than the insurance company does. It is deeply frustrating when a company that has never met you is making decisions about equipment that affects every aspect of your daily life.'\n\n**2. Be honest about the process**:\n'Here is what I can and cannot do. I can write a letter of medical necessity that documents exactly WHY you need an ultralight rigid-frame wheelchair with the specific features that the TiLite Aero Z provides \u2014 the titanium frame, the adjustable axle position, the specific geometry that fits your body. What I cannot ethically do is say that ONLY one specific brand will work if another manufacturer makes a comparable product with the same features.'\n\n**3. Explain the strategy**:\n'The most effective approach is to prescribe by FEATURES, not by brand name. I will document that you require a rigid-frame ultralight wheelchair weighing under 15 pounds, with fully adjustable rear axle position, titanium or carbon fiber frame for durability and weight, specific seat dimensions, and 3-degree camber. If the TiLite Aero Z is the only chair that meets all these specifications in your size, then it becomes medically necessary by default. Our ATP will document this in the evaluation.'\n\n**4. Discuss the appeal process**:\n'If the first appeal is denied, we have several options. I will request a peer-to-peer review where I speak directly with the insurance company's medical director. In my experience, physician-to-physician conversations are often more productive than paper appeals. We can also involve our social worker to help navigate the process, and organizations like United Spinal Association offer free advocacy support for wheelchair appeals.'\n\n**5. Discuss financial options if appeal fails**:\n'In the worst case scenario, if insurance approves a comparable but less expensive chair, some patients choose to accept the insurance-covered chair and pay the difference out of pocket to upgrade to their preferred model. Some manufacturers also have assistance programs. We can also explore whether your wheelchair basketball organization has equipment grants.'\n\n**6. Commit to partnership**:\n'I want you to know that I am on your side in this fight. I will write the strongest letter I can, and I will advocate for you throughout the appeal process. This may take some time, but we will work through it together.'\n\n**Key principles**: Validate frustration while being honest about limitations, prescribe by functional specification rather than brand, strategic approach to insurance justification, offer multiple pathways including appeal and out-of-pocket options, maintain advocacy partnership.")
B.aEw=s([B.aSK,B.aWr,B.aNQ,B.aYT,B.b3P,B.b2O,B.b_8,B.aRp,B.aX8,B.aTx,B.aSA,B.aS4],t.E)
B.Q3=new A.a5("special-wheelchair-seating-sci","Complex Wheelchair Prescription and Seating \u2014 C6 SCI",B.aEw,!1)
B.b_E=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'You are asked to perform an independent medical examination (IME) on a 50-year-old male warehouse worker who claims persistent low back pain and total disability 18 months after a workplace injury. He was lifting a 60-pound box when he felt a "pop" in his low back and developed immediate pain radiating into his left leg. An MRI at the time showed an L4-5 disc herniation. He underwent a course of physical therapy, two epidural steroid injections, and was offered surgery which he declined. He has been on total disability since the injury and receives workers\' compensation benefits. The insurance carrier has requested this IME to determine whether he has reached maximum medical improvement (MMI), his current impairment rating, and whether he can return to any form of work.')
B.aTp=new A.c("History & Systems Review:",'- **Injury Date**: 18 months ago, lifting injury at warehouse\n- **Initial Diagnosis**: L4-5 left posterolateral disc herniation with left L5 radiculopathy\n- **Treatment History**: 6 weeks of PT (reports minimal improvement), 2 lumbar epidural steroid injections (temporary relief lasting 2-3 weeks each), declined surgical consultation for microdiscectomy, currently takes oxycodone 10 mg QID and cyclobenzaprine 10 mg TID\n- **Current Complaints**: Constant low back pain 8/10, left leg pain radiating to foot 7/10, "cannot sit for more than 10 minutes," "cannot stand for more than 5 minutes," "cannot lift anything over 5 pounds," "cannot walk more than one block"\n- **Functional Claims**: States he cannot do any housework, cannot drive more than 10 minutes, requires wife\'s assistance for dressing and bathing, spends most of day in recliner\n- **Employment**: Warehouse worker for 15 years; job requires lifting up to 75 pounds, standing/walking 8 hours, frequent bending\n- **PMH**: Hypertension, obesity (BMI 34), prior workers\' comp claim 5 years ago for right shoulder (resolved)\n- **Legal**: Has retained an attorney; pending workers\' comp claim and potential third-party lawsuit\n- **Psychosocial**: Married, wife works part-time; reports depression and anxiety since injury; not seeing a mental health provider; sleeps poorly\n- **Records Review**: Extensive \u2014 initial ER visit, MRI reports, PT records, injection proceduralist notes, PCP records. PCP notes document 14 refills of oxycodone. Surveillance video (provided by insurer) shows examinee lifting groceries from car trunk and mowing lawn 2 weeks ago.')
B.aZK=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:",'**Vitals**: BP 148/92 mmHg, HR 82 bpm, BMI 34.\n\n**Observation**: Examinee ambulates to exam room slowly with antalgic gait favoring the left side. Uses no assistive device. Sits upright in chair during history (contradicts claim of 10-minute sitting limitation \u2014 interview lasted 45 minutes without repositioning). During formal exam, groans audibly with most maneuvers.\n\n**Lumbar Spine Exam**:\n- Forward flexion: 30 degrees (with exaggerated guarding); however, observed bending to untie shoes with approximately 60 degrees of flexion during undressing\n- Extension: 10 degrees\n- Lateral bending: 20 degrees bilaterally\n- Tenderness: Diffuse across entire lumbar region and bilateral paraspinals; exaggerated pain response to light palpation\n\n**Waddell Signs** (Non-organic/Behavioral Signs):\n1. **Superficial tenderness**: Widespread pain to light skin touch over lumbar region \u2014 **Positive**\n2. **Simulation \u2014 axial loading**: Pain reported with light pressure on top of the head \u2014 **Positive**\n3. **Simulation \u2014 rotation**: Pain reported with passive rotation of shoulders and pelvis together (should not stress lumbar spine) \u2014 **Positive**\n4. **Distraction \u2014 seated SLR**: In seated position, leg can be extended to 80 degrees without complaint while "examining the knee." Supine SLR limited to 30 degrees with severe pain \u2014 **Positive** (inconsistency)\n5. **Regional disturbance**: Reports stocking numbness of entire left leg from groin to toes (non-dermatomal) \u2014 **Positive**\n\n**Waddell Score: 5/5 \u2014 All categories positive**\n\n**Neurologic Exam**:\n- Motor: 5/5 bilateral lower extremities with breakaway weakness (gives way suddenly during testing suggesting submaximal effort) in left ankle dorsiflexion and EHL\n- Sensation: Reports complete numbness of entire left leg (non-dermatomal pattern \u2014 L5 radiculopathy would affect dorsal foot/lateral calf only)\n- Reflexes: 2+ bilateral knees and ankles, symmetric\n- Straight leg raise: Supine \u2014 positive at 30 degrees on left (severe pain); Seated \u2014 negative at 80 degrees (see Waddell signs above)\n\n**Surveillance Video Review** (provided by insurer): Shows examinee 2 weeks ago lifting two grocery bags from car trunk (estimated 15-20 lbs each), pushing a lawn mower for approximately 30 minutes, and bending repeatedly to pick up yard debris.')
B.b0E=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","The insurance carrier asks you to reconcile the objective findings with the subjective complaints and assess whether symptom magnification or malingering is present.")
B.aOR=new A.c("+ Interpret the Waddell signs. Differentiate symptom magnification from malingering. What other assessment tools can help determine effort and consistency?",'**Waddell Signs \u2014 Proper Interpretation**:\n\nWaddell signs were originally described as **behavioral indicators of illness behavior**, NOT as proof of malingering or faking. They indicate that psychological and social factors are contributing to the pain presentation. However, 5/5 positive Waddell categories, combined with significant inconsistency between observed function and reported limitations, raises serious concern.\n\n**Critical Distinction**:\n- **Symptom magnification**: Conscious or unconscious amplification of real symptoms. May be driven by fear-avoidance, catastrophizing, secondary gain (financial), or psychological distress. The patient may have genuine pain that is being amplified.\n- **Malingering**: Deliberate fabrication or exaggeration of symptoms for external gain (financial, legal). DSM-5 lists it as a "condition" to be considered when medicolegal context is present, symptoms are inconsistent with objective findings, and there is clear external incentive.\n- **Factitious disorder**: Fabrication of symptoms for the "sick role" without external gain (NOT applicable here given financial incentive).\n\n**This patient demonstrates**: Non-organic signs on exam (5/5 Waddell), significant inconsistency between observed and reported function (surveillance video vs. stated limitations, seated SLR vs. supine SLR), and clear external incentive (workers\' comp, pending lawsuit). This constellation raises concern for **symptom magnification with possible secondary gain motivation**, though definitive determination of malingering requires careful documentation and is ultimately a legal, not medical, determination.\n\n**Additional Assessment Tools**:\n1. **Functional Capacity Evaluation (FCE)**: Standardized, multi-day assessment of physical capabilities with built-in consistency checks. Identifies submaximal effort through coefficient of variation analysis.\n2. **Grip strength testing**: Rapid exchange grip test (alternating dynamometer positions) \u2014 inconsistent results suggest submaximal effort.\n3. **Symptom Validity Tests**: For cognitive complaints \u2014 TOMM (Test of Memory Malingering), Word Memory Test.\n4. **Questionnaires**: Pain Disability Index, Oswestry Disability Index \u2014 compare self-report to observed function.\n5. **Coefficient of Variation (CV)**: In FCE testing, a CV >15% across repeated trials of the same task suggests inconsistent effort.\n\n**Important Caveats**:\n- Waddell signs have limited sensitivity and specificity for malingering\n- Presence of non-organic signs does NOT mean absence of organic pathology \u2014 he DOES have a documented disc herniation\n- Psychological factors (depression, anxiety, catastrophizing) can produce non-organic signs without conscious intent to deceive\n- The physiatrist\'s role is to document objective findings and inconsistencies \u2014 NOT to make a legal determination of fraud')
B.aW2=new A.c("+ Based on your evaluation, has this patient reached maximum medical improvement? How would you assign an impairment rating?","**Maximum Medical Improvement (MMI) Determination**:\n\n**Definition**: MMI is the point at which a condition has stabilized and is unlikely to improve substantially with or without further medical treatment. It does NOT mean the patient is pain-free or fully recovered.\n\n**Assessment for This Patient**:\n- 18 months post-injury with no significant functional improvement in the past 6+ months\n- Has completed conservative treatment (PT, ESIs)\n- Declined surgical intervention (his right, but this is relevant to MMI determination)\n- No additional treatment modalities likely to produce substantial improvement\n- **Determination**: This patient has reached MMI for the purpose of this evaluation. Note: His decision to decline surgery does not preclude an MMI determination, but it should be documented.\n\n**Impairment Rating \u2014 AMA Guides to the Evaluation of Permanent Impairment**:\n\nMost workers' compensation jurisdictions use the **AMA Guides, 5th or 6th Edition**:\n\n**5th Edition (DRE Method \u2014 Diagnosis-Related Estimates)**:\n- **DRE Lumbar Category**: Based on clinical findings and radiculopathy status\n- DRE Category I (0%): No significant findings\n- DRE Category II (5-8%): Non-verifiable radiculopathy, loss of motion segment integrity\n- DRE Category III (10-13%): Radiculopathy \u2014 documented by imaging correlating with clinical findings\n- **This patient: DRE Category III (10-13% whole person impairment)** \u2014 documented L4-5 disc herniation with left L5 radiculopathy pattern (although current exam shows inconsistency, the initial documentation supports radiculopathy)\n\n**6th Edition (Grid-Based Approach)**:\n- Uses diagnosis-based impairment with functional history, physical exam, and clinical studies as modifiers\n- Adjustment for inconsistent exam findings may lower the modifier grade\n\n**Important Documentation Points**:\n- Rate based on OBJECTIVE findings, not subjective complaints\n- Document that behavioral signs limit the reliability of the physical examination\n- Note inconsistencies between examination, surveillance, and self-report\n- Impairment rating reflects the organic condition (disc herniation) \u2014 behavioral overlay is addressed separately\n- Distinguish between impairment (medical concept \u2014 objective loss of function) and disability (legal/administrative concept \u2014 inability to perform specific job duties)")
B.aQ1=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","The referring insurer asks you to provide return-to-work recommendations and address the examinee's current opioid use.")
B.aQJ=new A.c("+ What are your return-to-work recommendations and how do you address the opioid management in this IME context?","**Return-to-Work Recommendations**:\n\n1. **Not capable of returning to pre-injury warehouse position**: The essential job functions (lifting 75 lbs, 8 hours of standing/walking, frequent bending) exceed his demonstrated functional capacity and are inconsistent with a residual L4-5 disc herniation.\n\n2. **Capable of modified/sedentary to light duty work**: Based on the objective findings (including surveillance evidence of lawn mowing, lifting 15-20 lb bags, and 30+ minutes of sustained activity), the examinee demonstrates the physical capacity for:\n- Sedentary work (lifting up to 10 lbs, sitting with periodic position changes)\n- Light duty work (lifting up to 20 lbs occasionally, 10 lbs frequently)\n- Standing/walking up to 4-6 hours with breaks\n- No repetitive heavy lifting, no frequent bending from the waist\n\n3. **Vocational rehabilitation referral**: Recommend vocational assessment for retraining into a position consistent with his physical restrictions (e.g., warehouse supervisor/dispatcher role, forklift operator, inventory management).\n\n4. **Functional Capacity Evaluation**: Recommend a formal FCE to objectively quantify work capacity if the insurer requires more detailed documentation.\n\n**Opioid Concerns**:\n- Currently prescribed oxycodone 10 mg QID (40 mg daily = 60 MME/day) for 18 months\n- This exceeds the recommended duration for acute musculoskeletal injury\n- CDC guidelines recommend reassessment of opioid therapy if pain and function have not meaningfully improved\n- **Recommendations in IME report**:\n  1. Opioid use at this level for chronic non-surgical low back pain is not supported by current evidence-based guidelines\n  2. Recommend structured opioid taper with the treating physician\n  3. Transition to non-opioid pain management (gabapentin for any neuropathic component, duloxetine, physical therapy, cognitive behavioral therapy for pain)\n  4. Screen for opioid use disorder given prolonged use\n  5. Note: The examiner does NOT manage medications in an IME \u2014 recommendations are made to the treating physician\n\n**Additional Recommendations**:\n- Mental health evaluation and treatment for depression and anxiety\n- Weight management program (BMI 34 contributes to back pain)\n- Independent exercise program emphasizing core stabilization and aerobic conditioning\n- Consider CBT for pain-related catastrophizing and fear-avoidance behavior")
B.b27=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE",'You are preparing to write the IME report. The insurance adjuster calls and says: "We really need you to state clearly that this guy is faking. The surveillance video proves it."')
B.b03=new A.c("+ Discuss the ethical and legal obligations of the IME physician. How do you maintain objectivity while writing a defensible report?",'**Ethical and Legal Obligations of the IME Physician**:\n\n1. **No physician-patient relationship**: The IME does NOT create a treating physician-patient relationship. The examiner\'s obligation is to provide an **objective, evidence-based medical opinion** \u2014 not to advocate for the examinee OR the insurer.\n\n2. **Objectivity is paramount**: You are NOT the insurance company\'s doctor, despite being retained by them. You must resist pressure from either side. The phone call from the adjuster requesting that you "state clearly that this guy is faking" is inappropriate and must be handled firmly.\n\n**Response to the adjuster**: \'I appreciate you sharing the surveillance information, which I have reviewed and will reference in my report. However, my role as an IME physician is to provide an independent, objective medical opinion based on all available evidence. I will document the inconsistencies I observed and let the medical findings speak for themselves. I cannot and will not advocate for a particular outcome. If you have specific medical questions you would like me to address, please submit them in writing.\'\n\n3. **Report Writing Principles**:\n- **Document observations objectively**: "On supine SLR testing, the examinee reported severe pain at 30 degrees. During seated examination, the knee was passively extended to 80 degrees without pain complaint, representing a positive distraction test."\n- **Avoid conclusory language**: Do NOT write "the examinee is malingering" or "faking." Instead: "The examination demonstrated significant inconsistency between observed functional capacity and reported limitations, with 5 of 5 Waddell categories positive for non-organic signs."\n- **Reference surveillance objectively**: "Review of surveillance video dated [date] demonstrates the examinee performing activities including lifting objects estimated at 15-20 lbs and operating a push lawn mower for approximately 30 minutes, which is inconsistent with his reported inability to lift more than 5 lbs or stand for more than 5 minutes."\n- **Separate opinions from facts**: Clearly delineate what is objective finding versus clinical opinion.\n- **Answer the specific questions asked**: MMI determination, impairment rating, causation, work capacity.\n\n4. **Obligations to the Examinee**:\n- Inform the examinee at the start of the exam that no physician-patient relationship exists and that the findings will be reported to the requesting party\n- Conduct a thorough, respectful examination\n- If a potentially dangerous medical condition is discovered during the exam, you have an ethical duty to inform the examinee (e.g., if you found signs of cauda equina syndrome)\n\n5. **Record Keeping**: Maintain your own copy of the report and all reviewed records. IME reports are discoverable in litigation and may be subject to cross-examination. Every statement must be defensible.')
B.aQW=new A.c(u.j,"During the examination, the examinee becomes visibly agitated. He stands up, points at you, and says: 'You work for the insurance company, not me. You're just trying to cut off my benefits. My lawyer told me doctors like you are paid to deny claims.'")
B.aRg=new A.c("+ How would you de-escalate this confrontation while maintaining professional boundaries and completing the examination?","**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Stay calm and do not become defensive**:\nMaintain an even tone, neutral body language. Do not cross arms or stand up. Remain seated if possible to reduce perceived threat.\n\n**2. Acknowledge his frustration without agreeing with the accusation**:\n'I can hear that you are frustrated, and I understand this process is stressful. You have been dealing with pain and disability for 18 months, and I recognize that having your condition evaluated by a doctor you did not choose can feel adversarial. I appreciate you telling me how you feel.'\n\n**3. Clarify your role directly and honestly**:\n'I want to be clear about my role. I was asked to perform this evaluation to provide an independent medical opinion. I do not work for the insurance company, and I do not work for you. My job is to conduct a thorough, fair, and objective medical evaluation. I do not make decisions about your benefits \u2014 that is done by the claims adjuster and, if necessary, by a judge. My opinion is based solely on the medical evidence.'\n\n**4. Address the \"paid to deny\" accusation**:\n'I am compensated for my time conducting the examination, just as I am compensated when I see my own patients. My fee is the same regardless of what my conclusions are. I have no financial incentive to reach any particular conclusion, and I take that independence very seriously.'\n\n**5. Offer to continue with transparency**:\n'I want this evaluation to be as fair and thorough as possible. If there are things you want to make sure I know about \u2014 symptoms, limitations, how your pain affects your daily life \u2014 I want to hear them. My report will reflect everything I find, including the things that support your claim. If you would like to continue, I will make sure the exam is conducted respectfully. If at any point you want to stop, that is your right, though I will need to note in my report that the exam was incomplete.'\n\n**6. Set a boundary if aggression escalates**:\nIf the examinee becomes threatening or physically aggressive: 'I understand you are upset, but I need to ask that we keep this professional. If you are unable to continue calmly, we will need to reschedule or terminate the exam. I do not want to do that, but safety comes first for both of us.'\n\n**7. Document the interaction**: Note the confrontation, your response, and whether the exam was completed in your report. This is relevant to the behavioral observation section.\n\n**Key principles**: De-escalation techniques (calm tone, validation, seated position), role clarification, transparency about compensation and independence, maintaining professional boundaries, offering patient choice and control, documenting behavioral observations objectively.")
B.aFh=s([B.b_E,B.aTp,B.aZK,B.b0E,B.aOR,B.aW2,B.aQ1,B.aQJ,B.b27,B.b03,B.aQW,B.aRg],t.E)
B.PV=new A.a5("special-independent-medical-examination","Independent Medical Examination \u2014 Low Back Pain",B.aFh,!1)
B.aQH=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 48-year-old female is referred to the PM&R chronic pain clinic by her primary care physician for opioid management. She has been on chronic opioid therapy for 5 years following two lumbar surgeries (L4-5 laminectomy, then L4-5 fusion) for degenerative disc disease and spinal stenosis. She is currently prescribed sustained-release morphine 60 mg twice daily plus immediate-release morphine 15 mg up to 4 times daily as needed (total daily morphine equivalent of 120-180 mg/day). She reports her pain has actually worsened over the past year despite dose increases, she has developed significant fatigue and constipation, and she would like to reduce her opioid medications. However, she is terrified of the tapering process and has tried to reduce on her own twice, experiencing severe withdrawal symptoms both times.")
B.aO3=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **Pain History**: Chronic low back pain x 8 years; s/p L4-5 laminectomy (6 years ago, incomplete relief), s/p L4-5 posterior fusion (5 years ago, moderate improvement initially); pain described as constant aching in low back (5/10) with intermittent sharp radiating left leg pain (7/10 at worst)\n- **Opioid History**: Started on hydrocodone/acetaminophen after first surgery; escalated to morphine ER after fusion; current regimen for 3 years with two dose increases in the past year\n- **Current Daily Opioid Dose**: Morphine ER 60 mg BID + morphine IR 15 mg QID PRN (typical use is 3-4 doses PRN) = 120-180 mg morphine equivalents/day (MED)\n- **Prior Taper Attempts**: Two self-directed attempts (reduced by 50% abruptly) \u2014 both resulted in severe withdrawal (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, severe pain flare, insomnia, anxiety) and resumption of full dose within 3 days\n- **Opioid Side Effects**: Chronic constipation (requires daily polyethylene glycol and bisacodyl), fatigue/sedation, decreased libido, weight gain (15 lbs over 5 years), poor concentration\n- **Comorbid Conditions**: Generalized anxiety disorder (on buspirone 10 mg BID), insomnia (on trazodone 50 mg), obesity (BMI 32), osteopenia\n- **Psychosocial**: Divorced, lives alone, works part-time as a receptionist, limited social support; no history of substance use disorder; no tobacco; social alcohol (1-2 drinks/month since starting opioids)\n- **Prior Non-Opioid Treatments**: Has NOT tried: duloxetine, gabapentin, CBT for pain, physical therapy in the past 3 years, interventional procedures since surgery")
B.aSP=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 138/84 mmHg, HR 76 bpm, RR 14, SpO2 97% on RA, Temp 36.8C, BMI 32.\n\n**General**: Overweight female appearing her stated age. Moves slowly and cautiously but without focal distress.\n\n**Lumbar Spine**:\n- Surgical scar midline lumbar, well-healed\n- Forward flexion to 40 degrees (limited by stiffness, no radicular complaints)\n- Extension to 10 degrees with pain\n- Paraspinal tenderness bilaterally, non-specific\n- No step-off or instability palpated\n\n**Neurologic Exam**:\n- Motor: 5/5 throughout bilateral lower extremities. No focal weakness.\n- Sensation: Slightly diminished pin prick in left L5 dermatome (dorsal foot). Otherwise intact.\n- Reflexes: 2+ bilateral knees, 1+ bilateral ankles. Plantars flexor.\n- Straight leg raise: Negative bilaterally for radicular symptoms. Hamstring tightness at 60 degrees bilaterally.\n- Gait: Mildly antalgic, no assistive device. Able to heel and toe walk.\n\n**Opioid Risk Assessment**:\n- **ORT (Opioid Risk Tool)**: Score 4 (moderate risk)\n- **Current Opioid Misuse Measure (COMM)**: Score 8 (below threshold of 9 for aberrant behavior \u2014 low concern)\n- **PHQ-9**: 11 (moderate depression)\n- **GAD-7**: 12 (moderate anxiety)\n- **PEG Scale** (Pain, Enjoyment, General Activity): Pain 6/10, Enjoyment interference 7/10, General activity interference 7/10\n\n**Screening Labs**: TSH normal, testosterone low-normal (possible opioid-induced endocrinopathy), HbA1c 5.8% (prediabetes), vitamin D 22 ng/mL (insufficient).")
B.aRG=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","This patient presents with chronic opioid therapy at high dose with worsening pain \u2014 a pattern concerning for opioid-induced hyperalgesia \u2014 and multiple opioid-related side effects.")
B.aOL=new A.c("+ Explain opioid-induced hyperalgesia (OIH). How do you distinguish it from tolerance? Interpret the opioid risk screening tools and urine drug screen.","**Opioid-Induced Hyperalgesia (OIH)**:\n- **Definition**: A paradoxical state in which chronic opioid use leads to **increased pain sensitivity** \u2014 the opioids are actually making the pain worse.\n- **Mechanism**: Involves central sensitization via NMDA receptor activation, enhanced descending facilitation, and neuroplastic changes in pain-processing pathways.\n- **Clinical Features**: Diffuse pain that is MORE widespread than the original pain pattern, pain that worsens with dose increases (opposite of what tolerance would predict), allodynia (pain from normally non-painful stimuli), hyperalgesia to experimental pain stimuli.\n\n**OIH vs. Tolerance**:\n| **Feature** | **Tolerance** | **OIH** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Pain character | Same pain, same location | Pain MORE widespread, may be different quality |\n| Response to dose increase | Pain temporarily improves | Pain worsens or no improvement |\n| Response to dose decrease | Pain worsens | Pain may actually IMPROVE |\n| Mechanism | Receptor downregulation | Central sensitization |\n| Treatment | Dose increase (short-term) or rotation | Dose reduction, NMDA antagonists, opioid rotation |\n\n**This patient likely has a component of OIH**: Her pain has worsened over the past year despite dose increases, and her pain pattern is more diffuse than expected for her surgical anatomy.\n\n**Opioid Risk Screening Interpretation**:\n- **ORT Score 4 (Moderate Risk)**: Risk factors include female gender, age 45-65, history of depression/anxiety. Moderate risk warrants structured monitoring, not necessarily contraindication to opioids.\n- **COMM Score 8 (Below threshold)**: Current behavior does not suggest active opioid misuse. She takes medications as prescribed, no early refills, no lost prescriptions.\n- **SOAPP-R (Screener and Opioid Assessment for Patients with Pain)**: An alternative screening tool \u2014 14-item questionnaire, score \u226518 suggests higher risk for aberrant behavior.\n\n**Urine Drug Screen (UDS) Interpretation**:\n- Order a **comprehensive UDS with confirmation testing** (immunoassay alone has limitations)\n- Expect: Positive for morphine (prescribed). May be positive for hydromorphone (minor metabolite of morphine \u2014 does NOT indicate hydromorphone use).\n- Assess for: Presence of non-prescribed substances (benzodiazepines, illicit drugs), absence of prescribed medication (suggests diversion)\n- **Important**: Natural opioids (morphine, codeine) and synthetic opioids (fentanyl, methadone) require DIFFERENT assay panels. Standard immunoassay may miss fentanyl and methadone.\n- Consider checking the **Prescription Drug Monitoring Program (PDMP)** for multiple prescribers or pharmacies.")
B.aTz=new A.c("+ What adjuvant medications and non-pharmacologic treatments should be initiated BEFORE or DURING the taper?",'**Pre-Taper Optimization Strategy** \u2014 "Build the floor before removing the ceiling":\n\nThe key principle is to establish effective non-opioid pain management BEFORE beginning the taper, so the patient has alternative tools when opioid doses are reduced.\n\n**Pharmacologic Adjuvants**:\n1. **Duloxetine 60 mg daily**: SNRI with FDA approval for chronic musculoskeletal pain AND comorbid depression/anxiety. Start at 30 mg x 1 week, then increase. Dual benefit \u2014 addresses her PHQ-9 of 11 and GAD-7 of 12 simultaneously.\n2. **Gabapentin 300 mg TID** (titrate to 600 mg TID): For neuropathic component (left L5 radiculopathy). Start low, titrate slowly. Caution: may cause sedation/dizziness \u2014 coordinate timing with opioid taper.\n3. **Acetaminophen 650 mg TID**: Safe, effective baseline analgesic often overlooked in patients on opioids.\n4. **Topical NSAIDs or lidocaine patches**: For localized back pain without systemic side effects.\n5. **Muscle relaxant optimization**: Replace cyclobenzaprine (anticholinergic, sedating, contributes to fatigue) with tizanidine 4 mg at bedtime or baclofen if muscle spasm component.\n\n**Non-Pharmacologic Interventions**:\n6. **Cognitive Behavioral Therapy for Chronic Pain (CBT-CP)**: Gold standard psychological intervention. Addresses catastrophizing, fear-avoidance, and develops active coping strategies. Weekly sessions for 8-12 weeks. Critical for taper success.\n7. **Physical Therapy**: Reactivation program focusing on core stabilization, graduated aerobic exercise, flexibility. Address fear-avoidance of movement.\n8. **Mindfulness-Based Stress Reduction (MBSR)**: 8-week program shown to reduce pain intensity and improve function in chronic pain. Complements CBT.\n9. **Yoga/Tai Chi**: Evidence-based for chronic low back pain. Improves function, reduces pain, promotes self-efficacy.\n10. **Interventional procedures**: Consider medial branch blocks/radiofrequency ablation for facet-mediated pain, or updated epidural injection if radicular component is significant.\n\n**Address Contributing Factors**:\n11. **Sleep optimization**: Improve trazodone dose or switch to low-dose amitriptyline 10-25 mg (also helps neuropathic pain). Address sleep hygiene.\n12. **Vitamin D supplementation**: 2000-4000 IU daily (level 22 \u2014 insufficiency contributes to pain).\n13. **Endocrinology referral**: Low-normal testosterone may represent opioid-induced endocrinopathy. Monitor for improvement with taper.\n14. **Weight management**: Dietitian referral; exercise program. Excess weight worsens back pain.\n\n**Timeline**: Initiate adjuvant treatments and allow 4-6 weeks for therapeutic effect before beginning opioid taper.')
B.aTk=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","Adjuvant medications have been initiated and the patient is engaged in CBT and physical therapy. After 6 weeks, she reports some improvement in function and is ready to begin a structured opioid taper.")
B.b3w=new A.c("+ Design a structured opioid taper protocol for this patient. What withdrawal symptoms should be anticipated and how are they managed?","**Structured Opioid Taper Protocol**:\n\n**General Principles**:\n- **Slow and steady**: For patients on long-term opioids (>1 year), taper by **5-10% of the total daily dose every 2-4 weeks**\n- **Patient-centered**: Adjust pace based on tolerance. This is NOT a race.\n- Her prior failures were from **50% abrupt reductions** \u2014 far too aggressive\n- At 120 mg MED, a 10% reduction = 12 mg decrease every 2-4 weeks\n\n**Sample Taper Schedule** (starting from morphine ER 60 mg BID + IR 15 mg TID PRN = ~165 mg/day):\n\n**Phase 1 (Weeks 1-8): Eliminate PRN doses**\n- Week 1-2: Reduce IR morphine from 15 mg QID PRN to 15 mg TID\n- Week 3-4: Reduce to 15 mg BID\n- Week 5-6: Reduce to 15 mg daily\n- Week 7-8: Discontinue IR morphine entirely\n- Now at 120 mg/day (morphine ER 60 mg BID only)\n\n**Phase 2 (Weeks 9-24+): Taper ER formulation**\n- Reduce by 10-15 mg every 2-4 weeks:\n- Weeks 9-10: Morphine ER 60 mg AM + 45 mg PM (105 mg)\n- Weeks 11-12: 45 mg BID (90 mg)\n- Weeks 13-14: 45 mg AM + 30 mg PM (75 mg)\n- Weeks 15-16: 30 mg BID (60 mg)\n- Weeks 17-18: 30 mg AM + 15 mg PM (45 mg)\n- Weeks 19-20: 15 mg BID (30 mg)\n- Weeks 21-22: 15 mg daily (15 mg)\n- Weeks 23-24: 15 mg every other day, then discontinue\n\n**Total taper duration: approximately 6 months** (appropriate for 5 years of use)\n\n**Withdrawal Symptom Management**:\n| **Symptom** | **Management** |\n|---|---|\n| Anxiety, irritability | Hydroxyzine 25 mg PRN; optimize buspirone/duloxetine |\n| Insomnia | Trazodone dose increase; melatonin 3-5 mg; sleep hygiene |\n| GI symptoms (nausea, diarrhea, cramping) | Loperamide, ondansetron, dicyclomine |\n| Myalgias, arthralgias | NSAIDs (ibuprofen 400 mg TID), warm baths, gentle stretching |\n| Diaphoresis, rhinorrhea | Clonidine 0.1 mg BID (also helps anxiety and autonomic symptoms) |\n| Pain flare | Reassurance that temporary flare is expected; non-opioid analgesics; do NOT increase opioid dose |\n\n**Monitoring During Taper**:\n- Clinic visits every 2-4 weeks during active taper\n- PEG scale at each visit to track pain and function\n- PHQ-9 and GAD-7 monthly (monitor for worsening depression/anxiety)\n- UDS periodically to confirm adherence\n- PDMP check at each visit\n- Celebrate milestones \u2014 positive reinforcement of progress")
B.aNZ=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","As part of the taper program, you need to ensure appropriate safety measures and coordinate with other providers.")
B.b3D=new A.c("+ Discuss the role of the Prescription Drug Monitoring Program (PDMP), naloxone co-prescription, and criteria for addiction medicine referral.","**Prescription Drug Monitoring Program (PDMP)**:\n1. **Mandatory checking**: Most states now require PDMP review before prescribing opioids and at regular intervals during treatment (typically every 1-3 months).\n2. **What to look for**: Multiple prescribers, multiple pharmacies, overlapping opioid prescriptions, concurrent benzodiazepine prescriptions, escalating quantities.\n3. **This patient**: PDMP review should confirm single prescriber, single pharmacy, consistent fill patterns. Any anomalies warrant direct conversation with the patient.\n4. **Documentation**: Note PDMP review in each clinic visit note.\n\n**Naloxone Co-Prescription**:\n- **CDC recommends naloxone** for all patients receiving \u226550 MME/day (this patient is at 120+ MME/day \u2014 well above threshold)\n- Prescribe **naloxone 4 mg intranasal (Narcan)** or 0.4 mg auto-injector (Evzio) to keep at home\n- **Educate the patient AND a household contact** on signs of overdose (unresponsive, slow/absent breathing, cyanosis) and naloxone administration\n- Naloxone co-prescription does NOT imply addiction \u2014 frame as safety measure analogous to an EpiPen for allergies\n- During the taper, overdose risk is actually INCREASED if the patient relapses to her previous dose after a period of reduced tolerance\n\n**Addiction Medicine Referral Criteria**:\nRefer to addiction medicine or a substance use disorder specialist if:\n1. **Suspected opioid use disorder (OUD)**: Using DSM-5 criteria \u2014 look for loss of control (taking more than prescribed), craving, failure to fulfill obligations, continued use despite harm, withdrawal avoidance behavior\n2. **Failed structured taper**: If the patient is unable to reduce despite adequate support, non-opioid optimization, and behavioral treatment\n3. **Concurrent substance use**: Discovery of non-prescribed substances on UDS, alcohol escalation, illicit drug use\n4. **Medication-assisted treatment (MAT) consideration**: Buprenorphine/naloxone (Suboxone) may be appropriate if OUD is diagnosed \u2014 provides partial opioid agonism for pain AND treats addiction simultaneously\n5. **History of overdose** or escalating dangerous behavior\n\n**This patient currently does NOT meet criteria for addiction medicine referral**: COMM score below threshold, no aberrant behaviors, motivated for taper. However, maintain vigilance throughout the process.\n\n**Coordination with Other Providers**:\n- PCP: Inform of taper plan; ensure no duplicate opioid prescriptions\n- Pharmacist: Single designated pharmacy; pharmacist can assist with monitoring\n- Mental health: CBT therapist and psychiatrist should be aware of taper timeline to provide concurrent support\n- Emergency department: Provide the patient with a letter outlining her taper plan to prevent ER opioid prescriptions for pain flares")
B.aPT=new A.c(u.j,"At week 10 of the taper (now at 90 mg MED, reduced from 165 mg), the patient calls the clinic in distress. Her pain has increased, she slept only 3 hours last night, and she is panicking: 'I can't do this. The pain is unbearable. I need to go back to my old dose. I can't function without it. I should never have started this taper.'")
B.aZy=new A.c("+ How would you manage this crisis and support the patient through a difficult phase of the taper?","**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Acknowledge the distress and take it seriously**:\n'I can hear how much you are struggling right now, and I take that very seriously. You are not being dramatic, and this is not in your head. What you are experiencing \u2014 the increased pain, the poor sleep \u2014 these are real physiological responses to the dose change, and they are temporary.'\n\n**2. Normalize the experience**:\n'Almost every patient who goes through an opioid taper hits a rough patch somewhere in the process. This does not mean the taper has failed. It means your body is adjusting, and this phase will pass. The fact that you have already reduced from 165 mg to 90 mg is remarkable progress \u2014 you have cut your dose nearly in half, and you were still functioning well until this most recent step.'\n\n**3. Provide a concrete plan (not just reassurance)**:\n'Here is what I would like to do right now. First, we are going to pause the taper at your current dose. We will NOT go back up, but we will hold steady at 90 mg until your body has had a chance to equilibrate. Sometimes we need to sit at a dose for 4-6 weeks instead of 2 weeks before the next reduction. This is not a failure \u2014 this is how individualized tapering works.'\n\n**4. Address the specific symptoms**:\n'For the sleep, I am going to increase your trazodone to 100 mg at bedtime tonight and add melatonin 5 mg. For the pain flare, let us add ibuprofen 400 mg three times daily with food for the next 1-2 weeks, and I want you to do the relaxation exercise from your CBT homework before bed. For the anxiety component, we can add clonidine 0.1 mg at bedtime, which will help with both sleep and some of the physical withdrawal symptoms.'\n\n**5. Reframe the fear of going backward**:\n'You said you want to go back to your old dose. I understand the temptation, but I want to remind you of something: at your old dose, your pain was a 6 and getting worse. You had constipation, fatigue, low energy, and your pain was not well controlled. The old dose was not working \u2014 that is why we started this process. Going back would mean returning to all of those problems.'\n\n**6. Reinforce accomplishment and commitment**:\n'You have already done the hardest part. Cutting from 165 to 90 mg is a 45% reduction, and you did that. Your body is stronger than you think. I am not going anywhere \u2014 we will get through this difficult phase together. Can we schedule a follow-up visit in 3 days so I can check in with you in person?'\n\n**7. Safety check**:\n'One more thing \u2014 I always want to ask: are you having any thoughts of hurting yourself or feeling hopeless? It is important that I know, and there is no judgment if you are.'\n\n**Key principles**: Take the crisis seriously, normalize the experience, provide immediate symptom management, pause (do not reverse) the taper, reframe the desire to go back, reinforce progress, schedule close follow-up, safety screening for suicidality.")
B.aDj=s([B.aQH,B.aO3,B.aSP,B.aRG,B.aOL,B.aTz,B.aTk,B.b3w,B.aNZ,B.b3D,B.aPT,B.aZy],t.E)
B.Ql=new A.a5("special-chronic-opioid-taper","Chronic Opioid Tapering \u2014 Complex Pain Management",B.aDj,!1)
B.b1G=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 35-year-old male with C5 ASIA C incomplete spinal cord injury sustained 3 years ago in an all-terrain vehicle accident presents to the spasticity clinic with severe lower extremity spasticity that is interfering with his function, positioning, and quality of life. He has bilateral lower extremity spasticity that is most severe in the hip adductors and knee extensors. He is unable to perform self-catheterization due to adductor spasm, his wife cannot provide perineal care, and he is developing a right hip adductor contracture. He has tried oral baclofen up to 80 mg daily, tizanidine 24 mg daily, and diazepam 5 mg TID without adequate relief. The high doses of oral medications cause excessive drowsiness that impairs his participation in therapy and his ability to work as a call center representative from home.")
B.aTb=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","- **SCI Level/Classification**: C5 ASIA C (incomplete) x 3 years; motor incomplete with some voluntary movement in bilateral lower extremities (hip flexion 2/5, knee extension 3/5, ankle dorsiflexion 1/5)\n- **Spasticity Distribution**: Bilateral lower extremities >> upper extremities; worst in hip adductors, knee extensors, and ankle plantarflexors\n- **Current Anti-Spasticity Medications**: Baclofen 80 mg/day (20 mg QID), tizanidine 8 mg TID, diazepam 5 mg TID \u2014 all at maximum tolerated doses with inadequate spasticity control and dose-limiting sedation\n- **Functional Impact of Spasticity**: Cannot perform intermittent catheterization independently (adductor spasm), wife unable to provide perineal hygiene, painful spasms wake him 3-4 times/night, wheelchair positioning difficult (extensor thrusting pushes him out of chair), developing right hip adductor contracture (passive abduction limited to 15 degrees)\n- **Beneficial Aspects of Spasticity**: Uses extensor tone in bilateral lower extremities to assist with standing pivot transfers (a concern if spasticity is eliminated entirely)\n- **Botulinum Toxin History**: Has received 3 rounds of botulinum toxin A (onabotulinumtoxinA) injections to bilateral hip adductors and hamstrings \u2014 provided 6-8 weeks of partial relief each time, but insufficient and requiring increasingly high doses approaching the maximum recommended dose\n- **Functional Status**: Uses power wheelchair; requires moderate assist for transfers (standing pivot using extensor tone); modified independent in upper body ADLs; dependent for lower body ADLs/catheterization due to spasticity\n- **Employment**: Works from home as a call center representative (requires alertness \u2014 sedation from oral meds impairing performance)\n- **PMH**: Neurogenic bladder (on IC program when possible, otherwise indwelling catheter), neurogenic bowel (reflexic program), history of autonomic dysreflexia")
B.aYq=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 124/78 mmHg (no AD currently), HR 72 bpm, SpO2 98% on RA.\n\n**Spasticity Assessment**:\n- **Modified Ashworth Scale (MAS)**:\n  - Hip adductors: 3 bilaterally (considerable increase in tone, passive movement difficult)\n  - Hip flexors: 2 bilaterally\n  - Knee extensors: 3 bilaterally (sudden catch with resistance through range)\n  - Ankle plantarflexors: 3 bilaterally (ankle clonus 8-10 beats sustained)\n  - Upper extremities: MAS 1+ at elbow flexors bilaterally (mild, not functionally limiting)\n\n- **Penn Spasm Frequency Scale**: 4 (spontaneous spasms occurring more than 10 times per hour)\n\n- **Spasm Severity**: Severe flexor and extensor spasms triggered by position changes, light touch to lower extremities, and spontaneously. Strong adductor scissoring with any attempt at hip abduction.\n\n**Range of Motion**:\n- Right hip abduction: 15 degrees passive (contracture developing)\n- Left hip abduction: 25 degrees passive\n- Bilateral ankle dorsiflexion: -10 degrees (plantarflexion contracture bilaterally)\n- Bilateral knee extension: 0 degrees (full)\n\n**Neurologic Exam**:\n- Upper extremities: C5 muscles 4/5, C6 muscles 4/5, C7 muscles 3/5, C8-T1 muscles 2/5\n- Lower extremities: Hip flexion 2/5, knee extension 3/5 (difficult to assess through spasticity), ankle dorsiflexion 1/5, all other muscles 0/5\n- Reflexes: 3+ bilateral knees and ankles with sustained clonus. Babinski positive bilaterally.\n- Sensation: Impaired but partially preserved light touch and pin prick below C5 (consistent with ASIA C)\n\n**Skin**: Intact. No pressure injuries.\n\n**Seating**: Reclined in power wheelchair with pelvic strap and chest harness to prevent extensor thrusting. Frequent visible spasms during examination.")
B.aQI=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","This patient has failed maximum oral anti-spasticity medications and botulinum toxin injections. Intrathecal baclofen (ITB) therapy is being considered.")
B.aTd=new A.c("+ Describe the ITB trial protocol and patient selection criteria. How do you differentiate spasticity from spasm, and why does it matter for treatment?","**Patient Selection Criteria for ITB Pump**:\n1. **Severe spasticity** of spinal or cerebral origin (Modified Ashworth Scale \u22653 in affected muscle groups) \u2014 met\n2. **Failed adequate trials** of at least 2-3 oral anti-spasticity medications at maximum tolerated doses \u2014 met (baclofen, tizanidine, diazepam)\n3. **Functional impairment** from spasticity (hygiene, positioning, mobility, pain, sleep) \u2014 clearly met\n4. **Medically stable** for surgical implantation \u2014 yes\n5. **No active infection**, especially skin infection near potential pump pocket site\n6. **Adequate body habitus** for pump implantation (may be challenging in very thin patients)\n7. **Realistic expectations** \u2014 patient and caregivers understand the device, maintenance requirements, and limitations\n8. **Access to a pump management center** for refills, dose adjustments, and emergencies\n\n**ITB Trial Protocol**:\n1. **Purpose**: Demonstrate that intrathecal baclofen is effective before committing to surgical implantation\n2. **Method**: **Lumbar puncture** (typically L3-4 or L4-5) with injection of a **bolus dose of baclofen** into the intrathecal space\n3. **Starting dose**: Typically **50 mcg** for spinal spasticity (25 mcg for cerebral spasticity)\n4. **Monitoring**: Modified Ashworth Scale assessed at baseline and every 1-2 hours for 4-8 hours post-injection. Also monitor Penn Spasm Frequency Scale, vital signs, and subjective symptom relief.\n5. **Positive trial**: \u22651 point decrease in MAS score in targeted muscle groups within 4-8 hours of injection. Peak effect at 4 hours for bolus injection.\n6. **If 50 mcg is ineffective**: Repeat trial on a separate day with **75 mcg**, then **100 mcg** if needed. If 100 mcg produces no response, the patient is likely not a candidate.\n7. **Monitoring for complications**: Respiratory depression, excessive hypotonia, hypotension, headache (post-lumbar puncture), nausea.\n\n**Spasticity vs. Spasm \u2014 Important Distinction**:\n- **Spasticity**: Velocity-dependent increase in muscle tone due to upper motor neuron lesion. Measured by MAS. Continuous phenomenon worsened by rapid passive stretch.\n- **Spasm**: Involuntary phasic muscle contractions (flexor or extensor). Measured by Penn Spasm Frequency Scale. Episodic, often triggered by stimuli (touch, position change, bladder distension).\n- **Why it matters**: ITB is highly effective for BOTH spasticity and spasms, but some treatments (e.g., botulinum toxin) primarily address spasticity/tone without significantly reducing spasm frequency. Oral baclofen and diazepam address both. ITB allows more targeted dosing to treat both components with less systemic sedation.\n- **Document both separately**: Before and after treatment, track MAS (spasticity) AND Penn Spasm Frequency Scale (spasms) independently.")
B.aX6=new A.c("+ The ITB trial shows a dramatic response \u2014 MAS decreases from 3 to 1 in all tested muscle groups within 3 hours. The patient proceeds with pump implantation. Describe the pump system and the dose titration process.","**ITB Pump System Components**:\n1. **Pump**: Programmable, battery-powered pump implanted subcutaneously in the **anterior abdominal wall** (usually left lower quadrant). Contains a drug reservoir (typically 20-40 mL capacity). Battery life approximately **7 years** (newer models may be longer). Common models: Medtronic SynchroMed II.\n2. **Catheter**: Silicone catheter tunneled subcutaneously from the pump to the **lumbar intrathecal space** (typically entering at L3-4). Tip positioned at the **thoracolumbar junction (T10-L1)** for lower extremity spasticity targeting.\n3. **Programmer**: External device used by the clinician to adjust dose, delivery mode, and refill schedule wirelessly through the skin.\n\n**Dose Titration Process**:\n1. **Starting dose**: Typically **twice the effective trial dose** divided over 24 hours as a continuous infusion. If 50 mcg bolus was effective, start at approximately **100 mcg/day continuous infusion**.\n2. **Titration schedule**: Increase by **10-15% every 24-48 hours** during inpatient stay (first 3-5 days). Outpatient adjustments at clinic visits every 2-4 weeks thereafter.\n3. **Dose range**: Most spinal spasticity patients stabilize at **200-800 mcg/day**. Cerebral spasticity may require lower doses.\n4. **Delivery modes**:\n- **Simple continuous**: Same rate 24 hours/day\n- **Flex dosing**: Different rates programmed for different times of day (e.g., lower dose during the day to preserve some tone for transfers, higher dose at night for sleep and positioning)\n- **Bolus dosing**: Patient-activated boluses for breakthrough spasms (if enabled)\n\n**For this patient**: Consider **flex dosing** \u2014 lower daytime dose to preserve some extensor tone for standing pivot transfers, higher nighttime dose to reduce spasms that wake him.\n\n**Refill Schedule**:\n- Pump reservoir requires **percutaneous refill** through the skin into the pump's drug port\n- Refill interval depends on dose and reservoir size \u2014 typically every **1-6 months**\n- Refill performed in clinic using sterile technique with a specialized refill kit\n- **Never let the pump run empty** \u2014 abrupt baclofen withdrawal is a medical emergency\n\n**Critical Safety Information \u2014 ITB Withdrawal**:\n- **Life-threatening emergency**: Abrupt discontinuation of intrathecal baclofen (pump malfunction, catheter kink/break, missed refill) causes: rebound severe spasticity, hyperthermia, rhabdomyolysis, multi-organ failure, and death\n- Resembles neuroleptic malignant syndrome/malignant hyperthermia\n- **Emergency management**: High-dose oral baclofen (80-120 mg/day divided), IV benzodiazepines, cyproheptadine, dantrolene; ICU admission; emergency pump interrogation and refill\n- **Prevention**: Never miss a refill appointment; pump has low-reservoir alarm")
B.aV6=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","The pump has been implanted and the patient is being titrated in the inpatient rehabilitation setting. His spasticity has improved dramatically (MAS now 1-1+ throughout lower extremities), but the therapy team is concerned about his transfer ability now that his extensor tone has decreased.")
B.aXr=new A.c("+ How do you balance spasticity reduction with preservation of functional tone? Design the ongoing pump management and rehabilitation plan.","**Balancing Spasticity Reduction with Function**:\n\nThis is one of the most critical considerations in ITB therapy. Complete elimination of spasticity is NOT always the goal \u2014 some tone may be functionally beneficial.\n\n**Functional Benefits of Spasticity to Preserve**:\n- Extensor tone assists standing pivot transfers (this patient)\n- Leg stiffness may assist with standing frame use\n- Some tone helps maintain muscle bulk and venous return\n- Protects against osteoporosis to some degree\n\n**Strategy \u2014 Flex Dosing Program**:\n- **Daytime (8 AM - 10 PM)**: Lower dose \u2014 target MAS 1-1+ in hip adductors (enough reduction for catheterization and hygiene) while preserving some extensor tone for transfers. Example: 150 mcg/day rate.\n- **Nighttime (10 PM - 8 AM)**: Higher dose \u2014 target MAS 0-1 for comfort, reduced spasms, improved sleep, and positioning. Example: 250 mcg/day rate.\n- **Transition periods**: Program gradual ramp-up/ramp-down over 30-60 minutes to avoid abrupt tone changes.\n\n**Transfer Training Adaptation**:\n- Physical therapy must retrain transfers with the new baseline tone level\n- May transition from standing pivot to **sliding board transfer** if standing is no longer feasible\n- Strengthen hip extensors and knee extensors to compensate for reduced spastic support\n- Consider a **standing frame program** for weight bearing, bone health, and functional standing if transfers change\n\n**Ongoing Pump Management Plan**:\n1. **Clinic visits**: Every 4-8 weeks initially for dose optimization; every 3-6 months once stable\n2. **Refill schedule**: Based on dose and reservoir volume; set recurring appointments and ensure patient never misses\n3. **Annual assessment**: Document MAS, Penn Spasm Scale, functional status (SCIM-III), skin integrity, and patient-reported outcomes at each visit\n4. **Catheter monitoring**: Fluoroscopy or CT to assess catheter position if there is sudden change in spasticity (may indicate catheter migration, kink, or disconnect)\n5. **Battery replacement**: Surgical replacement of pump when battery approaches end-of-life (approximately 7 years); plan elective replacement surgery in advance\n\n**Rehabilitation Program**:\n- **ROM program**: Continue daily stretching to maintain gains, especially hip abduction and ankle dorsiflexion. With reduced spasticity, ROM should be easier to maintain and improve.\n- **Strengthening**: Capitalize on improved motor control \u2014 with spasticity reduced, volitional movement may be more accessible. Progressive resistance training for hip flexors and knee extensors.\n- **Bladder management**: Now that adductor spasm is controlled, retrain intermittent catheterization independence\n- **Seating**: Reassess wheelchair seating \u2014 may no longer need aggressive positioning hardware (chest harness, pelvic strap) with reduced extensor thrusting")
B.b1Z=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient is being discharged home with his new ITB pump. He asks about safety concerns, travel, and what to do in an emergency.")
B.aNk=new A.c("+ Discuss pump emergency protocols, MRI safety, and practical considerations for living with an ITB pump.","**Emergency Protocols \u2014 ITB Withdrawal is Life-Threatening**:\n\n1. **Patient/Caregiver Education Card**: Provide a wallet-sized card with:\n- Diagnosis and pump type/model\n- Current dose and refill schedule\n- Emergency contact numbers (pump management center, neurosurgeon)\n- Signs of withdrawal: rapidly increasing spasticity, fever, sweating, tachycardia, mental status changes\n- Signs of overdose: excessive drowsiness, respiratory depression, hypotension, flaccidity\n- Instructions for emergency personnel\n\n2. **Emergency Oral Baclofen Supply**: Prescribe oral baclofen to keep at home and carry when traveling. If pump malfunction is suspected, patient should take oral baclofen immediately (20-40 mg doses, escalate rapidly) and go to the emergency department.\n\n3. **Hospital Alert**: Ensure the patient's local emergency department has documentation of the ITB pump in their system. Provide a letter for the ER stating the pump type, current dose, and emergency management protocol.\n\n**MRI Safety**:\n- The **Medtronic SynchroMed II pump is MRI Conditional** under specific conditions:\n  - 1.5 Tesla MRI only (3T is generally NOT approved)\n  - Pump must be interrogated before AND after MRI\n  - Pump should be stopped (suspended) before entering the MRI scanner\n  - Specific absorption rate (SAR) limits must be followed\n  - Pump must be restarted and dose verified after MRI\n- **The patient should ALWAYS inform radiology staff about the pump before any MRI**\n- CT scans and X-rays are safe and unaffected by the pump\n\n**Travel Considerations**:\n- **Air travel**: The pump is safe for commercial air travel. Airport metal detectors and security scanners will detect the pump \u2014 carry the medical device card and physician letter.\n- **Altitude changes**: Theoretical concern for dose changes with significant altitude variation (drug delivery changes with pressure) \u2014 generally not clinically significant for commercial flight altitudes.\n- **Refill planning**: Schedule refills before travel. Identify a pump management center near the travel destination for trips lasting longer than the refill interval.\n- **International travel**: Carry a physician letter in English and the local language, current medication list, and emergency contact information.\n\n**Day-to-Day Considerations**:\n- **Pump site care**: Keep the surgical site clean and dry until fully healed. Monitor for signs of infection (redness, warmth, drainage, fever).\n- **Physical activity**: Avoid contact sports or activities with risk of abdominal trauma. Swimming, wheelchair sports (with abdominal protection), and exercise are generally safe.\n- **Pump alarm**: The pump has an audible alarm that sounds when reservoir is low or battery is depleted. Patient must respond to alarms promptly.\n- **Seatbelt**: Shoulder/lap belt may need padding over the pump site for comfort.\n- **Skin**: Pump pocket area may be visible as a mild abdominal bulge. Clothing adjustment may be needed.")
B.aOc=new A.c(u.j,"Before the ITB trial, the patient expresses significant anxiety. He says: 'I don't know about having a machine implanted inside me. What if it breaks? What if it makes things worse? I've already been through so much with this injury. I'm not sure I can handle another surgery.'")
B.aUu=new A.c("+ How would you address this patient's fears about ITB pump implantation?","**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Validate the fear and acknowledge what he has been through**:\n'Your concerns are completely reasonable, and I want you to know that hesitancy is normal. You have been through a major life-changing injury and years of managing your spasticity with medications that are not working well enough. Adding another medical procedure to that is a lot to consider. I do not take that lightly.'\n\n**2. Clarify the trial as a \"test drive\"**:\n'Here is something important that I want to emphasize: we are not asking you to commit to surgery today. The first step is a trial \u2014 a single injection in your back similar to a lumbar puncture. It wears off in a few hours. If it works and you feel significantly better, then we talk about the pump. If it does not work or you do not like how it feels, we do not proceed. You are completely in control of this decision at every step.'\n\n**3. Address the \"machine inside me\" concern directly**:\n'I understand the idea of having a device implanted feels foreign and maybe even frightening. The pump is about the size of a hockey puck and sits just under the skin on your abdomen. Most patients tell me that after the first few weeks, they forget it is there. It is not visible to others under your clothing, and it does not make noise or produce any sensation during normal operation. Many patients describe it as \"the best decision I ever made\" because of how much their quality of life improved.'\n\n**4. Address the fear of malfunction honestly**:\n'You asked what happens if it breaks. That is a fair question, and I want to be honest. Like any medical device, there are risks \u2014 the catheter can occasionally move or become blocked, and the pump will eventually need battery replacement after about 7 years. These are manageable situations that we monitor for at your regular clinic visits. If the pump ever stops working, there is an emergency protocol involving oral medication to keep you safe while we fix the problem. We will train you and your wife on exactly what to do.'\n\n**5. Reframe in terms of his current situation**:\n'Right now, the medications you are on are causing drowsiness that is affecting your work and your daily life. The spasticity is preventing you from doing your own catheterization, it is disrupting your sleep, and it is causing a contracture in your hip. The pump delivers medication directly to the spinal fluid at a tiny fraction of the oral dose \u2014 so you get better spasticity control with dramatically less sedation. For many patients, it is the difference between being alert and engaged versus spending the day in a fog.'\n\n**6. Offer connection with another pump patient**:\n'I know that hearing from me as your doctor is one thing, but hearing from someone who has actually lived with a pump is another. I have a patient who had a very similar situation to yours \u2014 he was nervous before the trial but agreed to go ahead, and he would be happy to talk with you about his experience. Would you be open to that?'\n\n**7. Give time and autonomy**:\n'There is absolutely no rush on this decision. Take the time you need to think about it, talk it over with your wife, write down your questions, and come back when you are ready. I will be here whenever you are.'\n\n**Key principles**: Validating fear, clarifying the trial as low-commitment, honest discussion of risks, reframing current suffering versus intervention risk, peer connection, patient autonomy and no time pressure.")
B.aDR=s([B.b1G,B.aTb,B.aYq,B.aQI,B.aTd,B.aX6,B.aV6,B.aXr,B.b1Z,B.aNk,B.aOc,B.aUu],t.E)
B.QY=new A.a5("special-intrathecal-baclofen-pump","Intrathecal Baclofen Pump \u2014 Spasticity Management",B.aDR,!1)
B.aPV=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 62-year-old male with a 20-year history of poorly controlled Type 2 diabetes mellitus and peripheral arterial disease is referred to the PM&R amputation rehabilitation clinic for pre-operative evaluation and rehabilitation planning. He has a non-healing right great toe ulcer that has progressed over 8 weeks to wet gangrene involving the first and second toes. Vascular surgery has determined that a right below-knee amputation (BKA) is necessary due to inadequate distal perfusion for wound healing at a more distal level. He is scheduled for surgery in 5 days. He is frightened about losing his leg and asks whether he will ever walk again. He also has significant peripheral neuropathy in the left (contralateral) foot with a prior healed neuropathic ulcer on the left fifth metatarsal head.")
B.b1T=new A.c("History & Systems Review:",'- **Diabetes History**: Type 2 DM x 20 years; HbA1c 9.2% (poorly controlled); on metformin 1000 mg BID and insulin glargine 40 units nightly + insulin lispro sliding scale; complications include peripheral neuropathy, retinopathy (s/p bilateral laser treatment), nephropathy (CKD Stage 3a, eGFR 48)\n- **Vascular History**: Peripheral arterial disease with prior right femoral-popliteal bypass graft 4 years ago (now occluded); absent right pedal pulses; left ABI 0.65 (moderate PAD); claudication in left calf at 1 block\n- **Wound History**: Right great toe ulcer x 8 weeks; failed conservative management (wound care, offloading, antibiotics for cellulitis); progressed to wet gangrene of 1st and 2nd toes with ascending cellulitis; current IV antibiotics (vancomycin + piperacillin-tazobactam)\n- **Contralateral Limb**: Left foot with dense peripheral neuropathy (absent protective sensation on monofilament testing); healed neuropathic ulcer at 5th metatarsal head (3 months ago); hammer toe deformity; calluses under 2nd and 3rd metatarsal heads\n- **Comorbidities**: Hypertension, hyperlipidemia, COPD (former 25 pack-year smoker, quit 5 years ago), obesity (BMI 33), coronary artery disease s/p 2-vessel CABG 6 years ago, depression\n- **Medications**: Metformin, insulin glargine, lispro, lisinopril, atorvastatin, aspirin, albuterol inhaler, sertraline, gabapentin 300 mg TID\n- **Functional Baseline**: Community ambulator with no assistive device but limited to 1 block by claudication; independent in ADLs; drives independently; retired postal worker; lives with wife in a ranch-style home with 3 steps to enter\n- **Psychosocial**: Married 35 years, supportive wife; two adult children; retired; limited social network; reports feeling "useless" since retiring')
B.aSs=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 148/88 mmHg, HR 78 bpm, RR 16, SpO2 94% on RA, Temp 37.6C (low-grade, likely infection-related), BMI 33, HbA1c 9.2%.\n\n**Right Lower Extremity** (Pre-amputation):\n- 1st and 2nd toes with wet gangrene \u2014 black, malodorous, with purulent drainage\n- Ascending cellulitis to mid-dorsum of foot\n- No palpable dorsalis pedis or posterior tibial pulses\n- Ankle-Brachial Index: 0.3 right (severe PAD)\n- Previous bypass graft scar on medial thigh\n\n**Left Lower Extremity** (Contralateral \u2014 CRITICAL to assess):\n- Skin: Dry, thin, shiny. Healed ulcer scar at 5th metatarsal head. Calluses under 2nd/3rd metatarsal heads. Mild tinea pedis interdigitally.\n- Nails: Thickened, dystrophic (onychomycosis)\n- Deformity: Hammer toes 2-5, mild hallux valgus\n- Vascular: Dorsalis pedis pulse faintly palpable, posterior tibial absent. ABI 0.65. Capillary refill 4 seconds.\n- Neurologic: Absent sensation to 10-g Semmes-Weinstein monofilament at 8/10 sites. Absent vibration sense at great toe. Absent ankle reflex.\n- Ulcer risk: HIGH (prior ulcer + neuropathy + PAD + deformity = Comprehensive Diabetic Foot Exam Risk Category 3)\n\n**General/Cardiopulmonary**: Mild bilateral expiratory wheezes. No JVD. No peripheral edema. Surgical scars consistent with CABG.\n\n**Nutritional Assessment**: Albumin 3.0 g/dL (low), prealbumin 14 mg/dL (low). Calorie intake estimated at 1500 kcal/day (insufficient for surgical healing).")
B.aSO=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","Vascular surgery has recommended a right BKA. The key decisions involve amputation level determination, perioperative risk assessment, and contralateral limb preservation.")
B.aO8=new A.c("+ How is the amputation level determined? What do the ankle-brachial index and transcutaneous oxygen pressure values indicate, and what is this patient's contralateral limb risk?","**Amputation Level Determination**:\n\nThe goal is to amputate at the most distal level that will still heal primarily, while providing the best functional outcome for prosthetic use.\n\n**Vascular Assessment Tools**:\n1. **Ankle-Brachial Index (ABI)**:\n- Normal: 0.9-1.3\n- Mild PAD: 0.7-0.89\n- Moderate PAD: 0.4-0.69\n- Severe PAD: <0.4\n- **Right ABI 0.3**: Severe PAD \u2014 unlikely to heal at the ankle or foot level. BKA is appropriate.\n- **Left ABI 0.65**: Moderate PAD \u2014 contralateral limb at significant risk. BKA heals reliably when ABI >0.5 at the amputation level.\n- **Caveat in diabetes**: ABI may be falsely elevated due to medial arterial calcification (Monckeberg sclerosis). Non-compressible arteries (ABI >1.3) require alternative assessment.\n\n2. **Transcutaneous Oxygen Pressure (TcPO2)**:\n- Measures local tissue oxygenation at the proposed amputation level\n- **>40 mmHg**: High likelihood of primary healing (>90%)\n- **30-40 mmHg**: Moderate healing potential\n- **<30 mmHg**: Poor healing potential \u2014 consider more proximal level\n- Should be measured at the proposed BKA level (anterior tibial, ~12-15 cm below tibial tubercle)\n\n3. **Additional Studies**:\n- **Segmental pressures**: Cuff pressures at thigh, calf, and ankle levels to identify the level of stenosis/occlusion\n- **Skin perfusion pressure (SPP)**: >30 mmHg at the proposed level predicts healing\n- **CT angiography/MR angiography**: Define vascular anatomy if revascularization is being considered\n\n**BKA Surgical Considerations**:\n- Standard BKA preserves 12-15 cm of tibia from the tibial tubercle (ideal for prosthetic fitting)\n- Posterior myocutaneous flap technique (long posterior flap) \u2014 provides well-padded, well-vascularized residual limb\n- Bone should be beveled anteriorly to prevent anterior tibial pressure on prosthetic socket\n\n**Contralateral Limb Risk \u2014 This Is a Critical Issue**:\n- **5-year contralateral amputation rate** in dysvascular amputees: **30-50%**\n- This patient's left foot has **ALL major risk factors**: neuropathy, PAD, prior ulcer, deformity, poor glycemic control\n- **Risk Category 3** per International Working Group on the Diabetic Foot: History of ulcer or amputation \u2014 highest risk category\n- Aggressive contralateral foot preservation is arguably AS important as the amputation rehabilitation itself")
B.aNA=new A.c("+ What perioperative medical optimization should occur before the BKA?",'**Pre-Operative Medical Optimization** (5 days available):\n\n1. **Infection Control**:\n- Continue IV antibiotics; obtain wound cultures to guide therapy\n- Monitor WBC, ESR, CRP trends\n- Ensure no ascending osteomyelitis above the planned amputation level (MRI or bone scan if concerned)\n\n2. **Glycemic Control**:\n- HbA1c 9.2% reflects chronic poor control; perioperative hyperglycemia increases surgical site infection risk\n- Target blood glucose 140-180 mg/dL perioperatively (avoid tight control <110 due to hypoglycemia risk)\n- May need insulin drip perioperatively; adjust basal/bolus regimen\n- Endocrinology consultation for long-term diabetes management optimization\n\n3. **Cardiac Risk Assessment**:\n- History of CABG and CAD \u2014 assess functional capacity. If <4 METs, consider cardiology clearance.\n- ECG, echocardiogram if not recent. Optimize medical therapy (continue aspirin, statin, ACE inhibitor).\n- BKA is a moderate-risk surgical procedure. Revised Cardiac Risk Index (RCRI) assessment.\n\n4. **Pulmonary Optimization**:\n- COPD with SpO2 94% at baseline \u2014 pulmonary function should be optimized pre-operatively\n- Incentive spirometry teaching pre-operatively\n- Albuterol nebulizer treatments PRN perioperatively\n- Smoking cessation confirmed (quit 5 years ago \u2014 good)\n\n5. **Nutritional Optimization**:\n- Albumin 3.0 and prealbumin 14 are both low \u2014 associated with poor wound healing\n- Start high-protein oral supplementation immediately (1.5-2.0 g/kg/day protein)\n- Caloric goal: 30-35 kcal/kg/day\n- Vitamin C 500 mg BID and zinc 220 mg daily for wound healing\n- Dietitian consultation urgently\n\n6. **Renal Considerations**:\n- CKD Stage 3a \u2014 adjust medications for renal function\n- Avoid nephrotoxic agents; ensure adequate hydration perioperatively\n- Monitor electrolytes closely given diabetes and CKD\n\n7. **Mental Health**:\n- Depression screening (he reports feeling "useless") \u2014 PHQ-9 baseline\n- Ensure sertraline dose is optimized\n- Pre-operative counseling about what to expect post-surgically\n- Consider psychology referral for pre-amputation counseling\n\n8. **Pre-Amputation Rehabilitation** (Prehabilitation):\n- Upper extremity strengthening for walker/crutch use post-operatively\n- Core strengthening\n- Single-leg balance training on the left leg (carefully, given neuropathy)\n- Wheelchair training for immediate post-operative mobility\n- Home accessibility assessment and equipment ordering (wheelchair, shower bench, grab bars)')
B.aZ4=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","The patient undergoes a successful right BKA. Postoperatively, his wound is healing well with a rigid removable dressing. He is transferred to acute inpatient rehabilitation on postoperative day 5. He is deconditioned, his blood sugars remain poorly controlled (200-350 mg/dL range), and he has phantom limb pain rated 5/10.")
B.aVd=new A.c("+ Design a comprehensive post-amputation rehabilitation plan including prosthetic candidacy assessment, diabetic foot care for the remaining limb, and phantom pain management.","**Post-Amputation Rehabilitation Plan**:\n\n**1. Residual Limb Management**:\n- Rigid removable dressing (RRD) for edema control and limb protection\n- Transition to elastic shrinker sock when wound is fully healed\n- Wound assessment daily; monitor for signs of infection or dehiscence\n- Desensitization program: tapping, massage, texture exposure progression\n- Avoid pillow under the knee (hip/knee flexion contracture prevention)\n\n**2. Prosthetic Candidacy Assessment**:\n- **K-Level Determination** (Medicare Functional Classification):\n  - K0: Does not ambulate\n  - K1: Limited household ambulator\n  - K2: Limited community ambulator\n  - K3: Full community ambulator\n  - K4: Active adult/athlete\n  - **This patient: Estimated K2** (limited community ambulator) given age, comorbidities (COPD, CAD, PAD, obesity), deconditioning, and contralateral limb disease. May progress to K2-3 with rehabilitation.\n- **Positive prognostic factors**: Pre-injury community ambulator, motivated, supportive wife, single-level home\n- **Negative prognostic factors**: Age 62, multiple comorbidities, contralateral PAD/neuropathy, poor nutritional status, obesity, deconditioning\n- **Prosthetic prescription** (for K2 dysvascular BKA):\n  - Socket: PTB (patellar tendon bearing) or TSB (total surface bearing) with gel liner\n  - Suspension: Pin/lock or suction\n  - Foot: SACH (Solid Ankle Cushion Heel) or single-axis foot \u2014 appropriate for K2 level; low maintenance, stable\n  - Consider: Dynamic response foot if patient progresses to K3\n\n**3. Therapy Program**:\n- **PT**: Bed mobility, transfer training, pre-gait activities, single-limb standing balance (with appropriate safety for neuropathic left foot), upper extremity and core strengthening, wheelchair mobility, gait training with walker when prosthesis is ready\n- **OT**: ADL training, bathroom equipment use, energy conservation, home modification planning\n- **Prosthetic gait training**: Progressive weight-bearing in parallel bars, walker, then single-point cane. Emphasis on equal step length and weight acceptance on prosthetic side.\n\n**4. Contralateral Limb (Left Foot) Preservation Program**:\n- **Daily foot inspection**: Patient and wife trained to check for redness, blisters, calluses, cracks, temperature changes daily\n- **Proper footwear**: Medicare Therapeutic Shoe Program (diabetic shoe benefit) \u2014 custom-molded shoes with accommodative inserts to redistribute pressure from metatarsal heads and hammer toes\n- **Offloading**: Custom insoles with metatarsal pad and total contact design\n- **Skin care**: Moisturize feet daily (not between toes), treat tinea pedis with antifungal cream, podiatry referral for nail care (onychomycosis treatment, safe nail trimming)\n- **Vascular monitoring**: ABI monitoring every 6-12 months; vascular surgery follow-up\n- **Neuropathy management**: Continue gabapentin; proper shoe fitting (no walking barefoot EVER); protective socks\n- **Weight bearing precautions**: The prosthetic side will bear significant load, but the remaining limb will also bear increased load. Monitor for stress fractures, Charcot neuroarthropathy.\n\n**5. Phantom Limb Pain Management**:\n- **Mirror therapy**: 15 minutes daily \u2014 place mirror to reflect the intact limb, creating the illusion of two limbs. Perform bilateral movements while watching the mirror. Evidence-based for phantom pain reduction.\n- **Pharmacologic**: Gabapentin (already on 300 mg TID \u2014 titrate to 600 mg TID). Add duloxetine for neuropathic pain + mood benefit. Consider pregabalin if gabapentin insufficient.\n- **TENS**: Applied to residual limb proximal to the surgical site.\n- **Graded motor imagery**: Laterality recognition \u2192 imagined movements \u2192 mirror therapy (progressive approach).\n\n**6. Glycemic Optimization**:\n- Endocrinology consultation for insulin regimen adjustment\n- Target HbA1c <7-8% (individualized for comorbidities and hypoglycemia risk)\n- Dietitian-led diabetes education and meal planning\n- Blood glucose monitoring QID with insulin adjustment protocol")
B.b2a=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","The patient is preparing for discharge. He needs a prosthesis, diabetic shoes for his remaining foot, and home modifications.")
B.aYX=new A.c("+ Describe the prosthetic prescription process, DME authorization pathway, and the Medicare Diabetic Shoe Program.","**Prosthetic Prescription Process**:\n\n1. **Physician prescription**: The physiatrist writes a detailed prosthetic prescription specifying:\n- Type of prosthesis (right BKA endoskeletal)\n- Socket type and suspension\n- Foot/ankle component\n- K-level determination with functional justification\n- Medical necessity documentation\n\n2. **Certified prosthetist (CP)**: Works with the patient to fabricate and fit the prosthesis. Must hold ABC (American Board for Certification) or BOC (Board of Certification) credentials.\n\n3. **Fitting and fabrication timeline**:\n- Diagnostic/check socket: 6-8 weeks post-amputation (once wound healed and limb volume stabilizing)\n- Preparatory prosthesis: Used for initial training while limb continues to mature\n- Definitive prosthesis: 6-12 months post-amputation when limb volume has stabilized\n- Socket adjustments and replacements as needed\n\n4. **Insurance authorization**:\n- Medicare Part B covers prosthetic devices (L-codes for billing)\n- Prior authorization required for many components\n- Detailed documentation of K-level, functional goals, and medical necessity\n- Replacement prosthesis covered when current device is worn or no longer fits (typically every 3-5 years for socket, 3 years for foot)\n\n**Medicare Therapeutic Shoe Program (Diabetic Shoe Benefit)**:\n- **Eligibility**: Patients with diabetes AND at least one of the following: peripheral neuropathy with callus formation, prior foot ulceration, prior amputation, foot deformity, poor circulation\n- **This patient qualifies** on multiple criteria\n- **Coverage**: 1 pair of custom-molded shoes + 3 pairs of custom inserts per calendar year, OR 1 pair of depth shoes + 3 pairs of inserts per year\n- **Process**:\n  1. **Certifying physician** (PCP or specialist managing diabetes) completes the Statement of Certifying Physician (Medicare form)\n  2. **Prescribing physician** (may be the same) writes the shoe prescription\n  3. **Qualified supplier** (pedorthist, orthotist, or prosthetist) fits and dispenses the shoes\n- **Important**: The certifying physician must document the qualifying condition within the 6 months preceding the shoe prescription\n\n**DME Authorization for Home Equipment**:\n- **Hospital bed**: May be needed if difficulty with transfers from standard bed height\n- **Wheelchair**: Manual wheelchair for use before prosthetic fitting and as backup\n- **Shower bench/transfer bench**: For safe bathing\n- **Grab bars**: Bathroom safety\n- **Ramp**: 3 steps at home entrance \u2014 portable ramp for wheelchair access; permanent ramp for long-term\n- **Walker**: Rolling walker for initial prosthetic ambulation training at home\n- All require physician prescription and medical necessity documentation\n- Medicare Part B covers DME at 80% after deductible (patient responsible for 20% copay)")
B.b1K=new A.c(u.j,"Two days before surgery, you visit the patient for the pre-operative rehabilitation consultation. He is sitting quietly in his bed, staring out the window. When you introduce yourself, he says with a trembling voice: 'I'm losing a part of myself. I don't know who I am without two legs. My wife says it will be okay, but she doesn't understand. Nothing is going to be okay.'")
B.aYa=new A.c("+ How would you counsel this patient who is grieving the anticipated loss of his limb?","**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Be fully present \u2014 do not rush this conversation**:\nPull up a chair. Sit at his level. Maintain eye contact. Do not check your phone or watch. Allow silence after he speaks.\n\n'I hear you. Losing a part of your body is one of the most difficult things a person can face, and what you are feeling right now \u2014 the grief, the fear, the sense of loss \u2014 all of that is completely valid. You do not have to pretend to be okay.'\n\n**2. Name the grief directly**:\n'What you are experiencing is a form of grief, and it is real. People grieve the loss of a limb in much the same way they grieve other losses \u2014 there can be denial, anger, sadness, bargaining, and eventually acceptance. These stages do not happen in order, and they are not linear. Some days will be harder than others, and that is normal.'\n\n**3. Acknowledge his identity concern**:\n'You said you do not know who you are without two legs. That tells me that your sense of self \u2014 your identity as a strong, capable man \u2014 is deeply important to you. I want you to hear this: you are not losing who you are. You are facing a change in your body, but the person you are \u2014 a husband, a father, a man who has worked hard his whole life \u2014 that does not change with this surgery.'\n\n**4. Provide honest, hopeful information**:\n'I am not going to sugarcoat this \u2014 the recovery process will be challenging, and there will be hard days. But I want to share something with you: the majority of patients who go through below-knee amputation with proper rehabilitation do walk again. You were walking before this happened, and you have the strength and determination to get back on your feet \u2014 literally. Our job is to help you get there.'\n\n**5. Address the gap with his wife**:\n'You mentioned that your wife says it will be okay but she does not understand. I suspect she is scared too and is trying to stay strong for you. One of the things that can really help is having an honest conversation together \u2014 with me, with a counselor, or just the two of you \u2014 where you both can express your fears. I find that couples who face this together, honestly, do better than those who try to protect each other from their feelings.'\n\n**6. Offer concrete emotional support**:\n'I would like to do two things before your surgery. First, I am going to have our psychologist come talk with you today \u2014 not because something is wrong with you, but because this is an incredibly stressful time and having professional support makes a real difference. Second, I know a patient who went through the same surgery about a year ago. He has volunteered to be available to talk with patients before their amputation. He has walked in your shoes \u2014 literally and figuratively. Would you be willing to speak with him?'\n\n**7. Create a bridge to the future**:\n'After the surgery, we are going to start working together right away. I will see you the day after your operation, and we will begin the process of getting you stronger and preparing you for a prosthetic leg. I want you to have something to look forward to, not just something to fear. You are not alone in this.'\n\n**Key principles**: Unhurried presence, naming grief directly, validating identity concerns, honest hope without false reassurance, addressing family dynamics, psychology referral, peer support, and creating a forward-looking narrative.")
B.aEn=s([B.aPV,B.b1T,B.aSs,B.aSO,B.aO8,B.aNA,B.aZ4,B.aVd,B.b2a,B.aYX,B.b1K,B.aYa],t.E)
B.QU=new A.a5("special-dysvascular-amputation-diabetic","Dysvascular Amputation and Diabetic Foot Care",B.aEn,!1)
B.aOu=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 20-year-old male active-duty soldier is transferred to your rehabilitation unit 6 weeks after bilateral transfemoral amputations sustained from an improvised explosive device (IED) blast during deployment. He also suffered a mild traumatic brain injury (GCS 14 at scene, brief LOC), bilateral tinnitus, and flash burns to his face and hands (healed). He underwent bilateral transfemoral amputations at a forward surgical team, with revision at a stateside military treatment facility. He is currently using a manual wheelchair and has not yet been fitted for prosthetics. He is quiet, withdrawn, and avoids eye contact.")
B.b3m=new A.c("History & Systems Review:",'- **Mechanism**: IED blast while on foot patrol. Bilateral lower extremity traumatic amputations in the field. Brief LOC (<5 minutes). No penetrating head injury.\n- **Surgical History**: Initial damage control surgery in theater. Bilateral TFA revision at Walter Reed 4 weeks ago. Right residual limb: 15 cm from greater trochanter. Left residual limb: 18 cm from greater trochanter. Both healed primarily with well-padded distal ends.\n- **TBI Screening**: Mild TBI \u2014 GCS 14, post-traumatic amnesia <24 hours. Current symptoms: intermittent headaches, difficulty concentrating, irritability. MACE score at time of injury was 22/30.\n- **Hearing**: Bilateral high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss. Constant bilateral tinnitus. Fitted with hearing aids.\n- **Pain**: Bilateral phantom limb pain \u2014 describes "crushing" sensation in both feet and "electric shocks" in left calf. Rates phantom pain 7/10. Residual limb pain bilateral 4/10 \u2014 worse with sitting.\n- **Psychological**: PHQ-9 score 18 (moderately severe depression). PCL-5 score 52 (above PTSD threshold of 31). Reports nightmares 3-4 nights/week, hypervigilance, avoids loud noises. Denies active suicidal ideation but states "I don\'t see the point anymore."\n- **Social**: Single. Was dating a woman for 2 years \u2014 she visited once in the hospital and has not returned calls since. Parents live in rural area, supportive but overwhelmed. High school graduate, enlisted at 18. No civilian work experience.\n- **Functional Status**: Wheelchair-dependent. Independent in wheelchair mobility on flat surfaces. Requires moderate assistance for transfers (bed, toilet, shower). Independent in feeding, grooming. Requires setup assistance for lower body dressing.\n- **Medications**: Gabapentin 600 mg TID, tramadol 50 mg Q6H PRN, trazodone 100 mg QHS, sertraline 50 mg daily.')
B.aXg=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Vitals**: BP 128/78 mmHg, HR 72 bpm, RR 14, SpO2 99% on RA, Temp 36.7C, BMI 26 (pre-amputation weight).\n\n**General**: Muscular young male in wheelchair. Flat affect. Makes minimal eye contact. Faded flash burn scars on bilateral hands and face.\n\n**Residual Limbs**:\n- **Right**: 15 cm from greater trochanter. Well-healed surgical incision. No skin breakdown. Mild distal edema. Shape: cylindrical, not yet conical. Soft tissue adequate for end-bearing. No neuroma tenderness on palpation. Hip ROM: flexion 110 degrees, extension -5 degrees (mild flexion contracture), abduction 35 degrees.\n- **Left**: 18 cm from greater trochanter. Well-healed. Mild adherent scar at distal end. No breakdown. Hip ROM: flexion 115 degrees, extension 0 degrees, abduction 40 degrees. Tender to deep palpation at distal-lateral aspect (possible neuroma).\n\n**Upper Extremities**: Strength 5/5 throughout. Grip strength intact. Healed superficial burn scars on dorsal hands \u2014 no functional limitation.\n\n**Neurological**: Alert and oriented x4. Cranial nerves intact except bilateral high-frequency hearing loss. Cognitive screen (MoCA): 26/30 (lost points on delayed recall and attention). No focal upper extremity deficits.\n\n**Psychological Observation**: Minimal spontaneous speech. Answers questions with short phrases. Becomes agitated when asked about the blast. Clenches jaw when phantom pain spikes.")
B.aZF=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","This patient presents with polytrauma including bilateral TFA, mild TBI, tinnitus, phantom limb pain, PTSD, and depression. You must prioritize the rehabilitation assessment.")
B.aST=new A.c("+ What is your approach to assessing prosthetic candidacy for a bilateral transfemoral amputee, and what factors determine the prosthetic prescription?","**Prosthetic Candidacy Assessment for Bilateral TFA**:\n\n1. **K-Level Classification**: This patient is a young, motivated, previously athletic individual \u2014 potential K3 (community ambulator with variable cadence) or K4 (active adult/athlete). Bilateral TFA significantly increases energy expenditure (>200% above normal), so even young patients may primarily use wheelchair for long distances.\n\n2. **Residual Limb Assessment**:\n- Right limb (15 cm): Short \u2014 limits lever arm for prosthetic control. May require ischial containment socket with higher trim lines. Adequate soft tissue padding is positive.\n- Left limb (18 cm): Better length for prosthetic control. Possible neuroma at distal-lateral aspect needs treatment before fitting (desensitization, possible injection, or surgical revision if refractory).\n- **Hip flexion contractures**: Right side has 5-degree flexion contracture \u2014 must be addressed before fitting (stretching program, prone lying). Flexion contractures in bilateral TFA prevent upright stance.\n\n3. **Prosthetic Component Selection**:\n- **Sockets**: Ischial containment (sub-ischial if limb length allows) with suction suspension or pin-lock.\n- **Knees**: **Microprocessor knees (C-Leg, Genium, or Rheo Knee)** are strongly indicated for bilateral TFA \u2014 they provide stance phase stability that mechanical knees cannot match. The stumble-recovery feature is critical for bilateral amputees who have no sound limb for balance recovery.\n- **Feet**: Dynamic response feet (e.g., Ottobock Triton) for energy return.\n- **Gait training**: Will require extensive parallel bar training, then walker, then forearm crutches. Independent ambulation without aids is unlikely for bilateral TFA but possible for short distances.\n\n4. **Energy Expenditure Considerations**:\n- Bilateral TFA walking requires >200% of normal energy expenditure.\n- Even with optimal prosthetics, wheelchair will be primary mobility for long distances and community mobility.\n- **Both wheelchair and prosthetics are medically necessary** \u2014 this is not either/or.\n\n5. **Concurrent Issues Affecting Prosthetic Rehab**:\n- Mild TBI: Attention and balance deficits may slow prosthetic training.\n- PTSD/Depression: Motivation, engagement, and pain tolerance all affected.\n- Phantom pain: Must be adequately managed \u2014 severe phantom pain interferes with prosthetic wearing.\n- Hearing loss: Affects balance (vestibular component) and communication during training.")
B.aQ6=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","You are developing a comprehensive rehabilitation plan for this patient with bilateral TFA, mild TBI, PTSD, depression, and phantom limb pain.")
B.aOx=new A.c("+ Outline a rehabilitation plan addressing all of this patient's diagnoses concurrently.",'**Integrated Rehabilitation Plan**:\n\n**1. Prosthetic Rehabilitation (Primary)**:\n- **Pre-prosthetic phase** (weeks 1-4): Residual limb shaping with elastic wraps or shrinker socks. Hip flexion contracture stretching (prone lying 30 min 2x/day). Core and upper extremity strengthening. Wheelchair skills training. Transfer training.\n- **Prosthetic fitting** (weeks 4-8): Diagnostic sockets \u2192 definitive sockets. Begin parallel bar standing and weight shifting. Progress to supervised ambulation.\n- **Advanced training** (weeks 8-16): Community ambulation. Stairs. Ramps. Falls training (how to fall safely and get up from floor with bilateral prosthetics).\n\n**2. Phantom Limb Pain**:\n- **Mirror therapy**: Not applicable for bilateral \u2014 use **virtual reality-based phantom limb therapy** or motor imagery training instead.\n- **Gabapentin**: Currently at 600 mg TID \u2014 optimize to 900-1200 mg TID if tolerated.\n- **Tramadol taper**: Transition away from opioid. Replace with duloxetine 60 mg (treats both neuropathic pain and depression \u2014 dual benefit).\n- **TENS**: Apply to residual limbs.\n- **Desensitization**: Progressive tactile stimulation of residual limbs.\n- **Left neuroma**: If confirmed, inject with corticosteroid/lidocaine. If refractory, surgical excision or targeted muscle reinnervation (TMR).\n\n**3. Mild TBI Management**:\n- Cognitive rehabilitation: Speech-language pathology for attention and memory strategies.\n- Headache management: Avoid opioids. Use acetaminophen, riboflavin, magnesium.\n- Serial cognitive monitoring (repeat MoCA at 3 and 6 months).\n\n**4. PTSD and Depression**:\n- **Psychiatry consultation**: Optimize sertraline (increase to 100-200 mg). Consider prazosin for nightmares.\n- **Evidence-based PTSD treatment**: CPT (cognitive processing therapy) or PE (prolonged exposure). Can begin during inpatient rehab.\n- **Recreation therapy**: Adaptive sports exposure \u2014 wheelchair basketball, sled hockey, handcycling. Peer mentoring from other veteran amputees.\n- **Safety monitoring**: PHQ-9 of 18 and "I don\'t see the point" \u2014 requires ongoing suicide risk assessment. No lethal means restrictions needed currently but reassess regularly.\n\n**5. Tinnitus and Hearing**:\n- Audiology follow-up for hearing aid optimization.\n- Tinnitus retraining therapy or CBT-based tinnitus management.\n- Ensure hearing aids are compatible with prosthetic training environment.')
B.aQy=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","This patient is a service member transitioning from military to VA care. He has no civilian work experience and will need long-term support.")
B.aYh=new A.c("+ Describe the systems of care available for this veteran amputee, including prosthetic funding, vocational support, and benefits.","**VA and Military Systems of Care**:\n\n1. **VA Amputation System of Care (ASoC)**:\n- Regional Amputation Centers provide comprehensive prosthetic, rehabilitation, and psychosocial services.\n- Lifetime prosthetic care \u2014 VA covers all prosthetic components, replacements, and repairs at no cost to service-connected veterans.\n- **Microprocessor knees**: VA covers these for appropriate candidates (unlike many private insurers). This patient qualifies.\n\n2. **VA Prosthetic Funding**:\n- Service-connected bilateral TFA = 100% VA disability rating.\n- All prosthetic components, wheelchair, home modifications, vehicle modifications covered.\n- Clothing allowance for prosthetic wear and tear on clothing.\n\n3. **Vocational Rehabilitation (VR&E / Chapter 31)**:\n- Provides education, training, and job placement for service-connected disabled veterans.\n- Can fund college education, vocational training, or on-the-job training.\n- This patient has no civilian work experience \u2014 VR&E is critical for his transition.\n\n4. **Adaptive Sports Programs**:\n- VA Adaptive Sports Grant Program funds local and national competitions.\n- Disabled American Veterans (DAV) and Paralyzed Veterans of America (PVA) sponsor programs.\n- Paralympic pipeline for motivated athletes.\n\n5. **Peer Support**:\n- VA Amputation Peer Support Program pairs new amputees with trained veteran peer mentors.\n- Critical for this patient given his isolation and depression.\n\n6. **Transition Support**:\n- Integrated Disability Evaluation System (IDES) for medical separation/retirement.\n- Transition Assistance Program (TAP) for civilian reintegration.\n- VA Caregiver Support Program if family members provide care.\n\n7. **Home and Vehicle Modifications**:\n- Specially Adapted Housing (SAH) grant: up to $109,986 for home modifications.\n- Special Housing Adaptation (SHA) grant: up to $44,299.\n- Automobile Adaptive Equipment grant for vehicle modifications.")
B.aVV=new A.c(u.j,"During a therapy session, the patient's phone buzzes. He looks at it, puts it down, and says flatly: \"That was my girlfriend. Ex-girlfriend. She came to the hospital once, looked at my legs \u2014 or where they used to be \u2014 and I could see it in her face. She hasn't been back. She texts sometimes, says she's sorry. I don't know what she's sorry for. For me? For herself? I'm 20 years old and I'm never going to be with anyone. Who is going to want this?\" He gestures at his residual limbs.")
B.aPg=new A.c("+ How do you address this young veteran's grief about body image, relationships, and intimacy after bilateral amputation?",'**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Stop the session. This matters more than reps.**\n"I hear you. And I want to talk about this, because what you are feeling right now is as real and as important as anything we do in therapy."\n\n**2. Validate the loss without minimizing.**\n"You are 20 years old. Six weeks ago you were a soldier, an athlete, a boyfriend. You had a body you trusted, and a future you could picture. And then in one second, all of that changed. The grief you are feeling \u2014 about your body, about your girlfriend, about your future \u2014 that is not weakness. That is a completely normal response to an extraordinary loss. Anyone in your position would feel this way."\n\n**3. Address the girlfriend situation honestly.**\n"What happened with your girlfriend is painful, and I am not going to sugarcoat it. Some relationships survive these injuries, and some do not. Her leaving says something about where she was in her life \u2014 not about your worth as a person. You are allowed to be angry about it. You are allowed to grieve that relationship. And you are allowed to feel afraid that it will happen again."\n\n**4. Challenge the belief directly but gently.**\n"You said \'who is going to want this.\' I want to push back on that, not with a motivational speech, but with facts. I have treated dozens of young amputees \u2014 bilateral amputees, veterans just like you. Many of them are in relationships right now. Some got married after their amputations. Some have children. The person who is right for you will see you \u2014 all of you \u2014 not the parts that are missing. But I understand that right now, at 6 weeks out, sitting in a wheelchair, that feels impossible to believe."\n\n**5. Acknowledge the intimacy concerns specifically.**\n"I also want you to know that physical intimacy is absolutely possible after bilateral amputation. Your body is different, but it is not broken. When you are ready \u2014 and there is no timeline for ready \u2014 we can connect you with resources that specifically address sexuality and relationships after amputation. There are peer mentors who have been through exactly this and can talk to you man-to-man about the things that feel too awkward to ask a doctor."\n\n**6. Connect to peer support.**\n"Speaking of which \u2014 there is a veteran in our peer support program who lost both legs in Afghanistan 8 years ago. He is married now, coaches his kid\'s soccer team from his chair. He has specifically offered to talk with new bilateral amputees. No pressure, but I think one conversation with him might be worth more than anything I can say. Would you be open to that?"\n\n**7. Address the bigger existential crisis.**\n"I also heard you say last week that you don\'t see the point anymore. I want to check in on that. When you say that, are you telling me you are having thoughts of hurting yourself, or are you telling me you feel lost and do not know what your life looks like now?"\n[Assess suicide risk directly based on response]\n\n**8. Offer hope grounded in reality.**\n"Your life at 25 is going to look very different from what you imagined at 18. That is true. But different does not mean less. The next year is going to be the hardest year of your life \u2014 prosthetic training, adapting, grieving. But you are 20 years old, you are strong, and you have resources that most people in this situation do not have. I am not asking you to be optimistic right now. I am asking you to stay in the fight long enough to see what comes next."\n\n**Key Principles**: Did not dismiss his grief or offer toxic positivity. Validated the relationship loss as real. Challenged hopeless thinking with concrete examples. Addressed intimacy directly without awkwardness. Offered peer mentoring as the most powerful intervention. Assessed suicide risk when the opening presented. Communicated hope without demanding positivity.')
B.aG4=s([B.aOu,B.b3m,B.aXg,B.aZF,B.aST,B.aQ6,B.aOx,B.aQy,B.aYh,B.aVV,B.aPg],t.E)
B.Qs=new A.a5("special-bilateral-transfemoral-amputation","Bilateral Transfemoral Amputation \u2014 Young Veteran",B.aG4,!1)
B.b_o=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 40-year-old male is admitted to acute inpatient rehabilitation for severe deconditioning following a complicated lumbar fusion (L4-L5, L5-S1). His surgical course was complicated by a wound infection requiring IV antibiotics and 6 weeks of bedrest. He has a documented history of opioid use disorder (OUD) that developed after his initial back injury 4 years ago, escalating from prescribed oxycodone to illicit fentanyl use. He was started on buprenorphine/naloxone (Suboxone) 16 mg/4 mg sublingual daily by an addiction medicine specialist 8 months ago and has been in sustained recovery. He has not used illicit opioids in 7 months, confirmed by serial urine drug screens. He reports persistent moderate low back pain (5/10 at rest, 7/10 with activity) despite the buprenorphine. He is motivated for rehabilitation but anxious about pain management. His wife accompanies him and expresses concern about "being around medications" in the rehab facility.')
B.aOo=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","**Past Medical History:** Chronic low back pain x 8 years (disc herniations L4-L5 and L5-S1), failed conservative management (PT, ESI x 3, NSAID therapy), lumbar fusion 3 months ago with wound infection, OUD diagnosed 3 years ago, generalized anxiety disorder, depression (in remission on sertraline).\n\n**OUD History:** Initially prescribed oxycodone 10 mg QID post-work injury. Escalated to 120 mg oxycodone daily over 2 years. Transitioned to illicit fentanyl when prescriptions were tapered. Two non-fatal overdoses (one requiring naloxone reversal by EMS). One prior residential treatment program (relapsed after 30 days). Successfully inducted on buprenorphine/naloxone 8 months ago. Stable on 16/4 mg daily. Attends weekly group counseling.\n\n**Current Medications:** Buprenorphine/naloxone 16 mg/4 mg SL daily, sertraline 100 mg daily, gabapentin 600 mg TID, acetaminophen 1000 mg TID, cyclobenzaprine 10 mg qHS, cephalexin (completing antibiotic course).\n\n**Social History:** Married, one child (age 6). Former warehouse worker \u2014 on long-term disability. Wife works as a dental hygienist. Attends NA meetings 2x/week. Has a sponsor. No current tobacco. No alcohol (avoids due to OUD). Stable housing in suburban apartment.")
B.aY2=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**General:** Thin male, appears older than stated age. Anxious but cooperative. Well-healed midline lumbar surgical scar with small area of residual erythema from wound infection (resolving).\n\n**Musculoskeletal:** Lumbar spine ROM restricted (flexion 30 degrees, extension 10 degrees). Paraspinal muscle guarding bilateral. Bilateral hip flexor tightness. Generalized muscle atrophy from prolonged bedrest.\n\n**Neurological:** Lower extremity strength 4/5 hip flexion bilateral, 4/5 knee extension bilateral, 5/5 ankle dorsiflexion bilateral. Sensation intact. Patellar reflexes 2+ bilateral, Achilles 1+ bilateral. Negative straight leg raise.\n\n**Functional Status:** Moderate assist for bed mobility, minimal assist for transfers, ambulates 50 feet with rolling walker (limited by pain and deconditioning, not neurological deficit). Independent with upper extremity ADLs. FIM score 78/126.\n\n**Behavioral Observations:** No signs of intoxication or withdrawal. Appropriate affect. Pupils 3 mm bilateral (expected on buprenorphine). No track marks. No drug-seeking behavior observed.\n\n**Vital Signs:** BP 128/78, HR 72, RR 14. No orthostatic changes.")
B.b0P=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","**Dual Diagnosis Framework:** Chronic pain post-lumbar fusion + opioid use disorder in sustained remission on medication for opioid use disorder (MOUD).\n\n**OUD Diagnosis (DSM-5 Criteria Met Historically):**\n- Tolerance, withdrawal, use in larger amounts than intended, unsuccessful efforts to cut down, craving, continued use despite physical and psychological harm, social/occupational impairment. Currently in sustained remission on agonist therapy.\n\n**Buprenorphine Pharmacology (Critical Knowledge):**\n- **Mechanism:** Partial mu-opioid agonist and kappa-opioid antagonist. Has a ceiling effect on respiratory depression (safer than full agonists) but DOES provide analgesia.\n- **Analgesic properties:** At 16 mg/day, buprenorphine provides analgesic effect equivalent to approximately 30-40 mg oral morphine equivalents. However, the analgesic duration (6-8 hours) is shorter than the OUD treatment duration (24 hours), which is why some patients on stable MOUD still report pain.\n- **Drug interactions in rehab setting:** Do NOT administer full mu-opioid agonists (morphine, oxycodone, hydromorphone) \u2014 they will be blocked by buprenorphine at the receptor and could precipitate withdrawal or be ineffective. Tramadol is contraindicated.\n- **Key principle:** Buprenorphine should NEVER be discontinued for pain management during rehab. Stopping MOUD dramatically increases relapse risk and is the single most dangerous action.\n\n**Pain Assessment Considerations:**\n- Chronic pain in OUD patients requires multidimensional assessment \u2014 pain severity, functional impact, psychological contributors (anxiety, catastrophizing), and distinction from opioid craving\n- Use validated tools: Brief Pain Inventory, PCS (Pain Catastrophizing Scale), SOAPP-R for ongoing risk assessment\n- His pain at 5-7/10 is expected post-fusion with deconditioning and is manageable with multimodal approach")
B.aYn=new A.c("+ Diagnostic question",'**Q: The patient reports his pain has increased significantly since admission (now 8/10). How do you differentiate undertreated pain from opioid craving or pseudo-addiction?**\n\n**A:** This is one of the most challenging clinical scenarios in rehabilitation medicine:\n\n**Systematic Assessment:**\n1. **Objective pain generators:** Examine for new surgical complications (wound dehiscence, hardware failure, infection recurrence). Order imaging if clinical concern. Increased activity in rehab is an expected cause of increased pain.\n2. **Buprenorphine adequacy:** Consider splitting the dose \u2014 8 mg BID instead of 16 mg daily. The analgesic effect lasts 6-8 hours while the anti-craving effect lasts 24 hours. Splitting improves around-the-clock pain coverage without increasing total dose.\n3. **Craving vs pain:** Ask directly \u2014 "Are you experiencing any urges to use?" Patients on stable MOUD appreciate direct, non-judgmental inquiry. Craving presents as restlessness, anxiety, irritability, and is relieved by addressing the craving (counseling, distraction), not by analgesics. Pain is typically localized, worsened by specific movements, and improved by rest/positioning.\n4. **Pseudo-addiction pattern:** If the patient is requesting medications early, appearing anxious about the next dose, or "clock-watching" \u2014 this may represent inadequate pain control rather than addiction behavior. The appropriate response is to optimize multimodal analgesia, NOT to restrict medications.\n5. **UDS monitoring:** Continue routine urine drug screens per facility protocol \u2014 but present this as standard care for all patients on controlled substances, not as surveillance specifically because of his OUD history.\n6. **Collaborative approach:** Contact his addiction medicine provider to discuss dosing adjustments \u2014 they should remain involved throughout the rehab stay.')
B.b2r=new A.c("+ Challenge question","**Q: The patient needs a painful procedure (trigger point injection for severe paraspinal spasm). He is anxious about pain with the procedure. How do you manage analgesia?**\n\n**A:** Pain management for procedures in patients on buprenorphine requires advance planning:\n\n1. **Do NOT stop buprenorphine for the procedure.** The outdated practice of stopping MOUD before procedures increases relapse risk and is no longer recommended by ASAM or APA.\n2. **Non-opioid first-line:** For a trigger point injection, local anesthetic (lidocaine) at the injection site is usually sufficient. Pre-treat with acetaminophen 1000 mg and consider ketorolac 15 mg IV.\n3. **Anxiolysis:** Given his anxiety, consider a one-time low-dose benzodiazepine (lorazepam 0.5 mg) 30 minutes pre-procedure, with monitoring. Use cautiously given OUD history, but a single supervised dose for procedural anxiety is appropriate.\n4. **If stronger analgesia needed (for more invasive procedures):** Short-acting, high-affinity opioids like **hydromorphone or fentanyl** can overcome buprenorphine receptor occupancy at higher-than-typical doses. This requires close monitoring and should be done in consultation with addiction medicine.\n5. **Post-procedure:** Return immediately to standard buprenorphine dosing. Do not prescribe additional opioids for post-procedure pain. Use ice, NSAIDs, and acetaminophen.\n6. **Document and communicate:** Note the plan in the chart, inform nursing and pharmacy, and discuss with the patient transparently. His anxiety about procedures likely stems from fear of relapse \u2014 reassure him that managed, supervised analgesia for a medical procedure is not relapse.")
B.aR0=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","**Multimodal Non-Opioid Pain Management:**\n- **Maintain buprenorphine** at current dose (16 mg daily) or split to 8 mg BID for improved analgesic coverage\n- **Acetaminophen** 1000 mg TID (scheduled, not PRN \u2014 ensures consistent baseline analgesia)\n- **Gabapentin** 600 mg TID (already on this \u2014 provides neuropathic and anxiolytic benefit)\n- **NSAIDs:** Consider short-course meloxicam 15 mg daily if no surgical contraindication (check with surgeon regarding fusion healing)\n- **Topical agents:** Lidocaine 5% patches to low back, diclofenac gel to paraspinals\n- **Cyclobenzaprine:** Continue 10 mg qHS for nocturnal spasm and sleep benefit\n- **Non-pharmacological (critical):**\n  - Graded therapeutic exercise (the most effective intervention for post-fusion pain)\n  - Heat/ice modalities\n  - TENS unit\n  - Cognitive behavioral therapy for pain (CBT-P) \u2014 evidence-based for chronic pain with OUD\n  - Mindfulness-based stress reduction\n  - Biofeedback for muscle tension and pain coping\n\n**Maintaining MOUD During Rehab:**\n- Buprenorphine must be administered by licensed pharmacy at the facility\n- Continue prescribed dose without interruption \u2014 document in admission orders as priority medication\n- Do NOT hold buprenorphine for procedures, fasting, or any other reason without addiction medicine consultation\n- Coordinate with outpatient prescriber for continuity\n\n**Addiction Counseling Integration:**\n- Continue weekly group counseling (telehealth if in-person meetings not available during rehab)\n- Encourage NA meeting attendance via telephone or virtual while inpatient\n- Sponsor contact should be facilitated \u2014 allow phone access for recovery support calls\n- Psychology consult for integrated pain/addiction counseling during rehab stay\n\n**Relapse Prevention During Rehab:**\n- Identify triggers: pain, boredom, isolation from recovery community, exposure to other patients' pain medications\n- Ensure roommate compatibility \u2014 avoid rooming with patients on high-dose opioid regimens if possible\n- Maintain structured daily schedule to minimize unoccupied time\n- Naloxone rescue kit should be part of discharge planning")
B.aO6=new A.c("+ Management question","**Q: On day 5 of rehab, the patient confides in his therapist that he found another patient's leftover oxycodone pills in the shared bathroom and has been thinking about them all day but has not taken them. What is your response?**\n\n**A:** This is a critical intervention moment \u2014 he disclosed a craving trigger rather than acting on it, which is a sign of recovery strength:\n\n1. **Immediate medication safety:** Remove the unsecured medications from the bathroom immediately. Report to nursing and pharmacy. This is a facility medication safety violation \u2014 all controlled substances must be accounted for and secured. Initiate a medication safety incident report.\n\n2. **Support the patient's disclosure:** \"I want to acknowledge that telling your therapist about this took courage. The fact that you are identifying triggers and reaching out instead of acting on them is exactly what recovery looks like. You did the right thing.\"\n\n3. **Clinical assessment:** Evaluate current craving intensity (0-10 scale), assess for any recent use (UDS), review buprenorphine adequacy (is the dose holding him?), and assess for emotional triggers (pain increase, homesickness, anxiety).\n\n4. **Intervention:** Increase buprenorphine dosing frequency if craving is persistent (split to TID dosing: 8 mg AM, 4 mg noon, 4 mg PM). Arrange urgent counseling session. Facilitate sponsor contact. Consider brief inpatient addiction medicine consultation.\n\n5. **System fix:** Implement locked medication storage, medication reconciliation at discharge for all patients, and bathroom checks. This is a systems issue, not just a patient issue.\n\n6. **Do NOT discharge or punish the patient.** Disclosing craving to a treatment provider is a positive recovery behavior. Punitive responses discourage future disclosure and harm the therapeutic relationship. Document as a clinical event, not a behavioral incident.")
B.aOh=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","**Regulatory Framework for Buprenorphine in Rehab (Updated 2023):**\n- **X-waiver elimination:** As of January 2023 (Consolidated Appropriations Act), the X-waiver requirement was eliminated. ANY physician with a standard DEA license can prescribe buprenorphine for OUD. The previous barrier of needing a special DATA 2000 waiver no longer exists.\n- **Practical implication:** The rehab attending can prescribe and adjust buprenorphine directly, though coordination with the outpatient addiction medicine provider is best practice.\n- **Facility requirements:** The rehab facility pharmacy must stock buprenorphine/naloxone. Some facilities may have institutional barriers \u2014 advocate for formulary inclusion if not available.\n\n**Harm Reduction Resources:**\n- Naloxone (Narcan) nasal spray prescription for discharge \u2014 available OTC in most states but ensure insurance coverage\n- Fentanyl test strips \u2014 provide information if returning to environment with potential exposure\n- Clean syringe access information (if applicable) \u2014 harm reduction does not equal endorsement of use\n\n**SAMHSA Resources:**\n- SAMHSA National Helpline: 1-800-662-4357\n- SAMHSA treatment locator (findtreatment.gov) for outpatient MOUD continuity\n- Ensure discharge plan includes confirmed appointment with addiction medicine provider within 7 days of discharge\n\n**Insurance and Coverage:**\n- Mental Health Parity and Addiction Equity Act requires insurers to cover MOUD at parity with medical/surgical benefits\n- Prior authorization for buprenorphine should not delay treatment initiation\n- If insurance barriers exist, manufacturer patient assistance programs and state-funded MOUD programs available\n\n**Rehab Facility Policy Considerations:**\n- Facility should have a MOUD policy \u2014 if none exists, advocate for creation\n- Staff education on MOUD is often inadequate and drives stigma\n- Ensure pharmacy can verify buprenorphine prescriptions and coordinate with external providers\n- Develop protocol for patients on methadone (requires coordination with opioid treatment program for daily dosing)")
B.b13=new A.c("+ Systems question",'**Q: The facility medical director says they have a policy against administering buprenorphine in the rehab unit because "it is an opioid and sends the wrong message to other patients." How do you respond?**\n\n**A:** This requires advocacy grounded in law, ethics, and evidence:\n\n1. **Legal mandate:** Under the ADA and Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act, patients on MOUD for OUD are considered individuals with a disability receiving treatment. Refusing to administer prescribed MOUD is **disability discrimination** and exposes the facility to legal liability. Multiple DOJ settlements have established this precedent.\n\n2. **Medical standard of care:** MOUD (buprenorphine, methadone, naltrexone) is the gold standard for OUD treatment, endorsed by WHO, ASAM, APA, and SAMHSA. Discontinuing MOUD increases relapse risk 3-5 fold and overdose death risk significantly. Not administering prescribed MOUD is a patient safety issue.\n\n3. **Evidence rebuttal:** "Sending the wrong message" reflects stigma, not science. Buprenorphine is FDA-approved medication treating a chronic brain disease. It is analogous to refusing insulin for diabetes because it "sends the wrong message about diet." Other patients\' perceptions are managed through confidentiality, not by withholding treatment.\n\n4. **Practical solution:** Propose a facility MOUD policy that includes staff education, standardized administration protocols, patient confidentiality protections, and coordination with addiction medicine providers. Offer to lead the educational initiative.\n\n5. **Escalation pathway:** If the medical director is intractable, escalate to facility administration, compliance/legal department, and if necessary, state medical board or advocacy organizations (Legal Action Center specializes in MOUD discrimination cases).')
B.b_k=new A.c(u.j,"**Scenario:** During nursing handoff, the night nurse says loudly enough for other staff to hear: \"I'm not comfortable giving Suboxone to that patient in Room 4. We're supposed to be helping people recover, not enabling their addiction. I didn't become a nurse to be a drug dealer.\"\n\n**This Requires Immediate, Direct Staff Education:**\n\n**Step 1 \u2014 Address privately and promptly:** Pull the nurse aside within the hour. Do not ignore the comment or delay.\n\n\"I heard what you said during handoff about Room 4's buprenorphine, and I need to discuss this with you directly because it is important for patient care.\n\nI understand that you may have personal feelings about addiction and opioid medications \u2014 many healthcare workers do, and those feelings are not uncommon. But I need to be clear about several things:\n\n1. **Buprenorphine is a prescribed medication for a diagnosed medical condition.** Opioid use disorder is a chronic brain disease with strong genetic and neurobiological components. Buprenorphine is FDA-approved treatment, not 'enabling.' It reduces mortality by over 50%. Refusing to administer it would be equivalent to refusing to give insulin to a diabetic patient.\n\n2. **Your comments at handoff were audible to other staff** and could influence how they interact with this patient. Stigmatizing language about patients with substance use disorders creates a hostile environment and can lead to undertreated pain, poor therapeutic alliance, and worse outcomes.\n\n3. **This patient disclosed his history and is actively in recovery.** He trusted us with vulnerable information. If he perceives stigma from his treatment team, he may disengage from rehab or conceal important clinical information.\n\n4. **Professionally and legally**, administering prescribed medications is within your scope and duty regardless of personal beliefs about the medication's purpose. If you have a genuine ethical objection, we can discuss accommodation, but the patient's care cannot be compromised.\"\n\n**Step 2 \u2014 Offer education:** \"I would like to arrange an in-service for the rehab nursing staff on medication for opioid use disorder. Would you be willing to attend? Understanding the neuroscience often changes perspectives.\"\n\n**Step 3 \u2014 Ensure patient care continuity:** Verify that buprenorphine was administered on time. If there is any concern about medication delays or refusal, reassign the patient to a willing nurse and address it through the chain of command.\n\n**Step 4 \u2014 Document if pattern continues:** If the nurse's behavior persists or affects patient care, involve nursing leadership and document as a professionalism concern.")
B.aYr=new A.c("+ Communication question","**Q: The patient's wife asks to speak with you privately. She says, \"I support his recovery, but I'm terrified of having any opioid medications in our house when he comes home. Can't he just stop the Suboxone before discharge?\" How do you respond?**\n\n**A:** Her fear is valid and reflects the trauma of living with someone with OUD. This requires empathetic education:\n\n\"Your concern shows how much you care about his recovery, and I want to acknowledge how difficult the past few years must have been for you. Living with a loved one's addiction is traumatic, and your fears are completely understandable.\n\nHowever, I need to share some important medical information about buprenorphine that I think will ease your concern:\n\n1. **Buprenorphine is fundamentally different from the opioids that were a problem.** It has a ceiling effect \u2014 it cannot produce the high that oxycodone or fentanyl does. At his prescribed dose, it blocks euphoria from other opioids. It is not a drug of abuse for him; it is the medication preventing relapse.\n\n2. **Stopping buprenorphine is the most dangerous thing we could do.** Studies show that people who stop MOUD have a 3 to 5 times higher risk of relapse and a dramatically higher risk of fatal overdose. His brain chemistry has been altered by years of opioid exposure, and buprenorphine normalizes it. Removing it would be like stopping blood pressure medication because someone is afraid of pills.\n\n3. **How long will he need it?** The current evidence supports long-term, potentially indefinite MOUD maintenance. There is no 'right' time to stop. His addiction medicine doctor will reassess periodically, but right now the priority is keeping him stable.\n\n4. **Safety at home:** The buprenorphine films should be stored securely \u2014 locked box, away from children. But the risk of diversion is low because he needs it for his own treatment.\n\n5. **For you:** I strongly recommend family counseling or Al-Anon/Nar-Anon meetings if you are not already attending. Your recovery and his recovery are connected but separate processes. You deserve support too.\n\nWould it be helpful to have a joint meeting with his addiction medicine doctor before discharge so all your questions can be addressed together?\"")
B.aE3=s([B.b_o,B.aOo,B.aY2,B.b0P,B.aYn,B.b2r,B.aR0,B.aO6,B.aOh,B.b13,B.b_k,B.aYr],t.E)
B.QA=new A.a5("special-oud-rehab","Chronic Pain with Opioid Use Disorder \u2014 MOUD in Rehab",B.aE3,!1)
B.aVZ=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 16-year-old male is referred to PM&R for gait abnormality and progressive left hip pain 14 months after a left femoral shaft fracture sustained in a skateboarding accident. The fracture was treated with intramedullary nailing and healed with 3 cm of shortening due to malunion. He has been compensating by toe-walking on the left and hiking his left hip during swing phase. He complains of left hip pain (4/10 with activity), low back pain that is new over the past 3 months, and fatigue with prolonged walking. He was previously very active in basketball and skateboarding but has been avoiding sports because "I look stupid when I walk." His parents are concerned about long-term consequences and want to understand all treatment options. His orthopedic surgeon has mentioned the possibility of limb lengthening surgery.')
B.aVf=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","**Past Medical History:** Left femoral shaft fracture 14 months ago (closed, midshaft, treated with antegrade IM nail). No other fractures. No metabolic bone disease. No prior orthopedic history. Childhood development normal.\n\n**Current Complaints:** Left leg feels \"too short,\" limping gait noticed by peers, left hip pain with activity (4/10), new low back pain (3/10) \u2014 worse after prolonged sitting and standing, fatigue with walking more than 15 minutes, avoids running.\n\n**Growth Status:** Tanner stage 4. Predicted adult height 178 cm (based on mid-parental height). Bone age radiograph shows approximately 1.5-2 years of remaining growth. Last growth spurt was 6 months ago.\n\n**Medications:** Ibuprofen 400 mg PRN for hip pain.\n\n**Social History:** 10th grader, previously played JV basketball (starting point guard). Has not returned to basketball this season. Friends have noticed his limp. Reports being teased by peers. Grades have dropped from A's to B-'s/C's. Spends more time alone in his room. Parents are divorced \u2014 lives primarily with mother, visits father every other weekend. Mother is a school teacher, father is an electrician. Both parents present and engaged but disagree on treatment approach (mother favors conservative, father wants surgery).")
B.aMG=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**Standing Alignment:**\n- Visible left leg shortening with compensatory left toe-walking and right knee flexion posture\n- Pelvis: left iliac crest approximately 2.5 cm lower than right by palpation\n- Mild functional lumbar scoliosis \u2014 convexity to the left, corrects with block testing\n- Block test: symptoms and alignment improve with 2.5 cm block under left foot\n\n**Limb Length Measurement:**\n- Clinical (ASIS to medial malleolus): Right 92 cm, Left 89 cm (3 cm discrepancy)\n- Scanogram radiograph confirms 3.0 cm true shortening of left femur\n- No tibial length discrepancy\n\n**Gait Analysis (Observational):**\n- Left stance phase: toe-walking, early heel-off, vaulting\n- Left swing phase: hip hiking, circumduction\n- Right stance phase: excessive knee flexion (compensatory lowering)\n- Cadence reduced, asymmetric step length\n- Trendelenburg sign positive on left (gluteus medius fatigue from altered biomechanics)\n\n**Hip Examination:** Left hip ROM full but pain with resisted abduction and terminal flexion. Right hip normal. No leg-Calv\xe9-Perthes signs. Patrick/FABER test positive left (pain in groin).\n\n**Spine:** Mild tenderness at L4-L5 paraspinals bilateral. Forward bend test shows no structural scoliosis (curve corrects). ROM full but low back extension reproduces pain.\n\n**Left Knee/IM Nail:** Well-healed surgical scars. Full ROM. No hardware prominence. Stable ligaments.\n\n**Neurological:** Intact sensation, 5/5 strength throughout (except left hip abduction 4+/5 with pain), reflexes symmetric.")
B.aTg=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","**Diagnosis:** 3 cm left femoral limb length discrepancy (LLD) due to post-traumatic malunion, with compensatory gait abnormalities, secondary hip pain, and functional lumbar scoliosis.\n\n**Radiographic Leg Length Measurement Methods:**\n- **Scanogram (teleoroentgenogram):** Gold standard \u2014 single long-cassette AP radiograph of both lower extremities with ruler. Measures true bony length from femoral head to ankle. Confirms 3.0 cm discrepancy.\n- **CT scanogram:** Most accurate method if available, lower radiation than full CT. Can identify specific location of shortening (femur vs tibia).\n- **Block test:** Clinical correlation \u2014 standing with calibrated blocks under short leg until pelvis levels. Functional LLD may differ from anatomic LLD due to joint contractures or foot deformities.\n\n**Compensatory Biomechanics:**\n- **Short leg side:** Equinus positioning (toe-walking), hip hiking (quadratus lumborum overuse), increased energy expenditure, gluteus medius overload (explains hip pain)\n- **Long leg side:** Knee flexion posture, increased ground reaction forces, predisposition to knee OA over time\n- **Spinal compensation:** Functional scoliosis with convexity toward short side, asymmetric facet loading (explains new back pain), risk of progressive structural changes if uncorrected during remaining growth\n\n**Scoliosis Risk Assessment:**\n- Current scoliosis is **functional** (corrects with leveling pelvis) \u2014 not yet structural\n- However, persistent asymmetric loading during remaining 1.5-2 years of growth could lead to structural scoliotic changes\n- Need standing full-spine AP radiograph to measure Cobb angle and monitor\n- If Cobb angle >10 degrees, more aggressive correction warranted")
B.aYz=new A.c("+ Diagnostic question","**Q: His standing spine film shows a 12-degree left lumbar scoliosis that corrects to 4 degrees with a 3 cm lift. How does this change your urgency for intervention?**\n\n**A:** This finding increases the urgency for definitive correction:\n\n1. **Significance:** A 12-degree curve is above the threshold for monitoring (>10 degrees) and the fact that it partially corrects with lift (to 4 degrees) confirms it is primarily functional but developing a **structural component** (8 degrees of functional correction but 4 degrees that does not correct suggests early structural change).\n\n2. **Growth remaining:** With 1.5-2 years of growth remaining (Tanner 4), there is both risk and opportunity. Risk: continued asymmetric loading during the growth spurt could accelerate structural scoliosis. Opportunity: correcting the LLD now while growth remains may allow the structural component to remodel.\n\n3. **Immediate action:** Implement full 3 cm shoe lift immediately (not gradually) to eliminate the biomechanical driver. This is no longer an elective decision \u2014 delaying correction risks progressive structural scoliosis.\n\n4. **Monitoring:** Repeat standing spine film in 3-6 months WITH the lift to assess curve response. If structural component progresses despite lift correction, orthopedic scoliosis consultation is needed.\n\n5. **Surgical LLD correction:** The structural scoliotic component strengthens the case for definitive limb lengthening surgery rather than lifelong shoe modification, especially given his age and remaining growth. This should be discussed with the orthopedic surgeon as a combined concern.")
B.aTV=new A.c("+ Challenge question","**Q: The orthopedic surgeon recommends limb lengthening with an external fixator (Ilizarov technique) estimating 3 months in the fixator followed by 3 months of rehabilitation. The patient asks your opinion. What is your role?**\n\n**A:** As the physiatrist, your role is to provide comprehensive rehabilitation counseling for the surgical decision, not to recommend for or against surgery:\n\n**Surgical Options Comparison:**\n- **Distraction osteogenesis (Ilizarov/Taylor Spatial Frame):** Can achieve 3 cm lengthening reliably. Requires external fixator for ~3 months (1 mm/day distraction). Complications: pin site infections (20-30%), joint stiffness, muscle contracture, refracture, nerve injury. Prolonged rehabilitation.\n- **Intramedullary lengthening nail (PRECICE/STRYDE):** Newer technology \u2014 eliminates external fixator. Magnetic-driven internal nail for gradual lengthening. Lower infection risk, better cosmesis, but higher cost and limited availability. May not be appropriate if existing IM nail is in place.\n- **Contralateral epiphysiodesis:** If significant growth remains, surgically arresting growth on the long (right) side could equalize over time. Less invasive but results in shorter overall stature and requires precise growth prediction. Timing-dependent.\n\n**Rehabilitation Perspective to Share:**\n- Post-surgical rehabilitation is intensive: ROM preservation during distraction, progressive weight-bearing, strengthening, gait retraining\n- Timeline: realistically 6-9 months from surgery to full athletic return\n- This will mean missing an entire basketball season and significant school disruption\n- Psychological readiness matters \u2014 the patient and BOTH parents need to be aligned\n\n**Conservative Alternative Review:**\n- A 3 cm shoe lift is a permanent, effective, non-surgical option\n- Modern shoe modifications can be cosmetically acceptable (internal lift + slight external build-up)\n- No surgical risks, no downtime, immediate correction\n- Limitation: requires lifelong use, limits shoe choices, may not fully address the adolescent's psychosocial concerns")
B.aSE=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","**Shoe Lift Prescription (Immediate Conservative Management):**\n- **Amount:** Full 3 cm correction \u2014 for discrepancies >2 cm in the setting of scoliosis, full correction is recommended rather than partial\n- **Design:** Combination internal heel lift (1-1.5 cm maximum inside shoe) + external sole build-up (1.5-2 cm) on left shoe. Full-length lift preferred over heel-only lift to maintain forefoot biomechanics.\n- **Footwear:** Athletic shoe with removable insole works best. Custom modification by certified pedorthist.\n- **Gradual accommodation:** Although full correction is the goal, start with 2 cm and increase to 3 cm over 2 weeks if the patient reports discomfort with immediate full correction.\n\n**Gait Training:**\n- Once lift is in place, retrain gait to eliminate compensatory patterns (hip hiking, vaulting, circumduction)\n- Treadmill gait training with mirror feedback\n- Cadence training with metronome\n- Progress from walking to jogging to running as gait normalizes\n\n**Core Stabilization Program:**\n- Address lumbar scoliosis and back pain with Schroth-based physiotherapy\n- Core strengthening: planks, dead bugs, bird dogs \u2014 emphasizing symmetric activation\n- Hip abductor strengthening: address left gluteus medius weakness contributing to Trendelenburg gait\n- Flexibility: hip flexor stretching bilateral, hamstring flexibility\n\n**Pain Management:**\n- Hip pain expected to improve significantly with lift correction (eliminates biomechanical overload)\n- Continue ibuprofen PRN short-term\n- If hip pain persists despite lift, MRI to evaluate for labral pathology from chronic compensatory mechanics\n- Back pain: core stabilization program is primary treatment; expect improvement with LLD correction\n\n**Sports Return Progression:**\n- Phase 1 (weeks 1-4): Gait normalization with lift, core strengthening, hip abductor rehab\n- Phase 2 (weeks 4-8): Progressive running program, agility drills\n- Phase 3 (weeks 8-12): Sport-specific basketball drills, cutting, jumping\n- Phase 4 (3+ months): Full return to basketball with lift in athletic shoes")
B.aMR=new A.c("+ Management question",'**Q: After 6 weeks with the shoe lift, his gait has improved but he is still not wearing it consistently, especially at school. His back pain is better on days he wears it but returns on days he does not. What do you do?**\n\n**A:** Adherence is the primary challenge in adolescent orthotic management:\n\n1. **Identify specific barriers:** Have a direct conversation WITH the patient (not just parents). Common barriers: cosmetic appearance ("it looks weird"), doesn\'t fit in preferred shoes (basketball shoes, casual shoes), peers noticed and commented, discomfort/heaviness, inconvenience of having only one modified shoe.\n\n2. **Address each barrier:**\n- **Cosmetic:** Refer to a skilled pedorthist who can modify popular brand-name athletic shoes with internal lifts that are virtually invisible. Some companies specialize in cosmetically appealing shoe modifications for teens.\n- **Multiple pairs:** Prescribe modifications for at least 3 pairs \u2014 school shoes, basketball shoes, casual shoes. Cost is justified by medical necessity.\n- **Peer concern:** Ask if a trusted friend knows. Sometimes having one friend who understands eliminates anxiety. Frame the lift as sports equipment, not a medical device \u2014 "professional athletes use custom shoe modifications all the time."\n\n3. **Motivational approach:** Link adherence to his stated goal of returning to basketball. "Every day without the lift is a day your back and hip are taking damage that will delay your return to the court."\n\n4. **Objective monitoring:** Schedule a follow-up standing spine film to show him the objective improvement with the lift. Adolescents respond to visual evidence.\n\n5. **Consider alternatives:** If he absolutely refuses external modification, discuss surgical options more seriously. A 16-year-old who will not wear a lift for 60+ more years of life may be a reasonable surgical candidate.')
B.b0q=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","**Pediatric Orthopedic Coordination:**\n- Multidisciplinary approach: physiatrist (rehabilitation, orthotic prescription, gait optimization), orthopedic surgeon (surgical options, growth monitoring), pedorthist (shoe modifications), physical therapist (gait training, strengthening)\n- Shared decision-making conference with family, including both parents given their disagreement about surgery\n- School coordination: 504 plan or IEP accommodations if needed for PE class modifications, elevator access if multi-story school\n\n**Growth Plate Considerations:**\n- **Critical timing window:** With Tanner 4 and ~1.5-2 years growth remaining, decisions must account for ongoing growth\n- **Anderson-Green growth remaining charts** or **Paley multiplier method** should be used to predict remaining growth discrepancy\n- If the 3 cm discrepancy is stable (fracture-related, not growth plate injury), it will remain 3 cm at skeletal maturity\n- If the growth plate was damaged (check with MRI), the discrepancy could INCREASE with remaining growth \u2014 serial scanograms every 6 months until maturity\n- **Epiphysiodesis timing:** If contralateral epiphysiodesis is considered, timing is critical and must be calculated based on remaining growth \u2014 too early results in overcorrection, too late results in undercorrection\n\n**Insurance and Cost:**\n- Shoe modifications: typically covered under DME benefit with prescription and medical justification letter\n- Multiple pairs: may require prior authorization documenting medical necessity for school, sports, and daily shoes\n- Limb lengthening surgery: covered if documented medical necessity (scoliosis, functional impairment, pain) \u2014 cosmetic concern alone may not meet criteria\n- Pre-authorization for surgery should include spine films showing scoliosis, gait analysis documentation, and failed conservative management\n\n**Academic Impact:**\n- School counselor notification about grades decline \u2014 may be related to pain, social withdrawal, or psychological distress\n- Consider educational evaluation if decline persists despite physical treatment\n- Sports clearance letter for basketball once gait normalized and pain controlled")
B.aUh=new A.c(u.j,'**Scenario:** During the appointment, after discussing the shoe lift prescription, the patient crosses his arms and says: "I\'m not wearing that. It looks stupid. My friends already call me \'limpy.\' I\'m not giving them more reasons to make fun of me. I\'d rather just deal with it."\n\n**Adolescent Adherence Strategies:**\n\n**Step 1 \u2014 Validate without dismissing:** "I hear you, and I\'m not going to pretend that what your friends think doesn\'t matter \u2014 it does, especially right now. Being 16 and looking different is hard. I\'m not going to force you to wear anything."\n\n**Step 2 \u2014 Redirect with honesty:** "But I want to be straight with you about what \'just dealing with it\' actually means. Right now, your spine is starting to curve because of the leg length difference. Your back hurts. Your hip hurts. And it is going to get worse, not better. That means more pain, more limping, and more reasons for people to notice something is different \u2014 not fewer."\n\n**Step 3 \u2014 Reframe the lift:** "Here is the thing \u2014 the limp is what people notice right now. A well-made shoe modification is invisible inside your shoe. Nobody can see it. But everyone can see a limp. So which actually draws more attention \u2014 the lift nobody can see, or the limp that everyone already notices?"\n\n**Step 4 \u2014 Give agency:** "I want to make a deal with you. Let me send you to the best shoe specialist I know. They will modify a pair of your own shoes \u2014 whatever brand you want \u2014 and I challenge you to wear them for two weeks and see if anyone notices. If after two weeks you still hate it, we will talk about other options. But I think you will be surprised."\n\n**Step 5 \u2014 Engage the athlete identity:** "You told me you want to play basketball again. I want that for you too. But you cannot play competitive basketball with a 3 cm leg length difference \u2014 your hip and back will not allow it. The lift is not a disability device; it is athletic equipment. It is the thing that gets you back on the court."\n\n**Step 6 \u2014 Address the bullying:** "The name-calling is not okay, and that is worth talking about separately. Has anyone at school \u2014 a teacher, counselor, coach \u2014 been helpful? Would it help if I wrote a letter to your school counselor explaining the medical situation?"')
B.aUj=new A.c("+ Communication question",'**Q: His divorced parents disagree openly in front of you. Mom says "No surgery \u2014 he\'s been through enough." Dad says "Just fix it permanently \u2014 a shoe lift is a crutch." The patient is silent. How do you handle this?**\n\n**A:** This requires managing parental conflict while centering the adolescent patient:\n\n**Step 1 \u2014 Acknowledge both perspectives:** "I can see you both want what is best for your son, and I respect that you each have a strong opinion. Both of your perspectives have merit \u2014 there are valid reasons to consider either approach."\n\n**Step 2 \u2014 Redirect to the patient:** "But before we continue, I want to hear from the person this actually affects the most." Turn to the patient: "This is your body and your life. What matters most to you right now? What questions do you have?"\n\n**Step 3 \u2014 Establish medical framework:** "Let me take the family dynamics out of this for a moment and share the medical facts:\n- A 3 cm discrepancy requires correction \u2014 doing nothing is not a safe option because of what is happening to his spine.\n- A shoe lift works immediately, has no surgical risks, and is the first-line treatment.\n- Surgery is a valid option for definitive correction but has real risks and a 6-9 month recovery.\n- These are not mutually exclusive \u2014 we can start with the lift and pursue surgery later if needed.\n- There is no emergency \u2014 we have time to make the surgical decision carefully."\n\n**Step 4 \u2014 Propose a structured plan:** "Here is what I recommend: We start the shoe lift today because his spine needs protection now. We schedule a follow-up in 3 months with new spine films. At that point, we will have objective data about how the lift is working. If the lift resolves the scoliosis and he is functioning well, surgery may not be needed. If the scoliosis is progressing or he cannot tolerate the lift, surgery becomes a stronger consideration. This gives us time, data, and does not close any doors."\n\n**Step 5 \u2014 Establish collaborative decision-making:** "I want all three of you \u2014 and both parents need to agree \u2014 before any surgical decision is made. I am happy to have a separate meeting with each of you if that would be more comfortable, and I can also arrange a joint meeting with the orthopedic surgeon so everyone hears the same information."')
B.aEa=s([B.aVZ,B.aVf,B.aMG,B.aTg,B.aYz,B.aTV,B.aSE,B.aMR,B.b0q,B.aUh,B.aUj],t.E)
B.QK=new A.a5("special-limb-length","Limb Length Discrepancy \u2014 Complex Orthotic Prescription",B.aEa,!1)
B.aVQ=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 24-year-old female presents to PM&R outpatient clinic requesting a sports wheelchair prescription and medical clearance for competitive wheelchair basketball. She sustained a T12-L1 burst fracture in a motor vehicle accident 18 months ago, resulting in L1 ASIA A paraplegia (complete). She completed acute inpatient rehabilitation and has been independent with a manual wheelchair for 12 months. She was a Division I college basketball player at the time of injury (junior year, starting shooting guard). She has been attending a local adaptive sports program for 3 months and was recently scouted for a regional wheelchair basketball team with Paralympic development pathway potential. She is driven and competitive. Her parents, who attend the appointment, express significant concern about injury risk and question whether competitive sports are "safe for someone in her condition."')
B.aRa=new A.c("History & Systems Review:",'**Past Medical History:** L1 ASIA A paraplegia (18 months post-injury), neurogenic bladder managed with intermittent catheterization, neurogenic bowel on timed program, history of one UTI (6 months ago, resolved), no pressure injuries, no DVT history. No autonomic dysreflexia (level below T6).\n\n**Current Function:** Independent with all ADLs from wheelchair. Drives independently with hand controls. Lives in accessible apartment with a roommate. Returned to college (senior year, sports management major). Performs daily wheelchair pushups for pressure relief. Transfers independently floor-to-chair and car.\n\n**Current Wheelchair:** Rigid-frame ultralight manual wheelchair (TiLite ZRA), 15-inch seat width, anti-tip casters, high-performance wheels. Well-fitted for daily use.\n\n**Sports Background:** 4-year varsity basketball player (high school + college). Averaged 14 points per game at DI level. Elite upper body conditioning pre-injury. Has been doing adaptive sports (basketball, tennis) recreationally for 3 months. Coach reports she has "exceptional court vision and shooting form already adapted."\n\n**Current Complaints:** Right shoulder pain (3/10 after intense practice sessions, resolves with rest). Occasional low back pain. Wants to increase training intensity to 5 days/week but concerned about shoulder durability.\n\n**Medications:** Oxybutynin 5 mg BID (bladder), docusate daily, multivitamin with calcium and vitamin D.\n\n**Social History:** Single, lives with college roommate. Parents live 2 hours away. Father is a high school basketball coach. Mother is a pediatric nurse. Both parents were at every college game. Strong family support but parental anxiety is notable.')
B.aML=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**General:** Athletic-appearing female in manual wheelchair. Well-nourished, muscular upper body. Positive affect, engaged.\n\n**Upper Extremities:**\n- Shoulder ROM: Full bilateral. Right shoulder with pain at end-range overhead press and horizontal adduction.\n- Rotator cuff testing: Right supraspinatus mildly tender with mild weakness on empty can test (4+/5). Neer impingement sign positive right. Hawkins positive right. Left shoulder normal.\n- Elbow/wrist/hand: Full ROM, 5/5 strength bilateral. Grip strength 35 kg bilateral (excellent).\n- Biceps: 5/5 bilateral. Triceps: 5/5 bilateral. Deltoid: 5/5 bilateral.\n\n**Trunk:** Core stability: able to maintain seated balance with perturbation in all directions. Moderate trunk control for wheelchair sports \u2014 compensates with upper extremity anchoring for extreme reaches.\n\n**Lower Extremities:** No voluntary motor function below L1 (ASIA A confirmed). Sensation absent below L1 dermatome. Reflexes absent bilateral lower extremities. Mild bilateral hip flexion contracture (10 degrees) \u2014 common finding.\n\n**Skin:** No pressure injuries. Ischial tuberosities \u2014 no erythema, no scarring. Well-maintained skin inspection routine.\n\n**Cardiovascular:** HR 62, BP 118/72. Excellent aerobic conditioning evident. No orthostatic symptoms.\n\n**Shoulder Imaging (obtained):** Right shoulder MRI: mild supraspinatus tendinosis with partial-thickness bursal-side tear (3 mm), mild subacromial bursitis. No labral pathology. No rotator cuff full-thickness tear.")
B.aRc=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","**Functional Classification for Wheelchair Basketball:**\n- International Wheelchair Basketball Federation (IWBF) uses a **1.0 to 4.5 point classification system** based on functional trunk control and lower extremity function\n- **L1 ASIA A classification:** She would likely be classified as a **1.0 player** \u2014 the lowest functional class. Players with no trunk control below the level of injury, no lower extremity function, and limited seated balance.\n- **Team composition rules:** A team of 5 players on court cannot exceed 14.0 total points. Having a 1.0 player means the team can field higher-point players in other positions \u2014 she has strategic value.\n- **Functional implications for play:** 1.0 players typically play guard positions (which aligns with her basketball background). Limited rebounding due to trunk control but excellent for shooting, passing, and floor generalship.\n- **Classification appeal:** Some L1 complete injuries may have functional trunk extension that could qualify for 1.5 \u2014 this should be assessed by a certified IWBF classifier.\n\n**Injury Risk Assessment:**\n- **Shoulder injuries:** Most common injury in wheelchair basketball (30-40% of all injuries). Rotator cuff impingement, labral tears, and overuse tendinopathy from repetitive pushing, shooting, and chair skills. Her existing partial supraspinatus tear is a risk factor.\n- **Hand/wrist injuries:** Contusions, fractures from falls and wheelchair contact. Gloves and taping strategies reduce risk.\n- **Pressure injuries:** Prolonged sitting in sports chair (less cushioning than daily chair), increased shear forces during aggressive play. Requires sports-specific pressure management.\n- **Falls/tips:** Wheelchair basketball involves frequent tips and falls. She must demonstrate safe fall techniques and floor-to-chair transfers.\n- **Thermal dysregulation:** L1 level has impaired lower body sweating \u2014 risk of heat illness in intense competition. Hydration and cooling protocols essential.\n- **UTI risk:** Increased with dehydration during intense training. Catheterization schedule must be maintained during tournaments.")
B.aPv=new A.c("+ Diagnostic question","**Q: Her right shoulder MRI shows a partial supraspinatus tear. Does this preclude her from competitive wheelchair basketball? How do you manage this?**\n\n**A:** A partial-thickness bursal-side supraspinatus tear does NOT preclude competitive wheelchair basketball but requires a structured shoulder preservation approach:\n\n1. **Severity assessment:** A 3 mm partial-thickness tear is mild and common in manual wheelchair users. Up to 70% of chronic SCI patients have rotator cuff pathology on MRI, many asymptomatic. The key question is functional \u2014 can she play with acceptable pain?\n\n2. **Pre-sport rehabilitation:** Before increasing to competitive intensity, complete a 6-8 week shoulder rehabilitation program:\n- Rotator cuff strengthening (infraspinatus, teres minor, subscapularis) with emphasis on external rotation\n- Scapular stabilizer strengthening (lower trapezius, serratus anterior)\n- Posterior capsule stretching (sleeper stretches)\n- Correct any wheelchair propulsion biomechanics contributing to impingement (semi-circular stroke pattern rather than arc pattern)\n\n3. **Sport-specific modifications:**\n- Shooting form analysis \u2014 minimize overhead repetitions during practice\n- Push technique in sports chair \u2014 optimize mechanics to reduce subacromial loading\n- Cross-training with swimming or handcycling for cardiovascular conditioning with less impingement risk\n\n4. **Monitoring protocol:** Reassess shoulder every 3 months during first competitive season. Repeat MRI if symptoms worsen significantly. If tear progresses to full-thickness or pain limits function, surgical consultation warranted.\n\n5. **Risk acceptance:** She must understand that competitive wheelchair basketball accelerates shoulder wear. Long-term shoulder preservation is the primary concern for lifelong wheelchair users. This is a quality-of-life calculation she must make with full information.")
B.aQm=new A.c("+ Challenge question","**Q: She asks about using a power-assist wheelchair for daily use to preserve her shoulders for basketball. Is this an appropriate strategy?**\n\n**A:** This is an excellent and forward-thinking question that reflects emerging best practice in SCI shoulder preservation:\n\n**Evidence supports dual-chair approach:**\n- Daily manual wheelchair propulsion accounts for the majority of cumulative shoulder stress in SCI patients \u2014 2,000-3,000 push strokes per day\n- Adding competitive sports on top of daily propulsion demands accelerates rotator cuff degeneration\n- Using power-assist wheels (e.g., SmartDrive, e-motion) or a separate power wheelchair for daily/community mobility reduces cumulative shoulder loading while preserving manual wheelchair skills for sport\n\n**Recommended approach:**\n- **For sport:** Continue with ultralight manual sports wheelchair (described below)\n- **For daily use:** Prescribe power-assist add-on (SmartDrive MX2 or similar) for her existing daily wheelchair. This reduces push force by 40-60% while maintaining active wheeling.\n- **For community distances:** Consider lightweight power chair or power-assist for campus mobility, grocery shopping, and community errands where the distance causes shoulder fatigue.\n\n**Insurance justification:** Frame the power-assist as medically necessary for shoulder joint preservation given objective MRI findings of rotator cuff pathology and the documented correlation between cumulative propulsion and progressive shoulder degeneration in SCI. Cite the Paralyzed Veterans of America Clinical Practice Guidelines on upper extremity preservation.\n\n**Important nuance:** This is NOT about admitting inability to use a manual chair \u2014 it is a proactive sports medicine strategy analogous to a professional baseball pitcher icing their arm between starts. Frame it this way for the patient to avoid perceived loss of independence.")
B.aTA=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT","**Sport-Specific Conditioning Program:**\n- **Cardiovascular:** Handcycling or arm ergometry 3x/week for 30-45 minutes at 70-85% max HR. Swimming 2x/week for shoulder-friendly cross-training.\n- **Strength:** Upper body periodized program \u2014 push/pull balance. Emphasize pulling exercises (rows, reverse flys) at 2:1 ratio to pushing to counteract wheelchair propulsion imbalance. Rotator cuff pre-habilitation daily.\n- **Sport-specific:** Wheelchair sprints, starts/stops, pivoting, chair skills (wheelies, tipping recovery). Shooting drills from wheelchair.\n- **Core/trunk:** Seated balance training with perturbation, medicine ball catches and throws, trunk rotation resistance.\n\n**Shoulder Preservation Protocol:**\n- Limit total daily shoulder load: track practice hours + daily propulsion\n- Mandatory rest days: at minimum 1 full rest day per week, 2 preferred\n- Ice after every practice (15 minutes, shoulder)\n- Anti-inflammatory use: PRN ibuprofen after intense sessions (not daily to avoid GI/renal effects)\n- Stretch routine: pectoralis, anterior deltoid, posterior capsule \u2014 before and after every session\n- Annual shoulder MRI surveillance while competing\n\n**Sports Wheelchair Prescription:**\n- **Frame:** Rigid titanium or aluminum, maximum lightweight construction (typically 10-12 lbs for frame)\n- **Seat:** Lower seat height than daily chair for court access and stability. Bucket seat with 10-15 degree dump for stability during contact.\n- **Wheels:** 25-inch or 26-inch cambered wheels (12-15 degree camber for wheelchair basketball \u2014 provides lateral stability and quicker turns). High-pressure tires for speed.\n- **Anti-tip:** Rear anti-tip casters mandatory per IWBF rules. Fifth wheel (front caster) optional.\n- **Strapping:** Abdominal and thigh straps to compensate for absent trunk control. Must meet IWBF equipment rules.\n- **Customization:** Seat width, depth, and backrest height customized by certified ATP with wheelchair sports experience.\n- **Cost:** $3,000-$6,000 for a competitive sports wheelchair.\n\n**Medical Clearance for Competition:**\n- Pre-participation physical examination including cardiovascular screening, shoulder assessment, skin check\n- Written clearance letter for IWBF registration\n- Establish UTI prevention protocol for tournament travel (hydration plan, catheterization schedule)\n- Thermal regulation plan for summer competitions\n- Emergency action plan: autonomic dysreflexia protocol (even though L1 is below typical threshold, awareness for team medical staff)")
B.aNF=new A.c("+ Management question",'**Q: During her first tournament, she develops a 2 cm x 2 cm Stage 2 pressure injury on her right ischial tuberosity. She has a critical game the next day. How do you advise?**\n\n**A:** This requires balancing competitive aspirations with skin integrity \u2014 a career-threatening injury if mismanaged:\n\n1. **Immediate assessment:** Stage 2 pressure injury (partial-thickness skin loss with exposed dermis) is painful-area adjacent and requires wound care. Assess for signs of infection, depth, and undermining. If deeper than Stage 2 or infected, she CANNOT play.\n\n2. **If clean Stage 2:** She CAN potentially play with modifications, but this is a significant risk:\n- **Sports chair cushion modification:** Add gel overlay or redistribute pressure away from the affected ischium. An ATP or seating specialist at the tournament can make rapid adjustments.\n- **Protective dressing:** Apply hydrocolloid or foam dressing (Mepilex) over the wound to reduce shear.\n- **Time limitation:** Reduce playing time \u2014 suggest to the coach she plays limited minutes with frequent substitution breaks for pressure relief.\n- **Between games:** Complete pressure relief (prone or side-lying). No sitting outside of game time.\n\n3. **Risk counseling to the patient:** "I understand this game matters to you. Here is the reality \u2014 if this wound worsens to Stage 3 or 4, you could be out for months, require surgery, and potentially end your sports career before it starts. One game is not worth that. If you play, it must be with modifications and limited minutes."\n\n4. **Post-tournament mandatory plan:** Full skin assessment by physiatrist within 48 hours. Complete offloading until healed. Evaluate sports chair seating system for causative factors. This injury suggests her sports chair cushion is inadequate \u2014 upgrade before next competition.\n\n5. **Prevention going forward:** Custom pressure-mapped cushion for sports chair. Skin checks before and after every practice and game. Education on recognizing early signs.')
B.b_D=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","**Paralympic Pathway:**\n- **Development pipeline:** Local adaptive sports program \u2192 regional team \u2192 national team identification camp \u2192 USA Wheelchair Basketball national team \u2192 Paralympic Games\n- **Classification:** Must be formally classified by an IWBF-certified classifier. Classification determines eligibility and point value. She should request classification at her first sanctioned tournament.\n- **U.S. Paralympic program:** Contact the National Wheelchair Basketball Association (NWBA) for developmental team registration. USA Basketball oversees Paralympic wheelchair basketball.\n- **Athlete identification:** Her DI college basketball background combined with L1 ASIA A classification makes her a high-value recruit \u2014 exceptional basketball IQ in a low-point player is rare.\n\n**Adaptive Sports Organizations:**\n- National Wheelchair Basketball Association (NWBA) \u2014 competitive league structure\n- Disabled Sports USA \u2014 multisport participation\n- Move United (formerly Disabled Sports USA) \u2014 connects athletes with programs\n- Challenged Athletes Foundation \u2014 grants for equipment and training\n- Local VA or community adaptive sports programs for initial exposure\n\n**Equipment Funding:**\n- **Insurance coverage for sports wheelchair:** Typically NOT covered by health insurance as a second wheelchair. Daily wheelchair is covered; sports chair is considered recreational equipment.\n- **Funding strategies:**\n  - Challenged Athletes Foundation grants (up to $5,000 for equipment)\n  - NWBA equipment grant program\n  - Kelly Brush Foundation grants for adaptive sports equipment\n  - Crowdfunding with compelling athlete story\n  - College disability services may have equipment loan programs\n  - Some manufacturers offer elite athlete sponsorship programs\n- **Advocacy:** Write letter of medical necessity framing sports participation as essential for cardiovascular health, mental health, bone density maintenance, and community integration \u2014 all documented health benefits of adaptive sports in SCI\n\n**Title IX and College Adaptive Sports:**\n- Emerging programs at universities are offering varsity or club wheelchair basketball\n- She may be eligible for adaptive sport scholarship considerations at graduate school level\n- NCAA does not yet govern wheelchair basketball, but the NWBA college division is growing")
B.aYb=new A.c("+ Systems question",'**Q: The adaptive sports program she attends is volunteer-run and has no medical oversight. Athletes frequently play through injuries. How do you address this systems gap?**\n\n**A:** This is a common and dangerous gap in adaptive sports programming:\n\n1. **Offer to serve as medical advisor:** Volunteer your time as a physiatrist consultant to the program. Even quarterly visits for pre-participation screening and injury assessment would significantly improve safety. Frame this as professional development and community service.\n\n2. **Develop a medical screening protocol:**\n- Pre-participation physical examination template specific to wheelchair athletes\n- Shoulder screening (rotator cuff, impingement tests) at season start and mid-season\n- Skin check protocol before and after practices\n- Emergency action plan posted at every practice/game\n- First aid kit with SCI-specific supplies (catheter kits, skin care supplies)\n\n3. **Athlete education program:** Develop a "shoulder preservation for wheelchair athletes" handout covering warning signs, self-management, and when to seek care. Create a skin inspection checklist for athletes.\n\n4. **Coach education:** Many adaptive sports coaches lack SCI-specific medical knowledge. Offer a training session covering: heat illness recognition, autonomic dysreflexia awareness, pressure injury prevention, shoulder injury warning signs, bladder management during competition.\n\n5. **Connect with regional resources:** Contact the NWBA or local Paralympic sport club for established safety protocols. Many have model policies that smaller programs can adopt.\n\n6. **Advocate for institutional support:** Approach the local hospital, medical school, or VA for sponsorship. A formal partnership provides medical credibility, liability protection, and potential funding for the program.')
B.aTZ=new A.c(u.j,'**Scenario:** The patient\'s parents request a private meeting without their daughter. Father says: "We were in the car behind her when the accident happened. We saw everything. We almost lost her. Now she wants to play a contact sport in a wheelchair going full speed? What if she falls and gets hurt worse? Can she become more paralyzed? We want you to tell her this is too dangerous."\n\n**Supporting Patient Autonomy While Validating Parental Fear:**\n\n**Step 1 \u2014 Acknowledge the trauma:** "I cannot imagine what it was like to witness your daughter\'s accident. That kind of trauma stays with you, and it makes complete sense that you are terrified of anything that could put her at risk again. Your fear comes from a place of deep love, and I respect that."\n\n**Step 2 \u2014 Address the medical concerns directly:**\n"Let me address your specific questions honestly:\n- Can she become \'more paralyzed\'? At her level (L1 complete), the risk of worsening her spinal cord injury from wheelchair basketball is extremely low. Her injury is complete and stable. Wheelchair basketball does not involve the types of forces that would threaten her spine.\n- Injury risks that DO exist: shoulder injuries, hand injuries, pressure sores, falls from the chair. These are real but manageable with proper equipment, training, and medical oversight. They are comparable to injury rates in able-bodied recreational sports.\n- The biggest health risks for someone with SCI are actually cardiovascular disease, depression, and social isolation \u2014 all of which are REDUCED by competitive sports participation."\n\n**Step 3 \u2014 Reframe the risk equation:**\n"I understand your instinct is to protect her. But consider this: your daughter was an elite athlete. Her identity, her confidence, her social world were built around basketball. The accident took her legs, but it did not take her competitive spirit, her court vision, or her love of the game. Wheelchair basketball gives her back a part of herself that the injury took away.\n\nThe research is clear \u2014 SCI patients who participate in adaptive sports have lower rates of depression, better cardiovascular health, greater life satisfaction, and longer life expectancy than those who do not. The safest thing for her long-term health may actually be to play."\n\n**Step 4 \u2014 Refuse to undermine her autonomy:**\n"I want to be transparent \u2014 I will not tell her this is too dangerous, because I do not believe it is. She is 24 years old, cognitively intact, and making an informed decision about her own body. My role is to make sure she competes safely \u2014 proper equipment, shoulder monitoring, skin protection, and medical oversight.\n\nBut your feelings matter too, and I think she would benefit from hearing your concerns directly. Would you be open to a family conversation where we discuss the safety plan together? I think if you see the precautions we are putting in place, it may ease some of your worry."\n\n**Step 5 \u2014 Offer ongoing parental support:**\n"Adjusting to your child\'s disability is its own journey, separate from hers. Have either of you spoken with a counselor about the accident? Many parents of SCI patients benefit from their own support. The trauma you experienced watching the accident may need its own attention."')
B.aQl=new A.c("+ Communication question","**Q: The patient finds out her parents asked you to discourage her from playing. She is furious and says, \"They still treat me like I'm broken. I'm not a child. Can you tell them to back off?\" How do you navigate this?**\n\n**A:** This requires mediating without taking sides while firmly supporting her autonomy:\n\n\"I understand why you are frustrated. You have worked incredibly hard to rebuild your independence, and having your parents try to limit you feels like it undermines everything you have accomplished. Your feelings are valid.\n\nBut I want to share a perspective: your parents are not doing this because they think you are broken. They are doing it because they watched you nearly die. They have PTSD from that accident \u2014 real, clinical trauma \u2014 and their fear response is to protect you from anything that looks even remotely risky. It is not rational, but it is real.\n\nHere is what I told them, and I want you to hear this directly: I told them I would not discourage you from playing because I believe competitive sports are good for your health, your mental well-being, and your quality of life. I told them the medical evidence supports adaptive sports participation.\n\nWhat I would like to suggest is a family meeting \u2014 all three of you and me \u2014 where I present the safety plan, the equipment specifications, and the medical monitoring I will provide. I will also address the medical evidence on adaptive sports benefits. Sometimes parents need to hear from a physician that their child is making a sound decision. It does not mean you need their permission, but their support will make this journey better for everyone.\n\nI am also going to suggest they consider counseling for the trauma of the accident. Their anxiety may be rooted in something that professional support could help with.\n\nIn the meantime \u2014 you are cleared to play. Here is your sports wheelchair prescription. Let's get you on that court.\"\n\nThis response validates the patient's frustration, reframes the parents' behavior with empathy rather than blame, supports autonomy without creating family division, and offers a constructive path forward.")
B.aFk=s([B.aVQ,B.aRa,B.aML,B.aRc,B.aPv,B.aQm,B.aTA,B.aNF,B.b_D,B.aYb,B.aTZ,B.aQl],t.E)
B.PY=new A.a5("special-adaptive-sports","Adaptive Sports Prescription \u2014 Wheelchair Basketball",B.aFk,!1)
B.aPS=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 65-year-old male was admitted to acute inpatient rehabilitation 10 days ago following a C5 ASIA C incomplete spinal cord injury from a ground-level fall off a ladder while cleaning gutters. He underwent emergent anterior cervical discectomy and fusion (C4-C6). Post-operatively, he has 2/5 to 3/5 strength in upper extremities and 1/5 to 2/5 in lower extremities with intact but reduced sensation below C5 (ASIA C). He was making slow but measurable functional gains in the first week \u2014 improved sitting balance, minimal gains in upper extremity function. Today, he refuses to get out of bed for therapy, refuses his medications, and tells the nurse he wants to leave the hospital against medical advice. When the attending physiatrist arrives, he states: "I have thought about this carefully. I would rather die than live like this. Let me go home. I am done."')
B.aZZ=new A.c("History & Systems Review:",'**Past Medical History:** Hypertension, type 2 diabetes, benign prostatic hyperplasia, mild cognitive impairment diagnosed 2 years ago (MMSE 25/30 at that time), remote history of depression treated with sertraline 15 years ago (discontinued after 2 years). No prior suicidal ideation or attempts.\n\n**Pre-Injury Function:** Independent in all ADLs and IADLs. Retired engineer. Active \u2014 golfed 3x/week, maintained his own home and yard. Drove independently. Volunteered at a local food bank. Described by family as "fiercely independent" and "hates asking for help."\n\n**Current Functional Status:** Moderate to maximal assist for all transfers, dependent for lower body ADLs, requires setup assist for feeding (tenodesis grasp developing but weak), neurogenic bladder (indwelling catheter, planned transition to intermittent catheterization), neurogenic bowel (digital stimulation program initiated).\n\n**Current Medications:** Lisinopril 20 mg daily, metformin 500 mg BID, gabapentin 300 mg TID, docusate/senna, famotidine, subcutaneous heparin, melatonin (refusing all as of this morning).\n\n**Social History:** Widowed 3 years ago (wife died of ovarian cancer). Two adult daughters \u2014 one lives locally (primary support), one lives out of state. Lives alone in a two-story home he built himself. Active member of Rotary Club. Strong social network but has been declining visitors since admission. His wife\'s death was described by his daughter as "devastating \u2014 he was never the same after."\n\n**Behavioral Changes Noted by Staff:** Initially engaged in therapy (days 1-5). Became withdrawn after a difficult bowel care session on day 6 where he was "humiliated." Has been declining therapy sessions since day 7. Sleeping poorly. Eating less than 50% of meals. Told his daughter yesterday, "Your mother was the lucky one."')
B.b0A=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:",'**General:** Appears older than stated age. Lying in bed with lights off, facing the wall. Responds to questions but with minimal eye contact. Grooming declined over past 3 days (unshaved, unwashed).\n\n**Neurological (last documented \u2014 day 8):**\n- C5 ASIA C: Motor incomplete\n- Upper extremities: Deltoids 3/5 bilateral, biceps 3/5 bilateral, wrist extensors 2/5 bilateral, triceps 2/5 right, 1/5 left, grip developing (trace)\n- Lower extremities: Hip flexors 2/5 bilateral, quad 1/5 right, 2/5 left, ankle dorsiflexion 0/5 bilateral\n- Sensation: Reduced but present (light touch and pinprick) in all dermatomes below C5\n- Rectal exam: Some voluntary anal contraction (confirms motor incomplete)\n\n**Mental Status Today:**\n- Alert and oriented x 3 (person, place, time). Knows he is in rehab.\n- Speech coherent, organized, no tangential thinking\n- Mood: "I just want to go home and be done with it"\n- Affect: flat, tearful intermittently\n- Denies hallucinations or delusions\n- Cognition: Baseline mild cognitive impairment \u2014 current testing may be confounded by depression\n\n**PHQ-9 (administered today):** Score 22/27 (severe depression range). Positive on item 9: "Thoughts that you would be better off dead or of hurting yourself" \u2014 endorsed as "nearly every day."\n\n**Vital Signs:** BP 148/92 (elevated from baseline, likely pain/distress), HR 88, afebrile.')
B.b1R=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING",'**Capacity Evaluation \u2014 Appelbaum Four-Component Model:**\n\nDecisional capacity is decision-specific and must be assessed systematically:\n\n**1. Understanding:** Can the patient comprehend the relevant information?\n- Test: "Can you tell me what the doctors have told you about your injury and your potential for recovery?"\n- He must demonstrate understanding of: his SCI diagnosis, the ASIA C incomplete status with potential for further neurological recovery, the goals and expected benefits of rehabilitation, the consequences of leaving AMA (medical complications, loss of insurance coverage, skin breakdown, UTI, DVT risk).\n- If he states "I am paralyzed and will never walk" \u2014 he may have an inaccurate understanding that affects his decision.\n\n**2. Appreciation:** Can the patient apply that information to his own situation?\n- Test: "Do you believe the information about recovery potential applies to you?"\n- He must appreciate that incomplete SCI (ASIA C) has significantly better prognosis than complete injury, and that continued rehabilitation could meaningfully improve his function.\n- If he says "Maybe others get better, but I won\'t" \u2014 this may reflect depressive cognitive distortion rather than rational assessment.\n\n**3. Reasoning:** Can the patient weigh alternatives rationally?\n- Test: "Can you explain how you reached your decision?"\n- He should demonstrate consideration of pros and cons, alternative options, and the impact on people he cares about.\n- Depressive reasoning may present as inability to envision any positive future \u2014 "All-or-nothing thinking" (if I cannot be fully independent, life is not worth living).\n\n**4. Expression of Choice:** Can the patient clearly and consistently communicate a decision?\n- He has clearly expressed a choice. This criterion is met.\n\n**Key Question: Does he HAVE capacity, or is depression IMPAIRING capacity?**\n- PHQ-9 of 22 with active suicidal ideation represents severe major depression\n- Severe depression can impair appreciation and reasoning without affecting understanding or choice expression\n- Pre-existing mild cognitive impairment may further complicate assessment\n- His capacity is questionable \u2014 formal psychiatric consultation is warranted BEFORE accepting his refusal as a capacitated decision\n\n**TBI Screening:**\n- Ground-level fall with head/neck trauma \u2014 was there loss of consciousness?\n- Review imaging for intracranial pathology\n- Mild TBI can impair judgment, increase impulsivity, and worsen depression\n- If no head imaging obtained, consider CT head')
B.b0O=new A.c("+ Diagnostic question",'**Q: The patient tells you, "I understand my injury is incomplete. I know I might get some movement back. But I don\'t care. Living like this is worse than dying." Does this statement demonstrate capacity?**\n\n**A:** This statement satisfies understanding but raises significant concerns about appreciation and reasoning:\n\n**Understanding: Adequate.** He correctly identifies his injury as incomplete with recovery potential.\n\n**Appreciation: Questionable.** His statement "I don\'t care" about recovery potential suggests he may not be able to appreciate the personal relevance of the medical information. A person with intact appreciation would be able to weigh the possibility of meaningful recovery against their current suffering. His inability to assign value to future recovery may reflect **depressive anhedonia and hopelessness** rather than a rational values-based decision.\n\n**Reasoning: Impaired.** The hallmark of depressive reasoning is **cognitive constriction** \u2014 the inability to envision any future other than the current state of suffering. A capacitated patient refusing treatment would typically say something like "I have considered the possibility of recovery, and even if I regain some function, the life I would have is not one I want for these specific reasons." His blanket "I don\'t care" suggests foreclosure rather than reasoned deliberation.\n\n**Clinical judgment:** This patient likely has **depression-impaired decisional capacity** for this specific decision (leaving AMA). He may have capacity for other decisions (choosing meals, declining a specific therapy session). Capacity is decision-specific and severity-proportional \u2014 the higher the stakes, the higher the bar for capacity.\n\n**Next step:** He cannot be held involuntarily solely for refusing rehabilitation. However, active suicidal ideation (PHQ-9 item 9, "your mother was the lucky one") triggers a duty to assess for imminent danger and initiate psychiatric evaluation. This is not about overriding autonomy \u2014 it is about ensuring his refusal reflects his authentic values rather than a treatable psychiatric illness.')
B.aPa=new A.c("+ Challenge question",'**Q: The psychiatrist evaluates him and concludes he has capacity to refuse rehabilitation. He wants to leave AMA. What are your obligations?**\n\n**A:** If a psychiatrist confirms capacity after thorough evaluation (including depression treatment discussion), the following obligations apply:\n\n1. **Respect his autonomy:** A capacitated patient has the right to refuse any treatment, including rehabilitation, even if the medical team disagrees. This is a fundamental ethical and legal principle.\n\n2. **However, capacity does not end the conversation:**\n- **Treat the depression first:** Request that the patient agree to a trial of antidepressant treatment (e.g., 2-4 weeks of SSRI/SNRI) before making an irreversible decision. Frame this as: "I respect your right to leave. But depression is a treatable medical condition that can change how you see the world. Would you be willing to try treatment for the depression before making this decision? If after treatment you still feel the same way, I will support your choice."\n- **Motivational interviewing:** Explore ambivalence. Use open-ended questions: "What would your life need to look like for you to want to continue?" This may reveal modifiable barriers (humiliation from bowel care, loss of independence, unresolved grief).\n- **Peer support:** Offer connection with SCI peer mentor \u2014 someone with similar injury level who has been through the same despair and recovered. Peer support is the single most effective intervention for SCI adjustment.\n\n3. **If he insists on leaving AMA:**\n- Complete AMA documentation including risks explained (DVT, pneumonia, pressure injury, UTI, loss of recovery potential, death)\n- Ensure safe discharge plan (even AMA patients deserve a safety plan): home health referral, primary care follow-up, psychiatric follow-up, DVT prophylaxis prescription, emergency contact plan\n- Prescribe antidepressant even if he refuses inpatient treatment\n- Provide crisis hotline numbers (988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline)\n- Contact his daughters with his permission (or without if imminent danger)\n- Document thoroughly: capacity assessment, risks discussed, patient\'s responses, discharge plan, follow-up arranged')
B.aVB=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT",'**Psychiatric Consultation (Urgent \u2014 Same Day):**\n- Formal capacity evaluation by psychiatry, specifically addressing depression\'s impact on decision-making\n- Suicide risk assessment (Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale)\n- Evaluate for adjustment disorder vs major depressive episode vs complicated grief (wife\'s death)\n- Medication recommendations: consider mirtazapine (benefits appetite, sleep, and mood \u2014 all impaired in this patient) or sertraline (prior positive response)\n- Assess whether involuntary hold criteria are met (state-specific \u2014 generally requires imminent danger to self or others)\n\n**Motivational Enhancement Strategies:**\n- **Identify the trigger:** The humiliating bowel care session on day 6 was the inflection point. Address this directly \u2014 "I heard the bowel care was a very difficult experience. Can you tell me about it?" Modify the bowel program to maximize dignity (same-gender aide, curtain privacy, scheduled timing, patient control of process).\n- **Restore agency:** He is a retired engineer who built his own house. Incorporate problem-solving and control into his rehab \u2014 let him direct his therapy schedule, choose therapy goals, make decisions about his care plan.\n- **Small wins:** Set achievable daily goals that demonstrate progress \u2014 self-feeding, electric wheelchair trialing, using phone independently. Counteract hopelessness with tangible evidence of function.\n\n**Addressing Modifiable Factors:**\n- **Pain:** Gabapentin may be inadequate \u2014 reassess pain level and optimize (neuropathic pain can drive depression)\n- **Sleep:** Insomnia worsens depression \u2014 optimize sleep hygiene, consider trazodone 50 mg qHS\n- **Grief:** Unresolved grief for wife \u2014 the SCI may have reactivated grief by making him feel "closer to death." Grief counseling referral.\n- **Isolation:** He is declining visitors \u2014 gently encourage his daughter to visit, facilitate phone calls with Rotary friends. Social isolation deepens depression.\n- **Catheter:** Indwelling catheter is a visible reminder of dependency \u2014 accelerate transition to intermittent catheterization or discuss options for autonomy.')
B.aZJ=new A.c("+ Management question",'**Q: He agrees to stay but refuses antidepressant medication, saying "I\'m not depressed, I\'m realistic." He will continue rehab but remains passively engaged. How do you proceed?**\n\n**A:** This is a common and challenging scenario \u2014 the patient conflates depressive hopelessness with realism:\n\n1. **Respect his medication refusal for now** but continue psychiatric follow-up. Forced medication requires a higher legal threshold than is present here.\n\n2. **Reframe the discussion:** Avoid arguing about whether he is "depressed." Instead: "You may be right that what you are feeling is a realistic response to a terrible situation. But here is what I observe \u2014 you are not sleeping, not eating, not enjoying visits from your daughters, and you cannot imagine any future that is worth living. Whether we call that depression or a realistic reaction, those symptoms are treatable and treating them will help you think more clearly about your options."\n\n3. **Non-pharmacological depression interventions:**\n- **Behavioral activation:** Structured daily schedule with pleasant activities (not just therapy). TV sports, audiobooks, phone calls with friends.\n- **Cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT):** Psychology consult for targeted intervention on hopelessness and all-or-nothing thinking.\n- **Peer mentoring:** The single most powerful intervention. A person with similar SCI level who has rebuilt a meaningful life can challenge his belief system in ways the medical team cannot.\n- **Chaplaincy/spiritual care:** If he has any spiritual framework, this resource can be valuable for existential distress.\n\n4. **Monitor closely:** Daily safety assessment (nursing to report any suicidal statements). Reassess PHQ-9 weekly. If depression worsens or suicidal ideation becomes more active (plan, intent, means), re-initiate urgent psychiatric evaluation and consider involuntary hold.\n\n5. **Set a revisiting point:** "I would like to make an agreement with you. Stay with us for two more weeks, participate in therapy to the best of your ability, and let us address your sleep and pain. In two weeks, we will have an honest conversation about how you feel and what you want to do next. Deal?"')
B.aUt=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","**Ethics Committee Consultation:**\n- Appropriate when there is conflict between patient autonomy and beneficence, and the clinical team is uncertain how to proceed\n- Ethics committee role: provide a structured framework for the clinical and ethical dilemma, review capacity assessment adequacy, ensure patient rights are protected while exploring all options to preserve life\n- Ethics consultation does NOT override the patient's autonomous decision \u2014 it provides guidance and documentation\n- Document the ethics consultation findings in the medical record\n\n**Legal Standards for Capacity:**\n- **Capacity vs competence:** Capacity is a clinical determination made by physicians. Competence is a legal determination made by courts. Physicians assess capacity; only judges declare incompetence.\n- **State-specific involuntary hold criteria:** Generally requires imminent danger to self or others due to mental illness. Passive suicidal ideation alone may not meet threshold. Active suicidal ideation with plan and intent typically does.\n- **Guardianship:** If the patient lacks capacity and there is no advance directive, temporary guardianship could be sought through the court. His daughter may petition. However, this is a slow process and should be a last resort.\n- **Advance directives:** Does he have a living will or healthcare power of attorney? If so, review them. If not, this is an important conversation once depression is addressed.\n\n**AMA Discharge Protocol (If Ultimately Pursued):**\n1. Documented capacity evaluation by psychiatrist\n2. Informed refusal: risks of AMA discharge explained and documented (specific to SCI: autonomic dysreflexia, DVT/PE, pressure injury, UTI/sepsis, respiratory compromise)\n3. Patient signs AMA form (if willing) \u2014 refusal to sign does not prevent discharge\n4. Safe discharge plan regardless of AMA status: prescriptions, home health, DME, follow-up appointments, emergency numbers\n5. Continue insurance coverage \u2014 contrary to common belief, AMA discharge does NOT automatically void insurance. However, some insurers may challenge coverage.\n6. Risk management notification\n7. Follow-up phone call within 24-48 hours\n\n**Documentation Requirements:**\n- Detailed capacity assessment with Appelbaum criteria addressed\n- Psychiatric consultation note\n- Ethics committee consultation note (if obtained)\n- All conversations with patient regarding risks, alternatives, and decision\n- Family communication and involvement\n- Safety assessment and plan\n- Everything the team did to address modifiable factors")
B.b22=new A.c("+ Systems question",'**Q: His local daughter wants to pursue guardianship to keep him in rehab. His out-of-state daughter disagrees and says "Dad has always been clear he would never want to live dependent on others." How do you navigate this family disagreement?**\n\n**A:** This family conflict adds a layer of complexity that requires careful management:\n\n1. **Do not take sides between family members.** Both perspectives are valid \u2014 one is prioritizing preservation of life, the other is honoring the patient\'s expressed values.\n\n2. **Clarify the medical/legal reality:**\n- Guardianship requires a court determination of legal incompetence, not just family disagreement\n- A psychiatrist has assessed him as having capacity \u2014 this makes guardianship difficult to obtain legally\n- Even if granted, a guardian is expected to make decisions the patient WOULD make, not what the guardian wants \u2014 this is the substituted judgment standard\n- If the patient has capacity, guardianship is inappropriate and could constitute unlawful restraint\n\n3. **Facilitate a family meeting (with the patient present and consenting):**\n- Include both daughters (in-person and video), the patient, psychiatry, social work, and the treatment team\n- Allow each person to express their perspective without interruption\n- Present the medical facts: ASIA C with recovery potential, treatable depression, modifiable barriers\n- Clarify the ethical framework: respect for autonomy does not mean abandoning the patient \u2014 it means ensuring the decision is truly his and not the depression\'s\n\n4. **Explore advance directives:** Ask the patient directly, in front of both daughters: "Before this injury, did you ever discuss with your family what you would want in a situation like this? Did you ever write anything down?" This grounds the conversation in the patient\'s own prior expressed values.\n\n5. **Recommend against guardianship at this time:** Document that the patient has been assessed as having capacity, that depression treatment has been offered, and that the clinical team is working to address modifiable factors. Guardianship should only be pursued if capacity is clearly absent, and that is not the current clinical determination.\n\n6. **Support both daughters:** Refer both to social work for caregiver support. The local daughter needs respite and emotional support. The out-of-state daughter needs to feel heard despite her physical distance.')
B.aOG=new A.c(u.j,'**Scenario:** You enter the patient\'s room. He is lying in bed staring at the ceiling. He says: "Doc, I have thought about this. I would rather die than live like this. I built my house with my own hands. I coached Little League. I took care of my wife until she died. Now I can\'t even wipe my own rear end. This is not living. Please just let me go."\n\n**This is the Highest-Stakes Communication in Rehabilitation Medicine:**\n\n**Step 1 \u2014 Stop. Sit down. Be fully present.** Do not stand over him. Pull a chair to his bedside. Make eye contact. Give him silence after he finishes speaking. This moment deserves your undivided attention.\n\n**Step 2 \u2014 Validate without agreeing or dismissing:**\n"I hear you. What you are describing \u2014 the loss of the things that made you who you are \u2014 is one of the most painful experiences a person can go through. I am not going to pretend I fully understand what this feels like, because I don\'t. But I believe you when you say it is unbearable right now."\n\n**Step 3 \u2014 Gently explore the suicidal statement:**\n"When you say you would rather die than live like this, I need to understand what you mean. Are you saying that life feels so painful right now that death seems preferable? Or are you telling me you are thinking about ending your life? Those are different things, and both matter."\n\n*If he endorses active suicidal ideation with plan or intent, this becomes an acute psychiatric emergency \u2014 activate 1:1 observation and psychiatric consultation immediately.*\n\n*If he endorses passive ideation (wishes he were dead but no plan to act), continue the conversation:*\n\n**Step 4 \u2014 Introduce doubt without invalidating:**\n"I am not going to argue with how you feel right now. But I want to share something I have seen in my career: almost every patient with a new spinal cord injury who tells me they would rather die is telling me about how they feel in this moment \u2014 not about how they will feel in six months or a year. And the research confirms this \u2014 the vast majority of SCI patients who report wanting to die in the acute phase later report being glad they survived.\n\nYou are making the biggest decision of your life during the worst week of your life, while you are grieving your wife, not sleeping, in pain, and dealing with experiences that would shake anyone. I am not asking you to be optimistic. I am asking you to consider the possibility that how you feel right now might not be permanent."\n\n**Step 5 \u2014 Connect to his identity:**\n"You told me you built your house with your own hands. That tells me you are someone who solves problems, who does not give up easily. I am asking you to apply that same toughness to this situation \u2014 not forever, just for a few more weeks. Give us time to treat the things that are making this unbearable \u2014 the pain, the sleeplessness, the bowel care that was humiliating. Let us fix what we can fix."\n\n**Step 6 \u2014 Make a concrete ask:**\n"Here is what I am asking: two more weeks. Not forever. Two weeks where we address the pain, the sleep, the dignity issues with bowel care. I will bring in someone who has been exactly where you are \u2014 a man with a similar injury who felt the same way and can tell you honestly what life looked like a year later. At the end of two weeks, we talk again. If you still feel the same way after we have addressed everything we can address, I will respect that. Deal?"')
B.b2G=new A.c("+ Communication question",'**Q: His daughter is in the hallway crying. She grabs your arm and says, "He told me he wished he had died in the fall. You have to do something. Can\'t you keep him here? He\'s not thinking straight." How do you respond?**\n\n**A:** This requires balancing family distress, patient rights, and safety obligations:\n\n"I can see how frightened you are, and I want you to know that I am taking this very seriously. Your father\'s statement about wishing he had died concerns me too, and I have already arranged for a psychiatrist to evaluate him today.\n\nLet me be honest with you about where things stand:\n\nFirst, your father is not in immediate danger right now. He is in a hospital with 24-hour nursing care and medical staff who are aware of his statements. I have placed him on closer monitoring as a precaution.\n\nSecond, I believe your father is severely depressed \u2014 his symptoms clearly meet criteria for major depression. Depression is a medical illness that can change how a person thinks and what they believe about the future. It is treatable. The challenge is that he does not see himself as depressed \u2014 he sees himself as realistic.\n\nThird, I have to be transparent about the limits of what I can do. If a psychiatrist determines your father has the mental capacity to make his own decisions, I cannot legally force him to stay in the hospital against his will. I know that is not what you want to hear, and it is not what I want either.\n\nBut here is what I CAN do:\n- I can treat his depression aggressively, even if he resists medication \u2014 there are therapy approaches that work.\n- I can address the specific things making him miserable: the bowel care humiliation, the pain, the sleeplessness.\n- I can connect him with someone who has lived through exactly what he is experiencing.\n- I can buy time by engaging him in small, meaningful activities and goals.\n- I can keep having honest conversations with him.\n\nI need your help too. Your presence matters more than you know. Even if he does not show it, you being here reminds him that his life has meaning to people he loves. Keep coming. Keep talking to him about normal things \u2014 not just about his injury.\n\nAnd I want to check on you as well. Watching a parent go through this is traumatic. Do you have anyone supporting you \u2014 a friend, a counselor, your sister? Our social worker can connect you with family support resources.\n\nI promise you I will not give up on your father. But I need you to trust the process and give us a little time."')
B.aEh=s([B.aPS,B.aZZ,B.b0A,B.b1R,B.b0O,B.aPa,B.aVB,B.aZJ,B.aUt,B.b22,B.aOG,B.b2G],t.E)
B.PK=new A.a5("special-capacity-determination","Capacity Determination \u2014 Patient Refusing Life-Saving Treatment",B.aEh,!1)
B.b1n=new A.c("Initial Presentation","A 30-year-old transgender woman (male sex assigned at birth, female gender identity) is admitted to acute inpatient rehabilitation following a T6 ASIA A complete spinal cord injury sustained in a motorcycle accident 3 weeks ago. She underwent posterior spinal fusion T4-T8 and had an uncomplicated post-operative course. She has been on feminizing hormone therapy (estradiol and spironolactone) for 4 years through an endocrinology clinic and has not undergone gender-affirming surgery. Her legal name has been changed and her insurance and identification documents reflect her female name and gender marker. She presents to the rehab unit appearing anxious. She tells the admitting nurse, \"I need to know that I will be treated with respect here. My last hospital experience was terrible \u2014 they kept calling me 'he' and put me in a room with a man.\" She is accompanied by her partner (cisgender female) and her mother, who is supportive.")
B.b0d=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","**Past Medical History:** No significant medical history prior to SCI. On feminizing hormone therapy since age 26. No history of DVT or thromboembolic events. No surgical history prior to spinal fusion.\n\n**Hormone Therapy Regimen:**\n- Estradiol valerate 20 mg IM every 2 weeks (last injection 10 days ago)\n- Spironolactone 100 mg BID\n- Duration: 4 years with good feminization (breast development Tanner III, redistribution of body fat, reduced facial hair growth)\n\n**SCI Details:** T6 ASIA A complete paraplegia. No sacral sparing. Neurogenic bladder (indwelling Foley catheter, planned transition to intermittent catheterization). Neurogenic bowel (digital stimulation program to be initiated). At risk for autonomic dysreflexia (T6 level).\n\n**Current Medications:** Estradiol valerate 20 mg IM q2 weeks, spironolactone 100 mg BID, gabapentin 300 mg TID (neuropathic pain), docusate/senna, subcutaneous heparin, famotidine.\n\n**Psychiatric History:** Treated gender dysphoria with hormone therapy. History of anxiety and depression (in remission on hormone therapy). No active psychiatric medications. Reports significant distress about how she will be treated in rehab setting.\n\n**Social History:** Works as a graphic designer (remote work \u2014 can resume from wheelchair). Lives with partner in an accessible apartment (first floor, one step to enter). Supportive mother. Father is estranged (rejected her after transition). Strong friend network in the LGBTQ+ community. Has health insurance through employer.")
B.b09=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:","**General:** Well-nourished female-presenting individual with breast development, feminine fat distribution, and reduced facial hair. Appears anxious but cooperative when addressed by correct name and pronouns.\n\n**Neurological:** T6 ASIA A complete paraplegia. Motor: 5/5 upper extremities, 0/5 lower extremities. Sensory level at T6 (intact above, absent below). Bulbocavernosus reflex present. No sacral sparing (no voluntary anal contraction, no sensation at S4-S5).\n\n**Cardiovascular:** HR 74, BP 110/70. No orthostatic symptoms. Baseline BP important to document for AD monitoring.\n\n**Musculoskeletal:** Upper extremity strength and ROM normal. Trunk control absent below T6. Mild bilateral hip flexion contractures (5 degrees) developing.\n\n**Skin:** Surgical incision healing well. No pressure injuries. Breast tissue \u2014 no abnormalities.\n\n**Genitourinary:** Foley catheter in place. Planned transition to intermittent catheterization. External genitalia \u2014 male anatomy present (has not had gender-affirming genital surgery).\n\n**Body Habitus Considerations:** Hormone therapy has resulted in female fat distribution pattern, reduced muscle mass in upper body compared to cis male peers, and breast development. These factors affect wheelchair fitting, transfer techniques, and functional expectations.")
B.aYL=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","**Hormone Therapy Management During Rehab:**\n\n**DVT Risk with Estrogen in SCI (Critical Issue):**\n- SCI itself carries a **markedly elevated DVT risk** \u2014 up to 80% in untreated patients in the first 3 months. Standard DVT prophylaxis (LMWH or UFH) is mandatory.\n- Exogenous estrogen further increases DVT risk by 2-4x through increased clotting factor production and decreased protein S.\n- **Combined risk:** The synergistic DVT risk of SCI + estrogen therapy requires careful risk-benefit analysis.\n- **Options:**\n  1. **Continue estrogen with enhanced prophylaxis:** Transition from subcutaneous heparin to therapeutic-dose enoxaparin (1 mg/kg BID) for the acute high-risk period (first 3 months). Add compression stockings and sequential compression devices. Monitor with serial D-dimer and lower extremity duplex ultrasound.\n  2. **Temporarily reduce estrogen dose:** Lower estradiol dose during the acute DVT risk period and resume full dose after 3 months when baseline SCI DVT risk decreases.\n  3. **Temporarily discontinue estrogen (NOT recommended as first option):** Abruptly stopping hormone therapy causes significant psychological distress, return of dysphoria, and physical symptoms (hot flashes, mood instability). Only consider if DVT occurs despite enhanced prophylaxis.\n- **Recommended approach:** Continue estrogen at current dose with enhanced DVT prophylaxis. The psychological harm of stopping hormones in a vulnerable patient is significant and the risk can be managed with aggressive anticoagulation.\n- **Endocrinology consultation** for collaborative decision-making.\n\n**Spironolactone Considerations:**\n- Anti-androgen + potassium-sparing diuretic\n- Monitor potassium closely \u2014 SCI patients may have altered renal function\n- May contribute to orthostatic hypotension in combination with SCI autonomic dysfunction\n- Continue unless potassium elevated or symptomatic hypotension\n\n**Body Habitus Considerations:**\n- 4 years of estrogen therapy has reduced upper body muscle mass \u2014 functional expectations for transfers and wheelchair propulsion may differ from cisgender male norms at T6 level\n- Breast development affects prone positioning for pressure relief and may require modified transfer techniques\n- Wheelchair seating must accommodate female fat distribution pattern")
B.aV7=new A.c("+ Diagnostic question","**Q: On day 5, she develops unilateral left calf swelling. D-dimer is elevated. How do you manage this given her estrogen use?**\n\n**A:** This is a high-stakes clinical scenario requiring immediate action:\n\n1. **Confirm DVT:** Order lower extremity duplex ultrasound stat. If positive for DVT:\n\n2. **Initiate therapeutic anticoagulation:** Transition to therapeutic enoxaparin (1 mg/kg BID) or unfractionated heparin drip, depending on renal function and IVC filter consideration.\n\n3. **Estrogen decision:** This is the critical question. A confirmed DVT while on estrogen in the setting of SCI requires a **multidisciplinary discussion** (physiatry, hematology, endocrinology, and the patient):\n- **Option A (preferred if DVT is small/non-limb-threatening):** Continue estrogen with therapeutic anticoagulation and close monitoring. Rationale: the DVT is likely multifactorial (SCI immobility is the primary driver, not estrogen alone). Stopping estrogen causes significant harm and may not prevent recurrence.\n- **Option B (if DVT is extensive/PE risk is high):** Temporarily reduce estrogen dose (not stop) while on therapeutic anticoagulation. Resume full dose once anticoagulation is therapeutic and stable.\n- **Option C (if recurrent DVT despite anticoagulation):** Consider IVC filter placement AND continue estrogen. Stopping estrogen entirely is a last resort.\n\n4. **Never make this decision unilaterally.** This must be a shared decision with the patient, fully informed of risks and options. She may prioritize hormone continuity over DVT risk, or she may choose temporary reduction \u2014 both are valid.\n\n5. **Long-term anticoagulation:** Given SCI + estrogen, she may need extended anticoagulation (6-12 months rather than standard 3 months). Hematology should guide duration.\n\n6. **Rehabilitation impact:** If on therapeutic anticoagulation, modify rehab to avoid activities with high fall/trauma risk. No contact sports or aggressive transfer training until anticoagulation is stable.")
B.aXQ=new A.c("+ Challenge question",'**Q: The patient asks about sexual function after SCI, specifically as a transgender woman. How do you approach this conversation?**\n\n**A:** Sexual health counseling for transgender patients with SCI requires specialized knowledge:\n\n1. **Initiate the conversation proactively:** Do not wait for the patient to ask. "Sexual health is an important part of rehabilitation that we discuss with all our SCI patients. I want to make sure we address this in a way that is relevant and respectful to you. May I ask some questions about your sexual health concerns?"\n\n2. **Assessment of function:** At T6 ASIA A, she retains reflex erection capability (sacral reflex arc intact \u2014 bulbocavernosus reflex present). Psychogenic arousal pathways (T11-L2) are disrupted. Sensation will be absent in the genital area.\n\n3. **Transgender-specific considerations:**\n- Estrogen therapy has likely altered erectile function and ejaculatory capacity pre-injury \u2014 many trans women on estrogen experience reduced erections and libido, which may align with or conflict with her sexual preferences\n- Her sexual practices and what constitutes satisfying sexual activity should be explored without assumptions \u2014 she may or may not use her natal genitalia during sexual activity\n- If she is considering future gender-affirming genital surgery (vaginoplasty), SCI adds surgical complexity \u2014 sensation, blood supply, and positioning considerations. This is a discussion for later in recovery but should be mentioned\n\n4. **Counseling approach:**\n- Address the patient AND her partner (with permission) \u2014 partner sexual adjustment is equally important\n- Discuss alternative erogenous zones above the injury level\n- Referral to a sex therapist experienced with both SCI and transgender patients (may require telemedicine for specialized expertise)\n- Address fertility preservation if relevant \u2014 sperm cryopreservation counseling if she has not already addressed this\n\n5. **Resources:** Refer to SCI-specific sexual health resources that are LGBTQ+ inclusive. Many traditional SCI sexual health materials are cisnormative and may not apply.')
B.aO_=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT",'**Rooming and Privacy Accommodations:**\n- **Room assignment:** She should be roomed as a female patient, consistent with her gender identity and legal documentation. If the facility has semi-private rooms, she should be roomed with a female patient.\n- **If no private rooms are available and a female roommate objects:** She has the legal and ethical right to appropriate rooming. The objecting patient should be offered a room change \u2014 the transgender patient should NOT be moved to accommodate the objection.\n- **Privacy for personal care:** Ensure privacy during catheterization, bowel care, bathing, and dressing. Use of a consistent care team who she is comfortable with is ideal.\n\n**Catheterization Considerations:**\n- She has male urethral anatomy \u2014 intermittent catheterization technique uses male catheter length (16 French, 40 cm)\n- Teach self-catheterization using correct anatomical approach while using affirming language\n- If she is uncomfortable with self-catheterization due to genital dysphoria, acknowledge this: "I understand that this part of your care may be particularly uncomfortable given how you feel about your body. We can work together to find an approach that minimizes distress."\n- Consider options: self-catheterization with mirror avoidance technique, touchless/closed system catheter kits, or if severe dysphoria, discuss suprapubic catheter as an alternative (though this has its own complications)\n\n**Appropriate Physical Examination:**\n- Perform examinations relevant to the clinical situation \u2014 genital/rectal exam for SCI is medically necessary\n- Explain the medical necessity clearly before any genital examination\n- Offer choice of examiner gender when possible\n- Use affirming anatomical language or the patient\'s preferred terms\n- Breast examination as clinically indicated (breast tissue is present from hormone therapy and has the same cancer screening needs as cisgender women)\n\n**Rehabilitation Program:**\n- Standard T6 ASIA A rehabilitation protocol: wheelchair mobility training, transfers, pressure relief, bowel and bladder management, upper extremity strengthening, community reintegration\n- Account for reduced upper body muscle mass from estrogen therapy in functional goal-setting \u2014 she may need a lighter wheelchair, may take longer to achieve independent transfers than cisgender male T6 SCI norms\n- Orthostatic hypotension management \u2014 spironolactone may exacerbate; monitor closely\n- Autonomic dysreflexia education \u2014 T6 level is at the threshold; catheterization and bowel care are common triggers\n\n**Emotional Support:**\n- The intersection of new SCI with pre-existing gender identity challenges creates a compounded adjustment burden\n- Psychology referral: therapist experienced with both SCI adjustment and gender identity\n- Peer support: connect with LGBTQ+ SCI support networks if available\n- Monitor for recurrence of depression/anxiety')
B.aPH=new A.c("+ Management question",'**Q: She is struggling with intermittent catheterization and reports severe distress: "Having to touch myself there multiple times a day is unbearable. It makes me feel like my body is betraying me all over again." How do you address this?**\n\n**A:** This is a collision between SCI medical necessity and gender dysphoria that requires creative, patient-centered problem-solving:\n\n1. **Validate the distress:** "I hear you, and I understand that this is not just a physical challenge \u2014 it touches something much deeper about how you relate to your body. Your feelings are valid, and we are going to find a way to manage this that respects both your medical needs and your well-being."\n\n2. **Explore the specific trigger:** Is the distress from touching natal genitalia? From visual confrontation with anatomy? From the frequency of the task? Understanding the exact nature of the dysphoria guides the solution.\n\n3. **Adaptive strategies:**\n- **Tactile avoidance:** Use a closed-system catheter kit with an introducer tip that guides the catheter without direct genital contact. Pre-lubricated, no-touch systems (e.g., SpeediCath Flex) minimize manual handling.\n- **Visual avoidance:** Teach catheterization by feel rather than using a mirror. Many patients master this technique.\n- **Reframing:** Work with psychology to reframe catheterization as a medical procedure separate from gender \u2014 "This is bladder management, not a statement about your identity."\n- **Routine normalization:** Establish a consistent, private, efficient routine that makes the task automatic rather than emotionally charged.\n\n4. **Alternative options if distress remains unmanageable:**\n- **Suprapubic catheter:** Surgically placed through the abdominal wall, avoiding urethral catheterization entirely. Has higher infection risk but eliminates the dysphoria trigger. Discuss pros/cons with urology.\n- **Mitrofanoff procedure (long-term):** Creation of a catheterizable stoma from the abdomen \u2014 avoids urethral access. Major surgery but may be appropriate if dysphoria is severe and persistent.\n\n5. **Do NOT minimize or dismiss:** Telling a transgender patient to "just get used to it" regarding dysphoria-triggering medical care is harmful and will damage the therapeutic relationship.')
B.aR3=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE",'**Staff Education on Gender-Affirming Care:**\n- **Pre-admission huddle:** Before the patient arrives, brief the nursing staff, therapy team, and support staff on:\n  - Correct name and pronouns (she/her) \u2014 document prominently in the chart\n  - The patient is a woman \u2014 treat and address her as such in all interactions\n  - Gender identity is not a diagnosis, a curiosity, or a discussion topic among staff\n  - Questions about surgical status or genitalia should be limited to what is clinically necessary\n- **Ongoing education:** Arrange in-service training on LGBTQ+ culturally competent care. The Fenway Institute and WPATH provide healthcare provider education resources.\n- **Zero tolerance for misgendering:** Intentional or repeated misgendering (using wrong pronouns or former name) is a form of harassment and should be addressed through the same channels as any other unprofessional behavior.\n\n**Documentation \u2014 Legal Name vs Chosen Name:**\n- **EHR documentation:** Use her legal name (which matches her female identity and legal documents) as the primary name in the medical record. This is consistent with her insurance and legal identification.\n- **If legal name change has NOT occurred (different scenario):** Many EHR systems now support a "chosen name" or "preferred name" field. The chosen name should appear on wristbands, door cards, and communication. The legal name remains in the demographic/billing section.\n- **Pronoun documentation:** Document pronouns (she/her) in a visible location in the chart \u2014 many EHRs have a dedicated field.\n- **Problem list:** "Gender dysphoria" should only appear on the problem list if clinically active and relevant to current care. Being transgender is not a diagnosis.\n- **Organ inventory:** For clinical safety, document the patient\'s anatomy accurately (e.g., "Patient has male urogenital anatomy \u2014 intermittent catheterization per male technique"). This is medically necessary documentation, not misgendering.\n\n**Regulatory Framework:**\n- Section 1557 of the ACA prohibits discrimination based on gender identity in healthcare settings receiving federal funding\n- Joint Commission standards include respectful, non-discriminatory care regardless of gender identity\n- Many states have additional protections\n- Facility should have a written gender identity non-discrimination policy')
B.aRD=new A.c("+ Systems question","**Q: A CNA on the rehab unit tells you privately, \"I have religious beliefs that prevent me from using preferred pronouns. I will provide good physical care, but I cannot call a biological male 'she.'\" How do you handle this?**\n\n**A:** This requires firm boundary-setting while maintaining professionalism:\n\n1. **Acknowledge the CNA's honesty** in raising this privately rather than misgendering the patient publicly. This is an opportunity for education, not punitive action (initially).\n\n2. **Set a clear boundary:** \"I appreciate that you shared this with me directly. I respect that you have personal beliefs, and those beliefs are your right. However, in this clinical setting, addressing patients by their correct name and pronouns is a professional requirement, not an optional preference. Using the correct pronouns is part of providing respectful care, the same way you would address any patient by their correct name.\n\nThis patient's legal name is her female name and her legal gender marker is female. Using 'she/her' pronouns is both medically and legally accurate.\"\n\n3. **Frame it as patient safety:** \"Misgendering a patient causes measurable psychological harm. In a patient who already has anxiety and depression history, and who is adjusting to a life-changing injury, being misgendered by her care team could trigger a mental health crisis, reduce her engagement in rehabilitation, and negatively impact her outcomes. As healthcare providers, our obligation is to 'first, do no harm.' This includes psychological harm.\"\n\n4. **Offer accommodations within limits:** \"If you feel you cannot use the correct pronouns despite this guidance, I will need to reassign you from this patient's care. That is not a punishment \u2014 it is ensuring the patient receives appropriate care and you are not placed in a situation that conflicts with your conscience. However, I want to be clear: the expectation for all staff is to use correct names and pronouns for all patients. This may come up again with future patients.\"\n\n5. **Escalation if needed:** If the CNA refuses to use correct pronouns AND refuses reassignment, this becomes a human resources matter. Document the conversation and involve nursing leadership.\n\n6. **Systemic solution:** Advocate for facility-wide mandatory LGBTQ+ competency training as part of annual required education (alongside cultural competency, safety training, etc.).")
B.b_L=new A.c(u.j,'**Scenario:** The patient\'s assigned roommate (a 55-year-old woman recovering from a stroke) requests a room change, telling the nurse, "I am not comfortable sharing a room with that person. I can tell she is really a man. I don\'t feel safe changing clothes in front of a man."\n\n**Mediating While Protecting Patient Dignity:**\n\n**Step 1 \u2014 Talk to the roommate privately (without the transgender patient present):**\n\n"I understand you have a concern about your roommate. I want to address this respectfully. Your roommate is a woman. She is a transgender woman, which means she was assigned male at birth but is legally and medically a woman. She has been living as a woman for years, is on hormone therapy, and her legal documents reflect her female gender.\n\nI understand this may be unfamiliar to you, and I am not here to judge your feelings. But I want to be clear: she has the legal and medical right to be housed in a room consistent with her gender identity, and our facility has a non-discrimination policy that protects her."\n\n**Step 2 \u2014 Address the safety concern:**\n"You said you do not feel safe. Can you help me understand what specifically concerns you? If it is about physical privacy during changing or personal care, I can assure you that all patients have access to privacy curtains, and personal care for both of you is always performed in private. Your roommate is recovering from a spinal cord injury and requires significant assistance \u2014 she is not a threat to your safety in any way."\n\n**Step 3 \u2014 Offer a constructive resolution:**\n"If you remain uncomfortable, I can explore whether a room change is available \u2014 but I want to be transparent that if a room change happens, it will be YOU who moves, not her. She has done nothing wrong and will not be displaced because of another patient\'s discomfort with her identity.\n\nHowever, I would encourage you to give it some time. Many people who are initially uncomfortable find that once they get to know their roommate as a person, the discomfort fades. She is a talented graphic designer with a supportive partner, and she is facing one of the hardest challenges of her life right now \u2014 just like you."\n\n**Step 4 \u2014 Protect the transgender patient:**\n- Do NOT inform the transgender patient about the roommate\'s complaint in detail \u2014 this would cause harm and violate the spirit of creating a safe environment\n- If the roommate continues to make hostile or derogatory comments, she should be moved immediately\n- If the transgender patient senses hostility and asks to be moved, accommodate her request \u2014 but frame it as her choice, not as a response to the roommate\'s prejudice\n- Document the incident for risk management and as data supporting the need for facility-wide education\n\n**Step 5 \u2014 Use this as a teaching moment for staff:**\n- Brief the nursing team on how to handle similar situations\n- Reinforce that the transgender patient\'s room assignment is correct and should never be changed to accommodate another patient\'s bias\n- Provide scripts for staff: "All patients are assigned rooms based on their gender identity, in accordance with hospital policy and federal law"')
B.b_3=new A.c("+ Communication question","**Q: During a team conference, a therapist refers to the patient as \"the transgender patient in Room 6\" rather than by name. Another therapist asks, \"So is it a he or a she?\" How do you address this in the moment?**\n\n**A:** Intervene immediately and calmly \u2014 this is a teachable moment, not a disciplinary one:\n\n\"Let me pause here for a moment. Her name is [patient's name], and she uses she/her pronouns. Let us refer to her by name, the way we do for every other patient in our conference.\n\nTo answer the question directly \u2014 she is a woman. She is a transgender woman, which means 'she' is the correct pronoun. If you are ever unsure about a patient's pronouns, it is completely appropriate to check the chart where it is documented, or to ask the patient respectfully.\n\nI also want to flag something else \u2014 referring to her as 'the transgender patient' rather than by name defines her by one aspect of her identity rather than as a whole person. We do not say 'the diabetic patient in Room 8' \u2014 we say their name. Let us extend the same courtesy.\n\nI know these conversations can feel uncomfortable, and I appreciate that you all are open to learning. No one is expected to be perfect, but we are expected to try. If you make a pronoun mistake, correct yourself briefly and move on \u2014 do not over-apologize, as that makes it about you rather than the patient.\n\nNow, let us get back to her rehabilitation plan. She is making excellent progress with transfers and is ahead of schedule on wheelchair mobility...\"\n\nThis response:\n- Corrects the behavior without shaming\n- Provides clear guidance on correct language\n- Normalizes asking when uncertain\n- Models the behavior you expect\n- Redirects to clinical care to avoid making the patient's identity the focus of the conference\n- Sets a standard that applies to all future patients")
B.aEA=s([B.b1n,B.b0d,B.b09,B.aYL,B.aV7,B.aXQ,B.aO_,B.aPH,B.aR3,B.aRD,B.b_L,B.b_3],t.E)
B.Rc=new A.a5("special-transgender-care","Transgender Patient in Rehab \u2014 Inclusive Care",B.aEA,!1)
B.aWv=new A.c("Initial Presentation","You are a physiatrist in an outpatient interventional spine clinic. You just completed a fluoroscopy-guided sacroiliac (SI) joint injection on a 52-year-old female with chronic low back pain and left-sided SI joint dysfunction confirmed on physical examination and diagnostic block. The procedure went technically well with good contrast flow pattern and the patient tolerated it without complications. As you are completing the procedure note, you review the original order and realize the injection was ordered and consented for the **right** SI joint, but you performed it on the **left** SI joint. You look back at the clinical notes and see that the patient's pain was originally documented as **right-sided** by the referring physician 2 months ago, but your own examination 1 week ago documented **left-sided** tenderness and positive provocative testing on the left. The patient is still in the recovery area. She has not yet been told about the discrepancy.")
B.aOT=new A.c("History & Systems Review:","**Patient Background:** 52-year-old female, chronic low back pain x 3 years attributed to bilateral SI joint dysfunction (right > left per referring provider, left > right per your exam). History of lumbar fusion L4-L5 (5 years ago) with adjacent segment degeneration. Works as an elementary school teacher. On gabapentin 300 mg TID and meloxicam 15 mg daily.\n\n**Procedure Details:**\n- Order: Right SI joint injection (placed by your clinic based on referring physician's laterality)\n- Consent: Signed for right SI joint injection\n- Pre-procedure verification: Time-out was performed but the discrepancy between the order (right) and your exam findings (left) was not caught\n- Procedure performed: Left SI joint injection under fluoroscopic guidance \u2014 technically successful, appropriate needle placement confirmed with contrast\n- Medications injected: 1 mL 0.25% bupivacaine + 40 mg triamcinolone\n\n**Immediate Assessment:**\n- Patient is hemodynamically stable, no procedural complications\n- The injection itself was performed safely and correctly from a technical standpoint\n- The left SI joint WAS clinically symptomatic on YOUR examination\n- However: the consent, the order, and the referring physician's laterality all specified the right side\n- The patient may actually benefit from the left-sided injection (your exam supported left-sided pathology)\n- The patient still needs the right-sided injection per the original treatment plan\n\n**Key Complexity:** This is not a clear-cut \"wrong side\" error in the traditional sense \u2014 there is clinical justification for the left-sided injection. However, it was not the procedure that was ordered or consented for, making it a deviation from the planned care regardless of clinical rationale.")
B.aXv=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:",'**Post-Procedure Assessment:**\n- Vital signs stable: BP 132/78, HR 72, SpO2 98%\n- Patient reports feeling well, no pain at injection site\n- No neurological deficits\n- No signs of adverse reaction to medications\n- Injection site clean, no hematoma\n\n**Review of Pre-Procedure Documentation:**\n- Referring physician note (2 months ago): "Right SI joint pain, positive FABER test on right, recommending right SI joint injection"\n- Your clinic note (1 week ago): "Bilateral SI joint tenderness, left greater than right. Positive Gaenslen test left. Positive FABER left. Positive thigh thrust left. Plan: SI joint injection starting with the more symptomatic left side. Will treat right side subsequently if needed."\n- Order entered by your medical assistant: "Right SI joint injection" (defaulted to referring physician\'s laterality rather than your updated clinical assessment)\n- Consent form: "Right SI joint injection" (signed by patient based on the order)\n- Time-out documentation: "Right SI joint injection" (read from the consent form)\n\n**Root Cause Identification:**\n- Discrepancy between your clinical assessment (left) and the order/consent (right) was present before the procedure\n- Multiple points of failure: order entry error, consent discrepancy not caught during pre-procedure review, time-out did not catch the mismatch between the order and the clinician\'s intent\n- You (the proceduralist) injected the side you clinically intended (left) but did not reconcile this with the written order or consent before starting')
B.b2c=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","**Adverse Event Classification:**\n- This is a **wrong-site procedural error** per The Joint Commission and CMS definitions, regardless of clinical justification\n- Classified as a \"Never Event\" (National Quality Forum Serious Reportable Event) \u2014 procedures performed on the wrong body part\n- The fact that the injected side was clinically appropriate does not change the classification \u2014 the procedure did not match the consent or the order\n- This is a **near-miss with patient contact** \u2014 harm was minimal (the injection was safe and potentially therapeutic) but the systems failure is significant\n\n**Patient Harm Evaluation:**\n- **Physical harm:** Minimal to none. The injection was performed safely on a clinically symptomatic joint. No adverse effects from the medication. The patient received a therapeutically appropriate intervention.\n- **Procedural harm:** The patient underwent a procedure she did not specifically consent to. She still needs the originally planned right-sided injection \u2014 an additional procedure and additional risk exposure.\n- **Trust harm:** The most significant potential harm is to the patient-physician relationship if disclosure is handled poorly or delayed.\n\n**Root Cause Analysis (Preliminary):**\n1. **Order entry:** Medical assistant entered laterality from the referral rather than from your examination note. No verification step between clinician assessment and order entry.\n2. **Consent process:** Consent was generated from the order, propagating the laterality error. Neither the patient nor the physician caught the discrepancy during the consent discussion.\n3. **Time-out failure:** The pre-procedure time-out read the consent form but did not independently verify laterality with the proceduralist's clinical intent. The time-out was procedural (checkbox) rather than substantive.\n4. **Cognitive factors:** You (the proceduralist) had the correct clinical plan in your head but did not verify it matched the paperwork. Confirmation bias \u2014 you assumed the paperwork matched your intent.\n5. **System design:** No hard stop between order laterality and proceduralist's documented plan.")
B.aRw=new A.c("+ Diagnostic question",'**Q: Is this truly a medical error if the injection was clinically indicated on the left side based on your examination? Does the clinical appropriateness change the reporting obligation?**\n\n**A:** Yes, this is a medical error, and no, the clinical appropriateness does NOT change the reporting obligation:\n\n1. **Ethical analysis:** The patient consented to a right-sided injection and received a left-sided injection. Informed consent is a legal and ethical requirement for any invasive procedure. Performing a different procedure than what was consented for is a violation of patient autonomy, regardless of whether the performed procedure was clinically beneficial.\n\n2. **Legal analysis:** Wrong-site procedures are "never events" per CMS and TJC. They must be reported through institutional channels and to the state health department (in most states). Clinical justification is a mitigating factor in potential liability but does not eliminate the reporting requirement.\n\n3. **Regulatory analysis:** CMS Conditions of Participation require reporting of all never events. Failure to report is a separate violation and could jeopardize the facility\'s CMS certification.\n\n4. **Nuance for this case:** In peer review and any subsequent legal proceedings, the fact that you had a documented clinical rationale for the left-sided injection is highly relevant. Your pre-procedure clinic note supports left-sided pathology. This shifts the root cause from a random wrong-site error to a **systems failure in order reconciliation** \u2014 a more defensible position, but still requiring full disclosure and reporting.\n\n5. **Personal learning:** The correct course of action would have been to recognize the order/consent discrepancy BEFORE the procedure, correct the order, re-consent for the left side, and then proceed. Going forward, this becomes a personal practice pattern \u2014 always verify that the consent matches your clinical intent, not just that a consent exists.')
B.aXA=new A.c("+ Challenge question",'**Q: Your medical assistant, who entered the incorrect order, is visibly upset and says, "This is my fault. Am I going to be fired?" How do you handle this?**\n\n**A:** This is an opportunity to model a just culture response:\n\n"First, I want to tell you that this is NOT solely your fault. Multiple people \u2014 including me \u2014 had the opportunity to catch this discrepancy, and none of us did. I am the one who performed the procedure without verifying the consent matched my clinical plan. The ultimate responsibility is mine.\n\nHere is how I think about this: you entered the order based on the referral, which is a reasonable thing to do. The problem is that we do not have a system in place to verify that the order matches my updated clinical assessment. That is a system gap, not a personal failure.\n\nYou are not going to be fired for a systems error. What I need from you is two things:\n1. Be honest in the incident report about what happened \u2014 exactly as it occurred, without embellishment or minimization.\n2. Help me design a better process so this cannot happen again \u2014 you understand the workflow better than anyone.\n\nIn a just culture, we do not punish people for honest errors within a flawed system. We punish reckless behavior. Entering an order from a referral is not reckless. If anything, the system failed you by not requiring a verification step."\n\n**Important distinctions in just culture:**\n- **Human error (this case):** Inadvertent action, slip, or mistake in a system with inadequate safeguards. Response: system redesign, not punishment.\n- **At-risk behavior:** Conscious deviation from safe practice (e.g., skipping time-out). Response: coaching, not punishment.\n- **Reckless behavior:** Conscious disregard of substantial risk (e.g., performing a procedure while impaired). Response: disciplinary action.\n\nThis MA\'s action was human error in a system without adequate checks. The appropriate response is to fix the system.')
B.b3G=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT",'**Immediate Patient Monitoring:**\n- Continue standard post-injection monitoring in recovery area (30 minutes)\n- Assess for any adverse effects from the injection (allergic reaction, vasovagal, neurological changes)\n- The injection itself was performed safely \u2014 no additional monitoring required beyond standard protocol\n\n**Correct-Side Injection Rescheduling:**\n- The patient still needs the right SI joint injection per the original treatment plan\n- Schedule the right-sided injection in 2-4 weeks (standard interval for bilateral injections)\n- New consent specifically for the right side with explicit laterality verification\n- Consider the left-sided injection as a therapeutic intervention for her documented left-sided pathology \u2014 it may provide clinical benefit and can count toward her treatment course\n\n**System Fix Implementation (Immediate):**\n\n**1. Time-Out Protocol Enhancement:**\n- Implement "active verification" rather than passive reading of the consent form\n- Proceduralist must state independently (without looking at consent): "I am performing [procedure] on the [side]"\n- This is then verified against the consent form by the nurse or technologist\n- Any discrepancy halts the procedure until reconciled\n\n**2. Order Entry Verification:**\n- Implement a required field: "Laterality verified by: [clinician name]" at order entry\n- Clinician must personally verify laterality matches their clinical assessment before order is finalized\n- Order cannot be entered by support staff without clinician co-signature on laterality\n\n**3. Consent Process Improvement:**\n- Consent discussion must include patient\'s own confirmation of the symptomatic side: "Can you point to the side that hurts most?"\n- Marking the intended injection site with indelible marker prior to procedure (already standard for surgical procedures but often omitted for injections)\n\n**4. Pre-Procedure Checklist:**\n- Add a mandatory reconciliation step: compare referral laterality, clinic exam laterality, order laterality, and consent laterality\n- If any discrepancy exists, the procedure cannot proceed until resolved\n\n**Documentation:**\n- Amend the procedure note to accurately reflect what occurred: "Left SI joint injection performed. Note: consent and order specified right SI joint. Discrepancy identified post-procedure. Patient notified. See disclosure note."')
B.aVn=new A.c("+ Management question",'**Q: At the 2-week follow-up, the patient reports 80% pain relief from the left-sided injection. She says, "I guess you injected the right side after all \u2014 the one that was hurting." How do you handle this?**\n\n**A:** This is a critical moment \u2014 the temptation to let the misunderstanding stand is significant but must be resisted:\n\n"Actually, I want to clarify something important. I injected your left SI joint, not your right. As I explained to you on the day of the procedure, there was a discrepancy between the original order for the right side and what I found on my examination, which pointed to the left side as the more symptomatic joint.\n\nThe good news is that the left-sided injection has clearly helped you \u2014 80% relief is an excellent result. This confirms that the left SI joint was indeed a significant pain generator, which is consistent with what I found on your exam.\n\nNow, the question is whether we still need to address the right SI joint. Based on how you are feeling, let me re-examine you today and we can make that decision together."\n\n**Why this matters:**\n- Letting the patient believe the right side was injected would compound the original error with a deception\n- If the right side is never injected and pain returns, the patient would be confused about her own treatment history\n- Accurate patient understanding of their treatment is essential for future medical decision-making\n- Transparency, even when uncomfortable, builds long-term trust\n\n**Clinical decision:** If she has 80% relief from the left injection alone, the right side may not need injection at this time. Reassess clinically and only inject the right side if residual right-sided symptoms warrant it. This case may demonstrate that the referring physician\'s laterality was incorrect and the physiatrist\'s examination was more accurate \u2014 useful information for the patient\'s long-term pain management plan.')
B.b3A=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE",'**Institutional Error Reporting:**\n\n**1. Internal Reporting (Immediate):**\n- File an institutional incident/safety event report through the hospital\'s electronic reporting system\n- Classify as a wrong-site procedure (Never Event)\n- Include all contributing factors identified in root cause analysis\n- Notify department chief/medical director\n- Notify risk management\n\n**2. Patient Safety Organization (PSO) Reporting:**\n- If the institution participates in a federally designated PSO, this event should be reported through the PSO channel\n- PSO reporting provides legal privilege and confidentiality protections for quality improvement activities under the Patient Safety and Quality Improvement Act of 2005\n- PSO analysis aggregates wrong-site events across institutions to identify common system failures\n\n**3. State Reporting:**\n- Most states require mandatory reporting of Never Events to the state health department\n- Reporting timelines vary (typically 5-7 business days after discovery)\n- Failure to report is a separate regulatory violation\n\n**4. CMS Reporting:**\n- CMS requires reporting of Never Events as a condition of participation\n- CMS does not reimburse for care related to Never Events \u2014 the facility absorbs the cost of the original procedure and any corrective procedures\n\n**Malpractice Risk Management:**\n- **Early disclosure reduces litigation risk:** Studies consistently show that transparent, empathetic disclosure reduces malpractice claims by 30-40% compared to concealment or defensive responses\n- **Contact professional liability insurer:** Notify your malpractice carrier within 24 hours of the event. This is typically a policy requirement.\n- **Documentation principles:** Document factually. Record what happened, when it was discovered, what the patient was told, and what corrective actions were taken. Do NOT use words like "error," "negligence," or "fault" in the medical record \u2014 use descriptive language. Reserve analysis for the privileged incident report.\n- **Apology laws:** Many states have "apology laws" (I\'m Sorry laws) that protect expressions of empathy and apology from being used as evidence of liability. Know your state\'s law.\n- **Risk of concealment:** NOT disclosing the error is far more legally dangerous than disclosing. If discovered later (and it will be \u2014 through billing, records review, or the patient\'s own observation), concealment transforms a defensible error into potential fraud.\n\n**Quality Improvement:**\n- Present de-identified case at department morbidity and mortality (M&M) conference\n- Develop laterality verification protocol applicable to all injection procedures\n- Audit compliance with enhanced time-out process quarterly\n- Consider simulation training for the procedural team incorporating laterality verification scenarios')
B.aZj=new A.c("+ Systems question",'**Q: A colleague who learns about the event says, "I would never disclose that. The patient benefited from the injection. Telling her just opens you up to a lawsuit for no reason." How do you respond?**\n\n**A:** This advice, though well-intentioned, is ethically and practically wrong:\n\n1. **Ethical obligation:** The AMA Code of Medical Ethics (Opinion 8.6) explicitly states that physicians must disclose adverse events and medical errors to patients. This is not optional. The patient has a right to know what was done to her body, especially when it differed from what she consented to.\n\n2. **Legal reality:** Non-disclosure is far more legally dangerous than disclosure. If the patient discovers the discrepancy through billing records, future imaging, or another provider\'s review, the concealment becomes evidence of consciousness of guilt. A malpractice case built on a concealed error is exponentially more damaging than one built on a disclosed error with corrective action.\n\n3. **Evidence base:** The Michigan model and similar programs demonstrate that **proactive disclosure plus early offer of resolution reduces malpractice claims, litigation costs, and defense expenses**. CRICO (Harvard\'s malpractice insurer) data shows that failure to communicate is a contributing factor in over 30% of malpractice cases.\n\n4. **Institutional mandate:** Most hospitals have mandatory disclosure policies for adverse events. Concealing a reported incident would be a separate policy violation.\n\n5. **Personal integrity:** "I understand your instinct to protect me, and I appreciate it. But I could not look this patient in the eye at her follow-up visit knowing I concealed something from her. If I cannot be honest with my patients when I make a mistake, I have no business asking them to trust me with their care.\n\nThe relationship I have with my patients is built on trust. One injection on the wrong side will not destroy that trust \u2014 but hiding it would."\n\n6. **Systemic concern:** If the colleague\'s advice reflects institutional culture, this represents a deeper patient safety problem that should be raised with quality leadership.')
B.aV2=new A.c(u.j,'**Scenario:** You walk into the recovery area where the patient is resting comfortably after the procedure. She smiles and says, "That was not bad at all. I hope it helps. When can I go back to work?" You need to disclose the wrong-site error.\n\n**Honest, Empathetic Disclosure Without Defensive Language:**\n\n**Step 1 \u2014 Sit down and establish presence:** Do not hover or appear rushed. Sit at the patient\'s eye level. Make eye contact. Your body language must communicate that this is important and you are giving it your full attention.\n\n**Step 2 \u2014 Begin with what happened, simply and clearly:**\n"Before we talk about your recovery, I need to share something important with you. I want to be completely transparent.\n\nDuring the procedure today, I injected your left SI joint. However, the order and your consent form specified the right SI joint. I discovered this discrepancy as I was completing the paperwork after the procedure."\n\n**Step 3 \u2014 Pause and let her process.** Do not rush to fill the silence.\n\n**Step 4 \u2014 Explain the context without making excuses:**\n"Here is the full picture: when I examined you last week, your left side was clearly the more symptomatic joint \u2014 the side causing you the most pain. That is the side I intended to inject and the side I did inject. However, the original referral specified the right side, and that is what was entered in the order and on the consent form. I did not catch the discrepancy between my clinical plan and the written documentation before the procedure. That is my responsibility."\n\n**Step 5 \u2014 Acknowledge the error directly:**\n"This should not have happened. Regardless of the clinical reasoning, you consented to a right-sided injection and received a left-sided injection. I am sorry this occurred."\n\n**Step 6 \u2014 Address safety and next steps:**\n"The good news is that the left-sided injection was performed safely and is likely to help your pain based on my examination findings. You are not in any danger. However, you still may need the right-sided injection as originally planned. I would like to schedule that in two to three weeks, and we will go through the entire consent and verification process again with enhanced safeguards.\n\nI have also reported this to our quality and safety team so we can improve our procedures to prevent this from happening to any patient in the future."\n\n**Step 7 \u2014 Invite questions and emotions:**\n"I know this is not what you expected to hear. You have every right to be upset, concerned, or to ask me any questions. How are you feeling about this?"\n\n**Language to AVOID:**\n- "It was technically the correct side anyway" (minimizing)\n- "The system failed" (deflecting personal responsibility)\n- "This never happens" (dismissive and likely untrue)\n- "You should be fine" (premature reassurance)\n- "It was just a misunderstanding" (downplaying)\n- "The referral was wrong" (blaming others)')
B.aVK=new A.c("+ Communication question",'**Q: The patient becomes angry and says, "How can I trust you to do the right side if you could not even do the correct side the first time? I want a different doctor." How do you respond?**\n\n**A:** Accept the response without defensiveness:\n\n"Your reaction is completely understandable, and you have every right to feel this way. If the situation were reversed, I would feel the same. Trust is earned, and I understand that my actions today have damaged yours.\n\nIf you would like a different physician to perform the right-sided injection, I will make that happen. I can refer you to my colleague Dr. [name], who is equally qualified, and I will provide a complete summary of your case so there is no gap in your care. Your comfort and confidence in your physician are essential.\n\nIf, after some time, you decide you are willing to give me another chance, I want you to know what I have already done to prevent this from ever happening again: we are implementing a new laterality verification protocol where I personally confirm the side before any order is entered, and we are adding a patient-confirmation step where I will ask you to point to the side we are treating before we begin. These changes will be in place before your next procedure, regardless of which physician performs it.\n\nEither way, I want to assure you of two things: first, the injection you received today was on a joint that my examination showed was symptomatic, so it may actually help your pain. Second, I will continue to be available if you have any questions or concerns, even if you choose to see a different physician going forward.\n\nIs there anything else you would like to know or anything else I can do for you right now?"\n\n**Key principles in this response:**\n- Do not argue or try to change her mind\n- Do not take her anger personally or respond defensively\n- Offer concrete alternatives (different physician) without making her feel she is overreacting\n- Demonstrate corrective action already taken\n- Leave the door open for the relationship to recover\n- Maintain focus on her needs, not your feelings\n- Do not grovel or over-apologize \u2014 that shifts the emotional labor to the patient to reassure YOU')
B.aEd=s([B.aWv,B.aOT,B.aXv,B.b2c,B.aRw,B.aXA,B.b3G,B.aVn,B.b3A,B.aZj,B.aV2,B.aVK],t.E)
B.R6=new A.a5("special-medical-error","Medical Error Disclosure \u2014 Wrong-Side Injection",B.aEd,!1)
B.aUW=new A.c("Initial Presentation",'A 22-year-old female is transferred to acute inpatient rehabilitation following a T12 burst fracture with ASIA A paraplegia sustained when she jumped from a highway overpass 4 weeks ago. She underwent posterior spinal instrumentation and fusion T10-L2. She was medically cleared by the trauma surgery team and psychiatrically cleared by the inpatient consultation-liaison psychiatry team for transfer to rehabilitation. Her psychiatric clearance notes indicate: major depressive disorder (severe, recurrent), borderline personality disorder traits, history of non-suicidal self-injury (cutting) since age 14, and one prior suicide attempt at age 19 (intentional medication overdose requiring ICU admission). She was started on fluoxetine 40 mg daily and quetiapine 100 mg qHS during the acute hospitalization. The psychiatrist notes she is "no longer acutely suicidal but remains ambivalent about survival" and recommends intensive psychological support during rehabilitation. She arrives on the rehab unit making minimal eye contact, answering questions with single words.')
B.b0i=new A.c("History & Systems Review:",'**Psychiatric History:**\n- Major depressive disorder diagnosed age 16, multiple medication trials (sertraline, escitalopram, bupropion \u2014 partial responses)\n- Borderline personality disorder traits: emotional dysregulation, unstable relationships, chronic emptiness, non-suicidal self-injury (cutting forearms) since age 14\n- Prior suicide attempt: age 19, acetaminophen overdose requiring N-acetylcysteine treatment and ICU admission\n- Current attempt: jumped from overpass after breakup with boyfriend of 3 years who discovered she was self-harming again. She called 911 herself within minutes of jumping.\n- Outpatient treatment: intermittent therapy compliance, no consistent therapist. Had been seeing a DBT-trained therapist for 6 months before the attempt (last session 2 weeks prior)\n\n**Current Medications:** Fluoxetine 40 mg daily, quetiapine 100 mg qHS, gabapentin 300 mg TID, docusate/senna, subcutaneous heparin, acetaminophen PRN (pharmacy flagged \u2014 limited quantity per dose, no bottles at bedside given overdose history).\n\n**Social History:** College senior (communications major) \u2014 was 6 credits from graduation. Lives in a college apartment alone (was living with ex-boyfriend until 2 months ago). Parents divorced when she was 8. Mother lives locally, is involved but "overwhelmed" per social work. Father is minimally involved (lives out of state, history of alcohol use disorder). Has a younger brother (age 19) at a different college. Small friend group at school. No employment currently. Health insurance through mother\'s employer.\n\n**SCI Details:** T12 ASIA A complete paraplegia. Neurogenic bladder (indwelling Foley, planned intermittent catheterization transition). Neurogenic bowel (program to be initiated). No sacral sparing. Intact upper extremities. DEXA scan shows low bone density (osteopenia \u2014 relevant given age and injury mechanism).')
B.b0p=new A.c("Physical Examination Findings:",'**General:** Thin (BMI 19), appears younger than stated age. Multiple healed linear scars on bilateral forearms (non-suicidal self-injury). Avoids eye contact. Answers questions with 1-2 word responses. No spontaneous conversation.\n\n**Neurological:** T12 ASIA A complete paraplegia. Upper extremities 5/5 throughout. Lower extremities 0/5 throughout. Sensation absent below T12. Bulbocavernosus reflex absent. No voluntary anal contraction.\n\n**Musculoskeletal:** Surgical incision T10-L2 healing well, staples removed. Upper body strength intact but deconditioned. No upper extremity contractures.\n\n**Skin:** Healed linear scars on bilateral volar forearms (multiple, parallel, various stages of healing \u2014 most recent appear several weeks old). No active wounds besides surgical incision. No pressure injuries. Ischial tuberosities intact.\n\n**Functional Status:** Moderate assist for bed mobility, maximal assist for transfers, unable to propel wheelchair (has not yet attempted). Dependent for all lower body ADLs. FIM score 52/126.\n\n**Mental Status:**\n- PHQ-9: 18/27 (moderately severe depression)\n- Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale (C-SSRS): Denies current suicidal ideation (intensity 0), but endorsed "passive death wish" \u2014 "I don\'t want to kill myself, but I wouldn\'t mind if I didn\'t wake up"\n- Beck Hopelessness Scale: 14/20 (moderate hopelessness)\n- Affect: flat, constricted range. Tearful when asked about the events leading to the attempt.\n- Insight: "I know jumping was stupid. But I still don\'t know why I should try to get better."')
B.aS5=new A.c("DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING","**Psychiatric Stability Assessment:**\n- The psychiatric clearance for rehabilitation means the patient is no longer in the acute phase of suicidal crisis \u2014 it does NOT mean she is psychiatrically stable or at low risk\n- **Current risk level: Moderate-High.** Risk factors include: recent high-lethality attempt, prior attempt, active passive suicidal ideation, borderline personality traits, recent relationship loss, chronic depression, self-injury history, ambivalence about survival, and new catastrophic disability\n- **Protective factors:** Called 911 herself (ambivalence about death, help-seeking behavior), mother involved, young age (greater neuroplasticity for adjustment), some prior therapy engagement, medications initiated\n\n**Suicide Risk Screening Tools:**\n- **Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale (C-SSRS):** Gold standard for structured risk assessment. Should be administered at admission and repeated at regular intervals (weekly minimum). Distinguishes passive ideation, active ideation without plan, active ideation with plan, and behavior.\n- **PHQ-9 Item 9:** Screens for suicidal ideation but is not sufficient alone. Her score of 18 with positive item 9 warrants comprehensive assessment.\n- **Beck Hopelessness Scale:** Hopelessness is the strongest predictor of future suicide attempts \u2014 her score of 14 (moderate) is concerning and should be tracked over time.\n- **SAFE-T (Suicide Assessment Five-Step Triage):** Structured framework for ongoing risk assessment.\n\n**Medication Safety:**\n- **Fluoxetine:** Appropriate choice \u2014 evidence base for depression AND for reducing impulsive aggression in borderline personality. Black box warning for increased suicidality in young adults (<25) \u2014 monitor closely during first 4-8 weeks. She is 22, within the warning range.\n- **Quetiapine:** Sedation benefit for sleep, mood stabilization benefit for BPD traits, low lethality in overdose compared to other options. Appropriate choice.\n- **Gabapentin:** Low overdose lethality, useful for neuropathic pain. Appropriate.\n- **Acetaminophen:** Given her prior overdose with acetaminophen, this medication must be strictly controlled \u2014 dispensed by nursing per dose, no bottles at bedside, pharmacy-flagged for quantity limits.\n- **Medications to AVOID:** Tricyclic antidepressants (lethal in overdose), large quantities of any medication accessible to patient, medications requiring sharp objects for administration (injectable formulations when possible alternatives exist).")
B.aZE=new A.c("+ Diagnostic question",'**Q: She denies suicidal ideation on the C-SSRS but her therapist reports she seems "disconnected" and "going through the motions." Is she safe?**\n\n**A:** Denial of suicidal ideation on a structured screening tool does NOT guarantee safety, especially in this patient:\n\n1. **Clinical vs instrument discrepancy:** Screening tools like C-SSRS capture what the patient is willing to report. Patients with borderline personality traits may:\n- Dissociate from their emotional state and genuinely not recognize ideation\n- Suppress disclosure to avoid perceived consequences (1:1 monitoring, transfer to psychiatric unit)\n- Experience rapidly fluctuating suicidal ideation that is absent at the moment of screening but present hours later\n\n2. **"Going through the motions" is concerning:** Emotional disconnection, flat affect, and passive compliance can represent:\n- **Dissociation:** A coping mechanism but also a risk state \u2014 dissociated patients can act impulsively without apparent forewarning\n- **Resigned hopelessness:** More dangerous than active distress \u2014 patients who have "given up" may not endorse ideation because they do not see a future worth saving\n- **Therapeutic suppression:** She may be complying with rehab to avoid consequences rather than because she sees value in recovery\n\n3. **Recommended response:**\n- Do NOT rely solely on C-SSRS scores for safety decisions\n- Increase frequency of informal therapeutic contact \u2014 daily psychology check-ins, not just structured assessments\n- Observe for behavioral warning signs: giving away possessions, sudden calmness after period of distress, withdrawal from relationships, searching for methods online\n- Maintain current safety precautions regardless of screening tool results\n- Staff who interact with her daily (nurses, aides, therapists) are often the first to notice changes \u2014 create a formal communication pathway for them to report concerns\n- Reassess medications \u2014 if her affect is this flat on fluoxetine + quetiapine, consider whether the medications are contributing to emotional blunting vs treating depression')
B.b_i=new A.c("+ Challenge question",'**Q: The patient asks her physical therapist, "What\'s the highest floor in this hospital?" The therapist is unsure if this is a safety concern or a casual question. How should this be handled?**\n\n**A:** This statement must be treated as a potential safety concern until proven otherwise, given the patient\'s history of jumping from a height:\n\n1. **Immediate therapist response:** The therapist should respond calmly and directly without showing alarm: "I noticed you asked about the highest floor. Can you help me understand what made you think of that?" This opens the conversation without accusation.\n\n2. **Regardless of her answer,** the therapist must:\n- Document the statement verbatim in the medical record\n- Report immediately to the treating physician and nursing\n- Report to the psychology/psychiatry team\n\n3. **Clinical assessment within the hour:** A member of the psychiatric care team (psychologist or psychiatrist) should conduct a focused risk assessment. The question has specific relevance because her method of attempt was jumping from a height \u2014 asking about high floors could represent:\n- **Method fixation:** Planning or fantasizing about repeating her attempt\n- **Trauma processing:** Intrusive thoughts about the attempt without active intent\n- **Casual inquiry:** Less likely given her specific history but possible\n\n4. **Safety response:** Until the assessment is completed, implement enhanced safety measures: 1:1 observation, restrict access to stairwells and upper floors, ensure windows are secured.\n\n5. **Staff education point:** In post-suicide attempt rehabilitation, ALL seemingly innocent statements about heights, falls, access to means, or "getting out" should be reported and assessed. It is far better to over-report and find no concern than to dismiss a genuine warning sign. Staff should never feel they are "overreacting" for reporting.\n\n6. **Create a culture of reporting:** Frame reporting as "caring about the patient," not "getting the patient in trouble." The patient herself may be ambivalently reaching out for help through this statement \u2014 missing it would be a failure of the system.')
B.b1t=new A.c("DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT",'**SCI Rehab with Concurrent Psychiatric Care:**\n- **Dual-track rehabilitation model:** Standard T12 paraplegia rehabilitation program (wheelchair mobility, transfers, ADLs, bowel/bladder management) running concurrently with intensive psychiatric/psychological treatment\n- **Therapy schedule adaptation:** Schedule rehab therapy sessions when the patient is most engaged (may not be early morning if sedated from quetiapine). Allow flexibility for therapy cancellations on high-distress days without punitive documentation.\n- **Trauma-informed approach:** All staff interactions should be trauma-informed. Avoid power struggles, respect her sense of control, explain all interventions before performing them, obtain verbal consent for hands-on therapy.\n\n**1:1 Monitoring Protocol:**\n- Continuous 1:1 observation during acute risk period (first 1-2 weeks minimum)\n- Trained sitter (not just nursing aide) who understands suicide precaution behaviors\n- Sitter must maintain line-of-sight at ALL times including bathroom (with privacy curtain but line of sight to feet)\n- De-escalation protocol: if the patient becomes agitated, sitter calls for nursing/behavioral health support rather than physical restraint (restraint is retraumatizing)\n- Gradual step-down: transition from 1:1 to every-15-minute checks only after formal reassessment by psychiatry\n\n**Sharps Restrictions and Environmental Safety:**\n- Room safety sweep: remove sharps containers from room, secure IV poles, remove plastic bags, ensure no ligature points (shower heads, curtain rods), window locks confirmed\n- No sharps at bedside: razors, scissors, nail clippers, glass cups\n- Medications dispensed one dose at a time by nursing \u2014 no bedside medication storage\n- Personal items searched (with patient\'s knowledge and consent when possible) for sharps, hoarded medications, or other potential means\n- Monitor for improvised self-harm: pen caps, broken plastic, abrasive surfaces \u2014 patients with self-injury history are resourceful\n\n**Therapy Engagement Strategies:**\n- **Do not force participation** \u2014 forced compliance increases resistance and damages the therapeutic alliance\n- **Motivational approach:** Find one thing she cares about and build around it. If she values independence: "Learning to transfer means you can leave a room whenever you want." If she values academics: "We can set up your laptop so you can complete your last 6 credits from here."\n- **Small goals:** Frame daily rehab goals as choices, not mandates. "Today you can work on wheelchair skills or transfer training \u2014 which would you prefer?"\n- **Peer support:** If available, connect with a young adult SCI peer mentor (NOT another post-suicide attempt patient \u2014 shared normalization of suicidality is dangerous)\n- **Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT) integration:** Given her BPD traits and self-injury history, DBT skills (distress tolerance, emotional regulation, mindfulness) should be incorporated daily by psychology')
B.aUB=new A.c("+ Management question",'**Q: During her third week, nursing discovers superficial cuts on her left forearm made with a broken plastic cup. She is calm and says, "It\'s not a suicide thing. It helps me feel something. I\'ve done this since I was 14." How do you respond clinically?**\n\n**A:** Non-suicidal self-injury (NSSI) in this context requires a calibrated response that takes the behavior seriously without overreacting:\n\n1. **Immediate medical care:** Assess the wounds \u2014 clean, dress, photograph for documentation. Determine if any wounds require sutures. Check tetanus status.\n\n2. **Remove the means:** Environmental sweep of her room for broken or sharp objects. Review all items provided to the patient.\n\n3. **Distinguish NSSI from suicidal behavior (but do not dismiss):**\n- Her statement is likely accurate \u2014 NSSI and suicidal behavior are distinct phenomena. NSSI is used for emotional regulation (feeling something when dissociated, releasing emotional pain, regaining control).\n- HOWEVER, NSSI is a significant risk factor for future suicide attempts, and in a patient with a recent high-lethality attempt, it signals that her emotional regulation is decompensating.\n- This is NOT "just cutting" \u2014 it is a red flag that her current psychiatric treatment is insufficient.\n\n4. **Clinical response:**\n- **Increase psychiatric involvement:** Urgent psychiatry reassessment. Consider medication adjustment (is fluoxetine effective? Is the dose adequate? Consider adding or switching medications).\n- **Intensify DBT skills training:** Daily distress tolerance skills practice. Teach replacement behaviors: ice cubes on skin, red marker on arms, intense physical sensation (holding ice, cold shower) as alternatives to cutting.\n- **Create a safety plan collaboratively WITH her:** Use Stanley and Brown\'s Safety Planning Intervention. Include her warning signs, coping strategies, reasons for living, and contacts for help.\n- **Do NOT punish or shame:** Confiscating privileges, expressing disappointment, or implementing restrictive measures as consequences will worsen the behavior. NSSI is a coping mechanism \u2014 removing it without replacing it leaves her with no emotional outlet.\n\n5. **Communication with her:** "I believe you that this helps you manage overwhelming feelings. I am not angry and you are not in trouble. But I am concerned because it tells me you are hurting more than the current plan is addressing. I want to work with you and the psychiatrist to find safer ways to manage those feelings. Can we do that together?"')
B.b04=new A.c("DOMAIN D: SYSTEMS-BASED PRACTICE","**Inpatient Psychiatry Coordination:**\n- **Consultation-liaison model:** Psychiatry provides regular (at minimum twice-weekly) follow-up during rehab admission. This should be a scheduled, predictable service \u2014 not just crisis-driven.\n- **Psychology:** Daily sessions during initial high-risk period, transitioning to 3-4x/week as stability improves. DBT-informed approach is ideal. Trauma processing (the attempt itself, the resulting SCI) should begin only when the patient is stabilized \u2014 premature trauma processing can destabilize.\n- **Medication management by psychiatry,** not physiatry. Coordinate medication changes to avoid impacts on rehabilitation participation.\n- **Communication structure:** Daily huddle between rehab team and psych team. Use a shared safety communication tool (e.g., structured handoff at shift change including current risk level).\n\n**Lethal Means Restriction:**\n- **Environmental controls:** Already described (room safety sweep, sharps restriction, medication control)\n- **Discharge planning for means restriction:**\n  - Home assessment before discharge: remove or secure firearms (if any), medications (all medications in a locked box managed by a designated person, not the patient), sharp objects, access to heights\n  - Lethal means counseling with the patient AND her mother: educate on the importance of means restriction as a life-saving intervention\n  - She should NOT live alone after discharge \u2014 arrange for mother or roommate cohabitation during the highest-risk period (3-6 months post-attempt)\n\n**Outpatient Safety Planning:**\n- **Crisis contacts:** 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline, Crisis Text Line (text HOME to 741741), local mobile crisis team number, her psychiatrist's after-hours number\n- **Safety plan:** Completed before discharge using the Stanley-Brown Safety Planning Intervention. Physical copies to patient, mother, and outpatient providers.\n- **Outpatient psychiatric follow-up within 72 hours of discharge** \u2014 the post-discharge period is the highest risk for completed suicide. Arrange this before discharge, not as a recommendation.\n- **Outpatient therapist:** Resume care with her pre-existing DBT therapist if possible (therapeutic relationship continuity is valuable). If not, arrange a warm handoff to a new therapist.\n- **SCI follow-up:** Physiatry follow-up in 2 weeks. SCI-specific support group. Ensure continuity of bowel/bladder supplies and equipment.\n- **Academic re-engagement:** If she wants to complete her degree, occupational therapy can facilitate adaptive technology setup and communication with the university disability services office.")
B.aXw=new A.c("+ Systems question",'**Q: Your rehab unit does not have a locked unit, 1:1 sitters are expensive and in short supply, and the nursing staff is uncomfortable caring for a suicidal patient. How do you address these systemic barriers?**\n\n**A:** These are real, common barriers that must be addressed proactively:\n\n1. **1:1 sitter resources:** If institutional sitters are unavailable, explore alternatives: family member (mother) as supervised companion during daytime hours (with training on what to observe and report), nursing student clinical placement focused on psychiatric nursing, telehealth-based virtual sitter technology (camera monitoring with centralized monitoring staff). Insurance authorization for 1:1 should be pursued \u2014 document medical necessity based on recent high-lethality attempt and ongoing passive suicidal ideation.\n\n2. **Unlocked unit safety:** Implement environmental modifications: assign a room closest to the nursing station, ensure visual line of sight from the hallway, use bed alarm for nocturnal monitoring, restrict access to exits (programming her wheelchair to require staff assistance for door access if possible), and implement structured check-in protocols (every 15 minutes at minimum).\n\n3. **Staff discomfort:** This is the most important barrier to address because it directly affects patient care:\n- **Education session:** Provide focused training for the rehab team on caring for post-suicide-attempt patients. Cover: risk assessment basics, warning signs, therapeutic communication, what to do when a patient makes concerning statements, documentation requirements.\n- **Reduce fear through structure:** Staff are uncomfortable because they feel untrained and responsible for preventing suicide. Provide clear protocols: "If the patient says X, do Y. If you observe Z, call the physician." Structure reduces anxiety.\n- **Normalize the clinical scenario:** Post-suicide-attempt rehabilitation is an expected part of SCI care \u2014 up to 10% of traumatic SCI results from self-harm. This is not an unusual case; it is a predictable one that the unit should be prepared for.\n- **Debriefing and support:** Schedule weekly team debriefs for staff caring for this patient. Acknowledge the emotional toll. Provide access to employee assistance program (EAP) for staff who are struggling.\n\n4. **If the patient decompensates to active suicidal crisis:** Have a clear transfer protocol to an inpatient psychiatric unit. Ensure the receiving unit can manage her SCI needs (wheelchair accessibility, bowel/bladder program). This may require a medical-psychiatric dual unit or creative co-management arrangement.')
B.aZp=new A.c(u.j,'**Scenario:** During a physical therapy session on day 12, the patient stops mid-transfer and says quietly to the therapist: "What\'s the point of any of this? I wish I had died. At least then I wouldn\'t be stuck in a wheelchair forever."\n\n**Immediate Response Protocol While Building Therapeutic Rapport:**\n\n**What the therapist should do immediately:**\n\n1. **Stop the therapy activity.** This statement takes priority over the session. Put down any equipment. Give the patient your full attention.\n\n2. **Respond with calm, non-judgmental presence:**\n"I heard what you said, and I am not going to pretend I didn\'t. What you just shared matters to me. Can you tell me more about what you are feeling right now?"\n\n3. **Do NOT:**\n- Panic or show alarm (this makes the patient feel her feelings are dangerous and she should hide them)\n- Say "Don\'t say that" or "You don\'t mean that" (invalidating)\n- Launch into a motivational speech about recovery potential (premature and dismissive)\n- Immediately leave to call someone (this feels like abandonment and punishment)\n- Share personal stories of adversity or compare her suffering to others\n\n4. **Listen actively.** Let her talk. She may have more to say. Silence is acceptable. Do not rush to fill it.\n\n5. **Validate without agreeing:**\n"It makes sense that you feel this way. You are dealing with something incredibly painful \u2014 both the injury and everything that led to it. Feeling like this is overwhelming and wanting an escape from it is understandable."\n\n6. **Assess for immediacy:**\n"When you say you wish you had died \u2014 are you telling me that you are thinking about hurting yourself right now, or are you telling me about how much pain you are in?"\n\n*If she endorses active ideation with plan or intent: activate emergency protocol \u2014 stay with her, call for help, do not leave her alone.*\n\n*If she describes pain/hopelessness without active intent: continue the conversation.*\n\n7. **Build the bridge to support:**\n"Thank you for telling me. I know it is not easy to say something like that out loud. I am going to stay with you right now, and I would also like to have [psychologist/psychiatrist name] come talk with you today \u2014 not because you are in trouble, but because what you are feeling deserves more support than I can offer in a PT session. Is that okay?"\n\n8. **After the immediate interaction:**\n- Document the statement verbatim in the medical record\n- Notify the attending physiatrist, psychology, and nursing immediately\n- Update the C-SSRS assessment\n- Review and update the safety plan\n- Continue 1:1 monitoring per protocol\n\n**For the attending physiatrist (you):**\n- When you see the patient later that day, do NOT pretend you do not know what she said\n- "I heard you had a hard moment during PT today. I wanted to check in with you about it."\n- This communicates that the team is connected and she is not being surveilled \u2014 she is being cared for\n- Reassess medication regimen with psychiatry\n- Consider whether rehab intensity needs temporary reduction to match her psychological capacity')
B.aOZ=new A.c("+ Communication question",'**Q: Her mother calls you in tears: "I blame myself for this. If I had been a better mother, she wouldn\'t have jumped. Now she\'s paralyzed because of me. What am I supposed to do?" How do you respond?**\n\n**A:** Caregiver guilt after a loved one\'s suicide attempt is nearly universal and requires its own therapeutic response:\n\n"I can hear how much pain you are in, and I want to take a moment to address what you just said, because it is important.\n\nYou are not the reason your daughter jumped. Suicide attempts are the result of a complex interaction of mental illness, emotional crisis, and overwhelming pain \u2014 they are not caused by parenting. Your daughter has a serious psychiatric illness that she has been struggling with since adolescence. Depression and borderline personality disorder are brain-based conditions, not reflections of how she was raised.\n\nI also want to point out something that tells me a great deal about your relationship: she called 911 herself within minutes of jumping. That act \u2014 reaching out for help in the worst moment of her life \u2014 tells me she has a connection to life and to the people who love her. That connection does not develop without a parent who cares.\n\nNow, let me address your question \u2014 what are you supposed to do:\n\n1. **First, take care of yourself.** You cannot support your daughter through this recovery if you are drowning in guilt and grief. I am going to ask our social worker to connect you with a support group for family members of suicide attempt survivors. I also strongly recommend individual counseling for you \u2014 not because something is wrong with you, but because what you are going through is traumatic.\n\n2. **Be present without being perfect.** Your daughter does not need you to fix this. She needs you to show up consistently, to sit with her even when she does not want to talk, and to let her know you are not going anywhere. You do not have to say the right thing \u2014 just being there says enough.\n\n3. **Learn about her conditions.** Understanding depression and borderline personality disorder will help you make sense of her behaviors without personalizing them. The National Alliance on Mental Illness (NAMI) has a Family-to-Family program that is excellent.\n\n4. **Help with the practical things.** Right now, the most tangible ways you can help are: attending family therapy sessions when invited, learning her bowel and bladder program so you can assist at home, helping prepare her apartment for wheelchair accessibility, and being her advocate with insurance and disability paperwork.\n\n5. **Set boundaries when needed.** Supporting your daughter through recovery does not mean sacrificing your own well-being. It is okay to ask for breaks, to say you are overwhelmed, and to set limits on what you can provide.\n\nYou are already doing something right \u2014 you are here, you are asking how to help, and you clearly love your daughter deeply. That matters more than you know."')
